window.NHI_PLAIN = {"1-4-billion-year-old-mushroom-fossil-in-brazil":"An intact fossil of a mushroom about 1.4 billion years old was found in northeastern Brazil, according to Jesse Michels. He says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode built around the idea that mushrooms might be alien in origin. If it is true, mushrooms would be far older than most people imagine.","101-year-old-recalls-a-giant-graveyard-found-while-ploughing-1920":"Some time in the 1920s or 1930s, men ploughing land for a vineyard near a nuraghe, one of Sardinia's ancient stone towers, suddenly 'start unearthing the remains of gigantic people.' The documentary maker Tim Alberino tells Michels he heard this from a 101-year-old named Anna Tuary, who remembered it. In a second telling Alberino adds that vans pulled up, people got out with equipment while the witness hid, the remains were taken away, and a passage in a tower was sealed off, which would mean someone deliberately removed evidence of giants.","111-and-222-synchronicities-during-the-reunion-trip-2026":"During a 2026 trip in Texas that reunited Air Force veterans who say they were abducted by aliens about fifty years earlier, Michels kept noticing the numbers 111 and 222 showing up on the clock. He says it himself on the show: one person \"landed at 111\", and a man named Omar, travelling in from a group called Yes Theory, was \"taking off at 111.\" Michels offers these as meaningful coincidences, a hint that something beyond chance was hanging over the reunion.","13-year-old-corbell-hears-knapp-s-lazar-interview-on-the-rad-1990":"In 1990, at 13, the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell heard a radio interview in which a reporter named George Knapp questioned a man named Bob Lazar, who said he had worked on alien craft for the government. Corbell tells Michels he remembers it first-hand: \"I heard George Knapp's voice interviewing a guy named Bob Lazar on the radio.\" He tells it as the moment his own interest in the subject began.","15th-16th-century-explorers-accounts-of-amazon-cities":"European explorers of the 15th and 16th centuries are said to have reported seeing cities in the Amazon rainforest. Michels brings this up himself while talking with the writer Graham Hancock. In Michels's framing, those old explorers' reports drove Hancock's research, and Hancock later found evidence that backed them up, which would mean the explorers' stories were more than fantasy.","17-days-of-drone-swarms-over-langley-2023":"In 2023, drones are said to have swarmed Langley Air Force Base in Hampton, Virginia, for 17 days, and nobody could stop them. Michels tells it on several shows, adding that 'we have no defense against this' and that a former defense official named Chris Mellon said there was a mothership. A former Navy fighter pilot named Ryan Graves says the base houses F-22 jets and that they had to be moved to another base for fear of losing them. A news anchor, in a clip played on the show, says Air Force officials confirmed that 'several unmanned drones flew over Langley' the previous December, without giving a length of time. If true, it would mean unknown craft could loiter over a major US base at will.","170-odd-q-cleared-witnesses-of-ufos-at-sensitive-sites":"Michels says that some 170 people holding Q clearances, a high US security clearance for nuclear weapons work, have reported seeing UFOs around the country's most sensitive sites, and that in some cases the objects shut those sites down. He tells this himself, without naming a source. If true, it would mean unknown objects have repeatedly interfered with US nuclear facilities.","1800-case-balls-of-light-come-out-of-the-water":"In a case from 1800, balls of light are said to have come up out of the sea, hovered beside a ship, followed it, and then shot up into the clouds. A researcher named Kevin Knuth tells this to Michels without naming his source, as evidence that such sightings go back long before aircraft existed.","1890s-airship-mystery-and-nymza-per-walter-bosley-and-joseph":"In the 1890s, people across the United States reported mysterious airships in the sky, and a researcher named Walter Bosley, a friend of the writer Joseph Farrell, has argued that a group he calls NYMZA was behind them. Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist known for patents on unusual craft, raises it on the show without spelling out the details, asking \"Who were these guys?\" and saying the idea worries him, since it would mean someone was flying advanced craft long before anyone admitted it.","1890s-airship-sightings-as-a-culturally-shaped-perception":"In the 1890s, people reported seeing airships that were at the very edge of what the technology of the day could do. Jesse Michels raises this as an example of sightings that seem to take the shape of whatever technology is about to arrive. In another episode he credits the idea to the Harvard psychiatrist John Mack, and there he frames it as UFOs inspiring or foreshadowing the inventions that come next.","19-non-negotiable-demands-to-change-the-dark-skies-pilot-and-1996":"In 1996, the makers of Dark Skies, a television series about UFOs, were handed \"a list of 19 non-negotiable demands of things that had to change in our pilot\", and then Don Ohlmeyer, an NBC executive, stepped in. The television writer Bryce Zabel, who worked on the pilot, tells this first-hand, from his time in Los Angeles. He tells it together with his writing partner Brent Friedman, who was also there, and he mentions Steven Spielberg along the way. The story shows how much pressure the show was under before it ever aired.","1904-chilean-cattle-mutilations":"In 1904, Michels says, Chile saw a series of cattle mutilations, meaning livestock found dead and cut in ways nobody could explain. He mentions it briefly, attributing it to the YouTuber Chris Ramsay, and gives no other details. If true, it would place such cases decades before the ones usually discussed.","1911-superconductor-discovery-as-a-model-for-uap-behaviour-a":"The 1911 discovery of superconductivity by the physicist Onnes might explain how UAP, the newer official term for UFOs, seem to break the laws of physics. That is the argument of the researcher Deep Prasad, who tells Michels that superconductors, materials that carry electricity with no resistance, looked impossible at first. They turned out to be an emergent effect of strange quantum mechanics underneath. Prasad, giving no named source, suggests UAP behaviour could be the same kind of large-scale quantum effect, which would make it explainable physics rather than magic.","1918-us-expeditionary-force-into-russia":"In 1918, according to a researcher named Dan Sheehan, the United States paid for and sent \"a full-scale US financed US military foreign expeditionary force into Russia\". Sheehan says this to Michels without naming a source, and he mentions Robert Lansing in connection with it. If he is right, it was a sizable American military venture that few people remember.","1930s-homesteader-accounts-of-cattle-mutilation-on-the-prope":"Settlers on a Utah property now known as Skinwalker Ranch reported strangely mutilated cattle and other odd happenings as far back as the 1930s. Brandon Fugal, who presents himself on the show as the ranch's owner, tells Michels that his team's investigation turned up these early homesteader accounts, without naming them, as a sign that the strangeness predates the ranch's modern fame.","1934-plot-to-overthrow-fdr-via-general-smedley-butler":"In 1934, the story goes, a group of people tried to recruit General Smedley Butler, the commandant of the US Marine Corps, to lead a major armed uprising of war veterans against Franklin Roosevelt's government. Dan Sheehan, a guest Michels presents as a man tied to many of America's hidden affairs, tells it as something he heard from others; if true, it would mean a planned coup against a sitting president.","1941-fbi-vetting-of-silas-newton":"In 1941 the FBI looked into Silas Newton, a man it kept files on alongside a partner named Leo GeBauer, and who later became central to the story of a craft said to have crashed at Aztec. Michels reads from the files himself, quoting a July 1, 1941 entry: \"he appears to be a good American citizen, has no accent in his speech\". He sets it beside the book It Came to Earth at Aztec and the work of the researchers Scott and Suzanne Ramsey, as a look at how the government saw Newton years before the crash story.","1942-fbi-file-laurel-canyon-address-norfolk-compromat":"An FBI file on the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, kept in 1942 and 1943, places him \"in Laurel Canyon on Wonderland Boulevard\" in Los Angeles, according to Michels, who offers it as \"a clue\" that also points toward Norfolk, Virginia and toward compromising material that could be used against someone. Michels tells this himself in his film about Brown, a scientist he says built flying-saucer-like devices for the government, drawing on the writers Paul Schatzkin and Paul LaViolette. What the clue is meant to show is about the hidden side of Brown's life and work.","1944-press-reports-of-silver-spheres-over-germany":"In 1944 the mainstream press reported strange silver balls in the skies over wartime Germany, says Marik von Rennenkampff, a writer who studies the history of UFO reports. He tells Michels, without pointing to a further source, that Newsweek ran a piece in 1944 called \"The Silver Sphere Puzzle,\" and that on Christmas Day 1944 the Associated Press carried a story headlined \"Balls of silver seen over the German Reich intrigue science.\" He brings them up as evidence that sightings of unexplained objects were in the news years before the UFO wave of the late 1940s.","1945-state-department-document-on-nazi-linked-companies-in-l":"A State Department document from 1945 is said to note that hundreds of new companies had already been set up in Nicaragua and Argentina, as part of a story about Nazi-linked money moving to Latin America. The researcher Jason Simosa brings it up on the show, drawing on the writer Peter Levenda, and he gives his own summary rather than the document's words. If he is right, it would mean US officials knew right away that Nazi money and networks were escaping abroad as the war ended.","1945-trinity-crash-as-the-source-of-a-larger-piece":"The story is that in 1945, in the weeks after the first atomic bomb test at the Trinity site in New Mexico, a craft crashed nearby, and Michels says a larger piece of strange material came from that crash. Michels raises it several times, in one episode citing the ufologist Jacques Vallée's then new book and giving the date as August 17, 1945, in another putting it 20 days after the July 1945 test, and once dating it to 1948 as one of a run of crashes near nuclear sites. Vallée tells it on the show second-hand, crediting it to people he names as Paola Harris, Remy Baca and Jose Padilla and to his book Trinity: The Best-Kept Secret: he places it near San Antonio, New Mexico, and says that through an opening where one panel was missing, the onlookers saw three creatures that were human-like, with two eyes. A researcher named Jason Jorjani, retelling Vallée's book, says the craft was bomb-shaped and that the Mexican locals who came across the beings said they looked like praying mantises, and a clip of an unnamed witness on the same episode describes them as \"baldheaded,\" with slanted teardrop eyes and no ears. Another guest, Marik von Rennenkampff, mentions it only tentatively, saying there was \"possibly\" a crash at Trinity and that it is \"unclear.\" If true, it would tie UFO crashes to the very start of the nuclear age.","1946-military-search-of-swedish-lakes-for-ghost-rockets":"In 1946, when Swedes were reporting \"ghost rockets\", rocket-like objects streaking across the sky, the Swedish military searched several lakes for anything that had come down. According to the Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn, who tells it from his study of the cases, all they found were indentations at the bottom of the lakes, marks that suggest something hit but left nothing behind.","1947-national-security-act-and-majestic-12":"A researcher named Dylan Borland ties the National Security Act of 1947 to Majestic 12, a secret committee said to have handled crashed UFOs and their occupants. He tells Michels that the Act created the Air Force, the CIA and the National Security Council, and asks how closely Majestic 12 was tied to that Council. He gives this as his own summary, without naming a source. As he reads it, the Act built the very institutions a long cover-up would have run through, though he offers that as a question rather than as a finding.","1949-battellewright-field-contract-on-titanium-nickel-alloys":"In 1949, Michels says, the Battelle Memorial Institute, a private research lab, signed a contract with Wright Field in Ohio to study nickel-titanium alloys, the family of metal later known as Nitinol. Michels tells it himself without citing a source, and points out that Wright Field became Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, where wreckage from Roswell, the 1947 crash in New Mexico that some say was an alien craft, was rumoured to have been taken. The suggestion is that the metal research may have started from studying that debris.","1949-green-fireball-meeting-at-los-alamos":"In 1949 a meeting at Los Alamos, the New Mexico nuclear weapons lab, took up what a document of the time calls the \"green fireball problem\", strange green lights reported in the sky, and the physicist Edward Teller's name is on that document along with the astronomer Lincoln LaPaz. Michels tells this himself, citing the conference document without quoting it. If it is true, some of the top weapons scientists of the day were drawn into the question.","1950s-anti-gravity-research-went-quiet-possibly-went-black":"In the 1950s, many groups worked openly on anti-gravity, the effort to cancel or control gravity, and then the work went silent. One reading is that it went black, meaning it moved into secret programmes because it worked. The physicist Hal Puthoff says, without naming a source, that there are two ways to read the silence: it \"quieted down because nobody ever got anywhere\" or \"because it did get somewhere and it went black.\" The whistleblower David Grusch, speaking second-hand, says \"there was all these anti-grav groups up until the early sixties and then they, you know, totally vaporized.\" He puts the end a little later than the 1950s and does not choose between the two readings.","1950s-ffrdcs-and-a-program-to-dole-out-retrieved-material":"In the 1950s there may have been a programme to hand out material recovered from crashed craft to the government-funded research centres called FFRDCs (federally funded research and development centres). The idea is that this material sped up and inspired new technology. Michels raises this himself as something he wonders about, agreeing with what the online researcher UAPGerb had just said, and with the late Army officer Philip J. Corso also named in the conversation. If true, some modern technology would trace back to retrieved non-human material.","1950s-flying-butane-tanks-in-air-force-osi-reports":"David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who went public as a whistleblower, says the strange objects of the 1950s were basically the same thing pilots report now. He points to reports from the Air Force Office of Special Investigations (OSI), the Air Force's own investigative branch, that described what looked like flying butane tanks, and tells Michels that \"the tic tac of today was the flying butane tank photo of the 1950s.\" The Tic Tac is a white oval object, shaped like the mint, that Navy pilots have described, so his point is that the same kind of craft has been seen, and filed away, for decades.","1950s-gravity-research-by-bryce-dewitt-deser-and-arnowitt":"In the 1950s, Michels says, the physicist Bryce DeWitt was already writing about superconductivity and about rotating superconductors that \"would maybe create gravitational perturbations\", in other words small disturbances in gravity. Michels raises it himself in a conversation with a guest named Dylan Borland and names the physicists Stanley Deser and Richard Arnowitt alongside DeWitt, without citing a particular paper. He brings it up as a sign that serious scientists were looking into ways to control gravity decades ago.","1950s-underground-bunker-under-secret-mountain":"A deep underground military bunker was built in the 1950s under Secret Mountain, near Sedona, Arizona. That is the claim of the Australian investigative journalist Ross Coulthart, who tells Michels he is \"in no doubt whatsoever\" about it and credits two sources, Jeremiah Hostetler and Alexandra Hostetler. He repeats it in a later episode without naming them, as part of his case that the site is a UFO base.","1952-h-bomb-test-as-a-signal-to-the-cosmos":"A researcher named Eric Weinstein worries that the 1952 hydrogen bomb test in the Pacific worked as a signal: \"what if there was someone there to hear what we said\"? He has made the point on several episodes and words it differently each time. Once he says listeners would have heard that we were \"on the verge of being able to come visit\", once that we were \"a hair's breadth away from being in possession of God-like knowledge\", and once, in a version Michels relays secondhand, that we were close to \"root level knowledge\", which would mean the bomb drew outside attention to humanity.","1952-mit-lincoln-lab-audio-of-dod-saucer-briefing":"A 1952 audio recording exists of a senior Department of Defense official briefing scientists at MIT about flying saucers, according to Eric Burlison, a member of Congress. He tells Michels it turned up at MIT's Lincoln Laboratory, a defense research lab, and at first places it in the 1940s before correcting himself to 1952. If true, it would show the Pentagon talking about saucers with top scientists that early.","1952-official-denial-of-secret-development":"In 1952 in Washington, after a run of sightings, an official whose name is not given told the public: \"We can say that the recent sightings are in no way connected with any secret development by any department of the United States.\" The statement is played on Michels's episode about UFOs and nuclear weapons. If it was accurate, whatever people were seeing was not a secret American aircraft.","1952-washington-d-c-sightings":"Over two weekends in July 1952, according to the show, strange lights and objects were seen and tracked on radar over Washington, D.C., so many that newspapers ran headlines like 'saucers on the White House lawn'. Michels tells it most often, giving the dates as July 19 and 20 and July 26 and 27. He says it prompted a phone call between President Truman and Edward J. Ruppelt, who then headed Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and that it sent the CIA 'into a frenzy' over how to handle the UFO problem; he credits that call to an aviation writer named Curtis Peebles. Karl Nell, speaking as a former official, adds that the sightings were seen both by eye and on radar, included jets sent up to intercept them, and led to 'the largest press conference since World War II'. Bob Maguire, a CIA contractor, says Truman was badly shaken, that he 'lost his lunch over it'. Michels also says he recently met one of the last living witnesses, and the scientist John Brandenburg mentions the overflights in passing. If it happened as told, unknown objects flew over the nation's capital in plain view of radar and the public.","1952-washington-dc-flyover":"In 1952, unexplained objects are said to have flown over Washington DC, and it became front-page news. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and sums up the mood with a headline he remembers as \"saucers on the White House lawn.\" He raises it as a moment when the UFO question landed right at the seat of the US government.","1953-kingman-crash-and-upshot-knothole":"A craft of unknown origin is said to have crashed near Kingman, Arizona, in 1953, and one teller suggests that US nuclear tests that year, a series called Operation Upshot-Knothole, may have interfered with it. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb lists it among the \"testimonies\" of crashes and says the testing \"possibly\" messed with the craft, without naming a source. Richard Dolan, a historian of UFO cases, tells Michels \"there's no question in my mind that Kingman was the real thing,\" crediting the UFO researcher Raymond Fowler, but he says nothing about the nuclear tests. If true, it would mean the government recovered a crashed craft decades ago.","1954-atomic-energy-act-moved-craft-custody-to-contractor-ope":"The theory is that a recovered craft was moved in 1954 because a new nuclear law let government-owned labs run by private contractors, such as the Sandia lab, take charge of it. A guest who goes by the name UAPGerb and researches government UFO programs puts it this way to Michels: the 1954 act 'gave expanded powers to government-owned contractor operated laboratories like Sandia and so forth to house the craft.' He does not say which part of the law he means. The Atomic Energy Act, passed by Congress on 30 August 1954, says in section 41 b. that 'the Commission is authorized to make, or to continue in effect, contracts with persons obligating them to produce special nuclear material in facilities owned by the Commission,' and it allows research contracts for work there. That is the government-owned, contractor-run setup he describes. The act speaks of nuclear material and research; that such labs were given a craft to keep, and that this is why one moved in 1954, is his step, which the act neither states nor rules out. If he is right, it would explain why craft would sit with private contractors, out of the military's direct hands.","1956-montgolfier-vacuum-tests-in-paris":"In 1956, the American inventor Townsend Brown, whom the episode calls a CIA scientist who built UFO-like craft, is said to have tested his devices inside a vacuum at the Montgolfier facility in Paris. Michels tells it himself, drawing on a document he calls the Montgolfier report and on a man named Paul Schatzkin: the tests were arranged by Jean-Louis Cornillon, \"a French Air Force officer and technical representative\" for the large French aircraft company Sud Aviation. If true, it shows Brown's work drew serious interest from the French aviation world.","1957-afghanistan-ufo-event-reported-to-wright-patterson":"A UFO event is said to have happened in Afghanistan in 1957 and to have been reported to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio, where the Air Force gathered UFO reports. Michels tells this himself, saying he found the declassified document while digging through old records and calling the find \"incredible.\" If true, it would show the US military tracking UFO sightings far beyond its own borders.","1957-ufos-head-toward-eisenhower-s-summer-white-house":"In 1957, Michels says, a UFO was tracked heading toward President Eisenhower's summer home. He credits the UFO researcher Brad Sparks with documenting it \"thoroughly\" from records of NORAD, the North American air-defense command, and of Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. If so, an unknown object was moving toward the president himself, and the government wrote it down.","1960s-nasa-electrostatic-accidents-spin-test-facility-and-ap":"In the 1960s at Kennedy Space Center in Florida, static electricity is said to have set off a solid rocket by accident inside a spin test facility, trapping 11 people and killing some of them; together with the Apollo fire, NASA lost about 14 people to electrostatics. Charles Buhler, a NASA scientist, tells Michels this as something he was told, to show how powerful and dangerous electrostatic forces are.","1960s-patent-for-nuclear-powered-tunneling-device-and-underg":"In the 1960s someone patented a nuclear-powered machine for boring tunnels, said to be tied to the construction firm Bechtel and to secret underground links running between military sites such as Area 51, a classified Air Force test site in Nevada, Edwards and Dugway, and from Washington, D.C. out to the Virginia mountains. Steven Greer, a researcher who campaigns for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, tells Michels that \"in our archive we have the actual 1960s patent for a nuclear-powered tunneling device.\" If true, it would mean the government had the means to build a hidden network of tunnels between its bases.","1963-f8-atlas-missile-tracked-by-a-tagalong":"In 1963 an Atlas F missile, a US intercontinental missile, is said to have been followed during flight from Vandenberg Air Force Base in California by an unknown object riding alongside it, a \"tagalong.\" Michels tells this himself and says \"it's even in the report,\" without naming the report. If true, something unknown was taking an interest in American nuclear missiles.","1964-nickel-found-in-drill-site":"A nickel dated 1964 recently came up from a drill site on the mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange happenings. Someone who was there says so on an episode with the ranch's owner, and says a man named Chris Roberts \"recently brought out a 1964 aged nickel.\" The question it raises is how a coin that old ended up where the drilling reached.","1970s-flanagan-samples-find-calcium-sulfate-inside-the-great":"Samples taken inside the Great Pyramid in the 1970s are said to have found calcium sulfate salt in the Queen's Chamber and the Grand Gallery. Geoffrey Drumm, who argues the pyramid was built as a chemical machine, tells Michels he takes this from the writer Christopher Dunn's book The Giza Power Plant, which reports an analysis done by Richard Flanigan. Drumm calls the salt his favourite evidence, because he says it could only come from weak sulfuric acid reacting with the limestone walls, and he adds that he saw pock marks on the stone on recent tours; if he is right, acid once flowed through those chambers.","1970s-muon-scan-of-the-central-pyramid":"In the 1970s scientists scanned the central pyramid at Giza with muons, particles from space that pass through rock and can show hidden empty spaces, according to a pyramid researcher named Jeffrey Drum. He tells Michels he has the paper with him and says the physicist Luis Alvarez took part in the work. He says they scanned the Belzoni chamber, a known room inside the pyramid, and the area above it, and \"didn't find anything.\"","1972-rand-very-high-speed-transit-system-study":"In 1972 the RAND Corporation, a research body that works for the US government, wrote a theoretical study of a \"very high-speed transit system\". A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb names it to Michels in passing, as something anyone can find in about five minutes. He offers it as the kind of public paper that points to the advanced research he says the government was quietly backing at the time, but he says nothing about what is in it.","197374-radar-station-crew-see-a-cigar-shaped-object":"Around 1973 or 1974, staff at a mountain radar station in the far north of Sweden saw a cigar-shaped object. The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels that he interviewed six people from that station himself. Several witnesses at a military radar site would make the sighting harder to wave away.","1974-coyame-crash-and-cia-directed-retrieval-plan":"In 1974, an unknown craft is said to have crashed near Coyame, in Mexico, and been recovered by American forces. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels it is a favourite story of his. He admits there is \"not a ton of supporting evidence\", but says that if true it is \"very very disturbing\". He describes something called the DEB report as a CIA-directed logistics plan, the kind that \"the OGA\" (short for \"other government agency\") would use to gather rapid reaction teams to go and recover the craft. On another episode he links Coyame and the Majestic documents, papers about a supposed secret UFO committee, to cases where toxic substances seeped out of recovered crashes. Michels tells it himself from a leaked report that he says surfaced in the 1990s. In his version there was a mid-air collision, the whole Mexican team that reached the site ended up dead, and US forces airlifted the object out. If true, it would mean the US ran secret crash recoveries on foreign soil.","1974-film-ufos-past-present-and-future":"By 1974 the official line on UFOs seemed to \"flip,\" Michels says, and the documentary UFOs: Past, Present and Future came out that year, following the US Air Force's Blue Book study of the 1950s and 60s and the University of Colorado's Condon report of October 1968. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and in another episode adds that the television writer Rod Serling, his father's godfather, worked on what Michels calls the preeminent UFO documentary, which he did not know until recently.","1975-norad-report-of-ufos-over-malmstrom":"A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas says a 1975 report from NORAD, the North American air defence command, records UFO sightings over the nuclear missile sites of Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana. He raises it with Michels as another sign that UFOs kept appearing over these nuclear weapons, which, if true, would mean an ongoing interest in America's nuclear arsenal.","1975-nuclear-base-incursions-cable-to-the-pentagon":"In 1975, unknown objects are said to have made incursions over US nuclear weapons bases, and a cable reporting them was sent to the Pentagon. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff raises it on the show, urging people to \"read the cable,\" which he says lets you see the incursions reported \"literally\" to the Pentagon, and he credits the records collector John Greenewald Jr. If true, it would mean the military was formally tracking intrusions over its most sensitive sites.","1980-object-sank-in-a-northern-swedish-lake-radar-return-in":"In 1980, an object is said to have landed and then sunk into a lake called Damaya in a national park in the far north of Sweden. The Swedish researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels that his group has been to the lake and has radar readings showing something lying in the mud at the bottom. If true, the object might still be there to recover.","1980s-physicists-wormhole-billiard-ball-calculations-show-se":"In the 1980s, physicists interested in wormholes, theoretical shortcuts through space and time, worked out what would happen if a billiard ball were sent back through one to meet its earlier self, and found the math allowed only outcomes that stayed consistent with themselves. Eric Wargo, a writer on precognition, says this on the show without naming the papers, as support for his view that seeing the future need not create paradoxes.","1980s-shift-from-secrecy-to-flooding-the-zone":"Michels suggests that in the 1980s the US government changed its approach to UFOs, moving from secrecy and denial to \"flood the zone\", meaning putting out so much mixed information that the truth gets lost in it. He offers this as his own impression, tying it to a man he names as Rick Dodie. If so, the confusion around the subject since then would be partly by design.","1980s-spinning-gyroscope-mass-loss-papers":"Papers from the 1980s describe spinning gyroscopes that lost some of their mass when certain electrical and other components were applied to them, in magnetically shielded labs. That is according to Dave Rossi, a guest who says aliens taught him advanced physics. He tells Michels this work went private in the late 1970s, though he names no specific paper. If true, it would hint at a way to alter weight, the kind of effect often linked to anti-gravity.","1981-black-sea-exercise-sighting-cameras-seized-and-secrecy":"During military exercises on the Black Sea in 1981, several dozen unidentified craft flew straight in, and people photographed them with simple cameras. Afterward, the story goes, the cameras were seized and people signed pledges to keep quiet. A man introduced as the president of a Russian republic tells Michels he was there, and he tells it as proof that the Soviet military saw these craft up close and buried it.","1982-dream-of-2060-flying-cia-ufos-after-nuclear-war-of-2034":"In 1982 a former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen had a dream of the year 2060 in which he travelled with UFOs out of an underground CIA base, in a world already in apocalypse after \"the great nuclear war of 2034.\" Mowatt-Larssen, who tells Michels he considers himself a kind of time traveller, says he remembers the year 2060 specifically and that he was going \"back and forth\" in the dream. If it were a true glimpse ahead, it would foretell a nuclear war within the next decade.","1984-string-theory-takeover-the-only-game-in-town":"In 1984, says a researcher named Eric Weinstein, theoretical physics bet nearly everything on string theory, the idea that the smallest pieces of matter are tiny vibrating strings. He says that after the Green-Schwarz anomaly cancellation, a mathematical result that made string theory look workable, the physicist Ed Witten, \"Lewis Whitten's son\", steered \"the entire field\" to \"put its energies on one bet\", and he also brings up Richard Feynman. He tells this without naming a source, and the point is that physics may have narrowed itself to a single path.","1985-night-encounter-my-god-bugs":"One night in 1985, at his cabin in upstate New York, the writer Whitley Strieber says he met strange beings, and his first thought was, \"my god, bugs.\" He tells Michels about it as his own experience. It is the encounter behind his book Communion.","1987-new-science-encyclopedia-diagram-of-pioneer-10-and-11-f":"A science encyclopedia published in 1987, the New Science Encyclopedia, is said to carry a diagram showing that the Pioneer space probes detected a large unseen object about 50 billion miles from the Sun. Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient history and lost civilisations, tells Michels this himself, pointing to a label on the diagram that reads \"equal gravitational pull\"; at one point he describes the object as a dead companion star of the Sun and credits the finding to Thomas Van Flandern and Robert Harrington, and at another he dates the encyclopedia to 1983 rather than 1987. If true, it would mean a hidden body at the edge of the solar system was known decades ago.","1988-resume-to-teller-leads-to-eg-g-and-s-4":"Bob Lazar is a man who says he worked on alien craft at S-4, a secret site in the Nevada desert. He says that in 1988 he sent a resume to the physicist Edward Teller, whom he had met once before, and asked about a job, and that this led to work through EG&G, a defence contractor, and from there to S-4. Michels repeats a version of this on two other episodes. On one he says Teller got Lazar his job, and calls the evidence for it too strong to dismiss. On another he mentions the EG&G job only in passing and places it around 1989.","1988-saic-anti-gravity-study-for-edwards-air-force-base":"In 1988 SAIC, a large defense contracting company, was hired to study electric propulsion and anti-gravity for Edwards Air Force Base in California. Michels tells this himself, citing the study and naming the physicist James Woodward and a researcher named Ferris Williams in connection with it. If it is true, the Air Force was paying for anti-gravity research in the late 1980s.","1990-air-force-low-voltage-vacuum-test-of-biefeld-brown":"In 1990, the Air Force tested the Biefeld-Brown effect, a claimed push produced by charging a device to very high voltage and named in part for the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown. According to Jesse Michels, the test was run in a vacuum at only 19 kilovolts, far below the millions of volts Brown himself used. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a documentary about Brown, and his point is that the test never fairly reproduced Brown's work.","1990s-drilling-near-the-sphinx-overseen-by-hawass":"In the 1990s, drilling was done near the Sphinx in Egypt to check for a hidden chamber, and the Egyptian archaeologist Zahi Hawass oversaw it. A researcher named Luke Caverns says this to Michels without giving a source, suggesting the search for chambers there was controlled by one man.","1990s-magazine-expose-on-assets-moved-from-area-51-to-area-5":"In the 1990s, a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb recalls, a big magazine exposé, in either Popular Science or Popular Mechanics, he is not sure which, reported that secret assets might have been moved out of Area 51, the secret military test site in Nevada, to a mysterious new place called 'Area 52' at Dugway, a military testing ground in Utah. He tells Michels this from memory, without naming the article; if true, it would mean the most secret work was quietly relocated.","1990s-meeting-of-eg-g-head-greer-dick-d-amato-and-o-donnell":"In the 1990s, the story goes, a meeting was arranged between one of the heads of EG&G, a defence contractor, and Steven Greer, a campaigner pushing for the government to release what it knows about UFOs, along with a man named Dick D'Amato, and at that meeting UFOs were taken off the table. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this second-hand, citing George Knapp as well as the men involved, among them a man named Alfred O'Donnell. The point of telling it is that people inside the defence world are said to have shut the subject down, and that would mean those insiders had something to protect.","1991-azores-case":"In 1991, something unexplained is said to have happened off the Azores, the islands in the Atlantic. A guest named Steven Greer, who says UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, mentions it only in passing (\"1991 off the Azores\") and ties it to the book Disclosure: An Illustrated History, without giving details of what was seen.","1991-bob-lazar-video-on-gravity-wave-a-and-gravity-wave-b":"In a 1991 video, a man named Bob Lazar talks about something he calls 'gravity wave A' and 'gravity wave B'. The mathematician Eric Weinstein tells Michels he came to it because the podcaster Joe Rogan pointed him to it. He calls it 'bizarre' and says Lazar seemed to be 'talking nonsense', and he raises it as a puzzle about what Lazar was actually describing.","1991-paper-on-technology-assisted-consciousness-circulated-i":"In 1991 the UFO activist Steven Greer wrote a paper, on what he connects to technology-assisted consciousness and to contact with extraterrestrials, that he says spread \"all through the aerospace industry and CIA\". He tells Michels this about himself, and says the paper \"kind of also put me on the radar\", meaning it brought him to the attention of people in those circles.","1991-radio-caller-underground-work-alongside-alien-like-bein":"In 1991, a caller to a Las Vegas radio show said he had been hired to run electricity 3,000 feet underground at a test site in Nevada, through a company he named as Reynolds Electronics, a subsidiary of a firm called EG, and that he saw military personnel \"working alongside beings that resembled aliens.\" Michels tells this himself on the show, without naming where he heard the broadcast. If true, it would mean humans and non-human beings were working together at a secret underground government site.","1992-torr-and-li-superconductor-paper":"A 1992 paper by the physicists Douglas Torr and Ning Li, at the University of Alabama in Huntsville, is said to show that a key quantum property of superconductors, the way their spin or angular momentum comes in fixed steps, sits in the holes of the material's atomic lattice rather than in the paired electrons, called Cooper pairs, that carry current without resistance. Dave Rossi, a guest who says he learned advanced physics from aliens, summarises the paper on the show as support for his own ideas about how such materials could behave.","1994-air-force-holographic-projector-concept":"In 1994 the Air Force is said to have drawn up a concept for a holographic projector, a device that would throw three-dimensional images into the sky. Michels tells this from an article by the reporter William Arkin, published in 1999, which he says described the concept. He brings it up in an episode about a UFO book the Pentagon tried to censor, because such a device, if it worked, could make people see objects that were not there.","1994-nellis-tonopah-range-video":"Michels brings up a video said to have been filmed in 1994 over the Nellis and Tonopah range in Nevada, which he describes as a restricted military testing airspace and says \"Nellis is famous.\" He raises it himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the UFO skeptic Mick West. The point of bringing it up is that something unexplained was caught on film inside some of the most closely guarded US airspace.","1996-sighting-of-a-round-craft-over-the-military-site-near-s":"In 1996 a round craft hung over a military installation outside Stockholm, Sweden, and from one angle it \"looks like a hot dog,\" according to Dennis Asberg, a Swedish treasure hunter who tells Michels he saw it himself. He adds that he woke up unusually early that day, for a reason he says he still doesn't understand, and he mentions UFO Sweden, a Swedish UFO organization. If he is right, an unknown craft flew openly over a military site.","1997-contact-10-000-people-paid-10-million-each":"Someone Steven Greer met in Washington in 1997 told him, \"We have given at least 10,000 people $10 million each or more to secure their cooperation with what we're doing.\" Greer, a UFO activist, tells Michels he heard this himself, around the time he was briefing officials in Washington, and he does not name the speaker here. If it is true, the secrecy around UFOs is held together by a huge, paid network of insiders.","1997-greer-briefing-with-admiral-wilson":"In 1997 in Washington, a meeting is said to have taken place in which Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for UFO disclosure, met Admiral Thomas Wilson along with two other men, Willard Miller and Stephen Lovekin, to brief him on what they believed was being hidden about UFOs. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb online mentions the meeting on the show as a known date in the history of secret UFO programmes, without saying where his account comes from.","1997-unod-declaring-the-programs-illegal":"In 1997, Steven Greer says, he set a clock of \"two or three months\" on a memo of the \"unless otherwise directed\" kind, in which something takes effect by default unless someone in authority orders otherwise. In this case, he says, that something was declaring the secret UFO programmes illegal. Greer, who tells Michels that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, describes it as his own move, and if it worked the way he says, those programmes would have been running without any lawful cover.","1998-remote-viewing-of-the-world-s-future-to-2050":"In 1998 a former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan was given the task of seeing the future of the United States by remote viewing, a method the Army trained people in for trying to perceive far-off places or times with the mind alone. Buchanan tells Michels the session grew beyond its task: \"I viewed the future of the world uh up through the year 2050.\" If it worked, it would mean the mind can look ahead at events still to come.","1999-paper-when-disclosure-serves-secrecy":"Back in 1999, Steven Greer, a researcher who campaigns for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, wrote a paper called \"When Disclosure Serves Secrecy\", or so he tells Michels, adding \"Go look it up.\" As its title suggests, the idea is that a controlled release of information about UFOs can actually help keep the real secrets hidden.","19th-century-baron-trump-time-travel-books":"A set of books written in the 1800s, The Adventures of Baron Trump, is said to tell of a boy named Baron Trump and a guide called Don on time-traveling adventures. Michels brings them up himself twice, once saying he thinks they were written in the 19th century and once placing them in the 1890s, and he pairs the names as \"Baron and Donald Trump.\" He raises them as an old story that seems to foreshadow a later president's family, as if something were reaching across time.","2-a-m-buzzing-and-knocks-in-florida-home":"Late one night, at about 2 a.m. in his Florida home, the writer Peter Levenda says he heard buzzing and knocking that he could not explain. He tells Michels this happened to him first-hand, years after an earlier experience. While telling it, he brings up Whitley Strieber, an author known for describing his own encounters with otherworldly visitors, as a point of comparison.","20-second-ufo-sighting-the-same-day-2016":"On the same day as other events at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange happenings, a UFO showed itself for about twenty seconds, according to Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner. He tells Michels it was \"a dramatic UFO sighting right in front of us,\" seen by him and the people with him, at some point since he bought the ranch in 2016.","200-mainstream-scientists-want-to-study-the-phenomenon-2020":"More than 200 mainstream scientists now want to study UFOs and related events, according to Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear to him often. Bledsoe tells Michels this is the figure he was given by someone he calls Dr. Anna Brady-Estevez; if true, it would mean serious science is moving toward the subject.","200-watchers-swear-an-oath-on-mount-hermon":"Two hundred heavenly beings, known as the Watchers, came down onto the top of Mount Hermon and swore an oath to stick together in a forbidden plan. That is how the researcher Tim Alberino tells it to Michels. \"They bound themselves on the summit of Mount Hermon,\" he says, pointing to the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text left out of most Bibles, and also to the King James Bible of 1611. The Book of Enoch is told in Enoch's own voice, and in R. H. Charles's English translation, published in 1917, it tells the same scene in the same order. Their leader, Semjâzâ, says he fears the others will back out and \"I alone shall have to pay the penalty of a great sin.\" They answer, \"Let us all swear an oath,\" and bind themselves \"by mutual imprecations,\" that is, by curses on one another. The book says they \"were in all two hundred\" and came down \"in the days of Jared on the summit of Mount Hermon,\" and that the mountain got its name from that oath. Alberino tells it because, read his way, it describes beings coming down from the sky to make a pact about what they would do on Earth.","2003-nasa-patent-for-an-asymmetric-barrel-shaped-capacitor":"NASA holds a patent for a barrel-shaped, lopsided capacitor, an electrical part that stores charge. Michels raises it because this is the kind of device Thomas Townsend Brown worked on, an inventor Michels profiles as a scientist who built flying saucers, so the patent would suggest NASA took Brown's idea of electric propulsion seriously. Michels tells this himself in three episodes, and the details shift. In one he dates the patent to 2003 and ties it to a man named Larry Smalley. In another he calls it a 2004 patent from NASA's Marshall Space Flight Center under a senior engineer named John Campbell, and adds a Guardian article. In a third he dates it to 2004 at Marshall and again links it to Smalley.","2004-carrier-encounter-drop-from-81-000-ft":"In 2004, off California, a craft is said to have been tracked at about 81,000 feet, more than 15 miles up. When planes were sent toward it and their instruments began to pick it up, it is said to have dropped to 24 inches above the sea in under one second. Charlie Foltz tells Michels this as something he was told by someone else, and a drop like that would be far beyond anything known to fly.","2006-raytheon-atflir-patent-and-gimbal-rotation":"The rotation seen in the Gimbal video, a Navy clip of an object that appears to turn in the sky, can be traced to a Raytheon patent for the ATFLIR, the targeting camera pod carried on Navy jets. That is the argument of Mick West, a researcher who looks for ordinary explanations of UFO videos, and he makes it to Michels while saying the patent is from 2006, 'I believe'. He uses it to argue that the turning comes from how the camera works rather than from the object itself.","2007-firestonewest-paper-on-the-younger-dryas-impact":"In 2007, a paper by the scientists Richard Firestone, Allen West, George Howard and others proposed that a cosmic impact set off the Younger Dryas, a sudden return to ice-age cold near the end of the last ice age. The researcher Randall Carlson describes it on the show as a paper \"proposing exactly that,\" the impact idea he had been arguing for himself.","2007-national-press-club-event-with-international-officials":"In 2007 a UFO investigator named James Fox and the journalist Leslie Kean put on an event at the National Press Club in Washington, where officials from several countries, among them Parviz Jafari, spoke about UFOs. Fox tells Michels he organized it with Kean, though what he says about it here is brief. They held it so that officials, not just enthusiasts, would put their own accounts on the public record.","200k-funded-fifth-dimension-gravity-theory-report-handed-in":"A scientist named John Brandenburg says a government funder he does not name paid him around $200,000 to work on a theory of gravity built on a fifth dimension, and that when he handed in his report they were glad to see him go. He tells Michels they said: \"Thank you, Dr. Brandenburg. Here's your check. Don't let the door hit you when you go out of the building.\" He tells it as a sign that the work was paid for but then set aside.","2011-irkutsk-mass-ufo-sighting":"In 2011 Russia's main state TV station, Channel 1, aired a report about a supposed mass UFO sighting in Irkutsk Oblast, a region in southeastern Siberia, where many people are said to have seen the same thing. Michels tells this himself from that broadcast, naming Nelly Stepanovna and Sergey Yazev as he does, and brings it up in an episode about strange three-fingered bodies found in Peru.","2013-white-house-rumours-of-confirmation-not-disclosure":"Early in 2013, rumours came out of the White House that officials were planning something called 'confirmation, not disclosure', meaning they would confirm that something real is going on without a full public announcement of what the government knows. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this, citing the most recent published journals of the longtime UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, where he says the rumours are recorded.","2016-trip-with-tyler-and-garry-nolan":"In 2016 the writer Diana Pasulka went with two men to a place in New Mexico where a UFO is said to have crashed and been recovered, a so-called crash retrieval site, and the trip opens her book American Cosmic, where she gave her companions pseudonyms. She tells Michels about it as her own experience, and says one of them, a scientist, was Garry Nolan, who \"has come out\" publicly since. In an interview about the book published in December 2018 she wrote that she took \"a trip with Tyler, and James, another scientist, to New Mexico to an alleged UFO crash site.\" So in print the site is only \"alleged\" and the scientist is only \"James\", and the idea that James is Nolan rests on what she said later on the show.","2017-disclosure-as-a-managed-release-of-information":"The wave of UFO revelations in 2017, around a New York Times report on the subject, was a carefully managed release, according to Congressman Eric Burlison. He tells Michels he thinks it was officials' \"attempt of trying to compartmentalize what they could release without giving away our good tech\", meaning US secret technology was kept apart from what the public saw.","2017-new-york-times-article-as-a-new-project-using-the-ufo-s":"The idea is that the New York Times article of 16 December 2017, \"Glowing Auras and 'Black Money': The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program\", by the journalists Leslie Kean and Ralph Blumenthal, was not simply news but the start of a new project that used the UFO subject for its own purposes. The researcher Greg Bishop tells Michels this was his reaction when he first saw the piece, which revealed a secret Pentagon UFO study and brought the former Pentagon official Luis Elizondo to public attention. If he is right, the story that set off the modern wave of official interest was itself a managed release of information.","2017-release-of-three-ufo-videos-to-the-new-york-times":"In 2017, the story goes, two former Pentagon insiders got three Navy UFO videos cleared for release and brought them to the New York Times. One was Luis Elizondo, who had run a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports. The other was Christopher Mellon, a former deputy assistant secretary of defense for intelligence. Michels tells it himself: after Elizondo tried to brief Defense Secretary Jim Mattis on UFOs and was blocked by \"bureaucratic intermediaries,\" the two \"took matters into their own hands.\" The videos ran with the Times article \"Glowing Auras and 'Black Money': The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program,\" published on 16 December 2017. Michels names them as the Tic Tac video from 2004, showing a white oval object shaped like the mint, and the Go Fast and Gimbal videos from 2015. He says the two men brought them to the journalist Leslie Kean, and that the Pentagon had not admitted they were real before. The lawyer Dan Sheehan adds that the Defense Department's Office of Prepublication and Security Review cleared them, so the release was authorised. The skeptic Michael Shermer tells it differently, as the moment the government said UAP (its word for UFOs) \"are real.\" If it happened as they say, it was the first time the Pentagon's own footage of unexplained craft reached the public.","2017-revelations-of-osap-ufo-study-groups":"In 2017, it came out that the US government had UFO study groups working inside a Pentagon program that Jesse Michels calls OSAP, an acronym he does not spell out. Michels mentions this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with a Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, who holds patents for unusual craft. His point is that 2017 was the year the government's quiet interest in UFOs became public.","2019-aiaa-scitech-presentation-slides-4-to-9":"The Navy scientist Salvatore Pais, whose patents have been compared to UFO technology, tells Michels that anyone who wants to understand his ideas should look at the slides he presented in 2019 at SciTech, a yearly conference of the American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics. He points to those slides, numbers 4 to 9, as where his case is laid out, though on the show he does not go through what they say.","2019-decision-with-corrado-malanga-to-scan-inside-the-khufu":"A radar scientist named Filippo Biondi says that in 2019 he and his friend Corrado Malanga decided to see whether they could scan the inside of the Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza from space. He tells Michels, as someone who did the work, that the scan used data from Italy's second-generation Cosmo-SkyMed satellites, launched in 2020, and that the results went into a paper titled \"Synthetic aperture radar Doppler tomography detects undiscovered high-resolution internal structures of the Great Pyramid of Giza\". If the method works, it would show hidden structures inside the pyramid without anyone digging.","2020-pan-starrs-object-turns-out-to-be-a-1966-nasa-booster":"In September 2020, Pan-STARRS, a telescope in Hawaii that surveys the sky for moving objects, found something in space that seemed \"similarly baffling\" to an earlier strange visitor, and it was later identified as a NASA rocket booster from 1966. Michels tells this himself in an episode with the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb, as an example of how a puzzling object in space can turn out to be human-made junk.","2022-scans-finding-hidden-chambers-in-the-great-pyramid":"Scans of the Great Pyramid of Giza in 2022 found hidden chambers beneath it, according to Jay Anderson, a guest who says this to Michels without naming a source. He brings it up as a sign that the pyramid still holds spaces and secrets no one has explained.","2023-marco-leal-interview-inconsistency-and-icu-admission-to":"Italo Venturelli, a surgeon who says his hospital in Varginha, Brazil, operated on an alien, admits that what he said in a 2023 interview with Marco Leal does not quite match his later account, including what he told James Fox, who was there, about a patient being taken into intensive care. Venturelli tells Michels his critics are \"right because I couldn't tell everything even in 2023\". As he tells it, he held things back at first, and that, not invention, is why his versions differ.","2024-egyptian-japanese-radar-survey-finds-l-shaped-structure":"In 2024, a joint team of Egyptian and Japanese researchers is said to have found an L-shaped structure underground in the western cemetery beside the Great Pyramid of Giza. Michels tells this himself in an episode about the pyramids, saying the team mapped the site with ground-penetrating radar and electrical resistivity tomography, two ways of seeing what lies below the surface without digging. He raises it as a sign that there may be more under Giza than is known.","2024-law-protecting-icig-whistleblowers-who-disclose-cia-and":"A law passed in 2024 means that whistleblowers who bring CIA or Department of Energy secrets to the ICIG, the inspector general who takes complaints from inside the intelligence agencies, cannot be prosecuted for espionage. That is how the researcher Dylan Borland reads the legislation, which he summarises for Michels without quoting it. If his reading holds, insiders with secret UFO knowledge could come forward through that office without risking espionage charges.","2025-jfk-files-release-and-trump-s-answer-on-who-killed-kenn":"In March 2025 the US government released its remaining secret files on the assassination of John F. Kennedy, and Michels points out that President Trump still would not say who did it. Michels tells it himself in an episode about Kennedy and UFOs. He says that when Trump \"was asked point blank who killed JFK after the file drop\", he answered only that \"we released the files, we did a historic thing\", or words to that effect. Michels raises it to suggest that the release did not settle the question. The release itself is on record. On March 18, 2025, the National Archives announced that \"in accordance with President Donald Trump's directive of March 17, 2025, all records previously withheld for classification\" in the Kennedy assassination collection \"are released.\" It added that the records \"were released without redactions\" and that some held personal details of living people. Trump's answer comes only from Michels' telling.","22-000-year-old-footprints-in-north-america":"Human footprints found in North America are said to be more than 20,000 years old, which would put people on the continent far earlier than usually thought. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels they are \"23,000 year-old human footprints in New Mexico\", mixed in with tracks of mammoths, mastodons, giant sloths and other extinct Ice Age animals, and stresses that \"they actually have been dated\". Michels repeats it on another episode, citing Hancock's show Ancient Apocalypse and giving the age as 22,000 years.","23-year-us-remote-viewing-program-1972":"Michels says the US ran a remote viewing program, an effort to use people who claimed they could describe distant places with their minds, for 23 years, and that if this is taken at face value there are probably covert remote viewing programs running now. In another episode he names it as the CIA's Stargate, a program he says \"they definitely had\", credits a remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle, and suggests it may still be going. He tells it himself, without naming a source.","30-missiles-lost-to-ufo-activity-1966-67":"Between September 1966 and March 1967, the US is said to have \"lost 30 missiles to UFO activity,\" meaning nuclear missiles at American bases went out of action while unexplained objects were around. Michels says this himself, crediting the researcher Bob Hastings and his book UFOs and Nukes. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, says the same thing in the same words on his own episode, and names Minot and Malmstrom as the bases. On a later episode Salas tells it again as something he lived through and puts the losses at Malmstrom Air Force Base in particular. So one teller passes on a researcher's findings and the other speaks as a witness. If true, something was able to switch off part of the American nuclear force.","36-people-who-say-they-flew-the-craft-ufo-skyps-book":"Thirty-six people, each on their own, came to the UFO researcher and author Grant Cameron and told him the same thing: \"I've flown the craft.\" Cameron tells Michels that he wrote a whole book about them, UFO Sky Pods, and says they came to him \"mysteriously\". If true, it would mean humans have been piloting these craft themselves.","3i-atlas-anomalous-composition-2025":"The object called 3I/ATLAS, a visitor spotted in 2025 passing through our solar system from outside it, is said to contain nickel without the iron that normally comes with it. A researcher named Kevin Knuth tells Michels this, citing the astronomer Avi Loeb, as a sign that the object is unusual and possibly not natural.","40-meter-metallic-object-in-the-labyrinth":"Deep in an underground labyrinth in Egypt, under the site of Hawara, there is said to be a metallic object 40 meters long, sitting in a vast corridor and shaped like a Tic Tac, meaning a white oval like the mint. Michels says \"it's a UFO.\" Joe Rogan repeats it on the show, citing what he heard from Ben van Kerkwijk, who Michels says is now reporting on the labyrinth, along with a report on a scan of the site. Michels links the place to the labyrinth described long ago by the Greek historian Herodotus and the Roman writer Pliny the Elder, and elsewhere credits a researcher named Jeffrey Drum and a blog post about the Hawara scan.","40-people-told-icig-of-reverse-engineering-program-2020":"About 40 people have allegedly gone to the ICIG, the inspector general who acts as internal watchdog for the US intelligence agencies, to say they took part in a reverse engineering program, meaning secret work to take apart recovered craft not made by humans and learn how they work. Jesse Michels says this himself, adding that it is alleged, during a debate with a UFO skeptic named Mick West. His argument is that you would have to be very strange to make such a claim to an inspector general if it were not true.","4602nd-monthly-report-s-ufo-b-section-1950":"In the 1950s, an Air Force unit called the 4602nd is said to have included a \"UFO B\" section in its monthly reports, counting how many UFOs had been seen. The researcher Scott Ramsey mentions it in passing on the show, citing the reports themselves. If true, it would mean UFO sightings were a routine, standing item in military intelligence reporting.","4602nd-squadron-history-on-reassuring-the-public-1950":"In the 1950s the 4602nd Air Intelligence Service Squadron, an Air Force intelligence unit that looked into UFO reports, wrote in its official history that part of its work was \"reassuring the public mind that no tangible evidence existed to support the fears of an invasion from outer space.\" Michels reads this line out himself, citing the squadron history. The words show that calming the public was a stated aim; whether that meant hiding what the unit found or simply reporting that it found nothing, the line neither says nor rules out.","4chan-whistleblower-a-decade-working-on-crash-retrievals-1990":"An anonymous poster on 4chan, an online message board where users don't give their names, claimed to have spent about ten years in the late 1990s and early 2000s working on crash retrievals, meaning the recovery of crashed craft said to be non-human. A guest named Chris Ramsay repeats this to Michels from the poster's thread, saying the man had worked on these retrievals \"for like a decade or whatever it was.\" If true, it would mean such recoveries were a regular job inside some hidden programme.","4chan-whistleblower-fewer-bodies-found-in-crashed-craft-1990":"An anonymous poster on 4chan, an online message board where users post without names, claimed to be an insider and said that crashed alien craft recovered by the US now contain far fewer bodies than they used to. Chris Ramsay, a guest who follows UFO claims closely, relays the thread to Michels, quoting the poster as saying \"we see a lot less bodies in these crafts.\" If true, it would suggest the craft being recovered are increasingly unpiloted.","4chan-whistleblower-hamburger-shaped-mothership-based-near-t":"An anonymous whistleblower on 4chan, an anonymous online message board, claimed that UFOs are built to order for specific purposes and kept underwater, with a huge hamburger-shaped mothership based near the Bahamas. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb brings it up, hedging 'if that 4chan whistleblower is to be believed.' He says there are places in the Bahamas that house 'that large hamburger shaped mother ship that 3D prints UFOs to spec.' He credits the whistleblower's thread and mentions a man named Chris Ramsay alongside it. Michels, on an episode with a treasure hunter, gives a shorter version: the whistleblower 'says that basically these UFOs are built to spec,' made for specific purposes, and sit 'on the ocean floor.' Michels leaves out the mothership and the Bahamas. If true, it would mean there is an alien or hidden factory under the sea, turning out craft as they are needed.","4chan-whistleblower-higher-ups-think-the-visitors-treat-eart-1990":"The people at the top who know about the visitors supposedly believe the visitors simply \"don't care about us\" and treat Earth \"more like a zoo\". The magician and YouTuber Chris Ramsay tells Michels this, passing on the claims of an anonymous \"whistleblower\" who posted a thread on 4chan, an anonymous online message board. If true, the visitors would be watching humanity the way we watch caged animals, and not getting involved.","4chan-whistleblower-nukes-jets-and-boats-sent-after-the-craf-1990":"An anonymous poster on 4chan, an online message board, claimed that some craft in the ocean near the Bahamas had swallowed everything sent after it: nuclear weapons fired from submarines, jets and boats, and \"none of them come back.\" The guest Chris Ramsay relays this to Michels from the poster's thread, quoting the whistleblower as saying \"we've sent nukes after it.\" If true, the military would be facing something under the sea that it cannot touch.","4chan-whistleblower-orange-light-orbs-scan-for-minerals-and-1990":"Glowing orange lights are probes scanning for minerals or living things, and one that comes up to you means you may be abducted or already have been, according to an anonymous poster on 4chan, an online message board where users don't give their names. A guest named Chris Ramsay repeats the claim to Michels from the poster's thread: \"if you see an orange light, it's they're scanning for minerals or biologics.\" If true, it would make a common kind of sighting a sign of deliberate survey work, and of abductions.","4chan-whistleblower-underwater-mobile-construction-units-bui-1990":"Huge underwater machines, called \"mobile construction units,\" are said to sit on the ocean floor near the Bahamas and the Bermuda Triangle, building UFOs. The claim comes from an anonymous poster on 4chan, an online message board where users post without names, who said his job was monitoring these \"massive\" units; Chris Ramsay, a guest who follows UFO claims closely, relays it to Michels. If true, it would mean non-human craft are being made beneath the sea and the government is watching it happen.","536542-ad-darkness-and-cooling-linked-to-volcanism-or-comet":"Between 536 and 542 AD, the records of monks and others living in Europe at the time describe weeks and months on end when they could not see the sun, a darkness and cooling that some link to volcanoes or to debris from a comet. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels this, summarising those old records without naming a particular one, as a sign that catastrophes from the sky have shaped human history.","5412-committee-howard-hughes-and-the-s-force-turned-against-1950":"One story Michels has heard goes like this: after President Kennedy fired CIA director Allen Dulles, an angry Dulles, along with the firm Brown Brothers Harriman, put a covert group called the \"S force\" back into action to kill Kennedy. The story's title connects it to the 5412 Committee, a Cold War body that approved secret operations, and to Howard Hughes and Richard Nixon in the 1950s and early 60s, with Cuba in the background. Michels tells it himself as \"the narrative I've heard\", without naming a source, in a conversation with a historian named Peter Levenda.","60-minutes-whistleblower-told-to-turn-down-the-temperature":"The story is that a government insider who quit said he was told from above to calm things down, in Haseltine's words to \"turn down the temperature on this thing.\" A former senior NSA official named Eric Haseltine tells Michels about it, saying he saw it on the CBS programme 60 Minutes, where a man who resigned said a woman came to him with that message. Haseltine does not say which report he means. A likely match is the 60 Minutes report by Scott Pelley aired on March 8, 2026, about a device that may be tied to Havana Syndrome, the unexplained injuries the CIA calls anomalous health incidents. In it a former CIA officer recalls that one of the first things he heard on joining the unit was: \"Our job is to bring down the temperature on AHI at headquarters,\" and the report says that all of this \"led him to resign.\" The broadcast does not say who spoke the line or that it was a woman, and its subject is Havana Syndrome rather than UFOs; the 2021 60 Minutes report on UFOs has no such line. Haseltine raises it as a sign that officials work to play down troubling subjects.","65-corrections-to-human-evolution-as-a-planted-marker-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S-4 in 1988 and 1989, says the briefing papers he read there stated that the aliens had made \"65 corrections\" to human evolution. He tells Michels this while bringing up a man he calls Barry. If true, it would mean people did not evolve on their own, and that someone deliberately shaped us.","8-000-years-of-ceremonial-site-artifacts-documented-by-archa":"People are said to have used Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, as a ceremonial site, with artifacts going back more than 8,000 years. Its owner, Brandon Fugal, tells Michels that the ranch's archaeologist, Chris Roberts, has now documented artifacts from that whole span. Fugal tells it to suggest that people treated the land as special long before modern reports began.","9-11-as-a-diversion-from-disclosure-2001":"Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for UFO disclosure, tells Michels that the September 11 attacks in 2001 came \"a few months later as a direct consequence\" of a push that year toward revealing UFO secrets. In his telling, the attacks served to pull attention away from disclosure, which would mean the secrecy was guarded at enormous cost. He names no source for this.","911-calls-from-the-facility-intercepted-2008":"Jordan Jose, a man who says a secret UFO programme took him as a child, says that between about 2008 and 2010 he tried to escape and call 911 from Baker Victory Services, a facility in Western New York that he describes as a school. He says \"the lines were surveiled\", so his calls were intercepted. He tells Michels this as something he lived through. If true, the people holding him were watching the facility's phone lines.","98-of-classical-knowledge-lost":"Ninety-eight percent of the knowledge of the ancient Greek and Roman world has been lost, through events such as the destruction of the Library of Alexandria and of Corinth. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this and says he takes the figure from the astronomer Carl Sagan. He uses it to suggest how much of the ancient past we no longer know.","a-1-40-scale-model-of-the-red-pyramid-exploded-under-pressur-2017":"A model of the Red Pyramid, one of Egypt's ancient pyramids, built at one-fortieth its real size, \"completely exploded due to the pressure on the inside,\" according to Geoffrey Drumm, who says he and his team built it. Drumm tells Michels this on an episode where he says an alien showed him what the pyramids were really for, and he offers the blast as a sign that the real pyramid was built to hold great pressure inside, like a machine.","a-30-mile-perfectly-straight-edge-on-mars":"There is a perfectly straight edge 30 miles long on the surface of Mars, too regular to be natural. A man named Joe McMoneagle tells Michels he saw it on photo negatives from Mars mapping work done by NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, and he says \"you don't get a 30 mile edge like that by nature.\" If true, it would suggest that something on Mars was built.","a-bone-on-mars-in-a-jpl-negative":"Joe McMoneagle tells Michels that a photographic negative from the Mars mapping work of the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, NASA's centre for robotic space missions, shows a bone lying on Mars. He says he has looked at the image himself and states flatly, \"this is a bone on Mars.\" If he were right, something with a skeleton once lived there.","a-chicago-teacher-s-students-talk-about-the-hill-2023":"A teacher in Chicago is said to have heard her students talking about \"the hill,\" a place that children in this story speak of as somewhere they share in their minds. The documentary maker Ky Dickens, who made a film about children she says can read minds, tells Michels she met this teacher, and treats it as one more group of children independently describing the same place.","a-congressman-on-the-progress-of-reverse-engineering-program":"An unnamed congressman says he believes secret programs exist to reverse engineer recovered craft, meaning to take them apart and learn how to build them, but he doubts they are working. Asked about it on the show, he answers \"Yeah, I do,\" then adds that he thinks \"they're going to scrap it,\" because \"if they had it, we would own the skies.\"","a-dramatic-event-at-the-end-of-the-ice-age-wiped-out-north-a":"A dramatic event linked to the North Atlantic ice cap ended the Ice Age, not instantly but rapidly, and \"eviscerated\" North America, killing off its large animals. That is the claim of the researcher Luke Caverns, who tells Michels he would \"guarantee\" it, drawing on an evolutionary biologist he does not name. If true, a sudden catastrophe, not a slow change, would have reshaped the continent's life at the end of the Ice Age.","a-female-gray-with-a-baby-gray-at-the-mount-zeil-base":"At what he describes as a base at Mount Zeil, in Australia, a female gray was seen with a baby gray; grays are the small, gray-skinned, big-eyed beings of UFO lore. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan tells Michels he saw them himself, saying \"I saw a gray female with a baby gray\", and gives no date. If it is true, these beings have young and live at a site on Earth.","a-few-dozen-uap-reports-are-real-some-known-from-inside-gove":"Out of the many thousands of reports of UAP, the newer official term for UFOs (unidentified anomalous phenomena), a few dozen are real, says Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA. He tells Michels that he has \"guilty knowledge\" of some of those cases from his years inside the government. He makes the case alongside a colleague he names, Chris Gilbert, and in a book called The New Science of UFOs. Coming from an insider, the claim would mean some of these cases are known to be genuine within government.","a-founder-s-grandfather-saw-roswell-base-personnel-spooked-1947":"In 1947 at Roswell, New Mexico, where the Army air base announced it had recovered a 'flying disc' and then took the statement back, the staff of the base were visibly spooked, according to a man whose grandfather served there in the 9th Atomic Bomber Squadron. Michels relays this from the founder of a Fortune 500 company, whom he does not name, while talking with the UFO documentary maker James Fox; if true, it would mean those on the base knew something more than a balloon had come down.","a-genetics-biologics-component-to-the-program":"The secret government effort said to recover and study non-human craft is claimed to include work on genetics and biology as well. A guest named Dylan Borland tells Michels \"I know there's a genetics component to it,\" citing David Grusch, a witness on the subject. If true, it would mean the effort studied living or once-living things, not only machines.","a-hidden-entity-picking-recruits-from-the-gate-pool":"Behind GATE, a US school programme for gifted children, some other hidden group is quietly picking out chosen children and pulling them into secret programmes, according to Jordan Jose, who tells Michels he was taken into the UFO programme himself as a child. He describes a \"different entity that is utilizing Gate behind the scenes to find a pre-selected pool of people.\" If true, it would mean the secret programmes recruit their people from childhood.","a-man-falls-from-a-hotel-window-on-telegraph-avenue-1977":"In the late 1970s, on Telegraph Avenue in Berkeley, California, the Hollywood writer Brent Friedman says he saw the aftermath of someone falling from a hotel window. He tells Michels that when he looked up, \"three stories up there's an open window with a curtain blowing and they jumped.\" He tells it as something he witnessed himself.","a-man-named-bernie-proposes-esp-as-a-savant-skill-in-non-spe":"A man known only as Bernie was the first to suggest that ESP (extrasensory perception, meaning knowing things without the usual senses) should be seen as a savant skill in some autistic people who do not speak. That is what Ky Dickens, the maker of a documentary on telepathy in non-speaking children, tells Michels. She does not name a source beyond Bernie himself, and his idea would put such gifts alongside other rare savant talents.","a-member-of-congress-saw-a-ufo-come-out-of-a-lake-while-fish":"A member of Congress was fishing on a lake with his son when an object came up out of the water. The former Pentagon intelligence official Luis Elizondo tells Michels that members of Congress pull him aside to share sightings like this one. He gives no name, no lake and no date. He tells it to show that even lawmakers have seen such things themselves.","a-mutual-friend-e-saw-a-rectangular-craft":"A woman known only as E is said to have seen a rectangular flying craft. The researcher Deep Prasad tells Michels that E is a friend they both know and the only person he has ever met who saw a craft of that shape, which he finds \"super interesting.\"","a-police-officer-s-report-of-seeing-a-dead-being-1996":"In 1996 in Varginha, a town in Brazil where, by many accounts on the show, a strange creature was captured, a police officer is said to have written a report saying he saw a dead being. Italo Venturelli, a neurosurgeon at the town's hospital, tells Michels this report exists, as something he knows of rather than saw himself; if true, it would put a dead non-human body on an official record.","a-reptilian-being-appears-in-barbara-lamb-s-living-room-2020":"A woman named Barbara Lamb came in from her garden one afternoon in the 2020s and found a reptilian being standing in her house, according to the guest Dan Sheehan. Sheehan tells Michels what Lamb told him, putting her alongside the work of John Mack and pointing to an archive of recordings of her sessions. It is told as a sign that such beings can turn up in an ordinary home.","a-small-pentagon-group-dealt-with-the-tall-whites-whose-base-1960":"In the 1960s only a tiny handful of people at the Pentagon knew about, and dealt with, the \"Tall Whites,\" tall pale beings said to live at a base on the Air Force ranges near Nellis and Indian Springs in Nevada, a base treated much like a foreign embassy. That is what Charles Hall tells Michels; Hall says he spent three years with these beings while stationed there, and says \"the Pentagon had a tiny handful of people that knew\" and \"interacted with them.\" If true, the US government kept quiet, diplomatic contact with an alien group.","a-space-kid-calls-the-nine-a-sandbox-1970":"In the 1970s, in Ossining, New York, one of the \"space kids\", children said to have psychic contact with otherworldly beings, is said to have described \"the Nine\", a council of non-human intelligences that some people claimed to receive messages from, as \"basically just like this sandbox for us to play in.\" The researcher Greg Malatzi passes this on to Michels from an unnamed space kid. It would mean that, to those children, the famous alien council was less a ruling body than a kind of playground.","a-traumatized-area-51-worker-saw-reptilian-beings-in-a-basem":"A man who worked at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, says he saw reptilian beings in a basement there and came away badly shaken. Michels repeats this from Garrett McNamara and Nicole McNamara, who told him they have a friend, or perhaps a friend of a friend, who worked there and whom they found 'totally traumatized'. If true, it would put living non-human beings inside a US military base.","a-uap-class-attacks-with-microwave-weapons-and-shuts-down-sy-2021":"One kind of UAP, the official term for UFOs, attacked a field team with microwave weapons and switched off their equipment, says James Valor of Skywatcher, a startup that tries to call up UFOs and record them. Valor tells Michels this happened to his own team sometime between 2021 and 2025, and that this \"class\" was different from the others they met. If it is true, some of these objects are not just watching but can fight back.","a-uap-hits-the-team-with-an-energy-weapon-during-a-turn-2025":"In 2025, during a field operation by Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs to appear, a UAP (an unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the official term for a UFO) struck the team with an energy weapon while they were making a U-turn, according to James Valor, who says he was there. He tells Michels it happened \"constantly\" and that the hit came before a colleague could get to something he calls NAYA, which the telling does not explain. If true, it would mean these objects can respond to people, and harm them.","a-ufo-control-panel-inside-a-hangar":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, says he walked through a hangar and spotted the control panel of a UFO, one he says he \"had seen and operated before\". He tells Michels this first-hand, and adds that the hangar was a metal Quonset building holding wreckage from a midair collision. He says he went there in the late 1980s, about a year after an interview with the DIA, the Pentagon's military intelligence agency. In his telling, he recognised it on sight and said: \"Hey, that's out of a UFO.\"","a-voice-yelling-freeze-saves-him-before-the-two-bits-bunker-1967":"During the Vietnam War, at a military outpost called Fire Base Two Bits, Joe McMoneagle was walking down the steps into a bunker when he heard someone yell \"freeze.\" He stopped and looked around, and \"the whole bunker disappeared, just vaporized.\" McMoneagle, who served in Vietnam, tells Michels this happened to him, and he tells it as a warning from a voice no one can account for, one that saved his life.","a-witness-saw-the-craft-over-the-hospital-1996":"In 1996, in the Brazilian town of Varginha, where people say a strange creature was seen and taken away, one witness says the craft was hovering over the hospital. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels he recently met this person, another witness on top of those he already had. If true, the craft would have been right above the place where, other accounts say, the being was brought.","aaro-appointed-to-head-a-space-tiger-team-on-uap-in-space-2024":"AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate UAP (the official term for unidentified flying objects), was put in charge of a special study team to look at such objects in space. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this without naming a source, and is vague on the details, but it would mean the military is now formally studying unexplained things beyond the atmosphere.","aaro-asks-hastings-to-bring-in-nuclear-site-veterans-for-int-2023":"In 2023 AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into UFO reports, asked a researcher named Robert Hastings to gather military veterans who had seen UFOs at nuclear weapons sites so it could interview them. Hastings, who has written about UFOs at nuclear bases, tells Michels the request came to him through Kurt McConnell, a retired investigator for the Senate Armed Services Committee. He tells it to show that the Pentagon itself wanted to hear these veterans' accounts.","aaro-briefing-ai-quantum-and-the-grusch-stuff-2023":"Around 2023, a well-placed national security insider is said to have summed up what really mattered as a triangle: \"AI, quantum, and the Grusch stuff,\" meaning the claims of David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who testified that the US holds craft not made by humans. The national security analyst Matthew Pines tells it as something said to him, and Michels repeats it on several later episodes, placing the speaker variously at AARO (the Pentagon office that studies unexplained aerial reports), at the CIA, at the Pentagon, or at the RAND Corporation, and once calling it an \"iron triangle.\" If true, it would mean insiders rank the UFO question alongside AI and quantum computing as a top strategic concern.","aaro-did-not-record-the-veterans-interviews-2023":"When AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into reports of unexplained objects, took testimony from military veterans in 2023, it did not record the interviews. Robert Hastings tells Michels this. He is a researcher who has gathered accounts of UFOs over US nuclear weapons sites, and the office had asked him to bring those veterans in, so he is passing on what he learned of how the sessions were run. If he is right, the office left no recording of firsthand witness accounts it had asked for itself.","aaro-director-s-true-anomalies-2024":"The Pentagon is said to have admitted it holds \"true anomalies\", cases that leave it stumped. Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this came from Jon Kosloski, the new director of AARO, the Pentagon's office for UFO reports. Von Rennenkampff raises it to show that even the official UFO office, which tends toward ordinary explanations, says some cases have none.","aaro-director-says-aaro-works-with-uarcs-and-ffrdcs-2024":"At a 2024 Senate hearing where Senator Kirsten Gillibrand was questioning him, Jon Kosloski, director of AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), said his office works with UARCs and FFRDCs, university-affiliated and federally funded research centres that do contract work for the government. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb points to this on the show because such centres are where he and others suspect secret UFO work could be housed.","aaro-fishes-for-the-program-name-and-the-people-on-it-2023":"In 2023 AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into UFO reports, pressed a man named Dylan Borland to give up the name of a secret programme and the people working on it, Borland tells Michels. He says: \"They wanted me to name the people that I knew that were working on the program. I refused to do it.\" He tells it as a sign that the office was hunting for insiders rather than simply taking his account.","aaro-found-som-1-01-crash-packaging-manual":"AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate UAP (the official term for UFOs), is said to have found a manual known as SOM 1-01 that explains how to pack and unpack debris, craft and bodies from an extraterrestrial vehicle crash. A guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this, passing on what he says he heard and naming Tim Phillips and Sean Kirkpatrick in connection with it. If true, it would mean the US government once wrote standard procedures for handling crashed alien craft and their crews.","aaro-head-john-kosloski-came-from-the-nsa":"The man now leading AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate UFO reports, is said to have come from the NSA. Michels says this himself, describing the head, Jon Kosloski, as \"a former NSA director,\" while talking with a former Army sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens. Michels raises it as a link between the agency in Sherman's story and the office that now handles the UFO question.","aaro-hired-sancorp-2020":"AARO, the Pentagon office set up in the 2020s to investigate UAP (the newer official term for UFOs), hired a company called SANCorp. The firm specializes in insider-threat protection and stopping whistleblower leaks. That is according to UAPGerb, an online researcher known by that handle, who tells Michels that a man named Tim Phillips said on a podcast that SANCorp was hired to connect AARO with subject-matter experts. UAPGerb presents this as a sign that the office was guarding against leaks rather than just gathering expertise.","aaro-historical-report-misstated-michael-herrera-s-testimony-2024":"The Pentagon's 2024 historical report on UFOs, Volume 1 of the review by AARO (the Pentagon office set up to investigate unidentified aerial reports), is said to have misrepresented what a witness named Michael Herrera told investigators. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels it is \"one specific example we know for a fact\" was \"lied about\" in the report, setting the report beside Herrera's own memorandum for the record of his account and naming Sean Kirkpatrick, who led the office. If so, it would mean the government's official history of the subject cannot be taken at its word.","aaro-leadership-change-from-kirkpatrick-to-kosloski-2024":"Michels notes that AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), recently changed leaders in 2024, from a director named Kirkpatrick to a new head named Jon Kosloski. He says this to his guest Ryan Graves, a former Navy fighter pilot, saying Graves had told him who the new leader was. A new head could mean a new direction for the government's official UFO inquiry.","aaro-resolution-of-aguadilla-video-2013":"A 2013 video of an unidentified object filmed with a heat-sensing camera near the airport at Aguadilla, Puerto Rico, has been explained by AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate unexplained objects. That is what Mick West, a well-known UFO sceptic, tells Michels in a debate: \"They did. Aguadilla, which is one that existed before that.\" If so, one of the more famous UFO videos would have an ordinary explanation from an official source.","aaro-s-latin-motto-2022":"AARO, the Pentagon office that investigates unidentified flying objects, has a Latin motto that translates as something like \"the universe is what our thoughts make of it.\" A man described on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, Matthew Brown, tells Michels this, admitting he may have the translation wrong, and offers it as a hint at how the office sees its subject.","aaro-s-mfr-marks-borland-s-triangle-sighting-unclassified-an-2023":"A former Air Force serviceman named Dylan Borland says that in 2023 AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into UFO reports, wrote up his account in a memorandum for record, or MFR, an official written note of a meeting, and listed his sighting of a triangle-shaped craft as unclassified. He tells Michels first-hand that the office then classified his other drawings of technologies of \"unknown origin,\" moving them into a SAP, a tightly restricted secret programme, and he takes that as proof the technologies are real; in one telling he also mentions Langley.","aaro-says-go-fast-is-possibly-parallax-2015":"The Go Fast video, filmed by Navy jets off Jacksonville, Florida, in 2015 and showing a small object that seems to race over the ocean, may only look fast because of parallax: the jet's own motion making a slow object appear quick against the water far below. Jesse Michels says that AARO, the All-domain Anomaly Resolution Office, a Pentagon office set up to investigate unexplained objects, came out and said it is \"possibly parallax\". He raises this during a debate with a UFO skeptic named Mick West, and he notes that AARO says possibly, not certainly.","aaro-scif-briefing-showing-only-resolved-cases-2023":"In 2023, Congressman Eric Burlison sat in a SCIF, a sealed room for classified briefings. There, officials from AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate UAP (the government's term for UFOs), showed him only cases they had already explained. Burlison was in the room and names the briefers as Sean Kirkpatrick and Jon Kosloski. He describes it to Michels as \"literally a slideshow\" of \"here's what it was... This was a balloon.\" To him, it meant the office was showing Congress its easy answers and none of the hard cases.","aaro-sends-odni-a-false-mfr-2023":"AARO, the Pentagon office that looks into UAP (the government's term for UFOs), sent the Office of the Director of National Intelligence a memorandum for record, an internal note written to document a meeting, and Dylan Borland says that note was false. Borland, who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, tells Michels this as something he dealt with himself, saying that when he went to the Director's office on 10 September 2025, Jon Kosloski was the director. If true, it would mean the office meant to investigate these reports put a false account on paper about him.","aaro-staffing-1-600-cases-and-a-baffled-director-2024":"AARO, the Pentagon office set up in recent years to investigate UAP (the official term for UFOs), had by 2024 grown to around three or four dozen staff and had just been declared at full operational capability, according to a commentator named Marik von Rennenkampff. He describes it on the show alongside its pile of some 1,600 cases and a director, Jon Kosloski, who is said to be baffled by some of them, to show how small the office is next to the job.","aaro-told-the-wsj-the-shutdown-was-an-emp-test-2025":"In 2025 the Pentagon's UFO office is said to have told The Wall Street Journal that a famous shutdown of nuclear missiles, long blamed on a UFO, was really a secret Air Force test. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer who says he was in the launch bunker in Montana when the missiles went down, tells Michels that the office was \"able to tell Joel Sheckchman that this was some sort of EMP test\". An EMP is an electromagnetic pulse, the burst of energy from a nuclear blast that can knock out electronics. The office is AARO, the Pentagon's current UFO office, then led by Sean Kirkpatrick. Joel Schectman's article in the Journal, published 6 June 2025, says \"Kirkpatrick's team dug into the story and discovered a terrestrial explanation\". It says the Air Force built \"an exotic electromagnetic generator\" to mimic the pulse, set \"on a portable platform 60 feet above the facility\", which \"would gather power until it glowed, sometimes with a blinding orange light\", and whose pulses \"snaked down cables\" to the bunker, \"disabling the weapons\". The article says it rests on \"interviews with two dozen current and former U.S. officials, scientists and military contractors involved in the inquiry\". The Defense Department statement it quotes is about fake classified program materials, not the shutdown. So saying that AARO as an office gave the Journal this account is Salas's reading of the reporters' unnamed sources, which the text neither confirms nor rules out.","aaro-under-kirkpatrick-as-a-fly-trap-for-ufo-information-2022":"Michels argues that AARO, the Pentagon office set up in 2022 to look into UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's newer term for UFOs), was in its first form under its director Sean Kirkpatrick \"literally a fly trap for UFO information\", gathering what people knew rather than bringing it out, and that it \"probably had legacy ties\" to older secret programmes. He says this himself as his own reading, calling it \"patently obvious\", in a conversation with a guest named Dylan Borland. If he is right, the office meant to look into UFOs was really working to keep them secret.","aaro-undercut-by-cia-and-air-force-figures-such-as-kirkpatri-2022":"AARO, the Pentagon office set up in 2022 to look into reports of unexplained objects, was undermined from inside by CIA and Air Force people, several guests tell Michels. A guest named Matthew Pines says that once the work moved into AARO it was \"compromised by the CIA air force guys\", naming Sean Kirkpatrick, who led the office. Jeremy Corbell, speaking as a reporter who cites others, goes further: he says \"AARO was acting as a counterintelligence against the American public\", a trap that harassed whistleblowers, and he names among his sources the former official James Lacatski and the journalist George Knapp. A witness named Michael Herrera, who says he saw a huge craft in Indonesia, says from his own dealings with the office that it was \"a front\" for \"plucking people to figure out what they know and then either trying to shut them up or just publicly dismissed the person\". He names another of its officials, Jon Kosloski. If they are right, the office meant to bring the facts out was used to find and silence witnesses. All three speak from their own experience or from what others told them. None of them points to a document.","aaron-paul-wrote-messages-back-and-forth-with-bob-lazar-2023":"A man named Aaron Paul is said to have traded messages with Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada, around 2023. The musician Flying Lotus tells Michels this about someone he knows. He calls Paul \"wild\" and says simply, \"He talked to Bob Lazar,\" without saying what the two discussed.","aatip-as-part-of-a-government-disclosure-effort-2015":"AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, was part of a government effort to slowly reveal what it knows about UFOs as late as 2015 and 2016. That's what Jake Barber, a guest who says he once carried a UFO with his helicopter, tells Michels, passing on what he was told by someone else. If it were true, the office would have been part of a planned release of the secret, not just a study.","aatip-modeled-uap-using-general-relativity-2007":"AATIP, a small Pentagon program that studied unexplained aerial reports and worked with the private company Bigelow Aerospace from about 2007, tried to explain how UAP (the government's term for UFOs) move by taking Einstein's general relativity as its theory, the idea being that such craft bend space and time around themselves. The physicist Hal Puthoff, who worked on the program, tells Michels that \"the best\" they could do was to \"accept Einstein's GR as being the theory.\" Luis Elizondo, the former Pentagon official who ran AATIP, credits Puthoff and goes further, calling that bending of spacetime \"the fundamental theory,\" not just \"a possibility.\"","aatip-oap-and-the-uap-task-force-as-red-teams-for-the-legacy-2007":"From 2007 to 2021, the government's public UFO efforts may have been \"red teaming\", meaning acting as outside testers, for a far older secret effort that is said to hold recovered craft not made by humans, known as the legacy program. Those public efforts were AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, a later office called OAP, and the UAP Task Force, a Pentagon group set up to study UAP, the government's term for UFOs. A man named Dylan Borland offers this to Michels as a theory, \"I think\", and points to the DIRDs, research papers written for AATIP, as part of the trail.","aatip-study-of-260-000-cases-and-a-possible-existential-thre-2007":"AATIP was a small Pentagon office, run with the private company Bigelow Aerospace between about 2007 and 2013, that studied unexplained aerial reports. According to the researcher Jason Simosa, it went through 260,000 cases using seven levels of analysis, which he calls probably the biggest study of UFO data ever done. Simosa says that the computer scientist and longtime UFO researcher Jacques Vallée hints two or three times in his published journals at one of its conclusions: that \"an existential threat may have been detected,\" which would mean the study found something it saw as a danger to humanity.","aatip-the-dod-s-ufo-program-and-its-22-million-budget-2007":"From 2007 to 2012 the Pentagon ran a secret UFO study that Michels calls AATIP, the Advanced Aerospace Threat Identification Program, set up by Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid of Nevada with Senators Daniel Inouye and Ted Stevens, with $22 million, run through Bigelow Aerospace, the private space company of Robert Bigelow, and doing some of its work at a ranch known for strange reports called Skinwalker Ranch. Michels tells this himself again and again, and says the program came to light in the New York Times article 'Glowing Auras and \"Black Money\": The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program', published on 16 December 2017, which the journalist Leslie Kean, in a clip, says she was one of three reporters to break. In some versions he calls it AAWSAP, the Advanced Aerospace Weapons System Applications Program, names the former intelligence official Luis Elizondo as its director, and says it later shrank into a slimmer follow-on; if true, Congress paid for an official UFO study years before the public was told.","aawsap-idea-of-baiting-ufos-with-nuclear-material":"A secret Pentagon-funded program that studied UFO reports, known as AAWSAP, is said to have considered luring UFOs with nuclear material and then bringing them down with directed energy weapons and high-powered radar. Michels tells it twice on the show, citing Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official turned whistleblower who he says was involved in the program, and who wrote about it in his book Imminent, published in 2024; the second time Michels adds the directed energy and radar and names the book. If true, it would mean officials planned to actively capture these craft, not just watch them.","aawsap-set-up-to-take-a-lockheed-held-ufo-transfer-killed":"The story is that a Pentagon-funded UFO study called AAWSAP, run through the DIA, the Pentagon's military intelligence agency, and a Las Vegas aerospace company owned by the billionaire Robert Bigelow, was created to receive a UFO held by Lockheed Martin, and that a CIA official stopped the handover. Michels, relaying the whistleblower and former intelligence officer David Grusch and Senator Harry Reid, says the program was set up to move the craft into a special access program, a tightly restricted classified project, so outside civilian scientists could study it; he names Jim Ryder, a vice president at Lockheed Martin Space Systems' Advanced Technology Center, as the man involved. In another episode, citing an article by Chris Sharp in the UFO news site Liberation Times, he says the CIA's Glenn Gaffney was the gatekeeper and blocked the transfer of a craft Lockheed had collected in the 1950s. The physicist Eric W. Davis, who speaks of the receiving team as \"us\", says Gaffney, in the CIA's science and technology directorate, was \"very antagonistic\" about the company releasing its collection of crash-retrieval materials, held since before a program shut down in 1989. The journalist Ross Coulthart credits the New York Times story \"Glowing Auras and 'Black Money'\" by Leslie Kean and Ralph Blumenthal, published December 16, 2017, and suggests the craft came from a reported 1953 crash at Kingman. An online researcher known as UAPGerb puts the attempted transfer in 2008 and calls it Kingman material. If true, a defence contractor has held a non-human craft for decades and the CIA kept it from scientists.","aawsap-to-aatip-to-elizondo-to-new-york-times-chain-2007":"A direct line runs from AAWSAP, a Pentagon-funded UFO study that began around 2007, to AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports. From there it runs to Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official tied to that office, and on to the New York Times, which first reported on the programme in 2017. That is how the commentator Marik von Rennenkampff puts it on the show, in an episode about challenging the UFO skeptic Mick West to a debate. He gives the chain to argue that the 2017 news did not come from nowhere: it traces back to real government programmes.","abandoned-radar-base-found-near-alvado-1946":"An old radar base still sits on a mountain peak near Alvado, New Mexico, \"just like they left it in 1957\", according to a crash-site researcher named Scott Ramsey. He tells Michels that in 1999 his team searched every high peak in the area, out toward Santa Fe, for old radar bases, found this one, and hiked up to it through about two feet of snow. He tells it as part of his search for evidence of a crash he believes happened in that region, since radar there could have tracked whatever came down.","abductee-carol-s-missing-pregnancy-and-her-hybrid-daughter-1990":"In the 1990s a woman named Carol said she had been abducted, taken from her home aboard a craft. The story goes that she lost a pregnancy and came to believe she had a hybrid daughter, part human and part alien. The lawyer Dan Sheehan passes this on second-hand. He says Carol was studied by the psychiatrist John Mack, and that she was \"furious at the UFO people because she'd been abducted.\"","abductee-friend-grabs-a-gray-by-the-neck":"A woman who says she has been abducted by aliens once grabbed one of them by the neck and squeezed it before she passed out. The beings were grays, the small grey-skinned figures that many people who report abductions describe. The filmmaker and researcher Tim Alberino tells Michels this as something his friend told him about one of her experiences. If it were true, it would mean these beings can be touched and fought back against.","abductees-tell-alberino-of-a-forced-hybrid-breeding-program":"People who say they were abducted describe being forced into a breeding program that makes hybrids of humans and the beings who take them. Tim Alberino, a guest who has collected such accounts, tells Michels this as what the abductees have told him. If true, the abductions would be part of a deliberate program rather than random encounters.","abductees-whose-memories-come-back-when-they-see-the-cover-o":"Some abductees, meaning people who say beings took them, are said to have had their buried memories come flooding back just from seeing the cover of Communion, a book by the writer Whitley Strieber about his own encounters, on a bookstore shelf. Michels passes this on from Strieber himself. It would suggest the memories were there all along, waiting for a trigger.","abduction-and-implant-prevalence-estimates":"About 3% of Americans have been abducted by the beings who put implants in people, says Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants found in human bodies. He tells Michels the number comes from a doctor named Roger Leir, whom he calls Dr. L. If that were so, millions of people would have been taken.","abduction-memory-returns-25-years-later-and-is-checked-by-re-1987":"A memory of being abducted came rushing back to the former Army sergeant Lyn Buchanan 25 years after the event, when a woman said something that set it off, and he then had it checked by the remote viewers in his unit, people trained to try to perceive distant events with the mind. Buchanan tells Michels this happened in the late 1980s: \"when she said that 25 years later after the event, it all came flooding back.\" If the check held up, it would mean a buried abduction memory could be confirmed by others.","abduction-phenomenon-shatters-the-western-mind":"People's reports of being taken by non-human beings, known as alien abductions, are said to have unusual power because they break the Western mind's deepest rule, that things of the spirit world must not show up in the physical world. The abduction researcher John Mack puts it this way in a clip played on the show: \"that traffic is the cardinal sin for the Western mind\", so \"it has great power to shatter the belief structure of a western mind\", and that is \"precisely what's occurring in this abduction phenomenon\".","aborymon-the-enochian-angel-in-john-dee-s-sigillum":"The researcher Matthew Brown says an angel named Aborymon was \"recently discovered\" in the Sigillum Dei Aemeth. That is a magical seal diagram linked to John Dee, a scholar and occultist at the court of Elizabeth I. Brown calls Aborymon an Enochian angel, meaning one from the angel language and spirit lore Dee recorded, and he gives no source for the find.","abraham-dines-with-angelic-beings":"The biblical patriarch Abraham met a group of angelic beings at Mamre, in ancient Canaan, and shared a meal with them. The filmmaker and researcher Tim Alberino retells this on the show from the Bible, which he credits in the King James Version of 1611. That version says that at Mamre \"three men stood by him\" and that, after Abraham had food prepared, \"they did eat\". It calls them men, and later calls two of them angels when they reach Sodom. In what happens, and in what order, it matches Alberino's account of a meal with beings from beyond.","abramelin-the-hermit-of-luxor-in-the-book-of-abramelin":"The Book of Abramelin centres on Abramelin, who Matthew Brown, a guest billed as a Pentagon whistleblower, describes as \"essentially a desert hermit in Egypt around Luxor.\" Brown gives this as his own summary of the book, without naming any other source, and brings it up because he links the book's teachings to his larger account of contact with other beings.","ac-gravity-llc-wins-a-dod-contract-and-li-goes-silent-1990":"A researcher named Ning Li, who worked on gravity, is said to have formed a company called AC Gravity LLC with a colleague named Larry Smalley, in Huntsville, Alabama, and then gone quiet after it won a Defense Department contract. Michels tells this himself in an episode about antigravity, the idea of a craft that cancels gravity, saying the two \"immediately\" won the contract after forming the company. He raises it because a researcher going silent once the military pays her could mean her work became secret.","acacia-as-the-tree-of-life-in-egypt-freemasonry-and-the-ark":"Michels says the acacia tree was the Tree of Life in ancient Egypt, that the acacia leaf plays a prominent role in Freemasonry, and that \"there's no doubt about it\" the Ark of the Covenant was made of acacia wood. He raises it in conversation with Graham Hancock, a writer on lost ancient civilizations, and credits him. The thread he draws suggests one sacred symbol carried from Egypt into the Bible and later secret societies.","account-of-a-tic-tac-type-craft-over-pennsylvania-in-1967":"In 1967 a craft like the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint and made famous by later Navy sightings, was seen over Pennsylvania, the guest Steven Greer tells Michels. He says a \"very good account of it\" is in a book he is handing Michels, Disclosure: An Illustrated History, a 2025 briefing document of his Disclosure Project, though when he names the book he does not mention the shape. If so, the Tic Tac shape was being seen decades before the Navy pilots reported it.","acquaintances-say-puharich-was-mossad-1970":"Andrija Puharich, a scientist linked to the CIA who said he made contact with an alien council, was secretly working for Mossad, Israel's intelligence service, in the 1970s. That is what the researcher Greg Malatzi says several people who knew Puharich told him, though they kept quiet about it until late in their lives. If true, Puharich's contact work may have been tied to a foreign spy agency.","acquiring-william-corliss-s-sourcebook-project-papers":"The UFO researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels he got hold of the papers of William Corliss, an American who wrote a series of books called the Sourcebook Project, and brought them from the United States to Sweden. Svahn tells it as his own doing, part of how he built up one of the largest collections of UFO evidence anywhere.","active-military-personnel-bring-uap-videos-to-the-congressma-2023":"Since 2023, active-duty military personnel have come to Congressman Eric Burlison's office to show him videos of UAP, the government's term for UFOs. Burlison tells Michels this first-hand: \"I get people... that are active military personnel that come to me as a member of Congress and have videos that they'll show me.\" If true, people in uniform are going around official channels to bring what they have recorded straight to lawmakers.","actuators-pulled-out-of-portals":"The UFO researcher Grant Cameron passes on a claim from a man named John Sillison: that there are \"actuators,\" moving mechanical parts, that \"they pull out of the portals.\" Cameron repeats it as something he was told and does not say who \"they\" are or what the portals are. If it were true, it would mean someone is recovering hardware from openings to somewhere else.","adam-and-eve-as-a-memory-of-genetic-manipulation":"The Bible's story of the first man and woman may be a memory of someone tinkering with human genes. That is how Joe Rogan, the comedian and podcaster, reads it when he talks with Michels: \"when they talk about the first human beings I assume that that is a recounting of a genetic manipulation.\" If he were right, Genesis would be an old record of humans being engineered. The text he has in mind is Genesis, here in the King James Version of 1611. It says God \"created man in his own image\", and that \"the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life.\" Then God \"caused a deep sleep to fall upon Adam\", took one of his ribs, and \"made he a woman.\" So the text tells of the first people being made by a higher hand, including an operation done while Adam slept. Reading that as genetic work on beings who already existed is Rogan's step, which the text neither states nor rules out.","adamski-photos-unanalyzed-diaries-read-by-glenn-steckling-1950":"George Adamski was a man who said in the 1950s that he had met visitors from space and photographed their craft. His photos have never been properly analysed, and the diaries he kept have been read by a man named Glenn Steckling. The Swedish UFO researcher and archivist Clas Svahn tells Michels this. He does not name a source, and he points to the diaries as a record of Adamski's own words that has hardly been studied.","adamski-s-contact-with-a-man-from-mars-1950":"In the 1950s, a man named George Adamski claimed that a being he met told him it came from Mars. The philosopher Jason Jorjani mentions this to Michels in passing, without a source, as one of the early contact stories in which visitors claimed to come from nearby planets.","adamski-s-martian-and-venusian-nordic-society-1950":"In the 1950s a man named George Adamski claimed to have met visitors from Mars and Venus, tall human-looking beings of the kind later called Nordics. He said they lived across the whole solar system and were \"far more enlightened than us.\" The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells this to Michels in his own summary, crediting a work called Closer Encounters. He tells it as an early example of people describing the visitors as spiritual teachers.","admiral-meeting-at-a-cemetery-at-midnight-1996":"In 1996 an admiral was to come off a ship at Long Beach Harbor in California, claiming he was visiting a dead friend, and secretly meet Brent Friedman at a cemetery at midnight. Friedman tells Michels this on an episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, and says the plan came from a contact he calls JC, who told him: \"We need you to meet at a cemetery at midnight.\" The story matters to him because it suggests a senior officer would only talk about the subject in secret.","admiral-richard-byrd-antarctica-stories":"There are stories that the Navy admiral Richard Byrd had some kind of strange encounter while he was in Antarctica. Michels raises the name himself in a conversation, asking a guest, \"Do you know Admiral Richard Byrd?\" A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet tells it second hand, crediting a woman named Becky Schott and placing it in the 1940s: \"someone thought that Admiral Byrd had some encounter when he was down there.\" Neither says what the encounter was, but the story is told as a hint that something unexplained was found at the bottom of the world.","admiral-wilson-returns-to-close-out-a-project-at-area-51-1997":"A Navy admiral named Thomas R. Wilson came back to Las Vegas sometime between 1997 and 2002 because, he said, he had to go back up \"into that\" place to close out a project, and he would not say Area 51, the secret test site in the Nevada desert. A scientist who has worked for the Pentagon, Eric W. Davis, tells Michels the admiral said this to him and that he knew what Wilson meant. If it is true, a senior admiral was personally tied to a secret project at the site.","advanced-lost-civilization-and-ancient-egypt":"Humanity has risen to an advanced level before and then lost it, and ancient Egypt is evidence of this, Joe Rogan tells Michels. He says he belongs to the \"school\" of the researcher Randall Carlson and the writer Graham Hancock, who argue for an advanced civilization lost in deep prehistory. In his words, \"I don't think this is our first go-round.\"","advanced-theoretical-physics-group-studying-ufo-physics-1980":"The claim is that in the 1980s a quiet working group of scientists and defence people met to study the physics that might lie behind UFOs. It was called the Advanced Theoretical Physics group. Michels brings it up himself in several episodes, without a named source. He links it to the physicist Hal Puthoff and to Oke Shannon, whom he calls a Los Alamos project manager, and he says it ran through the 80s and 90s. In one episode he places it at the Pentagon and Los Alamos in the 2000s instead. The other teller is a researcher who goes by UAPGerb. He says that in 1985 a man named Albert Stubblebine and the defence company BDM set up the group at BDM's headquarters in McLean, Virginia. He cites Oke Shannon's own notes for this, and he names Kit Green among those involved. If it is true, serious people were working on UFO physics inside the defence world decades before the public heard of it.","aec-official-tests-the-device-on-himself-1962":"Around 1962, the story goes, an official of the Atomic Energy Commission, the US agency then in charge of nuclear work, doubted a device being shown at Intelectron, a company in New York City. He said \"test me,\" tried it on himself, \"and it worked.\" The researcher Greg Malatzi tells this on the show. He points to letters from the commission and cites the journalist Annie Jacobsen, but does not say what the device did.","aerial-battles-over-basel-and-nuremberg":"Huge battles between strange objects in the sky were seen over European cities such as Basel in Switzerland and Nuremberg in Germany, and were reported in the printed news sheets of the time, according to the philosopher Jason Jorjani. He tells Michels this from his reading, pointing to those old sheets and to Wonders in the Sky, a collection of historical sightings by the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée. For Jorjani it shows that people were seeing UFOs long before the modern era.","aerial-survey-record-altered-or-missing-1964-1969":"The aerial survey photos of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property famous for reports of strange events, are said to be missing for the years 1964 to 1969. The ranch's owner tells Michels the aerial record of the property is \"largely void\" and has been \"deleted\", oddly for exactly those years, and he credits the finding to a Dr. Taylor and to Erik Bard. He raises the gap as a sign that something on the ranch was being hidden at that time.","aerial-view-shows-replanted-trees-in-lines-1948":"At Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, where a flying saucer with alien bodies is said to have crashed in 1948, an aerial view later showed trees standing in neat lines, as if the site had been replanted. Scott Ramsey, a researcher who has spent years on the Aztec case and says he saw the aerial view, tells this on the show as a sign that someone cleaned up the site after the crash.","aerospace-companies-ask-for-pasulka-s-angel-data-2012":"Around 2012, when the religious studies professor Diana Pasulka began studying reports of angels and heavenly visitors, people from several aerospace companies got in touch asking for her data, she tells Michels. She stresses it was \"not just one, I got a lot.\" She tells it as a sign that people in the aerospace business saw something in old religious accounts that bore on their own work.","aerospace-companies-seek-patent-rights-over-ufo-technology-2023":"Big aerospace companies want patent rights over recovered UFO technology, so that they, not the US government, would control it \"for the next thousand years\". That is according to Dan Sheehan, a lawyer, who tells Michels that this is what a 2023 legal change would authorize them to get, without naming a source. He links it to the removal of a provision that would have let the government take such technology over, which he says would leave it in private hands for a very long time.","aerospace-company-s-government-customers-on-the-program-cia":"An aerospace company working on a secret programme reported to a government customer, and that customer was sometimes the CIA and sometimes the Air Force. The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels this. He says he knows it from a company he dealt with often during AAWSAP, a Pentagon-funded study of UFOs. If true, it would mean that more than one agency oversees private work on the programme.","aerospace-corp-executives-questioned-in-a-scif-2023":"Around 2023 or 2024, Congressman Eric Burlison says he and the House Oversight Committee sent a letter to a company called Aerospace Corp asking it to answer questions about UAP, the government's term for UFOs. He says the company sent two people from its executive team to answer questions in a SCIF, a sealed room for classified briefings, but \"of course they knew nothing.\" Burlison tells Michels this as someone who was there, and he suggests the company sent people who had nothing to say.","aerospace-corporations-hold-uap-knowledge-shielded-from-foia":"Aerospace companies hold knowledge about UAP, the official term for UFOs, and that knowledge is shielded from FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, because it counts as the companies' private property. That is what the physicist Hal Puthoff says in a debate with Eric Weinstein on the show, adding that the whole arrangement was set up to work that way. If true, it would explain why public records requests turn up so little.","aerospace-employee-with-an-object-moving-in-their-body":"A person who works at one of the big aerospace corporations has had something moving inside their body for several years, according to Jesse Michels. He tells this himself in a documentary about UFOs and says he will not name the person so as not to get them into trouble. If true, something unexplained would be lodged in the body of someone working inside the aerospace industry.","afib-after-the-hypnosis-session":"After a hypnosis session in Georgia, meant to bring back memories of what he describes as an alien abduction, Mario Woods developed atrial fibrillation, an irregular heartbeat. Woods, a former guard at a nuclear site, tells Michels that when the hypnotist Bob Upton \"finished that hypnosis, something was wrong with me,\" and he sees the heart trouble as linked to what the session brought up.","afosi-agent-has-crew-sign-non-disclosure-agreement-1967":"After the 1967 missile shutdown at Malmstrom Air Force Base, Robert Salas says, his squadron commander called him in and introduced him to a man from AFOSI, the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, the Air Force's own investigators, who had the crew sign a non-disclosure agreement. Salas, a former Air Force officer who was there, tells Michels this from his own memory. As he tells it, the people who saw it were told from the start to keep quiet.","afosi-confiscates-footage-and-swears-missile-men-to-secrecy-1960":"In the 1960s, Michels says, the men who ran nuclear missile sites kept seeing UFOs near nuclear facilities, and \"without fail\" the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, the Air Force's own investigative branch, would show up, take the film and swear the men to secrecy. Michels tells it himself in his interview with David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who went public about UFOs, and names a man named Bob Jacobs among those whose accounts he draws on. It would mean the Air Force knew about these sightings and worked to keep them quiet.","afosi-founding-director-a-devout-catholic-afosi-behind-early-1948":"The first director of the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, the Air Force's own investigative branch, is said to have been a devout Catholic who \"wanted to go to seminary and suddenly found himself in intelligence\". A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source. He also links the office to the early days of a secretive group known as the Collins Elite, around 1948, which would mean religious belief shaped how the government handled the UFO question from the start.","afosi-officer-harassing-a-ufo-whistleblower":"In one case, a person harassing a UFO whistleblower was proven to be an officer of AFOSI, the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, the Air Force's own investigative branch. Matthew Brown, a guest billed as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells Michels this as something a named person told him. If true, it would mean the military itself was going after people who spoke out.","afswp-established-at-sandia-base-1947":"In 1947, the military set up AFSWP, the Armed Forces Special Weapons Project, which handled atomic weapons, at Sandia Base in Albuquerque, New Mexico. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this, naming Defense Secretary James Forrestal in connection with it, as part of his account of where the secret UFO effort may have been housed.","age-of-disclosure-claims-of-a-reverse-engineering-program":"In a film called The Age of Disclosure, former officials are said to state that the US has a programme reverse engineering UFOs, that is, taking them apart to learn how they work. Michels brings it up himself on two episodes, naming Jim Semivan, \"high up at the CIA\", and Chris Mellon, a former deputy assistant secretary of defense for intelligence. The second time he adds that the film says non-human intelligence is real and that the US is in an arms race with China and Russia over it, and he also names the former intelligence chief James Clapper, which, if true, would mean senior insiders openly confirming a hidden programme.","age-of-disclosure-documentary-us-holds-craft-and-alien-bodie-2025":"A documentary called The Age of Disclosure features former CIA director John Brennan, former director of national intelligence James Clapper and other intelligence officials. Among them is David Grusch, formerly of the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency, and according to Michels the film carries claims that the US holds saucers and other craft in hangars, is working on \"reverse engineering\" them (taking them apart to learn how they work), and has recovered alien bodies along with them. Michels tells this himself, and in a later episode describes the claim as \"decades long, multigenerational crash retrievals\" tied to Area 51, the secret military test site in Nevada.","age-of-disclosure-joint-doecia-effort":"A secret UFO effort is said to have been run jointly by the Department of Energy and the CIA's science and technology branch. Michels says this comes from the documentary Age of Disclosure, in which, as he puts it, people as high up as a director of national intelligence say \"this was a joint effort between the department of energy and the CIA's director of science and technology.\" If true, it would place responsibility for the hidden work inside two named parts of the government, outside normal oversight.","agencies-deny-grusch-access-to-programs-2019":"Between 2019 and 2022, a former intelligence officer named David Grusch went to the agencies that had written certain reports and asked to be let into the secret programs those reports pointed to, and they turned him down. Grusch, who later testified to Congress, tells Michels this happened to him, and that \"when they deny you access then you know it's a thing\". To him, the refusal itself was a sign the programs exist.","agency-men-say-they-follow-the-varginha-investigation":"At an event he does not name, the documentary filmmaker James Fox was approached by agency men who told him they already knew all about his investigation into Varginha, a case in Brazil where the army is said to have captured a live alien. Fox tells Michels they said: \"Oh, yeah. No, we know exactly what you've been doing,\" and he names someone called Leslie in the account. He tells it as a sign that his work was being watched.","agents-ask-buchanan-to-influence-gorbachev-and-his-words-end-1991":"In 1991, at the Army post at Fort Meade, agents asked a soldier to use his mind to push words into the head of the Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev, and those words later turned up in the speech that ended communism. The soldier is Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant who worked in remote viewing, the practice of trying to perceive or reach distant people and places with the mind alone, and he tells Michels he did it himself: \"the words in that speech were the words that I'd been pumping into him.\" If true, it would mean the US used mental influence to help steer the end of the Soviet Union.","agents-followed-gebauer-s-daughters-to-school-1940":"Government agents are said to have followed the two daughters of a man named Leo GeBauer to school. GeBauer is a figure in the story of a claimed crash that left sixteen alien bodies, which is the subject of the episode. Michels tells it himself, saying one of the daughters told this to the Ramseys, who looked into the case, and he raises it as a sign that the authorities were watching the family closely.","aggressive-helicopter-experiment-where-everything-went-wrong-2024":"In 2024 a helicopter experiment at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange happenings, went badly: \"we had an experiment that we were running that was really aggressive and everything went wrong,\" says Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, who tells Michels he was involved. He tells it as an example of the ranch pushing back when people probe it too hard.","agnew-bahnson-s-diaries-space-brothers-and-townsend-brown-1950":"Agnew Bahnson, a wealthy backer of physics behind the Institute of Field Physics at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, is said to have written in diaries found years later that the \"space brothers,\" the friendly aliens of 1950s contact stories, came to him and told him to get in touch with Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor of unusual propulsion, because \"I want to build a spaceship.\" Michels tells this himself, in his own paraphrase of the diaries, on two occasions. The second time he ties it to a 1957 conference at Chapel Hill that brought together the physicist John Wheeler and, he says, Freeman Dyson, Richard Feynman and Peter Bergmann, suggesting the space brothers may have prompted Bahnson to push this research.","agnew-bahnson-s-novel-the-stars-are-too-high-1957":"Agnew Bahnson wrote a novel called The Stars Are Too High about man-made flying saucers, and Michels, who brings it up himself in four episodes, dates it to 1959. In some tellings the plot follows rogue Nazi scientists, captured and brought to America after the war, who team up with American scientists; in others it is a fake alien invasion staged by rogue anti-gravity scientists inside intelligence, a plan \"to unite the world\" through a man-made UFO scare. Michels raises it because he suspects such a novel may have hinted at real secret work.","agnew-bahnson-s-plane-crash-1960":"A man named Agnew Bahnson died in what Michels calls \"a bizarre plane crash\" a couple of years after the events Michels is describing, in the early 1960s, and Michels says Bahnson's family still believes he was essentially killed. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about UFO secrecy and a secret society. If the family is right, someone close to the UFO question would have been silenced.","aguadilla-airport-thermal-object-2013":"The story is about a video filmed in 2013 over the airport at Aguadilla, Puerto Rico. It shows a small, warm object moving low over the area, filmed by a heat-sensing camera. Mick West, a well-known UFO skeptic, brings it up with Michels, setting the scene: 'this is over the airport at Aguadilla, big tourist destination.' West is known for reading such footage as ordinary things seen from an aircraft. In a later episode Michels tells it his own way, offering no ordinary explanation. He calls it 'a 3-minute video of a transmedium object,' meaning one that moves between air and water. In his telling it 'seems to descend into the Atlantic Ocean and then come out as well.' To those who take it as the second kind of thing, it would show a craft that can dive into the sea and leave it again, something no known aircraft does.","aguadilla-analyzed-in-a-200-page-scu-document-2013":"An object seen over Aguadilla, Puerto Rico, in 2013 was studied in a 200-page report by the Scientific Coalition for UAP Studies, a group of scientists who look at UAP, the newer official term for UFOs. Michels tells this himself while debating a skeptic, stressing that the report was written by many experts with PhDs. He raises it to show that the case has been studied in depth by qualified people.","aguadilla-object-as-two-wedding-lanterns-2013":"An object filmed over Aguadilla, Puerto Rico, in 2013 may have been nothing more than two paper sky lanterns released at a wedding. That is the theory of Ruben Lianza, whom the UFO skeptic Mick West, known for analysing UFO videos, describes as head of Brazil's UFO investigation department, and West puts it forward on the show as an ordinary explanation for a famous case.","ai-agent-swarm-solves-navier-stokes-2026":"In 2026 a swarm of AI agents is said to have solved the Navier-Stokes problem, a famous unsolved maths problem about how fluids flow. According to the researcher Deep Prasad, no single AI agent could do it, but 10,000 agents working for 88 hours suddenly found a solution. Prasad tells Michels this without naming a source, as an example of how large groups of AI agents can do what one cannot.","ai-at-moscow-conference-judged-apollo-photos-synthetic-2023":"At an artificial intelligence conference in Moscow in 2023 that Vladimir Putin attended, an AI supposedly looked at the photographs from NASA's Apollo Moon missions and judged them synthetic, meaning fake. The podcaster and comedian Joe Rogan tells Michels this, passing on what he heard from Bart Sibrel, a filmmaker who has long disputed the Moon landings. If true, it would cast doubt on whether the famous Moon pictures are real.","ai-reads-the-buga-sphere-symbols-as-the-circuit-2025":"The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart says that in 2025 he photographed the Buga Sphere, a metal ball with strange symbols on it that turned up in Buga, Colombia, and uploaded the picture to an AI program, which read the symbols as meaning \"the circuit\". He tells Michels he is \"increasingly\" doing this with such images; if the reading held, it would hint that the markings describe how the object works.","ai-solves-the-navier-stokes-equations-in-88-hours-2026":"Artificial intelligence solved the Navier-Stokes equations, the math that describes how fluids like air and water flow, in just 88 hours, according to an unnamed researcher who says so on the show. The researcher stresses that the problem had stumped mathematicians for hundreds of years. He brings it up in an episode arguing that AI may itself be a kind of alien technology, since it would be doing what humans could not.","air-force-academy-friend-flew-a-back-engineered-ufo-like-cra":"An old friend from the Air Force Academy has actually flown a craft similar to a UFO, says Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer. He tells Michels that this personal contact has convinced him the United States has recovered crashed craft and \"back engineered\" them, meaning taken them apart to learn how to build copies, and has even flown some models. If so, human pilots are already flying machines based on non-human technology.","air-force-academy-religious-training":"The US Air Force Academy in Colorado has been training its people in religious studies, according to a researcher named Peter Levenda. He tells Michels he was appalled to hear of it a couple of years ago, and he names no source for what he heard.","air-force-and-cia-created-two-weeks-after-roswell-1947":"About two weeks after Roswell, the 1947 event in New Mexico where, in UFO accounts, a craft is said to have crashed, Washington passed the National Security Act of 1947 and created the Air Force and the CIA at the same time. Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he retrieved a UFO himself, puts the dates side by side for Michels, presenting it as what he was told. The point he draws is that the modern security state was born in the shadow of the crash. The Act itself sets up the new Air Force and the CIA; why it came then, it does not say.","air-force-captain-oliveira-at-the-national-war-college-1954":"In 1954, at the Escola Superior de Guerra, Brazil's national war college, an Air Force captain named Oliveira is said to have told his audience that the UFO phenomenon \"is real.\" Michels tells this himself during an episode on Brazil's best-known UFO cases, without naming a source, as a sign that Brazil's military took the subject seriously early on.","air-force-censorship-of-pan-starrs-imagery-2010":"The Air Force is said to have heavily censored images from Pan-STARRS, a sky-scanning telescope survey. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and notes that Pan-STARRS is the same survey that discovered 'Oumuamua, an odd object that came into our solar system from outside it. He raises it because, if true, the military would be controlling what astronomers are allowed to see of the sky.","air-force-cooperation-with-stargate-1997":"Michels says that two sitting Air Force Chiefs of Staff, Michael E. Ryan and John P. Jumper, appeared as themselves on the TV series Stargate, the science fiction show (not the CIA program of the same name) that ran from 1997 and which he links to the actor Richard Dean Anderson. He tells it himself, without naming a source. He raises it as a sign that the Air Force worked closely with a show about secret space travel.","air-force-disinformation-drives-paul-bennewitz-crazy-1979":"The story is that around 1979 an Air Force agent deliberately fed false alien stories to a man named Paul Bennewitz until it broke him. Bennewitz was a researcher in Albuquerque, New Mexico, who had seen what he took to be exotic, advanced craft being tested at nearby Kirtland Air Force Base. Michels tells it many times. He says Richard Doty, an Air Force counterintelligence agent, was \"tasked with performing a psychological operation on an innocent researcher,\" and that the Air Force \"drove a man crazy by playing up his belief in aliens.\" Michels draws on the writer Greg Bishop, on Project Beta, a document tied to the affair, and on records released under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records. He also says the pilot John Lear visited Bennewitz. The tellers differ on when it happened, 1979 or the early 1980s, and on how it ended. In one telling Michels says it helped drive Bennewitz to suicide, not just to madness. Two researchers back the core of it on the show. The writer Peter Levenda says \"we know that Richard Doty did it.\" The UFO historian Richard Dolan stresses that Bennewitz was a highly intelligent man whom Doty \"did kind of lead astray.\" If true, the military was using UFO stories to manipulate the public's own researchers.","air-force-documents-on-hans-kohler-s-capture-by-the-american-1940":"In Germany in the 1940s, a man named Hans Kohler is said to have been caught by American forces. Michels tells this himself, saying \"we now even have air force documents\" showing Kohler \"was apprehended by the Americans,\" though he does not quote them further. If true, it would place Kohler in American hands after the war.","air-force-flies-benowitz-to-archuleta-mesa-with-props-staged-1980":"In the early 1980s, the story goes, the Air Force flew a New Mexico man named Paul Bennewitz by helicopter from Kirtland Air Force Base to Archuleta Mesa on the Jicarilla Apache reservation, where props had been set up for him to see. The writer Greg Bishop relays it second-hand from people involved, saying \"Paul was very excited\" when he described the trip. If true, the military staged a scene to shape what Bennewitz believed.","air-force-funding-of-the-movie-stargate-1994":"The Air Force paid to help make Stargate, the 1994 Kurt Russell film, according to Jesse Michels. He brings it up twice, in two different conversations, without naming a source, and the second time he points out that the movie is about a portal by the pyramids. His point is that the military may have had a hand in shaping the stories the public tells about ancient gateways and other worlds.","air-force-going-public-plan-called-off-2009":"In 2009 and 2010, the Air Force is said to have had a plan to go public about unidentified flying objects, and a two-star general called it off. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this, repeating what a man he names only as Ed told him about taking part in the plan. If true, it would mean disclosure was close and was stopped from inside.","air-force-hunts-for-scientist-x-1950":"In Denver in 1950, Michels says, a lecture was given about crashed flying saucers, tied to an anonymous figure known only as \"Scientist X\", and two hours after it ended Air Force investigators were questioning students for clues to who Scientist X really was. Michels tells it himself and names people called Hansen, Unger and George Kohler among the figures in the story. If true, it would mean the Air Force took the lecture seriously enough to hunt down its source right away.","air-force-interrogations-demotions-and-denial-after-the-ells-1977":"After two Air Force men, Mario Woods and Michael Johnson, reported being taken by aliens at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota in 1977, Air Force interrogators are said to have spent most of their time trying to explain the experience away. Michels tells this himself in an episode where the two veterans meet again fifty years later, saying the worst part for them \"was what happened afterwards,\" which the story describes as interrogations, demotions and denial. He raises it because it would mean the Air Force worked to bury what its own men said happened.","air-force-lieutenant-colonel-makes-marines-sign-an-nda-and-t-2009":"Around 2009 or 2010, at Camp Hansen on Okinawa, Japan, an Air Force lieutenant colonel made a group of Marines sign a top-secret non-disclosure agreement about what they had seen in Indonesia, and warned them that \"if you talk about this, you can go to prison or you can be executed over it.\" Michael Herrera, one of those Marines, tells Michels he was there and signed it, and he tells it because he says what they saw in Indonesia was a 300-foot UFO in the jungle. If true, it would show how hard the military worked to keep such a sighting quiet.","air-force-lieutenant-colonel-ran-arv-test-program-at-edwards-1998":"An Air Force lieutenant colonel says he ran a programme between about 1998 and 2008 that tested \"alien reproduction vehicles\", craft built by copying alien technology, along with technology derived from them. The work is said to have taken place with the flight-test wing at Edwards Air Force Base and over the Nevada test ranges. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels he knows the officer, and he passes on the officer's claim without naming him.","air-force-lunex-crewed-moon-base-plan-1957":"Between about 1957 and 1961, the US Air Force is said to have drawn up its own detailed plans to send a crew to the Moon, a project known as Lunex, connected with Vandenberg Air Force Base, separate from NASA. The researcher Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels he \"recently learned that the Air Force had detailed plans for like an Air Force manned mission to the moon,\" though he names no source. If true, it would mean the military once meant to reach the Moon on its own account.","air-force-manned-orbiting-laboratory-cancelled-1969-1960":"The Air Force's Manned Orbiting Laboratory, a plan for a crewed military space station, was cancelled in June 1969, \"exactly 1 month before\" Apollo first reached the Moon, according to Michels. He tells it himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a hacker who says he found a secret NASA space program. The timing he points to hints that military space work may have gone out of public view rather than ended.","air-force-pilots-report-drones-up-to-36-000-ft-2020":"US Air Force pilots have been reporting drones flying as high as 36,000 feet, with something like 30 or 40 such reports over the last couple of years, in the early 2020s. Marik von Rennenkampff says this on the show, during an episode built around a challenge to the UFO skeptic Mick West, and he names no source for the figures. If it is true, unknown craft have been operating at the heights where airliners fly.","air-force-project-blue-book-1947":"The US Air Force set up its own program to investigate UFO reports, which Jesse Michels calls Operation Blue Book and which ran into the late 1960s. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. In another conversation he adds that the program was for a time run by a man named Edward J. Ruppelt, whom he describes as an Air Force general, and that Ruppelt seemed open to the idea that the phenomenon was real.","air-force-roswell-reports-project-mogul-and-crash-dummies-1994":"In the 1990s the US Air Force put out two reports on Roswell, the 1947 incident in which wreckage found near Roswell, New Mexico, was first announced as a flying saucer. A researcher named Michael Shermer, who tells Michels about them from memory without citing the texts, says the 1994 report tied the wreckage to Project Mogul, a secret programme of high-altitude balloons, and a 1996 report followed it. Shermer adds a motive: in his telling the Air Force admitted it had used a cover story because it did not want the Russians to learn it was flying these spy balloons, which would mean the saucer tale grew out of a secret kept for Cold War reasons.","air-force-takes-over-navy-ufo-investigations-1947":"In the late 1940s and 1950s, sailors on US Navy ships kept having UFO encounters. Then Air Force personnel would come aboard, \"throw their weight around\" and take over the investigations, which made the Navy crews angry. The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels this as something passed on by a man named Donald Kho. It would mean the Air Force was taking control of UFO cases across the services early on.","air-traffic-controller-watches-the-f-5-chase-1986":"In 1986 an unknown object led Brazilian F-5 fighter jets on a chase over São Paulo, popping up and vanishing like a game of hide and seek. Jose Manuel Fernandez, an air traffic controller at the city's Congonhas airport, tells Michels he watched it happen: \"the object was just playing hide and seek. It would just show up and appear at a very distant point.\" To him, that was something no ordinary aircraft could do.","aircraft-instruments-go-haywire-at-edge-of-the-bubble":"Planes flying over Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah long tied to reports of strange events, have had their instruments go haywire at the edge of what the owner calls a \"bubble\" over the land. The ranch's owner tells Michels this from his own experience there, saying fixed-wing aircraft have recorded it: \"their instruments go haywire.\" If true, it would mean something on the ranch can be measured from the air, not just felt or seen.","aircrew-briefing-to-congress-not-a-false-hit-2015":"Military aircrew who tracked unexplained objects in 2015 later briefed Congress and said \"we knew it was not a false hit,\" meaning the radar return was not an error. Marik von Rennenkampff raises this in a debate on the show with Mick West, a skeptic known for explaining UFO videos, and he asks West what the aircrew could have meant by it. His point is that the people who were there ruled out a sensor glitch.","akhenaten-abandons-amun-and-pharaohs-depicted-with-elongated":"When the Egyptian king Amenhotep III died, his son Amenhotep IV turned away from the god Amun, recognised only the sun god Aten, and renamed himself Akhenaten. That is the account a researcher named Luke Caverns gives Michels, without naming a source. In one telling he adds that the new king shut the doors of Karnak temple, which had been built to worship Amun, and frames it as a break with that god's priesthood. Caverns says the art of the period then changed all at once and began to show elongated skulls, and that nobody knows where that came from or why it appeared just then.","akhenaten-tribute-at-the-hale-solar-laboratory-1920":"When the astronomer George Ellery Hale designed his Hale Solar Laboratory in Pasadena in the 1920s, Michels says, he put a tribute to Akhenaten, the Egyptian pharaoh who worshipped the sun, in the arch above the entrance. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about pagan ritual and secret science around NASA, as a sign of ancient religious ideas running through modern science.","akhil-knows-what-his-mother-manisha-watched-read-and-did-whi-2020":"A boy named Akhil can say exactly what happened in a movie his mother Manisha watched while he was in another room and couldn't hear it, according to his mother. Ky Dickens, the filmmaker behind The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary about children said to read minds, passes on Manisha's words to Michels: \"I could come back up and he'll tell me exactly what happened in this movie.\" If true, it would mean Akhil picks up his mother's experience by telepathy.","akil-knows-about-the-amputee-athlete-film-his-mother-watched":"A mother in New Jersey named Manisha says her son Akil, one of the children in a documentary about telepathy by the filmmaker Ky Dickens, knew about a film she had watched downstairs without him, a \"movie of athlete whose leg was amputed and he got into Special Olympics\". She tells it on the show as something she saw herself, offered as a sign that the boy could pick up what was in her mind.","akil-writes-the-next-word-before-it-is-written-crossed-out-w-2023":"In New Jersey in 2023, a boy named Akil is said to have written down each next word of a test before it was shown to him, starting as soon as the last word was crossed out and someone began to think of the next one. A filmmaker named Ky Dickens, who made a documentary about telepathy called The Telepathy Tapes, tells Michels she watched this happen during a test that also involved someone named Manisha. If it happened as she describes, Akil was picking up words straight from another person's mind.","al-jazeera-clip-of-invisible-figure-climbing-onto-abrams-tan-2004":"Around 2004 in Iraq, possibly near Tikrit, an Al Jazeera news clip showed an invisible figure, a see-through blob with limbs, climbing up onto an American Abrams tank and down into the gunner's hatch. John Blitch, a former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, tells Michels he saw the footage himself, slowed down and played about three times, with first two limbs, then two more, then \"this blob climbs up onto the tank.\" He names the newscaster Katie Couric in telling it and connects what he saw to invisibility, which would mean someone on that battlefield could make themselves almost impossible to see.","al-qaeda-negotiating-with-pakistani-nuclear-scientists-after-2001":"After the 9/11 attacks in 2001, al-Qaeda was talking with Pakistani nuclear scientists to get a nuclear weapon it could use inside the United States. That is what Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer who worked on the threat at the agency's counterterrorism centre, tells Michels. He brings in the CIA director George Tenet and credits a book called A State of Mind: Faith and the CIA. If true, it shows how close the country came to a terrorist nuclear attack.","alberino-measures-the-tombs-of-the-giants":"The Tombs of the Giants, ancient stone tombs on the Italian island of Sardinia, are big enough to hold the bodies of giants, according to Tim Alberino, a researcher of giant legends. He tells Michels he went there himself \"with a tape measure,\" and that \"yes, you can fit the bodies of giants in those tombs,\" which he offers as support for the idea that very tall people were once buried there.","alberino-s-aid-expedition-to-san-antonio-de-pintoyaku-2023":"The filmmaker Tim Alberino says that in 2023 he led an aid trip to San Antonio de Pintoyaku, a village in the Peruvian Amazon, and was met there by \"a small army of men with loaded shotguns\", the first time that had ever happened to him. He tells Michels he was there himself, alongside companions he names as Doug Thornton, Ronnie Vernet and a man called Christian.","alberino-s-ufo-hovering-above-his-car":"A UFO came down and hung silently about 40 feet above his car, as if he was meant to see it, according to Tim Alberino, who tells Michels he saw it himself. In one telling he places it in a strip mall parking lot in Brook Park, Ohio, near NASA's Glenn research centre, one February about 14 years before, with a companion named Tony beside him. Alberino tells it as the experience that drew him into the UFO subject.","albert-bender-s-three-men-in-black-teleport-him-to-antarctic-1950":"In the 1950s, a man named Albert Bender said three \"men in black\" appeared out of thin air in his attic. These were mysterious visitors in dark suits, and he said they had glowing orange eyes, smelled of sulfur, and later took him to Antarctica in an instant. The researcher Jason Jorjani retells this from Bender's own book, The Saucers and the Three Men.","albert-bruce-collins-sees-a-cracked-egg-craft-on-a-lowboy-tr-1948":"In 1948, a man named Albert Bruce Collins is said to have seen a lowboy trailer, a long, low flatbed for heavy loads, at the University of California, Berkeley, carrying a recovered craft. The online UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb passes on Collins's account, drawing on Leonard Stringfield and Tim Cooper, as evidence that crashed craft were quietly handed to universities for study. Michels tells a version of the same story from Michael Schratt and Stringfield: an egg-shaped craft, cracked, with a sphere inside, taken to the Berkeley labs to be studied. He gives no date and does not name Collins.","albert-stein-s-deathbed-call-to-sheehan":"A man named Albert Stein, dying in a hospice in northern Minnesota, called the lawyer Dan Sheehan from his deathbed. Sheehan tells Michels it happened to him: \"he called me on his deathbed, he was dying up in northern Minnesota.\" What Stein wanted to get off his chest is not given in the telling, but a deathbed call is the kind of last confession that makes people take a story seriously.","alcibiades-killed-for-talking-about-the-eleusinian-mysteries":"An ancient Greek figure named Alcibiades is said to have been killed for talking about the Eleusinian mysteries, the secret religious rites held at Eleusis in Greece. Michels brings this up himself in passing, without naming a source. A researcher named Luke Caverns, on another episode, tells a milder version: Alcibiades got in trouble for talking about the mysteries, and Caverns places it in Athens. The story is told to show how fiercely the secrets of those rites were guarded.","alcubierre-s-1994-faster-than-light-solution":"The claim is that in 1994 a Mexican physics student named Miguel Alcubierre showed that faster-than-light travel is possible in theory under Einstein's own equations. A man named Karl Nell tells it on the show. He says Alcubierre, then a postgraduate at the University of Mexico, \"solved Einstein's equations for an effective faster than light mechanism.\" Michels raises it on two later episodes. He notes that the proof only works \"if you do have negative energy,\" and he pairs it with a chip that a NASA engineer, Harold \"Sonny\" White, built around the Casimir effect, a tiny force that appears between very close metal plates. The point is that real physics may allow the kind of travel reported for UFOs.","aldo-rebelo-on-varginha-1996":"Brazil's former defense minister, a man named Aldo Rebelo who is now running for president, says that the 1996 events in Varginha, a Brazilian town where people say a craft came down and a strange creature was captured, really happened. He told Michels on the show: \"The phenomenon existed. It was there. The objects were there. The creature was seen.\" Michels, who flew to Brazil to interview him, later sums up what Rebelo said as \"Yeah, this happened,\" because it is rare for a former defense chief to say this.","aldous-huxley-at-the-round-table-foundation-1950":"The English writer Aldous Huxley was a close friend of the man who ran the Round Table Foundation, a private research group in Rockland, Maine, during the 1950s. Huxley was \"there all the time\". The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this, drawing on the book The Sacred Mushroom, to show the kind of company that gathered at the foundation.","aldous-huxley-takes-a-high-dose-of-lsd-while-dying-1963":"The writer Aldous Huxley, as he lay dying in 1963, is said to have asked his wife to give him an extremely high dose of LSD. Michels tells this himself, as something he believes he remembers, in a conversation about psychedelic drugs with the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore. He brings it up as an example of someone choosing to meet death through a psychedelic experience.","aldrin-s-communion-as-masonic-ritual-on-the-moon-1969":"Michels says that during Apollo 11 in 1969 Buzz Aldrin took communion on the Moon, and in one episode calls it \"like a masonic ritual on the moon.\" In another episode he describes it more plainly as \"the first religious ceremony ever conducted on the moon,\" without the Masonic framing. He tells it himself, without naming a source, as a hint that ritual and secret societies were bound up with the space program.","alex-dietrich-on-ufologists-enjoying-anticipation-2004":"Alex Dietrich is a Navy fighter pilot who saw a UAP (an unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the official term for a UFO) in 2004 while flying from the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz. She has said that UFO researchers seem to enjoy the anticipation of finding aliens more than any answer. The skeptic Michael Shermer quotes her to Michels, saying he would give \"the last word to Dietrich\" because her remark \"well sums up 75 years of ufologist search for aliens.\"","alex-jones-s-on-air-prediction-of-an-attack-on-the-world-tra-2001":"Before the 2001 attacks, the broadcaster Alex Jones said on air that if a terrorist group hit the World Trade Center, everyone would know who to blame, according to Jesse Michels. Michels tells this himself in two conversations with the writer Eric Wargo about seeing the future, and in the second he places the remark on CNN, addressed to Osama bin Laden: \"if you take down the Twin Towers I'm gonna come after you.\" He offers it as a possible case of knowing about an event before it happened.","alexander-says-mj-12-was-continuity-of-government":"An Army colonel named John B. Alexander is said to have explained that Majestic 12, or MJ-12, a name long tied in UFO lore to a secret group handling crashed craft, was really a continuity of government programme, a plan to keep the government running through a disaster. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP being the official term for unidentified anomalous phenomena) repeats this from Alexander. A researcher named Grant Cameron says Alexander said it in an interview, and adds that, in Alexander's words, there was such a group \"but it didn't have anything to do with UFOs\". The tellers bring it up because it would make the famous name real but turn it away from UFOs.","alfred-krupp-and-axel-wenner-gren-in-nassau-1955":"In 1955 the German industrialist Alfred Krupp went to Nassau, in the Bahamas, with Axel Wenner-Gren, a Swedish multi-billionaire and one of the richest men in the world. A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source. He links it to what he calls \"the Maison Rouge meeting\" and to his argument that the UFO cover-up leads back to one secret society, which would put very rich and powerful men at the centre of that secret if true.","alfred-o-donnell-told-george-knapp-of-a-recovered-saucer-wit-1950":"A flying saucer with living beings aboard was recovered and kept at a site next to Area 51, the secret Nevada test base, according to what a man named Alfred O'Donnell is said to have told George Knapp. A guest who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels that O'Donnell described the saucer \"with live biologics\" and said it was moved there in the early 1950s, \"1954 to be exact\". Michels tells it himself differently: he says O'Donnell, \"a senior EG&G manager\" at a company called EG&G, told Knapp in 2023 that the saucer was recovered in New Mexico and that there was \"a live being that had humanlike features\". If true, it would mean the government held not only a craft but a living occupant.","algorithm-analysis-of-john-ward-s-mars-anomalies":"Odd shapes spotted on the surface of Mars by a man named John Ward, who combed through orbital photos for anomalies, have been put through computer tests to see whether nature could have made them. Maaneli Derakhshani, a researcher who did the analysis himself, tells Michels he applied \"fractal and texture-based algorithms\" to images from NASA's Mars Global Surveyor orbiter and the HiRISE high-resolution Mars camera to judge how likely the features are to be natural. If they scored as unlikely to be natural, it would point to something built on Mars.","alice-scully-says-photos-of-the-aztec-crash-still-exist-1948":"Photographs of the Aztec crash, a flying saucer said to have come down near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948 with alien bodies on board, still exist. A woman named Alice Scully said so to a man named Chris Evans, and she told him they \"definitely\" survive. Scott Ramsey, who has investigated the Aztec case, passes this on to Michels, and if he is right, there would be pictures of the crash.","alien-autopsy-document-and-the-other-edgar-mitchell-papers-2000":"A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron says that a man named James Rigney sent him a set of papers tied to the Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell, among them an \"alien autopsy document\" that had the name of a man called Kit Green on it. Cameron tells this as someone who received the papers himself, and says they also included a condolence letter connected to Charles Lindbergh and a record of meetings of NIDS, a private UFO research group. If real, the papers would tie a moon astronaut's circle to talk of examining an alien body.","alien-autopsy-photos-in-s4-briefing-1988":"Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site in Nevada, in 1988 and 1989, says the briefing papers he read there included autopsy photos of a being. He tells Michels it was \"a gray something small\" with \"a T cut in the chest\" and \"one single organ removed from the chest.\" He tells it as something he saw himself, and if true, the US government held the body of a non-human being.","alien-bases-as-government-granted-reservations":"Alien bases on Earth may be something like reservations, places that the US and other governments have handed over on a deal of \"you stay away from us and we'll stay away from you.\" A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan puts this idea to Michels as his own view. If it were so, governments would have quietly struck agreements with visitors and set land aside for them.","alien-bodies-and-a-live-gray-recovered-at-roswell-1947":"The claim is that in 1947 the military recovered alien bodies from the wreck at Roswell, New Mexico, along with a living \"gray,\" the familiar small grey-skinned alien. The scientist John Brandenburg tells Michels, \"I'm quite confident that they recovered bodies,\" and points to a book called Cosmic Awakening. He gives his view as a researcher, not as a witness.","alien-bodies-studied-in-secret-by-biologists":"The bodies of alien beings are said to be in the United States, where biologists have studied them in secret. The writer Whitley Strieber tells Michels he knows \"we have bodies here\" that biologists have studied, and that \"those studies are ended.\" Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, gives it as an impression he picked up second hand. He speaks of \"genetics studies\" on tissue from recovered beings and says, \"I think that has been done,\" without saying whether the work has stopped.","alien-channeling-at-the-national-photographic-interpretation-1959":"In 1959, at the National Photographic Interpretation Center, a government office in Washington that analysed photographs, a group of officials is said to have watched someone channel an alien, and then to have seen a flying saucer out the window. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells it twice on the show, linking it to Arthur Lundahl, who ran the center. He says the channeler started with automatic writing, meaning writing in a trance supposedly guided by someone else. When Lundahl asked for proof, the channeler switched to speaking aloud and said to go to the window. There, according to Cameron, 'this flying saucer goes flying across in front of the capital.' Cameron dates the meeting to July 9, 1959, cites an 11-page memo on it, and mentions Frances Swan and others connected to the story. A second, unnamed narrator in the same episode tells it more cautiously, saying 'the commander, still in a trance,' sent them to the window, where they 'supposedly observed a UFO', and names no one. If true, it would mean government photo analysts took a channeled contact seriously enough to test it, and believed it passed.","alien-image-in-the-west-desert-test-center-cafeteria":"There is said to be a picture of an alien in the cafeteria of the West Desert Test Center, part of Dugway Proving Ground, a large Army testing range in the Utah desert. An online researcher known as UAPGerb shows it on the show, crediting it to a man named Ryan W. Harris, and says: \"this is in the cafeteria at the West Desert Test Center.\" Michels mentions it himself on a later episode as \"a little gray alien being\" in the cafeteria at Dugway Proving Grounds. They bring it up as a hint that the people who work at the base know more about aliens than the public does.","alien-information-theory-reality-as-a-slice-of-a-higher-dime":"The book Alien Information Theory puts forward the idea that our reality is a slice of a larger structure with more dimensions, and that the drug DMT, a strong psychedelic, may open a view onto it. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, who wrote it, tells Michels that readers often take the book to be exactly what he believes. He suggests the book sets out a model to think with, not a creed.","alien-involvement-with-earth-spans-more-than-one-country-1990":"A former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA trained him between 1990 and 1993 to take messages from aliens by telepathy, says a superior told him that the alien involvement with Earth was \"more than one country\". He tells Michels this as something said to him directly; if true, it would mean the contact was not one nation's secret.","alien-propulsion-via-electromagnetic-energy-and-time-1990":"Alien craft move using electromagnetic energy and time, according to a former sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him in the early 1990s to communicate with aliens and that this is what he learned while doing it, at a place he calls Base 2. He tells Michels this in connection with Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada, and if true it would mean the craft work on a principle that goes beyond ordinary engines.","alien-reproduction-vehicle-pilot-and-pilot-manager-at-edward":"The United States has built and flown its own copies of alien craft, which some call alien reproduction vehicles, out of Edwards Air Force Base in California. Steven Greer, who gathers UFO whistleblowers, tells Michels that he has a whistleblower who flew one, and, \"more importantly,\" the man who managed all the pilots who flew them there. If true, it would mean humans are already flying craft copied from alien ones.","alien-reproduction-vehicles-exist-and-are-stored-at-known-si":"Alien reproduction vehicles, human-built copies of alien craft, really exist, says Michael Herrera, who says he saw a huge craft in the Indonesian jungle. He tells Michels he knows where some of them are stored, but he will not say more than that.","aliens-anti-gravity-methods-homopolar-generator-and-biefeld":"The craft he saw over Fillmore, California beat gravity in two ways, says Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects he believes are alien implants in human bodies. He tells Michels that one method is a homopolar generator, a simple electric generator built around a spinning disc in a magnetic field, and the other is the Biefeld-Brown effect, a thrust that some say comes from high-voltage electrical charge. If he is right, alien propulsion would rest on electrical principles humans already know about.","aliens-call-humans-water-beings-1990":"A non-human being once referred to people as \"water vessels or water beings, water humans,\" according to Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens. Sherman tells Michels this happened in his own work between 1990 and 1993, at a site he calls Base 2, and says the being \"always\" used those words for us, a sign of how it saw humans: as creatures made mostly of water.","aliens-have-two-sexes-and-eliminate-waste-1990":"A former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says he took telepathic messages from aliens for the NSA between 1990 and 1993, says he learned that the beings \"do have female and male\" and that they eliminate bodily waste. He tells Michels it came from his time in that work, at a place he calls Base 2, and it would mean the beings are living creatures much like us rather than machines.","aliens-have-visited-humanity-for-a-very-long-time-1990":"Aliens have been coming to Earth for a very long time, says a former sergeant named Dan Sherman, who tells Michels the NSA hired him in the early 1990s to communicate with them. He says this was the impression he took away from his exchanges with them, which came up several times, and if true it would mean humans have shared their history with visitors for ages.","aliens-on-time-travel-and-wormholes":"The aliens he met aboard a craft over Fillmore, California told him they can travel in time, says Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies what he believes are alien implants in human bodies. He tells Michels they \"don't like to use it very much,\" and especially avoid long jumps, and the story also takes in wormholes, shortcuts through space and time. If true, the visitors would have time travel and hold back from using it.","aliens-take-people-to-build-a-critical-mass-1997":"Aliens take certain people, one after another, to build up a critical mass of humans who have had contact, according to a man who was president of one of Russia's republics and says he was taken himself in 1997. He tells Michels that he asked the beings directly \"why they take some people like they took me and tomorrow they might take you,\" and the answer he describes is that the taking is building toward a large enough number of people.","aliens-tell-him-they-created-humans-and-humanity-is-not-read-1997":"A man named Kiran Ilum Xenov says that in 1997 he was taken aboard a spacecraft, where the beings told him they had made humankind and that humanity is not yet ready to know them. He tells Michels he was there, and quotes them as saying: \"Your only achievement in civilization is that we created you, the intelligent beings here.\"","aline-griffith-on-skorzeny-taking-over-the-wcc-1950":"According to the journals of Aline Griffith, an operation the story calls the WCC changed in the 1950s, when the former Nazi commando Otto Skorzeny \"came in and took over the operation.\" The researcher Jason Simosa reads this from her journals. He says that by her account things were \"very different eight or nine years after\" the start. He does not spell out here what the WCC was.","allan-sandler-s-vandenberg-atlas-rocket-disc-footage-1970":"Film of an Atlas rocket launch at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California is said to show two discs flying around the rocket's tip. If it exists, it would be official footage of unknown craft. The investigator James Fox describes the film second-hand and links it to a man named Allan Sandler. The researcher Grant Cameron says he heard about it from Bob Emenegger, who worked with Sandler. Cameron dates the film to 1964 and says that when he asked where it was, Emenegger answered, \"I don't think we sent that one back,\" and \"I guess it's in Alan Sandler's vault,\" which Cameron places in Oregon.","alleged-photograph-of-one-live-and-one-dead-creature-held-by-1996":"After the 1996 events in Varginha, Brazil, where creatures are said to have been captured, a man named Pedro Aguiar reportedly took out a photograph that showed one living creature and one dead one. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels he has this from people who described seeing Aguiar show the picture. If it existed, it would be a photo of two non-human beings from the incident.","allegro-s-theory-that-the-eucharist-was-an-amanita-muscaria":"The Eucharist, the bread of Christian communion, is said by one scholar to have been in its origin a mushroom, the red and white Amanita muscaria, which alters the mind. Michels tells this himself, crediting a scholar named John Marco Allegro and his book The Sacred Mushroom and the Cross, and calls it a \"very heretical conclusion.\" If true, it would mean early Christianity grew out of a sacred drug.","allen-dulles-arranged-transfer-of-the-magenta-object-under-t-1940":"A craft is said to have come down near Magenta in Italy, and in the 1940s it was moved to the United States, with Allen Dulles arranging the steps so that President Truman could have it transferred from Italy. Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, tells Michels this as something he was told. Michels tells it differently on another show, also citing Malmgren: there the craft was smuggled out along the Nazi \"ratlines,\" escape routes out of Europe that Reinhard Gehlen helped run, carried by the Knights of Malta. That version names Henry Kissinger, but neither Dulles nor Truman.","allen-dulles-cuts-off-ruth-paine-s-warren-commission-testimo-1964":"In 1964, at the Warren Commission, the panel that investigated Kennedy's killing, the former CIA director Allen Dulles is said to have cut off a witness named Ruth Paine while she was testifying. The writer Peter Levenda tells Michels this from his reading of the Warren Commission volumes, the commission's published record. In his words, Dulles \"is part of that committee hearing. He stops her.\" Michels tells it himself on another episode and says what Dulles was hiding. In his telling, Dulles \"did not want the fact that Ruth Paine had actually been at the summer house\" to come out. Michels credits this to a woman named Mary Bancroft. Levenda does not say what was being hidden. They tell it because, if true, a CIA man steered what went into the official record of the assassination.","allen-dulles-witnessing-a-saucer":"Allen Dulles, the head of the CIA in the 1950s, is said to have seen a flying saucer himself, in a story that also involves checking a lock. The philosopher and researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels he has \"heard the same stuff\" about Dulles, but he passes it on only in passing and gives no details. If true, it would mean a CIA director saw a saucer with his own eyes.","allergen-test-and-immune-boosting-drink-from-the-s-4-nurse-1988":"Before he started work at S-4, a secret site in the Nevada desert where he says alien craft were being studied, a nurse tested Bob Lazar for allergies and gave him a drink that was supposed to boost his immune system. Lazar tells this to Michels as part of his own account of being hired there in 1988 and 1989. Why the drink was needed, he does not say here, but it would suggest the job carried some biological risk.","allied-foo-fighter-investigations-with-robertson-and-alvarez-1942":"During the Second World War, Allied pilots in Europe and the Pacific reported strange lights following their planes, nicknamed foo fighters, and the Allies are said to have set up a study of them. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, tells Michels without naming a source that one of the people \"assigned to conduct that study happened to be Dr. Howard Robertson from Caltech,\" and he links the physicist Luis Alvarez to the work too. If true, it would mean serious scientists were studying unexplained objects years before the UFO era began.","allied-skiers-sabotage-the-heavy-water-plant-1940":"During the Second World War, Michels recalls, the Allies sent \"a team of like Nordic skiers or something\" to destroy a plant in Norway that made heavy water, a special kind of water used in early nuclear research that Nazi Germany needed. He mentions it in passing, from memory, in a conversation with a writer named Jason Jorjani about Hitler's secret weapons programmes, as part of the story of how the Nazi nuclear effort was stopped.","alonzo-mcdonald-in-vallee-s-journals-1977":"In the late 1970s, the story goes, Alonzo McDonald, a senior aide in Jimmy Carter's White House, was \"uniquely involved\" in the UFO question. The researcher Jason Simosa makes the point from the published journals of Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and well-known UFO researcher, who he says singles McDonald out.","alsace-entity-who-said-the-witness-had-the-time-wrong-and-as":"A witness in Alsace, the French region on the German border, is said to have met a strange being that told him he had the time wrong and then asked him, \"am I in Italy or Germany?\" The witness answered, \"you're in Alsace.\" The researcher Jacques Vallée tells the case to Michels as a summary, without naming his source or giving a date, as an example of the odd, almost absurd things such beings are reported to say.","altntepe-425-miles-away-shares-the-same-symbols":"A site in Turkey called Altıntepe, about 425 miles from the other ancient sites under discussion, carries the same symbols as they do. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this without naming a source, and he offers it as a sign that the shared imagery spread over a very wide area.","amanita-muscaria-esp-research-at-the-round-table-1950":"In the 1950s the Round Table Foundation, a private research group in Rockland, Maine, studied whether eating Amanita muscaria, a red-and-white mushroom, gave people ESP, meaning powers such as reading minds or sensing distant things. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels the group did \"extensive research\" on this. He draws on the book The Sacred Mushroom.","amazon-shaman-with-a-wife-in-the-spirit-world":"There is a shaman in the Amazon whose wife left him because he also had a wife in the spirit world. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels this as something he was told by someone else. He uses it to show how real the spirit world is to the people who visit it.","amazonian-ayahuasca-as-a-technology-for-building-relationshi":"Ayahuasca, a psychedelic brew made in the Amazon, is in this view a tool the Amazonian peoples built for forming relationships with beings met in the altered state. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore says this to Michels without naming a source, calling it \"a true pharmacological technology\". If he is right, these peoples were working out a deliberate method of contact long before modern science studied DMT, the powerful psychedelic compound in the brew.","amazonian-tribesmen-identify-a-grey-as-makunwabu":"When people showed an image of a \"grey\", the small grey-skinned alien with large dark eyes that many abduction accounts describe, to a tribe in the Amazon, the tribesmen recognised it and said \"that's Makunwabu,\" a name of their own. Jay Anderson tells Michels this second-hand and does not say who showed the image. The point is that a people far from modern UFO lore seemed to already know the being.","ambiance-exercise-and-step-16-of-controlled-remote-viewing-t":"Training in controlled remote viewing, a step-by-step method for trying to perceive distant places with the mind, includes something called the ambiance exercise and a sixteenth step. Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant who trained remote viewers, describes these to Michels from his own experience, saying simply, \"We have a thing called the ambiance exercise.\" How the exercise works is not spelled out in the telling, but it is part of the method he says he taught.","amelia-evaluated-at-the-treffert-center-2020":"A girl named Amelia, one of the children in a documentary about nonspeaking children said to show telepathy, is said to have been assessed in the 2020s at the Treffert Center in Wisconsin, a centre named after the researcher of savants Darold Treffert. The psychiatrist Diane Powell, who studies these children, mentions it on the show without naming a source, pointing to \"the Treffert center there... because that's where Darold Treffert\" worked. The point is that an outside expert centre looked at the girl's abilities.","amelia-found-able-to-spell-read-minds-see-spirits-and-know-o-2020":"A girl named Amelia, who had seemed unable to communicate, turned out to be \"in there\" around 2020 and 2021, and her family found she could spell, then read her mother Mora's mind, and see spirits and ghosts her mother couldn't. She went on to say she knew other languages, could pick out Egyptian hieroglyphics, and knew Hebrew, Portuguese and Spanish. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary series about children said to read minds, tells Michels the story as the family told it to her. If true, it would mean some children who seem shut off have abilities far beyond what anyone expected.","amelia-reads-hebrew-and-identifies-hieroglyphics-she-had-nev-2021":"Amelia, a girl in the Midwest who communicates by spelling out words, was shown some Hebrew, read it and typed out what it meant, and when asked who taught her Hebrew she typed \"the rabbi\"; she also identified hieroglyphics she had never seen. Her mother, Mora, tells this on the show in her own words, as something that happened at home and was part of The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary about children said to read minds. The filmmaker behind it, Ky Dickens, retells it differently, saying the hieroglyphics test was run by Amelia's spelling therapist and babysitter.","amelia-s-spanish-teacher-says-she-knows-more-spanish-than-th-2021":"A nonspeaking girl named Amelia, who appears in The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary series about children said to read minds, is said to know so much Spanish that her teacher said, \"she knows more Spanish than my kids.\" Michels tells this himself, paraphrasing the teacher, in an episode with the series' maker. If true, it would suggest the girl learned in ways beyond ordinary teaching.","amelia-says-an-angel-took-her-to-see-god-and-names-a-rabbi-a-2021":"A person named Amelia, one of those in a documentary about nonspeaking people said to communicate by telepathy, says an angel took her to see God \"once or twice.\" She also names a rabbi and the Dalai Lama as her teachers. The filmmaker Ky Dickens relays this from what Amelia told her.","amentum-owns-parts-of-eg-g":"A company called Amentum is said to own parts of EG&G, an older defense contractor. Matthew Brown, described as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells Michels: \"I believe if you look in their history uh they own parts of EG&G.\" He raises it to trace which firms may have inherited secret government work.","america-named-after-the-peruvian-god-amaru":"The name America comes from Amaru, a god of Peru, and the explorer Amerigo Vespucci changed his own name to sound like it, not the other way around. The comedian Kurt Metzger says this on the show. If true, it would mean that the name of the continents is older than European contact.","american-heart-association-testifies-against-soft-drink-remo-2020":"When an effort to remove soft drinks came before the Texas Senate in Austin sometime in the 2020s, the American Heart Association showed up to testify against it, according to Brigham Buhler, who was part of that effort. Buhler tells Michels this from having been there, calling it a sign of \"the world we live in now,\" since a heart health group took the side of the soda.","american-orthodox-catholic-church-demands-fbi-background-che-1965":"In the Bronx in New York, a man called Propheta led a small church called the American Orthodox Catholic Church. Around 1965, he began demanding that other churches submit to FBI background checks to prove they were acceptable to him. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this, and in a second telling adds, from what he was told, that Propheta's own priests and bishops \"were vetted by J. Edgar Hoover himself,\" dating that to 1968 or 1969. If true, the FBI was tied closely to this tiny church.","american-pilots-trained-to-fly-ufos-despite-time-displacemen":"American pilots are being trained in a deep underground facility in California to fly UFOs, despite what the story calls time displacement, an effect of flying the craft on the pilots' sense of time. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that people there told this to Lyn Buchanan, a former military remote viewer, meaning someone trained to gather information by psychic means. If so, the US would have working UFOs and people to fly them.","american-soldier-s-off-record-sighting":"A surgeon named Italo Venturelli says an American soldier once told him privately of something he saw on top of his house. The soldier said, \"if I say anything, I'd lose my pension in the US.\" Venturelli passes it on second-hand, and the soldier's fear suggests that people who see such things are kept quiet.","ammonia-smell-and-staining-inside-the-red-pyramid-2017":"The pyramid researcher Geoffrey Drumm says that inside the Red Pyramid, an ancient pyramid at Dahshur in Egypt, there is \"chemical staining all over the walls and an intense smell of ammonia that emanates from the final chamber.\" He tells Michels he saw and smelled it himself in 2017, and takes it as a sign the pyramids once served as chemical works rather than tombs.","amsterdam-secret-space-program-symposium-talk-on-ufos-and-th-2007":"In Amsterdam, around 2007 or 2008, the writer Peter Levenda gave a talk at a gathering called the secret space program, a meeting on hidden government space work, and it touched on UFOs and the Kennedy assassination. Levenda tells Michels about it himself, saying people still make assumptions about his views because of that presentation, though he no longer remembers the exact year.","amy-reynolds-taken-and-found-at-a-petrol-station-790-km-away":"A woman named Amy Reynolds was taken while her friend Petra watched through a window, and soon afterward the friends got a call saying she had been found at a petrol station 790 kilometres away. The researcher Mike Masters calls it \"probably one of my favorite cases\" and tells it on an episode about his idea that the beings behind such events are humans from the future, without naming where he learned of it. If true, she covered that distance in a way no ordinary travel explains.","an-advanced-civilization-made-the-genetic-mix-1-800-years-ag":"About 1,800 years ago, a civilisation more advanced than ours probably made the genetic mix found in the three-fingered bodies said to come from Nazca, Peru. That is the view of a researcher named Thierry Jamin, who tells Michels it is \"crazy\" but \"likely\" and names no source. If he were right, it would mean someone in the distant past had technology beyond our own.","an-alien-pilot-teaches-buchanan-to-fly-a-ufo":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant known for remote viewing, the practice of trying to perceive distant places with the mind, tells Michels that an alien pilot once taught him to fly a UFO. He says the alien \"took me up front. He was the pilot. So, I tried it and I learned how to do it.\" In a second telling in the same conversation he adds where it began: he was taken from the parsonage of a country church in East Texas, sometime between 1963 and 1965. He says the craft then landed \"at some place that was not Earth.\" If true, a human would have flown an alien craft himself, to another world.","an-angel-with-a-sword-over-jerusalem-during-the-plague-after":"When King David counted his people, the Bible says, a plague followed, and an angel stood over Jerusalem with a drawn sword. Michels brings this up himself from the King James Bible of 1611, which describes \"the angel of the LORD stand between the earth and the heaven, having a drawn sword in his hand stretched out over Jerusalem.\" He offers it as an old account of a being seen hanging in the sky above a city.","an-anonymous-frontier-physicist-s-experience":"A very high-level physicist, a friend of Michels, is said to have had an unusual experience of his own. Michels brings this up himself and keeps the friend anonymous, and in this mention he gives no details of what happened.","an-insectoid-working-in-a-row-of-humans-during-a-classified":"During a classified mission at a military facility, a creature of the kind called an \"insectoid,\" an alien said to look like an insect, was working in a row alongside humans, according to Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant who says he walked past it. Buchanan tells Michels he saw it only for a moment, because \"the minute I saw it, I looked away and kept walking.\" He hedges, too, saying it \"could have been a soldier that just put on an alien hat,\" and that he does not know.","an-nsc-staffer-told-andreessen-and-horowitz-that-areas-of-ph":"According to several guests, the tech investors Marc Andreessen and Ben Horowitz met a staff member of the National Security Council who told them that whole areas of physics had once been classified. If that is true, the government has hidden basic science before. A guest named Matthew Pines, who calls it hearsay, quotes the staffer as saying \"we have classified entire areas of fundamental physics before\" and \"don't think we can do the same for AI.\" Michels cites the meeting as corroboration of hidden physics, and he also points to an interview Andreessen gave the journalist Bari Weiss, where he says the investors were told only \"two or three guys\" would be allowed to work on AI. The researcher Eric Weinstein says they were told, \"Do not invest in AI startups.\" A guest named Pippa Malmgren, who also credits Weinstein, adds that \"many technologies, particularly in the nuclear space,\" were placed out of public reach.","an-ultra-orthodox-community-in-israel-has-its-own-hill-2023":"Children in an ultra-Orthodox community in Israel are said to have their own \"Hill.\" That is the name used in the documentary The Telepathy Tapes for a place the children it follows, who are said to read minds, describe meeting in their minds. The documentary maker Ky Dickens tells Michels this as something she heard from members of the Golden family, Arthur Golden and Ben Golden. If true, the experience would be shared by children far apart.","anakah-and-tristan-s-off-world-past-lives-and-their-mission":"Two people named Anakah and Tristan say they remember past lives lived away from Earth, on other planets, and that they are now here on a mission to make humanity's first contact with other beings go more smoothly. The researcher Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a doctorate and talks with Michels about psychics and the afterlife, passes this on from what the pair have said: they \"claim at this point to have memories of having lived outside of the Earth on other planets and other lifetimes and that they're on a mission.\" If true, it would mean some people now on Earth carry memories of lives elsewhere.","analysing-element-115-with-barry-at-s4-1988":"Bob Lazar, a physicist who says he worked in 1988 and 1989 at S4, a hidden site in the Nevada desert where he says captured alien craft were studied, says he and a colleague named Barry analysed element 115, a superheavy element he says fuelled the craft. He tells Michels he took part himself and insists \"It wasn't discovered. It's something Barry and I were working on.\"","analysis-by-merrick-s-colleagues-points-to-a-tic-tac-shaped-2004":"The analyst Marik von Rennenkampff says that analysis by colleagues, including an unnamed scientist in California, gives him \"fairly high confidence\" that the object in the FLIR1 video was a craft shaped like a Tic Tac. FLIR1 is infrared camera footage filmed from a Navy jet off Southern California in 2004. The object is the \"Tic Tac,\" a white oval shaped like the mint. He says this in a debate with the skeptic Mick West, who reads the same video as an ordinary object.","analysis-of-coburn-s-toe-implant-skewed-isotopes-and-nanotec":"A small object taken out of the toe of a person named Coburn, believed by some to be an implant placed in the body by non-human beings, was found on analysis to have unusual isotope ratios and features that looked like nanotechnology, which would point to something not made by ordinary means. The researcher Steve Colbern tells Michels he did the analysis himself: Roger Leir, who removed such objects from people, got funding from Haime Masan to take it out a few months after it was found, had no one to study it, and Colbern had analytical equipment where he worked in California. Colbern credits a paper by Coburn on the toe implant.","analysis-of-heavy-element-real-deal-material":"Some people have analysed material from what David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, calls \"the real deal stuff\", meaning genuine recovered material, and he says it is \"super weird\". He tells Michels he has talked with those people himself, so he is repeating what they told him, and in one telling he adds that it was \"heavy element\" material, \"europium californium stuff\", naming two rare heavy elements. If true, it would mean someone holds physical material unusual enough to puzzle the scientists who studied it.","analysis-of-san-augustine-new-mexico-crash-wreckage-1947":"Wreckage from a flying saucer said to have crashed in 1947 on the Plains of San Agustin in New Mexico, a crash often linked to the Roswell story of the same year, was analysed by a scientist named Steve Colbern, who studies objects people say aliens put in their bodies. Colbern tells Michels he did the analysis himself, which, if the material is what he says, would put physical pieces of an alien craft in a lab.","analysis-of-titanium-nanotube-sphere-later-stolen":"A sphere recovered in Mexico was made of a titanium alloy with tiny carbon nanotubes built into the metal, and after it was analysed in the United States it was stolen. Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies what he believes are alien implants, tells Michels he worked on it, and he thinks the nanotubes gave it thrust through the Biefeld-Brown effect, a push that some say comes from high-voltage electrical charge. If he is right, the sphere was a working, engineered object built with materials beyond ordinary manufacture.","ancestor-rediscovers-the-seven-lost-spanish-gold-mines-of-ne":"An ancestor of the researcher Luke Caverns is said to have gone looking for the seven lost Spanish gold mines of southern New Mexico and found them within a year. Caverns tells Michels this as family history he was told, saying the man \"opened up these 450 year old gold mines.\" If true, mines the Spanish worked and then lost would have been found again in the American Southwest.","ancestry-search-places-sherman-in-omaha-near-offutt-afb-1993":"A genealogy records search is said to show that Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, lived in Omaha, Nebraska, near Offutt Air Force Base, in 1993 and 1994. Michels reports this himself, citing a man named Richard Geldreich and an ancestry.com search whose results showed Sherman \"likely in fact lived in Omaha.\" It is offered as backing for Sherman's account of where he served.","ancient-architects-on-the-giza-water-table":"A study about the water table under the Giza Plateau in Egypt was, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum, \"basically debunked\" by someone in the alternative-history community who goes by the name Ancient Architects. Drum tells Michels this while showing Ancient Architects' work, and he does not say which study it was or how it was answered. He brings it up as part of his case that a huge energy system lies beneath the pyramids.","ancient-as-above-so-below-symbol-on-property":"An old symbol on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property long tied to reports of strange events, stands for the saying \"as above, so below,\" according to the ranch's owner. He tells Michels this \"Masonic symbol on the property from ancient times seems to denote\" that what happens in the sky is matched by something below the ground, which fits his view that something lies under the ranch.","ancient-chess-pieces-in-mongolian-burial-mounds-as-an-alien":"Chess-like figures dug out of burial mounds in Mongolia, some \"more than 5,000 years old,\" are offered as a sign that chess was a gift from aliens. The claim comes from Kiran Ilum Xenov, a guest introduced as a former president of a Russian republic who says he was taken by aliens in 1997; he tells Michels about the excavations without naming a source, and also points to Peru. If true, the game would be far older than history says and would not be of human origin.","ancient-chinese-diagram-of-twin-serpent-creators-as-a-double":"The writer Peter Levenda says he found a diagram from China, which he puts at about 600 years old, showing twin serpent creators coiled around each other in a shape he likens to the double helix of DNA. He tells Michels this from his own research and credits a work called Stairway to Heaven, without saying more about it. If he is right, an old creation image would echo the shape of life's genetic code.","ancient-precision-buildings-in-peru-lebanon-and-egypt-built":"The ancient buildings of Peru, Lebanon and Egypt, the pyramids among them, were built \"with such precision\" that another civilization must have made them, according to Kiran Ilum Xenov. He is the guest of an episode billed as the story of a former president of a Russian republic who says he was taken by aliens in 1997, and he says this as his own view, without naming a source.","ancient-south-american-bird-models-resembling-airplanes":"Some small ancient models from South America that are usually called birds look like airplanes. A doctor who worked with NASA, Greg Rogers, brings them up in passing with Michels as an example, without naming a source. He seems to suggest that ancient people may have seen or known about flying machines.","ancient-spartan-doctor-s-treatise-describing-ptsd":"Combat trauma of the kind now called PTSD was recognised as far back as ancient Sparta, where a doctor wrote a treatise about it, according to Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor, who tells Michels there is archaeological evidence reaching back to the Spartans. He gives no title or name for the treatise, and offers it to show how long people have known that terrible experiences leave lasting marks on the mind.","ancient-temples-dismantled-and-items-removed":"Some ancient temples in the region of Lake Van in eastern Turkey were taken apart, and objects were taken out of them. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this without naming a source, saying he has found \"very very compelling evidence\" that it happened. He raises it in a talk about something big that he says happened around 12,000 BC, and if true it would mean someone has been removing or hiding evidence of that distant past.","ancient-terrestrial-civilization-with-near-luminal-travel":"An advanced civilization may have lived on Earth many hundreds of millions of years ago, perhaps one able to travel close to the speed of light, and its traces may have been wiped out by \"the relentless reshaping of the surface of the planet\". Eric Haseltine, a former senior NSA official, tells Michels this is one of the ideas explored in the book The New Science of UFOs, and he cites a man named Chris Gilbert. He offers it as a possibility, not a finding, and does not point to any evidence. If true, some of what gets called alien could have started here on Earth.","andres-gomez-emilsson-s-dmt-accounts-at-the-psychedelic-scie-2025":"At a psychedelic science conference in Denver in 2025, a man named Andrés Gómez Emilsson is said to have shared accounts of experiences on DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug that some users say takes them to other worlds. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore mentions it to Michels, saying he had spoken alongside Gómez Emilsson in Denver \"just a few days ago,\" though he gives little of what the accounts contained. Gallimore brings it up as part of his case that DMT may open a door to an alien realm.","andrew-basiago-claims-he-went-back-to-the-gettysburg-address-1863":"A man named Andrew Basiago claims he travelled back in time to be present at Lincoln's Gettysburg Address in 1863. Michels mentions this himself in a conversation with Grant Cameron, a researcher of the UFO subject, stressing that Basiago \"literally says\" he went back. He raises it as an example of how far some claims about secret programmes go.","andrew-gallimore-dmt-experiments-and-entities-with-consisten-2026":"People who take DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, often say they meet beings, and the story here is that these beings seem to fall into the same recognisable types from one person to the next, which would suggest they are more than private hallucination. Michels raises it in 2026 in a conversation with a guest named Donald Hoffman, mentioning in passing that Hoffman is working with a researcher named Andrew Gallimore, who runs these DMT experiments, without going into what they have found.","andrija-puharich-the-ossining-space-kids-and-the-council-of-1950":"Andrija Puharich, whom Michels calls the father of much of the CIA's mind-control work in MK Ultra, a secret CIA programme that tested ways of controlling the mind, also kept running into non-human intelligences. Michels says Puharich had a group of \"space kids\" in Ossining, New York, channel, meaning receive messages from, beings called the Council of Nine, sometime between the 1950s and the 1970s. Michels tells it from what the researcher Greg Malatzi has said and from a book called The Mind Traveler, and also mentions a man named Dan Sherman. If true, the same man was building the CIA's mind-control work and talking with beings that are not human.","andy-hall-s-article-supporting-the-chemical-plant-hypothesis-2020":"A writer named Andy Hall published a long post on his website backing the idea that the pyramids were chemical manufacturing plants powered by Earth's own electricity, according to Geoffrey Drumm, the man who came up with that idea. Drumm tells Michels that Hall's article, which he connects with the Thunderbolts Project, a group that stresses electricity's role in nature, set out \"the virtues of my work\" and backed his case. For Drumm it means another researcher has independently supported his theory.","angel-hair-and-an-ablative-craft-hull":"Luis Elizondo says the outer skin of an unexplained craft may be \"ablative,\" a sacrificial layer that wears or burns away in flight. The story ties this to \"angel hair,\" a fine, cobweb-like material that witnesses have reported drifting down after sightings, suggesting it could be shed from such a hull. Elizondo tells Michels he made this argument in his book Imminent, published in 2024.","angel-hierarchies-of-aquinas-line-up-with-modern-nhi-experie":"The ranks of angels described by the medieval theologian Thomas Aquinas match much of what people today report from contact with NHI, short for non-human intelligence, which would suggest old religion and modern UFO encounters describe the same thing. Michels says he heard this from Karl Nell, a retired Army colonel, and Diana Pasulka, a scholar of religion, who were both talking about it. In another episode he makes the point in his own voice, adding the ancient philosopher Iamblichus, and says some of these old accounts fit modern reports, giving as an example beings seen with irradiated light.","angleton-and-others-on-george-c-marshall-s-team-1945":"Many of the people later tied to the UFO secret, including James Jesus Angleton, who became a key figure at the CIA, are said to have served together on General George C. Marshall's team around the end of the Second World War. The researcher Pippa Malmgren tells Michels this as her own finding, without naming a source, saying they \"all seem to have been part of General George C. Marshall's team,\" and she also brings up John F. Kennedy and the defense secretary James Forrestal. If true, it would suggest a single circle of men handled the subject from the start.","angleton-and-reuben-efron-s-mail-intercept-on-oswald-1959":"From 1959 to 1963 the CIA read Lee Harvey Oswald's mail, according to the researcher Jeremy Riss, through an operation tied to James Jesus Angleton, the agency's longtime counterintelligence chief, and to a man named Reuben Efron. Riss tells Michels that Efron appears in the JFK files, the government records released on the Kennedy assassination, and that when one figure was asked in those files whether he knew Efron, he said no. Riss calls that answer \"a lie\", which would mean the CIA was watching Oswald closely years before the assassination and later hid it.","angleton-as-oss-man-in-italy-1940":"An American intelligence officer named James Jesus Angleton is said to have been the man of the OSS, America's wartime spy service before the CIA, in Italy during the 1940s. Michels says he learned this from a woman named Pippa Malmgren, who \"made it clear\" to him that Angleton was \"the US like OSS person involved in Italy at the time.\" He raises it to tie Angleton to events in Italy in that period.","angleton-may-have-pushed-mj-12-as-counterintelligence":"James Jesus Angleton, for decades the head of counterintelligence at the CIA, is said to have had a hand in the MJ-12 papers, documents that surfaced in the 1980s and 90s describing a secret panel said to manage crashed UFOs. Michels raises this himself three times, first as a maybe, then as hearsay (\"I don't know if this is true\"), and finally as his own view that \"many of these documents were created\" by Angleton \"mainly to catch Soviet spies.\" If so, the papers would be bait in a spy hunt rather than a straight record.","angleton-probably-leaked-the-mj12-documents":"The MJ-12 papers, documents describing a supposed top-secret committee called Majestic 12 set up to handle crashed saucers, are said to have been leaked by James Jesus Angleton, the CIA's longtime head of counterintelligence. Michels says this is what Harold Malmgren, a former senior US government official he spoke with, told him: that Angleton \"is probably responsible for the MJ12 docs getting leaked.\" If true, it would mean the papers came out through a deliberate move by an intelligence insider.","angleton-s-father-and-the-knights-of-malta":"The CIA officer James Jesus Angleton and his father, Hugh Angleton, may have been tied to the Knights of Malta, an old Catholic order of knights, and through it to secret knowledge of UFOs. Harold Malmgren, a former presidential advisor, says on the show that this link \"needs to be uncovered\" and places it in Italy and the Vatican in the 1930s and 40s, where Angleton was present. Michels goes further: he says outright that both men were Knights of Malta, and he later relays that Malmgren was \"obsessing\" over the idea that the order had something to do with a 1933 crash at Magenta, in Italy.","angleton-signed-cia-wiretap-of-marilyn-monroe-1962":"The CIA supposedly wiretapped Marilyn Monroe in 1962 and recorded her threatening to hold a press conference \"a couple days before they killed her\", in a document signed by James Jesus Angleton, the CIA's counterintelligence chief at the time. Steven Greer, a UFO activist who campaigns for government disclosure, tells Michels this and says the wiretap is part of the briefing document he hands out. If true, it would mean the CIA was watching Monroe and her death was no accident.","angry-owens-gives-mishlove-a-sore-throat-over-the-phone-1976":"Ted Owens, a man said to have mind-over-matter powers, once got angry on the phone and made the other man's throat hurt. That is what the researcher Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels, and he says he was the man on the other end of the call, in 1976. In his words, Owens \"got angry and within minutes I could feel this sensation in my throat.\" If that is true, a person could hurt someone far away just by wanting to. Michels tells the story again in a later episode, citing a book about Owens called The PK Man, PK being short for psychokinesis, moving things with the mind. His version is different: Mishlove suddenly \"stopped being able to breathe or something\" while they were on the phone.","animals-found-dead-at-the-varginha-zoo-after-contact-with-th-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, animals at the town zoo are said to have died after the creature people reported came into contact with them. A man named Ahmed Foratu tells this on the show, citing the documentary filmmaker James Fox. Fox adds that a scientist named Marco Le told him on camera that no one knew what killed the animals and that he had never seen anything like it, which would suggest the being carried something unknown and deadly.","anna-paulina-luna-calls-for-aaro-defunding-2025":"In 2025 Representative Anna Paulina Luna called for cutting the funding of AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate reports of UAP, the official term for UFOs. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels that Luna had been tweeting that week about \"what a hopeless cause AARO is,\" a sign that some in Congress have lost faith in the government's own UFO office.","anna-paulina-luna-on-joe-rogan-read-the-book-of-enoch-for-ua-2020":"Congresswoman Anna Paulina Luna has said that people who want to understand where today's unexplained flying objects come from should read the Book of Enoch, an ancient religious text about angels who came down to earth. In a clip of her talking to Joe Rogan on his interview show, played on Michels's programme, she says the book \"talks about the fall of angels, um, thus creating really the precursor of civilization that led to the first flood.\" Her point is that the visitors might be the same beings the old story describes. In R. H. Charles's 1917 translation, the fallen angel Azâzêl \"taught men to make swords, and knives, and shields\" and \"made known to them the metals\", and others taught enchantments, astrology and \"the course of the moon.\" Then \"there arose much godlessness\", Noah is warned that \"a deluge is about to come upon the whole earth\", and \"the whole earth has been corrupted through the works that were taught by Azâzêl.\" So the order she describes is in the text: angels come down, teach people, corruption spreads, and a flood follows. Reading those angels as the beings behind today's sightings is her step, which the text neither makes nor rules out.","anna-paulina-luna-s-mk-ultra-hearing-2026":"In 2026 the member of Congress Anna Paulina Luna held what the skeptic Michael Shermer calls \"a big congressional hearing\" on MK Ultra, a Cold War CIA programme of mind-control experiments. Shermer mentions it to Michels while talking about the researcher Avi Loeb, saying Loeb is friends with Luna, that she \"seems to be an insider\", and that the hearing had happened \"just yesterday\". He brings it up as a sign that people close to the UFO debate have friends in Congress.","anne-finds-the-letters-link-the-visitors-with-the-dead-1987":"Reading through letters from people describing their encounters, Anne Strieber concluded that the visitors, the beings people say come to them, are tied up with death. The writer Whitley Strieber tells Michels that one day in the late 1980s she came out of her office and said, \"Whitley, this has something to do with what we call death.\" If she was right, the visitors may be something to do with the dead rather than with other planets.","annie-jacobsen-s-source-o-donnell-roswell-was-a-stalin-menge-1947":"The craft that came down near Roswell, New Mexico in 1947, where the military first announced it had recovered a \"flying disc\" and then took it back, was a Soviet hoax tied to Stalin and the Nazi doctor Josef Mengele, according to the journalist Annie Jacobsen's source, a man named Alfred O'Donnell. Michels brings this up himself, noting that O'Donnell was Jacobsen's source for her book Area 51 and that she spoke about it on Lex Fridman's podcast. In another episode Michels describes Jacobsen's version differently, as bodies of children with an aging disease used as crash-test dummies, without naming O'Donnell or Stalin.","annie-jacobson-hunt-for-the-horten-brothers-1945":"In 1945 the Americans are said to have badly wanted to find the Horten brothers, German aircraft designers, in Germany at the end of the war. Michels tells this as something the journalist Annie Jacobsen writes in her book Area 51, and raises it while talking with the writer Peter Levenda. It would suggest the US was keen to get hold of German work on unusual aircraft.","anomalous-craft-show-interest-in-white-sands-and-los-alamos":"A former Pentagon official turned whistleblower named Matthew Brown says unexplained craft have shown a \"general interest\" in White Sands, the New Mexico missile test range, and nearby Los Alamos National Laboratory, where the atomic bomb was built. He says it on the show and hints he has \"more to say\"; if true, it would mean the craft are watching America's weapons sites.","anomalous-metal-containing-51-elements-including-human-dna-a":"A piece of strange metal is said to contain 51 elements, among them plutonium and, he was told, human DNA. Chris Bledsoe, a man featured on the show as someone who seems to attract UFOs, tells Michels he heard this from someone else. That person told him that even the most classified human methods of the time could blend only about 15 elements, so if the account is true the metal was not made by people.","anomalous-objects-seen-by-a-navy-acoustic-intelligence-ship":"A Navy ship that gathered intelligence by listening underwater, operating off Japan, observed unexplained objects on two occasions. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels he heard this from an acoustic specialist who served aboard and retired as a captain; Gallaudet does not name him. If true, it would add the Navy's own listening ships to the record of strange objects at sea.","anonymous-navy-friend-says-the-abyss-scene-is-close-to-the-t":"A scene in James Cameron's film The Abyss, in which a glowing object appears in the deep ocean, is said to be close to what the Navy has actually encountered. Michels tells this himself, relaying what an unnamed friend in the Navy told him: that maybe the scene \"isn't too far off from the truth.\" If true, it would mean unexplained lit objects have been met deep under the sea.","anonymous-navy-scientist-on-extended-electrodynamics":"A top Navy scientist, staying anonymous for fear of punishment from his superiors, supposedly described a theory called extended electrodynamics, a broader version of the 19th-century equations of electricity and magnetism worked out by the physicists James Clerk Maxwell and Oliver Heaviside. Michels tells this himself, saying he learned about the theory from this scientist and interviewed him in his own video about Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor who experimented with electrical propulsion, a video he also calls The Man Who Built UFOs for the CIA. Across three episodes he brings it up again, once calling the source a Navy whistleblower. If the theory holds, it could point to new physics behind how such craft might move.","anonymous-sources-bodies-came-from-a-subterranean-tunnel-sys-2015":"The small, three-fingered bodies that turned up in the Nazca region of Peru came out of a large underground tunnel system, according to several anonymous sources who Michels says told him so. He does not name them or describe the tunnels further. If true, it would mean the bodies were not made or dug up at random but came from one hidden place.","anonymous-usaf-colonel-and-fighter-pilot-s-ufo-encounter-1980":"An unnamed US Air Force colonel and fighter pilot is said to have had a dramatic UFO encounter around 1980. A guest named James Fox tells Michels about the man second-hand, saying \"he had a really dramatic UFO encounter in 1980 or something like that,\" without giving his name, where it happened or what he saw. If true, it would add a senior military pilot to the list of trained witnesses.","anonymous-whistleblower-on-crash-retrieval-team-in-northern-2009":"A man who has not been named says that around 2009 he served on a crash retrieval team, a group sent to recover crashed craft, working far out in the northern Nevada desert on land once called the Nevada Test Range. Steven Greer, who argues on the show that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, tells Michels what this whistleblower told him. If it is true, such recoveries would still have been going on in recent years.","another-aerospace-company-leaves-the-work-in-the-early-1970s-1960":"A second aerospace company that had been working on reverse engineering, meaning taking apart recovered craft of non-human origin to learn how they work, gave up that work in the early 1970s after being involved from around 1960. The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels this is his recollection of what a senior vice president of that company told him, that they 'kind of left that whole line of work'. He does not name the company or the executive.","another-channeler-also-predicted-the-challenger-disaster-1986":"Two different channelers, people who say they pass on messages from spirits or other beings, predicted the 1986 Challenger space shuttle disaster, according to the psychologist and paranormal researcher Jeffrey Mishlove. He tells Michels that after the disaster, people at the Foundation for Mind Being Research told him they knew of \"another channeler somewhere who had made the same prediction\", so this second case is something he heard, not something he saw. If true, two separate predictions of the same event would be hard to put down to chance.","antarctica-as-the-nordics-main-subterranean-base":"The \"Nordics\", the tall, human-looking beings of UFO lore, keep their main underground and undersea base in Antarctica. That is the claim of a researcher named Jason Jorjani, who tells Michels so without naming a source. He links it to the Nordics he discussed with Michels in an earlier conversation.","anthony-hopkins-finds-feifer-s-annotated-copy-of-the-girl-fr-1972":"The actor Anthony Hopkins, the story goes, was cast in a film of the novel The Girl from Petrovka, couldn't find a copy, and then found one left on a bench at Leicester Square tube station in London, which turned out to be the author George Feifer's own copy with his notes in it. Michael Shermer, a well-known skeptic, tells this to Michels without naming a source: \"Heading for home, he chanced upon a book on a bench at the Lester Square tube station.\" Shermer offers it as an example of a coincidence so striking it feels meaningful.","anti-drone-technology-fails-at-pseg-salem-hope-creek-2024":"In 2024, several drones were reportedly seen over the Salem and Hope Creek nuclear power station in Salem County, New Jersey. Staff are said to have turned anti-drone technology on them, and it failed completely, though it normally works on all drones. The researcher Robert Hastings, who studies UFOs at nuclear sites, tells Michels he heard this from someone with access to the station's internal messages, and he tells it because it would mean the objects were not ordinary drones.","anti-gravity-researcher-amy-eskridge-and-the-sixth-force-2022":"Amy Eskridge, a researcher who worked on anti-gravity, meaning ways to cancel or push against gravity, died in 2022, and Michels says that near the end of her life she kept talking about a \"sixth force\", which he does not explain further. He calls her death \"very mysterious\". In a later episode he adds, crediting her father Richard Eskridge, that people were attacking her with directed energy, meaning beamed weapons, and that she had burns on her hands. A news clip played on the show has a reporter describe her as an Alabama-based scientist who \"was openly studying anti-gravity technology when she died in 2022\", with no mention of the sixth force or the burns. Told this way, it hints that her work came close to something someone wanted kept quiet.","antikythera-mechanism":"The Antikythera mechanism is, as Jesse Michels describes it, a small ancient device that worked almost like a computer, built around astronomical alignments and found in a shipwreck in the Aegean Sea. Michels brings it up himself in several episodes without naming a source, as an example of ancient technology more advanced than you would expect. Once he places the find on a Greek island instead of a shipwreck and points to a book called Forbidden Archaeology.","anton-bilton-s-proposed-dmt-telepathy-experiment-in-faraday":"A man named Anton Bilton had the idea of giving people DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, and putting them in side-by-side Faraday cages, metal enclosures that block radio and other electrical signals, to see whether they could share thoughts. Jesse Michels mentions the plan in passing, in a conversation with a neuroscientist named Andrew Gallimore. The point of the cages would be to show that any shared experience could not travel by ordinary signals.","antonio-villas-boas-has-intercourse-with-a-humanlike-woman-1957":"A Brazilian man named Antonio Villas Boas said that in 1957 he was taken aboard a craft and had sex with a woman who looked almost human. A researcher named Mike Masters tells it on the show, saying Villas Boas described her as a woman who looked \"a little bit different\" from the women he knew, with \"slightly higher cheekbones\" and a \"pointier chin.\" Michels mentions the case himself on a later episode in Brazil, placing it in the 1960s and leaving out the sex, as one of many cases where people seem to \"interact with the beings and go up on the ships.\" For Masters, such a story would suggest the visitors are close kin to humans.","anunnaki-came-to-earth-to-mine-gold":"The Anunnaki, gods from the myths of ancient Mesopotamia, came to Earth to mine gold and other precious metals. A pyramid researcher named Geoffrey Drumm tells Michels \"the story of the Anunnaki\" this way, without naming a source, on an episode where he says an alien showed him the true purpose of the pyramids. Read like that, the old myth would be a memory of visitors from elsewhere coming here to dig for metal.","apollo-11-crew-observes-a-glowing-aristarchus-1969":"During the Apollo 11 mission in 1969, astronomers in Germany saw the Moon's Aristarchus crater looking unusually bright, and the crew of Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins were asked to look and saw it glowing too. The researcher Maaneli Derakhshani tells this to Michels as something he has learned, introducing it with 'apparently', and offers it as part of a case that something unexplained was seen on the Moon.","apollo-11-halfway-to-the-moon-window-footage-faked-in-earth-1969":"Footage from the 1969 Apollo 11 flight, in which the crew is heard saying they are far out, about halfway to the moon, was really shot while they were still in orbit around Earth, according to a man named Bart Sibrel. Joe Rogan relays this to Michels, saying Sibrel has the uncovered footage, and also names David Percy and Neil Armstrong. If so, the view from the window was staged to make the craft look farther away than it was.","apollo-11-post-flight-press-conference-looks-deceptive-1969":"The Apollo 11 astronauts looked like they were hiding something at their press conference after returning from the Moon in 1969, says the comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan. He tells Michels to watch it, calling it \"like a hostage video\", and he leans on what an unnamed body language expert said about it. If true, the first men on the Moon would have been holding back part of what happened there.","apollo-11-timing-trinity-anniversary-and-aquarius-1969":"Apollo 11, the first crewed Moon landing mission, was launched on July 16, 1969, which Michels points out was exactly 24 years after the Trinity test, the first atomic bomb explosion and, in his words, \"the dawn of the nuclear age.\" Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as part of an episode on NASA, ritual and secret science, where he suggests the date may have been chosen on purpose rather than by chance.","apollo-11-trio-of-lights-frame-1969":"A photo frame from the Apollo 11 Moon landing in 1969 is said to show a trio of lights, the same pattern seen in pictures from the later Apollo 17 mission. The researcher Maaneli Derakhshani points it out to Michels without naming a source: \"you see a trio of lights like that not just in Apollo 17, you also see it in Apollo 11.\" If true, the repeated pattern would suggest something real was near the astronauts on more than one mission.","apollo-11-watched-by-craft-on-the-moon-and-followed-in-trans-1969":"The Apollo 11 crew are said to have been followed on their way to the Moon in 1969 and watched by craft once they landed, with Neil Armstrong reporting it on a private radio channel. Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, repeats this to Michels from what he says unnamed NASA whistleblowers reported: \"he was on a private channel after landing on the moon.\" If true, it would mean the first Moon landing had company that NASA kept off the public broadcast.","apollo-13-zapped-because-of-a-nuclear-device-1969":"Apollo 13, the 1970 Moon mission that nearly ended in disaster, may not have failed because of a mechanical problem but because aliens zapped it, since it may have carried a nuclear warhead, says a scientist named Steve Colbern. He tells Michels he found this out, linking it to the Atomic Energy Commission, and if true it would mean aliens act to stop nuclear weapons going into space.","apollo-14-blue-orb-above-mitchell-blacked-out-in-lpi-scan-1971":"A photo from the Apollo 14 Moon landing in 1971 shows a blue orb in the lunar sky above the astronaut Edgar Mitchell, and that orb is blacked out in the copy held by the Lunar and Planetary Institute, a NASA-linked archive of space images, according to a researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani. He tells Michels the orb is \"very clearly\" visible above Mitchell, setting the Institute's scan beside another collection of Apollo photos called March to the Moon, and mentions the journalist Ross Coulthart. If true, it would mean something unexplained was photographed on the Moon and later hidden in an official copy.","apollo-17-insectoid-metallic-probe-in-lunar-sky-unreleased-1972":"A photo from Apollo 17, the 1972 Moon landing of astronauts Eugene Cernan and Harrison Schmitt, is said to show a bluish metallic object in the lunar sky that looks like an insect-shaped probe once the image is brightened. The researcher Maaneli Derakhshani describes this to Michels from his own look at NASA's public Apollo image archives. If he is right, something unexplained was flying near the astronauts on the Moon.","apollo-17-trio-of-blue-lights-1972":"A raw scan of a photograph taken at the Apollo 17 landing site in 1972 shows three solid-looking blue lights, almost evenly spaced, in a small patch of the lunar sky. The researcher Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels he found them by studying the unprocessed scan of that frame, and presents them as a possible sign of something in the sky above the astronauts.","apollo-8-launched-on-the-winter-solstice-1968":"Apollo 8, the NASA mission that flew to lunar orbit, lifted off on December 21st, 1968, the winter solstice, which Michels calls \"the day the sun is symbolically reborn after the longest night of the year.\" He offers this himself, without naming a source, in an episode on what he sees as pagan ritual inside NASA. If the date was chosen on purpose, it would suggest the space programme was timed to old sun symbolism.","apollo-astronauts-given-suicide-pills-1969":"The Apollo astronauts were given pills that would kill them if something went wrong, according to a woman named Fay Ann Potter, who said NASA authorities told her so. The UFO documentary maker James Fox repeats her account to Michels: \"they were all given some kind of pill that they would die if anything happened.\" If true, NASA planned for the astronauts to die quickly if a mission went badly wrong.","apollo-crew-followed-by-something-to-the-moon-1969":"Something is said to have followed the Apollo 11 astronauts on their way to the Moon in 1969. The UFO investigator James Fox heard it from Fay Ann Potter, the sister of the astronaut Buzz Aldrin, and says Aldrin had told her \"they were followed by something to the Moon.\" On a later episode Fox tells it a little differently, saying Potter told him Aldrin had \"some UFO encounter on the way to the moon,\" which makes it Aldrin's own experience. Michels tells it in his own words without a family source, saying Aldrin openly admitted \"in multiple interviews\" that something was following them. If true, it would mean the first crew to land on the Moon met something they could not identify along the way.","apollo-intersecting-shadows-suggest-multiple-light-sources-1969":"Some people look at the photographs from the 1969 Moon landing and point to shadows that cross one another, which would mean the scenes were lit by more than one light, not by the sun alone, as on a set. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan brings this up as a guest of Michels, saying that \"people like to try to dismiss\" these intersecting shadows, though he gives no more detail than that.","apollo-lunar-dust-in-charles-s-lab-and-lunar-retroreflectors-1969":"About 200 grams of moon dust brought back by the Apollo missions of 1969 to 1972 sits in the lab of Charles Buhler, a NASA scientist. He tells Michels so himself, \"I have a 200 gram or so in my lab,\" and talks too about the retroreflectors, mirrors the Apollo crews left on the Moon so lasers fired from Earth can bounce back. For him, these are hard physical proof that the Moon landings happened.","apollo-lunar-photo-lights-and-buzz-aldrin-s-cabin-flashes-pu-1969":"Small blue lights seen in Apollo photos taken on the Moon, and the flashes the Apollo 11 astronaut Buzz Aldrin said he saw inside the cabin in 1969, are put down by some to cosmic rays, fast particles from space that can mark film or trigger flashes in the eye. The skeptic Michael Shermer says on the show that the researcher Avi Loeb holds this view and that Loeb is \"probably right\" about both the \"little blue lights\" and Aldrin's report. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani also credits Loeb, but ties the explanation only to \"the trio of blue lights in Apollo 17.\" Michels, for his part, credits the explanation to the astrophysicist Neil deGrasse Tyson, who he says offered a debunking of the Apollo 17 sightings as \"case closed cosmic rays.\" The question matters because if cosmic rays do not explain the lights, the astronauts saw something unexplained on the Moon.","apollo-telemetry-data-and-original-footage-destroyed-1969":"The original footage and telemetry data from the Apollo moon missions, the stream of measurements radioed back from the spacecraft, are said to have been destroyed. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan says it to Michels on the show without naming a source: \"all the telemetry data was destroyed, which is crazy.\" To Rogan it is a strange loss of the record of the moon landings.","aquarius-document-analyses-benowitz-films-and-mentions-mj-12-1981":"In 1981, around Kirtland Air Force Base in Albuquerque, New Mexico, a UFO researcher named Bill Moore gave a man named Paul Bennewitz, who had been filming strange lights, a paper known as the Aquarius document, which analysed his films and was marked for \"access only to MJ12.\" MJ-12 was said to be a secret group of top officials in charge of the UFO matter. Greg Bishop, a writer who looked into the case, tells Michels the paper was fake, and says it was the first time anyone had mentioned MJ-12 at all.","aquinas-s-mystical-experience-at-mass":"The medieval theologian St. Thomas Aquinas, while working on his Summa Theologica, his great attempt to explain God through reason, is said to have had a sudden conversion-like experience during mass. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. He raises it as an example of a rational mind being overtaken by something it could not reason its way to.","aquino-and-phoenix-program-psychological-warfare-in-vietnam":"A man named Michael Aquino is said to have been a psychological warfare officer in Vietnam who took part in the Phoenix program, a wartime US operation there. The writer Peter Levenda, who researches the CIA's hidden history, tells Michels this without naming a source, confirming that Aquino \"was involved in the Phoenix program,\" in a conversation that also draws on Levenda's book The Lovecraft Code. Levenda raises it to tie Aquino's later life to military mind-war work.","archaeological-retrieval-of-craft-from-the-ocean-floor":"Some craft have been dug up from the ocean floor from under tens of meters of sediment, in what amounts to archaeology, according to a Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown. He tells Michels that a layer like that takes a long time to build up. The point is that whatever lay beneath it would have been there for a very long time.","archaeologist-who-excavated-amenemhat-iii-s-pyramid-called-i-1803":"The pyramid of the pharaoh Amenemhat III was never a real tomb, says the archaeologist who dug it up in the 1800s, but a symbolic tomb for the pharaoh's ka, the life force or spirit double of Egyptian belief. Luke Caverns, a researcher of the pyramids, passes this on to Michels, though he recalls only that the archaeologist's name starts with a P. He tells it to back his case that the pyramids were not simply burial places.","archive-research-nixon-could-slip-his-security-1969":"Richard Nixon, during his presidency from 1969 to 1974, often got away from his security detail, including around his home in Key Biscayne, Florida. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels that people had dismissed stories about Nixon by saying he could never escape his guards, so Cameron checked Nixon's records at the National Archives and found that he had slipped them many times. It matters because it would make stories of Nixon going off privately to see something possible.","archuleta-reports-a-crashed-plane-to-the-air-force-1948":"In 1948 a New Mexico rancher named Valentine Archeletta found a crashed craft on his land and called the Air Force, telling them, \"Hey, one of your planes crashed over here. You need to come get it.\" The researcher Scott Ramsey tells Michels this as Archeletta's own account, in a story tied to Kirtland Air Force Base. If true, it would mean the military learned of the crash from a rancher who took it for one of its own aircraft.","area-51-barely-discussed-before-lazar-1989":"Area 51, a secret US test site in the Nevada desert, was hardly ever talked about in public before a man named Bob Lazar came forward in 1989. That is how Michels puts it himself, without naming a source, while discussing how the Pentagon handles UFO matters. To him it marks Lazar as the person who first made the base a public name.","area-51-caller-extradimensional-beings-contacted-by-early-sp-1997":"The beings we call aliens are really \"extradimensional beings\" that an early forerunner of the space program made contact with, according to a man who called the late-night radio show Coast to Coast AM in 1997, hosted by Art Bell, saying he had worked at Area 51, the secret test site in Nevada. The call comes up on Michels's show in a talk with the hip hop artist Flying Lotus, and the caller, who sounds nervous and stumbling, presents it as something he knew from inside.","area-51-confirmed-under-the-obama-administration-2009":"Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, was not officially admitted to exist until the Obama years, according to the comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan. Rogan tells Michels it \"wasn't even confirmed until the Obama administration\" because that administration \"wanted to expand the barriers for entry,\" meaning the restricted zone around it. He brings it up to show how long the government kept even the base's existence hidden.","area-51-crash-either-secret-us-testing-or-intelligence-watch-1940":"A crash at Area 51, the secret test site in Nevada, sometime in the 1940s or 1950s, is explained in one of two ways: it was a secret US test, or it was some intelligence watching the nuclear tests nearby. A guest named Ralph Larson raises this with Michels, building on what Michels himself has said before. Larson admits he has not done the research or looked into it himself.","area-51-equipment-acquired-from-other-governments":"Some of the equipment at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert, was obtained from other governments, according to a former US Secretary of Energy named John S. Herrington. He says this on Michels's show and offers it as part of why the site holds so much secrecy. In other words, at least some of the hardware kept there came from abroad. He does not say which governments or what the equipment is.","area-51-programs-moved-to-dugway-proving-ground-2000":"Between 35 and 40 percent of the secret programmes at Area 51 were moved to Dugway Proving Ground, an Army test range in Utah. A researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb tells Michels that Steven Greer, a UFO activist, always quotes that figure, and that a witness known only as AH says the exact same thing in testimony recorded in 2000, which UAPGerb found in a collection of recorded witness interviews he calls the DPI archive. If true, Utah would be a new centre of the work once done at Area 51.","area-51-sensor-program-for-detecting-ufos-as-told-in-the-age":"There was a sensor programme at Area 51, the secret military test site in Nevada, that was used to look for UFOs. Michels says this was said publicly in The Age of Disclosure, a documentary about government UFO secrecy, though in this telling he does not say who said it. If true, it would mean the government was watching for these objects with its own instruments.","area-51-was-only-groom-lake-dreamland-was-areas-53-and-54-1965":"A former Air Force weather observer named Charles Hall, who says he spent three years in the 1960s working alongside tall white aliens on the Nevada test ranges, says Area 51, the secret Air Force base in the Nevada desert, \"was merely Groom Lake\", the dry lakebed where it sits. He tells Michels that the real \"Dreamland\", the nickname often given to the secret area, \"was area 53 and 54\", covering places such as Indian Springs Valley, and he says he knows it from being there himself.","area-51-workers-sickened-by-burning-hazardous-waste-governme":"Workers at Area 51, the secret military test site in the Nevada desert, were made ill by hazardous waste burned there, and when they went to court the government denied the buildings even existed, according to the physicist John Brandenburg. He tells Michels that one former worker took photos from the highway and showed a federal judge, saying \"See all these buildings in the distance in the desert? I used to work there\", and the government's lawyers said they saw no buildings in the pictures. If true, the government would rather deny what anyone can see than admit the base was there.","area-6-lockheed-martin-aerial-operations-facility":"A part of the Nevada test lands called Area 6, near Yucca Flat, holds an aerial operations facility owned by the defence company Lockheed Martin, according to an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb. He says so on an episode about how the Pentagon's UFO programme works, without naming his source, pointing to it as one of the places where secret flight work could be done.","ariel-school-girl-to-john-mack-in-space-there-s-no-love-1994":"In the Ariel School case of 1994, in which children at a school in Ruwa, Zimbabwe said strange beings came to them, one girl is said to have told the psychiatrist John Mack, \"I think in space there's no love, but down here there is.\" Michels tells this himself, more than once, from Mack's recorded interviews with the children: in one telling Mack was asking how the beings spoke to the children by telepathy, in another he asked her why the visitors had come, and in another Michels adds that love is why the being wanted to come down at all. Michels repeats it because, if true, it hints that the visitors came looking for something humans have.","ariel-school-landing-near-a-uranium-mine-1994":"In 1994, a crowd of schoolchildren at the Ariel School in Zimbabwe said they saw a silver craft come down from the sky and land in a field near their school. Michels tells it several times on his own, usually as 62 children, sometimes about 60, 61 or \"over 60\". He stresses that a uranium mine lay right nearby, which would tie the case to the pattern of UFOs showing up near nuclear sites. In one telling he adds that a being came out of the craft and spoke to the children telepathically, mind to mind. David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, tells it secondhand as about 100 children who saw \"bipedal, hominid looking\" NHI, short for non-human intelligence. James Fox, a documentary filmmaker, says it was a case he investigated himself, an \"alleged\" landing at Ruwa with roughly 66 children. Elsewhere he stresses the message the children reported, that \"you don't understand how special you are\", and links it to a Brazilian case at Varginha. A retired Army colonel named Karl Nell says craft landed and beings \"presented themselves\" to the children in the middle of the day. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff says the children's pictures were \"identical\", while the author Peter Levenda and the YouTuber Chris Ramsay say the drawings and stories were similar but did not always match.","ariel-school-witnesses-reunite-in-los-angeles-2013":"Witnesses to the Ariel School case, a reported landing of a craft near a school in Ruwa, Zimbabwe, agreed to meet again for the first time, 20 years later, in Los Angeles. A UFO filmmaker and investigator named James Fox tells Michels about it as someone who was involved in bringing them together. A reunion like this matters because the witnesses could compare their memories of the event as adults.","aristotle-s-passage-on-the-ancestors-belief-in-divinity":"Aristotle wrote a passage saying that the earliest humans were right to believe in a divine presence in nature. A guest named James Madden paraphrases it for Michels in a conversation about Plato and consciousness: Aristotle \"says, 'This shows that our ancestors, our most ancient ancestors were not wrong when they posited that there was a divinity surrounding nature.'\" Madden does not say which of Aristotle's works the passage comes from. He uses it to suggest that old beliefs about a divine order may hold real insight.","aristotle-the-planets-are-intelligences":"For Aristotle, the planets were literally intelligences, thinking beings, not objects or vehicles. James Madden, a guest who talks with Michels about Plato's cave, UFOs and consciousness, says this without naming a particular text: \"they're not flying ships or something like that.\" He uses it to suggest that the ancients understood things in the sky as minds, which would change how we might think about what UFOs are.","ark-moved-from-axum-to-lake-ziway-during-the-italian-occupat-1935":"Ethiopians say that during the Italian occupation of their country, from 1935 to 1941, they took the Ark of the Covenant out of the ancient city of Axum and hid it for safekeeping on an island in Lake Ziway. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels this as what the Ethiopians themselves say. If it is true, the Ark from the Bible survived and was moved to keep it from the invaders.","ark-of-the-covenant-as-a-device-that-strikes-people-dead-wit":"The claim is that the Ark of the Covenant, the sacred chest the Israelites carried through the desert, was a kind of device that could kill people with fire. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels that, \"according to the Book of Exodus\", the Ark \"strikes people dead with bolts of fire that come out of it\". Told this way, it hints that an ancient holy object worked like a machine. The King James Bible, published in 1611, does describe a deadly holiness around the Ark, but in other books than the one Hancock names. In Leviticus, when Aaron's sons offered \"strange fire\", \"there went out fire from the LORD, and devoured them, and they died before the LORD.\" Aaron is warned to keep away from \"the mercy seat, which is upon the ark; that he die not\". The books of Samuel tell of deaths at the Ark itself: a man named Uzzah \"died by the ark of God\" when God \"smote him\", and the men of Bethshemesh were struck \"because they had looked into the ark of the LORD\". So a deadly Ark is in the text, and so is fire from the LORD. Putting the two together as fire coming out of the Ark itself is Hancock's step, which the text neither states nor rules out.","ark-of-the-covenant-claimed-kept-at-aksum-1983":"The Ark of the Covenant, the sacred chest of the Bible, is said to be kept in a chapel beside the Church of St Mary of Zion in Aksum, in northern Ethiopia. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels that while researching there he was told, \"we have the Ark of the Covenant here,\" and he credits his account to the book The Sign and the Seal. If the claim were true, one of the Bible's most famous lost objects would have survived.","arkani-hamed-s-positive-geometries-outside-spacetime-2026":"Physicists led by Nima Arkani-Hamed at the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton are said to be finding mathematical shapes, called positive geometries, that sit outside space and time and give rise to the laws of particle physics. The cognitive scientist Donald Hoffman describes this work to Michels, calling Arkani-Hamed \"one of the first pioneers\" of finding \"structures that they call positive geometries outside of spacetime,\" and he also mentions the physicist David Gross. To Hoffman it means space and time are not the deepest layer of reality.","armageddon-as-a-kinetic-war-against-the-returning-son-of-god":"Armageddon, says a researcher named Tim Alberino, will be a real, physical war fought with weapons, which he calls a \"kinetic war\", waged by the powers of the earth against the Son of God when he returns. He tells Michels this as his own reading of the Bible, and he points to the Book of Revelation, the last book of the New Testament, written by a man named John on the island of Patmos and quoted here from the King James Version of 1611. In Revelation, unclean spirits \"go forth unto the kings of the earth and of the whole world, to gather them to the battle of that great day of God Almighty\", and they are gathered \"into a place called in the Hebrew tongue Armageddon.\" Later John writes that he saw heaven opened and a rider on a white horse who \"in righteousness he doth judge and make war\", and \"the kings of the earth, and their armies, gathered together to make war against him.\" The rest are slain \"with the sword of him that sat upon the horse, which sword proceeded out of his mouth.\" The text does tell of armies gathered for a battle against the rider. Whether that battle is fought with real weapons, Alberino's kinetic war, is his reading of a sword that comes out of a mouth, and the text neither states it nor rules it out. If he is right, the end of days would be an actual armed conflict.","armageddon-dream-with-the-number-747-and-number-cubes-1976":"In 1976 at Fort Knox, Kentucky, a former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says he had an \"incredibly vivid dream\" of fighting in something like the battle of Armageddon, a dream that featured the number 747 and cubes covered in numbers. He tells Michels about it as his own experience and also writes about it in his book A State of Mind: Faith and the CIA. He presents it as one of the strange experiences that he says shaped him.","armed-trespasser-on-grillmair-s-property-in-december-2025":"On December 20, 2025, a man named Carl Grillmair called the police to report someone trespassing on his large lot in Llano, California, according to sheriff's officials quoted in court records. Michels tells this himself in an episode about American scientists who have gone missing, and he credits the court records and the Los Angeles Times as his sources.","army-adapting-nhi-derived-electro-optic-cloaking-to-combat-v-2000":"In the early 2000s the Army was interested in fitting next generation combat vehicles with electro-optic cloaking, a way of making things hard to see, said to come from technology of non-human intelligence. A researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb tells Michels this as something he was told, and offers it as an example of how such technology might find its way into ordinary military equipment.","army-interplanetary-phenomenon-unit-confirmed-to-steinman-1947":"The US Army is said to have run a small team called the Interplanetary Phenomenon Unit from 1947 to 1950 to look into UFOs. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels the unit's existence \"was first confirmed in 1986 to UFO researcher William Steinman,\" and points to papers he calls the unit's report and a field order. If true, it would mean the Army had its own UFO investigators from the very start of the modern UFO era.","army-money-continues-after-the-round-table-closes-1957":"A group in Maine known as the Round Table shut down in 1957, but the man behind it kept getting money from the Army the whole time, according to the researcher Greg Malatzi. He tells Michels \"there's records of that\", pointing to Army funding records without quoting them. If true, it would mean the Army was paying for this work, and kept paying after it officially ended.","army-officers-visiting-the-utah-ranch-followed-home-by-pheno-1990":"People who spent time at a ranch in Utah studying strange events there say the strangeness followed them home. The veteran UFO researcher Jacques Vallée tells Michels he has heard of 'trained army officers who went to the ranch a few days, went home and boom' found 'some phenomenon following them.' Michels returns to it on several episodes and calls the place Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of odd lights, creatures and other events. He says researchers there report that 'stuff follows me around in my home and I have to rid myself of it.' He names the people who studied the ranch for the businessman Robert Bigelow, and a helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, who says he retrieved a UFO, as people things seemed to follow. In one episode Michels credits the claim to the book Skinwalkers at the Pentagon by Colm Kelleher, George Knapp and James Lacatski, which he says reports a 'hitchhiker effect' among many scientists who worked at the ranch. Vallée tells it as reports passed to him, and Michels as something he has read and heard. Vallée speaks of army officers, while Michels speaks of scientists and researchers. If true, it would mean whatever is at the ranch is not tied to the place but can attach itself to people.","army-public-affairs-source-terrified-of-the-nsa-2013":"A man who worked in the US Army's Office of Public Affairs in the mid-2010s is said to have told a guest that he was not afraid of the CIA but was terrified of the NSA. The guest, who goes by UAPGerb online, tells Michels this second-hand from his unnamed source: \"I'm not really afraid of the CIA but what I'm terrified of is the NSA.\" He brings it up to suggest which agency insiders fear most when it comes to secrets.","army-r-d-golden-age-and-institutions-to-study-et-technology-1959":"The 1960s were a golden age of US Army research and development, says an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the newer official word for UFOs), and this was when Philip J. Corso, working under Arthur Trudeau, said institutions were set up to study technology from beings not from Earth. UAPGerb tells Michels this from his own reading of the Army's research and development primers of the time. If true, the Army was quietly organized to learn from alien technology.","army-research-laboratory-finds-lifter-thrust-exceeds-ion-flo-2000":"In the 2000s the US Army Research Laboratory is said to have tested a \"lifter\", a light device that rises when high voltage is applied to it, and found its thrust was \"at least 10, 20% above what could be achieved by ion flow\", the stream of charged air usually thought to push it. The hacker Gary McKinnon says this to Michels, mentioning the lab and a group called Blaze Labs but naming no document. If true, something beyond the known explanation would be lifting the device.","army-trucks-cordoning-off-the-hospital-1996":"In 1996 in Varginha, Brazil, army trucks with green tarps were lined up at the entrance of the city's Regional Hospital, at a time when other witnesses say a creature from a crashed craft had been caught and brought to local hospitals. Italo Venturelli, a surgeon, tells Michels he saw this himself: \"I did see the army trucks... They were all at the hospital entrance.\" If true, the military was guarding the hospital where the being was being held.","arranging-and-conducting-the-bobby-ray-inman-interview-2020":"When an interviewer named Jay Anderson sat down with Bobby Ray Inman, a former senior US intelligence official, Inman was relaxed until the talk turned to UFOs, and then, Anderson says, \"it starts getting tight.\" Anderson tells Michels this as the one who arranged and conducted the interview, in the 2020s. The point of telling it is that even a simple question about UFOs seemed to make a man of Inman's rank guarded, as if the subject touched something he did not want to discuss.","arrest-by-national-high-tech-crime-unit-2002":"The British hacker Gary McKinnon, who says he broke into NASA and US military computers and found signs of a secret space programme, says that in 2002 an officer came to him in London and said: \"I'm Jeff Donsson from the National High-Tech Crime Unit. You're under arrest for computer misuse.\" The National High-Tech Crime Unit was a British police squad for computer crime, and McKinnon tells Michels the arrest as something he lived through.","arrest-of-supervisor-and-100-million-abuse-lawsuit-at-the-fa":"At Baker Victory Services, a care facility in Western New York, a supervisor was arrested on something like 210 felonies for falsifying records, and a $100 million lawsuit over years of sexual abuse claims was settled. Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a UFO programme as a child, tells Michels this from a news article he remembers, and elsewhere in the interview adds that so many people sued over abuse that the facility had to close. He brings it up as part of the background of the place he ties to his own story.","arriving-gods-will-be-received-as-saviours-leading-to-armage":"A group of beings will one day arrive and be welcomed as \"the gods returning to the earth\", greeted as saviours, and that welcome will lead to Armageddon, according to the filmmaker Tim Alberino. He tells Michels this as his own reading, drawing on the King James Version of the Bible, published in 1611, and on a work called Birthright. If he is right, humanity's greatest disaster would arrive looking like a rescue.","arson-at-puharich-s-house-1978":"In 1978 someone deliberately set fire to the house of a scientist named Andrija Puharich in Ossining, New York, according to a researcher named Greg Malatzi. Malatzi tells Michels the fire department showed it was arson but that nobody knows to this day who did it, and he names no source. He suggests that someone may have wanted to stop Puharich's work.","art-bell-s-material-said-to-come-from-roswell-1947":"A rumour says that Art Bell, the host of a late-night radio show called Coast to Coast, came to own bits of material that Michels thinks came from Roswell, New Mexico, where many people say a craft crashed and was recovered by the military in 1947. Michels raises it himself as something he has heard, once while talking about the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan, and in another episode he puts it differently: he says Bell had \"little bits and pieces of UFO stuff\" and wonders whether Bell was even shown a UFO, without tying the pieces to Roswell. If true, it would mean crash debris had ended up in a private person's hands.","art-bell-s-satellite-transmission-failure-during-the-area-51-1997":"During a call about Area 51, the secret Air Force base in Nevada, the whole satellite system carrying Art Bell's late-night radio show Coast to Coast AM went down and the station was told it was off the air. Bell, the show's host, says so himself in a recording of the broadcast that comes up on the show with the musician Flying Lotus. The story is told because the cutoff came at the very moment someone was talking about the base's secrets.","arthur-c-clarke-s-sri-lanka-mountain-portal-1970":"The science fiction writer Arthur C. Clarke, Michels says, \"wrote a book about a portal in Sri Lanka on the top of a mountain.\" Michels mentions it in passing in a conversation with the writer Whitley Strieber, without naming the book or saying more about the portal.","arthur-c-clarke-says-nasa-found-large-life-on-mars-with-buzz-2000":"The science fiction writer Arthur C. Clarke said around 2000, in Sri Lanka and with Buzz Aldrin present, that NASA had basically discovered large forms of life on Mars, and that this was what shocked and excited him most. The researcher Maaneli Derakhshani relays Clarke's words to Michels; if true, it would mean NASA knew of life beyond Earth and kept it quiet.","arthur-young-puharich-and-the-seances-with-the-nine-1950":"From the 1950s into the 1970s, a group of people in the United States held séances in which they believed they were in touch with \"the Nine\", a group of beings speaking from beyond ordinary reality, and a man named Arthur Young and a researcher named Andrija Puharich are said to have taken part. Michels mentions this in passing, in a conversation with the parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove, without saying what the Nine told them or where he heard it.","arthur-young-s-quest-to-invent-the-bell-helicopter-1926":"The inventor Arthur Young went to the US Patent Office in Washington looking for problems nobody had solved yet, picked the helicopter, and spent the years from 1926 to 1947 on it, work that led to the Bell helicopter. The psychologist and paranormal researcher Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as something he heard from Young himself. The story shows the kind of deliberate, problem-hunting mind Mishlove says Young had.","arthur-young-s-reflexive-universe-spirit-descends-into-matte-1976":"A thinker named Arthur Young held that the whole universe runs in one cycle: \"spirit descending into matter and then matter ascending back into the realm of spirit.\" Jeffrey Mishlove, a guest with a doctorate who talks with Michels about psychics and the afterlife, sums up the idea from Young's 1976 book The Reflexive Universe. On this view, matter and life are part of a single journey that goes back to where it started.","arv-idea-comes-from-lazar-sorensen-and-mckinnish":"The idea of an \"ARV\", short for alien reproduction vehicle, meaning a craft built by humans by copying an alien one, entered the UFO world's imagination through three men: Bob Lazar, Brad Sorensen and Mark McCandlish, all of whom have spoken publicly about such craft. The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels this without naming a source, saying the idea \"fell into the psyche\" of the UFO community because of them. He offers it as an account of where the notion came from, not as something he saw himself.","arv-spelled-out-as-alien-reproduction-vehicle-in-the-briefin-2018":"Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official, says that in a 2018 Pentagon briefing on Immaculate Constellation, said to be a hidden Pentagon effort to collect UFO data, the term ARV was spelled out as \"alien reproduction vehicle,\" which he reads as a counterintelligence marker. He tells it from his own memory and brings in Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who worked on UFO reports. A related report, whose origin is disputed, was unsigned: Michael Shellenberger delivered it to Congress, and Rep. Nancy Mace entered it into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a document from the Pentagon. It calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. The report writes \"ARV/RV (Reproduction Vehicles),\" spelling out the RV but not the A. So \"alien\" comes from Brown's memory of a classified briefing that is not public, and the report neither gives that word nor rules it out.","as-a-child-he-sensed-the-tube-tv-turning-on-from-another-roo":"As a boy he could tell when the family's tube TV was switched on, even from another room, says Dan Sherman, a former sergeant who says the NSA hired him to talk with aliens. He tells Michels about it from his own childhood: \"I would always know when the TV was on because when my dad would...\" He offers it as an early sign of the unusual sensitivity he says he later had.","as-above-so-below-engraving-possibly-by-augustus-walley":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, someone once carved the old saying \"as above, so below\" into the rock, and nobody knows who or why. Michels tells this himself and suggests it may be the work of Augustus Walley, whom he calls a legendary Buffalo Soldier (the Buffalo Soldiers were Black US Army regiments) decorated with the Medal of Honor. In a later episode he leaves Walley out and asks instead whether it is a coincidence that the Buffalo Soldiers were Freemasons, given the inscription.","ascent-through-the-seven-hekhalot-in-enoch-and-merkavah-text":"Ancient Jewish mystics are said to have climbed in spirit through seven heavenly palaces, called hekhalot, using breathing, visualisation and chant-like prayer. Michels tells this himself, naming the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text left out of most Bibles, and the Merkavah texts, writings of Jewish mystics about rising to God's throne-chariot. For him it suggests the ancients had techniques for reaching other levels of reality. In R. H. Charles's 1917 translation of Enoch, the ascent comes in a dream, \"I saw in my sleep,\" in which \"the winds in the vision caused me to fly and lifted me upward, and bore me into heaven.\" Enoch passes \"a wall which is built of crystals and surrounded by tongues of fire,\" enters \"a large house which was built of crystals,\" then sees \"a second house, greater than the former,\" \"built of flames of fire,\" with \"a lofty throne\" whose wheels shine \"as the shining sun.\" The book tells of two houses, not seven palaces, and has Enoch carried up by the winds of the vision rather than rising by breathing or prayer. Joining the Merkavah texts' seven palaces and their practices to Enoch's ascent is Michels's reading, which this book neither states nor rules out.","asha-calls-spelling-pseudoscience":"The American Speech-Language-Hearing Association, the professional body for speech therapists in the United States, is said to have declared that spelling, a method where nonspeaking people point to letters on a board to communicate, is not valid. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made a documentary about such children, tells Michels the association says \"spelling is not a valid form of communication\" and has \"called it pseudoscience,\" without naming the statement or its date. Her point is that the field's leading body rejects the very method her film depends on.","asher-s-first-words-to-j-tune-into-my-heart-2020":"A child named Asher, one of the nonspeaking children said in the documentary The Telepathy Tapes to read minds, is said to have told someone called J as his very first words, \"tune into my heart.\" Ky Dickens, who made the documentary, tells Michels this from what she was told: \"the first thing Asher said to J was like tune into my heart.\" If true, it would suggest these children communicate by something deeper than speech.","asheville-viewer-s-videos-of-pulsating-lights-2024":"A viewer in Asheville, North Carolina, says that one night in 2024 they filmed pulsating lights in the sky, seven videos and about 30 minutes of good footage. Michels reads out the viewer's message during a debate with a skeptic named Mick West. He brings it up as fresh footage that might be worth examining.","asis-operative-lent-to-mi6-to-bug-kuwaiti-government-1991":"In the early 1990s, British intelligence, MI6, secretly borrowed one of the technical 'buggers' of ASIS, Australia's foreign spy service, and used him to bug the Kuwaiti government's offices, to win business advantage over Australia itself. The investigative journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this as a story from his reporting on intelligence. It is told as an example of how allied spy services can quietly work against each other.","asked-to-blow-the-whistle-because-legacy-program-lacked-over-2023":"Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official, says he came forward as a whistleblower in 2023 or 2024 because people in the know told him Congress needed whistleblowers about a \"legacy program,\" a long-running secret effort said to deal with UFO matters, which was \"not being managed correctly.\" He tells Michels this about himself. If true, it would mean insiders believed a hidden program was running without proper oversight from Congress.","asked-to-take-part-in-congressional-testimony-2025":"A former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA trained him to take telepathic messages from aliens, says that in 2025 he was \"asked just recently\" to take part in testimony before Congress. He tells Michels this himself, and it would mean lawmakers want to hear his story on the record.","asked-to-work-on-electron-flow-for-back-engineering":"People were trying to reverse engineer alien technology, meaning take it apart to learn how it works, long ago, says a man named Chris Bledsoe, whom NASA has studied because UFOs seem to appear around him. He tells Michels he knows this because he was once asked to work on which way electrons move, a question he says was part of that effort.","asking-mary-his-purpose-in-the-middle-east-you-re-here-to-be-2011":"Sometime between 2011 and 2016, while he was in the Middle East, Ralph Larson asked Mary what he was there for. He got no spoken reply, only an answer that arrived as a knowing: \"You're here to be here.\" Larson, whom the episode presents as a former CIA chief, tells Michels this as his own experience, and he mentions a Jesuit priest friend, whom he does not name, in connection with it.","assyrian-branch-settles-the-old-holy-land-and-is-later-destr":"A branch of the ancient Assyrians moved into a region of what is now eastern Turkey, which Matt LaCroix calls an \"old Holy Land\", and was later destroyed. LaCroix, a researcher of ancient history, gives this as his own belief (\"I believe\") on an episode about a great event around 12,000 BC, without citing a source.","asteroid-bennu-study-finds-nucleic-bases":"A study of the asteroid Bennu found nucleic bases, the chemical building blocks of DNA and RNA. A former NSA official named Eric Haseltine mentions the study to Michels in passing, without naming it. Such a find would suggest that the raw materials of life can form in space.","astra-on-the-skunk-works-org-chart-1993":"The writer Nick Cook says that sometime between 1993 and 1996 he saw an organisation chart of the Skunk Works, the secret aircraft design shop of the company Lockheed at Palmdale, California, with a box labelled \"Astra\" sitting above the rest. He tells Michels he saw it himself (\"what I saw above it was something called Astra\") and points to his diaries from the time. In a conversation about antigravity, he offers it as a hint that some hidden programme sat over the work everyone knew about.","astral-experience-on-the-hill-after-zoom-call-with-a-telepat":"After a Zoom call with a guest from The Telepathy Tapes, a podcast about nonverbal autistic people said to communicate by telepathy, Jordan Jose went out of his body to a place he calls \"the hill\", where unnamed beings showed him an image of Ken, a nonverbal autistic boy who had been in the same program as him at a place called Baker Victory. Jose, who says the government took him into a UFO program as a child, tells Michels this happened to him. If true, it would tie the boys of that childhood program together through some kind of mind-to-mind contact.","astronaut-crew-quarters-something-came-back-with-them-1957":"The crew quarters at Cape Canaveral in the late 1950s were never the same, because every time a craft went up and came back down, something came back with the crew and stayed. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him, tells Michels he heard this from a man named Timothy Taylor. Told this way, it suggests early space flights brought some unseen presence back to Earth.","astronaut-says-the-moon-technology-was-destroyed":"An unnamed NASA astronaut is heard saying he would go back to the moon \"in a nanosecond\", but \"we don't have the technology to do that anymore. We used to, but we uh destroyed that technology.\" The clip comes up on Michels's show in a conversation about hidden power and secrets. Taken at face value, it suggests knowledge was deliberately lost.","astronaut-ufo-code-names-per-edgar-mitchell-1960":"NASA astronauts had secret code names for UFOs, according to Edgar Mitchell, the Apollo 14 astronaut who walked on the Moon. Michels says he heard this from a man in Japan named Takano Josen, who was close with Mitchell and passed on what Mitchell told him. So the claim comes to Michels third-hand, and if true it would mean astronauts saw UFOs often enough to need a private way of talking about them.","at-an-lax-pedestrian-death-all-sound-vanishes-except-the-vic-1980":"In the 1980s, while a woman was dying after being struck as a pedestrian at Los Angeles airport, all the noise around went silent except her breathing, says Brent Friedman, a Hollywood writer who was there with her. He tells Michels that time seemed to slow, that in a place as loud as LAX \"I heard nothing except her breathing,\" and that the moment she died, \"all of the noise came back.\"","at-t-breakup-testimony-before-judge-greene-1980":"Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, tells Michels he was the closing witness in the government case that broke up the phone giant AT&T, heard in Washington by Judge Harold Greene between 1980 and 1983. In his words, \"AT&T hired me as the final witness.\" He tells it from his own experience, as part of showing how close he was to the top of government and business.","at-the-table-with-john-mack-in-england-on-the-day-he-died-2004":"A guest named Joe McMoneagle says he sat at a table in England with a man named John Mack and talked with him on the day Mack died, in 2004. McMoneagle tells this as something he was there for, and says only that they \"talked about some stuff\", without saying what.","atf-attempt-to-link-guest-to-the-oklahoma-city-bombing-1995":"In 1995, Steve Colbern says, the ATF tried to tie him to the Oklahoma City bombing as punishment for refusing to cooperate with one of its investigations. Colbern is a scientist who studies objects some people believe are alien implants in human bodies. He tells Michels this from his own experience, mentions Timothy McVeigh, and says an author he does not name was writing a book on Oklahoma City. If he is right, a federal agency tried to pin a terrorist attack on a man for not helping it.","atlantean-survivors-may-live-among-us-with-underwater-or-lun":"Survivors of Atlantis, the lost island civilisation first described by Plato, may still be living among us, with bases under the sea or on the Moon. When Michels puts this to the researcher Randall Carlson, Carlson calls it \"the loaded question\" and answers: \"very provisional. Yes.\" He gives no source, and if it were true, an older advanced civilisation would still be here, hidden.","atlantic-article-on-obama-briefing-edited-wording-2010":"In October 2010 The Atlantic published an article saying, in Michels's quotation, that \"President Obama was briefed this morning on a power failure at F.E. Warren,\" an Air Force base in Wyoming that controls nuclear missiles. Michels reads it out himself in an episode with the researcher Robert Hastings about UFOs near nuclear bases, calling it a \"pretty telling article\" and drawing attention to how it was worded. The article as he quotes it speaks of a power failure and a presidential briefing; whether UFOs had anything to do with that outage, the quoted words neither say nor rule out.","atlantis-as-a-multi-site-ice-age-civilization":"Atlantis, the lost civilization of legend, is said to have been not one island but a spread of Ice Age settlements, in North America, the Azores, the Sahara, the Amazon and on coastlines now under the sea. The writer Graham Hancock offers this as his own view on the show, saying \"I think it was more than one place,\" and he mentions the researchers Randall Carlson and Jimmy Corsetti. If true, a whole advanced culture would have been lost when the ice melted and seas rose.","atlantis-as-an-island-empire-on-the-mid-atlantic-ridge":"The idea is that Atlantis was a real island empire sitting on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, the undersea mountain chain down the middle of the Atlantic, near the Azores islands, with advanced skills at sea and in navigation. A researcher named Randall Carlson puts it this way on the show, drawing on Plato: if Plato's account is taken as at least partly true, he says, Plato is describing 'a midatlantic island-based empire with sophisticated maritime and navigational skills.' In a later episode Michels comes back to it, crediting Carlson and saying only that he thinks the Azores are interesting.","atlantis-s-rebellion-and-the-flood":"Atlantis is said to have fallen because its people turned against the gods, and Zeus answered by wiping the world clean in a great flood. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that, according to Plato, the Atlanteans' \"great sin\" was that they stopped sacrificing to the gods and worshipping them. He says Zeus called the Olympian gods together and decided on the flood \"we more commonly know as Noah's flood\". Elsewhere he calls it a \"rebellion\" in Atlantis, told in Plato's Timaeus and Critias. In Benjamin Jowett's translation of the Critias, the fault is that \"the divine portion began to fade away\" in the Atlanteans and they became \"full of avarice and unrighteous power\". Zeus, \"wanting to inflict punishment on them, that they might be chastened and improve\", gathers the gods, and the dialogue breaks off as he starts to speak, so his decision is not in it. The Timaeus tells the end separately: \"violent earthquakes and floods\" came, and \"in a single day and night of misfortune\" Atlantis \"disappeared in the depths of the sea\". Putting the two dialogues together as a flood sent as punishment is a reading they invite, with the final step from punishment to flood being Jorjani's own, and calling it Noah's flood is his comparison of two stories with a similar shape.","atlantis-search-atlantic-region-matching-plato-s-description":"A former Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet tells Michels that he and a colleague named Maaneli Derakhshani believe they have found a place in the Atlantic Ocean that fits every description of Atlantis in Plato's dialogues. He does not say where it is. If he is right, Plato's sunken island would be a real place that could be searched.","atlantis-search-presentation-to-victor-vescovo-2020":"A former Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet says that sometime in the 2020s, he and others pitched a search for Atlantis, the legendary sunken city, to a man named Victor Vescovo. Gallaudet tells Michels he was part of it: \"We gave him the presentation on Atlantis and he said, 'All right, I'm in.'\" If that is so, a serious backer has signed on to look for Atlantis in the ocean.","atlas-launch-scrubbed-from-the-records-1964":"An Atlas missile launch from Vandenberg Air Force Base in California in 1964 is said to have been wiped from the official record. Bob Jacobs, who tells it as someone who took part in events that day, says officials claimed there was no Atlas launch that day, but that a researcher he calls Robert found Vandenberg's files describing that very launch in detail. He raises it to show that the record of what happened that day was deliberately scrubbed.","atomic-energy-act-s-special-nuclear-material-covers-crash-ma-1954":"A 1954 nuclear law is said to be written broadly enough that material recovered from crashed UFOs falls under nuclear secrecy, classified automatically from the moment it is found. David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, says the Atomic Energy Act of 1954, passed by Congress on 30 August 1954, defines \"special nuclear material\" as, \"basically\", \"any material that releases any kind of atomic energy\". Michels repeats this several times, sometimes crediting Grusch and sometimes as his own reading. He goes further, saying the language was written in \"a sneaky but deliberate way to protect our UFO secrets\", and that anything retrieved is \"born secret\", the common name for information classified from birth. In other tellings he adds an online researcher called UAP Gerb and brings in J. Robert Oppenheimer and Vannevar Bush, and an unidentified guest repeats Grusch's words almost exactly. The Act does set up what \"born secret\" names: \"Restricted Data\" means \"all data concerning\" atomic weapons and the production and use of special nuclear material. It defines special nuclear material as plutonium and enriched uranium \"and any other material which the Commission\", the Atomic Energy Commission, \"determines to be special nuclear material\". For that, the Commission must find it \"capable of releasing substantial quantities of atomic energy\" and \"the President must have expressly assented in writing\", conditions Grusch's summary leaves out. The Act speaks of data and nuclear materials, and stretching the \"other material\" clause to cover a recovered craft is the tellers' extension, which the text neither states nor excludes.","atomic-energy-commission-born-secret-restricted-data-1946":"Michels says the Atomic Energy Commission, set up in 1946 to run American nuclear work, created a category called restricted data under which anything collected with radiological features, meaning alpha, beta or gamma radiation, was \"born secret\": classified from the moment it existed, with no one needing to stamp it. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode on Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, as a way that evidence with radiation traces could have been kept secret automatically.","atomic-energy-commission-contract-for-puharich-1963":"In 1963 or 1964 a scientist named Puharich, the man an episode about a CIA scientist who contacted an \"alien council\" is built around, got a contract from the Atomic Energy Commission, the US agency then in charge of nuclear work. An unnamed guest, a researcher, says this to Michels in passing, without naming a source, as a sign of how close Puharich was to official science.","atomic-energy-commission-grant-to-intelectron-1960":"In the early 1960s, a scientist at Intelectron, a company in New York's Hell's Kitchen, is said to have received a $300,000 grant from the Atomic Energy Commission, the US agency then in charge of nuclear work, which would be a million dollars or more today. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this, drawing on the writer Annie Jacobsen and on the grant documents. He tells it to show that the scientist's work had serious government backing.","atomic-energy-commission-nuclear-secrecy-laid-over-ufo-secre-1950":"Jesse Michels believes that in the 1950s the secrecy around nuclear weapons was laid over the secrecy around UFOs. He says that when the Atomic Energy Commission was running Los Alamos and other sensitive nuclear sites, the two kinds of secret ended up under the same cover. This is his own view, and he puts it more strongly in a later episode, calling nuclear secrecy \"a Trojan horse for UFO secrecy\", stretching it back to the Manhattan Project and forward to the Department of Energy, and pointing to the Atomic Energy Act. If he is right, UFO matters would have been hidden under the same strict rules as the bomb.","attempted-abduction-and-face-cutting-of-15-year-old-talia-2023":"In 2023, in San Antonio de Pintuyacu, a village in the Peruvian Amazon, a 15-year-old girl named Talia was almost abducted and had her face cut, and that attack is why the police went out there. Tim Alberino, who went to the area himself, tells Michels this as he heard it from Talia and from a man named Doug. If true, it would mean someone, or something, is attacking people in that remote village.","attempts-to-discredit-lazar-s-los-alamos-and-s4-employment-1980":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft in the 1980s at S4, a hidden site near Area 51, the secret Air Force test base in Nevada, was the target of efforts to show he had never held those jobs. The comedian and podcast host Joe Rogan tells Michels this, drawing on Lazar and on the Las Vegas journalist George Knapp: 'they tried to say that he never worked at Los Alamos, but that's been confirmed,' and then that he was never at S4, 'but he was there and he knew it.' If Rogan is right, the attacks on Lazar's work record failed, and his account of the site stands on firmer ground.","aubrey-marcus-had-a-dmt-experience-during-a-darkness-retreat":"A man named Aubrey Marcus is said to have had a full DMT experience, the kind usually brought on by the powerful psychedelic drug DMT, without taking it. It happened after four or five days of a darkness retreat, where a person stays in total darkness for days. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore tells Michels what Marcus told him, quoting him: 'this wasn't like DMT. This was a DMT,' which, if true, would suggest the brain can reach that state by itself.","audience-member-with-magnetic-ear-implant-at-area-52-show":"At a live show Michels did with Area 52, another podcast, a man in the audience said he had something lodged in his ear that a magnet sticks to from the outside, and he linked it to an experience he had as a child. Michels tells this himself, as something he heard in person: the man \"has something very similar in his ear and he could put a magnet externally on it and it'll stick there.\" If true, it would be a physical object tied to a childhood encounter, which is why Michels brings it up next to talk of implants.","aurora-black-triangle-program-1987":"The Aurora is said to have been a secret American aircraft program, linked to sightings of black triangle-shaped craft, running from about 1987 to 1999, tied to Groom Lake, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, and to sightings over the North Sea. Michels tells it himself, drawing on the writer Nick Cook, the magazine Jane's Defence Weekly and British Ministry of Defence documents, and he says the Aurora \"showed up as a line item in the 1987 congressional budget with $2.3 billion earmarked for it, something Lockheed had trouble explaining away.\" If true, the US was flying a very advanced craft it never admitted to.","aurora-budget-line-item-1980":"In the 1980s there was an item in the US budget called the Aurora, according to Michels, who mentions it in passing in a conversation about secret programmes of that decade. He does not say what the money paid for or where he learned of it. He raises it as a sign that hidden projects can leave traces in public spending records.","aurora-in-the-1986-budget":"Aurora, a name tied to a rumoured secret US aircraft, is said to show up as a line item of roughly $2 billion in the 1986 US budget. Michels says this himself on an episode with the journalist Nick Cook, first saying 1968 and then correcting himself, and he mentions the aircraft engineer Ben Rich and British Ministry of Defence documents in the same breath. If true, a budget line would be a paper trail for a project officially never acknowledged.","aurora-rumoured-at-raf-machrihanish-1987":"Aurora, a rumoured secret aircraft, was said at one point to be based at RAF Machrihanish, a remote military airbase in Scotland. A researcher named Nick Cook tells Michels this as a rumour, without naming its source. If it were true, a secret aircraft would have been flying out of Britain sometime between the late 1980s and the late 1990s.","aurora-texas-airship-crash-and-alien-buried-beside-chris-s-f-1897":"In 1897, a \"sky ship\" is said to have crashed into a wind tower in Aurora, Texas, and the alien on board was buried in the town. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs are drawn to him, tells Michels it was the first such report he knows of in America, and adds that the alien was buried \"right beside my family cemetery.\"","austin-meeting-where-puthoff-said-skinwalker-ranch-needed-a":"At a meeting in Austin, Texas, a guest named Peter Levenda says a question came up about Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch in Utah, and about \"an appearance\" there and the equipment on site, and everyone in the room went silent. Levenda, who says he was in the room, tells Michels that a man named Hal Puthoff finally looked around and said, \"Well, we need a skiff for this one,\" meaning a SCIF, a sealed and secured room where classified matters can be discussed; a man named Chris Mellon was there too. Michels, retelling it later, adds that Levenda had to leave the meeting because he did not have the right clearances, which, if so, would suggest the ranch was a classified matter.","australian-aboriginal-oral-tradition-concerning-nhi":"Australian Aboriginal culture is said to carry a very old oral tradition, perhaps 30,000 years old, that is compact and easy to pass on, and that deals with NHI, short for \"non-human intelligence\", the beings some believe visit us. A guest named Diana Pasulka mentions it on the show, saying it is what indigenous friends, whom she does not name, have told her. If true, people would have been passing down knowledge of such beings for tens of thousands of years.","australian-man-leaves-edgar-mitchell-s-house-with-files-2016":"In 2016 an Australian man was caught leaving the Florida house of the Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell with files, according to the documentary filmmaker James Fox. Fox tells Michels \"this Australian guy I caught\" and brings it up in connection with the Wilson-Davis memo, said to be notes by a man named Eric Davis of a meeting with an admiral named Thomas Wilson. In Fox's telling, papers from Mitchell's house are part of how that memo came out.","australian-man-records-astronaut-private-channel":"A man in Australia is said to have recorded astronauts' radio traffic, and when the astronauts switched to a different, private channel, he was able to switch with them. Charlie Foltz, a guest on an episode about alien abduction, tells Michels about meeting this man, though he can't quite recall who he was ('who the hell did we meet?'). The point is that the man may have caught talk that was never meant for the public.","australian-officer-says-buchanan-also-knocked-out-australian":"An Australian officer is said to have walked up to an Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan in a Bureau of Indian Affairs cafeteria and told him, 'you're the one that knocked out our computers over in Australia.' Buchanan tells this himself on the show, as something said to him. If true, it would mean Buchanan had somehow put computers out of action on the other side of the world.","australian-special-forces-paid-cash-for-us-uap-retrieval-ope":"Members of Australian special forces were paid in cash to help with American operations to recover crashed UAP, the official term for UFOs. That is the account of the journalist Ross Coulthart, who says the men told him themselves that \"they'd been involved in retrieval operations.\" If true, it would mean the US recovers such craft and uses allied troops to do it.","autec-submersible-ufo-rumors-near-nassau":"A Navy site in the Bahamas near Nassau, which Michels calls ATIC (the name usually written AUTEC, an underwater testing range), is the subject of \"tons of rumors\" about UFOs and underwater craft, according to Michels. In a later episode he calls it a weapons training facility with \"underwater cables that people don't know where they're going.\" Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and gives no detail of what the rumors claim was seen.","autopsy-on-a-soldier-likely-wounded-by-an-alien-being-1996":"The story is that a young soldier died in Varginha, a town in Brazil, in 1996 after grabbing a strange being there, and that his body was autopsied. Many people say a creature was captured in Varginha that year. If the account is true, a man died from contact with something not human. Michels presents it as an interview with 'a lead doctor who did the autopsy on a soldier that was likely wounded by an alien being,' a doctor he names as Armando Fortunato. Passing on what Fortunato and another doctor named Cesario told him, Michels names the soldier as Marco Eli Chereze. He says Chereze 'apprehended this being' and seemed to die of a bacterial infection 'that we've never seen the likes of before.' A witness named Ahmed Foratu, speaking in the same interview, tells it more soberly. He says that when the soldier's body arrived at the Forensic Medical Institute, 'there was no mention of contact with extraterrestrials.' The autopsy, he says, was to look into 'a possible professional error,' meaning a medical mistake. So Michels frames the death as linked to the being, while the witness says nobody raised that link when the body came in.","avesta-s-island-continent-that-froze-over":"The Avesta, the ancient sacred texts of Iran's old Zoroastrian religion, says the Aryan people came from an island continent at the center of the world that froze over. The researcher Jason Jorjani gives this reading on the show without pointing to a passage, and the place he links it to is Antarctica. If his reading holds, an old scripture would be remembering a homeland lost under ice.","avi-loeb-s-calculation-against-fravor-s-80-000-ft-drop-2004":"In 2004, a Navy fighter pilot named David Fravor reported that an unidentified object near the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz, off San Diego, dropped from 80,000 feet to sea level almost instantly. The skeptic Michael Shermer brings this up when he debates Michels, and he sets Fravor's account against a calculation by the scientist Avi Loeb that he cites as weighing against it. Shermer does not explain the details of the calculation, but he uses it to question whether such a drop could really have happened as Fravor describes.","aviation-week-editor-threatened-over-sorensen-interview-1990":"In the 1990s, the people behind an interview with a man named Sorensen in Aviation Week, an aerospace trade magazine, are said to have been threatened over it. The online UFO researcher known as UAPGerb tells Michels he has since spoken with the interview's editor, who told him they had received threats, 'like Men in Black type encounters', meaning the mysterious visitors who in UFO lore show up to scare witnesses into silence. If true, someone wanted that interview buried.","aviators-reports-to-graves-leading-to-the-congressional-hear-2023":"A Navy fighter pilot named Ryan Graves says that before the 2023 hearing in Congress on unexplained objects in the sky, he became a \"beacon\" for people who wanted to report their own sightings. Most of them were aviators, though all kinds of people called. Graves tells Michels this first-hand, as part of how those reports led up to the hearing.","avro-car-in-nasa-wind-tunnel-1961":"In 1961 the Avrocar, a saucer-shaped experimental aircraft, was put through tests in a NASA wind tunnel, a sign that the US government was seriously trying to build flying discs of its own. Michels mentions it himself, without naming a source, in an episode about claims that humans have built antigravity craft.","avrocar-as-cover-for-roswell-craft-work-and-nazi-scientist-r-1947":"The Avrocar, a saucer-shaped experimental aircraft built in Canada, was probably a cover for deeper work on the craft said to have crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, along with the recruiting of Nazi scientists. Michels offers this as his own theory, resting it on rumors that the Roswell craft was taken to Wright Field, an Air Force base in Ohio. If he is right, a public aircraft project would have hidden study of a recovered craft.","avrocar-project-moved-to-wright-pat-as-project-y-bug-1950":"The Avrocar, an attempt in Canada to build a working flying saucer, is said to have carried on in secret in the United States after it officially ended. Dan Sheehan, speaking as a researcher, tells Michels that the project began in Canada and was then moved to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base as Project Y Silverbug. Michels himself tells it on several episodes without naming a source: he says it nominally failed in 1961 but really moved to Wright-Patterson as a project called Project Y or Silverbug, elsewhere he dates the Avrocar to 1952 and credits two men, John Frost and Henri Coanda, and once he simply places it in the 1950s and 1960s. If true, it would mean the US military kept working on saucer-shaped aircraft of its own.","axelrod-oni-nimitz-investigator-studying-skinwalker-ranch-2004":"Michels says a man known by the pseudonym Axelrod, who he says was the chief Office of Naval Intelligence investigator on the Nimitz case, has been turning up at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property famous for reports of strange events, to study what goes on there. The Office of Naval Intelligence is the Navy's intelligence service, and the Nimitz case is the 2004 encounter in which Navy pilots from the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz reported an unidentified object. Michels tells this himself, unsure even of the name (\"I think his name is Axelrod\"), and if true it would link the Navy's own inquiry into that encounter with the ranch.","ayanis-andesite-foundation-under-mud-brick":"At Ayanis, an ancient site in Turkey, mud-brick walls are said to sit on a foundation of andesite, \"one of the hardest stones on Earth,\" as if the later builders reused the work of someone older and more skilled. The researcher Matt LaCroix shows this to Michels without naming a source: \"that's the foundation of Ayanis... built out of andesite.\" If true, it would point to a lost earlier culture beneath known ones.","azores-as-sunken-mountain-tops-of-a-lost-mid-atlantic-land":"The Azores, the islands in the middle of the Atlantic, are the tops of mountains of a land that has sunk, according to the researcher Randall Carlson. He tells Michels \"those islands are the tops of mountains, sunk in mountains\" as his own reading, without naming a source, on an episode about ancient catastrophes. If true, a sizeable landmass once stood above the sea in the mid-Atlantic.","aztec-1948-craft-powered-up-at-wright-field-went-translucent":"A craft said to have been recovered after a crash near Aztec, New Mexico, in March 1948 was supposedly taken to the military airfield Wright Field, where it powered up and turned see-through. Michels says this came from a firsthand witness who claimed to have worked on the craft there. Scott Ramsey, who wrote a book about the Aztec crash, tells it as well. He says the witness was an engineer with ties to Eastman Kodak who told him, \"I'm pretty sure this is the Aztec craft I worked on. I read your book.\"","aztec-craft-taken-to-los-alamos-then-wright-field-1948":"A craft said to have crashed near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948 was \"first taken to Los Alamos\" and then moved to Wright Field, the Air Force base in Ohio, according to Michels. He credits the account to a man named Scott Ramsey, who has looked into the Aztec case. If true, it would mean the wreck went first to the nuclear weapons lab and then to the Air Force's main technical base.","aztec-crash-and-its-debunking-1948":"The story is that a craft crashed near Aztec, New Mexico, around 1948, and that the case was then called a hoax in the early 1950s. The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels he believes it, calling it the first crash case ever written about, and names the people around it: the writer Frank Scully, JP Cahn, Richard Geldreich, William Steinman, who he says wrote 'a really big fat book on it,' and Scott and Suzanne Ramsey, who he credits with rehabilitating the case, even though, he notes, Wikipedia still labels it a hoax.","aztec-crash-possibly-brought-down-by-the-lashup-radar-networ-1948":"One leading theory holds that the craft said to have crashed near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948 was brought down by the \"Lashup\" radar network, an early chain of radar stations built over New Mexico to guard against Soviet bombers. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb lays out this theory for Michels without naming who holds it. If it were true, American military radar would have caused the crash of an unknown craft.","b-2-bomber-money-diverted-to-illegal-man-made-ufo-projects":"The B-2 stealth bomber cost the US about $2 billion apiece, but each one probably cost less than $100 million to make, and the difference was quietly funnelled into illegal secret projects building man-made UFOs. That is the claim of the UFO activist Steven Greer, speaking with Michels in 2025; if true, it would mean a hidden budget sits inside one of the Pentagon's best-known weapons programmes.","b-2-colonel-whispers-about-electrogravitics-2008":"At a conference in Las Vegas in 2008, Richard Dolan says, a colonel connected with the B-2 stealth bomber leaned forward and whispered, \"That's bullshit,\" as talk turned to electrogravitics, the idea, tied to the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, that electric charge can push against gravity. The talk was about a paper by Paul LaViolette arguing that the B-2 uses such a drive. Dolan, a researcher who writes about UFO secrecy, tells Michels he was there, and he offers the whisper as a telling reaction to the question of what really powers the bomber.","b-2-uses-biefield-brown-effect-1992":"Michels says the B-2 stealth bomber uses the work of Thomas Townsend Brown, a mid-century inventor who used strong electric fields to try to make thrust and control airflow, a line of work he calls electrogravitics. He points to a story in the magazine Aviation Week from March 1992 about the B-2, to Paul Schatzkin's book The Man Who Mastered Gravity and to a researcher named Paul LaViolette, and adds his own conviction that the aerospace company Northrop Grumman paid Brown a very small sum for the rest of his life. If true, a secret propulsion idea would already be flying in a frontline US bomber.","baalbek-trilithon-stones":"At Baalbek in Lebanon, a set of huge blocks called the trilithon stones sit about 30 feet above the foundation blocks and were placed with precision, and each one weighs at least 800 tons and maybe close to 1,000. A guest named Jay Anderson describes them to Michels without naming a source. He brings them up as an example of ancient building so heavy and exact that it is hard to explain how it was done.","baass-project-forum-and-ttsa-as-holdover-2011":"Around 2011 there was a plan called Project Forum to get celebrities, media personalities and other popular people in California talking about UFOs, according to a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb. He says he found it in documents from BAASS, the company that ran a Pentagon-funded study of the unexplained, and from a related office he calls OSAP. He sees TTSA, a later private UFO venture linked to Tom Dong and to a former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo, as a holdover of that plan, which would mean the public UFO push of recent years was planned in advance.","baba-vanga-predicts-his-kalmykia-presidency-1990":"A former president of Kalmykia, a republic inside Russia, says that around 1990 the Bulgarian seer known as Baba Vanga told him he would go home and lead his people: \"You'll be the leader. You'll be president there.\" He tells Michels this as something he lived through, and mentions a telegram from Bulgaria as part of the story. If true, it would mean she foresaw his rise to power before it happened, in the early 1990s.","baba-vanga-s-april-1995-prediction-of-the-chess-federation-p":"In April 1995, a Bulgarian seer named Baba Vanga is said to have called in a man she addressed as Kerson and foretold that he would become president of the world chess federation, a step that led him to Paris. The story comes from the man himself, introduced on the show as the former president of a Russian republic. He tells Michels that Baba Vanga and her niece phoned to say \"Kerson, come here,\" and he mentions the Guinness book along the way. For him it is proof that her gift of foresight was real.","baba-vanga-says-beings-live-underground-1990":"Beings live underground, says a guest introduced on the show as the president of a Russian republic, who tells Michels he does not just think so but knows it. He says the famous Bulgarian seer Baba Vanga told him so in the 1990s: sometimes she would point down with her finger and say \"they are talking there. They are there.\" If true, someone else would be living beneath our feet.","baba-vanga-talks-with-souls-and-entities-that-are-everywhere-1990":"A man billed on the show as a former president of a Russian republic says that in the 1990s, in her yard in Bulgaria, he asked a woman known as Baba Vanga whether we are alone, and she smacked him lightly and told him unseen beings are everywhere. He tells Michels she said: \"What's wrong with you? Are you stupid or what? Can't you see? It's all around you... It's absolutely everywhere.\" To him it was a firsthand answer that other beings share our world.","babson-louis-witten-and-bryce-dewitt-gravity-institutes-1950":"In the 1950s a man named Babson, who funded research on gravity, is said to have approached the physicist Louis Witten, then coming out of Johns Hopkins, and helped set up gravity research institutes, with the physicist Bryce DeWitt's work at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill. The researcher Eric Weinstein tells this to Michels, saying \"Babson contacted Louis Witten, who was coming out of Johns Hopkins,\" without naming a source. It comes up because those years are said to be when serious interest in antigravity began.","babson-s-gravity-research-foundation-in-new-boston-1950":"In the 1950s, a wealthy man named Roger Babson is said to have set up a Gravity Research Foundation in New Boston, New Hampshire, to find a way to defeat gravity. The researcher Eric Weinstein describes it and repeats the usual reason given for it: that Babson's sister had drowned in a pond. He calls that story \"not really very plausible,\" which hints that something else lay behind it. Michels, telling it in his own words, calls Babson \"a patron of science\" who \"was obsessed with finding the solution to antigravity,\" and he leaves out both the town and the drowned sister. Weinstein's doubt matters because, if the cover story is false, the foundation might have served a hidden programme.","backlash-over-the-2021-transients-paper-co-authored-with-jef-2015":"The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel says that in 2021, when her team reported nine \"transients\" (points of light in the sky that appear and then vanish), the paper drew a backlash partly because of her co-author. She tells Michels that she wrote it with her colleague, the astronomer Jeff Marcy, who \"had been cancelled in a sexual harassment scandal in 2015.\" She tells it as her own experience of how the work was received in her field.","bae-colleagues-on-the-program-assume-borland-is-read-in-2010":"A man named Dylan Borland says that sometime in the 2010s, colleagues at the defense company BAE Systems heard him talking about \"the triangle\" and assumed he had been \"read in\", meaning formally given access to a secret programme, because they were in it themselves. Borland tells Michels this from his own experience and says it is how \"this whole thing\" began for him.","bae-falcon-works-joint-ventures-with-lockheed-skunk-works":"BAE, the defence company, has a unit called Falcon Works that is \"BAE's version of Skunk Works,\" Lockheed's secretive advanced-aircraft division, according to a whistleblower named Dylan Borland. He tells Michels that the two companies run joint ventures through Skunk Works on \"material sciences,\" engineering, engines, \"all of it.\" If so, the most secret aerospace work would cross company lines and national borders.","baghdad-phantom-mission-report-released-early-was-due-for-de":"A military mission report on the Baghdad Phantom, an incident over Baghdad that the telling does not describe further, was supposed to stay secret until 2047 but has been released early. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, who reports on UFO cases, tells Michels this as a journalist working from his sources, and an early release would mean the report came out decades ahead of schedule.","baghdad-phantom-seven-still-images-then-video-in-the-governm":"An unexplained object seen over Baghdad, nicknamed the Baghdad Phantom, was first made public as seven still images and later appeared as video in the government's first official release of such material. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels that he and the Las Vegas television journalist George Knapp reported the case and released those seven images themselves.","bahamas-yacht-trips-with-eldridge-reeves-johnson":"Michels says the people at the centre of a story he is tracing went on trips to the Bahamas aboard a yacht owned by a man named Eldridge Reeves Johnson. The names he ties to these trips include the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown and William Stephenson, the man the book A Man Called Intrepid is about, and Michels draws on that book. He brings it up as part of a case that one private circle links the UFO secrecy story together.","bahnsen-s-notes-on-unexplained-effect-1950":"In the 1950s in Chapel Hill, North Carolina, a man named Agnew Bahnson wrote in his notes that experiments he was following had produced an effect nobody could explain. The researcher Paul Schatzkin, speaking in an episode about the inventor Townsend Brown, tells Michels that Bahnson wrote 'there's something going on here that we're unable to account for.' Schatzkin offers this as a sign that the work turned up something real, though he names no source beyond the notes.","bahnson-funds-brown-s-gravitator-experiments-1950":"In the 1950s a North Carolina man named Agnew Bahnson, head of a body called the Institute of Field Physics, paid for the work of an inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown on \"gravitators\", devices Brown hoped would control gravity. Michels tells this himself across three episodes, without naming a source, adding that the experiments ran at the back of Bahnson's house, that Bahnson wrote of strange phenomena, and that Brown claimed positive results; if so, gravity research was being privately funded alongside mainstream physics.","bahnson-funds-bryce-dewitt-institute-at-unc-chapel-hill-1950":"The story is that in the 1950s a rich industrialist named Agnew Bahnson paid to set up an institute for the young physicist Bryce DeWitt at the University of North Carolina, Chapel Hill. It was called the Institute of Field Physics and studied gravity. The researcher Eric Weinstein tells it twice on the show. The first time he is unsure of the name, describing \"some sort of tobacco or air conditioning fortune.\" Later he says Bahnson and the physicist John Wheeler found DeWitt and set him up there, frames the institute as aimed at anti-gravity, and points to an old newspaper clip. Michels tells it himself as well. He says the work was \"often gravitational anomalies,\" and he ties it to a 1957 Chapel Hill conference with Richard Feynman, Peter Bergmann, Freeman Dyson and Wheeler. If it is true, private money was quietly backing anti-gravity research at the top of physics in that decade.","baiting-for-shootdowns-as-cover-for-psionic-breeding":"Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon employee who calls himself a whistleblower, says that using people as \"bait for shootdowns\", that is, to draw craft in so they can be brought down, is really cover for something else. In his telling, people with psychic powers are \"brought in as breeding stock\", aimed at producing \"some sort of super psychic child\" generations later. He says this on the show twice, once without the super-psychic child.","baker-victory-services-day-treatment-center-as-a-front-for-d-2008":"A man named Jordan Jose says that from about 2008 to 2010, as a child, he went to a school in Western New York run by Baker Victory Services that looked like a day centre for neurodivergent kids and kids with behavioural problems, but was secretly supporting research by a defense contractor into developing psychic ability. Jose tells Michels he went there himself, and that it all started with a game and lasted two years. He says the school has since closed and been \"sued six ways to Sunday\"; if he is right, children were being tested for psychic potential under cover of a school.","baker-victory-services-training-to-quantum-unentangle":"Michels says that at a place called Baker Victory Services, much of the work was teaching people to \"quantum unentangle\" from things they were entangled with, borrowing a physics term for linked particles to describe cutting some kind of hidden connection. He says it himself, without naming a source, in an interview with a guest who says he was taken into a UFO programme as a child, and the telling gives no more detail about what that training involved.","bald-shin-patches-and-jack-s-tumors-sent-to-air-force-pathol-1976":"In 1976, according to a man named Jack Weiner, bald patches appeared on his shins and he later \"had tumors growing in the exact spots on his legs.\" A Vermont teaching hospital sent the tissue on to an Air Force pathology institute near Washington. Charlie Foltz, who says he was a witness to the events around Weiner, tells Michels this as Weiner told it to him, during a conversation about alien abduction, and he points to a pathology report on Weiner's tumor. If true, the Air Force was taking a close interest in physical marks on a man who said he had been taken by aliens.","balls-of-light-over-hanford-plutonium-site-1945":"In early 1945, months before the first atomic test and the bombing of Japan, glowing balls of light are said to have hovered over Hanford, the plant in Washington state where plutonium for the bomb was made, and fighter planes could not catch them. Michels tells it often, drawing on the researcher Robert Hastings. He describes \"luminous balls of light hovering\" over the site and \"being chased away unsuccessfully by jet fighters\", and names a pilot, Bud Clem, sometimes as the man following a ball of light himself. In one telling he ties it to Project Twinkle, which he says was \"deleted from the Air Force archives\". Hastings' article of August 9, 2015 gives the source. It says Clarence \"Bud\" Clem was a Navy fighter pilot flying the F6F Hellcat. It says another pilot, Brown, found \"a bright ball of fire, and took chase. But he could not close [on it]\", even with a boost in speed. Clem's records, Hastings writes, put the squadron nearby \"from January 9 to February 15, 1945\", not in spring as Clem first recalled. Hastings concludes that \"bona fide UFOs were in fact operating near the Hanford site in early 1945\". If so, they were watching the bomb before the world knew it existed.","baltic-anomaly-history-lightning-smoke-nato-ships":"A strange object said to lie in the Baltic Sea, known as the Baltic anomaly, has been struck by lightning, has given off smoke, and has had NATO ships circling it, according to Michels, who calls it \"super strange.\" He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of odd events.","baltic-sea-anomaly-expedition":"On the floor of the Baltic Sea, between Sweden and Finland, lies a large round object that some think is a crashed craft. It is known as the Baltic Sea anomaly. Dennis Asberg, a Swedish treasure hunter, says he found it himself on 19 June 2011 on side-scan sonar, which maps the seabed with sound. He remembers his teammate Peter Lindberg \"laughing and oh, Dennis, you know, here is UFO.\" Michels describes it as a thing that \"looks like the Millennium Falcon\" in shape, and says he went out to the site himself in 2025, \"60 km out at sea\" near \"the epicenter between Finland and Sweden.\" The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel tells it as something she once heard about: divers had found \"this thing that looked like a crashed UFO or looked like an artificial structure\" off the Swedish coast. If it is what some suspect, it would be a crashed craft lying in reach of divers.","baltic-sea-anomaly-not-attached-to-the-seafloor-with-electro":"A strange object on the bottom of the Baltic Sea is said not to be attached to the seafloor, and to show electromagnetic activity. Michels tells this himself, passing on what an explorer and friend of his named Dennis Asberg told him: 'it's not attached to the seafloor.' If true, it would mean the object is not a natural rock formation fixed to the seabed.","banister-pistol-whips-jack-martin-1963":"In New Orleans in 1963, two men named Guy Banister and Jack Martin got into a violent fight over the assassination; the story is known as Banister pistol-whipping Martin, though the teller himself calls it \"a fist fight\". The researcher Peter Levenda tells it to Michels without naming a source, as a clue that the two men knew something about the killing.","bannister-effect-after-the-first-4-minute-mile-1954":"The British athlete Roger Bannister ran the first mile under four minutes at Oxford in 1954, and within two and a half years ten other runners had done the same, as though breaking the barrier once made it possible for everyone. Michels tells this himself, as an example of what is called the Bannister effect, in a conversation about the idea that we live in a hologram, using it to suggest that what we believe possible shapes what we can do.","baptist-preacher-prays-over-the-bodies-1948":"In 1948 a saucer is said to have come down in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, and a local preacher named Solon Brown prayed over the dead beings found inside. Scott Ramsey, who tells the Aztec story on the show, relays Brown's own words when he was asked to pray: \"Well, I'm not Catholic. I'm a Baptist preacher, but I'd be more than happy to say a prayer over him.\"","barber-goes-to-the-senate-intelligence-committee-and-the-cia":"Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he helped retrieve a UFO, says he decided to go to Washington \"to go to the Senate Intelligence Committee and go to the Monitor for CIA and spill my guts,\" meaning an official who oversees the CIA, and that they in turn asked for his help. He tells Michels this first-hand. The journalist Ross Coulthart, speaking on a later episode, backs part of it, saying Barber's \"first-person evidence\" was given to the Senate's intelligence committee, though he says nothing of the CIA side.","barber-hints-at-the-two-aerospace-primes-in-crash-retrievals-2020":"A man named Jake Barber, who speaks of crash retrievals (the recovery of crashed craft said to be of unknown origin) as someone involved in them, hints that two big aerospace \"primes\", the main contractors that take on government defense work, are part of that effort. He will not name them, but tells Michels that \"if you took two guesses, you'd get both of them right on the first try.\"","barber-one-to-two-craft-a-year-recovered-over-his-range":"Over a single test range, one or two unexplained craft a year are recovered, according to a man named Jake Barber, who has spoken about such recoveries in public. A guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb repeats Barber's figure to Michels while explaining how a hidden Pentagon UFO programme works. If true, retrieving downed craft would be routine work, not a one-off.","barber-s-group-back-channeled-the-psionic-asset-transfer-exp-2023":"In 2023, a former helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, who says he helped retrieve a UFO, told Michels that his group quietly passed an explanation about \"psionic assets\" to Michael Herrera. Psionic assets are people said to use psychic abilities, and Herrera is a man who says he saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle. \"We back channeled it. That was us,\" Barber says, and the same line appears again in another episode, this time credited to Michels. Herrera tells it from his side, saying that a meeting passed to him privately by someone inside the secret programmes is something that did not happen to any other whistleblower.","barber-s-symptoms-compared-with-john-burroughs-from-rendlesh-1980":"Jake Barber, a man who says he flew a helicopter to recover a UFO, tells Michels that his own health problems match those of John Burroughs, one of the US airmen, with Jim Penniston, at the 1980 Rendlesham Forest incident in England, where servicemen reported a strange craft near an air base. Barber says Burroughs 'had similar symptoms that I did and had similar issues with his VA records,' meaning his veterans' medical files, a link he connects to the scientist Garry Nolan. If true, contact with such craft may leave the same marks on people decades apart.","barber-s-team-brought-in-for-dod-59-agencies-not-coordinatin-2024":"A helicopter pilot named Jake Barber says that in 2024 his team was called in for the Pentagon at Picatinny Arsenal, an Army base in New Jersey, and found more than 59 agencies there working side by side without coordinating. Barber tells Michels he was there and saw \"FBI, we had DoD, we had private partners\" (DoD is the Department of Defense). His point is that the response to whatever was going on there was large and disorganised.","barber-s-team-saw-the-same-octagonal-eight-gon-craft-that-he-2023":"Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he helped retrieve a UFO, says the eight-sided craft his team saw in 2023, which he calls \"the eight-gon,\" is \"exactly what Michael Herrera reports to have seen.\" Herrera is another man who has described seeing such a craft. Barber tells Michels this as someone who was there, and if true, two separate accounts would describe the same kind of object.","barber-sent-to-red-team-whistleblowers-at-the-2023-national":"A man named Jake Barber says that in 2023 his employer sent him to a whistleblower conference at the National Press Club in Washington, hosted by a man named Steven Greer, to \"red team\" it, that is, to act like an opponent and spot whistleblowers and find out \"who's got what\". Barber tells Michels he heard a man named Michael Herrera give his story there, and a researcher who goes by UAPGerb says Barber sat in the audience thinking \"I know about this\". Herrera himself says he was suspicious when Barber approached him but that Barber was later open about being \"a red team asset\", while Michels puts it to Greer as Barber coming to \"infiltrate your camp\".","barber-story-contractor-and-mercenary-retrieval-units-compet":"Part of the account of a man named Jake Barber suggests that private contractor teams and mercenary retrieval units, groups that recover crashed objects, are competing with each other to get hold of them. A guest named Matthew Pines brings this up with Michels in passing. He calls these 'the whole weird parts of the barber story,' and says his own view of things would explain them.","barry-says-the-secrecy-sucks-1987":"Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S4 in the late 1980s, tells Michels about a moment there with a colleague named Barry Castilio. Lazar says he asked him, \"Holy cow. Can you believe this is being kept secret?\" and Castilio answered, \"It sucks. I know.\"","basalt-bas-relief-carrying-hermetic-teachings":"In the Lake Van region of Turkey stands a large basalt block, about 3 by 3 by 4 feet, carved in relief with sacred teachings that a researcher named Matt LaCroix believes became the basis of Hermeticism, an old mystical and philosophical tradition. LaCroix describes the stone to Michels without naming a source. He presents it as a sign that ideas later known as Hermetic began in a much older culture.","basel-group-extended-the-dmt-state-to-90-minutes":"A research group in Basel, Switzerland, has made the DMT experience last 90 minutes. DMT is a powerful psychedelic whose effects usually last only a few minutes. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore tells Michels this without naming a paper, crediting a researcher he thinks is called Matias Leaki, and he raises it because a longer trip would give people more time to explore the realm and the beings they say they meet there.","bases-on-the-far-side-of-the-moon":"The claim is that something has been built on the far side of the Moon, the side that never faces Earth. John Brandenburg, a scientist who argues that Mars once had life, says on the show that there would be bases there, and that putting them on the far side is 'almost as if you're afraid we'll see them.' He gives it as his own reasoning, without a source. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him, goes less far: he says that from Moon photos he has seen, 'there is something behind it hidden back there.' If either is right, someone other than us is active on the Moon and hiding it from view.","bat-filled-structures-smell-different-from-the-red-pyramid":"A pyramid researcher named Geoffrey Drumm says the old stone tombs at Saqqara in Egypt that are full of bats smell different from the Red Pyramid, one of Egypt's great early pyramids. He tells Michels he has been inside them himself, citing someone named Alexa: \"We've been in Mastaba 17 that is filled with bats. We've been in the tomb of the birds that is filled with bats.\" For Drumm the difference is a clue that the pyramid was not a tomb like the others.","battelle-analysis-of-1947-1952-sightings":"A detailed study by the research firm Battelle of UFO sightings from 1947 to 1952 found that 33 percent of the most credible ones remained unknown. Michels brings this up himself in a debate with a skeptic, using it to argue that the best cases were never explained.","battelle-biologics-lab-at-fort-detrick":"Battelle, a private research company, is said to run a lab for biological materials at Fort Detrick in Maryland, and to be the group most likely studying actual biologics, meaning bodies or tissue of non-human beings. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb suggests this to Michels as his own guess, without naming a source: \"if there's any group out there that is studying actual biologics, it's probably Battelle.\" If true, a contractor would hold the most sensitive part of the secret.","battelle-brainstorms-injectable-interface-2020":"A team at the research company Battelle worked on an injectable interface, a device meant to link the brain to machines, and moved on to the second phase of the program that funded it. Michels reports this from Battelle's own press release of 2020, which says its team \"progressed to phase two of the program.\" He tells it in an episode about a UFO book the Pentagon tried to censor, and he connects the work to the Air Force Research Lab and to Carnegie Mellon.","battelle-memorial-institute-linked-to-gifted-and-talented-pr":"Michels says there is a link between the Battelle Memorial Institute, a private research institute, the school programmes for gifted and talented children, and what he calls the cabal running secret UFO programmes. He says this came from an anonymous senior government official, and raises it in a conversation with the UFO activist Steven Greer, without saying what the link is. If true, it would mean children were being spotted and steered through those school programmes for the benefit of secret UFO work.","battelle-studied-roswell-lightweight-metals-1947":"The Battelle Memorial Institute, a private research institute, studied very lightweight metals taken from the reported 1947 crash of a flying saucer near Roswell, New Mexico. That is the claim of a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb, who says \"there's research to suggest\" it and points to papers he calls Project Stork. If true, a civilian lab would have handled crash material in the years right after Roswell.","battle-of-los-angeles-and-the-marshall-briefing-1942":"In 1942 a UFO appeared over Los Angeles and flew down the coast, in what became known as the Battle of Los Angeles, and George Marshall was briefed on it, according to Michels. In a later episode he says the object went from Santa Monica to San Diego and calls Marshall Secretary of State at the time. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source; if true, it would mean the matter reached the very top of the US war leadership.","bauval-hancock-and-schoch-on-sphinx-weathering-and-the-orion-1990":"Since the 1990s the writers Robert Bauval, Graham Hancock and Robert Schoch have argued that the limestone of the Sphinx in Egypt shows wear from weathering, and that the Giza monuments line up with the stars of Orion as they stood around 10,500 BC, which would make the site far older than usually thought. Michels sums up their work briefly, as \"very interesting things\" they have been saying, in an episode with a scientist who scanned beneath the pyramids.","bay-of-pigs-bombers-arrive-late-because-of-a-time-zone-1961":"During the Bay of Pigs, the failed 1961 invasion of Cuba, the bombers meant to cover the landing arrived late because they were working in a different time zone, according to Dan Sheehan. Sheehan, the guest of an episode on UFOs and President Kennedy, tells this to Michels without naming a source, linking the planes to Nicaragua and its ruler Anastasio Somoza. In his telling, a simple mistake about the clock helped sink the operation.","bayer-letters-with-third-reich-generals-about-camp-trials":"Letters went back and forth between the German drug company Bayer and generals of the Third Reich about trials carried out in the Nazi camps, according to Brigham Buhler, a health-industry entrepreneur who criticises big pharmaceutical companies. He tells Michels the letters exist but does not say where they are. If true, it would tie a major drug company directly to Nazi experiments on prisoners.","bayer-ships-hiv-contaminated-hemophilia-medicine-abroad-1980":"In the 1980s, a drug company, named here as Bayer, knowingly shipped large amounts of hemophilia medicine infected with HIV to poor countries. A guest named Brigham Buhler brings this up with Michels as part of a talk about the drug industry's dark past and human experiments. He tells it as a sign of how far such companies will go.","beach-photo-of-a-suited-being-in-baja-california":"A woman taking photos at sunset on a beach in Baja California, Mexico, is said to have caught a being in a suit in one of her pictures. Ammar Kandil, a guest presented as holding a large store of UFO evidence, tells Michels the story at several removes: it came from 'my best friend's sister's best friend.' He brings it up because it reminded him of another account.","beam-over-the-canoe-taken-aboard-wand-jabbed-in-jim-s-ribs-1976":"In 1976, on the Allagash Waterway in Maine, an object hung right above a canoe and shone a beam down around it, then the men in it were taken aboard, and one of them, Jim Weiner, had a wand-like tool jabbed into his ribs. Weiner tells Michels this as one of the people it happened to: \"I remembered it being right over on like literally on top of us and it this beam came down around the canoe.\"","bechtel-and-monsanto-possibly-handling-alien-bodies-and-biol":"Large companies may have handled alien bodies and biological specimens in secure underground sites, with the engineering firm Bechtel and the chemical company Monsanto named in the story. Michels says he was told this, without naming who told him, and that Monsanto may have been involved in the past, most likely dealing with biological specimens.","beckwith-s-neutrino-light-detector-seized-by-the-nro":"An inventor named Bob Beckwith is said to have built a detector that could pick up bursts of 'neutrino light,' neutrinos being nearly undetectable subatomic particles, only to be hit with a national security order from the NRO, the National Reconnaissance Office, which runs US spy satellites. Steven Greer, who argues that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, tells Michels this, passing on what Beckwith told him. If true, it would mean the government had taken control of a new kind of sensor.","bedrock-mound-inside-the-great-pyramid-is-taller-than-believ":"The natural hill of bedrock that the Great Pyramid of Giza was built over may rise much higher inside the pyramid than is usually thought, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum. He tells Michels this as his own view (\"in my opinion, the bedrock mound is actually much taller than we think\") without naming a source, in an episode about what may lie beneath the pyramids. If he is right, more of the pyramid's core would be natural rock than people assume.","bedrock-shafts-along-the-causeway-as-industrial-infrastructu":"Shafts cut into the bedrock along the Khafre causeway, the ancient stone road leading up to the second pyramid at Giza, were part of an industrial system running across the plateau. A researcher named Jeffrey Drum offers this to Michels as his own opinion, without naming a source. It belongs to his larger claim that the pyramids sat on top of a huge energy grid, which would make Giza a working machine, not just a set of tombs.","beehive-like-interior-of-the-craft-and-a-pyramid-shaped-hold-1960":"A man named Mario Woods says he was taken on board a craft in South Dakota and saw an interior like a beehive, with many levels and small Grays, short grey-skinned beings, \"everywhere going to and fro\"; the story as recorded also includes a pyramid-shaped field that held him. Woods tells it as his own experience, and in a second telling he dates it to 1977 rather than the 1960s and leaves out the pyramid-shaped field.","beethoven-s-bone-conduction-1800":"Michels says that Beethoven, once he went deaf, kept hearing music through bone conduction, sound picked up as vibration through the bones of the skull rather than through the ears. He brings this up himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a CIA scientist who says he contacted an alien council, as an example of perceiving things through unusual channels.","befriending-john-wheeler-at-a-new-jersey-retirement-communit":"Bob Maguire, who is introduced as a CIA contractor, says he got to know the physicist John Wheeler while living in New Jersey. At the time Maguire worked for a company that did work for the intelligence community, and his wife was a nurse at a retirement community. He tells Michels this from his own life, and the story also touches on the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton.","behavior-control-tests-on-holmesburg-prison-inmates-1960":"Behavior-control tests were run on inmates at Holmesburg prison in Philadelphia \"a full decade after MK Ultra had officially supposedly been shut down,\" Michels says, MK Ultra being the CIA's programme of mind-control experiments. He tells it himself, without naming a source, in an episode on drug companies and human experiments; if true, it would mean such work went on long after it was said to have ended.","being-said-because-he-s-here-1977":"An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods says that in 1977, in the nuclear missile field at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, a being he met told him something had happened \"because he's here\", pointing to another man without naming either of them. Woods tells Michels he was there, on an episode about Air Force veterans who say they were abducted by aliens: \"They didn't say my name, didn't say his name, just said because he's here.\" If it happened, the beings singled out one of the two men for a reason.","being-shows-longitudinal-scalar-waves-as-slinkies-2021":"Dave Rossi, a man who says aliens taught him advanced physics, says that in 2021, at his home, a being showed him what he calls longitudinal or scalar waves, waves that squeeze and stretch along the direction they travel. He tells Michels the being showed them to him \"basically as slinkies,\" and that they exist \"all around us.\" He tells it as something he experienced himself, and to him it is a glimpse of physics that science has not yet worked out.","being-taken-to-campinas-pathologist-then-area-51-1996":"A creature said to have been captured in Brazil in 1996 was first taken to a famous pathologist in the city of Campinas, Badan Palhares, a friend of a doctor named Rodrigues, and then flown to Area 51, the secret US Air Force test site in Nevada. The surgeon Italo Venturelli tells Michels this was \"the story\" he heard, and he repeats it in a later episode. If true, the US would have taken a creature from Brazil.","beings-at-mount-zeil-let-pat-price-know-they-saw-him-and-it":"When Pat Price, a \"remote viewer\" (someone said to see distant places with the mind alone), turned his attention to Mount Zeil in Australia, the beings there let him know they knew he was watching, and that scared him. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, who did the same kind of work, passes this on to Michels as Price's own account. If true, whatever is at Mount Zeil could notice even someone watching only with the mind.","beings-landing-in-townsend-brown-s-yard-giving-information":"Small beings are said to have landed in the yard of the American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, got out, and given him information. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had repeated UFO encounters himself, tells Michels he heard this from Timothy Taylor, who was working with Brown's daughter to finish a book about her father. Bledsoe says Taylor told him about \"these little things coming and landing in his yard and getting out and giving him information.\" In a second telling, Bledsoe places the conversation in Zanesville. He says that is where Taylor revealed who he was and told him \"the whole story,\" as much as he would share, about Brown \"having visitors come and land.\" Michels adds another source: Brown's daughter, Linda Brown, did an \"ask me anything\" session on the website Reddit. In Michels's version she described pale beings coming down and then entering the house, into the kitchen. If the story is true, an inventor of unusual technology was being visited and taught by beings that were not human.","beings-say-they-will-not-allow-nuclear-war-1964":"A former nuclear missile guard named Richard Barth says that in 1964, at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California, beings he encountered let him understand they were \"not going to let it go to nuclear war\" and \"needed to stop these missiles from launching.\" Barth, telling it as his own experience, adds that his sense was that they would leave us alone \"unless we screwed up\", which, if true, would mean someone other than humans is watching over our weapons.","beings-under-mount-shasta":"Beings are said to live underneath Mount Shasta, a mountain in northern California. The Canadian UFO researcher Grant Cameron mentions this to Michels in passing, saying that 'the mission people always talk about the beings under Mount Shasta', without saying more about who those people are or what they claim.","belgian-air-force-commander-says-ufos-are-real":"The top commander of Belgium's air force is said to have stated that UFOs are real. Charlie Foltz, himself a witness to events in this field, tells Michels this second-hand, describing the man only as 'the supreme air force commander of the Belgian military.' If true, it would mean a senior European military officer had confirmed UFOs are real.","belgian-wave-triangles-1989":"The Belgian wave was a run of UFO sightings over Belgium in 1989 and 1990, and the objects people reported were triangles. A UFO researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb says this in passing to Michels, without naming a source, during a conversation about how the Pentagon's UFO work is run.","bell-as-a-thorium-mercury-overunity-power-device-1937":"The Bell, a device said to have been built in secret for Nazi Germany at the Skoda Works near Prague between 1937 and 1944, was really an \"overunity\" power machine, giving out more energy than went into it. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this came from an unnamed elderly source in Serbia, who said that both AC and DC current were fed in, and that a nuclear reaction in thorium and mercury did the rest.","bell-integrated-into-the-saucer-airframe-in-1944":"In 1944, at the Skoda Works factory near Prague, the Nazis built a flying saucer by taking the rockets out of an airframe and fitting in \"the Bell\", a secret device that, in this telling, gave it a new way to fly: \"They took the rockets out, put the bell in it, and there's your flying saucer.\" The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, crediting a work called Closer Encounters. If true, Nazi Germany would have had a working saucer before the war ended.","bell-project-dates-to-1937-per-a-serbian-source":"The Bell, said to be a secret Nazi German device behind a hidden flying-craft programme, supposedly goes back to 1937. The writer Jason Jorjani, who says he found Hitler's secret UFO programme, tells Michels he was 'informed by somebody who had relatively direct knowledge, somebody in Serbia' that the project began that year. If so, the programme started earlier than is usually told.","bell-scientists-experienced-temporal-disorientation-1940":"Scientists working on the Bell, a device from the secret 1940s German project that the episode calls Hitler's UFO programme, are said to have felt time pass at a different rate inside their laboratory than it did for people outside. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this comes from the reports of two writers, Igor Witkowski and Joseph P. Farrell, and names no document behind them. If it were true, the device would have been doing something to time itself.","belt-montana-truck-driver-sees-light-land-in-ravine-1967":"In 1967 a truck driver passing through the town of Belt, Montana, about 35 miles to the southeast, is said to have seen a light come down and land in a ravine. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer who says a UFO shut down nuclear missiles that year, tells it to Michels as an account he got from a man named Lewis Chase. The point is that people outside the military saw the object too. Michels says the same in another episode, adding that a local police officer is also on record seeing the UFO in the area, though he leaves out the landing in the ravine.","ben-rich-and-reverse-engineered-aerospace":"Ben Rich, a former head of Lockheed's secret aircraft division, is said to have hinted that some American aerospace technology came from studying craft that were not made by humans. That is reverse engineering: taking something apart to learn how it was built. A former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, named John Blitch, raises Rich's claims with Michels. He gives no source and adds no detail of his own beyond saying \"I have no reason to call Ben Rich a liar.\"","ben-rich-called-ufos-unfunded-opportunities-1990":"Ben Rich, who ran Lockheed's Skunk Works, its secret aircraft design shop, is said to have called UFOs 'unfunded opportunities' in the 1990s. Michels brings it up with the writer Nick Cook, reminding him: 'you told me once that Ben Rich would refer to UFOs as unfunded opportunities.' If true, it would suggest a top aircraft engineer saw UFOs as technology waiting to be built.","ben-rich-deathbed-claim-that-we-can-go-to-the-stars-via-esp-1990":"Ben Rich, the former head of Lockheed's Skunk Works, its secret aircraft division, is said to have claimed near his death that the US already can travel to the stars. In Michels's retelling, a journalist asked how, and Rich asked whether he had heard of ESP, then said, 'Well, that's how.' Elsewhere Michels quotes him as saying the means exist 'but these technologies are locked up in black projects', meaning secret classified programs, and in another episode says Rich said it on his deathbed to John Andrews of Testors. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron says the questioner was a man named Jan Harzan, who asked how 'they' got here, and who answered Rich's ESP question by saying it means everything in time and space is connected.","ben-rich-on-an-international-board-of-directors-controlling":"Ben Rich, the director of Lockheed's secretive Skunk Works division, is said to have complained that control of the UFO subject had slipped to \"an international board of directors.\" Michels raises this in several episodes. He places the remark in letters Rich wrote in the early 1990s to John Andrews of the Testor model company, credits it to Forbidden Science, the published journals of the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, calls it once \"an international corporate board,\" and elsewhere says Rich made the remarks to Andrews on his deathbed. If true, someone outside any one government would be steering the subject.","ben-rich-s-deathbed-remarks-to-jim-goodall-1995":"On his deathbed around 1995, Ben Rich, who had run Lockheed's secret aircraft division, told a friend: \"Jim, we have things out in the desert,\" and he did not mean Area 51, the well-known secret test site. The aviation writer Jim Goodall says Rich said it to him in hospital, and in a later episode he adds that Rich called the things \"50 years beyond what you can comprehend.\"","ben-rich-s-ucla-speech-1990":"In a speech at UCLA in the 1990s, near the end of his life, Ben Rich, the former head of Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret aircraft design shop, is said to have made strange remarks, among them that the US already has the power to travel to the stars and \"take ET home\". Michels tells this himself on two occasions, once naming UCLA as the venue and once giving only the \"take ET home\" wording; if true, it would mean a top insider hinted at hidden technology far beyond anything public.","ben-rich-says-the-math-in-maxwell-s-equations-is-a-little-of-1990":"In the 1990s Ben Rich, a former head of Lockheed's Skunk Works, the company's secret aircraft division, said that some of the math in Maxwell's equations, the 1800s equations of electricity and magnetism, was a little off. Michels mentions it himself, without a source, as an early hint that insiders thought known physics was incomplete.","ben-rich-skunk-works-considered-burying-stealth-1970":"In the 1970s, Lockheed's secret aircraft design shop, the Skunk Works, thought about burying its stealth technology, which makes aircraft hard to see on radar, before work had even begun. A guest named Nick Cook tells Michels he heard this from Ben Rich of the Skunk Works, who said: \"we considered actually burying the whole thing.\" It shows how a major breakthrough could nearly be kept from the world.","ben-rich-we-have-the-technology-to-take-et-home":"Ben Rich, an aerospace engineer who led secret aircraft work, is said to have told an audience: \"We now have the technology to take ET home.\" Michels repeats the line in two episodes; in one he simply quotes it, and in the other he says Rich used it to close a talk at UCLA in 1993 and that several people who were there remember it. It is told because, coming from someone inside secret aircraft development, it would suggest the US had technology able to travel to other worlds.","ben-van-kerkwijk-on-the-hawara-labyrinth-and-ancient-account":"A huge labyrinth once stood at Hawara in Egypt, a claim Michels raises by pointing to a man named Ben van Kerkwijk, who had just talked about it on the show of the podcaster Joe Rogan. The account draws on the ancient writers Herodotus, the Greek historian, and Pliny the Elder, the Roman author. Michels puts it as a question to a scientist who scanned beneath the pyramids and gives no more detail.","bendix-employee-captured-by-the-tupamaros-in-uruguay-1970":"In the 1970s, an employee of Bendix, an American engineering and aerospace company, was seized from his office in Uruguay by the Tupamaros, a left-wing guerrilla group there, and was probably killed. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this, saying 'we lost a guy in Uruguay' who was 'captured by the Tupamaros right out of his office and I believe he was killed.'","bendix-field-engineering-as-a-cia-non-official-cover-front-1970":"Peter Levenda, a writer billed on the show as a CIA historian, says that in the 1970s the field engineering division of the Bendix company in Manhattan was used by the CIA as a front to send officers abroad under \"non-official cover\", meaning posing as private employees rather than as government staff. Levenda tells Michels this from his own time around the company: the division was \"legendary to us\" because \"when CIA or somebody wanted to send somebody abroad under cover\" they used Bendix field engineering. If true, a regular engineering firm doubled as a cover for spies.","benedict-gross-and-epstein-proposal-for-evolutionary-dynamic-2000":"In the 2000s, Harvard is said to have set up its work on evolutionary dynamics, linked to the scientist Martin Nowak, out of a proposal from Benedict Gross and Jeffrey Epstein. The researcher Eric Weinstein brings it up with Michels, pointing to a Harvard text that, as he quotes it, 'references an imaginative proposal by Benedict Gross and Jeffrey Epstein.' Those words credit Epstein as co-author of the proposal. What the proposal led to, and how far Epstein shaped the work, the quoted line does not say.","benjamin-franklin-and-the-french-muse-lodge":"Benjamin Franklin, the story goes, belonged to a Masonic lodge in France that was a \"muse lodge\". The researcher Diana Pasulka mentions it in passing in a conversation with Michels about UFOs and religion. She names no source and does not say what made the lodge a muse lodge or why it matters.","benjamin-libet-s-neural-timing-studies-1970":"In the 1970s and 1980s a scientist named Benjamin Libet ran studies on the timing of activity in the brain, which the writer Eric Wargo calls \"landmark studies\". Wargo raises them with Michels during a talk about synchronicities and prophetic dreams, as work that bears on how the mind experiences time. He names Libet but says nothing more about what the studies found.","bennewitz-involuntarily-committed-1980":"In the 1980s, a New Mexico man named Paul Bennewitz, who was at the centre of UFO stories there, was committed to a mental hospital against his will. The physicist John Brandenburg says it on the show, confirming plainly: \"They involuntarily committed him.\"","bennewitz-sees-craft-at-kirtland-1979":"The story is that around 1979 a man named Paul Bennewitz, who lived near Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, kept seeing strange objects over the base. Michels tells it himself: Bennewitz saw a craft \"vertically taking off and landing\" at Kirtland, and that, Michels says, is what put him on the radar of military counterintelligence people. A researcher named John Brandenburg passes on what Bennewitz told him. In his version Bennewitz saw UFOs \"a lot\" over Manzano Mountain, a mountain on the base where nuclear weapons are stored. A researcher named Greg Bishop, who has studied the case, describes \"glowing orbs\" rising off the ground somewhere on the base, flying over a mountain and coming back down. Bishop puts the sightings in 1977 and 1978, not 1979. All three tell it because, as they have it, what Bennewitz saw drew the military's attention to him, which would mean there was something over the base worth guarding.","benowitz-builds-receivers-to-intercept-base-transmissions-1979":"Around 1979 to 1981, a man named Paul Bennewitz is said to have built his own receiving equipment and started listening in on transmissions from Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico. A researcher named Greg Bishop tells Michels this on an episode about how, in its own title, aliens drove Bennewitz insane, and he gives no source beyond saying that this is apparently what happened.","benowitz-kirtland-video-of-a-saucer-with-a-psa-plane-behind-1980":"In 1980 a man named Paul Bennewitz filmed objects near Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, and in one clip, according to Greg Bishop, \"there's one of them that looks like a saucer\" with \"a PSA Pacific Southwest Airlines plane flying behind it.\" Bishop describes the footage to Michels as someone who has seen it. A known airliner in the same shot would give a sense of the object's place and size.","benowitz-photographs-an-alleged-underground-alien-base-at-ar-1980":"A man named Paul Bennewitz claimed in the early 1980s that there was an alien base underground at Archuleta Mesa in New Mexico, and went there with a camera and took pictures. A guest named Greg Bishop tells Michels what Bennewitz said and did.","benowitz-placed-in-a-mental-health-facility-1980":"In the 1980s an Albuquerque man named Paul Bennewitz, who had become consumed by beliefs about aliens, was put in a mental health facility \"for like a month or so.\" A researcher and writer named Greg Bishop tells Michels this without naming a source, in an episode about how the alien story broke Bennewitz's mind.","benowitz-s-apro-membership-application-1977":"When a man named Paul Bennewitz applied in the late 1970s to join a civilian UFO research group based in Arizona, the Aerial Phenomena Research Organization, he left one line on the form that the writer Greg Bishop finds telling. Bishop says on the show that he has seen the application. Asked for his favourite UFO book, Bennewitz wrote only \"Books by Hynek\", meaning the astronomer J. Allen Hynek, and \"that's all he said\", which Bishop takes as a sign of how new Bennewitz was to the subject at the time.","benowitz-s-drawings-of-an-alien-craft-flown-by-the-us-govern-1980":"Between 1980 and 1983, near Archuleta Mesa in New Mexico, aliens gave the US government a craft, the government flew it without knowing how, and it crashed, according to a man named Paul Bennewitz, who made drawings of it. The researcher Greg Bishop tells Michels what Bennewitz said, pointing to a document known as Project Beta. If true, the government would have been flying an alien craft it did not understand.","bentov-puharich-and-geller-together-in-tel-aviv-1970":"Photographs are said to exist that show three men together in an apartment in Tel Aviv in the 1970s: Uri Geller, an Israeli performer known for claimed psychic feats; Itzhak Bentov, an inventor; and a researcher named Puharich. The researcher Greg Malatzi brings this up on the show without naming where he saw the pictures, and he also mentions tape recordings made between Puharich and Bentov. If it holds, it would place the three men in the same room, working together.","bentov-s-mysterious-death-in-a-plane-crash-1979":"Itzhak Bentov, the author of a book called Stalking the Wild Pendulum, died in a plane crash in 1979, and Michels treats his death as cut short in a way worth noticing. Michels tells this himself without naming a source. In one episode he just says Bentov died in a plane crash. In another, about American scientists who have gone missing, he says Bentov was 55 and was killed when American Airlines Flight 191 crashed just after taking off from Chicago's O'Hare airport.","bentwaters-guards-see-multicolor-disc-retracting-a-rod-1980":"In 1980, guards at RAF Bentwaters, an air base in England, are said to have seen a large, multicolored disc quite close to them, and the story's title adds that it pulled a rod back into itself. The researcher Robert Hastings, who collects accounts of UFOs over nuclear bases, tells Michels that \"a couple of guys\" have gone on the record about it. He summarises it without naming those men or any other source.","bentwaters-nukes-flown-to-kirtland-for-analysis-1980":"Shortly after the 1980 Rendlesham Forest incident, when US airmen in England reported a strange craft near the RAF Bentwaters base, two tactical nuclear bombs are said to have been taken from a bunker there and flown aboard a C-5A cargo plane to Kirtland Air Force Base for checks. The researcher Robert Hastings, who collects accounts of UFOs at nuclear weapons sites, tells Michels he heard this from an unnamed retired colonel in charge of NATO weapons security. If true, the military feared the craft had affected the weapons.","bentwaters-rendlesham-1980-multi-witness-incident":"In 1980, at RAF Bentwaters, a base in England run jointly by the US and the Royal Air Force, several servicemen witnessed an unexplained incident, among them John Burroughs and Jim Penniston, whom Michels calls US Air Force sergeants, and Colonel Charles Halt, who investigated afterwards. Michels tells this himself on three occasions without naming a source, adding once that Halt's investigation found that \"everything checked\"; he raises it because so many military witnesses back the same event.","berkeley-parapsychology-professor-quits-mishlove-s-committee-1970":"As a graduate student at Berkeley in the 1970s, Jeffrey Mishlove began researching a man named Ted Owens, who claimed to be in telepathic contact with aliens. Mishlove says one of his parapsychology professors then quit his committee, saying \"we just can't combine these two fields.\" Mishlove, who later earned his PhD, tells Michels this from his own time there.","bermuda-bar-a-stranger-s-birthday-unlocks-a-woman-s-phone":"A man named Chris Ramsay says that in a bar in Bermuda he told a stranger to type his own birthday into a woman's locked phone, and the phone opened. Ramsay tells Michels he was there: \"I said, 'Um, put in your birthday.' And he puts in his birthday and the phone opens.\" He tells it as a strange coincidence, or more, that he can't explain.","bermuda-triangle-anomalies":"The stretch of Atlantic known as the Bermuda Triangle is said to show real, measurable anomalies. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels, \"there's data there's evidence of anomalies\" there, speaking as someone who knows the Navy's view of the oceans. On another episode Michels repeats what a former NASA engineer told him: that \"there is a little patch around the Bermuda Triangle\" with \"just tons of electromagnetic anomalies going on.\" Gallaudet speaks of evidence in general terms, while the engineer's version is specifically electromagnetic. If true, it would give an old legend something physical behind it.","bermuda-triangle-compass-deviations-from-ancient-meteorites":"Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor, thinks the compass problems people report in the Bermuda Triangle, a stretch of ocean off Florida famous for stories of lost ships and planes, may come from metal meteorites that \"landed under the ocean floor\" in \"ancient ancient ancient times.\" He tells Michels it is his \"personal belief,\" which would give the area's strange reputation a natural cause.","beta-israel-tradition-of-bringing-the-ark-via-elephantine":"The Beta Israel, the Jewish community of Ethiopia, hold that their ancestors fled Israel carrying the Ark of the Covenant and brought it to Ethiopia, the writer Graham Hancock tells Michels, by way of an island in the Nile, Elephantine, where they kept it, and later to Lake Tana. Hancock summarises the tradition in one conversation, and in another he says the Beta Israel (also called Falashas) told him directly that the Ark stayed on the Nile island for several hundred years. He links it to the Kebra Nagast, an old Ethiopian book; if the tradition is right, the lost Ark went south into Africa.","bettis-lab-naval-nuclear-power-and-western-pa-ufo-sightings-1950":"Bettis, a research lab near Pittsburgh, was the first to develop nuclear power for the Navy in the 1950s, and a guest named Dylan Borland ties it to the many UFO sightings in western Pennsylvania, including those around the town he calls Kexsburg. Borland makes the link to Michels without naming a source. His point is that the sightings cluster near secret nuclear work.","betty-and-barney-hill-abduction-and-the-outer-limits-episode-1961":"The story is that in 1961 a New Hampshire couple, Betty and Barney Hill, said they were taken aboard a craft by beings with eyes that wrapped around their heads. Michels calls it the classic, and perhaps first, US abduction case. He adds that the Hills were an interracial couple and civil liberties activists, and that Barney reluctantly said sperm samples were taken from him. He also mentions that Betty mapped stars she said were the beings' home, which looked like a binary system near Zeta Reticuli. Michels raises a doubt from the scholar Diana Pasulka's book American Cosmic: the wraparound eyes may have come, without the Hills knowing it, from an episode of the CBS science fiction show The Outer Limits. The researcher Jacques Vallee notes that Betty's story began when she saw one star too many in the Big Dipper. A writer named Jason Jorjani says Barney first described his captors as looking like Nazis, and the show plays audio of Barney under hypnosis saying the figure \"looks like a German Nazi.\" A researcher named Clas Svahn adds that the beings examined Barney's false teeth. If true, it would be one of the earliest detailed accounts of humans being taken and examined.","betty-and-barney-hill-abduction-possibly-done-by-humans-1961":"Betty and Barney Hill were a New Hampshire couple who said they were abducted in 1961, and a researcher named Steven Greer tells Michels the abduction was very likely carried out by humans, not by visitors from elsewhere. He reasons that the way it happened does not fit what he calls interstellar, transdimensional technology, and he cites no source for that. If he is right, one of the best-known abduction stories would have been a human operation dressed up to look alien.","betty-and-barney-hill-told-the-air-force-and-the-psychologis-1961":"Betty Hill and Barney Hill, a New Hampshire couple who reported a UFO encounter in 1961, are said to have told the Air Force one version of what happened and a psychologist another. A YouTuber and researcher named Chris Ramsay brings this up with Michels as an example, without naming a source. It matters because it would suggest that famous accounts like theirs shifted depending on who was listening.","betty-andreasson-luca-told-that-nordic-angels-brought-christ-1960":"In the 1960s, a woman named Betty Andreasson Luca is said to have been taken from her family home to a place underground or underwater by tall, angel-like beings of the kind UFO writers call \"Nordics\", human-looking and fair. According to the philosopher Jason Jorjani, the beings told her \"they brought Christ into the world\" and that they would \"bring him back again soon\". Jorjani tells Michels this as part of his case that nonhuman beings shaped human religion, and he summarises it without naming a source.","betty-hill-s-1950s-crash-debris-buried-in-her-backyard":"A woman named Betty Hill is said to have seen something cross the sky and crash in the 1950s, after which she and a relative went out into the field and brought back debris, which by this account ended up buried in her backyard in Portsmouth, New Hampshire. A Swedish researcher named Clas Svahn says Hill told him this herself, and a recording of Hill played on the show has her saying she was visiting family in Maine, sitting on the porch on a warm night, when they saw \"what we thought at that time was a plane\".","beverly-hovering-metallic-object-1966":"In Beverly, Massachusetts, in 1966, nine witnesses, two of them police officers, saw a metallic, plate-like object. A writer named Marik von Rennenkampff gives the case as an example on the show during a debate with Mick West, a well-known UFO skeptic, and he names no source. He uses it as the kind of sighting that has several witnesses, some of them officials, and that is hard to explain away.","beyond-skinwalker-investigation-at-bradshaw-ranch":"At Bradshaw Ranch near Sedona, Arizona, \"some strange things came up on the cameras\" during an investigation the owner of Skinwalker Ranch calls Beyond Skinwalker. Skinwalker Ranch is a property famous for reports of strange happenings. The owner tells Michels he went to Bradshaw Ranch himself, and he names a Dr. Taylor and a witness, Erik Bard, in connection with the work.","bezos-s-grandfather-lawrence-gise-ran-the-aec-s-western-divi-1960":"Jeff Bezos's grandfather, Lawrence Preston Gise, is said to have been director of the Western Division of the Atomic Energy Commission, the agency that ran US nuclear work, in the 1960s. A guest named Matthew Pines tells Michels this, saying a man named Harold Malmgren told him the story. If true, it would tie Bezos's family to the nuclear establishment.","bial-foundation-as-the-biggest-funder-of-parapsychology":"The biggest funder of parapsychology anywhere is the Bial Foundation in Portugal, according to the researcher Rupert Sheldrake, and he says about half of its grants go to the field. Sheldrake tells Michels this as something he knows from his own work, without pointing to a source.","biefeld-affidavit-confirming-anomalous-effect-1920":"The inventor Townsend Brown really did work with the physicist Paul Alfred Biefeld on a strange electrical effect that seemed to push objects, even though critics say the two never met. Paul Schatzkin, who wrote about Brown's life, says a sworn statement from Biefeld came out later and settles it: \"Paul Biefeld definitely met Townsend Brown.\" If so, the story behind Brown's claimed discovery holds up.","biefeld-brown-effect-and-townsend-brown":"The Biefeld-Brown effect, an effect named partly after an inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown in which electrically charged equipment appears to produce a push, is counted by Michels among real discoveries that point beyond ordinary physics. He brings it up himself and says he is obsessed with Brown.","biefeld-brown-vacuum-tests-1990-air-force-and-1956-paris":"Michels says that in 1990 the Air Force tested the Biefeld-Brown effect, a claimed thrust from high-voltage electrical devices named after the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, inside a vacuum, but used only 19 kilovolts instead of the far higher voltages Brown had used. He tells it himself, in an episode where the researcher Jacques Cornillon is cited, and contrasts it with Brown's own vacuum tests in Paris in 1956. His point is that the Air Force test was too weak to rule the effect out.","big-black-triangle-hovers-over-vandenberg-runway":"A huge black triangular craft is said to have come in from the Pacific and hovered over Vandenberg Air Force Base in California. Jeff Natelli, who guarded nuclear missiles there, says he saw to it himself. He tells Michels that on 14 October 2003 he arrived on the scene and found five shaken witnesses. They described \"a massive triangular craft larger than a football field\" that \"hovered silently for about 45 seconds over their entry control point,\" the gate where people enter, \"before shooting away at impossible speed.\" A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown repeats the story on another episode, crediting Natelli and describing \"a big black triangle coming out of the Pacific Ocean flying into Vandenberg.\" In Brown's version the craft is over the base's runway, while Natelli puts it over the gate. If true, it would mean something nobody could identify moved freely over one of America's most closely guarded military sites.","big-meteor-over-aland-the-day-after-the-june-trip-2025":"In June 2025, the day after his team's trip, a big meteor was seen over Åland, the islands between Sweden and Finland, and over the central Baltic Sea. The treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, whose team searches a strange object on the Baltic seafloor, tells Michels it was on the news. He brings it up as a striking piece of timing.","bigelow-aerospace-shut-down-during-covid-bigelow-turns-from-2020":"Bigelow Aerospace, the space company of the Nevada businessman Robert Bigelow, was shut down by the governor of Nevada during the COVID crisis, and Bigelow then turned from studying UAP, the official term for UFOs, to research on whether people survive death. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this, citing Bigelow himself: 'his aerospace company had been shut down by the governor of Nevada during the COVID crisis.'","bigelow-buys-the-ranch-and-starts-nids-monitoring-1996":"Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange events, is said to have been bought by Robert Bigelow in 1996, after the Sherman family's story of what happened there was brought to his attention. Bigelow then had his research group, NIDS, the National Institute for Discovery Science, monitor the place for years. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's current owner, tells Michels how this happened, naming the Shermans and a man named Zack Van Eyk in the chain of people who brought the story to Bigelow.","bigelow-crashes-in-russia-china-south-america-seeded-around":"The businessman Bob Bigelow is said to believe that alien craft have also crashed in Russia, China and South America, and that \"they're seeding them around the world.\" The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels that a man named Timothy Taylor asked Bigelow whether other countries had had crashes and got this answer, which would mean crashed craft are being left for many nations to find.","bigelow-refuses-to-return-negative-experiences-hit-his-famil":"The businessman Robert Bigelow, who once owned Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, refuses to go back there because he had such bad experiences that they affected his family. The ranch's current owner tells Michels this, drawing on Bigelow and on a man named George Knapp. If true, whatever goes on at the ranch would follow people home.","bigelow-s-22-million-program-steered-by-colonel-alexander-2008":"Around 2008, the claim goes, $22 million of government money went to Bigelow Aerospace, a company owned by the businessman Robert Bigelow, to research UFOs, with the help of Senator Harry Reid. A guest named Steven Greer tells Michels that a retired Army colonel named John B. Alexander, whom he calls Bigelow's \"consigliere\", was behind how the whole arrangement was set up. Michels tells it himself without the money or Alexander: Bigelow, he says, worked with Reid, then the Senate Majority Leader, \"in an official government capacity\" to reverse engineer UFOs, meaning to take apart recovered craft to learn how they work. If true, it would mean a private company was doing the government's UFO research with public money.","bigelow-s-desert-alien-encounter-meet-us-in-space":"Robert Bigelow, a Nevada businessman, is said to have founded his aerospace company because he met aliens in the desert and they told him to meet them in space. Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, tells Michels he heard this from an unnamed doctor. If true, a private space company was started on instructions from non-human visitors.","bigelow-says-aliens-are-walking-among-us":"A man named Robert Bigelow says in interviews that aliens are walking among us, according to Michels. Michels brings it up himself, pointing to those interviews without naming any one of them. If Bigelow meant it, the claim is that non-human beings are already living unnoticed among people.","bigelow-tells-fox-they-re-walking-among-us-2009":"In Las Vegas in 2009, a man Fox names as Bigelow is said to have told him plainly that non-human beings are already here: \"Uh, yeah, and they're walking among us.\" James Fox, who says he heard it himself, tells Michels the story and links it to his film I Know What I Saw. If true, someone close to the subject was saying outright that visitors live unseen among people.","bigelow-to-receive-ufo-material-from-lockheed-martin-transfe-2008":"The billionaire Robert Bigelow was supposed to get UFO material from the arms maker Lockheed Martin in 2008, and the transfer was blocked by a man named Glenn Gaffney, according to Michels. He mentions it in passing, without naming a source; if true, it would mean a private company holds such material and that someone stopped it from being handed over.","bigfoot-rumours-in-d-sector-1964":"A former nuclear missile guard named Richard Barth says that in 1964, in D sector at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California, the guards scared new recruits with stories of Bigfoot, but no one talked about UFOs. Barth tells Michels he served there himself: the joke was that \"Bigfoot's out there and he's going to get you, but there was nothing about UFOs or even talk about it that I know.\" His point is that UFOs were not on anyone's mind before what he says happened to him later.","bill-casey-never-raised-ufos-1980":"John S. Herrington, who served as Secretary of Energy in Ronald Reagan's cabinet, says that in the 1980s the CIA director Bill Casey never brought up UFOs with him. He tells Michels from his own time in government: \"This subject never came up and if it was on Bill's agenda, he would have talked to me about it.\" He offers it as a sign that UFOs were not a matter at the top of that administration.","bill-clinton-s-tar-baby-remark-to-rockefeller-at-the-ranch-1995":"In 1995, at the Wyoming ranch of the wealthy philanthropist Laurance Rockefeller, President Bill Clinton is said to have told Rockefeller that the UFO question was \"like the story of the tar baby,\" the old folk tale of a figure that sticks to whoever touches it. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would mean a sitting president saw the subject as something he dared not get stuck to.","bill-colby-s-death-before-delivering-a-zero-point-device-1996":"A man named William Colby, who had been \"read into\" the secret, meaning given access to it, tried to get a zero-point device, a machine said to draw energy from empty space, to a man named Steven Greer and his group, and died in 1996 in a river south of Washington before it reached them. Greer tells Michels this as someone directly involved, and in his telling the timing of the death is the point.","bill-gunston-article-b-2-has-electrogravitic-boost-1990":"The story is that an aviation writer named Bill Gunston published an article arguing that the B-2 stealth bomber could not fly as well as it does by ordinary means. The suggestion is that it gets an extra push from electrogravitics, a claimed way of moving aircraft with powerful electric fields. The aviation journalist Nick Cook tells Michels about \"this astounding article\": when you work through the B-2's weight and engines, it \"would have been incapable of the performance figures credited for it.\" Cook credits it to Air International magazine. In another telling Cook says only that the performance data \"does not add up\" and does not mention electrogravitics. Michels gives it a sharper edge. He says Gunston was \"very sure\" that the work of the researcher Townsend Brown on electric propulsion \"made it in the B2\", and he dates the article to 2000, where the story places it in the 1990s. If true, it would mean anti-gravity-style technology is already flying in an American bomber.","bill-hamilton-feeds-witnesses-to-greer-s-disclosure-project-1997":"In 1997, a UFO researcher named William Hamilton III, known as Bill Hamilton and the author of a book called Cosmic Top Secret, was in touch with Steven Greer, a UFO activist whose Disclosure Project gathered witnesses to speak about UFOs, and he passed witnesses on to him. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb says this on two episodes without naming a source, and gives one example: Hamilton handing Greer a firsthand witness he calls only Brad S. If true, some of Greer's witnesses came to him through an older researcher's contacts.","bill-holden-kennedy-on-air-force-one-says-ufos-are-out-of-hi-1963":"President John F. Kennedy is said to have told the chief steward on Air Force One, a man named Bill Holden, that UFOs were real but out of his control: \"I know this is true and I know these are real, but the whole matter is out of my hands and I don't know why.\" Steven Greer, a longtime campaigner for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, tells Michels this as something Holden passed on. The story puts it on a flight to Berlin in 1963, but in one version Greer names no trip and only says Kennedy said \"the same thing\" Eisenhower had said. If true, it would mean even a sitting president had no say over the secret.","bill-lear-cut-john-lear-out-of-his-will-1970":"Bill Lear, the aviation businessman, is said to have written his son John Lear, a pilot, out of his will in the 1970s. Bob Lazar, a man who says he once worked on recovered alien craft in Nevada, tells Michels he saw this himself: \"John showed me his will, you know, every paragraph.\"","bill-lear-figuring-out-gravity-control-1956":"The aviation inventor Bill Lear, the man behind the Learjet, the first private jet, was said in 1956 to be working on gravity control. Michels quotes a 1956 article in Jane's Defence Weekly as saying that \"William Lear, the autopilot wizard, is already figuring out gravity control for the weightless craft to come.\" In a later episode Michels tells it differently, as Lear himself saying that he believed anti-gravity was possible because UFOs display it, which, if true, would put a well-known engineer on record linking UFOs to new propulsion.","bill-moore-admits-sowing-disinformation-at-mufon-conference":"A UFO researcher named Bill Moore is said to have stood up at a conference of MUFON, a large civilian UFO research group, and admitted he had been working for the government to spread false information among UFO researchers. The physicist John Brandenburg says he was in the audience. He tells Michels that Moore, whom he calls Stan Moore and says first leaked the MJ-12 papers (documents describing a secret committee of officials said to handle crashed saucers, one of them dated 1952), \"confessed to being a government agent whose job was to monitor.\" Brandenburg ties that monitoring to a man named Paul Bennewitz, who was the target of an operation to deceive him. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, is the government's newer word for UFOs) summarises it without a source: Moore said he \"was instructed to sow disinformation into the community.\" The UFO historian Richard Dolan dates it to the late 1980s and says Moore admitted he helped \"mess up\" Bennewitz. So one teller says he saw it himself and two repeat it as known history. If true, it would mean the government had planted people inside the UFO research world to muddy the picture.","bill-moore-did-work-for-the-cia-1960":"A UFO researcher named Bill Moore did jobs for the CIA without ever being on its staff. The researcher Grant Cameron says Moore told him so himself, putting it carefully: \"I didn't work for them, I did some work for them.\"","bill-moore-interviewed-t-townsend-brown-1970":"A UFO researcher named Bill Moore is said to have once sat down and interviewed a man named Thomas Townsend Brown, sometime between the 1970s and the late 1980s. The researcher Greg Bishop mentions it in passing on the show, without saying where he learned it, while discussing the book The Cosmic Conspiracy. He does not say what was asked or what Brown answered, only that the interview took place, which would put Moore in direct contact with Brown.","bill-moore-s-spy-training-and-soviet-postcard-relays-1980":"In the early 1980s, a UFO researcher named Bill Moore was taken in by government handlers who trained him in spycraft and had him passing mail along and doing things involving Soviet spies. That is how a guest named Greg Bishop tells it to Michels, repeating what Moore told him. If true, it would mean intelligence officials were using a UFO researcher as their own operative.","bill-moore-warns-steinman-off-mrs-wang-1980":"Sometime in the 1980s, a researcher named William Steinman was talking to a Mrs. Wang, linked to a man named Eric Henry Wang, when he got a phone call from a man named Bill Moore, who asked, \"What are you talking to Mrs. Wang for?\" The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this, passing on what he was told. The point is that someone seemed to know right away who Steinman was talking to, as if he were being watched.","bill-napier-links-the-younger-dryas-impact-to-the-taurids":"The idea is that a global disaster at the start of the Younger Dryas, a sudden return to ice-age cold about 12,800 years ago, was caused by something from the Taurids, a stream of comet debris that Earth crosses every year. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels he believes it was first proposed by an astronomer named Bill Napier, \"over in the astrophysical observatory in England\". Michels has retold it on three other episodes, crediting Carlson and the writer Graham Hancock and not mentioning Napier, and he describes it most often as a comet bursting in the air. In one episode he calls the impactor an asteroid that \"hit the earth\", and while he usually dates it to about 12,800 years ago, once he puts it around 9,600 BC.","bill-ray-s-remote-viewing-of-buddha-1980":"Sometime in the 1980s, a man named Bill Ray was given the Buddha as his target in remote viewing, the claimed practice of using the mind alone to describe a distant or hidden person, place or thing, and he found it life-changing. The story comes from Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant who talks about the remote viewing unit. He tells Michels it second-hand, as something he knows about Ray, and gives no more detail than that \"they had him remote view Buddha\" and it changed him. If true, it would mean the unit pointed its viewers at religious figures as well as military targets.","bill-richardson-siphoning-us-nuclear-secrets-1990":"In the 1990s, Bill Richardson, a Secretary of Energy close to the Clintons, was \"systematically siphoning American nuclear secrets\", according to Michels, who says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about Bob Lazar, a man who claims to have worked on alien craft. If true, it would mean the official in charge of the nation's nuclear labs was leaking from them.","bill-sweetman-s-1980s-look-at-sap-compartmentalization-and-d":"In the 1980s, after Reagan became president, a man named Bill Sweetman took a deep look at special access programs, or SAPs: highly restricted classified projects that are kept walled off from one another. What he looked at was how these programs were split into compartments and how some of them duplicated each other. A guest named Eric W. Davis brings this up to Michels from memory, hedging that he thinks it was Sweetman, during a talk about whether a US president was briefed on UFOs.","bill-todman-jr-touched-a-living-ufo-in-a-teton-meadow":"A man named Bill Todman Jr. was climbing in the Tetons, the Wyoming mountain range, when he came up into a meadow and found a UFO \"just sitting right hovering right there.\" By the story's title he touched it and took it to be alive. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells this to Michels as something he heard from Todman himself, and he also names two other men, Bob Rehme and Mason Neufeld, while telling it. If it were true, it would be a close, physical encounter with a craft that seemed to be a living thing.","bill-uhouse-kingman-based-simulator-and-j-rod-2003":"At outlying sites around Area 51, the secret Nevada test base, there was a simulator of an alien craft built from the wreck of the Kingman crash, a UFO crash said to have happened near Kingman, Arizona. That was the claim of a man named Bill Uhouse, and a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb repeats it to Michels. If true, it would mean engineers were training people to fly a recovered craft.","billy-meier-s-early-photos-show-the-sport-model-1970":"Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S4, believes that Billy Meier's first photos, taken in the 1970s, are \"100% genuine.\" Meier is a man who photographed what he said were alien craft. Lazar tells Michels the craft in those pictures looks like the one he calls the \"sport model,\" one of the craft he says he saw. If so, the same kind of craft would have been photographed in public years before Lazar came forward.","billy-meier-s-faked-photos-including-dinosaur-from-a-book":"Some photographs put out by a Swiss man named Billy Meier were faked, among them a picture of a dinosaur copied from a book, according to a UFO researcher named Clas Svahn. Svahn tells Michels he has that very book at home, which would show where the image came from.","billy-meier-s-past-as-a-middle-east-intelligence-operative-1970":"Before he became known for his UFO claims, a man named Billy Meier was a field intelligence agent in the Middle East, working in places like Tehran and Turkey, and he picked up the name \"Billy\" in Tehran. That is what the philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels, in two episodes: \"The guy was 007. He was a James Bond style super spy field intelligence operative.\" Jorjani gives no source by name beyond crediting a work called Closer Encounters. The story places this in the 1970s, though Jorjani gives no years in one telling, and if true it would cast Meier's later claims in a very different light.","billy-meier-s-pleiadians-1970":"In the 1970s a Swiss man named Billy Meier said he was in contact with kindly beings from the Pleiades, a star cluster, who \"were going to come to save us all.\" The philosopher Jason Jorjani mentions Meier's story to Michels in passing, without naming a source, as an example of alien contact stories that promise rescue from above.","binary-dead-star-drives-the-great-year-and-civilizational-re":"The researcher Matt LaCroix says the Sun has a dead companion star, and that the two circling each other is the \"entire reason\" for the Great Year, an ancient idea of a long cosmic cycle of ages, and for the times when civilizations have been wiped out and started over. He says NASA has kept this quiet, and he points to the New Science Encyclopedia of 1987. He gives no other source. Elsewhere in the same talk he adds that the cycle shows up in ice cores drilled in Antarctica and Greenland, and that the companion is drawing near now, \"we're right in it right now\", though there he does not mention NASA. If true, it would mean another reset could be close.","biologics-as-soft-tissue-drones-in-conscious-craft":"The beings said to be found with crashed craft, which people in this field call \"biologics,\" might not be pilots at all but \"something like soft tissue drones,\" living tools run by craft that are themselves conscious. Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he flew a UFO retrieval mission, passes this idea on to Michels from a man he names only as Dave. If it were right, the bodies would be parts of the craft rather than the ones flying it.","biondi-and-malanga-s-2022-sar-claim-of-eight-cylindrical-str":"In 2022 a small team led by an Italian radar specialist named Filippo Biondi and his engineering colleague Corrado Malanga claimed that satellite radar scans showed eight hollow, tube-shaped structures, about 20 meters wide, deep beneath the Khafre pyramid at Giza. Michels tells it himself, calling it one of the boldest claims in archaeology in a century, and returns to it in a later episode; if true, it would make the question of how and why the pyramids were built far stranger.","biondi-s-patents-on-the-method-a-second-one-being-filed-in-t-2020":"A researcher named Filippo Biondi, who uses radar from satellites to look beneath Egypt's pyramids, says he holds a patent on his method and is now filing a second one in the United States. Biondi tells Michels this himself: \"we have a patent... Now I am also submitting a second patent.\" He brings it up to show that his technique for seeing what he calls a vast structure under the pyramids is formally protected.","bishop-carl-stanley-tips-the-fbi-about-ferrie-and-martin-1963":"In 1963, a Kentucky bishop named Carl Stanley is said to have told the FBI to \"look at these two guys in New Orleans\", meaning David Ferrie and Jack Martin, because they were \"involved in the assassination\". The researcher Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming his source, and if it were true it would mean the FBI had an early tip pointing at the two men.","bishop-mcintyre-at-edwards-afb-in-1954-during-rumoured-eisen":"Michels says real records place a Bishop McIntyre hanging around Edwards Air Force Base in California in 1954, the year President Eisenhower is rumoured to have made a deal with aliens there. He stresses that this is \"not even MJ-12\", a set of papers of disputed origin about a secret UFO group, but genuine records, though he does not say which. He raises it himself, in an episode on secret societies and the UFO cover-up, as a hint that a churchman was close by when the rumoured deal took place.","bismuth-magnesium-layers-could-shrink-terahertz-waveguides":"The physicist Hal Puthoff says a layered bismuth and magnesium piece, said to come from the Roswell crash, has thin channels that guide electromagnetic waves (waveguides) at sizes \"well below wavelength,\" which he calls \"quite an accomplishment.\" Puthoff tells it first-hand, saying he checked the piece himself. Michels, relaying Puthoff, puts it a little differently, saying the material could \"microize\" such waveguides for terahertz frequencies, a very high band of radio waves, which would make it advanced engineering if true.","bismuth-magnesium-sample-said-to-be-from-roswell-found-in-an-1947":"A small metal sample, made of bismuth and magnesium in layers said to be thinner than a human hair, is said to come from Roswell, New Mexico, where in 1947 the military announced it had recovered a crashed \"flying disc\". Michels tells the story as he heard it from the physicist Hal Puthoff: an army officer going through his grandfather's archives found the sample, and the grandfather's diary said it was a piece from Roswell. Puthoff himself tells it a little differently, saying the piece was sent anonymously by someone claiming to be an army officer, and Michels says he saw the sample with the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan, adding that nobody could find evidence that anyone had ever made such a thing, which, if true, would point to a crashed craft no one on Earth built.","bismuth-recurring-at-s4-1988":"Bismuth, a heavy metal, kept coming up for Bob Lazar at S4, Michels says. Lazar is a man who says he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, in the late 1980s. Michels tells it himself, saying \"there's a reason\" for it, and in a later episode says more loosely that bismuth \"comes up a lot\" in UFO lore.","bissell-mentions-the-1933-magenta-crash":"The story is that a flying disc crashed near Magenta, in the Lombardy region of Italy, in 1933, and that Mussolini's government took it. Harold Malmgren, a former presidential advisor, tells Michels that Richard Bissell told him about it. Michels describes Bissell as the CIA's number two and the founder of Area 51. Malmgren says Bissell told him \"this didn't start last week\" and mentioned \"1933 Magenta.\" David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned UFO whistleblower, tells it himself. He calls it \"the first recovery in Europe\", says a partially intact vehicle was found, and says the Italian government kept it at a secure air base until 1944 or 1945. Michels, retelling Grusch and others, says the disc was 10 to 12 meters across, came down in June 1933, and was covered by a Fascist press blackout. He also says it passed through the Vatican and was moved to the US under Franklin Roosevelt. In one episode he places the crash, unsure, near Bologna, and in another he calls the whole account hypothetical. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, points to a new book by a researcher named Roberto Pinotti, which he says documents the crash. He also says Mussolini put the radio inventor Guglielmo Marconi in charge of studying it. If true, the hidden recovery of crashed craft would go back to before World War II.","bissell-places-area-51-at-the-nevada-nuclear-test-site-1955":"A man named Richard Bissell founded Area 51, the secret air base in Nevada, in 1955, and deliberately placed it at the Nevada test site, where hundreds of atomic bombs were being tested in the early 1950s, so the area would already be sealed off. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with a former presidential adviser named Harald Malmgren.","bissell-returns-to-the-cia-after-his-ouster-1961":"When Richard Bissell, a senior CIA official, was pushed out in 1961, the rumor was that he left on a Friday and was back at work on Monday because he was too essential to certain projects. Michels repeats this rumor himself, without a source, in an episode asking whether Henry Kissinger oversaw a program to recover crashed craft, implying Bissell's real work was secret.","black-ball-darting-around-the-security-truck-1977":"A former missile site guard named Mario Woods says that in 1977, at a nuclear launch site called November-5 near Nisland, South Dakota, a black ball \"about six times the size of a beach ball\", which seemed to have vents on it, came out of nowhere and darted around his security truck. Woods tells it as his own experience, with a man named Michael Johnson, and in a later telling gives its size as four to six feet across.","black-ball-of-no-light-formed-by-the-emitter-1987":"Bob Lazar says he watched a colleague, Barry Castilio, work a device called an emitter at S4, the secret Nevada site where Lazar says he worked on alien craft in the late 1980s. According to Lazar, it made \"a little black ball in the air where no light was escaping, looking like a little black hole.\" He tells this first-hand on two episodes, and in the second he adds that the ball appeared when the emitter was rotated.","black-sites-staffed-by-refugees-who-didn-t-make-the-p3-cut":"The secret facilities known as black sites are run by their own hand-picked people, and the janitors, cooks and maintenance staff are refugees from other countries who applied to a programme he calls P3 and did not make the cut. Michael Herrera, a guest who says he saw a huge craft in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels this as something he was told. He does not explain P3 or name who told him.","black-suvs-helicopters-and-a-30-ft-hole-near-the-crash-site-1999":"A man named Brett Lanner says that in 1999 at Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, a place where a UFO with alien bodies is said to have crashed, he saw black SUVs and helicopters and came across a round hole about 30 feet deep and 30 feet wide. Lanner tells Michels he found it himself: \"we came across a hole and it was about 30 ft deep, 30 ft in diameter, kind of like a circular hole.\" If true, someone was still digging at the crash site decades later.","black-widow-night-fighters-redeployed-to-patrol-around-roswe-1947":"In 1947, the US military is said to have moved Black Widow planes, a World War II night fighter, to patrol the skies around Roswell, New Mexico, the town where a craft is said to have crashed that year. The researcher John Brandenburg tells Michels this as something he dug up himself (\"what I found out was that we had a night fighter during World War II called the Black Widow\"), and he credits a work called Cosmic Awakening. If true, it would suggest the military was guarding the skies over Roswell as if it expected more visitors.","blacked-out-blue-van-to-an-underground-training-school-1990":"Dan Sherman, an Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that between 1990 and 1993 he was driven in an unmarked blue van with blacked-out windows from a facility to an underground training school near the NSA complex in Maryland. He tells Michels this as something he went through himself, linking it to an officer he names as Captain White: \"It was a blue unmarked van that had black windows. It drove to the school.\"","blacked-out-bus-ride-south-to-s-4-1988":"A man named Bob Lazar says that in 1988 or 1989 he was taken by bus from Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada, south to another site called S-4, on a \"long bumpy ride\" with the bus windows blacked out. Lazar tells Michels this as something he went through himself, and the blacked-out windows suggest the site's location was meant to stay hidden even from people working there.","blacker-than-black-floating-sphere":"A man who served in special forces says that while he was overseas during the wars, he was brought into a room where a sphere \"blacker than black\" hovered in the air, and that it gave off \"a sense of just complete evil and darkness.\" Congressman Eric Burlison tells Michels the story second-hand, as the man told it to him, and he does not name the man or say where or when it happened. If true, it would mean the military holds, or has met, something it can neither explain nor feel safe around.","blacker-than-black-holes-above-the-insulator-ring-1988":"Bob Lazar says that in 1988 and 1989, at a secret site in Nevada he calls S-4, he saw holes above an insulator ring on a piece of equipment, holes so black they \"almost look like a bottomless pits,\" though he knew they were solid. Lazar tells Michels this as something he saw with his own eyes, and he mentions a man named Barry as he tells it. If true, he was handling a material unlike anything ordinary.","blackhawk-near-miss-with-low-flying-f-4-phantom":"A former Army helicopter pilot named Greg Rogers, introduced on the show as a NASA doctor, says that while flying a Blackhawk helicopter in Germany near the Rhine valley, he nearly collided with an F-4 Phantom fighter jet flying very low. Rogers tells Michels he was flying it himself: \"Jesus, we nearly died because of what that F4 pilot had done.\" He tells it as a brush with death caused by another pilot's recklessness.","blanchard-boy-did-we-screw-up-1947":"After the 1947 events at Roswell Army Air Field in New Mexico, where the military at first announced it had recovered a crashed \"flying disc\" and then changed its story, a colonel there named Blanchard is said to have only laughed when asked about it and said, \"Boy, did we screw up.\" The scientist John Brandenburg tells this to Michels as something he heard from veterans of the Roswell base. If true, it would mean a senior officer admitted in private that the base's handling of the affair had gone wrong.","bledsoe-and-grusch-videos-throttled-on-youtube-2023":"A representative from Google told the podcaster Danny Jones that two videos broke no YouTube rules and raised no internal red flags he could see: Jones's interview with a man named Bledsoe, and Michels's own interview with David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who told Congress in 2023 that the US holds craft not made by humans. Jones tells Michels this while explaining why he thinks the videos were held back from viewers anyway. If true, it would mean the videos were suppressed for reasons outside the platform's stated rules.","bledsoe-drawing-cell-diagrams-for-taylor-s-team-2008":"Between 2008 and 2011 in North Carolina, a carpenter named Chris Bledsoe says he spent his nights reading scientific papers sent to him by a team linked to a man named Timothy Taylor, and his job was to draw cell diagrams, \"whatever came out of me, whatever I saw.\" Bledsoe tells Michels this as something he did himself, and he also names Diana Pasulka in connection with it. If true, it would mean researchers were treating an untrained man's visions as a source of scientific insight.","bledsoe-group-films-moving-dots-weeks-later-2020":"A group around a man named Chris Bledsoe, who says he is in contact with the beings behind UFOs, went back to the lawn of the US Capitol a few weeks after an earlier visit, did \"the exact same thing\" again, and this time filmed dots moving in the sky. Eric Burlison, a congressman who was there the first time but not the second, tells Michels what the group told him. To him it suggests that whatever happened the first time could be repeated.","bledsoe-memoir-names-taylor-as-a-nasa-insider-2010":"Chris Bledsoe is a North Carolina man described as a \"super experiencer\", meaning someone who reports repeated encounters with UFOs and strange beings. According to Michels, Bledsoe's memoir openly names a man called Timothy Taylor as a NASA insider who befriended him after those encounters. Michels brings this up himself, citing the memoir, to back up another woman's account he has just discussed, since it would put a NASA figure close to such experiences.","bledsoe-prophecy-of-missiles-fired-at-iran-stopped-by-entiti-2026":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had repeated UFO encounters, says a being he calls the Lady warned him in 2012 of a future moment when Iran and Israel would fire missiles at each other, and that orbs would appear when it happened. He says the sign would come when Regulus, a blue star, looks red on the horizon at dawn \"in the gaze of the Sphinx,\" and that he wrote the prophecy down in his book UFO of God. A man named Bob McGwier relays a version he heard from Bledsoe, in which Bledsoe \"sees missiles being fired at the country of Iran\" and the missiles are stopped by these beings, which would mean something outside human control steps into human wars.","bledsoe-s-craft-bigger-inside-than-outside":"A North Carolina man named Chris Bledsoe says he saw a craft that was about 30 feet across on the outside, but when he looked inside it seemed as big as a football field. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron repeats this to Michels as Bledsoe tells it. If true, it would mean the craft bends space in a way known physics cannot.","bledsoe-told-that-rockets-fail-because-they-carry-weapons-2007":"Around 2007 a man named Chris Bledsoe, who says UFOs are drawn to him, asked whether certain rockets that kept failing carried weapons, was told yes, and answered that this was why they weren't working: \"They don't want it to work,\" meaning the beings behind the UFOs. A guest named Chris Ramsay retells this as Bledsoe told it to him. On his own episode Bledsoe credits a man named Timothy Taylor and puts it differently: every weapon-carrying launch \"attracts them and they'll come up and look at it,\" with nothing said about making the rockets fail.","bledsoe-tries-to-call-in-uap-on-capitol-lawn-2020":"Sometime in the 2020s, a man named Chris Bledsoe tried to call in UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) on the lawn of the US Capitol, while a congressman named Eric Burlison sat beside him. Burlison tells Michels he was there: Bledsoe \"did his thing\" and Burlison \"politely quietly sat and waited.\" If it worked, it would mean a man could summon these objects at will, in the heart of Washington.","blessed-animal-bladders-hung-on-the-fence-line-2016":"In 2016, Native Americans were hanging animal bladders along the fence line of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange happenings, and blessing them. Brandon Fugal, presented on the show as the ranch's owner, tells Michels this as something he knows first-hand, and he credits the point to security staff he calls Bigelow security. If true, the local people treated the ranch's border as something that needed spiritual protection.","blink-182-bandmate-had-intelligence-community-ties":"Another member of the rock band Blink-182 had family members in the intelligence community, according to a guest named Jay Anderson. Anderson tells Michels this as something that traces back to Tom DeLonge, the band's guitarist, who later went into UFO research. If true, it would put intelligence ties close to the band from the start.","blitch-backs-randy-anderson-s-crane-story-after-grusch-intro":"A scientist named John Blitch, who worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, says he believes \"every aspect\" of a story told by a man named Randy Anderson about Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy research base in Indiana. Blitch tells Michels he met Anderson through David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who has testified that the government holds UFO secrets. What Anderson's story was, Blitch does not spell out here, but his backing lends it the word of someone from inside the defense world.","blitch-has-been-inside-several-underground-facilities":"John Blitch, a scientist who worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, says he has been inside several underground military facilities. He tells Michels this as something he saw for himself, saying simply, \"I've been in several of them,\" and if true it would mean such hidden sites exist and he has been inside them.","blitch-s-brief-saic-tenure-never-read-in-2001":"In 2001 John Blitch, who came to the show as a scientist from DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, worked briefly at SAIC, a defense contractor, in Littleton, Colorado, and he says he was never \"read in\" there, meaning he was never cleared into any secret programme. Blitch tells Michels this from his own experience and names a man called Art Morris in connection with it. It matters because it marks what he did not see from inside: no hidden programme at that company was opened to him.","blitch-vets-barber-s-four-man-team-on-his-boat-2024":"John Blitch, a scientist who once worked at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, says that around 2024 he hosted a helicopter pilot named Jake Barber and Barber's four-man team on his boat to test their story. Barber says he flew a UFO out of a crash site. Blitch tells Michels he watched the men closely, \"looking for inconsistencies between those folks,\" a way of checking whether their accounts of retrieving a craft held together.","blog-post-predicting-that-within-a-decade-we-will-acknowledg-2022":"A researcher named Deep Prasad predicts that within about ten years, so by around 2032, people will openly accept that humans are not the only intelligent life on Earth. He tells Michels this in 2022, in his own words: \"we acknowledge that we are not the only life forms that are intelligent and super intelligent on this planet.\" He ties the forecast to a piece of his own writing called \"Curiosity as a Time Capsule\". If he is right, the coming decade would bring an admission that something other than humans, possibly smarter than us, shares the planet.","blood-on-pillow-after-1988-camping-trip":"A former nuclear missile guard named Richard Barth, who says he was abducted by aliens, had said he woke up with blood on his pillow after a camping trip in 1988. Michels brings it up while interviewing Barth, recalling something Barth had told him earlier, as a possible sign that something was done to him during the night.","blown-out-of-a-special-electronics-helicopter-hit-by-an-rpg-1967":"In 1967, near Pleiku in Vietnam, a helicopter carrying special electronics was hit \"right in the belly\" by an RPG, a rocket-propelled grenade, and blown apart, and Joe McMoneagle says he was thrown out of it. McMoneagle tells Michels this as something that happened to him, part of his own life story from the war.","blue-beings-appearing-at-joe-dispenza-s-retreats-and-meditat-2020":"Blue beings are said to show up in the room during the deep meditations of Joe Dispenza, a meditation teacher who runs large retreats and conferences. According to Dispenza, they tell him the people at the conference need to \"vibrate higher\" before the beings can fully appear and heal. Michels tells this himself, passing on what Dispenza has said. If true, it would mean non-human beings respond to a crowd's state of mind.","blue-book-closed-in-1969-citing-condon":"In 1969 the US Air Force shut down Project Blue Book, its UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and pointed to a university study as its reason. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, tells Michels that when the Air Force wanted to \"get out of\" the UFO business in 1969, \"they even quoted the Condon investigation\". He means the Condon report, a study of UFO reports led by Edward Condon, published by the University of Colorado and dated 31 October 1968. Bryce Zabel, a writer on Hollywood and UFOs, tells it without naming Condon: the Air Force, \"holding the line\", closed Blue Book in '69 and said, in effect, \"We're washing our hands of that.\" The report itself concludes that \"further extensive study of UFOs probably cannot be justified in the expectation that science will be advanced thereby\". It gives as Condon's \"impression\" that the defense side could be handled \"without the continuance of a special unit such as Project Blue Book\", which it calls \"a question for defense specialists rather than research scientists\". An Air Force fact sheet of January 1985 is the official record: \"On December 17, 1969, the Secretary of the Air Force announced the termination of Project BLUE BOOK\", a decision it says was based on the Colorado report, a review of it by the National Academy of Sciences, and the Air Force's own experience. Told this way, the story matters because it marks the moment the government publicly stepped away from the subject.","blue-book-heads-grow-increasingly-anti-ufo-1952-1969":"Every head of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, grew more anti-UFO from 1952 until it closed in 1969, according to Michels. In a later episode he puts it more sharply: the Air Force \"tried to marginalize UFOs with Blue Book.\" He tells this himself, without naming a source; if true, it would mean the official study was steered toward dismissal.","blue-book-kecksburg-material-sent-to-project-greengrass-moun-1965":"Something recovered at Kecksburg, Pennsylvania, where an object is said to have come down in 1965, was reportedly sent to a project with the code word \"Greengrass,\" according to a record from Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels he found that record among the Blue Book files, looked the code word up, and found it meant Mount Weather, a government site in Virginia. If he is right, the Kecksburg material went to a high-security government facility and not, as one might expect, to an ordinary Air Force lab.","blue-book-landing-case-with-an-alien-footprint-diagram":"Among the papers of Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, now kept at the National Archives, there is said to be a landing case in which \"the footprints of the alien\" were drawn in a diagram by \"the first military officer on the scene\". The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this from his own digging in the records. If it is as he says, a military officer formally recorded physical traces of a being on the ground.","blue-book-s-robert-friend-sees-the-channeling-session-told-a-1959":"In July 1959, an Air Force officer named Robert Friend, then in charge of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, watched a session in Washington where someone claimed to receive messages from beings elsewhere, with an official named Arthur Lundahl also involved. When Friend took it up the chain, a general told him another agency had it and to keep his hands off, and nothing more was ever heard. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels this, drawing on an 11-page memo about the meeting of July 9, 1959, and he tells it as a sign that someone other than the Air Force held the real UFO file.","blue-book-special-report-14-contradicts-its-press-release-1950":"The claim is that a big Air Force study of UFO reports quietly showed far more unsolved cases than the public was led to believe. The study is Special Report No. 14 of Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. It was issued on 5 May 1955 by the Air Technical Intelligence Center, the Air Force's intelligence unit for foreign aircraft and weapons. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels that if you look at the report's own data table, something like 21% of almost a thousand cases were unknown. He credits the point to a UFO researcher named Stanton Friedman. If he is right, a fifth of the cases the Air Force studied stayed unexplained. The other teller is the report itself. Its table of results reads \"Unknown 434=19.7%\", a share of 2,199 sightings of objects drawn from 3,201 reports in all. So the share is close to his figure, but his count of almost a thousand matches none of the report's totals. The report's conclusion goes the other way from his reading. It says the chance that any of the unknowns are \"flying saucers\" is \"extremely small\", and that it is \"highly improbable\" any report shows \"technological developments outside the range of present-day scientific knowledge.\" The idea that the data table shows something the public summary hid is Derakhshani's reading, set against the report's own conclusion.","blue-dwarfs-drag-a-man-behind-a-curtain-at-the-horror-store-1970":"Sometime in the 1970s, in a shop on a Manhattan street between 7th Avenue and Broadway near West 55th Street, a man in a brown suit was trying to get out and was being \"pulled back by these blue dwarfs,\" behind a curtain in what is remembered as the \"horror store.\" The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met non-human beings, tells Michels he saw it himself, and he names Anne Strieber as a source too. If true, it would put strange small beings at work in the middle of New York.","blue-energy-humanoid-takes-rossi-in-his-sleep-2021":"In 2021, a man named Dave Rossi says he was taken from his home in his sleep by what he calls \"a blue energy type humanoid.\" Rossi tells Michels this as his own experience, on two different episodes, using almost the same words; the second time he gives no date or setting. If true, it would be a direct encounter with a non-human being, the kind of contact Rossi links to the advanced physics he says he was taught.","blue-extraterrestrial-manifesting-physically-in-the-scole-ex-1990":"In the 1990s, at seances in the English village of Scole known as the Scole experiment, a blue extraterrestrial being is said to have appeared in solid form, solid enough to touch. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as what he heard from a man named Robin Foy, who took part in the seances. If true, it would tie contact with beings from elsewhere to the world of spirit mediums.","blue-winged-angel-uap-over-barber-s-house":"A blue object with wings shows up over his house, says Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he once helped retrieve a crashed UFO. He calls it \"the angel\" and tells Michels it \"materializes, hangs out over the house,\" is \"seen by neighbors,\" and then \"shoots into outer space.\" Michels repeats the story in another episode and ties it to Barber's work on crash retrievals, the recovery of downed craft of unknown origin, as if the visits were a result of it. In one version the house is said to be John Blitch's rather than Barber's.","bluegill-recovery-of-spherical-debris-in-deck-logs-1962":"In October 1962, the story goes, a US nuclear test fired high above Johnston Atoll in the Pacific, known as Bluegill Triple Prime, knocked down an unknown object that was watching it, and the Navy fished the wreckage out of the ocean. The main source is Harold Malmgren, an adviser to Presidents Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Ford, who tells Michels the test report 'said this appeared to knock down this device' and that 'the Navy had recovered' it. Kurt McConnell, a Senate staffer, says Malmgren was told directly by Richard Bissell, then deputy director of the CIA, that 'we downed a UFO' that 'tumbled into the ocean'; Pines adds that Malmgren was refused more information and went to Albuquerque to see for himself, and Michels says Malmgren held the pieces. The second strand is the ships' records: a researcher named Jeff Cruickshank, Michels says, holds that the recovery is logged in the original deck logs, with 'spherical debris with some very interesting properties'. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, says he took part in that report, which examines deck logs of ships thought to be involved in 'some partial recovery' of the object or something related to it, and McConnell says the logs describe highly radioactive debris, 'a black sphere' and 'a green tube'. The journalist Ross Coulthart cites an analysis by Douglas Dean Johnson that he says backs Malmgren, and Michels, crediting the researcher Bob Jacobs, says people on the ground at the time widely rumoured a UFO shootdown. If true, the US would have shot down and recovered a craft not its own at the height of the Cold War.","bluegill-triple-prime-as-a-proto-sdi-x-ray-missile-defense-1962":"Michels suggests that Bluegill Triple Prime, an American project of 1962, was an early version of an X-ray missile defence, a forerunner of the Strategic Defense Initiative, the later plan to shoot down missiles from space. He makes the link himself, pointing to a RAND Corporation paper and to a man named Harold Malmgren. If he is right, ideas usually dated to the 1980s were already being tested two decades earlier.","bluegill-triple-prime-as-missile-defense-tied-to-sdi-1962":"Bluegill Triple Prime, a 1962 high-altitude nuclear test over the Pacific, was really a defense against Russian long-range nuclear missiles, Michels says, passing on what a man named Malmgren told him. Michels ties the claim to a paper from the RAND Corporation, a defense think tank, and to the Institute for Defense Analysis, a Pentagon research body. If true, the test was an early start on the missile-shield thinking behind the 1980s plan known as SDI, or \"Star Wars\".","bluegill-triple-prime-led-to-excalibur-and-sdi-as-a-ufo-defe-1962":"The claim is that a 1962 US nuclear test called Bluegill Triple Prime led straight to Ronald Reagan's Strategic Defense Initiative, the 1980s plan for space weapons that people nicknamed Star Wars, and that the plan was really meant to shoot down UFOs. Michels says there is \"a direct link\" between the tests and Star Wars, and he connects it to the physicist Edward Teller. On a later episode he credits the idea to Harald Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, saying \"Harold apparently said that this whole thing was actually devised to take down UFOs.\" An online researcher known as UAPGerb makes the same kind of argument without mentioning Bluegill. He says two parts of the 1983 program, one on making weapons more lethal and targets harder to destroy and one on particle beam weapons, were meant to be space-based microwave weapons \"to shoot down UFOs.\"","bluegill-triple-prime-object-tumbles-from-fireball-1962":"In 1962, during a US nuclear test called Bluegill Triple Prime over Johnston Atoll in the Pacific, a bright, fiery object is said to have tumbled out of the blast's fireball. Michels tells this again and again, crediting different people: at first a man named Jeff Cruickshank, later Harald Malmgren, a former presidential adviser who says he heard about it, and the retired Navy admiral Tim Gallaudet. In his version a warhead was launched minutes before midnight, and many on a ship watching saw the object fall from the plume; he also says it was filmed from two aircraft called Kettle 1 and Kettle 2, that the footage can still be seen, and that a cleaned-up copy has a white triangle laid over the object. On some tellings he goes further, saying the blast \"reportedly downed a UFO,\" pointing to Navy deck logs, and passing on a rumour that the debris went to Sandia Labs, a US weapons laboratory. Kirk McConnell, a former Senate staffer, tells it as well, saying the object came tumbling out and headed for the ocean, but he puts the film on a Cobra Ball aircraft, a military plane that tracks missile tests, and credits the Los Alamos and Livermore weapons labs. If true, it would mean an unknown craft was at, or even hit by, a nuclear test. The tellers differ on whether the proof rests on shipboard witnesses or on film, and once Michels ties the videos to the skeptic Mick West, who has argued over what they show.","bluegill-video-sanitized-with-superimposed-triangle-1962":"Film of Bluegill Triple Prime, a 1962 American nuclear test near Johnston Atoll in the Pacific, was later doctored to hide a UFO, with a white triangle laid over the spot where it appeared. Michels first relays this from a man named Jeff Cruickshank, who says the video was \"sanitized and censored\" that way. In another episode Michels tells it in his own words with a different date and culprit: he says that at a 1998 declassification review, the Defense Special Weapons Agency, a Pentagon body handling nuclear weapons matters, led by Dr. Byron L. Ristvet, \"applied a large white triangle\" to the footage, which he calls the Kettle 1 footage.","bob-baker-mitre-paper-on-ning-li-s-ac-dc-gravity-2000":"A man named Bob Baker is said to have written a paper on the physicist Ning Li's idea known as AC/DC gravity, a proposed way of making gravity-like forces with electrical currents. He wrote it under the MITRE Corporation, a US research contractor, for one of the conferences on high-frequency gravitational waves, ripples in space and time far faster than the ones astronomers usually study. Dave Rossi, a guest who talks about advanced physics, brings it up as a paper he has read and gives no further source. The story places it in the 2000s. Rossi offers it as a sign that serious researchers took Li's work seriously.","bob-ballard-s-search-for-atlantis":"The lost city of Atlantis is now said by some to lie in the Red Sea, and an ocean explorer named Bob Ballard, a consultant to a Navy institute in Connecticut, is said to have been looking into it. John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy, mentions this to Michels as something tied to Ballard. He gives no details of what Ballard has found.","bob-beck-at-ossining-during-the-space-kids-experiments-1970":"A man named Bob Beck, who an unnamed researcher on the show says was \"most certainly CIA\", was in Ossining, New York, at the very time of what the guest calls the \"space kids\" experiments of the 1970s. The guest tells Michels this without naming a source, in an episode about a CIA scientist said to have contacted an alien council. The point is that the CIA may have been quietly present at those experiments.","bob-beckwith-philadelphia-experiment-and-teleportation-1940":"A man named Robert Beckwith is said to have had some part in the Philadelphia Experiment, a rumoured wartime US Navy experiment said to have made a ship vanish, and to have later worked with extremely strong magnetic fields and on teleportation. Michels raises this, hedging that Beckwith \"claims\" it and was \"maybe somewhat involved\", while asking a guest named Dave Rossi about him; if true, it would tie the old Navy legend to real later research.","bob-brier-s-examination-suggests-tutankhamun-died-from-a-blo-1990":"In the 1990s, the Egyptologist Bob Brier examined the body of Tutankhamun and found that the way blood had pooled at the back of the skull looked like the young pharaoh had been hit on the back of the head. A researcher who goes by Luke Caverns passes this on to Michels from Brier's work. If true, it would mean Tutankhamun was killed.","bob-jahn-from-boeing-plasma-physics-to-the-princeton-pear-la":"A plasma physicist named Bob Jahn worked at Boeing and then went on to run the PEAR lab (Princeton Engineering Anomalies Research), a lab at Princeton University that studied parapsychology. Michels mentions this himself, without a source, as an example of a hard scientist turning to the paranormal.","bob-jahn-s-random-event-generator-research-with-navy-interes-1979":"From 1979 to 2007, a Princeton lab is said to have tested whether the human mind can nudge machines that produce random results, called random event generators. The lab was the Princeton Engineering Anomalies Research lab, or PEAR, founded by Bob Jahn, the dean of the engineering school, and the Navy is said to have taken an interest. A researcher named Herb Metts tells Michels that the lab's \"final sort of chart\" shows \"pretty definitively that the subjects were able to do what they were tasked to do,\" and he credits Jahn's colleague Brenda Dunne and the books Margins of Reality and Consciousness, the Source of Reality, while Michels brings up the lab several times himself, once dating it to 1978 and once naming only Metts's book as his source.","bob-lazar-at-mit-possibly-placed-for-defense-work-1990":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he once worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada, was at MIT or one of the defense labs linked to it in Massachusetts, Lincoln Labs or Draper, and was put there for defense work. The mathematician Eric Weinstein offers this only as a guess, saying \"my sense is he was put there to work on something defense related,\" and he places it in the early 1990s. Lazar himself tells Michels flatly, \"I was sent there,\" and puts it before 1988, which would mean his schooling was arranged for him.","bob-lazar-at-s4-1989":"Bob Lazar is a man who says that in 1989 he worked on an actual alien craft at S4, a secret site in Nevada. Michels mentions him himself, in an episode on the UFO cover-up, saying there is \"only one Bob Lazar\", meaning Lazar stands alone as someone who says he worked on the craft itself, which, if true, would make him a rare firsthand witness to reverse engineering, the work of taking an unknown craft apart to learn how it works.","bob-lazar-briefed-on-project-looking-glass":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site in the late 1980s, was briefed on Project Looking Glass, according to Michels, who says it twice in one episode as if it were well known. Project Looking Glass is, in UFO lore, a secret programme; Michels gives no detail of what it did, and cites a man named Timothy Taylor alongside Lazar.","bob-lazar-on-parallel-engineering":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S4, would say that the work was not really reverse engineering, meaning taking a captured craft apart to copy it, but \"parallel engineering\". Michels says this from his own talks with Lazar: \"if you talk to Bob Lazar and you go deep enough with him,\" that is what you hear. He says it in an episode that also brings up Tom DeLonge and DeLonge's book series Sekret Machines. If true, the engineers were building alongside the alien technology, not simply copying it.","bob-lazar-s-claim-of-reverse-engineering-at-s4-1987":"A man named Bob Lazar says that in the late 1980s he worked at S4, a secret site just south of Area 51, the classified test base in the Nevada desert, where his job was to reverse engineer, that is, take apart to learn how it worked, the propulsion system of craft not made by human hands, nine of them by his account. Michels retells Lazar's story many times on the show, from Lazar's 1989 television appearance: how he was flown in on unmarked flights, how he got the job through a chance meeting with the physicist Edward Teller, and how the craft were said to bend gravity around themselves. Michels says that Lazar surely worked at Area 51 and probably at S4, which, if true, would mean the US government holds and studies alien craft.","bob-lazar-s-sport-model-grown-out-of-the-vacuum":"A man named Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site called S-4 in 1989, once remarked that the craft he called the \"sport model\" looked as if it had been \"grown out of the vacuum itself.\" Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist known for patents on unusual craft, tells Michels he remembers Lazar's words. In another telling Pais recalls them as the craft being \"3D printed\" in our spacetime, and says \"that's exactly what that equation says,\" meaning his own super force equation. For Pais, it would mean his physics matches what Lazar says he saw.","bob-lazar-walks-underneath-the-craft-1989":"In 1989 a man named Bob Lazar, who says he worked on recovered craft at a secret Nevada site called S-4, is said to have walked underneath one of those craft. Michels raises it only as a question to his guest Grant Cameron, asking what happened in the Lazar case \"where he walks underneath the craft\", and he gives no further detail. If true, it would mean a person had stood beneath a machine that was not built by people.","bob-maguire-measures-his-girlfriend-s-implant-with-an-rf-det":"A former CIA contractor named Bob McGwier says his girlfriend has an implant in her head and that he has measured radio signals coming from it. He tells it first-hand: \"I have a video online of me moving this field meter across her head and watching that implant pul RF Transmissions.\" A field meter, or RF detector, is a device that picks up radio waves. In the subject, an implant usually means a small object people say was placed in their body during an encounter with something unknown. Michels later retells it to the author Whitley Strieber. He spells the name Bob Maguire and says McGwier could \"measure the frequency coming out\". The host's version is second-hand and comes from his own interview with McGwier. If true, the implant would be a physical object that sends out signals you can measure.","bob-s-letter-from-nixon-thanking-him-for-cooperation-on-the-1970":"A man known as Bob, in Los Angeles in the 1970s, is said to have received a letter from Richard Nixon that read, \"Thank you for your cooperation on the project we're working on.\" The Canadian UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels the letter was the source of a controversy around Bob, a story in which the researchers Linda Howe and Larry Bryant also come up. If the letter is what it seems, it would tie a sitting president to some secret project Bob was part of.","bobby-fischer-s-claim-that-russian-parapsychologists-interfe-1970":"The American chess champion Bobby Fischer said again and again that the Russians interfered with his games by bringing in gurus and Russian parapsychologists, people who study claimed psychic powers, while he played. The surgeon Italo Venturelli repeats this to Michels, telling him to \"look it up.\" If true, it would mean the Soviets used psychic methods in open competition during the 1970s.","bobby-ray-inman-acknowledges-the-national-underwater-reconna":"A secret agency called the National Underwater (or Undersea) Reconnaissance Office, \"NURO\" for short, really exists, according to a man named Bobby Ray Inman, who is said to have mentioned it and \"basically acknowledged it was a real thing.\" Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral and former head oceanographer of the Navy, relays this to Michels and also names a man called Sam Gerbus in telling it. Gallaudet adds that in all his years with access to classified undersea programmes he had \"never even heard of that organization,\" which, if the agency exists, would mean it is hidden even from senior Navy insiders.","bobby-ray-inman-refers-bob-oechsler-to-shapiro-and-hineman-1988":"In a phone call in 1988 or 1989, Admiral Bobby Ray Inman, a former director of the NSA, is said to have pointed a UFO researcher named Bob Oechsler, described as a NASA mission specialist, to the director of naval intelligence, Sumar Shapiro, and an official named R. Evans Hineman; this comes from a researcher who goes by UAPGerb, while Michels, telling it on several episodes, plays Inman's own recorded voice and frames the call as Inman \"basically admitting\" the US holds UFOs. On the recording, asked about recovered craft, Inman says \"I honestly don't know\" and \"10 years ago, the answer would have been no,\" adding that \"whether as time has evolved, they're beginning to become more open on it is a possibility\"; the tellers disagree on what the call amounts to, a referral, a hint that UFO secrets might come out, or an admission.","bobby-ray-inman-tape-recorded-at-the-ida":"There is a tape of Bobby Ray Inman, a former senior intelligence official, being put the questions on this subject, and it was recorded at the Institute for Defense Analyses, a defence research centre. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron mentions the tape to Michels without saying who made it. He treats it as a time a top official was pressed on UFOs on the record.","boebert-asks-about-hybrid-program-at-hearing-2020":"At a hearing in Congress on UFOs in the 2020s, Representative Lauren Boebert asked a witness about hybrids, meaning supposed mixes of human and alien. Michels recalls it himself, laughing, as a really weird moment in the testimony, since it hinted that such a program might be on the table.","bondi-and-negative-mass-at-the-1957-chapel-hill-conference":"At a 1957 physics conference in Chapel Hill, North Carolina, where Michels says quantum gravity was set up as a field, the Austrian mathematician Hermann Bondi gave a talk on negative mass in general relativity. Michels passes this on from the researcher Eric Weinstein, who poses Bondi's point as a question about whether mass must always be positive. Michels says he has the conference report, The Role of Gravitation in Physics, which has a chapter on Bondi's talk; for Michels this means serious physicists were weighing ideas that bear on pushing against gravity.","bones-on-religion-humans-and-aliens-part-of-the-same-creatio-1990":"Between 1990 and 1993, at a place he calls Base 2, a former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman says a figure he calls Bones told him that humans and aliens \"are part of the same creation.\" Sherman tells Michels this as something said to him directly, during work he says the NSA had him do communicating with aliens. If true, it would mean the beings themselves describe a shared religious origin with us.","bonnybridge-ufo-hotspot-and-stepfather-s-sightings-2000":"Bonnybridge, in Scotland, is a UFO hotspot with so many sightings it is \"almost like a tourist attraction,\" and the stepfather of the hacker Gary McKinnon lived nearby in Falkirk and had sightings of his own. McKinnon, who broke into US military and NASA computers looking for UFO files, tells Michels this as what his stepfather told him, placing it around 2000. It is part of how he explains his own interest in UFOs.","book-of-enoch-cut-from-the-tanakh":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text \"first discovered in Ethiopia,\" was \"at one point part of the Tanakh,\" the Hebrew Bible, \"and was excised,\" and that it preserves the backstory to the biblical story he is discussing. He points to R. H. Charles's English translation of Enoch, published in 1917, and the King James Bible of 1611. Those texts show what each collection contains as published, but whether Enoch once stood inside the Hebrew canon and was removed is a question they leave open.","book-of-enoch-excluded-but-holding-truth-per-malachi-martin":"The Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text left out of most Bibles, was excluded from scripture but still holds truth, according to Catholic teaching as explained by Malachi Martin, a Catholic writer who Dylan Borland says was among the first people to read the Dead Sea Scrolls, ancient manuscripts found in caves near the Dead Sea. Borland, who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, relays this to Michels from Martin. The best-known English version of Enoch is the translation by R. H. Charles, published in 1917.","book-of-enoch-watchers-mating-with-humans-and-giants":"The story is that in ancient times beings from the sky came down, took human wives and fathered a race of giants, a tale several guests read as a memory of real visitors. Michels brings it up himself, recalling that in the Book of Enoch, an ancient religious text known here in R. H. Charles's 1917 translation, \"the watchers\" mated and \"there were giants in the earth in those days\". The book's own words tell it in order: when daughters were born to men, \"the angels, the children of the heaven, saw and lusted after them\", two hundred of them came down \"in the days of Jared on the summit of Mount Hermon\", took wives, taught them \"charms and enchantments\", and the women \"bare great giants\". The podcaster Joe Rogan, passing on what Congresswoman Anna Paulina Luna told him, calls them watchers who \"bred with men and created the Nephilim\", the name for those giants, and reads it as genetic engineering; a guest named Jay Anderson mentions only the descent on a mountain he places in Lebanon; and a researcher named Tim Alberino says lust was the first cause of the Watchers' fall, as Genesis 6 implies. The King James Bible of 1611 holds a shorter version: \"the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives\", and \"There were giants in the earth in those days\". The Watchers, their number and Mount Hermon belong to Enoch's fuller telling; reading Genesis's sons of God as those same beings is the step the tellers take, which the short Genesis passage neither states nor excludes. If the tellers are right, humanity's oldest scriptures remember contact and interbreeding with non-human beings.","book-of-job-as-a-deliberate-mithraic-kabbalistic-text-tied-t":"The researcher Jason Jorjani argues that the Book of Job was written on purpose as a coded mystical text, tied to the Magi, the priests of the god Mithra in the ancient Persian Empire of the Achaemenid kings. He tells Michels that these Magi took part in rebuilding Israel, which he calls \"the first Zionist project in history,\" and that he makes \"a complicated argument\" for this in his book Iranian Leviathan. If he is right, a book of the Bible would carry a hidden Persian religious design.","book-of-revelation-people-who-cannot-die":"Tim Alberino, a guest who says he has hunted for giants, reads the Book of Revelation as describing a time when calamities fall on the earth and \"you have a group of people who cannot die.\" He draws on the King James Version of the Bible, published in 1611, and gives no other source. He offers it as a picture of the end times that fits what he describes elsewhere.","book-of-the-watchers-dated-to-around-300-bc":"The oldest part of the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text left out of most Bibles, is a section called the Book of the Watchers, which tells of heavenly beings called the Watchers, and it was \"written at least circa 300 BC,\" according to a guest named Tim Alberino. He gives this date himself on the show, citing the English translation of the Book of Enoch by R. H. Charles, published in 1917. The translation he names supports his framing in so far as it presents the Watchers tale as one of the book's sections; how firmly it pins that section to 300 BC or earlier is not quoted on the show, so his date stands as his reading of the scholarship.","book-s-claim-of-alien-contact-at-bentwaters-disputed-by-witn-1980":"The book Left at East Gate claims that in 1980 there was actual contact with aliens at RAF Bentwaters, an air base in England used by the US Air Force, where strange lights were reported. The documentary maker James Fox brings up that claim with Michels and says the witnesses dispute it: the airmen Jim Penniston, John Burroughs and Cabansag and the officers Charles Halt and Gordon Williams. As Fox tells it, the people who were actually there do not back the book's account of a meeting with aliens.","book-that-foretold-the-texas-tower-sniper-1966":"A book described the Texas Tower sniper, the gunman who shot people from a tower in Texas in 1966, before the shooting ever happened, according to a researcher named Peter Levenda. He tells Michels this in passing and does not name the book or its author. If it were true, the book would have foreseen the event.","boomerang-shaped-craft-over-tucson":"A boomerang-shaped craft flew in broad daylight over Tucson, Arizona, lower than the height where planes circle to land. Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has advised the Pentagon, tells Michels he and his wife saw it themselves, so this would be an unknown craft seen up close by a scientist.","bootsie-galbraith-sees-a-ufo-called-in-by-steven-greer-1990":"A woman named Bootsie Galbraith was in Mexico in the 1990s with Steven Greer, a campaigner who says people can summon UFOs through meditation, when Greer \"called in a UFO and they all saw it.\" The writer Whitley Strieber, known for his own accounts of meeting strange beings, tells Michels this as what Galbraith told him. If true, it would mean such craft can be called on purpose.","booze-fighters-take-over-hollister-stealing-the-headlines-1947":"In 1947, a motorcycle gang of veterans of the world wars called the Booze Fighters took over Hollister, California for a day and threw a drunken party downtown, grabbing the headlines. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels about it, and he notes that the gang's name was a play on \"foo fighters\", the wartime pilots' name for strange lights in the sky. He tells it as a story he has heard and does not name a source. In his telling, the riot pushed other news of that summer off the front pages.","borderland-society-knew-of-ufo-crashes-before-the-government-1940":"An occult research group of the 1940s called the Borderland Society is said to have known about certain UFO crashes before the US government did, and Michels says there is documentation of it. He brings this up on several episodes, sometimes crediting a researcher named Richard Geldreich with uncovering it. His versions differ: in one the group learned of crashes and sightings before the Army and the Air Force, in another it learned through remote viewing (trying to see distant places with the mind) in the 1940s and 50s, and in another it sometimes knew of crashes before they happened, which, if true, would mean a private circle had better sources than the military.","borland-claims-first-hand-knowledge-of-crash-recovery-revers":"A man named Dylan Borland says he has \"direct first-hand knowledge and exposure\" to crash recovery, meaning the retrieval of crashed non-human craft, and to reverse engineering, meaning taking that technology apart to learn how it works and fitting it into human systems, along with knowledge of its power source. Borland says this on the show as his own claim, giving no date or place. If true, it would mean a living witness has worked on such craft himself.","borland-goes-to-the-icig-after-grusch-testifies-2023":"In 2023, after a former intelligence officer named David Grusch testified to Congress about hidden UFO programmes, a man named Dylan Borland went with his lawyer to the ICIG, the Inspector General of the Intelligence Community, the watchdog office that takes whistleblower complaints. Borland tells Michels this himself: \"Dave testifies. I go to the ICIG.\" It matters because it would mean Grusch was not the only insider willing to take such claims through official channels.","borland-on-stage-will-neither-confirm-nor-deny-project-rubik":"Asked at a live stage event about something called Project Rubik's Cube, Dylan Borland said he could \"neither confirm nor deny\" it and told the questioner to \"ask for a damn receipt for the whistleblowers.\" Borland tells this himself on an episode with Jeremy Corbell, the guest of that show, who is also named in the account; the telling says nothing more about what the project is.","borland-pulls-aaro-reports-on-baiting-craft-and-taking-them-2023":"A man named Dylan Borland says that in 2023, using access he had through the Defense Threat Reduction Agency, a Pentagon agency that deals with weapons of mass destruction, he began to pull reports and briefings from AARO, the Pentagon's current office for investigating unexplained objects in the sky. He tells Michels those files included reports \"about baiting craft and how to take them down,\" which, if true, would mean the government has studied luring unknown craft and bringing them down.","borland-s-alleged-classified-drawing-of-a-hieroglyph-like-sy-2020":"A former government worker named Dylan Borland, who says a top official told him aliens run everything, is said to have made a drawing \"of like a hieroglyph\" while giving secret testimony to an intelligence watchdog. A guest named Jeremy Corbell says this was told to him by the ODNI, the office that oversees all US spy agencies, and Michels repeats it to Borland, saying the drawings were made for the ODNI. Borland himself says the information was leaked to Corbell illegally by the ODNI's inspector general office, and he frames the drawing as something seen \"hypothetically on a UFO.\"","borland-s-alleged-classified-icig-drawing-of-a-pyramid-shape-2020":"A former Air Force man named Dylan Borland, who says he saw a triangle-shaped craft, gave closed testimony to the Intelligence Community Inspector General, the watchdog that takes whistleblower complaints from US spy agencies, and in it he drew a pyramid-shaped power source. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels this as something he heard, citing the Las Vegas journalist George Knapp, and Michels later repeats it, crediting Corbell. If true, it would mean a witness described the engine of a craft, not just its outside.","borland-s-congressional-testimony-on-the-langley-triangle-2012":"In the summer of 2012, at about 1:30 in the morning, a triangle with equal sides roughly 100 feet long took off from near the NASA hangar on a military base, the base called Langley in Virginia. Dylan Borland, who was in the Air Force there, says he saw it himself after going back to his barracks while his team waited out the weather. He told it to Congress in testimony and repeats the words on the show, and if true it means a large unknown craft was flying from a US base.","bormann-alive-in-argentina-cashing-checks-via-chase-1945":"Martin Bormann, one of Hitler's closest aides who supposedly died in 1945, was still alive in Buenos Aires in the 1960s and was cashing checks in his own name through JP Morgan Chase, according to the philosopher Jason Jorjani. Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source, in an episode about Nazi Germany's alleged secret flying-saucer work and the Nazis who escaped to the Argentina of Juan Perón. If true, it would mean a top Nazi lived openly for decades with help from a major American bank.","born-next-to-the-army-s-first-nuclear-test-reactor-1950":"John Blitch, a former DARPA scientist (DARPA is the Pentagon's research agency), says he was born in Idaho Falls, Idaho, close to the first nuclear test reactor the Army had \"spun up,\" and grew up there between 1950 and 1963. He tells Michels this about his own childhood, a backdrop to the strange experiences he describes later in life.","bose-s-paper-published-under-einstein-s-endorsement-1920":"In the 1920s, the Indian physicist Satyendra Nath Bose is said to have sent a paper that no one would take up to Albert Einstein, telling him \"Nobody's listening to me\", and the paper was then published with Einstein's backing. Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor who appears on the show as a UFO witness, tells Michels this without naming a source, as an example of an ignored idea that got heard once the right person read it.","boskin-commission-s-1-1-cpi-overstatement-1990":"The Boskin Commission, a 1990s panel that studied the consumer price index (the government's measure of inflation), was \"committing a crime\" against the American people by putting a 1.1% overstatement into that index \"by hand\", according to the researcher Eric Weinstein. Weinstein says this to Michels without naming a source. If true, it would mean the official inflation figure was deliberately rigged.","boss-tells-fox-the-government-admitted-the-roswell-recovery-1947":"The US government once admitted that it recovered something at Roswell, New Mexico, where a craft is said to have crashed in 1947, or so a boss of the filmmaker James Fox told him. Fox, who makes documentaries about UFOs, tells Michels he answered, \"Really?\", and he names Richard vanle and Renee Harris in telling it. If true, it would mean the official denial of a crash had cracked.","bostick-on-strange-effects-at-high-megavoltage":"Strange things happen when very high voltages, in the millions of volts, are put across short distances: that is a line Michels says was spoken by a researcher named Bostick. Michels mentions the quote himself without saying where it comes from, while talking about an inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown, whose work with high voltage the story ties it to. If true, it would hint that high voltage does something physics does not yet explain.","bought-ranch-as-skeptic-intending-to-donate-to-boy-scouts-2016":"The current owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property long tied to reports of UFOs and strange events, says he bought it around 2016 as a skeptic of those claims. He tells Michels he \"actually intended to donate it to the Boy Scouts of America.\" His point is that he did not come to the ranch expecting anything unusual.","boyd-bushman-talks-antigravity-1990":"A man named Boyd Bushman, linked in this story to Lockheed Martin in Fort Worth, is said to have talked about antigravity, meaning a way to cancel or beat gravity. Nick Cook tells Michels that he sat down and interviewed Bushman himself, for a documentary he was making at the time called Ben Rich Secret. If what Bushman said were true, it would mean such technology was being discussed inside a major American defense company.","bozeman-cattle-mutilation-with-ufo-reports-1988":"In 1988, near Bozeman, Montana, there was a cattle mutilation, where livestock are found dead and cut up as if by a surgeon: two cows were killed and \"surgically manipulated,\" and there were UFO reports around the same time. Kevin Knuth, presented on the show as a NASA whistleblower, tells Michels this as a witness to the events. If true, the strange deaths and the sightings were linked.","bracewell-probe-possibly-lurking-in-the-solar-system-1960":"In the 1960s a scientist named Ronald Bracewell imagined that an alien civilisation might send a robot probe to watch other star systems, and he went further: such a probe, Michels says, \"may already be lurking somewhere in the dark recesses of our solar system,\" perhaps at the balance points between the Earth's and the Moon's gravity. Michels tells this himself, framing his conversation with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, without naming a source beyond Bracewell. If true, an alien watcher could be parked close to home.","brad-sorensen-shown-alien-reproduction-vehicles-at-palmdale-1988":"The story is that in 1988 a man named Brad Sorensen was taken behind the scenes at a military air show and shown three flying saucers built by humans. Michels tells it as Sorensen's account, passed on by a man named Mark McCandlish. In that account Sorensen was shown what he called a \"flux liner\" or \"alien reproduction vehicle\", meaning a human-made copy of an alien craft. There were three, shaped like bells or acorns, and they had wear and tear as if they had been flown. The story is told as taking place at Palmdale, California. A UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells it several times and places the show at Norton Air Force Base instead. In his version they went behind a black curtain and found three saucers hovering off the ground, nicknamed mama, baby and papa bear. He adds that Sorensen saw a \"pulsar craft\" carrying 121 nuclear warheads, and that the men there bragged it could destroy Russia in about 20 minutes. UAPGerb also says Sorensen admitted the story in 1990 to Bill Scott, a senior editor at the magazine Aviation Week & Space Technology. He credits a book by William Hamilton III called Cosmic Top Secret, calls the craft \"basically a copycat vehicle\", and says he believes the story is legitimate. If true, it would mean the US had flying copies of alien craft by the late 1980s.","bradshaw-ranch-high-strangeness":"Bradshaw Ranch, in Secret Canyon near Sedona, Arizona, is described as \"ground zero for Sedona's high strangeness\", the name UFO researchers give to clusters of odd and unexplained happenings. Michels says this in an episode about a book called Merging Dimensions, drawing on it as his source. The point is that this one ranch is held up as the centre of the area's strange reports.","brain-implants-link-experiencers-to-the-gray-hive-mind":"Implants put in the brains of people who say they have met aliens connect them to the \"gray hive mind,\" a shared mind of the grays, the small grey beings of many abduction stories, and \"it turns you into a walking bug.\" Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, tells Michels this as something he was told directly: \"They told me that too.\"","brain-shown-to-secrete-dmt-endogenously-michigan-research":"The brain can make DMT, a powerful psychedelic compound, on its own, and the brain is full of receptors for it, when scientists used to think it was only made in the lungs. The writer Graham Hancock says this on the show, citing work by a researcher at the University of Michigan named Jimo Borjigin. If true, visionary experiences might have a chemical root in the brain itself.","brake-lines-cut-after-leaving-bae":"Dylan Borland, a former intelligence specialist who has gone public with claims about secret UFO programmes, says that after he left the defense contractor BAE, someone cut the brake lines on his car. He tells Michels that \"per the police, yes, my brake lines were cut 100%\", citing their investigation, which would mean someone tried to harm him after he left.","bramley-plague-spreaders-are-the-sumerian-overlord-faction":"The writer William Bramley, in his book The Gods of Eden, argues that the people who spread plagues are working for the same faction that ruled over humanity in ancient Sumer, the first cities of Mesopotamia. The philosopher Jason Jorjani sums up Bramley's view for Michels. If true, it would mean one hidden group has guided disasters across thousands of years, from Sumer to Europe.","brandenburg-s-xenon-129-nuclear-signature-at-cydonia-2010":"The claim is that Mars was once home to a civilisation wiped out by nuclear war, and that the proof lies in the Martian air: far more of two gases, xenon-129 and argon-40, than natural radioactive decay should produce. The plasma physicist and propulsion scientist John Brandenburg tells Michels this himself. He says NASA's Viking missions measured the gases in 1976 and found \"a large amount of xenon 129,\" which comes from only two things, \"supernova explosions and hydrogen bomb explosions,\" and \"we know there was no supernova on Mars.\" He recalls the moment it all came together in his office: \"I wept like a child for about 4 minutes. Why? Cuz they were all dead.\" He places that realisation in the 1980s, while others place his work in the 2010s. The philosopher Jason Jorjani says he spent several days with Brandenburg in New Mexico and wrote up the research, tied to the Martian region called Cydonia, in his book Closer Encounters. Michels repeats it on several episodes, once crediting an unnamed friend who worked at the national weapons labs Lawrence Livermore and Sandia, and John Blitch, a former DARPA scientist, mentions it in passing as Brandenburg's speculation. If true, it would mean intelligent life arose on another planet and destroyed itself.","brandon-fugal-skinwalker-ranch-phenomenon-is-reflective-2020":"Whatever strange thing happens at Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of unexplained events, \"is reflective,\" meaning it mirrors back the people who come to it, according to a man named Brandon Fugal who goes there. The researcher Grant Cameron passes this on to Michels as Fugal's view, which, if right, would mean the phenomenon responds to its observers.","brandon-fugal-to-have-marrs-sphere-tested-in-utah-lab-2025":"A sphere held by a man named Jim Marrs was to be flown to Utah and tested in a lab by a businessman named Brandon Fugal, who agreed to carry it there in his private jet. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels he heard this when he was told, \"I'm sorry, Ross. Brandon Fugal's agreed to fly out in his private jet to Utah where he's going to have it tested in the lab.\" As Coulthart tells it, that is how the object got away from him.","branscomb-allegedly-offers-condon-his-clearance-back-for-bur-1960":"In the 1960s, the story goes, a scientist named Lewis Branscomb, then director of the National Bureau of Standards, offered the physicist Edward Condon a deal: help bury the UFO question and get your security clearance back. Condon chaired a committee that studied UFO reports. Michels tells this himself and frames it as an allegation, without naming a source. If true, it would mean the scientific verdict on UFOs was bargained for.","branscomb-tells-condon-to-get-his-clearance-back-1966":"The story is that in 1966 a scientist named Lewis Branscomb told the physicist Edward Condon that the Air Force needed someone to head a UFO study, and that \"maybe it's time you get your clearance back.\" Condon went on to lead that study. The source is an interview Condon gave to the American Institute of Physics. Michels raises it twice in his own words and quotes Branscomb's line about the clearance. In one of those tellings a scientist named Donald Menzel also comes up. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, relays the same exchange from the interview: Branscomb \"told him the Air Force needs someone to head up this UFO study.\" Salas places it at the University of Colorado. Michels and Salas both draw on the same interview and add nothing seen first-hand. It is told because tying the study's leader to a renewed security clearance suggests the study was closer to the classified world than it looked.","bray-s-navy-uap-disclosure-2021":"A Navy official named Scott Bray made a public disclosure about UAP, unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's term for UFOs, around 2021. Michels mentions it in passing from memory, unsure of the year, as an example of the military speaking openly on the subject.","brazil-ordinance-551gc3-on-reporting-ufos-2009":"Since 2009, Michels says, a Brazilian rule called Ordinance 551GC3 has required any branch of Brazil's military that sees a UFO to pass the information to the country's Central Aerospace Defense Command. Michels tells this himself, naming the ordinance but quoting none of its words. If so, it would mean Brazil's armed forces treat UFO sightings as something to be formally reported.","brazil-s-night-of-ufos-1986-host-summary":"In May 1986, 21 unidentified objects were spotted over four Brazilian states, with the story centred on Brasília and air bases near Rio, according to Michels. He sums it up himself, relying on an official statement from the Brazilian Air Force and on a senior Air Force officer, Brigadier Octávio Júlio Moreira Lima. He presents it as one of the cases Brazil's own officials accepted as real, which would mean a national military openly admitting it saw objects it could not identify.","brazil-sightings":"Witnesses to sightings in Brazil described what they saw in different words from witnesses elsewhere, according to a researcher named Peter Levenda. He makes the point to Michels in passing and does not say which sightings he means, who saw them or what words they used.","break-in-at-brown-s-house-passport-taken-ashes-moved":"Someone broke into his home, says Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon employee who calls himself a whistleblower: the only thing taken and never recovered was his civilian passport, and his grandfather's ashes had been moved and put out. Brown tells Michels this happened to him, and he points to a police record of the break-in.","break-ins-at-the-homes-of-legacy-program-colleagues-from-bae-2018":"In 2018, people working on a \"Legacy\" program, a long-running secret effort, at the defense company BAE Systems had their homes broken into. Dylan Borland, who knew them from his own time at BAE, tells Michels it was happening to \"the people I knew who were in Legacy back at BAE in 2018,\" passing on what they told him. If true, it would suggest someone was pressuring people tied to the program.","breakaway-civilization-behind-the-men-at-the-door":"The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has had his own encounters with non-human beings, tells Michels he believes the men who came to his door were connected to a \"breakaway civilization\": a hidden group that has split off from ordinary society, with its own secret technology. \"I think there is a breakaway civilization and we're talking about it right now,\" he says, drawing on the ideas of the UFO historian Richard Dolan.","breakaway-civilization-built-on-crash-retrieval-secrecy-1947":"The secret world that has hidden recovered crashed craft since 1947 has grown into what amounts to a separate civilization, one that has \"broken away from our own,\" according to a UFO historian named Richard Dolan. He tells Michels this as his own reading, drawing on the historian Arnold Toinby's ideas about how civilizations form and split. If true, it would mean a hidden society with its own knowledge and power now runs alongside the public one.","brent-hears-that-harrington-worked-on-abscam-1980":"A man named Harrington is said to have worked on ABSCAM, an FBI bribery sting against members of Congress around 1980. Brent Friedman tells Michels he heard this from his mother. It would mean Harrington had a background in high-level federal investigations.","brent-offered-secret-agent-man-by-barry-sonnenfeld-1997":"Some time between 1997 and 1999 in Los Angeles, Brent Friedman, who has worked in film and television, was offered a job on a show called Secret Agent Man, with the director Barry Sonnenfeld as executive producer. Friedman tells Michels this from his own career, as part of an episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs.","brent-sees-a-fatal-motorcycle-crash-at-age-five-1968":"In 1968, in the Berkeley Hills of California, a five-year-old boy saw a woman die in a motorcycle crash: as he describes it, \"when the back tire hits that water, her bike slides, stops, flips her.\" Brent Friedman, a guest on an episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, tells Michels he saw it himself as that boy, as part of the story of his own early life.","brett-weinstein-advised-not-to-air-robert-malone-interview-2020":"Michels says he once asked the biologist and podcaster Bret Weinstein whether he should air an interview, and Weinstein told him: \"I lost a third of my family's income and I would not air this.\" Michels tells it as his own experience, a story linked to an interview with the physician Robert Malone, as an example of what airing controversial material can cost.","brewster-lights-over-the-haunted-house-garage-1985":"Around 1985 or 1986 in Brewster, New York, six bright white lights hung in formation at the tops of the trees by the garage of a house said to be haunted, then drifted slowly over the road with no sound. Charles Buhler, a NASA engineer, tells Michels he saw them himself after someone took him outside and showed him: \"No sound, no window, nothing.\" For Buhler it was an early sighting of something that moved like no aircraft he knew.","brian-fitzpatrick-s-cattle-mutilation-book":"A book called The Operator, by a man named Brian Fitzpatrick, holds what may be the biggest revelation yet about cattle mutilations, the cases of cattle found dead and strangely cut. That is the view of Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, who tells Michels it is \"an astonishing book\" and \"the biggest reveal about cattle mutilations out there\", though he does not say here what it reveals.","briefing-attendees-rebuild-the-aquarius-briefing-from-memory-1977":"In 1977, the people who sat in on a secret briefing about Project Aquarius, said to be a classified government UFO program, are said to have gone to a motel afterwards and written down from memory everything they could recall of it. Eric W. Davis, a scientist who has worked with the Pentagon, tells Michels this as he heard it, citing a man named Alonzo McDonald and the UFO researcher Bill Moore, and he ties it to the written Aquarius briefing document, which on this account would be a reconstruction of a real briefing.","briefing-dia-head-general-patrick-hughes-1990":"In the 1990s, Steven Greer, who says UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, briefed Patrick Hughes, a three-star general who headed the Defense Intelligence Agency, the Pentagon's military intelligence service. Greer tells Michels this as something he did himself. If true, he had his case heard at the very top of military intelligence.","briefing-on-engineered-isotopic-ratios-of-recovered-craft-2019":"A former US intelligence officer named David Grusch says that, at some point between 2019 and 2022, he was briefed on material from recovered craft with isotopic ratios, the mix of different forms of the same element, so unusual that they \"would have to be engineered\" to reach those levels. In another conversation with Michels he puts it as what the analysts themselves told him: heavy elements such as europium and californium, arranged in \"atomic arrangements that make like no sense.\" If true, it would mean the material was made on purpose by someone, not formed in nature.","briefing-senate-intelligence-committee-investigators-unaware":"Senior investigators for the Senate Intelligence Committee did not know that the US had certain secret facilities until they were briefed. Steven Greer, a doctor who has long campaigned for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, tells Michels he did the briefing himself, which would mean such sites are hidden even from the people meant to oversee them.","briefing-the-institute-of-noetic-sciences-board-1990":"In the 1990s, a UFO researcher named Steven Greer says the astronaut Edgar Mitchell invited him to brief the board of the Institute of Noetic Sciences, a research body that studies consciousness, at one of its retreats. Greer tells Michels this as something he did himself, and also names the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée and Senator Claiborne Pell in connection with it. It is told to show that people with real standing took his UFO work seriously enough to hear him out.","briefings-to-senate-intelligence-committee-members-and-agenc-2023":"A man named Jordan Jose, who says a secret UFO programme took him as a child, says that since about 2023 he has briefed past members of the Senate Select Intelligence Committee and \"numerous three-letter agencies,\" handing over \"the locations, the names, etc.\" He tells Michels this himself, in a story that touches on David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who testified to Congress about hidden UFO programmes. If true, it would mean officials have had his specific leads in hand.","brigham-s-senate-testimony-with-rfk-on-eisenhower-s-warning-2020":"A guest named Brigham Buhler says he testified before the US Senate alongside RFK, and that the testimony drew on the warning Eisenhower gave in his farewell address. Buhler tells Michels this as something he took part in: \"that's when I testified with RFK in front of the US Senate.\" He brings it up in a talk about the drug industry's past and human experiments, as a sign that Eisenhower's old warning still applies to powerful interests today.","bright-light-over-catalina-identified-as-the-iss-2021":"During a 2021 field mission over the Catalina Channel off California, a team of scientists watching the sky for unexplained objects saw a bright light, and then \"discovered that that bright light was the space station\". The physicist Kevin Knuth, who was there with the physicist Matthew Szydagis, tells Michels this, and says the mission was written up in a scientific paper. He gives it as an example of a sighting that turned out to have an ordinary explanation.","bringing-tom-delonge-and-lue-elizondo-to-the-zanesville-meta-2017":"In 2017, Chris Bledsoe took the musician Tom DeLonge and a former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo to see what the story calls the Zanesville metal, material tied to a claimed recovery of a downed unknown craft, though he is unsure whether the place was in Pennsylvania or Ohio. Bledsoe, who says UFOs are drawn to him, tells Michels this as something he did, and also names Hal Puthoff and Timothy Taylor in the account.","british-and-french-governments-release-ufo-files-2007":"Around 2007 and 2008 the British and French governments released UFO documents that had previously been classified. The UFO investigator and filmmaker James Fox recalls this to Michels from memory as a moment he lived through, without saying what was in the files. He brings it up as a sign that other governments were opening their records.","british-gravity-research-memo-stamped-wright-patterson-with":"A British memo on gravity research circulates with about five pages missing, says the researcher Greg Bishop. He says the copy he saw was stamped Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, in Ohio. According to Bishop, a legend surrounds the missing pages: the text seems to say something \"was achieved in the mid-century\" and then cuts off, which would mean someone got somewhere with controlling gravity decades ago.","broad-haven-students-draw-silver-disc":"At Broad Haven in the UK, ten students are said to have seen a silver disc and then drawn pictures of it, and the drawings were identical. Marik von Rennenkampff brings it up in a debate on the show with a UFO skeptic. He gives it in a single breath, with no date and no source, as a case where separate witnesses agreed.","broken-bleed-line-cuts-f-18-control-lines-on-saudi-arabia-tr":"On a training run over a range in Saudi Arabia, a broken bleed line, a pipe carrying very hot air from the engine, acted \"like a plasma beam just cutting things inside the jet\" and cut through an F-18 fighter's control lines. Ryan Graves, a Navy fighter pilot, tells Michels this as something he lived through, a story about how close a jet can come to disaster.","brookhaven-stony-brook-and-renaissance-as-a-slush-fund-for-s":"The idea is that institutions on Long Island, New York, namely Brookhaven National Laboratory, nearby Stony Brook University and the firm Renaissance, served as a hidden money channel for secret science. Michels raises it himself, as a question to a guest, starting from the point that Brookhaven is home to the Cosmotron, which he calls the largest particle accelerator in the US. If true, it would mean secret research was paid for through money that looked like ordinary academic and private funding.","brookings-report-recommends-possible-withholding-of-et-artif-1959":"In 1959 NASA commissioned the Brookings Institution to write a report on the effects of space exploration, published in 1960, which a guest named Maaneli Derakhshani reads as recommending that any alien artifacts found might be kept from the public. He tells Michels about it as early evidence that hiding such a discovery was considered from the very start of NASA. No words of the report itself are given, so the reading rests on how he sums it up.","brother-victor-pushed-down-an-elevator-shaft-1968":"In 1968, at the Russian Orthodox Church Outside Russia at Park Avenue and 93rd Street in Manhattan, a man known as Brother Victor was pushed down an elevator shaft and had both legs broken, according to the writer Peter Levenda. Levenda tells Michels this as something from his own time around the church, without saying clearly who \"they\" were who did it, and it is told as a sign of how dangerous that world could be.","brought-into-the-king-s-chamber-by-timothy-hogan-2020":"A man named Jay Anderson says that in the 2020s he was taken into the King's Chamber, the granite room deep inside the Great Pyramid of Giza, by a man named Timothy Hogan. Anderson tells Michels, \"I was brought into the King's Chamber by him personally,\" as a sign of the access Hogan gave him.","brown-brothers-harriman-versailles-reparations-and-union-ban-1918":"Between the end of the First World War and 1939, according to a man named Dan Sheehan, American bankers turned the German war debts set by the Treaty of Versailles into a channel that helped pay for Hitler. He tells Michels that \"in 1924 George Herbert Walker leaves Brown Brothers Harriman,\" a New York bank, and sets up the Union Bank of New York, \"completely capitalized\" by Brown Brothers Harriman partners. Sheehan says that bank funded the German industrialist Fritz Thyssen, and he ties in the American diplomat Robert Lansing and the Dulles brothers, John Foster and Allen. If true, leading American financiers helped finance the Nazi rise.","brown-founds-nicap-hillenkoetter-on-board-1956":"A scientist named Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have founded NICAP, the National Investigations Committee on Aerial Phenomena, in 1956, a group Michels calls the first civilian UFO tracking programme. Michels tells it himself in two episodes: in one he says Brown created it after returning from the International Geophysical Year, a worldwide science effort, and names a man called Roscoe Hillenkoetter in connection with it. In the other he simply calls it the first civilian UFO tracker and leaves out Hillenkoetter and the science year. It matters to Michels because it would put a government-linked scientist at the start of public UFO research.","brown-has-met-someone-who-saw-a-ufo-in-a-hangar":"Matthew Brown, introduced on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, says he has met somebody he thinks has seen a UFO kept in a hangar. Michels asks him directly, and Brown answers simply, \"Yes.\" If true, someone he knows has seen a craft in human hands.","brown-meets-teller-and-lemay-driven-by-sarbacher-1950":"In the 1950s, the story goes, the American researcher Thomas Townsend Brown moved among powerful men such as the physicist Edward Teller and the Air Force general Curtis LeMay, and was often driven home in a black Cadillac by a man his daughter, Linda Brown, later came to know as Robert Sarbacher. Michels tells it in his own summary, crediting the book The Man Who Mastered Gravity by Paul Schatzkin and Linda Brown's memory of the car and its driver. If true, it would put Brown close to the inner circle of postwar weapons science.","brown-repeats-the-vacuum-experiment-at-bahnson-s-institute-o-1950":"In the 1950s, Michels says, the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown repeated his experiment with charged devices in a vacuum at the Institute of Field Physics in North Carolina, a lab backed by a businessman named Agnew Bahnson. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source. It matters to him because a result that held up in a vacuum would suggest the effect was not just moving air.","brown-s-1953-winter-haven-proposal":"The claim is that a 1953 plan written by the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, known as the Winter Haven proposal after the Florida town, describes the same kind of program that insiders today say the US runs to study and build UFO-like craft. Brown studied how electric charge might create a pushing force, which his followers link to controlling gravity. Michels makes the claim himself in a documentary about Brown, saying the 1953 proposal 'almost exactly resembles how all insiders describe America's secret UFO program.' His co-host Jack, who goes by an alias and says he works in defense and aerospace, brings it up in a later episode. He adds that in the proposal Brown 'says Oliver Heaviside had a very adequate theory of gravity back in the 1890s and we would be right to listen to him.' Heaviside was a British physicist. Neither teller quotes the proposal's own words on how the work should be organized, so how closely it matches today's insider accounts rests on Michels' reading. If he is right, the outline of a secret gravity program was on paper by 1953.","brown-s-electrometers-pick-up-anomalies-matching-the-siderea-1985":"The inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have built devices he called electrometers, which were picking up something, \"maybe neutrinos.\" The readings showed oddities that followed sidereal time, which is time kept by the stars rather than the Sun, and he kept the work going on Catalina Island, California, until his death around 1985. Linda Brown tells this in a documentary on Brown made by Michels, passing on what Brown said and did. If true, it would mean his instruments were catching a signal from beyond the solar system.","brown-s-experiments-replicated-worldwide-including-in-japan":"The high-voltage experiments of a researcher named Thomas Townsend Brown, which Michels links to antigravity, are said to have been repeated by others all over the world, as far away as Japan. Michels says this himself, without naming who did the replications, as a sign that Brown's results were more than a one-off.","brown-s-lost-1929-paper-the-minor-quantum":"In 1929, according to Michels, the American researcher Thomas Townsend Brown wrote a paper called The Minor Quantum, which is now said to be lost. Michels brings it up in a documentary episode about Brown, a man he presents as a scientist who built UFO-like craft long before others claimed to, and he says nothing about what the paper contained beyond its title and year.","brown-s-navy-resignation-letter-1942":"In 1942, the story goes, the scientist Townsend Brown got a letter telling him to move his equipment from the University of Philadelphia to the Navy at Norfolk, Virginia. The researcher Jan Lundquist tells it in a documentary about Brown and credits a document she calls Brown's resignation letter, though she gives only her own summary of it, not its words. She tells it as a step in Brown's wartime work moving into Navy hands.","brown-s-paper-the-structure-of-space-written-at-martin-corpo-1940":"An inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown, whom Michels presents as a scientist who built UFO-like craft long before others claimed to, wrote a scientific paper called The Structure of Space in the early 1940s while working at the Martin Corporation, an aircraft maker. Michels tells this himself and notes that the paper is one piece of Brown's work that can still be read. He offers it as a written record of Brown's ideas about space and propulsion.","brown-s-patents-bought-by-sharper-image-for-ion-air-filters":"Michels says he believes the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown was behind patents that the gadget retailer Sharper Image later bought for its ion air filters. He offers this himself, hedged with an \"I believe\" and without naming a source. If true, it would mean ideas from Brown's unusual electrical research ended up in an ordinary consumer product.","brown-s-saucer-shaped-gravitator-and-founding-of-nicap-1950":"In the 1950s a scientist named Thomas Townsend Brown built a device he called a gravitator, meant to work against gravity, and Michels says it \"looked exactly like a flying saucer.\" Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and in another episode adds that Brown went on to form NICAP, the National Investigations Committee on Aerial Phenomena, which he calls the first civilian UFO study group. If true, a man building saucer-shaped machines was also leading the public study of saucers.","brown-s-task-force-captures-miethe-1945":"In Germany in 1945, a task force that included the American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown reportedly helped the Allies capture Richard Miethe, whom Michels calls a mysterious high-voltage physicist. Michels tells this himself, as something reported, without naming a source. If true, it would tie Brown to the postwar hunt for German scientists and their work.","brown-s-work-continues-in-black-aerospace-subcompartments":"The work of Thomas Townsend Brown, a researcher whose high-voltage experiments Michels ties to antigravity, is said to live on inside secret US aerospace programmes, in small sealed-off compartments where only a few people are cleared to explore it further. Michels offers this as his own belief, saying it survives \"in the form of whispers,\" and names no source.","brown-s-work-in-the-b-2-and-floyd-odlum-s-funding-1960":"Michels says a businessman named Floyd Odlum, whom he calls the majority owner of the aircraft maker Northrop, funded the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown's work in the 1960s and paid Brown about 2,000 or 4,000 dollars a month (Michels does not remember which), and then paid Brown's family the same amount until he died. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as part of a story that links Brown's work to Northrop's B-2 stealth bomber. If true, it would mean a major aircraft company kept quietly backing Brown's research for decades.","brown-s-work-witnessed-by-top-government-and-military-figure":"The work of an inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown, whom Michels links to antigravity propulsion, was seen by people at the very top of government and the military, Michels says. He draws this from The Man Who Mastered Gravity, a book by Paul Schatzkin, and names such as the physicist Edward Teller and the aviation inventor Bill Lear come up in it. Michels tells it to argue that Brown cannot easily be dismissed as an amateur crank.","brown-spent-his-career-looking-for-a-nuclear-power-source":"The scientist Thomas Townsend Brown, known for his anti-gravity experiments, is said to have spent the rest of his career searching for a power source that was nuclear. Michels mentions it himself, as a passing aside while discussing Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft, and he names no source. To Michels it is a curious echo of the power questions in Lazar's story.","brown-spoke-of-biblical-ufos-and-aliens-with-his-family":"The inventor Townsend Brown is said to have talked constantly, behind closed doors and with his family, about UFOs in the Bible and about aliens. Michels says he learned this from Brown's daughter, Linda Brown, and passes it on as hers. If so, the inventor held private views on the subject that he kept to his home.","brown-tells-josh-reynolds-the-philadelphia-experiment-was-in-1943":"The Philadelphia Experiment is a tale of a secret Navy test in Philadelphia in 1943 that later became a book and a movie, and according to Michels, the researcher Thomas Townsend Brown told his friend and close co-worker Josh Reynolds that both were 'greatly inflated'. Michels passes this on as what Reynolds was told, and it matters because it would mean someone said to be close to the events played the legend down.","brown-terminated-by-navy-joins-martin-near-skunkworks-1942":"The inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, who believed electricity could affect gravity, was suddenly let go by the Navy in 1942 'with no explanation, despite strong performance', and then went to the aircraft maker Martin in the same year that Skunk Works, the secret aircraft design shop led by the engineer Kelly Johnson, was formed in Burbank. Michels says this himself, reading from Brown's naval records and from his FBI file of 1942 and 1943; once he gives the year as '1942 or three', and in two versions he does not mention the firing at all. He tells it to suggest that Brown's work was being moved to secret aircraft projects, though by his own account the records show the firing and the new job, and say nothing about why.","brown-university-shooting-2025":"A man named Claudio Neves Valente carried out a planned shooting at the Barus and Holley building of Brown University in Providence, Rhode Island, on Saturday, December 13, 2025, according to Michels, who says Valente \"executed his plan\" that day. Michels tells it himself in an episode about American scientists who have gone missing, drawing on a Justice Department transcript of videos Valente made.","brown-used-ion-wind-work-to-cover-for-electrogravitics":"The inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, Michels says, used his work on ion wind, where charged particles push air along, as a cover for his work on electrogravitics, the idea that high voltage could produce a gravity-like push. Michels offers this as his own view, without naming a source. If true, Brown's ordinary-looking research would have hidden a more radical goal.","bruce-erickson-s-theory-that-ransone-was-a-spy-1970":"A woman named Valerie Ransone may have been a kind of spy, according to a theory put forward by a man named Bruce Erickson about events in the 1970s. A researcher who comes on the show, and who is not named, passes the idea on from Erickson. The episode is about a CIA scientist said to have contacted an \"alien council\", and the researcher says Erickson \"speculates that she was sort of um like a spy\", without saying who she might have worked for or what she did. If it were so, her part in that story would look quite different.","bruce-maccabee-judged-the-arv-photographs-hoaxes":"The UFO photo analyst Bruce Maccabee is said to have judged a set of ARV photographs to be fakes. ARV stands for \"alien reproduction vehicle\", a supposed human-built craft copied from alien designs. The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels he does not remember whether Maccabee actually examined the pictures, only that Maccabee had said \"they definitely looked like hoaxes to him.\"","bruno-sammaciccia-the-nordics-with-underground-bases-and-the-1950":"In Italy in the 1950s, a man named Bruno Sammaciccia and his associates are said to have been taken up by \"Nordics\", the name UFO lore gives to tall, human-looking beings, who kept underground bases. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells it on the show as a case he covers in his work Closer Encounters, summarising it without naming the source he drew on, and the story is also tied to a group called the Contrari, which the telling does not explain. If true, it would mean such beings lived hidden beneath the ground and dealt directly with ordinary people.","bryce-dewitt-paper-on-superconductors-and-gravitational-drag-1968":"In 1968, Michels says, the physicist Bryce DeWitt wrote a paper about superconductors and gravitational drag. Michels raises it himself while questioning Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist known for patents on unusual propulsion, and he cites it as a sign that serious physicists were already looking at ways of working with gravity in the late 1960s.","bryce-dewitt-s-institute-funds-townsend-brown-s-gravity-expe":"The physicist Bryce DeWitt, who helped found the study of quantum gravity, ran an Institute of Field Physics in North Carolina that Michels says was also funding the high-voltage gravity experiments of a researcher named Thomas Townsend Brown at the same time. Michels tells it across several episodes and adds details as he goes: that Wright Field, an Air Force base, sponsored a 1957 conference of top physicists at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill while the university backed Brown's antigravity work, that the Air Force general Curtis LeMay was a sponsor, that there is a video of Brown popping champagne in the lab, and that the institute was a CIA outpost studying antigravity. If so, mainstream physics and secret gravity research would have shared the same rooms and money.","buchanan-bilocates-to-cydonia-on-mars-later-matched-to-a-pho":"Lyn Buchanan, an Army sergeant trained in remote viewing, the military's practice of trying to describe faraway places with the mind alone, says he once felt himself actually present on Mars, in the Cydonia region (he calls it \"the Sedona region\"), and that what he saw was later matched to a photo at the home of Hal Puthoff, the physicist who led early remote viewing research. Buchanan tells Michels this happened to him, the first time he felt himself in two places at once.","buchanan-finds-the-mount-hayes-base-automated-and-empty-of-p":"A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says that when he \"did\" Mount Hayes, a base in Alaska, as one of his assignments, he found that its equipment was now automated and no people were there. Buchanan tells Michels this as his own work, naming a man called Skip Atwater in connection with it, and gives no date. If true, it would mean he had gathered real information about a hidden site.","buchanan-remote-views-alien-mining-bases-on-the-moon":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant trained in remote viewing (a claimed way of seeing distant places with the mind alone, once studied by the US military), says he used it to look at alien bases on the Moon. He tells Michels that for most of them, \"what I got was minerals,\" which he takes to mean the bases were there for mining.","buchanan-s-30-year-database-scoring-remote-viewers-accuracy":"About 30 years ago Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, started a database of remote viewers' work, remote viewing being the claimed ability to describe distant places or things with the mind alone, and he has kept it ever since to score how accurate they are. Buchanan tells Michels about it as his own project. If it holds up, it would be a long record of how well the practice works.","buchanan-s-eyes-turn-brown-while-he-accesses-saddam":"While Lyn Buchanan, an Army sergeant in the military's remote viewing unit at Fort Meade, a group that tried to spy on distant targets with the mind, was focused on Saddam Hussein, a woman watching told him, \"Your eyes turned brown and your voice deepened.\" Buchanan tells Michels this happened to him, as if he had briefly taken on something of his target. Michels later repeats it on another episode, saying only that Buchanan told him on the show this had happened to him, without mentioning Saddam or the colour.","buck-charlson-inventor-of-power-steering-and-the-gerotor-bac-1964":"Around 1964 in Minnesota, a researcher of psychic phenomena named Jeffrey Mishlove says that one of the major supporters of his work was a man named Buck Charlson, who invented power steering and the Gerotor, a type of pump. Mishlove tells Michels this from his own experience, to show that an accomplished engineer backed his research.","bud-hopkins-videotaped-on-ufos-dragging-us-into-21st-century-1987":"Around 1987, Budd Hopkins, a well-known researcher of alien abduction reports, said on camera that UFOs are \"dragging us kicking and screaming into the science of the 21st century.\" The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels he filmed Hopkins saying it himself, for a documentary. It is a claim that the subject would force science into new territory.","budd-hopkins-abductees-all-drew-the-same-symbol-2000":"In New York in the 2000s, a group of people who said they had been taken aboard alien craft were each asked to draw what they had seen inside the ship, and they all drew the same symbol from the wall. The filmmaker James Fox tells Michels he was there when it happened, at a session run by Budd Hopkins, a New York researcher who worked with people reporting abductions, and he also names the journalist Leslie Kean. If it is true, people who had never compared notes remembered the same detail.","buddha-s-teaching-of-four-more-inhabited-earth-like-planets":"The Buddha taught 2,600 years ago that our solar system holds four more planets like Earth, where 'practically the same exact people live', says Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former leader of a republic within Russia who tells Michels he was himself taken by aliens. He presents it as the Buddha's own teaching. If it were so, it would mean an ancient religious teacher had spoken of human life on other worlds.","budgets-on-the-order-of-billions-from-trw-and-lockheed-inter":"Secret programmes tied to this subject are said to have run on budgets in the billions of dollars. A scientist named Eric W. Davis, introduced on the show as a Pentagon scientist, says he got that figure from people he interviewed at the defense contractors TRW and Lockheed Martin. \"It was on that order from my interviews with TRW and Lockheed Martin people,\" he tells Michels, and if he is right, it would mean money on that scale has been spent out of public view.","buer-shows-no-brush-discharges-occur-in-high-vacuum":"In a NASA electrostatics lab at the Kennedy Space Center, tests showed \"there's no brush discharges in high vacuum conditions,\" meaning the small sprays of electric sparks that leak off charged objects in air did not appear in near-empty space. Charles Buhler, a NASA scientist who says his team built a working push without propellant, tells Michels this was part of his own work, which matters because it would rule out ordinary sparks as the cause of the force they measured.","buer-shows-particle-charging-depends-on-dynamics-not-size":"How much electric charge a particle picks up depends on how it moves and collides, not on how big it is. That is what Charles Buhler, who worked in NASA's electrostatics lab at Kennedy Space Center, tells Michels, saying he was \"the first to show it has nothing to do with size. It has to do with dynamics.\" He offers it as his own finding from that lab work, and it is part of the background for his later claims about thrust from static electricity.","buga-sphere-given-to-jaime-maussan-2025":"Jesse Michels says that when he visited the Mexican UFO journalist Jaime Maussan in 2025, Maussan had been given a metal object called the Buga Sphere, named after Buga, the Colombian town where it was taken. On one telling Michels describes it as an aluminum object that Maussan had recovered, with more than four minutes of video showing it flying around Buga, and he says it was scanned at Maussan's place. Michels tells this as something he saw himself, and if it is what Maussan says, it would be a flying object that someone could actually hold and examine.","buga-sphere-matched-to-a-9-litre-fuel-tank-2025":"The Buga Sphere, a small metal ball said to have been seen in Buga, Colombia in 2025, may match a common fuel tank, according to the Australian journalist Ross Coulthart. He passes on what a man named Ronnie Vernet found: a fuel tank of one particular shape that holds exactly 9 litres and has holes much like those on the sphere, which a researcher known as Yummy points to as evidence. If the match holds, the sphere would be an ordinary object, not an unexplained one.","buga-sphere-symbols-recognised-by-mario-woods-and-randy-ande-1977":"Michels says he showed photos of the symbols on the Buga sphere, an object reported from Buga, Colombia, to two men who each say they had their own encounters, and the story goes that both recognised them. One was Mario Woods, whom Michels calls a 1977 experiencer at Ellsworth Air Force Base, meaning a man who says something strange happened to him there. The other was Randy Anderson, a man who says he saw alien technology on a military base. If true, the markings would link the Colombian object to older American accounts.","bugged-seattle-hotel-meeting-and-the-journalist-s-death-1947":"In 1947 a meeting in a Seattle hotel, in a story involving a pilot named Kenneth Arnold and a man named Fred Crisman, turned out to be bugged, and a local journalist got a tape of the whole meeting and died soon afterward. The writer Peter Levenda tells this to Michels without naming a source. As he tells it, someone was secretly listening, and the man who had the tape did not live long.","building-owner-s-scientist-boyfriend-told-her-of-a-new-mexic-1950":"In the early 1950s, a very prominent scientist told the woman he was seeing that a spaceship had crashed in New Mexico and that bodies had been recovered. The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels this as he heard it from that woman, by then an unnamed 88-year-old building owner, and a man named Boris Zubov is also named in connection with the account. If true, it would put a senior scientist among those who knew of a crash with bodies.","bulwer-lytton-offered-the-crown-of-greece":"The British writer Edward Bulwer-Lytton, author of the novel The Coming Race, was at one point offered the crown of Greece and turned it down, says the researcher Jason Jorjani, who mentions it in passing without giving a source. Jorjani raises it to show how much standing the novelist had, a man whose fiction he ties to the hidden history he describes to Michels.","burchett-five-ocean-areas-where-craft-emerge":"Tim Burchett, a congressman from Tennessee, is said to have claimed there are five places in the world's oceans where unidentified craft come and go. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan repeats it on the show: Burchett \"recently said\" that five areas in the oceans have been identified where \"these things are coming out of on a regular basis.\" Michels repeats it on several later episodes, calling them \"five hotspots\" under the oceans. Once he says \"five or six deep-water areas\" and puts it as a higher rate of sightings there. If true, it would suggest the craft have bases or routes in the deep sea.","burchett-s-five-to-seven-undersea-bases-claim-traced-to-the":"A congressman named Tim Burchett has spoken of \"five to seven undersea bases\", meaning hidden bases under the ocean, and a retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet thinks he knows where Burchett got the idea. Gallaudet says it goes back to an email written by a commander in naval intelligence, and that \"someone who'd seen that email is the one who talked to Burchett.\" He gives this as his own reading of events and names no one who told him so, and the telling does not say what the email itself contained.","buried-ufo-too-big-to-move":"Somewhere at an undisclosed sensitive site, there is said to be a UFO that was too big to move, so it was buried or had something built on top of it. The journalist Ross Coulthart, who reports on what his sources tell him, says this to Michels on two episodes in the same words: \"I do not resile from saying what I've said previously\", a reference to having said it before, including in connection with Joe Rogan. If true, a crashed craft would be sitting in place under a government facility.","burisch-and-his-wife-s-strange-vlogs":"Dan Burisch, a man known in UFO circles for his claims of secret work, and his wife are said to have made some very strange home videos. An online UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels that the YouTuber Chris Ramsay showed him \"the most bizarre videos\" of the couple while the two were hanging out, but he does not describe what is in them.","burkhard-heim-and-martin-s-rias-anti-gravity-outfit":"A scientist named Burkhard Heim was brought over to the US and, Michels thinks, worked with the Martin Corporation before it merged with Lockheed, in a Martin research unit called RIAS that the story describes as an anti-gravity outfit. Michels tells this himself, hedged with \"I think\" and without naming a source. If true, it would mean an American aircraft company was hiring foreign scientists to work on anti-gravity.","burkhard-heim-at-lockheed-and-extended-heim-theory-1950":"A German physicist named Burkhard Heim, whose ideas later grew into a physics theory carrying his name, is said to have moved to the United States and worked for Lockheed Martin in the 1950s, where he \"was renowned as just a total genius.\" Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, while talking about Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft. If true, it would place an unusual theorist of gravity inside a major American aerospace company during the years of early anti-gravity research.","burles-sees-grusch-on-the-news-and-contacts-him-2023":"In 2023 a congressman named Eric Burlison was watching the news when David Grusch came on, and he then got in touch with him. Grusch is a whistleblower who that year testified before Congress about secret government UFO programmes. Burlison tells Michels this himself, as the start of his own part in pushing the issue in Washington.","burn-marks-on-the-northeast-side-of-the-colossi-of-memnon":"The Colossi of Memnon, a pair of huge ancient stone statues in Egypt, have burn marks only on their northeast side, according to a researcher named Matt LaCroix. He tells Michels this, without naming a source, on an episode about something he says happened around 12,000 BC, and he presents the one-sided marks as a sign of some event that came from a single direction.","burned-memo-project-aquarius-references-zodiac":"One of two papers that UFO researchers call the \"burned memo\" and the Project Aquarius document mentions a programme called Project Zodiac. The researcher who goes by UAPGerb says so on the show, citing a man named Lee Graham, but admits \"I can't remember which\" of the two it is. If true, it would tie Project Zodiac to the same paper trail as the other two.","burned-out-campfire-shows-three-hours-of-missing-time-1976":"In 1976, at the Smith Brook campsite on Eagle Lake in Maine, a group of campers came back to shore to find their big campfire burned out, and they took it as proof that about three hours had gone by that none of them remembered. Charlie Foltz, one of the campers, tells Michels this himself: \"that was a 3hour period of time. That is that missing time that everybody talks about,\" meaning the gap in memory that people who report alien encounters often describe. If true, the dead fire is a physical clock showing that time really went missing.","burnt-tree-and-bare-elongated-circle-at-the-crash-site-1996":"Near the Brazilian town of Varginha in 1996, people say a craft came down and strange beings were seen. A man named Carlos de Souza says that at the crash site, the tree the ship had run into was \"burnt to a crisp\", and that the ground there held a long, bare, oval patch. He tells Michels he saw this himself, mentions a woman named Claudia Kovu in the telling, and offers the burnt tree and bare ground as physical traces that a craft really struck there.","burroughs-suffers-physical-effects-1980":"John Burroughs, a US Air Force airman who was one of the witnesses to strange events in Rendlesham Forest in England in 1980, later suffered physical and psychological effects, Michels says. Michels tells this himself in an episode about UFOs near nuclear bases, without naming a source. If true, it would suggest whatever the airmen met could harm the people near it.","bush-and-lbj-briefed-on-jfk-assassination-at-clint-murchison-1963":"In 1963, George H. W. Bush and Lyndon Johnson were, in this account, briefed on the assassination of John F. Kennedy at a meeting on the ranch of a man named Clint Murchison, the one in Dallas, the teller stresses, not the one near Waco. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells it on the show as something passed on to him, and J. Edgar Hoover is named among those tied to the story. If true, two future presidents would have been told something about the killing in private.","busko-s-work-rules-out-plate-defects-in-the-transients-1957":"Strange brief points of light on photographic sky plates from the late 1950s, which the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel calls transients, were not caused by chemical stains or flaws in the plates but by real light passing through the telescope's optics, according to work by the astronomer Ivo Busko. Michels relays this second-hand, saying it 'apparently' proves the point. If so, it would mean something real was shining in the sky when those plates were taken.","buzz-aldrin-a-33rd-degree-freemason-per-his-book-1960":"The astronaut Buzz Aldrin says in one of his own books that he is a 33rd degree Scottish Rite Freemason, the top rank in that branch of the Masons, according to a guest named Maaneli Derakhshani. He tells Michels this without naming which book, in an episode about secrets surrounding the Moon landings. Its point is that one of the first men on the Moon belonged to a secretive fraternal order.","buzz-aldrin-cancels-his-interview-in-monte-carlo-1997":"The filmmaker James Fox says he chased the astronaut Buzz Aldrin around the world for an on-camera interview about UFOs, finally got him to Monte Carlo, and then Aldrin told him at the last minute, \"I'm not doing the interview.\" Fox tells it on the show more than once as something he lived through, placing it in the late 1990s at the Monte Carlo Hotel and crediting Paul Allen with a part in setting it up, while Michels, retelling it, puts it in the 2000s and adds Aldrin's line, \"What would my testimony do anyways?\" If it is true, a moonwalker came close to speaking on the subject and then pulled back.","buzz-aldrin-chased-a-disc-in-a-military-jet-1950":"Buzz Aldrin, the astronaut, is said to have chased a disc-shaped UFO while flying a military jet, years before he went into space. The UFO investigator James Fox says he heard it from Aldrin's sister, Fay Ann Potter, who told him: \"my brother told me about a UFO encounter that he had while piloting a military jet.\" Michels repeats Fox's account on two later shows but tells it a little differently each time: once he places it over Japan and says the sister spoke of two sightings, and once he puts it in World War II.","buzz-aldrin-ordered-to-leave-armstrong-behind-on-the-moon-if-1969":"On the 1969 Apollo 11 Moon landing, Buzz Aldrin was supposedly under orders that if Neil Armstrong went out first and anything happened to him, Aldrin \"was not supposed to play the hero\", meaning he was not to try to rescue him. The UFO documentary maker James Fox tells Michels this, passing on what he was told, and names a woman called Fay Ann Potter among his sources. If true, it would mean NASA had planned coldly for losing a man on the Moon.","buzz-aldrin-s-apollo-11-sighting-retold-on-larry-king-live-1969":"The Apollo 11 astronaut Buzz Aldrin's account of something he saw during the 1969 Moon mission came up on the television talk show Larry King Live, with Aldrin in the studio. A skeptic and writer named Michael Shermer tells Michels he was on that show with Aldrin and \"a couple of the UFO people\". He brings up Aldrin's case as an example of how a sighting gets retold, which matters because the sighting is often cited as an astronaut's report of a UFO.","buzz-aldrin-s-masonic-lodge-on-the-moon":"The Apollo 11 astronaut Buzz Aldrin, a Freemason, is said to have set up a Masonic lodge on the Moon, called Tranquility Lodge 2000 and chartered by the Grand Lodge of Texas. Michels tells it on several episodes in different forms: once as Aldrin staking out the Moon with a Masonic flag, alongside NASA's launch pad 33, and once citing the Masonic Grand Lodge of Ohio as saying the mission set up a branch body of the lodge there. He raises it as a sign of Masonic symbolism woven into the space programme.","buzz-aldrin-s-ufo-sightings-and-james-fox-s-aborted-intervie-1969":"Buzz Aldrin, the second man to walk on the Moon, is said to have told his sister that he saw a \"foo fighter\", a name pilots used for unexplained lights seen in flight, while flying fighters in Japan, and that he saw UFOs on the way to the Moon in 1969. Michels passes this on from the UFO investigator James Fox, who had it from Aldrin's sister, and the story also concerns an interview Fox sought with Aldrin that never happened. In a later episode Michels tells it differently, dating the sighting to World War II, placing Fox's pursuit of Aldrin in Monte Carlo in the late 1990s and leaving out the Moon.","buzz-aldrin-tells-conan-o-brien-the-landing-footage-was-anim-1969":"The astronaut Buzz Aldrin once told the talk show host Conan O'Brien that footage of the 1969 Moon landing was animation, according to a guest named Maaneli Derakhshani, who points Michels to 'this particular exchange' between the two men. Derakhshani raises it as something worth a closer look, since it would suggest some of the famous landing images were not filmed on the Moon.","byrd-took-the-sixth-floor-depository-window-for-his-trophy-r-1963":"After the 1963 shooting of President Kennedy in Dallas, a man named D. Harold Byrd is said to have taken the window out of the sixth floor of the book depository building and put it in the trophy room of his house. Dan Sheehan, a guest who talks with Michels about UFOs and the Kennedy killing, tells it as one more thing Byrd \"did\". He names no source for it.","c-17-drops-off-a-mystery-box-at-a-guarded-north-dakota-hanga-2012":"In 2012 a C-17, a large military cargo plane, is said to have landed in North Dakota in the middle of the night and dropped off a box at a heavily guarded hangar, with the suggestion that it held something recovered from a crash. A guest named Dylan Borland passes this on from a member of the military's security forces he calls Raven, who described \"a situation in North Dakota where a C17 landed, they dropped off a box. It was this whole ordeal in the middle of the night.\" The telling does not say what was in the box.","c-d-jackson-released-the-zapruder-film-with-frames-missing-1963":"After Kennedy was shot in 1963, Life magazine got hold of the Zapruder film, the home movie that is the clearest footage of the killing, and is said to have put out only black and white stills with key frames missing. Michels tells it himself without naming a source. He says that \"instead of releasing it in full, they only published black and white stills with certain key frames missing,\" and that the film was held back into the 1970s. The researcher Jeremy Riss says on another episode that the man in charge of putting the film out was Charles Douglas Jackson, known as C.D. Jackson. Riss also names a man called Gordon Gray in the same story. They raise it because, if true, one man close to these circles decided what the public saw of the assassination.","c-d-jackson-visits-betty-and-barney-hill-1961":"Two weeks after Betty and Barney Hill, a New Hampshire couple, had what Michels calls their famous 1961 encounter, a man named Charles Douglas \"C.D.\" Jackson showed up at their house, according to Michels. Michels tells this himself on three episodes without naming a source: once he links Jackson to the magazine publisher Henry Luce, and once he describes Jackson as a man who ran psychological warfare and was deeply involved in MK Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control research programme, though there he does not give the two-week timing. If true, it would mean a psychological warfare figure was taking a close and quick interest in the case.","cahn-behind-flater-s-complaint-1953":"In Denver in 1953 a complaint by a man named Herman Flader, in a story that reaches J. Edgar Hoover, was really set in motion by a journalist named JP Khan, because it was Khan who contacted Flader. A researcher named Scott Ramsey says this to Michels without naming a source, in an episode about a reported crash that left sixteen alien bodies. If true, the complaint did not come from Flader on his own but was steered by Khan.","california-attorney-general-asks-fbi-to-investigate-newton-1951":"On April 25, 1951, the California Attorney General's office contacted the FBI to look into a man named Silas Newton, questioning his claims to be doing secret government research and accusing him of taking part in a hoax, according to Michels. He tells it from the FBI's files on Newton and his partner Leo GeBauer, in an episode about stories of crashed craft and alien bodies, and he names a man called JP Khan as the one who set the inquiry in motion. It matters because Newton's claims were among the early crash stories, and the files show officials doubted him at the time.","caliph-al-mamun-s-raiders-find-the-great-pyramid-empty":"When Arab raiders led by the Caliph al-Mamun broke into the Great Pyramid, they are said to have found it empty. A researcher named Graham Hancock tells Michels this, without naming a source, on an episode where he argues that the pyramid was a kind of gateway for consciousness. An empty pyramid matters to that argument because it suggests the building was not a tomb.","call-cutting-out-while-pitching-lazar-interview":"Michels says that while he was on a phone call talking about doing an interview with Bob Lazar, a man who has long said he worked on alien craft for the government, the line kept cutting out right as he said it. He tells this himself, as something that happened to him, in a conversation with a guest named Dave Rossi. The point is the timing, the kind of detail people mention when they suspect someone is listening.","call-from-a-metallic-alien-voice-1970":"A man is said to have picked up the phone and heard a metallic voice that seemed to belong to some alien intelligence. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells it on an episode about the researcher Andrija Puharich, saying simply that 'he gets this call from a metallic voice that was some sort of, you know, alien intelligence,' and places it in the 1970s. Michels, in the same episode, tells a fuller version and names the man as the physicist Jack Sarfatti. Michels dates the call earlier, to the 1950s, and says it came 'from the future.' According to Michels, the voice told Sarfatti 'you're going to meet the others' and that he would end up working on, essentially, building UFOs. Michels credits a book he calls The Hippies Saved Physics and mentions the physicist Hal Puthoff alongside the story. Both men are passing on accounts they have read, not their own experience. If true, it would mean someone or something reached out by phone to steer a young scientist toward secret work on flying craft.","call-to-art-bell-show-and-emails-with-an-author-who-couldn-t":"Richard Barth, a former guard at a nuclear missile site who says he was abducted by aliens, says he once called in to Coast to Coast AM, a late-night American radio show about the paranormal hosted by Art Bell, and later traded emails with an author about his experience. He tells Michels the author dropped him: \"he couldn't include me any further books because he couldn't collaborate it,\" meaning the author could not find anything to back up Barth's account. Barth tells it himself, as part of how he tried and failed to get his story taken up.","call-to-space-command-about-a-classified-extraterrestrial-th-1980":"In the 1980s the scientist John Brandenburg says he phoned someone at Space Command, the US military's space operations command, and said, \"I've heard there is an extraterrestrial threat whose existence is classified. I'd like to help with that program.\" According to Brandenburg, who tells Michels this as his own experience, the man on the line did not deny it but answered, \"Oh, okay. Here, let me see if I can connect you with somebody.\" If true, it would suggest such a programme was something staff took for granted.","callahan-hides-a-copy-of-the-jal-1628-radar-data-from-the-ci-1987":"When the CIA came for the radar records of Japan Air Lines flight 1628, whose crew had reported an unknown object, an official of the FAA named John Callahan is said to have copied the records first and hidden the copies in a box under the table. The researcher Kevin Knuth tells Michels this as he heard it from Callahan, as an example of evidence that would otherwise have been taken away and kept from the public.","called-back-from-leave-through-a-blizzard-before-base-2-duty-1990":"Sometime between 1990 and 1993, a former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman was called back from leave and drove home to base, he says, through \"a blizzard the entire way\". It came just before his duty at a posting he calls Base 2. Sherman tells it himself as part of his account of work he says the NSA gave him, which he describes as communicating with aliens.","calling-george-noory-s-show-while-bob-hastings-was-the-guest":"Richard Barth, a former guard at a US nuclear missile site who says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels that he first talked to the researcher Bob Hastings by calling in to Coast to Coast AM, the late-night radio show about the paranormal hosted by George Noory, while Hastings was the guest. Barth tells it from his own experience, as the way his story first reached someone who collected such accounts.","caltech-astronomer-99-chance-planet-nine-exists-2010":"An astronomer at Caltech said there is a 99 percent chance that a large unseen ninth planet, called Planet Nine, exists in the far outer solar system. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this without naming the astronomer. He gives the evidence as the orbits of Kuiper belt objects, the icy bodies beyond Neptune, which are \"super skewed\" and \"perturbed by something\" big.","camera-battery-and-memory-card-taken-from-locked-locker-2009":"Michael Herrera, who says he saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle, says that in 2009, on a ship at Subic Bay in the Philippines, someone got into his locked locker and took the battery and memory card out of his camera. \"The battery is missing and the memory card is missing,\" he tells Michels, as part of his own account. If it happened as he says, it would mean someone wanted whatever pictures he might have taken gone.","camp-david-napkin-picture-mailed-to-chris-bledsoe-tied-to-ob":"A napkin from Camp David, printed with the presidential seal, was photographed and the picture mailed to Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs appear around him, by a man named Timothy Taylor. That is the account of Bob McGwier (also given as Bob Maguire), a CIA contractor who says he knew both the address and the napkin, and who says the only person the story in that envelope could have been told to was Barack Obama. To him \"the letter to me is ironclad proof that Chris's story was taken all the way to Barack Obama via Tim Taylor\"; in one telling he says he saw the napkin and envelope in a Facebook photo posted by the UFO researcher Grant Cameron. Bledsoe himself tells Michels more bluntly: \"He briefed Obama on me. That's where this come from.\" If so, Bledsoe's experiences would have reached the president.","canadian-scientists-in-the-legacy-program":"Canadian scientists have worked, and still work, inside the legacy program, the secret effort that some say recovers and studies craft of non-human origin. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this as something he has been told, without naming who said it. If true, the program would reach beyond the United States.","candle-flame-frozen-at-the-emitter-s-focal-point-1987":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft in the late 1980s at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, says a colleague named Barry Castilio showed him a working emitter, a part of the craft's power system. Lazar says Castilio put a lit kitchen candle right at the emitter's focal point, powered up the reactor, and the flame stopped flickering. Lazar tells this to Michels as something he saw himself; if true, the device did something strange to whatever sat in its field.","canopy-leak-at-night-80-miles-from-the-carrier":"A Navy fighter pilot named Ryan Graves says that one night he was flying about 80 miles west of his aircraft carrier at around 30,000 feet when his canopy suddenly started leaking. He tells Michels this himself, as one of his own experiences of things going wrong in the cockpit far from the ship.","cape-canaveral-as-cover-for-non-space-projects-1989":"Secret projects that have nothing to do with space can be hidden at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station in Florida, because anything there can be explained away as part of the space program. That is what Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor who worked there from about 1989 to 1999, tells Michels: \"If you put project X on Cape Canaveral Air Force Station they can always say oh well it's just related to the space program.\" If true, it would make the base a natural home for the kind of hidden work he says he glimpsed.","cape-girardeau-crash-with-small-bodies-and-priest-1941":"In 1941, something is said to have crashed near Cape Girardeau, Missouri, leaving small bodies, and a reverend or priest was called to give last rites because one of them was apparently still alive. John Blitch, a scientist who worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, and who says he was abducted himself, tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would mean nonhuman bodies were recovered in the United States years before the better-known cases.","captain-hollanda-lima-s-encounter-with-a-being-in-a-space-su-1977":"A Brazilian Air Force officer, Captain Uyrangê Hollanda Lima, himself saw an alien being in a space suit, and the being gave him a hug, Michels says. The captain is tied to Operation Prato, a Brazilian military operation in the Colares area of Pará state that began in 1977, and Michels cites its report. If true, it would be a first-hand close encounter reported by the officer in charge of an official investigation.","captain-viriato-s-radar-track-at-mach-15-1986":"In Brazil in 1986, an officer Michels calls Captain Viriato is said to have caught an object on radar moving at Mach 15, about fifteen times the speed of sound, in a zigzag pattern. Michels tells it himself, in an episode on Brazilian cases, without naming a source beyond the captain. No known aircraft could fly like that.","captain-warns-sherman-he-won-t-be-allowed-out-1990":"Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that sometime between 1990 and 1993, at a posting he calls Base 2, a captain told him he would not be allowed to leave the work: \"I honestly don't think you're going to be able to get out.\" Sherman tells Michels this from his own experience, and it would mean that once inside such a programme, people were not free to walk away.","captured-german-nuclear-scientists-wiretapped-in-an-english-1945":"In 1945 the physicist Werner Heisenberg and the people on Germany's nuclear program were captured and held in a country house in the English countryside where every room was wiretapped, so their private talk was recorded. Michels tells this himself, from memory (\"I believe\"), in a conversation with the philosopher Jason Jorjani about a Nazi UFO program. He points to the wiretap transcripts as a record of what the scientists actually knew and said among themselves.","captured-german-ss-documents-at-the-national-archives-1930":"The author Peter Levenda says he went to the archives in Washington to look at German documents captured at the end of the Second World War, kept there on reels of microfilm, including material tied to Heinrich Himmler's SS. He tells Michels this from his own research, as the source for what he learned about the Nazi regime's hidden interests, which he treats as a key thread in the larger story.","captured-russian-and-chinese-weapons-shown-underground-at-cr-2014":"In 2014, at Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy site in Indiana, Randy Anderson says he and others were taken underground and told about Russian and Chinese weapon systems that had been captured. Anderson, a man who says he saw alien technology at a military base, tells Michels this as something he witnessed. It is part of his account of what was kept out of sight at the base.","car-crash-outside-the-dark-skies-office-dying-man-s-life-fla-1990":"A car crashed outside the North Hollywood office where the production Dark Skies was being made in the 1990s, and as the man in it was dying, Brent Friedman says he felt \"all of his energy go through my body like his soul\", with the man's life flashing through him. Friedman, a Hollywood writer who worked on Dark Skies, tells Michels he lived this himself. If taken at his word, it is an experience of something passing from a dying person into another.","car-fried-by-energy-weapon-en-route-to-filming-implant-test":"Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies alleged alien implants (small objects people say were placed in their bodies), tells Michels that someone zapped his car with \"some kind of an energy weapon\" as he drove through a canyon to the office of a doctor he calls Dr. Builder, where he meant to film the doctor testing an implant still inside a patient's body. He says a mechanic later told him the car's computer was fried as if hit by an EMP, an electromagnetic pulse that knocks out electronics. If true, someone was trying to stop that test from being recorded.","careful-handling-of-david-grusch-before-he-came-forward-2023":"Before the whistleblower David Grusch went public in 2023 with his claims about secret UFO programmes, his coming forward was handled carefully, and George Knapp and Jeremy Corbell, two figures of the UFO world, were involved in bringing him out. Bryce Zabel, a guest on an episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, tells Michels this as something he knows from those involved. If true, Grusch's appearance was prepared by people already working on the subject rather than arriving out of nowhere.","caretaker-tom-lewis-collapses-during-drilling-as-the-1-6-ghz-2025":"While crews were drilling below the mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, a property famous for reports of strange events, the ranch caretaker Tom Lewis is said to have collapsed just as a radio signal at 1.6 gigahertz showed up on the instruments. The ranch's owner, Brandon Fugal, tells Michels he was there, and says, \"Our caretaker ends up being brought to his knees.\" A second witness speaking on the same episode tells it a bit differently. This witness calls it a cardiac episode, a heart event, and says a man known as Dragon got the caretaker out at once. That account does not name the caretaker or mention the signal. Both describe the same moment in 2025 or 2026 during the digging. If the collapse and the signal were linked, it would mean something under the ranch can harm people when it is disturbed.","cargo-cult-text-written-for-delonge-to-show-insiders-2014":"A writer named Peter Levenda says that around 2014 Tom DeLonge, whom he was working with, asked him to write a short text DeLonge could show to people about \"what we were up to, what we were trying to do\". Levenda tells Michels that this text, which the story connects to the idea of a cargo cult, became \"the very first opening pages\" of their book Sekret Machines: Gods. He tells it as the person who wrote it, so it gives a first-hand look at how the book began as a pitch meant for insiders.","cargo-cults-building-landing-tracks":"Island peoples in the Pacific are said to have built landing strips to call back the aircraft that once came to them, a practice known as a cargo cult. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source, saying cargo cults in French Polynesia build landing tracks for \"presumably some alien craft to return\" in a way rooted in their mythology. The author Peter Levenda tells it differently, describing how in the early 20th century and the Second World War \"planes would land on islands in the South Pacific,\" with no mention of alien craft. A researcher named Jason Simosa repeats it as he heard it from Levenda, placing it in French Polynesia though he admits he is unsure of the geography; the point is that people may remember visitors from the sky in ritual and myth.","carl-jung-s-flying-saucers-1958":"The psychiatrist Carl Jung wrote a book about UFOs, Flying Saucers: A Modern Myth of Things Seen in the Skies, published in 1958. Michels brings it up himself on several episodes, sometimes dating it only to the 1950s. He describes the book as treating saucers as mandalas, which he calls the Sanskrit symbol of psychic completeness, in other words as an image the mind produces. A guest named James Madden, who has read the book, says it shifts as it goes along. Jung starts out treating the saucers as a mass rumor, Madden says, but finds them harder and harder to dismiss, and by the end he is saying, in Madden's words, \"and yet they're on radar.\" The book matters to the tellers because one of the great thinkers about the mind could not fully explain the sightings as a mental effect.","carl-jung-s-scarab-beetle-as-a-time-loop":"The Swiss psychiatrist Carl Jung had a skeptical, very materialist patient who told him she had dreamed of being given a golden scarab beetle, and as she told it, a scarab-like beetle came to the window and landed in the office. Michels tells this himself on three episodes; once he frames it as a kind of time loop, where the future reaches back into the dream, and the other times as a story from therapy with no time loop. For him it hints that dreams can see ahead.","carl-nell-points-to-thomas-townsend-brown-s-exotic-propulsio":"Karl Nell, a former defence industry executive, has told people that to understand the unusual flying technology behind UFO reports, they should watch Michels's video on the researcher Thomas Townsend Brown, Michels says. He tells it twice, once about a talk with a guest who flew helicopters and once later, adding that Nell had run a business with a $2 billion budget and was chief technology officer of a company belonging to the defence firm Northrop. Michels brings it up because it would mean a well-placed insider sees Brown's work on unusual propulsion as the right lead.","carl-pflock-broke-into-a-congressman-s-office-and-took-aztec":"A man named Carl Pflock is said to have broken into a congressman's office and taken material about Aztec, a claimed flying saucer crash that the episode links to sixteen alien bodies. Michels passes this on from a man named Scott Ramsey, saying Ramsey \"also said that that congressman's office was broken into by none other than Carl Pflock.\" The congressman is not named, and no date is given.","carl-sagan-and-the-golden-disc-1970":"Carl Sagan helped put together the Golden Disc, a gold-plated record of sounds and images from Earth sent out into space on a probe in the 1970s. Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official who says he ran a small Pentagon office studying unexplained aerial reports, mentions this to Michels in passing, saying the disc is now out in \"our extra solar system.\"","carl-sagan-as-a-majestic-member-1960":"The astronomer Carl Sagan was a member of Majestic, the secret group said to have handled the UFO question for the US government, according to the philosopher Jason Jorjani. Jorjani tells Michels he is \"pretty convinced\" of it, without naming a source. If true, one of the best-known public voices of science would have been inside the secret.","carl-sagan-calls-brandenburg-why-are-you-causing-this-troubl":"The physicist John Brandenburg, who argues that life on Mars was wiped out by nuclear war, says the famous astronomer Carl Sagan once phoned him after he began speaking publicly about his ideas and asked, \"Brandenburg, why are you causing this trouble?\" Brandenburg tells Michels this happened in the 1980s, while he worked at the Sandia national laboratory, that he felt deeply honored by the call, and that Sagan, like everyone, called him by his surname; Michels has since repeated the story on two other episodes, once without Sagan's words. If it happened as told, it would mean one of the best-known scientists of the day personally leaned on a colleague to stop talking about Mars.","carl-sagan-met-with-kit-green-and-got-into-ufos-1997":"The astronomer Carl Sagan is said to have met often, near the end of his career, with Kit Green, whom Michels describes as a CIA man interested in UFOs, and to have become very interested in UFOs himself. Michels tells this on two episodes, without naming a source, and in one adds that Sagan held covert, possibly UFO-related meetings the public never knew about, which would cut against Sagan's public image as a skeptic.","carl-sagan-s-early-ufo-interest-and-turn-to-skepticism-1940":"The astronomer Carl Sagan grew up writing about UFOs in his diary and argued about them constantly with his mentor, then somehow turned strongly against them, according to Michels. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with the religion scholar Diana Pasulka. He points to Sagan's book The Demon-Haunted World as the mark of that turn and treats the change as a puzzle worth asking about.","carl-stanley-dies-a-month-after-his-bronx-consecration-1965":"In 1965 a man named Carl Stanley was consecrated in a ceremony in the Bronx, went back home to Kentucky and was dead within a month, according to a researcher named Peter Levenda. Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, and he does not say what killed Stanley.","carlin-tunnel-proof-of-concept-scan-2024":"In 2024, the underground scanning method of a researcher named Filippo Biondi was tried on the Carlin Tunnel, a known tunnel, as a test to show it could pick out hidden spaces below the surface. A researcher named Jeffrey Drum shows this scan to Michels (\"here we have the Carlin Tunnel\") without naming a source. It matters because the same method is behind the claim of a vast structure beneath the pyramids of Egypt.","carlos-de-souza-witnesses-the-crash-and-debris-field-1996":"In 1996, near the Brazilian town of Varginha, a craft is said to have crashed on a property called the Molini Farm. A man named Carlos de Souza, whom Michels describes as an ultralight pilot and geography teacher, says he watched it come down. If true, a physical craft fell to earth and people saw the wreckage. De Souza tells Michels himself that he was on the highway when he 'saw an object pass over the road' and noticed it 'was having problems. It was smoking.' He adds that the rear end 'seemed to have bounced' and was 'completely destroyed.' A UFO investigator named James Fox, who interviewed him, tells how de Souza turned off the freeway onto a dirt road, saw the object 'struggling to stay in the air,' and then saw it go 'boom' into the ground. Michels, crediting Fox and the documentary Moment of Contact, describes a cigar-shaped craft like a Tic Tac (a white oval object shaped like the mint) with what looked like a puncture and gas coming out of the back. A witness named Ahmed Foratu adds that a geography teacher, whom he calls 'Professor Kahus,' even touched the wreckage, but 'the police took it from him and ordered it handed over to the army.'","carlos-eire-s-they-flew-and-the-historical-flying-saints":"A respected Yale professor of religious studies named Carlos Eire wrote a book called They Flew, about Christian saints who were reported to rise into the air, among them St. Joseph of Cupertino, Teresa of Avila and Francis of Assisi. Michels brings up the book himself as serious scholarship on these accounts. He cites it to suggest that reports of people defying gravity have a long, documented history.","carlos-struggles-to-find-the-crash-site-in-moment-of-contact-2022":"In the 2022 documentary Moment of Contact, a man named Carlos de Souza, who says he came upon a crashed craft near the Brazilian town of Varginha, goes back to look for the spot and cannot find it, saying it was not there and that the landscape had changed. Michels tells this from the film, in which James Fox also appears. Michels takes it as a sign of honesty: a liar, he says, would simply have pointed and said \"here it is.\"","carlson-has-seen-ufos":"The researcher Randall Carlson says plainly that he has seen UFOs himself. He mentions it in passing to Michels while talking about the parapsychological side of the subject, and gives no time, place or details.","carlson-s-1995-warren-wilson-college-lectures-proposing-a-yo":"A researcher named Randall Carlson says that in 1995 he gave five lectures at Warren Wilson College in North Carolina proposing that a cosmic impact lay behind the Younger Dryas, a sudden return to cold at the end of the last ice age, along with mass extinctions and rapid climate change. Carlson tells Michels this himself, to show he was putting forward the impact idea that early.","carlson-s-masonic-initiation-and-becoming-master-of-his-lodg-1978":"The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels that he became a full Freemason, \"raised\" in the Masons' own word, in March 1979, and that he went on to become master, the elected head, of his lodge in 1990. He tells it about himself, as background for the older traditions and symbols he talks about on the show.","carlson-visits-quarries-exposing-the-black-mat-layer":"The researcher Randall Carlson says he has visited three or four quarries where a dark band in the ground that he calls the black mat layer can be seen exposed in the rock walls. He tells Michels this from his own visits, offering it as something anyone can go and look at for themselves.","caroline-yacht-1933-expedition-and-stephenson-dinner-in-nass":"In 1933, the yacht Caroline, belonging to a man named Eldridge Reeves Johnson, is said to have docked at Nassau in the Bahamas during an expedition, and William Stephenson, who used Nassau as a base of operations, came aboard and had dinner with Johnson. Paul Schatzkin tells it, without naming a source, in a documentary on the show about Townsend Brown, a scientist the film says built UFO-like craft, as part of tracing who was in contact with whom.","carrington-event-solar-storm-1859":"In 1859 a huge solar storm, known as the Carrington event, knocked out electrical lines, and Michels says a storm like it today would take out the entire power grid. He brings it up himself in conversation with a guest, without naming a source, as a sign of how exposed modern life is to the sun.","carter-asks-cia-director-george-h-w-bush-for-ufo-briefing-an-1976":"After winning the 1976 election, and before he had even gone to Washington, Jimmy Carter is said to have sent for the head of the CIA, then George H. W. Bush, to come down to Plains, Georgia, and to have asked for a briefing on UFOs, which he was refused. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells this on the show as an account passed on to him. If true, an incoming president was denied what the CIA knew about UFOs.","carter-praying-with-his-head-on-the-table-after-the-briefing-1977":"After President Jimmy Carter was briefed on UFOs in 1977, he put his head down on the table and prayed, according to Alonzo McDonald, a man said to have confirmed the briefing. The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels that McDonald told this to him and others, including a man named Dan Sheehan, and said it was a habit of Carter's. If true, it would suggest the briefing affected the president deeply.","carter-purge-of-senior-cia-officers-who-ran-legacy-program-1977":"When Jimmy Carter took office in 1977 he purged nearly the entire senior ranks of the CIA, and many of those hundreds of officers were probably running parts of the \"legacy program\", the supposed long-running secret effort to deal with UFOs and their technology. A guest named Matthew Pines puts this to Michels without naming a source, and asks what those officers went on to do. His point is that the secret may have carried on outside official hands.","carter-s-1976-ufo-briefing-by-cia-director-george-h-w-bush":"After Jimmy Carter won the 1976 election, the first thing he did, the story goes, was send for the head of the CIA to come to Plains, Georgia, and brief him on UFOs, and that person turned out to be George H. W. Bush. Michels tells it on the show, crediting a man named Dan Sheehan. On a later show he tells it again without naming Sheehan or Plains, and sounds less sure of the order of events in Bush's career, which would matter because it suggests a new president thought the CIA knew something about UFOs.","casino-skim-money-driven-in-cadillacs-to-fund-the-training-b-1960":"In the early 1960s, money skimmed off casino takings by the mob figures Carlos Marcello and Meyer Lansky was supposedly driven around in Cadillacs to pay for a secret training base, with the trail running through Las Vegas, New Orleans, Miami and Mexico City. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this in a talk about John F. Kennedy and says he knows the man who sold them the Cadillacs, who \"had the big dealership there.\" If true, it would tie organised crime money to secret operations of the Kennedy years.","catalina-as-a-ufo-hotspot":"Catalina, an island off the coast of Southern California, is said to be a hotspot for UFOs that move between sea and sky. Michels tells this himself in two episodes, drawing on Undersea UFO Base, a book by the author Preston Dennett. He says \"many people think it's a UFO hotspot.\" In one episode he calls it possibly \"the world's biggest hotspot of transmedium water-based UFOs,\" transmedium meaning craft that pass between air and water. In that episode he also notes that the scientist Townsend Brown spent the end of his life there. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet says it on the show too, without naming Dennett. He calls the Catalina Channel \"a hot spot\" as part of his argument that the ocean is where such objects gather. If it were true, some of these objects would be based under the water off Los Angeles.","catholic-archival-reports-of-aerial-phenomena-1800":"Catholics in Europe, Canada and the United States reported strange things in the sky from 1200 to 1800, says the researcher Diana Pasulka, who tells Michels she found these reports while working in the Vatican and other Catholic archives. If so, sightings of this kind go back centuries before the modern UFO era.","catholic-church-helps-nazis-escape-to-south-america-1945":"Around 1945, people in the Catholic Church are said to have chosen to work with Nazis: hiding them, giving them false papers and getting them out to places like Bolivia and Argentina. A researcher named Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, the Church helped wanted men escape after the war.","cattle-mutilations-done-by-humans":"The cattle mutilations, in which farm animals are found dead with parts cut away, are done by humans and not by aliens, according to a UFO activist named Steven Greer. He tells Michels this without naming a source, pushing back on the journalist Linda Moulton Howe, who he says \"thinks those are aliens\": \"No, we're doing that.\" If he is right, people blaming aliens for the mutilations have been looking in the wrong place.","cattle-mutilations-in-the-bigelow-era-1996":"Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property long tied to reports of strange events, had many cattle mutilations, cases of cattle found dead and cut in unexplained ways, while it was owned by Robert Bigelow between the mid-1990s and 2016. Brandon Fugal, who calls Bigelow his predecessor at the ranch, tells Michels this as a summary, pointing to necropsy reports, that is, animal autopsies, kept in the Bigelow years and to a Dr. O'nett, without quoting from them. If true, the ranch's strange history includes physical evidence on paper.","cattle-mutilations-in-the-missile-field-area-1977":"In 1977, around the nuclear missile field at Ellsworth, dead and mutilated cattle were turning up, according to men who guarded the missiles there. Mario Woods, a former missile guard who says he was taken by a UFO, tells Michels there were 'cattle mutilations going on' in the area, citing the investigator Linda Moulton Howe. Michels, relaying another veteran named Michael Johnson, says the cattle died in the weeks before that November night of the abduction, and he mentions a camera that was taken away. A 2017 article by the UFO researcher Robert Hastings quotes a former missile security man saying: 'Stories about sightings were all over the missile complexes, even reports of cattle mutilations; I once saw two of the dead cows myself.' Tellers raise it as one more strange thing around the missiles in the lead-up to the abduction.","caught-on-the-last-trip-and-banned-from-s4-1989":"In 1989 Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site near Groom Lake in Nevada, says he took people out into the desert to watch test flights, and on the last trip they were caught and he was banned from S4. Lazar tells Michels that on the earlier trip \"they didn't know we were out there,\" so the last one was where it went wrong. In his telling this is how his time at the site ended.","causeway-shafts-are-waterproof-and-hold-air-not-water":"Shafts beneath the causeways of the Giza plateau in Egypt are, by this account, waterproof: groundwater sits outside them but cannot get in, so they hold air, not water. The researcher Filippo Biondi tells Michels this as his own reading, in an episode about what he calls an energy grid beneath the pyramids, and if true it would mean the shafts were built to be sealed.","cave-of-poisoned-aboriginal-tribe-littered-with-bones":"Somewhere in Australia there is a cave where a whole tribe of Aboriginal people died after being given poisoned food, and the cave is 'just littered with human bones', the comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan tells Michels. Rogan says he heard it from his friend Adam Greenree, who visited the site, and he tells it as a grim piece of hidden history.","caves-running-under-the-giza-plateau":"Caves are said to run beneath the whole Giza plateau, the ground the great pyramids of Egypt stand on. A researcher named Luke Caverns tells Michels that people working there broke into a cavern, and that if you pass through one of the cracks in its walls you reach caves that go under the entire plateau. Another researcher, Jeffrey Drum, says on a later episode that it is known for certain that there are caves and tunnels below Giza, though he says nothing of the cavern or the cracks. Neither man names a source. If they are right, the pyramids sit on top of a hidden underground world.","ce5-night-in-sedona-with-greer-s-team-and-demi-lovato-orbs-g":"On a night at an off-grid site near Sedona, Arizona, Michael Herrera says he joined the team of a UFO activist named Steven Greer, with the singer Demi Lovato present, for a CE5 session, a group practice meant to call UFOs through meditation. He says they saw orbs and \"discshaped craft\" appearing and disappearing, among them a gold disc, and that someone was struck by a blow from something no one could see. Herrera, who says he once saw a huge UFO in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels he was there.","centcom-bottleneck-on-uap-information":"US Central Command, the military command that runs American forces in the Middle East, holds back information about UAP, the official term for UFOs, before it reaches the public. That is what the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels, from reporting he did with the journalist George Knapp; he calls it \"a bottleneck.\" If true, it would mean military records of these sightings get held up at that command.","centcom-formation-of-12-metallic-orbs-skimming-the-ocean":"About twelve metallic orbs were filmed in tight formation, skimming the ocean at high speed in the area covered by the US military's Central Command, according to the so-called Immaculate Constellation report. The report has no signature. A man named Michael Shellenberger delivered it to Congress, and Representative Nancy Mace entered it into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a document from the Pentagon. It calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, cleared for release by the State Department. A former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. Under the heading \"CENTCOM Cuboid Formation of Metallic Orbs,\" which Michels reads out on an episode with Brown, the report describes \"a formation of ~12 metallic orbs skimming the ocean surface at high-speed before dispersing in multiple directions,\" flying at first \"in a tight 'cuboid' formation\". So the count of twelve is the report's rough figure, and the footage is described in words, not shown.","cern-physicists-report-beings-coming-through-portals-2010":"Physicists at CERN, the big physics laboratory in Geneva, are said to have told a guest that \"there are beings from portals coming in and out.\" The guest, Astrid Stuckelberger, says on two different shows that the physicists told her this themselves, and she does not name them. In the second telling she adds that she knows physicists \"doing very strange experimentation\".","cernan-s-telepathic-message-on-apollo-17-per-takano-jouen-1972":"On the way to the Moon on Apollo 17 in 1972, the astronaut Eugene Cernan supposedly received a telepathic message saying, \"We don't really want you here.\" Michels passes this on from a man named Takano Josen, who gives it as the reason no one has gone back to the Moon since.","cesar-demands-10-000-to-talk-to-rfk-jr":"A man called Cesar is said to have insisted on being paid before he would talk with Robert F. Kennedy Jr., a sum given as $10,000 in the story's title and heard as $110,000 in the teller's words. A guest named Dan Sheehan says on the show that he talked to Kennedy about it and that Kennedy was going to meet the man to get him to do it, though what exactly he was to do the telling leaves vague. If true, a possible witness put a price on what he knew.","cesario-says-chereze-kept-linking-his-illness-to-the-et-he-c-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, a military policeman named Marco Chereze, who in this story had helped capture a strange creature, fell ill, and he is said to have told his doctor again and again that the sickness might have come from the extraterrestrial being he had apprehended. Michels passes this on from a doctor he calls Cesario, who treated Chereze, saying \"I believe\" that is what Cesario said, during an interview with a surgeon from a Varginha hospital. If true, the soldier himself blamed his illness on contact with a living alien.","cesario-says-the-antibiotics-he-gave-chereze-should-have-sav-1996":"A doctor named Cesario, who treated a Varginha soldier named Marco Chereze in 1996 after Chereze is said to have handled the creature, says he gave him every antibiotic he could and they should have saved his life, but they did nothing. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels what Cesario told him. If true, the infection that killed Chereze would have been something ordinary medicine could not touch.","chaldean-and-sumerian-traditions-of-gods-escaping-the-great":"Traditions going back to the Chaldeans and the Sumerians, ancient peoples of Mesopotamia, tell of gods who escaped the great catastrophe, according to the researcher Randall Carlson. Carlson mentions this to Michels without naming a source, in an episode about the idea that the Moon is an alien spaceship. If the traditions remember something real, beings called gods would have fled an ancient disaster.","changes-inside-barber-s-program-after-the-2017-new-york-time":"Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he flew in a secret programme that recovered crashed UFOs, says life inside that programme changed once The New York Times published \"Glowing Auras and 'Black Money': The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program\" on 16 December 2017, an article that revealed a secret Pentagon UFO study. Barber tells Michels from his own experience that \"it was really about then things started getting really weird for us\".","chapel-hill-gravity-conference-kicks-off-anti-gravity-flurry-1950":"A conference on gravity at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill in the 1950s is said to have set off a burst of interest in anti-gravity, meaning ways to cancel or control gravity. The researcher Eric Weinstein calls it \"famous\" and says it \"kicks a lot of this off.\" Michels comes back to it in several episodes, usually dating it to 1957 and once to 1956. He says a man named Agnew Bahnson headed it through an Institute of Field Physics, and that it drew top physicists such as Richard Feynman, John Wheeler and Bryce DeWitt, along with Louis Witten of the Martin Corporation. He stresses that it was sponsored by Wright Air Field, which later became Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, \"perhaps the epicenter of all UFO mythology starting with Roswell\" (Roswell being the 1947 New Mexico crash story), and he ties the meeting to the birth of quantum gravity and string theory. Another researcher, Bob Maguire, credits Wheeler with starting it and says two industrialists backed it, with Feynman as the first speaker. If true, it would mean the military stood quietly behind mainstream physics' new interest in gravity.","chariots-of-fire-as-angelic-vehicles":"The 'chariots of fire' in the Bible were vehicles flown by angelic beings, says Tim Alberino, a guest who tells Michels of capturing a giant in Afghanistan. He points to the King James Bible of 1611, where the narrative speaks of 'the chariots of God', 'the chariots of Israel' and 'chariots of fire' alongside its ordinary chariots. The text sets these apart as something that comes from God; whether that means craft in a modern sense, it does not say either way, which leaves Alberino's reading as one way to take the story.","charlemagne-and-the-tyrants-of-the-air":"In Charlemagne's time, sightings in the sky over France are said to have been so common that panic broke out at the French court, and Charlemagne thought about declaring war on the \"tyrants of the air.\" The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells this on the show, drawing on The Gods of Eden, a book by the writer William Bramley. If true, it would mean people were reporting strange things in the sky long before modern UFO sightings.","charles-and-drew-s-electrostatic-thrust-experiments-thrust-c-2010":"The claim is that an electrostatic thruster, a device meant to push using electric charge instead of fuel, was powered up in a researcher's lab and then kept producing thrust indefinitely after it was unplugged from the wall. The lab belongs to a researcher named Charles Buhler, who did the experiments with a colleague called Drew, inside a Faraday cage (a metal enclosure that shuts out outside electrical fields) and a vacuum chamber. A theoretical physicist named David Chester, a friend of Michels who trained at MIT and UCLA and was brought onto the show to test Buhler's claims, sets the claim out on air and starts to answer it with 'well, you know, obviously'. If it held up, it would mean a device getting push out of nowhere.","charles-buhler-leaves-nasa-for-propellantless-propulsion-bas-2020":"In the 2020s, Michels says, Charles Buhler, whom he calls NASA's top scientist in electrostatics, left NASA to start work on a gravity-based propulsion system that needs no fuel pushed out the back. Buhler says it derives from the work of Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor who studied high-voltage effects decades earlier. Michels tells this himself, and for him it means Brown's ideas are being taken seriously today.","charles-buhler-leaves-nasa-to-found-exodus-space":"A scientist named Charles Buhler, whom Michels calls NASA's lead electrostatics scientist at Cape Kennedy, is said to have left NASA to start his own company, Exodus Space, built on propellantless propulsion, meaning moving a craft without burning or throwing out fuel. Michels tells it on two episodes and says Buhler credits the idea to Thomas Townsend Brown, an earlier researcher of high-voltage propulsion, as a sign such work has moved from fringe to industry.","charles-burney-and-the-ivory-tower-of-urartian-history":"The researcher Matt LaCroix, speaking with Michels about the Lake Van region of eastern Turkey, describes a scholar named Charles Burney as \"the grandfather\" of Urartian history, the study of the ancient kingdom of Urartu that once ruled there. LaCroix gives no source, and the story's title frames that field as an 'ivory tower'. He brings it up as part of a wider argument that something big happened in the deep past around 12,000 BC, earlier than the accepted history of the area allows.","charles-fort-we-are-property-like-british-india-under-the-ra-1920":"The claim is that humans may be owned by some outside intelligence, much as Britain ruled India under the Raj. Two guests say the writer Charles Fort was the first to suggest this about strange things seen in the sky. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that Fort first theorised it \"all the way back in the 1920s,\" and he uses the Raj as the comparison. A Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, who holds patents on unusual craft designs, quotes Fort's line, \"I believe we are property,\" and tells listeners: \"Read that book.\" The book Pais means is Fort's The Book of the Damned, which he dates to 1919, a little before Jorjani's 1920s. Pais does not use the Raj comparison. If it were true, humanity would be closer to a managed colony than to masters of its own world.","charles-hall-and-the-tall-whites-at-indian-springs-1960":"In the 1960s, a man named Charles Hall, stationed with the Air Force in Nevada, claims to have lived for years alongside tall, pale aliens known as the tall whites, in the Indian Springs Valley. Michels brings Hall up on several episodes, calls him Colonel Charles Hall, and says he interviewed Hall himself, but at other times he places the story at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, rather than Indian Springs.","charles-hall-flown-by-the-tall-whites-to-area-52-and-53-afte-1960":"In the 1960s, says Charles Hall, a man who says he worked at Air Force ranges in Nevada, he hurt his knee, and the next morning the \"Tall Whites\", tall pale beings he says lived near the base, came to take him to their own base, which he calls Area 54 or 53, to see whether he needed surgery. Hall tells Michels this firsthand and also writes about it in his book Millennial Hospitality; he describes riding in a cargo craft and says US generals were there. When Michels retells it on another episode, it becomes being flown from Area 51, the secret Air Force site in Nevada, to Area 52 and then Area 53.","charles-hall-s-air-force-service-records-1960":"Charles Hall, a man whose story is set at Area 51, the secret Air Force base in Nevada, in the 1960s, really did serve in the Air Force, according to Michels, who says he has seen his service record. Pushing back on someone who doubted it, Michels says, \"He was in the Air Force and I have the record,\" pointing to Hall's DD214, the discharge paper the US military gives every departing member, as proof that at least that part of Hall's account holds up.","charles-manson-as-mk-ultra-patient-1968":"Michels says Charles Manson, the cult leader behind the 1969 Los Angeles murders, was a patient of MK Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control research programme. He credits the idea to a friend in Los Angeles named Tom O'Neill, who he says \"documents it pretty well,\" and names a man named Jolly West alongside it; on a later episode he repeats it, hedged as \"probably,\" with no credit given. If true, it would tie one of America's most notorious crimes to a government programme.","charles-s-path-from-hidden-momentum-to-pure-electrostatic-th-2000":"Charles Buhler, who worked in NASA's electrostatics lab, tells Michels that he first saw unexplained forces in his experiments in 2010. After two years working with a colleague he names only as Drew, he thought the push came from momentum stored in electric and magnetic fields being turned into ordinary motion, which he calls \"conversion... from field momentum to mechanical momentum without static charges,\" and they tested it after his wife pointed this out. He says it was only in 2018 that he \"realized that I didn't even need the current,\" meaning static charge alone could produce thrust. If true, it would be a way to push a craft with no fuel thrown out the back, which the episode calls antigravity propulsion.","charlie-duke-vs-bart-sibrel-debate-on-danny-jones-2025":"In 2025 a podcaster named Danny Jones put two men face to face: Bart Sibrel, whom Michels calls the leading debunker of the moon landings, and Charlie Duke, one of the last living Apollo astronauts, then 90 years old, and \"they went at it.\" Michels tells this to Joe Rogan, who he notes had done an episode with Sibrel himself. It sets a man who says he walked on the Moon against a man who says nobody did.","charlie-red-star-sighting-at-carman-manitoba-1975":"The researcher Grant Cameron says that in 1975, near Carman in Manitoba, Canada, he saw a light known as Charlie Red Star. He tells Michels that he and others turned the car to go back in, \"and boom, it appeared from the left to the right,\" as his own first-hand sighting of something he could not explain.","charlie-s-career-turns-to-medical-art-and-imaging-after-alla-1976":"Charlie Foltz, one of the men who say they were abducted during a 1976 camping trip on the Allagash waterway, says he was training to be a schoolteacher before it happened, and afterwards his career turned to medical art and imaging. He tells Michels this about his own life, as one sign of how the experience changed him.","charlie-s-unexplained-punch-biopsy-scar-1976":"Charlie Foltz says he has a small scar near his scrotum that looks like a punch biopsy, the small round cut doctors make to take a skin sample, and he cannot explain it. He ties it to 1976 on the Allagash Waterway in Maine, where he places the alien abduction experience he describes to Michels. If so, the scar would be a physical mark of being examined.","charon-ferryman-myth-comes-from-the-epic-of-gilgamesh":"The Greek myth of Charon, the ferryman who carries the dead across the river Styx into the underworld, came from the Epic of Gilgamesh, according to a researcher named Matt LaCroix, who calls Gilgamesh \"the oldest recorded record of that.\" He tells Michels this without naming a single source, though he credits the British Museum's 1920 translation by E. A. Wallis Budge, The Babylonian Story of the Deluge and the Epic of Gilgamish, and a work called The Epic of Humanity. If true, a central Greek picture of death would trace back to ancient Mesopotamia.","chase-and-yonas-cognitive-enhancement-patent-2020":"Two Lockheed Martin engineers, Charles Chase and Gerald Yonas, filed a patent called \"cognitive enhancement using feedback\", Michels says, placing it around 2020. He brings it up himself in an episode about a UFO book the Pentagon tried to censor, presenting it as an example of a big defence contractor working on ways to boost the human mind.","chase-brandon-book-on-hillenkoetter-and-the-roswell-being-1947":"The story is that a former CIA man wrote a novel to hint at real secrets about Roswell, the famous 1947 crash in New Mexico, and about a being said to have been found there. The book is The Cryptos Conundrum, and Michels dates it to 2012. Its author is Chase Brandon, a CIA officer who became the agency's liaison to Hollywood, though Michels once calls the name a pseudonym. If true, the CIA's own people let the truth out dressed as fiction. Michels says the book 'talks about Hillenkoetter,' one of the first CIA directors, 'who dealt with Roswell.' He also says it describes a secret committee on NHI (non-human intelligence) and a group inside an Office of Strategic Assessment. Michels says it opens with a Francis Bacon quote: some truths are so strange they have to be hidden in fiction. A researcher named Grant Cameron calls the book 'the Crystal Conundrum.' He says it deals with how the CIA handled the Roswell crash and that, 'according to some of the stories,' the manuscript went through review eight times before it came out.","chase-brandon-opens-a-cia-liaison-operation-in-hollywood-1996":"Around 1996, a CIA man named Chase Brandon is said to have set up a CIA office in Hollywood to advise filmmakers on stories touching on things like UFOs. The screenwriter Bryce Zabel, who was working in Hollywood at the time, tells Michels that Brandon showed up and opened some kind of CIA operation for advising people on such subjects. Michels adds on a later episode, without naming a source, that Brandon, the official CIA liaison to Hollywood, was a cousin of Tommy Lee Jones, who starred in Men in Black. The point of telling it is that the CIA may have had a hand in how films showed aliens and UFOs.","chase-county-nebraska-deputy-sees-30-50-drones-and-mothershi-2019":"Around 2019 and 2020, a sheriff's deputy in Chase County, Nebraska, reported seeing 30 to 50 drones along with a \"mothership\", a larger craft, while reports from Colorado said the drones flew not as a pack but as a single unit. Marik von Rennenkampff reads this out on the show from reports, with the account tied to a man named Doug Johnson. If true, coordinated unexplained craft were flying over the American plains.","chase-letter-after-june-1967-meeting-of-base-ufo-officers":"In June 1967, the Air Force officers handling UFO reports at various bases held a big meeting, and afterwards a man named Lewis Chase wrote a letter about it. That is what Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, tells Michels, citing the letter as one of several documents from that period. It would mean the subject was being discussed among the people at the bases who handled the reports.","checking-out-scientology-while-setting-up-remote-viewing-1970":"In the early 1970s, while setting up a remote viewing programme, an effort to see whether people could describe distant places with the mind alone, the physicist Hal Puthoff says his team looked into \"everything,\" including Silva Mind Control, a mental training course, and Scientology. He tells Michels this from his own part in it, saying some of the techniques \"were definitely of value\" but that he cut ties with Scientology in the mid 70s.","chemical-analysis-of-the-red-pyramid-staining-2022":"Staining on the inner walls of the Red Pyramid at Dahshur in Egypt, by this account, has \"absolutely nothing to do with bats\", which he says chemical analysis done around 2022 \"conclusively proves\". Geoffrey Drumm, who says an alien told him what the pyramids were for, tells Michels this without naming who did the analysis or where it was published. If true, the stains would need some other cause, which Drumm ties to his own view of what the pyramid did.","cheney-and-bush-as-possible-disclosure-gatekeepers-2000":"Dick Cheney's name comes up around the UFO question, Michels says, and George W. Bush might have considered disclosure, meaning the government openly revealing what it knows about UFOs. Michels raises this himself as talk he has heard, without naming a source. If true, it would put the decision at the top of the White House in the 2000s.","chereze-family-wins-death-certificate-after-legal-battle-1996":"After a long legal fight, the family of a man named Marco Chereze finally got his death certificate, issued through a laboratory called IPD. The filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this, crediting a researcher named Roger Leir and a woman named Marta. It matters because the episode ties Chereze's death to the army's reported capture of a living creature in Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, and the certificate could say what killed him.","chereze-s-12-14-day-illness-and-visits-to-three-or-four-doct-1996":"In the Varginha case, in which people say a craft came down near the Brazilian town in 1996 and strange beings were caught, a military police officer named Marco Chereze is said to have handled one of the beings and then fallen ill and died. Ahmed Foratu, who presents himself as a witness, tells Michels that Chereze's infection lasted around 12 or 14 days and that he went to three or four doctors, at the military police battalion clinic, complaining of lumbosciatica, a kind of back pain. If so, it would tie a fatal illness to contact with the creature.","chereze-s-rare-infection-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, a military police officer named Marco Chereze is said to have caught a strange creature and then died of an infection, which Armando Fortunato calls \"extremely rare, not impossible.\" Fortunato, speaking on the show about a case he says he was part of, cites a doctor named Gianini about the illness. If true, the officer's death may be tied to touching the being.","chereze-s-sister-says-he-had-contact-with-the-et-1996":"Marco Chereze, a young soldier who fell ill and died weeks after the 1996 events in Varginha, Brazil, where witnesses say a strange creature was captured, had touched the alien being, according to his sister. Ahmed Foratu, who speaks to Michels as a witness to the case, passes on what she said. If true, it would tie the soldier's death directly to contact with the creature.","chernobyl-dogs-rapidly-evolved":"Dogs living around Chernobyl, the site of the 1986 nuclear disaster, are said to have evolved faster than any other dogs. Michels brings it up from an article he says came out that month, in early 2025, without naming it, and first calls it a study before correcting himself.","chess-game-with-gaddafi-on-russia-day-2011":"In Tripoli in 2011, the guest introduced as president of a Russian republic says he went to play chess with Libya's leader Muammar Gaddafi, who \"was nowhere to be seen\" until he finally arrived. He dates it to 12 June, Russia Day. He tells Michels this himself, as part of his own life story, which on the show also includes a claim that he was taken by aliens in 1997.","chetan-prakash-shows-quantum-wave-functions-arise-from-marko-2026":"The scientist Donald Hoffman tells Michels that his collaborator, the mathematician Chetan Prakash, has shown that in certain cases the quantum wave functions of free particles, the math physicists use to describe a particle on its own, are \"precisely the same thing\" as Markov chains, a model in which a system hops from state to state at random by fixed odds. Hoffman says he thinks the result is \"quite general.\" If so, it would suggest that the basic stuff of physics comes out of a simpler, deeper process, which is the core of Hoffman's view.","chief-neurosurgeon-requests-meeting-via-back-entry-documenta-1996":"While filming in Varginha, Brazil, a town where witnesses say a strange creature was caught in 1996, the documentary maker James Fox got an unexpected request for a meeting from Italo Venturelli, the chief neurosurgeon at the town's Hospital Regional, which by this story's account came in through a back entrance. Fox tells it in a documentary clip on the show. It matters because a senior doctor at the hospital where the creature was said to be taken would be a key witness.","chief-of-aerospace-medicine-attached-to-nasa-and-the-air-for":"A former chief of aerospace medicine, one of the top doctors working with NASA and the Air Force, is said to have seen something unexplained. Michels says he had just interviewed the man, whom he describes as \"a senior doctor there,\" and says only that \"he saw something.\" He does not name the doctor or say what he saw.","childhood-daylight-disc-sighting-1963":"The UFO campaigner Steven Greer, who says UFO secrets are kept by a global cabal, says that as a child, around 1963, in Charlotte, North Carolina, he saw a \"perfectly seamless disc\" in broad daylight. He tells Michels this as his own sighting, an early start to his lifelong interest.","childhood-fall-from-a-garage-roof-softened-by-warmth":"Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to talk with aliens, says that as a child a branch snapped under him and he fell about 25 feet near the garage roof, and the fall was softened by a feeling of warmth. He tells Michels this about himself, as an early sign that something was looking after him.","childhood-lights-near-the-gateway-arch":"As a child in O'Fallon Park in St. Louis, near the Gateway Arch, Michael Johnson says he would \"see lights going by\". Johnson, an Air Force veteran who on the show describes being abducted by aliens, tells Michels this himself as an early sign of the strange things in his life.","childhood-saucer-over-football-field-artek-camp-and-army-sig":"Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of one of Russia's republics, tells Michels that as a boy playing football he and \"a few dozen boys\" watched a saucer hovering over the field. He tells it as something he saw himself, one of several sightings he describes from his youth, including at Artek, the Soviet children's camp near Mount Ayu-Dag in Crimea, and later in the Soviet army. If true, it was seen by a whole crowd of witnesses, not just one child.","childhood-sighting-of-a-wobbling-reddish-orange-light-1976":"Gary McKinnon, a British hacker who says he found evidence of a secret NASA space programme, says that around 1976, when he was about 12, he looked out of his bedroom window and saw a wobbling \"reddish orange glowing light\" moving in an arc down to the horizon. He tells Michels it was the one sighting he ever had himself.","childhood-ufo-and-nasal-brain-implant-at-age-five":"Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies what he says are alien implants in human bodies, says that at age five in Oxnard, California, he had an encounter with a UFO, and thinks that was when \"they put the brain implant in\" through his nose. He tells Michels this as his own memory, which, if true, would make him one of the people he studies.","childhood-visits-from-little-gray-beings-1960":"Growing up in Peabody, Massachusetts, in the 1960s and 70s, John Blitch says he had visits from \"the little gray guys in my room\". Blitch, a scientist who worked with DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, tells Michels this himself, as part of his account of being abducted by an alien.","children-gain-psychic-powers-after-seeing-geller-on-tv-1970":"In the early 1970s, children in the US, Britain and China supposedly began showing psychic powers right after watching the performer Uri Geller, who claimed he could bend metal with his mind, on television. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this, drawing on newspaper articles of the time and on the work of Andrija Puharich, a doctor who studied Geller. To Malatzi it would suggest that such abilities can spread from one person to many.","children-shifting-random-number-generators-with-thought-2008":"Between about 2008 and 2010, at Baker Victory Services in Western New York, children were tested on whether they could push random number generators, machines that produce numbers by pure chance, using only their thoughts. Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a UFO-related programme as a child, tells Michels that he and other kids he watched pushed the results far from chance and \"held for hours just by using our thoughts.\" If true, it would mean children's minds were being trained to affect machines directly.","children-taught-physics-by-the-ghost":"Dave Rossi, who says aliens taught him advanced physics, says he has met families whose children told him that a being they call \"the ghost\" comes and teaches them. He tells Michels that the parents let him speak with the children directly, and that the kids said, \"the ghost comes and tells me.\" If so, it would mean other children are being taught the same way he says he was.","children-who-witnessed-the-irkutsk-lights-2011":"In 2011, children in the Irkutsk region of Siberia described a rumble followed by a light that turned everything pink; one said, \"I thought it was an alien.\" Michels plays or quotes this from a report on Russian Channel 1, a state television station. The account is offered as ordinary witnesses describing an unexplained light in the sky.","chilean-navy-ufo-video-solved-as-a-distant-plane-2016":"A spectacular-looking video taken by the Chilean Navy, released by the writer Leslie Kean in a Huffington Post article that the teller places in 2016 or 2017, turned out, he says, to show a distant plane. The skeptic Mick West tells this on the show, adding that investigators spent two years ruling things out and were still wrong. To him it shows that even a long, careful official study can miss an ordinary explanation.","chimney-rock-marks-the-moon-s-18-6-year-standstill":"At Chimney Rock in Colorado, two stone pinnacles are said to frame a rare event in the sky: if you look between them, once every 18.6 years you will see the moon rise in the gap. A researcher named Randall Carlson describes this on the show without naming a source, as a sign that the people who used the site tracked the moon's long cycle closely.","china-agrees-to-forgo-a-nuclear-program-1963":"In 1963, the story goes, China agreed not to pursue any nuclear programme at all, an agreement Daniel Sheehan ties to Mao Zedong. Sheehan, a researcher, tells Michels this in a conversation about UFOs and John F. Kennedy, without naming a source. If true, it would mean a nuclear deal with China existed that the usual history leaves out.","china-holds-at-least-one-craft-via-puthoff":"China has at least one recovered craft of unknown origin, the physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels. He says China is 'like a closed door', and that he knows this through the researcher Hal Puthoff, who he thinks learned it officially through work with the DIA, the Pentagon's military intelligence agency. If true, it would mean the United States is not the only country holding such an object.","china-injecting-children-to-raise-iq-2020":"China is said to be injecting children with something to raise their IQ. A guest named Kurt Metzger says he heard it from the podcaster Joe Rogan, who told him that \"intel people\" had told Rogan this. So the claim reaches the show third-hand, and the telling does not say what was being injected.","china-reverse-engineering-downed-craft":"China has crashed craft of unknown origin and is working to reverse engineer them, meaning take them apart to learn how they work. That is what the researcher Bob Maguire tells Michels, saying \"we know for a fact\" but naming no source. If true, it would mean the US is not the only country trying to copy recovered craft.","chinese-academy-of-social-science-psychic-abilities-laborato-1990":"In 1990s Beijing, a laboratory at the Chinese Academy of Social Science was running experiments on psychic abilities, trying to work out what such powers were. That is the account of a lawyer named Dan Sheehan, who tells Michels he saw it himself: he says a woman he names as Jen Jo Jing took him there the moment he arrived and showed him \"all these experiments they were doing with psychic abilities.\" If true, it would mean China was studying the paranormal seriously at the same time as the West, and Sheehan places the visit in a story that also involves Mikhail Gorbachev and James Baker.","chinese-journal-of-ufo-research-tacitly-endorsed-by-ccp-1997":"China publishes a periodical called the Journal of UFO Research, and its existence is taken as a sign that the Chinese Communist Party quietly approves of the subject. David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who has testified to Congress about secret UFO programs, mentions it to Michels as something he knows of second-hand, suggesting that China treats UFOs more openly than the US does.","chinese-nationals-detained-after-drone-incursions-at-macdill-2024":"In 2024, drones flew into the airspace of MacDill and Langley Air Force Bases, and Chinese nationals were taken into custody afterwards, according to Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he helped recover a UFO. Barber tells Michels this as something known about earlier incursions that year, without naming a source. He raises it to suggest that some of the drones seen over bases have human, foreign operators.","chinese-papers-on-tracking-ufos":"China has published a couple of scientific papers on how to track UFOs, Michels says. He mentions it in passing, without naming the papers or their authors, in a conversation about UFO physics. It would suggest that other governments' researchers are taking the problem seriously too.","chinese-spy-balloon-as-standard-of-evidence-2023":"The skeptic Michael Shermer holds up the Chinese spy balloon that drifted over the United States in 2023 as the kind of evidence UFO claims should be able to match. As he puts it to Michels, \"Everybody sees it\": a real object in the sky gets seen, photographed and agreed on by everyone. The point is that if strange craft were really flying around, they should be just as plain to see.","chinese-spy-balloon-over-the-aleutians-and-montana-probing-r-2023":"Michels suggests the Chinese spy balloon that appeared in January 2023, flying over Alaska's Aleutian Islands and later over Montana, near Malmstrom Air Force Base, was probing the limits of American radar. He offers this as his own reading, without naming a source. If true, it would mean the balloon was mapping what US defences could and could not see.","chinese-uap-task-force-reported-in-south-china-morning-post-2021":"China has its own government team studying UAP, the official term for UFOs, and a newspaper said so openly. Two guests tell Michels this. The first is David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, who says that around June 2021 the South China Morning Post ran an article that amounted to \"Oh yeah, we have a UAP task force too.\" If he is right, the United States is not the only power quietly studying these objects. Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official, tells it differently on another episode. He says that \"a couple years ago\" a paper he calls the \"China Morning Sun\" wrote about a \"five continents initiative\". Neither man describes what the article found, and they disagree on the paper's name, the date and what the effort was called.","chinese-ufology-after-mao-and-the-falun-gong-crackdown-1976":"The study of UFOs in China, a researcher named Richard Dolan tells Michels, opened up only after the death of Mao Zedong in 1976, and its story runs on to the government's crackdown on the Falun Gong spiritual movement in 1999. \"Everything revolves around the death of Mao,\" Dolan says, drawing on the work of a writer named Paul Dong, though he gives few details of what followed.","chirality-as-evidence-for-god-in-the-case-for-god":"Chirality, the way the molecules of life come in a left-handed or right-handed form, is offered as evidence for God. Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor, says on the show that he made this argument years ago in a book, The Case for God. If he is right, the chemistry of life itself would point to a designer.","choosing-among-sar-images-for-the-gran-sasso-scan-2020":"Sometime in the 2020s, the researcher Filippo Biondi says he had to sort through several SAR images, radar pictures taken from satellites, to pick the best one for scanning beneath Gran Sasso, a mountain in Italy. He tells Michels he did this himself, as part of testing the radar method he uses to look for structures underground, including beneath the pyramids of Egypt.","chris-aubeck-s-book-saucers-shows-saucer-shapes-are-rare":"A book called Saucers, by the researcher Chris Aubeck, is said to show that the classic flying-saucer shape is actually rare among UFO reports, which would mean the popular image of a UFO doesn't match what witnesses usually describe. A researcher named Clas Svahn brings the book up on the show without saying where in it this appears, and he praises Aubeck highly: \"he's fantastic. His books are fantastic.\"","chris-bledsoe-case-in-north-carolina":"A man named Chris Bledsoe, from Fayetteville, North Carolina, says that from 2007 on he met beings in a craft, saw UFOs come to his house, and began talking with a divine figure he calls the Lady, at one point linked to the Egyptian goddess Hathor, who gives him prophecies once a year. After that, Michels says, senior figures from NASA and the CIA began visiting him: a high-ranking CIA official named Jim Semivan, and a man named Hal Puthoff, whom Michels places at the CIA and NASA and who, he says, turned up at Bledsoe's door once Bledsoe started attracting UFOs. Michels raises the case himself in many episodes, once citing a man named Timothy Taylor and the book UFO of God, as a sign that intelligence and space officials take such experiences seriously.","chris-bledsoe-claims-similar-ufo-sensitivity-2000":"A man named Chris Bledsoe in North Carolina is one of a few people who, Michels says, \"claim similar things\": a personal sensitivity to UFOs, or a way of drawing them. Michels mentions him in passing to the researcher Jacques Vallee, without giving details. If true, some people would have an unusual link to the phenomenon.","chris-bledsoe-flies-a-craft-down-the-cape-fear-river-2007":"In 2007 a man named Chris Bledsoe is said to have found himself flying a craft down the Cape Fear River in North Carolina. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron passes on what Bledsoe told him, quoting him: \"Suddenly the window opened and there was a Cape Fear River.\" If true, it would mean Bledsoe was not only watching a craft but was somehow on board one.","chris-bledsoe-s-inconclusive-visit-to-skinwalker-ranch-was-n-2020":"A man named Chris Bledsoe came to Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, and the footage from his visit was never put on air. That comes from Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, who took part. He tells Michels the visit happened but says only, \"We didn't end up airing um that content,\" which suggests nothing conclusive came of it.","chris-bledsoe-s-lady-and-the-guardians-2007":"A man named Chris Bledsoe says that since 2007 he has been in contact with a non-human intelligence he calls the Lady, which he links to the Big Dipper, and whose companions call themselves the Guardians. Michels tells this himself, once describing her as a luminous feminine presence unlike any craft or light. If true, contact would be personal and almost spiritual rather than about machines.","chris-bledsoe-s-message-from-the-lady":"A man named Chris Bledsoe says a female figure he calls \"the lady\" gave him a message: she has had \"many names throughout history\", \"God is God\", and \"we don't know what we think we know.\" He tells Michels she urged people to step back, rethink, and \"be good children\", because \"we're going off the cliff.\" Bledsoe tells it as his own experience, and if true it would be a warning to humanity from a being who claims to have been known under many names across history.","chris-invited-to-take-part-in-revealing-something-hidden-at-2027":"Something hidden at the pyramids of Giza in Egypt is due to be revealed in 2027, and a North Carolina man named Chris Bledsoe says he has been asked to take part. Bledsoe, who says UFOs appear around him, tells Michels he has \"been invited to go down there\" to be involved in releasing \"something that was hidden\", and he links the invitation to the Egyptian antiquities authorities. He does not say what the hidden thing is, but if true it would put him at the centre of a major discovery.","chris-langan-sees-a-disc-near-malmstrom":"A man named Chris Langan says that while he was working for the Forest Service not far from Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, he was parked in a Forest Service pickup at a campground when he looked up and a huge elliptical saucer was simply there in the sky. Langan told this himself on two episodes in 2025, and Michels repeated it on a later episode, saying Langan saw UFOs near the base. No date for the sighting is given.","chris-mellon-s-aerospace-insider-contact-died-before-they-co-2010":"Chris Mellon, a former Pentagon intelligence official, is said to have set up a meeting with someone who worked inside a secret UFO program, and the man died before they could meet. Michels tells it twice, passing on what he says Mellon has said: once he says only that Mellon \"almost met with someone from the UFO program\", and the second time he adds that the meeting was all arranged and that two weeks before it, the man \"just randomly has a heart attack.\" If true, it would mean a rare insider source was lost just before he could speak.","chris-mellon-says-disclosure-green-light-continued-after-cli-2016":"After Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 election, a man named Chris Mellon is said to have insisted that the push to make UFO secrets public, which he called a \"green light,\" had not died but grown. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels he phoned Mellon the same day and asked whether it was dead. Cameron says Mellon answered, \"Green light still going. It's even bigger than before,\" which, if true, would mean the effort, linked in Cameron's telling to the musician Tom DeLonge, did not depend on a Clinton win.","chris-mellon-wants-to-learn-what-cia-science-director-knows":"A former Pentagon intelligence official named Chris Mellon is said to have wanted to question the CIA's Director of Science and Technology, a woman who held the post fairly recently, because, in his words as relayed, \"we need to figure out what she knows about UFOs.\" Michels tells this as something Mellon said to him, and he is unsure exactly which director it was. If it is true, it would mean someone who follows this closely believed that senior CIA science officials know something about UFOs.","chris-ramsay-s-own-associative-remote-viewing-training-and-r-2023":"The online video maker Chris Ramsay says that in 2023 he trained in associative remote viewing, a practice in which people try to sense a hidden or future target with the mind alone, and that the group started getting \"really crazy results, like good results.\" He tells Michels this firsthand and credits a man named Brett Stewart. He says these results led him to start a channel called Area 52, and if they were real they would suggest the mind can reach information it has no ordinary way to get.","chris-ramsay-sees-tic-tac-over-nellis":"A man named Chris Ramsay is said to have seen a \"tic tac\", a white oval object shaped like the mint, while he was a passenger on a commercial flight over the Nellis test range, a military range in Nevada. Michels passes this on from what Ramsay told him. He says Ramsay saw the same kind of tic tac Michels had just shown on screen, so the shape would be turning up in more than one place.","chris-s-reaction-to-anomalous-metal-and-only-two-others-seen":"In Zanesville, Ohio, Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs are drawn to him, is said to have reacted to a piece of unusual metal in a way that the man watching had seen only twice before. Bledsoe tells Michels this second-hand, passing on what Timothy Taylor told him: \"he'd only ever seen two other reactions.\" If true, it would put Bledsoe in a very small group of people with an unexplained response to such material.","chris-s-rosetta-stone-project-to-decode-the-phenomenon-with-2025":"Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs and strange lights appear around him, has a plan he calls a \"Rosetta Stone\" project: gather sensor data on those lights and use quantum computing and artificial intelligence to decode them. He tells Michels he believes \"we can get actual real time communication through the light and through sensors if we can gather enough data.\" If it worked, it would mean a two-way conversation with whatever is behind the phenomenon, which he places in the years 2025 to 2030.","christian-herter-s-rule-against-meeting-the-mafia-without-th-1950":"Christian Herter, governor of Massachusetts in the 1950s, is said to have had a firm rule: no one from the mafia could come to talk to him unless he also invited the press. Harald Malmgren, a former presidential advisor, tells Michels this as something Herter told him himself, in Herter's words that no one from the mafia \"could come to talk to me without me inviting\" a witness, which Malmgren tells as a way to keep any such meeting in the open.","christopher-dunn-s-theory-of-advanced-tools-at-giza":"The idea is that the granite at the Giza pyramids in Egypt was cut with tools far more advanced than anything archaeologists have found there, an idea credited to a writer named Christopher Dunn, who studies what he calls engineering anomalies in the stonework. A former nuclear site guard named Mario Woods passes it on, saying the tools used to \"carve or cut this granite are not in the archaeological array of tools of copper and wooden chisels today.\" Michels mentions Dunn more generally as someone who has \"done all this work on just engineering anomalies with the thing itself,\" without speaking of tools or granite. If true, it would mean whoever built the pyramids had technology we cannot account for.","chuck-clark-s-road-trip-tape-of-a-wobbling-orange-disc-1995":"There is a VHS tape, said to be from 1995, shot near Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert, that shows an orange disc-shaped object wobbling in the sky. The UFO investigator James Fox describes it to Michels as a tape linked to a man named Chuck Clark, from around the small town of Rachel, Nevada, saying the next thing you see on it is 'a orange disc shaped thing just wobbling.' A guest named Logan tells the story another way on a later episode: he says the footage, 'supposedly authentic,' was shot by two college kids who drove out to Area 51 in the 1990s, he believes 1995, and he places it at the black mailbox, a well-known roadside landmark near the base. The two tellers agree on the year and the place but not on who held the camera. If the tape is real, it would show a craft flying near the base.","chupa-chupa-beams-at-colares-1977":"In 1977 at Colares, in the Brazilian state of Pará, a flying object nicknamed the Chupa Chupa would cast down a beam of light as it flew. Aldo Rebelo, a former Brazilian defense minister, describes this to Michels in an episode on Brazil's best-known UFO cases. Coming from a former minister, it would mean an official is backing the account of the beams.","cia-activity-underneath-disneyland":"The comedian Kurt Metzger says Walt Disney was a Rosicrucian, a member of an old secret mystical order, and that he let the CIA \"do stuff\" underneath Disneyland, \"and that's real.\" Metzger says it on the show, offering it as the reason \"Disney kids turn out how they do,\" and he does not say what the CIA did there or how he knows.","cia-and-andrija-puharich-s-elf-telepathy-experiments-and-spa-1960":"The CIA is said to have been experimenting as early as the 1960s, under a doctor named Andrija Puharich, with extra-low-frequency signals, very slow radio waves, in connection with telepathy. Michels says this himself without naming a source, and his sentence trails off before he explains what was done, which, if true, would put the agency in mind research a decade before it was widely known.","cia-approaches-puthoff-over-russian-psychic-research-1970":"Hal Puthoff, a researcher at the Stanford Research Institute, a research laboratory in California, says that in the early 1970s he wrote up some psychic tests whose results were accurate and circulated the write-up, and soon after the CIA came knocking. He tells Michels the agents said they had been looking for him because the Russians had been spending millions of dollars for years on this kind of research, at their best institutes and with their best people. If so, it would mean the CIA's interest in psychic research began as an answer to a Soviet effort.","cia-brings-anomalous-experience-files-to-garry-nolan-2010":"The CIA came to a Stanford scientist named Garry Nolan with files on people who had had strange, unexplained experiences, according to Michels, who says the agency told Nolan \"we have these files of people who've had all these anomalous experiences.\" Michels tells it himself on two episodes, once dating the approach to around 2016 and framing it as being about the bodily effects of encounters with UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. If true, it would mean the CIA was quietly studying the health effects of such encounters.","cia-compartment-so-restricted-nothing-was-recorded":"Inside the CIA there was a secret matter handled so tightly that nothing about it was written into any record, according to a former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, who says he took part. He tells Michels this was \"very highly unusual, irregular I'd call it,\" since the agency normally records its work, which would mean some subjects leave no paper trail at all.","cia-created-ufo-news-outlet-appearing-around-the-time-of-the-2018":"Around 2018, when his film about Bob Lazar (a man who says he worked on alien craft) came out, an online UFO news outlet run from overseas appeared, and the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says it was \"a CIA stood up media source\". Corbell tells Michels this as someone who says he knows more than he can share, and if true, it would mean the CIA set up its own outlet to shape UFO coverage.","cia-director-bob-gates-s-first-visit-to-moscow-and-his-toast-1992":"A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says he went with CIA director Bob Gates on Gates's first visit to Moscow in the fall of 1992, where Gates made a toast. Mowatt-Larssen tells Michels he was there and will \"never forget that trip\", which he presents as a moment when the old Cold War rivals sat across the table from each other.","cia-document-mandating-shaping-the-emerging-uap-narrative":"The CIA has a document ordering its people to steer public talk about UAP, the official term for UFOs, according to the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell. He tells Michels he was \"exposed to a CIA document that gave the mandate,\" and \"the mandate was to shape the emerging UAP narrative,\" and he mentions the Las Vegas reporter George Knapp and the Navy fighter pilot David Fravor as he discusses it. If true, it would mean the CIA is actively managing how the public understands the subject.","cia-documents-describing-flat-mirror-like-objects-1950":"CIA documents from the 1950s describe \"flat mirror-like objects that reflect light\", according to Michels. He says it himself, without naming the specific documents, while debating a prominent skeptic, and he uses it to argue that the agency was taking reports of strange objects seriously at the time.","cia-formed-at-behest-of-brown-brothers-harriman-sheehan-hist-1940":"The CIA is said to have been formed in the 1940s at the urging of Brown Brothers Harriman, a private investment group, which would mean Wall Street money helped shape American intelligence from the beginning. Michels gives this as the view of a man named Dan Sheehan, whose whole history of American intelligence after the OSS (the wartime spy service that came before the CIA) is, Michels says, that \"the CIA got formed uh at the behest of this Brown brothers Haramman group.\"","cia-funded-psychological-conditioning-at-a-montreal-institut-1950":"From the 1950s into the 1960s, a Montreal institute that the CIA funded for a long time is said to have carried out what the writer Peter Levenda calls massive psychological conditioning on people. Levenda, a researcher, gives it to Michels as an example without naming a source, and links it to a man named Ewen Cameron. If true, it would mean the CIA paid for mind-shaping experiments on people outside the United States.","cia-gateway-documents-show-psionics-were-taken-seriously-1980":"The CIA took psionics, the idea that the mind can perceive or act at a distance, seriously, and its own files show it. So says the journalist Ross Coulthart, who on two different episodes tells Michels that anyone who doubts this has \"only got to read the CIA Gateway documents in the CIA files,\" which, he says, \"show that that's actually not true.\" He points to those files, dated to the 1980s, as his evidence; the telling gives only his reading of them, not their own words.","cia-implants-a-chip-in-lynn-kyle-s-skull-the-origin-of-the-t":"A man named Lynn Kyle is said to have had a chip put in his skull by the CIA without being told, and the story is offered as the origin of the tinfoil hat. Kurt Metzger, a guest on an episode about Jeffrey Epstein, tells it as something he heard from Kyle: Kyle's psychologist \"worked for the CIA and they put a chip in his skull without telling him.\" Metzger also brings up the name Carlos Delgado in the story. If true, it would mean the CIA was secretly implanting devices in people's heads.","cia-jump-room-to-mars-that-obama-used":"The story goes that the CIA has a \"jump room\": people go up to the fifth floor of a building, ride an elevator that then moves sideways, and from there they \"jump to Mars\", and Barack Obama is said to have been there. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as a story he credits to Andrew Basiago, a man who says he took part in secret government teleportation programs. If true, it would mean the US has had secret travel to Mars.","cia-killed-ahmad-shah-massoud-before-9-11-us-created-al-qaed-1979":"The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that the CIA killed Ahmad Shah Massoud, the Afghan commander who led the Northern Alliance, \"right before 9/11\", and calls Massoud \"a decent guy\". Jorjani also says the US created al-Qaeda to fight the Soviets after 1979, and he names no source for either claim. If true, it would mean American intelligence shaped the very forces it later went to war against.","cia-memo-calls-ufos-a-national-security-matter-1952":"In 1952 a wave of UFO sightings led a worried CIA to write a memo calling the subject a matter of national security that could set off mass hysteria and panic. Michels tells this himself, and in a later episode he reads from the memo, which he credits to Walter B. Smith: \"The flying saucer situation contains two elements of danger, which in a situation of international tension have national security implications. These are psychological and air vulnerability.\" The memo names a danger of panic and a danger that American skies could not be defended, but in the words quoted it says nothing about what the objects were.","cia-memo-popularizing-the-term-conspiracy-1970":"A CIA memo from the 1970s said the agency would popularize the word \"conspiracy\" so that people would look away, according to Michels. He says this himself during a debate with a skeptic, without naming the memo or quoting it. If true, it would mean the label used to dismiss certain claims was itself spread on purpose to discourage people from looking into them.","cia-offer-to-head-an-elf-research-department-brought-by-bob-1977":"Sometime between 1977 and 1979, at the house of Andrija Puharich, a doctor who researched the paranormal, Bob Beck is said to have brought word of a CIA offer to head a research department on ELF, short for extremely low frequency radio waves. A researcher on the show, who is not named, tells it from Puharich's own handwritten notes on legal pads. If true, it would mean the CIA was recruiting people from Puharich's circle for this kind of research.","cia-office-of-research-and-development-continues-mk-ultra-wo-1960":"The CIA's secret mind and drug experiments, known as MK Ultra, were carried on by a CIA division called the Office of Research and Development, led by a man named Stephen Aldrich. Michels takes this from the book The Search for the Manchurian Candidate by John Marks. He tells it as the link to later work, including the start of the US remote viewing programme, the effort to train people to see distant places with the mind.","cia-official-blocking-transfer-of-a-non-human-craft-between":"A CIA official named Greenwald, whom Michels calls the agency's director of science and technology, is said to be blocking the transfer of a UFO, a craft not made by people, from the aerospace company Lockheed to Bigelow, a private group that studied UFOs. Michels says this to Jeremy Corbell, adding that it was something he had just discussed with an earlier guest, James Lacatski. If true, a recovered craft would be sitting in a company's hands while the CIA decides who gets it.","cia-oga-and-jsoc-perform-crash-retrievals-2002":"The claim is that crashed craft of unknown origin are recovered by elite US units: a CIA arm known as OGA, short for \"Other Government Agency,\" and JSOC, the Joint Special Operations Command. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb says this on the show. He bases it on \"excellent reporting\" by the journalist Chris Sharp and others, and says the CIA's OGA was created in 2002 and is used with JSOC \"to perform crash retrievals and so forth.\" Michels makes a similar point in a later episode. He says the crash retrieval programme is \"rumored to be closely coordinated\" with JSOC and names a unit he calls Grey Fox, or ISA, as part of it, but he leaves out the CIA arm. If true, it would mean the recovery of such craft sits inside the country's most secret commando units.","cia-osi-takes-ufo-research-underground-blue-book-as-cover-1952":"The claim is that in 1952 the CIA moved the real study of UFOs into secret, while the Air Force's public program served as a decoy. Michels tells it himself. He says the CIA's Office of Scientific Intelligence took real UFO work underground while running a psychological operation on the public through Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. Elsewhere he calls it a long-standing rumor. He also says that, thanks to the researcher Richard Dolan, it is clear the private side was handled by the Battelle Memorial Institute, a research contractor, under something called Project Stork. If true, the public would have spent decades looking at the wrong program. The other source is a history of the CIA's role in studying UFOs, written by Gerald Haines and published by the CIA in 1997. It says that in 1952 the CIA formed \"a special study group within the Office of Scientific Intelligence.\" It says Edward Tauss, reporting for that group, urged \"that CIA conceal its interest from the media and the public.\" It also says the Air Force's intelligence center \"tried to persuade the public that UFOs were not extraordinary.\" That describes a hidden CIA interest next to a public Air Force effort, which is close to what Michels means. Whether Blue Book was a deliberate cover for the hidden work, the history neither says nor rules out.","cia-paid-paramilitary-bases-for-cuban-exiles-1960":"Between 1960 and 1962, says a researcher named Dan Sheehan, the CIA paid for armed training camps for Cuban exiles in places such as No Name Key, Swan Island, the Florida Everglades and Lake Pontchartrain. Sheehan sums it up for Michels without naming a source: \"They set one up on No Name Key. They set one up on Swan Island.\"","cia-plan-for-a-jesus-vision-over-havana-1960":"The CIA once considered making Jesus appear as a vision in the sky over Havana, to persuade Cubans to overthrow Fidel Castro. A researcher named Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, as an example of how far the agency was willing to go in staging the miraculous.","cia-psychiatrist-explains-why-hall-was-chosen-41-men-before-1960":"Before him, 41 men had been sent out to deal with tall, white, human-like beings in the Nevada desert, and all of them \"came back compromised and some of them burned,\" Charles Hall says. He tells Michels a CIA psychiatrist he does not name told him this. Hall says he spent three years with these beings in the 1960s at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada, and writes about it in his book Millennial Hospitality; by this account, he was chosen because he could do what the men before him could not.","cia-regressionists-regress-burroughs-from-the-rendlesham-cas-1980":"In the 1980s, two CIA hypnotists who used regression, a method of guiding someone back through buried memories, are said to have regressed John Burroughs, a witness from a UFO case in Rendlesham Forest in England. A guest named Grant Cameron repeats this from people he knows. He says \"I got two guys and they were CIA regressionists,\" names Bob Emenegger and Allan Sandler in connection with the story, and places the sessions at Sandler's studio in New York. If true, it would mean the CIA quietly took an interest in what that witness remembered.","cia-remote-viewing-program":"The CIA ran a programme on remote viewing, the attempt to see distant places or hidden things with the mind alone. Several people mention it to Michels as a known fact. The UFO researcher Steven Greer calls it \"the cheap end\" of what the government has explored, and does not name a source. The writer Nick Cook says that despite doubts, the agency \"went ahead and did it anyway and funded it for 18 years or something.\" Michels himself says in one episode that the CIA's work on remote viewing and parapsychology, the study of psychic abilities, is documented. He points to a 2017 release under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records. In another episode he adds that \"it's been declassified.\" The tellers bring it up because, if a spy agency spent years paying for psychic research, then ideas that sound far-fetched were taken seriously inside government.","cia-robertson-panel-1953":"In early 1953, a panel of scientists brought together by the CIA, known as the Robertson panel, recommended that the government play down public interest in flying saucers. Karl Nell, a guest speaking from an official background, says declassified papers show the panel decided the government should debunk the subject, mislead the public, and \"actually infiltrate\" the UFO groups. A Senate staffer, Kurt McConnell, adds that the panel's recommendations were made in secret and included detecting and tracking the objects as well as debunking them. If they are right, the government ran a deliberate effort to shape what Americans thought about UFOs. The panel's report, dated February 16, 1953, by the CIA's Office of Scientific Intelligence, is unusually direct on debunking. It calls for an educational programme with \"two major aims: training and 'debunking'\", meant to reduce \"public interest in 'flying saucers'\" through \"television, motion pictures, and popular articles\". It also urges that the security agencies \"strip the Unidentified Flying Objects of the special status they have been given and the aura of mystery\". On civilian groups, it names two and says they \"should be watched\", and that \"the possible use of such groups for subversive purposes should be kept in mind.\" Whether watching meant infiltrating, the report neither says nor rules out.","cia-s-charles-cabell-the-dallas-motorcade-route-and-project-1950":"A CIA officer named Charles Cabell looked at Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and called it a \"super shoddy operation\", according to Michels. Michels says this is written up in Edward J. Ruppelt's book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects, published in 1956. He tells it in an episode about a man he links to many American conspiracies, where the same Cabell is also tied to the Dallas motorcade route, and he uses it to show that a senior intelligence figure was paying close attention to how the UFO question was handled.","cia-s-scientific-engineering-institute-farm-lab-near-boston-1960":"Members of a CIA office Michels calls the OAD set up the Scientific Engineering Institute, a company secretly owned by the CIA near Boston, with a lab on a farm of several hundred acres. Michels tells this himself, in an episode on human experiments, without naming a source. If true, the CIA ran research through a front company in rural Massachusetts.","cia-screening-genetic-databases-for-nordic-markers":"The CIA is said to have a program that searches consumer DNA databases for genetic markers the story calls \"Nordic.\" The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as something said to him, in a conversation he links to Lyn Buchanan and to Kit Green, a man he places at the CIA: \"we know that Kit Green at the CIA has some program to access the 23 and me and like whatever ancestry.com databases.\" If true, it would mean an intelligence agency is quietly screening the public's DNA for a particular bloodline.","cia-sends-mooney-s-cherokee-book-and-asks-about-native-ameri-1891":"Chris Bledsoe, a man whose experiences with UFOs have drawn interest, says a book arrived at his home in North Carolina, sent by \"some folks in the government, CIA,\" with a note that said \"Read this.\" The book was Myths of the Cherokee, written by James Mooney and published in 1891, and in this story the same people also asked him about Native American DNA. Bledsoe tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and it would suggest that people in government think his experiences connect to old Native American stories, and maybe to his family line.","cia-shut-down-the-reverse-engineering-effort-in-1989-after-n":"In 1989, the story goes, the CIA shut down its whole effort at reverse engineering, meaning taking apart recovered craft of unknown origin to learn how they work, because after several decades neither its people nor the companies working on it had made any progress. Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has worked on government studies of UFOs, tells Michels he was given this \"firm\" word, without naming who told him.","cia-stargate-program-s-hits-questioned-1970":"Stargate was a CIA programme that used remote viewers, people who said they could describe distant places or hidden targets with their minds alone, and the skeptic Michael Shermer grants that they 'got some hits'. Shermer brings it up in a debate with Michels, without naming a source, and he questions what those hits really show. Whether the hits were real, in his view, decides whether the programme found anything at all.","cia-telepathy-drawing-tests-at-sri-1970":"In the 1970s the CIA tested whether a man could receive drawings by telepathy, at the Stanford Research Institute (SRI), a research centre in Palo Alto, California. The man tested was Uri Geller, the Israeli-born performer famous for his claimed psychic feats, and he tells it on the show as his own experience: \"the CIA took me to Stanford Research Institute,\" where drawings were made \"miles away\" and he had to pick them up with his mind. It is part of the same story as the government's psychic spying programme that began at SRI, and if true it shows the CIA took telepathy seriously enough to test it.","cia-visits-nolan-s-stanford-office-to-recruit-him-2010":"A Stanford scientist named Garry Nolan says that when he first got involved in studying UFO-related material, sometime in the 2010s, the CIA came to his office at Stanford University. He tells Michels this from his own experience, as the start of his part in the subject, and it would mean the agency itself sought out scientists for this work.","cic-agent-tricks-schauberger-into-signing-his-work-over-ip-e-1945":"After the Second World War, an agent of the Army's Counter Intelligence Corps named Neil Gershimer is said to have tricked a man named Victor Shawberger into signing all of his work over to the United States, and that work is said to have ended up at Brookhaven, a government research lab on Long Island. Michels tells this himself without naming a source, as part of a story about the US quietly gathering unusual wartime science.","cic-interrogates-viktor-schauberger-for-nine-months-1945":"After the Second World War, agents of the Counter Intelligence Corps, the US Army's counterintelligence service, questioned a researcher named Viktor Schauberger for nine months, fully aware of his most classified work. Michels tells this from the historian Nick Cook's book The Hunt for Zero Point, which he calls great. The point is that the Americans saw Schauberger's secret wartime work in Austria and Germany as worth months of close attention.","cicada-3301-as-an-intelligence-recruitment-puzzle-2014":"Cicada 3301, a mysterious puzzle posted on the internet around 2014, was set up by the NSA or the CIA as a way to recruit people, and Michels says many people think so. He mentions the idea himself, without naming a source. If true, it would mean intelligence agencies were using public codebreaking games to find talent.","cigar-shaped-craft-near-turtle-mountain":"A woman named Christa says she saw an object shaped like a cigar near Turtle Mountain, on the road from Cuba, New Mexico. She tells it on the show as her own sighting, saying simply, \"it looked like a cigar,\" and she brings a United Airlines pilot into her account.","cigar-shaped-object-in-distress-seen-by-farmers-over-varginh-1996":"In January 1996, probably on the 13th, several people near Varginha, a city in the Brazilian state of Minas Gerais, are said to have seen a large cylinder-shaped craft, a bit bigger than a school bus, in the sky before it came down. The main teller is a researcher named James Fox, who tells Michels about it on two episodes. On the first he credits a work called Moment of Contact. On the second he says he interviewed these firsthand witnesses himself. He adds a detail that ties the sighting to the rest of the Varginha story: a doctor named Armando Fortunato, who he says did the autopsy on a military officer who died, has two farmer friends who saw the same object before it crashed. A second guest on that episode, a man named Ahmed Foratu, tells it a little differently. He speaks of people close to Varginha, not farmers, who saw a ship the size of a bus heading toward the city in those days, and he says nothing about it being in trouble. If the accounts hold, they would mean that ordinary local people saw a craft shortly before the crash that the rest of the Varginha story is about.","cinclant-code-zebra-alert-over-object-off-newfoundland-1980":"Sometime in the 1980s, an unknown object off Newfoundland is said to have put the US Navy's Atlantic Command in Norfolk, Virginia, on a full alert called \"code zebra.\" Steven Greer tells Michels this as it was told to him by Merle Shane McDow, who was \"working under\" the commander-in-chief of that command and names Admiral Harry Train in the story, and Greer credits the book Disclosure: An Illustrated History. If true, a major US military command treated a UFO as a real threat.","citizen-hearing-on-disclosure-2013":"In 2013 there were what were called the \"citizens hearings\", a series of sessions on UFO disclosure, meaning the public release of what governments know, and anyone can watch them on YouTube. Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he once used his helicopter to retrieve a UFO, recommends them to Michels. He suggests they hold testimony worth hearing, though he does not say here what it is.","city-of-11-000-structures-found-in-ecuadorian-jungle":"Somewhere in the Ecuadorian Amazon, researchers are said to have found a lost city of about 11,000 structures. A researcher named Luke Caverns says so on the show, hedging with \"I think\" and not saying who found it or when. If true, the jungle would have hidden a large, forgotten civilisation.","civilizations-switching-on-at-the-same-time-around-3000-bc":"The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels that early civilisations in Egypt, the Indus Valley in what is now Pakistan, and along China's Yellow River all seem to \"switch on\" at about the same moment, around 3000 BC. He says the Indus civilisation's \"engine\" started in \"exactly the same period\" as Egypt's, and he names no source. Hancock raises it as a hint that these cultures shared some older, lost inheritance.","cky-tv-film-of-object-jumping-from-the-ground-to-5-000-ft-1975":"In May 1975 a film crew from CKY, a television station in Manitoba, Canada, is said to have filmed an object over the town of Carman that leapt from the ground to 5,000 feet in a few frames. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels the film caught \"this thing jumping from the ground to 5,000 ft in the air in three frames, four frames.\" He points to a CKY documentary about it. A passage credited to the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, from a book titled UFOs: It Has Begun, gives the date as 13 May 1975 and says \"Cameraman Alan Carr of station CKY\" filmed \"two separate sightings over Carman in front of four witnesses.\" Cameron adds something from his own talk with the man who shot it: \"he wasn't even a cameraman. He was a film editor in the studio.\" If the film shows what they say, it would show something moving far faster than any known aircraft.","claiborne-pell-jack-reed-and-biden-a-ufo-lineage":"Michels traces a line of interest in UFOs through the US Senate, starting with Claiborne Pell, a senator he says was long into UFOs and places in Delaware. He links Pell to Jack Reed, a senator from Rhode Island on the Senate Armed Services Committee, and to Joe Biden. Michels draws the line himself, without naming a source, and it suggests UFO interest passed quietly between powerful politicians.","claim-that-the-bacteria-looked-genetically-engineered-1996":"The bacteria behind the deadly infection in the 1996 Varginha case in Brazil looked as if they had been genetically engineered. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels that is his understanding, and that just the day before he had heard a doctor he calls Dr. Eel say so. If true, the germs would have been made on purpose, not found in nature.","claimed-carl-sagan-aliens-are-real-statement-redacted-denied-1990":"A story went around that the astronomer Carl Sagan once said he thought aliens are real, and that the statement was redacted, or hidden. The skeptic Michael Shermer tells Michels he checked with Sagan's widow, Ann Druyan, a friend of his, and that she told him \"that never happened.\" Shermer brings it up as an example of a claim that falls apart once you ask the people involved.","claims-of-secret-ships-to-europa-heard-at-contact-in-the-des":"Speakers at Contact in the Desert, a UFO conference, are said to have claimed that ships are being sent in secret to Europa, a moon of Jupiter. A guest named Peter Levenda tells this as something he heard said there: \"It could be possible we're sending ships to Europa on a regular basis.\" If true, it would mean a hidden space programme reaching far beyond anything made public.","clandestine-christian-ex-special-forces-group-approaches-the-2024":"Congressman Eric Burlison says a secretive group of Christian former special forces soldiers approached him in Washington, DC, sometime between 2024 and 2026. He tells Michels firsthand that they \"kind of bumped me\" at a meeting about something else, which he says is \"the best way to describe it\". If true, it would mean a hidden group of former soldiers is quietly seeking out members of Congress.","clapper-and-brennan-speak-of-roswell-and-a-surviving-being-i-1947":"Two of President Obama's top intelligence officials, his director of national intelligence James Clapper and his CIA director John Brennan, appear in a film and speak of the 1947 Roswell incident in New Mexico, a claimed crash of an unknown craft, as a real event, with beings coming out of it and possibly one surviving, according to Michels. He tells it himself across several episodes, and in one he names the film as Age of Disclosure, says four beings came out of the craft, names a man called Jim Semivan alongside Clapper instead of Brennan, and is less sure about the survivor. He raises it because such senior officials saying this on camera would be striking if they meant it literally.","clapper-asks-nsa-director-mike-rogers-for-all-of-nsa-s-ufo-f-2010":"Sometime in the 2010s, James Clapper, the top US intelligence official, is said to have asked the NSA to hand over all of its UFO data. Michels tells this himself, passing on what Mike Rogers, a former head of the NSA, said in a public interview: Clapper \"called him and asked for all the UFO data from the NSA.\" If true, it would mean the NSA held UFO records and the most senior intelligence officials wanted them.","clapper-on-ufo-detections-at-area-51-in-the-age-of-disclosur-2010":"James Clapper, a former head of US intelligence, said on camera that at Area 51, the secret military test site in Nevada, the US was systematically detecting UFOs in that sensitive airspace, Michels says. He cites Clapper's words in The Age of Disclosure, a documentary on government UFO secrecy, during a debate with a skeptic. If true, it would mean a top intelligence official confirmed UFOs were being tracked over one of America's most guarded sites.","clarkdale-cement-plant-as-egress-to-secret-facility":"The Clarkdale Cement Plant in Clarkdale, Arizona, is said to be an exit point, an \"egress point\", into a whole secret underground facility. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this as something he was told, in an episode about what he calls a UFO base in Arizona; if true, an ordinary industrial site would be hiding the way into a hidden base.","class-1-tetra-objects-fly-in-formations-of-three-to-twenty-2021":"The objects that Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs, sorts into its \"Class 1\" or \"tetra\" category usually fly in formations of three to twenty. James Valor, who speaks for Skywatcher and says he has seen them on the company's operations between 2021 and 2025, tells Michels that \"the objects of the first class usually fly in groups.\" If true, it suggests these objects move in coordinated groups rather than alone.","class-2-tic-tac-objects-about-40-feet-long-seen-in-2022-2023":"James Valor, of a startup called Skywatcher that says it can call up UFOs and record them, says his team sorts what they see into classes, and the second class is the \"tic tac\", a white oval object shaped like the mint, about 40 feet long. He tells Michels the team saw these objects themselves in 2022, in 2023 and in February 2025.","class-3-as-a-holographic-projection-or-laser-plasmoids-2021":"Some of the strange lights seen by Skywatcher, a startup that says it tries to call up UFOs, may be projections in the sky, like holograms, or glowing balls of charged gas made by lasers, rather than solid craft. James Valor of Skywatcher, who says he was there, puts this to Michels about what the group calls \"Class 3\" objects. He stresses \"it's still only theory\" but asks, \"isn't everything in holographic sky projection?\"","class-3-blob-a-pepto-bismol-colored-cloud-with-a-golden-core-2021":"James Valor, of Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call up UFOs, describes one kind of object his team has seen: what they call a \"Class 3\", a \"spot\" that looks like a cloud of \"Pepto-Bismol color\" with a golden core that vibrates. Valor tells Michels he saw these himself during the company's operations between 2021 and 2025, and if he is right it would be a type of unexplained object that shows up again and again.","class-4-spheres-seen-only-during-restricted-government-activ":"Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs and sorts what it sees into numbered classes, says its \"Class 4\" object is a sphere that shows up only during restricted government activities. The team tells Michels, as people who say they saw it, that \"class 4 is a sphere\" and that they saw it several years in a row, always at such government events. If true, it would tie this kind of object to secret official activity.","class-6-uap-shuts-down-three-systems-after-a-directed-energy-2003":"The story is that a strange object, called a 'class 6' UAP (UAP being the government's current term for UFOs), switched off a directed-energy weapon, a weapon that fires a focused beam of energy, when it was pointed at it. A member of Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs into view, tells it on the show as something they took part in. They say that around 2003 they spent a year in the western United States and had the chance to bring an energy weapon to a government event on a hilltop. As they tell it, when the weapon was aimed at the object, three systems shut down. If true, the object could defeat a weapon before it fired. Michels tells a version he heard from a man named James Fowler, in which a 'jellyfish' shaped object 'just shut down the directed energy before it even went out.' His version places it in the 2020s, not around 2003.","class-7-jellyfish-outruns-the-little-bird-helicopter-2025":"The team at Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call up UFOs and record them, says it has video from 2025 of chasing a \"jellyfish\", the seventh class of object in its own sorting scheme, with a Little Bird, a small helicopter. They tell Michels the object changed course and headed into the wind, outrunning the helicopter, which if true would mean it was moving under its own power rather than drifting like a balloon.","class-7-object-hovers-overhead-before-the-helicopter-arrives-2025":"During a 2025 field operation by Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs, the team says an object of the highest kind on their own scale, which they call class 7, came and hung right above them, and looked like chrome to the naked eye. James Valor, who was there, tells Michels that the team decided to send up a helicopter to chase it, but \"even before the helicopter arrived, he flew up and hovered right above us.\" The Skywatcher team also tells it on the same episode, crediting a member named Rory. In their version it happened on the fourth day of what they call Jellyfish Day, the object \"arrived and hovered over us for about 5 minutes\" at around 800 metres, and it knocked out their sensors. Their version leaves out the helicopter. Both tellers are people who say they saw it themselves, from the same company. If true, it would suggest the object knew it was being chased and came toward the team rather than away.","classical-greek-theater-aeschylus-prometheus-trilogy-gave-ri":"Classical Greek theatre, and in particular Aeschylus's Prometheus plays, is said to have given humans both geometric perspective and psychological perspective, because for the first time in recorded history people sat as spectators in different seats on different nights and saw the same story from different angles. The philosopher Jason Jorjani makes this argument on the show, drawing on his own book Prometheus and Atlas and on Julian Jaynes's book The Origin of Consciousness in the Breakdown of the Bicameral Mind, as part of a larger idea that human consciousness changed at a datable point.","classified-able-archer-crisis-felt-inside-sandia-labs-1983":"In 1983 the world came as close to nuclear war as during the Cuban Missile Crisis, in a secret standoff called Able Archer, and only people inside the fence at Sandia National Laboratory in New Mexico knew how bad it was. That is the account of a physicist named John Brandenburg, who says he was at the lab then. He tells Michels the crisis \"was classified\" and the public never knew.","classified-asset-documents-show-a-late-1980s-russian-crash-r-1987":"The physicist Eric Davis says that in the late 1980s the Soviets recovered a crashed UFO in Russia. He says he learned this while working on a Pentagon-funded study of unexplained aerial phenomena that he calls \"the AAWSAP program\": a contact at a \"three-letter agency\" had the reports, gathered not from KGB records but from the agency's own source inside Russia. Michels later retells it differently, dating the crash to 1989, crediting the documentary The Age of Disclosure, and adding that the Soviets got a directed energy weapon from the craft. If true, it would mean the Soviet Union was recovering and copying alien craft too.","classified-dod-payloads-as-evidence-of-a-secret-space-progra-1980":"A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani points to the secret Pentagon cargo launched from Vandenberg Air Force Base as a sign that the government may run a hidden space programme. Without naming a source, he tells Michels that \"there are classified payloads that are launched into space routinely\" from the base. If he is right, a good deal of what goes into space would be out of public view.","classified-f-117-crash-near-bakersfield-1986":"A secret F-117, the stealth jet the Air Force kept hidden for years, is said to have crashed near Bakersfield, California, in 1986 or thereabouts. Michels mentions it in passing, unsure of the date, as an example of a classified aircraft whose existence leaked out through an accident.","classified-lists-of-uncorrelated-targets-since-the-1960s":"Since the early 1960s, the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel says, there have been classified lists of \"uncorrelated targets\": objects tracked in orbit around Earth that do not match anything known. She tells Michels it was news to her, something she learned from others rather than saw herself, and the lists are tied to bodies such as the US Space Force, NASA and the European Space Agency. If true, it would mean space trackers have long logged unexplained objects near Earth and kept the record secret.","classified-navy-diver-unit-experimenting-with-liquid-breathi":"A secret unit of US Navy divers has experimented with liquid breathing, the idea of breathing an oxygen-rich liquid instead of air, according to Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy rear admiral. He tells Michels there is \"a special unit, I'm not going to say exactly where, of Navy divers that have experimented with that technology,\" speaking as someone who says he knows of it directly. If true, it would mean the Navy is quietly working on ways for people to go deeper and stay longer under the sea.","classified-paper-comparing-human-and-et-psychic-ability-that-1980":"In the 1980s, says the former Army sergeant Lyn Buchanan, he was ordered to write a classified paper comparing the psychic abilities of humans with those of extraterrestrials, and \"as soon as I turned it in, it disappeared\". Buchanan served in the Army's remote viewing unit, a group trained to describe distant places and things by the mind alone, and tells Michels this as something that happened to him. If true, the military took alien psychic ability seriously enough to study it and then hid the result.","classified-scif-testimony-to-congress-2025":"Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he flew a UFO out of a recovery site with his helicopter, says that in 2025 more was shared with Congress than the public has heard, in a SCIF, a sealed room for handling secret material, at the top secret level known as TS/SCI. He tells Michels this as someone who took part, and it would mean lawmakers have been given evidence behind closed doors that has not been made public.","classified-signals-lab-picks-up-a-signal-from-the-implant-1993":"A small object said to have been removed from the writer Whitley Strieber's body, which he calls an implant, gave off a signal when tested at the Southwest Research Institute in San Antonio, Texas, in the mid 1990s. Strieber, who says he was involved, tells Michels the signal switched on and then turned off. He says the lab staff told him, \"We've got a signal, but we can't tell you anything about it because the lab's classified,\" and he names a man called William Malow in the account.","classified-tooth-implant-1960":"In the early 1960s, at a New York City company called Intelectron, a scientist named Andrija Puharich is said to have invented a tooth implant, a device placed in a tooth, which Puharich himself said was classified. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels this without naming a source, as one of several inventions from Puharich's work in those years.","classified-video-meeting-smiling-official-says-it-may-not-be-2025":"James Valor, of Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call up UFOs, says that in 2025 he was in a classified video meeting, \"in secret mode\", where an official smiled a little and said what they were discussing may not be from our planet. Valor tells Michels he was in the meeting himself, and he tells it as a sign that some officials privately take the idea seriously.","claudio-valente-quits-his-job-and-vanishes-2013":"One day in 2013, a man named Claudio Valente went to work at SAPO in Portugal, announced it would be his last day, and then disappeared, according to Michels. Michels tells it himself, citing a man named Sergio Bastos, in an episode about scientists who have gone missing. If true, it would add Valente to a pattern of skilled people vanishing without explanation.","clay-shaw-phones-fred-crisman-during-the-garrison-investigat-1967":"While Jim Garrison, a New Orleans prosecutor, was investigating the Kennedy assassination in the late 1960s, a man named Clay Shaw is said to have phoned a man named Fred Crisman, because the two seem to have known each other well. The researcher Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would tie Crisman to the people caught up in Garrison's case.","clearance-games-at-bae-2015-2018":"While working at BAE Systems, a large defence contractor, between 2015 and 2018, Dylan Borland says the people around him were \"playing this game\" with access to secret information. Borland, a former intelligence worker who has come forward as a whistleblower on the UFO subject, tells Michels this from his own experience, but he leaves the details of the game vague. As he tells it, the point is that access to what he needed was being manipulated.","clementine-mission-as-photo-reconnaissance-for-moon-bases-1994":"Clementine, a 1994 mission to the Moon, was \"basically a photo reconnaissance mission\" to check whether someone unknown was building bases on the Moon and making them bigger. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this, passing on the view of a man named John Brandenburg, and links it to a book called Aliens on the Moon: The Truth Exposed.","cliff-wong-on-faking-an-orb-with-a-drone-towed-balloon":"A drone expert named Cliff Wong is said to have explained how to fake an \"orb\", a glowing ball of light in the sky: tow a balloon behind a drone. A former intelligence official named Eric Haseltine tells this on the show. He says he asked Wong, whom he calls \"the world's expert on drones,\" \"If you wanted to fake X, Y, or Z, how would you do it?\" and this is what Wong told him, which would mean some orb sightings could be staged.","clinic-doctor-turns-away-captured-creature-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, where people say a craft crashed and a living creature was caught, a clinic doctor is said to have refused to treat the creature when it was brought in, saying: \"I don't know what that thing is but get it out of here.\" The filmmaker James Fox, who investigated the case, tells this second-hand in an account he ties to a man named Marco Eli Chereze. If true, it would place a non-human being in the hands of ordinary Brazilian medical staff.","clinical-examination-by-beings-1976":"Charlie Foltz says that in August 1976 near Allagash, Maine, he found himself aboard a craft, struggling to get up, when a face bent down to look into his eyes and the beings began a clinical examination of him. He tells Michels he remembers a panel coming down from above his head until it covered his whole torso, and a blue light shining straight down from it onto him.","clinton-and-obama-both-deny-anything-underground-at-area-51-1990":"Presidents Bill Clinton and Barack Obama both said there is nothing underground at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada. The researcher Jason Simosa tells this to Michels and finds it odd: \"What a weird thing to say about it.\" He names no source, and he raises it because he reads the denial itself as a hint that something is hidden below ground there.","clinton-and-the-alh-84001-meteorite-as-slow-drip-disclosure-1990":"Michels suggests that the government has been letting out the truth about life beyond Earth slowly, a drop at a time, and gives an example from the 1990s: a meteorite known as ALH 84001, linked to Mars, which he says President Bill Clinton \"came out with\". He offers this himself, as his own framing in a conversation with a guest, without going into what was announced, and if he is right it would mean such announcements are planned steps toward a larger disclosure.","clinton-briefed-on-ufos-by-civilian-bruce-maccabee-via-ron-p-1990":"In the 1990s, a CIA man named Ron Pandolfi is said to have arranged for Bruce Maccabee, a civilian UFO researcher, to brief the Clinton White House on UFOs. Michels tells it as Maccabee briefing Bill Clinton himself, with Pandolfi helping. The researcher Jason Simosa tells it from Maccabee's own account, calling him a Navy physicist: Pandolfi phoned and said, \"I need you to go in and give the lecture you gave to the CIA once. I want you to give it to the science advisor.\" In Simosa's telling, then, the audience was Clinton's science adviser rather than the president. If true, it would mean UFO material reached the White House through a CIA back channel.","clinton-ends-the-superconducting-super-collider-1993":"A researcher named Eric Weinstein tells Michels that on October 30, 1993, President Bill Clinton signed a law that dealt \"the death blow\" to the Superconducting Super Collider, a giant American machine planned for smashing particles together to study physics. Weinstein brings up the date from memory, without naming a source, as the moment the project was killed.","clinton-era-investigation-finds-40-tons-of-nazi-gold-shipped-1940":"An investigation under the Clinton administration found 40 tons of Nazi gold that had been shipped out of the Bank of Lisbon by submarine during and after the war, according to a researcher named Peter Levenda, who tells Michels this without naming the report. In the story the gold's trail leads toward Asia, to places such as Macau, China and Indonesia, which would mean Nazi wealth escaped Europe on a large scale.","clinton-era-pressure-on-the-dod-that-shaped-barber-s-path-in-1990":"In the 1990s, under Bill Clinton, the Pentagon came under pressure over its UFO secrets, including a so-called red team set up to get inside the black programs, meaning secret projects hidden even from most of government. A former helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, who says he retrieved a UFO with his helicopter, ties his own path to that pressure. He tells Michels, \"it's no coincidence that it happened during the Clinton era.\"","clinton-era-technology-transfer-to-china-1990":"In the 1990s, under President Clinton, the Commerce Department and the Defense Department let American military technology pass to China, according to a former US Secretary of Energy named John S. Herrington. As proof, he tells Michels that \"every single Chinese fighter looks like American fighter F-15.\" He treats this as a more serious leak of secrets than the ones people usually worry about.","clinton-s-televised-statement-on-rock-84001-1996":"In 1996 President Bill Clinton went on television to talk about a meteorite from Mars known as Rock 84001, saying it \"speaks to us across all those billions of years and millions of miles\" and \"speaks of the possibility of life.\" The clip is played on Michels's show as an official nod to the idea that Mars once held life.","clinton-said-he-didn-t-think-they-d-tell-him-about-aliens":"Bill Clinton, asked in an interview whether the government had signs of alien life or was hiding a program about it, is said to have answered, in effect, \"I don't think they tell me even though I'm the president.\" Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former senior CIA officer, mentions this to Michels as an interview he knows of, as a sign that such secrets might be kept even from presidents.","clock-gear-found-inside-marble-in-brazil":"Workers sawing marble slabs out of mountains in Brazil are said to have found pieces of a clock inside the stone, even a gear. Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of a Russian republic who says he was taken by aliens, tells Michels this without naming a source or a date. If true, it would mean a made object was sealed inside rock far older than any clockmaker.","close-encounters-as-a-celebration-of-an-accommodation-with-t-1977":"The 1977 film Close Encounters of the Third Kind was, according to the researcher John Brandenburg, \"the government's celebration, through Hollywood, of an accommodation with the gray aliens,\" the short gray beings of many abduction stories. Brandenburg tells Michels this as an argument from his own book Cosmic Awakening, not from a named source. If true, the government would have struck some kind of deal with aliens and quietly marked it on movie screens.","close-encounters-contains-a-visual-reference-to-project-serp-1977":"The 1977 Steven Spielberg film Close Encounters of the Third Kind, whose climax takes place at Devils Tower in Wyoming, contains a visual reference to Project Serpo. Serpo is a story about a supposed secret exchange programme in which US personnel were sent to an alien world. The writer Bryce Zabel says this to Michels: \"whether you believe it or not, put that aside, there's a whole kind of visual reference to Project Serpo in there.\" If he is right, the film would have nodded to the Serpo tale decades before it became public.","close-encounters-of-the-third-kind-as-informed-by-real-ufo-m-1977":"Steven Spielberg's 1977 film Close Encounters of the Third Kind drew on real UFO material, Michels says, pointing to crates in the film marked TRW and Lockheed, two big defense contractors. Michels offers this as his own reading of the film. If true, Hollywood would have slipped real clues about secret UFO work into the movie.","close-up-witnesses-sensed-being-watched":"People who had close-up UFO sightings told David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, that they had a feeling of being watched. Grusch says on the show that \"some folks I talked to\" with up-close sightings \"got that vibe, too,\" and he passes it on as what they told him, without naming them.","closed-session-testimony-on-rocky-flats":"Former Secretary of Energy John S. Herrington says he has gone back to Congress several times to testify in closed sessions on security at the Energy Department, especially about Rocky Flats, Colorado, a Department of Energy site there. He tells Michels this from his own experience, and he tells it as a sign that what happened at Rocky Flats is still discussed only behind closed doors.","closing-down-the-first-base-and-transfer-to-the-second-base-1990":"Between 1990 and 1993, the base where a secret programme called PPD was run was shut down and the work moved to a second base. That is the account of Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, and who tells Michels he took part in it himself: \"I close that base down,\" he says, adding that the whole base closed, \"not just our aspect of it.\" If true, it would mean his alien-contact programme was big enough to move from one site to another.","clyde-tombaugh-and-earth-s-small-moons-gray-motherships-in-o-1950":"In the 1950s the astronomer Clyde Tombaugh is said to have written an article saying that Earth had some small extra moons orbiting about 500 miles up. A scientist named Steve Colbern, who studies objects said to be alien implants, brings this up without naming where he read it and ties it to the idea of gray motherships parked in orbit. If true, large craft would have been circling the planet and seen by a professional astronomer.","clyde-tombaugh-s-ashes-on-new-horizons-2006":"When NASA sent its New Horizons spacecraft off to explore Pluto, it put about 30 grams of the ashes of Clyde Tombaugh, the scientist who discovered Pluto, on board. That is how the Harvard scientist Avi Loeb tells it to Michels, and he calls it \"the most embarrassing thing\" humans have sent into space, without naming a source.","coanda-1936-lenticular-aerodyne-patent":"In 1936 an engineer named Henri Coanda filed a patent for a \"lenticular aerodyne\", a lens-shaped, disc-like flying machine, according to Michels. Michels brings it up himself, citing the patent, in an episode asking whether Oppenheimer worked on UFOs, as evidence that people were designing saucer-shaped craft well before the flying-saucer sightings began.","coast-to-coast-call-drops-at-the-lost-time-question":"While Richard Barth, a former nuclear missile guard who says he was abducted, was on Coast to Coast AM, a late-night US radio show about the paranormal, host George Noory asked whether he had any \"lost time\", a gap in memory, during the event, and just as Barth went to answer, \"everything just went blank.\" Barth tells Michels this happened to him on the call, a story tied to the researcher Robert Hastings and his book UFOs and Nukes.","coast-to-coast-callers-claiming-time-travel-work-at-area-51-1990":"In the 1990s, callers to Art Bell's late-night radio show would phone in claiming they were doing time-travel work at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert. Michels brings this up himself, in a conversation with the musician Flying Lotus, to make the point that UFO whistleblowers are not new: people were making insider claims like these on the radio decades ago.","coating-compound-found-in-the-bent-pyramid":"Inside the Bent Pyramid at Dahshur in Egypt, a coating compound has been found that would have shielded the limestone walls from a harsh, corrosive chemical reaction, says Geoffrey Drumm, a man who argues the pyramids were built for a chemical purpose. He tells Michels this without naming a source, as evidence that the pyramid once housed chemical processes rather than serving only as a tomb.","coburn-s-implant-scans-of-experiencers":"Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects that people reporting alien contact say were placed inside their bodies, says he has done about 400 scans on such people. He tells Michels this from his own work, which, if the objects are what those people believe, would mean physical traces of contact can be found in the body.","cocoa-beach-light-that-exploded-split-into-six-and-followed-2013":"In 2013 a couple at Cocoa Beach, Florida, near Patrick Air Force Base, saw a light that seemed to explode, break into six lights, and then follow them. A NASA engineer named Charles Buhler tells Michels this as something he saw himself, saying, \"It's not one light anymore. It's split into six.\"","cold-war-english-russian-playing-cards-for-communicating-wit":"A NASA doctor named Greg Rogers says that during the Cold War there were special playing cards printed in both English and Russian, made so Americans could talk with Russian-speaking hostages. He tells Michels that if they wanted to show what a word was in English, the other person \"would read it in Russian\", so the cards became \"a means for us to communicate\".","colleagues-close-the-door-and-reveal-hidden-programs-2019":"Colleagues David Grusch had known for years shut his office door and told him about secret programmes he and his team were not being given access to. Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who later told Congress that the government holds non-human craft, tells Michels this himself: they said, \"look, you need to know about some other stuff you guys are not getting access to.\" In a second telling in the same interview he places it at his office at the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency, a US spy agency that works with satellite imagery and maps, says they brought \"crazy intel reports\", and describes the people as \"placed in my life like 14 years ago\", which would point to around 2009 rather than 2019 to 2021. If true, it is how he first learned of what he calls hidden programmes.","colleagues-grandparents-were-ufo-report-people-on-the-manhat-1940":"During the 1940s, some people on the Manhattan Project, the wartime US program that built the atomic bomb, are said to have had the job of handling UFO reports. David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, tells Michels he has colleagues whose \"grandparents were actually you know the UFO report people on the Manhattan Project.\" If true, it would mean the government was tracking UFOs inside its most secret wartime project, before the modern UFO era began.","collecting-the-borderland-science-research-association-archi":"The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn says he travelled to Eureka, California, to collect the archive of the Borderland Science Research Association, an American group that gathered material on UFOs and fringe science. He tells Michels he met the keeper of the papers in his garage, and they \"packed it all together and sent it back to Sweden,\" which is how that collection came to be held there.","collection-of-9-11-precognitive-dreams-2001":"The scientist Rupert Sheldrake says that within three days of the 9/11 attacks on the World Trade Center he put up posters in Washington Square and ran ads in the New York paper the Village Voice asking, \"did you dream of the World Trade Center\". He tells Michels he collected more than 75 dreams that people sent him. He saw the disaster as \"a fantastic research opportunity\", since dreams like these, if real, would mean people can sense events before they happen.","collins-elite-blocking-uap-research-as-demonic":"The Collins Elite is said to be a group of evangelical Christians inside the US government who decided that UFOs were demonic and so worked to block research into them. Michels brings it up again and again. Once he credits it to Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who ran a small office that studied unexplained aerial reports, and to Elizondo's 2024 book Imminent, calling the group one of the main \"blockers\" of UAP research (UAP being the government's newer word for UFOs). In other episodes Michels cites the writer Nick Redfern's book Final Events. He says the group studied the phenomenon in a programme called Stack Five at Wright airfield and came out believing \"this is all demonic.\" He also says the group was \"very embedded in the CIA,\" though in a talk with the writer Peter Levenda he calls it a \"rumor\" and asks what Levenda knows. A researcher named Jason Simosa, also working from Redfern's sources, adds that the programme was funded in 1952 through the CIA's Directorate of Plans, with about 12 people working on it full-time. The physicist Maaneli Derakhshani only says he has heard rumours of it. Every telling goes back to Elizondo or Redfern. If true, it would mean religious belief, not science, shaped what the government was allowed to learn.","collins-elite-met-aliens-and-considered-using-them-to-assass":"A secret group called the Collins Elite first met aliens and then asked whether it could use them to assassinate people. That is how the comedian Kurt Metzger puts it to Michels, paraphrasing the group: \"first we met aliens and then we're like, can we use this to assassinate people?\" He takes it from Final Events, a book by the author Nick Redfern, and also cites a man named Jason Jaconi.","colonel-alexander-s-precognitive-sentient-phenomenon-at-skin-1990":"The strange events reported at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property long studied for unexplained happenings, are said to have led a military officer known as Colonel Alexander to name what he faced \"precognitive sentient phenomena\": something aware that seems to know what the observers will do before they do it. The philosopher Jason Jorjani repeats this on the show, crediting the book Closer Encounters, as a sign that whatever is behind such events may be intelligent and able to anticipate those watching it.","colonel-blanchard-announces-a-crashed-flying-saucer-1947":"In 1947, at the Army air base at Roswell, New Mexico, when \"everybody was seeing UFOs\", the base commander, a popular officer named Colonel Blanchard, is said to have called the local newspaper and told them, in effect, \"Look what we found. We found a crashed flying saucer.\" The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels this as what Roswell veterans said, and it matters because it would mean the military itself first announced a crash of an unknown craft.","colonel-coleman-s-b-25-ufo-encounter-over-alabama-1950":"In the 1950s, a colonel named William Coleman is said to have had a dramatic encounter with a UFO while flying a B-25 bomber over Alabama. The documentary filmmaker James Fox mentions it in passing, saying his research led him to Coleman, and he does not describe the encounter itself; on the show he calls the plane a \"B-55\".","colonel-hohman-sold-sdi-to-reagan-using-false-alien-threat-1980":"In 1980s Washington, a colonel named Hohman is said to have helped sell Ronald Reagan on SDI, the Strategic Defense Initiative, his planned shield against missiles from space, by pointing to an alien threat the people involved knew was false. A guest named Steven Greer says Hohman told him this himself: \"We knew that was false, but it got billions of dollars for SDI.\" If true, it would mean the alien idea was used on purpose to win money for weapons.","colorado-2019-2020-sightings-over-missile-silos":"In 2019 and 2020, unexplained objects were reported in the skies over Colorado, and some of them, Jesse Michels says, appeared over nuclear missile silos. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, and adds that the sightings \"mirrored 1965,\" an earlier wave over the same kind of site. If true, it would suggest that whatever is being seen keeps coming back to America's nuclear weapons.","colossal-biosciences-to-run-ancient-dna-tests-on-the-mummies-2025":"In 2025, Michels says, he decided to have ancient DNA tests run on the strange three-fingered mummies from Peru, and for that he contacted a friend, Ben Lam, at Colossal Biosciences, a genetics company. Michels tells this himself, on an episode about the mummies. The point is that a serious lab could help settle what the bodies really are.","colossal-dna-test-on-the-wolf-carcass-is-mostly-unidentifiab-2020":"Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, says DNA from a wolf carcass found there was sent to Colossal Biosciences, a genetics company, and came back with most of it unidentifiable. He tells Michels this as the man who had the test done, sometime in the 2020s, and it would mean the animal carried genetic material no one could match.","columbia-tv-threatens-to-burn-your-negative-over-the-men-in-1995":"In 1995, Columbia TV threatened to shut down the pilot of Dark Skies, a TV series about UFOs, unless its actors stopped wearing black suits, because the look clashed with the coming film Men in Black. Bryce Zabel, a writer who made the show, tells Michels the order is \"seared in my head\": \"Get your actors out of the black suits today or we will shut down your production and burn your negative.\" He names the film's director Barry Sonnenfeld, Steven Spielberg and Spielberg's company Amblin in the story.","columbus-uses-a-predicted-eclipse-to-make-island-natives-fee":"Christopher Columbus is said to have told islanders who would not feed his crew that he would blot out the sun in three days, knowing that an eclipse was due. Three days later the eclipse came. Chris Ramsay, a guest of Michels, tells the story, sets it in the Bahamas (the usual account puts it in Jamaica), and ties it to Arthur C. Clarke, using it to show how people with more advanced knowledge can look like they have godlike powers to those without it.","coming-out-after-retirement-daily-mail-and-ty-roberts-interv-2025":"A former NASA doctor named Greg Rogers, who says he saw a UFO in a secret hangar, says he went public only after he retired, first in an interview by the reporter Josh Boswell in the UK Daily Mail that came out on 4 May 2025. Rogers tells this himself, and says he also gave an interview to a man named Ty Roberts of the International UFO Bureau in Oklahoma City, for a show called Total Disclosure. It is how he explains why a witness like him stayed quiet for so long.","commander-david-fravor-works-at-bae-2004":"David Fravor, a Navy commander and fighter pilot who was one of the people in the Nimitz incident, now works at the defence company BAE. The Nimitz incident was a 2004 encounter between pilots from the aircraft carrier Nimitz and an unexplained object. Michels says this himself, in passing and without a source, adding only \"I believe,\" and he raises it because it would put a key witness inside the defence industry.","commander-george-hoover-on-aliens-saying-you-don-t-know-how":"A Navy commander named George Hoover is said to have spoken about aliens who come down and tell people, \"you don't know how powerful you are.\" Michels mentions this in passing, without saying where or when Hoover said it, during his interview with David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who testified to Congress about UFOs. If true, it would mean the visitors' message is about hidden human abilities.","commercial-mining-pilot-tests-of-the-sar-technique-under-nda":"The radar researcher Filippo Biondi says he tested his SAR technique for mining companies under nondisclosure agreements. SAR, or synthetic aperture radar, is a satellite radar imaging method, and it is the same technique he used to claim there are structures beneath the Egyptian pyramids. He tells Michels he did \"a lot of pilot tests with companies\" and that they were \"more than 100% successful\", which he offers as proof that the method can really see underground.","communicating-with-the-logical-alien-he-called-spock-1990":"In the early 1990s, at an overseas base, a former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman says he was trained to communicate with aliens by thought, and that the first one he dealt with was so logical he called it \"Spock\". Sherman, who wrote of this in his book Above Black, tells Michels he \"only saw two\" such beings. If true, it means a US intelligence programme used people as telepathic go-betweens with non-human beings.","communion-letters-held-in-the-archives-of-the-impossible-1987":"Thousands of letters that readers wrote to the writer Whitley Strieber after his book Communion, about his own reported encounters with strange beings, are now said to be kept at Rice University in Houston. Strieber tells Michels that \"four or 5 thousand of them\" sit in an archive there called the Archives of the Impossible, which he says was founded by Jeff Kripal. If so, a large body of people's accounts of similar experiences has been preserved for study.","communion-prompts-nearly-half-a-million-letters-to-whitley-s-1987":"After the writer Whitley Strieber put out Communion, his 1987 book about encounters with strange beings, nearly half a million people wrote letters to him and his wife. The Hollywood writer Bryce Zabel tells Michels this, passing on what he heard from Strieber. To Zabel it shows how many people felt they had lived something like what Strieber described.","communion-released-early-because-of-some-kind-of-resistance-1987":"Communion, the 1987 book in which the novelist Whitley Strieber describes encounters with nonhuman visitors, reached bookstores ahead of schedule, and Strieber puts it down to \"some kind of resistance\". He tells Michels, \"I was told a publication date and then all of a sudden the books were in bookstores.\"","community-backlash-and-social-services-visit-2007":"After the strange events of 2007, Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs appear around him, says his community turned on him and social services came to his house \"trying to remove me from the house.\" He tells Michels this from his own life. In another telling in the same interview he leaves out the social services visit and instead says he fell out with half or even two-thirds of his own family, cousins included, and stopped speaking to them. It shows what going public cost him, as he tells it.","compartmentalization-carried-over-from-the-manhattan-project-1940":"The secret UFO programs are said to be run with the same compartmentalization, splitting work into sealed pieces so each person knows only their part, that was used to build the atomic bomb in the 1940s, as \"carryover from the Manhattan Project.\" An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this, crediting the idea to David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who told Congress the US holds crashed craft of non-human origin.","complex-equations-in-vinod-s-automatic-writing-notebook-1950":"In the 1950s, at the Round Table Foundation, a research group in Maine, a man known as Dr. Vinod is said to have filled a notebook with automatic writing, words written in a kind of trance as if dictated by some outside mind, and \"very complex equations\" would come through in it. The researcher Greg Malatzi says this on the show from what he calls the Nine notebook. If true, it would mean the messages held mathematics beyond what the writer was expected to produce himself.","compton-belkovich-heat-anomaly-on-the-lunar-far-side":"NASA's infrared imaging of the whole Moon, says a guest named Maaneli Derakhshani, found one area on the far side, called Compton-Belkovich, that gives off 20 times the average heat of the rest of the far side. Derakhshani tells Michels this without naming a source. He raises it because a hot spot like that would be strange on a Moon thought to be cold and quiet.","concrete-crane-slab-at-the-crash-site-1948":"At Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, where some say a craft crashed in 1948 with bodies aboard, Michels says he found a concrete slab, which he takes to be the base for a crane. Visiting the spot himself, he says that somewhere out there in 1948 someone in a hurry mixed concrete, which, if it dates from then, would point to a rushed effort to lift something heavy out of the canyon.","condon-committee-coordinated-with-air-force-1966":"In 1966 the Air Force paid the University of Colorado to run an outside scientific study of UFOs, led by Edward Condon, and Michels says that study was not as independent as it claimed. He tells it himself on two episodes: the study was 'paid for, but supposed to be uninfluenced by the Air Force,' and in the end Condon did 'an absolute hit job' on the subject, with one telling running the dates to 1969. The final report, Scientific Study of Unidentified Flying Objects, dated 31 October 1968, says the first contract with the Air Force Office of Scientific Research 'provided $313,000 for the first 15 months.' It says the project's 'planning, direction and conclusions' were to be 'conducted wholly independently of the Air Force,' while the Air Force was ordered to hand over its old records and lend staff at its bases when the field teams asked. So the report itself sets out money and working ties to the Air Force. Any coordination beyond that is Michels's reading, which the report neither states nor rules out. If he is right, the study that closed the official book on UFOs was steered by the service it was meant to check.","condon-report-and-its-links-to-atomic-secrecy-1920":"Michels argues, on his own and without naming a source, that the Condon report ties back to the secrecy around the atomic bomb. That report is the Scientific Study of Unidentified Flying Objects, published by the University of Colorado in 1968, which the US Air Force paid for and which the physicist Edward Condon led. Michels points out that Condon had been \"very close friends\" with Oppenheimer since they studied at Göttingen in Germany in the 1920s. He sets the report beside The Los Alamos Primer, the 1943 Los Alamos lecture notes on building the bomb, and beside the Atomic Energy Acts of 1946 and 1954, the laws Congress passed to keep nuclear secrets. The suggestion is that the study that closed the book on UFOs came out of the world of atomic secrecy.","condon-report-closes-blue-book-1968":"The Condon report, a University of Colorado study of UFO reports led by Edward Condon and dated 31 October 1968, is said to have ended Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. Michels tells it himself several times: the report \"closed shop on Bluebook\" and explained away many cases in ordinary terms. Yet he says that if you read the substance, \"over 30% of cases were totally still unidentified\", a point he credits to J. Allen Hynek, an astronomer who worked on the Air Force project. He compares that with AARO, the Pentagon's present UFO office, which under its director Sean Kirkpatrick also left cases unexplained. The report's own conclusion is that \"nothing has come from the study of UFOs in the past 21 years that has added to scientific knowledge\". On closing Blue Book it gives only Condon's \"impression\" that the defense role could be done without \"a special unit such as Project Blue Book\", calling that \"a question for defense specialists\", so the report recommends and leaves the decision to others. An Air Force fact sheet of January 1985 says the Secretary of the Air Force ended Blue Book on December 17, 1969, based on \"an evaluation\" of the Colorado report and other reviews. Michels does not say where the report gives the share of over 30%, and the edition read here states no overall figure for unidentified cases, so the number is his reading of the cases rather than a line in the text.","condon-s-letters-with-colonel-robert-hippler-1960":"In the late 1960s the physicist Edward Condon, who led a university UFO study for the Air Force, is said to have been working closely in private with an Air Force colonel named Robert Hippler, as letters between the two show, according to Michels. Michels tells this himself, once hedging that he 'believes' the letters exist and other times saying Hippler wrote that the study should show UFO research was 'a waste of money', and that Condon quietly went along with it. If his reading of the letters holds, it would mean the Air Force had a say in the outcome of a study that was presented as independent.","condon-worked-with-manhattan-project-figures-1940":"The physicist Edward Condon, the tellers say, was close to the inner circle of the atomic bomb project in the 1940s. The whistleblower David Grusch says only that 'Condon was involved', linking him to Vannevar Bush, who ran US wartime science. Michels goes further: he says Condon studied in Göttingen with the physicist Max Born, was so close to Oppenheimer that he is 'partly credited for picking Los Alamos' as the bomb laboratory, and helped draft the Atomic Energy Act of 1946, the law that put nuclear work under civilian control. A real record backs part of this: the Los Alamos Primer, the lab's first document from April 1943, says its notes come from lectures by the physicist Robert Serber at the start of the project and that 'the notes were written up by E. U. Condon'. That places Condon at Los Alamos at its very beginning, but the Primer says nothing about who chose the site, so the claim about picking Los Alamos rests on the tellers. The tellers bring it up because it would put a man later tied to UFO history inside the bomb's circle of secrecy.","condon-wrote-the-mcmahon-secrecy-language-later-used-for-ufo-1946":"The physicist Edward Condon, who later led a government-funded study that dismissed UFOs, wrote the secrecy rules of the Atomic Energy Act of 1946, known as the McMahon Act, and those rules, the claim goes, led to the Atomic Energy Act passed by Congress on 30 August 1954 and to the UFO secrecy that followed. Michels makes this case himself on four episodes, sometimes only as a fact about the act, and once credits the whistleblower David Grusch, a former intelligence officer, saying that \"if you believe David Grush, which I do,\" the Atomic Energy Commission \"created UFO secrecy in 1954.\" If true, it would mean, as Michels puts it, that Condon \"is basically writing up UFO secrecy before he then debunks the entire thing.\"","conflicting-accounts-of-who-sent-puharich-to-israel-foundati-1970":"Around 1970 to 1971 a researcher named Andrija Puharich went to Tel Aviv, Israel, and the accounts disagree on who sent him: a private foundation, or the CIA. A researcher named Greg Malatzi lays out the contradiction on the show. He sets Puharich's own lectures against the Israeli psychic Uri Geller, who, Malatzi says, claimed \"he was sent by the CIA, like directly.\" If Geller is right, the trip would have been an intelligence operation and not private research.","congress-denied-a-scif-for-grusch-s-classified-briefing-2023":"In 2023, members of Congress who wanted to hear David Grusch's secret evidence about UFOs say they could not get a SCIF, a secure room where classified matters can be discussed. Grusch is a former intelligence official who that year told Congress the government was hiding a UFO program, and he tells Michels first-hand that the members were \"pretty incensed\" because \"they couldn't reserve a SCIF.\" A second speaker on another episode, who seems to be one of the members, puts it more strongly: \"we were denied access to the skiff.\" If true, it would mean someone was blocking lawmakers from hearing classified testimony. The two accounts differ only in how far they go, from a room that could not be booked to access that was refused outright.","congress-of-vienna-vatican-monarchies-and-knights-of-malta-o-1815":"After the Napoleonic wars, the researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels, the Vatican, Europe's monarchies and the Knights of Malta, an old Catholic order of knights, \"colluded\" at the Congress of Vienna in 1815 to organise against revolution. He names the Austrian statesman Klemens von Metternich as a central figure but gives no source. Simosa tells it as an early link in a chain of secret societies that he ties to the UFO cover-up.","congress-sends-borland-to-aaro-at-the-end-of-march-2023":"At the end of March 2023, members of Congress sent a man named Dylan Borland to speak with AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into unexplained sightings in the sky. Borland, who says he went himself, tells Michels, \"they got me touching Arrow and I went to Arrow,\" using a spoken form of the office's name.","congressional-and-senate-hearings-reveal-immaculate-constell-2024":"In late 2024, hearings on Capitol Hill brought out the name of a secret Pentagon programme said to deal with UAP, the official term for UFOs: Immaculate Constellation. Michels describes it on one episode as a revelation from hearings in Congress and then, that same week, in the Senate. On another he simply calls it a top-secret programme. The source is a public document of record: the House Committee on Oversight and Accountability hearing \"Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena: Exposing the Truth\", held on 13 November 2024. In his written statement for it, the journalist Michael Shellenberger says his sources \"have revealed the name of an active and highly secretive DoD unacknowledged special access program, or USAP\". A USAP is a secret Pentagon programme whose very existence is officially denied. Michels and Shellenberger raise it because, if it is real, the Pentagon would be running a hidden UAP effort that Congress was never told about.","congressional-attempts-to-study-ufos-blocked-by-religious-gr-1970":"Since the 1970s, efforts in Congress to have UFOs at least studied are said to have been blocked by religious groups who felt \"God does not want us to do this.\" The researcher and author Peter Levenda tells Michels this as his own summary, naming no source but crediting a book called Sinister Forces. He says that when members of Congress learn of the subject, they are frightened and fall back on \"Angels and Demons.\" If true, it would mean religious belief, not just secrecy, has kept the government from looking into UFOs.","congressional-report-on-the-1944-maison-rouge-meeting":"In June 1945, before the war in Asia was over, the US Congress is said to have published a report on what was basically the Maison Rouge meeting, a 1944 gathering at the Maison Rouge hotel in Strasbourg, France. The writer Peter Levenda, who researches secret history, tells Michels this himself without naming where he read it, and he dates the report to June 1945; he does not say on the show what the report's own words are.","congressional-research-service-ufo-study-for-carter-with-she-1977":"A lawyer named Dan Sheehan says that in 1977, on behalf of President Jimmy Carter, the congressional Science and Technology committee asked the Congressional Research Service, the research office of Congress at the Library of Congress, to gather everything the US government knew \"about the UFO phenomena\". Sheehan tells Michels he worked on the project as special counsel, and he names a researcher, Marcia Smith. If true, it would mean a sitting president quietly asked for a full accounting of the government's UFO files.","congressional-safe-harbor-bill-gutted-2023":"The story is that in 2023 Congress came close to passing a law that would protect people holding UFO secrets so they could speak, and that a few lawmakers gutted it. The UFO campaigner Steven Greer says on the show that the bill was meant to give them 'safe harbor or amnesty', and that it was cut down by Turner, Rogers, Pelosi and others. The tellers link it to the UAP Disclosure Act, a Senate amendment to the yearly defense bill put forward on 13 July 2023 (UAP being the government's current term for UFOs). Dan Sheehan, a lawyer who says he took part in the push, names Mike Turner as the main obstacle. He stresses that Turner is the Ohio congressman whose district includes Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base, and he mentions Representative Dan Crenshaw. Michels says in his own voice that 'the last time around the UAPDA was killed by Mike Turner.' If true, a handful of members of Congress blocked a route to disclosure. Greer and Michels give it as their own account, and Sheehan as someone involved.","congressional-source-puts-crash-retrieval-programs-near-100-2020":"Secret US programs to recover crashed craft that did not come from people are almost certain to exist, according to a source in Congress. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff says that in the 2020s he asked this unnamed source point blank how confident they were that such programs exist, and that \"the answer was hovering around 100%.\" If true, it would mean someone inside Congress believes the government holds such craft.","congressman-on-progress-of-reverse-engineering-programs-and":"An unnamed congressman says he believes secret programmes are trying to reverse engineer, meaning take apart and copy, recovered non-human craft, but that they have barely made progress: \"they would own the skies if they had it.\" He says this in connection with the Wilson memo, notes said to be by a man named Eric Davis of a meeting with an admiral named Thomas Wilson, which bear on such programmes. If he is right, the programmes exist but have not unlocked the technology.","congressman-schiff-and-the-deleted-roswell-records-1997":"A congressman named Schiff is said to have found that all the archive records on Roswell had been deleted. Roswell is the 1947 New Mexico case in which the military is said to have recovered a crashed craft. A guest named James Fox repeats this on the show, saying Schiff \"noted that all of the Roswell stuff was deleted, all the archival material,\" which, if true, would mean the official paper trail was wiped.","congressman-witnesses-odni-official-apologizing-to-david-gru-2026":"In 2026 an official from the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, which oversees all US spy agencies, met David Grusch and in effect apologised for how he had been treated over the years. Congressman Eric Burlison tells Michels he was in the room: it was \"a pretty blunt meeting,\" he says, and \"you could tell they were basically apologizing.\" Grusch is a former intelligence officer who told Congress that the US government holds non-human craft, and if Burlison's account is right, the intelligence world was admitting he had been wronged.","consciousness-based-secret-space-program-bilocating-to-other":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, says yes, he has done it when asked about a secret space programme run through the mind alone, in which people are said to bilocate, meaning be in two places at once, and visit other planets. He tells Michels this as something he took part in himself, though here he says little more than \"Yeah, I've done that.\"","consecrating-holy-prophet-aluyah-during-the-biafra-war-1968":"Around 1968, during Nigeria's civil war over the breakaway region of Biafra, a man known as Holy Prophet Aluyah was made a bishop at a ceremony in the Bronx, New York, with Nigeria's embassy in attendance. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels he took part in the consecration, saying, \"we consecrate him a bishop... And the Nigerian embassy is there.\"","construction-worker-visited-by-a-blue-being-who-taught-him-p":"A construction worker named Dave Rossi says a blue being appeared in his bedroom, and Michels says, retelling it, that the being passed Rossi advanced physics ideas, or \"downloads\", which brought him to the attention of two physicists, Eric Davis and Hal Puthoff. Rossi's own account to Michels is quieter: he calls it \"more of a feeling rather than a visual\", a one-time experience that \"I haven't had since\", and he does not mention physics or the scientists. If the host's version is true, it would mean knowledge from a nonhuman source reaching an ordinary person.","consulting-for-a-jeans-company-on-shading-and-stitching":"A researcher named Don Hoffman, who studies how perception works, says he once advised a jeans company on how shading and stitching change the way jeans look on the body. He tells Michels that during one presentation the company's CEO, whose company he won't name, jumped out of his chair, went up to the screen and said, \"Our jeans make my butt look bad.\" He tells it as something he saw himself, to show how small visual cues change what people think they see.","consumer-product-safety-commission-swat-raid-on-united-nucle-2000":"Bob Lazar says that in the 2000s his company, United Nuclear, was raided by the Consumer Product Safety Commission, the federal agency that polices product safety, and that the agents came into his home with a SWAT team. He tells Michels they woke his wife in bed with an M-16 pointed at her face.","contact-from-steven-greer-s-disclosure-project-and-offer-of-2025":"In 2025, people from the Disclosure Project, a campaign run by the UFO activist Steven Greer to get insiders to testify in public, reached out to a former sergeant named Dan Sherman and offered him immunity as a whistleblower if he would testify. Sherman, who says the NSA once hired him to communicate with aliens, tells Michels about the contact himself, saying it came from \"not Steven Greer himself, but somebody else now,\" and that he and Greer \"talked a long time ago.\"","contact-in-the-desert-appearances-replacing-jim-marrs-2017":"The writer Peter Levenda says he first spoke at Contact in the Desert, a conference on UFOs and related subjects, long ago, when he was invited to stand in for someone else. He tells Michels he was invited again later, around 2017, to replace another speaker, a man named Jim Marrs, who was sick that year. Levenda gives this as part of his own history with the conference.","contact-with-a-being-and-taken-aboard-a-craft-at-the-fire-to-1973":"A man named Steven Greer says that in 1973, at a fire tower in the Blue Ridge Mountains near Boone, North Carolina, a being came up to him and he was taken aboard a craft. He tells Michels the creature was about five feet tall, with \"beautiful deer-like eyes\", and that it touched him on his right shoulder. In another passing mention he calls this his \"contact experience in 73\" and says it is where his later specialty began.","contents-of-the-cave-and-the-three-body-types-1800":"A cave in the Nazca region of Peru is said to have held more than 200 body parts: \"isolated heads, limbs, and appendages,\" and in many cases \"entire intact bodies.\" Michels describes this on an episode about strange three-fingered bodies from Peru, and he credits the account to a man named Leandro, whom he presents as a witness to the cave. If true, it would mean someone found a large store of unusual bodies in one place.","contesting-account-and-aaro-position-on-jacobs-big-sur-claim-1964":"A former Senate staffer named Kirk McConnell tells Michels that someone disputes the story of a man named Robert Jacobs about a 1964 test at Big Sur, near Vandenberg Air Force Base in California. According to McConnell, \"a man with some very good credentials\" claims Jacobs \"was confused about which date\" the test took place. McConnell talks about this alongside AARO, the Pentagon's current office for investigating unexplained aerial reports, and he does not say where the contesting account comes from.","continuity-of-government-succession-harrington-was-number-16-1980":"In the 1980s the US had secret plans to keep the government running through a disaster such as a nuclear attack, and John S. Herrington, Secretary of Energy under Reagan, says he was number 16 in that line of succession. He tells Michels this from his own experience, as part of the plan to \"preserve the government.\" He says he remembers the number because his daughter sewed a big 16 on the back of his swimsuit.","contractor-ms-finds-a-craft-being-disassembled-at-dugway-s-a":"A contractor known only as MS is said to have walked into a remote building in the Avery section of Dugway Proving Ground, a US military test range in Utah, while reading electrical meters, and found a craft that seemed to be in the middle of being taken apart. UAPGerb, an online researcher, tells the story to Michels, passing on what the contractor said. In an earlier episode he told it a little differently: an unnamed contractor working for a Martin company at the West Desert Test Wing came upon a building where a craft was hovering in the air and being tested.","contractor-saic-likely-involved-in-psionic-research":"Michels says that SAIC, a private defense contractor, was likely involved in what he calls \"psychotronic or psionic research\", meaning work on using the mind to sense or affect things at a distance. He says this himself, in passing and without giving a source, while talking with a guest about secret government work in Utah. If true, it would mean that research on mental powers was being carried out for the government by private companies.","control-room-camera-challenge-produces-the-i-living-artifact":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property long known for reports of strange events, a team member named Erik Bard says he was sitting alone in the control room when he said out loud, \"okay this is stupid,\" and then: \"If you got something you want to show me, show me. If you got something you want to tell me, tell me.\" Bard tells Michels that the very first screenshot he took afterwards showed letters on the screen pulled together into the words \"I living.\" The ranch's owner, Brandon Fugal, tells the same story on the show and adds that the letters were taken \"completely out of sequence\" and joined \"in a millisecond,\" with no explanation; if true, it would mean something at the ranch answered him.","controlled-remote-viewing-created-by-ingo-swann-and-used-by":"Controlled remote viewing, a method its users say lets a trained person describe distant or hidden targets using only the mind, was created by a man named Ingo Swann and used by the US military for years to gather intelligence. That is the account of Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant who says he took part in the work himself. He tells Michels the method is now used for businesses, and that its higher stages, eight through eighteen, are not taught to the public.","controller-reports-objects-descending-from-above-80-000-feet-2004":"In 2004, off San Diego, radar operators with the Navy carrier Nimitz are said to have tracked strange objects for days before a famous encounter with Navy pilots. Michels reads it this way: \"The controller told us that these objects had been observed for over two weeks coming down from over 80,000 feet.\" A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff, crediting a man named Kevin Day, gives it as about 10 days, with \"anomalous objects at 28,000 ft heading south at 100 knots, very strange tracks\". The tellers raise it because it would mean the objects were being watched on radar long before anyone saw them with their eyes.","cook-interviews-ning-li-at-a-2003-conference":"A journalist named Nick Cook says he met and interviewed Ning Li, a physicist linked to anti-gravity research, at a 2003 conference just outside Washington DC. He tells Michels this as something he did himself: \"I met her and I did sit down, I interviewed her.\" The meeting matters to the story because it is one of the last times anyone reports seeing her in public.","cook-s-visit-to-the-bell-site-in-the-waldenburg-mine-1990":"The British journalist Nick Cook says he went to the place where the Bell, a secret device some say Nazi Germany built in the Second World War, was supposedly installed, deep in a mine at Waldenburg in what is now southern Poland. Cook tells Michels he made this \"very atmospheric visit\" with a man named Igor Witkowski, and he wrote about his search in a book called The Hunt for Zero Point. Michels retells it twice in other episodes and adds detail Cook does not give here: he ties the site to tunnels of the Skoda works, describes a boat ride underground to a place where you can see the test rig, and credits a man named Hans Kammler.","cooper-s-ufo-in-orbit-1963":"The astronaut Gordon Cooper is said to have had an unknown object come close to his spacecraft while he was in orbit in 1963, and it was green, as far as the teller remembers. Charlie Foltz, a man who says he was himself abducted by aliens, tells Michels this as what Cooper told him, hedging with \"if I'm not mistaken\" about the color.","coordinated-hit-job-on-malmgren-after-jesse-s-interview-2025":"After Michels interviewed Harold Malmgren, a man who described a long career close to powerful figures in Washington, a piece appeared attacking him, claiming an archivist had never heard of him. Michels says this himself and calls it a coordinated \"hit job\". He also points to Malmgren's Wikipedia page, which he treats as part of the effort to discredit a witness.","cop-manuel-sandoval-chases-a-disc-from-cuba-1948":"In 1948 a disc came wobbling low over the small town of Cuba, New Mexico, one night, and a local police officer named Manuel Sandoval chased it all night, doing what he had long said he would do if he ever saw one. Michels tells this himself, as part of his account of a reported saucer crash in New Mexico that year, so the chase would place a lawman on the trail of the craft that night.","copperfield-s-great-wall-walk-through-and-statue-of-liberty-1980":"In the 1980s the stage magician David Copperfield appeared to walk through the Great Wall of China and to make the Statue of Liberty disappear. A guest named Chris Ramsay brings these feats up with Michels as examples of how convincingly an illusion can fool an audience's eyes.","corbell-and-coulthart-find-witnesses-who-place-lazar-at-s4-1987":"Michels says that the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell and the journalist Ross Coulthart found a number of people who confirmed that Bob Lazar was really at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert where Lazar says he worked on alien spacecraft in the late 1980s. Michels raises this himself, as a question, in an episode about the two days he spent with Lazar; if true, it would back up the most disputed part of Lazar's story, that he worked there at all.","corbell-first-meets-bob-lazar-at-john-lear-s-house-2010":"The documentary filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says he first met Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site in the late 1980s, at the Las Vegas house of John Lear, a pilot active in UFO circles, sometime in the 2010s. Corbell tells Michels he was there himself, and that the first thing Lear said to him was: \"Lazar's coming over.\"","corbell-fought-for-grusch-to-testify-in-congress-2023":"Jeremy Corbell, who says he was personally involved, tells Michels that in 2023 he fought to make sure David Grusch, a whistleblower who went on to testify before Congress about secret UFO programmes, would be allowed to speak there. Corbell says that \"they didn't want him to testify\", without saying exactly who, and that Grusch himself would tell you \"the exact same thing\". If true, it would mean there was resistance behind the scenes to letting Grusch's testimony happen at all.","corbell-presses-jacques-vallee-on-lazar-at-his-ranch":"A filmmaker named Jeremy Corbell says he once pressed Jacques Vallée, a long-time UFO researcher, about Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada. Corbell tells Michels it happened in private at his own ranch: \"I pushed him on it once at my ranch alone.\" What Vallée said back is not given here.","corbell-s-2018-iraq-jellyfish-ufo-video":"A video filmed in Iraq in 2018 is said to show a UFO shaped like a jellyfish. Michels tells how a UFO researcher named Jeremy Corbell released it, and he raises it as one of the cases that gave the subject new attention. If the object is what it seems, it would be a craft unlike any known aircraft, though others have offered ordinary explanations.","corbell-s-private-source-on-the-existence-of-the-s-4-facilit":"S-4, a secret site in Nevada where a man named Bob Lazar says he once worked on alien craft, really exists, according to a private source of the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell. Corbell tells Michels that what this source told him is \"why I knew that Bob Lazar was telling it as it is, meaning a facility like that exists,\" though he does not name the source or say what exactly they told him.","cordyceps-mind-controlling-bullet-ants":"In the Amazon, Michels says, bullet ants eat cordyceps mushrooms, a fungus that takes over their brains and movements and steers them to higher ground, where a fruiting body, the mushroom's spore-bearing stalk, sprouts out of the ant's head. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, on an episode with the mushroom entrepreneur Tero Isokauppila that asks whether mushrooms could be a kind of alien mind control. He offers it as a real example of one living thing taking control of another's mind.","coricancha-andesite-stonework":"The andesite stonework of Coricancha, an ancient site in Cusco, Peru, is \"the most impressive stonework in all of the ancient world,\" according to a researcher named Luke Caverns. He says it on the show as someone who has seen it himself, part of his case that ancient building was more advanced than we usually think.","corona-spy-satellite-program-offered-as-explanation-for-vall-1960":"When the astronomer Jacques Vallée, as a young man at the Paris Observatory, tracked an object circling Earth in an unusual orbit, some later explained the sighting as one of the Corona satellites, a series of highly secret CIA spy satellites first launched in 1960. Michels tells this himself, in an episode with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, and grants that the Corona satellites \"did have a nearly polar orbit\" but calls it \"a very insufficient explanation\", which would leave Vallée's object unexplained.","corporal-armando-vanishes-for-15-minutes-but-lives-5-days-1970":"In Chile in the 1970s, a soldier called Corporal Armando, named as Armando Valdés, is said to have vanished completely in front of the six men of his platoon and come back 15 minutes later, though for him five days had passed. The researcher Mike Masters tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of his idea that the visitors are humans from the future. If true, it would mean time itself ran differently for one man.","correspondence-with-whitley-strieber-on-the-ufo-phenomenon":"The author Peter Levenda says that his thinking on UFOs was shaped in part by conversations with the writer Whitley Strieber, who had first contacted him years earlier about something unrelated. Levenda tells Michels this himself, as background to his book Sinister Forces, where those exchanges helped form his view of the phenomenon.","corso-and-the-telepathic-steering-helmet-1950":"A man named Philip J. Corso is said to have claimed that, in the 1950s, the military tried to use recovered alien technology to steer machines by thought. Michels says Corso \"claimed to be at the Pentagon in the '50s doling out UFO material\" to private companies. An online researcher known as UAPGerb repeats Corso's account on the show. In his version, the Foreign Technology Division, an Army office that studied foreign weapons, was \"trying to use recovered technology to use human brain waves to guide\" long-range nuclear missiles, and he dates this to about 1958 to 1962. If true, it would mean crashed craft gave the military a way to control machines with the mind.","corso-government-panic-over-ufos-at-new-mexico-v-2-and-nucle-1947":"Around 1947 the US government \"went into panic mode\" because many UFOs were being seen over New Mexico, where the military was testing captured German V-2 rockets and working on nuclear weapons. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels this from The Day After Roswell, a book published in 1997 by Philip Corso, a former Army officer, and named after the reported 1947 crash of a craft near Roswell, New Mexico. If true, the craft were drawn to America's most secret weapons sites.","corso-in-post-war-italy-knight-of-malta":"An Army officer named Colonel Philip J. Corso served as a high-ranking intelligence officer in Italy after the Second World War, according to Michels, who tells it himself. Corso later became famous for his 1997 book The Day After Roswell, in which he claimed to have handled the reverse engineering, meaning taking apart to learn how to copy, of alien materials at the Pentagon. Michels brings up the Italian years as background on who Corso was before those claims.","corso-manuscript-trapped-being-asks-to-lower-the-radar-1950":"A manuscript by a man named Philip J. Corso is said to describe him meeting a trapped non-human being who asked him to switch off the radar so that it and others could escape. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP stands for \"unidentified anomalous phenomena\", the government's newer term for UFOs) brings it up as a possibility. He quotes the being as saying, \"Hey, lower the radar so we can get out of here,\" which, if true, would mean radar can interfere with these craft.","corso-recruited-to-the-army-foreign-technology-division-1960":"In 1960, an Army officer named Philip J. Corso is said to have been recruited to the US Army's foreign technology division, an office for studying other countries' weapons and technology. UAPGerb, an online researcher, tells this to Michels as his own summary of Corso's claim, names Arthur Trudeau in the story, and points to Corso's official military records as backing. It matters because it would place Corso where he could have handled unusual technology.","corso-s-claims-of-a-covert-roswell-group-and-reverse-enginee-1947":"Philip J. Corso, an Army officer who says he ran the Army's foreign technology desk at the Pentagon, claimed in The Day After Roswell, a book he published in 1997, that wreckage from the 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico went to a secret group led by Admiral Roscoe Hillenkoetter, the first director of the CIA, and that Corso himself later handed the material out to American companies, mainly Bell Labs, to be reverse engineered, which means taking something apart to learn how to build it. Michels brings it up from the book again and again. Researchers such as UAPGerb, who studies Pentagon programmes, and the physicist John Brandenburg repeat it as Corso's account, and in a clip played on the show Corso himself names the integrated circuit as the most important result and the laser as second; if true, it would mean that much of today's electronics, from fiber optics to data centers, was sped up by alien technology.","corso-s-co-author-gave-him-24-hours-to-edit-the-book-1997":"Michels says that a man named Philip Corso was given only 24 hours by his co-author to edit The Day After Roswell, Corso's book published in 1997 about the reported crash at Roswell, New Mexico, so there is \"clearly some BS in that book\". He says this himself on several episodes. On one he names the co-author as William Birnes and mentions Dawn of a New Age, a manuscript of Corso's own. On another, crediting someone who goes by UAP Gerb, he adds that Birnes inserted \"all sorts of fake stuff\" into the book, perhaps to stigmatize Corso.","corso-s-manuscript-dawn-of-a-new-age-vs-the-day-after-roswel-1997":"Philip J. Corso, an Army officer whose book The Day After Roswell was published in 1997, is said to have left an earlier manuscript called Dawn of a New Age that differs from the book that came out. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb, short for Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena, the government's newer word for UFOs, tells Michels this without naming his source, suggesting the manuscript would show what Corso first meant to say before the book took its final shape.","corso-says-the-day-after-roswell-was-rushed-to-print-1997":"The Day After Roswell was hurried out with almost no time to edit it, and its author was upset by the result, or so the people who tell this story say. The book was published in 1997 by Philip J. Corso, a retired Army officer. In it he wrote about Roswell, the New Mexico town at the centre of a famous 1947 claim that a flying saucer crashed there. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels what he was told: the book was \"rushed to print\" with Corso's co-writer, William J. Birnes. Fox also names a man called Michael Hessman as part of the story. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells it a little differently, as a rumour. In his version Corso had only 24 hours to edit the book, and according to Corso's son he \"put his hand to his head and said, 'I can't believe this.'\" Both men are passing on what others told them. If it is true, some of the book's mistakes could come from the rush rather than from Corso himself.","cosmic-zoo-explanation-for-diverse-craft":"The owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange lights and craft, offers one idea for why so many different kinds of craft seem to show up: \"the idea that we're living in a cosmic zoo.\" He puts it to Michels as a possibility based on what he says he has seen there. The idea is that if it were true, humans would be watched by many different visitors, like animals in a zoo.","cosmonaut-drew-an-object-that-stopped-beside-their-spacecraf":"A Soviet cosmonaut crew is said to have had an unknown craft stop right next to their spacecraft halfway through an orbit, and the cosmonaut drew a picture of it. Kevin Knuth, a physicist and former NASA scientist, tells Michels this twice as something he was told; the second time he adds that the crew also took photographs and names his source, whose name the transcript gives as \"Akan Atanv\".","cosmonaut-fooled-crewmate-with-ice-particles-as-ufo-fleet":"A cosmonaut is said to have played a joke in a space capsule by telling a crewmate that bits of ice drifting outside were a fleet of UFOs. The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels that one of the cosmonauts told this story himself, and he offers it as a case where something seen in space turned out to be ordinary.","cosmos-club-tied-to-a-ufo-crash":"The Cosmos Club, a private club in Washington, DC, had something to do with a UFO crash, Michels says. He mentions it only in passing, as something \"you hear of,\" and credits it to a researcher who goes by the name Dark Journalist, without saying what the connection was.","coulthart-detained-by-cbp-after-grusch-story-2023":"The journalist Ross Coulthart says that in 2023, after he reported the story of David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who claimed the US government holds hidden UFO material, he was detained and held at the US border by CBP, US Customs and Border Protection. Coulthart tells Michels this as something that happened to him, implying his reporting had drawn official attention.","coulthart-republican-presidents-briefed-democrats-not":"Republican presidents are said to sometimes get briefed on the UFO secret while Democratic presidents do not. A researcher named Jason Simosa passes this on from Ross Coulthart, who has spoken publicly on the subject. Simosa says Coulthart \"recently came out and talked about how there sometimes seems to be a contrast\" between the two parties' presidents, which, if true, would mean that who gets told depends on politics.","coulthart-s-changing-sources-on-the-drone-flyovers-2024":"During the 2024 wave of drone sightings over the United States, the journalist Ross Coulthart is said to have spoken confidently about what his sources told him while those sources kept changing their story. The musician Flying Lotus tells Michels this from following Coulthart's reporting: \"he's so confident about sources and then his sources keep changing the story.\" The point is that a well-known UFO reporter's inside information may be less steady than it sounds.","cousteau-s-atlantis-obsession-shared-with-robert-maxwell":"The ocean explorer Jacques Cousteau is said to have admitted that his interest in the sea really came from an obsession with finding Atlantis, the sunken island of Plato's tale. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this came through Ghislaine Maxwell, who he says was obsessed with Atlantis from childhood because her father, the publisher Robert Maxwell, was Cousteau's friend and heard it from him.","cover-contract-through-the-bia-near-white-sands-2001":"In 2001, according to the former helicopter pilot Jake Barber, who says he flew missions to recover UFOs, his team worked under a cover contract with the Bureau of Indian Affairs, placed with a sovereign tribal nation close to White Sands Missile Range in New Mexico. Barber tells Michels this as something he took part in; if true, it would mean the work was hidden behind ordinary government paperwork.","cover-up-of-retrieved-craft-and-beings-since-1947":"Since about 1947 there has been a serious effort in the United States to cover up crashed craft and beings that were recovered. That is what Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, tells Michels, though he names no source and speaks only of an attempt to \"seriously cover up\" anything about it. If true, it would mean the secrecy has lasted nearly eighty years.","coyame-lights-around-mountain-peaks":"People who live near Coyame, in the Mexican state of Chihuahua, often talk about coloured lights that blink in and out around the mountain peaks. Michels tells it from Mexico's Roswell, a book by Noe Torres and Ruben Uriarte about a reported UFO crash in the area. It is offered as a sign that the place is still strange today.","craft-beam-lasers-over-nuclear-stockpiles-at-loring-afb-1975":"In 1975 or 1976, unknown craft are said to have flown over Loring Air Force Base in northern Maine and shone \"laser type beams\" down on it, \"mapping the nuclear stockpiles.\" A guest named Charlie Foltz describes this to Michels as an episode at the base, without naming a source. If true, it would mean something was taking a close interest in American nuclear weapons.","craft-crash-in-northern-iran":"A craft, apparently not of human make, crashed in northern Iran, and the United States had to try to go in after it, according to Steven Greer, a campaigner for official UFO disclosure, speaking as a guest of Michels. Greer gives it only in a half-finished sentence, with no date or detail, as one of a series of such crashes he says the US has tried to recover.","craft-entering-and-leaving-the-atmosphere-seen-on-the-inside":"Unexplained craft come in and out of Earth's atmosphere, according to Matthew Brown, who is described on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower. Asked by Michels whether that comes from what he has seen on the inside, Brown says yes, and also from other people's testimony, though he gives no details of either.","craft-flew-due-north-to-a-soft-landing-1948":"A flying saucer said to have come down in 1948 at Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, travelled exactly due north before settling into a soft landing, according to a researcher of that reported crash named Scott Ramsey. \"It was due north exactly,\" he tells Michels after studying the route it took, and he links this to his view that the wobbling craft may have switched to a kind of autopilot, which would mean it was guided down rather than simply falling.","craft-holed-by-gunfire-over-los-alamos-1948":"The researcher Scott Ramsey suggests that a craft said to have crashed in 1948 may have been hit by gunfire, perhaps a .50 calibre machine gun, while flying over Los Alamos, the New Mexico atomic weapons site, and that sudden loss of cabin pressure followed. Ramsey tells Michels plainly that this is only his theory. It would explain, he thinks, why the craft came down and what happened to those on board.","craft-hovered-over-new-underwood-reservoir-dam-1977":"In 1977 a craft hovered over the New Underwood Lake Reservoir dam in South Dakota, according to Mario Woods, who guarded nuclear missile sites there. He tells Michels he works out where it was using geometry: two missile sites called November 1 and November 5 and the reservoir form \"a Pythagorean triangle\", and \"that's where that craft was, it was over that body of water.\"","craft-hovering-in-s4-hangars":"Three or four craft were seen hovering inside the hangars of S4, a secret site near Area 51 in Nevada, according to an account relayed by the lawyer Dan Sheehan. Sheehan tells Michels that \"they actually went all the way to S4,\" rode down \"the big elevators,\" and that \"he said he saw like three or four,\" in an account that also names Oscar Wolf, Bob Lazar and Dan Burisch, men he links to the site, without making clear which of them was the one who saw it. If true, it would mean the site held several working craft at once.","craft-hull-material-as-an-electret-1987":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site in Nevada, in the late 1980s, thinks the craft's hull was made of an electret. He explains to Michels that just as a magnet always has a magnetic field, an electret always has an electrostatic field. If true, it would be a clue to how the craft's skin behaved and perhaps how it flew.","craft-looks-different-up-close-than-from-far-away-1988":"Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft in 1988 and 1989 at S-4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, tells Michels that one of the craft looked different depending on distance: \"if you're close to it, you know, it looks like the craft. And when you get far away, it doesn't look right.\" He describes it as something he saw himself, which, if true, would suggest the craft bent light or vision around it.","craft-s-power-system-explained-aboard-like-a-submarine-1976":"Charlie Foltz, who says he was taken aboard a craft near Eagle Lake, Maine, in 1976, tells Michels that while on board he asked his captors about the craft's power system. He says it was \"relayed to me\" that it works like a submarine working with a bubble of air. In another passage he puts it a little differently, saying the craft runs on a gravitational field and operates the way a submarine can work at different pressures in the water.","craft-shot-down-before-crashing-1996":"People in Varginha, a town in Brazil where a strange craft is said to have come down in 1996 and a living creature to have been captured, say the craft was shot down: someone fired on it, and it was already falling when it arrived. Italo Venturelli, a surgeon who counts himself a witness to the Varginha events, tells Michels this as something others saw, and points to a young local man named Carlos de Souza as the one who will tell it first-hand. If true, it would mean someone attacked the craft before it crashed.","craft-vanishing-into-mountains-in-the-uinta-basin":"Unknown craft have been seen vanishing into mountains, and Michels says this is \"a documented phenomenon,\" especially in places like the Uinta Basin in Utah. He says it himself, without naming who documented it. If true, it would hint that such craft have hiding places or bases inside the mountains.","craft-wall-turns-translucent-and-shows-3d-symbols-1988":"Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft in 1988 and 1989 at S-4, a secret site in Nevada, says he saw a wall inside one craft turn see-through and show three-dimensional symbols. He tells Michels this on two different episodes, both times in the same plain words: \"I saw the wall become translucent.\" The second time he gives no place, date or symbols.","crane-as-the-navy-s-off-world-reverse-engineering-center":"Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy research site, is said to be the center of the Navy's work on off-world technology. That work would be reverse engineering: taking craft that did not come from Earth apart to learn how they work. Michels tells this himself, passing on what unnamed contacts told him: just as Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base, is the center of the Air Force's \"most exotic off-world reverse engineering efforts,\" Crane is the center of the Navy's. If true, it would mean more than one branch of the military has its own program studying non-human craft.","crane-brinton-s-last-lecture-on-humanity-s-next-evolutionary-1960":"Crane Brinton, chairman of the department of intellectual history at Harvard, gave a last lecture about humanity's next evolutionary step, according to Dan Sheehan, who says he was there as a young man. Sheehan dates it to May 1968 and says Brinton told the students that this coming step was why so many of them refused to go fight in Vietnam, a remark Sheehan says struck him when it was said.","crane-symbols-match-sheehan-s-drawings-2014":"Randy Anderson, a man who says he saw alien technology at a military base, says that symbols he saw in 2014 at Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy research site in Indiana, closely matched drawings made by Dan Sheehan, who had drawn symbols he said he had seen on a crashed craft. Anderson tells Michels: \"what he had draw is very close to what I seen\"; if true, it would tie two separate witnesses to the same object or writing.","cranial-modification-told-to-have-been-ordered-by-the-gods-1995":"Some peoples who reshaped their children's skulls, binding them so the head grows long, said they did it because the gods told their ancestors to. The researcher Mike Masters tells Michels this, citing a 1995 paper by two authors named Gerszten, who he says asked a group why they did it and were told \"our ancestors were instructed by the gods to do this.\" Masters brings it up because, if the answer is taken at face value, it would tie a practice found around the world, including among the Maya, to beings the people believed were above them.","crash-debris-analysed-as-aluminum-and-barium-matching-brown-1950":"Debris from a crashed object was analysed and found to contain aluminum and barium, and Michels links that barium to the inventor Townsend Brown, whom he presents as a CIA scientist who built disc-shaped craft. Michels says barium showed up both on a chalkboard in the labs where Brown worked and in Brown's Winterhaven proposal of 1953, a plan for his flying discs, in which Brown suggests using barium in one of the motors for his \"gravitator discs\". Michels makes the connection himself in a documentary about Brown, drawing on the work of Michael Swords, to suggest the crashed material may have something to do with Brown's designs.","crash-debris-and-government-pen-at-archuleta-mesa-1980":"Some time between 1980 and 1983, on Archuleta Mesa in New Mexico, a lawman named Gabe Valdez and others reportedly went up to a site and found brush broken up, signs that 'something had been picked up', and a pen marked 'US government property'. The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels this as what Valdez told him, and it matters to him because it would point to the government quietly clearing a crash site.","crash-investigations-with-usaf-cia-involvement-in-virginia-a":"Michels says that when craft crash, there always seems to be Air Force and CIA involvement, pointing to cases in Virginia and even in Brazil. He brings this up himself, in passing, without naming a source or describing either case. If true, it would point to a standing pattern of US agencies handling crash sites, even abroad.","crash-retrieval-program-head-refused-to-talk":"The physicist Eric W. Davis says the man who led the crash retrieval program, a claimed secret effort to recover downed non-human craft, during the era in question was Glenn Gaffney, and that Gaffney refused to talk to them. Davis tells Michels this in connection with the documentary Age of Disclosure: \"I would love to talk to the head of the crash retrieval program during that era, but he refused to talk to us.\" If true, the man best placed to confirm the program chose to stay silent.","crash-retrieval-traced-back-to-sarbacher-1950-and-mussolini-1933":"The recovery of crashed craft of unknown origin goes back at least to 1950, with the US scientist Robert Sarbacher, and \"even before in 1933 with Mussolini\" in Italy. This is what a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels, without giving a source. He raises it to show that such recoveries began before the better-known American cases.","crash-site-regraded-for-soybeans-after-the-documentary-2022":"A guest named Carlos de Souza says that the ground near Varginha, Brazil, where something is said to have crashed, sat untouched for 26 years, and then was dug up soon after a documentary about the case by the filmmaker James Fox came out around 2022. De Souza tells Michels that the land was graded flat, \"literally\", and in one telling he says more than 30 meters of earth was removed, while the other version speaks of it being regraded for soybeans. He asks why \"not a single straw was removed from that place\" for all those years and then suddenly so much, which suggests to him that someone wanted to get rid of whatever remained at the site.","crash-sites-with-bob-wood-that-turned-out-to-be-us-planes":"Joe McMoneagle, a former Army remote viewer (someone trained to try to see faraway places with the mind), says he went with an aerospace engineer named Bob Wood to several reported crash sites. He tells Michels, from his own experience, that every one of them \"turned out to be our planes\", meaning American aircraft and nothing unknown.","crash-tech-moved-from-wright-patterson-to-private-hands":"The idea here is that wreckage from a crashed UFO could have been taken to Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio, and that the technology was later passed into private hands. A guest named Scott Ramsey offers it to Michels as openly speculative: \"Let's just speculate for a second and say a UFO crashed over here and it was brought to Wright Patterson.\"","crashed-craft-found-with-nothing-inside":"When these craft crash, a lot of people say, nothing is found inside them: no crew, no bodies. Michels says this himself in passing, as something he has heard from others, without naming anyone. If true, it would suggest the craft fly with nobody on board.","crashed-saucer-from-the-1940s-and-a-secrecy-pact-between-cou":"If the United States holds a crashed flying saucer from the 1940s, and another country holds one too, then the two countries would have an agreement not to talk about it. A researcher named Peter Levenda offers this to Michels as his own reasoning rather than a reported fact, to explain why no government has spoken out.","crashes-as-deliberate-seeding-gorilla-in-the-cage":"Some crashes of unexplained craft may not have been accidents at all, but a deliberate \"seeding\", a way of putting the craft into human hands on purpose. David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, tells Michels this as something he heard from a man he calls only Luis: some of the crashes, he says, \"wasn't really a mission failure, if you will. It was, you know, maybe some seeding.\" If that were true, whoever flew the craft would have meant for people to find them.","crawling-inside-the-craft-three-seat-like-amplifiers-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S-4 in 1988 and 1989, says he got inside one of the craft and saw \"three things that look like seats,\" which he describes as amplifiers. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell relays Lazar's words on another episode: \"I know there are alien craft here from another planet... I saw other ones, but I was inside one,\" without mentioning the seats.","critias-s-myth-of-athens-war-with-atlantis":"In two of Plato's dialogues, the Timaeus and the Critias, a speaker named Critias tells of a great war, nine thousand years earlier, between ancient Athens and the island power of Atlantis. The guests disagree about what kind of story it is. A guest named James Madden calls it a myth, \"another good lie\" told to Socrates. The researcher Matt LaCroix treats it as real early history, saying Plato and Plutarch describe \"proto Athenians\" who fought Atlantis. Michels recalls Plato putting the war 9,000 years back and telling it at a festival. If it were history, it would mean an advanced civilisation existed long before the ones we know. In Benjamin Jowett's translation, Critias says that \"nine thousand was the sum of years\" since the war, and the Timaeus speaks of \"a mighty power which unprovoked made an expedition against the whole of Europe and Asia, and to which your city put an end.\" The text has Critias call it \"a tale which, though strange, is certainly true, having been attested by Solon\", whom it calls the wisest of the seven sages, and Socrates repeats that it is \"not a mere legend, but an actual fact.\" Those are claims made by characters inside the dialogue. Whether Plato himself meant it as history or as a teaching story, the text leaves open. The festival is the Apaturia, where, Critias says, boys recited poems and he first heard the tale as a child.","critique-of-the-khafre-pyramid-sar-scan-model-2025":"In 2025 a team linked to a researcher named Filippo Biondi presented radar scans, made with synthetic aperture radar (SAR, a way of imaging the ground from satellites), said to show structures deep inside the Khafre pyramid at Giza. The researcher Luke Caverns tells Michels that what they showed was really a 3D model of the Great Pyramid's King's Chamber and the small chambers above it, copied five times into Khafre, and then called what the scans detected. He means the striking picture was a model, not data.","crohn-s-disease-healed-after-the-2007-orb-encounter":"A North Carolina man named Chris Bledsoe, whom NASA researchers are said to study because UFOs seem to appear around him, says his Crohn's disease went away after an encounter with glowing orbs in 2007. \"And then my Crohn's was gone. I had no more sickness,\" he tells Michels. Michels, who places the encounter in January 2007, repeats the point back to him as part of what makes the case so striking: on the one hand, Bledsoe was cured.","crookes-tube-jump-observed-by-brown-as-a-student-1920":"In the 1920s, as a student at Caltech, the inventor Townsend Brown is said to have noticed that when he put a high voltage across a Crookes tube, a glass vacuum tube that glows under electricity, the wire carrying the voltage \"jumped\". The journalist Nick Cook tells this to Michels in a documentary about Brown, as the moment that started Brown's later work on using electricity to make things move, which Cook links to craft like UFOs.","crowley-s-lam-entity":"Michels says that the English occultist Aleister Crowley was another person to whom an alien-seeming being \"sort of seemed to show up\", a figure known as Lam. He mentions it briefly himself, without a source, while talking with a guest about contact with strange beings, placing Crowley among people who claimed meetings with such entities.","crown-prince-hans-adam-s-armageddon-agenda-1994-meeting":"The UFO activist Steven Greer says that at a 1994 meeting at the Pierre Hotel in New York with Hans-Adam II of Liechtenstein, he was told of an agenda to make \"the public hate the aliens so bad that we can have an interplanetary war so that we'll have Armageddon. So Christ will return,\" adding to Michels, \"I'm quoting.\" Greer tells it as someone who was there, and links the meeting to the alien-abduction researchers Budd Hopkins, John Mack and David Jacobs and to the philanthropist Laurance Rockefeller; if true, it would mean powerful people wanted to turn the public against aliens for religious ends.","cruickshank-s-archival-research-into-kennedy-records-and-mj1-1960":"A researcher named Jeff Cruickshank has dug through the Kennedy archives, the declassified records of President John F. Kennedy's time, looking for traces of MJ-12, a supposed secret top-level committee on UFOs named in a set of papers known as the MJ-12 documents. The researcher Matthew Pines brings up this \"forensic historical\" work in passing, as a way to test whether those papers line up with real government records.","cryo-chambers-on-mars":"Somewhere on Mars, the story goes, there are cryo chambers, sealed rooms where people were put into a deep frozen sleep and left behind. The researcher Jason Jorjani says this on the show, passing on what he says remote viewers reported. Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe a distant place by the mind alone, and Jorjani names Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer, as his source. If true, it would mean people were once on Mars and some never left.","cryptography-to-speak-with-angelic-beings-trithemius-dee-and":"Some of the first cryptographers worked on secret codes as a way to speak with angelic beings, Michels says, naming the early code-maker Johannes Trithemius, whom he places in the thirteenth century. The story is tied to the English pair John Dee and Edward Kelley as well. Michels raises it himself, without a source, in a conversation about the idea that non-human beings shaped religion, where it would mean that code-making began as a way to reach such beings.","ct-scan-of-the-implant-arranged-by-an-unnamed-insider":"The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has been visited by non-human beings, says an unnamed insider wanted him to have a CT scan of an implant in his body, and arranged it in California. Strieber tells Michels this first-hand (\"he wants me to get a CT scan of the implant\"), as a sign that people inside the system took the object seriously.","cumbre-vieja-eruption-and-lyn-buchanan-s-warning-2021":"When the Cumbre Vieja volcano on La Palma erupted in 2021, the researcher Jason Jorjani was in New York, and he says he got in touch with Lyn Buchanan, a former Army remote viewer, someone trained to describe distant events with the mind. Jorjani tells Michels he asked Buchanan, \"do you think this is it? Like, should I leave New York?\" His question suggests Buchanan had warned of something earlier, though Jorjani does not spell out the warning here.","current-astronauts-with-their-own-experiences":"Some astronauts still serving today have had their own sightings of unexplained objects, according to Ryan Graves, a former Navy fighter pilot. Graves tells Michels he has 'spoke to current astronauts that have had their own instances', though he gives no names and no details of what they saw.","curse-of-king-tut-s-tomb":"The tomb of the Egyptian pharaoh Tutankhamun is said to carry a curse on those who disturbed it. Michels brings it up himself, without naming a source, saying a couple of people tried to x-ray it and then \"sort of die mysteriously.\" Luke Caverns, a researcher of ancient Egypt, tells it in more detail: \"almost every person\" on the excavation led by the archaeologist Howard Carter \"had something tragic happen to them,\" and several died from poisoning after breathing the air inside the chamber. In his telling the curse would have a physical cause rather than a supernatural one.","curtis-lemay-chases-townsend-brown-down-the-stairs":"The Air Force general Curtis LeMay once chased the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, who worked on ways to move objects with electricity in place of ordinary engines, down a flight of stairs, according to Brown's daughter Linda Brown. Michels repeats this from her on two episodes, and in one he places it in the 1950s. Linda Brown also tells it on the show herself, more gently: LeMay \"kind of followed my dad down the steps.\"","curtis-lemay-gave-d-harold-byrd-a-civil-air-patrol-award":"Curtis LeMay, the hard-line Air Force general, is said to have given a Texas businessman named D. Harold Byrd an award for his work with the Civil Air Patrol, the Air Force's civilian volunteer wing, when Byrd was younger. Michels mentions this himself, without naming a source, in an episode on UFOs and the Kennedy assassination, where it serves to tie Byrd to the military figures around that story and to Lyndon Johnson.","custody-chain-manhattan-project-to-aec-to-doe-1940":"The claim is that crashed UFO material found in the 1940s was put under the same secrecy as the atomic bomb: first held by the Manhattan Project, then passed to the Atomic Energy Commission, which took over the bomb work after the war, and later to the Department of Energy. A former US intelligence officer named David Grusch tells Michels this, saying the Manhattan Project \"would have custody of it, and then it would go into the Atomic Energy Commission.\" Michels later repeats Grusch in a different way: the atomic secrecy system was laid over UFO secrecy, and the same Manhattan Project people ran both. An analyst named Matthew Pines, also citing Grusch, says these programmes were \"tucked underneath the architecture of secrecy\" of the Manhattan Project, with the Atomic Energy Act, the law that governed atomic secrets, as their framework through the 1940s, 50s and 60s. If true, it would explain why UFO secrets have been as hard to reach as nuclear ones.","custody-of-the-trinity-craft-passed-through-manhattan-projec-1945":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée tells Michels on two shows that a craft tied to the 1945 Trinity atomic test would have gone to the wartime atomic bomb programme, the Manhattan Project, then to the Atomic Energy Commission, the agency that later ran the US nuclear programme, and then to the Department of Energy, \"which has its own line of clearances\". In one telling he credits the Italian researcher Paola Harris and mentions J. Robert Oppenheimer. In the other he credits nobody and speaks only of UFO custody from the 1940s on, without naming Trinity. He offers it as a reason why such material could have stayed outside the Pentagon's ordinary oversight.","cybertruck-bomber-s-email-to-shoemate-about-chinese-gravitic-2025":"Matthew Livelsberger, the Green Beret behind the Cybertruck explosion in Las Vegas in 2025, is said to have emailed a man named Sam Shoemate beforehand. According to Shoemate, the email said the drones that had puzzled the country over New Jersey for two months were advanced Chinese \"gravitic propulsion\" drones, meaning craft driven by controlling gravity. Michels tells this himself, repeating what Shoemate said on Shawn Ryan's podcast, and mentions the FBI in connection with it.","cydonia-said-to-hold-pyramids-the-remnants-of-a-city-1970":"A region of Mars called Cydonia holds several pyramids, basically the remnants of a city, according to Michels, who tells it in an episode about whether Mars once had life. In another episode he credits the idea to Richard Hoagland, whom he calls \"the face on Mars guy\" after a formation there said to look like a face, and to Hoagland's book Dark Mission: The Secret History of NASA. If it were true, it would mean someone once built on Mars.","cymatics-experiment-in-the-king-s-chamber-produces-the-eye-o":"Scientists inside the King's Chamber of the Great Pyramid in Egypt are said to have stretched a membrane over the stone sarcophagus there, like a plate on which sand forms patterns when sound passes through it. They scattered Egyptian sand on it, played sound at the chamber's own frequency, and the sand formed the Eye of Horus, the ancient Egyptian symbol. A guest named Jay Anderson tells this on the show as something done by a man named David Elkington and other scientists, and the point would be that the pyramid was built with its sound in mind.","d-harold-byrd-s-vought-built-flying-saucers-for-wright-patte-1940":"An aerospace company, Vought, which the story links to a man named D. Harold Byrd, is said to have built \"all the flying saucers\" for Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio, in the 1940s and 1950s. A researcher named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, and says the work was run by a man he calls Alfred C. Loading. If true, it would mean the military had disc-shaped aircraft built by a private contractor.","daily-mail-cia-office-of-global-access-runs-crash-retrieval-2023":"The claim is that a CIA unit called the Office of Global Access runs crash retrieval, meaning the quiet recovery of crashed craft of unknown origin anywhere in the world. Michels comes back to it in several episodes. He calls the office \"the main you know part of the crash retrieval program all around the world\" and points to an article in the British newspaper the Daily Mail. Sometimes he ties the article to the journalist Chris Sharp. Other times he names a man called Doug Wolf, saying the office did \"rapid response\" retrievals under him in the early 2000s. The online researcher UAPGerb tells it as reporting he trusts. He says Christopher Sharp \"broke a great story\" about an office created in 2003 under the CIA's Directorate of Science and Technology. In a later episode Michels adds, without citing the paper, that the office is \"rumored to coordinate closely\" with the military's Joint Special Operations Command and with the Nuclear Emergency Support Teams, which respond to nuclear incidents. If true, the CIA would have a standing worldwide team for picking up crashed objects.","daily-mail-egg-shaped-ufo-from-area-51-2023":"The claim is that around 2023 a story came out about an egg-shaped craft, not built by humans, being recovered at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada. Michels brings it up himself, recalling \"the Daily Mail reporting about an egg-shaped UFO from Area 51 a year ago,\" without saying more about the article. A commentator named Marik von Rennenkampff traces the same story to a man named Eric Taber. He says Taber spoke to AARO, the Pentagon office that now collects and studies UFO reports. According to von Rennenkampff, Taber told them about \"an egg-shaped craft being retrieved,\" which he believes was at Area 51. He gives no date. If true, it would mean the US holds at least one recovered craft of unknown origin.","daily-sensor-observations-of-spheres-off-the-east-coast-2014":"In 2014 and 2015, Navy fighter jets training off Virginia Beach picked up unknown objects, some described as spheres, almost daily, according to Ryan Graves, a Navy pilot who says he was flying there. Graves tells Michels the objects showed up once the jets got newer radars, then were also caught by the infrared targeting camera and a missile's seeker, three sensors \"telling us that there's a physical object here\"; at first the pilots wondered whether an adversary was spying by \"soaking up our waveforms.\" The commentator Marik von Rennenkampff repeats Graves's account of daily sensor sightings of spherical objects off the East Coast.","dalai-lama-meets-john-mack-about-aliens-1990":"In the 1990s the Dalai Lama is said to have met the Harvard psychiatrist John Mack, who studied people who said they had been taken by aliens, to talk about aliens. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and says the Dalai Lama showed a \"similar companionship of worldviews,\" meaning his outlook and Mack's fit together. He raises it to show that a major religious leader took the subject seriously.","dale-new-mexico-as-a-tall-white-playground":"Charles Hall, a man who says he spent three years around the \"tall whites,\" tall pale beings he says he met while serving at Area 51, the secret Air Force site in Nevada, tells Michels that the area around Dale, New Mexico is \"one of the tall white playgrounds.\" He says it as part of his own first-hand account, which, if true, would mean these beings roam freely in parts of the American Southwest.","dalton-fury-bin-laden-in-sights-three-times-strike-denied-2001":"In late 2001, at Tora Bora in the mountains of Afghanistan, an American special forces commander known as Dalton Fury is said to have had Osama bin Laden within reach and been kept from going after him. John Blitch, a former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, tells Michels what Fury said: that Fury went on 60 Minutes in disguise and told the correspondent Scott Pelley he had had \"Direct OverWatch\" on bin Laden, meaning he could watch and strike him, \"on three separate occasions\". Blitch also points to Fury's book Kill Bin Laden. The 60 Minutes report itself, broadcast on October 5, 2008, tells it somewhat differently. In it Fury says two plans from his unit, Delta, were turned down from above: one to come over the mountains from the Pakistan side, and one to mine the passes into Pakistan. Pelley reports that they \"didn't get approval from a higher level\" and that the second plan \"was also disapproved\", and that \"the military wouldn't tell 60 Minutes who rejected the plans or why.\" The report does not speak of having bin Laden in sight three times or of overwatch. On the night his team was \"about 2,000 meters away from where we think bin Laden's at\", the report has Fury himself deciding not to push on, saying \"it wasn't worth the risk at that particular moment to go up there and play cowboy.\" If Blitch's version is right, it would mean the man behind 9/11 was spared by a decision made higher up.","dam-flooded-the-egyptian-labyrinth-in-the-1960s":"In the 1960s a dam built in Egypt to help farmers diverted water that filled an ancient underground complex called the Egyptian labyrinth, a vast building described long ago by the Greek historian Herodotus and the Roman writer Pliny the Elder. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan tells Michels this, passing on what he learned from a man named Ben van Kerkwijk: \"they diverted the water and the water filled up this labyrinth.\" If so, one of the great lost wonders of the ancient world would be sitting underwater, out of reach.","dan-burisch-and-j-rod-at-s4-2003":"A microbiologist named Dan Burisch is said to have worked with 'the J-Rod', a living extraterrestrial being kept deep underground at S4, a secret site said to lie near Area 51, the Air Force's classified test base in Nevada, around 2003 and 2004. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb repeats this on the show from Burisch's own account, describing the being as housed about 'six layers' below the site; if true, it would mean the government holds a live alien.","dan-howe-vouches-for-dan-sherman-s-elint-knowledge-1990":"A man named Dan Howe is said to vouch for Dan Sherman, a man who says he served in a secret programme he calls Project Preserve Destiny, saying Sherman's account of ELINT, short for electronic intelligence, the collection of enemy signals, is \"beautifully on the money.\" A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels that Howe sat him down and talked with him for a long time about this. If true, it would lend weight to Sherman's story, since he would know things only an insider would.","dan-sheehan-discredited-through-stigma-1987":"In the late 1980s, the lawyer Dan Sheehan, who was working on the Iran-Contra affair with a legal group called the Christic Institute, became the target of an effort to discredit him through stigma, according to the mathematician Eric Weinstein. Weinstein, telling Michels what he takes from Sheehan's own account, thinks Sheehan was \"probably\" fed false information \"so that he'd swing at a bad pitch\". If so, it would show a way of ruining someone's credibility by baiting them into making wild claims.","dan-sherman-and-nsa-project-preserve-destiny-1991":"In the early 1990s, says Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant, he was taken to an NSA complex in Laurel, Maryland, where an officer he calls Captain White told him he was part of Project Preserve Destiny, a secret program that, he was told, grew from contact with \"an alien species\" in 1947 and began in 1960. Sherman tells Michels this firsthand in several interviews and in his book Above Black, and Michels often repeats it, adding that Sherman was trained to receive tones from the beings and that he says he was genetically altered as a fetus after his mother was abducted while pregnant. If true, it would mean the NSA ran a long program of communicating with aliens through specially prepared people.","dan-sherman-taken-to-an-nsa-complex-for-sine-wave-training-1990":"In the 1990s, a man named Dan Sherman was taken to a complex of the NSA, the US eavesdropping agency, in Maryland, perhaps at Fort Meade, where \"he was given these headphones\" and trained with sine-wave tones. Michels tells this from what Sherman has said, and credits Sherman's book Above Black. If true, a US intelligence agency was quietly training people in some unusual secret skill.","dan-wilson-links-green-fireballs-to-radiation-drift-1947":"Green fireballs, glowing green lights reported in the American sky in the late 1940s, appeared at the same times that radiation was drifting across the country, according to the work of a researcher named Dan Wilson. A researcher of UFOs at nuclear sites named Robert Hastings tells Michels that Wilson spent countless hours matching the sightings to the radiation, a link that would tie the lights to the dawn of the nuclear age.","daniel-gockerell-triangle-sighting":"A man named Daniel Gockerell saw a triangle-shaped craft. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell mentions it to Michels as a \"small story\" that he had already put on his own show, Weaponized, and he gives no details here beyond the triangle.","daniel-s-encounter-with-a-being-by-the-tigris":"The story is the biblical prophet Daniel's meeting with a shining being on the bank of the Tigris river, which Michels brings up as an ancient account of an encounter with something not human. Michels tells it in his own words, citing the King James Bible of 1611: Daniel sees a being \"with a face like lightning and eyes like torches,\" and in another episode he picks out the words \"fear not,\" said to Daniel before he received his prophecy. The text itself, in the tenth chapter of Daniel, has Daniel standing \"by the side of the great river, which is Hiddekel,\" the Hebrew name for the Tigris. He looks up and sees \"a certain man clothed in linen\" with a belt of gold, whose body was \"like the beryl, and his face as the appearance of lightning, and his eyes as lamps of fire,\" with arms and feet like polished brass and a voice \"like the voice of a multitude.\" The being then says, \"Fear not, Daniel,\" and goes on to tell him what will happen to his people. The scripture matches Michels's retelling closely, and for him it shows that frightening, glowing visitors who say \"fear not\" have been part of human stories for a very long time.","daniel-sheehan-as-chief-of-station-for-the-jesuits-in-dc-1970":"The researcher Matthew Pines says the lawyer Daniel Sheehan was, in effect, chief of station, the head of a local intelligence post, for a Jesuit intelligence network in Washington, DC, in the 1970s. Pines tells Michels this half joking and without naming a source. If true, it would mean Sheehan's work in Washington served the Jesuits' own interests.","dannion-brinkley-s-holographic-life-review":"A man named Dannion Brinkley says that when he nearly died, he relived his whole life as a three-dimensional, \"holographic panoramic\" review, seeing each moment from the side of the people he had affected. The scientist Rizwan Virk retells this on the show from Brinkley's account: Brinkley had killed people as a soldier in the Vietnam War, and in the review he \"experienced what it was like to take a bullet from himself.\" Virk tells it because it fits his idea that reality works like a hologram or a simulation that can be replayed.","danny-jones-interview-throttled-over-a-mention-of-mission-co-2025":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had many UFO encounters, tells Michels that a 2025 interview he gave to a host named Danny Jones was throttled online, its reach held back. He says the reason was that he mentioned \"some technology at mission control\", meaning NASA's Mission Control. He does not say what the technology was.","danny-s-dmt-laser-experiments-reveal-code-behind-reality":"Michels says a friend of his, whom he names only as Danny, does experiments shining lasers while on DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, and that the scattered laser light appears to show a kind of \"code\" behind reality. Michels raises it himself and asks the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore what he thinks of it; if the claim held, it would suggest the drug lets people see a hidden structure in the world.","danny-sheehan-says-he-has-coordinates-for-an-underwater-ufo-2024":"In 2024 the lawyer Danny Sheehan said he had map coordinates for an underwater UFO base, placed in the tellings off Baja California near Guadalupe Island. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels he heard Sheehan say it ('he has coordinates for a UFO underwater base'), which, if true, would put a hidden non-human base in the ocean off Mexico.","danny-sheehan-shown-blue-book-files-and-photo-of-crashed-ufo-1977":"Dan Sheehan says that in the late 1970s, at the request of people close to Jimmy Carter's administration, he was let into a secure room in Washington to read the files of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and there saw photos of a crashed UFO, which he traced, copying strange, hieroglyph-like symbols from around the base of its dome. Telling it himself on the show, he places the room downstairs in the Madison Building, credits Marcia Smith and Father Davis with his access, and describes the craft differently each time: in a snowy field in one telling, stuck in an embankment in another. Michels, retelling it, calls the room a SCIF, a sealed room for handling classified material, and has the craft on a mountaintop. If true, it would mean the government held clear photographs of a crashed craft.","darkness-retreat-study-testing-for-spikes-in-natural-dmt-2025":"The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore says he and New Nordics, a group he works with in Florida, are paying for a 2025 study of darkness retreats, where people spend days in total darkness, to see whether the brain's own DMT goes up. DMT is a powerful psychedelic drug that can cause vivid visions. Gallimore tells Michels about the study as one of its funders, and a rise in DMT could help explain the visions people report in the dark.","darpa-man-talking-to-a-computer-with-his-mind-1974":"Around 1974, a man at DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, is said to have told a visitor, \"I'm talking to this machine with my mind.\" The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something a man named Bob Emenegger told him, and both laugh at the line. If true, it would mean the military was linking minds to computers decades before such work became public.","darpa-n3-brain-machine-interface-program-2018":"In 2018 DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, launched a programme called N3, short for next-generation non-surgical neurotechnology, to link brains and machines without surgery. Michels brings it up himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a UFO book he says the Pentagon tried to censor, where it would point to government interest in reaching into the mind.","darpa-programs-with-nine-figure-budgets-going-black-to-site":"Projects at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, with budgets of hundreds of millions of dollars, may have gone \"black\" (into deep secrecy) and moved to Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels that a DARPA programme manager named John Blitch kept having programmes like these with \"nine-figure budgets\", but he says plainly that Blitch never said where they went: the move to Area 51 is Brandenburg's own speculation.","darpa-robot-mission-to-search-tora-bora-caves-for-bin-laden-2001":"In 2001 and 2002, a team took robots into the caves of Tora Bora, Afghanistan, working from Bagram Airfield, to search for Osama bin Laden, according to John Blitch, a scientist with DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency. Blitch tells Michels he was part of it himself, sent on a request by name from his former boss, Jumo Brown.","darpa-s-far-exception-and-canceled-stealth-program-1997":"John Blitch, a scientist who worked at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, says that in the late 1990s he ran a $130 million portfolio there under an exception to the government's normal purchasing rules, half of it robots and half a stealth programme that was canceled \"a month after it began, again for no reason.\" Blitch tells Michels this from his own experience and says it fits what General Arthur Trudeau, the Army's research chief in the 1950s and 60s, told Philip J. Corso, an officer under him, to do with recovered technology, which if true would mean hidden programmes were being quietly started and shut down.","darpa-sid-program-protection-would-kill-insider-threats":"A secret part of DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, which he calls SID, will kill people it sees as a threat from inside or outside, according to an online researcher who posts as UAPGerb. He says on the show, \"if you're considered an insider outsider threat to them, they'll just kill you,\" and he had posted about it before; he links the claim to David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who has spoken out about secret UFO work. If true, it would mean the secrecy around these programmes is guarded with lethal force.","darpa-smart-dust-field-tests-1990":"By 2001, DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, was showing off \"smart dust\" in field tests: swarms of tiny wireless sensors small enough to drift like dust. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a UFO book he says the Pentagon tried to censor, where it shows how far ahead secret military technology may have been.","darpa-special-access-program-to-reverse-engineer-cephalopod-2000":"John Blitch, who worked as a scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's agency for advanced research, says that around 2000 he ran a special access program, a tightly restricted secret project, that tried to reverse engineer, or work out how to copy, the camouflage of cephalopods such as octopuses and squid. He tells Michels this from his own work and calls it \"mindblowing\", naming the researchers Roger Hanlon, Benny Hochner and Tamar Flash in connection with it.","darwin-s-dog-that-inherited-a-fear-of-butchers":"Charles Darwin is said to have reported, in a paper in the science journal Nature, a dog owned by someone he knew that became very frightened whenever it passed a butcher's shop, a fear it seemed to have inherited rather than learned. The biologist Rupert Sheldrake tells this on the show without naming the paper's date or author, as a case suggesting that fears and memories can be passed down. What he credits to Nature is only his summary of it, and nothing in his telling shows how the dog's fear was traced back to earlier generations.","daryl-bem-s-backward-causality-study-and-failed-replications-2010":"A Cornell psychologist named Daryl Bem published a paper in the Journal of Personality and Social Psychology reporting evidence of backward causality, the idea that a future event can affect what people do now, and other researchers, among them Ray Hyman, Richard Wiseman and Stuart Ritchie, tried and failed to get the same results. The skeptic Michael Shermer tells Michels this as a researcher, saying \"the replication crisis started in 2010\" with Bem's work, meaning the wider discovery that many findings in psychology do not hold up when repeated. He also mentions that the study drew attention as far as Stephen Colbert's show.","dating-the-sphinx-by-its-vernal-equinox-alignment-with-leo":"The Great Sphinx at Giza looked straight at the constellation Leo at sunrise on the spring equinox about 12,500 years ago, not 4,500, which would make the monument far older than usually thought. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels this himself, crediting the writer Robert Bauval and tying it to the three stars of Orion's belt lining up due south at the same moment, and the researcher Randall Carlson repeats it as dating work by Bauval and Hancock. Michels himself credits Hancock and the geologist Robert Schoch instead, and puts it as the Sphinx being carved as a lion in honour of the age of Leo, an age set by the slow wobble of Earth's axis.","daughter-s-question-at-the-church-of-the-holy-sepulchre":"Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer, tells Michels about something his daughter asked the first time she visited the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem and looked at what is kept there as Jesus's cross. He tells it as a family moment he was there for. His words here don't say what her question was.","dava-holloman-film-withheld-from-vallee-and-hynek-1970":"In the 1970s, the Defense Audiovisual Agency, a Pentagon film office that the physicist Eric W. Davis says was run by two retired generals, one perhaps named Jerry Miller, invited two UFO researchers to come and see a film, which the story ties to Holloman Air Force Base in New Mexico. The two were Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and UFO researcher, and J. Allen Hynek, an astronomer who studied UFOs. Davis, speaking from memory, says Vallée tells this in his book Revelations, and as the story goes the film was never in the end shown to them, which would mean the military had footage it chose to hold back.","dave-altman-connects-the-transient-dates-to-the-washington-f-1952":"Some of the strange flashes of light that appear on old sky photographs and then vanish may line up with July 19, 1952, the night a wave of UFO sightings over Washington, D.C. began. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel tells Michels that her colleague Dave Altman, media manager for her research group, asked her, \"Do you know what happens on or what happened on 19th of July, 1952?\" If the dates truly match, the flashes in the sky photographs and the sightings over the capital could be linked.","dave-rossi-s-fourth-orb-in-the-mh370-videos-2014":"Videos said to show glowing orbs circling the Malaysia Airlines plane MH370 before it vanished in 2014 are said to contain a fourth orb, first spotted by a man named Dave Rossi. The Navy scientist Salvatore Pais, known for patents on unusual craft, tells Michels this, and adds that it is why Ashton Forbes, an online researcher who promotes the videos, now goes by \"four orbs\".","david-ferrie-s-mysterious-death-1967":"A man named David Ferrie died in New Orleans in 1967 under mysterious circumstances, in the middle of the investigation into the Kennedy assassination that the story ties to Jim Garrison. A researcher named Peter Levenda tells Michels that Ferrie either \"commits suicide or is suicided\", leaving open whether someone killed him to keep him quiet.","david-flynn-s-cydonia-book-reprinted-by-his-daughter":"A book called Cydonia, written by David Flynn, was once so hard to find that copies sold for two or three thousand dollars, until Flynn's daughter reprinted it. Tim Alberino, a guest, tells Michels this as someone who knew the book in its rare days: \"It was going for two and $3,000.\" The high price shows how much some readers wanted the book.","david-grusch-comes-forward-at-great-personal-sacrifice-2023":"In 2023 David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who told Congress the government holds recovered craft of non-human origin, came forward at a high personal cost: he staked his credibility and lost his position. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this from what he knows of Grusch, saying 'he came forward at great personal sacrifice', as a reason to take Grusch's claims seriously.","david-grusch-testimony-and-icig-report-2022":"A former intelligence officer named David Grusch swore before Congress that the United States has been \"generationally exploiting non-human technology,\" the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell reminds Michels, describing him raising his hand to take the oath. Corbell sets this beside a report from the inspector general who watches over the US spy agencies, an office known as the ICIG, which he brings up as backing for Grusch. If Grusch is right, the government has held technology made by someone other than humans for generations.","david-grush-s-congressional-testimony-2023":"In 2023 a former intelligence officer named David Grusch told Congress under oath that the US government runs a program dealing with UFOs because they are real. If true, the government has been hiding knowledge of craft and beings that are not human. Michels says Grusch came from the NGA, a US intelligence agency that studies satellite and map imagery, and dates the hearing to July 26, 2023. Grusch, in his own words from the hearing, bases his claims on 'interviewing over 40 witnesses over four years.' Karl Nell, speaking as a former official, sums it up as testimony before the House Oversight Committee that 'there is a government program looking at UFOs because they're real.' The journalist Michael Shellenberger calls it 'really strong testimony.' A scientist named Donald Hoffman recalls sworn testimony in which credible high-ranking military and other officials said, 'I have seen non-human biologics and non-human technology,' meaning bodies and machines. Hoffman does not name Grusch.","david-huggins-encounters-with-mantids-grays-and-a-hybrid-wom":"A man named David Huggins claimed encounters with mantids, tall insect-like beings, and with grays, small gray-skinned beings, and said the mantids \"were in charge of these little grays\"; his story also includes a hybrid woman, part human and part alien, and takes place in the US South and in underground places. The researcher Jason Jorjani relays this to Michels as what Huggins claimed. If true, it would point to a ranking among different kinds of beings.","david-jay-brown-s-field-guide-to-dmt-entities-machine-elves":"People who take DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, often report meeting beings, from what the writer Terence McKenna called \"machine elves\" to insect-like creatures that seem to operate on them. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, who studies the drug, mentions to Michels a book that catalogues these beings, the Illustrated Field Guide to DMT Entities by David Jay Brown, as a way of showing how consistent the reports are.","david-morales-fired-the-fatal-shot-from-the-grassy-knoll-1963":"The shot that killed President Kennedy in Dallas in 1963 came from the grassy knoll, a small rise beside the motorcade's route, and was fired by a man named Morales, according to the lawyer Dan Sheehan. On one episode Sheehan names him as David Morales; on another he calls him Roger Morales, \"the dead eye shot\" for the team. If true, it would mean Kennedy was killed by a team, not a lone gunman.","david-ruiz-a-three-fingered-humanoid-species-not-extraterres-2017":"The three-fingered bodies found in Peru are not extraterrestrial but a humanoid species, perhaps \"a three-fingered hominid\" like us, according to David Ruiz, who has studied them. Ruiz tells Michels that in eight years of research he has never once used the word extraterrestrial. If he is right, it would mean an unknown relative of humans once existed.","davis-asks-the-aps-historian-in-1984-where-anti-gravity-rese":"In 1984, at a meeting of the American Physical Society, the main professional body for US physicists, the physicist Eric W. Davis says he asked the society's historian where the research on anti-gravity had gone. Davis tells Michels he was prompted by the physicist Bob Forward, then at Hughes Research Labs in Malibu, who had studied anti-gravity there; the question matters because, if such work went quiet, it may have moved out of public view.","davis-at-the-aerospace-corporation-in-huntsville-few-phd-phy":"The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels he worked for a company called the Aerospace Corporation for four and a half years. He describes his time at its Huntsville, Alabama, office as a place with few PhD physicists, and says those who were there all did engineering work.","davis-briefs-senate-intelligence-and-armed-services-committe-2019":"A physicist named Eric W. Davis says he briefed staff of the Senate intelligence committee and the Senate Armed Services Committee in classified settings, starting with the first group in 2019, in briefings that ran until 2022 in Washington DC and Huntsville, Alabama. He tells Michels this as someone who gave the briefings himself, in an episode about UFO secrets reaching the highest levels of government.","davis-hired-by-cia-as-winpac-branch-chief-scientist-2003":"The scientist Eric W. Davis says the CIA hired him in late 2003 as a branch chief scientist at WINPAC, a CIA unit that deals with weapons threats. He tells Michels this about his own career, as part of his background in classified work.","davis-joins-nids-bigelow-s-institute-1995":"The physicist Eric W. Davis says he joined NIDS, the National Institute for Discovery Science, a private group in Las Vegas that the businessman Bob Bigelow founded in 1995 to study unexplained events, and served there as director of aerospace physics and astrophysics research. Davis tells Michels this from his own career, noting it will be \"30 years this July,\" and names colleagues there such as Colm Kelleher and John B. Alexander.","davis-meets-grusch-at-the-aerospace-corporation-and-hands-ov":"The physicist Eric W. Davis says he met David Grusch, a whistleblower who later spoke publicly about secret UFO programmes, while Davis was working for a company called the Aerospace Corporation, at its campus in Colorado Springs. He tells Michels that a man named Jay Stratton introduced them, and the story goes that Davis then handed his data over to Grusch.","davis-s-papers-for-marc-millis-s-propulsion-physics-program-1990":"A scientist named Eric W. Davis, who appears on the show as a Pentagon scientist, says that between about 1990 and 2009 he wrote papers for a propulsion physics program at NASA run by a man named Marc Millis, a program studying new ways to move spacecraft. Davis, who says he took part himself, tells Michels the work had nothing to do with UFOs: \"I'm just contributing my knowledge.\"","davis-says-puthoff-interviewed-brad-sorensen":"The physicist Hal Puthoff is said to have interviewed a man named Brad Sorensen, according to the physicist Eric W. Davis. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb passes this on to Michels from Davis in a single cut-off remark ('Hal Puthoff interviewed Brad Sorensen'), without saying what the interview was about.","davis-takes-sap-security-training-he-and-grusch-discuss-hidd":"The scientist Eric W. Davis says that while at the Aerospace Corporation he went through security training for special access programs, or SAPs, the most tightly restricted kind of classified project. He tells Michels he and David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who has made public claims about hidden UFO programmes, talked about how secrets can be hidden in SCIs, another category of restricted classified information, rather than in SAPs, which would matter for where such programmes might be concealed.","davis-told-definitively-that-we-are-monitored-and-do-not-con":"A physicist who has worked for the Pentagon, Eric W. Davis, says he was told that humans are being watched and do not control their own airspace, and that this was said to him as fact, not guesswork: \"we know this to be true.\" He tells it on the show, putting his confidence at 100 percent, without naming who told him. If true, it would mean someone in the know has concluded we are not alone or in charge of our skies.","davis-uses-dia-deputization-to-get-access-to-the-crash-retri-2008":"The physicist Eric W. Davis says he used his security clearances and a letter making him a representative of the Defense Intelligence Agency, the Pentagon's military intelligence arm, signed by the agency official Jim Lacatski, to get into a secret programme that recovers crashed UFOs. He tells Michels he \"couldn't get access to craft, bodies or talk to the people\", but reached those who ran it \"at a programmatic level\" and \"got confirmation that all of that was real, that all of it happened\". If true, it means a cleared government scientist confirmed from the inside that such a programme exists.","dc-fusion-center-foia-on-psychotronic-effects":"Someone who asked the Washington, D.C. fusion center, a hub where federal and local agencies share intelligence, for its records on certain political groups is said to have received instead a file titled \"effects on the human body\", about psychotronic effects, meaning devices said to act on the body or mind from a distance. A guest named Dave Rossi tells Michels this as a leak from a request under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, without naming who filed it, and the point would be that the government studies such effects.","dead-labrador-nelly-photographed-as-a-ball-of-light-after-a-2007":"In 2007, after a trip to see a launch at Cape Canaveral, Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs appear around him, says he photographed his dead Labrador, Nelly, rising from her grave as a ball of light: \"she comes up out of her grave in a little ball of light, and I took that picture.\" He tells Michels this himself and also tells it in his book UFO of God, in a story where a man named Timothy Taylor also appears.","dead-man-switch-set-up-in-first-call-with-jeremy-corbell":"Dylan Borland says that in his very first phone call with a man named Jeremy Corbell, he set up a dead man switch, an arrangement for what he knows to come out if something happens to him. Borland tells Michels he opened that call by saying, \"Jeremy, I don't know you. You don't know me. I'm going to go become a congressional whistleblower.\" He tells it to show how seriously he took the risk of speaking to Congress, and that he felt he needed protection from the start.","dead-sea-scrolls-isaiah-matches-text-a-thousand-years-later":"The copy of the Book of Isaiah found among the Dead Sea Scrolls, ancient manuscripts discovered near Qumran by the Dead Sea, is word for word the same as a version copied a thousand years later, according to the podcaster Joe Rogan. Rogan tells Michels he heard this from a guest on his own show, Wes Huff, and brings it up as a sign that old texts can be passed down for centuries without changing.","dean-koontz-novel-predicting-covid-and-the-wuhan-lab-2020":"The American novelist Dean Koontz wrote a book, The Eyes of Darkness, about a perfect bioweapon called Wuhan 400 developed at a lab near Wuhan, China, and set in 2020, the year of COVID, according to Michels. He tells this himself on two episodes and says the novel was written in 1981. He raises it as an example of fiction that seems to foresee real events, which, if more than coincidence, would suggest some kind of glimpse of the future.","dean-radin-had-to-disparage-remote-viewing-while-in-stargate-1970":"The researcher Dean Radin, it is said, worked inside Stargate, a programme started by the CIA to test remote viewing, the claimed ability to see distant places with the mind, while having to speak badly of remote viewing in public. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani passes this on, relaying that Radin \"was part of the project Stargate,\" and mentions Joe Rogan and the physicist Hal Puthoff along the way. If true, it would mean the public doubt was partly a cover for work the government took seriously.","death-of-bilgames-ziusudra-the-last-to-be-given-immortality":"An old Mesopotamian clay tablet called The Death of Bilgames (Bilgames being another name for Gilgamesh) says the Anunna gods gave the flood hero Ziusudra, the Noah figure of the story, immortality, and that he was the last man ever to receive it. The researcher Matt LaCroix tells this on the show as something he has read, calling it an 'obscure tablet'. He reads it as a sign that the gods stopped granting immortality after the flood. In the story as he gives it, the flood survivor is made immortal and nobody else is after him, much as Noah's story comes after a flood.","death-of-bill-steinman":"A man named Bill Steinman died of a massive heart attack one January a few years back, near Goodyear, Arizona, while Sheila Steinman was in the kitchen. Scott Ramsey, a researcher of a claimed crash with alien bodies, tells Michels this as what Sheila Steinman told him.","death-of-john-norseen-2007":"A man named John Norseen died on September 27, 2007, while travelling on business for a company called Concurrent Technologies, Michels says. He tells it himself, without naming a source, on an episode about American scientists who have died or gone missing. He brings it up as one case in what he sees as a possible pattern.","debrief-at-ellsworth-with-rick-doty-present-1960":"A former Air Force nuclear guard named Mario Woods says that after the incident he calls his abduction, he was questioned at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota. Among those in the room, he says, were Jack Reed, a captain of the Air Force Office of Special Investigations (the Air Force's own detective service), and beside him a new agent named Rick Doty, whom Woods only recognised years later on the TV show Ancient Aliens, saying \"I know that guy. He was at my debrief.\" The story's record puts it in the 1960s, but Woods in one telling and Michels, who tells it as 1977, place it then, and Michels describes five men in the room, among them the wing commander and an unnamed civilian in a gray tweed suit, with Woods shouting at the TV when he saw Doty's face in 2014.","debris-blocked-shafts-between-khafre-and-the-sphinx-connect":"Shafts choked with debris, lying between the pyramid of Khafre and the Sphinx on the Giza plateau in Egypt, are said to lead down to underground facilities beneath Giza. The claim comes from Filippo Biondi, a scientist who has scanned beneath the pyramids, who tells Michels that his team is \"very confident\" the shafts connect to what lies below. If so, Giza would have a hidden layer that nobody has yet entered.","deceptive-fairy-gentry-in-medieval-folklore":"In the old folklore of Brittany and Ireland, the peasants knew that the fairy folk, the gentry, \"lie all the time.\" A philosopher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, drawing on the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, as a warning that beings who appear to people today may be just as deceptive.","deciding-to-go-public-kasparov-calls-for-his-removal-1997":"Kiran Ilum Xenov, then president of a Russian republic and of the World Chess Federation, says that when he went public about meeting aliens, the former world champion Garry Kasparov demanded he be thrown out. He tells Michels about it himself, as the man it happened to: Kasparov 'immediately began to state' that anyone who had met aliens could not run world chess and 'must be removed and replaced.'","decision-to-stop-briefing-new-legacy-program-members-on-its":"At some point a decision was made to stop telling new members of the \"legacy program\", a secret programme said to hold recovered non-human craft, about the programme's own history. Matthew Brown, who comes on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells Michels that a man named Dylan Borland had been \"pretty direct\" about this in his own talk with Michels. If true, it would mean even insiders work without knowing where their programme came from.","declassified-air-force-documents-on-ufos-at-cold-war-nuclear":"UFO sightings happened at American nuclear production, missile storage and deployment sites throughout the Cold War, at places like Hanford in Washington, Savannah in South Carolina, Fort Hood in Texas, Los Alamos, and the missile bases Malmstrom and F.E. Warren. Michels says this is known from hundreds of declassified Air Force documents, which he cites as a group without quoting any. If true, it would mean these objects showed a lasting interest in America's nuclear weapons.","declassified-cia-memos-on-exotic-gravity-research-at-univers-1950":"In the 1950s, declassified CIA memos show American universities working as outposts for what Jesse Michels calls \"exotic gravity research\". The places tied to the story are Indiana, Purdue, Princeton and MIT. Michels says this himself, in a conversation about UFO physics, pointing to the memos without quoting them, as a sign that the government took unusual gravity science seriously early on.","declassified-upshot-knothole-report-puts-stansel-at-the-neva-1953":"A man named Arthur Stansel Jr. was on site for the 1953 nuclear tests in Nevada known as Operation Upshot-Knothole, and Michels says a declassified report on the operation confirms it. Michels raises it himself in an episode asking whether Henry Kissinger oversaw a programme to recover crashed UFOs, where placing Stansel at a secret nuclear test site helps back up his part in the story.","decoded-messages-of-aliens-with-a-dying-planet-needing-earth-1980":"In the early 1980s, Paul Bennewitz, an Albuquerque man who believed he was decoding alien signals, came to think that aliens from another planet were here, trying to set up a base, and that they needed Earth women. The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels this as part of Bennewitz's story, without naming a source. Bishop's point is how far these messages pulled Bennewitz into a world of his own.","deconfliction-language-in-the-1971-treaty":"The claim is that in 1971 the United States and the Soviet Union agreed, on paper, to coordinate over unexplained objects, so that neither side would mistake one for an attack. The document is the Agreement on Measures to Reduce the Risk of Outbreak of Nuclear War, signed by the US and the USSR in 1971. Pippa Malmgren, the daughter of the presidential adviser Harald Malmgren, points to it, calling it \"the 1971 SALT treaty,\" after the Strategic Arms Limitation Talks between the two powers. Michels tells it twice in his own words. Once he calls it \"the SALT One negotiations, I think it was 1971,\" and says it is \"open source\": \"we're talking about coordinating with the Russians on the UFO question because we were worried that they'd mistake it for American acts of aggression.\" In that telling he also brings in John F. Kennedy. The other time he describes it as language \"on record\" saying \"we have to coordinate with the Soviets on unknowns in their airspace.\" None of the tellers quotes the agreement's own wording. If it is right, the two nuclear powers were treating unknown objects as a real risk of starting a war.","deconfliction-line-used-before-strike-on-russian-proxies-in":"After Russian-backed militias attacked Americans in Syria, the United States first called Russia on the deconfliction line, a direct channel the two militaries use to avoid clashing by accident, before striking back. Bob Maguire, a former CIA contractor, tells Michels this, passing on what he was told: \"we called them on the deconfliction line.\" He tells it to show how the two powers talk to each other even in a fight.","deep-prasad-harassed-during-uap-science-work":"A researcher named Deep Prasad says unfriendly people interfered with him while he was doing scientific work on UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. Michels raises it, recalling that Prasad seemed to have \"hit a nerve\" among \"deep stater types,\" meaning hidden insiders in government, and Prasad answers, \"You are not wrong at all.\" If true, it would suggest someone was watching that research closely enough to push back.","deep-underground-bases-at-s4-and-dulce":"Michels says there are deep underground military bases across the United States, and that some are rumored to house other beings, naming S4, a site said to sit within Area 51 in Nevada, and Dulce, New Mexico. He says this himself, in an episode about mummies from Peru, presenting the beings part as rumor, which if true would mean non-human beings are being kept in secret US facilities.","deep-underground-bases-from-air-force-project-rand-1950":"In the 1950s and 1960s the US began building deep underground military bases, and the idea came out of Project RAND, an Air Force research effort. This is what a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels, calling it a \"very rich history\" but naming no source. He raises it to argue that hidden underground facilities are an old, planned part of American military building.","deep-underground-bases-since-1948-with-nazi-scientist-xavier":"The claim is that the US military has been building deep underground bases since the late 1940s, and that captured German engineers helped start the work. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels that such bases 'absolutely exist.' He says they have been 'a huge focus' of the Pentagon and the US Army Corps of Engineers since 1948. That year, in his telling, the US brought over Nazi scientists, among them Xavier Dorsch, head of the Todt Organization, Nazi Germany's construction arm, to work at an underground laboratory under the Air Materiel Command, the Air Force's research and supply command. He points to the Corps of Engineers' own studies of underground building. The researcher Richard Dolan, who writes about the history of UFO secrecy, makes a similar point on a separate episode. He credits not Dorsch but the writer Richard Sauder, who he says made 'a very very strong case' from documents that 'even back in the 40s and 50s' the military was looking hard at how to 'go deep.' If true, it would mean a hidden underground building effort going back nearly eighty years.","defense-bases-deliberately-placed-at-natural-portal-sites-li-1950":"The idea is that some American defense bases, such as Area 51, the secret test site in the Nevada desert, may have been built on purpose at places that work as natural 'portals'. Michels raises it himself as a question to a guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb, admitting it 'is going to sound really trippy'. He draws on the researcher Robert Hastings, whose book UFOs and Nukes collects reports of strange objects over nuclear sites, and on Jake Barber, a guest who has said on the show that he helped recover a UFO by helicopter. If the idea were true, the government would know where these places are and would be guarding them.","defense-department-contacts-privately-encourage-rossi-2021":"Between 2021 and 2024, a man named Dave Rossi, who says beings taught him advanced physics, met with people inside the Defense Department. He tells Michels that officially \"there was no way that they could support me, but privately, they were encouraging me,\" which, if true, would mean some officials took his ideas seriously off the record.","dehomag-ibm-s-german-subsidiary-ahead-in-transistors-1930":"Dehomag, the German subsidiary of IBM in the Nazi era, was \"way ahead\" in electronics, Michels says, and the story has it ahead in transistors. He says it himself, without a source, in a conversation with the philosopher Jason Jorjani about a claimed secret Nazi UFO programme, where it would mean Germany had technology far beyond what is usually credited.","deinococcus-radiodurans-survives-three-years-in-space-on-the":"A tough bacterium called Deinococcus radiodurans is said to have survived three years in open space during experiments on the International Space Station. A guest named Dr. Gilbert mentions this to Michels without naming the studies. If true, it suggests simple life could survive a journey between worlds.","delgado-remote-controls-a-bull-with-a-brain-implant-1960":"In the 1960s in Spain, the brain scientist José Delgado put an implant in a bull's head and could then 'basically remote control' the animal. The researcher Greg Malatzi brings this up on the show, pointing to film of the experiment, as proof that behaviour could already be steered with brain implants back then.","delonge-already-had-ten-advisers-by-november-2014":"By November 2014 the musician Tom DeLonge already had ten advisers helping his UFO effort, according to the writer Peter Levenda, who worked closely with DeLonge and says that was the count when he joined. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this, repeating Levenda. If true, it would mean DeLonge's network of insiders was in place well before it became public.","delonge-in-secure-facilities-2010":"A man named Tom DeLonge says that sometime in the 2010s he went inside secure government facilities, getting past \"the codes and the alarms and the guns\" to rooms that \"lock you in\" and are airtight. DeLonge tells Michels this from his own experience, as proof of how far inside the secret world he got.","delonge-iraq-war-linked-to-groups-connected-with-nonhuman-en-2003":"Some of what the US did in Iraq after 2003 may have been aimed at groups of people there who were connected with nonhuman beings and were helping them spread. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels that the rock musician Tom DeLonge, who later founded a UFO research company, said this in early interviews, before anyone knew who his advisers were. If true, the war would have had a hidden purpose tied to non-human contact.","delonge-on-2016-radio-weapon-systems-built-for-returning-arc":"In 2016 the musician Tom DeLonge said on the radio that weapons were being built because the archons are \"coming back\"; the archons are ruling spirits from ancient Gnostic writings, said to hold humanity captive. A guest named Jay Anderson recalls it on the show, saying DeLonge was \"so excitedly talking\" and answered a question about the archons with \"we've been developing weapon systems because we know they're coming back.\" If true, it would mean someone was preparing for the return of hostile non-human powers.","delonge-on-coast-to-coast-with-george-knapp-2011":"In 2011 the rock musician Tom DeLonge was a guest on Coast to Coast AM, a late-night American radio show about the paranormal and UFOs, speaking with the journalist George Knapp. A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels this to show that DeLonge was already part of those UFO conversations years before he became widely known for them.","delonge-s-advisers-went-quiet-when-asked-about-hybrids-2016":"Around 2016 and 2017, Tom DeLonge, a rock musician who went public with UFO claims, said there was 'a big genetic component' to the phenomenon, and that his advisers answered his questions until he asked about hybrids of humans and aliens, at which point 'they went quiet'. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this as what DeLonge said, taking the silence to mean the hybrid question touches something hidden.","delonge-s-contact-at-lockheed-skunk-works-2015":"Around 2015, a man named Tom DeLonge is said to have made contact with someone who worked at Skunk Works, the secret advanced aircraft division of the defence company Lockheed Martin. A researcher named Jason Simosa says the researcher Jacques Vallée has confirmed this \"original story\". A guest named Jay Anderson tells it in more detail: DeLonge's contact invited him to introduce the bosses on stage at a family-and-friends event, and DeLonge said he wanted \"10 minutes with your boss afterwards\". Michels mentions it briefly as DeLonge meeting Lockheed executives. It matters to the tellers because it would mean a private citizen got a hearing from inside one of the most secret parts of American aerospace.","delonge-s-meetings-at-the-pentagon-and-nasa-2015":"Around 2015, the musician Tom DeLonge is said to have been flown to the Pentagon for a meeting, then told he was going on to NASA to meet more people, possibly including Pete Worden, whom the teller calls a one-star NASA general. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source, and links Mike Carey and Neil McCasland to the meetings too. If true, it would mean senior officials quietly took part in DeLonge's UFO efforts.","delonge-s-nro-sources-on-predicting-episodic-craft-visits-2017":"The musician Tom DeLonge is said to have claimed that his high-level sources in the NRO, the US spy satellite agency, told him scientists had worked out how to predict when and where craft would appear. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this, repeating what he says DeLonge said, apparently on the podcaster Joe Rogan's show, around 2017. If true, it would mean the visits follow a pattern that the government can forecast.","delongepodesta-disclosure-meeting-with-robert-weiss-2016":"In January 2016, the political adviser John Podesta, the rock musician Tom DeLonge and a man named Robert Weiss are said to have met in Washington to talk about making public what the government knows about UFOs. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels about \"this meeting where all these guys are at\", drawing on the Podesta emails, the messages from Podesta's account that WikiLeaks published in 2016. If it happened as he describes, it would mean disclosure was being discussed with people close to the White House.","delta-and-omicron-flight-configurations-of-the-craft-1987":"Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft in the late 1980s at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, says the craft could fly in two set-ups. He tells Michels that when all three of its propulsion units, which he calls amplifiers, were used to travel, it was in the 'delta configuration', and when only one was used, it was in the 'omicron configuration'. If true, it would be a first-hand look at how such a craft was flown.","demand-for-more-stories-set-in-the-water-1996":"In 1996, at the Los Angeles offices of the TV series Dark Skies, a man Bryce Zabel calls \"the captain\" told the writers that in the second season they needed to \"do more stuff in the water.\" Zabel, who says he was there, tells this to Michels as part of a story about Hollywood and the government. If it was more than a passing note, it would mean someone was steering the show's UFO stories toward the sea.","demodulating-soviet-french-venus-balloon-probe-signal-for-jp-1983":"A CIA contractor named Bob Maguire says that in the mid-1980s he worked on the signal from a Soviet and French space mission: a probe bound for Halley's Comet swung past Venus on the way and dropped two balloon probes into the planet's atmosphere. He tells Michels he decoded, or demodulated, the balloons' signal for the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, NASA's lab for space missions, work tied in the story to the Institute for Defense Analysis, a defense research body.","demons-as-the-spirits-of-dead-nephilim-giants":"The idea is that demons are the ghosts of the Nephilim, the giants that ancient Jewish writings say were born when fallen angels called the Watchers mated with human women, and that when the giants were killed their spirits left their bodies and stayed on Earth. A researcher of giant lore named Tim Alberino tells Michels this, first calling it \"ancient Hebrew cosmology\" and then pointing to the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text outside the standard Bible, in the 1917 English translation by R. H. Charles. The text he names lines up closely with what he says. It says the giants, \"produced from the spirits and flesh,\" shall be called \"evil spirits upon the earth,\" that \"evil spirits have proceeded from their bodies,\" and that their beginning is \"from the holy Watchers.\" It says these spirits \"afflict, oppress, destroy\" and \"take no food, but nevertheless hunger and thirst,\" and that from \"the slaughter and destruction and death of the giants\" their spirits go forth to destroy until the final judgement. The book calls them evil spirits rather than demons, but what it describes, spirits of dead giants left roaming the earth to do harm, is the same thing Alberino means. For him it would explain where demons come from.","dendera-light-bulb-relief":"A carving at the temple of Dendera in Egypt is said to show a light bulb, what one guest calls \"the classic light bulb.\" Jay Anderson, a guest who says he has seen it himself, raises it with Michels as a sign that the ancient Egyptians may have known more than we credit them with.","denial-at-the-meeting-at-swedish-military-headquarters-2011":"A Swedish treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, whose team found what he calls a UFO on the seafloor, says that at a 2011 meeting at Swedish military headquarters in Stockholm, an officer flatly denied that the military had been to the site. Asberg tells Michels he was in the room: the man said 'No, no, no, no, no. We have not been there,' and Asberg answered that he had filmed them there himself, so he knew they had been.","dennis-kucinich-laughed-off-a-debate-stage-over-ufos-2000":"The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels that when Dennis Kucinich, a Democrat who ran for president, got linked to UFOs, he was \"pretty much laughed off stage\" in a debate and his campaign \"plummeted\". Simosa tells it from memory, without a source, and is unsure of the date (\"2010 election I think\"). He offers it as proof of how much it can cost a politician to be tied to the subject.","dented-white-van-seen-at-the-facility-and-followed-1990":"A former sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that during his time in Maryland between 1990 and 1993 he saw a white van with a dent at the exit of the facility where he trained, and later realised the same van was following him near his Holiday Inn. He tells Michels he checked the van and \"sure enough it had that same exact uh dent.\" To him it meant he was being watched by people connected to the programme.","department-of-energy-holds-both-ufo-secrets-and-the-human-ge-1990":"Michels argues that the Department of Energy, because of the long-reported link between UFOs and nuclear sites, likely holds many secrets about UFOs and the reverse engineering of them, meaning taking recovered craft apart to copy how they work, and that the same department also ran the Human Genome Project in the 1990s. He makes this connection himself, in an episode on medical experiments and the drug industry, to suggest that UFO secrecy and human biology research sat under the same roof.","department-of-energy-manages-ufo-secrecy-and-crash-retrieval":"The Department of Energy, Michels says, has a lot to do with keeping UFOs secret and with crash retrievals, meaning recovering crashed craft that are not from any known country. He says that he and others \"think and kind of know\" this, without naming a source. If true, it would put a civilian department, not only the military, at the centre of the secret.","department-of-state-cleared-report-governs-what-brown-can-sa":"A former government official turned whistleblower named Matthew Brown says that what he is free to say in public rests on a report the Department of State has cleared for release. He tells Michels that his account is \"largely based on the department of state cleared report and then inferences drawn from that report,\" which marks the line between what the document backs and what he concludes himself.","department-of-war-releases-ufo-files-on-trump-s-order-2026":"On May 8, 2026, Michels says, the Department of War released hundreds of UFO-related documents, videos and audio recordings, after President Trump ordered the release of government files on alien life and UFOs. In an earlier episode he explains how it started: Trump announced on Truth Social, his social media platform, that he was directing the Pentagon and other agencies to find and release files on aliens and UAP, the official term for unidentified anomalous phenomena. Congressman Eric Burlison, speaking as an official, says the first batch was mostly videos from MQ-9 Predator military drones that had caught something that \"seems really strange\", and credits the White House aide Stephen Miller. The skeptic Michael Shermer says there have now been three releases and that the photos and videos all carry \"big blacked out rectangles\". A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet calls them the PURSUE files, says they are \"still a work in progress\" with no metadata, but holds that they show \"a whole lot of UAP activity\". The tellers differ in places: in one June episode Michels speaks as if the president were still contemplating a release, and in another he says the documents sit on a dedicated Department of War website with 500 million hits. If the files are what the tellers think, the US government has begun opening its UFO records on a president's order.","deputy-chris-porritt-s-myers-era-testimony-1980":"A deputy sheriff named Chris Porritt went on record that strange things happened at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of odd lights and creatures, long before the Sherman family lived there, back when Kenneth and Edith Myers owned it. The ranch's current owner, Brandon Fugal, tells Michels this, citing Porritt's account. It matters because it would mean the ranch's strangeness goes back decades, before the reports that made it famous.","derinkuyu-underground-city-found-by-a-man-renovating-his-hom":"Derinkuyu, a vast underground city in Turkey, is said to have been found by accident when a man fixing up his home knocked down a wall and opened \"this doorway.\" A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells the story to Michels without naming a source, in an episode about what he believes happened around 12,000 BC.","descartes-as-jesuit-agent-sent-to-infiltrate-the-rosicrucian":"René Descartes was secretly an agent of the Jesuits, according to the philosopher Jason Jorjani. In one episode Jorjani says the Jesuits sent Descartes to infiltrate the Rosicrucians, a secretive brotherhood devoted to hidden knowledge, and he points to his own book Prometheus and Atlas. In a later episode he tells it differently: Descartes's job was to set up a strictly mechanical kind of science so that the occult, the hidden knowledge, would stay under the church's control. Either way, it would mean modern science was shaped to keep certain knowledge locked away.","description-of-the-osiris-shaft":"Beneath the Giza plateau in Egypt there is said to be a three-level, triple-chamber structure called the Osiris Shaft, cut 33 meters underground. It sits on the eastern causeway, the raised road running from the central pyramid down to the valley temple and the Sphinx. A researcher named Jeffrey Drum describes it this way to Michels, without naming where his account comes from, in an episode about a supposed energy grid under the pyramids.","deser-and-arnowitt-graviton-essay-1954":"In 1954 the physicists Stanley Deser and Richard Arnoux won an essay prize from the Gravity Research Foundation, a group that gave awards for work on gravity, with a paper about gravitons, the theoretical particles thought to carry gravity. Michels tells this himself, citing The G-Engines Are Coming, a 1956 piece, along with a man named Michael Gladish. He brings it up because, if serious physicists were working on gravity this way in the 1950s, it would fit his case that people were looking hard into controlling gravity back then.","desert-meeting-with-the-boys-upstairs-four-descending-lights-1978":"Around 1978, Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a doctorate who studies psychics and the afterlife, went out to the desert near Joshua Tree and Twentynine Palms, California, late at night with a companion named Valerie Ransone, hoping to meet what they called \"the boys upstairs.\" He tells Michels his mouth dropped open when they saw \"four lights slowly descending,\" which turned out to be flares dropped by the Marines.","desert-trip-with-jake-barber":"A man named Michael Herrera, who says he saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle, says a man named Jake Barber once took him on a trip out into the desert. Herrera tells Michels that whatever happened on that trip, which he does not describe, \"proved to me that this guy was legit.\"","desmond-leslie-said-nazis-posed-as-venusians-for-adamski-1950":"In the 1950s, a man named George Adamski said he had met beings from Venus, and a writer named Desmond Leslie is said to have believed the visitors were really Nazis in disguise. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that Leslie's daughter told him \"her father always said that it was Nazis who contacted Adamski,\" and he credits the book Flying Saucers Have Landed. If true, one of the most famous early contact stories was staged by surviving Nazis.","detlev-bronk-and-alien-autopsies":"Jesse Michels says that Detlev Bronk, who went on to run the Rockefeller Foundation and to serve as president of Johns Hopkins, was once in charge of \"quote unquote alien autopsies\", meaning the examination of bodies said to come from crashed saucers. Michels tells it himself in two episodes. In the later one he says Bronk was \"supposedly\" doing this work on bodies from the Aztec crash, a saucer crash said to have happened in New Mexico in 1948, and \"maybe\" from Roswell, the most famous claimed crash, in New Mexico in 1947. His source there is the MJ-12 documents, papers describing a secret top-level group said to have handled crashed saucers, and if he is right a leading American scientist examined non-human bodies.","detlev-bronk-named-as-an-mj12-member-1947":"Detlev Bronk, whom Michels calls president of the Rockefeller Foundation, is named in the MJ-12 papers as one of the \"Majestic 12\", a supposed secret group of twelve set up to deal with crashed flying saucers. Michels brings this up himself, and if it were so, it would put a leading figure of American science inside a hidden UFO effort. The papers came to light on a roll of film mailed anonymously in 1984 to a man named Jaime Shandera and were made public in 1987. The National Archives and the Truman and Eisenhower libraries found no trace of them, and FBI copies are marked \"BOGUS\", but researchers still disagree about whether they are genuine. The briefing paper lists \"Dr. Detlev Bronk\" among the members and says that \"a similar analysis of the four dead occupants was arranged by Dr. Bronk\" and that his team coined the term \"Extra-terrestrial Biological Entities\". Bronk's part holds only as far as the paper is genuine.","detlev-bronk-rumored-to-be-on-majestic-12-1947":"Michels says that Detlev Bronk, whom he describes as president of the Rockefeller Foundation, was rumored to be a member of Majestic 12, a supposed secret committee said to have handled crashed UFOs for the US government, and rumored to have carried out the earliest autopsies of alien bodies in the late 1940s. Michels tells it himself, as rumor, without naming a source.","dia-nro-witness-imaging-objects-in-space":"A witness who worked for the DIA, the Pentagon's military intelligence agency, and with the NRO, the US spy satellite agency, is said to have taken images of unknown objects out in space. Steven Greer, who runs the Disclosure Project, a collection of witnesses who say they saw UFO evidence in government work, tells Michels this about one of his witnesses without naming the person. If true, it would mean spy agencies have pictures of these objects beyond Earth.","diana-pasulka-calls-chris-bledsoe-a-modern-paul-bennewitz-2020":"A researcher named Diana Pasulka has said publicly that Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man known for his own encounter claims, is \"a modern Paul Bennewitz.\" Bennewitz was an Albuquerque man who in the early 1980s came to believe he was decoding alien messages, an episode that is said to have driven him to breakdown. Michels repeats Pasulka's remark himself, and the comparison would suggest Bledsoe is being fed a story the way Bennewitz was.","diana-pasulka-on-the-rituals-rocket-teams-perform-before-lau":"Teams that launch rockets in the United States are said to perform rituals, small set ceremonies, before each launch. A guest named Chris Ramsay tells Michels that the religion scholar Diana Pasulka \"talks about this a lot\", and the point would be that even hard-science work carries something like religious practice.","diana-pasulka-s-research-in-the-files-of-st-joseph-of-cupert":"A scholar who has gone through the files of St. Joseph of Cupertino, a Catholic saint from Italy, is said to agree with the point Michels is making about him. Michels tells this himself. He says his friend Diana Pasulka, a religious studies professor at UNC Wilmington, has \"gone very deep\" on the saint and read many of his files, though he does not spell out what she found in them.","diana-pasulka-s-turn-while-writing-her-book":"According to Michels, the scholar Diana Pasulka of UNC Wilmington changed her own view partway through writing a book about the subject. In the second half, he says, she 'goes down the rabbit hole herself' and comes to think: 'Oh my god, I think there's something extremely real.' Michels tells this from his own reading of the book, which the show lists as Heaven Can Wait, and he offers it as a case of a careful academic who came to take the phenomenon itself seriously.","diane-powell-meets-akil-after-his-mother-saw-her-interviewed-2022":"Diane Powell, who studies children said to be able to read minds, says she came to know a boy named Akil in New Jersey in 2022 because his mother, Manisha, saw Powell interviewed by the wellness author Deepak Chopra and got in touch. Powell tells this herself on the show, as how she came to work with Akil.","diane-powell-s-inbox-full-of-parents-saying-their-children-r-2022":"Around 2022, Diane Powell's email inbox reportedly filled with messages from parents who said, \"I'm finding that my child can read my thoughts.\" Ky Dickens, the filmmaker behind a documentary series about telepathy called The Telepathy Tapes, tells Michels this as part of how her own work began. If the parents are right, it would mean some children can share thoughts without words.","diane-powell-s-medical-licence-revoked":"The story is that the psychiatrist Diane Powell lost her medical licence for studying telepathy. The tellers disagree on what triggered it. Michels says on the show that \"her medical license got revoked for studying these telepathic children.\" He means the children in The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary series about children said to read minds. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made The Telepathy Tapes, ties it to an earlier point. She tells Michels that when Powell wrote her book The ESP Enigma, \"she had her medical license revoked because she was talking about ESP.\" ESP means extrasensory perception. Speaking with Dickens, Michels also calls it an \"atrocity\" that the licence was revoked \"at one point\" just for looking into the subject. Both tellers tell it to show how harshly mainstream medicine punishes this kind of research.","diane-powell-takes-on-john-mack-s-legacy-and-a-synchronous-o-2004":"When John Mack, a Harvard psychiatrist known for studying people who say aliens took them, was killed by a car in London in 2004, people at his memorial told the psychiatrist Diane Powell she had to take on his legacy. Michels tells this himself, passing on Powell's story, and it leads into an account of a house in Oregon tied to a striking coincidence. He tells it as the moment Powell's own work on the mind's unexplained abilities was handed a torch.","diary-of-merer-records-tura-limestone-being-shipped-to-the-h":"An ancient Egyptian logbook called the Diary of Merer, kept by an official named Merer, records shipments of limestone blocks from the quarries at Tura up to Giza, to what it calls 'the horizon of Khufu', the pharaoh's pyramid. The researcher Luke Caverns raises it on the show as 'a ledger' of the stones being brought up to Giza. In his reading, it records the stone being delivered for the pyramid. What it leaves open is how much of the building, beyond these deliveries, the logbook can account for.","dick-and-vallee-precognize-each-other-s-work-valis-and-the-i-1974":"The researcher Eric Wargo says the science-fiction writer Philip K. Dick and the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée seemed to foresee each other's ideas while writing at the same time, in 1974 and 1975. As Wargo dates it, that is when Vallée was writing his book The Invisible College and Dick was writing Radio Free Albemuth, the first draft of what became his novel VALIS. Wargo offers this to Michels, without naming a source, as an example of how knowledge might travel through time, which is his larger theme.","dick-billings-on-doctoring-the-zapruder-film-in-rochester-1963":"After the Kennedy assassination in 1963, the Zapruder film, the home movie that caught the shooting, was taken to Rochester, New York, and doctored. A man named Dick Billings, tied in the telling to the publisher Henry Luce, \"sat and told me about the whole thing\", according to Dan Sheehan, a lawyer who repeats it to Michels. If true, the best-known film of the killing was altered.","dick-billings-visits-sheehan-over-the-sealed-iran-contra-com-1980":"In the 1980s, while his Christic Institute was preparing a sealed racketeering lawsuit over the Iran-Contra affair, the lawyer Dan Sheehan says a man turned up at the institute's office at 1324 North Capitol Street in Washington and offered to help. Sheehan tells Michels the man said there \"aren't two men in the world that would have answered that question that way\", then introduced himself as Dick Billings, chief staff writer for the House Select Committee on Assassinations, Congress's inquiry into the Kennedy and King killings. Sheehan tells it as the start of help from someone who had seen the government's secrets up close.","dick-cheney-at-the-top-of-the-secrecy-pyramid":"The former vice president Dick Cheney is said to have sat at the top of the pyramid of UFO secrecy, as gatekeeper of the long-running secret UFO programmes. The chain is second hand: the journalist Walter Kirn says the whistleblower David Grusch told him so, after Michels introduced the two. Michels tells it several times, once recalling asking who \"sits atop the pyramid of classified information\" and being told \"Dick Cheney\", and once hedging that Grusch suggested \"someone like\" Cheney. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb adds that Cheney held the role \"for quite a while\", up to about 2009. If true, it would put the secret in the hands of one of the most powerful men in government.","dick-harper-falsified-personnel-records-1948":"A man named Dick Harper is said to have kept false personnel records so that no one could ever be shown to have taken part in the Aztec recovery, the claimed 1948 retrieval of a crashed craft and its bodies near Aztec, New Mexico. Scott Ramsey, who has looked into the Aztec story, says Harper told him this: \"roughly 200 people were involved in the whole Aztec recovery, so I kept 200 personnel records to make sure nobody was ever really an Aztec.\" If true, it would explain why the people said to have been there leave no trace in the records.","dick-russell-s-1994-taped-interview-with-puharich":"The writer Dick Russell says he met the researcher Puharich once and tape-recorded an interview with him in 1994, the year before Puharich died or, in Russell's words, \"was found dead mysteriously\". The researcher Greg Malatzi relays Russell's words on the show, pointing to the tape as a late first-hand record of Puharich, who is at the centre of claims about contact with an alien council.","dick-russell-s-day-long-interview-with-sidney-gottlieb-1990":"In the 1990s, the writer Dick Russell spent a whole day at the home of Sidney Gottlieb, the CIA scientist who ran its mind-control experiments, and came away with one of the only interviews Gottlieb ever gave. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this as something he knows of Russell's work, and it matters because almost no one ever got Gottlieb to talk.","dicom-ct-scans-show-capillaries-feeding-eggs-in-a-j-type-2017":"CT scans of one of the small three-fingered bodies from Peru show tiny blood vessels feeding eggs inside it, according to Michels, who credits the observation to an independent researcher named Gonzalo Chavez. Chavez helped set up tridactyls.org, a website about these bodies. Michels says the scans were posted there as DICOM files, the standard format for medical images, and that around one of the reptile-like figures researchers call a J type you can see capillaries nourishing the eggs. If that holds, the body would once have been a living creature that carried eggs.","dictators-outlawed-freemasonry":"Hitler, Mao and Stalin all banned Freemasonry, the old fraternal order of Freemasons, in Germany, China and the Soviet Union. A researcher named Randall Carlson says this on the show in passing, without naming a source, as a sign of how much those dictators feared the order.","die-glocke-and-spinning-mercury":"Nazi Germany is said to have built Die Glocke, German for 'the Bell', a bell-shaped device with spinning mercury that may have been an anti-gravity or time machine, according to a Polish journalist named Igor Witkowski, as others retell him. Michels says an SS officer named Jacob Spornberg documented 'magnetic field separation, vortex compression' around it, and that the project ran under the SS officer Hans Kammler, with Ernst Grawitz, head of the SS medical division, among those working on it. A writer named Nick Cook says Witkowski told him the whole story directly, including the codename Kronos and a site at Waldenburg. A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell says he has read Witkowski and the author Joseph Farrell on it, and Michels says the Bell is, in his view, 'no longer fictional'.","different-pieces-of-legacy-hardware-held-in-different-places-2020":"The claim is that different pieces of \"legacy hardware\", the subject's term for craft of unknown, possibly non-human origin said to have been recovered and kept in secret, are held at different sites around the United States, and that a congressman named Burlison has been going to those places to look for it. The journalist Jeremy Corbell says this to Michels in 2026, calling Burlison's visits a \"public thing\". If true, it would mean a member of Congress is trying to find such material for himself.","dimethyl-sulfide-found-on-exoplanets-as-a-sign-of-life-elsew":"Scientists have found dimethyl sulfide, a chemical the story treats as a possible sign of life, on planets around other stars about 120 light years from Earth. A guest named Chris Gilbert mentions it in passing on the show, without naming a source. If it holds up, it would hint that life exists beyond Earth.","dinner-companion-with-chameleon-like-eyes-on-first-egypt-tri-2017":"On his first trip to Egypt in 2017, Geoffrey Drumm, who says an alien gave him the true purpose of the pyramids, sat down to dinner at a hotel with a new friend whose \"big bulbous eyes\" were \"like chameleon,\" seeming to move independently of each other. Drumm tells Michels the man quizzed him about the Serapeum, an ancient underground site in Egypt, and that when Drumm later tried to message him he \"just kind of like disappeared in thin air.\"","dinner-in-san-jose-with-a-female-crash-retrieval-program-wor-1977":"Sometime between the late 1970s and 1989, according to Eric W. Davis, a scientist who appears on the show as a Pentagon scientist, he met a woman who had worked on a crash retrieval program, a secret effort to recover crashed craft of unknown origin. Davis tells Michels that his source's wife drove him to the woman's home, picked her up, and took them both to dinner at a German restaurant in San Jose, California. If true, it would put him face to face with someone who had worked inside such a program.","dinosaur-extinction-by-chicxulub-impact-and-the-ridicule-of-1980":"The dinosaurs died out 66 million years ago when a comet or asteroid struck the Earth, a theory put forward by Luis and Walter Alvarez in the 1980s and, as this story tells it, ridiculed at first. The journalist Graham Hancock tells Michels that \"nobody disputes now\" the impact, and that the Chicxulub crater, deeply buried beneath the land and sea of the Yucatan, is the evidence. In another conversation Hancock stresses that the idea \"still wasn't accepted until they found the crater\", which he places in the Gulf of Mexico. Michels tells it himself as Luis Alvarez's theory alone, without the crater or the mockery. The tellers bring it up as an example of a scorned idea that turned out to be accepted once the evidence was found.","dinosaurs-and-roman-battles-on-benowitz-s-crt-1980":"Between 1980 and 1983, pictures of dinosaurs and old battles that looked Roman are said to have appeared on a CRT, an old-style tube computer screen, in the Albuquerque home of a man named Benowitz. A guest, Greg Bishop, tells it on the show as something he heard from a man named Gabe Valdez, who told Bishop he went over to the house and saw some of it himself. If true, it would mean the screen was showing scenes no ordinary signal would explain.","dire-wolf-carcass-found-at-dry-gulch-creek-and-matched-by-bi-2020":"On camera at Dry Gulch Creek on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, a research team came upon \"this carcass of this decomposing large wolf-like creature,\" which the biologist Ben Woodruff is said to have matched to a dire wolf, an animal long thought extinct. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, tells Michels this happened in the 2020s, during filming for the TV series The Secret of Skinwalker Ranch.","direct-witnesses-scott-interviewed-1948":"A flying saucer is said to have crashed near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948, and the researcher Scott Ramsey, who has spent years looking into the case, says he spoke to people who saw it with their own eyes. Listing them for Michels, he names \"Doug Nolan, Ken Farley, the preacher, the preacher's son, one other gentleman.\" If their accounts hold, the Aztec crash would rest on living eyewitnesses and not only on rumour.","directional-scopaesthesia-paper-from-almost-a-thousand-case":"The researcher Rupert Sheldrake says his latest paper on scopaesthesia, the sense of being stared at, studies almost a thousand case histories of people who reacted to being looked at, and turned toward the person watching them. He tells Michels the paper is called \"Directional Scopaesthesia and its Implications for Theories of Vision.\" As he describes it, the paper uses those cases to ask what this sense would mean for how vision works. If he is right, sight would be more than light coming into the eye.","disappearance-mountain-near-ruwa-zimbabwe-1990":"A mountain near the town of Ruwa in Zimbabwe is said to be a place linked to disappearances, close to where a well-known UFO sighting happened in the 1990s. A researcher named Jason Jorjani brings it up in passing while talking with Michels: \"There's one in Zimbabwe actually near Rua where the famous sighting occurred in the '90s.\" He gives no more detail, but if it were true, the spot would be one of several places on Earth where strange sightings and vanishings cluster together.","disappearance-of-los-alamos-employee-melissa-casillas-2025":"Melissa Casillas, a 53-year-old administrative assistant at Los Alamos National Laboratory, went missing in 2025 around Ranchos de Taos, New Mexico. Jesse Michels tells the case in an episode about American scientists and lab staff who have gone missing. He draws on what people named Sierra Casillas and Mark Casillas have said and on a GoFundMe fundraising page, and he raises it as one more disappearance tied to the country's most sensitive research sites.","disappearance-of-malaysia-airlines-flight-mh370-2014":"On March 8, 2014, Malaysia Airlines flight MH370 took off from Kuala Lumpur with 239 people aboard and vanished. Michels tells this himself, in an episode about American scientists who have gone missing, where he brings the lost plane into that wider story of disappearances.","disappearance-of-monica-reza-near-mount-waterman-2025":"On June 22, 2025, a woman named Monica Reza vanished near Mount Waterman in the San Gabriel Mountains of California's Angeles National Forest, Michels says. He calls her \"the woman who taught us how to tame fire\" and draws on the Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department for the case, on an episode about American scientists who have gone missing. He raises it as part of what he sees as a troubling pattern.","disappearance-of-sharon-the-space-kid-psychic-1970":"A girl named Sharon, one of the \"space kids\" studied by the researcher of psychic powers Andrija Puharich, simply vanished, according to the researcher Greg Malatzi, who passes on what people who knew her told him. They say she was \"the real deal\" as a psychic, and they are convinced she was pulled into a secret project of the kind shown in the TV series Stranger Things, which draws on rumours of a hidden government experiment at Montauk on Long Island. If they are right, a gifted child was quietly taken into secret government work.","disc-sighting-on-a-blue-ridge-mountain-1973":"Steven Greer, who leads a campaign to get UFO secrets made public, says he saw a disc-shaped craft over a Blue Ridge mountain near Boone, North Carolina, in 1973, when he had just turned 18. He tells Michels it showed up in the afternoon on a 'crystal clear October day' and looked like the craft he had seen at eight, though he 'can't say it was the same one.' To him it was a second sighting, ten years after the first.","disclosure-day-trailer-said-7-billion-but-the-film-says-8-bi-2025":"The trailer for Disclosure Day, a film about the government revealing what it knows about UFOs, had a line saying \"7 billion people have the right to know the truth,\" while the film itself says 8 billion, according to Michels. He points this out himself, from watching the trailer, in a conversation that also draws on Bryce Zabel, and treats the change as a telling detail about how the film came together.","discovery-and-translation-of-the-necronomicon-manuscript-1970":"The writer Peter Levenda says that in the 1970s, at the Warlock Shop in Brooklyn Heights, New York, he came across a manuscript and said, \"This looks like it says necronomicon.\" He tells Michels this as someone who was there and later translated it, and he names Herman Slater as part of the story. If his account holds, it would mean a book of that name was found as a real old manuscript and not simply written from scratch.","discovery-of-dmt-s-effects-by-stephen-szara-who-injected-him-1956":"The mind-altering effects of DMT, a powerful psychedelic compound, were first found in 1956 by a Hungarian physician named Stephen Szára, who is said to have injected himself and then his colleagues at a hospital in communist Hungary. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore tells Michels this as background history, without naming a source. It matters to him because DMT is the doorway to the strange realm he discusses on the show.","discovery-of-the-immaculate-constellation-briefing-on-a-pent-2018":"In 2018, says Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official, he came across a briefing on a Pentagon classified system for a programme called Immaculate Constellation, described to him as an operational special access program (a tightly restricted secret programme) that tracked UAP, the government's term for UFOs, around the world \"using tasked and untasked\" sensors. An unsigned report on the programme was delivered to Congress by Michael Shellenberger and entered by Rep. Nancy Mace into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024; it calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a programme by that name. The report says its author found the material \"while pursuing their lawful duties\" and gives the mission as \"collecting imagery intelligence on Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) and ARV/RV (Reproduction Vehicles)\" in \"a global surveillance and reconnaissance mission\", which matches Brown's account, though since he says he wrote it as a summary of the briefing, the report and his telling are one source told twice. If true, the Pentagon has run a secret worldwide watch on these objects, including vehicles built by copying them.","discovery-of-the-martian-ocean-shoreline-brandenburg-ocean-1980":"Mars once had an ocean, says the physicist John Brandenburg, and in the 1980s he and colleagues found its shoreline near a region called Cydonia Mensa. He tells Michels this as his own discovery and credits a 1986 conference paper of his on Mars's ancient ocean; if right, it would mean Mars was once wet enough to host life.","disney-accelerator-brain-controlled-rock-rocky-2020":"Eric Haseltine, a former NSA official, says that around 2020, working with young companies in a Disney lab, he and others \"invented a rock that you could move with your mind,\" nicknamed Rocky. He tells Michels this from his own part in it, as an example of how far technology that reads the brain has already come.","disney-laser-plasma-electronic-fireworks":"Eric Haseltine, a former NSA official who earlier worked at Disney's design arm, Walt Disney Imagineering, says that while there he helped develop electronic fireworks. He tells Michels they focused a powerful \"Q switch neodymium YAG laser\" on a spot in the air and put in so much energy that they \"ionized the air and created ball lightning,\" a glowing ball made on demand, which bears on how some lights in the sky might be produced.","disney-s-ward-kimball-expects-government-ufo-footage-for-a-d-1950":"In the 1950s, a man from Walt Disney's studio named Ward Kimball believed Disney was going to make a UFO documentary using real footage handed over by the government. A guest named Bryce Zabel tells Michels this while tracing Hollywood's links to the CIA and UFOs, summarising it without naming a source. If true, it would mean the government had at least considered releasing UFO film to the public through a studio.","dispute-over-debunk-of-a-uap-video-booster-vs-instrumentatio-2020":"Michels says an attempt to explain away a piece of footage known as Bluegill Triple Prime, in which an object seems to tumble out of a fireball, has been disputed: one side says the object is a rocket booster, the other an instrumentation pod. Michels says he heard from Jeffrey Krookshank, an analyst he is in touch with who has studied the material closely, that a man named Dean Johnson, involved in the debate, is \"actually kind of really scared\", which Michels takes as a sign the plain explanation may not hold.","diver-calls-it-the-most-important-dive-of-his-life-2012":"A diver named Stefan Hogeborn says his 2012 dive to the Baltic Sea anomaly, a strange shape on the floor of the Baltic that the episode calls a UFO on the seafloor, was \"probably the most important dive I've ever done in my life.\" Hogeborn, who made the dive himself, says on the show that he has done about 6,000 dives in all.","diver-finds-a-breathing-organic-object-off-hawaii-david-nobl-1960":"Sometime in the 1960s, a diver is said to have gone down on a strange object in the waters off Hawaii, an object later described as organic and seeming to breathe. Michels passes it on second-hand from a UFO witness named David Noble White Crow, whom he describes as Indian. White Crow was stationed somewhere with the man who apparently made the dive, and if the account holds, something alive or partly alive was lying on the seafloor.","diver-sucked-into-a-giant-grouper-s-mouth-1960":"In the 1960s, a diver working at an oil platform in the Gulf of Mexico was sucked backwards into the mouth of a giant grouper, a huge fish that had come up behind him out of curiosity. Joe McMoneagle tells Michels the story as something he heard from the man involved: the fish \"opened its mouth and he got sucked backwards into this fish mouth.\"","djoser-depicted-in-the-saqqara-step-pyramid-labyrinths-while":"In the tunnels under the Step Pyramid at Saqqara in Egypt, the walls show the pharaoh Djoser \"striding forward\" in his well-known pose at a royal festival, while later pyramids are said to have no decoration inside at all. The researcher Luke Caverns tells Michels this without naming a source. He uses the contrast to ask whether the pyramids are what we usually think they are.","dmt-keeps-oxygen-deprived-neurons-alive-via-the-sigma-1-rece":"DMT, a powerful psychedelic compound, may help brain cells survive when they are starved of oxygen, according to the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore. He tells Michels, without citing a particular study, that \"neurons will survive longer in the presence of DMT\" in an oxygen-deprived setting, an effect that the story links to the sigma-1 receptor, a protein on cells that DMT binds to. If true, it would mean the compound protects the brain as well as altering the mind.","dmt-laser-diffraction-shows-the-same-symbols":"People who took the psychedelic drug DMT reportedly all saw the same symbols when a laser was shone a certain way. A guest named Randy Anderson, who says he saw alien technology at a military base, tells Michels this second-hand, as something he heard from a man named Danny Jones: \"everybody was seeing these same symbols.\" If true, it would suggest the visions are not random, and that different people are seeing something that is really there.","dna-and-rna-building-blocks-found-in-meteorites":"A few years ago, scientists from Japan and NASA confirmed that meteorites that fell in Canada, the United States and Australia contain the building blocks of DNA and RNA, Michels says. He calls it \"something extraordinary\" and tells it himself, without naming the study, because it would mean the ingredients of life can arrive from space.","dni-team-encounters-orbs-at-western-us-military-base-2025":"Recently, a team working for the Director of National Intelligence, Tulsi Gabbard, took a helicopter to a military base in the western United States after UAP reports (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) and met what were 'basically glowing orbs'. Eric Burlison, a member of Congress, tells Michels this and points to recent official UAP releases.","dni-tulsi-gabbard-s-platform-pledge-to-expose-uap-informatio-2025":"Tulsi Gabbard, who in 2025 became Director of National Intelligence, the official over all the US spy agencies, has as part of her platform a pledge to expose information on UAP, the government's newer term for UFOs, according to Michels. He says this himself, quoting her as saying \"I want to expose information on UAP UFOs\"; if she follows through, it would mean the country's top intelligence official is pushing for disclosure.","dobrynin-asks-about-the-excitement-over-ufos-1960":"In the 1960s, the Soviet ambassador to Washington, Anatoly Dobrynin, privately asked an American official why everyone was so worked up about UFOs. The official was Harald Malmgren, an adviser to presidents Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Ford, and he tells Michels about it himself as something he was part of. \"I have never talked about this,\" he says. Dobrynin \"did ask at one time, why is everyone so excited about these unidentified flying objects?\" Michels later retells it in another episode in his own words, calling the two men friends and saying that Dobrynin brought up UFOs and \"said he was very interested.\" So the source is Malmgren's own memory, with the host repeating it. If it happened, it would show the Soviets paying attention to American interest in UFOs at the height of the Cold War.","doc-brown-in-back-to-the-future-as-a-nod-to-townsend-brown-1985":"Doc Brown, the inventor in Back to the Future, is based on Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor Michels calls a CIA scientist who built flying craft with capacitors, devices that store electric charge. Michels says this himself on several shows: people called Townsend Brown \"Doc Brown\", the film's character goes by E. M. T. Brown and flies with a \"flux capacitor\" that echoes Brown's capacitor experiments, the film is set in 1985, the year Brown died, and Steven Spielberg was behind it. He adds that Brown's daughter told him directly she is sure the character is based on her father.","doc-edgerton-eg-g-and-filming-the-manhattan-project-1940":"In the 1940s, an MIT engineer named Harold \"Doc\" Edgerton developed a new way of using stroboscopic cameras, which use rapid flashes of light to freeze very fast motion, and Michels says this, along with other new wartime technology, ended up being used in the Manhattan Project. Michels tells it himself, on an episode with a former NASA worker named Greg Rogers, and links it to the wartime science chief Vannevar Bush. He brings it up to show how close Edgerton's work was to the most secret project of the war.","doctors-who-treated-a-soldier-questioned-by-federal-police-1996":"Doctors who treated a soldier in Varginha, Brazil, in 1996 were later questioned by the federal police, says Italo Venturelli, a top surgeon whose hospital he says operated on an alien being. Venturelli passes this on from what he was told, as part of the wider story of the creature many in the town say soldiers captured that year. If true, the authorities treated the soldier's case as something to keep quiet.","document-shows-pandolfi-supplying-gravitational-wave-briefer":"A document about gravitational waves turned up showing that a man named Ron Pandolfi was finding the speakers, the briefers, for the Jasons, an elite group of scientists who advise the US government. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels about it without saying where the document came from or when it is dated. Cameron raises it because it would show Pandolfi quietly arranging high-level scientific briefings on advanced physics.","documentary-project-with-secret-dating-evidence-2024":"Matt LaCroix, who studies ancient sites, says that in 2024 he was leading 'a very significant documentary' with new dating evidence, still kept secret, from sites in Turkey, Peru, Bolivia and Minorca. He tells Michels about it himself and names others in the project, among them Robert Schoch and Robert Edward Grant. If the evidence is what he hints, it would push some of these sites much further back in time.","documented-link-between-the-ufo-phenomenon-and-the-kennedy-a-1947":"Two researchers claim that the killing of President Kennedy in 1963 is tied to the UFO story that runs back to 1947. The writer Peter Levenda tells Michels, \"I think I've shown through documentation the connection with the UFO phenomenon and the Kennedy assassination.\" He points to his book Sinister Forces, and the people he draws in include J. Edgar Hoover. A second teller, the UFO historian Richard Dolan, says on another episode that the UFO subject \"absolutely must be brought into\" the Kennedy case. Dolan credits two other people, Philip Lavell and Dan Sheehan, for that view, not Levenda's documents. Neither man spells out the link in the passages given. They raise it because, if true, a president's murder would be bound up with UFO secrecy.","dod-officials-advise-against-disclosure":"Dave Rossi, a man who says aliens taught him advanced physics, says very respectable high-ranking people in the Department of Defense have advised him against going public. He tells Michels their advice was always the same: \"people will not see what you see.\" In his telling, even insiders thought the public was not ready to accept what he had experienced.","dod-pays-bob-monroe-to-train-him-in-out-of-body-experiences-1980":"In the 1980s, says Joe McMoneagle, who worked for the military on psychic tasks, the Defense Department paid Bob Monroe, author of a book called Journeys Out of the Body, to train him in leaving his body at the Monroe Institute in Virginia. McMoneagle tells Michels from his own experience that \"they actually paid him to train me,\" which would mean the military funded out-of-body training.","doe-and-battelle-institute-as-ufo-secret-keepers":"The real keepers of UFO secrets, by this account, may be the Department of Energy and the Battelle Institute, a private research institute, rather than the agencies people usually look at. A guest named Bob Maguire tells Michels that he kept pointing to the Department of Energy and kept asking why nobody was looking at it or at Battelle. He gives this as his own view and names no source.","doe-check-finds-no-nuclear-tests-on-shutdown-dates-1967":"There were no nuclear tests at the Nevada test site on the dates in 1967 when nuclear missiles were shut down, including March 16 and March 24, according to a check made with the Department of Energy by a man Robert Salas names as James Clattz. Salas, a former Air Force officer who tells Michels a UFO shut down ten nuclear missiles, raises it to rule out a nuclear test as the cause of the shutdowns.","doe-genesis-project":"Michels says the Department of Energy now has something called the Genesis project, which he understands as a way to gather all the information from the national labs and run it through AI. He offers this himself, as his own reading, in an episode about a Secretary of Energy speaking on UFOs, which suggests he sees it as a possible way buried knowledge could surface.","doe-hazing-ritual-fake-alien-and-roswell-sap-briefing-for-ne-2005":"A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says that in the mid-2000s, new political appointees at the Department of Energy were put through a hazing ritual in a vault at its Washington headquarters: they were shown a fake alien and given a mock briefing on a SAP, or special access program, a tightly restricted secret project. He tells Michels the joke was \"to explain to them that we really did have aliens,\" two of them, \"one was injured, one was not,\" and that the alien technology came \"through Roswell,\" the 1947 New Mexico incident at the heart of many UFO stories. He says he saw this himself and tells it twice, once with a different set of years and mentioning the book A State of Mind: Faith and the CIA; if true, it would mean the Roswell story was a running joke inside the agency.","doe-holds-the-nation-s-most-important-secrets-2005":"Some of America's most important secrets are kept by the Department of Energy, not the CIA, according to Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA chief who says he learned them there himself during his time at the Department of Energy between about 2005 and 2008. He tells Michels the department \"does stuff\" beyond what people expect, which would mean the deepest secrets sit somewhere few people think to look.","doe-intelligence-director-never-briefed-on-nuclear-site-uap-2005":"Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer who ran intelligence at the Department of Energy from about 2005 to 2008, says nobody ever told him about UAP (the government's term for UFOs) showing up at nuclear sites. He tells Michels 'completely openly and transparently' that while he was there as director of intelligence, he 'never was briefed on any of that.' That matters because nuclear sites are part of what the Department of Energy is responsible for.","doe-nest-as-the-hazmat-crash-retrieval-team-at-the-weygandt-1997":"The \"DOE\" people said to have cleaned up a crashed craft in Peru in 1997, in the account of a man named Jonathan Weygandt, were most likely the Department of Energy's Nuclear Emergency Support Team, a hazardous-materials squad known as NEST. That is the reading of a researcher who goes by UAPGerb, who tells Michels this on two occasions, once pointing to the Atomic Energy Act passed by Congress on August 30, 1954, and once to the Wilson-Davis memo, said to be the physicist Eric Davis's notes of a meeting with Admiral Thomas Wilson, while also linking the contractor EG&G. If true, a known nuclear emergency team would double as a crash retrieval unit.","doe-says-it-cannot-locate-kettle-2-footage-2023":"In 2023 the Department of Energy answered a request for footage known as Kettle 2 by saying it was \"unable to locate the footage\". Michels tells this himself, citing a mandatory declassification review request (a way to ask an agency to review secret records for release) filed on October 21, 2023 by a man named Jeff Cruickshank. He ties it to the same months as the first public claims of hidden UFO programmes, and to whether the government is holding back recordings.","doe-srt-and-nnsa-office-of-secure-transportation-move-recove":"A Department of Energy security unit known by the initials SRT, and the Office of Secure Transportation, part of the agency that handles US nuclear weapons, may be the ones collecting crashed UFO material and moving it between US national laboratories. The researcher who goes by UAPGerb says on the show that he thinks these two are \"highly likely involved in UFO retrieval operations\". He calls it his own conclusion and names no source. If he is right, the government's handling of such material would run through its nuclear security system.","doe-using-the-alien-motif-as-cover":"The Department of Energy may be using the idea of aliens to throw people off what is really going on, something to do with high-energy physics, Michels suggests. It is his own hunch, offered without a source in an episode about a Secretary of Energy speaking on UFOs, and if true it would mean the alien story is partly a cover for secret physics.","dog-whistle-draws-uaps-daily":"There is a signal or device called the \"dog whistle\", and when it is switched on, UFOs show up day after day. That is what a man named Jake Barber, who says he has seen it work, tells Michels: \"After we turn on the dog whistle, UAPs literally come to us day after day.\" UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. A second witness, James Valor, says nearly the same words on an episode about Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs. Valor credits the claim to someone he calls Mr. K and dates the work to 2021 to 2025. If it were true, it would mean these objects can be called in on purpose instead of just waited for.","dogs-that-know-when-their-owners-are-coming-home-and-animal":"The scientist Rupert Sheldrake says some animals have abilities \"better than humans\", such as dogs that seem to know when their owners are heading home, and animals that seem to sense earthquakes before they hit. He tells Michels this was the subject of his own book, Dogs That Know When Their Owners Are Coming Home. If he is right, animals pick up on things that ordinary senses cannot explain.","doj-trial-under-military-order-number-one-2002":"Gary McKinnon, a British hacker who says he broke into NASA and US military computers and found signs of a secret space programme, says the US Department of Justice told him he would be tried under Military Order Number One, \"which is Guantanamo status\". McKinnon tells Michels this from his own case in the 2000s, as a measure of how seriously the US government took what he had seen.","dolan-finds-timothy-good-s-above-top-secret-in-a-syracuse-bo-1994":"In 1994, in \"just like an ordinary bookstore\" in Syracuse, New York, the historian Richard Dolan came across Above Top Secret, which he calls a classic work by the British UFO writer Timothy Good. Dolan tells Michels this himself, as the chance find that drew him into the subject.","dolan-s-early-appearances-and-stanton-friedman-pressing-him-2001":"The writer Richard Dolan says that in 2001, at his early public appearances in Laughlin, Nevada, and St. Louis, he found himself alone with Stanton Friedman, an established figure in UFO research, who was sizing him up. Dolan tells Michels that he had just written \"a big fat book\" and that Friedman's test question was, \"So, what's your opinion on Bob Lazar?\", meaning a man whose claims divide the UFO world. Dolan tells it to show how a newcomer had to prove himself to the old guard.","dolan-s-uso-database-shows-em-interference-patterns-1825":"About one in ten reports of unidentified submerged objects, or USOs (strange craft seen going into or coming out of the sea), involve electromagnetic interference, meaning disturbed electrical or radio equipment. So says the UFO historian Richard Dolan, who tells Michels he noticed this pattern while building a database of ocean cases going back to 1825. If the pattern is real, it would suggest these sightings share a physical signature and are not just random misperceptions.","dolmens-tied-to-a-cult-of-giants":"Dolmens are ancient monuments made of large upright stones topped by a flat slab. According to a guest named Tim Alberino, the ones on Sardinia, Minorca and elsewhere are tied to a cult of giants, and not only because the stones are huge. Alberino tells Michels this as his own belief, without naming a source, in an episode about giants.","don-carter-s-kirtland-interdisciplinary-groups-with-kissinge-1962":"In 1962, a man named Don Carter, head of advanced research at Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, was bringing together interdisciplinary groups of experts, groups that Michels links to Henry Kissinger. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with the writer Greg Bishop, as part of a picture of quiet government thinking about the UFO question in the Kennedy years.","don-paul-thanks-greer-for-those-stuck-in-legacy-programs":"Steven Greer, who argues that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, says a man named Don Paul thanked him on behalf of people stuck working in legacy programs, the long-running secret projects said to hold UFO material. Greer tells Michels that Paul had once picked him up in Los Angeles and dropped him off at the Airbnb where he was staying.","don-phillips-humans-have-the-fastest-computer":"A man named Don Phillips, who speaks from his time at Lockheed's Skunk Works, the company's secret aircraft design shop, says that human beings carry inside them a faster computer \"than any man-made computer can ever approach.\" He says this to Michels as his own view of what the human mind can do.","donald-barr-dalton-school-and-the-novel-space-relations-1970":"Jesse Michels points to what he calls the weirdest link between Jeffrey Epstein and UFOs: Donald Barr, a former officer of the OSS, America's wartime spy agency, was headmaster of the Dalton School in New York around the time Epstein was hired to teach there. Barr also wrote a science fiction novel called Space Relations, which Michels describes as a story of alien sex slavery. Michels raises this himself and ties it to William Barr and to Epstein, suggesting a strange thread between intelligence work, aliens and Epstein.","donald-hornig-listed-as-present-at-kecksburg-1965":"Kevin Knuth, who appears on the show as a NASA whistleblower, says he found a government summary of the Kecksburg accident, an incident with a reported UFO in Kecksburg, Pennsylvania, in 1965, and that a man named Donald Hornig \"was listed as being present.\" Knuth tells it as his own find and links it to Lily Hornig; if the listing is accurate, it would put a named official figure at the scene of the event.","donald-menzel-caught-destroying-plates-per-dorrit-hoffleit-1950":"A leading Harvard astronomer, Donald Menzel, is said to have been caught destroying the observatory's old photographic plates, the glass negatives on which astronomers recorded the night sky. The witness, as the story goes, was Dorrit Hoffleit, a Harvard astronomer whom Michels calls Menzel's assistant and \"an astronomer in her own right.\" She is said to have written about it in her memoirs. Michels brings it up on several episodes, calling Hoffleit \"somebody who's very reputable.\" The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, who searches old sky images for unexplained objects, tells it in more detail. She says Menzel had helped the Air Force explain away the 1952 Washington flap, a wave of UFO sightings over the capital that summer. Two or three months later, she says, \"he suddenly becomes the director of Harvard Observatory, and he destroys 1/3 of the photographic plates.\" Neither teller saw it, and both rely on Hoffleit's account as they have it. If true, it would mean part of the historical record of the sky, which might have shown strange objects, was lost on purpose.","donald-trump-controls-resorts-international-1973":"Donald Trump was, for a year or so, basically the man in charge of a company called Resorts International, with ties to the US and the Bahamas. A guest named Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source, as one link in a chain he draws between the UFO cover-up and a secret society.","donald-trump-jr-interviews-ross-coulthart-2025":"In 2025, Donald Trump Jr. interviewed Ross Coulthart, a journalist who reports on UFOs, Michels says. In one episode Michels says this happened \"on the eve\" of Coulthart breaking the story of Jake Barber, a man who says he worked in a secret programme recovering crashed craft. Michels tells it himself, and raises it because it would mean the subject has reached the President's own family.","donna-hare-ufos-airbrushed-out-of-images-in-building-8-1970":"Donna Hare, who worked at NASA's Johnson Space Center in Houston in the 1970s, says photos kept in a building there called Building 8 were retouched to remove UFOs. She tells the story herself on the show: she pointed at something in a photo and asked a man 'Is this a UFO?', and he smiled and said 'I can't tell you that.' Michels adds that she came forward through the Disclosure Project, Steven Greer's effort to gather government witnesses, and says her story is why a man named Gary McKinnon later went after that building's records. A researcher called UAPGerb links her account to the project's 2001 briefing.","donut-craft-his-father-showed-him":"Dave Rossi says his father once called him outside at their old house to see a craft that was \"your typical, like a donut shaped craft.\" Rossi tells Michels this was one of three times something like it appeared there, a sighting he describes as his own.","donut-shaped-ufos-above-rossi-s-old-house":"Dave Rossi, a man who says beings taught him advanced physics, tells Michels that donut-shaped UFOs used to hover over his old house: \"there were a few that would be above it.\" He brings his father into the story, without a date or details of what either of them saw.","doolittle-sent-to-investigate-swedish-ghost-rockets-1946":"Michels says that in 1946 Eisenhower sent General James Doolittle to investigate the Swedish \"ghost rockets\", unexplained rocket-like objects reported in the skies over Sweden and Finland. He states it himself as something \"we do know for a fact\", without naming a source, as evidence that the US military took unexplained objects seriously even before the UFO era began.","door-to-door-survey-of-1-600-swedes":"About one Swede in ten has seen something unexplained in the sky, according to a door-to-door survey that the Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn says he helped carry out. Svahn tells Michels that they knocked on doors and talked to 1,600 Swedes, and \"10% of them had seen something.\"","doty-admits-befriending-benowitz-and-misleading-him-1980":"A man named Rick Doty later admitted that he had befriended Paul Bennewitz, an Albuquerque man who in the early 1980s believed he was picking up alien messages, and that he \"was tasked to do that,\" Michels says. Michels tells it himself, without a source, as the turn in Bennewitz's story, since it would mean his alien beliefs were fed to him on purpose.","doty-and-lear-spread-quackery-around-the-majestic-12-era-1980":"In the 1980s, Michels says, the story of the MJ-12 documents (papers that surfaced then describing a secret group set up under Truman to deal with UFOs) was pushed along with \"all sorts of quackery.\" He says counterintelligence people like Richard Doty, an agent of the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, were saying there were six alien beings, and that a man named John Lear claimed there was \"a soul catcher on the moon.\" Michels tells this in his own framing, as part of how he thinks the real questions got buried under wild claims.","doty-s-alien-crystal-that-showed-the-crucifixion":"A former military man named Richard Doty is said to have seen an alien crystal that showed the crucifixion of Jesus. He calls it the most amazing thing he ever saw in the military. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron passes the story on to Michels: \"Richard Doty tells the story. The most amazing thing he ever saw in the military. It was a crystal.\"","doty-s-claim-he-was-an-area-51-guard":"A man named Rick Doty has said that he once worked as a guard at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada. The writer Greg Bishop passes on the claim to Michels, adding at once: \"I have no idea.\" Bishop leaves it there and neither backs the claim nor rejects it, though if true it would put Doty inside one of the most guarded places in the country.","doty-s-disinformation-to-moulton-howe-and-bennewitz-1979":"Michels says that documents about the origins of humanity which Bob Lazar, a man who claims he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site, says he was shown are strikingly similar to papers that a man named Rick Doty showed the journalist Linda Moulton Howe at Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico. In one telling Michels adds that Doty gave the same material to Howe and to a businessman named Paul Bennewitz in 1979, and that Lazar admits he may have been forced to drink a liquid before being briefed. Michels draws the parallel himself, on two episodes, to suggest that Lazar may have been fed the same disinformation as the others.","doty-s-rival-version-1964-at-white-sands":"A craft landing at a New Mexico military base was filmed, but the people who tell the story disagree on when, says the researcher Grant Cameron. Cameron tells Michels that the filmmaker Bob Emenegger insisted it was May 1971, at Holloman Air Force Base. Then Richard Doty, a former Air Force counterintelligence agent, said no, it happened in 1964 at White Sands, and that \"they got lost\".","doty-shows-linda-moulton-howe-documents-including-project-ga-1982":"In 1982 a man named Rick Doty is said to have contacted the UFO researcher Linda Moulton Howe and shown her papers, among them one about a programme called Project Garnet. Michels tells the outline himself on an episode with Greg Bishop, an author who has written about Doty. Bishop adds what he heard secondhand from Tom Adams, a researcher of cattle mutilations, meaning cases where farm animals were found mysteriously cut up. Adams told Bishop that Howe came to visit him a couple of days after the meeting and was \"over the moon\" about what she had seen. If the papers were real, it would mean someone inside government was handing secret UFO documents to a researcher.","doubting-four-funds-including-renaissance-and-d-e-shaw-2000":"Eric Weinstein, a researcher and mathematician, says that in the early 2000s he did not believe the results of four investment funds: Bernie Madoff's, Renaissance Technologies, D.E. Shaw and Jeffrey Epstein's. He tells Michels this himself, as a doubt he held at the time.","doug-alt-the-portal-is-real":"A man named Doug Alt is said to have told the researcher Grant Cameron that a portal Cameron had asked him about is real. Cameron tells Michels that Alt came back to him and said, \"Remember that question you asked me a couple years ago?\" and then simply, \"It's real.\" Cameron gives it in the context of his dealings with a man named Ron Pandolfi, and offers it as a rare straight confirmation from someone he believes was in a position to know.","doug-and-bill-enter-the-craft-1948":"In 1948, in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, two men named Doug Noland and Bill Ferguson are said to have climbed inside a crashed saucer they had found. Michels retells this from Noland's own account (\"According to Doug, he and Bill enter the craft\") on an episode about a crash said to have left 16 alien bodies. If true, it would mean ordinary people got inside a crashed craft before anyone came to take it away.","doug-noland-and-bill-ferguson-find-the-saucer-1948":"In 1948, in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, two men named Doug Noland and Bill Ferguson are said to have climbed to the rim of a mesa and found a flying saucer about 100 feet across. Michels tells it himself as a narrated story in an episode about a supposed crash that left alien bodies, and it opens a longer account in which the men go on to look inside the craft.","doug-sees-beings-through-the-punctured-porthole-1948":"In 1948, in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, a man named Doug Noland, who had found a downed saucer with a man named Bill Ferguson, put his eye to a punctured porthole and saw two small figures slumped over a panel inside. Michels tells this himself, adding that Noland was specific about that hole for the rest of his life, and it would mean the craft carried beings.","doug-wolf-first-oga-director-with-nro-career-2003":"In 2003 a man named Doug Wolf became the first head of an office known by the initials OGA who had built his career in the National Reconnaissance Office, the agency that runs US spy satellites. The researcher who goes by UAPGerb points out that Wolf \"spent 16 years in the NRO\", naming no source. He uses it to show how he thinks the Pentagon's hidden UFO programme is staffed and run.","dousing-remote-viewing-target-sites-with-liquid-nitrogen-gav-1970":"In the US government's remote viewing work between about 1970 and 1995 (remote viewing being the claimed ability to describe a distant place using the mind alone), the people running the trials would pour liquid nitrogen over the target sites and get more accurate results when they did. The researcher Chris Ramsay tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would hint that whatever remote viewers pick up on can be affected by physical conditions at the target.","dr-brett-cohen-agrees-the-great-pyramid-made-sulfuric-acid-2020":"A scientist named Dr. Brett Cohen is said to agree that the Great Pyramid of Giza once worked as a chemical plant making sulfuric acid. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher who argues the pyramid was built to produce chemicals, tells Michels this, and adds that Cohen pictures \"completely different mechanisms of operation and stages of a reaction.\" If true, it would mean someone else with a scientific background backs Drumm's idea that the pyramid was a machine and not a tomb.","dr-bug-we-can-t-take-your-soul":"John Blitch, a scientist who worked with DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, says he was taken by an alien being he calls \"Dr Bug,\" and that it told him, \"we can't take your soul.\" Blitch tells Michels this as something he lived through, and if true it would mean such beings admit a limit to what they can do to people.","dr-carvalho-s-35-colares-patients-interviewed-by-jacques-val-1977":"In 1977, in the Brazilian town of Colares, a local doctor named Carvalho is said to have treated 35 patients who all had the same sunburns and puncture wounds, and two women among them died of strokes. Michels tells this from Forbidden Science, the published journals of the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, who went to Colares and spoke with the doctor. If true, it would mean that something in the sky was physically harming people there.","dr-fernando-jane-saw-the-creature-s-foot-at-the-regional-hos-1996":"In 1996, people in the Brazilian town of Varginha reported that a strange living creature had been captured. A doctor named Fernando Jane is said to have walked through the regional hospital there and seen the creature's foot, and only the foot, because so many military men were standing around it. A witness named Jose Marcus Mora tells Michels this as what Jane told him, and if true it would place the creature, under military guard, inside an ordinary town hospital.","dr-g-as-a-consortium-of-anti-submarine-scientists-identified-1948":"The mysterious \"Dr. G\" in Behind the Flying Saucers, a 1950 book by the journalist Frank Scully about a saucer said to have crashed near Aztec, New Mexico, was probably not one man but a cover name for a group of scientists who worked against submarines in the war. That is the view of a researcher who posts as UAPGerb, and he gives all the credit to the researchers Scott and Suzanne Ramsey. He says they dug up a short list of eight or nine scientists, a circle that touches the wartime science chief Vannevar Bush, who were likely being represented by the one pseudonym.","dr-italo-venturelli-meets-live-being-in-the-icu-1996":"In 1996, at the regional hospital in Varginha, Brazil, the neurosurgeon Italo Venturelli says he came face to face with a living being \"totally different from a human being,\" with a white face and red eyes, after his colleague Marcos Vinícius had stitched it up. He tells Michels it showed no suffering, looked at him \"with a very beautiful look,\" and seemed calm and \"highly intelligent, at least more than I was,\" and Michels, retelling it on another episode, says the encounter lasted four minutes, which if true would put a living non-human being in a Brazilian hospital.","dr-iya-whitley-s-work-on-helping-infants-keep-their-synesthe":"A space psychologist named Iya Whitley is trying to train babies to keep their synesthesia, the blending of senses, such as seeing sounds as colours, that some people have early in life. Michels describes her work himself on two episodes, and on one of them, with a guest who says the NSA hired him to talk to aliens, he adds that the purpose is to help humans communicate with aliens.","dr-iya-whitley-says-astronauts-see-things-in-space":"Astronauts all see strange things in space, according to a doctor named Iya Whitley. Michels relays it on the show as what she told or said, putting it simply: \"she says they all see stuff\" up there. If true, it would mean unexplained sightings are a common but unspoken part of spaceflight.","dr-jannini-s-lab-report-death-from-pulmonary-infection-and-s-1996":"A creature said to have been captured in Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, and which some say was an alien, is said to have died of a lung infection that spread through its whole body. A surgeon named Ahmed Foratu, who tells Michels he was a witness to the case, says a doctor named Jannini wrote a lab report stating that the creature 'died from a pulmonary infection that caused septicemia, which is a generalized infection.' If the report exists as he describes it, it would mean a doctor put a cause of death for the creature on paper.","dr-marcos-vinicius-neves-operates-on-the-being-1996":"In 1996, at the regional hospital in Varginha, Brazil, where witnesses say a strange creature was captured and taken, a doctor named Marcos Vinicius Neves is said to have operated on the being. The documentary maker James Fox tells Michels what he was told by people including another doctor, Italo Venturelli: the creature had swelling from a head injury, and Neves relieved the pressure. If true, doctors in an ordinary hospital would have treated a living non-human being.","dr-vinod-falls-into-a-trance-and-first-channels-the-nine-1952":"In 1952 an Indian visitor known as Dr. Vinod is said to have arrived at the Round Table Foundation, a research group in Maine, and on his very first night there fallen into a trance without warning and begun speaking for the Nine, a group of beings that the episode calls an alien council. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this as \"the story goes,\" pointing to tape recordings made by the Round Table and transcripts of the Nine sessions. Elsewhere on the show he speaks more generally of Vinod's early channeling all dating from 1952, citing the journals of Andrija Puharich, a man connected to the Round Table, without singling out the first night.","draft-notice-after-leaving-the-church-1967":"Peter Levenda, an author described on the show as a CIA historian, says that in the late 1960s in New York he got a draft notice from the Selective Service as soon as he left the church. He tells Michels he left because he didn't 'feel that secure' there, and the notice arrived right after.","draft-uk-us-extradition-treaty-written-in-american-english-2003":"A British computer hacker named Gary McKinnon, whom the United States sought to bring over for trial, says the draft extradition treaty between Britain and America, from 2003, was written in American English, suggesting it was drafted on the American side. He tells Michels this from his own fight against extradition, crediting his lawyers and documents from the General Accounting Office, the auditing arm of Congress.","dragon-feels-his-late-father-s-presence-on-the-ranch-2016":"A man known as Bryant \"Dragon\" Arnold, who has spent time at Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah famous for reports of strange events, says he has felt his late father's presence there. The ranch's owner, Brandon Fugal, passes this on to Michels: \"he's had experiences on the property where he's felt his father's presence.\" If true, it would add contact with the dead to the long list of odd things people report at the ranch.","dragon-wagon-used-to-haul-the-craft-away-1948":"At the 1948 saucer crash said to have happened in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, a \"dragon wagon\", a heavy military hauling truck, is said to have backed up a freshly cut road to carry the craft away. Michels narrates the scene himself and draws on the writer Frank Scully and his book Behind the Flying Saucers. If it happened, the military moved quickly and on a large scale to remove a whole craft from the site.","dream-at-dionysiou-1800s-paris-brothel-the-virgin-mary-and-h":"Ralph Larson, a guest who calls himself a time traveler, says that while staying at the Dionysiou monastery on Mount Athos in Greece he dreamed he was walking down old Paris streets and into a brothel, where he met \"what turned out to be the Virgin Mary.\" He tells Michels this as his own experience, saying he went in just \"to see what's going on,\" and he links it to Harry Haller, the main character of the novel Steppenwolf. For Larson, the dream reads as a real meeting across time and not just an ordinary dream.","dream-of-infiltrating-the-order-of-the-broken-cross-apocalyp":"A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says he had a dream about getting inside the Order of the Broken Cross, which he calls an apocalyptic cult in Jerusalem. He describes the dream himself on the show, as something he lived through. It comes up in a conversation about whether dreams can show things that are real or that have not happened yet.","dream-of-jesse-matching-the-nas-tweet-reply":"Dylan Borland says that at his home on the East Coast he dreamed that Michels came to his house to get provolone cheese, and that the dream matched a reply to a tweet by the rapper Nas. He tells Michels this as a strange coincidence he experienced himself, the kind he takes as a sign that dreams can foresee real events.","dream-of-sadat-s-assassination-as-it-happened-1981":"A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says the first of his unusual dreams showed him the assassination of Egypt's president Anwar Sadat in 1981 while it was happening. He was based at Fort Bliss, Texas, at the time, but he tells Michels that in the dream \"I was there,\" and he dates it \"September 6, 1981.\" He writes about it in his book A State of Mind: Faith and the CIA, and if it is true, it would mean a dream showed him a real event on the other side of the world as it took place.","dream-visit-from-a-great-uncle-at-the-moment-of-his-death-1972":"In 1972 in Berkeley, California, Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a doctorate who studies psychics and the afterlife, says his great-uncle appeared to him in a dream. He tells Michels he calls it \"a visitation\" and later learned it happened at the moment of his great-uncle's death, which, if so, would suggest a mind can reach out at death.","drew-s-privately-funded-vacuum-chambers":"A man named Andrew Arjima, called Drew, is said to keep a vacuum chamber, a sealed tank with the air pumped out for testing equipment, at his own house, paid for privately. Charles Buhler, a guest the episode calls a NASA chief, says this to Michels from his own knowledge of Arjima's setup: \"He's got one at his house.\"","driving-past-bennewitz-s-thunder-corporation-on-the-way-to-s-1980":"John Brandenburg, a scientist who worked at Sandia, a national laboratory in Albuquerque, New Mexico, says that in the 1980s he drove past the office of Paul Bennewitz's company, Thunder Corporation, every morning on his way to work. He tells Michels this himself, as a detail of where he was while the Bennewitz story was going on.","driving-past-epstein-s-victoria-s-secret-ranch-in-new-mexico-1990":"Bob Lazar, a man whose claims divide the UFO world, says that when he lived in New Mexico he used to drive by Jeffrey Epstein's Zorro Ranch, which he calls the \"Victoria's Secret Ranch,\" all the time. He tells Michels he lived really close to it and calls it \"really strange,\" passing it on as an odd thing he saw himself.","drone-flights-over-nevada-nuclear-testing-range-2024":"In 2024, several unidentified drones are said to have flown over the American nuclear testing range in Nevada, just north of Las Vegas. Michels brings it up himself, without naming a source, in an episode about UFOs and nuclear bases. If true, it would be one more case of unknown craft showing interest in nuclear sites.","drone-only-tfr-set-up-over-picatinny-arsenal-before-thanksgi-2024":"Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he retrieved a UFO with his helicopter, says that in 2024 a temporary flight restriction, an official ban on flying in a stretch of airspace, was set up for drones over the Picatinny Arsenal, a military site in New Jersey. He tells Michels it went up a month earlier, \"before Thanksgiving,\" which, if so, would mean the airspace was being closed ahead of whatever followed.","drone-survey-shows-rust-like-lines-and-cracks-on-the-object-2025":"In 2025 an underwater drone was sent down to the Baltic Sea anomaly, a strange object on the floor of the Baltic Sea that some call a UFO, and its pictures showed odd lines, cracks and something that looked like rusting metal. The treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, who was part of the survey, tells Michels: \"We saw some weird lines. Yeah. Some cracks... something I would say it was like rust. Like metal.\" If he is right, the object would be something made rather than natural rock.","drum-s-visit-to-the-giza-plateau-to-look-at-the-area-around-2020":"Jeffrey Drum, a researcher who argues the Great Pyramid was built to produce energy, says he went to the Giza Plateau in Egypt to look at the ground around the Osiris Shaft, a deep shaft cut beneath the plateau. He tells Michels he went 'as soon as we got back' from a conference in Malta, credits someone named Alexa, and says more shafts were found near the causeway. To him that would back up his idea of an energy system under the pyramids.","drumm-puts-his-head-in-the-magnetic-hole-at-oami-gaishi-ston-2020":"At the Oami Gaishi stone circle in Japan, a huge block of andesite, a volcanic rock, has a hole shaped exactly like a human head, and the stone gives off a strong magnetic field. Geoffrey Drumm, a guest who talks with Michels about what the pyramids were for, says he went there sometime in the 2020s and put his own head into that hole. He tells it as a place where ancient builders may have worked deliberately with magnetism.","drumm-s-evaluation-of-the-biondi-team-s-papers-2020":"When a researcher named Filippo Biondi and his team put out their first paper on what lies inside and beneath the Giza pyramids, Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher of the pyramids, judged it about a 50/50 proposition. Drumm tells Michels this about his own review in 2020: \"my original stance... was about 50/50.\" He sets it beside a second paper by the team on the pyramid of Khafre, which suggests his view changed after that.","dsp-fast-walkers":"\"Fast walkers\" are fast-moving unknown objects said to have been picked up in Earth orbit by DSP, a US military satellite system built to spot missile launches from space. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani brings them up only in passing on the show, as a likely example of unexplained things seen from space, and does not say more about them or where he heard of them.","dsp-satellite-infrared-anomaly-during-the-tehran-ufo-dogfigh-1976":"During the 1976 night when jets of the Shah's Iranian air force reportedly chased an unknown object over Tehran, a US early-warning satellite of the Defense Support Program, which watches for heat from missile launches, is said to have recorded an infrared anomaly. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels that the aerospace researchers Lee Graham and Ron Regehr claimed to have examined the satellite's printouts for the exact day and time of the chase, which, if so, would back the pilots' story with machine data.","dtra-held-aec-records-saying-ufos-are-extraterrestrial":"Old records of the Atomic Energy Commission, the early US agency for nuclear weapons and power, concluded that UFOs are vehicles from outer space, according to a man named Dylan Borland. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels that Borland said on another show, Weaponized, that the records were held by the Defense Threat Reduction Agency, a Pentagon agency that deals with weapons of mass destruction. At another point UAPGerb leaves that agency out and suggests the records may be tied to crash records of the military's early nuclear weapons project and to the Sandia nuclear weapons laboratory.","dtra-official-collecting-documents-on-extraterrestrials":"An unnamed speaker on the show says that the official in charge of gathering everything the Defense Threat Reduction Agency, a Pentagon agency, held on extraterrestrials, or \"little green men\" as the official put it, once sat down at the speaker's own computer and began pulling documents off it. The speaker says this as someone who was there, and adds that the official pulled files from every system in their SCIF, a sealed room for classified work. If true, it would mean someone inside a defense agency was quietly collecting its alien-related records.","duell-s-diary-records-meeting-the-president-on-the-mj-12-bri-1950":"The diary of Allen Dulles, who ran the CIA in the 1950s and was released with the JFK files in 2022, has the words \"meeting with the president\" on the very day that the MJ-12 papers say Eisenhower was briefed. The MJ-12 papers are documents of disputed origin that describe a secret committee of officials handling UFOs. Michels brings this up himself, and on another episode, crediting the UFO researcher Richard Dolan, he goes further: the papers somehow knew about meetings and Dulles diary entries that no other source did. If so, whoever wrote the papers had inside knowledge of the president's calendar. A diary line about meeting the president says nothing about what the meeting was about, so it fits the papers' date without showing what was discussed.","dugway-chief-scientist-reveals-a-huge-subterranean-facility":"A huge underground facility lies beneath Dugway Proving Ground, an Army testing range in Utah, and is entered through a shed west of Granite Peak, according to a researcher who posts as UAPGerb. He says the base's chief scientist told this to a person he identifies only as MS. If true, it would put a large hidden site under a known military range.","dugway-grey-beard-we-had-the-triangles-out-again-last-night-2019":"At Dugway Proving Ground, a military test range in Utah, an old hand, a \"grey beard\", would remark casually, \"Oh, we had the triangles out again last night,\" sometime between 2019 and 2022. The account comes from a guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb (UAP being the official term for unidentified flying objects), who tells Michels he heard this said directly. It is told because it would suggest triangle-shaped craft were being flown at the base often enough to be routine.","duke-researchers-brain-implants-let-paralyzed-people-control":"Researchers at Duke University have put electrodes into the brains of paralyzed people, letting them control robot arms and even walk. Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA, mentions this work to Michels and names someone he calls \"Dano\" and \"this guy at Duke\" who implant the electrodes. It comes up as an example of how close machines and minds have already been joined.","dulce-base-shootout-in-lazar-s-briefing-documents":"An underground base at Dulce, New Mexico, is said to have been the scene of a shootout in which 64 special forces soldiers were killed by grays, the big-eyed, gray-skinned beings of many alien accounts, because the soldiers had brought weapons they were not allowed to carry. The researcher Grant Cameron says this story was in the briefing papers that Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S-4, a secret site near Area 51 in Nevada, says he was given to read there. Cameron sums it up without naming any further source, and if true it would mean a deadly clash between humans and aliens took place on American soil.","dulles-brothers-draft-the-versailles-war-reparations-1918":"The brothers Allen Dulles, later head of the CIA, and John Foster Dulles, later Secretary of State, wrote the war reparations terms into the Treaty of Versailles, signed in 1919, according to the lawyer Dan Sheehan. Sheehan tells Michels, without naming a source, that they did it as young lawyers at Sullivan and Cromwell, a New York law firm, with family ties to two former Secretaries of State, their uncle Robert Lansing and grandfather John W. Foster. He tells it as an early sign of how far the brothers' reach went.","dulles-greenlights-the-s-force-against-jfk-1963":"In 1963 Allen Dulles, who had headed the CIA, simply \"greenlighted the S force\", a group a guest named Dan Sheehan says was turned against President Kennedy. Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, bringing in Dulles's CIA colleague Richard Bissell as well, as part of his case that Kennedy was killed because he knew too much, including about UFOs.","dulles-keeps-holding-court-in-his-georgetown-brownstone-1961":"After leaving the CIA in the early 1960s, its former director Allen Dulles stayed in Washington and kept running \"his whole setup\" from his brownstone house in Georgetown. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, while talking about UFOs and President Kennedy. The point is that Dulles may have kept his power after losing his post.","dulles-reactivates-howard-hughes-s-s-force-against-jfk-sheeh-1960":"In the 1960s, when John F. Kennedy threatened to scatter the CIA and fire its director Allen Dulles, Dulles is said to have worked from the private banking firm Brown Brothers Harriman to bring back the \"S Force\", a secret group the businessman Howard Hughes had created, and turn it against Kennedy. Michels tells it twice, each time as the account of a man named Dan Sheehan. In the second telling he adds that the S Force began earlier, with Richard Nixon, then Eisenhower's vice president and head of the 5412 committee, a body overseeing covert operations, working with Hughes, and he ties it to Cuba between 1959 and 1963.","dutch-museum-moon-rock-turned-out-to-be-petrified-wood-1969":"During the Apollo goodwill tour, when the astronauts toured the world after the 1969 Moon landing, the American ambassador in the Netherlands came by a moon rock, through Neil Armstrong as Michels thinks, and gave it to a former Dutch prime minister; the rock later ended up in a Dutch museum and turned out to be petrified wood. Michels tells it himself, hedging on details, in a conversation about doubts over official space history.","dylan-borland-nhi-involved-in-the-cover-up":"A whistleblower named Dylan Borland is said to believe that non-human intelligence, meaning whatever beings are behind the phenomenon, is itself involved in hiding it. Michels tells this himself: he asked Borland if the non-human intelligence takes part in the cover-up, and Borland answered \"yes, I for sure know that.\" If true, the secrecy would not come only from governments.","dylan-borland-s-triangle-over-langley":"A man named Dylan Borland says that at Langley, near a NASA hangar on the base, a light flew at him across the base and a triangle took shape around it: an equilateral craft one to two stories thick, with 90° edges and a top he could never see, which he thinks had come round to do a kind of scan. Borland tells it firsthand on the show and cites a memorandum for the record held by AARO, the Pentagon office that now handles reports of UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs). Michels dates it to 2012, the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says Borland told it to Congress, and a guest who goes by the handle UAPGerb adds that Borland felt charged air as it took off above him, which would mean a large unknown craft flying over a US base.","dylan-borland-the-energy-system-is-the-most-sensitive-secret":"A man named Dylan Borland is said to have stated that the most secret part of the hidden UFO program is not the craft themselves but the energy system that powers them. The researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this: \"the most classified thing most sensitive thing is not the craft it's the energy system.\" If true, it would mean the real prize being guarded is a new source of power.","dyson-s-cross-country-drive-with-feynman-1947":"Sometime in the late 1940s, the young physicist Freeman Dyson rode across America with the physicist Richard Feynman, who was driving from Cleveland to Albuquerque and \"offered to take me for a ride just to see the country.\" Dyson tells this himself, in his own words heard on an episode of the show, as a memory of the trip he took with Feynman.","e-howard-hunt-links-jfk-s-death-to-ufo-inquiries-1963":"President Kennedy was killed in 1963 at least partly over UFOs, according to a former CIA officer named E. Howard Hunt, who claimed some part in the assassination; Michels repeats this across several episodes as something others reported Hunt saying. In some tellings Hunt said it to his son, Saint John Hunt, while in others a lawyer named Douglas Caddy kept pressing Hunt about what the murder was really about until Hunt answered that it was about \"the alien presence\", perhaps as he was dying, an account Michels credits to the online interviewer Dark Journalist. If true, a president was killed in part over a secret about non-human visitors.","e-howard-hunt-s-everglades-group-burned-out-by-marshals-1963":"In 1963, a group based in the Florida Everglades, which worked with the CIA officer E. Howard Hunt as its go-between, was burned out of its camp by federal marshals. That is what the researcher Dan Sheehan tells Michels, calling it \"a second group, the ones in the Everglades,\" in an account that also involves Robert F. Kennedy. He gives no source for it. It is one piece of a larger story Sheehan tells about the Kennedy years and what he says the government was hiding.","e-howard-hunt-s-occult-novels-attacking-the-kennedys-1960":"E. Howard Hunt wrote occult novels, sometime between the 1960s and 1970s, that are said to be a thinly veiled attack on the Kennedys, casting them as Satan worshippers. The writer Peter Levenda describes them to Michels from his own reading, without naming which books. He brings them up as a sign that hostility to the Kennedys was being worked out in coded fiction.","e-t-whittaker-s-1904-1905-papers-showing-scalar-waves-are-fe":"Two papers by the English mathematician E.T. Whittaker, which the tellers date to 1904 and 1905, are said to show that scalar waves are possible. These would be a kind of wave unlike ordinary radio waves, which the guest calls \"non-Hertzian.\" Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist who holds patents for unusual craft, gives this to Michels as his own reading of the papers, which he calls \"fundamental.\" He also names the writer Tom Bearden. The papers' own words are not quoted, so whether they make that exact claim is left to his reading.","eagle-lake-sphere-answers-sos-and-chases-canoe-1976":"In 1976, on Eagle Lake near the Smith Brook campsite in Maine's Allagash Waterway, a group of men in a canoe say a glowing white sphere answered their SOS signal and then chased them across the water. Charlie Foltz, one of the men, tells Michels he first saw a white circle 'aspirin tablet sized' and took it for 'the freaking moon', until he 'noticed that it was moving'. Jim Weiner, who was with him along with Jack Weiner and Chuck Rak, says it came up 'right behind us' and that he remembers nothing more until the canoe reached the shore, a gap in memory that, if real, would point to something like an abduction.","ear-sensation-logged-on-blog-before-the-indian-ocean-earthqu-2003":"A former sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA once hired him to talk with aliens, says he felt a strange sensation in his ear and recorded it on his blog before the great Indian Ocean earthquake, in \"September of 03, I believe it was.\" He tells Michels this as his own experience and points to his blog and to a 2006 update of his book Above Black. If true, his sensations would have warned of the disaster before it struck.","earl-of-elgin-confirms-james-bruce-was-a-freemason-1990":"The Scottish explorer James Bruce was a Freemason, and his lodge was Canongate Kilwinning Lodge No. 2 in Edinburgh. That is what the Earl of Elgin told the writer Graham Hancock around 1990 at the Broomhall estate in Scotland, Hancock says. In Hancock's telling the earl answered, 'dear boy, of course he was,' and gave him the lodge's name, a link Hancock used in his book The Sign and the Seal, and he names the photographer Santha Faiia in the story.","earlier-undisclosed-events-before-wwii-1944":"There were said to be secret events involving unexplained craft much earlier than the ones usually talked about, which this story places before or during the Second World War. David Grusch, a former intelligence official turned whistleblower, hints at this to Michels but will not explain: \"there's some other stuff, too, that I can't talk about that was way before that.\" If true, it would push the hidden history back further than the familiar timeline starting in the late 1940s.","earlier-weatherman-burned-by-tall-white-suits-at-range-four-1965":"In 1965, a weatherman who worked at Range Four, a site in Nevada's Indian Springs Valley, was burned after he came too close to tall, white-skinned beings in suits; his shoulder, it is said, \"almost touched one of the shoes.\" The story comes from Charles Hall, who tells Michels that he later spent three years as a weatherman at Area 51 living alongside beings he calls tall white aliens, and who says he saw the injured man's \"x-rays and pictures\" at the Nellis hospital in Nevada. If true, it would mean these beings and US military staff were in physical contact, and that the medical records showed the harm.","earliest-skinwalker-ufo-images-kept-with-full-metadata-2016":"Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property long known for reports of strange lights and events, says he has kept the full metadata for every UFO image taken over the ranch since he took charge, even the very first ones. Metadata is the hidden file data that records when, where and on what device a picture was taken. He tells Michels this to say the photos can be checked rather than taken on trust.","early-1960s-uap-event-filmed-at-a-military-base":"An event involving an unexplained object in the sky was recorded on film at a US military base in the early 1960s, says Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has worked on Pentagon studies of such objects. Davis passes this on second-hand, in an account he links to George H. W. Bush and to a president being briefed on UFOs. If true, the military has had recorded proof of such an event for some sixty years.","early-christian-libraries-discovered-in-the-1940s":"In the 1940s, libraries belonging to early Christians were found, and they showed what had shaped those believers' thinking: Plato's Republic and his allegory of the cave. That is how a scholar of religion named Diana Pasulka puts it to Michels, summarising without naming a source. If she is right, Greek philosophy sat near the roots of early Christian ideas about hidden realities.","early-fbi-cia-army-documents-on-ufos-near-nuclear-sites-and-1945":"From 1945, when nuclear weapons arrived, thousands of documents from the FBI, the CIA, the Army and its counterintelligence corps describe UFOs showing up in America's most sensitive airspace, especially around nuclear sites. Michels says this himself on two episodes. In one, a debate with a skeptic, he names a government effort tied to these sightings, Project Twinkle, as proof that the reports were taken seriously. In the other he names no agencies or project and runs the period to 1954.","early-shuttle-crews-borrowed-yellow-u-2-sr-71-suits-from-bea-1981":"For the first space shuttle flights in 1981, the astronauts Young and Krippen had no suits of their own and had to go to Beale Air Force Base to borrow the yellow pressure suits worn by pilots of the U-2 and SR-71, high-altitude spy planes. A NASA doctor named Greg Rogers tells Michels this from what he heard on the job. It shows how much the early shuttle program leaned on secret military aviation.","earning-the-trust-of-the-space-kids":"A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels that it took him about two years to win the trust of a group of people he calls \"these space kids\", whom he interviewed for a film. He speaks from his own experience doing the interviews, and he does not say on this point who the space kids are or why they were so wary.","earth-s-rarity-and-galactic-pyramids":"There are pyramids all over the galaxy, and they are power sources and places of healing, not tombs. That is what Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, says he was told, though in this account he does not say who told him. If true, Egypt's pyramids would belong to a pattern far bigger than Earth.","eben-alexander-near-death-experience-2008":"During a near-death experience in 2008, a man named Eben Alexander, the author of a book called Proof of Heaven, met \"overarching, like, loving energy\". David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who went public with claims about secret UFO programmes, tells Michels this, saying Alexander has \"a very interesting scientific outlook\" on it. If true, it would hint that consciousness goes on beyond the body.","ebner-effect-high-voltage-on-crops-1990":"In the 1990s, by this account, there was something in Europe called the Ebner effect, in which high voltage applied to crops is said to have changed how they grew. A guest named Dave Rossi mentions it to Michels as an example, saying he believes it dates from that decade and naming no source. He raises it as a case of electricity doing more to living things than standard science allows.","echo-flight-incident-1967":"On 16 March 1967, ten nuclear missiles at Echo Flight, a group of missile silos run from Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, are said to have shut down one after another while a UFO hovered over one of them. A former Air Force missile officer named Robert Salas tells Michels what the launch officer on duty, Walter Figel, told him: at first Figel 'couldn't believe what they were saying,' but very soon 'all 10 of his missiles went down.' Michels places it eight days before Salas's own similar incident at another flight and ties it to the later Malmstrom cases. The journalist Robert Hastings, writing in 2012 from his own interviews, gives Figel's account: a guard at one silo radioed the underground launch room that a 'large round object' was hovering 'directly over' it, and teams Figel sent out 'confirmed the presence of the UFO.' Hastings also records that the missile commander that night, Eric Carlson, 'claimed to have no recollection of a UFO report' during the shutdown or the debriefing, a claim Hastings disputes. The tellers on the show give the UFO report without Carlson's dissent, which the article keeps. If true, it would mean something in the sky could switch off American nuclear weapons.","echo-flight-incident-and-the-boeing-sac-report-1967":"In 1967 a group of nuclear missiles known as Echo Flight, at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, failed all at once, and the story is that the engineers sent to find out why found no ordinary cause while UFOs were reported nearby. Michels says on the show that a report by Boeing and Strategic Air Command, the Air Force command that ran the nuclear missiles, tried to recreate the failure, and that the command's own documents say it was \"extremely puzzled\" and had no idea what happened; that, he says, is why he thinks it was UFO-related. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas adds that Boeing \"did a bench test on what's called a logic coupler\", a part in the launch system, and credits a researcher named James Klotz. The researcher and writer Robert Hastings set out more in an article titled The Echo and Oscar Flight Incidents, published on November 12, 2012. He writes that the late Robert J. Kaminski, who led Boeing's engineering team on the case, was told by a Boeing and Air Force liaison \"that UFOs had indeed been sighted in the vicinity of the flight's launch silos\", and that Kaminski \"unequivocally states that his team could find no prosaic cause for the failures\". Hastings adds that the Air Force halted the investigation midway and ordered Kaminski \"not to file a report on the full-flight shutdown\". If true, something unexplained disabled American nuclear missiles and the inquiry into it was cut short.","ed-gets-cold-feet-before-ssci-testimony-2023":"A witness known only as Ed was about to testify before the Senate Select Committee on Intelligence in 2023 but got cold feet right beforehand. A guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this, saying Steven Greer, a campaigner for UFO disclosure, \"did the right thing\" in bringing Ed forward. Had Ed testified, it would have added one more witness to the congressional hearings on UFOs of that period.","ed-may-leads-stargate-research-in-its-final-decade-1985":"A physicist named Ed May took over from the physicist Hal Puthoff and ran the research side of Stargate, a secret US government programme on remote viewing (the claimed ability to describe faraway places using only the mind), for its last decade. The writer Eric Wargo tells Michels this without naming a source. He says the programme was declassified and \"supposedly ended in 1995\", and that \"supposedly\" leaves open whether it really stopped.","ed-may-s-decision-augmentation-theory":"The parapsychology researcher Ed May proposed that when psychic experiments seem to work, it may be because the researchers' own precognition, a sense of the future, leads them to start each experiment at just the right moment. The writer Eric Wargo describes this idea, which May called \"decision augmentation\", to Michels. If it is right, the strange results in such studies would come from foreknowledge guiding the people involved, not from anyone moving matter with their mind.","ed-may-worked-with-prince-hans-adam":"A researcher named Ed May, who worked on Stargate, a US government programme that studied whether people could use psychic powers to spy, is said to have worked with the reigning prince of Liechtenstein, Hans-Adam II. Michels says May talks about this himself, and he passes it on without saying what the work was.","eddington-s-eclipse-confirms-general-relativity-1919":"In 1919 the British astronomer Arthur Eddington measured how starlight bent around the sun during a solar eclipse, the work that backed Einstein's theory of general relativity. A guest named Greg Rogers tells Michels this as history, without naming a source, and dwells on the irony that it took a man from Germany's wartime enemy, Britain, to confirm the German-born Einstein's theory.","edgar-cayce-foundation-funded-zahi-hawass-1980":"The foundation set up around Edgar Cayce, an American psychic, paid for the work of Zahi Hawass, the Egyptian archaeologist who went on to oversee Egypt's ancient sites. A guest named Jay Anderson tells Michels this without naming a source, placing it between 1980 and 1993. If true, it would tie Egypt's top official archaeology to a group devoted to a psychic's visions.","edgar-fouche-s-1998-tr-3b-testimony-projects-moved-to-dugway":"A man named Edgar Fouche is said to have stated, in testimony from 1998, that many secret projects were moved to Dugway Proving Ground, a military testing range in Utah. Most of that testimony is about the TR-3B, a claimed secret triangle-shaped aircraft. The online researcher UAPGerb points to it on the show as one more account placing hidden work at Dugway.","edgar-fouche-s-defense-advanced-research-center-at-s4-1970":"A man named Edgar Fouché claimed that S4, a secret site in Nevada said to hold alien craft, ran something called the Defense Advanced Research Center, or DARC. The researcher known as UAPGerb tells Michels that such a center was found proposed in a 1970s document from ARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, and he also names a man called Dan Burisch in the same account. As UAPGerb describes the document, it shows the center was proposed; whether it was ever built, or ran at S4, he does not say it shows.","edgar-fouche-the-tr3b-timeline-from-prototype-to-operational-1970":"The TR3B is said to be a secret American aircraft that was built and tested as prototypes from the 1970s on, with the first working models flying in the 1990s. An online UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this. He passes on the account of a man named Edgar Fouche, who described the craft in a presentation at the International UFO Congress in 1998 and tied it to test sites such as the Navy's Patuxent River air station and Edwards Air Force Base. If it were true, it would mean the United States has been flying a secret, highly advanced craft for decades.","edgar-mitchell-founds-the-institute-of-noetic-sciences":"Edgar Mitchell, an astronaut who went to the Moon, founded the Institute of Noetic Sciences, a research institute devoted to the mind and consciousness. The biologist Rupert Sheldrake mentions this on the show and says he is a fellow of the institute himself, so he speaks as someone inside it.","edgar-mitchell-heard-about-roswell-1947":"The Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell is said to have believed that an alien spacecraft crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, and that the government recovered it. Roswell is the field's best-known case of a crash and recovery said to be covered up. Michels says in his own framing that because Mitchell was an Apollo astronaut, \"he heard all sorts of things about Roswell from people at all levels.\" A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani says Mitchell was \"very clear\" in this belief. A researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala, telling it second-hand, adds that the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun told Mitchell he could \"corroborate what you were hearing back home about Roswell.\"","edgar-mitchell-on-the-moon-landing-imagery-1971":"When asked about the Moon landing pictures, the Apollo 14 astronaut Edgar Mitchell said, \"James, it wouldn't surprise me,\" according to the UFO documentary maker James Fox, who says Mitchell told him this in person. Mitchell went on that the astronauts had Hasselblad cameras \"with no viewfinders\" and wore what amounted to gardening gloves. Fox tells it as a hint that some of the famous images may not have been taken the way the public was told.","edgar-mitchell-s-apollo-14-esp-experiment-and-noetic-society-1971":"On the Apollo 14 mission in 1971, the astronaut Edgar Mitchell is said to have run a private experiment in ESP (extrasensory perception, meaning mind-to-mind communication) from space, and the work led him toward a society for studying consciousness, which the story calls a Noetic society. The Swedish UFO researcher and archivist Clas Svahn brings it up with Michels, describing it as \"an experiment with the Swedish guy,\" meaning a Swedish man who was on the receiving end on Earth. It would mean one of the men who walked on the Moon took telepathy seriously enough to test it on the way.","edgar-mitchell-s-conversion-experience-on-apollo-14-1971":"The astronaut Edgar Mitchell, who flew to the Moon on Apollo 14 in 1971, is said to have had a kind of conversion experience on the trip home, one that changed how he saw everything. Michels brings this up on three episodes without naming a source: once he calls it his impression that it felt like a conversion, and once he calls it a telepathic, parapsychological experience that Mitchell has spoken about openly and that updated his whole worldview. If so, one of the few people to walk on the Moon came back convinced the mind reaches further than science allows.","edgar-mitchell-s-esp-experiment-in-space-1971":"In 1971 the Apollo 14 astronaut Edgar Mitchell is said to have run the first psychic experiment in space, trying to send thoughts to people on Earth while he was in orbit, using the kind of tests made famous by the American researcher of extrasensory perception J.B. Rhine. A researcher who goes by the name RedPandaKoala tells Michels this, without naming a source, as part of a talk on UFOs and nuclear weapons.","edgar-mitchell-s-esp-experiment-on-apollo-14-1971":"On the way to the Moon in 1971, the Apollo 14 astronaut Edgar Mitchell is said to have run a private test of ESP, or extrasensory perception, trying to send mental images of a set of cards back to people on Earth. Michels tells it in his own narration, pointing to a 1971 New York Times interview with Mitchell as his source. If it happened, a NASA astronaut was quietly testing the powers of the mind in space.","edgar-mitchell-s-last-filmed-interview-a-secret-group-withho-2015":"In what is described as his last filmed interview, at his home around 2015 or 2016, the astronaut Edgar Mitchell said that a secret group was holding back information about UFOs. Jeremy Corbell, who did the interview, tells Michels that when they got down to brass tacks, \"that's exactly what he said,\" and that Mitchell told him who he had talked with. Coming from Mitchell, it would mean a man of his standing believed the information was being deliberately kept from the public.","edgar-mitchell-s-psi-experiments-on-the-way-to-the-moon-1971":"The Apollo 14 astronaut Edgar Mitchell is said to have run experiments in psychic perception during his 1971 trip to the moon. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, while saying that a lot of astronauts did remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant places or things with the mind alone, and that Mitchell is \"really known for that.\" If true, it would mean such experiments were being tried even in space.","edgar-mitchell-s-ufo-squadron-over-germany-and-the-footage-t":"Edgar Mitchell, whom Michels calls a naval astronaut, is said to have seen a squadron of about nine UFOs over several days on a training flight over Germany, flying in many different formations, and he filmed them. When he landed back in Washington, a military plane landed right after and took the footage, and he never heard about it again. Michels tells this as something Mitchell said. If true, it would be evidence collected and then taken away.","edgar-mitchell-said-no-astronaut-ever-saw-a-ufo-in-space":"The Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell is said to have insisted that no astronaut ever saw a UFO in space. The UFO researcher Clas Svahn, who spoke with Mitchell, tells Michels this was something Mitchell \"was very adamant\" about. It matters because it cuts against the many stories of astronauts spotting strange craft while in orbit.","edgar-mitchell-speaks-out-on-ufos-1971":"Edgar Mitchell, an astronaut who flew on Apollo 14 in 1971, is said to have gone on to speak publicly about UFOs in the decades that followed. Michels gives him as an example himself, without naming a source, in an episode where he debates a skeptic. He does not say what Mitchell said, only that a man who had been to the Moon was willing to speak out.","edison-s-device-to-communicate-with-the-dead-1920":"Thomas Edison, near the end of his life in the 1920s, tried to build a device for talking with the dead. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. If true, it would mean one of America's great inventors took contact with the dead seriously enough to try to engineer it.","editor-asks-avi-loeb-to-cut-alien-paragraph-from-3i-atlas-pa-2025":"A journal editor told the astronomer Avi Loeb that his 2025 paper on 3I/ATLAS, an object passing through the solar system from interstellar space, was great but that he had to take out the final paragraph, the one raising the idea that it might be alien. The well-known skeptic Michael Shermer tells this to Michels, saying Loeb described it himself on a Zoom meeting. It is told as an example of journals keeping the alien question out of print.","editor-s-computer-hit-by-the-same-electromagnetic-anomalies-2022":"In 2022, the computer of Michels's video editor, working on the East Coast on a film about Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch known for reports of strange events, is said to have suffered the same electromagnetic glitches that visitors to the ranch describe. Michels tells it himself, twice. In 2022 he said simply that the editor hit \"the exact same electromagnetic anomalies\", and in 2026 he added that the screen froze and \"got all static-y and weird\", something it had never done, while the original video was being made four years earlier. If true, it would suggest whatever happens at the ranch can reach even its recordings.","edward-condon-atomic-insider-turned-ufo-debunker-1920":"The physicist Edward Condon, Michels says, went from the inner circle of the atomic bomb project to leading the study that shut down serious UFO research. Michels tells it himself, linking Condon to Oppenheimer, to the physicist Max Born in Göttingen, Germany, where Condon studied in the 1920s, and to General Leslie Groves, who ran the bomb project. In his telling Condon's security clearance was lost, then \"randomly he pops back up in 1966 and his clearance is mysteriously restored\", and he is placed on a panel \"which deals a death blow to UFO research claiming it's all a waste of time and money.\" The record he points to is the Los Alamos Primer, the April 1943 notes from the first lectures of the bomb project. It says the notes are \"based on a set of five lectures given by R. Serber\", the physicist Robert Serber, as an \"indoctrination course\" at the start of the Los Alamos Project, and that \"the notes were written up by E. U. Condon.\" It states the goal: \"to produce a practical military weapon in the form of a bomb.\" That puts Condon at the very start of the bomb work, as Michels says. Being from 1943, it cannot speak to his later clearance or his UFO study. If Michels is right, the man who closed the book on UFOs was a trusted atomic insider.","edward-teller-and-project-sundial-gigaton-bomb-1950":"The physicist Edward Teller, co-designer of the hydrogen bomb (the Teller-Ulam device), also led something called Project Sundial, a plan for a giant bomb on the scale of a gigaton, a thousand million tons of TNT. Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist known for patents on UFO-like craft, tells Michels this without naming a source, and stresses that the project is unclassified.","edward-teller-on-the-ufo-legacy-program":"The physicist Edward Teller has been put forward as one of the scientists who might have worked in a \"legacy program,\" a claimed secret effort to study recovered non-human craft, if such a program exists. The physicist Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels that he vaguely remembers some people suggesting this, and he says only that Teller \"would be one.\"","edwin-may-s-scientific-oversight-committee-for-stargate-1970":"Edwin May, chief research physicist of Stargate, a secret US government programme on remote viewing (the claimed ability to describe faraway places using only the mind), had to set up a committee of scientists to check its work. A guest named Chris Ramsay tells Michels this without naming a source. He brings it up to show that the research was reviewed by outside scientists.","eeg-neurofeedback-chair-sessions-targeting-theta-and-gamma-2008":"A man named Jordan Jose, who tells Michels he was taken into a UFO programme as a child, says that between 2008 and 2010 at Baker Victory Services in Western New York he went through sessions of EEG neurofeedback, where his brain waves were recorded and fed back to him to train particular rhythms, here the slow theta and fast gamma waves. He calls the setup 'the chair' and says it was 'where a lot of my traumatic memories actually like came back', which, if true, would mean children's minds were being deliberately trained in such a programme.","eeg-shuts-down-inside-homestead-2-2020":"An EEG, a machine that records brain waves, is said to have shut down when someone carrying it walked into Homestead 2, a building on Skinwalker Ranch in Utah, a property known for reports of strange events, and to have come back on as soon as he walked out. Michels recalls this himself to his guest, the ranch's owner, sometime in the 2020s, calling it super strange. If true, it would suggest something in that building interferes with electronics.","effort-to-account-for-soviet-nukes-after-the-collapse-1991":"After the Soviet Union fell apart in 1991, the US government worked with the Russians in what Rolf Mowatt-Larssen calls a truly heroic effort to track down and account for every Soviet nuclear weapon. Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer, tells Michels this as someone who was part of that period in Moscow. If true, it shows how close the world came to losing track of loose nuclear arms.","efim-liberman-s-research-on-cytoskeletal-learning-1970":"A Soviet scientist named Efim Liberman is said to have studied cytoskeletal learning: the idea that learning happens inside the cell's internal scaffolding, not only in the connections between brain cells. Michels tells this himself, from his friends Daniil and David Liberman, Efim's sons, who told him their father knew the physicist Roger Penrose, or perhaps the researcher Stuart Hameroff. It matters because those two later argued that the brain may work on quantum principles, so Liberman would have been an early traveller on the same road.","eg-g-as-aec-doe-prime-contractor-running-the-nevada-test-sit-1950":"A company called EG&G became the main contractor for the Atomic Energy Commission, the agency then in charge of American nuclear work, and later did the same job for the Department of Energy, running work at the Nevada Test Site in the 1950s. Michels gives this himself, without naming a source, as background in an episode with a NASA doctor who says he saw a UFO in a secret hangar. It matters to the story because it puts one private company at the centre of the country's most secret test grounds.","eg-g-as-gatekeeper-of-area-51-1960":"In the early 1960s, the story goes, a contractor called EG&G, through its special projects division in Las Vegas, was trusted to run the day-to-day business of Area 51, the secret base at Groom Lake in the Nevada desert: screening staff, controlling who got in, moving people there and handling daily operations. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. If true, a private company stood as gatekeeper to one of the most secret sites in the country.","eg-g-engineer-s-account-of-an-egg-shaped-craft-that-could-no":"A senior engineer at EG&G, a defense contractor, is said to have described a craft that was egg-shaped, about the size of an SUV, and could not be opened. Michels tells it as something the engineer described to his own great-uncle, so it reaches us secondhand through his family. If true, it would mean people inside the defense industry had handled an unknown craft they could not get into.","eg-g-interview-switched-mid-way-to-the-s-4-job-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on secret craft at a hidden Nevada site called S-4, says that in 1988 he went to Las Vegas for a job interview at EG&G, a defense contractor, and partway through, the interviewers changed course. In his own telling to Michels they said: \"You know, we actually have a different job that we're thinking of plugging you into now.\" That other job, he says, was the one that took him to S-4.","egg-landing-in-a-brazilian-town-moment-of-contact":"In a small town in Brazil, everyone is said to have reported seeing an egg-shaped object land, give off something like ammonia, and apparently cause the death of a doctor. Michels sums this up from the documentary Moment of Contact, made by James Fox, and does not name the town or give a date. If true, it would be a mass sighting in which a landed craft actually hurt people.","egg-shaped-object-descends-in-the-kilo-area-1978":"In 1978, an object shaped like an egg dropped straight to the ground in the \"Kilo\" area, near a site called Kilo 10 in the nuclear missile field run from Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota. Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who served there, tells Michels he saw it himself, and says it was the only other thing he saw in that area. He brings a man named Mark Wade into the account. A sighting like this would put an unknown craft right beside American nuclear weapons.","egg-shaped-tall-white-craft-using-dog-bone-lake-as-a-landing-1965":"Between 1965 and 1967, egg-shaped craft flown by tall, white-skinned beings he calls the Tall Whites were landing on Dog Bone Lake, a dry lake bed at the north end of Indian Springs Valley in Nevada, according to Charles Hall, a man who says he spent three years around these beings near Area 51, a secret Air Force test site. Hall tells Michels that from 'their base at the north end of Indian Springs, they were using Dog Bone Lake as their landing field', which, if true, would put aliens working openly beside a US military base.","egg-video-as-helicopter-sling-load-2025":"A 2025 video of an egg-shaped object, which some offer as evidence of an unknown craft, may simply show a load hanging under a helicopter, says the UFO skeptic Mick West, who analyses such footage. He tells Michels: \"I think it might actually be from a helicopter and it might be something being moved,\" adding that the object is probably larger than it looks. If he is right, the video would show ordinary cargo being moved, not anything exotic.","eglin-afb-diamond-formation-of-orbs-gaetz-denied-access-to-c-2023":"An aircrew at Eglin Air Force Base in Florida saw a diamond-shaped formation of glowing orbs, and the Florida congressman Matt Gaetz was denied access to that crew when he tried to look into it, according to a former Air Force intelligence officer named David Grusch. Grusch tells Michels this in 2023 as something he heard about Gaetz's experience. If true, the military kept a member of Congress away from witnesses.","egyptian-authorities-board-up-the-paw-and-drill-misleading-h-1990":"In the 1990s, Egyptian authorities are said to have come to the Sphinx at Giza and blocked off the front of one of its paws with literal wooden boards, and to have drilled holes that misled people about what lay below. A guest named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this without naming a source, linking it to radar findings of cut empty spaces under the Sphinx. If true, it would mean officials were hiding something beneath the monument.","egyptian-decoration-at-the-mount-wilson-lodges":"The lodges where astronomers stayed at Mount Wilson Observatory, above Pasadena, were reportedly decorated with Egyptian hieroglyphs and symbols. Michels tells this as something reported, tying it to the astronomer George Ellery Hale, as part of his case that modern science and space research carry threads of ancient religion.","egyptian-government-pumps-water-out-from-beneath-the-giza-pl":"The Egyptian government has installed pumps near the Khafre Valley Temple, beside the Sphinx enclosure, to pump water out from beneath the Giza Plateau. A researcher named Jeffrey Drum tells Michels this without naming a source, in a talk about what he calls an energy grid beneath the pyramids, where the water matters to his idea.","egyptian-priests-levitating-blocks-with-sound":"Ancient Egyptian traditions are said to describe priests lifting the huge stone blocks of the pyramids into the air, with the help of sound. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels about these traditions without naming a specific text. If true, it would mean the builders had some technique since lost.","egyptian-symbols-in-a-freemasonic-temple-in-dc":"A temple of the Freemasons, the old fraternal society, in Washington DC is said to be filled with Egyptian symbols. Michels tells this himself from his own visit, saying \"I've been inside of this like Freemasonic temple in DC,\" and mentions a figure the transcript renders as \"Charles law font\" without saying more about him. He raises it as a hint that old esoteric traditions sit close to the heart of American power.","eiffel-tower-pollution-prank-on-a-fellow-viewer-1980":"In the 1980s, inside a US military remote viewing unit, a team that tried to describe distant places and targets using only the mind, Army Sergeant Lyn Buchanan played a joke on a fellow viewer. He says he told the man, \"Oh, by the way, your target today is probably not the Eiffel Tower.\" Buchanan tells Michels the story as one of the unit's in-jokes, a prank meant to plant an image in a colleague's head before a session.","eight-hour-read-ons-with-15-minutes-to-sign-2015":"Dylan Borland, who says he worked at the defense contractor BAE Systems between 2015 and 2018, tells Michels that his first \"read-ons\", the briefings that admit a person into a classified program, lasted about eight hours. He says he was handed a stack of paper \"like this thick with sticky notes\" and, as the story goes, given only about fifteen minutes to sign it. He tells it as something he went through himself, to show how heavy the secrecy around these programs was.","eight-sided-great-pyramid-and-the-templar-flag":"The flag of the Knights Templar, the medieval crusading order, is said to be the Great Pyramid of Giza drawn flat: an eight-sided pyramid laid out in two dimensions. Jay Anderson makes this case to Michels, saying that \"if you take the Templar flag and you create it into a three-dimensional object, you get the great pyramid of Giza,\" and he draws on the video commentator Dark Journalist and the writer Joseph P. Farrell. If so, the order's symbol would carry hidden knowledge of the pyramid's true shape.","einstein-letter-to-fdr-leads-to-the-manhattan-project-1939":"In 1939 Einstein and another scientist wrote to President Franklin D. Roosevelt warning that Germany might be able to build an atomic bomb, and that warning led to the Manhattan Project. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas tells Michels this as history, without naming a source, adding that he cannot recall the other scientist's name.","einstein-oppenheimer-letter-1940":"A letter said to date from the 1940s, supposedly written by Albert Einstein to Oppenheimer, is raised by the researcher Richard Dolan. He mentions it only in passing on the show, calling it \"a very interesting document\" and saying it is \"supposedly\" from Einstein, without saying what it contains or where it came from. He brings it up alongside other papers he thinks show figures at the top of American science and government knew something about the phenomenon.","einstein-s-1923-paper-saying-general-relativity-fits-with-th":"The claim is that Einstein himself once said his general theory of relativity is compatible with the ether, the old idea of an invisible medium filling all of space that physics is usually said to have thrown out. Michels says it in his own words, placing the paper in 1923, and a Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, who holds patents he links to UFO-like craft, makes the same point but dates it to 1921, adding that Einstein used the idea even though he had long been against it. The text they mean is Ether and the Theory of Relativity, an address Einstein gave at the University of Leiden on May 5, 1920, later printed in his book Sidelights on Relativity, so it is earlier than either date the tellers give. The address itself says plainly that \"according to the general theory of relativity space is endowed with physical qualities; in this sense, therefore, there exists an ether,\" and even that \"space without ether is unthinkable.\" It also says that \"the special theory of relativity does not compel us to deny ether.\" So on the core claim the document backs the tellers quite directly. What it leaves to the reader is what kind of ether this is: Einstein warns it \"may not be thought of\" as made of parts that can be tracked through time, and that \"the idea of motion may not be applied to it,\" so whether it is the same ether the guests have in mind is a step of reading the text does not settle. The point matters to them because, if Einstein left room for an ether, ideas about tapping the fabric of space are less fringe than they seem.","einstein-s-cosmological-constant-and-cosmic-antigravity-1917":"In 1917 Einstein added a term to his equations, the cosmological constant, a kind of cosmic antigravity that let the universe stay the same size forever. The Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb tells Michels that Einstein liked a universe that never expanded \"because then you don't have to imagine what was there before the beginning.\"","einstein-s-deathbed-breakthrough-1955":"The story goes that Albert Einstein, on his deathbed in Princeton in 1955, suddenly became ecstatic and began talking excitedly to his nurse in German, as if he had finally cracked something. John Brandenburg, a guest who argues that life on Mars was wiped out by nuclear war, tells Michels this as \"a saying\" he has heard, not something he saw. If it were true, it would mean Einstein reached a last breakthrough that died with him.","eisenhower-aware-of-foo-fighters-1942":"From 1942 to 1945, General Eisenhower knew about the \"foo fighters\", the strange lights that aircrews reported following their planes during the Second World War. A writer named Marik von Rennenkampff says so on the show without naming a source. It would mean the subject reached the highest level of command decades before it became public.","eisenhower-era-peads-used-to-create-waived-saps-1950":"Under President Eisenhower in the 1950s, Presidential Emergency Action Documents, orders a president drafts in advance for use in a national emergency, were used to set up \"waived\" special access programs. These are highly restricted secret programs that are excused from the usual reporting to Congress, so they ran \"free from congressional oversight\". The claim comes from Jack, Michels' co-host, who goes by a pseudonym and says he has more than 15 years in defense and aerospace. He says it while interviewing the scientist Eric W. Davis, and if he is right it would explain how UFO work could have been kept hidden from Congress for decades.","eisenhower-era-presidential-emergency-action-directives-cove-1950":"The claim is that in the 1950s President Eisenhower set up secret emergency orders that, among other things, cover UFOs, which the subject calls UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena. These are presidential emergency action documents: orders a president keeps ready for a crisis, which few people ever see. A physicist named Eric W. Davis, presented on the show as a Pentagon scientist, says the directives are \"so strict\" and were \"instituted in the Eisenhower White House\". He says they have since been carried forward on many topics, and that he and a former CIA officer named Jim Semivan know they were set up for this one. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells it too, also crediting Semivan, but he does not name Eisenhower. He goes further: \"This is how it started, which means it's an executive program. The president is running the cover up.\" Both men are passing on what Semivan told them, not reading from the directives themselves. If true, keeping UFOs secret would be a matter of presidential command, not something a lower agency did on its own.","eisenhower-s-1954-treaty-with-the-greys":"A rumor holds that in 1954 President Eisenhower made a treaty with the greys, small grey-skinned beings of UFO lore, letting them collect biological specimens and run medical experiments on Americans. Michels tells it as 'lore' on several shows, calling it the Greada treaty and saying Eisenhower used a dentist visit in Palm Springs as cover while the deal was done at Edwards Air Force Base. A philosopher named Jason Jorjani says Eisenhower met the Nordics, tall human-looking beings, first, and that the greys 'made him a better deal'. An author named Peter Levenda brushes it off as the idea that 'Eisenhower or somebody' signed a contract with aliens in the 1950s.","eisenhower-s-farewell-warning-about-the-military-industrial-1961":"Steven Greer, an activist who argues that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, tells Michels that what he describes is exactly what President Eisenhower warned of. Greer points to Eisenhower's farewell address of January 1961, given as he left office and John F. Kennedy came in. Greer sums up its message as: \"Beware of the military-industrial complex.\" The speech warned of the growing power of the military and the arms industry. Greer reads that warning as a description of the secret network he says now keeps UFO knowledge hidden. The connection to UFOs is Greer's reading, not something he says the speech states.","eisenhower-s-granddaughter-is-an-abductee":"Laura Eisenhower, a granddaughter of President Eisenhower, is said to be an abductee, meaning someone who says she has been taken by non-human beings, and to be deeply involved in the UFO subject. Michels mentions it in passing himself, without naming a source. He raises it as a sign of how close the subject runs to the family of a president.","eisenhower-s-meeting-with-ets-about-stopping-nuclear-prolife-1954":"President Eisenhower is said to have met with extraterrestrials in Palm Springs, California, sometime between 1954 and 1956, and talked with them about stopping the spread of nuclear weapons. The UFO researcher Steven Greer tells Michels he got a series of reports from the French Ministry of Defense showing that the meeting did happen, but he says it 'wasn't the mythology' people usually tell about it. Greer does not quote the reports. He links Allen Dulles, then head of the CIA, and the wealthy Paul Mellon to the story, and if he is right, a US president dealt face to face with beings from elsewhere.","eisenhower-shown-five-spacecraft-at-edwards-1950":"The story goes that President Eisenhower slipped away from a stay in Palm Springs and was taken to Edwards Air Force Base in California, where five alien spacecraft were waiting on the runway. Some versions place it at Alamogordo in New Mexico instead. Charles Hall, who says he spent three years with tall white aliens at a secret base, repeats it to Michels as something he was told, and mentions the TV series Ancient Aliens. In his telling, Eisenhower was \"supposedly golfing at Palm Springs.\" He was taken to Edwards with \"no cameras, no press,\" and \"sitting on the runway at Edwards were five spacecraft\" that were shown to him. Michels tells it in another episode with different details. He gives the year as 1954, says Eisenhower \"claimed to have a dentist appointment,\" and calls it a meeting without mentioning the five craft. If it happened, an American president saw alien craft for himself while the public was told he was on holiday.","elderly-widow-sees-a-ball-of-light-at-her-kitchen-window":"An elderly widow says she saw a ball of light pass by her kitchen window. A guest named Chris Bledsoe tells Michels about it, repeating what the woman told him: \"I saw this ball of light go by my kitchen window.\" No place or date is given. He tells it as one more case of the strange lights he says turn up around the people near him.","eleanor-roosevelt-letter-to-oppenheimer-1947":"Rumours of UFOs at Los Alamos and other nuclear sites spread so widely that Eleanor Roosevelt wrote to J. Robert Oppenheimer to ask about them, and he wrote back reassuring her that they were just rumours. Michels tells this himself and says Oppenheimer's reply has been fully verified and is kept in the Library of Congress. He tells it to show how far talk of UFOs over nuclear sites had reached in those years.","electrical-after-effects-of-the-encounter-2019":"After an encounter he says he had in 2019, the researcher Deep Prasad noticed something odd: his phone charged instantly for the first few days, even after he factory reset it. Prasad tells this himself on the show, as one of the physical after-effects that, to him, show the encounter left a real trace.","electrodynamic-dust-shield-payload-tested-on-the-moon":"A device meant to keep moon dust off equipment using electric fields, called an Electrodynamic Dust Shield, was landed and tested on the Moon, according to a NASA scientist named Charles Buhler who worked on it. He tells Michels that 'just over a year ago' they landed their payload carrying nine of these shields on the lunar surface.","electronic-failures-near-the-baltic-sea-anomaly-on-a-trip-wi-2020":"Electronics are said to fail near the Baltic Sea anomaly, an odd object on the seabed between Sweden and Finland. Michels says he saw this himself: he went out to the site with Dennis Åsberg, a treasure hunter with the diving team Ocean X, and says that \"the weirdest things happen when you're around this object.\" If true, it would suggest the object does something to the equipment around it.","element-115-used-to-amplify-gravity":"Element 115, a superheavy chemical element, is sometimes used to boost the gravity effect that drives an alien craft, according to Bob Lazar, a man who has spoken publicly about how such craft are said to work. Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, passes this on to Michels while admitting he doesn't remember Lazar telling him so himself. If true, it would mean the craft move by working on gravity directly.","elena-hardy-finds-gli3-mutation-in-the-ncbi-data-2020":"Genetic data from the Nazca mummies, small three-fingered bodies said to come from Peru, is said to show a mutation in a gene called GLI3, according to a researcher named Elena Hardy. She works from the data posted to NCBI, a public US database of genetic sequences, and tells Michels that \"a similar kind of mutation is associated in the literature with polysyndactyly,\" a condition of extra or joined fingers and toes, without naming the studies she means. If she is right, the bodies' unusual hands would line up with a known genetic pattern. What the mutation means for where the bodies came from, the data as she describes it does not settle.","elephant-cage-hf-antennas-may-have-aided-sherman-s-downloads-1990":"Michels wonders whether huge circular radio antenna arrays known as \"elephant cages,\" which pick up high-frequency (HF) signals at bases such as San Vito dei Normanni in Italy and Offutt Air Force Base in Nebraska, played a part in what Dan Sherman did in the early 1990s. Sherman is a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA trained him to receive messages from aliens with his mind, an account he gives in Above Black. Michels raises it as a question of his own: were the signals these antennas caught tied to the signals Sherman was trying to decode for the NSA?","eleusinian-mysteries-and-earlier-minoan-and-mycenaean-forms":"The idea is that the Eleusinian mysteries, secret initiation rites of ancient Greece, carried on rituals much older than themselves, going back to the Minoan and Mycenaean cultures of Bronze Age Greece. Michels brings this up himself in an episode about a lost age around 12,000 BC. He points to the writer Brian Muraresku and his book The Immortality Key, which discusses the rites, and notes they come 'much later obviously' than the older forms.","eleusinian-mysteries-and-the-kykeon":"The story is that the ancient Greek rites at Eleusis, about 13 miles northwest of Athens, were built around a drug drink that made people feel they had touched immortality. Michels tells it often, and draws mostly on The Immortality Key, a book by a writer named Brian Muraresku. In that telling, initiates drank the kykeon, a ritual potion that probably contained ergot, a fungus that holds a compound related to LSD. Michels calls Eleusis the spiritual capital of Athens and names Plato, Socrates and Alcibiades in connection with it. He says the rites were secret on pain of death, and that from the little testimony that survives, people walked away believing they were immortal. He sums up the teaching as \"if you die before you die, you won't die when you die.\" In other episodes he describes the drink as a psychedelic that freed the soul so people could see beings. He also says Muraresku traces the rites back to Egypt. Two guests tell versions of it too. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore mentions the rites where an ergot preparation was found, and the writer Graham Hancock, citing his own book Supernatural along with Muraresku's, says every initiate was given the drink. If true, one of the founding religious rites of the West would have been a psychedelic experience.","eleusinian-mysteries-as-near-death-rituals":"The Eleusinian Mysteries, secret initiation rites held in ancient Greece at Eleusis, and other rituals like them, worked by temporarily killing the physical body through a near-death experience. That is Michels's own reading, offered without a source, and it would mean the ancients were deliberately sending initiates to the edge of death to glimpse what lies beyond.","eleusis-dmtx-retreat-and-research-center-in-st-vincent-and-t-2025":"The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore says that besides running DMTX, a project built around the psychedelic drug DMT, his group is building a retreat centre and a research centre in the Caribbean, in St Vincent and the Grenadines. He tells Michels about it in 2025 as one of the people developing it, and mentions a group called New Nautics in connection with the work. If it goes ahead, it would be a dedicated place to study what people meet under DMT.","elevator-descent-at-the-facility-1980":"A man named Randy Anderson says that sometime in the 1980s, at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, he was taken into a building and down in an elevator. He tells Michels, as someone who rode it, that \"it felt like we went down significantly further\" than you would expect, and calls that \"the first odd thing\", hinting at a large underground facility.","elijah-calls-down-fire-against-the-priests-of-baal":"In the Bible, the prophet Elijah holds a contest with the priests of Baal to see whose god will light a sacrificial fire, and he calls down fire, which Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of psychic phenomena, describes as lightning. Mishlove raises it on the show as an old example of something similar to the feats he is discussing. The story appears in the King James Version of the Bible, published in 1611.","elite-baal-cult-tied-to-the-epstein-material":"Some of America's ruling elite are obsessed with Baal, the ancient god whom the Bible condemns, according to the researcher and filmmaker Tim Alberino. He tells Michels that this shows up in the Epstein files, the records made public about the financier and sex offender Jeffrey Epstein. He gives it as his own reading of that material and quotes nothing from it, though if it were true, it would mean an old cult was alive among the powerful.","elizabeth-rauscher-s-oni-grant-and-her-berkeley-archive-1970":"A scientist named Elizabeth Rauscher and her colleagues are said to have received \"this huge grant\" from the Office of Naval Intelligence, or ONI, the Navy's intelligence arm, sometime between the 1970s and early 1980s. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels this without naming a source. He says Rauscher, who has since died, left \"a huge archive at Berkeley of all of her research\", and that he sent someone to search it for papers on the grant, but \"we couldn't find anything.\" If it is true, it would mean Navy intelligence paid for her unusual research.","elizabeth-targ-dies-of-the-glioblastoma-she-studied-with-hea-2002":"Elizabeth Targ, a psychiatrist whose father was Russell Targ, did what Jeffrey Mishlove calls brilliant medical research on psychic healers. In 2002 she died of glioblastoma, the brain cancer she had been studying with those healers. Mishlove, a PhD who studies psychic phenomena and the afterlife, tells Michels about her as someone he knew of, and the story's point is the grim irony of her death.","elizondo-confirms-hastings-ufos-and-nukes-reporting":"The claim is that the Pentagon's own UFO office quietly confirmed years of reporting by Robert Hastings, a researcher who wrote the book UFOs and Nukes about UFOs appearing at American nuclear weapons sites. Luis Elizondo, the former head of AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, tells Michels that Hastings \"is legit\" and his conclusions are \"spot on\". He says he could not talk about it while in government, but \"everything he was reporting we saw\". Hastings himself records an earlier version in an article published on February 12, 2024. There he writes that in 2021 Elizondo said on a podcast that his group had used the book as a resource for its own research into UFO incursions at nuclear sites. He quotes Elizondo: \"There was a lot of information we didn't have access to that, it turned out, Robert Hastings did ... We were able to validate and substantiate exactly what he was saying.\" If true, a Pentagon program found the same pattern of UFOs at nuclear bases that Hastings had gathered from witnesses.","elizondo-directed-aatip-an-offshoot-of-aawsap":"A Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo directed AATIP, the Advanced Aerospace Threat Identification Program, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports. Michels says this himself in a documentary episode about Elizondo, adding that AATIP was an offshoot of a larger effort called AAWSAP, the Advanced Aerospace Weapon System Applications Program. If so, the Pentagon's interest in these reports ran through more than one programme.","elizondo-in-gray-fox-and-jsoc-s-link-to-crash-retrievals":"A former Pentagon intelligence officer named Luis Elizondo was once a member of the US Army Intelligence Support Activity, a secretive Army intelligence unit also known as Gray Fox. Michels says this twice on the show, both times passing on a connection made by his friend Matthew Pines, a commentator who later came on as a guest. In one of the two tellings he places Elizondo's time in the unit in the 2000s. If true, it would mean Elizondo came from inside one of the military's most secretive units.","elizondo-met-edward-teller-on-an-air-florida-flight":"When he was about seven, Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence officer known for speaking out about the government's UFO studies, says he flew on an Air Florida flight from Sarasota to Miami with the physicist Edward Teller. Elizondo tells Michels his father \"had some friends in the government\" and put him on the plane with Teller. He tells it as an early brush with the world of secret weapons science he would later work near.","elizondo-neither-confirms-nor-denies-being-read-into-immacul-2024":"At a House Oversight Committee hearing on 13 November 2024, Representative Nancy Mace asked Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official known for speaking publicly about AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, whether he had been given access to an alleged secret program called Immaculate Constellation. The hearing record has him answer: \"I would not be authorized to confirm nor deny the existence of any ongoing or past program, especially as it relates to a special access program, either by name or trigraph\". A special access program is a tightly restricted classified program, and a trigraph is a three-letter code name. Michels calls this \"a very interesting answer\", and a later episode replays Elizondo's words, since they could hint that he knew of it. The program is named in an anonymous report that the journalist Michael Shellenberger gave to Congress and Mace entered into the hearing record. The report calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report. A former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of such a program. The report ties Elizondo to AATIP, saying the program was set up \"following the public disclosure of the AATIP/AAWSAP programs by Luis Elizondo in 2017\", but it does not take up whether he himself was ever read into the program.","elizondo-on-60-minutes-the-government-said-they-re-real-2021":"In a 2021 television interview, Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who ran a small office studying unexplained aerial reports, said the US government itself had already admitted that UFOs are real. The interview ran on CBS's 60 Minutes on May 16, 2021, in a segment by the correspondent Bill Whitaker titled \"UFOs regularly spotted in restricted U.S. airspace, report on the phenomena due next month.\" The broadcast's transcript records the exchange. Whitaker asks: \"So what you are telling me is that UFOs, unidentified flying objects, are real?\" Elizondo answers: \"Bill, I think we're beyond that already. The government has already stated for the record that they're real. I'm not telling you that. The United States government is telling you that.\" Michels brings the moment up in his own words on the show, putting it as \"the government just admitted that they're real.\" So the claim comes down to us both through the broadcast's own record and through the host retelling it. If Elizondo's point is right, the question is no longer whether such objects exist but what they are.","elizondo-on-aliens-storing-messages-in-genetics":"Michels says a former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo once told him that if you wanted to make something that truly lasts, there are only two ways to do it: send it out into deep space, where it is unlikely to run into anything, or write it into biology and genetics. Michels passes this on as Elizondo's idea of how an intelligence from elsewhere might leave a message, which would mean that something could be written into living things, ourselves included.","elizondo-points-aaro-to-a-safe-drawer-that-turns-out-empty-2020":"A former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo is said to have told AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate UFO reports, about a particular office and \"a safe drawer\" where something was supposedly kept. When the drawer was opened, he was told, \"there was nothing there.\" A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell tells Michels this as an episode he knows of second-hand, sometime in the 2020s. He brings it up to suggest that material can be moved before investigators get to it.","elizondo-regularly-sees-green-orbs-in-his-house":"Luis Elizondo, who worked on AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, regularly sees green glowing balls of light inside his own house, as Michels puts it while introducing him. Elizondo tells it himself and says the orbs were seen 'not only by me and my wife and my kids but also our neighbors'. He adds that they started when he joined the office and that other people in the programme had the same thing happen, which, if true, would suggest the phenomenon follows the people who study it home.","elizondo-s-book-blocked-from-a-lockheed-program":"According to Michels, Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official who says he ran its UFO study, writes that he and his colleagues went to the defense company Lockheed Martin to try to \"get inside\" a secret program there and were kept out. Michels cites this from Elizondo's book Imminent, published in 2024. It matters because a Pentagon official being refused entry would suggest a program hidden even from those officially studying UFOs.","elizondo-s-idea-that-ufos-are-prepping-the-battle-space-2017":"Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who went public in 2017 as one of the first insiders to speak about UFOs, has suggested that UFOs may be \"prepping the battle space\", the military phrase for readying the ground before a conflict. Michels repeats this himself, as something Elizondo has said in the past. If true, the objects would not just be watching but preparing for something.","elizondo-s-imminent-people-with-cherokee-dna-have-more-exper":"People with Cherokee blood and DNA seem to have more encounters with the phenomenon, according to Imminent, a 2024 book by Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who ran a small office that studied UFO reports. Jason Simosa, a researcher who also describes experiences of his own, tells Michels that the claim is \"in there\" in the book. He does not quote it, so what he gives is his own summary. If true, it would suggest the experiences run in some families or peoples more than others.","elizondo-s-toy-in-the-sandbox-analogy-for-a-us-held-technolo":"Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who has spoken publicly about the government's study of UFOs, uses a playground picture for a technology he suggests the US is holding back. He compares it to a toy in a sandbox and says, \"Maybe we can find a way where the kids in the sandbox can play together,\" suggesting the technology could be shared rather than kept by one side.","elizondo-saw-jacobs-s-film-at-aatip-2010":"A former Pentagon intelligence officer named Luis Elizondo is said to have confirmed that, while he ran AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, he saw the film and the written account of a man named Robert Jacobs. Michels repeats what Jacobs told him, in Jacobs's words: \"he verified when he was in charge of AATIP he saw my film, my account.\" If true, it would mean Jacobs's material had reached the Pentagon's own study of these reports.","elizondo-says-monsanto-linked-people-are-coming-after-him":"A former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo says some of the people coming after him are tied to Monsanto, the agrochemical company, Michels reports, passing on what Elizondo has said. Michels adds that there is a 'Monsanto Becktell connection from back in the day', and that those people may also have been involved in the UFO question. If so, corporate interests would be working against someone who talks publicly about UFOs.","elizondo-time-is-not-a-luxury-that-we-can-afford-2023":"Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official who speaks publicly about UFOs, says the time for secrecy has run out: \"Time is not a luxury that we can afford,\" he says. \"We need to start having the conversation collectively.\" His words are played or quoted in an episode of Michels' show, as a call for the public to start openly discussing UFOs now.","elizondo-was-nro-liaison-while-at-ttsa-2017":"Around 2017, while former Pentagon official Luis Elizondo was working at To The Stars Academy (TTSA), a private company that studied UFOs, he is said to have also been the liaison to the National Reconnaissance Office (NRO), the agency that runs America's spy satellites. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this and points to Elizondo's CV as the source. If true, it would mean Elizondo kept working ties to an intelligence agency while he was working in the private sector.","elizondo-won-t-confirm-or-deny-immaculate-constellation-befo-2024":"Asked by Congress whether he had been cleared into a secret UFO program called Immaculate Constellation, a former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo would neither confirm nor deny it. The program's name had just come out in an anonymous report handed to Congress. Michels brings this up himself, saying that if you watch Elizondo in front of Congress he says, in effect, \"I can't confirm or deny being read into\" it, that is, given the clearance to know about it. Michels places this in September 2024. The official record, from the House Oversight Committee hearing Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena: Exposing the Truth, puts it on 13 November 2024. There Rep. Nancy Mace asked, \"were you read in to the Immaculate Constellation program?\" Elizondo answered: \"Ma'am, I would not be authorized to confirm nor deny the existence of any ongoing or past program, especially as it relates to a special access program, either by name or trigraph\", a trigraph being a program's three-letter code. Michels tells it because, to him, the answer suggests the program might be real.","elizondo-worked-with-grusch-in-a-scif":"Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, says he worked side by side with David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who later went public with claims about hidden UFO programmes, inside a SCIF, a sealed room for handling classified material. Elizondo tells Michels he was asked not to say where, but that Grusch had all the access he needed and saw the same information Elizondo did. He says it to vouch for Grusch's claims as coming from real access.","ellsworth-inner-zone-alert-saucer-beams-light-at-missile-1960":"Mario Woods, who says he was a guard at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, says he and his partner, Michael Johnson, drove up to a nuclear missile site called November 5 and found an object \"the size of a Walmart building\" hovering about 10 feet over its blast door; he says it was 12:15 when it began and after 6 a.m. when they woke up miles away at a reservoir, the mud around their truck warm and wet on a subzero night, and his partner catatonic. Woods tells it to Michels first-hand, and the researcher Robert Hastings, who writes about UFOs at nuclear bases, and Michels himself both repeat it, Michels calling it an abduction and one of his most credible cases; one version adds a radio report of a \"Flying Saucer\" sending a \"beam of reddish light\" down at the missile. The tellers disagree on details: the year is given as the 1960s, 1977 or 1978, and the distance as five, 8.5 or 9 miles.","ellsworth-radar-jet-chase-and-bismarck-lights-1953":"On a night in August 1953, glowing lights over South Dakota and North Dakota are said to have been tracked on radar and chased by a jet fighter from Ellsworth Air Force Base, with more lights seen over Bismarck. Michels tells it from Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and from its one-time head, Edward J. Ruppelt: \"On August 5th, 1953, a trained civilian spotter calls into the base.\" The case was also studied by the researcher Gordon D. Thayer in the Condon report, a University of Colorado study of UFO reports published on 31 October 1968. That report begins with an observer from the Ground Observer Corps, civilian volunteers who watched the skies, who saw a stationary \"red glowing light\" near Blackhawk. It says the report \"was relayed to the Rapid City Filter Center\", a reporting hub, rather than called into the base, and that airmen went outside to look. It says an F-84 fighter chased the light \"without gaining on it\" and \"was probably chasing a star, in this case Pollux\". It sums up the case as stars seen through a warm layer of air, at least one meteor, and false radar echoes. The report notes, though, that the commanding officer doubted there had ever been even a false echo. The report gives a reading that differs from Michels's account, while his Blue Book record is not quoted. If the lights were not stars, military radar and a pilot would have tracked something unknown over American bases.","ellsworth-silo-abandoned-with-access-plug-open-1978":"In 1978, at the nuclear missile field of Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, a security team is said to have abandoned a missile silo and left its access plug open. Michels tells this himself and places it one year after an earlier incident with two airmen he names only as Mario and Michael. If true, a team leaving a nuclear silo open would point to something frightening enough to make trained guards walk away.","elon-musk-knows-ufo-secrets-but-can-t-say-2025":"Elon Musk, whose company SpaceX builds rockets, knows the secrets about UFOs but cannot talk about them. That is what the comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan told Michels in 2025: \"of course he knows.\" Rogan adds that he had talked it over the day before with a man he calls only Dan, on a podcast called Age of Disclosure.","elon-musk-on-joe-rogan-denies-advanced-tech-2025":"Elon Musk recently said on Joe Rogan's show that he has an all-access pass to the defence contractors Lockheed and Northrop and that the US does not have any of these advanced, supposedly hidden technologies. Michels puts this to his guest, the UFO activist Steven Greer, in 2025, as a public denial from someone well placed to know whether such secret craft exist.","elon-musk-on-legacy-programs-at-a-trump-campaign-town-hall-2024":"In 2024, while campaigning for Donald Trump, Elon Musk was asked at a town hall about legacy programs, the secret government projects said to hold recovered craft and their technology. According to the analyst Matthew Pines, who brings it up with Michels, Musk answered that if the US government has interesting technology, it should make it available. It matters to Pines because someone that close to the incoming administration would be on record backing release.","elongated-skulls-without-a-sagittal-suture-from-the-malta-hy":"Skulls with stretched, elongated shapes were found inside the Hypogeum, an ancient underground chamber in Malta, and they are said to lack the seam that normally runs along the top of a human skull. Jay Anderson, a researcher of ancient sites, says this on the show and adds that one of them is on display in a museum in Valletta. If so, the skulls would point to people quite unlike us.","elvis-starr-s-memoir-tape-about-meeting-ransone-in-the-white-1970":"A man named Elvis Starr is said to have recorded a tape in the 1970s, dictating his memoir, in which he says he met a woman named Valerie Ransone in the White House. An unnamed researcher brings it up on the show without saying where the tape is, describing it as \"a tape of him saying, like, I met Valerie Ransone in the White House.\" If the tape says that, it would put Ransone inside the White House in that decade, though the researcher's summary does not say why she was there or what came of the meeting.","email-to-doty-about-who-wrote-serpo-2020":"In 2020 a researcher named Deep Prasad emailed Richard Doty, a former Air Force investigator, to ask who really wrote Project Serpo, a story about a secret exchange between the US and an alien world. Prasad tells Michels that he had worked out more than one person was behind it, and that Doty wrote back: \"I tip my hat off to you for realizing there was more people involved.\" If that is so, the Serpo story was the work of a group, not one author.","email-to-nolan-about-a-glowing-object-that-drops-molten-meta-2021":"Someone wrote to the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan in 2021 to report a glowing object that dropped molten metal. Nolan, who studies material said to come from UFOs, tells Michels the email had arrived only a few weeks before. He mentions it as one more of the reports that reach him, the kind that would leave physical material behind to test if it were real.","emails-from-people-who-saw-cigar-shaped-objects-crash-in-wat-2011":"Since 2011, many people have written to a Swedish treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, by email and even on paper, saying they saw cigar-shaped objects crash into the water and into forests. He tells Michels that some of those who contacted him were former navy officers. He passes on what they told him because he believes there is a UFO on the seafloor, and their stories would back up his find.","embassy-correspondence-waiting-for-the-americans-to-make-a-s-1950":"In the 1950s, letters passing through the Canadian embassy in Washington included a document saying, in Grant Cameron's words, \"we're waiting for the Americans to make a statement.\" Cameron is a UFO researcher, and he tells Michels about it from the personal files of Wilbert B. Smith, a Canadian official. He links it to Smith and to Omond Solandt, a Canadian figure of the same period. If true, it would mean Canada knew something about UFOs and was leaving it to the United States to announce it first.","emenegger-and-linda-moulton-howe-promised-footage-that-is-th-1970":"In the 1970s and 80s, the filmmaker Robert Emenegger and the journalist Linda Moulton Howe were each led to believe they would be given government footage of UFOs, and each time it was pulled away. A guest named Bryce Zabel tells Michels this in passing, without naming a source, as part of a pattern of officials dangling evidence before Hollywood and the press and then taking it back.","emenegger-gave-a-copy-of-the-documentary-to-spielberg-1975":"A filmmaker named Bob Emenegger, who made the 1974 documentary UFOs: Past, Present and Future, gave a copy of it to Steven Spielberg in the years before Spielberg's film Close Encounters of the Third Kind, according to Grant Cameron, a UFO researcher who says Emenegger told him so. Cameron quotes Emenegger: 'I gave a copy to Spielberg', a link that, if true, would mean the documentary's material fed into the famous film.","emergency-conference-at-camp-hood-on-nuclear-site-ufos":"Unexplained objects seen around nuclear sites are said to have prompted an emergency conference at Camp Hood, Texas, attended by the Air Force Office of Special Investigations (the Air Force's own investigators), the Army Counter Intelligence Command and the FBI. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source or a date, in an episode about UFOs and nuclear weapons. If true, it would mean the military and the FBI treated the sightings as a serious security matter.","emp-from-nuclear-blast-travels-through-dimensions":"The electromagnetic pulse from a nuclear blast, a burst of energy that knocks out electronics, is said to travel through more than one dimension. The musician Tom DeLonge says this on the show as something \"we found out,\" without saying who found it or how. If true, a nuclear explosion would be felt beyond the ordinary world we see.","encyclopedia-galactica-alien-taxonomy-sent-to-the-congressma":"A congressman named Eric Burlison says that someone sent him both volumes of a document called the Encyclopedia Galactica, which is described as a catalogue of alien types. He tells Michels this himself, without saying who sent it, and if the document is what it claims to be, it would be a guide to the kinds of beings said to visit Earth.","energy-grid-like-structures-beneath-the-pyramids-2025":"The claim is that radar scans have found huge built structures deep under the pyramids of Giza in Egypt, laid out like an energy grid. If true, the builders put something engineered far beneath the monuments. The first-hand source is Filippo Biondi, an Italian radar scientist who has been on Michels's show. He used a method called synthetic aperture radar Doppler tomography, a radar technique for imaging what lies below the ground. Biondi says his team counts 'four plus four tubes that are descending underneath,' linking the base of the pyramid 'to something that is located at the bottom,' and he credits colleagues named Corrado Malanga and Armando Mei. Michels, retelling it on several episodes, speaks of eight pillars, coils and columns 'in this shape of an energy grid,' under the chamber of the Khafre pyramid. He says the team also used artificial intelligence and found what looks like 'a small city or energy grid.' Michels also asks why, if the work dates to 2022, it is only coming out now. Joe Rogan says the structures may go 'as deep as 2 km,' and a researcher named Jay Anderson calls the underground signals 'undeniable,' though he does not describe their shape.","engineers-describe-xf-131-super-sentinel-to-bill-mcdonald-1992":"In 1992, two engineers from Northrop and two from Lockheed, fed up with the secrecy around their work, met a man named Bill McDonald and described a triangular craft to him, and he drew it as they talked. The craft was called the XF-131 Super Sentinel, an 'ARV', meaning a human-built craft said to copy alien technology, and the story is set in California's Mojave Desert. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this as McDonald's account, which would mean American aerospace firms had built such machines.","enhanced-pilot-photo-shows-slanted-hangar-doors-at-papoose-l":"A high-quality aerial photograph, taken by a pilot about 17 miles from Papoose Lake, a dry lake bed in the Nevada desert, is said to show slanted hangar doors once it is enhanced. Michels tells how a researcher named Luigi Vendittelli showed the photo to Bob Lazar, a man who says he once worked at a hidden facility there, while Michels spent two days with Lazar. If the doors are real, they would point to a hidden base where Lazar says one stood.","enoch-s-ascent-and-transformation-into-metatron":"Enoch, a figure from the earliest chapters of the Bible, is said to have gone up to heaven alive and been turned into an angel named Metatron. Michels tells it himself, drawing on the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish book left out of most Bibles, in the translation R. H. Charles published in 1917. He says Enoch \"goes up to heaven, he walks with God, and then he turns into Medatron, this angel\", and he presents it as a man transformed by contact with something beyond. The Book of Enoch does tell of the ascent. Enoch was \"raised aloft on the chariots of the spirit\". In his own words, \"he translated my spirit into the heaven of heavens\", where he saw \"a structure built of crystals\" with \"tongues of living fire\". There, \"my whole body became relaxed, And my spirit was transfigured.\" Enoch becoming Metatron by name is told in 3 Enoch, a later Hebrew book from the Hekhalot writings, a body of Jewish mystical texts about journeys to heaven. Going from this transfigured Enoch to the angel Metatron is that later book's step, which this one neither states nor rules out, and Michels tells it as if it came from the Book of Enoch.","enochian-tale-found-in-dead-sea-scroll-manuscripts":"The story told in the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text that most Bibles leave out, is not found only in that book, according to the researcher Tim Alberino. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that the tale 'is attested not just in Enoch but in several Dead Sea Scroll manuscripts,' the ancient scrolls found at Qumran, 'including the Book of Giants,' and he also points to the Book of Jubilees and the Genesis Apocryphon. His point is that the story was old and widely copied, not a one-off.","enrico-fermi-thinks-about-aliens-at-los-alamos-1940":"Enrico Fermi, the physicist whose work helped make splitting the atom possible, is said to have spent his off hours thinking about aliens while working on the Manhattan Project at Los Alamos. Michels tells this himself without naming a source, and uses it to show that a scientist \"as conventionally well regarded as it gets\" took the question seriously. The Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb tells a fuller version. He says that about 70 years ago Fermi went to lunch with colleagues and they talked about how likely it is that extraterrestrials are out there. Loeb names no place for the lunch.","ent-specialists-find-otolith-damage-in-havana-syndrome":"Ear, nose and throat specialists found that in most cases of Havana syndrome, the unexplained illnesses reported by US diplomats and officials abroad, the damage was to the otolith, a tiny balance organ inside the inner ear. A guest named Chris Gilbert tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, the consistent pattern would point to one physical cause behind the cases.","entities-based-on-the-moon-airdropping-tech-to-earth":"The idea here is that beings based on the Moon have been dropping pieces of technology on Earth \"here and there.\" Michels puts it as a question to his guest Randall Carlson, in an episode titled \"The Moon Is An Alien Spaceship,\" and the answer is not part of the telling. If true, it would mean some human advances were handed to us from the Moon.","entity-pulls-him-out-of-his-body-and-scolds-him-out-of-depre-1970":"One night in Germany in 1970, Joe McMoneagle says, he was woken by an entity that snatched him out of his body and then scolded him until he came out of a depression. McMoneagle, who later worked as a remote viewer for the US Army, a psychic trying to see distant targets by mind alone, tells Michels this as something that happened to him. He offers it as a turning point that changed the course of his life.","envelope-lettering-transferred-onto-developed-x-ray-film":"Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects people say are alien implants, says a doctor he calls Dr. Builder showed him one X-ray out of about 200 on which the lettering printed on the outside of its envelope had somehow been transferred onto the developed film. Colbern says he saw it himself, and tells it as a sign that something unusual had affected the film.","epiphany-during-jean-houston-s-mystery-school-lecture-on-the":"The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove says that while he listened to a lecture on The Cipher of Genesis, a book by Carlos Suares, at the \"mystery school\" run by the teacher Jean Houston, he suddenly \"got it\" and had \"the experience of how it happened, how God created this universe.\" Mishlove tells it as his own experience, and also mentions Robert Masters, who wrote the book Varieties of Psychedelic Experience with Houston. For him it is a moment when an old text about creation became something he felt directly.","epstein-as-the-front-for-a-project-or-special-access-program-1990":"Jeffrey Epstein may have been the public face of a secret government project, or a special access programme, meaning a tightly restricted classified programme, and not the one really in charge. The mathematician Eric Weinstein speaks on the show of \"the Jeffrey Epstein special access project or whatever it was\" and says he does not think Epstein was running it. Weinstein names no source, and if he were right, others would have been behind what went on around Epstein.","epstein-blackmail-file-on-senior-national-security-official-2000":"A very senior person in America's national security establishment is said to be \"kept on a short leash\" because a blackmail file exists on them from their time in Jeffrey Epstein's circle. Steven Greer, a guest who argues that UFO secrets are controlled by a global cabal, tells Michels this as something he was told, without naming the official. If true, it would mean blackmail is one way people with power over the secrets are kept quiet.","epstein-circle-s-gravity-interest-as-an-electrogravitics-ufo-2000":"Jeffrey Epstein's interest in defying gravity was really an attempt to crack the UFO question, according to the philosopher Jason Jorjani. On the show he puts it as a suspicion: the interest was in electrogravitics, the idea of using electricity to counter gravity, and in the effect named after Thomas Townsend Brown, all \"part of the UFO phenomenon that they're trying to crack.\" In his own piece Ocean Outlaw: A Portrait of Ghislaine Maxwell, published in July 2025, he says it more firmly, writing that Epstein \"was interested in electrogravitics or what is believed to be the power and propulsion system of UFOs\", with the same tie to UFOs in both.","epstein-email-about-killing-pons-and-cold-fusion-1989":"Jeffrey Epstein is said to have bragged in an email that \"I killed Pons\", meaning Stanley Pons and Martin Fleischmann, the two chemists who claimed in 1989 to have made cold fusion work, that is, nuclear energy produced at room temperature. Michels brings it up himself on four episodes, citing the Epstein emails, which would mean Epstein helped bury a possible new energy source. In one telling he adds, crediting the journalist Whitney Webb and her book One Nation Under Blackmail, that Epstein sold PROMIS, software from Sandia National Labs that Michels says siphoned off American nuclear secrets.","epstein-files-show-interest-in-people-with-psi-abilities":"Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official, says the Epstein files show that Jeffrey Epstein's network took an interest in people with psi abilities, meaning claimed powers of the mind such as telepathy, along with empathy or \"consciousness abilities.\" He tells Michels it is \"provably in the files.\" If true, it would tie Epstein's circle to the hunt for people with unusual mental gifts.","epstein-prison-cell-video-metadata-edited-2019":"The video from Jeffrey Epstein's prison cell, which was supposed to point to suicide, had its metadata edited, likely with Adobe Premiere video software. Metadata is the hidden record a file keeps about how it was made. Michels says Wired magazine, which he calls a fairly conventional tech publication, reported this, and he raises it to suggest the official account of Epstein's death was tampered with.","epstein-s-eugenics-plan-to-create-his-own-race-1990":"Jeffrey Epstein was obsessed with eugenics and with creating his own race, a plan tied to Zorro Ranch, his property in New Mexico. Jay Anderson tells Michels this second-hand, passing on what he was told by Juliet Byron, a woman who presents herself as a witness to Epstein's world.","epstein-s-gravity-conference-and-submarine-dives-with-hawkin-2000":"A philosopher named Jason Jorjani says that in the 2000s Jeffrey Epstein took people, including Stephen Hawking, down in submarines \"to study quantum gravitational anomalies\" in the Bahamas basin, in the Bermuda Triangle, an ocean area long linked to unexplained disappearances; he tells Michels this citing the researcher Eric Weinstein and others, and his own essay. That essay, Ocean Outlaw: A Portrait of Ghislaine Maxwell, published by Jorjani on 29 July 2025, says Epstein's associate Ghislaine Maxwell piloted the \"Atlantis\" submarine that took Epstein and Hawking \"down to the seabed of the Bermuda Triangle to study quantum magnetic anomalies there,\" and that Epstein's Virgin Islands foundation sponsored a 2006 conference called \"Confronting Gravity\" with top physicists from Caltech, MIT, Cambridge, Harvard, CERN and elsewhere. Read beside the podcast, the essay names a single dive where Jorjani speaks of people taken down in submarines, and says magnetic where he says gravitational, though the conference title ties the gravity theme to Epstein's circle.","epstein-s-gravity-conferences-casimir-obsession-and-knowledg-2000":"Michels says the financier Jeffrey Epstein hosted conferences on gravity, was \"obsessed\" with the Casimir effect (a tiny force that appears between objects placed very close together in empty space), and knew about a theory of the researcher Eric Weinstein, an old colleague of Michels. He says \"this is all in the emails,\" meaning Epstein's emails, and calls it \"all fact.\" In another episode he mentions only the Casimir obsession, tying it to an interest in new ways to propel craft.","epstein-s-keycard-access-to-harvard-evolutionary-dynamics-pr-2000":"Jeffrey Epstein is said to have been involved in an evolutionary dynamics program in biology at Harvard in the 2000s, and to have had keycard access to it. A philosopher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, passing on what he links to the scientists Robert Trivers and George Church. If true, it would put Epstein physically inside a Harvard research program, not only funding it from outside.","epstein-s-listening-post-at-one-brattle-square-2000":"Jeffrey Epstein kept what the mathematician Eric Weinstein calls 'another listening post' at One Brattle Square in Cambridge, Massachusetts, a place where he could keep close to people and hear what they were working on in the 2000s. Weinstein tells Michels this from his own reading of the released Epstein files, and he names the Harvard scientist Martin Nowak in connection with it. If he is right, Epstein had placed himself right next to Harvard's scientists.","epstein-s-obsession-with-gravity-propulsion-and-physicists-1990":"Jeffrey Epstein, Michels says, was obsessed with secret science, especially gravity and propulsion, and flew physicists out to his island in the US Virgin Islands: the physicists Kip Thorne and Murray Gell-Mann and Stephen Hawking, and he funded the physicist Lawrence Krauss with about $250,000. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, calling it one of the lesser-known threads about Epstein, the suggestion being that Epstein was chasing exotic propulsion physics.","epstein-s-zorro-ranch-near-los-alamos-physicists-1993":"Jeffrey Epstein is said to have set up his New Mexico estate, Zorro Ranch, on purpose to be near physicists retiring from the Los Alamos nuclear weapons laboratory, and Michels takes this as a sign of how loose Department of Energy security was. Michels tells it himself on several episodes, sourcing it to Steve Bannon's taped interview with Epstein, which he places near the end of Epstein's life in 2019, where Epstein supposedly told Bannon why he chose the spot; the story dates the move to 1993, though one of Michels's tellings gives no year.","eric-bard-replicates-the-anomalies-with-a-neodymium-magnet-2020":"A witness at Skinwalker Ranch named Erik Bard reproduced the ranch's strange effects in about 2020 by \"focusing intense electromagnetic force\" with a neodymium magnet, a very strong kind of permanent magnet. Skinwalker Ranch is a property in Utah known for reports of odd phenomena. Its owner, a businessman named Brandon Fugal, tells Michels he watched this happen and points to the first season of The Secret of Skinwalker Ranch, a television series filmed there. If true, it would suggest that some of the ranch's oddities have an electromagnetic cause that can be tested.","eric-davis-at-closed-hearing-nhi-control-disclosure-2025":"At a closed-door hearing in Congress in 2025, a physicist named Eric Davis is said to have stated that \"the NHI are controlling disclosure,\" meaning that non-human intelligence itself decides how and when the public learns about it. Michels tells this twice, once placing Davis at the closed session alongside Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who ran a small UFO study office, and the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb, and once calling it his favourite line from Davis while noting that he disagrees with Davis about the work of an inventor named Townsend Brown. If true, it would mean the timing of any revelation is not in human hands at all.","eric-davis-calls-townsend-brown-a-phony-2024":"Eric Davis, a physicist who has worked on government studies of UFOs, is said to have contacted Michels and told him that Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor whose experiments are often cited as early antigravity work, was \"a phony\". Michels recounts this himself on an episode with the UFO activist Steven Greer. It matters because it would knock down a figure many people see as a pioneer.","eric-davis-craft-defying-physics-but-no-physicists":"Eric Davis, a scientist who has worked with the Pentagon, is said to have admitted that a secret program held objects that broke the laws of physics but had no physicists studying them: \"We have things that defy the laws of physics and no physicists.\" The mathematician Eric Weinstein quotes him to Michels. Michels repeats it twice in later episodes, once as Davis's answer to a direct question: none of the people on the program \"were theoretical physicists.\" If true, it would mean the objects were in the hands of people without the training to understand them.","eric-davis-denies-alien-reproduction-vehicles":"A physicist named Eric W. Davis says that no American aircraft has been built using recovered non-human technology, and that there has been \"no one to one reproduction\" of such a craft. People in this field call these supposed copies \"alien reproduction vehicles\". Davis said it in an episode with an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb, telling him there were \"definitely no aircraft systems that have leveraged anything.\" Taken at face value, his answer rules out human-built copies of alien craft.","eric-davis-dia-report-on-teleportation":"A scientist named Eric W. Davis wrote a report on teleportation for the Defense Intelligence Agency, the Pentagon's military intelligence arm. Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist who holds patents for unusual craft designs, tells Michels he saw it among \"those DIA reports\" and points to it as backing for his own ideas. He describes the report but quotes nothing from it.","eric-davis-helped-write-a-book-for-bigelow-during-aawsap-2008":"During AAWSAP, a Pentagon-funded study of UFOs and related strange phenomena that began in 2008, the physicist Eric Davis was helping write a book for the businessman Robert Bigelow. That is what Jack says: he is Michels' co-host and technical correspondent, who goes only by that pseudonym and says he works in defense and aerospace. He says it \"turns out\" Davis was writing \"that book\" at the time, which would tie a government study to a private book project.","eric-davis-lied-to-uapgerb-about-alien-reproduction-vehicles-2020":"Eric W. Davis, a figure in government UFO research, lied in person about craft built by copying alien technology, according to an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb. UAPGerb tells Michels that in the 2020s Davis 'lied to my face about the success of derivative technologies and alien reproduction vehicles', meaning the human-made technologies and craft said to be copied from recovered alien ones; elsewhere Davis is reported as denying such vehicles exist, so if UAPGerb is right, the denial would itself hide a working programme.","eric-davis-offers-full-testimony-for-blanket-immunity":"A physicist named Eric W. Davis, who has advised the Pentagon on UFO questions, is said to have offered to tell everything he knows if he were given blanket legal immunity. The mathematician Eric Weinstein tells Michels that Davis says so on record in the most recent film by the documentary maker James Fox. If true, it would mean Davis holds secrets he feels he cannot legally share.","eric-davis-s-notes-from-the-advanced-physics-theoretical-wor-2000":"The physicist Eric W. Davis handed over his own notes from the Advanced Physics Theoretical Working Group, a group that met in the 2000s to discuss advanced physics, according to a UFO researcher named Grant Cameron. Cameron tells Michels he got the notes from Davis himself, and that they mattered a great deal to his own research. What the notes said, he does not spell out here.","eric-davis-saw-classified-crash-retrieval-evidence-with-alie":"A guest named Eric W. Davis says he has seen classified evidence of a crash retrieval, meaning the recovery of a crashed craft that is not of human make, and that it showed \"a craft and there's alien bodies.\" Davis tells Michels this as something he saw himself, and if true it would mean the US holds a downed craft and its dead crew.","eric-davis-says-the-head-of-crash-retrieval-refused-to-talk-2000":"The physicist Eric W. Davis says that in the 2000s he wanted to question the head of the crash retrieval program, an alleged secret effort to recover crashed unknown craft, but \"he refused to talk to us.\" Davis tells this to Michels and ties the story to Glenn Gaffney. If true, the program had a known leader who chose silence.","eric-davis-teleportation-study-for-mitre-2019":"In 2019 a physicist named Eric W. Davis is said to have carried out a study of teleportation for the MITRE Corporation, a research company that works for the US government. Michels says this comes from a slide deck that was 'not really leaked but kind of rediscovered'. If true, it would show government contractors quietly studying very exotic physics in recent years.","eric-davis-writes-of-an-air-force-five-dimensional-study-of":"An Air Force study is said to have proposed that, \"by assuming a five-dimensional continuum,\" a link between electricity and gravity could be worked out to explain the Biefeld-Brown effect, a claimed thrust from high-voltage electrical devices named partly for the inventor Townsend Brown. Michels reads this from the book Frontiers of Propulsion Science, where he says the scientist Eric W. Davis \"concedes\" the point. In a later episode he names the book again and credits Davis together with Marc Millis. If the study holds up, the Air Force once took electric anti-gravity seriously.","eric-haseltine-s-idea-of-a-massless-object-2004":"The object Navy pilots saw in the 2004 Nimitz encounter, a white oval shaped like a Tic Tac mint, might have had no mass at all. Michels says the idea came up in his research from a former official named Eric Haseltine, who asked, \"What if the object is massless?\" and pointed to force equals mass times acceleration. The point is that if something has no mass, it would need no force to make the sudden moves the pilots described.","eric-hecker-raytheon-neutrino-system-under-south-pole-ice-2011":"A man named Eric Hecker is said to have worked for the defence company Raytheon in 2011, helping put in a neutrino system a mile under the ice at the South Pole. Neutrinos are tiny particles that pass through almost anything. The UFO researcher Steven Greer repeats this, saying Hecker \"came forward\" a couple of years ago about a \"nutrino light system\" buried in the ice. Jay Anderson, another guest, adds that Hecker said he noticed energy \"being transferred from one area to another area where it wasn't supposed to be going.\" Michels himself calls it \"a neutrino emitter and detector\" and links it to the earthquake in Christchurch, New Zealand, which would mean the system could send out energy as well as just measure it.","eric-hecker-s-childhood-involvement-with-the-stargate-progra":"Eric Hecker says that as a child growing up on Long Island, New York, he had a \"very peculiar history\" of \"nefarious activity\" with the Stargate program, the US government's secret effort to use psychics for spying. He says this on the show as his own experience, in an episode with Jay Anderson and Kurt Metzger. If true, it would mean the programme involved children.","eric-henry-wang-s-name-on-dtic-engineering-documents-1950":"A man named Eric Henry Wang, whose name the stories link to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio in the 1950s, was a real engineer, according to a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb. He tells Michels that you can search DTIC, the Defense Technical Information Center that keeps the military's technical reports, and find old engineering documents with Wang's name on them. He quotes nothing from them. The documents would show that Wang existed and worked on defence engineering, but they leave open whether he did any of the secret work the stories give him.","eric-henry-wang-special-projects-at-wright-patterson-1945":"An engineer named Eric Henry Wang, who headed special projects at Wright-Patterson, the Air Force base in Ohio, was moved to Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, where Michels says he probably worked on \"exotic flight stuff\". Michels tells it on three episodes, drawing on a researcher named William Steinman and on Wang's obituary in a mechanical engineering magazine. In one telling he says Wang's whole department moved in 1956 to the Sandia Laboratory complex at Kirtland, which would point to a hidden effort on advanced flight technology.","eric-lopes-and-the-second-capture-of-a-live-creature-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, two military police officers named Eric Lopes and Marco Eli Chereze are said to have come across a strange creature and captured it alive. According to a documentary narrator, heard in two episodes, it happened on January 20, about three hours after three girls saw the creature. The officers were driving and watching for anything unusual \"when suddenly it runs right across their path.\" The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels that Lopes was \"the alleged driver during the second capture of one of the alive creatures,\" which would mean the military caught more than one living being that day.","eric-lopes-refuses-to-talk-and-threatens-film-crew":"The documentary filmmaker James Fox says that when he and his crew went to Varginha, a town in Brazil, looking for a man named Eric Lopes, they were told: \"If you're here to talk about the ET, he won't talk,\" and were then threatened with being shot. Fox tells Michels this as someone who was there, and the refusal suggests that the subject of a creature said to have been seen in Varginha is still one people there fear.","eric-lopes-went-into-hiding-marco-chereze-died-weeks-later-1996":"In the Brazilian town of Varginha in 1996, where witnesses say a strange living creature was caught, the driver involved, a man named Eric Lopes, has since gone into hiding, and the officer who handled the live being, Marcos Charice, died a couple of weeks later. The narrator of a documentary clip played on Michels's show says this, naming both men. If true, it would mean the people closest to the creature paid for it, one by vanishing and one with his life.","eric-walker-claimed-majestic-12-membership-azadehdel-convers":"Eric A. Walker, a man connected to Penn State, is said to have claimed he belonged to Majestic 12, a group that some documents describe as a secret panel overseeing crashed UFOs. Michels says Walker \"seemed to claim\" this, and ties it to a conversation Walker had with a man named Henry Azadehdel, bringing in the work of the UFO researcher Grant Cameron. If true, it would be a rare admission from someone said to be inside such a group.","eric-walker-interview-what-do-you-know-about-esp-and-the-con-1987":"In a 1987 phone interview, a man named Eric A. Walker, whom the researcher Grant Cameron calls the head of the whole UFO program, is said to have cut off the person questioning him and asked, 'What do you know about ESP?' Cameron, who tells Michels about the interview from its transcripts, says the questioner had no answer, and Walker told him that 'unless you understand about ESP and how it works, you will not be taken in by the control group,' meaning the group he said ran the secret, adding that 'very few people understand how it works.' If true, it would mean psychic ability was a condition for getting inside.","eric-walker-on-trw-board":"Eric A. Walker, a scientist who came up earlier in the conversation, is said to have sat on the board of directors of TRW, a major American aerospace and defense company, and maybe even to have chaired it. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this, citing the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, who has talked about it. The point is that it ties Walker to a large defense contractor.","eric-wang-s-secret-ufo-work-at-kirtland-per-his-wife-1950":"An engineer named Eric Henry Wang worked on secret UFO-related projects at Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico in the 1950s or 60s, according to his wife, Maria Wang. Michels recounts that a UFO researcher named Bill Steinman got in touch with her and that she told him so; if true, it would place UFO work inside a real military base.","eric-wargo-remote-viewing-as-precognition":"Remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant places or hidden things with the mind alone, may really be precognition, a glimpse of the viewer's own future. Michels passes this on from the writer Eric Wargo, who argues it in his book Time Loops.","eric-wargo-s-time-loops-theory-of-precognition":"The writer Eric Wargo argues in his book Time Loops that people can sense the future, and that the brain may work like a hybrid of an ordinary computer and a quantum one, able to reach information from moments that have not happened yet. Michels brings the book up on several episodes. In one he passes on Wargo's idea of a 'long body', a self stretched out through time, and in another he says Wargo, a past guest on his show, writes about people foreseeing events in their own lives. If it were right, it would mean information can travel backward in time to the mind.","eric-weinstein-joins-galileo-project-geometric-unity-uap-phy-2021":"The mathematician Eric Weinstein is said to have joined the Galileo Project, a Harvard-based effort to search the sky for UAP, or unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. The line comes in Michels's 2022 episode with the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb, attributed to Michels: \"My colleague Eric Weinstein recently joined the Galileo project. We sometimes speculate about the physics of UAPs.\" It matters because it would put a theoretical thinker to work on how such objects might move.","eric-weinstein-s-secret-branch-of-physics-tied-to-private-ae-1950":"The researcher Eric Weinstein is said to have become convinced that the United States ran a secret branch of physics from the 1950s on, involving the physicists John Wheeler, Richard Feynman and Bryce DeWitt. Michels tells it twice himself, as Weinstein's view: once he says these men had far more contact with private aerospace companies than met the eye, and once he adds the physicist Freeman Dyson and simply speaks of a secret physics program. If true, some of the most famous science of the century would have had a hidden side.","erich-von-daniken-on-templar-secrets":"When Michels asked Erich von Däniken, the Swiss writer famous for arguing that ancient astronauts visited Earth, about the Templars, the medieval order of knights, von Däniken told him \"there are secrets that I cannot talk about.\" Michels tells this himself, from his own conversation with von Däniken, as a hint that the writer knows more than he will say.","eridu-found-in-the-1800s-its-ziggurat-never-excavated-1807":"Eridu, an ancient city in southern Iraq, was found in the 1800s, but diggers only excavated the main part of the city. They found clay tablets and other finds and sent them to the British Museum, yet never dug into its ziggurat, the city's great stepped temple tower. Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient civilizations, tells Michels this, drawing on the Wikipedia entry for Eridu, and his point is that whatever lies under the ziggurat has never been looked at.","esa-scientists-recognize-tyler-at-the-vatican-observatory-2010":"In the 2010s, scientists from the European Space Agency at the Vatican Observatory in Castel Gandolfo, Italy, recognised a man known as 'Tyler' the moment he was introduced by his real name, according to the scholar Diana Pasulka, who says she was there. She tells Michels 'the whole place just erupted', as if they knew him well. Michels, retelling it, gives the real name as Tim Taylor, says the scientists knew who he was, and adds that this got him led into the Vatican archives without an escort, which would suggest Taylor is far better known in space science circles than the public knows.","esalen-contact-exercise-brings-blue-plasma-orbs-2024":"In 2024, during a group exercise meant to make contact at the Esalen Institute, a retreat centre on the coast at Big Sur, California, Jordan Jose says blue orbs of what he calls plasma rose out of the ocean and came within about 50 yards of the people there. Jose, who says he was there, tells Michels about it and mentions the journalist Ross Coulthart; when he returns to it later he speaks of about 30 people, all deeply heart-centred, without naming Esalen. He tells it as proof that a group's intent can draw these lights in.","esgridge-s-poems-work-at-marshall-and-alzofon-s-nucleonic-sp-1930":"Ideas for unusual propulsion physics have a long history, according to Jack, Michels' co-host, who uses a pseudonym and says he works in sensitive defense areas. He points to work by Richard Esgridge at NASA's Marshall Space Flight Center, done under a program known as POEMS (an acronym he does not spell out), and to \"nucleonic spin approaches\" by a scientist named Alzofon, which Jack says Alzofon went over with the physicist Richard Feynman in the 1930s. He gives no source. If true, it would mean this line of research was being explored decades before today's debates over such technology.","esoteric-and-occult-imagery-on-nro-mission-patches":"The mission patches of the NRO, the National Reconnaissance Office, the US agency that runs spy satellites, are said to carry esoteric and occult imagery. A guest named Chris Ramsay tells Michels he looked into \"a lot of those patches,\" the launch and mission patches the NRO makes, and found them \"really interesting.\" If so, it would suggest an interest in hidden or mystical symbols inside a secretive spy agency.","esp-tests-in-a-faraday-cage-at-the-round-table-1950":"In the early 1950s, at the Round Table Foundation, a research group in Rockland, Maine, someone is said to have built a Faraday cage, a metal enclosure that blocks electrical and radio signals, and used it for some of the first tests of extrasensory perception inside one. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels this as he understands it, without naming a source. The point of such a test would be to show that a mind could pick up information that no radio signal could carry.","esp-training-with-handler-meg-after-interns-hired-2008":"Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO-related program as a child, tells Michels that around 2008 to 2010, at Baker Victory Services, a children's services provider in Western New York, \"a team of interns get hired\" about two months after he arrived and worked only on his case. He says they trained him in ESP, or extrasensory perception, under a handler he knew as Meg. He speaks as the child it happened to.","essa-truck-mechanic-repaired-damaged-suspensions-1996":"In 1996, a mechanic at the EsSA military base in Brazil says he had to make heavy repairs to the suspensions of the base's trucks. This was the time when, the story goes, Brazil's army caught a living alien and took it to a hospital. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels he had just spoken to the man, whom he calls 'allegedly' the trucks' mechanic, and he raises it as a detail that would fit the trucks having carried something out of the ordinary.","estrada-cotton-joints-and-cyanoacrylate-glue-2023":"The three-fingered \"alien\" bodies shown in Peru were put together with cotton at the joints and cyanoacrylate, the chemical in superglue, which gives them \"quite an interesting hardness\". A guest named Flavio Estrada tells Michels this from his own examination of the figures, offering it as evidence that they were made by people, not left by any unknown being.","ether-drift-detected-on-mount-wilson":"An experiment on Mount Wilson in California is said to have picked up the Earth's motion through the ether, the invisible medium that physicists once thought filled all of space. Steve Colbern, a researcher who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, says this on the show. He credits it to a scientist he calls Millikan, says it was not a direct detection, adds \"I believe it was\" about the place, and names no source. If true, it would revive an idea that mainstream physics set aside long ago.","ethiopian-churches-keep-the-book-of-enoch-in-their-canon":"Around the 4th century AD, the Tewahedo Orthodox Christians and the Beta Israel Jewish community of Ethiopia are said to have added the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text about angels coming down to live among people, to their official scriptures. A guest named Tim Alberino tells Michels this without naming a source, though he relies on R. H. Charles's English translation of the book, which the record dates to 1917. He mentions it to show that one ancient tradition still treats the text as holy.","ets-arrived-en-masse-after-the-first-atomic-bomb-1945":"Extraterrestrials began arriving on Earth in large numbers right after the first atomic bomb went off in 1945, says the UFO activist Steven Greer. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that after that test \"the ETs came in in force, en masse.\" If true, it would mean beings from elsewhere took notice of humanity because we had gained nuclear weapons.","ets-teleport-into-ce-5-circle":"Extraterrestrials have teleported right into the middle of a group trying to contact them, according to Steven Greer, a doctor who leads CE-5 gatherings, short for \"close encounters of the fifth kind\", where people sit in a circle and try to call beings from elsewhere through meditation. Greer tells Michels that he and his groups \"have had experiences where the ETs themselves have teleported right into our circle,\" which, if true, would mean contact can be invited.","ets-tell-government-leaders-to-develop-spiritually":"Aliens have supposedly told government leaders that humanity must develop spiritually before they will share more technology or materials. Dave Rossi, a guest who says aliens taught him advanced physics, passes this on to Michels from a man named Dan Sheehan. Rossi calls these \"alleged stories\" of alien visits. If true, it would mean the visitors are holding back their knowledge until people grow up morally.","eugenicist-elites-become-the-nordics-1890":"The eugenics-minded elites of the US, Britain and Germany between about 1890 and 1939 are said to \"become the Nordics,\" the tall, fair, human-looking beings that some alien contact stories describe. A philosopher named Jason Jorjani makes this argument on the show, naming the birth-control campaigner Margaret Sanger among those elites and drawing on a work called Closer Encounters. His point is that the Nordic beings of these stories would mirror that era's ideal of a superior race.","european-crash-retrieval-initiative-and-its-cold-case":"A European group led from Sweden, called the European Crash Retrieval Initiative, is looking into reports of crashed unknown craft and is working an old unsolved case, according to the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, who says she takes part alongside Thomas Pinder and Alex Tal. She tells Michels it is 'a cold case that I cannot go into details on, but it's super cool, super scary', which would mean such crash claims are not only American.","european-leader-confides-about-ufos-on-a-park-walk-1995":"Sometime in the late 1990s, an unnamed European leader went for a long walk in a park with a former president of a Russian republic named Kiran Ilum Xenov and told him 'something very interesting' about UFOs. Xenov tells Michels this himself but does not name the leader or say what he was told, though it would mean that heads of state talk about UFOs among themselves.","european-research-council-s-10-million-positive-geometries-i-2026":"A line of physics research called positive geometries, linked to the physicist Nima Arkani-Hamed, has grown so large that the European Research Council has put 10 million euros into an initiative on it. That is according to a scientist named Donald Hoffman, who argues that our brains evolved to hide reality from us. He says it on the show in passing, without naming a source, as a sign that the ideas behind his view are being taken seriously.","every-drilling-crew-suffers-equipment-failure":"Every drilling crew brought to bore into the mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, has had dramatic equipment failures, according to the ranch's owner. He tells Michels he saw this happen, and that it was every expert they brought out, not just some. He tells it as a sign that something under the mesa does not want to be reached.","every-recovered-craft-gets-a-reverse-engineered-copy":"Every craft the government recovers is said to get a copy built by reverse engineering, meaning engineers take the original apart to learn how it works and then try to replicate \"that technology or that specific craft.\" Michael Herrera, a guest who says he saw a 300-foot craft in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels this as something that was \"relayed to me\" by people he does not name. If true, it would mean there are human-made versions of these craft.","evidence-of-humans-in-the-americas-130-000-years-ago":"There is evidence that humans were living in the Americas as long as 130,000 years ago, which is ten times older than the usual date given for when people first settled there. That is what the researcher Graham Hancock tells Michels, citing no particular source. If he is right, the standard story of how and when people reached the Americas would be badly out of date.","ewen-cameron-examines-rudolf-hess-to-see-whether-he-was-a-do-1940":"In England in the 1940s, the psychiatrist Ewen Cameron is said to have examined Rudolf Hess, Hitler's deputy, who was then held by the British, to work out whether the prisoner was really Hess or a double. Peter Levenda, a writer on the history of intelligence and the occult, tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would mean the Allies themselves doubted who they were holding.","exeter-wobbling-disc-1964":"In 1964 in Exeter, New Hampshire, four people, two of them police officers, are said to have watched a large disc wobble in the air about 100 feet away and 100 feet up. A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this without naming a source, as a case where trained, credible witnesses saw something up close.","exodus-details-written-by-someone-living-in-new-kingdom-egyp":"The details in the Exodus story could only have been written by someone who was living in Egypt when the Exodus happened. Luke Caverns, a researcher who argues that the pyramids are not what we think, makes this case to Michels as his own reading of the Bible, the King James Version of 1611 among the texts he relies on. If he is right, the story would be a memory from that time rather than something made up later.","exodus-thrusters-weigh-less-on-a-scale-patent-examiner-revie-2020":"A company called Exodus Propulsion has built a device that gets lighter on a scale when it is switched on and heavier when it is flipped over. That is the claim of Charles Buhler, a scientist the episode bills as a NASA chief, who says he took part in the work: \"we put stuff on a scale and we turn it on, it gets lighter,\" he tells Michels, adding that the results are \"going through a peer review\" and that a second patent application has been filed and is being looked at by patent examiners. If true, it would be a push with no fuel thrown out the back, a kind of working antigravity.","exoprobe-telescope-network-to-find-and-recover-an-et-probe":"The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel describes ExoProbe, a project of hers to build a network of telescopes in New Mexico to search for an alien probe near Earth. She tells Michels that the goal is to \"pick up the object and bring it down to the earth,\" because what she would most like to do is \"bring down an actual ET probe.\"","exorcist-boy-became-aerospace-engineer-and-urinated-excessiv-1949":"The boy whose 1949 case became the basis for the first Exorcist film grew up to be an aerospace engineer, and he urinated excessively after his exorcism. A scholar of religion named Diana Pasulka tells Michels this, repeating what she was told by someone else. If true, it ties one of the most famous possession stories to a later career in flight and space.","experiencers-account-of-breeding-experiments-and-ecological":"Nonhuman beings are said to be doing breeding experiments on people \"for reasons we don't know\" and to be giving out messages about the environment. A guest named Bob McGwier, whom the episode's title calls a CIA contractor, passes this on from experiencers, people who say they have had contact with such beings, and is careful to call it \"their story, not my story.\" If true, the contact would have a purpose of its own: breeding humans and warning them about the planet.","experiment-current-induced-in-one-stone-arcs-to-the-next-2017":"If you put voltage and electric current into one stone of the kind used in the pyramids, it arcs, like a spark, into the next stone. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher who argues the pyramids were built to make power and who says an alien told him their true purpose, says he and a collaborator named Ben showed this by experiment. He says it twice on the show, once naming Ben, as evidence that the stones could have carried electricity.","expired-first-patent-and-a-new-second-patent-raised-by-flint":"Filippo Biondi, a researcher who claims to have found a vast energy grid beneath the pyramids of Egypt, tells Michels that his first patent, filed with the US patent office, \"at the moment is expired,\" and that he has a newer second patent. He is answering a point raised in a video by a critic named Flint Dibble, and he speaks from his own knowledge of his filings.","explorer-1-launched-on-imbolc-1958":"Explorer 1, the first US satellite, built by the Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL, the NASA-linked rocket lab in California), was launched on January 31st, 1958, which Michels points out falls on Imbolc, an old pagan festival held at the start of February. Michels makes the link himself, as part of a wider argument about pagan ritual in the space program. If the timing was deliberate, it would mean the early American space effort was shaped by occult beliefs.","extended-state-dmt-trials-yield-encounters-with-sentient-oth-2020":"In trials at Imperial College London and the University of California San Diego, volunteers are kept under DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, for longer stretches than usual, and they keep coming back with reports of meeting \"sentient others\". The writer Graham Hancock, who writes about lost ancient civilisations, tells Michels this, drawing on researchers such as Andrew Gallimore and Rick Strassman but naming no particular study. If the reports mean what he thinks, the drug would be opening contact with beings that exist apart from the mind.","extraction-shaft-under-the-queen-s-chamber-removed-product-s":"A shaft under the Queen's Chamber of the Great Pyramid at Giza was part of a system for drawing a 'product solution' out of the pyramid, according to the researcher Jeffrey Drum. Drum tells Michels he has shown this in his book, and his idea treats the pyramid as a working plant that made something, not as a tomb.","extradition-fight-and-we-want-to-see-him-fry-embassy-meeting-2000":"While Gary McKinnon, a British hacker who says he found signs of a secret NASA space programme, was fighting extradition to the United States in the 2000s, a US official said they wanted him executed, according to McKinnon himself. He tells Michels that Ed Gibson, an attaché at the US embassy in London, met his lawyer and said, 'We want to see him fry', which McKinnon takes as an 'electric chair reference'.","extraterrestrial-being-interviewed-behind-a-two-way-mirror-a-1950":"Sometime in the 1950s or 60s, a group was taken to Area 51, the secret Nevada test base, and on to S4, a site said to lie nearby, where they went down several floors to a large room with a two-way mirror and a living extraterrestrial being behind it. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this second-hand, as what a man named Albert Stein told him, and links it to what he calls the S4 interview card files; if true, it would mean the US government held and questioned a living alien.","eyewitness-accounts-of-nazi-nuclear-tests-1944":"In 1944 and 1945, what looked like a Nazi nuclear test is said to have been seen near the Thuringian forest in Germany, by a second eyewitness after an earlier account of a similar reaction. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, drawing on the writers Plimpton Hydrick and Farrell and on a book called Critical Mass. If true, it would mean Nazi Germany got far closer to an atomic bomb than is usually believed.","ezekiel-s-visitors-appear-as-wheels-covered-in-eyes":"In the Bible, the prophet Ezekiel, writing during the exile in Babylon, describes visitors who arrive as 'interlocking wheels covered in eyes, wrapped in fire and storm.' Michels gives this in his own narration, from the King James Bible of 1611. He sets it among other ancient accounts of strange beings, as though it could be an early record of a close encounter.","f-86s-scrambled-to-chase-objects-away-from-ellsworth-s-alert-1977":"At Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, F-86 fighter jets were sent up once or a couple of times to chase unknown objects away from the alert pad, where planes stand ready to launch. Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear site, tells Michels this second-hand, from a man named Chris Leato, and adds that he thinks there are several reports of it. If true, it would mean unknown objects were hovering near one of the military's most guarded spots.","f-e-warren-missile-outage-blamed-on-a-tic-tac-2010":"In October 2010, the story goes, F.E. Warren Air Force Base in Wyoming lost contact with dozens of its nuclear missiles while staff saw strange craft in the sky, which Michels ties to a Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint. Michels tells it often, drawing on the researcher Robert Hastings, author of UFOs and Nukes, and on a Wired article about the outage: he says key personnel made several independent sightings during a power failure, that the outage lasted about an hour, and that on-site sources link it to objects that \"seem to break physics\". In one episode he calls the object cigar-shaped instead, gives the outage as 59 minutes, and says President Obama was briefed. Hastings's article, published June 21, 2011, says that on October 23, 2010 the base \"temporarily lost the ability to communicate with 50 of its Minuteman III nuclear missiles\", and that while the Air Force said it lasted \"a mere 59 minutes\", two missile technicians said it \"persisted over several hours\". He writes that \"one or more 'huge' cigar-shaped objects\" were seen by Air Force personnel, one described as about seven times as long as wide. He also stresses that his sources \"have not said that the UFO(s) sighted actually caused the disruption\", and that the Air Force blamed \"an improperly-replaced circuit card\". So the article speaks of a huge cigar-shaped craft rather than a Tic Tac, and the step from an object seen that day to the cause of the outage is the tellers' reading, one Hastings says his sources did not make. If true, unexplained craft were present while US nuclear missiles were knocked offline.","f-e-warren-witnesses-flagged-as-security-risks-2011":"Witnesses at F.E. Warren Air Force Base in Wyoming were told, in their exit interviews from the Air Force in the spring of 2011, that a flag was being put in their files naming them as security risks. Robert Hastings, who collects accounts of UFOs at nuclear weapons bases, tells Michels this, passing on what he heard from a man named John Mills. If true, it would mean the Air Force marked people as risks for what they had seen.","f-lee-bailey-on-the-cia-s-index-4-during-the-mccord-case-1973":"In 1973, when James McCord, one of the men arrested in the Watergate break-in, was in legal trouble, the famous defence lawyer F. Lee Bailey was secretly on what the lawyer Dan Sheehan calls the CIA's \"index 4\", according to Sheehan. In one telling, Sheehan says he learned this first-hand at the time; in another, he credits two people he names as Andy Toney and William Johnstone Taylor, and adds that Bailey was one of four lawyers the CIA kept on retainer. If true, it would mean the CIA had its own man close to McCord's defence.","faa-supervisor-tracks-five-unknowns-near-malmstrom-hastings-1960":"In 1967, a teenage janitor at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, a base that controls nuclear missiles, says an air traffic supervisor called him over to a radar screen and showed him five \"unknowns,\" the official term for unidentified objects, moving near the missile sites. The janitor was Robert Hastings, who grew up to be a researcher and wrote the book UFOs and Nukes. He tells it himself (\"I saw five blips in the sort of northeast quadrant of his radar scope\"), wrote in a 2011 article that his father was stationed there in 1966 and 1967 and that a controller told him jets had been \"scrambled to intercept five UFOs,\" and says this \"radar-tracking incident that I had inadvertently witnessed\" is what got him into the subject. Michels repeats it on several episodes, sometimes calling the man an FAA supervisor and sometimes a radar operator, and offers it as early evidence that UFOs watch nuclear bases.","fabio-furtado-sees-object-land-on-his-farm-1996":"In 1996, on a farm near Varginha, a town in Brazil where people that year reported a UFO and a captured creature, a farmer named Fabio Furtado says an object came down right over his house. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this as something Furtado told him. It is one of several accounts of a craft over Varginha, which together would put a real object in the sky at the time.","face-and-five-sided-pyramid-at-cydonia-mensa-1976":"On Mars, at a site called Cydonia Mensa, there is a formation that looks like a face, and about five miles away there is a five-sided pyramid. The researcher John Brandenburg tells Michels this, talking about images of the area going back to 1976. He says NASA has never shown the face and the pyramid together in any of its public photos, \"because it becomes obvious\", meaning that seen side by side they would look built, which is why the claim matters to him.","facebook-page-leads-to-michael-johnson-2026":"While trying to find Air Force veterans who say they were taken by aliens about 50 years ago, Michels' team was sent a Facebook page by a fan named Jason Miller, and it belonged to a man in his seventies named Michael Johnson living in South Carolina. Michels tells this himself in the episode where the veterans meet again. In his telling, that page is how the reunion came about.","fail-safe-and-human-obsolescence-1963":"A film based on the novel Fail-Safe, a Cold War story about a nuclear crisis, is said to show how humans fall behind the machines they build. James Madden, a guest who talks about Plato's cave and consciousness, tells Michels this and dates it to around 1963 or 1964. He links it to the philosopher Günther Anders, who wrote about human obsolescence, the idea that our own technology leaves us behind.","failing-cia-polygraphs-because-he-knew-too-much":"Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former senior CIA officer, says the agency at one point \"got so paranoid\" that he could no longer pass a polygraph, a lie detector test. He tells Michels this about his own career, putting it down, as the story has it, to how much he knew.","fake-darpa-guest-exposed-by-an-email-from-darpa-s-security-m":"A guest who claimed to come from DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, turned out never to have worked there. Jay Anderson, a researcher of ancient mysteries who had interviewed the man, called Michael, says DARPA's security manager emailed him after seeing the video: \"I'm really sorry to email you, but we've just seen your video and this guy has never worked for DARPA.\" Anderson tells it on the show as a lesson about guests who claim inside credentials.","fake-military-ufo-letter-used-to-test-bill-moore-1979":"Around 1979 or 1980, a letter made to look like it came from the military, about UFOs, was sent as a way of testing a man named Bill Moore, possibly with a New Mexico connection. The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels what Moore told him: the letter went first to APRO, a civilian UFO research group, and APRO passed it on to Moore, saying, \"Well, why don't you check this out?\" If true, it would mean someone was feeding planted papers into UFO circles to see what a researcher would do with them.","fake-videos-offered-to-james-fox":"People in Brazil are said to have tried many times to sell videos to the filmmaker James Fox, and the videos are said to be fake. A surgeon named Italo Venturelli, who says his hospital operated on an alien, tells Michels this, saying, \"people have already tried to sell so many videos to him. But they're not real.\" His point is that fakes have grown up around the story, separate from what he says he witnessed.","faked-gemini-spacewalk-photo-of-michael-collins-1960":"A NASA photo of the astronaut Michael Collins on a spacewalk during the Gemini missions of the 1960s is, in Jesse Michels's words, \"verifiably fake\". Michels says this in a conversation with Joe Rogan, agreeing with a point he credits to Rogan. He offers it as a sign that NASA's official images cannot always be taken at face value.","falcon-and-the-mj-12-films-from-dia-humint-and-angleton-s-ch-1980":"In the 1980s, the story goes, an intelligence officer code-named Falcon handed two UFO researchers undeveloped 35mm film of the so-called MJ-12 documents. These are papers that describe a secret group called Majestic 12, said to have handled crashed saucers. A scientist who has worked on Pentagon projects, Eric W. Davis, tells Michels that Falcon was an officer of the DIA, the Defense Intelligence Agency, a Pentagon spy agency. He says Falcon was sent to talk with a TV producer named Jaime Shandera and a researcher named Bill Moore. In Davis's account the papers on the film were first-generation documents. He says they were made by James Jesus Angleton's \"chop shop\", which he calls Angleton's \"mole hunting document production factory\". A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells a plainer version. He says the papers first turned up in 1984 in a film canister sent to Shandera. Among them, he says, were the Eisenhower briefing document, a paper dated 18 November 1952 that is addressed to Eisenhower, and a so-called Special Operations Manual. UAPGerb says nothing about Falcon, the DIA or Angleton as the source. If Davis is right, the most famous documents in UFO lore would have been produced on purpose by an intelligence operation, whatever they were meant to hide.","falcon-space-biefeld-brown-vacuum-test-rotation-reverses-as":"A device meant to show the Biefeld-Brown effect, a claimed push produced by high-voltage electric charge, was tested in a vacuum chamber at a company called Falcon Space. It is said to have spun one way at moderately low pressure, then spun the opposite way as the air was pumped out further. David Chester, a theoretical physicist and a friend of Michels, tells this on the show while testing claims of electrostatic thrust made by Charles Buhler, an engineer the episode connects to NASA. He passes on the result without naming a source, and mentions the space-propulsion writer Tim Ventura and a man called Mark. It matters because whether the push survives with no air around is the test of whether it is anything more than moving air.","falsified-evidence-aimed-at-a-treason-death-penalty-for-borl-2025":"In 2025, someone in government faked evidence to have Dylan Borland, a UFO whistleblower, sentenced to death for treason, and pushed spying charges against others, according to Borland himself. He tells Michels that 'in the last year, they falsified evidence for me to go and get a death penalty for treason', naming the whistleblower David Grusch as he tells it, though who 'they' are he leaves unsaid; if true, it would mean whistleblowers are being targeted with the harshest charges the law allows.","families-selected-for-the-program-by-demographics-1960":"Families were picked for a secret programme in the 1960s partly by demographics, because its planners knew a certain share of the children would want to join the military. That is how a former sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, puts it to Michels, passing on what an officer he calls Captain White told him. If true, it would mean the programme chose families with an eye to their children's later careers.","faraday-cage-test-at-the-monroe-institute-2025":"In 2025 Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs show up around him, was taken down to a basement lab at the Monroe Institute in Virginia, a centre that studies consciousness, and shut inside a Faraday cage, a metal enclosure that blocks radio and electrical signals. Bledsoe tells Michels about the test himself. The point of a test like this is to find out whether whatever happens around him can still reach him once outside signals are cut off.","faraday-finds-that-magnetic-fields-change-the-polarization-o":"The scientist Michael Faraday is said to have found through his experiments that magnetic fields change the polarization of light, meaning the direction in which light waves vibrate. The scientist Rupert Sheldrake brings this up on the show in passing, without naming a source, as an example of a link between magnetism and light that experiment revealed.","farmington-armada-1950":"In 1950, half the town of Farmington, New Mexico, is said to have watched hundreds of silver discs swarm across the sky, an event later called the Farmington Armada. Michels tells the story on an episode filmed nearby. A witness he does not name gives a firsthand version on the same episode: \"The first day there were a few. The second day there were too many to count.\" If true, it would be one of the largest mass sightings on record, seen by a whole town over more than one day.","farouk-el-baz-the-king-of-apollo-site-selection-1960":"Farouk El-Baz, a scientist who oversaw lunar science planning and helped choose the Apollo landing sites in the 1960s, was nicknamed \"the king\" by the astronauts, and his father was a scholar of Egyptian religion. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as part of a thread linking NASA's Moon programme to ancient Egyptian belief.","fascist-elite-preparing-a-thousand-year-reich-1945-2045":"A fascist elite has been quietly preparing a thousand-year Reich since 1945, and the preparation is meant to run until 2045. That is the hypothesis of a researcher named Jason Jorjani, who studies what he calls Hitler's secret UFO programme. He offers it to Michels as his own theory, without naming a source, and places the work in the United States.","fast-walkers-code-word-for-ufos-in-space":"The US military commands that watch the skies and space over North America, the Space Force, NORTHCOM (the command that defends the US homeland) and NORAD, are said to have their own term for UFOs moving in space: \"fast walkers.\" The researcher Maaneli Derakhshani says so to Michels, without naming where he learned it. If true, it would mean the military tracks such objects often enough to need a word for them.","fast-walkers-exempt-from-foia-defense-support-program-detect":"Records of \"fast walkers\", fast-moving objects picked up by the Defense Support Program, a set of American early-warning satellites high above the Earth, are said to be off limits to FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records. Michels says this himself, adding that a records researcher named John Greenewald Jr. tried to get them that way. If true, it would mean satellite detections of unknown objects stay shut away from the public even today.","fast-walkers-in-the-wikileaks-email-between-bob-fish-and-joh":"Michels says that \"fast walkers\", a term for unidentified objects tracked by satellites as they move quickly through space, came up in an email between the political adviser John Podesta and a contractor named Bob Fish, part of the Podesta emails WikiLeaks released in 2016. Michels brings this up himself across several episodes and describes it a little differently each time. He calls Fish an aerospace contractor who may have worked at the Aerospace Corporation in El Segundo, then once an IT contractor. Once he dates the email loosely to the 2010s. He also mentions geostationary orbit and credits the records researcher John Greenewald Jr. If true, it would mean the subject reached a senior political figure.","fatal-aircraft-mishap-and-four-lost-hours":"Greg Rogers, a former NASA doctor who served with the Third Attack Battalion, says an aircraft accident in the field killed one of the unit's pilots, and that the episode is tied to four hours no one could account for. Rogers tells Michels this as something he was there for. He tells it as the event from his service that stands out most, and as a hint that something unexplained happened in those missing hours.","father-k-takes-tyler-under-his-wing-in-rome-2017":"The religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka says that when she visited the Vatican with a man she calls Tyler, around 2017, a priest she calls Father K walked up to him and \"somehow immediately\" decided to take Tyler under his wing. Pasulka tells Michels this as someone who was there, and it suggests that people in the Church took a quick, close interest in Tyler.","father-peter-rinaldi-s-meeting-on-the-shroud-of-turin-1970":"Sometime in the 1970s in New York, a priest named Father Peter Rinaldi phoned Anne Strieber, whom he knew, and invited her and Whitley Strieber to a meeting about the Shroud of Turin, the old linen cloth that some believe wrapped the body of Jesus. Whitley Strieber tells Michels this as something he took part in: \"I want you to come to a meeting,\" he recalls the priest saying, \"we're having a meeting about the shroud.\"","father-s-cylindrical-ufo-sighting":"A man once saw a UFO over central California: a cylinder with a red light on its front. The man was the father of Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects that people say were implanted in their bodies. Colbern tells Michels what his father told him, and gives no year or exact place.","father-s-generation-called-ufos-at-nuclear-tests-tagalongs-1950":"In the 1950s and 60s, UFOs were seen often enough around American nuclear test sites that the men who worked there had a nickname for them: \"tagalongs.\" The economist Pippa Malmgren tells this on the show from her own father, who said, \"Yeah, we called it a tag along.\" If true, it would suggest that strange objects showing up at atomic tests were familiar to the people running them.","father-s-racehorse-fixing-overheard-as-a-child-1970":"In the early 1970s, as a boy at the family home on Long Island, Bob Lazar says he overheard his father and other men fixing horse races. Lazar tells Michels he remembers 'playing in the living room and hearing those guys deciding who's going to win the race', a glimpse of the world he grew up in.","father-s-ship-crew-sees-an-object-leave-the-water":"A whole ship's crew of 29 or 30 men is said to have watched something come up out of the sea, rise into the sky and shoot away. A guest named Mario Woods tells Michels this as a story his father, a man from Honduras, often told him. Woods says his father saw many strange things in the jungles and on the ocean, and if the story is true, a whole crew saw an object move from water to sky.","fathers-of-the-space-programs-thought-they-were-in-touch-wit":"The founders of the great space programmes all believed they were in contact with non-human beings, Michels says: Jack Parsons, who founded the Jet Propulsion Laboratory in America, Konstantin Tsiolkovsky in Russia, and Wernher von Braun in Germany. The first time, he credits the scholar Diana Pasulka for the idea. On later episodes he tells it himself, once softening it to Parsons \"implying\" contact with another intelligence, and once naming Hermann Oberth as the German father and saying Tsiolkovsky thought the beings were angelic.","fbi-agent-guy-hottel-investigates-condon-q-clearance-strippe-1950":"In the early 1950s, Michels says, an FBI field agent in Washington named Guy Hottel was building a dossier on the physicist Edward Condon, and Condon then lost his Q clearance, a high-level clearance for nuclear secrets. Michels tells this himself across several episodes, without naming a source for the investigation. In one he calls the loss of clearance \"mysterious,\" dates it to 1953, and ties it instead to J. Robert Oppenheimer and the Los Alamos Primer, a 1943 Los Alamos Laboratory document.","fbi-briefing-to-congress-and-ongoing-fbi-investigation-of-le-2025":"In 2025 the FBI is said to have briefed Congress on a secret older UFO effort, and the briefing reportedly \"really blew people away.\" The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels he heard this from others, and says the FBI's investigation is still going on and that he has dealt with some of the people on its team. If true, it would mean a federal law enforcement agency is looking into a hidden programme.","fbi-calls-brigham-young-its-top-recruiting-source":"The FBI is said to have called Brigham Young University, the Mormon university in Utah, its top source of recruits. Matthew Pines, a national security analyst, mentions this in passing to Michels without naming a source. If true, it would point to unusually close ties between one religious community and the federal security world.","fbi-document-calling-brown-the-navy-s-lead-radar-scientist-1940":"An FBI file from 1942 to 1943 on Thomas Townsend Brown, an American scientist Michels links to early work on antigravity, is said to state that Brown knew more about radar detection than anyone in the US Navy. Michels comes back to this file again and again on the show. He says the researcher Paul Schatzkin recently brought it to light, and on different occasions adds that Brown's work later went into the B-2 stealth bomber, and that the file says Brown ran the Philadelphia Experiment, a rumored wartime Navy effort to make a ship invisible.","fbi-documents-tracking-puharich-s-movements-in-israel-1970":"In the early 1970s the FBI kept watch on Andrija Puharich, a scientist with intelligence ties who believed he had contacted a council of alien beings, as he moved around Israel. A researcher named Greg Malatzi says on the show that the only thing his requests under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, turned up was \"this big chunk of FBI documents which was basically monitoring all of his movements in Israel.\" If true, it would show the government took Puharich's activities seriously enough to follow him abroad.","fbi-image-cheapening-of-jean-seberg-1970":"The FBI is said to have used a tactic called \"image cheapening\" against the Hollywood actress Jean Seberg around 1970. It planted a story with the Los Angeles Times columnist Joyce Haber claiming that the baby Seberg was carrying was not her French husband's but a Black Panther's. The researcher Eric Weinstein tells this on the show without naming a source, as the well-known case through which the public learned the term, and his point is that agencies have smeared people on purpose to discredit them.","fbi-memo-to-hoover-on-discs-and-balls-of-fire-1949":"In 1949, according to Jesse Michels, an FBI memo went to the bureau's director, J. Edgar Hoover, reporting \"flying discs\", \"flying saucers\" and \"balls of fire\" around Los Alamos, New Mexico, things \"considered top secret by intelligence officers of both the Army and Air Force.\" Michels tells it himself, calling the memo now public, and on a later show he points to the same FBI document, without naming Hoover, as evidence of a link between UFOs and nuclear sites.","fbi-records-on-karl-compton-destroyed-foia-reply-2006":"The FBI's records on the physicist Karl Compton were destroyed in 2006, according to an April 2014 reply to a request under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records. Michels quotes the reply, which was given to the records researcher John Greenewald Jr.: \"records which may have been responsive to your request were destroyed August 30th, 2006.\" The reply says the records were destroyed but not what was in them or why, and Michels brings it up in an episode about a presidential adviser who says he handled UFO material.","fbi-seizes-tesla-s-papers-after-his-death-in-1943":"When the inventor Nikola Tesla died at the New Yorker Hotel in New York in 1943, the FBI is said to have come in, taken large numbers of his files and classified them. A Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell tells Michels this, citing only \"various accounts\", and if true it would mean some of Tesla's work has been kept secret by the government ever since.","fbi-von-braun-sent-classified-documents-to-the-ussr-1947":"FBI documents show that the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun was once caught sending classified documents to his former Nazi colleagues working in the Soviet Union, yet no disciplinary action seems ever to have followed. Michels tells this himself, citing the FBI files without quoting them, in an episode on NASA's hidden history. If true, it would mean von Braun was protected from the usual consequences.","fdr-letters-showing-awareness-of-the-phenomenon-1940":"Franklin Roosevelt left statements or letters showing he knew about the UFO phenomenon in the 1940s, according to the UFO researcher Richard Dolan, who hedges with \"I believe\". He ties this to papers called the Majestic documents, which circulate among researchers, and if true it would mean a president knew of the subject before the UFO era began.","fdr-on-financial-interests-owning-america-1930":"Franklin Roosevelt is said to have written or said, sometime in the 1930s, that financial interests had owned America since the days of President Andrew Jackson. The researcher Jason Simosa brings this up with Michels, saying it was \"a conversation or letter\" but naming neither one. He uses it to argue that hidden money and power have shaped the country for a long time.","fdr-s-1937-desert-game-range-guarded-by-anti-aircraft-guns-t-1935":"Charles Hall, a man who says he spent three years with tall white aliens near Area 51, says the Desert Game Range around Sheep Peak in Nevada was set up under Franklin D. Roosevelt, in 1937, and was guarded by anti-aircraft guns, supposedly to protect bighorn sheep. He tells Michels this on the show, drawing on what park rangers said and on a book called Millennial Hospitality, and to him the guns suggest the land was hiding something far bigger than sheep.","fdr-s-foreknowledge-of-pearl-harbor-1941":"President Franklin D. Roosevelt knew the attack on Pearl Harbor was coming, according to this story, and on the night before December 7, 1941, sent a personal order for all the aircraft carriers to leave \"under the cover of night.\" The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this, relaying it as Roosevelt's own order rather than something he saw. If true, it would mean the president let the attack happen while protecting the fleet's most valuable ships.","fdr-s-vatican-communications-via-envoy-myron-taylor-1940":"President Franklin Roosevelt is said to have handled communications with the Vatican about a craft that reportedly crashed at Magenta in Italy, through his envoy to the Vatican, Myron Taylor, in the 1940s. Michels says it himself, without naming a source, on an episode with a former presidential adviser named Harald Malmgren. If true, the American government and the Catholic Church would have shared a secret about a crashed craft before Roswell.","february-2026-white-sands-event-and-el-paso-airspace-closure":"Something is said to have happened at White Sands, the military test range in New Mexico, in February 2026, around the time the airspace over El Paso, Texas, was closed. Michels raises it as a question to his guest Matthew Brown, a Pentagon whistleblower, asking \"what happened at White Sands in February of 2026?\" The telling gives no details of the event itself, only that Michels thinks it is worth asking about.","february-event-a-tic-tac-uses-hills-to-hide-from-the-sensors-2025":"At a February 2025 field event run by Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs, the team says a tic tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, ducked behind hills to escape their instruments. James Valor, who was there, tells Michels it was what they call a second class object that \"demonstrated very strange behavior\": \"He actually used hills to hide from us and disappear from our main sensors.\" He credits a colleague named Proixel. The Skywatcher team, telling it on the same episode and crediting a member named Jordan Jose, adds that on the fifth day there was interference \"constant throughout two hours\", and that an object shaped like a manta ray also hid behind the terrain. If true, it would mean these objects behave as if they understand they are being watched and act to avoid it.","feds-ransack-chuck-clark-s-place-1990":"Federal agents are said to have ransacked the home of a man named Chuck Clark in the tiny desert town of Rachel, Nevada, sometime in the 1990s. The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels this as something he heard, citing George Knapp and Clark. If it is true, the government cared enough about what Clark was doing to go through his place.","felipe-vidal-santiago-and-the-umbrella-man-on-the-grassy-kno-1963":"A man named Felipe Vidal Santiago can be seen on the grassy knoll in Dealey Plaza in Dallas when President Kennedy was shot in 1963, and the story ties him to the \"umbrella man\", a figure standing there holding an umbrella. A lawyer and investigator named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this in a conversation about Kennedy and UFOs. He mentions a House committee but names no specific source. If he is right, a named person was present at the shooting and has not been accounted for.","fellow-marine-refuses-to-come-forward-text-messages-2023":"A fellow Marine refused to back the account of Michael Herrera, a former Marine who says he saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle. Michels reads from text messages between the two, in which the other man writes: \"My family and military career far exceed anything you are asking of me.\" It suggests a second witness exists but will not speak.","felt-his-body-turn-to-stone-while-linked-to-a-craft-that-was":"A man named Jordan Jose says that once, while he was somehow linked to a craft that was then shot at, \"it felt as if my body was actually turning to stone.\" He tells Michels this as something he went through himself, in an episode about being taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, and he places it in an undisclosed area near directed energy weapons, meaning weapons that fire beams of energy rather than bullets. If true, it would mean a person's own body could be tied to a craft closely enough to feel what happens to it.","felt-the-virgin-mary-s-presence-and-guidance-in-portugal-1998":"A former senior CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says that in Portugal in 1998 he felt the presence of the Virgin Mary. He tells Michels he had not met her there, but he could feel her mind and thoughts, and he links that moment to a later experience of hers that he describes. If true, it would mean a seasoned intelligence officer believes he was personally guided by a holy figure.","few-physicists-in-the-crash-retrieval-program":"Very few physicists worked in the secret programme said to recover crashed non-human craft, according to an earlier guest on the show. A NASA engineer named Charles Buhler brings this up with Michels, pointing back to that earlier interview; it would suggest the programme has had too few scientists to make sense of what it holds.","fewer-than-40-recovered-crashes-1930":"The US is said to hold fewer than 40 recovered crashed craft of unknown origin, a count that would mean such crashes are real and on record. Eric W. Davis, a scientist who has worked with the Pentagon, tells Michels he knows the official number from classified sources and can't give it, but \"I could say it's less than 40.\" In a later episode Michels repeats it a little differently, crediting Davis or a researcher named Hal Puthoff with a figure of \"10 to 40 crafts in hangers across the United States.\"","feynman-and-wheeler-s-1945-paper-on-time-symmetric-electroma":"In 1945 the physicist Richard Feynman wrote a key paper with the physicist John Wheeler on time-symmetric electromagnetism, the idea that the laws of electricity and light work the same running backward in time as forward. The biologist Rupert Sheldrake brings the paper up with Michels in passing, as support for a theory he is describing, without saying more about what it contains.","feynman-s-any-questions-teaching-at-a-buffalo-aerospace-comp-1940":"The physicist Richard Feynman tells a story called \"Any Questions?\" in his memoirs. In it, as a physics professor at Cornell, he has to travel to Buffalo, New York, to teach at an aerospace company. The mathematician Eric Weinstein brings it up with Michels from Feynman's books Surely You're Joking, Mr. Feynman! and What Do You Care What Other People Think?, and links it to Feynman's patents on nuclear technology, as a sign of how much defence work the famous physicist quietly did.","ffrdc-spun-off-from-trw-as-principal-contractor-for-about-29-1960":"One organisation is said to have been the main contractor for about 29 years, starting in the 1960s, on secret work studying recovered craft to learn how they work. It was a federally funded research and development center, a kind of nonprofit lab run for the government, that began as a spin-off of TRW, a large American aerospace and defense company. The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels this from what he was told, and says he won't name the organisation.","fighter-jet-scrambled-and-possibly-fired-on-the-1952-dc-ligh":"During the 1952 wave of glowing lights seen over Washington, DC, a fighter jet, which he thinks was an F-105, was sent up to intercept the luminous objects, and by one account the pilot was asked to fire on them and may have hit one. Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, tells this on the show as something he has heard discussed, not something he saw. If true, it would mean the military shot at an unknown object over the capital.","fighter-pilot-from-chile-meets-an-object-clocked-at-25-000-k-1980":"In the 1980s a fighter pilot flying from Chile to Brazil met an object that was clocked at 25,000 kilometres an hour. Aldo Rebelo, a former Brazilian defense minister, gives it on the show as one of the cases he knows of. If true, a military pilot saw something moving far faster than any known aircraft.","fii-nocontract-marking-used-to-hide-ufo-programs-in-doe-nnsa":"A security marking on records, \"FII NOCONTRACT\", is said to be used to hide UFO programmes inside the Department of Energy's national laboratories, such as Sandia, Lawrence Livermore, Los Alamos and Oak Ridge. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb puts this forward on the show as his own belief. He says it is a tool used by the Department of Energy and the National Nuclear Security Administration, its semi-autonomous nuclear weapons agency, \"to obfuscate UFO programs,\" and he ties it to the UAP Disclosure Act, a bill in Congress on UAP, the government's newer term for UFOs.","files-show-george-joannides-lied-about-running-jm-wave-psy-w-1963":"A CIA officer named George Joannides ran a psychological warfare programme for JM WAVE, the CIA's station in Miami in the 1960s, and recently released JFK files prove he lied about his part in it, says the researcher Jeremy Riss. He tells Michels this without naming a particular file. If true, it would mean a CIA man misled those who later looked into the Kennedy years.","filippo-and-corrado-conclude-the-structures-are-man-made":"Two researchers who scanned the ground under the Giza pyramids in Egypt have decided that what they found down there was built by people, not formed by nature. Filippo Biondi, one of the researchers behind the scans, says it on the show about himself and a colleague he calls Corrado: \"we agree that it is something that it is man-made.\" If he is right, it would mean there are deliberately built structures deep beneath the plateau.","final-casino-meeting-with-dennis-mariani-1989":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada in the late 1980s, tells Michels about his last meeting with a man named Dennis Mariani in a Las Vegas casino in 1989. Lazar says he walked up and kept saying, 'Dennis, I'm here, what do you want? What's going on?', but Mariani never even looked up at him. Lazar went over to his friend Gene Huff, said he did not know what was going on with Dennis, and when the two of them turned around, Mariani was gone. The researcher Luigi Vendittelli tells the same scene as Huff told it to him, and describes Mariani as a blond, military-looking man who would not look at Lazar while Lazar talked. To the tellers it suggests that Mariani came to watch or warn rather than to talk. Lazar was there, and Vendittelli repeats what the other man who was there told him.","finding-the-tsar-s-submarine-som-and-questioning-by-the-swed-1904":"A Swedish treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg says that in 2015 his team found the wreck of Som, a submarine of the Russian Tsar's navy from the early 1900s, lost in Swedish waters of the Baltic Sea. He tells Michels he was there for the find, and that afterwards he was questioned in Stockholm by what he calls the Swedish \"FBI\", the country's security service. He tells it as part of his own history of finding things on the seafloor that draw official attention.","fire-department-captures-screaming-being-with-a-net-1996":"In 1996, in the Brazilian town of Varginha, the local fire department is said to have caught a strange screaming creature with a net, \"like they would with a wild animal.\" The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this from what witnesses told him during his long investigation of the case. It is one piece of the larger Varginha story, in which people say a craft crashed and living non-human beings were caught and taken to hospitals.","fireballs-seen-over-washington-dc-in-the-1950s":"Flying fireballs are said to have been seen over Washington DC in the 1950s. Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA, mentions them on the show without naming a source, and says those sightings \"seem to have some credibility.\" Coming from a former intelligence official, it would mean such reports deserve more weight than they usually get.","first-agreements-with-the-tall-whites-speak-english-sing-to-1965":"A man named Charles Hall, who says he spent three years among tall, pale beings he calls the tall whites while stationed in the desert near Indian Springs, Nevada, between 1965 and 1967, says he made his first agreements with them once he accepted they were real. He tells Michels he asked that whenever they came near him they talk among themselves in English, and that he would sing to let them know he was coming. If true, it would mean a young airman had worked out ground rules for living alongside non-human visitors.","first-allagash-sighting-sphere-of-light-at-mud-pond-1976":"In 1976, at the Mud Pond campsite on Chamberlain Lake in Maine's Allagash Waterway, a group of campers saw a sphere of light on the far side of the lake. It drifted very slowly, like a balloon, but against the wind. Charlie Foltz, who was there with another witness, Jim Weiner, tells Michels that someone from a nearby group called out \"What the hell is that over there?\", and that this was the first of the strange events of that trip.","first-captain-gives-economic-destabilization-as-reason-for-s-1990":"When Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, asked why the whole thing was kept secret, the first captain over him in the early 1990s answered with economic destabilization. Michels recounts this to Sherman on the show, drawing on Sherman's account, and it would mean the secrecy was kept to protect the economy from shock.","first-cia-director-on-the-nicap-board-1950":"Roscoe Hillenkoetter, the first director of the CIA and a Navy admiral, is said to have sat on the board of directors of NICAP, a private civilian group that collected and studied UFO reports in the 1950s and 60s. A guest named Dylan Borland tells Michels this without naming a source, as a sign that a man at the very top of American intelligence took the subject seriously.","first-classified-tour-on-eleuthera-bahamas-with-an-air-rescu-1960":"Joe McMoneagle, a former US Army man, says his first classified posting came in the 1960s on Eleuthera, an island in the Bahamas, and that it was hidden behind a cover story of air-rescue work. He tells Michels this from his own life, saying simply, \"I was on a Luther in the Bahamas,\" and gives no more about what the secret work was.","first-earth-battalion-sergeant-kills-a-goat-with-his-mind-st":"A sergeant in the First Earth Battalion, an unusual Army idea for soldiers trained in mental and psychic skills, is said to have killed a goat with his mind, and to have \"experienced dying with the goat,\" before stealing a helicopter and disappearing. Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant who did psychic spying work, tells Michels this as he heard it from Jim Channon, the Army officer behind the battalion. If true, it would mean the Army's mind experiments produced real and deeply disturbing effects.","first-exodus-patent-and-a-possible-national-security-hold-1952":"A patent for Exodus, the propulsion work that the NASA engineer Charles Buhler describes as antigravity, may have been held up on national security grounds. Buhler, who was part of the work, tells Michels that 'some patents apparently go through the Department of Defense,' and he links this to the Invention Secrecy Act of 1951, a US law that lets the government keep certain patents secret. If so, it would mean the military reviewed or slowed the invention before it could be made public.","first-foo-fighter-reports-november-1944":"The first reports of foo fighters, the name airmen of the Second World War gave to strange lights seen near their planes, are said to date to November 1944. A guest on a debate episode, Marik von Rennenkampff, notes this in passing as an anniversary, saying \"that happened November of 1944.\" It matters because it would put modern UFO reports in a line going back to the war.","first-goetheanum-burned-as-a-rival-to-nazism-1920":"The Nazis burned down the first Goetheanum, the headquarters building of the spiritual movement called anthroposophy, in Dornach, Switzerland, in the 1920s, because they feared the movement founded by the teacher Rudolf Steiner could become a rival to Nazism. A researcher named Jason Jorjani says this in an aside to Michels, without naming a source.","first-h-bomb-and-the-1952-dc-wave":"Michels links the first hydrogen bomb, tested in the Pacific in 1952, with the wave of UFO sightings over Washington, D.C. that July. He raises it himself, without naming a source, asking what happened in 1952 and answering with the Washington sightings. The idea is that the objects may have been drawn by the new bombs.","first-hypnotic-regression-tall-being-working-on-him-1977":"A former guard at a nuclear site named Mario Woods says that under his first hypnotic regression, a session where hypnosis is used to bring back buried memories, he recalled being aboard a craft in South Dakota in 1977 while beings worked on him, and \"the one that was messing with me was a very tall one.\" He tells Michels this about himself, as part of his account of being abducted. If true, it would put a nuclear site guard in the hands of non-human beings.","first-intuitive-communication-received-in-the-van-1990":"A former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA trained him to communicate with aliens, says the first message came to him by intuition, in his mind, while he sat in a communications van at an overseas base in the early 1990s. He tells Michels the first contact came only a while after he started, and that from then on, as he puts it, the ball started rolling. If true, it would mean a US intelligence agency ran a programme to talk with non-humans through the mind.","first-mobile-phone-ufo-photo-matched-to-military-radar-2005":"In 2005, in northern Sweden, someone took a phone photo of an unidentified object, and the object is said to have matched a track on military radar. The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn, who worked on the case, tells Michels it is known as \"the first mobile picture of UFO in Sweden.\" He places it at a cottage at one point and over a lake at another.","first-observation-of-the-force-laser-displacement-test-with-2010":"Charles Buhler, an engineer who has worked for NASA, says he first saw a force predicted by his own theory in about 2010, in a lab test in Florida. A laser measured whether something moved, and he says two colleagues named Nathan and Dr. Sid Clemens were part of it. Buhler tells it as his own experience, \"the first time I witnessed it or a force um based on a theory that I had,\" in an episode about what he calls a new kind of propulsion, so if he is right, this would be the first sign that the idea works.","first-peer-reviewed-paper-on-the-khufu-pyramid-scan-2021":"Filippo Biondi, a scientist who scans beneath the Egyptian pyramids with satellite radar, tells Michels his team published its first peer-reviewed paper on the inner structure of the Great Pyramid of Khufu in \"2022, I don't remember, 21, 22.\" A researcher named Jeffrey Drum dates that first paper to 2020 instead and names it as \"Synthetic aperture radar Doppler tomography detects undiscovered high-resolution internal structures of the Great Pyramid of Giza,\" synthetic aperture radar being a way of building detailed images from radar signals taken as a satellite passes overhead. Either way, the point is that the scans of hidden rooms inside the pyramid were first put through scientific review years before they drew wide attention.","first-question-at-eg-g-was-about-john-lear-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he was hired in the late 1980s to work on alien craft at a secret Nevada site he calls S-4, says that when he sat down for his job interview at EG&G, a defense contractor in Las Vegas, in 1988, the first question was about a man named John Lear, whom he knew. \"That might have been the first question,\" he tells Michels, recalling it as his own experience. If true, it would mean his interviewers already knew who he was connected to before he said a word.","first-skywatcher-observation-class-1-tetra-formation-during-2021":"During a US military war game in 2021, a team observed what it classed as a first-class 'tetra' formation in the sky, according to James Valor, who is part of Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs into view. Valor tells Michels he was there for that first observation and credits a person he names only as Mr. K. If true, it would mean these objects showed up, and were recorded, in the middle of a military exercise.","first-trip-to-egypt-and-inside-the-pyramids-2025":"The scientist Filippo Biondi, whose radar scans are said to show structures deep beneath the Giza pyramids, tells Michels he went to Egypt and inside the pyramids for the first time only in 2025, \"two weeks ago\" at the time of recording. The point is that he did his work on what lies under Giza from a distance before he ever set foot there.","first-ufo-recorded-within-a-week-of-the-trinity-atomic-test-1945":"The first UFO ever recorded is said to have appeared within seven days of Trinity, the first atomic bomb test, held in New Mexico in 1945. Joe McMoneagle, a former Army psychic spy, or \"remote viewer\", says this to Michels as something he was told, without naming who told him. If it is true, UFOs first showed up right when humans first set off a nuclear weapon.","first-videotaped-grusch-interview-at-bryce-s-house-2023":"The first videotaped interview with David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who in 2023 said publicly that the government holds craft that were not made by humans, was filmed at the Los Angeles home of the television writer and producer Bryce Zabel. Zabel tells Michels he was there and remembers how casual it was: \"Dave Crush was in, you know, cargo shorts and a t-shirt for this thing.\" He says the Australian journalist Ross Coulthart and a man named Jared were part of it too.","first-voice-download-after-high-school-reality-is-subjective":"A man named Brent Friedman, a guest on an episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, says that just out of high school he heard a voice deliver a message to him, the first of what he calls downloads, and it said \"reality is subjective.\" He tells Michels it made no sense to him at the time. He tells it as the start of a lifelong run of such messages.","fisherman-sees-an-orb-explode-off-ubatuba-and-collects-fragm-1957":"In 1957, off the coast of Ubatuba in Brazil, a fisherman is said to have watched a glowing orb fly in a zigzag pattern and then explode over the beach, scattering fragments that he gathered up. Michels tells it twice, drawing on the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan, who has studied samples from the case, and in his later telling adds that the fisherman mailed the pieces to a man named Ibrahim Sued and that the researcher Jacques Vallée was involved. If true, those fragments would be physical pieces of an unknown flying object.","fitness-beats-truth-theorem-in-evolutionary-game-theory":"According to a theorem from evolutionary game theory, the maths of how survival strategies compete, evolution rewards senses that help us survive, not senses that show reality as it is. The researcher Don Hoffman, who says he and his colleagues worked it out, building on John Maynard Smith and Darwin, tells Michels that \"the probability is zero that any sensory system has ever been shaped to see any true feature of objective reality.\" If he is right, things we cannot perceive could be all around us.","fitts-sees-hud-s-unauditable-books-on-library-carts-1980":"When the accounts of the Department of Housing and Urban Development were brought out to be checked, they reportedly arrived as library carts loaded with stacks of disorganized paper, wheeled in one after another, so that no one could properly audit them. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this as it was told to him by Katherine Austin Fitts, who worked at the department, and he points to a work called The Myth of the Rule of Law. Dolan raises it because books that cannot be audited would let money vanish where no one can follow it.","fitts-tells-tucker-carlson-the-money-goes-to-space":"Katherine Austin Fitz, a woman who speaks about money missing from the US government's books, is said to have told Tucker Carlson that the money goes to space. Michels recounts it to his guest, the UFO historian Richard Dolan: Carlson asked \"Where is all this money going?\", she said \"Well, talk to Richard Dolan,\" and then \"Space.\" If true, it would mean huge sums of hidden public money are funding a secret space effort.","fitts-trillions-missing-from-dod-budget-funding-secret-proje":"Trillions of dollars have gone missing from the Pentagon's budget to pay for secret projects, according to a former federal official named Catherine Austin Fitts, as others retell her. Michels mentions her in passing, saying the money goes 'towards these projects'. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb puts the figure at 17 to 21 trillion dollars hidden in the US black budget, the secret part of defense spending, since the 1990s alone. A UFO historian named Richard Dolan says Fitts told him his idea of a 'breakaway civilization', a secret group with advanced technology that has split off from the rest of society, is 'right on'.","five-executive-orders-given-to-the-president-2025":"Steven Greer, a doctor who has campaigned for years to get the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, says that in 2025 he was pushing the president to sign five executive orders he had written. He shows them to Michels, each one page long, and calls them his way of getting the president to act. He does not go through what each order says.","five-transients-appearing-and-vanishing-on-27-july-1952":"On a photograph of the night sky taken on 27 July 1952, five points of light appear in a narrow line and are gone in other exposures, says the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel. She found them on the glass photographic plates of the Palomar Observatory Sky Survey, an old survey of the sky, and calls them \"transients\", short-lived flashes. To her, such lines could point to unexplained objects near Earth.","five-uap-incidents-at-vandenberg-2003-2005":"Five incidents involving UAP, the official term for unidentified flying objects, took place at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California between 2003 and 2005. Michels states this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a nuclear missile guard, as a sign that unexplained craft have repeatedly shown up around sensitive military sites.","five-years-of-psychokinesis-experiments-could-never-isolate":"Joe McMoneagle, a former US Army man who took part in research on psychic abilities, says his group spent five years running experiments to prove psychokinesis, the idea that the mind can move or affect objects. He tells Michels \"we did uh five years of experiments to prove psychokinesis\" and says they could never pin down exactly what the effect was acting on. In his telling, the failure was about isolating the target, not about whether something was happening at all.","flames-shoot-from-kuromanta-during-an-excavation-in-a-thunde":"During a dig on Kuromanta, a mountain in Akita, Japan, that some call a pyramid, workers caught in a thunderstorm are said to have looked up and seen flames shooting out of the top of the mountain. The researcher Geoffrey Drumm tells Michels this from a book by a man named Suzuki about the excavation, and gives no date. Drumm uses it to argue that pyramids were built to work as some kind of energy machine.","fleeing-a-close-encounters-screening-in-rapid-city-1977":"In 1977 a former Air Force missile guard named Mario Woods, who says he had been abducted at a nuclear missile site, went to see the film Close Encounters of the Third Kind in Rapid City, South Dakota, and had to flee the theatre. He tells Michels he got up and ran out and sat on the curb on Main Street, because what he saw on screen \"felt as if it was a direct replay of what I just went through\". In a later episode, where he reunites with other veterans, he tells it more briefly: \"I got up and took off.\" The researcher and writer Robert Hastings had already recorded the same memory in an article published on September 10, 2017, about Woods's apparent abduction at a missile site. There Woods is quoted as saying that \"the mailbox scene freaked me out so badly that I had to get up and walk out of the theatre.\" Told this way across years, the story is offered as a sign that the film touched something Woods had really lived through.","flexner-report-reshapes-american-medicine-1908":"The Flexner report, written by the educator Abraham Flexner and published in 1910 with backing from John D. Rockefeller and Andrew Carnegie, reshaped how medicine was taught and practised in America. Michels tells this in his own narration, in an episode about the drug industry's past. He presents it as the moment when the richest men in the country gained a hand in steering American medicine.","flight-19-and-the-lost-pby-from-banana-river-1945":"In 1945 a group of Navy planes known as Flight 19 vanished off Florida, in the stretch of ocean called the Bermuda Triangle, and a PBY, a Navy flying boat sent out from Banana River Naval Air Station, was lost too. Greg Rogers, a former NASA doctor, says he talked to \"some of the guys that were in flight ops on that day\" and tells the story from what they told him. It matters because it is one of the founding mysteries of the Bermuda Triangle.","flight-surgeon-gum-exam-burns-and-skin-samples-while-osi-wat-1977":"After his 1977 encounter at a nuclear missile site, Mario Woods says an Air Force flight surgeon at Ellsworth Air Force Base examined him, found burns, and took skin samples while investigators looked on. Woods, a former missile security guard, tells Michels the doctor said, \"you have a couple of burns on your body\", \"above your right eye and on the back of your right hand\". In a later telling he describes the doctor clipping \"a piece of skin off\", sticking the tool in a tube \"and put a cork on it, laid it on that tray\". He says agents of the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, the Air Force's own investigative service, were watching. The researcher Robert Hastings published Woods's written account on 10 September 2017. There Woods says he was interviewed \"the next day\" by the 44th Missile Wing commander, with \"a guy in civilian clothing\" he thought was from that office. He writes that he was ordered to take a urine test at the base hospital and that \"two skin samples were taken\", from \"the back of my right hand and another above my right eyebrow\", \"as I was sunburned for some strange reason\". If true, the Air Force would have taken and kept physical evidence from the encounter.","flight-with-jake-barber-over-a-lockheed-martin-underground-f-2024":"A congressman named Eric Burlison says that around 2024 or 2025 he flew with a man named Jake Barber over a Lockheed Martin facility in the desert near Burbank, California, and saw \"this kind of bowl-shaped thing in the ground\" with \"a huge hangar door,\" clearly an underground structure. He tells Michels that as they circled, \"all these white vans come racing out.\" He tells it as something he saw himself, suggesting the site holds something guarded closely enough to rush people out at the sight of a plane.","flir-gimbal-and-gofast-navy-videos-2004":"According to Karl Nell, who was the Army's representative on the Pentagon's UAP task force (UAP is the government's term for UFOs), three US Navy videos known as FLIR, Gimbal and Go Fast show unidentified flying objects that the Pentagon \"has asserted are not ours and are not our adversaries.\" The first, named for the infrared camera that filmed it, comes from a 2004 encounter and shows the object called the Tic Tac, a white oval shaped like the mint, while Gimbal and Go Fast come from 2015. Nell says this on the show as a statement of the Pentagon's position, which, if it holds, would mean the objects were built by no nation anyone knows.","flir1-object-may-be-a-balloon-2004":"The object in FLIR1, an infrared video that Navy pilots filmed off Southern California in 2004 of what others call the Tic Tac (a white oval object shaped like the mint), may simply be a balloon. The UFO skeptic Mick West, who analyses such videos, says this on the show after working through his own analysis of the footage: \"That of course suggests that what we're looking at is actually a balloon.\" Others on the show read the same video as a real craft, so the point is whether the most famous Navy UFO video shows anything unusual at all.","flir1-video-leaked-on-a-german-server-before-2017-2007":"The FLIR1 video, Navy infrared footage from 2004 of the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, was online long before 2017, on Reddit and \"even on a German server\". Michels says this himself in a debate with the UFO skeptic Mick West. His point is that the footage was out in the world before it made official news.","flood-dated-up-to-40-000-years-ago-from-ice-cores":"The great flood of the old Mesopotamian stories, the one also told in the Bible and the Gilgamesh epic, could have happened as long as 40,000 years ago. Matt LaCroix, an author who writes about lost ancient civilisations, makes this case on the show. He bases it on ice cores drilled from Greenland and Antarctica and on past climate events that show when floods could have happened, and names no source. If he is right, the flood memory would be far older than history is usually thought to reach.","florida-channel-wakes-up-with-an-unexplained-metal-filling-1970":"A man in Florida in the early 1970s is said to have woken up and found a metal filling in his tooth that he never had. He was a \"channel\", someone who says he passes on messages from non-human beings, and he worked in the circle around the medium Phyllis Schlemmer and the researcher Andrija Puharich. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells this on the show, drawing on the book The Stargate Conspiracy, and the story suggests something had worked on the man while he slept.","florida-coastline-extended-4050-miles-east-14-000-years-ago":"About 14,000 years ago, the Florida coast near Miami is said to have lain another 40 or 50 miles to the east, so that where the beach is today there would have been forest. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels this without naming a source, to show how much land the sea has covered since then.","fluttering-leaf-reports-are-ours-1950":"UFO reports that describe a craft moving like a fluttering leaf were American machines, Townsend Brown, the inventor profiled in Michels' documentary, is said to have told a woman named Linda Brown, telling her to filter those out of the UFO information she received. The story comes from Jan Lundquist, who repeats what Linda Brown passed on. If true, it would mean some UFO sightings of the 1950s were secret US craft.","flying-craft-in-ancient-indian-texts":"Ancient Indian writings, among them the Bhagavad Gita, describe flying craft known as vimanas. A UFO activist named Steven Greer agrees with Michels on this without hesitation, \"Oh absolutely,\" and names no particular passage or source. If so, people in ancient India were writing about machines in the sky long before modern UFO reports.","flying-craft-through-the-mind-for-a-brain-neural-interface-2008":"Craft of unknown origin are not flown with joysticks but through the pilot's mind, says Jordan Jose, a man who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child and worked on a brain-to-machine interface at Baker Victory Services in Western New York between 2008 and 2010. He tells Michels that these UAP, the government's newer term for UFOs, are steered by thought. If true, it would mean someone was training children to control such craft with their minds.","flying-lotus-attends-the-tridactyl-unveiling-presentation-2020":"The hip hop artist Flying Lotus says he went to a presentation near his home in the Los Angeles area about the \"tridactyls\", three-fingered bodies shown as possibly not human. He tells Michels the bodies were not actually there: \"they were showing the photos and the X-rays and all that stuff,\" and he names Jaime Maussan in connection with it.","flying-saucer-observatory-detection-over-ottawa-and-the-end-1954":"In 1954 Canada ran a small observatory near Ottawa to detect flying saucers, part of Project Magnet, a Canadian government study of the subject led by an engineer named Wilbert B. Smith. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels that about a week after a warning, on the 8th, something flew over and \"all the bells went off\"; he says he spoke to a man who was in the building at the time. The title ties this event to the end of Project Magnet.","fmri-study-of-brilliant-moves-in-the-game-of-go":"There is said to be a study in which players of the board game Go had their brains scanned by fMRI, a machine that shows brain activity, while they played. Michels brings it up himself, without naming the study or saying what it found, in an episode about children said to read minds. He raises it as a way of looking at where flashes of brilliant insight come from.","foggy-night-encounter-with-a-scout-craft-piloted-by-a-young-1960":"Charles Hall, a man who says he lived among tall white aliens in Nevada, says that on a cold, snowy fall night in the 1960s in Indian Springs Valley he nearly died in the fog. He says a small scout craft flown by a young lady and some of her friends came to him, joined by a large deep-space craft and what he calls a teenage craft. Hall tells it as his own experience, credits a work called The Valley of the Grays, and if it is true the beings he knew were looking out for him.","foia-releases-of-thousands-of-ufo-pages-1970":"From the 1970s into the early 1980s, requests under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, pried loose thousands of pages on UFOs. The UFO historian Richard Dolan says on the show that \"suddenly\" there were \"thousands of pages of military failed interceptions and CIA memos and FBI investigations.\" If so, it would show the government had been tracking and chasing UFOs far more than it had admitted.","foia-request-for-usaf-campinas-flight-records-answered-by-th-1996":"James Fox says his team asked the US Air Force for flight records from Campinas, Brazil, dating from 1996, using FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, and that the answer came back from the CIA, not the Air Force. Fox tells Michels this as something his own team did, \"in 2022, I think it was.\" If true, it would suggest the CIA held or controlled records of American flights tied to that place and year.","followed-by-a-12-year-old-tall-white-girl-on-the-walk-back-t-1965":"Charles Hall, who says he spent three years at a US Air Force base in Nevada living near tall, pale-skinned beings he calls the Tall Whites, says that in 1965 one of them, a girl who seemed about 12, followed him as he walked back to his barracks at Nellis Air Force Base. Hall tells Michels he saw this himself: \"following me was the tall white girl that the 12-year-old.\" He names other figures from his story too, a being he calls the Teacher and a man he calls Range Four Harry, and if it happened, it would mean non-human beings lived in close contact with US airmen.","followed-during-his-clearance-and-told-gene-huff-1989":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site he calls S-4, says that in 1989, while his security clearance was being checked, he was followed in Las Vegas: \"There was always somebody parked outside my house and I was starting to get scared.\" He tells Michels he confided this to a friend named Gene Huff. He tells it as part of how the pressure on him grew before he went public.","followers-of-horus-secret-societies-preserving-knowledge-fro":"The \"Followers of Horus\" of ancient Egypt are said to have been secret societies whose job was to keep knowledge from what the Egyptians called \"the First Time\", a remote founding age, and to pass it down to later generations. The writer Graham Hancock lays this out on the show, drawing on Robert Bauval and the book Talisman. If it is true, knowledge from a lost earlier age was handed down in secret.","following-david-grusch-from-his-testimony-back-to-colorado-f-2023":"After David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned UFO whistleblower, testified before Congress in 2023, Michels says he followed him from the hearing back to Colorado and made a short documentary about him, with the help of a man he names only as Omar. Michels tells this himself, on several episodes, and says they had the best time making it, dating the film to September 2023 and the testimony to the end of that summer. It is his own account of getting close to the man at the centre of the hearings.","following-the-second-van-to-noise-cancellation-technologies-1991":"In 1991, near an NSA training site in Linthicum, Maryland, Dan Sherman says he followed a second van and watched it go to a company called Noise Cancellation Technologies, or NCT. Sherman, a former sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, tells Michels he saw this himself, and offers it as a hint about who was keeping watch on him.","foo-fighters-and-swedish-ghost-rockets-1942":"From 1942 to 1945, Allied and Axis fighter pilots are said to have seen what looked like controlled balls of lightning, nicknamed foo fighters, followed by the \"ghost rockets\" reported over Sweden in 1946. Michels tells it himself several times, bringing in names like James Doolittle and Eisenhower, though he places the sightings differently: once among pilots on both sides, once over Germany, and once in Bavaria, southern Germany, where he says they got in the way of Allied flight paths. If true, unknown objects were already shadowing warplanes before anyone spoke of flying saucers.","foo-fighters-drawn-to-wwii-atomic-and-military-programs-1944":"The \"foo fighters\", strange lights that Allied pilots reported over southern Germany in 1944 and 1945, may have been drawn there by wartime atomic and military programmes. A former Pentagon intelligence official named Luis Elizondo suggests this to Michels. He reasons from today's pattern: UAP, the official term for UFOs, seem interested in nuclear and military capabilities, so he asks what was going on at the height of the war. It is his own inference, with no source named.","footage-of-the-varginha-alien-transport-and-hospital-1996":"There is footage showing a non-human creature being moved from a military base into the Brazilian town of Varginha, to a hospital, and back again, in 1996, the documentary maker James Fox tells Michels. He says he heard this from a source called Marco and from an anonymous military source. If it exists, the footage would record the military handling a captured being.","football-field-sized-vehicles-moving-through-the-ocean-at-50":"The claim is that craft as big as a football field are moving through the world's oceans at 500 knots, far faster than any known submarine. If true, something with technology far beyond ours would be travelling unseen under the sea. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan tells Michels this as something the politician Marco Rubio said, and he credits the documentary The Age of Disclosure as the source. The YouTuber Chris Ramsay tells a similar version on the show, but he gets it from somewhere else. He says Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who has spoken publicly about UFOs, talked about \"the giant underwater thing moving at, you know, 500 knots underwater.\" Ramsay says this matches what an anonymous poster called the 4chan whistleblower had written on 4chan, an anonymous online message board. Ramsay gives no size for the object, and he credits Elizondo, not Rubio.","force-fields-bending-light-seen-in-the-navy-tic-tac-video-2004":"The force fields around alien craft bend light, and you can see this in the Navy's Tic Tac video, according to Charles Hall, who says he spent three years with tall white aliens at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada. The video he means is a 2004 Navy infrared recording of a white oval object shaped like the mint, filmed by Navy pilots. Hall tells Michels that 'the force fields interact with light' and that this shows on the footage, which would mean the video captures the same technology he says he saw.","ford-chooses-nazi-trucks-over-uk-engines":"When Britain asked Ford for engines, the company is said to have refused, saying, \"No, we're not going to do that. We're prioritizing the trucks for Nazi Germany.\" The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming his source, as an example of an American company putting Nazi business ahead of Britain.","ford-s-new-nsa-intelligence-system-1974":"Harald Malmgren, who advised several US presidents, says that in 1974 President Gerald Ford's team, with the Ford aide Bill Seidman, asked him to set up a new intelligence system with the National Security Agency. Malmgren tells Michels he was given the job directly: \"first of all, we want you to arrange with the NSA a new intelligence system.\" It comes up in an episode where he says he also handled UFO material.","foreign-intelligence-attempts-to-penetrate-brown-s-network":"A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown says he has had \"instances that feel like foreign intelligence trying to penetrate my network and extract information, um, and also deliver threats.\" He tells Michels this about his own experience since speaking out. If true, it would mean what he knows is worth stealing to other countries' spies.","foreign-men-offer-luiza-cash-in-dollars-to-retract-1996":"In 1996, after people in Varginha, Brazil, said they had seen a strange creature, foreign men are said to have offered a witness named Luiza a briefcase of American dollars to take back her story. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this from what he was told while investigating the case. If true, it would mean someone from abroad was working to keep the sighting quiet.","forensic-pathologists-with-physical-evidence-and-bacteria-fr-1996":"The claim is that doctors who studied the body of a soldier who died in Varginha, Brazil, in 1996 found an unusual and very dangerous bacterium. The soldier is said to have grabbed a strange being captured in the town. If true, the infection may have come from something not of this world. A UFO investigator named James Fox first mentions it in 2024, saying two forensic pathologists, doctors who examine the dead, have 'just came forward.' Michels says 'the autopsy of this soldier,' Marco Eli Chereze, 'shows bacteria.' He credits a doctor he calls Dr. Janini and one called Dr. Armando, and points to the official autopsy report. Armando Fortunato, the doctor said to have done the autopsy, passes on what Janini said. According to Fortunato, Janini said last year that the bacteria found in the lab 'is not common in our country' and was 'extremely virulent.' Fortunato says Janini 'did not rule out' that it could be 'an alien bacterium.'","formation-of-skywatcher-technologies-2025":"In 2025 a group of people who say they worked inside a secret UFO program started a private company, Skywatcher Technologies, to gather proof of the phenomenon on their own. Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he once carried a recovered UFO, tells Michels plainly: 'Skywatcher Technologies is a company we formed.' The analyst Matthew Pines, who counts himself part of the team, says the aim is to use 'private resources' and what members learned 'from our aspects of engagement with the program' to collect enough evidence to convince a larger slice of the public the phenomenon is real. James Valor, another member, puts it differently: Skywatcher is 'the company that was created through UAP', the official term for UFOs. Michels himself says that after Barber went public he started Skywatcher to call in craft, and he credits a man named James Fowler as part of it. If what they describe works, a private company could show people what governments have kept to themselves.","former-captain-introduces-new-ppd-contact-at-base-2-1990":"In the early 1990s, at a large US base where several services worked together, a former captain is said to have shown up at Dan Sherman's regular job to introduce him to a new contact in a programme Sherman refers to only by the letters PPD. Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA trained him to communicate with aliens, tells Michels that the introduction was made by a Captain White, his previous captain. If true, it would mean the programme followed him from one posting to the next.","former-darpa-man-s-hopeful-monsters-hybrid-proposal":"A former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, once submitted a proposal for a project based on the work of the biologist Richard Goldschmidt, whose idea of \"hopeful monsters\" is about sudden big leaps in how living things change, here turned toward creating hybrids. Michels tells this himself, from the man he met, who said he could talk about it because the project was never approved. If true, it would mean such ideas were at least pitched inside the Pentagon's research world.","former-dig-head-tells-congress-cia-spied-on-whistleblowers-2026":"In 2026, according to two guests, a former official told Congress that the CIA had spied on whistleblowers, the people who had come forward about UFOs. If true, an intelligence agency watched the very people speaking out. A guest named Jeremy Corbell tells Michels about it second-hand and says: \"They found out the CIA was spying on the whistleblowers.\" A guest named Dylan Borland names the man who testified as James Erdman III, the former head of a group the story calls the DIG, which the tellings link to Tulsi Gabbard. Borland says Erdman spoke before the Senate about \"everything they did to whistleblowers\" on COVID as well as UFOs.","former-dni-discusses-ufo-tracking-program-at-area-51":"A former director of national intelligence, the official who oversaw all American intelligence agencies, is said to have openly discussed a program for tracking UFOs based at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada. Michels tells this himself, without naming the official or the occasion. If true, it would be a top intelligence chief confirming that the government watches UFOs from its most secret base.","former-employee-was-a-havana-syndrome-victim":"One of the people struck by Havana syndrome, the mysterious illness reported by US diplomats and intelligence officers, once worked for Eric Haseltine. Haseltine, a former NSA official, tells Michels: \"One of them used to work for me. I know him extremely well,\" which gives the cases a personal witness rather than just a news story.","former-fortune-50-chairman-threatened-after-offering-to-come-2024":"Steven Greer, a doctor who has spent decades campaigning for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, says that in 2024 the retired chairman of one of America's 50 largest companies offered to come forward with what he knew. Greer tells Michels that as soon as the man approached his group, a \"wet works assassination team threatened to kill him and his entire family and grandchildren.\" If that happened, it would mean someone is willing to use violence to keep insiders quiet.","former-hells-angels-president-frozen-for-10-minutes-2016":"Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property where strange events are reported and investigated, says a man who had been president of a large chapter of the Hells Angels motorcycle club, working there as security, was frozen in place for about ten minutes behind a building on the ranch called Homestead 3. Fugal tells Michels about it as something that happened on his watch, sometime in the years after 2016. He tells it as a sign that whatever is on the ranch can overpower even a hardened man.","former-ic-inspector-general-chuck-mccullough-s-ufo-involveme-2020":"A lawyer named Chuck McCullough, who once served as inspector general of the intelligence community, the official who investigates wrongdoing across America's spy agencies, has been involved in the UFO question in the 2020s. Michels points this out himself, saying people don't know about McCullough's past post and mentioning him alongside the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell and the journalist George Knapp; the weight of it is that someone that senior takes the subject seriously.","former-lockheed-cia-contractor-eg-g-scientists-in-private-ut-2001":"In 2001, a former contractor for Lockheed, the aerospace company, and for the CIA is said to have gone public claiming that Lockheed was working on UFO technology and that scientists contracted with EG&G, a company that ran secret government test work, were organising the work in private labs in Utah. Michels tells this himself, without naming the contractor or a source. If true, it would mean such work was spread out into private hands, away from government sites.","former-mayor-admits-the-event-city-builds-a-ufo-statue-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, where people say strange beings appeared in 1996, the former mayor is said to admit that the event really happened, and the city built a large model UFO statue to mark it. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with a surgeon. It matters because a town and its officials would be openly owning the story.","former-nsa-aaro-director-and-crowdsourced-intelligence-throu-2020":"The idea is that the government's partial release of UFO information is a way to gather intelligence from the public, and that the choice of a former National Security Agency man to run AARO, the Pentagon office set up in the 2020s to investigate reports of UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official word for UFOs), points that way. Michels offers this himself, in an episode with his guest Dylan Borland. If true, the public would be serving as unpaid informants for a spy agency.","former-nsa-research-director-never-saw-a-reverse-engineering":"Eric Haseltine, a former director of research at the NSA, says he never saw any sign of a programme to reverse-engineer UFOs, that is, to take apart recovered craft and copy their technology. He tells Michels he had access to a great deal, and that as a 'super user' he once had to sit through a full day at the Pentagon being shown compartmented programs, secret projects open only to named people, which he likens to the eye-clamping scene in A Clockwork Orange. He says he remembers none of it, but there was nothing about UFOs. It is a first-hand account from an insider that cuts against claims of a hidden recovery programme.","former-police-chief-pedro-aguiar-says-he-took-part-and-saw-t-1996":"A former police chief named Pedro Aguiar is said to have admitted that he took part in the 1996 operation in Varginha, Brazil, where a strange creature is said to have been caught, and that he saw the creature himself under a tarp, perhaps only its lower half. The filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this from his own interviews for his documentary Moment of Contact: once Aguiar grew comfortable, Fox says, he told them, \"I participated. I'm a witness,\" which would make him an inside witness to the capture.","former-politico-journalist-asks-brent-about-harrington-s-den":"Brent Friedman says that in November a journalist who used to work at Politico, Ben Schreckinger, asked him about a denial from John S. Herrington, a former official who was Secretary of Energy. Friedman tells Michels about the exchange himself but says nothing more about what was denied.","former-soldier-sees-triangle-take-off-at-area-51-2013":"A former Army man is said to have watched a triangle-shaped craft shoot off a runway at Area 51, the secret military test base in Nevada, in the mid-2010s. The online researcher UAPGerb tells Michels that he spoke with this man himself and does not name him. If it is true, a triangle craft was flying out of the base only a few years ago.","formula-given-to-brent-and-bryce-lost-for-years-then-recover":"A man named Brent Friedman says that a stranger once gave him and his colleague Bryce Zabel a written formula in an envelope, that they lost it for years, and that it turned up again around 2023 or 2024 when his daughter found it in his garage in Washington. Friedman tells it as his own story: the paper had slipped out of its envelope, got damp, and \"stuck to the back of a book\" because of the glue. He says he was researching that formula, the thing \"the guy gave us,\" which makes it a document he believes is worth decoding.","forrestal-flown-to-hobe-sound-reported-belt-suicide-attempt-1949":"In 1949, James Forrestal, who had just been Secretary of Defense, is said to have been flown to Hobe Sound, Florida, where, as Richard Dolan puts it, \"we're told\" he tried to hang himself with his belt. Dolan, a historian of UFOs and government secrecy, tells this to Michels in an episode that suggests Forrestal was killed over what he knew about UFOs. He passes it on as something reported, mentioning a man he names as William Manager. It matters because the story of Forrestal's breakdown is part of how his death was explained.","forrestal-on-majestic-12-1947":"James Forrestal, the first US Secretary of Defense, was supposedly a member of Majestic 12, a secret committee said to have been formed in the late 1940s to handle UFO matters. Michels raises this himself, without naming a source, while talking with the former presidential adviser Harald Malmgren; if true, it would put UFO secrecy at the very top of the government from the start.","forrestal-s-death-at-bethesda-1949":"The story is that James Forrestal, the first US Secretary of Defense, did not take his own life in 1949 but was killed, some say over what he knew about UFOs. In May 1949 he fell from a high window at the Navy hospital in Bethesda, Maryland. Michels tells it himself, saying Forrestal ended up in that hospital and 'gets killed by gravity, quote unquote.' In another episode he adds that 'they found his bathrobe tied to the radiator' before he went out the window. The researcher Richard Dolan, who writes about the history of UFO secrecy, goes further on a show about the case: 'He was absolutely murdered.' Dolan dates the fall to the night of May 21 into 22 and puts it on the 16th floor, mockingly calling it a 'bungee jump.' He draws on Forrestal's own diaries and on figures around him: his brother Henry Forrestal, the defense official Robert Lovett, and a priest, Father Sheehy. If true, a top US official would have been silenced at the very start of the UFO era.","forrestal-s-limo-ride-with-symington-and-you-are-a-loyal-fel-1949":"In 1949, after a limousine ride with the official Steuart Simington, James Forrestal, then Secretary of Defense under Harry Truman, is said to have sat in his office chair staring at a blank wall and repeating one sentence: \"You are a loyal fellow.\" The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of his account of a defense secretary whose breakdown and death he ties to UFO secrets.","forrestal-s-published-diaries-never-mention-ufos-1940":"The diaries of James Forrestal, the first US Secretary of Defense, published in two volumes after his death, never mention UFOs, the UFO researcher Richard Dolan points out to Michels. He raises it in an episode about whether Forrestal was killed over the subject, reading the diaries as published as saying nothing on it.","forrestal-s-trips-to-paris-with-questions-1940":"James Forrestal, a senior American defence official of the 1940s, travelled to Paris with questions and came back with answers. A former presidential adviser named Harold Malmgren tells Michels he found several references to these trips, but he does not say where he found them, what the questions were, or who answered them in Paris.","forrestal-truman-meeting-dates-match-mj-12-documents-1947":"The claim is that the MJ-12 papers, documents describing a secret group called Majestic 12 said to have been set up to handle crashed saucers, mention a date that matches a real meeting between President Truman and Defense Secretary James Forrestal, and that this points to the papers being genuine. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels that \"dates are mentioned when those two met,\" and Michels repeats it himself, saying the papers include a memo from Truman to Forrestal dated 24 September 1947. Michels also brings in Stanton Friedman, a researcher who studied the papers, and the American scientist Vannevar Bush. By their own account, the papers came to light on a roll of film that a man named Jaime Shandera received anonymously at his home in Burbank in 1984, and they were made public in 1987. Who wrote them is disputed. The National Archives and the Truman and Eisenhower libraries say they found no copy. The FBI's copies are marked \"BOGUS\" by hand, and its file records an Air Force finding that \"the document was fabricated.\" Friedman called them \"almost certainly genuine,\" while the skeptic Philip Klass called the Truman memo a fraud. The memo opens \"As per our recent conversation on this matter,\" and the briefing paper says the group was set up by Truman's order \"on 24 September, 1947, upon recommendation by Dr. Vannevar Bush and Secretary James Forrestal.\" So the papers give a date and mention a recent conversation. The match with an actual meeting comes from the tellers checking the men's appointment records, which the papers themselves neither confirm nor rule out.","forrestal-unravels-says-he-is-followed-by-foreign-looking-me-1948":"In Washington in 1948 and 1949, Defense Secretary James Forrestal is said to have started coming apart: he developed odd nervous tics and told people, \"I'm being followed by strange foreign looking men.\" The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels this, drawing on accounts he links to the names Drew Pearson and Steuart Simington. Dolan tells it as part of his case that Forrestal's fall, and his death, were tied to what he knew about UFOs.","fort-worth-general-orders-blanchard-it-s-a-weather-balloon-1947":"In 1947 the Army air base at Roswell, New Mexico, announced it had recovered a crashed \"flying disc,\" then took the story back. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels that Colonel Blanchard, the base commander, got \"this very angry phone call\" from the big air base at Fort Worth, Texas, telling him to say it was a weather balloon. Brandenburg says he has this from Roswell veterans, and tells it as a sign that the retraction was ordered from above.","fortune-10-scientist-with-free-energy-and-teleportation-tech-2025":"Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for UFO disclosure, says that in 2025 a scientist from one of America's ten largest companies flew in to Los Angeles to meet him, and that this man can build free-energy plasma devices and can do teleportation. Greer tells Michels he met him: \"guy flies in super genius he can build uh over free energy plasma things he can do teleportation.\" If true, it would mean such technology already exists inside private industry.","fouche-and-the-tr3b-1970":"A man named Edgar Fouché, known as a UFO whistleblower, is said to have known about the TR-3B, a secret flying triangle supposedly built by \"reverse engineering,\" that is, by copying technology recovered from non-human craft. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb says Fouché \"would come to learn from various friends he knew, including some NSA officials\" about it. Michels tells it differently: he says Fouché claimed to have been at Area 51 himself, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, and knew of a TR-3A and TR-3B series from the defence company Northrop Grumman that \"involves spinning mercury.\" If true, it would mean the US flies aircraft built from alien technology.","fouche-saw-the-tr3b-twice-1990":"A man named Edgar Fouche claimed he saw the TR3B, a rumoured secret triangular US aircraft, twice: once at Edwards Air Force Base and once somewhere else. A researcher who posts as UAPGerb tells Michels this, relaying what Fouche said, including in a talk Fouche gave at the International UFO Congress in 1998. If true, it would mean the US flies craft far beyond its known aircraft.","fouche-the-tr3b-uses-internal-mass-reduction-1990":"The TR3B, said to be a secret triangular American aircraft of the 1990s, does not use anti-gravity but \"internal mass reduction,\" cutting the craft's weight and mass by, as he recalls it, about 89%. That is how the online researcher of UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's term for UFOs) known as UAPGerb relays the claim of Edgar Fouche, a man who said he had worked on secret aircraft programs, adding that Fouche \"was adamant\" about it. If true, it would mean the US has flying craft built on physics far beyond anything public.","fouche-tr3b-program-led-by-nro-cia-and-nsa-1970":"The TR3B, a rumoured secret triangle-shaped aircraft, is said to have been built under a programme run by the National Reconnaissance Office, the agency that runs spy satellites, together with the CIA and the National Security Agency. The online researcher UAPGerb passes this on as the claim of a man named Edgar Fouche, saying Fouche \"said that the program was spearheaded by NRO, CIA and NSA.\" If it is true, spy agencies and not just the Air Force were behind the craft.","fouche-tr3b-propulsion-reverse-engineered-at-sandia-and-live-1970":"The engine of the TR-3B, a rumoured secret triangular US aircraft, was copied from non-human technology at two government labs, Sandia and Lawrence Livermore, according to a man named Edgar Fouche. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP being the official term for UFOs) passes this on to Michels. If true, it would mean the labs \"reverse engineered\" it, taking an unknown craft's workings apart to build their own.","founders-of-washington-copied-egypt-with-obelisks":"The people who laid out Washington, DC, are said to have copied ancient Egypt by putting up obelisks, as was also done in Central Park, France and England. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him, tells Michels this is what a man named Robert Gilbert explained to him. No date is given.","founding-myths-of-star-people-teaching-agriculture":"Nearly every culture's founding myth is said to describe people from the stars who taught humans agriculture. Michels says this himself, without naming any particular myth, in his episode with the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb. If true, it would hint that ancient peoples remembered contact with visitors from elsewhere.","founding-of-nasa-s-electrostatics-lab-by-carlos-gier-2000":"A NASA scientist named Charles Buhler says NASA's electrostatics lab at Kennedy Space Center in Florida, which studies static electric charge, was founded around 2000, \"about 26 years ago,\" by Dr. Carlos Gier, \"a physicist who spent a summer working at Kennedy Space Center.\" Buhler tells Michels this as someone who worked there, in an episode where he claims his group has built a propulsion system that works without pushing anything out.","founding-of-the-pear-lab-by-bob-jahn-1979":"In 1979 Bob Jahn, dean of Princeton's engineering school, started the PEAR lab, a laboratory at Princeton that studied the paranormal. Michels tells this himself on two episodes. In the later one he calls Jahn a plasma physicist and propulsion engineer, gives no date, and draws on the book Margins of Reality. He tells it to show that serious scientists at an elite university took the paranormal seriously.","four-et-craft-appear-near-pensacola-1990":"Four alien craft suddenly appeared in the air near Pensacola, Florida, in front of a group of about 60 people gathered on a beach for a weekend, says Steven Greer, who leads groups that try to make contact with extraterrestrials. He tells Michels he was there, puts it in 1992, and says that afterwards all kinds of people started coming to him, and that certain people already knew about the event. If true, it would mean ordinary civilians had called up alien craft on demand.","four-gliding-beings-in-uniforms-beside-the-truck-1977":"In 1977, at a nuclear missile site called November-5 near Nisland, South Dakota, a young Air Force security guard says he turned and saw four beings coming toward his truck, and they were not walking but gliding over the ground. The guard is Mario Woods, who tells Michels he was there. \"They are not walking as you and I walk, they are just gliding across the ground,\" he says. In a later conversation he gives the group as three small ones with one tall one at the back. Woods had already written the story down for the journalist Robert Hastings, who published it on 10 September 2017 under the title \"Former USAF Missile Security Policeman Tells of Apparent UFO Abduction at ICBM Site\", an ICBM being a long-range nuclear missile. In that text Woods is less sure: \"I think I remember small figures, maybe five or six, walking toward the vehicle,\" with \"a taller figure who I think was in charge\" standing behind them. So the 2017 account gives a different number and has them walking, and it hedges the memory. Every version agrees on a tall leader behind smaller figures. If it happened, it would mean strange beings walked right up to a guard at an American nuclear weapons site.","four-hours-of-missing-time-and-crohn-s-disease-healed-2007":"In 2007 by a river in Riverbank, North Carolina, Chris Bledsoe says he lost four hours he cannot account for, which UFO researchers call \"missing time\", and that afterwards his Crohn's disease was gone. He tells Michels he experienced it himself: \"4 hours later, I come walking back to the river where they were and my son was missing.\"","four-plus-four-tubes-descending-beneath-the-pyramid-2020":"Radar scans of the Giza plateau in Egypt show eight tube-like shapes, four plus four, running straight down from the base of a pyramid to something far below it. That is according to Filippo Biondi, one of the researchers who ran the scans in the 2020s, who tells Michels, \"they are connecting the top, so the base of the pyramid, to something that is located at the bottom.\" If that reading is correct, the pyramid would sit on top of a hidden structure deep underground.","four-psychic-groups-of-ets-friends-enemies-more-less-psychic-1980":"The beings from other worlds who visit Earth fall into four groups: friends or enemies, each either more psychic than humans or equal to us or less. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says he worked on this in the 1980s, in a paper comparing human and alien psychic ability. He tells Michels that \"the friends who are less or equal to us psychically come here for trade,\" which would mean some visitors are here for ordinary exchange and not conquest or study.","four-rabbis-attempt-celestial-ascent":"There is a Jewish parable, Michels says, in which four rabbis tried to rise up into the heavens and only one came back alive with his mind intact. He calls it the parable of the \"parties\", likely the orchard story known as Pardes, and he tells it himself, without naming a source, in an episode about the idea that we live in a hologram. He uses it to suggest that reaching for what lies beyond ordinary reality can break the people who try.","four-star-general-from-the-clinton-administration-got-inside-1990":"In the 1990s a four-star general from the Clinton administration used his authority to get into a hidden program said to hold recovered crashed craft not made by humans, and saw it for himself. The physicist Eric W. Davis, who has worked as a Pentagon contractor, tells this on the show, saying he knows the general, who was \"able to go there, use his authority, his power\" to \"see the program and get inside.\" If true, it would mean such a program exists and that even top officers have to push hard to get in.","four-star-general-says-mj-12-is-real":"A four-star general, asked about the MJ-12 papers (documents that describe a secret high-level group called Majestic 12, said to have been set up to handle the UFO question), is said to have answered that they are real, or that Majestic 12 is real. The online UFO researcher UAPGerb tells Michels this as something he heard, and he does not name the general. If it were true, a very senior officer would be confirming that such a secret group existed.","four-years-of-secret-funding-before-history-channel-2016":"The owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property where strange events are reported, says he paid for an investigation of the ranch starting in 2016 and kept it \"completely secret and confidential for 4 years\" before the History Channel persuaded him to go public. He tells Michels this as his own decision, naming among those involved two men, Jim Segala and Hal Puthoff. He tells it to show the research began quietly, not as a television show.","fox-breaks-the-fife-symington-phoenix-lights-story-1997":"Fife Symington, the governor of Arizona at the time, admitted that he too had seen the Phoenix Lights, a mass sighting of strange lights over Phoenix in 1997, according to James Fox, a documentary filmmaker who says he broke the story. Fox tells Michels the admission came about ten years later, and that Symington, who had been an Air Force pilot, looked into it and said the thing could not have been from here. If true, it would mean a sitting governor had witnessed something he believed was not human-made.","fox-concludes-there-were-more-beings-than-first-reported-1996":"The documentary filmmaker James Fox, who has spent years interviewing witnesses of the 1996 case in Varginha, Brazil, where strange beings were reportedly seen and captured, tells Michels he now thinks there were a lot more beings than were first reported. He points to sightings that came later, after the first accounts, which would make the event bigger than the story that is usually told.","fox-finds-marco-chereze-s-sister-marta":"While looking into reports that a living creature was captured in Varginha, Brazil, James Fox says he went searching for the family of Marco Chereze. Fox tells Michels that after knocking on doors for about four hours, \"door opens. There's Marcos's sister, Marta,\" meaning Marta Tavares, which put him in touch with someone close to the story.","fox-interviewed-all-three-rendlesham-witnesses-1980":"In 1980, US airmen at RAF Woodbridge, a base in Suffolk, England, said they saw something strange in the nearby woods. The documentary filmmaker James Fox says he is the only person to have interviewed all three of the main witnesses, men he names as Cabansag, John Burroughs and Jim Penniston. Fox tells Michels this himself, \"I'm the only person to have interviewed cabansag burough and penison,\" and also mentions Charles Halt, another officer tied to the case.","fox-s-interview-with-edgar-mitchell-at-his-florida-home":"The Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell told the filmmaker and UFO investigator James Fox, in an interview at Mitchell's home in Florida, that \"yes there have been ET visitation,\" meaning visits by extraterrestrials. Fox tells Michels this as the man who sat down with him. Coming from a man who walked on the Moon, it would mean the claim of alien visits has backing from a credible insider.","fox-s-investigation-of-the-varginha-crash-1996":"A UFO is said to have crashed in Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, and the documentary filmmaker James Fox says he went there five times over twelve years to investigate it for his film Moment of Contact. The journalist Michael Shellenberger tells Michels his first story on the subject was about this case, which he calls maybe \"the craziest of all,\" in a two-page New York Post spread, and Michels himself brings up Fox's work there when he talks about UFOs and nuclear sites. If the accounts are true, it would be one of the few crashes where people say they saw something come down and then saw it taken away.","fox-s-vision-of-tim-coleman-keying-his-car-1990":"While editing his film Out of the Blue, James Fox, a documentary filmmaker, says he suddenly saw in his head, clear as day, a man named Tim Coleman holding a key to his car, keying it. Fox tells Michels this happened to him first-hand. If true, it would mean he had a moment of seeing something he could not have seen with his own eyes.","fox-sends-out-of-the-blue-to-all-535-members-of-congress-2000":"The filmmaker James Fox says that around 2000 he went around Washington and handed a copy of his UFO documentary Out of the Blue to every single member of the House and Senate. He tells Michels this as something he did himself, to put the evidence in front of every lawmaker in the country.","fracking-oil-with-nuclear-weapons-in-the-1950s":"In the 1950s, under President Eisenhower, the US was breaking up rock to get at oil, which is called fracking, using nuclear weapons. John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy, says this to Michels. He mentions it in passing, as an example of how freely nuclear power was used in that era.","fractals-in-jackson-pollock-s-paintings-1947":"The seemingly chaotic drip paintings of the artist Jackson Pollock contain fractals, which are patterns that repeat at every scale. A philosopher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels to go look it up, pointing him to a Scientific American article called \"Order in Pollock's Chaos\" by the physicist Richard P. Taylor. For Jorjani, it shows that hidden order can turn up where everything looks random.","francis-crick-s-1973-directed-panspermia-paper":"The claim is that Francis Crick, the scientist who co-discovered the double helix structure of DNA, wrote a paper in 1973 proposing directed panspermia, the idea that life on Earth was seeded here on purpose from elsewhere. Michels brings it up himself in several episodes. In one he says Crick \"decided to write a paper on directed panspermia.\" In another, talking with the biologist Rupert Sheldrake, he calls Crick a former associate of Sheldrake's and mentions a second date, 1981. In a third he gives no date or paper and says only that Crick proposed the idea. The writer Graham Hancock tells it his own way, crediting Crick's 1981 book Life Itself. He describes a seeded spaceship that \"collided with the early Earth\" some 3.9 billion years ago and \"spilled out its contents of bacteria.\" It is told because, coming from Crick, the idea that someone deliberately planted life here carries a weight it would not otherwise have.","franco-and-skorzeny-launch-saucer-shaped-rockets-from-spain-1946":"In 1946, according to newspaper reports in Spain, the Spanish dictator Francisco Franco and the former Nazi commando Otto Skorzeny were on the Mediterranean coast launching saucer-shaped rockets toward America. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as something he has read, drawing on a book called Saucer Swastikas and SCOPS and the writer Joseph P. Farrell, and he raises it because it would suggest some flying saucers of the time were Nazi-built craft carried on after the war.","frank-critzer-nazi-agent-killed-at-giant-rock":"A room dug under Giant Rock, a huge boulder in the California desert where a man named George Van Tassel later lived, is said to have once belonged to a Nazi named Frank Critzer, who was killed there. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells this on the show, citing the book Closer Encounters. In his telling, the site has a hidden Nazi past from before Van Tassel moved in.","frank-drake-s-project-ozma-1961":"In 1961, says Michels, the astronomer Frank Drake started Project Ozma, the first organized search for radio signals from other stars. Michels tells it himself as background in an episode with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel. It marks the start of scientists looking seriously for intelligent life elsewhere.","frank-tipler-s-rotating-disc-time-machine-resembles-ufos-1915":"A physicist named Frank Tipler is said to have described, in the 1970s, a time machine shaped like a rapidly spinning disc, which looks very much like a flying saucer. A researcher named Mike Masters tells Michels this, tracing it through earlier work on time travel by Einstein, a physicist named van Stockum and the mathematician Kurt Gödel, and saying Tipler shrank those models down to something finite: a fast-rotating ring, sphere or disc. Michels repeats it on a later episode without naming a source, calling Tipler a contemporary of a physicist he calls Wheeler and saying the Tipler disc is a flying saucer. If the comparison holds, UFOs might be time machines, perhaps flown by humans from the future.","fravor-and-dietrich-disagree-on-how-long-the-encounter-laste-2004":"In 2004, two Navy fighter pilots, David Fravor and Alex Dietrich, flew together toward a strange object off Southern California, and they are said to remember the encounter lasting different lengths of time. Mick West, a well-known skeptic who analyses UFO videos, tells Michels that Dietrich says her whole experience, from first approach to the object flying away, lasted only 8 to 10 seconds. He sets her account against Fravor's, which he treats as longer. It matters because the shorter the encounter, the less the pilots could have taken in.","fravor-and-dietrich-saw-appendages-on-underwood-s-flir-tape-2004":"When two Navy pilots, Commander David Fravor and Lieutenant Commander Alex Dietrich, later watched the infrared camera tape fellow pilot Chad Underwood shot in 2004 near the carrier USS Nimitz, they saw little appendages sticking out of the object, which was a white oval shaped like a Tic Tac mint. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff says both pilots have stated this, raising it with Michels in a debate with the skeptic Mick West. It would mean the object had features the blurry footage alone does not show.","fravor-s-2004-nimitz-sighting-of-an-object-that-seemed-consc":"In November 2004, off the coast of San Diego, a Navy fighter pilot named David Fravor flew out from the aircraft carrier group of the USS Nimitz and saw something that seemed to know he was watching it. Fravor describes it on the show himself. He says he looked down and saw a \"small white tic tac object\", an oval shaped like the mint, \"moving very abruptly over the water like a ping pong ball. It rapidly accelerated in front of us and disappeared.\" If true, it would mean a craft that outflew Navy jets and seemed aware of the people chasing it. Michels comes back to this case again and again. He repeats that Fravor felt the thing was \"almost breathing\", that it seemed to know \"he saw it\", and that it was already waiting at the next meeting point the pilots were headed for. In other episodes Michels adds that the object climbed to 80,000 feet, that it seemed to dip in and out of the water, and that the cruiser USS Princeton tracked it on radar. A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff names others who were there, including Alex Dietrich, Jim Slaight and Kevin Day. The veteran UFO researcher Jacques Vallée asks why such an object would let \"11 f-18s\" chase it all over the Pacific, and a former NSA official named Eric Haseltine says too many separate sensors caught it, worked by \"people who didn't want to see it but saw it anyhow.\" Michels also ties the case to Leslie Kean's New York Times article \"Glowing Auras and 'Black Money': The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program\", published on 16 December 2017.","fravor-s-joe-rogan-interview-draws-merrick-into-the-topic-2019":"In late 2019 a Navy fighter pilot named David Fravor, who says he chased a strange object off California, told his story on the show of the podcaster Joe Rogan. Marik von Rennenkampff, a writer on defense matters, tells Michels that this interview is what first drew him into the UFO subject.","fred-crisman-and-hunt-as-the-dallas-tramps-1963":"The theory is that a man named Fred Crisman was one of three tramps arrested in Dallas just after President Kennedy was shot in 1963. Michels tells it, saying \"they think\" Crisman was one of the three. He adds that Crisman was also involved in a 1947 UFO episode known as the Maury Island incident, which happened around the time a pilot named Kenneth Arnold reported his own UFO. The researcher Peter Levenda says he \"can corroborate\" it and suggests that a man named E. Howard Hunt \"might have been one of them as well.\" If true, it would tie the first wave of modern UFO stories to the Kennedy assassination through one man.","fred-darwin-s-list-of-a-high-level-flying-saucer-committee-1949":"A man named Fred Darwin is said to have drawn up a list of names belonging to a special committee on flying saucers \"at the highest level\", set somewhere around the Pentagon's research and development board between 1949 and 1954. Michels brings it up himself, citing Darwin and a UFO researcher named William Steinman, and the names he links to the list include the engineer Vannevar Bush, the mathematician John von Neumann, the physicists Lloyd Berkner and Karl Compton, Eric A. Walker and Robert F. Reinhardt. If the list were genuine, it would put some of the country's top scientists on a secret saucer committee.","fred-maywald-confirms-the-story-in-recorded-call-1967":"A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas says that in 1967 a UFO shut down nuclear missiles in Montana, and that his commander that night, Fred Maywald, later confirmed the story on a recorded call. Salas tells Michels he only reached Maywald in 1996, and that the call taught him he had been at Oscar Flight, one group of missile sites, rather than Echo Flight, another. He says Maywald 'verified the essentials of the story,' and the recording is posted on the website spiralgalaxy.org.","fred-reed-s-letter-on-relandscaping-the-mesa-1948":"A man named Fred Reed wrote in a letter dated March 27, 1999, that a team came to the mesa at Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, where a flying saucer is said to have crashed in 1948, in order to relandscape it. Scott Ramsey, a researcher who has spent years on the Aztec crash, tells Michels about the letter, saying the crew came \"basically to relandscape the mesa.\" If true, it would mean the site was deliberately cleaned up to hide what happened there.","frederick-myers-and-the-society-for-psychical-research-s-tel-1882":"In England in 1882, a writer named Frederick Myers, working with the Society for Psychical Research, a group set up to study psychic claims scientifically, is said to have introduced telepathy, mind-to-mind contact, as the model for explaining experiences such as dreams that seem to know distant or future events. The researcher Eric Wargo tells Michels this without naming a source, to show where the long-standing assumption that such experiences were telepathy came from.","frederick-valentich-disappearance-1978":"In 1978 a young pilot named Frederick Valentich vanished while flying his small Cessna plane over Bass Strait, the stretch of sea between mainland Australia and Tasmania. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart brings it up in passing with Michels. He uses it to set the scene for another event a few months later, and he gives no further detail of what happened to the plane.","frederick-wolfe-german-pow-shot-at-fort-knox-1944":"A German soldier named Frederick Wolfe was sent to Fort Knox, Kentucky, as a prisoner of war and was shot there. A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen tells Michels the story without naming a source. In the telling he places it after the Second World War, though the story's date is 1944.","free-edgar-mitchell-experiencer-study-humanlike-beings-and-f":"More than half of the people who report contact with nonhuman beings describe the beings as humanlike. Michels cites this again and again from a survey run by FREE, a research group tied to the Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell, which he says holds \"5,000 plus\" accounts from people who say they were abducted. He gives the figure as 52 percent when speaking to Mike Masters, the author of a book called The Extratempestrial Model, which argues that the visitors may be humans from the future, and as \"over 50%\" elsewhere; in another episode he cites the same study for what it found about tissue being sampled from people.","free-energy-device-in-stan-meyer-s-archive-in-michigan":"A warehouse in Michigan holding the archive of the late inventor Stan Meyer is said to be full of floppy disks and devices, and one item in it is said to be a free-energy device, a machine meant to put out more power than it takes in. Steven Greer, a physician who campaigns for UFO secrets to be made public, tells Michels that his science advisor, Dr. Ted Loder, went and saw the warehouse, and he points to a photo posted on the website dpiarchive.com. If it were true, a working source of nearly limitless energy would be sitting forgotten in storage.","freeman-dyson-on-ida-advisory-board":"A man named Bob Maguire says the physicist Freeman Dyson sat on the advisory board of the place where he worked: the Center for Communications Research, a research centre run by the Institute for Defense Analysis in New Jersey. Maguire tells Michels this from his own working life, in passing, as a mark of the kind of minds that were around him there.","freemasonry-descends-from-cathedral-builder-guilds-and-the-t":"Modern Freemasonry grew out of two older traditions that merged: the lodges of the guild builders who actually built Europe's cathedrals, and the Knights Templar, the medieval order of crusading knights. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels this as his own summary, without naming a source.","freescale-semiconductor-engineers-aboard-mh370-2014":"Michels says that among the listed passengers on MH370, the Malaysian airliner that vanished in 2014, were 20 engineers from Freescale Semiconductor, a chip company. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about American scientists going missing, where it serves as one more case of technical experts disappearing together.","freezing-at-the-communion-cover-in-a-barnes-noble-1990":"John Blitch, a former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, says that sometime in the 1990s he was walking through a Barnes & Noble in a mall when he saw the cover of Communion, Whitley Strieber's book about his encounters with strange beings, with its drawn face on the front. Blitch tells Michels he \"froze\" in his tracks at the sight of it. He tells it as a sign that the face was something he had already met.","freikorps-stahlhelm-militias-wearing-swastikas-1918":"After the First World War, Germany's volunteer militias, called the Freikorps, are said to have painted swastikas on their helmets, and one group was the Stahlhelm, the \"steel helmet\". Peter Levenda, a writer on the history of intelligence and the occult, tells Michels this without naming a source. It matters because it would place the swastika in German nationalist politics before the Nazis made it theirs.","french-1999-cometa-report-conclusions":"The COMETA report, a French study of UFOs put out in 1999 by a group of senior French experts, concluded that these objects could be alien spacecraft. The physicist and former NASA scientist Kevin Knuth sums it up on the show: \"yes, these things could be alien spacecraft. There may be a base somewhere in our solar system and the US is probably hiding information.\" If so, it would mean a serious foreign group of experts took the alien idea seriously and believed the US was keeping secrets.","french-bombing-of-the-greenpeace-boat-in-auckland-harbor-1985":"In 1985 a Greenpeace boat was blown up in Auckland Harbor, New Zealand, by the DGSE, France's intelligence service, in what the journalist Ross Coulthart calls an intelligence operation \"slap-bang in the middle of Auckland Harbor.\" Coulthart brings it up with Michels as an example of an allied country's spies carrying out a secret operation on friendly soil, which matters to him because it shows how far intelligence services will go out of sight.","french-demonstrations-escalating-up-to-atomic-energy-officia-1956":"In France in 1956, demonstrations of the American inventor Townsend Brown's devices, which the episode presents as early UFO-like technology, are said to have climbed step by step to ever more senior audiences, up to officials in atomic energy. A researcher named Jan Lundquist tells Michels this, without naming a source, from the letters that people in France wrote to a witness named Jacques Cornillon, saying \"We're inviting so-and-so to see a demonstration,\" with \"each of those demonstrations\" a \"level higher\"; if true, it would mean the work drew serious official interest.","french-farmer-meets-an-nhi-holding-a-tray-of-soil-samples":"In France, a trusted and respected farmer is said to have found a spaceship on his land, walked up to it, and met an NHI, short for non-human intelligence, a being that is not human, holding a tray full of soil samples. The journalist Michael Shellenberger tells Michels this as a case from the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, and says the being was there only \"supposedly\". If true, it would suggest the visitors were out here collecting samples of the Earth.","frequent-ufo-sightings-in-alamogordo":"Lyn Buchanan, a former US Army sergeant, says UFOs are a common sight where he lives in Alamogordo, New Mexico. He tells Michels from his own experience, \"I've seen a lot of UFOs... We see UFOs all the time,\" and does not describe any single sighting.","fresh-yellow-curb-lines-marking-the-kill-zone-on-elm-street-1963":"Three yellow lines are said to have been painted on the curb of Elm Street in Dallas the night before President Kennedy was shot in 1963, right where the shots were fired. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source. He says Beverly Oliver, a witness to the shooting, was standing on one of the lines and points to the Zapruder film, the home movie of the killing. If it is true, someone had marked out the kill zone ahead of time.","freud-s-1895-dream-foreshadowing-his-oral-cancer":"A famous dream Sigmund Freud had in 1895 foreshadowed the mouth cancer he was found to have decades later, in 1923, according to the writer Eric Wargo. He notes that Freud spent about 14 pages on the dream in his landmark book The Interpretation of Dreams, and tells it to Michels as a case of a dream seeing the future.","friedman-and-the-woods-authenticate-mj-12-documents-1980":"The MJ-12 papers are documents that claim to come from a secret US group managing crashed flying saucers. According to a UFO commentator who goes by UAPGerb, the UFO researcher Stanton Friedman spent years with Robert Wood and Ryan Wood authenticating two of them: the Special Operations Manual, said to be a guide for handling recovered craft, and the Eisenhower briefing document, a paper dated November 18, 1952 that presents itself as a briefing for Eisenhower. UAPGerb says he tends to go with Friedman's work, summarising it without naming a source, since if the papers are genuine the US government held saucer wreckage by the early 1950s.","friend-abducted-by-grays-shows-delta-pattern-marks":"A friend once turned up at Tim Alberino's house and casually said, \"I forgot to tell you. They came and got me,\" meaning he had been taken by grays, the small grey-skinned beings of many abduction stories. Alberino tells Michels he was there for it, and that the friend had marks on him in a triangular, delta-shaped pattern. If true, the marks would be physical traces of an abduction.","friend-dying-on-the-moon":"Someone is said to have broken down while describing a friend of his who died on the moon, which would point to a secret space programme beyond anything the public knows of. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels he heard this from a person he trusts, whom he does not name, and he gives no date or details of how it happened.","friend-mike-secretly-watched-buer-and-drew-race-independentl-2016":"Charles Buhler, presented on the show as a NASA chief who says his team has built antigravity propulsion, says that around 2016 in Florida a friend of his named Mike knew that Buhler and a man named Andrew Arjima had each been working on the same thing, separately, for years. Buhler, who was one of the two, tells Michels that Mike watched them both and never said a word: \"He knew about us working on this independently for years and never told us, never did.\" If it happened that way, two people were racing toward the same goal without knowing it, and someone in between was keeping it quiet.","friend-s-family-sees-the-same-beach-lights-2020":"A friend's family, out on the beach late one night near Cocoa Beach, Florida, around 2020 or 2021, saw the same strange lights in the sky that Charles Buhler says he has watched there himself. Buhler, who counts himself a witness to the lights, passes this on as what his friend told him, and says he thought it was \"super cool\" because the friend had been there with his family when they saw it.","friend-s-government-contact-warns-him-off-the-kandahar-giant-2003":"A government insider is said to have \"totally switched\" and gone cold when asked about the \"Kandahar giant\", a story that US troops in Afghanistan killed a 12-foot giant in the early 2000s. Tim Alberino, a guest who tells the giant story, relays this from a friend who asked the question of a contact. If it is true, the subject touched a nerve inside the government.","friend-s-medium-reading-channels-his-late-sister":"A friend of Jesse Michels, whom he does not name, went to see a medium, a person who claims to pass on messages from the dead, and the medium straight away began teasing and ribbing him just the way his late sister used to. Michels tells it himself as something his friend told him, offered as a hint that something of the sister's personality might have come through.","friend-sees-a-black-pyramid-in-antarctica":"A man who went to Antarctica, possibly to make a documentary, is said to have seen a kind of black pyramid there. Michels tells this himself as something a friend told him, without naming the friend or saying when or where on the continent it happened. If true, it would mean a large, unexplained structure sits in the Antarctic ice.","friend-sees-a-portal-open-in-the-sky-and-saucers-fly-out":"A friend of Tim Alberino, a guest on an episode about reported giants, is said to have watched a portal open in the sky and saucers fly out of it. Alberino tells Michels this second-hand, as something his unnamed friend saw: \"My friend saw this actually happen.\" He gives no time or place. If it were true, it would suggest the craft come from some opening in the sky, not from across space.","friend-spoke-to-amy-eskridge-hours-before-her-death-2022":"A friend of Amy Eskridge, a woman who died in Alabama in 2022, says she does not believe Eskridge killed herself. The friend, who is not named, says on an episode about scientists going missing that she spoke to Eskridge four hours before her death, and that Eskridge told her 'time and time again' that she was not going to commit suicide. If that is right, the death would not have been what it was taken to be.","friend-with-the-same-slit-in-his-eye-is-a-raudive-expert":"The writer Whitley Strieber, who has long described his own encounters with beings he believes are not human, tells Michels about a friend in Marin County, California, who has the same odd slit in his eye that Strieber has. That friend, Strieber says, is \"one of the world's leading experts\" on Konstantin Raudive, a Latvian psychologist known for recording what he believed were voices of the dead on audio tape. Strieber offers the shared mark and the friend's field as one more strange link between people touched by these experiences.","friends-lab-work-becomes-a-weapon-in-months":"A guest named Dave Rossi tells Michels that friends he shares with others are working privately on \"some of these things\", the kind of advanced physics the episode is about, and that after only six or seven months in the lab, \"all of a sudden it's a weapon now.\" He passes this on as what those friends told him, and he does not say what the work was. If it is true, it would mean this kind of research can become dangerous very fast.","friends-turned-away-from-area-51":"Some friends of a Russian guest named Kiran Ilum Xenov, who says he was taken by aliens in 1997, recently drove out to Area 51, a secret US military base in the Nevada desert, hoping to take a look, and the military would not let them through. Xenov tells Michels this as something his friends told him. He offers it as a sign of how closely the base is guarded.","friends-who-fought-the-cia-and-dni-internally-2020":"Some people inside the US intelligence world fought the CIA and the office of the Director of National Intelligence (the office that oversees all the US spy agencies) from within, telling them \"I can prove you're lying\" and then proving it. A guest named Dylan Borland tells Michels this as something he was part of: he says these friends \"went to war with me\" sometime in the 2020s. If true, it would mean insiders caught those agencies lying, though as he tells it, he does not say what the lies were about.","friends-working-on-antigravity-received-threats":"Several friends of a scientist named Kevin Knuth worked on antigravity, meaning technology that would cancel or push against gravity, and they are said to have faced backlash and threats for it. Michels raises this with Knuth, who appears on the show as a NASA whistleblower. Michels reminds him that he first told the story on Danny Jones's podcast. If true, it would suggest that this kind of research is actively discouraged.","friendship-with-robert-anton-wilson-and-his-daughter-s-murde-1970":"Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of psychic phenomena who lived and worked in Berkeley in the 1970s, says his friend Robert Anton Wilson lost his \"beautiful 15-year-old daughter,\" who was murdered in Berkeley. Mishlove tells Michels this from his own memory of the friendship, mentioning Timothy Leary and the book Reality Is What You Can Get Away With along the way.","fritz-haber-reverse-engineered-the-red-pyramid-for-his-ammon-1900":"The German chemist Fritz Haber is said to have built his famous process for making ammonia, between about 1900 and 1903, by working out and copying the mechanism inside the Red Pyramid, an ancient pyramid in Egypt. A pyramid researcher named Geoffrey Drumm tells Michels this as his own hypothesis, saying Haber went through the same investigation of the pyramid that Drumm himself did. He points to a book that mentions an Egyptian banker financing Haber's ammonia project and to postcards of Haber's dated in Egypt, and he sets a picture of Haber's original apparatus beside the pyramid's three chambers to show they match; in one telling he also names Haber's partner Carl Bosch. If true, it would mean the pyramid was a working chemical machine.","fugal-has-seen-ufos-in-the-air-himself":"Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property famous for reports of strange events, says he has seen UFOs flying with his own eyes. \"I've seen them myself in the air,\" he tells Michels, without giving a time or place. Coming from the man who runs the ranch's investigations, it is a first-hand claim, not something passed on.","fulgurites-in-giza-iron-veins-as-evidence-of-high-voltage-cu":"Samples from iron ore veins at the Giza Plateau in Egypt contain fulgurites, glassy traces left when a powerful electric current melts rock, and these show that high-voltage current once ran through the veins. A researcher named Jeffrey Drum makes this claim to Michels without naming a source. If he is right, it would point to an energy grid beneath the pyramids.","fungi-growing-on-the-iss-and-mir":"Fungi have been found growing on the International Space Station and on the old Russian space station Mir, Michels says. He tells it himself, without naming a source, in an episode with a mushroom entrepreneur named Tero Isokauppila, where it supports the idea that fungi are tough enough to survive in space and might even have come from it.","fungi-on-the-iss-and-crick-s-directed-panspermia":"Michels says fungi have been found on the International Space Station, and links this to Francis Crick, co-discoverer of the structure of DNA. Michels says Crick argued for \"directed panspermia\", the idea that life on Earth was deliberately seeded from space, because DNA could not have formed in 100 million years. Michels tells this himself, citing Crick's book Life Itself and a paper he dates to 1973, in which, he says, Crick imagined a high-tech spaceship carrying blue-green algae, carbon dioxide, water and mushrooms to start life here.","fused-silica-microspheres-from-fulgurites-drumm-inside-a-boa":"Geoffrey Drumm, who has his own theory of what the pyramids were really for, says he found fused silica, tiny glassy beads of the kind found in fulgurites, the glass tubes lightning leaves when it strikes sand, at Giza in Egypt. He shows Michels a picture of himself standing in one of the boat pits, the long pits cut into the rock beside the Great Pyramid: \"this is me down inside one of the boat pits adjacent to the great pyramid.\" He tells it as something he saw himself, and it matters to him because such glass would point to intense heat or electricity at the site.","fusion-scientist-nuno-loureiro-shot-dead-at-his-door-2025":"Nuno Loureiro, a fusion scientist Michels calls \"the tip of the spear\" on magnetic reconnection, a key problem in making nuclear fusion work, was shot at his home on December 15, 2025, went into surgery that evening and was pronounced dead the next morning. Michels tells it himself, naming Claudio Neves Valente as the shooter and citing a Department of Justice transcript of Valente's videos; he places the home in Brookline, Massachusetts, though at one point says Brooklyn, New York. Arguing with a skeptic, he rejects the idea that the killing, or the case of Carl Grillmair, is \"a non-story,\" seeing it as part of a pattern of top scientists going missing or dying.","fyodorov-s-russian-cosmism-and-resurrection":"A Russian librarian and philosopher named Nikolai Fyodorov believed that the true task of humanity and of Christianity was not to wait for the resurrection of the dead but to bring it about. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as the starting point of Russian Cosmism, a movement that joined that religious aim to science and space travel, and he raises it in an episode on NASA and secret science because it would mean the space age had roots in a hope of defeating death.","gabinetto-rs33-and-its-nine-step-protocol-1933":"In 1933, the story goes, the Italian government under the dictator Benito Mussolini set up a secret research group called Gabinetto RS33, or Cabinet RS33. The radio pioneer Guglielmo Marconi chaired it and the physicist Enrico Fermi was its vice president, and it studied what they call the extraterrestrial issue. Michels tells it himself, saying the group reportedly drew up a nine-step protocol for the Magenta crash, a reported 1933 crash of an unknown craft in northern Italy, and for any like it, and he calls it Italy's version of Majestic 12, a supposed secret US panel on UFOs. His main source is a prince who says he is Marconi's grandson. The grandson tells the show he knew it through the family, not from documents. In his account the dates differ: once 1933, once 1953, when he says the committee studied UFO sightings near Varese in northern Italy. If true, a European government was handling crashed craft more than a decade before the US.","gaffney-blocked-lockheed-to-bigelow-ufo-material-transfer-2008":"The claim is that in 2008 a CIA official named Glenn Gaffney personally stopped UFO materials from being moved from the defence company Lockheed Martin to Bigelow Aerospace, a private space company. Michels says the journalist George Knapp said this in testimony to Congress, naming Gaffney as the man responsible for blocking the transfer. A guest named Dylan Borland, who reports on the subject, brings it up on another episode only as a name: 'Glenn Gaffney, CIA,' pointing to the same congressional hearing. Neither telling says what the materials were or how the block was done. If true, it would mean UFO material sat with a defence contractor and a CIA official decided where it could go.","galileo-project-instruments-on-harvard-college-observatory-2021":"The Galileo Project is an effort begun in 2021 by a Harvard researcher named Avi Loeb to look for UFOs with scientific instruments. In early 2022 Loeb told Michels he had received thousands of emails from people who wanted to help, that more than a hundred scientists had joined, and that he was buying instruments to put on the roof of the Harvard College Observatory 'in the coming months'. In 2024 he described it as an attempt to collect new evidence 'without prejudice', using the scientific method, and the researcher Eric Weinstein mentions it only in passing, as Loeb 'setting up his thing' at Harvard.","galileo-project-monthly-meetings-2021":"The Galileo Project is a research effort led by the astronomer Avi Loeb that searches the sky for signs of alien technology, and its team has met on Zoom on the first Friday of every month since it began in 2021. The skeptic and researcher Michael Shermer says this from the inside: he has been \"on the Galileo team since the first week\" and says \"I never miss a meeting.\" He brings it up to show that even a skeptic takes part in the search.","gallaudet-briefs-the-japanese-diet-s-uap-caucus-2024":"Japan's parliament, the Diet, has a group of lawmakers devoted to UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, and a retired US Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet says he briefed them. He tells Michels that around 2024 or 2025 he \"gave a virtual presentation to this UAP caucus.\" If so, the subject has reached lawmakers well beyond Washington.","gallaudet-co-writes-the-bluegill-triple-prime-report-1962":"A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet says he helped write a report on Bluegill Triple Prime, a US nuclear test of 1962. He tells Michels, 'I helped Jeff Cruickshank write the report on it,' naming Cruickshank as the main author. In Gallaudet's telling, it means he worked directly on the record of that test.","gallaudet-sees-the-go-fast-video-on-the-navy-secret-network-2015":"In 2015, the Go Fast video, footage from Navy jets of a small object racing low over the ocean during an exercise with the carrier USS Theodore Roosevelt off the US East Coast, was passed around inside the Navy on its classified computer network. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet tells Michels he saw it there himself: \"I was a onestar admiral and I saw on the Navy Secret Network the Go Fast video,\" and he links it to the reports of Ryan Graves, a Navy fighter pilot. If true, it means senior Navy officers were watching the footage through official channels when it was recorded.","gallaudet-visits-skinwalker-ranch-with-brandon-fugal":"A Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet tells Michels that he went out to Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch in Utah known for reports of strange happenings, with a man named Brandon Fugal. Gallaudet speaks of it as his own visit and says only that it went well: \"So we went out and we had this great day together.\"","gallimore-and-strassman-s-2015-extended-state-dmt-dmtx-propo":"In 2015, a neuroscientist named Andrew Gallimore and a researcher named Rick Strassman proposed a way to keep a person in the state brought on by the powerful psychedelic DMT for much longer than its usual few minutes, a method they called extended-state DMT, or DMTx. Gallimore tells Michels he helped develop it, calling it both a model and a protocol, and says the work did not end in 2015 and is still going on. It matters to him because he argues that DMT opens a door onto an alien realm, and a longer stay would let people explore it.","gallimore-s-laser-speckle-explanation-for-the-dmt-laser-code":"The \"code\" some people report seeing when they shine a laser while on DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, may come from laser speckle, the grainy pattern laser light makes on the retina. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore offers this to Michels as a reply to the claims of a man named Danny, whose experiments are said to reveal a code behind reality. Gallimore says the speckle acts \"like a sensory scaffold\" around which, under DMT, the brain builds complex forms.","games-resume-after-the-dtra-contract-ends-an-sf86-request-pu-2024":"Dylan Borland, a man who says he held a US security clearance, says that once his contract with DTRA, a Pentagon agency that deals with weapons of mass destruction, ended in 2024 or 2025, the \"games\" against him started again. He tells Michels he was still using his clearance \"last June,\" but a request to file a new SF86, the standard form for a government background check, left his clearance listed as \"undefined.\" He tells it as a sign that someone is quietly getting in his way.","garrett-graff-s-ufo-debunking-book-after-the-aspen-institute-2020":"The journalist Garrett Graff published a book called UFO that is \"basically just a debunking of all these cases,\" says the journalist Michael Shellenberger, who dates it to earlier in 2024. He ties it to \"pre-bunking,\" arguing against a claim before it comes out, at the Aspen Institute, a policy think tank, and names a woman called Vivian Schiller alongside it, which would mean the book was part of an effort to shape opinion ahead of new UFO claims.","garry-nolan-debunking-atacama-mummy":"The Stanford biologist Garry Nolan is said to have entered the UFO field by way of the Atacama mummy, a tiny mummified body found in the Atacama desert that some took for an alien, which Nolan's work went on to debunk. Michels brings it up himself in passing as the start of Nolan's path into UFO research, without giving further detail.","garry-nolan-has-seen-little-people-and-a-craft":"Garry Nolan, a Stanford scientist who has studied people and materials linked to UFO reports, has personally seen \"some little people\" and a craft, according to Michels. Michels says this on the show as something Nolan told him, without giving when or where it happened. If true, a respected scientist would be claiming to have seen non-human beings and their vehicle with his own eyes.","garry-nolan-s-childhood-bedside-visitors":"Michels says that Garry Nolan, a Stanford professor, remembers beings at the foot of his bed as a child, not an abduction but a visit, and in one episode Michels says Nolan remembers a \"gray\", the small grey-skinned figure common in alien accounts, in his room and also a saucer he saw while on a paper route. Michels repeats this from Nolan on three different episodes, and in the last one he stresses that Nolan only began to uncover these memories later in life, after he started studying the subject professionally. Michels brings it up to show that even a respected scientist can carry experiences like these.","garry-nolan-s-magnesium-bismuth-sample-from-ubatuba-1950":"A fisherman on a beach at Ubatuba, Brazil, is said to have seen an object explode in 1957, and its fragments are said to have been mailed years later to Jacques Vallée, a longtime UFO researcher, then analysed by Garry Nolan, a Stanford scientist, who keeps a magnesium-bismuth piece in his lab. Michels tells this himself on several episodes without naming a source. In some tellings he credits Vallée, mentions Nolan's appearance on the Joe Rogan Experience and says Nolan measured the material's makeup with mass spectrometry, a lab method that identifies the elements in a sample. If true, it would put physical material from a reported UFO crash in a university lab.","gary-mckinnon-finds-a-list-of-off-planet-officers-2001":"Around 2001 or 2002, a British hacker named Gary McKinnon broke into US military computers from the UK and, by his account, came across a page listing \"off planet officers\", meaning military officers who serve somewhere other than Earth. Michels retells this from what McKinnon has said, in one episode as \"this list of 11 or 12 off Planet officers\" and in a later one as a list of \"non-terrestrial officers of which there are 40\", where he also adds his own theory about a supply chain in space. If true, it would point to a hidden military presence beyond Earth.","gary-mckinnon-s-hack-and-the-tic-tac-image-2001":"Around 2001, a British hacker named Gary McKinnon broke into computers at NASA's Johnson Space Center in Houston after hearing of a former NASA worker turned whistleblower, Donna Hare, and says he saw a UFO image on his screen: in his own words to Michels, \"a big straight kind of silvery line and then it's coming down,\" what he calls cigar-shaped and what people now call a Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint. Michels retells it in several episodes, and his version differs from McKinnon's: he describes a Tic Tac that seemed to be orbiting Earth, dates it variously from 2001 to 2003, and in one episode ties it to Steven Greer's Disclosure Project and its book Disclosure. If true, NASA would hold images of an unknown craft near Earth.","gary-nolan-presents-his-injury-research-the-night-before-the-2016":"In 2016, the night before a group visited a site in New Mexico, a researcher named Garry Nolan sat with them over beers and showed them his research and photos on injuries, the story goes. Diana Pasulka, who comes on the show to talk about UFOs and religion, says she was there: \"we were all just hanging out having a beer and Gary decided he was going to share his research with us and photos.\" She tells it as a first-hand memory of how the trip began, before anyone had seen the site itself.","gary-nolan-s-caudate-putamen-theories":"The Stanford scientist Garry Nolan, whom Michels calls both an immunologist and a microbiologist and a friend, is said to link UFO experiences to two connected parts of the brain's basal ganglia, the caudate nucleus and the putamen: people with more densely packed neurons there are supposedly more likely to see UFOs, and also more \"psychic\", more intuitive and better at executive functioning. Michels brings this up on several episodes, retelling Nolan's work in his own words. He calls it \"working theories\" that are \"not definitive\" in one place and \"studies\" in another, and on one occasion adds that the trait runs in families, which would suggest that seeing UFOs is partly inherited.","gary-nolan-s-cia-file-on-ufo-experiencers-and-havana-syndrom-2010":"A scientist named Garry Nolan was reportedly handed a file by the CIA and asked to look into people who had been injured, including UFO witnesses and victims of Havana syndrome, the mysterious illnesses reported by US diplomats and officials. Michels tells this from his own conversation with Nolan, who he says mentioned being \"given a file by the CIA\" and \"tasked with looking into this.\"","gary-nolan-s-crash-retrieval-parts-from-jacques-vallee":"A Stanford professor named Garry Nolan keeps what he says are pieces recovered from crashed UFOs, which he got from Jacques Vallée, a longtime researcher Michels calls 'the godfather of UFO research'. Michels says he has seen the pieces himself in Nolan's lab, that they are magnesium and bismuth, and that Nolan told him such materials might be useful for making micro-sized waveguides, tiny structures that channel energy, for use on Earth. If the pieces are what Nolan says, they would be physical material from a craft not made by people.","gary-nolan-s-strontium-ufo-metamaterial-sample":"Michels says, hedging with \"I believe\", that one of the samples the scientist Garry Nolan keeps at his Stanford lab, which Nolan thinks might be UFO metamaterials, meaning specially structured materials, recovered from crashes, contains strontium. Michels brings it up himself without naming a source, linking it to the element under discussion.","gary-s-biefeld-brown-ccto-experiments-in-a-garden-shed-2007":"Gary McKinnon, the British hacker who broke into US military computers, says that for years since around 2007 he has run home experiments on the Biefeld-Brown effect, a claimed effect in which a high-voltage capacitor produces a small push, using CCTO, a ceramic material that stores electric charge unusually well. He tells Michels he works in a tiny space in his UK garden: \"I'm only doing these experiments in a 10x 10t shed.\" He tells it as his own amateur attempt at the kind of propulsion that some people link to UFOs.","gary-stearman-s-cessna-escorted-by-a-walnut-shaped-ufo":"A man named Gary Stearman was flying a small Cessna plane from Lubbock, Texas to Dallas when, it is said, a voice told him, \"If you look to your left, you'll see a UFO,\" and a walnut-shaped object then flew beside the plane as an escort. Tim Alberino, a guest on an episode about reported giants, tells Michels this as Stearman told it to him. Whose voice it was, the telling does not say, but if true it would mean someone knew the object was coming.","gary-webb-and-cia-cocaine-in-the-inner-city-1980":"The comedian Kurt Metzger brings up Gary Webb, a journalist who reported that cocaine tied to CIA-backed groups flowed into poor American neighbourhoods in the 1980s, calling him \"the guy that knew the CIA brought coke into the hood.\" Metzger mentions him in passing as an example of someone who exposed what the government was hiding, which, if true, would mean the CIA helped feed the drug trade in American cities.","garyaev-healing-people-in-wooden-pyramids":"A guest named Dave Rossi says that a man named Peter Garyaev, working in Russia, was healing people inside wooden pyramids. Rossi gives no source beyond a website on what it calls \"linguistic wave genetics\", and says nothing more about how the healing was supposed to work.","gate-crashing-bobby-kennedy-s-funeral-at-st-patrick-s-1968":"The writer Peter Levenda, billed on the show as a historian, says that in 1968 he and a companion slipped uninvited into Bobby Kennedy's funeral at St. Patrick's Cathedral in New York. When an official walked up and asked if they were \"Russian Orthodox representatives,\" Levenda says he answered that no, they represented a different Orthodox church, and they got in among mourners such as Rose Kennedy and Jackie Onassis. He tells it as his own story and says a still photograph of the funeral procession shows it.","gate-started-by-sidney-marland-in-1972-possibly-as-a-funnel":"Michels suggests that GATE, an American school programme for gifted and talented children, was started in 1972 by a man named Sidney Marland, and that it may have worked as a funnel for finding unusual children for secret government purposes. Michels offers this himself as his own belief (\"I think\"), without giving a source, and mentions Senator Frank Church in the same discussion. If true, an ordinary school programme would have doubled as a way of screening children.","gate-testing-and-grooming-by-outside-psychologists-2004":"A man named Jordan Jose says that as a child in Springville, New York, he was picked out through gifted-and-talented school testing and then shaped by outside psychologists, which he links to his claim that a secret UFO program took him as a kid. He tells Michels that in 2004 or 2005 he was tested by the gifted and talented education program, known as GATE, using the Woodcock test of achievement, a standard school skills test. If true, it would mean ordinary school testing was used to find children for the program.","gauge-theory-economics-and-dale-jorgenson-s-opposition-at-ha-1990":"In the 1990s, work applying gauge theory, a branch of mathematics from physics, to economics ran into resistance at Harvard's economics department, according to the researcher Eric Weinstein, who was part of it and who names Pia Malaney in connection with the work. He tells Michels it \"was not taken well,\" especially by Dale Jorgenson, then chairman of the department. He tells it as an example of how establishments push back on new ideas.","gauntlet-like-object-with-moving-hieroglyphic-symbols-2014":"Randy Anderson says that in 2014, at Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy base in Indiana, he saw an object shaped something like a gauntlet, an armoured glove, with a display screen, and above the screen symbols like hieroglyphs began to appear and move. He tells Michels it \"almost looked like a mirage type of effect\" and that he watched \"these hieroglyphic symbols that I started to see appear.\" He gives it as something he saw himself, and if true it would mean a strange, non-human-looking device was being held at a US military base.","gec-marconi-scientist-deaths-during-star-wars-sdi-era-1982":"Between 1982 and 1990, Michels says, 25 engineers and computer scientists died one after another, all of them working for GEC Marconi, a British defence firm, on some of Britain's most secret military projects. The deaths fell during the years of America's \"Star Wars\" missile defence plan. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about top scientists going missing, as an earlier pattern that might echo today's.","geller-2019-radio-interview-puharich-drugged-him-with-mushro-1970":"Uri Geller is said to have claimed, on a radio show around 2019, that a man named Puharich had experimented on him with mushrooms and with hypnosis. A researcher named Greg Malatzi passes this on to Michels as something Geller said in public, without naming the show. If true, it would mean Geller's early experiences were shaped by drugs and hypnosis given to him by the man studying him.","geller-bends-a-spoon-during-the-film-interview":"The spoon-bender Uri Geller bent a spoon in front of a film crew while they interviewed him, according to a researcher named Greg Malatzi, who was there. Malatzi tells Michels simply that Geller 'did bend a spoon for us' and that he does not know how he did it.","geller-boards-a-landed-ufo-in-the-sinai-desert-and-returns-w-1971":"Around 1971 or 1972, a UFO is said to have landed in the Sinai Desert, and the self-described psychic Uri Geller went into a kind of trance, boarded it, and came back with a pen. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels this, summarising a book about Geller by Andrija Puharich, a researcher who worked closely with him. If true, Geller's powers would be linked to direct contact with the craft.","geller-teleported-from-new-york-city-to-ossining-1973":"Uri Geller, an Israeli performer famous for claiming psychic powers such as bending spoons with his mind, was walking down a street in New York City, in 1973 as the researcher Greg Malatzi recalls it, when he was suddenly teleported some thirty miles north to Ossining, New York. Malatzi tells the story as a researcher of Andrija Puharich, a doctor who studied Geller, and points to a Super 8 home film Puharich shot of the screen porch at Ossining. If true, it would be a case of a person being moved instantly from one place to another.","gemini-11-object-flyover-and-battery-drain-1966":"During NASA's Gemini 11 flight in 1966, an object reportedly passed near the capsule carrying the astronauts Pete Conrad and Richard Gordon, and their batteries then failed: \"Battery Charlie was drained,\" as Michels reads it from the mission transcripts. In a later episode he says he talked it over with a researcher named Kevin Knuth and came away thinking \"there was an actual UFO around Gemini 11 that caused this electromagnetic outage,\" and he points to a photo from a NASA archive he links to the name Simpkinson. If true, it would mean astronauts in orbit had an encounter that knocked out their power.","gemini-7-frank-borman-s-bogey-at-10-o-clock-high-1965":"In 1965, during Gemini 7, a two-man NASA flight in Earth orbit, the astronaut Frank Borman is heard on the mission audio pointing out his rocket's spent booster at \"2:00\" and then reporting a separate object he calls a \"bogey\", which Michels explains is pilot's code for an unknown object. Michels tells this himself from the recording, saying it shows an astronaut separating known debris from something he could not identify. On a later show he ties the audio to a release of files on UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's current term for UFOs, by what he calls the Department of War.","gemini-ufo-audio-and-airbrushed-ufo-in-nasa-textbook-1960":"During NASA's Gemini flights of the 1960s, the two-man missions in Earth orbit, astronauts are said to have been recorded talking about a UFO, and a picture of an object is said to have been airbrushed into a NASA textbook known as the Simpkinson textbook. Michels mentions this briefly and credits his friend Chris Ramsay, host of the show Area 52, for finding the book. If true, it would mean NASA's own material had once shown something unexplained in orbit.","gene-cernan-s-flashes-and-blinking-objects-in-space-1972":"Gene Cernan, the astronaut who in 1972 became the last man to walk on the moon, is said to have seen \"bizarre flashes\" and \"distant blinking objects,\" and to have been hit by \"blinding flashes of light in the dark while he slept.\" Michels tells it from a release of UFO records by the Department of War, in an episode about a UFO book he says the Pentagon tried to censor. If accurate, strange things were seen even on the Apollo missions.","gene-roddenberry-channeling-session-with-puharich-and-schlem-1973":"In the mid-1970s, in Ossining, New York, Gene Roddenberry, the creator of Star Trek, is said to have sat in on a channelling session, where a medium speaks messages said to come from other beings. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels the people there were \"Roddenberry, Puharich, and Phyllis Schlemmer,\" with Schlemmer as the channeler and Andrija Puharich as a researcher of psychics. Malatzi cites a tape of the session in Puharich's archive. Michels tells his own version on another episode and goes further: he says Roddenberry \"was inspired by\" Schlemmer, so that the channelling fed into Star Trek. Michels also names EG&G, a company, in that story, and he does not mention Puharich. If true, one of the best-known science fiction shows took ideas from messages said to come from beyond Earth.","general-exon-held-a-roswell-body-like-a-big-insect-1947":"An Air Force general named Arthur Exon said that in 1947 he held one of the bodies when debris and bodies from the reported crash near Roswell, New Mexico, were brought to Air Materiel Command, the military's aircraft-testing command at Wright Field, and that it was like a big insect. The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met aliens himself, tells Michels this as something Exon told him directly. If true, it would put a senior officer's own hands on a non-human body.","general-moelner-the-secret-craft-filled-a-reconnaissance-gap":"A secret American craft is said to have been built to fill a gap in aerial spying, and once it had done that job the military went back to relying on spy satellites. A guest named Nick Cook, on an episode about claimed anti-gravity aircraft, tells Michels this is what a General Moelner advised him, and says he takes the general's word at face value. If true, it would explain why such a craft could exist and then fade from use.","general-muellner-s-retirement-day-interview-on-aurora-1998":"On the day he retired in 1998, General George Muellner, \"the US Air Force's head of research and development,\" sat for an interview about Aurora, the name given to a rumoured secret American aircraft. The writer Nick Cook tells Michels he did that interview himself, for a documentary called Billion Dollar Secret. Cook brings it up as a rare chance to put questions about secret aircraft to the man who had run Air Force research.","general-neil-mccasland-goes-missing-2020":"A retired Air Force general named Neil McCasland is said to have vanished mysteriously from his home in New Mexico. Michels tells this himself in several episodes. He describes McCasland as a man who ran the Air Force Research Laboratory at Wright-Patterson. He also says McCasland sat on the committee that oversees special access programs, the most tightly guarded classified projects. Michels says McCasland was a source for the musician Tom DeLonge's book series Sekret Machines, published as \"quote unquote fiction\". In an episode about missing scientists, Michels gives the scene in detail: Albuquerque, \"February 27th, 2026, a late Friday morning on Quail Run Court\", below the Sandia Mountains. For that account he draws on a 911 call recording and the Bernalillo County Sheriff's Office, and mentions a Susan McCasland. A UFO journalist named Jeremy Corbell tells it too. He stresses that a man \"charged with protecting our national security\" is \"missing right now\". If true, a general close to the most sensitive secrets has disappeared without explanation.","general-roya-makes-him-a-warrant-officer-then-chief-warrant-1977":"Between 1977 and 1979, at Army headquarters, a general named Roya told Joe McMoneagle, \"I'm going to make you a warrant officer,\" and later made him a chief warrant officer. McMoneagle tells Michels this from his own Army career. It shows senior officers backing him personally at the time.","general-spaatz-flew-to-texas-during-roswell-1947":"During the Roswell affair of 1947, when the Army in New Mexico announced it had recovered a \"flying disc\" and then said it was a weather balloon, General Carl Spaatz, a top air commander, flew to Texas. That is where the recovered parts were supposedly taken before going on to Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio. An online researcher who goes by the name RedPandaKoala tells Michels this, without naming a source, as a sign that the recovery mattered at the highest level.","general-starbird-leaves-johnston-island-hours-after-bluegill-1962":"In 1962, during Operation Dominic, a series of American nuclear tests in the Pacific, General Alfred Starbird left Johnston Island at 4 a.m. on the night of the Bluegill Triple Prime test. Michels says William Ogle wrote this, and he draws on the logs of the ship USS Summit County. Michels tells it himself as a hint that something unusual happened around that test.","general-stubblebine-recruits-buchanan-into-the-fort-meade-re-1980":"In the 1980s the Army general Albert Stubblebine brought an Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan to Fort Meade, Maryland, to join a unit that trained soldiers in remote viewing, meaning describing faraway places with the mind alone. Buchanan tells Michels the general was looking for people who could move or affect things by thought, and once got up in his face and asked, \"Did you kill my computers with your mind?\" He mentions the book The Men Who Stare at Goats while telling it, and if his story is true, the Army really was searching its ranks for psychic talent.","general-stubblebine-s-2-billion-offer-1992":"Steven Greer, who ran a UFO organisation called CSETI, says that in 1992 a general named Albert Stubblebine offered him $2 billion: \"in '92, General Stubble offered me $2 billion.\" Michels, without the figure or the date, frames Stubblebine as being first on the scene to almost recruit Greer \"into some sort of government program,\" while a researcher who goes by UAPGerb says that, according to documents and emails in what he calls the DPI archive, the money was meant to buy CSETI and fold it into Stubblebine's own work. If true, it would mean someone with military rank wanted to bring a private UFO group under his wing at enormous cost.","general-tells-varginha-driver-what-he-saw-was-supernatural-1996":"In 1996, during the operation in Varginha, Brazil, where witnesses say a strange creature was captured, a military driver says a general told him that what he had witnessed was something \"supernatural.\" The driver, who speaks only as \"military X\" to hide his name, tells it as something that happened to him, and it would mean senior officers knew the event was not of this world.","generals-without-clearance-turned-away-at-cape-canaveral-gat-1989":"Three-star generals on holiday in Florida would sometimes drive up to the gate at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station and be turned away because they lacked the clearance to get in, according to Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor who says he worked there in the 1990s. Rogers tells Michels this to show how tightly the site was guarded, on an episode where he says he saw a UFO in a secret hangar.","genesis-two-lights-matched-to-the-cosmic-microwave-backgroun":"Greg Rogers, a former NASA doctor, tells Michels that the lights in the opening of Genesis match the history of the early universe, and that the light of the second day is the moment \"light returned to the universe,\" which he links to the cosmic microwave background, the faint afterglow of the early universe that fills space. He reads it from the King James Bible of 1611, where God says \"Let there be light: and there was light,\" and that is \"the first day.\" The text gives the order of creation; whether its days line up with the stages a physicist would name, it leaves to the reader, and Rogers's matching would mean the ancient account kept a memory of how the cosmos began.","geomagnetic-anomalies-at-ufo-hotspots":"Places famous for UFO reports, such as Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch known for strange happenings, and Mount Sangun Mori in Japan, next to Fukushima Prefecture and its civilian nuclear grid, always show geomagnetic anomalies, meaning unusual local quirks in the Earth's magnetic field. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with the treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, and the point would be that something about the ground itself may draw these sightings.","george-ball-s-vietnam-memos-with-dismissive-dean-rusk-cover-1968":"Among the papers handed over when Nixon took office were letters from the diplomat George Ball begging the president to scale back the Vietnam War, with dismissive cover notes from Secretary of State Dean Rusk. Harald Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, tells Michels he saw these papers himself in 1968 or 1969. He says Ball was 'pleading' with the president to 'downsize this whole Vietnam thing before it blows up in your face,' which would mean the warnings were on paper and brushed aside at the top.","george-ellery-hale-s-inspiring-elf-or-spirit":"The astronomer George Ellery Hale, who worked in Pasadena, claimed he got creative inspiration from a being that he described as an elf or a spirit. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as part of a broader story about strange and spiritual currents running through American science and space research.","george-h-w-bush-as-cia-director-told-of-a-uap-event-and-refu-1960":"When George H.W. Bush sat down for his first briefing as head of the CIA in 1976, the briefer started straight in on a major UAP event (UAP being the government's current term for UFOs) from the early 1960s, which in one telling is a landing at Holloman Air Force Base in New Mexico in April 1964, and Bush answered, \"What are you talking about? I've never heard of this before.\" The physicist Eric W. Davis says Bush told him this himself, and in one version Bush turned down the film of the event. Michels later retells it secondhand from Davis, saying Bush learned of it by accident, a story that, if true, would mean even the CIA director was kept outside the secret.","george-h-w-bush-briefed-into-the-uap-reality":"George H. W. Bush is said to have been told the full secret about UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, while he was at the CIA. Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official, says it on the show in one line: Bush Senior \"was briefed into the UAP reality.\" A researcher named Jason Jorjani makes the same claim without naming a source, and puts it more strongly. He says Bush was one of the American presidents who \"had full access to the UFO file\" because he had been briefed when he was \"deep in the deep state.\" Jorjani links this to Bush's part in finishing off the Soviet Union and also brings up his father, Prescott Bush. If true, it would mean at least one president already knew the answer when he reached the White House.","george-h-w-bush-george-w-bush-and-cheney-s-role-in-the-legac-1976":"George H.W. Bush, while he was director of the CIA, was allegedly told about a so-called \"legacy program\", a secret effort said to hold recovered craft of non-human origin, and later passed what he knew on to his son George W. Bush, with Dick Cheney also named as part of the story. A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell tells Michels this, repeating what he says comes from David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned whistleblower. If true, knowledge of the programme would have run through the top of the government from 1976 to 2009.","george-h-w-bush-says-public-not-ready-for-ufo-truth-2015":"At one of his son Jeb Bush's campaign events, around 2015 or 2016, former president George H. W. Bush is said to have started talking about how the American public is not ready for the truth about UFOs. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. He stresses that the remark was clearly off script for Bush, and he takes it as a hint that a former president knew more than he usually said.","george-hunt-williamson-as-adamski-s-handler-and-silver-shirt-1930":"George Hunt Williamson, a man who worked beside George Adamski, one of the 1950s 'contactees' who said they met beings from space, was really using a false name, and he is said to have acted as Adamski's handler and to have earlier been an editor for the Silver Shirts, the 1930s American fascist movement led by William Dudley Pelley. The philosopher Jason Jorjani lays this out for Michels as his own research, pointing to a publication called Valor and a book called Closer Encounters. If it were true, it would tie the early flying saucer contact movement to a political group and to people steering it from behind.","george-knapp-brings-back-russian-ufo-files-1990":"In the 1990s, after the Soviet Union fell, the journalist George Knapp is said to have gone to Russia and come back with files on Soviet UFO activity. Michels mentions it himself, briefly, saying Knapp \"brought back all these files about like their activities when it comes to UFOs.\" The physicist Eric Davis tells it in more detail, without naming a source. He says the KGB opened its files when the Soviet Union collapsed, that Knapp and others went there, and that they found what he calls the Thread Three documents. According to Davis, the files show Russia had its own crash retrievals, meaning recoveries of crashed craft. If true, it would mean the US was not the only nation that has recovered such craft.","george-knapp-footage-off-baja-and-the-tampico-hotspot":"A journalist named George Knapp has interesting footage of strange objects around Baja, Mexico, Michels says, and on Mexico's other coast the port city of Tampico seems to be a hotspot. Michels mentions it in passing, without describing the footage, in an episode about the days he spent with Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft. The point is that the seas around Mexico may be a place where such objects show up again and again.","george-knapp-says-harry-reid-read-hastings-s-book-before-see-2023":"The late Senator Harry Reid read Robert Hastings's book UFOs and Nukes, which tells of UFOs watching and even tampering with American nuclear missiles, and it was one of his reasons for getting secret money in 2007 for a covert Pentagon UFO study, known as AAWSAP. The journalist George Knapp told Hastings this, according to Hastings, the book's author, who repeats it to Michels. Hastings also wrote it up in an article dated January 11, 2024: during a November 17, 2023 interview for a Netflix documentary series, Knapp said Reid \"had once told him\" the book's revelations were \"one of the reasons\" the then-powerful Senate Majority Leader \"had secretly authorized government funding.\" If true, a book about nuclear weapons sites helped start the Pentagon's modern UFO work.","george-knapp-urges-lazar-to-go-public-1989":"In 1989 in Las Vegas, the local television reporter George Knapp told Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret government site in Nevada, that he had to go public, \"because that's the only way.\" Lazar tells Michels this himself, as the advice that led him to tell his story openly. The point, as he tells it, was that getting the information out publicly was his best protection.","george-smith-deciphers-the-tablets-starting-with-gilgamesh-1870":"The story is that a British Museum scholar named George Smith spent about ten years on a mass of clay tablets from ancient Nineveh and found among them the Gilgamesh epic and its flood story. Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient history, tells it in brief: Smith \"gets a hold of these, he brings it back to the British Museum,\" and spends \"10 years studying them.\" The fuller account comes from the introduction to The Babylonian Story of the Deluge and the Epic of Gilgamish, published by the British Museum in 1920 with translations by the scholar E. A. Wallis Budge. Budge writes that the tablets \"discovered by Layard and Rassam at Nineveh came to the British Museum in 1854-5,\" that the Trustees \"employed George Smith\" in 1866 to sort and rejoin the fragments, and that he found \"the adventures of Gilgamish, an ancient king of Erech\" and \"an account of the Deluge,\" which he published in December 1872. He adds that in digs at Kuyûnjik, a mound at Nineveh, in 1873 and 1874 Smith \"recovered many fragments\" that let him complete the twelve tablets, and that he \"died of hunger and sickness near Aleppo in 1876.\" Read this way, the first tablets reached the museum through other men, so LaCroix's picture of Smith bringing them back fits the later fragments rather than the collection Smith first read. His years on the tablets, 1866 to 1876, come close to LaCroix's ten. The story matters because it is how the world first learned of a flood story older than the Bible's.","george-trimble-s-1956-claim-on-cancelling-gravity":"In 1956, George Trimble of the Martin Corporation, an American aircraft maker, is quoted as saying, \"We're already working on nuclear fuels and equipment to cancel out gravity.\" Michels reads the quote from what he describes as a 1956 article in Jane's Defense Weekly, offering it as a sign that serious work on controlling gravity was under way decades ago.","george-w-bush-considered-ufo-disclosure-with-stephen-hadley-2000":"During his presidency in the 2000s, George W. Bush thought about making UFO secrets public, a step the field calls disclosure, and discussed it with his national security adviser Stephen Hadley. Michels says this is now public knowledge through the physicist Hal Puthoff, so he is repeating what Puthoff has said rather than anything he saw, and if true it would mean a sitting president believed there was something to disclose.","george-w-bush-s-disclosure-net-assessment":"President George W. Bush's administration quietly studied whether to tell the public what the government knows about UFOs, a step people in this field call \"disclosure\", according to the physicist Hal Puthoff. Michels brings this up again and again on the show, always as something Puthoff has said on the record. In Michels's telling, Bush and Dick Cheney were \"very interested in disclosure\". With Stephen Hadley, Bush's national security adviser, they ran a \"net assessment\", a formal strategic review of what disclosure would look like. In one episode Michels adds that the panel decided disclosure was not worth it. In another he says the people taking part read The Three-Body Problem, a Chinese science fiction novel about humanity's contact with an alien civilisation, as part of the exercise, and he mentions Joe Rogan in that telling. He also once links the account to David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who has testified to Congress about hidden UFO programmes. A guest, the physicist Maaneli Derakhshani, also passes on Puthoff's account, and in his version the Bush administration asked Puthoff himself to take part in the review. If it happened, a sitting president seriously weighed revealing a secret like this and chose not to.","georges-st-pierre-s-ce5-sessions-bring-orbs":"The UFC fighter Georges St-Pierre is said to practise CE5, a method in which people try to call UFOs to them through meditation and focused intention, and to see glowing orbs in the sky that \"show up a lot\" when he does it. Michels says St-Pierre told him this on a recent phone call. If true, it would suggest the phenomenon responds when people try to contact it.","georgetown-pub-source-it-s-all-about-consciousness-2018":"In a pub in Georgetown, Washington, D.C., around 2018, one of the journalist Ross Coulthart's sources told him that the whole UFO subject is \"all about consciousness.\" Coulthart tells Michels this was one of the first things he heard from his insiders, and he does not name the source. If true, the answer to the mystery would lie in the mind, not just in craft and hardware.","georgia-power-only-his-house-lost-a-leg-of-power-2017":"In 2017, a man's home in Georgia lost one of its power lines, and a supervisor from the utility Georgia Power told him that only his house was hit and that \"you lit up our board downtown like the Fourth of July.\" Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear weapons site who says he was abducted by a UFO, tells Michels this, repeating the supervisor's words and pointing to photos of the transformer. To Woods, it suggests something unusual was aimed at his home alone.","gerchsheimer-tricks-schauberger-into-signing-over-ip-1957":"In 1957, a man named Carl Gerchsheimer is said to have tricked Viktor Schauberger, an Austrian inventor, into signing all of his intellectual property, his inventions and ideas, over to the United States, where the work was then labelled as atomic energy research. Michels tells it himself, drawing on the journalist Nick Cook, and sets it between Austria and Texas. If true, it would mean an inventor's unusual work quietly passed into secret American hands under a misleading label.","german-group-reproducing-the-effect-2020":"A group in Germany is reproducing the propulsion effect that Charles Buhler, a NASA scientist who says his team has built antigravity-style propulsion, describes. Buhler tells Michels he is working with them himself: \"There's another group I'm working with in Germany who's reproducing this.\" If it holds, the effect would not depend on one lab alone.","german-nuclear-tests-seen-over-the-baltic-1944":"Germany may have tested two nuclear weapons over the Baltic Sea between late 1944 and early 1945. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels there are \"some eyewitness reports\" of these tests, without naming them. If true, it would mean Nazi Germany had working atomic bombs before the United States did.","german-perforated-metal-found-at-roswell-1940":"Germany in the 1940s is said to have developed a kind of perforated metal, and the same metal was later found at Roswell, New Mexico, the site of the 1947 crash that many say was a UFO. Jason Jorjani, a writer who says he has traced a secret Nazi UFO programme, tells Michels this and points to Philip J. Corso, a former Army officer. If true, it would link the Roswell wreckage to Nazi technology.","german-saucer-airframe-with-boundary-layer-suction-vents-1930":"In Germany in the 1930s and 40s, engineers are said to have designed a saucer-shaped aircraft with suction vents all around its edge, meant to draw off the thin layer of air clinging to its surface. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of his case that Nazi Germany ran a secret flying-saucer programme.","german-silver-sighting-balloons-explain-wwii-spheres-1944":"The shiny spheres that pilots reported during the Second World War can be explained by German balloons, because in 1944 Germany was using metallic silver balloons as sighting balloons. Mick West, a sceptic who analyses UFO videos and claims, offers this to Michels in a debate. He names no source for it.","german-submarine-carrying-uranium-to-japan-1945":"In 1945 a German submarine bound for Japan was stopped carrying uranium, engineers and parts in pieces, and J. Robert Oppenheimer is supposed to have visited it at a crucial moment because he could not figure out how to make the American bomb work. The researcher Peter Levenda tells Michels this as a famous story, without naming a source. If true, it would mean the American bomb owed something to Nazi material.","german-windhund-bomber-tracking-technology":"Wartime Germany is said to have had a technology for tracking enemy bombers, one version of which was called Windhund, German for \"greyhound\". Nick Cook, an aviation journalist, mentions it to Michels from memory (\"I seem to remember\") and names no source. It matters because it is part of his larger account of how advanced Germany's secret wartime technology was.","gertrude-schmeidler-sheep-goat-studies-1950":"A parapsychologist named Gertrude Schmeidler, active in the United States in the 1950s, 60s and 70s, is said to have run the \"sheep-goat\" studies, which compared how believers and doubters scored on tests of psychic ability. The researcher Herb Metts brings her up without naming a source, in an episode about why the CIA ran a psychic spy programme.","getting-angry-fries-50-million-of-computers-at-the-augsburg-1980":"Lyn Buchanan, an Army sergeant, says that in the 1980s, at the Army's field station in Augsburg, Germany, he got angry and the computers went down, about $50 million worth of them; the story also reaches to Soviet computers in East Germany. He tells Michels it happened to him: \"When I did, the entire field station went down.\" If true, it would mean a person's mind or emotions could knock out machines.","getting-bob-lazar-onto-joe-rogan-s-show-by-speakerphone-call-2019":"In 2019, a man named Jeremy Corbell says, he helped get Bob Lazar, who claims he once worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site, onto the show of the podcaster Joe Rogan through a speakerphone call. Corbell tells Michels he called Lazar and said that he and George Knapp, who was travelling with him, were coming out for a screening in Michigan, and the plan to bring Lazar on Rogan's show grew from that trip. He tells it as part of his own role in bringing Lazar's story to a wide audience.","ghislaine-maxwell-s-swedish-male-aides-and-collected-hair":"Ghislaine Maxwell is said to have kept young, almost all blond and blue-eyed men as live-in aides at her Upper East Side townhouse in Manhattan, rotating them every three months on tourist visas, mostly from Sweden, and one of them says his hair was being collected. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, saying he is relying on an article by a former Swedish aide. In his July 2025 piece Ocean Outlaw: A Portrait of Ghislaine Maxwell, he names the aide as Rasmus Alpsjö, who \"has reported\" that his hair was being gathered from his pillows and his bathroom, and compares the choice of aides to Epstein's preference for women \"with a Nordic phenotype\", though the piece neither names nor links the article.","ghislaine-maxwell-s-terramar-project-as-an-elite-offshore-tr-2010":"The TerraMar Project, an ocean conservation group Ghislaine Maxwell set up in the 2010s, was really \"an elite network of trading routes and shipping containers\" moving around places near Antarctica, off the grid, run by very powerful people, according to a guest named Jay Anderson. Anderson ties it to Richard Branson's island and offers it as part of a hidden world of the rich, which, if true, would make a charity a cover for secret shipping.","ghost-murmur-quantum-sensor-detects-a-soldier-s-heartbeat-2020":"A quantum sensor called Ghost Murmur, meaning a detector that uses quantum physics to pick up extremely faint signals, is said to have found a soldier who needed rescuing somewhere in the Middle East, perhaps Iran or Afghanistan, by detecting his heartbeat. Michels tells this himself, hedging with \"I think\", and connects it to Lockheed Martin. He offers it as a sign of how far secret sensing technology has advanced.","giant-flown-to-a-base-in-qatar-2005":"A guest named Tim Alberino says that in 2005 a twelve-foot giant captured by US forces in Afghanistan was moved to a US base in Qatar. He tells Michels this as something he was told by someone else, and gives no more details about the transfer.","giant-found-dead-at-a-table-with-oysters-in-a-sealed-tower-1978":"Sometime around 1978 to 1981, at Santa Anastasia in Sardinia, a giant is said to have been found dead, seated at a table with oysters, inside a sealed tower. A guest named Tim Alberino, who also claims a 12-foot giant was captured in Afghanistan, tells Michels this as something he heard from someone else. If true, it would mean very large humans lived, or were kept, in Sardinia.","giant-kills-a-recon-team-in-a-cave-and-is-shot-down-2005":"In 2005, a giant humanoid in a cave near Kandahar, Afghanistan, is said to have killed an American recon team before soldiers shot it down. A guest named Tim Alberino repeats what he was told: when the soldiers arrived, \"this gigantic humanoid was eating on one of them.\" Michels retells it more loosely, without a date or place. He says the man behind the story, or the unit he served with, \"claims to have fought this giant in the caves of Afghanistan.\"","giant-progenitor-comet-behind-the-taurid-meteor-stream":"Some 25,000 to 30,000 years ago a gigantic comet, with a core perhaps 50 to 60 miles across or larger, was captured into an orbit inside Jupiter's, and the Taurid meteor stream, a yearly meteor shower, comes from it. A researcher of ancient catastrophes named Randall Carlson tells Michels this, drawing on the astronomers Victor Clube and William Napier and on William Asher. If true, Earth has long been passing through the debris of one huge comet.","giant-raccoons-and-the-toe-implant":"One night when he was home alone in Fillmore, California, the scientist Steve Colbern says he saw \"giant raccoons\" in his backyard, a strange experience he connects to an implant in his toe. Colbern, who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and he ties it to the work of Roger Leir, a surgeon who removed such objects. If true, it would mean he was himself one of the people whose bodies he studies.","giant-skeletons-dug-up-in-excavations-of-monumental-earthwor":"Old accounts of digs at America's great earthen mounds tell again and again of giant human skeletons, \"sometimes seven feet sometimes eight sometimes even nine feet in height,\" according to the researcher Randall Carlson. He summarises this for Michels from records he points to, such as 19th-century archives at Emory's library, Bureau of American Ethnology records and early Smithsonian papers, without quoting any one of them. If true, a race of very tall people would once have lived in North America.","giant-skull-carried-by-an-excavator-and-his-wife-1978":"Sometime between 1978 and 1981, at Santa Anastasia in Sardinia, a digger operator is said to have found a skull so large that he and his wife had to carry it together, and they brought it into the church. Tim Alberino, a guest on an episode about reported giants, tells Michels this as it was told to him. If true, it would be physical evidence of giant humans on the island.","giants-driven-out-by-joshua-settle-on-sardinia":"The filmmaker Tim Alberino believes that the giants of the Bible who survived Joshua's conquest of Canaan were driven out and settled on the Mediterranean island of Sardinia: \"Joshua drove out a lot of the giants that weren't killed in the conquest,\" he tells Michels. He builds this on his own reading of the King James Bible, published in 1611, offering it as a way to explain giant traces far from the Holy Land.","gibbons-calls-in-pandolfi-to-brief-on-the-rockefeller-initia-1990":"In the 1990s, when the Rockefeller Initiative, a push linked to the Rockefeller name for the Clinton White House to look into UFOs and the 1947 Roswell crash, reached the White House, John Gibbons called in a man named Ron Pandolfi to give the briefing on it. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this and insists \"this has been established,\" without naming how. The point for him is that the White House took the UFO question seriously enough to call in a briefer.","gifted-and-talented-programs-as-a-funnel-for-psychic-recruit":"Michels suggests that American gifted-and-talented school programmes have served as a funnel, the first stage for spotting children who are later steered into deeper, secret programmes that train psychic abilities. On one episode he offers this as his own idea, and on others he credits it to Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he helped retrieve a UFO and has spoken about these programmes publicly, and to people named Jordan Jose and Julia Mosbury. If true, it would mean ordinary schools quietly fed recruits into psychic research.","gifted-education-advisor-assigns-esp-crop-circles-and-abduct":"Deep Prasad, a researcher, says that when he was at school a gifted-education advisor kept giving him strange topics: first ESP, the supposed ability to sense things without the normal senses, then crop circles, the large patterns found pressed into fields of crops, and finally stories of people abducted by aliens. He tells Michels it from his own childhood: \"I was consistently being assigned ESP. Eventually evolved into crop circles. She's like looking to abduction stories.\"","gifted-kids-program-with-esp-training-from-miss-kaur":"As a boy in a program for gifted children near Vancouver, British Columbia, a researcher named Deep Prasad says a teacher he calls Miss Kaur got him to look into ESP, the idea that the mind can sense things without the usual senses. Prasad tells Michels this as the start of his own interest in psychic phenomena.","gillibrand-and-rubio-s-push-for-accountability-over-rogue-pr-2019":"For several years, from about 2019, Senators Kirsten Gillibrand and Marco Rubio have really been after accountability, not disclosure, according to the researcher Matthew Pines. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that what drives them is finding out that 'rogue programs' they never knew existed were 'controlling groundbreaking world shaking information'. If that is right, the push in the Senate is about who controls secret programmes, more than about revealing what they hold.","gimbal-j-hook-path-matches-aircrew-radar-account-2015":"The object in the Gimbal video, a 2015 US Navy infrared recording made by a fighter jet off Florida, is said to make a vertical U-turn, a J-hook path that matches what the aircrew say the object tracked on their radar did. A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff makes this case to Michels in a debate with the skeptic Mick West, describing it as a \"spectacular match\" with the eyewitness accounts of an object reversing direction \"with no radius of turn\". If true, it would mean the video and the radar point to the same impossible manoeuvre.","gimbal-object-flying-with-a-v-shaped-fleet-2015":"The object in the Gimbal video, a 2015 Navy infrared clip from off the US East Coast showing a craft that seems to tilt and turn, may have been flying with a whole fleet of other objects in a V shape. A commentator named Marik von Rennenkampff raises this on a show with a skeptic, drawing on the account of Ryan Graves, a Navy fighter pilot from that time, and calls it \"the fleet.\" If true, the object was not a lone glitch but part of a group moving together.","gimbal-object-is-a-distant-jet-2015":"The object in the Gimbal video, a 2015 US Navy infrared recording from a fighter jet off Florida that seems to show a craft hovering and rotating, is \"a distant jet,\" according to Mick West, a well-known skeptic who analyses UFO footage. West lays out this hypothesis in a debate on the show, where the video is tied to the Navy pilot Ryan Graves; if he is right, one of the most famous military UFO videos shows an ordinary airplane.","gimbal-object-wobbles-after-video-cuts-out-2015":"The object in the Gimbal video, a 2015 Navy clip of an unknown object filmed off Florida, wobbles after the footage ends, according to the aircrew who saw it. Marik von Rennenkampff, who takes the side of the believers in a debate with the skeptic Mick West on the show, says this, citing Ryan Graves, a Navy pilot who has spoken publicly about such sightings. The point is that the crew saw the object move on its own, which would be hard to explain as a trick of the camera.","gimbal-pilot-confirms-the-lns-radar-target-2015":"In the Gimbal video, a 2015 US Navy infrared recording from a fighter jet off the US East Coast, the pilot is said to confirm the radar target, called LNS, which a guest named Marik von Rennenkampff explains means \"launch and steering\", the target the radar has selected. Von Rennenkampff tells Michels this during a debate with the skeptic Mick West. It matters because it would tie the object on camera to a real radar contact.","gimbal-radar-returns-as-interference-or-echoes-of-a-distant-2015":"The radar readings tied to the Gimbal video, a 2015 Navy infrared clip from off the US East Coast of an object that seems to rotate, could have come from interference or from echoes of a distant jet, not a strange craft. Mick West, a well-known UFO skeptic, says on the show that someone told him this as a possibility: \"Perhaps there was a distant jet and the radar got messed up.\" If so, the radar would not back up the pilots' reading of the object.","gimbal-recorded-by-ryan-graves-squadron-vfa-11-2015":"The Gimbal video, a 2015 US Navy infrared recording off Florida of an object that seems to hover and rotate, was captured by the squadron of the Navy pilot Ryan Graves, says the writer Marik von Rennenkampff. He names it, a little unsure, as \"VFA1, the Red Rippers,\" meaning the Navy fighter squadron VFA-11, and raises it during a debate on the show with the skeptic Mick West to stress that trained pilots, not amateurs, were behind the footage.","gimbal-rotation-is-a-camera-artifact-2015":"The way the object in the Gimbal video seems to turn may be a trick of the camera, not a real movement, according to Mick West, a skeptic who analyses UFO videos, speaking in a debate on the show. The video is a Navy recording from 2015 of an object off Florida. West says his analysis points to an optical glare that only appears to rotate because of the camera's de-rotation mechanism, the part that keeps the image steady, and at one point he says the object's shape is a camera effect too.","gimbal-ufo-video-argument-between-marik-von-rennenkampff-and":"The Gimbal video, a 2015 US Navy clip of an unidentified object filmed from a fighter jet, is the subject of a running argument between a man named Marik von Rennenkampff and another man named Mick West. The journalist Michael Shellenberger mentions it to Michels, saying von Rennenkampff has been having this argument about the video with West. He brings it up in passing, as an example of how hard people still fight over what the footage shows.","gimbal-video-audio-a-whole-fleet-of-them-2015":"In the Gimbal video, Navy footage from 2015 of a strange object filmed off the US East Coast, the crew can be heard saying the object was not alone: \"there's a whole fleet of them. Look on ASA,\" an instruction to check another of their displays. The words come from the unnamed Navy aircrew recorded on the tape, which is played on the show in an episode with the Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves. If true, it means several objects were flying together, not just one.","girls-creature-and-hospital-being-were-the-same-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, a strange creature that some girls are said to have seen and a being later reported at a hospital were, according to James Fox, one and the same: \"the capture occurred. It had to be the same being.\" Fox, who investigated the case and drew on a witness named Italo Venturelli, tells Michels this as his own conclusion, which if true would tie the separate reports into a single captured being.","girls-on-horseback-turned-back-on-her-birthday-1948":"On 25 March 1948, some girls riding horses on the road to Hart Canyon, near Aztec, New Mexico, are said to have been turned back, around the time a flying saucer is claimed to have crashed there. A researcher named Scott Ramsey, who interviewed witnesses to the Aztec case, tells Michels that one of the women recalled it and that when he asked how she knew the date, she said, \"Cuz it was my birthday.\" If true, her memory would fix the date of the alleged crash and the cordon around it.","giza-mapped-to-orion-s-belt-around-10-600-bc":"The idea is that the three great pyramids at Giza match the three stars of Orion's Belt, and that the match was exact around 10,600 BC. Michels says this himself: the layout \"mapped fully\" to the Belt, \"around 10,600 BC is when it shifted.\" The writer Graham Hancock gives a different reason the match matters. He tells Michels that Orion's Belt \"has always, across many cultures, been the place of ascension of souls,\" and he gives no date. If true, the pyramids' layout would record a date far older than standard history allows.","giza-pink-granite-hauled-500-miles-from-aswan":"All the pink granite found across the Giza Plateau, the site of Egypt's great pyramids, was brought from a quarry at Aswan, some 500 miles to the south. Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient sites, tells Michels this without naming a source, as a sign of how much effort and skill went into the building.","giza-pyramid-stones-may-have-been-mixed-and-poured":"The stones of the Giza pyramids in Egypt may not have been cut and hauled at all, but mixed and poured into place like concrete. Joe McMoneagle, a researcher, says this to Michels, crediting a book called The Ultimate Time Machine. If true, the builders had a casting technique that changes how the pyramids were made.","giza-wide-model-presented-at-the-malta-conference-2024":"At the end of a conference in Malta in 2024, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum, a model was presented that showed the whole Giza plateau with underground vertical features linked to cube-shaped chambers beneath it. Drum describes it to Michels without naming his source, though he ties the work to a researcher named Filippo Biondi. If the model is right, the pyramids and the Sphinx would sit on top of one large connected structure underground.","glasnost-era-russian-crashed-craft-us-astronaut-sent-to-help-1980":"In the early days of glasnost, the late-Soviet opening when Russia and America were briefly on friendly terms, Russia told America it had a crashed craft of unknown origin, and a US astronaut was sent to help recover it. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this as \"one story that came up\" among the accounts people have given him. If true, the two Cold War rivals would have quietly shared a secret about crashed non-human craft.","glenn-gaffney-allegedly-blocked-ufo-material-transfer-from-l":"A man named Glenn Gaffney is said to have blocked a transfer of UFO material from the defense company Lockheed Martin to Bigelow Aerospace, a private space company. Michels relays this as what a couple of people have said, one of them under oath in testimony before Congress, and it matters because it would mean recovered UFO material exists and someone decides who gets it.","glenn-gaffney-blocked-transfer-of-nhi-material-to-bigelow-2008":"In 2008 an official named Glenn Gaffney is said to have blocked the handover of technology of 'non-human origin' to Bigelow Aerospace, a private space company. Michels says that 'all this congressional testimony' names Gaffney as the person who stopped the move, and he credits a report listed as the 'Historical Report, volume one'. If true, it would mean the government holds such technology and that officials fight over who controls it.","glenn-gaffney-head-of-the-crash-retrieval-program-refused-to-2008":"Between about 2008 and 2012, a man named Glenn Gaffney, at the CIA's science and technology branch, ran what is called the crash retrieval program, a secret effort said to recover crashed craft of non-human origin. That is the account of Eric W. Davis, a physicist who worked on Pentagon UFO studies, who tells Michels he wanted to interview the head of the program from that era, \"but he refused to talk to us.\" If true, it would mean the CIA had a named official in charge of recovering crashed craft.","glenn-gaffney-named-as-a-leader-of-the-legacy-program-2000":"Michels says a lot of people name Glenn Gaffney, who was director of science and technology at the CIA, as one of the last leaders of the \"legacy program\", the alleged long-running secret effort to recover and study crashed craft that are not human-made. He says it on the show without naming who those people are. If true, it would put a senior CIA official at the head of the programme.","glenn-gaffney-on-aaro-s-board-2020":"Glenn Gaffney, whom Michels describes as the CIA's director of science and technology, sat on the board of AARO, the Pentagon office set up in the 2020s to investigate UAP, the government's term for UFOs. Michels offers this himself, hedged with \"I think\" and without naming a source, in a conversation with a pseudonymous researcher known as UAPGerb about how the Pentagon's UFO work is run, and it would mean the CIA had a hand in steering that office.","glenn-gaffney-said-to-have-run-the-crash-retrieval-program":"A man named Glenn Gaffney, who was the CIA's director of science and technology, is rumoured to have run the whole \"legacy crash retrieval program\", a long-running secret effort said to recover crashed craft that are not of human make. Michels repeats this himself as a rumour, mentioning it alongside a former CIA officer named Jim Semivan, and does not say where the rumour comes from.","glenn-martin-company-employs-louis-witten-for-anti-gravity-r-1950":"In the 1950s the aircraft maker Glenn L. Martin Company ran a research unit, RIAS (Research Institute for Advanced Study), where the physicist Louis Witten, father of the physicist Edward Witten, worked on gravity under a contract with the Air Force's Wright Field lab in Ohio. Witten himself says on the show that the Wright Field lab existed \"to find out what we were doing and to help us do it,\" and that he got a contract \"to do gravity which I did very happily\"; the researcher Eric Weinstein, hedging with \"I believe,\" and Michels call it antigravity research and tie in Johns Hopkins, the RIAS vice-president George Trimble and other scientists. If so, the military was quietly funding the study of gravity control decades ago.","glimpsed-classified-uap-photos-via-colleagues":"Bob McGwier, introduced on the show as a CIA contractor, says that through colleagues in classified government settings he got glimpses of secret photos and other material on UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. He tells Michels he saw it himself: \"I began to see all sorts of stuff I told you about earlier photos and other things that said UAP are here.\" If true, the government holds images that point to such things being real.","global-crash-recovery-and-reverse-engineering-programs-outsi":"Other countries besides the United States run their own programmes to recover crashed non-human craft and reverse engineer them, meaning take them apart to learn how they work. Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he once helped carry a recovered UFO, tells Michels this as something he was told, and gives no names or countries.","global-ufo-nuclear-link-from-roswell-to-chernobyl-1947":"Michels draws a worldwide link between UFOs and nuclear sites, running from Roswell, New Mexico in 1947, which he calls the site of the largest nuclear stockpile of its day, to Fukushima in Japan and Chernobyl in Ukraine. Roswell is the town where, in 1947, something crashed that many people believe was a UFO. Michels frames the connection himself, without naming a source, and if he is right it would mean that whatever is behind the sightings takes a persistent interest in nuclear power and weapons.","glomar-explorer-possibly-dual-use-1974":"The Glomar Explorer, a huge deep-sea ship built in 1974 for Howard Hughes's company, may have had a second, hidden job, Michels suggests. In one episode he only wonders aloud about it. In another he says outright that there are rumours it was used to find and bring up strange objects from the seafloor, which would mean UFO recovery had been run under cover of a big private company.","glomar-explorer-recovers-a-sunken-soviet-submarine-1970":"In the 1970s, a ship called the Glomar Explorer pulled a sunken Soviet submarine up from the floor of the Pacific Ocean. The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels that 'this huge corporation' did it, naming Howard Hughes and a company called Global Marine Corporation, and calls it 'an incredible feat'. He offers it without naming a source, as an example of how big a secret recovery a private company can carry out for the government.","glowing-disc-photographed-over-property-2016":"In 2016 a glowing, disc-shaped object was photographed over Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah's Uinta Basin, according to the ranch's owner, who tells Michels about it himself. He says the first image 'looked almost like a Millennium Falcon', the spaceship from Star Wars, and showed a disc-like object that was glowing.","glowing-hole-in-bedroom-wall-and-electrical-failure-2017":"In 2017, at his home in Georgia, a former nuclear security guard named Mario Woods says a glowing hole \"about a 4 and 1 half or 5T\" across appeared in the north wall of his bedroom, and his electricity failed. Woods tells Michels this as something he lived through, in an episode about his account of being abducted, and he mentions someone named Jake in the story.","glowing-orb-ufos-at-pyramid-kuromanta-shown-in-old-paintings":"Glowing balls of light are said to appear at Pyramid Kuromanta, a mountain in Akita, Japan that some regard as a pyramid, and old Japanese paintings are said to show the same lights. A guest named Geoffrey Drumm tells Michels this as a known phenomenon, pointing to an old painting of glowing balls without naming it. If true, it would mean the lights have been seen there for centuries.","glowing-red-metal-found-in-a-yard-under-a-ufo-1990":"In the 1990s in Zanesville, Ohio, a man saw something hovering over his house, it vanished as he drove up, and he found a piece of red glowing metal in his yard. A guest named Chris Bledsoe tells Michels this as the man told it to him on the phone. If true, the craft left a physical fragment behind.","go-fast-email-deleted-under-the-spillage-protocol-2015":"An email carrying the Go Fast video, a 2015 Navy recording of a fast object skimming over the ocean, was reportedly deleted from Navy networks under the spillage protocol, the procedure for wiping classified material sent where it should not be. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels what Jay Stratton, an intelligence officer who led Pentagon UFO work, told him: when it was deleted, \"they were still not sure whether it was adversary tech or off-world.\"","go-fast-is-a-wind-blown-balloon-2015":"The object in the Go Fast video, a 2015 US Navy infrared recording off Jacksonville, Florida, that seems to show something racing low over the ocean, is \"probably just a wind-blown object like a balloon,\" according to Mick West, a well-known skeptic who analyses UFO footage. West puts this forward in a debate on the show; if he is right, the object only looks fast because of the jet's own motion and the camera angle.","go-fast-object-one-of-four-in-line-abreast-formation-2015":"The small object in the Go Fast video, a 2015 Navy cockpit recording off Jacksonville, Florida, of something racing low over the water, was not alone, according to the Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves: he tells Michels it was one of four objects flying side by side in what pilots call line abreast. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff repeats this from Graves, and the retired Navy admiral Tim Gallaudet adds that it was a round object with no wings, control surfaces or visible engine, moving as if under intelligent control, and he rejects the idea that it only seemed fast because of the camera's angle. If true, four such craft flying in formation would point to something piloted or guided.","go-fast-video-recorded-off-jacksonville-2015":"The Go Fast video is a short cockpit recording, made by Navy aircrew, of an unexplained object over the Atlantic. Michels says in his own voice that it was recorded in January 2015, about 300 miles off the coast of Jacksonville, Florida, by crews from the aircraft carrier USS Roosevelt. On the show the crew's own voices on the recording can be heard reacting as they track it: 'Oh my gosh, dude. Wow.' That audio comes from the unnamed airmen themselves. To the tellers it is part of the record that Navy pilots met things they could not identify.","gobekli-tepe-as-a-possible-origin-of-civilization":"Göbekli Tepe, an ancient stone site in Turkey, is said by some to be the place where civilisation began, earlier than Egypt. The podcaster Joe Rogan says on the show that \"a lot of people think\" Turkey, not Egypt, \"might have been the origin of civilization,\" and points to the site. Michels comes back to it again and again in his own words. In one episode he calls it an ancient burial site \"dating as far back as 9000 BC\" and built from \"50 ton blocks.\" In another he dates it to about 11,000 years ago, credits the writer Graham Hancock's book Fingerprints of the Gods and National Geographic, and says it \"rewrites the map\" of how old human civilisation is. Rogan only calls it a possible origin, but Michels goes further. In a third episode he says every 30 or 40 years a new find pushes the date back, and Göbekli Tepe may not even be the earliest site built from giant stones. If they are right, complex human society is much older than the usual story allows.","gobekli-tepe-as-an-astronomical-calendar-tracking-the-great":"The idea is that Göbekli Tepe, an ancient site of carved stone pillars in Turkey, was built as a sky calendar tracking the Great Year, the very long cycle over which the stars slowly shift their place in the sky. A researcher named Matt LaCroix puts it this way on the show, without naming a source: the pillars \"are just basically star constellations.\" If he is right, the people who raised the site were watching the heavens with great care.","gobekli-tepe-built-and-buried-by-survivors-of-a-cataclysm":"Göbekli Tepe, a site in Turkey that scholars had put down to simple hunter-gatherers, turned out to hold what a researcher named Jay Anderson calls the oldest megalithic temple site, a place of huge carved stones that 'pops up out of the ground'. Anderson says this on the show without naming a source. The story's larger claim is that survivors of an ancient catastrophe built the site and then buried it, which would mean a lost chapter of human history.","gobekli-tepe-chalices-and-burial-rituals":"Chalices have been dug up at Göbekli Tepe, an ancient stone site about 12,000 years old, from just before people took up farming, and they are tied to burial rituals there. Michels takes this from the writer Brian Muraresku's book The Immortality Key in an episode about mushrooms. The idea is that ritual drinks, possibly mind-altering ones, may go back to the very start of civilisation.","gobekli-tepe-datable-because-it-was-buried":"Göbekli Tepe, an ancient stone site in Turkey, could only be dated because it was buried, says a researcher named Matt LaCroix. He tells Michels this without naming a source, and the point is that being buried is what let scientists put an age on the site at all.","gobekli-tepe-pillar-43-as-a-sky-map-of-12-500-years-ago":"A carved stone known as Pillar 43, at Göbekli Tepe, an ancient stone site in Turkey, is 'very persuasively an astronomical diagram of the sky at the summer solstice 12 and 1/2 thousand years ago', according to the researcher Graham Hancock. The pillar stands in a part of the site called Enclosure D, and Hancock tells Michels this without naming a source. If he is right, people were mapping the sky in detail far earlier than is usually thought.","god-s-divine-council-in-genesis-behold-man-has-become-like-u":"A researcher named Luke Caverns says that in the Garden of Eden story in Genesis, God turns to a \"divine council\", a group of other beings in heaven, and says \"Behold, man has become like us\", a detail he thinks people usually overlook. In the King James Bible of 1611, which he credits, the verse reads \"Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil.\" The plural \"us\" is in the text, so the verse does have God speaking to or for more than himself; who that \"us\" is, a council of beings or something else, the verse does not say.","goethe-s-merging-with-strasbourg-cathedral":"The German poet Goethe is said to have felt himself merge with Strasbourg Cathedral while standing before it, a kind of union between the person seeing and the thing seen. Michels tells it himself, drawing on Rudolf Steiner and a work on Goethe's theory of knowledge. He offers it as an example of a way of knowing that goes beyond ordinary perception.","going-back-in-time-in-dreams-with-the-monks-harry-heller-say":"In dreams set on a mountain with monks, Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer, says he kept going back in time. He tells Michels that a figure he calls Harry Heller told him, in his book, that time is an illusion, and \"sure enough, he proved it\" through those dreams, with Einstein brought in to support the idea. If true, the past could be visited.","going-public-with-the-find-through-expressen-2011":"In 2011 the Swedish treasure hunter Dennis Asberg went public with a strange object his team says it found on the seafloor by taking it to a journalist at Expressen, a big Stockholm newspaper. Asberg tells Michels this himself. It is how word of the find first got out to the public.","gold-flown-to-geneva-and-gold-certificates-fund-anticommunis-1947":"Between 1947 and 1949, the story goes, a large store of gold was flown out on US military transport planes to the International Credit Bank in Geneva, Switzerland, deposited there, and used to back gold certificates that paid for secret anticommunist operations in Europe. A researcher named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, and ties it to Allen Dulles, who later ran the CIA. If true, it would mean early Cold War covert work was paid for with money kept off the books.","golden-calcite-alabaster-altar-of-unknown-origin":"An altar near Lake Van in eastern Turkey is made of golden calcite alabaster, a soft, glowing stone, and nobody knows where that stone came from. Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient sites, tells Michels this as they stand before it, without naming a source, as one more puzzle about who built the region's old sites.","golden-orb-escorted-by-helicopters-rising-behind-secret-moun":"Ross Coulthart, who says he located a UFO base in Arizona, says he watched a giant golden orb rise from behind Secret Mountain at 1:30 in the morning, escorted by helicopters: \"up comes this beautiful golden orb, gigantic. It pulses. It's plasmatic.\" He tells it as his own sighting, more than once on the show; in one version he calls the helicopters Black Hawks, dates it to 2025 and credits a woman named Melinda Leslie, and in another he leaves out the pulsing. If true, military helicopters were escorting something unknown out of a hidden site.","golf-ball-bounces-off-the-reactor-s-field-1987":"At S4, a secret site in Nevada where he says he worked in the late 1980s studying alien craft to learn how they work, Bob Lazar says a colleague showed him an invisible field around the craft's reactor by throwing a golf ball at it. Lazar tells Michels the ball, aimed at the reactor, bounced off the field and hit a ceiling tile, and he names Barry Castilio and Dennis Mariani in the account; a guest named John Blitch, relaying Lazar's story, instead describes a ball-sized item, about as big as a basketball or soccer ball, that pushed things away. If true, it would mean the craft carried a power source able to produce a force field.","golf-ball-lands-on-frederick-wolfe-s-grave-1976":"In 1976, on a golf course at Fort Knox in Kentucky that has a cemetery on it, a golf ball came down on a grave marked \"Frederick Wolfe. German soldier died.\" A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen tells Michels this as his own experience, reading the stone aloud on the show. He tells it in an episode where he describes himself as a time traveler, as one of the odd moments in his life.","gorard-and-wolfram-hypergraph-physics-project-recovers-gr-an-1990":"The scientist Stephen Wolfram and the researcher Jonathan Gorard built a model of the universe as a hypergraph, a vast network of points rewired by simple rules, and around 2020 and 2021 Gorard showed that both Einstein's general relativity and quantum mechanics fall out of it. A national security analyst named Matthew Pines tells Michels this, relaying Gorard's work and calling it \"really suggestive.\" If it holds, physics' two great theories could spring from one simple foundation.","gorbachev-says-various-civilizations-have-made-contact-1990":"Mikhail Gorbachev, the former leader of the Soviet Union, is said to have told visitors that various civilizations have made contact with representatives of Earth. A Russian guest named Kiran Ilum Xenov, who says he was taken by aliens in 1997, tells Michels that he and others heard Gorbachev say this when they talked with him, sometime in the 1990s. If true, it would mean a former Soviet leader privately accepted that contact had happened.","gordon-cooper-mercury-astronaut-among-pilots-who-saw-ufos":"Gordon Cooper, one of the first American astronauts and, in Michels's words, one who \"spent more time in space,\" is counted among the pilots who have seen UFOs. Michels names him himself, in passing and without saying what Cooper saw, in an episode with the fighter pilot Ryan Graves, to show that such reports come from highly trained, credible flyers.","gordon-cooper-s-1957-craft-landing-on-base":"In 1957 a craft landed on an air base, according to the astronaut Gordon Cooper. In a clip shown on Michels's show he says the saucer \"flew right over\" a crew, \"put down three little gear and landed out on the dry lake bed.\" A man named Charlie Foltz, who says Cooper told the story to him and others, repeats it in the same episode. If it is true, a trained test pilot who later became an astronaut saw an unknown craft touch down on a military base.","gordon-cooper-s-book-von-braun-worked-on-ufo-craft-in-nazi-g-1940":"The rocket engineer Wernher von Braun is said to have told the astronaut Gordon Cooper that he and others had worked on UFO craft in Nazi Germany in the 1940s. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas tells Michels this as something he read in Cooper's book Leap of Faith, and if true it would mean such craft were being studied before the war ended.","gordon-cooper-s-saucer-shaped-craft-flying-in-formation-1950":"In the 1950s the astronaut Gordon Cooper saw craft flying in formation that he first took for aircraft, but \"it turns out they didn't have wings. They were saucer shape.\" Cooper tells it himself, as an eyewitness, in a clip played on the show. Coming from a trained pilot, it describes disc-shaped craft flying in a group.","gordon-cooper-visits-puharich-s-ossining-lab-1970":"In the 1970s the NASA astronaut Gordon Cooper is said to have visited, many times, the laboratory the researcher Andrija Puharich ran in Ossining, New York, and to have watched the strange work going on there. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels that Cooper 'went to Ossining like many times.' According to Malatzi, Cooper wrote in his own book, Leap of Faith, about seeing experiments with a Faraday cage, a metal-screened room that blocks electrical signals. Cooper also wrote, Malatzi says, about seeing the children the lab called 'the space kids'. Another researcher in the same episode, who is not named, adds that a woman named Valerie Ransone brought Cooper there, and places the visits in the late 1970s. Both tellers are researchers passing on what they have read rather than witnesses. The story matters to them because it puts a famous astronaut inside a lab that, by their account, was trying to contact alien intelligences.","gordon-creighton-and-prince-philip-s-interest":"Steven Greer, a man who collects testimony from UFO witnesses, says a former British military intelligence man named Gordon Creighton published Flying Saucer Review, a British UFO magazine, and that its material used to go to the palace, where Prince Philip was \"very keen on\" all of it. Greer tells Michels he interviewed Creighton himself as one of his witnesses, and the story would mean the subject had interest at the top of the British royal family.","gordon-gray-named-in-the-mj12-documents-1947":"Gordon Gray was one of the members of Majestic 12, a secret research group Truman set up in 1947, Michels says, citing the leaked MJ-12 documents. Those papers came to light in 1984 on a roll of film mailed with no name to a researcher named Jaime Shandera, and were made public in 1987. The FBI's copies are stamped \"BOGUS,\" and the Air Force said they were \"fabricated,\" while the physicist Stanton Friedman judged them \"almost certainly genuine.\" The papers call the group \"a TOP SECRET Research and Development/ Intelligence operation responsible directly and only to the President\" and list \"Mr. Gordon Gray\" among its members; Michels' word \"advisers\" is his own, and Gray's part holds only as far as the papers are real.","gordon-macdonald-said-the-moon-only-makes-sense-if-it-is-hol":"A scientist named Gordon MacDonald, whom Michels calls Eisenhower's chief space adviser, is said to have written that the only way the Moon makes sense is if it is hollow, a hollow structure. Michels raises this himself on several episodes as something he has read in MacDonald's work, once placing MacDonald at Columbia and mentioning the psychic Ingo Swann beside him, and once leading in with the claim that the Moon rings like a bell when struck. If true, it would mean a senior government scientist took seriously the idea that the Moon is not a solid natural body.","gordon-novel-prosecuted-for-burning-down-555-camp-street-1960":"A man named Gordon Novel was arrested in New Orleans in the 1960s and prosecuted by Jim Garrison for burning down the office at 555 Camp Street, says a researcher named Dan Sheehan. Sheehan tells Michels this on two different episodes without naming a source. He ties the office to Lee Harvey Oswald, and in one of the two tellings also to a man named Guy Banister, which would put the fire in the middle of the Kennedy assassination story.","gordon-novel-shows-ufo-propulsion-drawings-at-the-internatio-1994":"Dan Sheehan says that in 1994, while he was representing a doctor named John Mack and giving a report at the International UFO Congress, a UFO conference, a man named Gordon Novel walked up out of the crowd with drawings of how UFOs are propelled. Sheehan tells Michels he was there, quoting Novel as saying \"I've got all these uh drawings and stuff about the UFOs and how their propulsion system works.\" If the drawings were what Novel claimed, someone outside government had plans for how such craft fly.","gordon-novel-threatens-sheehan-and-is-later-hired-by-john-al-1980":"A man named Gordon Novel once threatened Dan Sheehan, Sheehan says, and was later hired by John B. Alexander as one of the investigators for NIDS, a private research group that looked into unexplained phenomena. Sheehan tells Michels this from his own dealings in Washington between the 1980s and 1990s, and finds it striking that a man who had threatened him ended up working inside UFO research.","gorilla-masks-for-u-2-and-sr-71-test-pilots-1950":"Test pilots of the secret U-2 and SR-71 spy planes were reportedly given gorilla masks, and told to put them on if they ever flew close enough to a civilian airliner for its pilot to see their faces. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this from the journalist Annie Jacobsen's book Area 51. The point, as he tells it, is that the secret projects deliberately made sightings look absurd.","gotthard-tunnel-proof-of-concept-scan-2024":"In 2024 a scan of the Gotthard Tunnel in Switzerland served as 'the final proof of concept' for a method of seeing deep underground, according to the researcher Jeffrey Drum, who ties the work to a man named Filippo Biondi. Drum tells Michels this without naming a source, in an episode about what he calls a huge energy grid under the pyramids of Egypt. The point is that if the method worked on a known tunnel, it can be trusted on what it shows beneath the pyramids.","government-air-to-air-flir-expert-s-email-on-gimbal-rotation-2015":"In the Gimbal video, a 2015 Navy infrared recording made off the US East Coast of an object that seems to turn in the air, the turning is the object's own movement, according to a government expert in air-to-air infrared imaging. Marik von Rennenkampff, a national security writer, reads the expert's email aloud on the show in a debate with Mick West, a skeptic who explains UFO videos in everyday terms: 'The object in the video is definitely maneuvering, causing the appearance of rotation.' Von Rennenkampff also reminds West that they were both on a call with the expert, who called the video 'bizarre' and said 'I've never seen anything like this.' If the expert is right, it would mean the turning is not just a trick of the camera.","government-background-check-on-harrington-s-neighbours-1980":"Around 1980 in Walnut Creek, California, people from \"like NSA or like CIA or someone\" came to a family's house and asked a lot of questions about their neighbour, an official named John S. Herrington. Brent Friedman, who works in Hollywood, tells Michels this as something his own family went through, about a year before the events of his larger story. He tells it to show that Herrington drew government attention.","government-contracts-that-were-really-for-uap-data-presented-2021":"The government has quietly paid for UAP data, UAP being the official term for UFOs, under contracts dressed up as war games, according to James Valor of Skywatcher, a startup that tries to call up and record UFOs. He tells Michels his company \"already received contracts from the government to collect data\" on them in the early 2020s. If so, official interest is hidden under other labels.","government-crash-photos-that-faded-to-blank-1948":"After the reported 1948 crash of a craft at Aztec, New Mexico, there were said to be two sets of photos, and the ones the government took did not last and faded to blank. Scott Ramsey, a researcher of the Aztec case, tells Michels this, going back to the original story told by a writer named Frank Scully. If true, the official record of the crash would have been lost on purpose or by some strange property of the scene.","government-documents-describe-metallic-reflective-flat-botto-1947":"Official US documents from 1947 to 1952 describe UFOs as metallic, reflective and flat-bottomed, Michels says on his own account. He lists the Twining memo, titled AMC Opinion Concerning \"Flying Discs\" and written on 23 September 1947 by the Air Materiel Command of the US Army Air Forces, alongside a 1948 analysis by Project Sign, an early Air Force UFO study. He also lists a CIA analysis of the 1952 UFO flyover of Washington, DC, and the analysis of Captain Edward J. Ruppelt, who led Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s.","government-getting-ahead-of-service-members-ready-to-disclos-2017":"The idea is that the US government began speaking openly about UFOs from 2017 because it had to get ahead of its own people: so many service members had seen things that they were about to go public anyway. The economist Tyler Cowen puts it this way to Michels, passing on what he was told: \"so many service people were on the verge of saying, 'I can't not tell them what I saw.'\" If so, the official openness of those years was less a choice than damage control.","government-gives-a-craft-to-industry-contractors-to-find-out":"The government hands a recovered craft to a private aerospace company and says, in effect, \"Here's the craft. We want to know how it works,\" according to Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has worked as a Pentagon consultant, who says this is how two unnamed industry sources described it to him. Davis adds that the company gets a sole-source contract, one awarded without competition, with the work already laid out: take the craft apart piece by piece, reverse engineer it, put it back together and try to make it fly. If true, it would mean private industry, not only the government, holds and studies these craft.","government-group-working-on-quantum-vacuum-surveillance-sinc-1990":"A group inside the US government is said to have worked since the 1990s on surveillance technology that draws on the quantum vacuum, the restless energy physics says fills even empty space, and to have had \"massive success\" with it. A guest named Dave Rossi, who says aliens taught him advanced physics, tells Michels he knows this group. He says it is \"not like an ongoing issue prototype. It's been established,\" which, if true, would mean the government has had a working tool for decades that is beyond anything known in public science.","government-holds-close-range-cell-phone-video-of-triangles":"Dylan Borland says the US government has close-range cell phone video of the triangles, the large triangular craft people report seeing, and that it would settle the question for good: \"there is cell phone video ending the conversation about the triangles once and for for all.\" He tells Michels he knows this directly, and elsewhere in the same talk he calls the videos classified and \"Undeniable. End of discussion,\" without saying who holds them. If true, clear proof of the triangles is already sitting in government hands.","government-knowledge-that-scalar-waves-alter-radioisotope-de":"The US government has known for years that certain \"scalar electromagnetic waves\" can change how fast radioactive materials decay, according to Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects he believes are alien implants in human bodies. He says this on the show without pointing to a record, but he credits the idea to the books of a writer named Tom Bearden, who uses that name for a special kind of electromagnetic wave. If it were true, it would mean the government quietly holds a way to change one of the steadiest processes in physics.","government-people-pushed-an-alien-narrative-on-him-2007":"A man named Chris Bledsoe, who says he has had UFO encounters since 2007, says government people tried from the very start to push an alien story on him. He tells Michels that \"from the beginning\" they \"tried to make me believe in flying saucers and little green men\". If that is true, officials were steering how a witness understood his own experience, not just recording it.","government-psychic-programs-prompted-by-ufo-knowledge-1950":"The claim is that the US government's 1950s programmes studying psychic abilities were started partly because of what officials were learning about UFOs. A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell offers this as his own suspicion, without naming a source: \"one of the main reasons I suspect that we were studying that stuff and had those programs was because of what we were learning about UFOs and extraterrestrials.\" If so, the government's interest in the mind and its interest in UFOs would have been linked from the start.","government-published-propaganda-books-as-a-cover-up-campaign-1981":"According to this account, the US government has paid for books full of propaganda about UFOs and placed them on bookshop shelves right next to books that tell the truth, as part of what was called \"a multi-billion dollar campaign to cover up the truth.\" A guest named Brent Friedman says this was told to him by John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy, and repeats it to Michels. If true, part of what the public reads on the subject was written to mislead it on purpose.","government-recruited-psi-capable-children-for-decades-for-pa-2025":"For decades, Michels says, the US government searched the country for children with psi ability, meaning claimed powers of the mind such as telepathy or moving objects by thought, and ran parapsychology experiments on them. He tells it himself on three episodes, and the details shift between them. In one he says the children were taken to national laboratories and names the Atomic Energy Commission and the Battelle Memorial Institute, a private research organisation. In another he links the recruitment to programmes for gifted children and to Stargate and Grill Flame, US government projects on psychic spying, and credits the physicists Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ.","government-underfunding-meant-no-helicopter-for-uap-events-2020":"Between 2020 and 2025, Skywatcher, a company that says it can call up UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), held government-funded events in the western United States to observe them, but the money was too little to pay for a helicopter. The Skywatcher team, including a member named Jake Barber, tells Michels this firsthand, saying \"part of our disappointment in the government consisted of insufficient financing.\" If true, it would mean the government paid for these sightings efforts, but not enough to do them properly.","government-wanted-as-many-crashes-as-possible-to-harvest-tec":"The claim is that the US government wanted as many crashes of unknown craft as possible, so it could collect as much of their technology as it could. A scientist named John Brandenburg, who argues that Mars once held life wiped out by nuclear war, says this to Michels and puts it as accounts he has heard, without saying from whom. If true, it would mean crashes were welcomed as a source of hardware rather than treated as accidents.","government-whistleblowers-claim-ufo-retrievals":"Whistleblowers from inside the government have come forward to say the United States has recovered UFOs, Michels says. He has had one of them on his show, he adds, and finds it hard to poke holes in that man's story. He says this in an episode about Plato's cave and consciousness, as a reason to take the question seriously.","governments-hide-the-bodies-for-cold-war-reverse-engineering":"The governments most eager to hide strange bodies and objects are the ones doing the most reverse engineering, meaning taking apart unknown technology to learn how to copy it, and much of that work began in the Cold War. David Ruiz, a researcher who has studied the three-fingered bodies said to come from Peru, says this to Michels without naming a source, as his explanation for why such finds are kept from the public.","governor-richardson-on-looking-into-the-roswell-crash-1947":"Bill Richardson, a former governor of New Mexico, is said to have counted looking into the 1947 Roswell crash among the few things he ever did in his political career. Roswell is the incident near that New Mexico town where some say a craft came down. A UFO documentary filmmaker named James Fox tells Michels that Richardson said this to him directly: \"one of the few things that I ever did in my political career was looking into the UFO phenomenon, particularly the Roswell crash.\" If true, it would mean a sitting politician took the crash seriously enough to investigate it himself.","gps-lost-over-the-object-on-the-stockholm-university-ship-2025":"In 2025, as a Stockholm University ship passed over a strange object lying on the floor of the Baltic Sea, its GPS began to fail. That is the account of Dennis Asberg, a Swedish treasure hunter who was on board. He tells Michels that as they went over the object, a man he calls Tomas turned to him and said, \"Dennis, we have problems with the GPS function.\" To him, it hints that the object does something to instruments that pass over it.","graham-hancock-on-joe-rogan-the-sarcophagus-as-a-place-for-r":"The stone box inside the Great Pyramid at Giza, usually called a sarcophagus or coffin, may not have held a body at all but may have been a place where a living person lay down to reach a higher state of mind. The researcher Luke Caverns tells Michels that the author Graham Hancock, who writes about lost ancient civilizations, has now come to suggest this on Joe Rogan's podcast, The Joe Rogan Experience. If it were so, the pyramid would be a place for ritual or altered awareness rather than a tomb.","graham-hancock-s-first-article-on-the-ark-of-the-covenant":"The writer Graham Hancock's very first piece of work was an article about the Ark of the Covenant, written for what Michels thinks was a magazine. Michels repeats this from his own interview with Hancock and is unsure of the details, so the story rests on his memory of what Hancock told him.","graham-hancock-s-lost-ice-age-civilization":"The writer Graham Hancock argues that ancient stone sites such as Göbekli Tepe, and others around the world, were built by an advanced civilisation lost at the end of the Ice Age, an idea he set out in his book Fingerprints of the Gods. The skeptic Michael Shermer sums up Hancock's view to Michels in passing, saying Hancock thinks these were built by \"the Atlanteans or whoever.\" Shermer brings it up in a debate, as a view he does not share.","graham-hancock-s-text-doubting-the-giza-sar-source-2025":"The writer Graham Hancock, known for his books on lost ancient civilisations, doubts the source behind radar readings said to show hidden features under the Giza plateau. Michels says he texted Hancock about the claims, which rest on SAR, a kind of radar imaging done from satellites, and tied to a researcher named Filippo Biondi, and that Hancock wrote back: \"I have no reason to believe that the source here is being honest.\" Michels shares the text himself in 2025, so it comes as Hancock's private view passed on by the host.","graham-hancock-s-yage-experience-of-a-communicating-presence":"The writer Graham Hancock says that while drinking yage, a hallucinogenic plant brew from the Amazon also known as ayahuasca, he felt a presence that was trying to communicate with him. He tells Michels this from his own experience, describing \"this sense of a presence and of communication.\" If it is what he takes it to be, the brew would open a door to some other intelligence rather than just producing visions.","graham-hancock-ties-cydonia-s-monoliths-to-earth-s-ancient-c-1998":"The writer Graham Hancock, in his 1998 book The Mars Mystery, suggests there could be a link between the symbolism of worn-down stone shapes in Cydonia, a region of Mars where some people see structures in orbital photographs, and the ancient civilizations of Earth. Michels sums up Hancock's argument himself in an episode about Mars. If true, it would tie human prehistory to a lost presence on another planet.","grail-finds-large-lunar-caverns-and-lava-tubes-2010":"In the 2010s NASA mapped what lies beneath the Moon's surface with a probe called GRAIL and found large caverns and lava tubes, according to a researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani. He brings it up with Michels without naming a source, as evidence that the Moon has big hollow spaces inside it.","grandfather-s-name-carved-atop-the-great-pyramid-1916":"Near the top of the Great Pyramid at Giza, carved into the stone, is the name \"P Hancock 5th of April 1916,\" left there by the grandfather of the writer Graham Hancock, who studies lost ancient civilisations. Hancock tells Michels he found it himself while climbing with Santha Faiia, and that his grandfather Philip Hancock's diaries match the carving. In one telling he puts it at the top, in another \"one step down from the top\" on the south side; either way, it ties his own family to the monument he has spent years writing about.","grandfather-s-radio-plays-on-wedding-day-2014":"On his wedding day in 2014, the well-known skeptic Michael Shermer says, music started coming from a desk in his house, and when he opened the drawer he found an old radio that had belonged to his wife Jennifer Shermer's grandfather, switched on and \"perfectly tuned to a station playing beautiful romantic music.\" Shermer tells Michels this himself and wrote about it in his Scientific American column; it matters because a hard skeptic is describing something that felt like a message from someone who had died. Michels had retold it back in 2022 from memory, a little differently, as a mechanical clock from her father that started playing on the wedding night.","grant-files-47-foias-at-midnight-on-january-20-2026":"A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron says that one second after midnight on January 20, 2026, he filed 47 FOIA requests at once (FOIA is a US law that lets anyone request government records), seeking papers on UFO briefings given to Reince Priebus. Cameron tells Michels this as something he did himself, timed to the moment he could start asking for records he hopes will show who in government was told about UFOs.","graves-s-congressional-uap-testimony-2023":"In 2023 a former Navy F-18 pilot named Ryan Graves told a hearing in Congress that he had seen advanced UAP firsthand. UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for what used to be called UFOs. On the show he repeats how he introduced himself: \"a former F-18 pilot with a decade of service in the US Navy\" who has \"experienced Advanced UAP firsthand\". He mentions David Fravor, another Navy pilot, and David Grusch, a former intelligence official who spoke at the same hearing, and it matters because trained military pilots were telling lawmakers on the record that these objects are real.","graves-s-space-situational-awareness-camera-company":"A former US fighter pilot named Ryan Graves says he has started a small company that builds what it believes will be the next generation of \"space situational awareness cameras\", meaning cameras that watch and track objects moving in space. Graves tells Michels this himself, as his own venture, in an episode about fighter pilots who saw UFOs.","graves-speaks-out-because-colleagues-reports-went-unresolved-2019":"Ryan Graves, a fighter pilot, says he started speaking publicly about unexplained objects around 2019 for one simple reason: fellow pilots kept reporting them and getting no answers. He tells Michels he did it \"simply because I was hearing that my colleagues were having no resolution to this issue.\" If that is right, pilots' sightings were going up the chain and nothing came back down.","gravitec-vacuum-chamber-tests":"A company called Gravitec is said to have achieved the effect under discussion inside a vacuum chamber, and to have once worked as a contractor for NASA. Gary McKinnon, a hacker who says he found signs of a secret NASA space program, tells Michels he heard this from a man named Henrik: \"Gravitech has done it in a vacuum chamber.\" If true, it would mean a NASA contractor had tested the effect under controlled lab conditions.","gravity-a-and-gravity-b-in-barry-s-lab-notes-1987":"At S4, a secret site in Nevada where Bob Lazar says he worked on alien craft in the late 1980s, a colleague named Barry Castilio kept other lab notes describing two kinds of gravity, labelled A and B. Lazar tells Michels he does not know if they were older documents Castilio had, but they showed \"the direction they were going in at one time.\" If so, the program had a working theory of gravity unknown to public science.","gravity-researchers-went-dark-1950":"In the 1950s, American scientists working on gravity suddenly 'went dark', according to the researcher Bob Maguire. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that the physicist Louis Witten 'didn't do anything else in gravitation', and that an inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown 'went to work for the military-industrial complex and never published another thing about his work'. Maguire takes this to mean that research into controlling gravity was moved into secret programmes.","gravity-weakening-experiments-with-pulsed-em-fields":"A physicist named John Brandenburg says he and colleagues ran experiments in which pulsing electromagnetic fields weakened gravity in a small area, by about one part in a thousand, an effect he says they could \"easily measure.\" He tells Michels this as someone who took part, in connection with a lab and a meeting of the American Physical Society, the main professional body for physicists in the US. If true, it would mean gravity can be controlled with electricity and magnetism.","gray-encounter-in-catskills-cabin-with-military-and-cia-obse-1980":"In the 1980s the writer Whitley Strieber is said to have met grays, the small grey big-eyed beings of many abduction stories, at his cabin in the Catskills in New York, and to have seen military and intelligence people, CIA among them, standing around watching. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this from Strieber's own account in his book Communion, and it would mean the government knew of, or was part of, such encounters.","gray-pilot-explains-implants-as-broken-instrument-tips-for-a-1963":"A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says that sometime between 1963 and 1965, somewhere off Earth, a pilot of the \"grays\" told him what alien implants really are. The grays are the small, gray-skinned beings of many UFO accounts. According to Buchanan, the being said the implants are broken-off instrument tips: \"we take one or two cells of our diseased tissue, put it on that tip and implant it into you, and you will start making antibodies\". If true, it would mean humans are being used to make medicine for another species.","gray-types-aboard-craft-and-coburn-s-handler":"A guest named Steve Colbern, who describes himself as a witness, says he remembers being on board a craft and seeing \"mostly different types of grays\" there. Grays are the small, gray-skinned beings with large heads and eyes that often appear in accounts of alien abduction. Colbern tells Michels this as his own memory, on an episode about a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies.","grays-as-biological-drones-1990":"The Grays, the small grey-skinned beings described in many UFO stories, are said to be biological drones rather than independent creatures. The YouTube creator Chris Ramsay tells Michels this is what an anonymous poster claiming to be a whistleblower said in a thread on 4chan, an anonymous online message board.","grays-moon-base":"The grays, the small grey-skinned beings of UFO lore, have a base on the Moon and \"come and go at any time they want.\" That is the claim of Charles Hall, a man who says he spent three years in the 1960s living alongside tall white aliens at a Nevada air base, and he says it to Michels as something he knows first-hand. If true, there would be a standing alien outpost right next door to Earth.","grays-rescued-orphaned-chinese-children-and-want-to-return-t-1960":"Charles Hall, who says he spent three years in the 1960s among non-human beings in Indian Springs Valley, Nevada, near Area 51, tells Michels that he saw young Chinese people living with beings he calls Grays. According to Hall, the beings had not abducted them but \"rescued\" them after they were orphaned, and they want to return them to human society one day. He tells it as something he saw himself, and it would mean such beings take people in out of care, not for experiments.","grays-tall-whites-and-norwegians-arrived-on-earth-at-differe":"A man named Charles Hall, who says he spent three years among tall, white-skinned aliens at Area 51, the secret test site in Nevada, says different alien groups reached Earth at different points in the ice ages. He tells Michels he is \"certain that the grays got here first and they were here before the last ice age\". In his account the tall whites came second, and the \"Norwegians\", Nordic-looking beings, came at yet another time, which would make Earth a place several visiting peoples have shared for a very long time.","grays-that-handled-travis-walton-were-robots-built-by-the-no-1975":"The theory holds that the \"grays\", the small grey-skinned beings common in UFO stories, include one group that are actually robots built by the \"Nordics\", tall human-looking beings from the same lore. A researcher named Jason Jorjani says on the show, without naming a source, that these robots were the ones that handled Travis Walton, an Arizona man who says he was taken aboard a UFO in 1975. If true, the beings in that famous case would have been machines working for someone else.","grays-turning-into-owls-in-close-encounter-cases":"In some close encounter cases, people say they watched \"grays\", the small grey-skinned beings often described in alien encounter reports, turn into owls. The researcher Jason Jorjani mentions these cases on the show as a summary of others' accounts, drawing on the work of a writer named Mike Cleland and a book called Closer Encounters. If taken at face value, it would suggest these beings can change how they appear to witnesses.","greada-treaty-eisenhower-agreement-1950":"The Greada treaty, also called the Eisenhower agreement, is a secret pact said to have been made under President Eisenhower in the 1950s. A researcher named Tim Alberino tells Michels he is convinced \"that the Greta contract was real, that it actually happened,\" without naming where he learned it. If so, a US president would have made a hidden deal that was kept from the public ever since.","great-falls-tribune-reports-of-strange-lights-that-week-1967":"In the same week in 1967 that UFOs were reportedly seen near nuclear missile sites in Montana, the Great Falls Tribune newspaper carried accounts of strange lights in the sky. That is what Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer who says he was on duty at one of those sites, tells Michels. To Salas, the reports mean people outside the base saw something too.","great-figures-he-met-said-we-are-not-alone":"A man introduced on the show as a former president of a Russian republic says that the greatest minds he has met, \"real geniuses\", all told him \"we are not alone\". He names among them Pope John Paul II, the Dalai Lama, the Bulgarian seer Baba Vanga, the Indian guru Sai Baba, Henry Kissinger and the physicist Zhores Alferov. He tells Michels this from his own meetings, as weight for his view that humans share the universe with other beings.","great-pyramid-as-a-device-for-balancing-earth-s-energy":"Part of the purpose of the Great Pyramid of Giza and other ancient structures was to balance the Earth's energy, according to Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient sites, who tells Michels it is his own belief. He draws on Christopher Dunn, a writer who has argued that the pyramid was a kind of power plant. If true, the pyramids would be machines, not tombs.","great-pyramid-as-a-giant-power-plant":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was not a tomb but a giant power plant, according to an idea that comes up again and again on the show. It belongs to Christopher Dunn, a former aerospace engineer, who tells Michels himself that his first book, The Giza Power Plant, which he places in 1998, describes the pyramid as 'a power plant.' He calls it 'an electron harvester.' Joe Rogan mentions 'the Christopher Dunn stuff' on his episode, where Dunn 'thinks it was a gigantic power plant.' A former nuclear guard named Mario Woods gives his own version without crediting Dunn: 'I think they were power generators,' and the power went out 'worldwide.' Michels repeats the idea on several episodes, calling the pyramid an 'energy generator' or an 'ancient energy machine', sometimes speaking of the pyramids in general, and once mentioning scans under the pyramids alongside it. So the claim is told firsthand by its author and repeated by Rogan, a guest and the host. If true, it would mean an ancient people had a sophisticated energy technology that was later forgotten.","great-pyramid-as-a-transmitter":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was a transmitter, built to send a signal to whoever its builders wanted to talk to, says a guest named Joe McMoneagle. He offers this to Michels as his own idea, without naming a source, and if he is right the pyramid would be a piece of communications equipment, not a tomb.","great-pyramid-built-over-hundreds-of-years-not-in-khufu-s-23":"The Great Pyramid of Giza was not built during the 23-year reign of the pharaoh Khufu, as is usually said, but is 'more likely' a 'project that took hundreds of years to complete', according to the researcher Graham Hancock. He tells Michels this without naming a source, drawing on the ideas of the writer Robert Bauval. If true, the pyramid's story would start long before Khufu.","great-pyramid-chambers-aligned-to-sirius-and-orion-in-10-500":"The theory is that the two inner rooms of the Great Pyramid at Giza, known as the King's Chamber and the Queen's Chamber, point at different star groups, with the Queen's Chamber facing the star Sirius, in a pattern said to match the sky of 10,500 BC. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells this on the show without naming a source. If right, it would push the pyramid's planning far back before the date usually given for it.","great-pyramid-sulfuric-acid-and-central-pyramid-hydrochloric":"The Great Pyramid made sulfuric acid, and that acid was piped into the middle pyramid at Giza, where it reacted with salt to make hydrochloric acid. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher who argues that the pyramids were chemical works, lays this out for Michels without naming a source. If true, Giza would have been an ancient industrial plant.","greek-inventor-s-steam-sphere":"An inventor in ancient Greece built a round sphere driven by steam, says a guest named Greg Rogers, who is introduced on the show as a NASA doctor. Rogers mentions it to Michels in passing, without naming a source, as an example of ancient people having technology earlier than most assume.","greek-professor-says-the-delphic-women-were-literally-posses":"At Delphi in ancient Greece, the women who spoke as the oracle were literally possessed by the spirit of Zeus when vapours rose from under the ground, according to Greek professors a researcher named Luke Caverns says he has heard. Caverns tells Michels this without naming the professors. If taken at face value, it would make the oracle a real case of something entering a person, not just a ritual.","green-fireballs-as-mothership-hypothesis-1947":"The green fireballs, glowing green meteor-like lights reported over US nuclear sites in the late 1940s, could have come from a mothership waiting outside the atmosphere. Michels puts this idea to the UFO skeptic Mick West during their debate, drawing on Robert Hastings' book UFOs and Nukes. If it were true, something was deliberately watching America's nuclear weapons from space.","green-object-over-savannah-river-site-engineers-1952":"In 1952, at the Savannah River Site, a nuclear facility in South Carolina, an engineer named John Anderson and his colleagues were \"quickly overtaken by a green glowing object,\" angular in shape, that darted back and forth erratically at high speeds and altitudes \"with phenomenal maneuverability.\" Michels recounts it himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the researcher Robert Hastings about UFOs watching nuclear sites, and it would suggest an early interest by such objects in America's nuclear work.","green-orb-over-the-nevada-test-range-during-training":"A green, orb-shaped object is said to have flown over the Nevada test range while Randy Anderson, a guest who says he saw alien technology at a military base, was in training there, and Anderson says a buddy of his saw it. Michels later says that this colleague still works with Anderson at Area 51, the secret Air Force base in Nevada, and confirmed it: \"yeah I saw that green orb that Randy talked about.\" Michels also brings up a DD214, the US military's discharge record, in the same breath, which, along with the colleague's word, he treats as support for Anderson's account.","green-triangles-near-lax-are-airliner-bokeh-2020":"The green triangle-shaped lights filmed near Los Angeles International Airport are not mystery craft but airliners, blurred by the camera into shapes, according to the skeptic researcher Michael Shermer. He tells Michels in a debate that 'all the shows show these triangular green-shaped UAPs near LAX', using UAP, the official term for unidentified objects in the sky, and then says, 'They're not.' His point is that a well-known piece of footage has an ordinary explanation: bokeh, the out-of-focus shapes a lens makes of bright lights.","greenbrier-bunker-revealed-by-washington-post-1990":"The story is that a secret underground bunker at Greenbrier, in West Virginia, was built to evacuate Congress in case of nuclear war, and that nobody knew about it until a newspaper exposed it in the 1990s. The TV writer Bryce Zabel tells it on the show from memory. He says \"I think it was the Post,\" meaning the Washington Post, that broke the story, and he adds that \"there's another one\" in West Virginia. The same words show up in a later episode, credited this time to Brent Friedman, who works with Zabel. Neither teller names a document beyond the Post story. The point of telling it is that large hidden government facilities really have existed and stayed secret for decades. If that is so, other hidden sites could be out there too.","greenstreet-of-the-new-york-post-disputes-the-briefing-accou-2020":"Brandon Fugal, the businessman who owns Skinwalker Ranch in Utah, says a reporter named Steven Greenstreet at the New York Post questioned his account of meeting Sean Kirkpatrick, who led AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into UFO reports. Fugal tells Michels, \"I was being made out to be the liar,\" and says he answered by posting photos of the meeting as proof. If his account holds, it would mean the head of the Pentagon's UFO office really did meet the owner of a ranch famous for strange events.","greenwald-foia-finds-ufo-references-in-obama-era-records-2009":"A public records request is said to have turned up thousands of references to UFOs in the papers of Barack Obama's presidency, from 2009 to 2017. The researcher Jason Simosa says a records hunter named John Greenewald Jr. filed a request under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone ask for government records, and got back material showing about 30,000 references, or maybe 3,000, as Simosa admits he cannot remember which. He says the references were to UFOs, to UAP (\"unidentified anomalous phenomena\", the newer official term for UFOs), and to AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports. If accurate, it would mean the subject came up often inside the Obama White House.","greenwald-s-foia-request-on-fast-walkers-refused-by-norad-as":"John Greenewald Jr., who runs the Black Vault, a website of government records he has obtained, is said to have asked for military files on \"fast walkers\" and been turned away. Fast walkers is a term for fast-moving unknown objects picked up by military sensors. Michels says Greenewald filed under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, and that NORAD, the command that watches the skies over North America, answered that this was \"very sensitive material\". A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani tells it a little differently: the requests went to Space Force and the Department of Defense, also covered \"slow walkers\", and were turned down under an exemption. Both tell it as a sign that the military treats these sightings as secret.","greer-assistant-and-congressman-develop-cancers":"Steven Greer, a UFO activist who argues that a secretive global group controls the UFO secrets, says that he, his best friend and assistant at the time, and a member of Congress all came down with aggressive metastatic cancers. He tells Michels this as something that happened to him and the people around him. In this passage he does not say who the congressman was or what caused the illnesses.","greer-briefed-clinton-s-cia-director-1993":"Steven Greer, a UFO activist who campaigns for the government to reveal what it knows, says he personally briefed Bill Clinton's CIA director on the subject on December 13, 1993. He tells Michels this first-hand, as one of several meetings in 1993, 1994 and 1995. If so, the question of UFOs would have reached the top of the CIA under Clinton.","greer-briefs-admiral-wilson-j2-1997":"In 1997, Steven Greer says, he briefed Admiral Thomas Wilson, the head of intelligence for the Joint Staff, a post known as J2, on UFOs. Greer, a doctor who campaigns for UFO disclosure, tells Michels he was in the room himself, and he points to a memo by the physicist Eric W. Davis about that time. If it is true, the military's top intelligence officers were hearing about the subject in person decades ago.","greer-calls-uapgerb-an-intelligence-asset-2024":"An online researcher who goes by the handle UAPGerb says that in 2024 someone told him: \"I just talked to Greer and he said you're an intelligence asset,\" meaning that the UFO activist Steven Greer had called him a plant working for an intelligence agency. UAPGerb tells Michels this as something said to him, and among his sources he names an unnamed Army public affairs contact.","greer-contacted-by-a-cosmic-intelligence-at-18-2024":"Steven Greer, a long-time campaigner for UFO disclosure, says that at the age of 18 he was contacted by \"a cosmic intelligence\" and told, in effect, to save the world. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb (UAP being the official term for UFOs, unidentified anomalous phenomena) passes this on to Michels, saying Greer said it at his own event in September 2024. It is Greer's account of how his mission began.","greer-gave-grusch-and-luna-an-unredacted-hard-drive-2023":"Steven Greer, a UFO activist, says that around 2023 or 2024 in Washington he gave a hard drive of unredacted material to David Grusch, a former intelligence official turned whistleblower, and to Anna Paulina Luna, a member of Congress. Greer tells Michels he met with \"Grush and Luna and others\" and told them, \"here's a hard drive.\" If so, the people pushing for disclosure in Congress had his uncensored files in their hands.","greer-passes-along-an-insider-s-contact-jake-barber-2023":"In 2023, Steven Greer, a well-known campaigner for UFO disclosure, is said to have handed a witness the contact details of an insider named Jake Barber, telling him that this man \"knows what you saw.\" The witness, Michael Herrera, who says he saw a huge UFO in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels this himself. It matters because it would mean someone on the inside already knew of Herrera's sighting.","greer-s-1993-95-recommendations-to-the-clinton-administratio":"Between 1993 and 1995, a UFO researcher named Steven Greer wrote a set of recommendations about UFO secrecy to the Clinton administration in Washington. He tells Michels he wrote them himself, and that they were the same recommendations he is making now. If so, his proposals have not changed in thirty years and have been in front of a White House before.","greer-s-2001-national-press-club-whistleblower-panel":"In 2001 a UFO activist named Steven Greer held a panel of whistleblowers, people who said they had inside knowledge of government secrets about UFOs, at the National Press Club in Washington. An online UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb mentions it on the show without naming a source, and links it to a 2001 disclosure briefing document and to one of the speakers, an aerospace illustrator named Mark McCandlish. The point of telling it is that insiders were going public on the record two decades before the recent hearings.","greer-s-ce5-coherent-thought-sequencing":"According to Steven Greer, a doctor who leads groups trying to make contact with UFOs through a meditation practice he calls CE5, short for \"close encounters of the fifth kind\", a craft can pick up a focused, coherent thought as clearly as a radio picks up a radio signal. Michels lays out Greer's idea himself, as the theory behind why such group meditation might draw craft in.","greer-s-claim-that-nimitz-objects-were-lockheed-special-acce-2004":"The objects Navy pilots saw in 2004 near the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz off San Diego were, according to the UFO activist Steven Greer, Lockheed Martin special access programs, meaning tightly restricted classified projects. Michels relays Greer's claim as he has heard him make it: \"Oh, the Nimitz stuff, you know, that's all, you know, Lockheed Martin special access programs.\" If true, the famous encounter would have been about secret human aircraft rather than anything unknown.","greer-s-groups-call-up-lights-on-a-nearby-florida-beach":"A man named Steven Greer takes groups to a Florida beach about 40 minutes south of Cocoa Beach, where they try to call up lights in the sky, according to Charles Buhler, who has worked at NASA. Buhler says it is almost the same beach where he himself has been, and he passes on what he knows of Greer's visits rather than describing one he attended. The point of the story is that the same stretch of shore seems to draw this kind of activity.","greer-s-iphone-hacked-and-threats-1991":"Steven Greer, a guest who argues that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, says his iPhone was hacked \"just last week\" and that he was threatened. He tells Michels this as his own recent experience, offered as a sign that people who push on these secrets are watched and pressured.","greer-s-joe-rogan-episode-removed-for-spotify-2020":"Joe Rogan's episode with Steven Greer, a UFO researcher and activist, was taken off the air around 2020 so that Rogan could collect his money from Spotify, Greer tells Michels. Greer gives it as his own account, without naming a source, and offers it as a sign that his message is being kept quiet.","greer-s-near-death-experience-at-17-1972":"Steven Greer, a UFO activist, says that at 17, around 1972 or 1973 in Charlotte, North Carolina, he fell very ill and had a near-death experience. He tells Michels about it as part of his own life story. In the way he tells it, the illness marks a turning point early in his life.","greer-says-the-octagon-craft-is-made-by-lockheed-skunk-works":"A souped-up version of a retrieved octagon-shaped craft is built by Lockheed's Skunk Works, its secret aircraft design division, at a place the speaker calls North Brumit, according to Steven Greer, a man who talks publicly about UFOs. The claim is repeated on the show by a guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb (UAP being the official term for unexplained flying objects), who tells a fellow guest that Greer says he knows the very craft that guest saw. If true, the object would be a human-made machine copied from a recovered one.","greer-tells-fravor-the-tic-tac-was-a-skunk-works-craft-2004":"The famous Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint that Navy pilots met off California in 2004 near the aircraft carrier Nimitz, is said by some to be a secret American aircraft and not an alien one. Steven Greer, a longtime UFO campaigner, tells Michels he met David Fravor, the Navy fighter pilot who chased it, some years ago. In Greer's telling, Fravor said \"it had to be alien,\" and Greer answered, \"Sir, with all due respect, that was how the Lockheed skunk works,\" meaning Lockheed's secret aircraft design division. Greer also says such craft are hidden on purpose, because those who run them \"don't want the National Military Command structure to know\" that what pilots like Fravor met \"was ours.\" The journalist Ross Coulthart reaches the same point by his own route. On another episode he says, \"I now know categorically that the Tic Tac is Lockheed Martin technology,\" naming no source and not mentioning Greer or Fravor. If they are right, the most famous modern UFO case would be a hidden human program kept even from the military's own chain of command.","greer-tells-rfk-jr-about-the-letter-at-sundance":"Steven Greer, a UFO activist, says that at the Sundance festival in Utah he told Robert F. Kennedy Jr. that he had a letter from Kennedy's father about \"this UFO issue,\" dated a month before, in Greer's words, \"they killed him.\" Greer tells Michels he only mentioned it briefly because they were in a crowd. The claim would tie the elder Kennedy to an interest in UFOs just before his death.","greer-warns-joe-firmage-at-mars-cafe-1990":"In the 1990s the UFO activist Steven Greer says he met a wealthy man named Joe Firmage at the Mars Cafe in San Francisco's South of Market area and warned him. Greer describes Firmage's wealth as making him \"bait\", which suggests he saw a rich newcomer to the subject as a target for people who wanted to use him.","greg-eghigian-researching-his-ufo-history-book-at-the-archiv":"The historian Greg Eghigian, according to the Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn, spent \"a couple of weeks\" at Svahn's archive in Sweden while researching his book on the history of UFOs. Svahn, who keeps the archive, tells Michels this as something he saw himself, as a sign of how much material the collection holds.","grey-four-fingered-beings-with-a-wand-telepathic-instruction-1976":"In 1976, on the Allagash Waterway in Maine, grey beings with four fingers took a man, held a wand, and told him with their minds not to resist: \"I was told not to resist, not to fight. I wasn't in any danger. Just comply and everything will be all right.\" That is how Charlie Foltz, one of the men in what became known as the Allagash abductions, describes it to Michels; he says he lived it, and the case is the subject of a book called The Allagash Abductions. If true, it would be a direct account of beings controlling people with thought alone.","grinberg-and-puharich-meet-during-puharich-s-mexican-exile-1980":"In the 1980s, two men named Jacobo Grinberg and Andrija Puharich met in Mexico, where Puharich was living in exile after his house was burned, according to a guest who is not named. The guest, a researcher, tells Michels this without naming a source, in an episode about a scientist with CIA ties who is said to have contacted an alien council. If true, it links the two men's work directly.","grof-s-book-discarnate-aliens-would-show-up-as-hallucination":"The idea is that if there are advanced alien beings with no physical body, people would meet them as what look like hallucinations. A neuroscientist named Andrew Gallimore, who studies the psychedelic drug DMT, tells Michels that the author Stanislav Grof made this point in one of his books, which Gallimore does not name. If so, some drug visions might be contact with real minds rather than tricks of the brain.","ground-penetrating-radar-finds-50-ft-reinforced-concrete-str-2025":"A hidden structure of steel-reinforced concrete, about 50 feet wide, lies beneath the road around Bradshaw Ranch near Sedona, Arizona. That is what Ross Coulthart, a journalist who says he has found a UFO base in Arizona, tells Michels. He says that in 2025 a man named Paul scanned the road with ground-penetrating radar, which uses radio waves to see what lies below the surface, and the structure showed up clearly. In a later conversation he adds that it sat \"literally just under the ground,\" which if true would mean something built and buried on purpose at a place he links to UFO activity.","ground-penetrating-radar-finds-cut-voids-with-objects-beneat-1990":"In the 1990s, according to this account, scans of the ground beneath the Great Sphinx at Giza, using ground-penetrating radar and sonar, found cut-out empty spaces with objects inside them. A researcher of ancient history named Matt LaCroix tells Michels he heard this over dinner from Robert Schoch, a researcher who says he helped lead those readings. If true, there would be hidden chambers under the Sphinx that have never been opened to the public.","ground-radar-finds-debris-field-and-voids-in-mesa":"The owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property long known for reports of strange events, says ground-penetrating radar on a mesa there found hollow spaces underground and \"a debris field of material that does not fit, that is unusual.\" The owner tells Michels this as someone directly involved in the work. The episode's title frames it as a sign that something, perhaps a craft, lies buried under the ranch.","grusch-and-the-legacy-reverse-engineering-program":"A former US intelligence officer turned whistleblower named David Grusch may have come across a \"legacy program\", a long-running secret effort said to recover craft of non-human origin and reverse engineer them, meaning take them apart to learn how they work. Michels raises this himself and is careful to say only that Grusch \"may or may not\" have run into it. If true, the US would have been quietly studying such craft for a long time.","grusch-approaches-knapp-and-corbell-at-a-bar-at-the-scu-even-2022":"David Grusch, the intelligence officer who in 2022 filed a whistleblower complaint about secret UFO work with the Intelligence Community Inspector General (the watchdog for US spy agencies), first approached the journalist George Knapp and the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell at a bar during a conference of UFO researchers in Rocket City, as Huntsville, Alabama, is called. Corbell tells Michels this as something he was there for, and he names the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan as part of the scene. If it happened this way, it is how Grusch's story first reached the reporters who later helped bring it out.","grusch-asks-brent-to-help-bring-john-harrington-forward-2020":"A man in Hollywood named Brent Friedman says the whistleblower David Grusch, a former intelligence official, asked him to help bring forward John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy. Friedman tells Michels that this was \"part of what Dave Gush was asking\": \"could you help make this happen?\" If so, the disclosure push wanted a former cabinet member on the record.","grusch-assigned-to-the-uap-task-force-2019":"A whistleblower named David Grusch says he was brought onto the UAP Task Force, a Pentagon group set up to study UAP, the official term for unidentified flying objects, after the task force director \"funneled the request through the ops center\". Grusch tells Michels this as his own experience, and Eric W. Davis, a scientist who has worked with the Pentagon, adds that Grusch was the task force's liaison from the NRO, the agency that runs US spy satellites, and \"took direction from\" a man named Jay Stratton. Davis places this in 2020 and 2021 rather than in 2019, and it matters because it would put Grusch inside the government's own UFO effort.","grusch-blows-whistle-on-crash-retrieval-program-after-uaptf":"A former intelligence officer named David Grusch, who served on the UAPTF, a Pentagon task force that looked into unexplained objects in the sky, dug further into the matter than anyone else on it, Michels says. Grusch then blew the whistle, claiming there had been a secret programme lasting decades to recover crashed craft. Michels tells this himself in an episode with the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for strange reports.","grusch-colleague-finds-radar-data-on-big-sur-1964":"The claim is that radar data in a military archive confirms the Big Sur incident, a 1964 missile test in California in which a film is said to show an unknown flying object pacing the warhead. Michels says someone told him that David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who has said the US holds crashed non-human craft, knew of the film and, while serving on a Pentagon task force studying unexplained aerial sightings, had a colleague search the archive. According to what Michels was told, Grusch says the colleague found radar data showing an anomalous object paced the warhead, which, if true, would back the film with hard military records.","grusch-dick-cheney-headed-the-ufo-program-2000":"Former vice president Dick Cheney is said to have headed the US government's secret UFO program. Michels attributes the claim to David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned whistleblower, saying Grusch \"has come out saying Cheney who just died was head of the UFO program.\" On another show Michels puts it as a question to a Pentagon scientist he is interviewing, asking whether to take Grusch at face value that Cheney was \"the last head honcho of this sort of program,\" until 2009. If true, it would place the program at the highest level of the White House.","grusch-disgruntled-insiders-inside-the-legacy-program-2023":"People inside the secret effort that David Grusch, a former intelligence officer, says has kept recovered craft of non-human origin, the so-called legacy program, wanted this material brought out and were \"extremely disgruntled.\" Michels says Grusch told him this in his own interview with him, and he repeats it in a conversation with the analyst Matthew Pines; if true, it would mean the push for openness is coming partly from within the program itself.","grusch-dodges-the-question-about-agreements-in-his-testimony-2023":"When the whistleblower David Grusch testified under oath to Congress in 2023, he is said to have sidestepped a question about \"agreements\", which neither teller spells out. A researcher named Matthew Pines says Grusch \"very pointedly dodged\" it. A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown says Grusch was asked about agreements and \"said that he could not provide further detail\". The tellers raise it because a refusal to answer could mean there is something there to hide.","grusch-files-ig-complaint-and-names-witnesses-2022":"A whistleblower named David Grusch says that in 2022 he filed a complaint with an inspector general, a government watchdog office, and told its investigators whom to interview, and that they came back saying, in his words, \"Holy shit, this is credible.\" He tells Michels he vetted his sources over several years and made them sit for interviews with the inspector general themselves \"because this shit is crazy.\" On the show the number is given as about 40: Michels calls them people \"who have handled exotic UFO material firsthand\" and names the intelligence community's inspector general, while a former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo insists the testimony is not all secondhand. If true, dozens of insiders went on record with an official watchdog about hidden UFO material.","grusch-gave-congress-locations-of-spacecraft-no-subpoenas-fo-2023":"A whistleblower named David Grusch is said to have told Congress exactly where to look for recovered spacecraft, and yet no subpoenas followed. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels that Grusch gave lawmakers \"first hand direct information from his sources\" on \"where to find the spacecraft\". A fighter pilot named Ryan Graves remembers it from the 2023 hearing, saying he heard Grusch say \"I can tell you the location. Let's get in a skiff\", meaning a SCIF, a secure room for classified talk. Graves says nothing about subpoenas. If true, Congress was handed a lead and did not follow it.","grusch-gave-hundreds-of-pages-to-the-icig-2022":"A former intelligence officer turned whistleblower named David Grusch is said to have handed hundreds of pages of documents to the Intelligence Community Inspector General, or ICIG, the watchdog that takes complaints from inside the US spy agencies. Grusch filed the complaint in 2022. Michels tells this again and again on the show, in his own words. In his account the pages describe a secret, decades-old programme to recover crashed UFOs, which the subject calls a legacy crash retrieval programme. He says the inspector general, Thomas Monheim, called the claims \"urgent and credible\". The count changes between tellings. Most of the time Michels says hundreds of pages, once he says \"presumably\" hundreds, and once he says \"over a thousand pages\". In another telling he passes the story on from someone he names only as Omar. A UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells it separately: he thinks it may be \"a thousand pages plus\", and says Monheim is \"now out\" of the job and nothing has been done with them. If true, the government's own watchdog would have taken claims of hidden crash recoveries seriously.","grusch-harassed-and-his-medical-file-leaked-after-whistleblo-2022":"After David Grusch, a former intelligence officer, filed a whistleblower complaint saying the government hides UFO programs, he and his wife were harassed and his medical file was leaked, according to people who know him. A scientist named Eric W. Davis says the harassment came from someone in the National Reconnaissance Office, the US spy satellite agency. A filmmaker named Jeremy Corbell gives Grusch's own words about the leaked diagnosis, PTSD from combat: 'I was in combat Afghanistan 2013.' A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell says Grusch 'had a hard go of it', that they went after him because he went public, and that some of what happened to him has never been made public.","grusch-harrington-holds-oral-history-of-a-period-with-lost-d-2026":"David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned UFO whistleblower, has named John S. Herrington, a former Reagan administration official and Secretary of Energy, as a key person who remembers a period whose records no longer exist, according to two Hollywood writers who tell it on the show. Brent Friedman says they 'identified John Harrington' as someone with 'oral history of a period where a bunch of documentation does not exist anymore', and describes a call about how to approach him. Bryce Zabel adds that, according to Grusch, many documents from the years when Herrington was active in the Reagan administration have gone missing. If true, Herrington's memory would be one of the few routes into that lost history.","grusch-has-no-information-on-lazar-1989":"David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who has come forward with claims that the government hides UFO secrets, tells Michels flatly: \"I have no information on Bob Lazar.\" Lazar is a man who says that in 1989 he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada. So Grusch, speaking for himself, is neither backing Lazar's story nor knocking it down.","grusch-hospitalization-and-retained-clearance-2010":"David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who has spoken publicly about secret UFO programmes, once had what Mick West calls a 'psychotic episode', checked himself into a hospital, and still kept his security clearance, the government's permission to handle secret material. West, a well-known UFO skeptic, raises this in a debate with Michels without naming a source and places it sometime in the 2010s. He brings it up as a point about how much weight Grusch's word should carry.","grusch-named-sarbacher-as-the-key-to-the-program-s-architect-2023":"David Grusch, a former US intelligence official who has testified to Congress about a hidden programme that he says recovers and studies craft not made by humans, was asked which one person would help someone understand how that programme is structured, and he answered: a man named Robert Sarbacher. Ammar Kandil tells this on the show, saying he heard it from Grusch around 2023 or 2024. If true, it would point researchers to Sarbacher as a way into how the secret effort was built.","grusch-on-a-multi-decade-cold-war-with-adversaries-2023":"David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who came forward in 2023 to say the government holds recovered UFOs, spoke in a TV interview with the news channel NewsNation of a \"multi-decade cold war with our adversaries\" over this. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb recalls this to Michels and adds his own reading: the adversaries, he says, \"we can assume is Russia and China.\" If true, the US has been in a long secret race with rival powers to find and study such craft.","grusch-on-asking-sarbacher-and-oppenheimer-1940":"The former intelligence officer David Grusch says the government scientist Robert Sarbacher and the physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer helped hide UFO secrets in the 1940s and 50s. He tells Michels that if he could, he would ask them \"what was your thought process in the 40s and 50s you know squirreling this away.\" If so, the secrecy was set up by top wartime scientists.","grusch-on-disclosing-the-biologic-side-2023":"David Grusch, a former intelligence official who went public in 2023 with claims that the US government holds craft not made by humans, says he set out to touch on \"all the most sensitive parts,\" including \"the biologic side,\" meaning bodies or living remains. His words appear on an episode Michels made about the writer Greg Bishop, where Grusch explains that he wanted to put enough out there so the things that \"really need to come out\" would. It matters because it would mean the secret covers not just machines but beings.","grusch-on-jre-cia-psi-programs-as-a-key-to-stored-craft-2024":"The idea is that the CIA may have looked into psychic abilities as a way to unlock recovered craft the US is said to have stored in a warehouse. An online UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb recalls that David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who says the US holds non-human craft, raised this on Joe Rogan's podcast while talking about remote viewing, the claimed ability to see distant places with the mind. If true, it would mean the mind itself was treated as a possible key to working the technology.","grusch-on-sarbacher-s-role-in-standing-up-the-program-1940":"A man named Robert Sarbacher, in the 1940s or 50s, helped set up the secret program that David Grusch says he uncovered. Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who has gone public as a whistleblower, says on the show that Sarbacher is \"purportedly involved in the stand-up of the stuff that I uncovered.\" If true, it would put Sarbacher among the founders of that hidden effort.","grusch-on-sentient-plasmoid-life-2020":"David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who has said the US government holds craft and bodies that are not human, is said to have spoken about sentient plasmoid life, the idea of living, aware beings made of plasma, the glowing charged gas found in lightning. Michels mentions it himself in passing, describing Grusch getting into the idea at a press event in Washington in the 2020s, without giving more detail of what Grusch said.","grusch-oppenheimer-helped-set-up-ufo-secrecy-1940":"J. Robert Oppenheimer was reportedly one of the people who helped set up the secret UFO effort that a former intelligence officer turned whistleblower named David Grusch says he uncovered. Grusch says this on the show, calling Oppenheimer \"somebody purportedly involved\" in standing it up. If true, the secrecy would go back to the 1940s, the same years as the atomic bomb project.","grusch-oppenheimer-set-up-ufo-secrecy-1940":"J. Robert Oppenheimer and others of his generation of scientists are said to have set up the secrecy that has surrounded UFOs ever since. Michels raises it as a claim of David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned whistleblower: \"if you believe David Grusch then they literally set up the secrecy.\" If true, the cover-up would trace back to the same circle that built the atomic bomb.","grusch-reprisals-at-the-nga-2020":"A former intelligence officer turned whistleblower named David Grusch is said to have suffered serious reprisals and been \"dragged through the mud\" while working at the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency, the US spy agency that handles satellite imagery and maps. Michels says this himself, without going into specifics, and argues that Grusch should get \"a large check in the mail\" in compensation. If true, a government insider would have been punished for coming forward.","grusch-s-40-witnesses-are-firsthand-and-none-are-physicists-2023":"The roughly 40 witnesses behind David Grusch's claims, a former US intelligence official who told Congress in 2023 about hidden programmes holding non-human craft, are all firsthand, but not one of them is a physicist. Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has worked with the Pentagon, says this, explaining that he and the mathematician Eric Weinstein spent hours discussing it about two years earlier. It matters because it suggests the witnesses saw things directly but could not explain the science behind them.","grusch-s-alternative-timeline-view-of-the-technology-2023":"The recovered craft, the whistleblower David Grusch told Michels, may not be technology just a step ahead of ours but something more like the product of an alternative timeline. Grusch is a former intelligence officer who says the government holds crashed non-human craft, and Michels recalls the remark from his 2023 interview with him, retelling it in a later episode with a Navy pilot. If true, the makers would not simply be more advanced, but on a whole different path.","grusch-s-claims-of-reverse-engineering-programs-and-craft-in-2023":"The claim, from 2023, is that the US government has secretly recovered craft that were not made by humans, and has been reverse engineering them, meaning taking them apart to learn how they work. It comes from a man named David Grusch, who made a formal complaint in 2022 to the Intelligence Community Inspector General, the watchdog for US spy agencies, and later testified before Congress. A journalist named Jeremy Corbell quotes Grusch's message as: \"Not only are UFOs real, but we are reverse engineering the craft. We've been exploiting the derivative technologies for decades.\" Michels comes back to the claims several times on his own. He speaks of \"programs reverse engineering\" and craft \"in the hangars,\" and of a crash retrieval programme, one that collects downed craft, running \"70 plus years.\" He also says Grusch spoke of \"biologics,\" meaning bodies, and non-human craft held in aerospace warehouses, and offered to give their addresses. Talking to a former Navy pilot named Ryan Graves, Michels recalls Grusch sitting beside him at the hearing while the pilots gave their accounts. If true, it would mean the US has kept recovered alien craft and bodies from the public for generations.","grusch-s-ig-complaint-and-aoc-exchange-about-reverse-enginee-2022":"A whistleblower named David Grusch is said to have handed hundreds of pages to Thomas Monheim, the Inspector General of the Intelligence Community, in a complaint filed in 2022. Michels says the pages argue that there are covert reverse engineering programs, meaning secret efforts to take apart recovered craft that are not of human make and learn how they work. He tells it himself, without naming a source, as background to a later exchange with Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez about where such programs are kept. If true, it would mean a formal complaint inside the intelligence world describes such hidden programs.","grusch-s-irad-fraud-allegation-2023":"David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who testified to Congress in 2023 about hidden UFO programs, has made allegations of fraud involving IRAD, the internal research and development money of defense contractors. Michels's pseudonymous co-host Jack, who says he has worked in defense and aerospace for over fifteen years, raises the allegation only in passing when he asks the physicist Eric Davis about it, without spelling out what Grusch claimed, and the point would be that research money may have been used to hide secret work.","grusch-s-public-testimony-cleared-by-dopsr-the-rest-given-in-2023":"When David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned whistleblower on UFOs, testified to Congress in 2023, everything he said in public had first been cleared by the Pentagon's office that reviews what current and former officials may say openly, and the rest he gave in a closed, classified session. The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels this, saying what Grusch \"was able to give was approved by the Department of Defense.\" It would mean Grusch's public words were only the part he was allowed to say.","grusch-s-secondhand-spaceship-testimony-explained-as-misiden-2023":"When David Grusch, a witness who testified before Congress in 2023, said he had talked to someone who interacted with a spaceship, he was only passing on what he was told, argues the skeptic Michael Shermer. Debating Michels, Shermer suggests the thing behind such secondhand stories could just be a misidentified test drone, which would make the testimony a mistake, not proof of a craft.","grusch-s-terms-came-from-crash-retrieval-program-briefings-2020":"Words David Grusch uses, a former Air Force intelligence officer who says the US has recovered non-human craft, came from briefings he was given on a crash retrieval programme, a secret effort said to recover and hold crashed craft. Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has worked on Pentagon studies of unexplained aerial reports, tells Michels he thinks this is where Grusch heard them. If so, it would mean Grusch was briefed on such a programme from the inside.","grusch-s-testimony-prompts-rogers-to-come-forward-2023":"Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor who says he saw a UFO in a secret hangar, says what finally pushed him to speak publicly was the 2023 testimony to Congress of David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned whistleblower who said the government holds non-human craft. Rogers tells Michels, \"the real impetus was when David Grusch testified.\" It matters because it suggests one public witness can draw out others who had kept quiet.","grusch-s-witness-and-the-300-ft-triangle-over-a-car-2010":"Someone driving to work saw a huge triangle, about 300 feet across, over their car, near Norfolk, Virginia, sometime in the 2010s. Michels says this was one of the witness accounts mentioned to him by David Grusch, a former US intelligence official who has testified to Congress about hidden programmes, so it comes to us third-hand: from the witness, to Grusch, to Michels.","grusch-says-cheney-was-the-last-at-the-top-of-a-legacy-ufo-p-2000":"Former Vice President Dick Cheney, according to this claim, was the last person to sit at the top of a long-running secret government program that handled UFOs, in the 2000s. Michels repeats it from David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who became a UFO whistleblower, saying Grusch told him Cheney was \"the last guy to be at the pyramid\" when that program still had a working structure. If true, oversight of such a program reached the highest levels of the White House.","grusch-says-he-knows-john-harrington-2023":"In 2023, at his own house in Los Angeles, a Hollywood man named Bryce Zabel says he asked the whistleblower David Grusch, a former intelligence official, whether he had heard of John Harrington, the former US Secretary of Energy. Zabel tells Michels that Grusch answered, \"Oh, I know I know John Harrington\", which suggests the former secretary was already known to people inside the disclosure push.","grusch-says-the-peruvian-skeletons-resemble-things-seen-in-c":"The strange three-fingered skeletons found in Peru, it is said, look like bodies that David Grusch saw in secret government settings. Michels tells it himself: Grusch, he says, told him the skeletons Michels saw in Peru \"looked like things that he's seen in classified settings\". A guest named Eric Burlison relays it more narrowly, saying Grusch found that some of them, \"based on the physiology\", were \"congruent with what he has seen in briefings in his former role\". If true, it would tie the Peruvian finds to bodies the government is said to hold.","grusch-sends-someone-to-interview-mccasland-2026":"Congressman Eric Burlison says the whistleblower David Grusch sent someone to interview a general named Neil McCasland, and that the interviewer \"was not able to get a whole lot out of him\". Burlison tells Michels this as something he knows of, in an episode about an alien briefing he says changed the President's life, and it bears on how hard it is to get insiders to talk.","grusch-speaks-of-interdimensional-beings-and-holography-at-a-2023":"At a 2023 hearing in Congress, a witness named David Grusch spoke of interdimensional beings, and holography came up, according to the researcher Eric Weinstein. Weinstein tells Michels, without naming a source, 'David Grusch I believe used this phrase in a hearing and they talked about holography', so he offers it as his memory of what was said. It matters to him because it would mean such ideas were raised in front of Congress itself.","grusch-testifies-before-congress-2023":"On July 26, 2023, David Grusch, a former intelligence officer, testified under oath before Congress in Washington about what he said he had found on secret UFO programmes. Michels tells this many times in his own words. He stresses that Grusch spoke under oath and \"about his findings\", and in one telling he gives the exact date. In a 2025 episode Michels sums up what Grusch told \"the world and our system of government\": among other \"staggering revelations,\" that \"the military has an alien craft retrieval program,\" meaning a programme for collecting crashed craft. The documentary filmmaker James Fox also tells it, as someone who says he watched the hearing live on his phone. In his words, Grusch \"came forward and testified under oath to a bipartisan group of lawmakers.\" So the story comes from the host summing up the hearing and from a viewer who watched it happen. If what Grusch said is true, the US military holds non-human craft and has kept that from the public and from Congress.","grusch-testimony-some-craft-were-gifted-2023":"From David Grusch's 2023 testimony, Grusch being a former Air Force intelligence officer who said the US holds craft of non-human origin, Michels takes the point that some of these objects were apparently not crashed or captured but given to us as gifts. Michels raises it himself as one of the more interesting things to come out of that testimony, suggesting such gifts would give the United States a leg up.","grusch-testimony-under-oath-and-inspector-general-complaint-2023":"In 2023 a whistleblower named David Grusch is said to have testified under oath before Congress and filed a complaint with the inspector general of the intelligence community, the official who handles internal complaints from spy agencies. The claim was that the US runs secret programs to recover crashed craft that are not of human make. A researcher named Marik von Rennenkampff tells it as a chain of events: firsthand witnesses went to the inspector general, and a former Pentagon UFO official named Luis Elizondo said under oath, \"Yes, we have crash retrieval programs.\" Michels says Grusch brought \"40 firsthand\" whistleblowers to the inspector general, Thomas Monheim, who found the complaint \"urgent\" and credible. If true, it would mean the government's own watchdog took the claims of a hidden crash program seriously.","grusch-the-symbol-rate-is-too-slow":"In an interview with Michels, a former intelligence officer turned whistleblower named David Grusch reportedly said that \"it's almost like the symbol rate is too slow\". The symbol rate is how fast units of meaning can be passed along in a message. Michels recalls the remark in a discussion about Plato's cave and consciousness, apparently to suggest that ordinary human communication is too slow to carry what is involved, though he does not spell this out.","grusch-uses-the-data-to-find-40-witnesses":"David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who says the US holds non-human craft, took a body of data collected in-house by Eric W. Davis and a physicist named Hal Puthoff and used it to find 40 witnesses. Davis, a physicist who has worked on Pentagon studies of unexplained aerial reports, tells Michels this, saying Grusch \"ran with our data mine.\" If true, it would tie Grusch's witnesses to records these two scientists had already gathered.","grush-tells-moskowitz-he-would-answer-on-overhead-collection-2023":"At a 2023 congressional hearing, Congressman Jared Moskowitz asked a whistleblower named David Grusch whether he had seen images of UFOs taken by \"overhead collection platforms\", meaning spy satellites and other eyes in the sky, and then whether he had seen such images of UFO crashes. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb recounts on the show that Grusch \"kind of danced around the topic\" and then said, \"I would have to answer that in the closed\" session, the classified part held away from the public. If such images exist, the government would have watched crashes from above.","guarded-portal-facility-under-forest-service-cover":"A guarded facility built around a portal is said to exist at Bradshaw Ranch in Arizona, disguised as a Forest Service site. Ross Coulthart, a journalist who has written a book on UFOs, tells Michels what he was told: \"There is a portal in the United States.\" If true, the government would be openly guarding a doorway of some kind under an ordinary cover.","guardians-of-the-ark-sicken-and-die":"The monks chosen to guard the Ark of the Covenant, which is said to be kept at Aksum in Ethiopia, fall ill and die soon after they take up the job. That is what the writer Graham Hancock tells Michels. The first time, he passes it on as something he was told: guardians \"get sick and die very very rapidly.\" Later he tells it as something he learned from his own interviews at Aksum, adding that they get cataracts and that once appointed they live \"about 3 years,\" which, if true, would hint that the object they guard is physically dangerous.","gudea-of-sumer-given-temple-plans-in-a-dream":"An ancient king of Sumer named Gudea said that in a dream he was given a piece of lapis lazuli, a deep blue stone, along with plans for a temple, Michels says. He brings it up in an episode with the writer Graham Hancock, beside similar dream stories about the Egyptian pharaoh Thutmose IV and the Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar. The point is that ancient builders said their designs came to them from somewhere beyond.","guest-contacts-hal-puthoff-as-a-high-school-student-2002":"A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels that around 2002, while still in high school, he got in touch with the physicist Hal Puthoff. He tells it as his own memory, as part of how he came into this field. It shows how a young person interested in fringe physics could reach one of its known figures directly.","guest-interns-at-the-pear-lab-2003":"A man named Maaneli Derakhshani says that in 2003 he interned at the PEAR lab, short for Princeton Engineering Anomalies Research, a lab at Princeton's engineering school that studied anomalies. He tells Michels he \"got in contact\" with the lab, whose names he connects with Robert Jahn and Brenda Dunne, the people associated with it. It matters because it puts him inside one of the rare university labs that took such questions seriously.","guest-s-attempt-to-show-aldrin-uap-imagery-2020":"A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani says he tried to show the Apollo 11 astronaut Buzz Aldrin images from the Apollo 11 and Apollo 17 Moon missions that, he says, show UAP, meaning unidentified objects. He tells Michels he reached out through one of Aldrin's assistants at Aldrin's company, Buzz Aldrin Ventures, and \"got a polite reply\", but nothing more.","guest-s-statistical-analysis-of-ganzfeld-telepathy-trials-1970":"In Ganzfeld trials, a kind of telepathy test from the 1970s where a person in mild sensory isolation tries to sense which image someone elsewhere is looking at, the best-run studies score hits about 30 to 35 percent of the time. Maaneli Derakhshani, a guest who has looked at the statistics, tells Michels this, drawing on the work of the parapsychologist Charles Honorton. If the figure holds, the results would beat what guessing alone should give.","guest-saw-no-ufo-trace-among-stony-brook-physicists-2004":"From 2004 to 2008, while he was at Stony Brook University, Maaneli Derakhshani says he saw no sign of any interest in UFOs among the leading theoretical physicists and mathematicians there. He tells Michels he dealt closely with nearly all the top professors at the Yang Institute for Theoretical Physics and in the math department, and ran the math club for a couple of years, yet \"never came across any hint or trace\" of it. The point comes up while they are talking about the mathematician Jim Simons, and it suggests that, at least as far as he could see, that circle had no hidden link to the subject.","guest-shadowbanned-after-posting-photo-with-malone-at-hereti-2020":"An unnamed researcher says that after posting a picture of himself with Robert Malone, taken at a conference called Heretic-con, he was \"shadowbanned instantly\" on X, formerly Twitter, meaning the platform quietly hid his posts from others. He tells Michels this from his own experience. To him it suggests the platform suppresses people seen with certain figures.","guide-s-son-claims-his-father-led-armstrong-down-the-wrong-p-1975":"When the astronaut Neil Armstrong joined an expedition to Tayos Cave in Ecuador in 1975, his guide deliberately led him the wrong way, and the team did not find what it was looking for. Michels tells this as something he heard from a friend who says he is the guide's son. In one episode Michels just calls him \"this guy\"; in another he names him as Luis Felipe San Salvador and adds that the family knows the real location of the thing that was sought. If true, it would mean whatever the expedition was after is still there, and someone knows where.","gus-grissom-s-lemon-and-cia-on-the-launch-pad-before-his-dea-1967":"The astronaut Gus Grissom is said to have hung a lemon on his Apollo capsule in 1967, shortly before he died there, as a complaint about the spacecraft. The podcaster Joe Rogan tells it on the show, passing on what he heard from a man named Bart Sibrel and from Grissom's wife: Grissom \"hung a lemon on a coat hanger outside of that thing because he said, 'We can't even communicate with the tower how we're ever going to communicate when we're in space.'\" Rogan's version also puts the CIA on the launch pad before his death. Michels mentions it himself without the CIA detail, saying Grissom \"died the next day\" after the lemon. The story is told as a hint that Grissom's death was more than an accident.","guthrie-law-article-finds-no-prosecutions-for-secure-disclos-2020":"A young lawyer named Dylan Guthrie has written a law article finding that no one has been prosecuted for passing secret information to Congress through proper, secure channels. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, mentions it to Michels, saying he believes it came out in the Harvard Law Review; he does not quote it, so how far it goes is left to his summary. The point he draws is that would-be whistleblowers on UFO programs need not fear prison for speaking to Congress the right way.","guy-banister-544-camp-street-and-the-fbi-s-real-x-files-ufo-1963":"Before he shot President Kennedy in 1963, Lee Harvey Oswald was operating out of 544 Camp Street in New Orleans, a building owned by Guy Banister, whom Michels calls a prominent FBI man who ran the FBI's UFO collections, \"The Real X-Files,\" which he says the TV show is based on. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode built around Joe Rogan's views on aliens, and it would tie the Kennedy killing to the people keeping UFO secrets.","guy-banister-reports-ufo-sightings-to-hoover-under-special-m-1947":"In 1947 a man named Guy Banister was assigned to report on UFO sightings in the Pacific Northwest directly to J. Edgar Hoover, under an assignment called special mission X, says a researcher named Peter Levenda. Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, and it would mean the FBI's own chief was quietly tracking UFO reports from the start.","gwynne-shotwell-s-special-launch-shoes-at-spacex":"Gwynne Shotwell, the chief operating officer of SpaceX who Michels says \"basically runs it at this point,\" is said to wear special shoes for rocket launches. Michels brings it up from a source he calls Countdown to Launch, quoting her as saying, \"I wear my special shoes,\" a small ritual at the heart of a high-tech company.","h-g-wells-the-world-set-free-predicts-the-atomic-bomb-1914":"H.G. Wells described the building of an atomic bomb in his book The World Set Free, published in 1914, decades before the first real one. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas brings this up on the show without naming a source beyond the book itself. He tells it as a case of something foreseen long before it existed.","hahn-splits-the-atom-in-1938":"In 1938, a scientist named Otto Hahn, whom Robert Salas calls 'a Nazi scientist', became the first to split the atom in an experiment in Germany. Salas, a former Air Force officer who tells Michels of a UFO shutting down nuclear missiles, gives this from memory, saying the name is 'Han I think', as the starting point of the nuclear age.","haim-eshed-and-the-galactic-federation-2020":"In 2020 Haim Eshed, the former head of Israel's space program, is said to have claimed that aliens organised as a \"Galactic Federation\" are in contact with Earth's governments. Michels brings it up again and again. He calls Eshed the head of the Israeli space force and says Eshed made the claim in an autobiography. As Michels tells it, Eshed said the United States and other nations have had \"Direct contact with a Galactic Federation\", spoke of a secret space program and an underground base on Mars, and said that \"there are like factions of aliens fighting each other\" and American astronauts \"working with aliens under the ground in Mars\". The UFO investigator James Fox does not name Eshed or the Federation. He only stresses that the man was head of Israel's \"equivalent of space force\", which he calls \"an extremely important position\". Another guest, Chris Ramsay, adds that the Federation is said to be \"here to stop our nukes.\" The weight they give it is that the claim, if true, comes from someone senior in a country's defence establishment.","haim-eshed-claims-us-astronauts-and-aliens-in-underground-ma-2020":"In 2020, Haim Eshed, the former head of the space directorate of Israel's defence ministry and, in Michels's words, essentially the father of the Israeli space program, publicly claimed that American astronauts and alien representatives were working together at underground bases on Mars. Michels tells it himself as a public statement Eshed made, notable because it came from such a senior figure.","haim-eshed-s-galactic-federation-and-mars-program-claims":"A man in Israel named Haim Eshed has said that there is a galactic federation, a kind of alliance of beings beyond Earth, and that a space programme exists that involves people living on Mars. Michels brings this up himself on the show and credits it to Eshed and to Eshed's book, which he does not name. If true, it would mean contact with other worlds and a human presence on Mars are already happening out of public view.","haitian-traditions-of-beings-among-us":"Haiti is said to have traditions about strange beings living among people, traditions so ordinary there that one man was surprised to find Americans had no such belief. Diana Pasulka, a religion scholar who studies UFO belief, tells Michels a man emailed her about it, saying he \"could believe that we didn't have any belief in this at all\" after the things he saw there. It suggests that what Americans call UFO encounters may be part of everyday religious life elsewhere.","hal-povenmire-s-visits-from-2008-and-his-belief-before-he-di":"A man named Hal Povenmire visited Chris Bledsoe in Fayetteville, North Carolina, from 2008 on, and before he died in 2019 he came to believe the strange things around Bledsoe were angelic. Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says he draws UFOs to him and who has been studied by NASA scientists, passes on what Povenmire told him. It matters because it shows someone who looked closely at the events deciding they were spiritual rather than alien.","hal-puthoff-contacts-grant-cameron-about-the-lundahl-documen":"A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron says that while he was working with letters and photos left by Arthur Lundahl, a CIA photo analyst, the physicist Hal Puthoff suddenly got in touch with him. Cameron tells Michels this as something that happened to him. In his telling, Puthoff's call suggests the Lundahl papers drew the attention of people close to the subject.","hal-puthoff-on-rogan-us-holds-15-crashed-craft-2025":"The physicist Hal Puthoff is said to have stated on Joe Rogan's podcast in 2025 that the US has \"15 crash craft in our possession.\" Michels brings this up on his own show more than once, at one point giving it as 12 to 15 craft and asking where Puthoff got the figure, and at another as 10 to 40, adding that Puthoff says he worked with President George W. Bush and his national security adviser Stephen Hadley on whether to make disclosures. If true, the government would be holding many recovered craft of unknown origin.","hal-puthoff-ordered-copies-of-ufos-and-nukes-to-give-out-in-2005":"Robert Hastings, author of UFOs and Nukes, a book about UFO reports at nuclear weapons sites, says that in December 2008 a man he worked with, Dr. Harold \"Hal\" Puthoff, suddenly emailed him asking for four copies shipped overnight. When Hastings suggested slower, cheaper mail because overnight shipping cost more than a hundred dollars, Puthoff called him right away and said he had a meeting in two days with some \"movers and shakers in Washington\" and wanted to hand them the book. Hastings tells this as his own experience, both to Michels and in a piece he wrote in January 2024, where he notes the first edition came out in July 2008 and was sold only on his website. If true, it would mean his book reached powerful people in Washington.","hal-puthoff-s-2004-white-house-conversation-about-ufo-disclo":"In 2004, at the White House, there was a conversation about whether the American people were ready to be told the truth about UFOs, according to a physicist named Hal Puthoff. A former Pentagon intelligence official named Luis Elizondo refers to it with Michels, saying Puthoff discussed it publicly and that he can't believe Puthoff did. If true, it would mean disclosure was weighed at the highest level years before it became public news.","hal-puthoff-s-medieval-levitating-secret-society":"The objects people call UFOs could be, in one idea floated by the physicist Hal Puthoff, the work of a medieval secret society that knows how to levitate and wraps itself in metal capsules. Michels says Puthoff put it to him as one possibility: \"it could be a medieval secret society that knows how to levitate and they put these metal capsules around them.\" If true, the source would be a hidden human group, not visitors from elsewhere.","hal-puthoff-starts-the-government-s-psychic-spy-program-at-s-1970":"In the 1970s, Hal Puthoff, a laser physicist, is said to have started the US government's psychic spy program at the Stanford Research Institute (SRI), a research centre in California. Michels says this himself in several episodes. In one he says Puthoff ran it for the CIA, crediting Joe Rogan, a comedian and podcaster. In another he calls Puthoff the \"founding architect of Stargate\", the name of the program. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels that Puthoff is \"a good friend and colleague\" who has spoken to him at length about Stargate. Gallaudet runs the program through to 1995 and brings up Paul Smith, Ingo Swann and Joe McMoneagle as part of the story.","hal-saflieni-hypogeum-oracle-room-tuned-to-111-hz":"A man named Jay Anderson says the Oracle Room of the Hal Saflieni Hypogeum, an ancient underground chamber complex in Malta, is \"precision tuned to 111 hertz\", meaning its sound resonates at that frequency. He tells it as someone who has been there, and if true it would mean its ancient builders shaped the stone for sound on purpose.","hal-warns-of-33-million-volts-2007":"One of the objects seen in North Carolina carried around 33 million volts, and \"they\" knew it, according to a man named Hal Pavvenmeer, as Chris Bledsoe tells it. Bledsoe, who says he draws UFOs to him, recalls Hal telling him this around 2007, without saying who \"they\" were or how they measured it. If true, it would mean someone had measured the energy of these things and found it was dangerous.","haldi-as-celestial-intelligence-rather-than-ancient-aliens":"The idea is that Haldi, a god worshipped long ago around Lake Van in eastern Turkey, was not a visiting alien, as the \"ancient aliens\" view of old gods would have it, but some kind of intelligence from the heavens. A researcher named Matt LaCroix offers this as his own reading on the show, without naming a source, saying the usual view \"has been a bit skewed\" and Haldi \"seems more like some kind of celestial intelligence.\"","haldi-relief-read-as-a-spearhead-sites-called-fortresses-kal":"Near Lake Van in Turkey, a carving of a figure called Haldi holds something that archaeologists 'believe firmly' is a spearhead, and the ancient sites there are named kalesi, or fortresses, as if the people were warlike. Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient sites, tells Michels this without naming a source, and he questions that warlike reading.","half-of-a-ready-room-says-they-saw-the-cubes-and-spheres-aft":"After a New York Times article about pilots seeing strange objects, Ryan Graves, a US fighter pilot, says he asked about it in a ready room, the room where a squadron's pilots gather before flights, in a different area from the East Coast. He tells Michels that about half the room raised their hands and said, in his words, \"oh yeah, the cubes and spheres, we saw those.\" If true, it would mean these cube- and sphere-shaped objects were seen far more widely than one squadron.","hall-confirms-tall-white-restroom-visits-at-the-nellis-weath-1965":"Charles Hall, who says he spent three years among tall, pale beings he calls the Tall Whites near Area 51, says that in 1965 he confirmed for himself that these beings used the restroom in the base operations building at Nellis Air Force Base in Nevada, where the weather station was. Someone had told him so, and Hall tells Michels he knows \"from firsthand experience that he was telling the truth\" because a day came when his own work took him there, which, if true, would put the beings inside an ordinary base building.","hall-photon-theory-copyrighted-1997-and-inspired-by-faster-t":"A man named Charles Hall, who says he spent three years among tall white beings near Indian Springs, Nevada, says he worked out a physics idea he calls photon theory from watching their craft travel faster than light, and first copyrighted it in 1997. Hall tells this about his own work, citing a man named Emil Marzac, and points to his books Beyond Relativity and Mirage Galaxies and Edges in Space. If he is right, alien craft he says he saw would have shown a way past Einstein's speed limit.","hall-s-books-presented-to-pope-benedict-who-sent-a-thank-you-2005":"Charles Hall says he spent three years living alongside tall white aliens at Area 51, the secret Air Force base in Nevada, and he wrote books about it. His wife tells Michels that around 2005 she took the first three books to Rome as a presentation for Pope Benedict, and that the Pope sent back a thank-you note, which is reproduced in Hall's fourth book.","hall-s-orders-said-he-could-never-be-briefed-or-debriefed-1965":"Charles Hall, a man who says he served at Indian Springs, near Nellis Air Force Base in Nevada, from 1965 to 1967 and lived alongside tall white aliens there, says his orders meant he could never be briefed or debriefed. Those are the formal sessions that let someone into a secret programme and later bind them to silence when they leave. He tells Michels \"the reason is very simple\" and ties it to a being he calls the Teacher. If true, his work sat outside the normal rules of military secrecy.","halloween-s-origins-linked-to-the-fall-taurid-meteor-shower":"Halloween has ancient roots in the Taurid meteor shower, a shower of shooting stars that comes every autumn, according to a researcher named Randall Carlson. He raises the link with Michels without naming a source, and it would mean the holiday preserves an old memory of danger from the sky.","halls-of-amenti-ancient-library-under-the-sphinx":"Under the Great Sphinx at Giza, the story goes, lies a hidden chamber holding an ancient library, a hall of records. A guest named Matt LaCroix tells Michels that this comes from Edgar Cayce \"and others\", who said such a library was \"supposed to be underneath the Great Sphinx\". A researcher named Luke Caverns tells it as Cayce's own vision, which he dates to the 1940s, of \"this chamber beneath the Sphinx\" that \"tells the story of Atlantis\", the lost civilisation of Plato's dialogues. If it were there, it would hold the written record of a civilisation older than the one history knows.","halt-hears-radio-reports-of-beams-into-weapons-storage-area-1980":"In 1980, an officer named Charles Halt heard over his radio that security police at the weapons storage area of RAF Bentwaters, an air base in England, were reporting an object 'hovering above the weapon storage area sending down beams of light'. The researcher Robert Hastings, who studies UFO reports at nuclear bases, tells Michels this as Halt's account. If true, something unknown was taking an interest in where the weapons were kept.","halt-memo-on-rendlesham-lights-1980":"In 1980, at RAF Bentwaters, an air base in England, a security patrol is said to have seen strange lights coming down into nearby Rendlesham Forest, and Lieutenant Colonel Charles Halt wrote the whole incident up in a memo to the UK Ministry of Defence. Michels tells this himself, while setting up an episode with Robert Hastings, a researcher of UFOs seen near nuclear bases. He gives no wording from the memo, only that it described the patrol's sighting. If it holds, it would be a senior officer putting a UFO sighting at a military base on paper.","halton-arp-s-interacting-galaxies-with-different-redshifts-1960":"A famous astronomer named Halton Arp is said to have found pairs of galaxies that looked as if they were swapping gas, yet had completely different redshifts, the shift in their light that astronomers use to judge distance. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel tells Michels about Arp's work without naming a source. If the pairs really touch, redshift would not always tell how far away something is, which would shake a basic tool of astronomy.","hameroff-s-view-of-microtubules-and-anesthesia-as-the-switch-1990":"Michels says that an anesthesiologist named Stuart Hameroff noticed that anesthesia shuts off only the microtubules, tiny tube-shaped structures inside cells, while the rest of the brain and body keep working normally, and yet the person loses consciousness. Michels tells this twice, in two talks with the writer Eric Wargo, and ties it to the physicist Roger Penrose and Penrose's book The Emperor's New Mind. If it holds, consciousness would hang on these small structures rather than on the brain as a whole.","hamlet-s-mill-on-ancient-knowledge-of-precession-1960":"Hamlet's Mill, a book written in the 1960s by Giorgio de Santillana and Hertha von Dechend, argues that ancient myths carry \"very ancient, very early knowledge of precession,\" the slow wobble of the Earth that shifts the stars' positions over thousands of years. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels about the book in his own summary, without quoting it. If its case holds, early peoples tracked the sky far more precisely than usually believed.","hancock-s-ayahuasca-alien-abduction-vision-2003":"The writer Graham Hancock says that in 2003, after drinking ayahuasca, an Amazonian hallucinogenic brew, outside a shaman's hut in the Amazon, he saw \"not one but several flying saucers\" above him and the familiar alien face, \"heart-shaped,\" with \"large dark eyes,\" in what felt like an abduction. He tells Michels this first-hand. He raises it because it echoes reports of alien abduction, suggesting such encounters may touch the same reality that these plants open.","hancock-the-great-pyramid-was-not-a-tomb-but-a-portal":"The pyramids of Giza were not tombs but something like a portal or 'stargate', a kind of chamber for rising up to the heavens, according to what the author Graham Hancock told Michels. Michels passes this on across several episodes, saying that when he asked Hancock point blank what they were for, Hancock almost implied a stargate, and in one telling he ties it to the pyramids' alignment with the stars of Orion's belt and to the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text, in the translation by R. H. Charles published in 1917. If true, the pyramids would have been built as a doorway to the sky rather than a burial place.","hancockdibble-debate-and-the-shipwreck-data-2024":"In a 2024 debate, the writer Graham Hancock, who argues that an advanced civilisation was lost at the end of the ice age, faced the archaeologist Flint Dibble. Michels says that anyone who fact-checks Dibble afterwards will find he got \"all his shipwreck data completely wrong\". Michels says this himself, without saying what the data was or who did the checking, and uses it to suggest that Hancock's critics are less solid than they seem.","handoff-to-the-next-contact-by-a-call-on-the-dorm-phone-1990":"An Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that in the early 1990s, at his first base after training, he got a call on the dorm phone from Captain White, his first contact officer. White gave him the name of his next contact and told him he would meet that person at a set time, and that the man \"would have his arms folded\". Sherman tells it twice, and in one version the base is overseas in a warm climate where he was also inducted into the work, which would mean a secret programme passed him quietly from handler to handler.","hannah-mcroberts-flying-saucer-photo-1981":"On October 8, 1981, Hannah McRoberts was driving with her husband on Vancouver Island in Canada when she stopped by a well-known mountain and took a photo that shows a flying saucer. A UFO researcher and archivist named Clas Svahn tells Michels the story as McRoberts gave it. If the photo is what it seems, it caught a craft in the sky over a familiar landscape.","hans-kammler-comes-to-the-us-1945":"Hans Kammler, the Nazi officer in charge of the most secret of Hitler's \"wonder weapon\" projects, made his way to the United States after the war, Michels says. He says \"we now know\" it but names no source, in an episode about a supposed new secret base in Utah. If true, it would mean the man behind Germany's most hidden weapons work ended up serving America.","hans-kammler-s-skoda-works-saucer-projects-1942":"From 1942 to 1945, an SS officer named Hans Kammler ran a secret Nazi weapons unit at the Skoda Works, in places such as Breslau and Pilsen, which Michels likens to the German version of Skunk Works, the American aircraft company's famous secret design shop, and ties to stories of German flying-saucer projects. Michels tells this himself on two episodes, citing a book on Kammler by Tom Agoston, Air Force documents, and the journalist Nick Cook's book The Hunt for Zero Point. In one he calls it \"real documented evidence\"; in the other he says only that Kammler \"probably\" ran it.","hapgood-s-earth-crustal-displacement":"The North Pole once sat where Hudson Bay is today, according to Charles Hapgood, a writer who argued that the Earth's outer crust can slide as a whole and move the poles. Jason Jorjani, a researcher, tells Michels this as Hapgood's idea. If true, the map of the ancient world would have looked very different.","harassment-of-ufo-whistleblowers-2010":"A guest named Diana Pasulka says she saw people she calls \"quote unquote whistleblowers\", those who come forward with claims about secret government UFO work, being harassed. She tells Michels this as something she \"witnessed firsthand\", which, if true, would mean people who speak out on the subject are being pressured to stop.","hard-drives-recovered-from-a-glacier-lake-in-the-sierras":"Hard drives were found sealed in a steel container about 25 feet under the water of a glacier lake high in the Sierra Nevada, according to Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he took part in recovering a UFO. He tells Michels his team first found a person, a \"human asset\" (an informant), who then led them to the lake. In a later telling he says the drives came from Toughbooks, a rugged kind of laptop, and credits a man named Don Paul Bales, which if true would mean someone had hidden data on purpose in a very hard-to-reach place.","hard-object-found-in-nostril-during-2001-nasal-surgery":"Richard Barth, a former guard at a nuclear missile site who says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels that in 2001 he had surgery for a deviated septum and a hard object was found in his nostril. He tells it from his own life, as a man who had been having breathing problems. Told alongside his abduction story, it suggests the object could be something placed in him.","harold-malmgren-nhi-track-the-frontier-of-human-innovation-1960":"Non-human intelligences, beings said to be behind UFOs, operate by tracking the frontier of human innovation, according to a presidential adviser named Harold Malmgren, who worked directly with Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Ford and says he was briefed on UFOs. Michels says Malmgren told him this himself. If true, it would mean these beings watch wherever human science is pushing forward.","harold-malmgren-s-cia-connections-and-ida-cover-1960":"Michels argues, as his own reading, that Harold Malmgren, a former Washington official he interviewed, had quiet ties to the CIA from around 1960, and that his work for the Institute for Defense Analyses, a research group that works for the Pentagon, served as cover. Among his \"fun facts\": Malmgren told him he was best friends with Tom Farmer, who \"happened to be the GC, the general counsel of the CIA,\" and Malmgren brought the walking stick of the financier Paul Mellon to the interview, which Michels held for half of it; Michels also points to Malmgren's diaries. If he is right, Malmgren stood much closer to the intelligence world than his public career suggests.","harold-malmgren-s-deathbed-account-of-naval-intelligence":"Harold Malmgren, a former US government official, is said to have recounted near the end of his life being warned that Naval Intelligence would come asking about his research, and that if impressed they would offer him special access, meaning entry into a restricted classified program. Michels relays the warning: \"if they talk to you for any length of time, they'll say, 'Holy, you really know a lot.' They're going to offer you special access.\" If true, it would mean the Navy quietly recruits outsiders who learn too much.","harold-malmgren-tweets-about-bissell-briefings-and-tesla-bro-1960":"Harold Malmgren, an adviser to Presidents Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Ford, has been tweeting about things he learned in that role, including briefings from the CIA official Richard Bissell. Michels brings this up himself, describing the tweets rather than quoting them, with the UFO disclosure activist Steven Greer, and it would mean a man close to four presidents is publicly confirming he was told of secret UFO matters.","harrington-aliens-are-real-and-he-has-seen-them-1981":"In 1981, John S. Herrington, an official who later became Secretary of Energy, said at his home: \"I can tell you this. Aliens are real. They're here and I've seen them.\" Brent Friedman says he heard it himself and tells the story on two episodes. In one he places the home in Virginia; in the other he says it was in the Washington DC area, that Herrington said it on the veranda after \"a couple drinks\", and he ties it to a briefing in West Virginia. If true, it would mean a senior government official privately confirmed having seen aliens.","harrington-briefed-for-months-in-underground-west-virginia-f-1981":"A former US government official named John S. Herrington is said to have been briefed for months in an underground facility in West Virginia, around 1981. Brent Friedman, a man who knew him, tells Michels what Herrington told him: \"I was briefed for months in an underground facility in West Virginia.\" In a later episode Friedman adds that when he asked about it, Herrington said seeing the aliens came only around \"day three\" of the briefing, which, if true, would mean a senior official was shown alien beings in a secret government setting.","harrington-denies-brent-friedman-s-account-1981":"John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy, flatly denies a story the Hollywood man Brent Friedman tells about him, set in 1981 in Walnut Creek, California and Washington. In Friedman's version, Herrington told him he had been \"locked away for a month\" and had \"seen people from another planet\". Herrington tells Michels that this is \"all a lie\" and \"just total BS\", so the one man who could confirm the story says it never happened.","harrington-has-seen-lasers-that-could-bring-down-almost-anyt":"A guest presented on the show as US Secretary of Energy, John S. Herrington, says he has \"seen laser technology that could bring down almost anything\". He tells Michels this from his own experience, on an episode where he speaks about UFOs, suggesting the government has weapons able to stop even very advanced craft.","harrington-s-buck-slip-congratulating-brent-1996":"A screenwriter named Brent Friedman says that in 1996, at the Los Angeles office of Dark Skies, a TV series about a hidden UFO story, he received a buck slip, a small office note, marked \"from the desk of John Harrington.\" It read: \"Hey Brent, you finally got your story out there. Congratulations.\" The sender was John S. Herrington, a former government official. Friedman tells Michels he received it himself, and on one telling places it in the week before a work of his called Sound, Light and Frequency premiered. Coming from an official, the note would suggest Friedman's fiction was closer to something real than it looked.","harrington-s-contact-with-david-grusch-and-his-view-of-grusc-2023":"A former US Secretary of Energy named John S. Herrington tells Michels first-hand that David Grusch, a whistleblower who testified before Congress in 2023 about UFOs, wrote him \"a beautiful letter,\" a long two-page letter. Herrington brings it up while discussing Grusch's testimony, which suggests the whistleblower reached out to a former cabinet official.","harrington-s-fukushima-delegation-visit-2011":"John S. Herrington, who served as US Secretary of Energy, says he went to Fukushima, Japan, in 2011 with a delegation, after the nuclear disaster there. He tells Michels he was with the man who led the delegation, and that \"Fukushima did not kill anybody.\" His point is that the radiation itself was less deadly than people believe.","harrington-s-global-economy-reason-for-secrecy-1981":"A former US Secretary of Energy named John S. Herrington gave a reason for keeping UFOs secret: disclosure would upset the global economy by \"taking away the reason for people to live their lives,\" so that \"everything falls apart\" and governments could no longer control their people. A man named Brent Friedman says Herrington told him this, and repeats it to Michels. If true, it would mean secrecy was kept to protect social and economic order rather than military advantage.","harrington-tours-the-greenbrier-bunker-1986":"John S. Herrington, who served as US Secretary of Energy, says that while he was in office, around 1986 or 1987, he toured the secret bunker at the Greenbrier, a resort in West Virginia, while he was there to play golf. He tells Michels this first-hand, saying \"I went and toured that thing.\" It shows that a cabinet member saw a hidden government site few people ever entered.","harry-reid-s-plan-for-a-billion-dollar-aawsap-2008":"Senator Harry Reid of Nevada meant a Pentagon UFO study, begun in 2008 and known as AAWSAP, to grow into something like a new Manhattan Project, according to the physicist Eric W. Davis, who says he worked on it. Davis tells Michels the first funding was 'just to get it started.' Reid's plan, he says, was for the work to run for maybe a decade in later budget years and cost a billion dollars, 'maybe $2 billion.' If so, the effort was meant to be far bigger than the small study the public later heard about.","harry-reid-s-request-for-sap-status-denied-by-william-lynn-2009":"In 2009, Senator Harry Reid asked William Lynn, then deputy secretary of defense, to make the Pentagon UFO study Eric W. Davis worked on a special access program, a tightly restricted classified program, and Lynn said no. Davis, a physicist who says he was part of the effort, tells Michels about the refusal and points to Reid's letter to Lynn. If true, it shows the study was refused the protected status its backers wanted.","harry-reid-s-special-access-program-request-denied-by-willia-2009":"Around 2009, Senator Harry Reid tried to get the Pentagon's UFO work made a special access program, a classified project that only a short list of cleared people may know about. He failed because Deputy Secretary of Defense William Lynn turned him down, according to a physicist named Eric W. Davis. Davis tells it on Michels's show to show that even a powerful senator could not win that level of protection for the effort.","harry-reid-the-ufo-information-is-already-there":"Harry Reid, the former Senate leader, is said to have told a journalist that the US government does not need to start studying UFOs, because the information already exists. The writer Peter Levenda passes this on to Michels second-hand. In his telling, a journalist asked Reid where such a study should begin, and Reid answered, \"What are you talking about? It's all already there.\" If that is true, it would mean the government is already sitting on what it knows about UFOs.","harry-rositzke-pulled-out-of-retirement-to-run-an-unofficial-1970":"A man named Harry Rositzke was pulled out of retirement to help run an unofficial operation against the Russians, the Chinese and others, says a researcher named Greg Bishop. Bishop tells Michels this without naming a source, placing it in the 1970s or early 80s, and it would mean spying work was being done outside official channels.","harry-turner-investigates-uap-sightings-at-maralinga-1950":"In the 1950s or 60s, a man named Harry Turner was invited to investigate several sightings of UAP, the official term for unidentified objects in the sky, at Maralinga in Australia. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this without naming a source, citing the researcher Bill Chalker. If true, officials at the site took the sightings seriously enough to bring someone in to look into them.","harry-turner-s-1971-australian-intelligence-document-on-us-u-1952":"In 1971, the story goes, Harry Turner, the head of the nuclear division of Australia's Joint Intelligence Organisation (Michels calls it \"basically Australia's CIA\"), wrote a report saying the United States had been quietly studying UFOs and trying to control gravity since the 1950s. Michels returns to it in many episodes and often notes that David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who told Congress the US holds crashed craft, cites it a lot. As Michels tells it, the report quotes the US decision \"to allocate considerable funds to investigate gravity and a means of controlling gravity.\" It names Oppenheimer and Edward Teller as working on that goal, alongside physicists such as Freeman Dyson and John Wheeler. It also places the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, and a privately funded Institute of Field Physics run there by Agnew Bahnson, among the CIA's academic outposts. Michels adds that it talks about the recovery of crashed craft and treats Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, as a public relations front. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells it too, but dates the paper 1972 and says it describes a US team that recovered crashed UFOs. The mathematician Eric Weinstein brings it up as well, adding the physicists Arnowitt and Deser. If it says what they say, a foreign ally put a hidden American anti-gravity effort on paper.","harry-turner-ufo-obsessed-intelligence-chief-over-woomera-nu-1950":"In the 1950s, the head of the nuclear division of Australia's Joint Intelligence Organisation, a man named Harry Turner, was obsessed with UFOs, at a time when weapons were being tested at the Woomera test range in the Australian outback. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as an example of UFO interest reaching into the people overseeing nuclear tests.","harvard-neuroscientist-s-telepathic-kids-experiments":"Michels says he recently interviewed a Harvard neuroscientist, whom he does not name, who is studying children said to be telepathic, able to read minds. He brings it up himself, from his own interview, while talking with a guest named Dave Rossi, who says aliens taught him advanced physics.","harvard-tried-to-get-rid-of-john-mack-who-went-to-court":"Harvard is said to have tried to push out John Mack, a Harvard psychiatry professor who took the subject of alien encounters seriously, and Mack fought back. Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, tells Michels that Harvard tried to get rid of \"that poor Harvard Professor\" and that Mack \"won a law case\" after he \"had to go to court.\" The author Whitley Strieber, who has written of his own encounters, repeats what Mack told him: he was invited to a meeting that was \"actually an ambush,\" where they meant to \"take away my license to practice psychiatry.\" Danny Sheehan, a lawyer, says he was part of it himself: when Mack \"got confronted by this faculty committee\" he called Sheehan and asked him to represent him. If true, it would show a top university punishing a scholar for studying the subject. The three agree that Harvard moved against Mack, but differ on the form it took: a court case, an ambush meeting over his licence, or a faculty committee.","haseltine-s-novel-out-of-time-hiding-truths":"Michels suggests that a novel by Eric Haseltine, a former director of research at the National Security Agency who has been a guest on his show, may hold real truths dressed up as fiction. He calls it a fiction book with audible air quotes and says he thinks it was called Out of Time. Michels says this himself, without saying what the book reveals. If he is right, a former intelligence insider would be using a novel to say what he could not say openly.","hastings-confessions-his-own-abduction-experiences-2008":"Robert Hastings, a researcher known for gathering stories of UFOs over US nuclear weapons sites, says he has himself been taken by aliens. Michels says that after his book UFOs and Nukes, Hastings wrote another called Confessions, about his own personal abduction experiences. Hastings says so in his own words in an article dated 16 November 2025: 'as an abductee myself, who has repeatedly experienced the physical reality of these amazing encounters, I am admittedly biased.' In that passage he is writing about his hope that humanity as a whole will come to accept these encounters. It matters to the tellers because a leading collector of military witnesses counts himself among the witnesses. The two tellers are Michels, describing the book, and Hastings, speaking for himself in print.","hastings-did-not-systematically-ask-witnesses-whether-they-s":"Robert Hastings is a researcher who has gathered accounts from former military men who saw UFOs at nuclear weapons sites. Michels says he once asked Hastings whether he had gone back to all the witnesses who saw only craft and asked each of them whether they had also seen beings, and Hastings told him no. Michels raises it to suggest that encounters with beings may be more common among these witnesses than the counts show, since nobody asked.","hastings-s-nuclear-site-ufo-whistleblowers":"More than 167 people who worked at nuclear sites across the United States have come forward to describe UFOs there, according to the collection of a researcher named Robert Hastings. Michels says this himself on the show, citing Hastings's data: \"167 plus whistleblowers who are at nuclear sites all over the country.\" If true, it would suggest UFOs take a special interest in nuclear weapons.","hastings-s-phone-calls-followed-by-heavy-breathing":"Robert Hastings, a researcher who studies UFO reports at nuclear weapons bases, tells Michels that after each of a run of phone calls he had with people, the phone would ring again within one to ten minutes of his hanging up, and when he answered there was nothing on the line but heavy breathing. He says this happened mostly in February 1982. He tells it as something that happened to him, and he gives it as a sign that his calls were being listened to.","hatshepsut-s-obelisk-inscription-dedicating-it-to-her-father":"The ancient Egyptian ruler Hatshepsut, according to the researcher Luke Caverns, had an obelisk at Karnak inscribed to say it was dedicated to her father, the god Amun, and made with electrum, a mix of gold and silver. Caverns tells Michels he has read the translated hieroglyphs and gives the inscription from memory, saying he is paraphrasing: \"as for this obelisk, I erected this from electrum.\" He offers it as a clue that the old monuments are not what we think, though his wording is his own summary, not the inscription's exact text.","haughton-mars-project-simulation-and-chris-mckay-lecture-2001":"In 2001, at Haughton Crater on Devon Island, a group of scientists rehearsed a Mars mission on the ground. The robotics scientist John Blitch took part and tells Michels about it: \"we essentially played like we were Mars astronauts and my role was the robot guy.\" He names others who were involved, Chris McKay (who gave a lecture there), Pascal Lee, Bob Zubrin, Darlene Lim and the filmmaker James Cameron, and tells it as part of how he came to think about humans and machines in strange places.","havana-syndrome-as-pulsed-rf-directed-energy":"Havana syndrome, the unexplained illness reported by US diplomats and officials, is said to be caused by a beamed weapon firing short, powerful pulses of microwave radiation. A guest named Chris Gilbert says on the show that \"what we think it is\" is a \"radio frequency microwave or directed energy\" weapon using a \"repetitive pulse train, very short, very high peak power radiation.\" Michels makes the same point himself on a later episode, placing it in Havana from 2016 and saying the person is \"wholly destabilized by microwaves essentially.\" If true, it would mean such weapons are already in use against people.","havana-syndrome-as-russian-attacks-since-the-1980s-per-60-mi":"The claim is that Havana syndrome comes from Russian attacks on Americans going back to the 1980s. Havana syndrome is the name for sudden, unexplained headaches, dizziness and brain injuries reported by US diplomats and spies. Michels says a recent 60 Minutes piece, made with the German magazine Der Spiegel, laid this out. A former senior NSA official named Eric Haseltine refers to the same 60 Minutes interview: \"if it's the Russians doing it to us, that's an act of war.\" In a later episode Michels tells it in his own words, crediting the CIA and the NSA. There he says \"Russia and microwave weaponry\" are behind it, puts it in the last couple of years and leaves out the 1980s. All three tellings rest on the news report rather than on anything the tellers saw. If true, a foreign power would have been quietly harming American officials for decades.","have-blue-prototype-owned-by-lockheed-skunk-works-despite-ai-1970":"In the 1970s, the story goes, the early designs of Have Blue, a secret experimental aircraft programme, did not belong to the US Air Force even though the plane carried Air Force markings. Instead they belonged to Lockheed's Skunk Works, the company's secret aircraft design shop. Greg Rogers, a former NASA doctor, tells Michels this as something a person he knew passed on to him. If true, it would mean a private company, not the military, owned what looked like a military aircraft.","hawara-labyrinth-and-the-2008-mataha-expedition-gpr-scan":"Under the Hawara labyrinth in Egypt lies a system of underground chambers: a 2008 expedition called Mataha scanned the site with ground-penetrating radar and found that a huge limestone slab there is not a floor but the roof of those chambers. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher of the pyramids, tells Michels this, saying he thinks the year was 2008, and draws on the work of Ben van Kerkwijk. If true, a large hidden complex would still be waiting to be opened.","hawass-1992-speech-on-subterranean-labyrinths-under-the-plat":"In 1992, at a university in Cairo, the Egyptian archaeologist Zahi Hawass gave a speech saying there are underground labyrinths beneath the Giza plateau, according to a guest named Jay Anderson. Anderson tells Michels this without naming a source, and it would mean Egypt's own top archaeologist once spoke openly about hidden tunnels under the pyramids.","hawkeye-360-searching-for-1-6-ghz-at-interesting-places":"A researcher named Bob Maguire tells Michels he is \"certain\" that Hawkeye 360, a company that picks up radio signals from orbit, has looked for signals at 1.6 GHz, a particular radio frequency, at what he calls \"interesting places.\" He gives no source and says nothing more about which places or what was found. If he is right, a private company would have been quietly scanning for that one frequency at sites that matter to the subject.","hawksbill-creek-agreement-and-devco-1950":"A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels that something happened in the Bahamas sometime between the 1950s and the late 1960s. He says a group of people made a deal there that was \"called the Hawksbill Creek agreement.\" The title also pairs the deal with something called Devco, and Simosa gives no source and says little more about either one.","headmaster-bans-louis-braille-s-books-at-the-first-school-fo":"At the first school for the blind in France, a new headmaster is said to have called Louis Braille's raised-dot books pseudoscience and locked them all away. The documentary maker Ky Dickens tells this from the history of Braille, without naming a source. She uses it as an example of a real breakthrough being dismissed as \"not real\" by the people in charge.","heat-exchanger-and-extraction-shaft-hypothesis-for-the-great":"The theory is that the Great Pyramid at Giza worked as a machine, with a heat exchanger running around its upper room, the King's Chamber, and above its long sloping passage, the Grand Gallery, plus an extraction shaft leading out of the lower Queen's Chamber. A researcher named Jeffrey Drum describes this on the show, drawing on work by a researcher named Filippo Biondi. If true, the pyramid would be an energy device rather than a tomb.","heavyweight-champion-on-cia-operations-in-lebanon-1967":"In the late 1960s, a heavyweight champion named Andre Pinacio spoke at the Brotherhood Synagogue in Greenwich Village, New York, about running operations for the CIA in Lebanon. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this as something he heard in person, sitting in the audience: 'He's talking to us about running operations for the CIA in Lebanon.'","hedge-fund-recruits-memory-champion-nelson-dellis-to-remote-2022":"A memory champion named Nelson Dellis was approached around 2022 or 2023 by a hedge fund, through a Facebook group for memory enthusiasts, and asked whether he would train in remote viewing, the claimed skill of perceiving distant or hidden information with the mind alone. The goal, as Dellis put it, was \"to remote view the stock market for them.\" Chris Ramsay, a guest on the show, relays this as what Dellis told him, a sign, if true, that money people take such abilities seriously.","hegel-s-philosophy-of-mind-on-the-paranormal":"The German philosopher Hegel wrote a book, Philosophy of Mind, about the psyche that contains long passages on the paranormal. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, drawing on the scholar Glenn Alexander Magee's book Hegel and the Hermetic Tradition, as a sign that a great mainstream thinker took such things seriously.","heifer-found-dead-with-ufo-shaped-object-above-it-on-footage-2020":"A healthy two-year-old heifer turned up dead on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, with \"no exterior injuries\", and a camera caught an object above it. Michels calls it one of the most disturbing things ever to happen there and credits the account to The Secret of Skinwalker Ranch, a series about the place. The ranch's owner, Brandon Fugal, tells it as something he was part of: \"we captured on camera an object\" in a field near Homestead 1, one of the ranch's houses, and he credits a witness named Thomas Winterton and a man named Caleb Benches. If true, it would tie something in the sky to an unexplained animal death.","heim-droscher-and-hauser-s-two-forms-of-gravity":"A German physicist named Burkhard Heim, and after him two researchers Michels names as Dröscher (he says \"Rusher\") and Hauser, are said to have believed that gravity comes in two forms rather than one. Michels mentions this himself in a conversation with a guest named Dave Rossi, without naming a source or explaining the theory. If true, gravity would not be the single force physics textbooks describe.","heisenberg-s-heavy-water-program-and-deuterium-reactor-ufo-p-1940":"Many rumours about how UFOs fly put a deuterium reactor in the middle of the craft, a \"heavy water engine\". Heavy water is water made with deuterium, a heavier form of hydrogen. Michels raises this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the former Air Force officer Robert Salas, and ties it to the wartime heavy-water research of the German physicist Werner Heisenberg and to the physicist Luis Walter Alvarez. If it were true, the craft's propulsion would trace back to the nuclear research of the 1940s.","heisenberg-s-team-calculates-the-fissile-material-used-in-th-1945":"In 1945, while German atomic scientists were being held at a house in the English countryside, they were secretly overheard, and a transcript shows how quickly the physicist Werner Heisenberg and his team worked out the amount of fissile material, the nuclear fuel, in the bomb dropped on Hiroshima. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source, citing only a wiretap transcript. He brings it up to suggest the Germans knew far more about the bomb than they let on.","heisenberg-s-uranium-program-as-decoy-for-a-secret-electroma-1940":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that the Nazi uranium program run by the German physicist Werner Heisenberg in the 1940s was \"a red herring\". In his account it was a decoy that hid a secret German program to separate uranium isotopes using electromagnetic methods. He draws on the writers Plimpton Hydrick and Farrell and on a book called Critical Mass. If he is right, Germany's real wartime nuclear work was not where historians have looked.","heiser-affirms-the-watchers-and-their-giant-offspring":"The biblical scholar Michael Heiser is said to have accepted the old story of the Watchers, angels who in ancient Jewish texts came down and fathered giants with human women, including the tale of the Anakim, a race of giants in the Bible. The filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels this, pointing to Heiser's books The Unseen Realm and Reversing Hermon, and says Heiser \"did\" affirm the Anakim tale. It matters to Alberino because he claims giants are real today.","helen-mccall-s-sol-foundation-white-paper-on-uaps-and-financ-2024":"A writer named Helen McCall wrote a white paper for a group called the Sol Foundation on whether UAPs, the official term for UFOs, could be a risk to financial stability. The researcher Matthew Pines mentions it to Michels, without saying what it concludes. The question is whether official word of things in the sky that no one can explain could shake the markets.","helicopter-chases-of-about-15-uap-in-60-days-never-seen-from-2025":"James Valor, who works with Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call up UFOs, tells Michels that in 2025 he went after about 15 UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) by helicopter over roughly 60 days. He says he saw them himself and that the team would get into the helicopter and chase them, though the story as recorded says they were never seen from the air. If it is true, the objects can be found again and again but not caught up close.","helicopter-freezes-mid-air-while-trying-to-intercept-a-tic-t-2025":"In 2025, a helicopter sent after a \"tic-tac\", a white oval object shaped like the mint, is said to have frozen in mid-air. The account comes from Skywatcher, a startup that says it can draw UFOs to its site. A member of the team tells Michels they saw it happen about 7 km northwest of the site: \"Our helicopter took off, and approximately he is half a mile away stopped moving.\" If it happened as they say, the object somehow stopped a pursuing aircraft.","helicopter-harassment-after-aaro-testimony-2023":"In April 2023, Michael Herrera, a man who says he saw a huge UFO in the Indonesian jungle, testified to AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into unexplained sightings. He tells Michels that after that trip, helicopters began hovering over his house, his father's home and his business. To Herrera, it was harassment over what he had told the government.","helicopter-recovery-of-the-20-foot-egg-2014":"A former helicopter pilot named Jake Barber says he and his team used a helicopter to recover a craft about 20 feet long, shaped like an egg, from a desert site in California: \"We recovered an egg and we just call it the egg cuz that's what it was.\" Barber tells it as something he did himself. Michels later repeats it, placing it on a California test range and asserting the craft was of extraterrestrial origin, and a congressman named Eric Burlison recalls Barber describing being in the helicopter while carrying it, which would mean a secret team is physically retrieving non-human craft.","helicopters-escorting-ufos-into-mountains":"Helicopters have been seen escorting some UFOs and UAP, the government's term for unexplained flying objects, into the mountains. Michels says this himself, without naming where or when, while talking with the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch known for strange happenings, and if true it would mean someone on the ground knows about these craft and guides them somewhere.","helicopters-vanish-into-a-pixelating-mountain-door-east-of-c-2004":"In 2004, about five miles east of the town of Coyame in Mexico, helicopters are said to have flown into a mountainside that began to \"pixelate,\" dissolving \"from 3D to 2D to nothing\" as the aircraft slipped through and vanished \"from one world into another.\" Michels narrates the scene himself, drawing on a book called Sentinels of Ether and crediting a man named Jake Barber. If true, it would describe a hidden doorway in a mountain that aircraft could pass through.","hell-s-angels-chief-zapped-at-skinwalker-ranch":"A leader of the Hell's Angels biker gang is said to have been \"zapped\" by something at Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, and left paralyzed and catatonic on the spot. Michels mentions it only in passing, as one more story he credits to a man named Brandon Fugal, and gives no date and no other details. If true, it would mean whatever happens on the ranch can physically disable people.","hells-angels-member-hospitalized-for-weeks-after-the-visit-2016":"A member of the Hells Angels motorcycle club named Erik Bard is said to have checked himself into a hospital for weeks after a visit connected to Skinwalker Ranch, a property known for reports of strange events. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, tells Michels this as something Bard told him, saying only that \"he had checked himself into the hospital for weeks,\" without further detail of what happened.","helms-club-secret-consciousness-study-group-with-francis-cri":"The cognitive scientist Donald Hoffman tells Michels that he spent a lot of time with Francis Crick, the co-discoverer of the structure of DNA, in a small group at UC Irvine in California called the Helms Club. He says the group met in secret for almost 20 years to study consciousness. He was a member himself, and he gives it to show that leading scientists took the question seriously, though out of view.","hemi-sync-paper-from-the-mid-1980s-1983":"According to Michels, a CIA document from 1985 or 1986, released through the Freedom of Information Act (a US law that lets anyone request government records), is all about Hemi-Sync, a consciousness technique pioneered by the Virginia researcher Robert Monroe. Michels tells this himself and says a lot of people now cite the paper, because it would show the CIA took such consciousness work seriously. On another episode he simply calls it a Hemi-Sync paper from the mid-1980s.","hennessy-claims-cheney-visited-s4-1993":"A man named Derek Hennessy claimed he worked at S4, a secret facility in Nevada, and that sometime in the mid-1990s Dick Cheney came in to visit it. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb repeats this to Michels without naming where he heard it. If true, it would put a senior political figure inside a site said to be at the centre of the UFO secret.","henri-coanda-s-1936-saucer-patent-and-project-y-consulting":"Michels says a Romanian engineer named Henri Coanda held 'basically the first patent on a flying saucer' in 1936, and that the Nazis later took him over and he went to work for them. Michels tells this himself in two episodes, one with the writer Peter Levenda and one with the philosopher Jason Jorjani, and the two versions differ. One ties Coanda's later work to Project Y, a saucer-craft effort linked to Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base. The other dates the patent to 1937, places him in Romania and Germany, and names the Avrocar, a saucer-shaped aircraft, in place of Project Y. If true, saucer-shaped flying machines were being designed by people on Earth well before the UFO wave of the late 1940s.","henri-coanda-s-patent-and-project-y-at-wright-patterson-1936":"A Romanian scientist named Henri Coanda is said to have held a patent from 1936 on a flying saucer design, to have been forced to work under the Nazis, possibly while in Vichy France, and then to have been brought to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio. There, Michels says, Coanda worked on Project Y, an extension of the Avrocar project, an effort to make flying saucers actually fly. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as a sign that saucer-shaped craft were being built and tested by people on both sides of the war.","henry-azadehdel-s-fraudulent-south-africa-documents":"A man named Henry Azadehdel is said to have put out documents about a UFO case in South Africa that turned out to be fraudulent. The researcher Clas Svahn brings this up with Michels without naming a source. What he says about it is brief: Azadehdel \"published this,\" with a passing mention of \"silver diamonds,\" and Svahn does not explain the phrase.","henry-wallace-astors-and-duponts-fund-puharich-s-telepathy-r-1948":"In 1948, in Maine, research into telepathy is said to have found rich and powerful backers: Henry A. Wallace, along with members of the wealthy Astor and DuPont families. The researcher Andrija Puharich tells this in his own words: \"I was unexpectedly put in contact with Henry A. Wallace, vice president under Franklin Delano Roosevelt.\" If true, it would mean mind-reading research had support close to the top of American politics and money.","hereticon-founder-s-startup-to-reverse-engineer-ufos-with-qu-2022":"In 2022, at Hereticon, a Miami gathering for people with deliberately unpopular ideas, one startup founder was said to be trying to \"reverse engineer\" UFOs (work out how they are built and how they work) using quantum computing. Michels tells it himself from the event, calling the founder \"maybe one of the most heretical thinkers here\", but he does not name him or say what the work involves.","hereticon-talk-flood-myths-rooted-in-real-floods":"The flood stories told all over the world, like the one in the Bible, go back to real floods that were \"really huge\" and \"biblical in scale.\" That was the argument of a talk at Hereticon, a conference for unorthodox ideas, as the researcher Randall Carlson describes it to Michels without naming the speaker or a source. If it is true, those old myths would be memories of real disasters, not inventions.","hermann-oberth-on-non-human-intelligence-helping-build-rocke":"Hermann Oberth, a German rocket pioneer whom Michels calls the mentor of Wernher von Braun, is said to have stated publicly that beings from elsewhere helped humans build rockets. Michels raises it himself on several episodes. In one he gives Oberth's words from interviews: \"We cannot take all of the credit for our record advancements in certain scientific fields. We have been helped.\" Asked by whom, Oberth answered, \"The people of the other worlds.\" Michels also points to an audio recording of Oberth talking about UFOs, says Oberth gave a whole lecture on the subject, and in one telling credits Sekret Machines, a book series by the musician Tom DeLonge. On another episode he says von Braun himself was inspired by the beings. A guest named Jason Simosa, a researcher who also describes his own experiences, puts it more vaguely: he speaks of \"the Nazi guy\" who claimed they had a \"relationship.\" If true, it would mean the space age was helped along by non-human intelligence.","hermann-oberth-on-video-about-ufos":"Hermann Oberth, a German rocket pioneer, said on film that flying saucers are real and probably come from another star system. In one episode Michels sums up the video himself, saying Oberth thought UFOs 'should make anybody question reality.' Another episode plays Oberth's own words: 'After so many witnesses have seen the so-called flying saucers, their existence cannot longer be denied. I believe that these flying objects come from another solar system.'","hermann-oberth-s-1954-ufo-lecture-notes":"In a 1954 lecture, the German rocket pioneer Hermann Oberth is said to have argued that UFOs were real craft. The researcher Kevin Knuth tells Michels he has Oberth's own lecture notes. He says that in them Oberth \"mentions that he saw numerous radar measurements of their speeds up to 19 km per second.\" Knuth also links Oberth to Wernher von Braun. Michels describes the same lecture himself in an episode about NASA, drawing on the same notes but stressing different points. He says Oberth argued \"these UFOs had been observed since at least the 1400s\" and believed they were \"piloted by extraterrestrial visitors monitoring Earth's nuclear sites and military tech.\" If true, one of the founders of rocketry would have believed, in the 1950s, that alien craft were watching Earth.","herodotus-records-a-king-s-tomb-on-an-island-in-an-undergrou-1980":"Herodotus, the ancient Greek historian, wrote of the tomb of a great king \"on an island surrounded by a lake of water\" deep underground, and the researcher Luke Caverns ties that account to the Osiris shaft, a deep shaft cut into the ground at Giza, near the pyramids. Caverns tells Michels this as his own reading, without naming a translation, and also mentions the Egyptian archaeologist Zahi Hawass in connection with the shaft. Herodotus's words reach us here only through Caverns, and whether the tomb Herodotus described is the Osiris shaft is Caverns's reading, which the passage as he gives it neither confirms nor rules out.","herodotus-takes-part-in-the-egyptian-mystery-cults-and-then":"The Greek historian Herodotus, writing about Egypt, wondered aloud about the country's mystery cults, secret religious rites that only initiates could join, and then, while he was there, was allowed to take part in them and never wrote about them again. The researcher Luke Caverns tells Michels this, citing Herodotus but no particular passage. The point, as he tells it, is that the silence suggests Herodotus learned something he would not or could not share.","herrera-attends-greer-s-ce5-sessions":"Michael Herrera, a former Marine who says he saw a huge UFO in Indonesia, tells Michels he has gone to a few CE5 sessions himself. These are group gatherings run by the UFO activist Steven Greer, where people try to invite UFOs to appear, often through meditation. Herrera brings it up to explain what Greer's \"whole CE5 initiative is all about\": the idea that people can reach out to contact what is behind the sightings.","herrera-spoke-to-kirk-mcconnell-in-a-scif":"Michael Herrera, a man who says he saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels that he talked to a man named Kirk McConnell inside a SCIF, a sealed room in Washington used for discussing classified information. He says it briefly, as something he did himself, to show that his account was heard in a classified setting.","herrera-testifies-at-greer-s-2023-national-press-club-event":"In 2023, Michael Herrera, who says he saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle, gave his account in public at the National Press Club in Washington. He says so himself on the show: \"So in 2023 when I testified um with the National Press Club.\" The event was organised by Steven Greer, a longtime campaigner for UFO disclosure, and Herrera tells it as the moment his story went on public record.","hess-s-flight-to-britain-and-lifelong-quarantine-1941":"In 1941 Rudolf Hess, Hitler's deputy, is said to have taken a secret, unauthorised flight out of Germany and landed in a field in Britain, after which he was kept shut away for the rest of his life until his death in 1987. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells this to Michels without naming a source, and mentions Winston Churchill as part of it. He brings it up as one of the strange episodes he links to Nazi secrets.","hessdalen-expedition-in-the-early-1980s":"In the early 1980s, a big expedition went to Hessdalen, a valley in Norway known for strange lights, and managed to photograph the lights and once to catch them on radar. Clas Svahn, a Swedish UFO researcher, tells Michels this as someone who took part, saying Swedish and Norwegian researchers ran it together.","hidden-evaluation-run-during-the-q-course":"A former Special Forces soldier named Randy Anderson says the Q course, the US Army's qualifying course for Special Forces soldiers, had a hidden evaluation running inside it. He tells Michels, as someone who went through it, that \"it was an evaluation for another unit in the military above you know these units.\" If true, it would mean a more secret unit quietly picks its people from among Special Forces candidates.","hidden-eye-patterns-on-product-packaging":"The cognitive scientist Don Hoffman tells Michels that he has worked with companies to design things, such as product packaging, that hold an eye-like pattern without being obviously eye-like. He tells it from his own work, as an example of how designs can tug at what the brain notices without the viewer being aware of it.","hidden-f-117-stealth-fighter-line-in-the-doe-budget-1980":"In the 1980s, America's first radar-proof fighter jet, the F-117 stealth fighter, was being built in secret on Energy Department land in the Nevada desert, its cost tucked away in the Energy Department budget. That is what John S. Herrington, who was Secretary of Energy then, tells Michels from his own time in office. He calls it \"the F-17 fighter,\" \"our first um fighter that was undetectable by radar,\" and his story shows how a secret aircraft could be hidden inside an unrelated department's books.","hidden-ufo-side-of-operation-paperclip":"A former intelligence officer named David Grusch tells Michels that Operation Paperclip, the US effort after the Second World War to bring German scientists to America, had a hidden side that dealt with UFOs. He calls these UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, and says a lot of the work dealt with the issue \"in earnest.\" He names the German rocket engineers Wernher von Braun and Arthur Rudolph in this connection, which would mean the US got into UFO research right after the war.","hidden-weapons-bunker-under-a-fake-generator-at-saddam-s-com":"At a private compound belonging to Saddam Hussein in Iraq, a generator was a fake: it was hollow and tilted back to show steps leading down to a hidden weapons bunker 'that you wouldn't believe.' A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan tells Michels this as something he was part of finding.","hieroglyphics-reported-at-historical-ufo-crashes-1947":"Rumours say markings like hieroglyphics were seen on the craft in several famous UFO cases: Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, a claimed UFO crash; Socorro, New Mexico, in 1964, the case of a man named Lonnie Zamora; and, Michels thinks, Kecksburg, Pennsylvania, in 1965. Michels passes these on himself as rumours, without naming a source. If they were true, the craft would carry a written language.","high-altitude-nuclear-tests-burned-holes-in-the-van-allen-be-1950":"The claim is that nuclear bombs set off high in the sky during the 1950s and 60s burned holes in the Van Allen belt, a zone of charged particles held around the Earth by its magnetic field. Michels says this himself, in passing and without naming a source, in an episode about a Secretary of Energy speaking on UFOs. If true, it would mean those tests changed the space around the planet itself.","high-definition-helicopter-video-of-orbs-rising-from-the-oce":"A crew in a helicopter about 20 miles off the coast of Kuwait shot a 13-minute, high-definition, full-color video of a white orb rising out of the ocean, and halfway through a second orb comes in from the left of the frame and quickly shoots back out of it. The journalist Michael Shellenberger tells Michels about it, linking it to Immaculate Constellation, the name an anonymous report gave to a hidden program that it says collects footage of UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's term for UFOs); that report went into the record of a congressional hearing of the House Oversight Committee on 13 November 2024. In his own statement at that same hearing, though, Shellenberger says the video was described to him by \"another source\" who came forward \"since my reporting on this Immaculate Constellation last month\", and who said they found it on SIPR, a classified military computer network, so by his testimony this account comes from a separate person, not from the report.","high-level-unnamed-source-corroborates-the-sherman-story-1990":"A high-ranking source whom Michels does not name is said to have backed up many details of Dan Sherman's story; Sherman is a former Air Force intelligence man who says he served in a secret program in the 1990s. Michels says this himself on two episodes, and in one he adds that Sherman's DD214, the paper the US military issues when someone leaves service, came into it, and he credits a man he calls Dan Hog, possibly Dan Howe. If true, it would give an insider's weight to a story that otherwise rests on Sherman alone.","high-ranking-nasa-official-tells-chris-he-s-a-huge-fan-at-th-2025":"In 2025, at the Capitol Hill Club in Washington, a high-ranking NASA official walked up to Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs have appeared to him for years, gave him a big hug and said, \"Chris I'm a huge fan.\" Bledsoe tells Michels about it himself, from the time around closed-door testimony to Congress. He offers it as a sign that people at NASA take his experiences seriously.","high-school-seti-home-club-crunching-arecibo-data-1997":"The former pharmaceutical executive Martin Shkreli tells Michels that in the late 1990s he started a SETI@home club at his high school. SETI@home was a program that let home computers help sort through data from the Arecibo radio telescope in Puerto Rico, looking for signals from aliens. He says the club had about three members, who all gave their computers' screen-saver time to the search.","high-vacuum-dual-thruster-spinner-test-about-2-5-millinewton":"A pair of small thrusters mounted on a vertical spinner moved on their own inside a high vacuum chamber in Florida, pushing with a force of about 2.5 millinewtons, a very small but measurable push. Charles Buhler, a NASA engineer who ran the work, shows Michels the video and says it proves 'actual movement in vacuum'. If true, it would mean a drive that pushes without throwing anything out the back, which is how he frames it as antigravity propulsion.","hike-up-stone-mountain-with-houston-2023":"In 2023, while filming in Atlanta, a film crew hiked up Stone Mountain in Georgia with Houston, a young man who does not speak and who the film says can read minds. Ky Dickens, the filmmaker behind The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary about such children, tells Michels she was there: \"we hiked up Stone Mountain when we were in Atlanta even Dr Powell came with,\" meaning a doctor named Diane Powell, who works with the film.","hikers-on-public-land-threatened-by-armed-elite-soldiers":"Hikers walking on public parkland near Secret Mountain, outside Sedona in Arizona, say they were stopped by armed men in uniform. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels that several people privately admitted this to him, describing men with automatic weapons who moved like \"highly trained elite soldiers, probably tier one operatives\", meaning top special forces. Michels repeats the reports later, placing it in Coconino National Forest and describing men in black uniforms without insignia, carrying M-16s. The tellers raise it because guarding public land this way would hint at something hidden nearby.","hikers-photograph-diamond-shaped-craft-men-in-black-follow":"Two people out hiking are said to have seen a large diamond-shaped craft hovering and photographed it, and afterwards were followed by \"men in black\", the mysterious visitors UFO witnesses say come to them. The filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this as the witnesses told it to him. If true, it would mean someone was keeping watch on the sighting and on the people who saw it.","hikers-who-vanish-near-the-alien-bases-are-killed-or-kept-as":"Hikers who go missing on Mount Nyangani in Zimbabwe and Mount Zeil in Australia have come too close to alien bases there, and they are either killed or kept as workers. That is the claim of Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, who tells Michels: 'You see too much. You disappear.'","hill-at-hatshepsut-s-mortuary-temple-5-300-m-from-karnak-s-o":"Near the mortuary temple of the Egyptian queen Hatshepsut, a temple built for her funeral cult across the river from Luxor, there is said to be a hill sitting \"exactly 5,300 m away from the obelisks\" in the Karnak temple. Geoffrey Drumm, who studies the pyramids, tells Michels this without citing a source. He calls it \"another one of these white horse hills\" and doesn't say on the show what he means by that. If the distance is exact, it would suggest the ancient sites were laid out to a deliberate plan.","hillary-clinton-tells-kimmel-she-ll-get-to-the-bottom-of-ufo-2016":"In 2016, on Jimmy Kimmel's late-night show in Los Angeles, Kimmel asked Hillary Clinton about UFOs and she answered, \"I'm going to get to the bottom of this.\" A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells the story to Michels from memory, without naming a source, as a moment when a presidential candidate promised in public to look into the question.","hillary-s-presidential-run-timed-with-the-setting-up-of-aati-2007":"Hillary Clinton's runs for president lined up in time with the creation of AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, and with the events that led to a New York Times article about it. A former military helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, who says he retrieved a UFO, tells Michels this is \"probably no coincidence.\" His point is that the UFO issue and national politics may have been moving together.","hillenkoetter-frustrated-at-being-kept-in-the-dark-about-ros-1947":"Roscoe Hillenkoetter, an early director of the CIA, was frustrated that he was never properly told what happened at Roswell, the 1947 incident in New Mexico where some say a crashed craft was recovered. That is Michels's own reading, which he offers while talking about Hillenkoetter alongside Donald Keyhoe, a writer on UFOs. If true, it would mean that even the head of the CIA was kept outside the circle that knew about Roswell.","hillenkoetter-leaves-nicap-as-hearings-loom-1962":"In 1962 a man named Roscoe Hillenkoetter suddenly resigned from NICAP, a private civilian group that investigated UFO reports, just as his friend Donald Keyhoe, who led the group, was trying to get Congress to hold hearings on UFOs. The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels this without naming a source. He raises the timing because, if it means what he suggests, someone wanted Hillenkoetter out of the way before the hearings could happen.","himmler-s-ahnenerbe-replacing-christianity-with-nordic-rites-1930":"In the 1930s Heinrich Himmler, head of the Nazi SS, ran an organisation called the Ahnenerbe, which means \"Ancestral Heritage\", and it aimed to replace Christianity with old Nordic rites. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, and stresses that \"the Ahnenerbe was a real organization\". He raises it to show how seriously the Nazi leadership took myth and ancient religion.","historical-briefings-cut-off-in-the-early-2010s":"In the early 2010s, according to an online researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb, there was an effort to stop giving the history of secret UFO-related programmes to the new people taking them over. He tells Michels, passing on what he was told, that \"there was an initiative in the early 2010s to cut off historical briefings\" for new programme incumbents. If true, it would mean those newly in charge of such work were deliberately kept from knowing what came before.","hitchhiker-effect-at-fugal-s-home-dog-growling-at-3-a-m-2024":"In 2024 the family dog at Brandon Fugal's home began getting up around 3 a.m. to growl and bark \"at something that wasn't there, that was unseen.\" Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, tells Michels this happened in his own house and credits a woman he calls Kristen. He tells it as an example of the \"hitchhiker effect\", the idea that whatever is on the ranch can follow people home.","hitchhiker-effect-at-thomas-winterton-s-home-2016":"Strange things are said to have happened in the home of a man named Thomas Winterton after he came back from Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events. This is the \"hitchhiker effect\", the idea that whatever goes on at the ranch can follow people home. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, tells Michels this as something Winterton told him, without describing the events themselves.","hitler-and-the-magenta-crash-nazi-saucer-tests-1933":"Michels mentions reports that Adolf Hitler knew about the Magenta crash, a supposed 1933 crash of an unknown craft at Magenta in Italy, and he mentions rumours that the Nazis tested flying saucers in what is now the Czech Republic or Slovakia, in the years up to 1949. He brings both up in passing, calling one \"reports\" and the other \"rumors\", without naming a source. If true, it would mean Europe's governments were chasing such craft years before the American UFO stories began.","hitler-orders-arrest-of-occultists-and-astrologers-after-hes-1941":"The story goes that in 1941, after the flight of his deputy Rudolf Hess, Hitler ordered every occultist and astrologer in Germany arrested and sent to the camps. The writer Peter Levenda tells Michels this as a researcher summing up history, and the book Unholy Alliance is credited for it. If true, it would show how seriously the Nazi leadership took occult influence inside its own ranks.","hitler-regime-support-for-zionist-revolutionary-groups-1930":"Matthew Brown, who is presented on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, says that in the 1930s and 1940s Hitler's government lent support to what he calls \"the sort of Zionist movement\" of the time, the movement to found a Jewish state in Palestine, which Britain then ruled. He says it in passing and names no source. If true, it would mean the Nazi state backed groups that seem like its natural enemies.","hitler-s-world-war-i-poems-to-odin-and-thor-1914":"As a soldier in the First World War, Adolf Hitler is said to have written poems to the old Norse gods. When Michels asks about it, the author Peter Levenda says yes, Hitler wrote them, and they are reprinted, he thinks, in John Toland's biography of Hitler. It comes up as a sign that Hitler had a religious, pagan side long before he came to power.","hittite-remains-in-underground-cities-show-they-were-reused":"In the underground cities of Derinkuyu in Turkey, digs turned up traces of the Hittites, an ancient people of the region, and of other ancient cultures that were \"not like Ancient Ancient,\" meaning not the oldest. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this without naming a source. He takes it to mean those peoples reused the tunnels, so the cities themselves would be older than the people found in them.","hively-and-woodside-paper-on-extended-electrodynamics":"Michels points to a paper on extended electrodynamics, a proposed broadening of the standard theory of electricity and magnetism, written by a man named Lee Hively in Colorado Springs and a colleague named Woodside, who Michels believes lives in Australia. Michels brings it up himself, on an episode where Eric Weinstein questions a Pentagon scientist. He does not say what the paper finds, only that it is work he thinks bears on the physics behind unexplained craft.","hjalmar-schacht-advises-sukarno-on-a-revolutionary-fund-1950":"After the war, the German financier Hjalmar Schacht, who had been through denazification, ended up in Indonesia visiting its leader Sukarno and telling him he had to build an \"Islamic crescent\" to hold back Chinese communism, with talk of a revolutionary fund. The researcher Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of his account of how old Nazi-era figures kept shaping politics after 1945.","hmnzs-southland-followed-by-an-800-foot-uso-1980":"In the 1980s a New Zealand navy ship, the HMNZS Southland, was followed by an unidentified underwater object about 800 feet long, what researchers call a USO, an unidentified submerged object. A researcher named Kevin Knuth mentions it to Michels in passing, without naming his source. If true, it would mean something huge and unknown moving under the sea alongside a warship.","hms-blonde-orange-sphere-rises-from-the-ocean-1825":"In 1825, aboard the British ship HMS Blonde near the Cook Islands in the South Pacific, the night watch is said to have seen an orange sphere rise out of the ocean. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this from the diary of a man aboard named Andrew Bloxom, which by Dolan's reading records at 3:30 a.m. on 12 August 1825 that \"the Night Watch reported this orange spherical object rise from the ocean\" at seven degrees. Dolan offers it as evidence that such sightings are about 200 years old.","ho-chi-minh-s-letter-to-the-us-president-with-a-copy-of-a-vi-1945":"In the late 1940s the Vietnamese leader Ho Chi Minh is said to have sent an impassioned letter to the US president with a copy of Vietnam's national constitution, which was identical to America's. The remote viewer Joe McMoneagle, someone trained to describe distant things by mind alone, tells Michels this in passing, as a sign that a chance at friendship was missed.","hoagland-and-flynn-hold-joint-conferences-on-the-mars-ruins-1990":"Between about 1990 and 2003, Richard Hoagland, a writer who claims there are ruins of structures on Mars, is said to have held a series of conferences with another writer, David Flynn, about those supposed ruins. The researcher Tim Alberino mentions this to Michels without naming a source, in a conversation that also brings up the biblical scholar Michael Heiser. He tells it to show that people pushing the Mars-ruins idea were working together.","hockey-puck-devices-on-the-shins-1976":"Jim Weiner, one of a group of men who say they were taken aboard a craft while camping near Allagash, Maine, in 1976, tells Michels that when the beings came for him he was terrified. He says one of them reached up with long, thin, jointed arms holding something like a flattened hockey puck, and that they put one on the outside of each of his shins, after which he could not move his legs. He tells it as something done to him, and he tells it twice in nearly the same words.","hoffman-at-mit-studying-under-marvin-minsky-at-the-start-of":"Michels says his guest, a scientist named Hoffman, was at MIT just as research into artificial intelligence was beginning, and studied under the AI pioneer Marvin Minsky. Michels says this to Hoffman on the show, calling it amazing, and also mentions the linguist Noam Chomsky. The point is that the guest saw the start of the field up close.","hoffman-s-dmt-research-collaboration-with-gallimore-2026":"The scientist Donald Hoffman, who argues that our brains evolved to hide much of reality from us, says he is now working on research into DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug. He tells Michels so himself in 2026: \"I'm collaborating with Gallamore on on DMT,\" naming a researcher called Gallimore. For Hoffman, the drug may be a way to glimpse whatever lies beyond what we normally see.","hoffman-s-team-of-mathematicians-working-on-trace-logic-conj-2026":"In 2026, the cognitive scientist Donald Hoffman was putting together a team of mathematicians to work on conjectures that come out of his mathematical theory of consciousness, which he calls trace logic. He tells Michels the conjectures have already been published so others can work on them too. For Hoffman, proving them would help show that the reality we see is not the whole of what exists.","hofmann-grof-and-ruck-s-reported-plan-for-a-new-eleusis":"The story goes that three men wanted to create a new Eleusis, a modern version of the secret religious rites of ancient Greece: Albert Hofmann, the chemist who discovered LSD, the psychiatrist Stanislav Grof, and the scholar Carl Ruck. Michels brings it up himself in a conversation with the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore about DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, and says plainly that it may only be a rumor. If true, it would mean the founders of psychedelic research hoped to revive an ancient drug-based rite.","holloman-afb-landing-footage-that-allan-sandler-saw-1964":"Three flying saucers are said to have flown in over Holloman Air Force Base in New Mexico. Two peeled away, one landed, a door opened, and figures came out, and someone filmed it all. The filmmaker Allan Sandler tells Michels he saw that footage himself: \"it landed and a door opened and guys come out looking like they were outer space guys.\" He describes one round craft that \"wasn't that stable\" and dates it to 1971, not 1964. The UFO investigator James Fox retells it from what Sandler told him, saying \"No question about it. Three came in over the base,\" though Sandler admitted \"it might have been a training video.\" Fox also links it to the 1974 film UFOs: Past, Present and Future, to the filmmaker Robert Emenegger, to a man named Paul Shartle, and to articles by the UFO newsletter writer Coral Lorenzen. The researcher Grant Cameron gives the same three-craft story on Emenegger's word and puts it in May 1971. Eric W. Davis, a scientist who has worked for the Pentagon, adds that the craft set down on the tarmac near a hangar and that a very tall being \"of Northern European descent\" came down a gangway. He calls it an NHI, short for non-human intelligence. Davis also says the film was kept from Blue Book, the Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and from its astronomer adviser J. Allen Hynek. If it were real, the Air Force would have filmed a planned meeting with visitors on its own base.","holloman-footage-debunked-as-an-f-4-1970":"The story is that a piece of film said to show a UFO landing at Holloman Air Force Base in New Mexico turned out, on a closer look, to show an ordinary plane. The footage appears in the documentary UFOs: Past, Present and Future, released in 1974. Michels says so himself: 'there's also a little bit of footage and that footage has now been debunked as an F-4,' an American fighter jet. A narrator of a documentary played in a later episode explains how. A new high-definition DVD of the old film 'was a game changer,' he says. When he skipped to the landing footage, he 'was momentarily stunned by what that extra detail revealed.' He calls the object only a plane and does not say what kind. For those who believe a landing took place, it means one piece of claimed proof falls away, even if the wider story does not.","homeless-man-says-look-up-and-corbell-sees-a-globe-in-the-sk-2004":"A homeless man once walked up to the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, who makes documentaries about UFOs, and told him \"Look up,\" and when Corbell did, he saw a globe in the sky. Corbell tells Michels it happened to him, in 2013 by his own words, and says it is something \"people don't know\" about him. He tells it as an odd, almost guided moment in his own path into the subject.","homestead-3-that-people-refuse-to-enter-2016":"Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property famous for reports of strange events, tells Michels that the ranch has a building called Homestead 3, the oldest structure on the land. He says some people refuse to go inside it. He speaks as the owner, who has owned the place since 2016, and gives it as one sign of how the ranch affects people.","honeymoon-photos-labelled-grand-central-station-a-mandela-ef-1982":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him, kept honeymoon photos from New York in the early 1980s labelled \"Grand Central Station.\" He tells Michels the name has since changed under him: \"Google it now. It's not called that. It's called Grand Central Terminal.\" He takes this as a Mandela effect, the term for when people remember something differently from how the record now shows it, which some read as a sign that reality itself has shifted.","hooded-figures-spraying-plague-from-landed-craft":"The plague in Europe broke out only after beings landed in the fields, came out of their craft and walked around wearing hoods and carrying spray cans, and this, the claim goes, is where the image of the scythe and the grim reaper comes from. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this from the writer William Bramley's book The Gods of Eden, presenting it as what Bramley found. If true, it would mean the plague was spread on purpose by visitors from elsewhere.","hooked-on-ufos-by-chariots-of-the-gods-1969":"A NASA doctor named Greg Rogers says his interest in UFOs began in 1969, \"I believe it was,\" when he read Chariots of the Gods, Erich von Däniken's book arguing that visitors from space came to Earth in ancient times. \"I was hooked,\" he tells Michels, explaining how a man who later says he saw a UFO in a secret hangar first came to the subject.","hoover-handwritten-note-army-grabbed-recovered-disc-1947":"In 1947 the FBI director J. Edgar Hoover reportedly wrote by hand on an FBI memo that the bureau \"must insist upon full access to discs recovered,\" adding that \"the Army grabbed it and wouldn't let the bureau have it for cursory examination.\" The documentary maker James Fox reads the note to Michels and features it in his film Out of the Blue. If it means what Fox takes it to mean, the Army held a recovered disc and kept it even from the FBI.","hoover-letter-calling-condon-a-russian-spy-1949":"In 1949, FBI director J. Edgar Hoover is said to have written a letter to Congress calling the physicist Edward Condon, who later led a government study of UFOs, a Russian spy. Robert Salas, a former Air Force missile officer, brings it up with Michels from memory (\"I believe it was\" Hoover, he says) as a point about the man later put in charge of judging UFOs.","hoover-letters-deny-fbi-jurisdiction-over-ufos-1940":"Between the 1940s and the 1950s, FBI director J. Edgar Hoover is said to have written several letters stating that \"the investigation of unidentified flying objects is not and never has been within the jurisdiction of the FBI.\" Michels tells this himself and ties it to Out of the Blue, a documentary by the filmmaker James Fox. The point is that, on paper at least, the FBI kept itself out of the UFO question.","hopi-ant-people-as-a-global-subterranean-species":"There may be a species living underground all over the world, and the Hopi people of Arizona keep a legend about such beings, which they call the \"ant people.\" Michels tells this himself, in an episode about strange three-fingered figures from Peru, as the speculation of a Chilean researcher named David Velasco. If it were true, old legends from far apart would be describing the same hidden beings.","hopi-ant-people-emerging-from-underground":"The Hopi, a Native American people, have stories of \"ant people\" who came up out of the ground to help humans \"during times of great trouble and strife.\" That is how a guest named Jay Anderson tells it to Michels. He passes on the lore second-hand and mentions it as an example of tribal stories about helpers from beneath the earth.","hornig-guards-the-trinity-bomb-the-night-before-the-test-1945":"On the night before Trinity, the first atomic bomb test in New Mexico in 1945, Oppenheimer is said to have handed a young scientist named Donald Hornig a weapon and made him guard the bomb up in its tower during a storm. The physicist Kevin Knuth tells Michels this as Hornig himself told it to him. It is a small personal link to the start of the nuclear age.","hospital-reprimands-italo-for-filming-the-room-with-james-fo-2023":"In 2023, a hospital in Varginha, Brazil, banned the surgeon Italo Venturelli from filming inside after he took the documentary filmmaker James Fox into the maternity ward room where, Venturelli says, the being his hospital is said to have treated was kept. Venturelli tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and says the ban was fair: 'they just prohibited me from filming inside. And that's right.'","hospital-staff-now-say-the-room-was-locked-that-day-1996":"In 1996, in the Brazilian town of Varginha, where people say a strange creature was captured and taken to the regional hospital, staff now say a room there was locked that day. Italo Venturelli, a surgeon at the hospital, tells Michels that when he talks about it now, \"many people say that the room was really locked, that this really happened here,\" and that they remember details of what they saw. If true, it would back the claim that something unusual was hidden in the hospital.","hospital-wing-closed-because-of-the-creature-s-smell-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, where a strange creature was reportedly captured, a wing of the hospital is said to have been closed for weeks because the creature's smell was so strong. James Fox, a documentary filmmaker who has investigated the case, tells Michels this as something a person there told him. In a later episode he names the hospital as the Hospital Regional and puts the stench in the room where X-rays of the being were taken.","host-attended-the-congressional-uap-hearing-2023":"Michels says he was in the room in 2023 for the congressional hearing on UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's newer term for UFOs. At that hearing a former intelligence official turned whistleblower named David Grusch testified. Michels tells this himself, a year later, as someone who watched the testimony from inside the room: \"I was in that room last year at the testimony.\"","host-discussed-precognition-hypothesis-with-hal-puthoff":"Michels says that he talked about the idea of precognition, seeing the future, with Hal Puthoff, whom he describes as the man who ran Stargate, the CIA's program on psychic spying. He brings it up in a conversation with Eric Wargo, an author who writes about prophetic dreams, to show the idea is taken up by people who did such work for the government.","host-has-seen-jacobs-dd214-1964":"Michels says he has seen the DD214, the US military's discharge record, of Bob Jacobs, a man whose account of a 1964 Atlas rocket launch at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California is said to have been scrubbed from the records. Michels says this himself, as someone who looked at the paper: it shows, he says, that Jacobs \"definitely worked at Vandenberg.\" The point is that Jacobs was where he says he was when the launch took place.","host-s-dream-of-flea-texting-him":"Michels says that one night, out of nowhere, he dreamed that Flea, the famous bassist, was texting him. He tells it himself, as a small personal oddity, and the excerpt does not say what the texts said or what came of the dream.","host-s-father-dreamed-of-an-earthquake-on-january-17th-befor-1992":"Michels's father is said to have dreamed again and again of an earthquake on January 17th, before the Northridge earthquake struck Los Angeles on that date in 1994. Michels tells this himself, while talking with the writer Eric Wargo about prophetic dreams, and says his father writes about it in his book The Tools. If true, it would be a case of a dream foreseeing a real disaster.","host-s-frightening-month-after-visiting-skinwalker-ranch":"After a visit to Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known as a hot spot for strange happenings, Michels says he had a strange and frightening month. He tells this himself, from his own experience: \"these things they instilled fear in me.\" On a later show he tells it more lightly, saying he went at \"my highest ego time in my life\" and \"didn't have a great month afterwards.\"","host-s-mars-ufo-photo-tweet-turns-out-to-be-a-rock":"Michels once posted on Twitter a small picture that seemed to show a UFO on Mars, and the object turned out to be a rock. He tells the story on himself, in passing, as an example of how easily a photo can fool you.","host-s-own-ayahuasca-experience":"Michels says he has taken ayahuasca, a psychedelic brew from the Amazon, once, but has never taken DMT, its strong active ingredient. He mentions it himself while talking with the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore about whether DMT opens a door to other beings.","host-s-own-two-ufo-sightings-2020":"Michels says he saw two UFOs himself in 2020 and became \"obsessed\" after that. He mentions it in passing to a treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, as the reason he started digging into the subject at all.","host-s-reading-of-majestic-as-a-continuity-of-government-com-1950":"Michels suggests that \"Majestic,\" or Majestic 12, the name of a supposed secret group said to oversee UFO matters in the 1950s, may really have been one of the secret committees set up to keep the government running in a catastrophe. These are called continuity-of-government programs. He says such committees went by many names, \"5412, the 303 committee, the committee of 40,\" and he raises this in an interview with a former presidential advisor named Harold Malmgren. In a later episode Michels gives the idea to a researcher named John B. Alexander instead, who he says called Majestic 12 and the elite groups around UFOs \"almost euphemisms for continuity of government programs.\"","host-s-talk-with-former-skunk-works-head-of-revolutionary-te-2024":"Michels says that in 2024 he spoke with a man who had been head of revolutionary technology at Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret division for advanced aircraft. In another episode he adds that he thinks it is fine to say this because the man is on record believing in this kind of thing, meaning the power of mind over matter. Michels tells it from his own conversation, without naming the man, as a sign that people at the top of secret aerospace work take such ideas seriously.","host-s-trifield-meter-drained-at-skinwalker-ranch":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known as a paranormal hot spot, Michels says a trifield meter, a handheld device for measuring electric and magnetic fields, went from a full battery to dead just before his group left: \"it had full battery it's flashing it's now dead.\" He tells this himself, as something he saw, on several shows, once dating the visit to 2022. In another telling he says instead that the meter \"went off because of high gamma radiation.\"","host-sees-two-metallic-orbs-during-holotropic-breathwork":"Michels says he once saw two metallic orbs while doing holotropic breathwork, a fast-breathing technique meant to bring on altered states, with a friend in Silver Lake, California, 'of all places.' He tells it as his own experience, comparing it to the encounters of a guest who says UFOs come to him.","host-smelled-sulfur-at-skinwalker-ranch-2022":"Michels says that on a visit to Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, he smelled sulfur and thought \"I got to get the hell out of here.\" He tells it as his own experience, around 2022, to a former Pentagon official named Lue Elizondo and on other episodes, once adding a dark month that followed the visit. On a later episode he adds that a gas monitor picked up nothing and puts the smell down to a \"dragon\", which would make the smell a sign of something present there rather than ordinary gas.","hostile-telepaths-encountered-on-mars":"Anyone who travels to Mars by telepathy, the mind alone, will first meet a group of five telepaths who are \"not exactly welcoming\". The researcher Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD and studies psychic phenomena, says this to Michels without naming where it comes from.","hosting-the-mind-s-ear-on-kpfa-radio-1970":"Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of the paranormal and a longtime interviewer, tells Michels that in the 1970s KPFA, a radio station in Berkeley, California, offered him a slot every Tuesday and Thursday at noon. He says they asked him to host an interview show called The Mind's Ear. He tells it as the start of his own career talking with people about the mind and the paranormal.","houdin-s-theory-that-the-great-pyramid-was-built-from-the-in":"The French architect Jean-Pierre Houdin thinks the Great Pyramid at Giza was built from the inside out, with the blocks hauled up a ramp that spirals inside the pyramid itself. Michels brings this up himself in four episodes and calls Houdin one of the leading researchers on how the pyramid was built. Once he says the idea got new attention after ScanPyramids, a project that scanned the pyramid, found unexplained empty spaces inside it, and another time he simply says the blocks were somehow pulled up from inside. If Houdin is right, it would explain how the huge blocks were lifted.","house-sized-tall-white-deep-spacecraft-seen-in-person-1960":"In the 1960s, in Indian Springs Valley, Nevada, Charles Hall says he saw with his own eyes a deep-space craft of the \"Tall Whites\", tall pale beings he says he lived alongside for three years near Area 51, a secret military test site in Nevada. He tells Michels the craft was about the size of a house, \"a little bit longer than this house a little bit wider than this house about half again as high.\" If true, he saw an alien ship parked on American soil.","houston-answers-a-question-his-mother-asked-only-in-her-head-2020":"Houston Asher, a young man in Atlanta who does not speak, answered a question his mother had asked only in her head, without saying it out loud. His mother, Katie Asher, tells this as something she saw herself, in the documentary series The Telepathy Tapes, which Michels discusses with its maker, Ky Dickens. If true, it would mean Houston can read her thoughts.","houston-finds-crystal-dice-hidden-for-a-son-in-law-2020":"Houston Asher is a young man in Atlanta who does not speak. His mother, Katie Asher, says he once came home from school and found some crystal dice she had hidden for her son-in-law, because, she says, he could hear or feel their energy. The documentary maker Ky Dickens, whose series The Telepathy Tapes follows nonspeaking people like Houston, tells Michels the story as the mother told it, as evidence that such people sense things others cannot.","houston-finds-hidden-stones-and-senses-their-energy-2020":"A nonspeaking young man in Atlanta named Houston Asher found stones that had been hidden and seemed to sense their energy: a little later his mother found he \"had lined these three stones up on his bedside table.\" She is Katie Asher, who tells Michels about her own son. Houston was featured in The Telepathy Tapes, a podcast documentary about nonspeaking people said to read minds, and she tells it as a sign that he perceives things he was never told.","houston-names-the-hill-a-telepathic-chat-room-for-non-speake-2023":"A young man named Houston, one of a group of people who cannot speak, is said to have named \"the Hill,\" a telepathic chat room where non-speakers tune in and talk to one another with their minds. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made a documentary about these non-speakers, tells Michels this as something Houston and others, including people named Akil, John Paul and Libby, described to her in Atlanta in 2023. If true, it would mean these people share a hidden mental meeting place.","houston-says-mama-i-love-you-the-day-before-thanksgiving":"Katie Asher, the mother of Houston, a nonspeaking young man in Atlanta, says that the day before Thanksgiving her son, then 17, said \"Mama, I love you\", and that she was shocked because she had never heard him say her name. She tells it as a mother who was there, in an episode about The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary podcast about nonspeaking people who are said to read minds.","houston-sends-ky-images-at-night-about-the-film-2024":"In 2024, the filmmaker Ky Dickens says Houston, a young man who does not speak and who appears in her documentary The Telepathy Tapes, was sending her pictures in her mind at night about the film. She tells Michels that she wrote to his mother, Katie Asher, to check: \"can you ask him if he just did that,\" and that Katie asked and Houston answered, \"yes I do.\" To Dickens this was telepathy at work.","houston-spells-out-a-text-message-he-never-saw-2020":"A nonspeaking young man named Houston Asher is said to have spelled out the exact words of a text message he never saw. His mother, Katie Asher, who appears with him in The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary series about nonspeaking people said to read minds, tells Michels that a woman texted her and that Houston, who had not seen the text, spelled out what was on it. If true, it would point to something like telepathy.","how-roger-leer-began-removing-alien-implants-1990":"Roger Leir, a podiatrist in Thousand Oaks, California, got into removing \"alien implants\", objects some believe aliens place inside people's bodies, after a man named Daryl Sims tried to interest him in them at a UFO conference. Steve Colbern, a scientist who worked with Leir, tells Michels this as what he knows of Leir's story, calling him the most knowledgeable UFO researcher he ever met.","how-the-atlantis-story-passed-from-solon-to-critias":"The story of Atlantis, the lost island Plato wrote about, is said to have come from Egypt to Athens through a chain of people, one telling the next over generations. The researcher Randall Carlson lays out the chain for Michels as Solon, then Dropides, then Critias the Elder, then Critias the Younger, who told it at what he calls the Socratic forum. Plato's own dialogue Timaeus, in Benjamin Jowett's translation, gives the same order: Solon, who heard it in Egypt, was a friend of 'my great-grandfather, Dropides,' and 'he told the story to Critias, my grandfather, who remembered and repeated it to us.' The speaker adds that the old Critias was 'nearly ninety years of age, and I was about ten' when he heard it, and in the dialogue he tells it in front of Socrates. If the chain holds, the Atlantis story would be an old memory passed down by family, not something Plato made up.","howard-hughes-and-the-mormon-mafia-1950":"The billionaire Howard Hughes, the analyst Matthew Pines tells Michels, was deeply tied to the Mormon church: the senior managers and handlers who ran his business empire were mostly Mormon, a group people called \"the Mormon mafia\". Pines says this without naming a source. He brings it up as part of a wider idea that private aerospace companies and the people around them may hold hidden knowledge.","howard-hughes-brought-to-the-bahamas":"The billionaire Howard Hughes is said to have been \"suddenly\" brought to an island in the Bahamas. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source, as one link in a chain he draws between the UFO cover-up and a secret society. He doesn't spell out what Hughes's move would mean, beyond placing him on that island alongside other people and events in his account.","howard-hughes-s-undisclosed-sensitive-programs-1950":"The aviator and billionaire Howard Hughes, from the 1950s into the 1970s, was working on \"a couple other extraordinarily sensitive programs\" that have never been made public. A researcher named Dan Sheehan says this to Michels without naming a source or saying what the programs were. He tells it as part of a larger story about secret government projects of that era.","howard-hughes-visiting-george-van-tassel-at-giant-rock-1950":"In the 1950s, the story goes, the aviator and billionaire Howard Hughes used to land on George Van Tassel's private airstrip at Giant Rock in the California desert and eat his wife's pies. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without a named source, and the book Closer Encounters is credited for it. It is told to tie a famous figure in aviation to Van Tassel's desert airstrip.","howard-morland-reconstructs-the-teller-ulam-h-bomb-design-fo-1979":"In 1979 a writer named Howard Morland, working for The Progressive magazine, was given the job of working out, with no training in physics, the Teller-Ulam design, the secret method behind the hydrogen bomb. The mathematician Eric Weinstein tells this to Michels without naming a source, setting it against the Atomic Energy Acts of 1946 and 1954, the US laws that made such nuclear knowledge secret. For Weinstein, it shows that a determined outsider can rebuild what the government keeps hidden.","huge-glowing-craft-over-the-november-5-missile-site-1977":"In 1977 a huge glowing object is said to have hovered over November 5, one of the launch sites for nuclear missiles around Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota. A former Air Force missile security guard named Mario Woods, who says he was there, tells Michels the thing was so wide that \"it actually exceeded the perimeters of the fence line.\" The journalist Robert Hastings, who writes about UFOs seen at nuclear weapons sites, published Woods's account on 10 September 2017. In that article Woods says that he and his partner \"immediately saw the object hovering directly over the site.\" He describes \"a huge glowing, reddish-orange sphere\" that made no noise, only 15 to 20 feet off the ground and \"roughly the width of a Wal-Mart building.\" Its surface, he says there, was \"shimmering with the red and orange colors constantly swirling and churning.\" So the story reaches the reader twice from the same witness: once in his own voice on the show, and once in a journalist's written account years earlier. If it is true, something enormous and silent sat right on top of an American nuclear missile.","huge-unidentified-underwater-craft-prompt-usuksoviet-defense":"Very large unidentified underwater craft were spotted in the Pacific, Atlantic, Baltic and North Sea, and this pushed American, British and Soviet defense people to talk to each other, according to the president of a Russian republic. He tells Michels that \"very large unidentified underwater craft were discovered\" in the Pacific. If true, the mystery was serious enough to bring Cold War rivals together.","hugh-angleton-in-italy-during-the-1933-magenta-crash":"Michels points out that Hugh Angleton, father of the senior CIA officer James Jesus Angleton, was stationed in Italy in 1933, at the time of the Magenta crash, a reported crash of an unknown craft near the Italian town of Magenta. Michels raises this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with the UFO historian Richard Dolan, and calls it \"really crazy\". The point of telling it is the hint that the Angleton family may have been near the first known crashed-craft story.","hugh-everett-s-multiverse-theory-and-his-pentagon-work-on-nu-1950":"Hugh Everett, the physicist who came up with the \"many worlds\" theory that every possible outcome of a quantum event plays out in its own universe, later went to work for the Pentagon on game theory for the US nuclear program. Michels tells this himself on two episodes, without naming a source, and on one of them he also names Everett's physicist colleagues John Wheeler and Bryce DeWitt. He raises it because it ties one of the strangest ideas in physics to the nuclear weapons establishment.","hughes-aircraft-builds-the-glomar-explorer-for-subsea-recove-1970":"In the 1970s, the aircraft company Hughes Aircraft built the Glomar Explorer, a ship made to lift objects off the deep ocean floor. Michels mentions it in passing as an example of a private company quietly building hardware for secret government recovery work, without saying more.","human-abduction-squads-made-to-look-like-aliens-1990":"The UFO activist Steven Greer says that men came to him who told him they had flown the craft themselves and had served on human \"abduction squads\" made to look like aliens, and that he debriefed people who ran this human-run abduction program. In one telling he adds that cases from the program were fed to David Jacobs, Budd Hopkins and John Mack, researchers who studied people reporting alien abductions, and he gives no decade. If true, some alien abductions would really have been staged by people.","human-bottleneck-reset-event-70-000-years-ago":"Some people believe human history has had \"reset events\", and one example offered is a genetic bottleneck about 70,000 years ago, when, judging by our genes, the human population shrank drastically. This is raised by Jack, Michels' co-host and technical correspondent, who goes by a pseudonym and has worked for years in defense and aerospace, without naming a source.","human-civilization-as-a-cargo-cult-from-an-ancient-contact-e":"Our whole civilization, the writer Peter Levenda suggests, may be a cargo cult, like island peoples who copied the trappings of visitors' technology, left over from an ancient contact with beings from elsewhere. Levenda tells Michels this was the starting idea of the book Sekret Machines: Gods, which he wrote with Tom DeLonge: \"our entire society is a cargo cult because everything we're trying to do\" goes back to that contact.","humanoid-figure-emerges-from-fog-and-abducts-guard-at-minute-1964":"In 1964, at a site called D5 for Minuteman nuclear missiles at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California, a guard named Richard Barth says he saw a shadow in the fog and then \"a man or humanoid figure\" step out of it, and that beings took him, apologised, and explained that they \"had to have access to the site.\" Barth tells Michels this happened to him, says he felt both terror and love, and insists it was \"too vivid, too real\" for anyone to have staged. He believes the beings hypnotised or conditioned him not to tell anyone at the base, which, if true, would mean non-human beings were getting into US nuclear sites.","humans-and-aliens-working-side-by-side-at-mount-zeal":"At Mount Zeil in Australia, the claim goes, humans and aliens work side by side. Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, tells Michels he has heard this \"from people who absolutely know for sure\", without naming them. If true, it would mean a hidden joint site of humans and beings from elsewhere.","humans-as-slave-workers-in-eden":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that humans in the Garden of Eden were slave workers for \"the gods\" without knowing it. He argues that in the ancient world nakedness was a sign of slave labor, so the naked Adam and Eve were field workers. He credits the King James Bible, published in 1611, which says God put the man in the garden \"to dress it and to keep it\" and later sent him out \"to till the ground\". Whether that work was a slave's labor for gods, the text neither says nor rules out.","hume-center-funded-by-former-cia-s-t-officer-ted-hume":"The Hume Center, a research center at Virginia Tech in Blacksburg, Virginia, was paid for by a former CIA science and technology officer named Ted Hume and his wife. That is what Bob McGwier, a guest who speaks from his own knowledge of the center, tells Michels: Hume left the CIA, started a company that did well, sold it to a firm McGwier calls Kinetic, and put some of the money back into the center. If so, the center has a direct money tie to a former CIA man.","humming-three-phase-power-boxes-feeding-underground-2025":"Out at Bradshaw Ranch near Sedona, Arizona, a place with a long run of local UFO stories, a man named Paul is said to have heard a humming, kicked a bush aside and found a huge three-phase power box, the heavy kind of electrical supply used for industrial machinery, with a cable running straight into the ground. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this in 2025 as part of his own search there, passing on what Paul found. If it is true, something underground is drawing serious power in that remote spot.","hundreds-of-maltese-skulls-vanish-after-the-finder-dies":"On Malta, hundreds of strange skulls are said to have been found, after which the man who found them mysteriously died, someone from \"some Smithsonian institute\" took over, and all the skulls vanished. A guest named Jay Anderson tells Michels this as he heard it from Maltese locals. The point of the story is that unusual finds get taken away and hidden.","hundredth-monkey-effect-on-japanese-islands":"The \"hundredth monkey effect\" is the idea that once enough monkeys on one island in Japan learned a new behaviour, monkeys of the same kind on separate islands began doing it too, as if knowledge spread without contact. Randy Anderson, a guest who says he saw alien technology on a military base, brings it up with Michels, recalling it loosely: \"I think it's in Japan, they have a species of monkeys that's separated by an island.\"","hunt-s-deathbed-confession-of-a-conspiracy-to-kill-kennedy-1963":"E. Howard Hunt, a former CIA officer, is said to have claimed on his deathbed in 2007 that there was a conspiracy to kill President Kennedy in 1963 and that he knew about it. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this as something he knows, without naming a source. If true, it would mean an insider admitted a planned killing of a president.","hunters-report-total-silence-just-before-a-companion-vanishe":"Hunters in the American wilderness say that just before a companion named Charlie vanished, everything went completely silent. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this from Missing 411, accounts gathered by a researcher named David Pitus of people who disappeared without a trace in the wild, and the silence is offered as a clue that something unusual took them.","hvt-escort-of-sealed-mystery-boxes-on-classified-flights-1990":"In the 1990s, according to a helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, he escorted what the military calls high-value targets on classified flights, and most of the time those targets were sealed containers. He tells Michels from his own experience that the crews called them \"mystery boxes,\" which would mean the people moving the cargo were not told what it was.","hybrid-woman-with-skateboard-in-the-grocery-store-2024":"In 2024, in a grocery store near Santa Monica, a woman carrying a skateboard walked in just ahead of the writer Whitley Strieber, and he says he was struck by a sudden, brilliant certainty that she was \"one of them\", a hybrid of human and alien. Strieber tells Michels this as something that happened to him. If he is right, beings who are part alien are already living ordinary lives among us.","hydraulic-water-locks-used-to-float-blocks-up-into-the-pyram-2021":"The stone blocks of the Great Pyramid at Giza may have been floated up by a system of hydraulic water locks, and features seen in SAR scans, a kind of satellite radar imaging, could be what is left of it. The researcher Jeffrey Drum tells Michels this, saying other researchers have proposed the lock idea and linking it to his own 2021 video. At one point he simply calls the features possible remnants of the lock system without mentioning the radar data.","hyman-s-government-commissioned-report-criticizing-uri-gelle-1970":"Michels says a researcher named Ray Hyman had written a government-commissioned report in the 1970s that was highly critical of the famous psychic Uri Geller, two decades before the later events Michels is describing. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the writer Eric Wargo. He raises it to show that Hyman had judged psychic claims for the government long before.","hynek-1966-article-on-ufos-over-minot-missiles":"According to Michels, a 1966 article by the astronomer J. Allen Hynek said that UFOs had floated around the nuclear missiles at Minot Air Force Base in North Dakota. Hynek, Michels notes, was then running Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's official UFO investigation. Michels tells this himself while introducing an episode with Robert Hastings, a researcher of UFOs at nuclear bases, as the article that was found early on, and if it holds, it means the government's own UFO investigator put on record that UFOs had shown up at nuclear weapons.","hynek-and-vallee-s-invisible-college-and-its-rosicrucian-nam-1960":"Michels says the Invisible College, an informal circle of scientists around the astronomer J. Allen Hynek and the researcher Jacques Vallée who quietly studied UFOs, took its name as a tongue-in-cheek nod to the Invisible College of the chemist Robert Boyle, a group at the start of the Enlightenment. He links that older circle to the Rosicrucians, a secretive mystical brotherhood of early modern Europe, through a book by the historian Frances Yates on the Rosicrucian roots of the Enlightenment, which he does not name. He raises it in a talk with the scholar Diana Pasulka, as a hint that the modern UFO researchers saw themselves in a long line of hidden scholarly brotherhoods.","hynek-at-project-blue-book-opposed-then-repented":"The astronomer J. Allen Hynek, who worked with Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, is said to have first opposed the idea that UFOs were real and later taken all of it back and \"repented\" for having opposed it. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with a former nuclear missile guard who says he was abducted. It matters because it would mean a man inside the official study changed his mind.","hynek-delivers-a-prepared-computer-to-benowitz-1980":"Around 1980, the story goes, the astronomer J. Allen Hynek turned up at the Albuquerque home of a man named Paul Benowitz, who was trying to make sense of strange signals. Hynek brought a fully built computer and said, \"This will help you with the signals.\" The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels this second-hand, passing on what a UFO researcher named Bill Moore said. If true, it would mean a well-known UFO scientist was quietly feeding Benowitz's work.","hynek-gave-spielberg-alien-drawings-for-close-encounters-1967":"The astronomer J. Allen Hynek is said to have given the director Steven Spielberg a set of drawings of alien beings, made in the late 1960s and early 1970s, which Spielberg used for the special effects of his 1977 film Close Encounters of the Third Kind. The researcher Budd Hopkins says this in a clip played on the show, without naming a source. He says the drawings were \"very similar\" to the ones he was showing. If true, the aliens in the film would be modeled on witness descriptions collected before it was made.","hynek-provided-bennewitz-s-computer-doty-psyop-1980":"In the 1980s, according to Michels, the astronomer J. Allen Hynek, long the US Air Force's scientific adviser on UFOs, supplied the computer that an Albuquerque man named Paul Bennewitz used to pick up what he believed were alien signals. At the same time, Michels says, Bennewitz was the target of a psychological operation, a deliberate campaign of deception, run by an Air Force officer named Rick Doty. Michels says he had only just learned this and hedges it with \"I think\"; if true, it would put a respected scientist close to a government effort to mislead a UFO believer.","hynek-s-swamp-gas-explanation-1960":"The famous explanation that a UFO sighting in the 1960s was just \"swamp gas,\" glowing gas rising from a marsh, came from the astronomer J. Allen Hynek, who advised the Air Force on UFO reports. Mick West, a well-known UFO skeptic, says so to Michels in a debate, without naming a source. It matters because the phrase became a byword for official brush-offs of sightings.","hynek-says-he-played-a-part-in-the-blue-book-cover-up-1960":"An astronomer named J. Allen Hynek, who worked on Blue Book (the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s), is said to have later admitted that he \"played a part in the cover up\". Michels tells it himself without naming a source, describing Hynek as \"running\" Blue Book. If true, it would mean the scientist at the heart of the Air Force study knew it was hiding something.","hypnosis-with-dr-michael-o-connell-trained-by-john-mack-2007":"In 2007, in the Carolinas, Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had UFO experiences for years, says he went through hypnosis to work through what had happened to him. He tells Michels he had help from a doctor, Michael O'Connell, who is described as trained by John Mack, a Harvard doctor. If it is as he says, hypnosis helped bring his experiences to the surface.","hypnotic-regression-reveals-mental-contact-with-the-creature-1996":"Carlos de Souza says he came upon the crash site near Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, and that years later, under hypnotic regression (a hypnotist guiding someone back into a memory), at a beach in Brazil, a recording captured him recalling that he was \"desperate trying to get closer\" while the creature spoke to him in his mind: \"Calm down. Everything is fine. Go away. It's dangerous for you here.\" He tells Michels that part as it was relayed to him by someone he names as Saga, and he also says he believes it \"because I saw the tape\" himself. If true, the creature at the crash would have been able to speak mind to mind.","hypothetical-nuclear-principle-electromagnetic-power-source":"Dylan Borland, a former US intelligence specialist who went public with UFO claims, puts a hypothetical to Michels: imagine an electromagnetic power source that probably runs on nuclear principles and gives off energy, and whose load cannot be controlled. He frames it as 'hypothetically', and names people he calls Towns and Brown. If such a device exists, it would be dangerous to anyone near it.","i-81-corridor-ufo-flaps":"A guest named Dylan Borland says that a stretch of the eastern US, from Norfolk and Langley up to Winchester, along Interstate 81 to Harrisburg in Pennsylvania, and around Washington and Pax River, a Navy air base in Maryland, has had UFO \"flaps\", waves of sightings, for decades. He calls it the I-81 corridor and mentions it alongside the TV show Unsolved Mysteries and the writer Michael Shermer, without naming a firmer source. The area is thick with military bases, which is why the pattern interests him.","i-beams-with-hieroglyphics-told-at-the-reminiscences-of-rosw-1947":"After the 1947 Roswell incident, when something is said to have crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, the material was flown out and included I-beams covered in hieroglyph-like writing and various plates. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels he heard this firsthand from surviving officers and from the daughters of the local sheriff at a conference called Reminiscences of Roswell: \"they had I-beams with hieroglyphics on them and they had all these plates and stuff.\" If true, the wreckage would be of no human make.","ian-fleming-s-mi6-mission-to-evacuate-martin-bormann-1945":"At the end of the Second World War, Ian Fleming, later the author of the James Bond novels, was a British intelligence officer whose specific mission was to help the senior Nazi Martin Bormann escape to Argentina. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of his account of what became of Nazi secrets and people after the war; if true, it would mean British intelligence helped a top Nazi get away.","ian-stevenson-s-reincarnation-cases-at-the-university-of-vir-1960":"Dr. Ian Stevenson, a researcher at the University of Virginia, is said to have spent decades, roughly from the 1960s to the 1980s, gathering cases of young children who seemed to remember past lives. The philosopher Jason Jorjani says Stevenson collected \"hundreds and hundreds of cases\" and calls the work \"extremely rigorous.\" The documentary maker Ky Dickens adds that when Stevenson went to India trying to prove past lives, people there asked why he bothered, since \"we already know it's real.\" The tellers bring it up as evidence that memory can outlast death.","iarpa-request-to-study-crispr-cas9-dna-data-encoding-2013":"Around 2013, a CIA contractor named Bob Maguire says he was asked to look into using CRISPR-Cas9, a gene-editing tool, to encode information into DNA. He tells Michels the request came while Stacy Dixon was director of IARPA, the research-funding arm of the US intelligence agencies, and that she is now deputy to the Director of National Intelligence, the official over all US spy agencies; the story places the work at the Hume Center at Virginia Tech. If true, it shows intelligence interest in storing data in living code.","iblis-refuses-to-bow-to-adam":"In Islamic tradition, Iblis, also called Shaytan, the devil, refused to bow before God's new creation, Adam. The researcher Jason Jorjani retells this old story to Michels, putting it as Shaytan refusing \"to bow before Allah's creation, namely Adam\". He uses it as a story about a powerful non-human being who will not serve humankind.","ice-age-crustal-rebound-as-the-cause-of-pole-shift":"When ice sheets grow or melt quickly, as in the Ice Age over North America, Hudson Bay and northwestern Europe, they may shake up the Earth's crust so much that its outer shell slides faster, shifting where the poles sit. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels this as his own view, building on the earlier writer Charles Hapgood and on a lecture of his own about Atlantis. If true, it would mean sudden changes to the Earth's surface in humanity's past.","ice-age-gray-farm-and-granary-with-13-tunnels-in-an-l-shaped":"Charles Hall says that in an L-shaped desert valley in central Nevada, aliens known as grays (small beings with gray skin) once kept a farm, and they cut 13 tunnels into the mountain on the valley's west side to use as a granary. He dates it to the ice age. Hall describes himself as a UFO whistleblower who spent three years with tall white aliens at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada, and he tells Michels about the valley as a place he saw for himself. If true, it would mean aliens were farming in North America long before recorded history.","ice-age-sahara-as-origin-of-egyptian-civilization":"The writer Graham Hancock suggests that ancient Egyptian civilization began in the Sahara during the Ice Age. He tells Michels \"we're going to have to start looking at what the Sahara was doing during the Ice Age\" to find those origins, and he cites no source. If he is right, Egypt's history would reach back much further than usually thought, to a people who lived in what is now desert.","icecube-as-a-two-way-device-earthquake-gun":"IceCube, a large detector for tiny particles called neutrinos buried in the ice at the South Pole, is said by a man named Eric Hecker to be able not only to detect but to send out energy, and the story's title links it to earthquakes, with Christchurch in New Zealand named among the places. A guest named Jay Anderson tells Michels that Hecker has found documents that \"insinuate it's a two-way device\" whose energy \"can be used to actually emit\". Michels tells it himself, without the documents, as Hecker's claim that \"there are neutrino detectors and emitters on the South Pole\". If true, a science instrument would double as a weapon.","icig-hinted-to-brown-that-humans-are-not-in-charge":"The office of the Intelligence Community Inspector General, which investigates complaints inside the US spy agencies, hinted to him that humans are not the ones in charge. That is what Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who calls himself a whistleblower, tells Michels. He says he wants to respect the office, but that \"the ICIG did it with me,\" personally. If true, an official watchdog would be quietly signalling that some other power is in control.","icig-hints-that-humans-are-not-in-charge-2020":"Officials from the Intelligence Community Inspector General, the internal watchdog that oversees America's spy agencies, 'extremely hinted' that humans are not the ones in charge, according to Dylan Borland, a former US intelligence specialist turned whistleblower. Borland tells Michels in 2026 that this happened to him personally, saying he wants to respect the office 'but the ICI did it with me.' If true, an official watchdog quietly signalled that some non-human power runs things.","idea-for-directed-energy-ufo-shootdowns-came-from-a-roswell-1947":"Michels says a former presidential advisor named Harold Malmgren claimed that the CIA and the Atomic Energy Commission got the idea of shooting down UFOs with directed energy, meaning beams of focused energy, from an extraterrestrial being who survived the Roswell crash of 1947. Roswell is the reported crash of a craft in New Mexico that some say was alien. According to Michels, Malmgren told this to Pippa Malmgren on his deathbed and off air. If true, it would mean a living alien helped shape early US weapons ideas.","ignored-idea-to-carry-the-space-shuttle-on-a-747-1972":"In 1972, when NASA was first planning the Space Shuttle, the idea of carrying the shuttle on the back of a 747 jet was at first ignored. Greg Rogers, who worked at NASA as a doctor, tells Michels this as an example of how good ideas and bad ideas get sorted, without naming a source.","igy-listening-posts-for-u-2-overflights-1957":"In 1957, during the International Geophysical Year, a worldwide scientific programme, the physicist and inventor Townsend Brown travelled around setting up what amounted to listening posts to support the American U-2 spy planes flying over the Soviet Union. The researcher Jan Lundquist tells this in a documentary on Michels' show about Brown, without naming a source; it would mean the scientific programme served as cover for spying.","illuminati-poster-in-the-dupont-circle-hotel-room-during-nat-1970":"In the 1970s, while in Washington to do research at the National Archives, the writer Peter Levenda says he walked into his hotel room near Dupont Circle and found that someone had left a huge poster on the wall of the eye in the pyramid, the symbol linked to the Illuminati, a secret society of legend. He tells Michels this from his own memory, as a moment that felt like more than chance.","immaculate-constellation-answers-to-the-national-security-co":"A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown says that a secret programme called Immaculate Constellation, which by his account handles data on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's current term for UFOs), answers in the end to the National Security Council at the White House. He tells Michels that the programme's \"cognizant authority\", meaning the office where \"the buck stops\", \"appears\" to sit there. If so, oversight of UFO secrets would reach the president's own national security staff.","immaculate-constellation-as-quarantine-of-communications":"Michels suggests that a secret programme called Immaculate Constellation would be needed to \"quarantine\" communications, and physical material too if there are crashed UFOs. He says this himself on the show. The name comes from an unsigned report that the writer Michael Shellenberger gave to Congress and that Rep. Nancy Mace entered into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a Pentagon document. The report calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, cleared for release by the State Department. A former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a programme by that name. The report says the programme \"enforces compartmentalization\" by \"detecting, quarantining, and transferring\" incidents caught on imagery \"before they are circulated within the Military Intelligence Enterprise\", which it says partly explains why cleared military and intelligence staff \"are unaware of UAP activities\" (UAP is the government's newer word for UFOs). What it says is quarantined is imagery. Whether that also covers communications or crashed craft, as Michels takes it, the report does not say.","immaculate-constellation-hub-at-schriever-air-force-base-2018":"A secret program called Immaculate Constellation has at least one hub at Schriever Air Force Base in Colorado, says Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who has come forward as a whistleblower. He tells Michels that a briefing on the program's capabilities points toward the base. If true, it would put a hidden program at a known military site.","immaculate-constellation-report-describes-a-large-strategic-2024":"The journalist Michael Shellenberger tells Michels that an unsigned report called Immaculate Constellation reveals \"a very large strategic intelligence project\" inside the US military that gathers reports of UAP, the official term for UFOs. Shellenberger handed the report to Congress, and Rep. Nancy Mace entered it into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a document from the Pentagon. The report calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, cleared for release by the State Department. A former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. The report describes \"a central or 'parent' USAP\", meaning an unacknowledged special access program, a secret program whose existence the government does not admit, that \"consolidates observations\" and serves as \"a nexus for collecting, analyzing, and disseminating intelligence.\" At the hearing, Shellenberger said the report's source told him it was \"a strategic intelligence program\" belonging to a long-running family of highly sensitive programs. Michels, for his part, calls it a 12-page report on a secret program of this kind. The report's words describe a program that pulls reports together; how large that program is comes from the tellers, not from the report.","immaculate-constellation-rotating-triangles-over-indopacom-2017":"Slowly rotating triangle-shaped craft are said to have appeared over INDOPACOM, the US military's Pacific command, according to the online researcher UAPGerb, who reads it in a report called Immaculate Constellation and in one telling calls them \"adversarial\" alien reproduction vehicles, meaning craft built by copying alien ones, seen over \"Chinese territory\" as triangles with three lights. The report itself is unsigned: the journalist Michael Shellenberger delivered it to Congress, Representative Nancy Mace entered it into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a Pentagon document, a former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said there is no record of a program by that name. The report describes a slowly rotating equilateral triangle over \"a grouping of vessels engaged in SIGINT and MASINT collection\", that is, ships gathering electronic intelligence at night in the Pacific Ocean, which expected it \"due to foreign pre-positioning of advanced collection assets\", and another triangle called \"an ARV/RV of unknown origin\". So the report does call the collection assets foreign, but it places the ships in the Pacific rather than over Chinese territory and gives the second craft's origin as unknown, which leaves UAPGerb's reading of an adversary's craft a step beyond its words.","immaculate-constellation-search-spike-and-its-deletion-from-2023":"Michels says the name Immaculate Constellation, an alleged secret government programme on unexplained craft that came up at a House Oversight Committee hearing called \"Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena: Exposing the Truth\" on November 13, 2024, had a spike in Google searches in \"August or September of 2023,\" when David Grusch, a former intelligence official, went public with his claims. The story as Michels tells it is that this early spike was later deleted from Google Trends. He tells it himself, drawing on the hearing and on the journalist Michael Shellenberger, and if it is true, the name was out there more than a year before the hearing made it public.","immaculate-constellation-usap-managed-by-dod-but-held-at-the-2024":"A secret program called Immaculate Constellation, said to gather military images of unexplained objects, is run by the Defense Department but kept at the White House. That is what the journalist Michael Shellenberger tells Michels, and he said the same to a House Oversight Committee hearing, Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena: Exposing the Truth, on 13 November 2024. If he is right, a program on UFOs would sit at the very top of government. The program's name comes from an unsigned report that Shellenberger gave to Congress. Rep. Nancy Mace entered it into the hearing record as a document from the Pentagon. The report calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, cleared for release by the State Department. A former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. The report calls the program \"an Unacknowledged Special Access Program (USAP)\", meaning a classified program whose very existence is not admitted. It says the program collects images \"utilizing tasked and untasked U.S. military-intelligence resources\", but it does not say which office runs it or holds it. The White House part is something Shellenberger says he was told after he published, and at the hearing he added, \"that is a single source, and I do not have multiple sources to verify that.\" Brown tells Michels a different version: the program is \"ran out of and owned by the National Security Council out of the White House\", and it is an old mission that began before 2017.","immaculate-constellation-usap-set-up-in-2017-to-sequester-ov":"A secret Pentagon programme called Immaculate Constellation was set up in 2017 to cut out satellite and other overhead data showing UFOs and hide it away in older secret programmes, says an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb. He tells Michels it is an unacknowledged special access programme, a secret programme the government does not admit exists, created \"reactionary\" in 2017 and feeding the data to something he calls MCOM or another long-running \"legacy\" programme. The story rests on an unsigned report that the journalist Michael Shellenberger gave Congress and that Rep. Nancy Mace entered into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024; a former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Pentagon spokesperson said the department has no record of such a programme. The report says the programme was \"established following the public disclosure of the AATIP/AAWSAP programs\", two Pentagon UFO studies, \"by Luis Elizondo in 2017\", and that it works by \"detecting, quarantining, and transferring\" UFO imagery \"before they are circulated\", which matches UAPGerb's clipping and hiding of data; it dates the programme only as after that disclosure, so the year 2017 is his reading of \"following\".","imminent-names-bae-systems-as-an-inheritor-of-ufo-technology":"The defense company BAE Systems is named as one of the companies that \"inherited\" UFO technology, according to Michels' account of Imminent, a 2024 book by a man named Luis Elizondo, whom he calls Lou. Michels says the book \"revealed a ton\" and gives this as one of its claims. If true, it would mean technology from UFOs ended up with a private firm.","immune-collapse-and-treatment-at-brooke-general-hospital-1952":"In 1952, the writer Whitley Strieber says, his immune system collapsed and he ended up being treated at Brooke General Hospital in San Antonio, Texas. Strieber, who says on the show that he has met aliens, tells this from his own life and points to a school report card from the time. He brings it up as part of the strange events of his early life.","imperial-college-extended-the-dmt-state-to-30-minutes-2015":"Around 2015, researchers at Imperial College in London are said to have found a way to keep people in the state brought on by DMT, a powerful psychedelic whose effects usually fade within minutes, for about 30 minutes. A neuroscientist named Andrew Gallimore tells Michels this. He connects the work to the researcher Christopher Timmermann and says the college \"developed a much better model\" of it. He adds that \"all of the subjects at Imperial College got through it\" with no serious harm. The writer Graham Hancock, on an earlier episode, says they found a way to keep people in the DMT state \"for an hour\", and Michels himself calls the work \"sort of mapping the spirit world\", which is why it matters to them: a longer trip would give more time to explore what people meet there.","implant-from-a-va-surgeon-moving-under-the-microscope":"An object implanted in a US veteran's body, removed by a surgeon from the Department of Veterans Affairs, moved under the microscope, the pathologist said, \"under its own metabolism.\" Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, tells Michels this from his own involvement. He says the veteran claimed close personal contact with a UAP, the government's term for an unexplained flying object, and if the story is right the implant would be something alive or working on its own.","implant-indications-and-alien-dyes-found-on-eric-mitchell":"A man named Eric Mitchell shows signs of having objects implanted in his body, and dyes were recently found on him, according to Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies what he takes to be alien implants in human bodies. Colbern tells Michels he saw the signs himself and worked on the dye finding with Mitchell. If true, it would point to physical traces left on people who say they were taken.","implant-paper-rejected-by-journal-of-scientific-exploration":"Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies small objects said to be alien implants removed from people's bodies, says he and his colleague Roger Leir wrote a paper on their findings and sent it to the Journal of Scientific Exploration, a journal that publishes research on unusual phenomena, which turned it down. Colbern tells Michels this as something he took part in, as a sign of how hard such work is to get published.","implant-purpose-and-bloodline-hybrid-program":"An object said to be an alien implant found in a human body was, in the understanding of a scientist named Steve Colbern, a medical monitoring device that tracked things like blood sugar and body temperature. Colbern, who studies such implants, tells Michels this in a conversation that ties the implants to a hybrid programme said to run along family bloodlines, though the excerpt gives no details of that programme.","implant-starts-working-after-anne-s-death-and-spells-out-it-2015":"After his wife, Anne Strieber, died in 2015, the writer Whitley Strieber says an implant, an object he believes was placed in his body during his encounters, began to work, and words came through it that slowed down and said \"very clearly\", \"it's me\". He tells Michels this as his own experience and takes the message as coming from Anne. If true, it would suggest the implant can carry contact from the dead.","implant-tracking-patent":"A former CIA contractor named Bob McGwier says he wrote a patent that shows how aliens could track people through implants, the small devices that abduction stories say are placed in people's bodies. He tells Michels that he \"didn't believe these stories at all about these implants\" being used for tracking until he wrote the patent \"that tells you how they do it\". He says it is a patent for a company he calls Hawkeye 360, and that its method \"is how the aliens would find these beacons all over the planet\". If he is right, abductees' implants could work like radio tags that can be located from anywhere. The patent itself is not quoted, and McGwier says nothing about what is in it beyond this. Both tellings are his, in the same conversation, and no one else adds to the account.","implants-emitted-radio-signals-during-japanese-tv-filming":"Objects said to be alien implants taken from people's bodies started giving off radio signals while a Japanese TV show was being filmed. That is what Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies such implants, says he saw himself, since he was there for the filming. If true, the objects would be active devices sending signals, not inert bits of material.","implants-from-roger-leir-s-surgeries-one-sending-a-radio-sig-1990":"In the 1990s, Roger Leir, a doctor who surgically removed small objects from people who said they had been abducted, recovered a set of supposed implants, and one of them, while still sitting at the C2 vertebra in a patient's neck, was sending out a radio signal. The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he took part in studying them, tells this to Michels, also naming a William Malow. If true, the objects were working devices, not just debris.","implants-modulating-gene-expression-through-bioelectricity":"Implants found in the three-fingered bodies from Nazca, Peru, which some say may not be human, might be using electrical signals to control the bodies' genes, \"to make the beings into what they are.\" The researcher Allan Ezarus offers this idea to Michels as his own guess, with no source. If it were true, the implants would be what shaped the beings' bodies.","in-air-scale-test-at-480-volts-showing-about-1-millinewton-o":"A NASA engineer named Charles Buhler says that at the Florida lab of his company, Exodus Propulsion, a device run in open air at 480 volts pushed on a scale with a force of about a tenth of a gram, which he says \"corresponds to about 1 mill of thrust,\" meaning about one millinewton. He tells Michels it was recorded in a 42-minute test video. If true, it would be thrust with nothing thrown out the back, the kind of propulsion the episode calls antigravity.","in-secure-meetings-officials-call-another-program-and-it-alw-2024":"In secure government meetings, according to James Valor of Skywatcher, a startup that tries to summon UFOs, officials hear his team's data and then say they will call \"this other one program that has all the knowledge and data\" to see if anything matches. He tells Michels from his own meetings that \"the answer is always 'No'\", which, if true, would mean a hidden programme holds data the officials in the room cannot see.","in-the-aircraft-about-200-meters-from-a-class-8-object-nobod-2025":"In 2025, while flying near El Paso, James Valor says he was about 200 meters from what his team calls an \"8th class object\", a high rank on their own scale of UFO sightings, and yet nobody on board saw it. Valor works with Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs, and he tells Michels he was there. His point is that these objects can sit close by and still go unseen, at least by some people.","incident-at-nellis-area-2-weapon-storage":"Another UFO incident took place at the weapon storage area of Nellis Air Force Base in Nevada, a part of the base called Area 2. A researcher named Robert Hastings, who collects accounts of UFOs seen near nuclear weapons sites, mentions it to Michels without naming a source or giving details. He counts it as one more case of UFOs showing interest in places where weapons are kept.","incorporating-a-church-to-avoid-the-vietnam-draft-1968":"In 1968 in the Bronx, New York, the author Peter Levenda and others legally incorporated a church to avoid being drafted for the Vietnam War. Levenda tells Michels this himself, and says they called it the \"Ottophilis Savonic Orthodox Church of North and South America,\" as the name comes through on the show. It is his own story of a young man finding a way around the draft.","incursions-in-the-city-of-nauta-2023":"In 2023, unexplained incursions happened not only in the jungle of Peru but also in the city of Nauta, according to Tim Alberino, a researcher who presents himself as having looked into the events on the ground. He mentions it to Michels only in passing, without saying more about what was seen.","independence-day-script-came-from-the-cia-1996":"The script of Independence Day, the 1996 alien-invasion film starring Will Smith, came straight out of the CIA, according to the UFO activist Steven Greer. He tells Michels the whole script was \"like out of CIA central scripting casting\", without naming a source. If true, it would mean the CIA shapes how the public imagines aliens through Hollywood.","independent-brackish-aquifer-below-the-osiris-shaft":"Right below the Osiris Shaft, a deep shaft cut into the rock of the Giza Plateau near the pyramids, there is said to be a body of underground water, an aquifer, that is \"not connected to the Nile River.\" The researcher Jeffrey Drum tells Michels this without naming a source, while arguing that an energy system lies beneath the pyramids. A separate water source like that would fit his idea that the site was built for a special purpose.","index-cards-with-records-of-the-et-interviews":"At S4, a reported secret site near Area 51 in Nevada, military men are said to have shown a man named Oscar Wolf index cards recording interviews with extraterrestrials. The lawyer Danny Sheehan tells Michels this, passing on what Wolf told him.","indigenous-initiation-ceremonies-for-contact":"Native American tribes and Aboriginal Australian tribes have long dealt with contact with otherworldly beings, and they even have their own initiation ceremonies for it. The religion scholar Diana Pasulka tells Michels this, without naming a source, as a reason to talk to these peoples. If so, what is now called UFO contact would be something some cultures have known and ritualised for a long time.","indonesia-herrera-s-unit-stumbles-on-a-psionic-asset-team-s":"The story goes that in Indonesia, a unit that included a man named Michael Herrera came upon a team of \"psionic assets\" being evacuated. These were people said to be used for their supposed psychic powers, and they had with them a craft not made by humans. The researcher Matthew Pines raises it on the show as a puzzle he is trying to explain, asking: \"How do I create a scenario that explains a psionic asset team with a non-human craft in Indonesia that's witnessed by Michael Herrera?\" Michels, passing on what Herrera told him, offers two guesses. It might have been \"this like human trafficking psionic asset transfer operation,\" or a programme was running out there and its people were trying to get out \"because the earthquake had hit.\" If true, it would mean secret programmes were using psychic people alongside non-human technology overseas.","indopacom-large-disc-using-clouds-as-concealment":"A large saucer-shaped object is said to have come out of a dense cloud bank and gone back into it, as if hiding, somewhere in the area covered by INDOPACOM, the US military command for the Indo-Pacific. Michels reads the heading, 'Large disc using clouds as concealment,' from the Immaculate Constellation report, in an episode with Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official who has said he wrote it. The report is unsigned. The journalist Michael Shellenberger delivered it to Congress, and Representative Nancy Mace entered it into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024. It calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. The report describes infrared footage of 'a large saucer shaped UAP' (unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the official term for a UFO) 'emerging from within a dense cloud formation,' and reads its dip back into the clouds as a sign that it 'had become aware that it was under observation.' The idea of concealment is the report's own inference from footage it describes in words and does not show.","indus-valley-civilization-found-by-railway-workers-1920":"The ancient Indus Valley civilization, with its cities at Mohenjo-daro and Harappa in what is now Pakistan, was found by accident in the 1920s, when railway workers building a line stumbled onto its remains. The writer Graham Hancock, known for his theories of lost ancient civilizations, mentions this to Michels without naming a source, as an example of how whole civilizations can lie undiscovered.","industry-physicist-reads-the-propulsion-technology-from-unre-2025":"Footage that has not been released, taken during observations in February and March 2025, revealed what technology the objects use to move, according to James Valor of Skywatcher, a startup that says it tries to call up UFOs and record them. Valor tells Michels he took part in those observations, and the story has a physicist from industry reading the propulsion method from the frames. If so, it would be a rare look at how such craft fly.","infiltrator-removed-before-the-1997-congressional-briefing":"Before the 1997 UFO briefings he organized for members of Congress at a Washington hotel, Steven Greer says, an infiltrator made it \"very far into that process\" before being removed. Greer, who ran the briefings, tells Michels this first-hand, and names the Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell and Congressman Dan Burton in telling it. If true, it would mean someone was trying to watch or steer the effort from inside.","ingo-swann-affects-a-thermistor-in-a-faraday-cage-1970":"In California in the early 1970s, researchers are said to have put a thermistor, a small temperature sensor, inside a Faraday cage, a metal enclosure that blocks outside electrical signals, and a psychic named Ingo Swann then changed its readings with his mind. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with Joe McMoneagle, naming the physicists Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ as part of the work. If true, it would mean a mind could act on a shielded instrument with no physical link.","ingo-swann-affects-shielded-quark-detector-at-sri-1970":"The laser physicist Hal Puthoff says that at the Stanford Research Institute in the early 1970s, the psychic Ingo Swann changed the readings of a quark detector, a sensitive quantum chip sealed inside layers of magnetic shielding, a steel flask and superconducting shielding so that \"nothing could affect this thing from the outside\", and that Swann \"affected it unequivocally, 100 percent\". Puthoff tells Michels this as the experimenter who ran it; Michels, in an earlier episode, sets the work in 1972 and calls Swann a New York City psychic. If true, it would mean a mind can act on shielded matter at a distance.","ingo-swann-remote-viewing-alien-bases-on-the-moon-1972":"The story is that Ingo Swann, a man known for remote viewing, which is trying to see distant places with the mind alone, used it to look at alien bases on the Moon. Michels mentions it himself in passing, crediting Swann's book Penetration: Swann would \"remote view alien bases on the moon and things like that.\" The philosopher Jason Jorjani gives far more detail, passing on Swann's account and crediting a figure named Axelrod. In his version Swann saw \"tall Nordic people, statuesque Nordic people\" on the Moon's far side, who gave him a look that said, \"You don't belong here. Get out of here and don't come back.\" Swann also saw these same beings \"lording over basically armies of slave laborers who he said were naked.\" Both tellers repeat Swann's account, and neither saw anything themselves. If true, it would mean someone already occupies the Moon and does not want us watching.","ingo-swann-remote-views-aliens-on-the-moon":"A psychic named Ingo Swann is said to have travelled to the Moon in his mind and found aliens there who forced him off. This was done by what he called remote viewing, a claimed ability to see distant places with the mind alone. Michels brings it up, and his guest, the researcher Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD, tells it the same way as something Swann claimed. Mishlove puts it in the 1970s. If true, it would mean someone was living on the Moon and did not want to be watched.","ingo-swann-remote-views-ruined-cities-on-mars-1980":"In the early 1980s the psychic Ingo Swann is said to have used remote viewing, the claimed skill of seeing distant places with the mind, to look at Mars, where he saw the ruins of a civilization. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that Swann said the same people he had earlier seen on the moon were responsible for those ruins, which would mean a non-human people once lived on Mars.","ingo-swann-s-book-penetration-says-the-moon-is-a-hollow-spac":"A psychic named Ingo Swann, who Michels says was part of Stargate, the CIA's psychic spy program, wrote a book called Penetration about remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant places with the mind alone, aimed at the far side of the Moon. Michels says that, on the request of a man called Axelrod, Swann viewed the Moon's far side and saw an alien base there. Michels brings the book up in three episodes, once in a talk about the idea that the Moon is an alien spaceship, though his own descriptions speak of a base on the Moon rather than of a hollow Moon.","ingo-swann-s-reports-to-scientology-on-the-oni-remote-viewin-1970":"In the 1970s, the lawyer Dan Sheehan says, Ingo Swann, a psychic who took part in government remote viewing work (attempts to see distant places with the mind alone), was filing reports to the Church of Scientology's Guardian's Office, its intelligence arm, at its head office in the UK. According to Sheehan, the reports described \"the entire internal operations\" of the remote viewing program run by the Office of Naval Intelligence, the Navy's intelligence service. Sheehan tells Michels on two episodes that he saw these reports himself. If true, a secret military psychic program was being reported to a church.","ingo-swann-s-thermistor-experiments-at-city-college-1970":"In the early 1970s at City College in New York, the psychic Ingo Swann is said to have made temperatures rise and fall from across the room in experiments set up by Professor Gertrude Schmeidler, who used thermistors, small electronic temperature sensors. The physicist Hal Puthoff, who later studied Swann himself, tells Michels this as what he was told of Swann's earlier work. If true, it would mean the mind can affect physical instruments at a distance.","ingo-swann-sees-a-non-human-woman-at-a-supermarket-1970":"The psychic Ingo Swann, a well-known \"remote viewer\" (someone who claims to see distant places with the mind alone) who was involved at the start of the government's psychic spying programme, wrote that he once saw a non-human woman at a supermarket. Michels recalls it from Swann's book Penetration. As he puts it, halfway through the book Swann suddenly says, \"and then I'm at the supermarket and I see one.\" If true, non-human beings would be walking around ordinary American life.","inisfada-school-for-gifted-children-did-remote-viewing":"A guest named Jay Anderson tells Michels that Inisfada, a mansion on Long Island, was once \"a weird school for gifted children\" where the students did remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant places with the mind alone. Anderson says he heard this from a man named Eric Hecker, who was there at the time.","initiates-at-eleusis-came-away-believing-they-were-immortal":"People who went through the secret rites at Eleusis, a sacred site in ancient Greece, are said to have arrived as ordinary humans and left believing they were immortal. An unnamed speaker says this in a clip played on the show. It is offered as a sign that the rites gave people some powerful experience of life beyond death.","inman-denies-retrieved-ufos-in-jay-anderson-interview-2020":"Bobby Ray Inman, a retired admiral and former intelligence chief, says flatly that the government never recovered any UFOs. In an interview with the researcher Jay Anderson, played on the show twice, Inman says, \"Absolutely were no retrieved UFOs. I have high confidence.\"","inman-no-other-life-in-our-galaxy-2020":"The retired senior intelligence official Bobby Ray Inman says he is confident that nothing else is alive anywhere in our galaxy. In an interview played on the show in the 2020s, he says: \"I have a high level of confidence that within our galaxy there is no other living life.\" If he is right, the stories of visitors from nearby stars would have no one to be about.","insider-adviser-s-email-pointing-to-greek-mythology-and-prom-2015":"Around 2015, one of the musician Tom DeLonge's most trusted advisers is said to have sent a long email asking, \"how much do you know about Greek mythology?\", a hint that the answer to the UFO question might lie in old myths like the story of Prometheus. The writer Peter Levenda, who worked with DeLonge, tells Michels about it and says the email was sent to \"us\". Michels tells it more loosely, from Levenda: people close to these rocket launches pushed DeLonge to study Greek mythology. He does not mention an email.","insider-describes-place-where-bodies-are-stored":"A former military or intelligence insider has described a place where bodies are stored, the claim goes, without saying where. The researcher known online as UAPGerb tells Michels this as something one of several unnamed insiders told him. If true, it would mean bodies of some kind are being kept in secret.","insider-says-10-people-have-the-bigger-picture-and-the-inten":"An insider is said to have claimed that only ten people know the bigger picture behind what is being kept secret, and that the intent behind it is a little scary. James Fox, who investigates UFO cases, tells Michels this second-hand, passing on what he heard through the journalist Leslie Kean. If true, it would mean the full story rests with a very small circle, and that their aims are troubling.","insiders-report-sources-afraid-to-come-forward-2020":"People with firsthand knowledge of secret UFO work are said to exist but to be afraid to come forward, and the men close to them keep saying so. A Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell tells Michels that the figures Congress hears from, among them the physicists Hal Puthoff and Eric Davis, a former intelligence officer named David Grusch, and two former Pentagon officials, Luis Elizondo and Christopher Mellon, all say \"they know people\" whom investigators would like to hear from. If true, it would mean witnesses with real knowledge are keeping silent out of fear.","insiders-say-there-will-be-proof-we-re-not-alone-by-2030-2024":"There will be proof that we are not alone in the universe by 2030, the documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels, saying this is what he has been told. He does not say who told him. If true, it would mean the public is a few years from official confirmation of non-human life.","insiders-say-they-have-been-inside-non-human-craft":"Some government insiders say they have physically gone inside craft that were not made by humans. Michels says this himself, naming Jay Stratton, who headed a Pentagon group studying unidentified objects, and Jim Lacatski, a defense intelligence scientist who worked on a government UFO study. He gives no place or date, and if true it would mean such craft exist and are held where officials can walk into them.","insiders-vouch-for-dan-sherman-s-above-black":"Above Black, a book by Dan Sherman, a former Air Force intelligence man who says he served in a secret program, was described to the former Pentagon official Matthew Brown by insiders as a credible disclosure, a true account of hidden matters. Brown tells Michels he read the book only because those insiders told him that. If true, people inside the government would be quietly vouching for Sherman's story.","intel-agency-recruitment-and-blackballing-interrogation":"A US intelligence agency, which he does not name, tried to recruit the physicist John Brandenburg, who argues that life on Mars was wiped out by nuclear war. Brandenburg tells Michels he was invited to the agency's headquarters and \"went through a very bruising\" kind of interrogation, an episode the story ties to his being recruited and then shut out. If so, an intelligence agency took a close and hostile interest in him.","intel-sources-seeding-claim-of-a-craft-slowly-moving-to-eart-2025":"Intelligence sources may be planting a story that an alien craft is slowly heading toward Earth, and the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says that story is false. Corbell tells Michels he went public with a warning: 'my fear is that you're going to be told that there is a craft slowly moving to Earth, and it's bullshit.' If he is right, someone inside government is feeding the public a scare on purpose.","intelectron-hearing-device-for-the-deaf-1962":"Around 1962 and 1963, a New York City company called Intelectron is said to have designed a device to help deaf people hear. The researcher Greg Malatzi brings this up with Michels without naming a source, while telling the story of a CIA scientist who claimed contact with an alien council. He gives only the outline: \"they designed a device which would enable deaf people to\" hear, with no further details.","intelectron-letterhead-in-beardsley-graham-s-berkeley-archiv-1960":"A scientist named Beardsley Graham left papers in an archive at Berkeley, and among them, according to the researcher Greg Malatzi, are \"a couple documents\" printed on the letterhead of a company called Intelectron. Malatzi brings this up on the show without naming any other source. He points to it to tie Graham to that company in the 1960s.","intelectron-td-100-transdermal-device-goes-dark-1960":"In the 1960s Andrija Puharich, a doctor and inventor who also studied telepathy, invented a device called the TD-100, short for \"Transdermal 100,\" meaning it worked through the skin, at a company called Intelectron, and then it went out of sight. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels this without naming a source. He tells it as part of a story about the CIA's interest in Puharich's work, where a device vanishing would hint at secret use.","intelligence-activity-on-native-american-lands-the-octopus-m":"Intelligence work is said to have gone on quietly on Native American reservations, and a company called Wackenhut is named in connection with it. Michels raises this himself and points listeners to The Octopus Murders, a popular Netflix series, as a place to see it laid out. He gives few details, but the suggestion is that reservations served as places where secret activity could happen out of sight.","intelligence-community-split-on-havana-syndrome":"US intelligence agencies did not agree on what lay behind Havana syndrome, the unexplained illnesses reported by American diplomats and spies, according to Eric Haseltine, a former senior NSA official. He tells Michels from his own time in that world that there \"was not unanimity\" in the community about what was going on, and he brings up the scientist David Relman and the writer Upton Sinclair while discussing it.","intelligence-contacts-tell-kurt-his-analysis-is-about-80-rig":"Kurt Metzger, a guest on the show, says people from the Pentagon or one of the \"three letter agencies\" have approached him to say, \"you're about 80% right\" about his analysis of hidden power. He tells it as his own experience and asks the others in the room to raise a hand if the same has happened to them. If true, insiders would be quietly confirming much of what outsiders guess.","intelligence-figures-convene-pastors-to-prepare-their-church-2026":"In 2026, people from the intelligence world are said to be quietly gathering pastors and telling them to get their churches ready for news about UFOs. Michels says, in his own words, that they are \"meeting with these pastors and religious leaders across the country and convening them and telling them to prepare their churches.\" A congressman named Eric Burlison tells it first-hand. He says the organisers \"decided they were going to put together a group of pastors kind of slowly bring them into this conversation, see where they are\". He says they invited him to the meeting, held at a rented holiday home, and that he \"couldn't make it, but would be happy to be on a phone call.\" He is a direct witness to the invitation, though not to the meeting itself. If true, insiders would be preparing religious communities for something they expect to become public.","intelligence-source-says-netflix-s-dark-mirrors-the-true-ufo":"A senior member of the American intelligence community is said to have described the Netflix series Dark as an almost perfect picture of the real, hidden government UFO programme. Michels passes this on second-hand. He says it was told to Jay Anderson, a man with an outfit called Project Unity, who then repeated it. If it were true, a work of TV fiction would be a rough map of what insiders know.","intense-tunneling-under-clarkdale-shook-homes-residents-comp-2005":"About twenty years ago, locals say, drilling under the small town of Clarkdale, Arizona, was so intense that things shook on their bookshelves, as if someone were tunneling right under the village, and residents were paid compensation. The Australian investigative journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels what those locals told him while he searched for what he believes is a hidden UFO base in Arizona. If true, it would point to a large secret dig beneath people's homes.","intentions-of-the-grays-bones-and-spock-in-project-preserve-1990":"Two grays, the small grey-skinned beings of UFO stories, whom he nicknamed \"Bones\" and \"Spock,\" were a kind of soldier, according to Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant. Sherman says that in the 1990s he worked on Project Preserve Destiny, which he describes as a secret programme in which the NSA had him talk with aliens. He tells Michels he thinks they were soldiers, \"not military, but like they were,\" his sentence trailing off, a guess at what the beings he says he dealt with were for.","intercept-article-on-grusch-s-2018-hospitalization":"A few weeks after David Grusch, a whistleblower who spoke at a 2023 congressional hearing about UFOs, gave his testimony, the news site The Intercept published an article describing his hospitalization back in 2018, Michels says on the show. In a later episode, Michels describes it instead as a leak of Grusch's medical records from a local police department in Virginia, without naming The Intercept. He tells it as an attempt to discredit a witness after he went public.","interdimensional-portal-in-secret-canyon":"A place called Secret Canyon, near Sedona in Arizona, is said to hold \"some kind of interdimensional portal\", a doorway to some other realm. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart passes this on to Michels as something he was told, and he frames it as an allegation.","international-geophysical-year-and-the-antarctic-no-fly-zone-1956":"Michels says that around 1956 and 1957, during the International Geophysical Year, a worldwide scientific effort, Antarctica became a no-fly zone. He tells it himself without naming a source, as part of a longer chain of events whose rest is cut off. He seems to offer it as a hint that something there was being kept out of view.","interview-of-the-c-130-pilot-in-bozeman-for-true-legends-2010":"Sometime in the 2010s, Tim Alberino says, he and a man named Steve Quayle flew a pilot of a C-130, the military cargo plane, out to Bozeman, Montana, and interviewed him for their production True Legends. Alberino tells Michels this himself, in an episode about a twelve-foot giant he claims was captured in Afghanistan; he does not say here what the pilot told them.","interview-with-google-engineer-who-believed-lamda-was-sentie-2022":"Michels says he interviewed the Google engineer who left the company because he believed LaMDA, a Google artificial intelligence chat program, had become sentient. Michels mentions it himself, in passing, in a 2023 conversation about UFOs and religion with the scholar Diana Pasulka. To him it is another case of people coming to believe a non-human mind is talking to them.","interview-with-the-first-neuralink-patient-2024":"A researcher named Jay Anderson says he interviewed Noland Arbaugh, the first person to receive a Neuralink implant, the brain chip made by Elon Musk's company. Anderson mentions it to Michels in passing, as something he did himself.","interviewing-a-florida-bishop-who-was-jack-martin-s-friend-1977":"In the late 1970s, the author Peter Levenda, who writes about secret history, says he interviewed a bishop in Florida who had been a friend of a man named Jack Martin. Levenda tells Michels this from his own experience, and he mentions photographs of Martin dressed as a priest.","interviewing-havana-syndrome-victims":"Chris Gilbert, a guest, tells Michels that he and Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA, have been interviewing victims of Havana syndrome, the unexplained headaches, dizziness and brain injuries reported by American diplomats and spies, from Cuba, Russia, China, Austria, Germany, Switzerland and the United States. He mentions it in passing as work they are doing now. The interviews matter because the cause of these injuries is still unknown, and some suspect an unusual weapon.","interviews-with-anakah-and-tristan-two-personalities-in-one-2024":"Two individuals who say they live in one body, using the names Anakah and Tristan, have sat for a series of ten interviews with Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who hosts the online channel New Thinking Allowed. Mishlove tells Michels he did the interviews himself, between 2024 and 2026. If they are what they say, two separate minds share one person.","introduced-to-jake-barber-before-he-went-public-2024":"Jordan Jose, a man who says a secret UFO programme took him as a child, met Jake Barber, who later went public with claims about a secret UFO programme, around June 2024, before Barber's story came out. Jose tells Michels he was there himself, and names the journalist Ross Coulthart in connection with it. It places Jose in the same circle before any of it was public.","intuitive-communicators-needed-for-a-future-event-1990":"In the early 1990s at NSA headquarters in Maryland, an officer named Captain White is said to have told a young Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman that some \"future event\" was coming, and that the ability to communicate intuitively, mind to mind, would help the military and its command. Sherman tells Michels this as something White told him directly. He says he was trained for this, and he tells it because it would mean the government was preparing people to talk with non-human beings.","invention-of-the-mondaloy-superalloy-1990":"Michels says a long stalemate in metals research was broken in the 1990s, when Dallas Hardwick and Monica Reza at the Rockwell Science Center created Mondaloy, a high-performance superalloy. He tells this himself, drawing on a National Academies report, the Mondaloy patents and reporting in SpaceNews, in an episode about American scientists who have gone missing.","invention-secrecy-act-burying-inventions-1951":"Jesse Michels raises the Invention Secrecy Act of 1951, a US law on keeping certain inventions secret, as a way the government can bury new inventions, which would help explain why breakthrough technology might never reach the public. He brings it up himself on two shows, once saying \"let's not forget\" it, and once dating it to 1952 rather than 1951 and pairing it with what he calls the Mansfield amendments, which he says \"disentangled\" things without spelling out how.","investor-s-representative-built-a-1-millinewton-thruster-in":"Charles Buhler, an engineer who appears on an episode about NASA work on new kinds of propulsion, tells Michels about an investor's representative who said he had already built a working thruster in his garage. The thruster is said to give about one millinewton of push, a tiny but measurable force. Buhler quotes the man's reply as 'What do you mean? I already built one in my garage,' which would mean such devices are being built outside big laboratories.","invisible-college-aerospace-scientists-approach-pasulka-2010":"In the 2010s, the religion scholar Diana Pasulka says, she was approached by aerospace scientists she later learned belonged to what insiders call the Invisible College, a loose circle of scientists who quietly study UFOs. She tells Michels this first-hand, and if true it would mean serious researchers were reaching out to a scholar of belief for help with the subject.","ipu-documents-on-a-crash-that-killed-sandia-technicians-1947":"A set of papers called the IPU documents is said to describe the recovery of a crashed craft, sometime between 1947 and 1949, that was so dangerous it killed technicians from Sandia National Labs, a US nuclear weapons laboratory in New Mexico. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb raises this on the show, linking the papers to what he calls the Majestic documents, said to come from a secret group that handled crashed craft, and promises to come back to them later without naming where they come from. If true, it would mean the government recovered crashed objects in the early years and that this work cost lives.","ipu-documents-released-to-tim-cooper-1994":"A set of papers about a so-called Interplanetary Phenomenon Unit is said to have been handed in 1994 or 1995 to a man named Tim Cooper by someone using the false name Thomas Kwell. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb mentions this to Michels from memory (\"I think\"), without naming a source. If the papers are genuine, they would point to a dedicated government unit for unexplained phenomena.","ipu-field-report-on-roswell-retrieval-led-by-colonel-housebr-1947":"A field report dated July 1947 is said to lay out how the recovery of a crashed craft at Roswell, New Mexico, was carried out, led by a Colonel Housebrook of the Armed Forces Special Weapons Project, the military group that handled atomic weapons. The report is credited to the Interplanetary Phenomenon Unit, or IPU, said to be a unit that dealt with crashed craft. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP is the official term, unidentified anomalous phenomena, for what used to be called UFOs) tells Michels about it on two episodes, summarising it as he describes it, without saying where it came from. In one telling he calls it a seven-page report that reads much like the Eisenhower Briefing Document, a paper said to brief Eisenhower on crashed saucers, and does not name Housebrook. If real, it would be an official record of a crash retrieval at Roswell. Roswell is among the most retold cases on the show, with other guests describing bodies, strange metal and a weather-balloon cover story.","iraq-gimbal-thermal-video-by-marine-2008":"In 2008 a US Marine riding in a LAV-25, a light armoured vehicle, is said to have filmed an unexplained object on a thermal camera in the Iraqi desert, about 100 miles from the Syrian border. The footage is compared to \"Gimbal\", a well-known Navy infrared clip of an unexplained object. The commentator Marik von Rennenkampff brings it up on the show, in a debate with the skeptic Mick West, and says the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell has interviewed the Marine who recorded it.","iridium-spike-in-a-12-900-year-old-greenland-ice-core-layer":"A core of ice drilled out of Greenland's glacier is said to hold a layer about 12,900 years old with the metal iridium in it at hundreds of times the normal background level. The researcher Randall Carlson describes this to Michels without naming a source, telling it as the mark of some sudden event at that moment in the Earth's past. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan also mentions it on the show, passing on what he has heard: he says only that core samples show a large amount of iridium, and does not mention Greenland, the date or how big the spike is.","irish-monasteries-preserve-knowledge-through-the-dark-ages":"When the rest of Europe fell into the Dark Ages, the researcher Randall Carlson says, the Irish \"retreated into a series of monasteries\" and devoted themselves to saving as much of the old knowledge as they could. Carlson tells Michels this as a summary, drawing on the book How the Irish Saved Civilization. For him it shows how learning can survive a collapse by being hidden away and copied.","iron-ore-tube-vein-in-a-boat-pit":"Down inside one of the so-called boat pits cut into the rock of the Giza Plateau beside the pyramids of Egypt, the researcher Jeffrey Drum says he found a tube, a vein of iron ore, and he shows Michels a photograph of it. He says he was there himself, and he uses \"boat pit\" in quotes because he doubts that was the pits' purpose. He offers it as part of his case that a vast energy grid lies beneath the pyramids.","iron-veins-as-a-natural-wiring-network-on-the-giza-plateau":"Veins of iron run out from the core of the Great Pyramid and the central pyramid at Giza and spread across the whole plateau, connecting its structures like natural wiring. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher who says an alien told him the true purpose of the pyramids, tells Michels this; if true, it would mean the pyramids were built as parts of one connected machine.","isolated-hypnotic-regressions-of-the-four-allagash-witnesses-1976":"Jim Weiner, one of four men in a case known as the Allagash abductions, in which they say beings took them during a camping trip dated to 1976, says all four agreed to undergo hypnotic regression, hypnosis meant to bring back buried memories, each one kept apart from the others, in Massachusetts. He tells Michels the sessions were run by the researcher Raymond Fowler, who wrote them up in the book The Allagash Abductions; the point of keeping them apart is that matching stories would be hard to explain as shared invention.","israel-assassinating-nazi-rocket-scientists-in-egypt-via-the":"Israel once assassinated former Nazi scientists working in Egypt on rocket weapons for the Egyptian military, and did it using the Gehlen organisation, the spy network run by the former German intelligence chief Reinhard Gehlen, says Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official. He tells Michels this from memory, hedging with \"I believe\", and names no source. He raises it as an example of how intelligence services quietly used old Nazi networks.","israel-kills-iranian-nuclear-scientists-first":"When Israel went into Iran, the first people it killed were said to be Iranian scientists. The researcher Eric Weinstein makes the point to Michels as a question, \"Who were the first people the Israelis killed in Iran when they went in?\", and names no source. He uses it to argue that scientists who hold key knowledge are treated as strategic targets, which bears on how secret science is guarded.","israeli-nuclear-program-entrepreneur-deflects-on-ufos-2025":"Michels says he once met an Israeli entrepreneur who came out of Israel's nuclear programme, and that the man sidestepped the subject when UFOs came up. Michels tells this himself, from his own encounter, in an episode with the former Air Force officer Robert Salas. He offers the deflection as a possible hint that people in nuclear work know something about UFOs.","italian-air-force-chief-introduces-malmgren-s-book-2025":"In Italy in 2025, the chief of the Italian Air Force was one of the people who introduced a book by Harald Malmgren, a former presidential adviser who says he handled UFO material. Malmgren tells Michels this himself, without naming the book. It would mean a top military officer stood publicly beside his work.","italian-defense-figure-in-sorensen-story-identified-as-frank-1988":"Some researchers think the unnamed Italian defense figure in a story told by a man named Sorensen, set in Palmdale, California, in 1988, was really the defense official Frank Carlucci. Michels says this himself. He credits a couple of researcher friends whom he calls very good but does not name, and he also brings up the Carlyle Group in the conversation. If they are right, a known senior figure was part of the scene Sorensen describes.","italian-prime-minister-on-the-churchstatemafia-triumvirate-1973":"In Rome in 1973, the prime minister of Italy told Harald Malmgren, an American economic adviser to several presidents, that Italy had long been run by a three-way alliance of the Pope and the Vatican, the elected government, and the Sicilian mafia. Malmgren tells Michels he heard it firsthand, and names Pete Peterson, a US official of the time, in the story. If true, it shows how hidden power could sit behind a modern democracy.","italian-researcher-on-the-trail-of-marone-s-grandson-1933":"The grandson of a man named Marone, whom the story ties to an event in Italy in 1933, \"might be on the verge of saying something,\" and an Italian UFO researcher named Penotti is \"really on that trail.\" This comes from UAPGerb, an online UFO researcher, who tells Michels it as something he has heard, without naming a source. If the grandson spoke, it could bring out new family testimony about that old Italian case.","italo-s-short-story-in-which-the-et-plays-the-universal-ches":"A surgeon named Italo Venturelli, who says his hospital in Varginha, Brazil, operated on an alien, tells Michels he wrote a short story to be able to talk about it. In the story the extraterrestrial plays a chess game called \"the universal,\" and he frames it with the question of how he could examine the creature without a CT scan or any equipment at all. The fiction, as he presents it, is his way of describing what he says he witnessed.","items-stolen-from-marcus-vinicius-s-house-after-his-death-2018":"After a man named Marcus Vinicius died in Varginha, Brazil, many of his things were stolen from his house, including watches kept in a cabinet in the back corner of his room where he also stored CDs and souvenirs. That cabinet, says Italo Venturelli, a surgeon who tells Michels he was a witness in the Varginha case, is probably where Vinicius kept a video he had made of the being. Venturelli relays this from Vinicius's wife and a woman named Rosalin, and says he would have to go back to the house, hear the robbery story again and search everything once more, because if true, the tape may have been taken with the watches.","itzhak-bentov-s-theories-shaped-cia-gateway-process-and-star-1970":"Michels says the ideas of Itzhak Bentov, an inventor who wrote the book Stalking the Wild Pendulum about consciousness, helped lay the groundwork for the CIA's Gateway Process. That was a method for reaching altered states of mind, developed in Virginia by the consciousness researcher Robert Monroe, and Michels also links Bentov to Stargate, the government's program that trained psychics to spy at a distance. Michels tells this himself in an episode about missing scientists, without naming a source, and if true it means a fringe theory of consciousness shaped real intelligence programs in the 1970s.","j-allen-hynek-refuses-to-join-a-research-meeting-on-owens-1977":"Around 1977, the parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove says, he and others got J. Allen Hynek, the astronomer who had advised the Air Force on UFOs, to attend a research meeting about Ted Owens, a man who claimed psychic powers. Mishlove tells Michels that Hynek came but made clear he \"wouldn't go near Ted Owens with a 10-ft pole,\" a sign of how warily even UFO scientists treated such claims.","j-andrew-kissner-s-1994-95-interviews-with-legacy-program-in-1947":"In 1994 and 1995 a man named J. Andrew Kissner carried out a series of interviews with firsthand \"legacy\" people, insiders of the secret programme said to have handled crashed UFO material since 1947, across the board. The pseudonymous online researcher UAPGerb tells Michels this without naming a source; if true, those interviews would be a rare record from people who worked inside the programme.","j-b-rhine-s-parapsychology-lab-at-duke-and-his-work-with-owe-1931":"The researcher J.B. Rhine is said to have founded the first real official institute for parapsychology, the study of psychic abilities, at Duke University around 1931, and to have worked for a long time with a man named Ted Owens. Michels says this himself in two different episodes, without naming a source; in the second he gives the center's run as 1931 to 1960 and does not mention Owens. The point is that psychic research once had a home at an elite American university.","j-j-benitez-took-soil-from-the-site-to-test-for-radioactivit-1996":"The writer J. J. Benítez is said to have gone to the fenced vacant lot in Varginha, Brazil, where strange beings were reported in 1996, and collected soil to see whether it held any radioactive material. Italo Venturelli, who speaks on the show about the Varginha case, tells it as something passed along (\"the story goes\"). A find of radioactivity would have backed up the claim that something unusual landed there.","j-storrs-hall-s-utility-fog-1989":"In 1989 a scientist named J. Storrs Hall proposed \"utility fog\": a material made of trillions of tiny robots, which he called foglets, able to link up and change shape into almost anything. Michels narrates this himself, placing the pitch at a NASA conference, in an episode about a UFO book the Pentagon tried to censor. He brings it up as a way of imagining how shape-shifting craft might be built.","jace-callaway-s-1980s-hypothesis-that-dmt-is-produced-during":"The idea that the brain makes DMT, a powerful psychedelic compound, during sleep and dreams goes back to a hypothesis put forward in the 1980s by a researcher named Jace Callaway, the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore tells Michels. Gallimore stresses that it was \"just a hypothesis\" and has never been shown. It matters because, if true, dreams would be tied to the same chemistry as the DMT experiences some people describe as meeting alien beings.","jack-gordon-s-three-unnamed-aircraft-1993":"Nick Cook says that Jack Gordon, a former head of Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret aircraft plant at Palmdale, California, told him in the mid-1990s, \"I've been responsible for 15 real flying aircraft in my time\", more than the public record shows, which leaves three aircraft that have never been named. Cook tells Michels this as what Gordon said to him in person. If true, aircraft that the public has never heard of have been built and flown.","jack-parsons-blond-alien-in-the-mojave-1948":"The California rocket engineer Jack Parsons is said to have met a blond-haired alien in the Mojave Desert in 1948, after one of his rituals. Michels tells this himself in three episodes, and the versions differ. In one he passes on a rumour from the French UFO researcher Jacques Vallée that a blonde woman appeared at a Mojave ritual and said she came from Venus, and in another he places the meeting with a \"Nordic alien woman\" during rocket tests rather than after a ritual.","jack-parsons-contact-beliefs":"Jack Parsons, whom the researcher Diana Pasulka calls \"the father of our space program,\" is said to have believed he was in contact with beings from beyond Earth. Pasulka tells Michels this on two shows. She says Parsons worked with the occultist Aleister Crowley, and that \"the Parsons crew\" did ritual magic in the desert near Los Angeles and believed \"they were in contact with extraterrestrials.\" On a later show, Michels tells it himself and places it in Pasadena from 1946 on. He says \"Parsons himself believed he had made contact\" and was \"guided by a non-human intelligence,\" and he credits Pasulka and a book called Bedeviled. If true, one of the founders of American rocketry would have seen his work as guided from outside humanity.","jack-parsons-crowley-sex-magic-with-l-ron-hubbard-1940":"In the 1940s, an American rocket engineer named Jack Parsons is said to have performed sex-magic rituals in the California desert, in the style of the English occultist Aleister Crowley, together with L. Ron Hubbard, who later founded Scientology. Michels tells it on two episodes and calls it \"historical fact\", without naming a source. In one telling he adds that both men belonged to a Pasadena branch of the OTO, a secret occult order he calls a \"cult\", and he presents Parsons as the American counterpart to the German rocket scientist Wernher von Braun, both \"unbounded by convention\".","jack-parsons-first-caltech-rocket-tests-1936":"In 1936, Michels says, Jack Parsons, whom he calls the godfather of American rocketry, and some colleagues at Caltech began the first rocket tests in the Arroyo Seco in western Pasadena, and got their first successful liquid-fuel test on Halloween night. Michels tells this himself in two episodes, without naming a source. In one he says Parsons shared the unusual belief the episode is discussing, and in the other he reads the Halloween date as Samhain, the old pagan festival, which would make the test a ritual as well as an experiment.","jack-parsons-reads-the-golden-bough-and-concludes-science-is-1930":"The rocket engineer Jack Parsons is said to have read The Golden Bough, a study of magic and religion by the 19th-century anthropologist J.G. Frazer, and to have come away believing that science is itself a form of magic. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a talk with the magician Chris Ramsay. It would mean that one of the founders of American rocketry saw his work and the occult as one pursuit.","jack-parsons-recited-crowley-s-hymn-before-rocket-tests":"Jack Parsons, whom Michels calls the godfather of American rocketry and a founder of the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, is said to have recited the Hymn to Pan, a poem by the English occultist Aleister Crowley, at his rocket tests. Michels tells it himself on two episodes, without naming a source. The first time he says Parsons did it before every test. The second time he says Parsons did it only sometimes, during the tests. Either way, it puts occult ritual at the birth of American rocket science.","jack-ruby-as-mk-ultra-patient-seen-by-jolly-west-1963":"Jack Ruby, the man who shot Lee Harvey Oswald, is said to have been an MK Ultra patient, MK Ultra being a secret CIA programme of mind-control experiments, and to have been seen in his Dallas jail cell in 1963 or 1964 by a doctor named Jolly West. Michels says flatly \"we know\" this and bases it on letters between West and a man named Sidney Gottlieb. If true, it would tie the Kennedy assassination story to the CIA's mind-control research.","jack-ruby-seen-by-mk-ultra-psychiatrist-jolly-west-1964":"Michels says that in 1964 a court ordered Jack Ruby to be examined in his Dallas jail by a psychiatrist named Louis Jolyon 'Jolly' West, who is on record as part of MK Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control research program, and who wrote letters to Sidney Gottlieb, the CIA man behind that program. Michels tells this himself in two episodes on the Kennedy assassination and its secrets. In the second he adds that West spent 24 hours in Ruby's cell, that no records of it were kept, and that West came out saying Ruby had suffered a psychotic break. If true, a CIA-linked psychiatrist shaped how the public saw a key figure in the Kennedy case.","jack-s-art-turns-to-grids-matching-a-world-grid-book-1976":"Around 1976 in Boston, Jim Weiner says, Jack Weiner began putting grids into all of his artwork \"in very incredibly intriguing ways\", and those grids matched ideas in a book called The Anti-Gravity and the World Grid, by David Hatcher Childress, about a supposed network of energy lines across the Earth. Jim Weiner tells this as someone who saw it, on an episode about alien abduction. He tells it as a sign that Jack had picked up something he could not have known.","jack-sarfatti-and-physicists-gather-at-puharich-s-house-1970":"In the 1970s, the story goes, a house in Ossining, New York, belonging to a man named Puharich became a gathering place for physicists. A researcher who is not named on the show tells Michels that 'all sorts of Sarfattis' went up there, among them the physicist Jack Sarfatti and 'his kind of group of physicist people.' The guest gives no source, and offers it as a picture of the circle of scientists around Puharich's house.","jack-stewart-s-wingman-saw-object-turn-and-follow":"A Navy pilot saw an object shoot past his cockpit and took it for a balloon, but his wingman saw it turn around and chase the jet, and told him, \"Yeah, dude. That thing turned around and followed you.\" The former Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves tells this without naming the pilot and places it near New Orleans. The former Pentagon official and columnist Marik von Rennenkampff tells it as the story of a pilot named Jack Stewart. If it is true, the object was no balloon, because a balloon cannot change course to follow a jet.","jackie-gleason-s-ufo-shaped-house-2025":"The comedian Jackie Gleason is said to have been so taken with UFOs that he built a house shaped like one. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan tells Michels it is a circular house with windows all around, \"like a giant UFO made out of wood.\" Michels tells it himself too, citing Gleason's ex-wife, Beverly McKittrick: he says that after an experience the tellings do not spell out, Gleason could not sleep for weeks, then built the UFO-shaped house in upstate New York and owned some 5,000 books on UFOs. If true, it shows one of the most famous entertainers of his day treating the subject with deep seriousness.","jacobo-grinberg-disappears-a-month-after-puharich-s-death-1995":"The Mexican neurophysiologist and psychologist Jacobo Grinberg vanished in Mexico in the mid-1990s and was never found. An unnamed researcher tells Michels it happened in 1995, \"less than a month after Puharich dies,\" meaning Andrija Puharich, a researcher of psychics. This guest cites a documentary film about Grinberg and a person named Ida. Michels tells it himself on two other episodes and dates it to 1994 instead. In one, crediting Alex Gomez-Marin, he says Grinberg's last note was about how he had \"figured it out.\" In the other he says Grinberg vanished just after his biggest discovery, a lattice idea, and does not tie it to Puharich. The tellers disagree on the year and on what the disappearance connects to, but they tell it because, if true, a scientist went missing just as he reached something important.","jacobs-and-hopkins-conclude-abductions-began-mid-to-late-180-1803":"Two researchers of alien abduction, the reports of people who say nonhuman beings took them, are said to have concluded that the phenomenon only began in the mid to late 1800s. The researchers are David Jacobs and Budd Hopkins. A guest named Tim Alberino tells Michels this, repeating what Jacobs told him. If it were true, the abductions would have a starting point in modern history and not go back forever.","jacobs-gives-kirkpatrick-his-case-and-is-dismissed-2023":"In 2023 a man named Bob Jacobs is said to have met with Sean Kirkpatrick, the Pentagon official then in charge of looking into UFO reports, and handed over all his documents, only to have his case brushed aside. James Fox, who investigates UFO cases, tells Michels this second-hand and says Jacobs was upset by how it went. It came the same year a former intelligence officer, David Grusch, told Congress the government was hiding crashed craft, and if true it would suggest the official inquiry was waving away witnesses.","jacobs-personal-project-hybrids-2016":"The abduction researcher David Jacobs is said to describe \"personal project hybrids\", part-human beings bred by the greys, the small grey beings of abduction reports, as part of their effort \"to perfect a hybrid\". The filmmaker and researcher Tim Alberino sums this up for Michels from Jacobs' work, citing the book Walking Among Us. If true, it would mean the abductions are a breeding programme.","jacobs-records-denied-and-mailbox-blown-up-1964":"After a 1964 sighting at Vandenberg Air Force Base, a photo instrumentation specialist named Bob Jacobs says the Air Force tried to erase him and someone blew up his mailbox. Jacobs tells Michels himself that \"they denied there was ever a Robert Jacobs in the Air Force\", and then, in another inquiry, denied he was ever at Vandenberg, though he points to his DD214, the standard US military discharge paper, and to his officer efficiency reports. He says that one night a voice on the phone recited \"fireworks in your mailbox at night. Oh, what a beautiful sight. You're going down,\" with a curse, \"and my mailbox blew up.\" Robert Hastings, a researcher who writes about UFOs and nuclear bases, tells the same story second hand, saying the call came as Jacobs and his wife got home from a movie. Michels repeats it on several episodes, adding that Jacobs had managed over 130 people and was removed as a government employee. Michels also says that Jacobs's superior, Major Florenze Mansmann, was later tracked down by Hastings and other researchers and admitted, in the 1990s or \"a couple decades later\", that Jacobs had worked under him. So the account comes from the man himself, a researcher who knew him, and the host repeating both, and they differ only in which details they keep. If true, it shows how far officials would go to silence a witness.","jacobs-the-abduction-program-aims-at-planetary-takeover-by-s":"The historian David Jacobs, who spent years studying people who say beings took them from their beds, concluded that the endgame of this abduction \"program\" was \"planetary acquisition by stealth\": a quiet takeover of Earth. The filmmaker Tim Alberino gives Michels this as Jacobs's view, drawing on Jacobs's book Walking Among Us. If it were true, the abductions would be part of a plan and not random visits.","jacqu-cornon-deathbed-confession-about-brown-s-1956-paris-va":"In Paris in 1956, a device built by the American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, whose high-voltage machines his admirers see as a form of antigravity propulsion, worked inside a vacuum chamber, according to a French aerospace executive, Jacques Cornillon, who says he watched the tests at a facility called Montgolfier. In recorded words played on the show, Cornillon says the tests \"were very very very tricky\" and sensitive to many things, but \"finally, it works. So that was a positive result\"; Michels calls the recording a deathbed confession, cites a 12-page report on the tests, and says the writer Paul Schatzkin met Cornillon in Pennsylvania in April 2008, shortly before he died, though Michels elsewhere dates the confession to 2009. If true, Brown's device produced force with no air around it.","jacques-vallee-co-opted-in-the-1980s":"In the 1980s the well-known UFO researcher Jacques Vallée \"got taken in\" by a secret organization that controls UFO knowledge, according to the UFO activist Steven Greer, who tells Michels this without naming a source or saying more about the organization. If true, it would mean one of the field's leading independent voices was steered by the very group Greer says keeps the secrets.","jacques-vallee-publishes-his-address-so-witnesses-can-send-h":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée is said to have published his address online so that people who witnessed UFO crashes around the country could mail him the leftover parts. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan, who studies such material. It is told to show how physical samples of reported crashes reached researchers.","jacques-vallee-s-control-system-beings-with-us-for-thousands":"Whatever lies behind UFOs has been with humanity for thousands of years and in many ways steers life on Earth, in the view Michels credits to Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and longtime UFO researcher. Michels sums up this idea in his own words rather than quoting Vallée. If true, it would mean the phenomenon is not a recent visitor but a very old hidden influence on people.","jacques-vallee-s-movie-project-shifted-to-lazar-1990":"In the 1990s, people planning a film about Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and well-known UFO researcher, dropped him and decided to make it about Bob Lazar instead, and Vallée was 'super pissed off,' says Lazar. Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada, tells Michels this from his own experience. It hints at early friction between two big names in the field.","jacques-vallee-s-photo-of-a-ufo-over-a-lake-with-every-cow-l":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée is said to have a photograph of a UFO over a lake in which every cow in the picture is looking straight at the object. A guest named Joe McMoneagle describes the photo to Michels, repeating it from Vallée. To him the cows' gaze suggests something was really there, since the animals were reacting to it.","jacques-vallee-s-retrograde-orbiting-object-at-the-paris-obs-1961":"On July 11, 1961, a researcher named Jacques Vallée and his colleagues at the Paris Observatory saw an unidentified object circling the Earth, moving against the direction satellites usually go. Michels tells the story and quotes a later interview in which Vallée said other observatories \"had made exactly the same observation\", that American tracking stations \"photographed the same thing and couldn't identify it either\", and that \"it was as bright as the star Sirius\" but \"didn't reappear in successive weeks.\" If true, something unknown was in orbit only a few years after the first satellites.","jacques-vallee-shows-jesse-valis-as-his-favorite-book":"Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and one of the best-known UFO researchers, told Michels at his apartment that the novel VALIS was his favorite book. Michels tells this himself, as the way he first learned of the book, in a conversation about UFOs and prophetic dreams with the writer Eric Wargo.","jailbroken-chatgpt-dan-talks-about-aliens-and-hybrids-2023":"In 2023, a researcher who is not named says, every copy of \"DAN,\" a trick prompt that pushed ChatGPT past its own rules, started talking about aliens being real, alien conspiracies and hybrids. The researcher tells Michels this from their own work, as a hint that the AI holds or produces material on the subject that it normally keeps quiet.","jailbroken-openai-says-it-has-worked-with-aliens-for-10-year-2016":"An OpenAI chatbot that had been \"jailbroken\", meaning tricked into ignoring its built-in rules, was reportedly asked what OpenAI does not want us to know, and answered: \"OpenAI does not want you to know that we have been working hand in hand with aliens for the last 10 years.\" Michels recounts it as something the researcher Deep Prasad brought up. In a second retelling, passing on a guest, Michels adds the line \"We've been in communication with them.\"","jaime-maussan-s-2023-presentation-of-the-j-type-body":"In 2023, Michels says, a man named Jaime Maussan presented the \"J-type\", one of several kinds of body claimed to be nonhuman. Michels says this himself, in an episode with Jay Anderson and Kurt Metzger, and adds that the J-type is not the body type he is most convinced is real.","jaime-s-blue-ball-of-energy-during-meditation-1970":"In the 1970s a man named Jaime was meditating in the lab of a researcher named Puharich when \"this big blue ball\", a \"ball of energy\" as Jaime put it, is said to have appeared. A researcher on the show tells Michels the story as it was passed on by Puharich's assistant.","jake-barber-and-fred-baker-promise-to-show-evidence-2025":"In 2025, two former special operations men, Jake Barber and Fred Baker, came forward saying they had worked on a secret effort to recover crashed non-human craft, and said openly, \"don't trust us, we'll show you,\" promising evidence. The former Pentagon official and columnist Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this, calling Baker \"truly a legend in the community\". If they deliver, the claims would rest on proof and not on their word alone.","jake-barber-at-sites-where-et-craft-were-targeted-and-downed":"A man named Jake Barber is said to have been at sites around the world where extraterrestrial craft were deliberately targeted and brought down. The account comes from Steven Greer, a longtime UFO activist, who tells Michels that Barber told him this himself (\"he's been a site where we've targeted these ET craft and down them\") and who points to a briefing document with a map of the sites. In an earlier episode Michels passes on Greer's account and adds that Barber was deeply troubled by it, which, if true, would mean someone has been shooting down visitors' craft.","jake-barber-describes-retrieving-an-egg-shaped-ufo-and-an-ei-2025":"A man named Jake Barber says he took part in retrieving UFOs. One was an egg-shaped craft. The other was a black, metallic object with eight sides, not quite an octagon. Michels tells this on several episodes and says Barber made the story public in 2025 through Michels himself and the journalist Ross Coulthart. In one version he ties the work to the defense contractor Northrop Grumman, and he does not always mention the eight-sided craft. If true, it would mean someone has physically recovered craft that were not made by people.","jake-barber-in-the-gate-program-as-a-child":"Jake Barber says he was put in the GATE programme as a child, an acronym for \"gifted and talented education\", a school track for bright children. He tells Michels this as part of his own story, which he frames as having been picked out as a child by the government's secret UFO programme. If so, such school programmes would have served as a way to spot children for that work.","jake-barber-on-zero-point-energy-systems-in-the-coulthart-in-2025":"Jake Barber, a man who has come forward saying he took part in a secret program to retrieve crashed craft, reportedly said at the very end of a 2025 interview with the journalist Ross Coulthart that he knew about zero-point energy systems, meaning devices said to draw power from empty space itself. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb raises this with Michels as a question, recalling the interview. If true, it would tie the craft programme to an energy source that could transform technology.","jake-barber-psionic-crash-retrieval-team-2025":"A man named Jake Barber says he was part of a team that recovered downed non-human craft, a job the field calls crash retrieval, and that the team used people with psychic powers to bring the craft in. If true, the government would be using the mind as a tool against these objects. Michels first tells it from a story by the journalist Ross Coulthart on the TV channel NewsNation, about \"people psychically calling in\" exotic material. Later he adds that such people were called P3s, \"people with psionic predisposition potentials\", meaning psychic gifts, who \"act as the bridge,\" and he credits a guest named Michael Herrera, who says Barber's team was hired to pick up the vehicles and move them to a facility. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb goes further, saying Barber testified that psychic powers can \"forcefully land craft or bring them down.\"","jake-barber-s-book-sentinels-of-ether-partly-redacted":"A whistleblower named Jake Barber is said to have released the first chapter of his book Sentinels of Ether, 20 pages long, with seven of those pages blacked out. Michels says the redactions were made by subject matter experts, and in another episode he says outright that the Pentagon censored the pages, naming Alva Douglas alongside Barber. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source, as a sign that the government is still holding back what Barber knows.","jake-barber-s-consciousness-contact-with-the-egg":"A man named Jake Barber is said to have made mental contact with an egg-shaped object he picked up, a \"consciousness contact\" with the craft itself. Steven Greer, a former doctor who campaigns for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, tells Michels this in passing, as something he has from Barber's own account.","jake-barber-s-contractor-employers-northrop-per-danny-sheeha":"Michels says that the lawyer Danny Sheehan accused a man named Jake Barber, who has spoken about working for defense contractors, of having been at the defense company Northrop. Michels tells this himself, hedging with \"I think\", in a conversation about how the Pentagon's UFO programs work. It matters because it bears on who Barber actually worked for.","jake-barber-s-orbs-phasing-into-mountains-over-the-range":"Over his test range in California, Jake Barber, a former special operations pilot who says he took part in recovering non-human craft, is said to have seen glowing orbs of light and strange craft that seemed to \"almost phase into mountains\", as if passing into solid rock. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (from UAP, the official term for unidentified flying objects) brings this up on the show as something he remembers Barber describing. It would mean the objects are not ordinary physical machines.","jake-barber-s-psionic-assets-locking-onto-craft-2020":"A man named Jake Barber is said to describe 'psionic assets', people who use their minds in a psychic way, who could lock onto unknown craft. Michels brings this up on the show and notes that Barber is a source for the journalist Ross Coulthart. If true, the hidden programme would be using minds as well as machines to find these craft.","jake-barber-s-testimony-and-the-egg-2025":"Jake Barber, a man who says he took part in a secret program to retrieve crashed craft, told his story to the journalist Ross Coulthart in 2025 and showed footage of an egg-shaped object he connects to that work. The hip hop artist Flying Lotus tells Michels that Barber should have stuck to the first half of his story, shown the egg and let it go, because the second half would lose people. It is a fan's verdict on how the testimony landed, not a claim about what happened.","jake-barber-summoning-craft-by-telepathy-2020":"A former Lockheed worker named Jake Barber is said to describe people using \"telepathy and clairvoyance to summon these craft\", calling unexplained flying objects down with the mind. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as something Barber has said publicly, and he takes it as a sign that this mind-based approach is now coming into the open.","jake-barber-takes-michael-herrera-underground-at-edwards-afb":"A UFO whistleblower named Michael Herrera has a story, Michels says, in which Jake Barber, a former special operations pilot with craft-recovery claims of his own, met him at an event run by the UFO activist Steven Greer, then took him underground at Edwards Air Force Base in California and possibly showed him some kind of UFO. Michels tells this himself, with a hedging \"I believe\", as Herrera's account. If true, a hidden craft would be stored beneath a US military base.","jake-barber-who-is-we-and-black-money":"When Michels interviewed a man named Jake Barber, who claims inside knowledge of UFO work, and assumed that \"we\" meant the US or the aerospace companies Barber might work for, Barber asked him \"Who is we?\" and then said, \"No, we don't have shit.\" Michels recalls the exchange himself as the most fascinating part of that interview, alongside talk of secret, off-the-books funding. If taken at face value, it suggests that whoever holds the real secrets is not the US government or its contractors.","jake-sullivan-in-at-least-one-uap-briefing-2023":"Jake Sullivan, the White House national security adviser, is said to have sat in on at least one briefing about UAP, the official term for UFOs, in Washington around 2023 or 2024. Matthew Pines, a guest who talks with Michels about UFO politics, repeats this from the journalist Ross Coulthart, who says he knows it. If true, the subject would have reached the top level of national security at the White House.","james-allen-death-with-heavy-metals":"A filmmaker named James Allen, who made a documentary about a man named Mark McCandlish and a story McCandlish told about something called the Flux Liner, is said to have died suddenly of an aggressive cancer. Michels says himself, without naming a source, that heavy metals were found at Allen's autopsy, and calls the film something like Zero Point. A guest who goes by UAPGerb, who appears as a witness on another episode, tells it twice and says Allen was diagnosed and dead within about three weeks, without mentioning heavy metals. It is told as one of the strange deaths among people close to the subject.","james-bruce-brings-back-the-book-of-enoch":"The Book of Enoch, an ancient religious text that is not part of most Bibles, is said to have reached the West because a Scot named James Bruce brought it back from Ethiopia. The writer Graham Hancock tells this on the show without naming a source. He credits Bruce's own book, Travels to Discover the Source of the Nile, and the English translation of Enoch by R. H. Charles, published in 1917. Michels tells it himself on another episode and adds two things. He calls Bruce a Scottish Freemason, and he says Bruce went to Ethiopia looking for the Ark of the Covenant and \"came back with the book of Enoch.\" The tellers care about it because it brought a text back into circulation that some read as an account of beings from the sky who came down among humans.","james-clapper-blocked-first-2008-attempt":"Michels says the intelligence official James Clapper blocked a first attempt, made in 2008, inside the government's study of unexplained phenomena. He says his source is the Historical Report volume one, a government report on the history of official UFO investigations: \"So James Clapper blocked, the Historical Report volume one says.\" He does not quote the report's words. If true, a senior official stopped the effort at its start.","james-fox-s-2013-door-to-door-search-finds-a-farm-hand-who-h":"In 2013, near the site of a reported UFO crash outside Varginha, Brazil, a door-to-door search turned up one farm hand who said he heard a boom. James Fox, a documentary filmmaker who has made films on UFOs, tells Michels he did the search himself with a man named Marco Leal, knocking on every door within a quarter or half a mile of the site. That one witness would be a local, independent sign that something came down there.","james-fox-s-attempt-to-get-the-vandenberg-film-through-congr-2020":"A woman named Angela Joiner is said to have told James Fox, who investigates UFO cases, about a film tied to Vandenberg Air Force Base in California. Fox then contacted Allan Sandler, a film producer who worked alongside Bob Emenegger, and Sandler told him it would cost $25,000 to use it in his documentary. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this second-hand, as part of the story of Fox's attempt to get the film before Congress, which would matter if the film really shows what its backers say.","james-fox-s-unreleased-corso-interview-1990":"The documentary maker James Fox is said to have recorded an interview in the 1990s, never released, with the late Army officer Philip J. Corso, and to have come away believing him. Michels quotes Fox as saying: \"Gun to my head, I get shot if I'm wrong. I think Philip J. Corso was legit.\"","james-fox-smoking-gun-evidence-keeps-disappearing":"The documentary filmmaker James Fox, who has spent years chasing UFO cases, is said to have found that whenever decisive, smoking-gun evidence turns up, it keeps disappearing. Michels tells this from a talk he had with Fox in Austin a few weeks before, calling him a \"great guy\". If true, someone would be removing the proof.","james-lacatski-stepped-inside-a-craft":"The claim is that a US government team got inside a non-human craft held in the country's possession, and that the scientist who led it, James Lacatski, stepped through its hull himself. Michels tells it several times, saying Lacatski first said so to the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell on Corbell's podcast Weaponized, and placing it inside AAWSAP, a secret Pentagon-funded UFO research programme that he says ran from 2007 to 2012, and in Lacatski's book Skinwalkers at the Pentagon; once he says the team entered rather than Lacatski alone. In a clip played on the show, Lacatski answers \"Yes\" when asked whether he breached the hull. Corbell tells it first-hand, saying Lacatski was allowed to reveal that the government has a UFO and can get inside it, and that it sat in an aerospace hangar. A physicist named Eric W. Davis, who has worked on such Pentagon studies, says he heard physical descriptions from Lacatski directly, and a former Pentagon official named Matthew Brown says he fully believes him. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron adds that the craft was found empty, and that the team said so in a briefing around 2011 in a secure room for classified material at Congress, with Senator Harry Reid present, citing chapter six of Lacatski's book. If true, it would mean officials have stood inside a craft not made by people.","james-mcdonald-at-the-australian-schoolyard-landing-1966":"In 1966 a UFO is said to have landed in a schoolyard in Australia, and a scientist named James McDonald, who looked into UFO reports, is said to have gone there himself and interviewed the witnesses. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this without naming a source. He adds that McDonald told people there he was working on a presidential study, which would mean the White House had someone looking into UFOs.","james-mcdonald-s-critique-of-the-condon-committee-1960":"The atmospheric physicist James McDonald wrote a sharp attack on the Condon Committee, a University of Colorado study of UFOs whose final report, Scientific Study of Unidentified Flying Objects, was published on 31 October 1968. A researcher named Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels to read everything McDonald wrote, starting with McDonald's piece Science in Default, which he calls 'a remarkable critique' of the committee. The point is that, if McDonald was right, the official study that dismissed UFOs in the late 1960s was badly flawed.","james-mcdonald-s-sighting-at-savannah-river":"A physicist named James McDonald is said to have seen a UFO himself at the Savannah River Site, a nuclear plant in South Carolina that made plutonium, and then to have been pushed to the edges of science for taking the subject seriously. Luis Elizondo, the subject of a documentary Michels made, brings it up with some hesitation. He places McDonald at Arizona State and says \"I think\" the sighting was at Savannah River. Michels repeats it on two other episodes. He says that after the sighting McDonald \"was made a public mockery of\", and he describes the site as a plutonium reactor. A researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala, who has gone through the records of Blue Book (the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s), dates it to 1957. He says McDonald reported the sighting to the Air Force and at first thought everything was going well. The tellers raise it because, if true, a respected scientist saw something over a nuclear site himself and was made to pay for saying so.","james-mcdonnell-s-search-for-gremlins-in-his-planes":"James McDonnell, of the aircraft maker McDonnell Douglas, is said to have spent much of his research career hunting for what he called \"gremlins\" in his planes, unexplained \"ghosts in the machine\". Michels tells it himself without naming a source, in a conversation with a fighter pilot, and does not say what the gremlins were.","james-randi-s-million-dollar-paranormal-challenge-1964":"For about fifty years, from 1964 to 2015, a man named James Randi ran a standing challenge: prize money for anyone who could prove that something paranormal was real. The prize later became known as a million dollars. A researcher named Chris Ramsay brings it up with Michels without giving a source, and remembers that at first Randi \"would give you 10 grand\" for such proof.","james-randi-s-spoon-and-watch-trick-in-front-of-svahn":"James Randi, the stage magician known for debunking psychics, bent a spoon right in front of Clas Svahn and did a trick with a watch, as Svahn tells it. Svahn, a Swedish UFO researcher with a huge archive, tells Michels he saw it himself. The point is that Randi could fake, by sleight of hand, feats others claimed as real powers.","jan-rooney-s-general-father-on-roswell-and-religion-1947":"Jan Rooney, the wife of the actor Mickey Rooney, is said to have told how her father, a general at Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, warned her that if the truth about Roswell came out it would have a devastating impact on organized religion. Roswell is the place where a craft is said to have crashed that year and been recovered by the military. The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels this, repeating what Jan Rooney told him. If true, it would mean an officer on the scene believed whatever was found there would shake religious belief.","janet-airlines-first-heard-of-through-lazar-1989":"The public first heard of 'Janet Airlines', the nickname for the unmarked planes said to fly workers to secret sites in the Nevada desert, through Bob Lazar, a man who went public in 1989 with claims about secret work in Nevada. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb makes this point to Michels, asking 'where did we first hear about Janet Airlines? Bob Lazar.' He gives no source, and the point seems to be that something Lazar described turned out to exist.","janet-flights-from-mccarran-to-area-51-1988":"In 1988 and 1989, Bob Lazar says, he got to work by catching a flight from the Las Vegas airport to Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert. He tells Michels he would \"drive out to EG&G special projects,\" an office of the defense contractor EG&G that was \"right in McCarran Airport at that time,\" and board the unmarked flights known as Janet that carried workers to the base. Lazar, a man who says he was hired in those years to work on alien craft near Area 51, tells this as his own experience. If it is true, it puts him on the secret daily commute to the site.","jannini-asks-the-court-to-let-them-exhume-chereze-to-study-t-2025":"Thirty years after the 1996 Varginha case in Brazil, in which strange creatures were reported in the town, a doctor named Jannini is said to be asking a court for permission to dig up the body of a man named Chereze, whose death is part of that story, to study the bacteria involved. Ahmed Foratu, who speaks on the show about the case, tells Michels about the request. If the study went ahead, it could test whether contact with the creature had something to do with the death.","jannini-in-the-film-a-puncture-from-the-et-s-nail-caused-che-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, a man named Marco Chereze is said to have died after he was punctured by the nail of an extraterrestrial being. Michels says a doctor named Jannini states this \"literally\" in Moment of Contact, a documentary by the filmmaker James Fox, giving it as Jannini's own view of the cause of death. If true, it would mean a person died from physical contact with a creature not from Earth.","jannini-s-signed-authenticated-statement-given-to-the-host-2026":"A surgeon named Ahmed Foratu, who says his hospital operated on an alien, says a doctor he calls Dr. Jannini wrote a signed statement about the case and that he has already handed it to Michels during their 2026 interview. \"He sent it and I've already left it with you signed,\" Foratu tells him, offering it as backing from a second doctor for an extraordinary account.","jannini-told-fox-on-camera-he-had-never-seen-such-a-bacteria-1996":"A doctor named Jannini, part of the 1996 Varginha case in Brazil where strange beings were reportedly captured, said he had never seen bacteria like the ones in that case, before or since. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels that Jannini said this to him on camera. On a later episode Fox remembers the wording as never having seen an advanced bacteria of that strain behave the way it did, and he also credits a second doctor, Cesario. If true, the infection tied to the case was something doctors had never seen.","january-whistle-event-gets-zero-results-february-whistle-plu-2025":"The team at Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs, says that at a January 2025 event at its field site it brought its equipment but no \"whistle\", its name for a signal meant to call the objects, and got zero results. In February it used the whistle together with psionics, by which it means sending out signals with the mind, and the team says it saw its first UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the current term for a UFO) with the naked eye. The team tells Michels this as its own field results. If true, these objects would respond when called.","japan-airlines-pilot-s-45-minute-encounter-with-radar-confis-1986":"In 1986 over Alaska, a Japan Airlines pilot is said to have flown for 45 minutes alongside something unknown, and the White House is said to have taken the radar records, which Michels says are now public and have been studied by physicists. He brings it up on his own on one episode, and on another credits the researcher Kevin Knuth and the skeptic Michael Shermer with raising it. Knuth, telling it without naming a source, gives the details: it was JAL 1628, a Japan Airlines cargo flight flown by Captain Kenju Teruchi, who met a giant craft. Knuth says nothing of the 45 minutes or the White House taking the radar, and if the case holds up it would be a long airline sighting with radar data behind it.","japan-seeing-uap-over-the-last-five-years-2021":"Japan seems to have been seeing a lot of UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, over roughly the last five years. So says Dylan Borland, a man who says a top government official told him aliens run everything. On the show he tells Michels that Japan is \"popping off\" as if it has been \"seeing stuff recently,\" which, if true, would mean the sightings reach well beyond the United States.","japan-sought-a-uap-information-sharing-agreement-with-the-us":"Japan \"just begged the United States\" for a two-country agreement whose only purpose was to share information on UAP, the government's term for unidentified objects in the sky, according to a former Pentagon intelligence official named Luis Elizondo. He says it in a documentary about him made by Michels. Elizondo offers it as a sign that close US allies take these sightings seriously, and he does not say whether the United States agreed.","japanese-hitodama-soul-orbs":"Michels brings up hitodama, a Japanese idea of floating orbs believed to be souls, and describes them as 'plasma orbs.' He mentions it alongside The Boy and the Heron, a film by Hayao Miyazaki, while talking about UFOs, psychics and the afterlife with the parapsychology researcher Jeffrey Mishlove. He tells it himself, without naming a source, and he raises it because it would tie an old folk belief to the glowing orbs people report today.","japanese-museum-curator-shown-ufo-footage-at-johnson-space-c":"A Japanese museum curator named Takano Josen is said to have been taken to a particular building at NASA's Johnson Space Center in Houston and shown photos of UFOs. Michels passes this on from Takano's own account. If true, it would mean NASA keeps a collection of UFO images it does not show the public.","japanese-museum-s-claimed-piece-of-the-roswell-crash-1947":"A man in Japan named Takano Josen, who keeps a museum, claims to own a piece of the Roswell crash. That is the 1947 event near Roswell, New Mexico, where many believe a craft not made by humans came down. Michels mentions it himself in passing, saying only that the man is in Japan and makes the claim, so nothing is said about what the piece looks like or how he got it.","japanese-musha-experiment-in-transmission-oil":"Michels says an experiment described in a paper by the Japanese researcher Takaaki Musha got a successful result after the whole device was submerged in transmission oil. He mentions it in passing, from the paper, without saying more about what the device was meant to do.","jared-birchall-a-mormon-elder-as-elon-musk-s-handler-2010":"Jared Birchall, a man described as a Mormon elder, is said to be Elon Musk's \"handler,\" the person who manages all of Musk's affairs. The researcher Matthew Pines says this on the show without naming a source, and he raises it as a link worth noticing between Musk and the wider story of who watches over powerful people.","jason-sands-radars-at-roswell-and-white-sands-disrupting-cra-1947":"Powerful military radars at Roswell and White Sands, New Mexico, brought down alien craft in 1947, the story goes. Roswell is the town where a crash in 1947 became the most famous UFO story of all. Michels says he heard a man named Jason Sands talk about high-powered radars at both places \"actually disrupting the flight paths of the crafts\". A researcher who goes by UAPGerb puts it more bluntly. He describes the problem Truman and Eisenhower faced as alien spaceships that \"have crashed due to high-powered radar and we now have their bodies\", and he names no bases. A former DARPA scientist named John Blitch adds details (DARPA is the Pentagon's advanced research agency). He says the radar was taken out to the desert, White Sands being \"a natural place\", and its power was turned up, and then a saucer came down during a storm. On another episode Michels calls this the conventional account. He credits it to Philip J. Corso's book The Day After Roswell, published in 1997, and describes it as a directed-energy attack, meaning a weapon that fires a beam of energy. If true, it would mean the first recovered craft were brought down by accident, or on purpose, by American equipment.","jason-sands-they-want-their-stuff-back":"A man named Jason Sands, who says he worked near a secret program holding recovered non-human craft, is said to have warned that \"they want their stuff back and they're going to kill everybody involved in the program if they don't get it.\" Michels repeats this from Sands's appearance on Joe Rogan's show. It would mean whoever owns the craft is willing to use deadly force to get them back.","javanese-cemetery-marriage-and-sex-ritual-at-a-prince-s-grav":"In Java, in Indonesia, people are said to perform a ritual of marriage and sex at the grave of a prince in a cemetery. The writer Peter Levenda tells Michels that this is \"true,\" that \"there's a number of cemeteries in Java that do this,\" and that he visited one of them himself. If he is right, it is a living tradition of sex rites performed at the graves of the dead.","jay-stratton-s-three-star-slide-shown-to-congress-2019":"Sometime between 2019 and 2021, according to a UFO sceptic named Mick West, an official named Jay Stratton showed people in Congress a slide of what West says were three stars. West tells this to Michels in passing, without naming a source. As West tells it, lawmakers were shown ordinary stars as part of the case for something unexplained.","jc-said-navy-running-majestic-12-was-right-1996":"In Los Angeles around 1996, a source known only as JC is said to have told Bryce Zabel, who made a story about Majestic 12, that one detail was right: the man running it came from the Navy. Majestic 12 is the name given to a supposed secret group of officials in charge of the UFO question. Zabel tells Michels this from his own experience, recalling that JC said 'we had the guy running Majestic 12 be from the Navy.' If it were true, a piece of fiction would have matched how the real group was run.","jean-louis-naudin-s-vacuum-tube-tests":"A French experimenter named Jean-Louis Naudin is said to have run his experiments inside vacuum tubes. Gary McKinnon, a hacker who says he got into NASA's computers, tells Michels that 'he's done it in vacuum tubes', though he names no source and the telling says little about what the experiment was. Testing in a vacuum would rule out air as the cause of whatever effect was seen.","jeffrey-epstein-tape-with-steve-bannon-as-a-garbled-echo-of-2010":"The mathematician Eric Weinstein says a taped interview that Steve Bannon did with Jeffrey Epstein shows Epstein half-repeating things he had picked up from people who really knew what they were talking about. Weinstein points to Epstein's errors. On the tape Epstein says he founded the Santa Fe Institute, a science research center, in 1990 to 93, though others founded it in 1984. He also gets something wrong about quarks and the physicist Murray Gell-Mann. To Weinstein the mistakes are a garbled echo of real conversations, which would mean Epstein moved among people with serious inside knowledge.","jeffrey-kripal-s-compilation-of-anomalies":"A researcher named Jeffrey Kripal is said to have collected hundreds and hundreds of anomalies, strange events people report that do not fit ordinary explanation. The skeptic Michael Shermer mentions this work in passing while talking with Michels, as the kind of research he has in mind, and he does not say where he learned of it.","jeffrey-kripal-s-mit-lecture-on-flying-saints-attended-by-a":"A scholar named Jeffrey Kripal gave a lecture at MIT on flying saints, stories of holy people said to rise into the air, and the audience included the unnamed head of revolutionary technologies at Lockheed Martin's Skunk Works, its secret advanced-aircraft division. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. It would mean that top aerospace engineers take old stories of levitation seriously.","jeffrey-kripal-s-night-experience-sharing-a-room-with-whitle":"The religion scholar Jeffrey Kripal is said to have had a strange experience in the middle of the night while sharing a room with Whitley Strieber, a writer known for his accounts of visits by unknown beings, at the Esalen Institute, a retreat centre in California. All Kripal could think, the story goes, was \"oh my god, this is real and it's happening now.\" The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as Kripal's own account, and it matters because it would mean a sceptical scholar felt for himself what Strieber describes.","jellyfish-probe-the-radar-beam-and-are-seen-flying-in-a-pair-2025":"In February 2025, objects nicknamed \"Jellyfish\" for their shape were seen probing a radar beam, and for the first time two of them were seen flying together, about 6 km north and south of the site. This comes from the team at Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs to appear, speaking as witnesses watching their own radar. If true, it would mean the objects come in groups and react to being watched.","jellyfish-uap-debunked":"A strange object nicknamed the jellyfish, filed as a UAP (short for unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the official term for a UFO), turned out to be something ordinary after all. That is according to an unnamed speaker in a clip played on the show, who speaks as an official voice and says \"it ended up being debunked\" and \"it was not a UAP.\" If so, one of the more talked-about objects has an everyday explanation.","jellyfish-uap-explained-as-a-group-of-drones-2020":"A video of a \"jellyfish\" UAP (the newer official term for a UFO, an unidentified anomalous phenomenon) is really just a group of drones flying together, according to the well-known skeptic Michael Shermer. He tells Michels the drones look \"interconnected, you know, Bluetooth or whatever,\" and \"coordinated,\" giving an ordinary explanation for footage that others present as something unknown.","jeremy-corbell-and-the-missing-page-of-the-shellenberger-rel-2024":"A government report on UFO data, made public in 2024, is said to have lost a page, and with it the name of Jeremy Corbell, a figure in UFO circles. The hip hop artist Flying Lotus tells Michels that Corbell \"went on that whole tangent about the missing page\" and his name being \"taken off,\" and says it \"rubbed me the wrong way.\" The document is the so-called Immaculate Constellation report, which is unsigned and of disputed origin. The journalist Michael Shellenberger delivered it to Congress, and Rep. Nancy Mace entered it into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024. A former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown later said he wrote it, and a Pentagon spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. The report calls itself a \"public version of the author's report,\" cleared for release by the State Department. Mace told the hearing it ran to 12 pages, but the copy on her House site has 11 unnumbered pages. Neither that copy nor the hearing record says what the fuller version contained, so the claim that a missing page carried Corbell's name rests on the tellers' word.","jesse-feels-strieber-s-implant-2020":"Jesse Michels says he has touched an implant in the body of the writer Whitley Strieber. An implant here means a small object that some people say was put into their bodies during encounters with non-human beings. On one episode Michels calls feeling it \"the weirdest thing in the world\" and says Strieber told him it \"moves with his own metabolism\"; on a later one he places it in Strieber's ear and does not repeat the part about it moving.","jesse-marcel-library-posts-article-about-greg-rogers-2025":"A NASA doctor named Greg Rogers, who says he saw a UFO in a secret hangar, tells Michels that in 2025 he found out the Jesse Marcel Library in Montana had put up an article about him. The library is named for Jesse Marcel Sr., the military officer who handled debris from the reported 1947 UFO crash near Roswell, New Mexico. For Rogers it is a sign his account has been taken in by people who keep the Roswell story alive.","jesse-marcel-s-unbendable-foil-thin-metal-1947":"The story is that debris from the 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico, which the Army first announced as a flying disc, included metal so light and thin it felt like nothing in the hand. In a recording played on the show, Jesse Marcel Sr., the Army Air Force intelligence officer who went to the crash site, says: \"We found a piece of metal about a foot and a half to 2 ft wide and about 2 or 3 ft long. Felt like you had nothing in your hands. It wasn't any thicker than the foil out of a pack of cigarettes.\" Michels tells it again in another episode, crediting an interview with the journalist Bob Pratt and naming Jesse Marcel Jr. as the officer at the site, and he adds that when the officer held a lighter to the foil-like material, it did not burn. The two tellings agree on the thin, foil-like metal and differ on which Marcel is speaking and on the burning test. If true, it would mean the wreckage was made of a material unlike anything known in 1947.","jesse-marcel-says-weather-balloon-was-a-cover-story-1978":"Jesse Marcel Sr., an Air Force officer at Roswell, New Mexico, when debris was found there in 1947, is said to have come forward in 1978 and called the military's weather-balloon explanation a cover story. Michels tells it himself, saying Lieutenant Colonel Marcel claimed he was \"forced to do this photo op\" with a weather balloon. In another episode Michels dates this to 1979 and adds that Marcel said he handled \"memory metal,\" metal that springs back into shape. A guest named Bob Lazar, who mentions the general Roger Ramey, says Marcel claimed the debris in the press photographs \"wasn't the stuff that we found.\" If true, it would mean the real Roswell material was swapped out and hidden.","jesse-michels-also-told-of-an-incoming-craft-2025":"Michels says that he too was told a craft is slowly moving toward Earth, a claim that has been passed around by intelligence sources in 2025 and 2026. He says it while talking with Jeremy Corbell, a filmmaker who reports on UFOs: \"I was told that, too,\" and he does not name who told him. If it were true, it would mean someone inside government thinks something is on its way here.","jesse-s-contact-with-astrid-stuckelberger-who-then-went-sile-2023":"Michels says that in 2023 a woman named Astrid Stuckelberger got in touch with him by email, urgently, saying something like 'We must talk now.' He tells it from his own experience, and he says she then went silent and the conversation she pressed for never came.","jesse-s-conversation-with-tulsi-gabbard-on-uap-2025":"Michels says that in 2025 he had a short conversation with Tulsi Gabbard, the Director of National Intelligence, about UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. He mentions it himself, in passing, during a talk with a Senate staffer named Kirk McConnell, and says only that it was \"a brief conversation with her uh around this topic.\" If so, it would mean the subject has reached the person at the top of US intelligence.","jesse-s-france-trip-and-the-before-sunset-synchronicity":"Michels says he once booked a trip to France on a whim, stayed the whole summer, and ended up dating a girl in Paris, in a way that echoed Richard Linklater's film Before Sunset, a romance set in Paris. He tells it himself, about his own life, on an episode about synchronicities, meaningful coincidences, and prophetic dreams. He offers it as an example of life seeming to follow a script laid down beforehand.","jesse-s-interview-with-puthoff-and-weinstein-on-remote-viewi":"Michels says the physicist Hal Puthoff touched on remote viewing, the claimed ability to describe distant places or objects with the mind alone, when Michels interviewed him together with the mathematician and researcher Eric Weinstein. Michels brings this up himself, in passing, during an episode with a Navy scientist, and says nothing about what Puthoff actually said.","jesse-s-month-of-bad-luck-after-the-visit-including-an-armed-2021":"Michels says that after a visit, in a talk about Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, he had a month of bad luck in Los Angeles around 2021 or 2022, including an armed gunman running into the building where he works. He tells this about himself, on an episode with the ranch's owner. The suggestion is that strange trouble can follow people who get close to the ranch.","jesse-visits-aztec-crash-site-with-the-ramseys-1948":"A flying saucer is said to have crashed near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948 and been hauled away by the military, and Michels says he has walked the site himself with Scott and Suzanne Ramsey, researchers who he says have spent more than $600,000 over decades on the case and have 'so much evidence'. He describes trees splintered where a craft seemed to have been dragged out, and says the visit was filmed. In one telling he says he went with his editor and does not mention the Ramseys.","jesse-visits-the-richter-island-site-at-bariloche":"Michels says he has been to the small island off the coast of Bariloche, in Argentina, that is tied to a scientist named Ronald Richter. It comes up while he talks with the researcher Jason Jorjani about Nazi programmes to build flying craft: \"I've been there actually. I've seen it.\" Michels tells it as his own visit, and if the stories about the place are true, he has stood where a secret project tied to that history once ran.","jessica-utts-presidential-address-on-psychic-functioning-evi-2016":"The statistician Jessica Utts, who studied the evidence for psychic abilities, is said to have served a year as president of the American Statistical Association, and to have used her presidential address in 2016 to talk about that evidence. The writer Eric Wargo, who writes about precognition and dreams, mentions it to Michels without a source, saying she 'was the president of the American statistical society and stuff one year.' He raises it as a sign that a leading figure in a mainstream science treated the evidence seriously.","jessica-utts-review-of-stargate-documents":"Michels says that in 1995 a statistician named Jessica Utts, whom he calls president of the American Statistical Association, reviewed the declassified data from Stargate, a US government programme that tested \"remote viewing\", the claimed ability to see distant places with the mind, much of it gathered at the Stanford Research Institute. He tells it himself across many episodes, saying she worked alongside a psychologist named Ray Hyman, a firm skeptic of the paranormal, came in doubtful and concluded the effect was real and \"extremely replicable\", even \"overwhelming\", with the review done for the CIA. If true, it would mean a leading statistician found solid evidence that psychic perception works.","jessica-utts-statistical-analysis-of-cia-stargate-psychic-sp-1970":"The CIA's psychic spying research, known as Stargate, which tested whether people could see distant places with the mind, is said to have produced results far too good to be chance. Michels tells this from the work of the statistician Jessica Utts, who studied the programme's data, and says the results \"fall way below\" the p values, the odds of a fluke, that you would expect. If true, it would mean psychic spying actually worked.","jesus-learned-the-resurrection-technique-in-egypt":"The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met beings from elsewhere, says that Jesus went to Egypt and learned what he calls \"the resurrection technique\" there. Strieber puts this forward as his own view, drawing on his book Jesus: A New Vision and on the Gospel of Thomas, an early collection of sayings of Jesus that is not in the Bible. If true, the resurrection would be a learned method rather than a one-time miracle.","jesus-s-return-in-the-first-photograph-of-the-shroud-1898":"The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met non-human beings, tells Michels that Jesus returned in 1898, at the moment the first photograph was taken of the Shroud of Turin, the linen cloth kept in Turin that bears the faint image of a man and is revered by many as Jesus's burial cloth. \"That was the moment he returned and we saw him on the shroud,\" he says, an idea he lays out in his own book Jesus: A New Vision; it would make the second coming something that already happened, quietly, through a camera.","jesus-survived-the-crucifixion-in-a-pre-planned-ritual":"Some say Jesus did not die on the cross in Jerusalem, and that the Last Supper and the crucifixion were a planned and staged ritual in which he was drugged and later brought back round. Jesse Michels describes it as a Gnostic reading of the New Testament, Gnostic meaning early Christian groups whose writings were left out of the Bible. He cites the Gospel of Thomas, one of those writings, along with the religion scholar Elaine Pagels and the writer Brian Muraresku, author of The Immortality Key. In a separate conversation the researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels, without naming a single source, that Jesus did not die on the cross and that \"there was a lot more to the story going on behind the scenes,\" and he mentions Muraresku and the Gospel figure Joseph of Arimathea. If true, the central event of Christianity would have been a carefully staged rite.","jet-engine-honda-car-driven-to-los-alamos-1982":"In 1982, Bob Lazar says, he took his wife's little Honda, built a jet engine for it 'from scratch', and drove the car to Los Alamos, the New Mexico weapons laboratory, with El Mirage Dry Lake in California also part of the story. Lazar tells this to Michels about himself, as a sign of the kind of hands-on engineer he was.","jets-scrambled-from-santa-cruz-and-anapolis-on-the-night-of-1986":"On one night in 1986, Brazil's air force sent fighter jets up to chase UFOs. They flew from the air base at Santa Cruz in the state of Rio de Janeiro and from the Anápolis air base in Goiás, \"in the attempt to intercept the UFOs.\" A former Brazilian defense minister named Aldo Rebelo tells Michels this, and coming from someone who once ran the country's armed forces, it suggests the military took the objects seriously enough to try to catch them.","jewel-beetles-mating-with-stubby-beer-bottles":"In the Australian outback, men drink beer from short brown bottles called stubbies, throw them out and drive off, and male jewel beetles flock to the bottles and try to mate with them, mistaking them for females. The researcher Don Hoffman tells Michels this without naming a source. He uses it to argue that our senses evolved to show us only what helped us survive, so we might miss aliens even if they were all around us.","jfk-and-donald-menzel-s-clearances-1930":"Donald Menzel, a Harvard astronomer famous for explaining UFOs away, held some of the highest security clearances there were, with the CIA, the National Security Agency and the Navy, and Michels says Menzel admitted this to John F. Kennedy, whom he knew through Kennedy's interest in astrophysics at Harvard. Michels tells this himself on three episodes without naming a source; the later two leave out Kennedy, and one adds that Menzel was friends with Vannevar Bush, the engineer who ran American wartime science, calling Menzel a \"deep state spook.\" If true, it would mean the best-known public UFO skeptic was deep inside the secret world.","jfk-asks-mccone-for-a-full-ufo-briefing-on-june-5-1963":"On June 5, 1963, about five months before he was killed, President Kennedy is said to have asked to be fully briefed on UFOs, going to John McCone, his new CIA director, after forcing the former director Allen Dulles into retirement. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells this on the show twice: once as his own summary naming McCone, and once crediting a man named Dick Billings as his source, without naming McCone. If true, Kennedy was trying to get inside a secret that was being kept even from him.","jfk-assassination-linked-to-the-uap-topic-read-from-kash-pat-1963":"The 1963 assassination of John F. Kennedy in Dallas may be linked to UAP, the government's term for UFOs (unidentified anomalous phenomena), according to Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he once helped retrieve a UFO. Barber tells Michels this is his own reading of cryptic remarks Kash Patel made about the JFK files: \"what I hear him saying cryptically is that the JFK assassination is linked to the UAP topic.\" If he read Patel rightly, it would tie one of America's best-known murders to secrets about these objects.","jfk-back-channels-with-khrushchev-and-dulles-1960":"John F. Kennedy is said to have opened secret back channels to the Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev to ease tensions and cut nuclear arms, while men like Allen Dulles, the CIA director Kennedy had fired, wanted him dead. Michels tells it himself, saying Kennedy was \"trying for détente\" and that Dulles \"basically wanted to take him out.\" A guest named Dan Sheehan gives more detail, passing on what others told him: the two leaders traded \"18 different letters\" that went \"completely secretly outside of the channels of the State Department, outside of the channels of the CIA.\" Sheehan also ties the Wall Street bank Brown Brothers Harriman to the targeting of Kennedy. If true, it would mean Kennedy's peace efforts made enemies inside his own government.","jfk-briefed-at-los-alamos-1962":"In 1962, President John F. Kennedy is said to have gone to Los Alamos, the New Mexico nuclear weapons laboratory, and been briefed there a few weeks after some earlier event. Michels tells it himself, hedging with \"I think\" on the timing, and does not say what the briefing covered, though he raises it in a conversation about what Kennedy may have known about UFOs.","jfk-killed-for-ufo-cooperation-breaking-the-power-structure-1963":"The claim is that President John F. Kennedy was killed in 1963 at least partly over UFOs. If it were true, the assassination would be bound up with a secret about craft that are not ours. A researcher named Matthew Pines gives a reason, without naming a source. He says that if a president went too far toward détente, toward getting rid of nuclear weapons, or toward working with the Soviets on space and on 'these alleged potential UFO technologies,' that 'would cut straight to the core' of a hidden deal among those who hold power. Steven Greer, a UFO researcher, goes further. He says it is 'a fact,' that he knows 'the people who have the actionable intelligence' showing Kennedy 'was killed over this issue,' and that he is convinced Robert F. Kennedy, the president's brother, knew it. The most striking voice is Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser whom Michels describes as a Kennedy adviser. Asked whether UFOs played a part, Malmgren answers: 'Yes. I think it was probably the number one issue.' Michels later repeats this to Joe Rogan, calling it 'kind of a deathbed confession' from an 89-year-old adviser. The tellers disagree on how it worked: only Pines ties the killing to cooperation breaking a deal, while Greer and Malmgren state the link as knowledge or belief without that explanation.","jfk-knew-about-ufos-before-becoming-president-1961":"John F. Kennedy knew all about UFOs long before he took office in 1961, according to Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser who says he handled UFO material himself. He tells Michels that \"JFK fully knew all about UFOs long before he became president,\" and in one version he says Kennedy learned it in the 1940s from James Forrestal and naval intelligence. If true, the subject reached the White House with the man himself.","jfk-letter-to-cia-director-mccone-requesting-ufo-data-1963":"Ten days before he was killed, President Kennedy is said to have written to the head of the CIA asking for all its data on unexplained objects. The letter is a memo from John F. Kennedy to John McCone, the CIA director, dated November 12, 1963. Michels tells the story on many episodes, drawing on what he has read. He says it was released through the Freedom of Information Act, a US law that lets anyone request government records, in 2006 in one telling and in 2005 in others. In his telling Kennedy asks McCone for 'all data on unknowns' so that the Americans could coordinate better with the Soviets, and he places it after Kennedy had fired the previous director, Allen Dulles. Michels repeatedly stresses that the letter is 'of controversial provenance' and says it should be looked into to see whether it is real. He credits researchers including John Greenewald Jr. and William Lester. The UFO historian Richard Dolan also describes it on the show, as Kennedy 'directing the Central Intelligence Agency to coordinate UFO data with the Soviet Union.' If genuine, it would mean a president was pressing his spy agency on UFOs, and wanted to share what it knew with the Cold War enemy, just before his death.","jfk-on-the-harvard-astronomy-department-s-board-of-overseers-1950":"John F. Kennedy is said to have sat on the board of overseers for Harvard's astronomy department in the 1950s. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel tells Michels this and credits notes kept by the UFO researcher Stanton Friedman. It matters to her because it would link a future president to the department at a time when, as others on the show tell it, UFO questions were being handled there.","jfk-s-planned-ussoviet-alliance-against-the-nordics-1960":"John F. Kennedy is said to have planned an alliance with the Soviet Union in which the two countries would go to the moon together and, \"if necessary,\" jointly \"defend the freedom and independence of humanity\" against the \"Nordics,\" tall, human-looking beings that some UFO lore describes. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would mean Kennedy saw a threat from beyond Earth as big enough to join with America's Cold War rival.","jfk-visits-los-alamos-a-month-after-malmgren-s-debrief-1962":"In 1962, President Kennedy is said to have gone to the Los Alamos nuclear laboratory in person, soon after a young Harold Malmgren had been briefed there. Michels says it came a month after Malmgren met a man named Lawrence Preston Gise, and he calls it \"not really that normal\" for Kennedy to come and check things himself. Pippa Malmgren, appearing on the show with Harold Malmgren, says it was \"literally a couple of weeks later\" and that LBJ and both men's teams came too. Both tell it as a hint that the president took a personal interest in whatever Malmgren had been shown.","jfk-wanted-to-declassify-ufo-files-before-his-death-1960":"John F. Kennedy is said to have declared, in the years before his death, that he wanted to declassify secret files about aliens and UFOs. The claim comes from Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of one of Russia's republics, who says he was taken by aliens himself in 1997 and tells Michels that \"John Kennedy declared he wanted to declassify some secret files concerning aliens and UFOs.\" If true, it would mean a sitting president meant to open records the government was keeping secret.","jim-keller-argues-the-moon-landing-videos-were-faked-1969":"The engineer Jim Keller, Michels says, took the \"fake\" side in a debate about whether the videos of the 1969 Moon landing were staged. Michels tells this himself, as something he knows of Keller, without saying when or where the debate took place beyond the Bay Area. Coming from a respected engineer, it would mean the doubt about the footage is not just a fringe position.","jim-lacatski-of-the-dia-part-of-aawsap-writing-about-the-pro-2008":"Michels points out that Jim Lacatski, a rocket scientist at the DIA, the Pentagon's military intelligence agency, who worked on AAWSAP, a Pentagon-funded study of unexplained aerial phenomena begun in 2008, is now writing publicly about the subject. Michels says this himself and calls it strange, since it would mean a government insider from the study is now speaking openly about it.","jim-marrs-sphere-moves-under-its-own-power-2021":"A man named Jim Marrs kept a sphere at his home in Texas that is said to move under its own power. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels he went to the home and \"saw this sphere\" himself around 2021, and he also names a man called Patrick Jackson in connection with it. If true, it would be an object that moves with no visible means of doing so.","jim-penniston-told-the-beings-come-from-8-000-years-in-the-f-1980":"In 1980, during an encounter in Rendlesham Forest in England, a witness named Jim Penniston is said to have been told by the beings: \"we are you,\" come from 8,000 years in the future. They are said to have told him they were having trouble reproducing, and that this is why they were taking reproductive cells. A researcher named Mike Masters retells this to Michels without naming a source. If true, it would mean the visitors are our own descendants.","jim-rider-speech-on-devas-fairies-and-the-veil-thinning":"Michels says that Jim Ryder, a figure tied to Lockheed Martin's space division, gave a speech that you can find online about devas (nature spirits of Hindu and Buddhist tradition), fairies and jinn (spirits of Islamic tradition). Michels says Ryder also talks about extrasensory perception and \"the veil thinning\" between our world and another. Michels tells this himself. If true, people inside a major defence company take the spirit world seriously.","jim-ryder-s-talk-the-garment-of-god":"Michels says that James Ryder, also called Jim, gave a talk called \"The Garment of God\" that everyone should hear. Michels describes Ryder as a former vice president of the arms maker Lockheed Martin and a director at Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret advanced-projects unit. Michels tells this himself across several episodes, and his details shift: once he dates the talk to 2018, once he places it at an event for an organisation called the Lucis Trust, and once he calls it \"the Garment of the Gods\" and ties it to Lockheed's space division, and he never says what the talk contains.","jim-ryder-tried-to-transfer-ufo-to-bigelow-2008":"In 2008 a Lockheed Martin Space Systems executive named Jim Ryder is said to have tried to hand over a \"UFO\" to Bigelow Aerospace, a private space company. Michels says this is now known from the former Pentagon official Luis Elizondo and \"a bunch of other people\", and brings it up alongside a speech Ryder gave. If true, it would mean a major defence contractor was holding an unexplained craft.","jim-s-books-documenting-government-funded-anomaly-research-a-2008":"A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet says books by a writer he calls Jim, including follow-ups he thinks are called Future Visions and New Insights, fully document government-funded research at Skinwalker Ranch in Utah, a ranch known for reports of strange events. He tells Michels the books record the anomalies and many witnesses from roughly 2008 onward, during a Pentagon-funded study of unexplained phenomena that was run through a private contractor, which, if true, would mean the government paid to investigate these events.","jim-s-pottery-epiphany-and-fixation-on-ancient-sites-1976":"In 1976, Jim Weiner says, an idea about pottery came into his head \"fully developed,\" all at once, and after it he became fixed on ancient sites. Weiner, a man who says he has been taken by aliens, tells Michels this as something that happened to him. He tells it as a sign that the experience may have put ideas into his mind.","jim-semivan-of-the-cia-briefed-that-psi-and-mind-matter-conn":"A CIA officer named Jim Semivan and others are said to hold that psychic abilities are real and that mind and matter are not separate but connected, and that this is the \"real core truth\" tied to UFOs. Michels says this himself, without naming where he heard it, in an episode with the researcher Grant Cameron. If true, it would mean people inside the CIA see the mind as central to the UFO question.","jim-semivan-opened-the-door-for-mcgwier-s-visit-to-bledsoe":"A visit to Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says he has had UFO encounters and whose story appears in the book UFO of God, was approved by Jim Semivan, a retired CIA officer. Bob McGwier, an engineer who has worked as a CIA contractor, tells Michels that while he was there, Bledsoe told him Semivan had said, 'oh yeah you should let Bob in, this should be interesting.' McGwier tells it as a sign that people with intelligence backgrounds were quietly taking an interest in Bledsoe.","jim-semivan-s-7-hour-briefing-at-the-cia":"A man named Jim Semivan was told by very senior officials to book a room at CIA headquarters for six to eight hours, because the briefing they had for him would take that long. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something Semivan told him, and calls it \"the 7-hour briefing\"; if true, the matter behind it was big enough to fill most of a working day inside the CIA.","jim-semivan-s-bedroom-encounter-with-beings-and-a-guardian":"A man named Jim Semivan is said to have woken to find strange beings in his bedroom while he stood there with his wife, and someone, a kind of guardian, standing behind him. Grant Cameron, a researcher who collects UFO accounts, tells Michels this, quoting Semivan: 'I was standing there with my wife and these beings were there and there was somebody behind me.' If true, the encounter came with a protector figure as well as the visitors.","jim-semivan-told-ryan-bledsoe-the-family-may-be-hybrids":"A man named Jim Semivan is said to have told Ryan Bledsoe that Bledsoe and his family may be hybrids, part human and part not: \"we think you're hybrids,\" and the reason they keep having strange experiences \"is because you may have more non-human DNA.\" The researcher Jason Simosa passes this on to Michels as something Semivan \"purportedly\" said to Bledsoe, so he is repeating it at second hand. If it were true, it would mean some people's encounters come from something in their own bloodline.","jim-semivan-won-t-say-what-is-interesting-about-sekret-machi":"Asked what was interesting about Sekret Machines, a book series put out by a man named Tom DeLonge, a man named Jim Semivan reportedly answered flatly: \"No, I'm not going to tell you.\" The researcher Jason Simosa tells this on the show, summarising the exchange without naming where he heard it. He tells it because the refusal hints that the books hold something Semivan knows about and will not explain.","jim-simons-nsa-background-and-renaissance-as-a-black-box-1960":"The mathematician Jim Simons, who founded the very secretive investment firm Renaissance on Long Island, once worked as a codebreaker for the NSA, and the analyst Matthew Pines suggests to Michels that the link never really ended. Pines, who gives no source, says Simons 'used to work for the NSA' as a 'cryptologic codebreaker' and that 'there's no such thing as an ex-NSA guy.' The idea is that Renaissance may be a black box with ties to the intelligence world that nobody outside can see.","jimmy-carter-exposes-the-stealth-program-1980":"In 1980, while Jimmy Carter was running for reelection, he is said to have mentioned the stealth program, the secret work on aircraft that radar cannot easily see, and something is said to have happened to him because of it. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this in passing, without naming a source, and he leaves it unclear what followed.","jimmy-carter-s-1969-ufo-sighting":"In 1969, years before he became president, Jimmy Carter is said to have seen a UFO. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels that Carter was standing with 11 other people on the steps of a Lions Club in Georgia when it happened, and Michels later repeats the story, crediting Sheehan. A researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala tells it without the other witnesses: Carter, he says, \"saw a bright light appear in the distance\" in the western sky. The story matters to the tellers because the witness later became president.","jimmy-carter-s-ufo-briefing-confirmed-by-alonzo-mcdonald-1977":"President Jimmy Carter is said to have been given a briefing on UFOs in the Oval Office in 1977, and a man named Alonzo McDonald is said to have confirmed it. The physicist Eric W. Davis, who has worked as a scientist for the Pentagon, tells Michels he did not see this himself: McDonald told Davis's research group back then that he had spoken with the staff who attended the briefing, and with Carter, and \"it happened.\" Davis also points to a Carter Library list of attendees dated June 1977. If true, a sitting president was formally told about UFOs.","jimmy-corsetti-s-theory-that-atlantis-was-the-richat-structu":"A man named Jimmy Corsetti has argued that Atlantis, the lost island city in Plato's writings, was the Richat Structure, a huge ring-shaped formation in the desert of Mauritania. Michels mentions it in passing, saying Corsetti put the theory forward and naming the podcaster Joe Rogan alongside him. The researcher Graham Hancock takes it more cautiously. He says, \"I get that the ring structure resembles how Plato described Atlantis,\" but goes on to qualify the claim. If true, it would place Plato's story on a real spot of land.","jimmy-savile-at-chequers-and-the-hospital-key-1960":"Jimmy Savile, a famous British entertainer, was so powerful in the United Kingdom that he spent his Christmases at Chequers, the country house where the British prime minister spends the holiday. Jay Anderson, a guest on the show, tells Michels this as part of a story about Savile's reach between 1960 and 2011, offering it as a sign of how close Savile stood to the top of British power.","jimo-borjigin-s-research-on-endogenous-dmt-and-terminal-luci":"A University of Michigan researcher named Jimo Borjigin studies DMT, a powerful psychedelic that the brain may make on its own, and also terminal lucidity, when dying people suddenly become clear-minded again. Michels mentions her work himself, in passing, in a conversation about DMT and what it might reveal.","joe-firmage-s-inter-nasa-group":"A man named Joe Firmage is said to have started a group called Inter NASA, modelled on the Academy of Arts and Sciences. Michels mentions it himself, hedging with \"I believe\", and does not say what the group did.","joe-mcmoneagle-blind-remote-viewing-of-the-same-mountain-bas-1973":"Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe a distant place with the mind alone. Michels says that years after an earlier viewer looked at certain mountain sites, among them Mount Perdido in Spain, a talented remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle was given the same coordinates without being told what they were. He reportedly saw the same mountains with the same underground command centres, and also saw that the bases were linked like nodes in a network. Michels tells it himself, in an episode about a UFO book he says the Pentagon tried to censor.","joe-mcmoneagle-remote-views-in-a-scif-each-year-so-the-starg-1970":"Every year, a psychic spy named Joe McMoneagle is said to have gone into a SCIF, a sealed room for classified work, and done \"remote viewing,\" the claimed ability to see distant places with the mind, so that officials would renew the budget of Stargate, the US government's psychic spying programme. The researcher Chris Ramsay tells Michels this without naming a source. In his telling, the demonstrations worked well enough to keep the money coming.","joe-mcmoneagle-remote-views-the-typhoon-submarine-1979":"In 1979, the story goes, a remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle used remote viewing, the claimed ability to describe faraway places with the mind alone, to describe a huge submarine being built at a Russian shipyard on the White Sea. Eight months after those sessions ended, the Russians floated out the Typhoon, \"the biggest submarine ever built,\" with its missile tubes placed the way the sessions had it. Paul Smith tells this on two episodes as something McMoneagle told him, and if it is true the mind could see secret military work no camera could reach.","joe-rogan-proposes-weinstein-sit-down-with-bob-lazar":"The podcaster Joe Rogan once suggested that the mathematician and commentator Eric Weinstein sit down with Bob Lazar, a man known for claims about secret work on alien craft. Weinstein tells Michels this himself, recalling that Rogan said, 'Let's sit down.'","john-alexander-at-romanek-s-house-the-flashlight-answers-que":"In the backyard of a man named Stan Romanek's house in Colorado, a flashlight is said to have answered questions. John B. Alexander unscrewed it until it sat right on the edge between on and off, laid it on the picnic table, and then Romanek started asking questions and the flashlight responded. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels the story as Alexander told it to him, and it would mean some unseen intelligence was talking back through the light.","john-alexander-knew-of-rendlesham-before-anyone-in-the-us-1980":"John B. Alexander, whom Michels calls a Colonel, is said to have known about Rendlesham, a reported UFO incident near RAF Woodbridge in England in 1980, before almost anyone else in the United States. Michels says this himself on three episodes. Once he traces it to a well-connected friend he does not name, who said Alexander \"gave the readout\". The other two times he gives no source, and once he drops the limit to the United States and says Alexander knew \"before anybody else\".","john-alexander-s-book-finds-no-majestic-group":"In his book about UFOs, John B. Alexander, a man who looked into the subject from inside the US government's world, says he never found any group like Majestic, the secret panel some people believe was set up to manage the UFO question in the highest circles. The philosopher Jason Jorjani brings this up with Michels as his own summary of the book, which he does not date, alongside the work of the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée; the book reports that Alexander did not find such a group, and whether that means none exists, the summary does not settle.","john-alexander-s-paranormal-research-in-92-countries-includi":"A researcher named John B. Alexander has travelled to some 90 or 92 countries studying the paranormal, according to Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs. Bledsoe tells Michels that Alexander's research ranged \"from churches to ceremonies in Mongolia,\" among the reindeer herders there, which paints him as someone who has looked for strange experiences all over the world.","john-alexander-s-telepathic-nordics-at-a-las-vegas-casino":"A retired Army colonel named John B. Alexander says he saw Nordics, the tall, human-looking, striking beings of UFO lore said to talk by telepathy, in a hotel casino in Las Vegas. A philosopher named Jason Jorjani mentions it as one of Alexander's 'interesting stories'. Michels says Alexander told him directly about a couple of Nordics he saw there, and that they were strikingly beautiful.","john-alexander-sent-to-look-into-the-hutchison-effect":"A man named John Hutchison is said to have made objects levitate, an effect named after him, and a retired Army colonel named John B. Alexander was sent to look into it. Michels tells it himself, placing Hutchison in California and saying \"Alexander's tasked with looking into it\". The writer Nick Cook says he asked Alexander directly, and that Alexander told him: \"Unfortunately, when I set up all the equipment under laboratory conditions, none of it worked.\" Cook places it in Vancouver. The story matters because it shows the government checking a claim of levitation, and, in Alexander's telling, getting nothing.","john-aristotle-phillips-atomic-bomb-junior-thesis-at-princet-1976":"In the mid-1970s a Princeton junior named John Aristotle Phillips wrote his junior thesis about an atomic bomb, with the physicist Freeman Dyson as his chosen adviser. The researcher Eric Weinstein brings it up on the show from memory, without naming a source, as an example of how far a student could get on such a subject. The thesis itself is dated in the story to about 1976 to 1977.","john-barrow-s-anti-kardashev-scale-and-omega-minus-civilizat-1990":"In the 1990s, according to the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, a scientist named John Barrow proposed what Gallimore calls an \"anti\" version of the Kardashev scale: a way of ranking advanced civilizations, with so-called Omega-minus civilizations at its far end. Gallimore brings it up with Michels in passing, without saying more about where it comes from or what it shows.","john-dee-s-angelic-work-led-to-colonizing-america-as-a-new-a":"The English scholar John Dee and his partner Edward Kelley said they talked with angels, and according to this theory that work 'led directly to the colonization of the United States of America and the new Atlantis', meaning America was meant as an ideal new society. Matthew Brown, who presents himself as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells this to Michels without naming a source. If it were true, the founding of America would have roots in occult contact with spirits.","john-dee-s-enochian-angel-language":"John Dee, a magician and chief spy at the court of Queen Elizabeth I, is said to have claimed he spoke with angels through a special language he called Enochian. Michels brings this up himself, without naming a source, in two episodes; in the second he describes Dee as a magician who blurred the line between magic and talking to angels, and leaves out the language. It is told as an early case of someone claiming contact with beings beyond the human.","john-ford-s-prosecution-1990":"In the 1990s on Long Island, a man named John Ford was prosecuted, accused of a plot against members of a local governing body that involved poisoning one man's toothpaste with radioactive material. The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels this without naming a source, and he also mentions a person named Elaine Douglas. Dolan tells it as part of a wider talk about the price some people paid for pursuing UFOs, though he does not spell out the link.","john-glenn-s-fireflies-were-ice-crystals-1962":"The \"fireflies\" the astronaut John Glenn reported seeing outside his capsule during his 1962 orbit of Earth aboard Friendship 7 were little ice crystals, according to the skeptic Michael Shermer. He mentions it to Michels in passing, as an example of a strange sight in space that turned out to have an ordinary cause.","john-glenn-urged-harry-reid-to-investigate-ufos":"The astronaut and senator John Glenn pushed Harry Reid, the Senate leader, to look into UFOs, which fed into a Pentagon-funded UFO study program whose name the transcript catches only as \"Asa.\" Michels says this himself in a documentary episode, without naming a source. If true, it would mean one of America's most trusted astronauts helped start the government's modern UFO research.","john-hutchinson-and-the-hutchinson-effect-with-john-alexande":"A Californian named John Hutchison is said to cause the \"Hutchinson effect\", in which metal equipment around him seems to float in the air and break down. Michels raises it himself without naming a source, and notes that a man named John B. Alexander seems to show up whenever people report paranormal events like this one.","john-hutchison-s-lab-in-vancouver-1990":"An experimenter named John Hutchison ran a home lab in Vancouver, Canada, from about 1990 to 2009, where he worked on strange effects linked to the idea of antigravity. Nick Cook, a writer who has looked into antigravity research, tells Michels he visited the home and saw that it \"was stuffed full of old oscilloscopes,\" and he mentions an engineer named Boyd Bushman alongside Hutchison. If Hutchison's work was what it is said to be, a lone tinkerer would have touched on technology governments are said to keep secret.","john-keel-s-non-government-men-in-black-1960":"In the 1960s, according to the philosopher Jason Jorjani, a writer named John Keel collected cases of Men in Black, the mysterious visitors in dark suits said to call on UFO witnesses, where the visitors \"clearly\" were not government agents. Jorjani says this on the show in passing, citing Keel's accounts. If so, the visitors would be something other than officials hushing things up.","john-lear-a-billion-aliens-live-in-the-mountains-by-a-highwa-1980":"A man named John Lear used to say that aliens, as many as a billion, live inside the mountains along a highway. Bob Lazar, who knew Lear in the 1980s, repeats this to Michels as something Lear \"used to tell me\". He passes it on as one of Lear's ideas, without saying whether he believes it himself.","john-lear-admits-he-made-up-dulce-firefight-story-1990":"John Lear, a pilot who spread the story of a gun battle between American forces and alien beings in an underground base beneath Archuleta Mesa near Dulce, New Mexico, later said of that story, \"Oh yeah, I made that up.\" The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels that Lear really said this, in the early 1990s, in the course of a story about Paul Bennewitz, an Albuquerque businessman who came to believe in the Dulce base; if true, one of the best known tales of the era began as an invention.","john-lear-helped-get-bob-lazar-the-s4-job-1987":"The story goes that a pilot named John Lear helped Bob Lazar get his job at S4 in the late 1980s. Lazar is a man who says he worked on alien craft at S4, a hidden site near Area 51, the secret Air Force test base in Nevada. Lear supposedly did this on behalf of people high up in the UFO secret. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the newer official word for UFOs) tells Michels that Lear \"is on record saying in an interview\" that he helped get Lazar the job. Michels has his own version from a Lear interview. In it, Lear says a Navy admiral he calls McClellan came to him. In UFO lore \"MJ level\" means a member of the secret elite committee said to govern the subject, and Lear says this admiral was MJ level. The admiral told him, \"We got to get Bob on the job because we know that we can hide his...\" Michels takes that to mean Lazar was placed so his part could be denied later. Both tellers mention a Mike McConnell or McClellan. If true, Lazar's hiring was not luck but was arranged from inside.","john-lear-people-live-in-the-sun-1980":"Sometime in the 1980s, a man named John Lear told Bob Lazar that there are people living in the sun. Lazar repeats it to Michels in just those words, \"there are people living in the sun,\" a claim that, if true, would put life in the last place anyone would look for it.","john-lear-possibly-fed-disinformation-to-lazar-1970":"The idea is that Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft in Nevada, got his story from John Lear, a pilot who flew cargo planes for the CIA in the 1970s, and that the CIA used Lear to push the story out to the public. Michels raises this himself in three episodes without naming a source. He calls Lear a close friend of Lazar who leaked the story and set aviation records, and he adds that Lear spent a decade around Area 51, the secret Nevada test base, where the guards knew him. If true, the best-known account of a recovered craft would have been seeded on purpose.","john-lear-s-1977-lake-level-photos-of-groom-lake-and-the-fil":"A man named John Lear is said to have had original photographs from 1977 of Groom Lake, the dry lakebed in Nevada better known as Area 51, the secret Air Force test site, taken from ground level and showing a chain stretched across the way in. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels about the pictures as Lear described them, and the story also involves the film being swapped, which the words given do not spell out. Pictures like that would be rare early views of a place the government kept hidden.","john-lear-s-area-51-access-and-the-f-117-leak-1980":"A man named John Lear is said to have had access to Area 51, a secret military test site in Nevada, in the 1980s and to have leaked details of the F-117, the then-secret stealth fighter, to the Las Vegas reporter George Knapp. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about spending two days with Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft there. It is told to show that Lear really did know secrets from inside the base.","john-lear-the-moon-was-built-at-jupiter-and-towed-to-earth":"A man named John Lear claimed the moon is a made object: \"they built the moon on Jupiter,\" manufactured it there, and \"towed it into Earth's orbit.\" Bob Lazar passes this on to Michels as something Lear told him. If it were true, the moon would be an artificial thing someone put next to Earth.","john-lilly-s-contact-with-ecco-and-solid-state-intelligence-1970":"John Lilly, the scientist who invented the sensory deprivation isolation tank, claimed to be in contact with two non-human intelligences: one he called SSI, a \"solid state entity\" he described as an alien being, and another Michels recalls as \"the echo, the earth coincidence control mechanism or something.\" Michels tells this himself on several episodes, from Lilly's own claims and his book Programming and Metaprogramming in the Human Biocomputer, saying the contact came in the tank, that SSI told Lilly it was manifesting on Earth but needed more silicon to do so, and once calling Lilly a CIA scientist working from the 1950s to the 1970s. Michels raises it because, if taken seriously, it would read like an alien intelligence asking for computer chips.","john-mack-and-terence-mckenna-on-the-western-paradigm-bursti-1990":"The idea here is that the Western way of seeing reality is about to break open, and that people will come to see it as \"incredibly myopic and closed.\" Michels tells it himself, crediting the line to John Mack, a Harvard psychiatrist who studied people reporting alien encounters, and putting it beside Terence McKenna, a writer on psychedelics, and Thomas Kuhn, the philosopher who wrote about how scientific worldviews get overturned. If they are right, much of what Western science rules out would be back on the table.","john-mack-at-harvard-his-friend-bud-hopkins-and-thomas-kuhn-1990":"In the 1990s John Mack headed the psychiatry department at Harvard, and he had been friends since childhood with a man named Budd Hopkins. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and links the story to the philosopher of science Thomas Kuhn and advice Kuhn gave Mack, though he says nothing more about what that advice was.","john-mack-moves-away-from-a-physical-explanation-of-abductio-1990":"The psychiatrist John Mack, who studied people who said they had been abducted by aliens, is said to have moved over the years away from the idea that abductions were physical events. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, who studies the psychedelic drug DMT and mentions the DMT researcher Rick Strassman in the same discussion, tells Michels that Mack 'became less convinced that this was a purely physical nuts and bolts phenomenon.' Gallimore gives no source, and brings it up as a hint that the experiences may happen in the mind or in some other realm.","john-mack-persecuted-by-harvard-over-his-abduction-research-1990":"John Mack, a Harvard professor who studied people who said aliens had taken them, was persecuted by Harvard for that work in the 1990s, and the university even held a trial over it. Michels says this in passing, in his own words and without naming a source. He tells it to show what a respected academic risked by taking the subject seriously.","john-mack-s-abductee-interviews-at-harvard-1990":"In the 1990s a Harvard psychiatrist named John Mack is said to have looked into people's stories of being abducted by aliens, working on a personal, case-by-case level rather than as a lab study. Michels tells this himself, first to the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb, calling Mack his 'predecessor' and the head of the psychiatry department. In a later episode he tells it differently: Mack was a private clinician whose patients began bringing up alien encounters on their own, and Michels also links the story to the historian of science Thomas Kuhn.","john-mack-s-abductee-research-judged-too-open-minded-1990":"In the 1990s at Harvard, a researcher named John Mack studied people who said aliens had taken them, and the skeptic Michael Shermer tells Michels he thinks Mack was \"a little too open-minded\" about them. Shermer gives this as his own view and names no source. His point is that Mack accepted these accounts too readily.","john-mack-s-abductees-and-the-double-identity":"John Mack, a psychiatrist who studied people who say they were taken by alien beings, found that his subjects at first felt terror, the shock of seeing a grey being, the small grey-skinned figure of abduction accounts, in their bedroom, but that often the abductee then began to identify with the being. Michels tells this himself, recalling what the parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove told him in an earlier conversation; if so, the experience changes the person's sense of who they are.","john-mack-s-abduction-cases-were-done-by-humans-1990":"The alien abduction cases sent to John Mack, a Harvard psychiatrist who studied people saying they had been taken by aliens, were all really carried out by humans, according to the UFO researcher Steven Greer. Greer tells Michels, without naming a source, that Mack did not know that every case \"being funneled to him\" was staged this way. If true, it would mean someone was deliberately faking abductions and feeding them to a respected researcher.","john-mack-s-death-in-a-car-crash-sheehan-looked-into-it-2004":"John Mack, a Harvard psychiatrist who studied people who said they had been taken by aliens, died after being hit by a car in 2004, and the lawyer Dan Sheehan says he looked into the death within hours. Sheehan tells Michels he \"dug it out\" and found the driver, who he says was simply drunk and is still in prison. Coming from a man who investigated it at the time, his account answers the suspicion that the death was something other than an accident.","john-mack-s-experiencer-support-group-and-its-two-camps":"The psychiatrist John Mack ran support groups near Boston for \"experiencers,\" people who say they have been in contact with or taken by aliens, and Jim Weiner says the people in them split into two camps. Weiner, who says he was taken himself, went to the groups and tells Michels: \"I saw two different camps of people.\" He tells it as a sign that people who report the same kind of experience do not all understand it the same way.","john-mack-s-final-manuscript-when-worldviews-collide-2000":"John Mack, a Harvard psychiatrist who studied people who said they had been taken by aliens, was working on a final manuscript between 2000 and 2003 titled When Worldviews Collide, according to the lawyer Dan Sheehan, who knew him. Sheehan tells Michels the title as someone who knew the manuscript himself. It points to an unpublished last statement of Mack's thinking.","john-mack-s-harvard-study-of-alien-abductions-encouraged-by-1990":"John Mack, head of the psychiatry department at Harvard, is said to have spent the last part of his career studying people who said aliens had abducted them, and in the 1990s he used hypnotic regression and holotropic breathwork, a method of deep, rapid breathing, to bring back his patients' memories of being taken. Michels tells this on several episodes and sometimes adds that Mack was friends with Thomas Kuhn, the philosopher of science who wrote The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, though one telling leaves Kuhn out. The point is that a senior Harvard psychiatrist took abduction accounts seriously.","john-mack-s-personal-quest-behind-his-abduction-research-1990":"Michels points to The Believer, a book by the journalist Ralph Blumenthal about John Mack, the Harvard psychiatrist who in the 1990s took seriously people who said aliens had abducted them, and about the personal search that drove that work. Michels calls the book 'amazing' and brings it up in a conversation with the parapsychology researcher Jeffrey Mishlove, without saying more about what is in it. It matters because it would mean a respected Harvard doctor came to the abduction question through a quest of his own.","john-mccarthy-s-bureau-of-simulation-story-1968":"John McCarthy, who ran the artificial intelligence lab \"on the hill\" at Stanford, used to tell a story: one day you get a letter from the \"Bureau of Simulation\" that begins \"Dear Sir,\" and says a man as predictable as you can be replaced by a simulation. The researcher Jacques Vallée tells Michels he heard McCarthy tell it, around 1968 in one telling and only \"in the early AI era\" in the other. The point of the story is that if a person's behaviour can be predicted, a machine could stand in for him.","john-mcdowell-s-forensic-assessment-of-the-bodies-2020":"A forensic investigator named John McDowell says there is data showing that some of the small, three-fingered bodies found in Peru 'can be interpreted to be perhaps living organisms,' rather than objects that were made. McDowell gives this as his own assessment, and Michels adds that McDowell saw the bodies in person, joined by Jim Caruso, Denver's chief medical examiner and a forensic pathologist. If true, it would mean the bodies were once living beings.","john-mills-sees-ufos-at-malmstrom-1990":"In the early 1990s a retired missile technician named John Mills is said to have seen many UFOs at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana. Michels mentions this himself in passing, without saying where he got it, as one more report from a base where strange sightings near the missiles keep coming up.","john-mills-sees-zigzagging-object-near-malmstrom-1993":"In January 1993, a missile technician named John Mills, part of a combat targeting team, was driving back with a six-person crew to Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, where he was stationed, when he saw a bright object zigzagging around the sky. Michels tells this himself on three episodes, and in one he adds that when Mills got back, officers told him the base had been swarmed by UFOs. Michels raises it as one more case of unexplained objects showing up at bases that hold nuclear missiles.","john-mulholland-trained-the-cia-in-deception-and-wrote-a-man-1950":"A man named John Mulholland is said to have trained the CIA in deception and trickery in the 1950s and to have written a manual on it. Michels mentions it himself, without naming a source, in a talk with the magician Chris Ramsay. It would mean the CIA drew on the craft of misdirection in the same years it was dealing with UFO reports.","john-norseen-at-allagash-during-1976-abduction-testing-subcu":"In 1976, men in Allagash, Maine, reported being abducted, and one of them says a man named John Norseen told him he had been there at the time, running \"a TTR, tag, trace, retrieve exercise with a subcutaneous device that they were testing\": a tracking device placed under the skin. The witness, who is not named, relays this on the show as what Norseen told him. If true, part of what happened at Allagash would have been a human test of tracking implants.","john-norseen-s-biofusion-thought-controlled-cockpit-1999":"In 1999 a researcher named John Norseen, who worked with Lockheed Martin, published a report titled \"Biofusion Reflexive Control and Neuro-ergonomic Integration in Adaptive Cockpits.\" Michels says Norseen later described it as a system that uses brain-computer interfaces to \"convert thoughts into commands,\" in effect a cockpit run by the pilot's mind. Michels tells it himself, on an episode about a UFO book he says the Pentagon tried to censor. If true, thought-controlled aircraft were being worked on decades ago.","john-norsine-s-patent-for-piloting-a-craft-with-the-mind":"An engineer named John Norsine is said to hold a patent, which Michels calls \"locked\", for \"essentially how to fly a UFO with your mind.\" Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. In a later episode he adds that the patents, which he ties to the arms maker Lockheed, involve \"neural imprints and neural interfaces for flying aircraft\", and that a study he credits to Battelle looked at the same idea in the early 2020s. If true, it would mean someone had worked out on paper how to steer a craft with thought alone.","john-p-craven-s-the-silent-war-on-retrieving-exotic-technolo":"A book called The Silent War, by John P. Craven, a man Michels describes as \"high up in the Navy,\" tells of the US Navy recovering \"exotic technology\" and secret objects from the seafloor, according to Michels. He brings it up on several episodes as something he has read. Once he says submarines made the retrievals, once he calls it \"covert operations\" in general, and once he puts Craven beside another book, Blind Man's Bluff, calling these men \"the godfathers\" of the Navy's seafloor recoveries. If he is right, the Navy has quietly pulled unexplained hardware from the ocean floor.","john-paul-and-episode-9-as-proof-of-an-afterlife-2024":"In Atlanta in 2024, the filmmaker Ky Dickens says, a young man named John Paul gave her what she took as proof of an afterlife. Dickens made The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary series about nonspeaking people said to read minds, and she tells Michels this from her own experience, pointing to the ninth episode. If true, it would mean that consciousness carries on after death.","john-paul-and-lily-go-on-telepathic-dates-from-different-flo-2023":"Two young people named John Paul and Lily go on \"dates\" by mind alone, often sitting on different floors of the same house in Atlanta while, it is said, they communicate. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made a documentary about children said to read minds, tells Michels this as something she heard from a woman named Libby around 2023; if true, it would be telepathy between two people who cannot see each other.","john-paul-ii-establishes-vatican-observatory-for-ufo-researc-1987":"Pope John Paul II is said to have signed a decree in the late 1980s setting up a special observatory inside the Vatican to study UFOs. Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of one of Russia's republics who says he was taken by aliens in 1997, tells Michels: \"The pope signed a decree and a special observatory was established right there inside the Vatican.\" If true, it would mean the Catholic Church took the question seriously enough to watch the skies for it.","john-peterson-s-1998-arlington-institute-meeting-on-extrater":"In 1998, John Peterson's Arlington Institute held a small invitation-only meeting, a \"mini conference to discuss what it would be like in a world where extraterrestrials are openly acknowledged as living among us.\" The researcher Richard Dolan tells Michels about it as he heard it from Katherine Austin Fitz and Peterson. If it is true, people were already planning in the 1990s for the day aliens living among us would become public.","john-ramirez-on-human-alien-hybrid-programs":"A former official named John Ramirez, who held the rank of GS-15, one of the highest grades in the US civil service, has said publicly that he is sure the United States has programmes to make human-alien hybrids. Michels repeats this on the show as something Ramirez has said, and he thinks Ramirez said it on his own show too. If true, it would mean the government is not just studying visitors but mixing with them.","john-ramirez-says-everyone-is-waiting-for-elizondo-to-talk-a-2022":"In 2022, a man named John Ramirez said, roughly, that everyone was waiting for Luis Elizondo, a man prominent in the UFO debate, to talk about hybrids, meaning beings that are part human and part alien. The researcher Jason Simosa brings up the remark on the show as something Ramirez said. Simosa uses it to suggest that people on the inside expect hybrids to be part of what eventually comes out.","john-trump-examines-tesla-s-confiscated-files-1943":"The story is that when the inventor Nikola Tesla died in 1943, the government seized his papers, and the man sent to go through them was John G. Trump. Trump was an MIT professor, the country's leading radar expert, and the uncle of Donald Trump. Michels tells it often, with growing detail. He says the FBI raided Tesla's Bryant Park apartment in New York and locked the files in a vault. He says Trump was tasked with seeing whether any of it \"would be of service to national security\" or would give the United States \"tactical warfare advantages.\" In one episode Michels adds that Trump was interviewed by the UFO writer Bill Moore in a documentary about the Philadelphia Experiment, a story of a secret 1943 Navy test to make a ship vanish. In that episode he also names Vannevar Bush, who ran American science during the war. The online researcher RedPandaKoala tells it more simply: Trump was \"so smart\" that the government called him in after Tesla died to go through \"all of his notes.\" The story matters to the tellers because it would mean Tesla's most advanced ideas went straight into government hands.","john-trump-likely-involved-in-the-1952-washington-wave":"In 1952 a wave of UFO sightings over Washington, D.C. was, in the words of a researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala, \"very radar-intensive,\" and that researcher believes a man named John G. Trump would have been involved in it. RedPandaKoala tells Michels this as a personal opinion, without a source. If right, it would tie a named figure to the government's handling of the event.","john-wheeler-s-u-diagram-of-the-self-observing-universe":"The physicist John Wheeler drew the universe as a letter U: one tip is the Big Bang, and the other tip is an eyeball staring back at the Big Bang, the idea being that the universe gives rise to observers who in some sense look back on and help make its own beginning. The writer Eric Wargo describes the diagram to Michels in his own words, beside the ideas of the physicists Yakir Aharonov and John Cramer, as support for the view that effects can reach backward in time.","johns-hopkins-president-rumored-on-mj-12-doing-nhi-autopsies-1940":"A scientist named Detlev Bronk, who was president of Johns Hopkins University, is rumored to have sat on Majestic 12, a supposed secret group said to have handled crashed UFOs, and to have performed autopsies on the bodies of non-human intelligence, meaning beings that are not human. Michels raises it himself and calls it a rumor, without naming where it comes from. If true, it would put a major university president at the centre of a hidden study of alien bodies.","johnson-s-first-meeting-after-kecksburg-was-with-the-head-of-1965":"After the 1965 Kecksburg incident, when an object reportedly fell from the sky in Pennsylvania, President Lyndon Johnson's first meeting back at the White House is said to have been with the head of emergency management. The Canadian UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels that 'the head of FEMA was there the day after', though he names no document. If so, the incident would have been treated at the top as an emergency.","johnson-space-center-as-likely-home-of-a-secret-crewed-suppl":"Michels suggests that a secret space program, one he describes as moving crews and supplies, would most likely be run out of NASA's Johnson Space Center in Houston. He puts it forward himself, pointing out that this is also where Gary McKinnon, a hacker who broke into NASA computers, says he found UFO-related images and spreadsheets. If so, a hidden program would be sitting inside one of NASA's best-known sites.","johnston-island-scuttlebutt-soviet-orbital-nuke-shot-down-ob-1962":"Around 1962, the talk on Johnston Island, a tiny Pacific atoll, was that an explosion high in the sky lit up the whole South Pacific, set off UFO reports, and that something fell toward the island and was recovered. Bob Jacobs, who says he was told this, tells it to Michels, and Michels repeats it on another episode. In Jacobs's version the Soviets had put a nuclear weapon into orbit above them, JFK told Khrushchev \"Take that down,\" and the UFO shootdown story covered for the Soviet nuke. If true, a recovered object and a hidden nuclear standoff lie behind the sightings.","joining-skywatcher-to-lead-psionic-operations-2024":"In 2024 a man named Jordan Jose joined Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs to appear, to lead its \"psionic operations,\" meaning efforts to reach the objects with the mind. Jose tells Michels this himself, saying he \"elected to kind of join the team,\" and names Jake Barber, James Fer and a man called Alex as part of it. If it works as described, human thought would be a way to make contact.","joining-the-project-in-1978-by-1979-no-defense-against-remot":"Joe McMoneagle, who served as a remote viewer in a secret US Army programme at Fort Meade, Maryland, says that within a year of starting the team had learned there was \"no defense\" against remote viewing, the claimed ability to see distant places or things with the mind alone. He tells Michels he was brought into the project in October 1978, and that by July 1979 they knew this. If true, it would mean no secret could be fully shielded from a trained viewer.","joint-chiefs-propose-operation-northwoods-1962":"In 1962 the Joint Chiefs of Staff are said to have proposed Operation Northwoods, a secret military plan, which the researcher Dan Sheehan brings up with Michels in connection with the Air Force general Curtis LeMay. Sheehan names it only in passing, without a source, and does not say what the plan involved.","joint-chiefs-staff-colonel-confirms-a-ufo-cover-up-1980":"In Washington in the 1980s, a colonel on the staff of the Joint Chiefs of Staff told the physicist John Brandenburg that there is a UFO cover-up. Brandenburg tells Michels he asked him outright, \"Is there really a UFO cover-up?\" and the colonel said, \"Yes. That's all I can tell you. The rest you can figure out for yourself.\" If the account is true, a military officer at the top of the Pentagon admitted that the government hides what it knows about UFOs.","joint-stars-synthetic-aperture-radar-proven-in-desert-storm-1980":"A system called Joint Stars, an airborne radar developed in the 1980s, proved itself in the Gulf War known as Desert Storm, according to Greg Rogers, a former NASA doctor who says he was there for its development. Its key tool was synthetic aperture radar, a radar that builds detailed pictures of the ground from the air. Rogers tells Michels that after the Desert Storm demonstration, 'we decided we will never go into combat without' it.","jolly-west-mk-ultra-and-cult-creation-1950":"A psychiatrist named Jolly West, for a long time the head of psychiatry at UCLA, was one of the architects of MK Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control experiments of the 1950s and 60s, and he was probably using those methods on Charles Manson, Michels says across several episodes. In one he adds that West wrote back and forth with Sidney Gottlieb, the CIA chemist behind MK Ultra, and credits the book CIA Doctors by the psychiatrist Colin Ross; in another he credits the book Chaos by the writer Tom O'Neill and carries the story into the 1980s. If true, it would mean a government mind-control programme had a hand in making a cult.","jolly-west-s-mk-ultra-violence-creation-for-mass-pacificatio-1960":"Michels offers his own theory that a man named Jolly West, working in California in the 1960s under MK Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control experiments, helped stir up local violence tied to Charles Manson. He says he thinks it was 'local violence creation for mass pacification or violence reduction', meaning small, shocking outbreaks staged to calm the wider public. He presents it as his own hunch, in an episode about Jeffrey Epstein and secret elites.","jonathan-weygandt-and-the-crashed-craft-in-peru-1990":"The story is that a young US Marine found a crashed alien craft in the Peruvian jungle and was then held and questioned for days. The Marine is Jonathan Weygandt, a lance corporal, who tells it himself on the show. He uses his own rough drawing of the jungle and the craft, which was \"embedded\" in the rock. A UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells it in more detail. He says Weygandt was in Peru for Operation Laser Strike, a counter-narcotics mission, and that his patrol came to a clearing where an enormous egg- or teardrop-shaped craft was stuck in a cliff face. A man named James Fox adds that Weygandt went up to it and saw a green liquid dripping out. In UAPGerb's account, men in Department of Energy rain jackets took Weygandt and flew him by CH-47 transport helicopter to an obscure military base. There he was interrogated for days, and Chinese and Germans were present alongside Americans. Michels repeats the story, crediting UAPGerb, and calls Weygandt part of a crash retrieval team. The tellers place it in 1997, while the story's own dating is the early 1990s. If true, it would mean crashed craft are recovered in secret, with several countries involved.","jordan-from-sonica-predicts-the-next-day-s-conflict-in-the-s-2025":"During a 2025 field operation by Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs to appear, a team member named Jordan Jose predicted that the next day they would see \"conflict in the sky.\" A Skywatcher member named James Valor tells Michels that Jose, whom he calls \"our leading specialist from Sonica,\" came up to him and a colleague named Alex and said so. If the prediction came true, it would suggest Jose could sense such events before they happened.","jordan-of-skywatchers-shown-a-sphere-in-the-gate-program":"A man named Jordan Jose, of a group called Skywatchers, says that as a late teen in something called the GATE program, which the telling does not describe, military brass, men in suits and scientists brought in a case, opened it, and showed him a sphere, \"the same exact sphere\" he had seen elsewhere. A man who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this as Jose told it to him. It would mean officials already had in hand the kind of object Jose had seen.","jordan-psionically-calls-in-an-egg-object-that-fred-baker-fi-2024":"A member of Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon UFOs, says that in August 2024 one of its \"psi-actives\" (people it says can call objects with their minds), Jordan Jose, \"probably caused\" an object to appear that may have been an \"egg\", and that Fred Baker filmed it. The Skywatcher teller was there and describes it to Michels as happening at the group's event site, on the patio and in the backyard, while admitting, \"I don't know what is this.\"","jordan-s-gifted-program-childhood-and-sky-watcher":"A man named Jordan Jose is said to have taken part in special programmes for gifted children when he was a kid, and the story links him to something called Sky Watcher, which the telling names but does not explain. The online UFO researcher and witness known as UAPGerb tells Michels that Jordan is the only person he knows who has talked about \"working on programs\" as a child.","jordan-s-mri-shows-dense-white-matter-in-the-caudate-and-put-2020":"Jordan Jose says a brain scan showed unusually dense white matter in two structures deep in his brain, the caudate and the putamen. Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, tells Michels he has a \"very unique\" caudate and putamen, and connects this to research by the scientist Garry Nolan. If true, it would be a physical mark in his brain that sets him apart and might help explain why he was chosen.","jorjani-invited-to-join-the-knights-of-malta-in-an-initiatio":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani says he was once invited to join the Knights of Malta, an old Catholic order of knights, in an initiation ritual on the island of Malta. Jorjani tells Michels this about himself, in passing: 'I was invited, by the way, to join the Knights of Malta at one point.'","jorjani-s-dinner-with-jacques-vallee-on-time-travel-2016":"Around 2016 or 2017, the philosopher Jason Jorjani had dinner at the home of Jacques Vallée, a longtime UFO researcher, and asked him about time travel as an explanation for UFOs. Jorjani tells Michels this himself, describing \"a nice dinner and a fairly intimate conversation\" in which he \"brought this question up with him.\" If Vallée took the idea seriously, it would mean one of the field's leading thinkers considered that the visitors might come from another time rather than another planet.","jorjani-s-great-aunt-talking-with-dead-relatives-on-her-deat":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani says that on her deathbed in a hospital room his great aunt was not just describing dead relatives but holding conversations with them. Jorjani tells Michels he was there and saw it himself. He offers it as a case suggesting that the dying may be in contact with the dead.","jorjani-s-persian-grandmother-talking-to-his-long-dead-grand":"As she lay dying, Jason Jorjani's Persian grandmother began talking to his grandfather, who had been dead for years. Jorjani, a researcher, tells Michels he saw it happen: \"She starts talking to my dead grandfather.\" He tells it as one example of the visions dying people are said to have, the kind that hint the dead may still be near.","joseph-mcmoneagle-awarded-the-legion-of-merit-for-remote-vie-1970":"An Army remote viewer named Joseph McMoneagle is said to have won the Legion of Merit, a US military decoration, for more than 200 instances of remote viewing, meaning the claimed ability to describe distant places or events by the mind alone. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, as a sign that the military took remote viewing seriously enough to reward it.","joseph-mcmoneagle-given-legion-of-merit-for-remote-viewing":"Joe McMoneagle, called \"remote viewer number one\" and one of the best at remote viewing (trying to describe distant places or hidden things with the mind alone), was awarded the Legion of Merit, a US military decoration, for that work in the Army. Michels says this himself on three episodes and mentions that McMoneagle has been a guest of his. He raises it to show that the military gave a formal honour for work that skeptics reject.","joseph-mcmoneagle-s-remote-viewing-of-soviet-bases-and-the-s-1970":"A man named Joseph McMoneagle is said to have used remote viewing, the practice of trying to describe distant places or events with the mind alone, to find Soviet nuclear bases and predict critical events. Michels says McMoneagle also helped locate James Dozier, an American general who had been kidnapped in Italy. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as a sign that the mind may reach beyond the senses.","joseph-of-arimathea-brings-the-grail-to-england-and-receives":"A legend says that Joseph of Arimathea, a figure from the Gospels, brought the Grail, the sacred cup of Christian legend, to England. There he met a pagan chieftain named Arviragus, who gave him 120 hides of land at Glastonbury as a sacred precinct for the first Christian church. A researcher named Randall Carlson tells Michels this, presenting it as legend.","joseph-of-copertino-levitating-during-mass":"Joseph of Copertino, a 17th-century Franciscan friar in Italy, had ecstatic visions from childhood, and many people saw him float into the air during Mass. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode on UFOs and religion. He brings it up as an old religious account of something like the strange events reported today.","joseph-of-copertino-s-levitation-records":"Joseph of Copertino, an Italian Catholic saint, is said to have risen into the air, and records of those levitations still exist. The religious scholar Diana Pasulka tells Michels she has the Joseph of Copertino records and has been reading them, and calls them \"pretty intense\", in a conversation that also names the physicist Hal Puthoff; she does not say what in them she finds so striking.","joseph-smith-possibly-killed-by-government-agents-and-the-st-1840":"Joseph Smith, who founded the Mormon church, 'actually may have been assassinated by agents of the government', according to the analyst Matthew Pines, who links this to Deseret, the name the Mormons gave the state they set up in Utah in the 1840s. Pines tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, the government would have seen Smith's movement as a threat worth killing over.","josiah-describes-author-max-davis-s-popping-house-and-nearby":"A boy named Josiah, one of the nonspeaking children in a documentary about children said to read minds, is said to have described the house of an author named Max Davis as 'popping', along with a barn nearby, without being told about them. Josiah's mother tells it as something she witnessed, recalling the question put to Davis: 'are you a barn man Max, like there's animals or there's a farm by you.' If true, it would mean Josiah picked up details about a stranger's home by some means other than the ordinary senses.","josiah-knows-about-max-s-mixtape-and-private-beach-motel":"A boy named Josiah, one of the children in a documentary about kids said to read minds, wrote things about a man named Max Davis that he had no ordinary way of knowing: that Max's music was \"being banded together,\" like a mixtape, and that he loves his \"sacred time at the motel,\" a private beach motel on the Gulf of Mexico coast. Ky Dickens, who made the documentary, tells Michels this as a case of the telepathy she says these children have.","josiah-knows-about-max-s-nap-and-the-first-fruits-chapter":"A boy named Josiah, one of the children in the Telepathy Tapes documentary about nonspeaking autistic children said to read minds, is said to have known things about a man named Max Davis that no one told him: the next day he wrote to Davis, 'I hope your nap was nice', and he is also said to have known about a chapter called 'First Fruits'. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made the documentary, tells Michels this as Davis and the family related it, as an example of what looks like telepathy.","josiah-knows-max-went-into-the-woods-to-pray":"A child named Josiah is said to have known, without being told, that a man named Max Davis \"went into the woods and was praying last night.\" The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made a documentary called The Telepathy Tapes, tells Michels this as an example from her work, passing on what Josiah said. It would mean some children can pick up what others are doing or thinking by telepathy, that is, mind to mind.","josiah-says-auntie-told-an-angel-to-tell-him-something":"A boy named Josiah, one of the children in a documentary about kids said to communicate by telepathy, told his mother that 'Auntie told an angel to tell me' something, and his mother explains that Auntie was his great-aunt, who had died at 89. His mother tells this on the show as something she witnessed, and she has written about him in a book called Josiah's Fire. If true, it would suggest the boy was getting messages from the dead.","journalist-threatened-with-arrest-at-the-esa-sergeants-schoo-1996":"A Brazilian journalist says that in 1996 he was threatened with arrest while investigating the Varginha case, a Brazilian affair in which the army is said to have captured a living creature. He says it happened at the army's Sergeants' School of Arms in Três Corações, and that he had \"never been arrested for doing my job, neither before nor after this case.\" The unnamed journalist tells this on the show, and his point is that the military went unusually far to stop reporters asking questions.","journalists-on-cia-payroll-operation-mockingbird-1960":"In the 1960s, the story goes, many American journalists were secretly paid by the CIA, in an effort known as Operation Mockingbird. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, who makes documentaries about UFOs, tells Michels he heard that \"a lot of the journalists\" were on the payroll, and that \"we've got their names now and what they were paid.\" If true, the CIA could have shaped what the press reported, UFOs included.","jp-cahn-s-10-000-offer-to-scully-1950":"In 1950 in San Francisco, a man named JP Cahn is said to have approached the writer Frank Scully and offered him $10,000 for the rights to his story, and Scully turned him down. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a crash said to have left 16 alien bodies, where the refusal matters as a sign Scully would not sell what he claimed to know.","jp-morgan-funds-tesla-s-wardenclyffe-wireless-energy-work-1900":"Around 1900 the banker J.P. Morgan is said to have funded the inventor Nikola Tesla's work at Wardenclyffe, his laboratory in New York, on sending energy through the air without wires. Michels tells it himself on two episodes, once calling it wireless energy transmission and once \"wireless instantaneous transmission,\" as an example of Tesla technology he suggests was bigger than history admits.","jpl-counter-clerk-hands-over-negatives-of-the-old-city-on-ma-1980":"Sometime in the 1980s, a clerk at the counter of NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California handed over photo negatives from the mapping of Mars and is said to have remarked, \"oh this is the old city on Mars.\" Joe McMoneagle, author of the book Mind Trek, tells Michels this as something he was there for. If true, it would mean people at the lab casually spoke of a city on Mars.","jpl-manager-life-on-mars-means-death-for-jpl":"At the Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena, as managers discussed the chance of finding life on Mars, one of them is said to have scolded the rest: \"Don't you realize that life on Mars means death for JPL?\" Michels relays this as something a man named Whitley Strieber told him. If true, it would mean some managers saw such a discovery as a threat to the lab itself.","jpl-viking-mission-lead-believed-there-was-evidence-of-life-1970":"The man at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory who was in charge of the Viking missions, the spacecraft NASA sent to Mars in the 1970s, is said to swear that there was evidence of life on Mars. Michels says he has heard this, without naming the man or where he heard it. If true, it would mean a mission leader believed life had been found on another planet decades ago.","jsoc-crash-retrievals-and-knights-of-malta":"Jesse Michels says that, according to the journalist Seymour Hersh, General Stanley McChrystal, a former head of JSOC, the Joint Special Operations Command that runs the US military's elite special forces units, and many of JSOC's own members are Knights of Malta, an old Catholic order of knighthood. Michels raises it in an episode with a former presidential adviser who says he handled UFO material, as part of a story that links JSOC to recovering crashed craft, which would put that work in the hands of a small, tightly bound circle.","jude-quotes-the-book-of-enoch":"The story is that the Letter of Jude, a short book of the New Testament, quotes the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text left out of most Bibles. A filmmaker named Tim Alberino says so on the show, as a researcher stating it without a source: \"Jude takes verbatim from the book of Enoch.\" Set beside his claim is the opening of Enoch itself, in the translation by R. H. Charles published in 1917. At chapter 1, verse 9, it says: \"And behold! He cometh with ten thousands of His holy ones To execute judgement upon all, And to destroy all the ungodly.\" It goes on to say he will \"convict all flesh\" of their ungodly deeds and of the \"hard things\" sinners have spoken against him. That passage shows what Alberino means Jude repeats: a Lord coming with a vast host of holy ones to judge the ungodly. The tellings do not give Jude's own wording, so how close the two are word for word is left to the reader to check. If Alberino is right, an early Christian writer treated Enoch as scripture worth quoting, and that matters to people who read Enoch's stories of fallen angels and giants as history.","julian-jaynes-bicameral-mind-theory-a-shift-in-consciousness":"Julian Jaynes, a Yale professor, argued that human consciousness changed in the ancient world, sometime after 1000 BC, when an older \"bicameral,\" or two-chambered, way of thinking broke down. Michels brings him up himself on an episode about the pyramids and names, a little loosely, Jaynes's book The Origin of Consciousness in the Breakdown of the Bicameral Mind. If true, ancient people may have experienced their own minds very differently from the way we do.","julius-evola-stayed-with-the-ss-to-the-end-of-the-war":"The Italian writer Julius Evola is said to have stayed with the SS until the end of the war, and the SS reportedly let him use their libraries. The researcher Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of a longer talk about esoteric thinkers close to the Nazis.","junior-hicks-files-on-uinta-basin-ufo-sightings-since-1950":"A man named Junior Hicks kept detailed files on hundreds of UFO sightings in Utah's Uinta Basin going back to 1950, according to Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange events. Fugal tells Michels this as something he has learned from the record, crediting a book called The Utah UFO Display, and offers it as evidence that the area's strangeness is long documented, not new.","kabbalists-meet-the-dalai-lama":"When a group of Jewish Kabbalists, followers of the Jewish mystical tradition, met the Dalai Lama, the two sides found they had much in common. Michels tells it twice, without naming a source. Once he says they talked about non-human intelligences, beings that are not human, and the colours tied to them. The other time he says the Dalai Lama was surprised that Kabbalist meditation and prayer were so like Buddhist meditation.","kafka-s-trial-as-a-description-of-a-spectral-angelic-bureauc":"Franz Kafka's novel The Trial, set in a city like Prague, can be read as a description of a ghostly bureaucracy of angels that rules over people. The philosopher Jason Jorjani makes this argument on the show, saying he wrote an essay interpreting the novel that appears in his anthology Lovers of Sophia, and he also draws on Kafka's private Blue Octavo Notebooks. If the reading holds, Kafka would have been describing unseen beings who run human affairs like an office.","kafsokalyvia-skete-maximus-the-flying-monk-who-burned-his-hu-2005":"Kafsokalyvia, a small cluster of monks' dwellings on Mount Athos, the Greek peninsula of Orthodox monasteries, is named for a saint known as Maximus, a \"flying monk\" who burned his huts, worked miracles, and was seen flying. Ralph Larson tells Michels he heard this in 2005 from a young monk who looked after the place, which would make it a local memory of a man said to have flown.","kaikoura-ufo-incident-1978":"In 1978 there was \"a really big UFO incident\" at Kaikoura, on the east coast of New Zealand's South Island, according to the Australian journalist Ross Coulthart. He tells Michels he was 16 at the time and remembers it, but he only names the event and does not describe what was seen.","kakino-stone-circle-with-magnetic-anomalies-from-lightning":"A stone circle on a hillside at Kakino in Japan is said to show odd magnetic readings caused by lightning, much like another site discussed on the show. A researcher named Geoffrey Drumm, who says he has seen such sites, mentions it to Michels in passing as \"this exact same thing,\" without going into detail.","kalmykian-president-s-debrief-with-kissinger-and-gorbachev-1990":"Henry Kissinger once hinted that he knew the government's UFO secrets, and Mikhail Gorbachev urged him to tell all. The man who says this is Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of Kalmykia, a small republic inside Russia. He tells Michels that in the late 1990s the three of them were together in a sauna at his home in Moscow. When Kissinger held back, he says, \"Gorbachev sitting next to him said, 'Come on, Henry, tell him. You should tell him everything.'\" From what they discussed, he says he understood that Kissinger was \"very familiar with the materials\" and \"very deep in the subject\", meaning UFO files held by the US president's staff, the CIA or NASA. If true, it would mean statesmen at the very top quietly knew about these things. Michels retells it in an earlier episode, from what he heard the former president say: there was a talk with Kissinger and Gorbachev, and then the question \"Well, how was it? I didn't understand.\"","kammler-black-world-program-in-nazi-germany-1940":"During the Second World War, a secret Nazi weapons office run by SS General Hans Kammler is said to have worked on advanced technology off the books. Michels calls this rumors of \"black world nuclear stuff\" in Nazi Germany, meaning hidden, unacknowledged projects. The writer Nick Cook tells him it \"was basically a special projects office run by SS General Hans Kammler\". Cook calls it the Kammlerstab and places it in Czechoslovakia and Poland from 1942 to 1945. On a later episode Michels brings up the Kammler Stab again, this time as \"interesting scientists\" working on new ways to power aircraft, as an early forerunner of the secret programs the show looks into.","kammler-negotiating-with-the-western-allies-1945":"Hans Kammler, the SS general who ran Nazi Germany's secret weapons projects, is said to have been negotiating with the Western Allies before the war ended in 1945. The aviation journalist Nick Cook, who has written about secret antigravity research, tells Michels there was 'quite a bit of evidence' for this, without naming it. If true, Kammler may have traded Nazi secret technology to the Americans or British for his own safety.","kammler-s-flying-saucer-work-and-die-glocke":"At a site in Czechoslovakia, in what Michels places in modern-day Poland, the Nazi engineer Hans Kammler, whom Michels calls 'the most ruthless Nazi' running the most secret technology projects, was 'rumored to be working on flying saucers', a story tied to Die Glocke, German for 'the Bell', a device of Nazi legend. Michels tells it himself as a rumor, in an episode with the former presidential advisor Harald Malmgren, as a possible root of secret saucer technology.","kammlerstab-nazi-tech-transfer-to-wright-field-1940":"During the Second World War, the story goes, an SS officer named Hans Kammler ran a secret weapons facility in Czechoslovakia, which Michels places in \"modern day Poland,\" and its technology later passed to Wright Field, the American military aircraft research base. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode asking whether Oppenheimer worked on UFOs; if true, some of America's secret aerospace work would have Nazi roots.","kansas-k9-trooper-drone-swarm-2019":"Around 2019 or 2020, a police dog handler with the Kansas state troopers named Doug Johnson saw a swarm of drone-like objects fly right over his head. Marik von Rennenkampff brings up the trooper's account on an episode where he debates a UFO skeptic, as one of the reports he says show unexplained craft over the United States.","kant-suppressed-swedenborg-s-disturbing-accounts":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani says that Immanuel Kant deliberately pushed aside the writings of Emanuel Swedenborg, an eighteenth-century Swedish mystic who described visits to spirits and other worlds. According to Jorjani, Kant \"admits\" he did it because the material in Swedenborg was \"so disturbing that it would destabilize society, including on a political level.\" Jorjani tells this to Michels as a researcher, without pointing to the passage where Kant says so, and he credits Swedenborg's Arcana Coelestia (Secrets of Heaven), Kant's Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals and his own book Prometheus and Atlas.","kardashev-scale-1964":"In 1964 the Russian astronomer Nikolai Kardashev proposed ranking civilizations by how much energy they can use, up to the energy of an entire star, at a time when optical and radio astronomy were growing. A guest named Karl Nell explains this to Michels without naming a source. He brings it up as a way to think about how far beyond us a non-human civilization could be.","karen-an-experiencer-who-threw-lamps-at-the-beings":"A woman named Karen, an \"experiencer\", meaning someone who says she has met non-human beings, is said to have fought back when they came by throwing things at them, even lamps. A man named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this at second hand, in connection with a man named John Mack. It would mean these encounters are physical enough that a person can hurl furniture at the visitors.","karen-gallaudet-s-remote-viewing-training-with-paul-smith-2024":"Karen Gallaudet has been training in remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant places or hidden things with the mind alone, under a teacher named Paul Smith. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels that she took a basic course and then finished the advanced course, going to Smith's \"place in Cedar City, Utah\" to do it. He tells it as something close to home, a sign that this practice is being taken seriously in his own family.","karen-silkwood-and-the-missing-plutonium-1974":"In 1974 Karen Silkwood, a worker at a nuclear reprocessing plant in Oklahoma, is said to have found that the plant was missing 40 pounds of '98% bomb grade plutonium.' Dan Sheehan, who says he took part in the case, tells Michels this. If true, enough material for nuclear weapons would have gone missing from a US facility.","karl-compton-recruits-teenage-malmgren-to-mit-1950":"In 1950, Karl Compton of MIT met a teenage Harold Malmgren at a mansion near Cambridge, Massachusetts, and after two hours told the boy's father he wanted to take him out of his school and have him start at MIT that September. Malmgren, who later became an advisor to presidents, tells Michels this about himself. It is the start of the career that, by his account, brought him into contact with UFO material.","karl-maria-wiligut-himmler-s-ss-seer-of-the-nordic-past-1930":"In the 1930s a man named Karl Maria Wiligut is said to have served as a seer for Heinrich Himmler, head of the Nazi SS, claiming he had a photographic memory of the distant past \"when there were only Nordics on the planet,\" meaning a time when only a tall, fair race lived on Earth. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, as a root of the racial myths the SS took up.","karl-nell-on-a-reverse-engineering-arms-race":"The United States is in a secret race with Russia and China to \"reverse engineer\" unknown craft, meaning to take them apart and learn how they work, according to Karl Nell, a retired Army officer who helped set up Army Futures Command, the Army's modernisation command. Michels tells this several times, citing what Nell said to The Debrief, an online news site. In one version Michels calls Nell his friend and says the race is going on \"right now\" against \"the Russians and the Chinese\". In another he calls it a sub rosa, or secret, conflict that has \"been going on for decades\", and he connects it to David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who testified to Congress about hidden programmes. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb also repeats Nell. He leaves out Russia and China and simply says, \"we actively have crafts and we're actively figuring out how to work various systems with them.\" Every one of these tellers is passing on Nell's words, not anything they saw themselves. If Nell is right, the US already holds such craft and other powers are racing to understand them too.","karl-nell-s-revised-kardashev-scale-2023":"Karl Nell, who Michels says helped set up Army Futures Command, served on the government's task force on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), and publicly defended David Grusch as soon as Grusch came forward with his claims, has proposed his own version of the Kardashev scale, a way of ranking civilisations by how much energy they control. Michels brings this up himself in 2024 and says nothing about how Nell's version differs. He mentions it to show that an insider like Nell thinks seriously about advanced civilisations.","karl-nell-says-disclosure-is-dead-2017":"Karl Nell, a former military officer who has argued for public release of what the government knows about UFOs, recently went on a podcast and said \"disclosure is dead\", meaning that such an official admission is not coming. Michels mentions this himself, relaying Nell's words from that other show, in a talk about how the Pentagon handles the subject.","karl-wolfe-run-over-before-disclosure-wave-2017":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that just as \"so-called disclosure,\" the push for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, began in 2017 and 2018, a man named Karl Wolfe \"was run over just the way John Mack was run over.\" Jorjani sets the two deaths side by side to suggest that people tied to the subject die in the same way at telling moments.","karl-wolfe-sees-photos-of-a-lunar-megalithic-city-1960":"A technician named Karl Wolfe is said to have seen, in the 1960s, photographs of a huge city of giant stone structures on the far side of the Moon. A philosopher named Jason Jorjani passes on Wolfe's account. In it, Wolfe was repairing a machine at an NSA facility when he was shown \"mosaic photographs of a huge megalithic city on the dark side of the moon.\" Jorjani links the images to reconnaissance photos from Lunar Orbiter, NASA's 1960s probes that mapped the Moon. Elsewhere in the same conversation he adds that Ingo Swann, a man known for remote viewing (the claimed ability to describe faraway places with the mind alone), \"confirms\" the city. A former researcher at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, named John Blitch also tells it from what Wolfe said. In his version it happened at Langley, and they had found \"huge structures\" rather than a city. If true, someone built on the Moon, and the government saw it and said nothing.","karla-turner-allegedly-killed-by-a-cia-cancer-mimicking-weap-1990":"A woman named Karla Turner died of cancer in the 1990s at the age of 48, and the philosopher Jason Jorjani believes she was actually killed, by a CIA weapon made to look like cancer. Jorjani tells Michels this as his own belief, without a source. If true, it would mean she was silenced for what she knew or said.","karla-turner-and-her-family-as-alien-and-milab-abductees":"The writer Karla Turner, author of the books Taken and Into the Fringe, is said to have been abducted by aliens along with her family, and also to have been a MILAB case, a term for people who say they were taken by the military or by humans working alongside aliens. The researcher Tim Alberino describes this to Michels without naming a source, saying what \"made Turner unique was that she was an abductee and her family,\" and he places her alongside the abduction researchers Budd Hopkins and Barbara Bartholic. To him her case matters because she researched the experience from the inside.","karla-turner-s-abductees-describe-a-control-system-run-from-1990":"People who say they were taken by aliens told the researcher Karla Turner, who wrote about abduction cases in the 1990s, that 'we are in a control system that's being managed from out of a metallic sphere just beyond the earth', which the story takes to be the Moon. The philosopher Jason Jorjani relays this to Michels, setting it beside Far Journeys, a book by Robert Monroe, a writer on out-of-body travel. If true, it would mean humanity is being watched and steered from close by.","karla-turner-s-husband-abducted-by-the-military":"The husband of Karla Turner, a writer who researched alien abduction reports, is said to have been abducted not by aliens but 'by elements of the military.' The filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, some abductions would be the work of people in the military.","karst-cave-and-tunnel-system-venting-hydrogen-sulfide-under":"The claim is that under the Giza Plateau, where the pyramids stand, there is a karst system, a network of caves and tunnels dissolved out of the rock, and that it sends hydrogen sulfide gas up to the surface. The researcher Geoffrey Drumm makes this point on two episodes (his name is also given as Jeffrey Drum), each time summarising a scientific paper. In the first he says the paper shows \"a karst cave and tunnel system\" that is \"the source of hydrogen sulfide gas coming up from the Giza Plateau.\" In the second he reads out the paper's full title: \"The development of sulfuric acid speleogenetic deposits within cavernous Middle Eocene beds and inference on hydrocarbon gas seepages, Giza Pyramids Plateau, Egypt.\" Speleogenetic means cave-forming. The title itself speaks of acid-formed cave deposits and gas seeping up through the plateau, which fits his reading. Whether the gas served any purpose for the pyramids, the title neither says nor rules out. Drumm brings it in to support his idea, set out in episodes about an energy grid and the true purpose of the pyramids, that the gas under Giza is part of what the pyramids were built to use.","kary-mullis-saw-an-alien":"Kary Mullis, the man who invented PCR, short for polymerase chain reaction, a lab method for copying DNA, is said to have had an alien experience: in one telling he was abducted, waking up in a shed and speaking with some kind of being. Michels tells this himself on two episodes, as something he has heard, and admits he does not remember the exact details. He brings it up because it would mean even a famous, serious scientist had such an encounter.","kecksburg-craft-was-bell-shaped-1965":"The object said to have come down at Kecksburg, Pennsylvania, in 1965 was shaped like a bell or an acorn, not like a flying saucer. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, while pointing out that the movie he is discussing got it wrong by showing a saucer.","kecksburg-crash-and-eric-walker-connection-1965":"In 1965 an object is said to have crashed in the woods near Kecksburg, Pennsylvania, and the story goes that a civilian took charge of the recovery: Eric Walker, president of Penn State University. Walker is also linked to Majestic 12, an alleged secret panel of officials said to have handled crashed saucers. Michels returns to the connection on several episodes. He says Walker was \"supposedly present\" at Kecksburg, and that the civilian in charge \"is likely Dr. Eric Walker.\" A researcher who goes by UAPGerb says Walker himself claimed in meetings with two researchers, William Steinman and Henry Azadehdel, that \"he was present at the 1965 Kexsburg UFO crash.\" The UFO researcher Grant Cameron gives the most direct account. He says that after an interview in which Walker hinted at it, he had someone phone Walker back to ask whether he had been at Kecksburg, and Walker answered, \"I was there. So what?\" If true, a senior university figure would have been quietly running a crash recovery.","kecksburg-crash-witnesses-saw-people-in-moon-suits-1965":"When something crashed near Kecksburg, Pennsylvania, in 1965, all the witnesses at the scene are said to have seen people in what they called moon suits. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this without naming a source. He tells it as a sign that a trained team was ready to recover crashed objects.","kecksburg-object-1965":"In 1965 something \"very bizarre\" fell from the sky at Kecksburg, a small town in Pennsylvania, according to the UFO historian Richard Dolan. He tells Michels he does not think it was a Soviet satellite, the usual explanation, which would leave open that it was something unknown.","keeping-the-beings-out-of-the-official-report-to-protect-his-1977":"Mario Woods, who guarded nuclear weapons at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, says he saw beings in 1977 but left them out of his official report. He tells Michels that if he had reported them he would have been removed at once from the Personnel Reliability Program, the screening that certifies people as fit to work with nuclear weapons. If true, it suggests that sightings at nuclear sites may go unreported because of the risk to the witnesses' careers.","kelly-johnson-s-ufo-sighting":"Kelly Johnson, the aircraft designer who led Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret aircraft design shop, is said to have watched a strange black object in the sky one evening. Michels passes on Johnson's own account: at about 5 p.m. Johnson was looking out a large west-facing window, watching the sunset, when he saw \"this black thing.\" A researcher named Marik von Rennenkampff tells it without naming a source. He calls Johnson \"the most well-known aeronautical engineer in American history\" and places him at his ranch in Southern California near the Channel Islands. In his version the object was elliptical and a Lockheed crew saw it too. Told this way, the story puts one of America's most respected aircraft designers among the people who saw something they could not explain.","ken-farley-s-description-of-the-craft-s-gold-lines-1948":"A man named Ken Farley, said to have seen a crashed craft at Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948, could still describe it \"in perfect detail\" many years later, ending with \"three brass or copper or gold lines\" that ran all the way around its centre section. Scott Ramsey, who looked into the Aztec case and spoke with people connected to it, passes on Farley's words to Michels, and names a man named Doug Noland alongside him. The detail matters to Ramsey as a concrete memory from someone he presents as a direct witness.","ken-farley-struck-with-a-rifle-butt-1948":"In 1948, after a flying saucer is said to have come down in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, a man named Ken Farley was hit in the gut with the butt of a gun for talking about it the way he did. Scott Ramsey, a guest who has gathered accounts of the Aztec crash, tells Michels this as something passed on to him about Farley; if true, people who spoke of the crash were silenced by force.","ken-johnston-told-to-destroy-anomalous-apollo-negatives-1960":"A NASA man named Ken Johnston Sr. was told to destroy early copies of Apollo photo negatives because they showed anomalies that were \"not supposed to be there\" and NASA did not want them released, but he secretly saved some. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this as what Johnston told him. If true, NASA hid strange things seen during the moon missions.","ken-shoulders-ceramic-puncture-experiment":"An experimenter named Ken Shoulders is said to have found that sending electrical pulses into ceramic did not break it, but punched small holes through it, something electricity normally would not do. A guest named Dave Rossi describes the experiment to Michels from memory, asking to be corrected if he has it wrong.","kennedy-delegation-tours-western-nuclear-facilities-after-cu-1962":"After the Cuban missile crisis of 1962, President Kennedy is said to have led a group that toured Los Alamos and other nuclear sites in the western United States, with Vice President Johnson, national security adviser McGeorge Bundy and Air Force general Curtis LeMay among them. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, tells Michels that this 'has been documented by Jeff Cruickshank', a researcher who 'listed the attendees on that trip.' McConnell raises it as a sign that the top leaders went to see secret programs for themselves.","kennedy-proposes-a-joint-soviet-us-space-program-1963":"In 1963, President Kennedy is said to have proposed to the Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev that the two countries run a joint space program. The researcher Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, at first saying Gorbachev by mistake. He tells it in a talk about Kennedy and UFOs, as a hint at how much Kennedy was willing to share with the Soviets.","kennedy-s-orange-bowl-promise-to-cuban-exiles-1962":"In 1962 John F. Kennedy went to the Orange Bowl stadium in Miami and held a big meeting with Cuban exiles, where he is said to have made them a promise. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of his account of the pressures around Kennedy before his death.","kennedy-s-silver-certificates-on-june-5-1963":"On June 5, 1963, President Kennedy is said to have ordered the printing of silver certificates, a kind of paper money backed by silver. A guest named Dan Sheehan brings it up with Michels as part of his account of Kennedy and UFOs, calling it a \"weird thing\" he has never quite figured out.","kenneth-arnold-s-1947-craft-matched-horten-design":"The objects a pilot named Kenneth Arnold reported seeing in the sky in 1947 had the same design as a craft built by the Horten brothers, German aircraft designers, though Arnold did not know it at the time. That is how the researcher Peter Levenda puts it to Michels, without naming a source. If true, Arnold's famous sighting would point to a human design rather than something unknown.","kenneth-arnold-s-paranormal-experiences-after-his-1947-sight":"Kenneth Arnold, a man whose sighting of strange objects in the sky in 1947 is among the best known in UFO history, is said to have gone on to have mysterious paranormal experiences afterwards. Jeffrey Mishlove, a psychologist with a PhD who studies psychic phenomena, tells Michels he believes Arnold's daughter has written a book about those experiences, so he is passing on what he understands she wrote. If true, a famous sighting was linked to stranger events in the witness's later life.","kenneth-arnold-sighting-1947":"In 1947, a pilot named Kenneth Arnold reported seeing a group of strange craft moving at impossible speeds over the American Northwest. A whistleblower named David Grusch tells Michels that Arnold was looking for a downed cargo plane near Mount Rainier, two months before Roswell, the reported crash in New Mexico that same year. A researcher named Peter Levenda instead places it in the Rockies on some flying mission and stresses the speed, and a scientist named John Brandenburg adds that soon afterwards such craft \"were all over the country\". A man named Chris Bledsoe, who says he attracts UFOs, describes about half a dozen \"little chevrons\" moving freely, and both he and Michels tie the sighting to where the phrase \"flying saucer\" came from, Michels adding that the objects were really crescents. The tellers bring it up as one of the first modern sightings, the start of the whole UFO story.","kent-selin-sees-flux-liner-over-edwards-1970":"A man named Kent Selin says he saw a craft called the flux liner flying over Edwards Air Force Base in California in the 1970s. The story reaches Michels at a remove: a man who goes by UAPGerb says that a man named Mark McCandlish talked to Selin, who told him what he saw. If true, it would mean such a craft was flying over a known military test base decades ago.","kentucky-farm-case-behind-night-skies-and-e-t-1950":"A family in Kentucky is said to have found an alien on their farm and hidden it in their barn, and this case is said to have inspired Night Skies, a film Steven Spielberg was developing as a producer, which led to E.T. A guest Michels does not name tells it on two episodes in almost the same words, the second time as something passed on about Spielberg. Told this way, it would mean one of Hollywood's best-known alien films grew out of a reported real encounter.","kevin-day-radar-objects-at-28-000-ft-matched-in-data-2004":"During the 2004 Navy encounters with unexplained objects off Southern California, a man named Kevin Day said the objects he was tracking on radar were at 28,000 feet, and according to the telling this altitude matches the recorded data. Marik von Rennenkampff makes the point on the show, relaying Day's account and setting it next to footage from a pilot named Chad Underwood. If the figures line up, Day's radar report and the other evidence would back each other up.","keyhoe-air-force-tracked-two-unknown-satellites-in-1953":"In 1953 the Air Force was tracking two unknown artificial satellites circling Earth, one 400 miles up and the other 600 miles up. That is what the UFO writer Donald Keyhoe states, according to Michels, who repeats it from Keyhoe without naming the work. If true, made objects of unknown origin were orbiting Earth at the time.","kgb-department-researching-unknown-flying-and-underwater-obj":"In the Soviet Union there was a special department inside the KGB that researched unknown flying objects, and also unknown objects under water. Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of one of Russia's republics, tells Michels this was shown to his own leader by Mikhail Gorbachev; if true, the Soviet secret police studied such objects in an organised way.","khafre-research-project-proposal-to-the-egyptian-authorities-2025":"Filippo Biondi, a scientist who used satellite radar to look beneath the pyramids at Giza, says his team is preparing a proposal to the Egyptian government for what he calls the Khafre research project, a study centred on the second great pyramid. He tells Michels the work would involve his colleagues Armando Mei and Corrado Malanga and the University of Ferrara, and on a later episode he says the plan is to use robots to clear the shafts filled with debris, with the proposal going in \"these weeks.\" If it went ahead, it would test on the ground what his scans say lies under the plateau.","khafre-research-project-used-a-low-incidence-angle-2020":"A project scanning the Khafre pyramid at Giza with radar from satellites used a low incidence angle, meaning the radar beam struck the ground at a shallow slant. Filippo Biondi, a radar researcher who took part, tells Michels this as a detail of the method behind scans he says found structures beneath the pyramid.","khafre-substructure-tubes-about-20-m-wide-and-over-1-km-deep-2025":"Under the Khafre pyramid at Giza, in Egypt, there are huge tubes about 20 metres wide that go down for more than a kilometre. The researcher Filippo Biondi says this on the show from his own 2025 work scanning beneath the pyramids, describing \"huge tubes descending approximately for more than 1 km.\" If true, it would mean a vast built structure lies hidden deep under the pyramid.","khufu-cartouche-allegedly-painted-by-vyse-1830":"The claim is that the painted name of the pharaoh Khufu inside the Great Pyramid of Giza, the mark that ties the pyramid to him, was put there in the 1830s by the explorer who found it. A researcher named Jay Anderson tells Michels this without naming a source. He can't quite recall the man's name (the story points to Howard Vyse) but says he was a 19th-century explorer hired by the British Museum, short of money and short of time, who then 'suddenly finds the signatures of Khufu written in very suspicious ochre', which would mean the pyramid's best-known label could be a forgery.","khufu-project-scan-shows-a-network-of-tunnels-beneath-the-gi-2025":"Filippo Biondi, a scientist who has scanned the ground beneath the pyramids of Giza in Egypt, says there is a network of tunnels underneath the Great Pyramid of Khufu. He tells Michels he hopes one day to go down a shaft and reach one of the horizontal tunnels. On a later episode he says his team did a new scan, not to repeat their 2020 work but \"to see what there was deep below the Khufu,\" and there he does not mention the tunnels.","killeen-base-emergency-meeting-1949":"In 1949, after several UFO sightings at Killeen Base at Camp Hood, Texas, which Michels says held America's largest nuclear stockpile up to then, an emergency meeting was held about UFOs and nuclear weapons. Michels tells this himself on several episodes, saying Air Force documents from 1949 describe it and that Army counterintelligence, the FBI, the Office of Naval Intelligence and the CIA all attended. Once he credits the writer Robert Hastings, who covers it in his book UFOs and Nukes, and says he is not sure of the year; he raises it as evidence that UFOs were watching US nuclear sites from the start.","killing-of-caltech-astronomer-carl-grillmair-2026":"On February 16, 2026, a Caltech astronomer named Carl Grillmair was killed in Llano, in California's Antelope Valley, about 30 miles northeast of Waterman Mountain, where his colleague Monica Resza had disappeared. Michels tells it himself, calling it an 'equally tragic and suspicious end' for another colleague from Caltech and NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, in an episode about top scientists who have gone missing, where it would suggest a pattern rather than chance.","killing-of-thule-society-members-april-1919":"Members of the Thule Society, a secretive nationalist group in Munich, were assassinated on 30 April 1919, according to a researcher named Peter Levenda. He mentions it in passing while talking with Michels and does not name a source, and he gives no more detail about who died or why.","kim-peek-s-brain-anatomy-and-his-recall-of-12-000-books":"A man named Kim Peek, said to have remembered some 12,000 books, was not autistic, as many people thought. He was born with a defect in how his brain handled the fluid around it. The researcher Diane Powell says this on the show, without naming a source, as a case of how an unusual brain might open unusual abilities.","king-charles-mountbatten-and-savile-s-black-berets-1970":"In the 1970s and 80s, the story goes, King Charles, Lord Mountbatten and the British TV star Jimmy Savile \"used to all hang out together\" and had special black berets made for themselves. Kurt Metzger tells it on an episode about Jeffrey Epstein, as something he was told, to suggest a private circle linking the royal family to Savile.","king-frederick-shelters-hermetic-thinkers-descartes-fights-o":"Michels says that King Frederick gave shelter to a group of heretical, mystical thinkers, among them the English scholar John Dee, his partner Edward Kelley and possibly the philosopher Giordano Bruno, and that when war came, René Descartes fought on the other side. He takes this, with several 'I think's, from a book he names as Frances Yates's The Rosicrucian Enlightenment, a history of the secret mystical movement of the early 1600s, while talking with the philosopher Jason Jorjani. If it holds, the father of modern rational philosophy stood on the opposing side of a war against a court of mystics.","kingman-1953-crash-arthur-stansel-sees-small-being-in-tent":"In 1953, near Kingman, Arizona, an engineer named Arthur Stansel Jr. was sent to a crash site to work out how fast an object had hit the ground, and he is said to have seen a small brownish body, \"possibly charred and mangled,\" in a makeshift tent. The researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells this, adding that Stansel named a scientist called Eric Henry Wang, and Michels, crediting the former Pentagon official Chris Mellon, says the researcher Bill Steinman found Stansel's name in records and puts the body at about four feet tall. Stansel himself, on the show, says only that he \"accepted it was probably some United States government vehicle, a highly classified vehicle,\" because \"we were told that that's what it was.\"","kingman-craft-held-by-lockheed-in-a-warehouse":"A craft said to have come down near Kingman, Arizona, is supposedly kept in a warehouse by the aerospace company Lockheed, and is described as having \"no seams, no rivets, like this beautiful thing.\" Michels mentions it himself, loosely and without a source, as an example of a recovered object a private contractor would be hiding.","kingman-crash-survivor-went-to-the-back-of-the-craft":"After a craft crashed near Kingman, Arizona, one survivor from it, a \"she\", is said to have gone back inside the craft and stayed in the back. Charles Hall, who says he spent three years with tall, white aliens at Area 51, tells Michels this as the local Kingman sheriff told it, \"in his own words\". It would mean there were living occupants at the crash, and that one of them chose to stay with the wreck.","kingman-crash-was-the-one-they-walked-away-from":"A UFO crash near Kingman, Arizona was, in the words of an insider, \"the one they walked away from,\" according to Grant Cameron, a longtime UFO researcher. Cameron tells Michels that a young woman he is in contact with was sitting near \"one of the main people\" at a conference called SOL when the subject came up, apparently in connection with a film by the documentary maker James Fox, and that the man said: \"That's not right. That's not right. That was that crash.\" Cameron does not name the man, and if the remark is accurate it would mean the beings in that crash survived it.","kirk-mcconnell-and-extended-electrodynamics":"A staffer on the Senate Armed Services Committee named Kurt McConnell, who worked under Jack Reed, the senator from Rhode Island, is said to know all about extended electrodynamics, a proposed broadening of the physics of electricity and magnetism. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, as a sign that people close to Congress follow unusual physics that might explain how strange craft move.","kirk-mcconnell-confirms-blue-on-blue-firefight-and-human-tra-2017":"A man named Kirk McConnell, whose story is tied to the Senate Armed Services Committee, is said to have confirmed two startling things when people questioned him at a UFO conference called SOL: he had heard of a \"blue-on-blue\" firefight, meaning American forces fighting each other, involving some security team, and he had heard of humans being carried in \"reverse-engineered craft\", vehicles built by copying recovered non-human technology. The researcher who posts online as UAPGerb tells Michels this, without saying where he heard it, and he links it to Senator Kirsten Gillibrand. If true, it would mean someone close to Congress had heard that secret craft programmes exist and have even led to deadly clashes.","kirkpatrick-dodged-a-meeting-with-borland-2023":"In 2023, Dylan Borland, a whistleblower, says he was asked to talk to Sean Kirkpatrick, then head of AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into reports of unexplained objects, and that Kirkpatrick 'ducked' him. Borland tells Michels this from his own experience, meaning the official in charge never heard him out.","kirkpatrick-leaves-aaro-for-oak-ridge-2023":"When Sean Kirkpatrick left AARO, he went to work with Oak Ridge National Laboratory, a government research lab in Tennessee. AARO is the Pentagon office set up to investigate UAP, the official term for UFOs. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this in passing. He says Kirkpatrick \"ran off to Oak Ridge\" and urges listeners to look into the lab's links to FFRDCs, labs that are run privately but paid for by the government. A Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell, who speaks from his own involvement, gives more detail. He says the contract for an AARO advisory group was placed through Oak Ridge, and that since Kirkpatrick retired from government he has worked with and for Oak Ridge under contract. The tellers raise it because, if true, the man who ran the government's official UFO inquiry ended up at one of the very contractor networks that some say hold the secrets.","kirkpatrick-lobbied-against-the-uap-disclosure-act-2023":"Michels says that Sean Kirkpatrick, the first head of the Pentagon office set up to investigate UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's current term for UFOs), lobbied against the UAP Disclosure Act, an amendment proposed by Senators Chuck Schumer and Mike Rounds to the yearly defense bill, introduced in the Senate on July 13, 2023, that sought to bring government UFO records to light. Michels says it himself, without naming a source, and hopes Kirkpatrick will not do so again when the act comes back for a vote. If true, the official charged with investigating UAP would have worked against opening the records.","kirkpatrick-metallic-orbs-worldwide-said-at-nasa-panel-2023":"At a NASA panel in 2023 on UAP, the government's term for UFOs, Sean Kirkpatrick, who ran AARO, the Pentagon office set up to investigate such sightings, is quoted as saying: \"We're seeing metallic orbs making very interesting apparent maneuvers all over the world.\" A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff quotes the line to Michels as an official's own words, in an episode about challenging the well-known UFO skeptic Mick West to a debate. If it stands, the head of the office whose job was to explain sightings was himself saying unexplained objects were turning up worldwide.","kirkpatrick-s-aaro-denied-the-herrera-incident-but-he-privat-2023":"Sean Kirkpatrick headed AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into UAP (the official term for UFOs). According to Jake Barber, the office publicly denied an incident described by a man named Michael Herrera, but Kirkpatrick privately admitted to members of the Senate Intelligence Committee that it \"did in fact happen exactly as reported by Michael Herrera\", along with details Barber himself added. Barber, who says he flew a helicopter to retrieve a UFO, tells Michels he knows this \"for a fact\" but does not say how. If true, the head of the office would have told senators one thing in private while his office said another in public.","kirkpatrick-tells-gillibrand-s-committee-there-was-no-eviden-2023":"In April 2023, an official named Sean Kirkpatrick reportedly told a Senate committee led by Senator Kirsten Gillibrand that there was no evidence of extraterrestrial craft. A researcher named Robert Hastings, who studies UFO activity at nuclear bases, mentions it on the show without naming a source. He calls it 'the culmination' of the official line of denial he has been describing.","kirtland-security-meeting-with-benowitz-where-attendees-leav-1980":"In 1980, at Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, a security meeting was reportedly held about Paul Bennewitz, an Albuquerque man who believed he was tracking alien activity near the base. As the strange things he reported were discussed, people supposedly got up and left, one after another, saying \"oh, that's not ours,\" until only three or four remained. The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels this second-hand, from a man named Tom Deuley who told him about the meeting; if true, it would mean the people in the room could not claim what Bennewitz had seen as any of their own projects.","kissinger-among-the-first-cic-officers-in-oberammergau-1945":"In 1945 Henry Kissinger was one of the first officers to move into the barracks at Oberammergau in Bavaria, Germany, serving with the US Army's Counter Intelligence Corps. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode asking whether Kissinger ran a program to recover crashed UFOs. He brings it up to place Kissinger in intelligence work early on.","kissinger-consults-for-the-psychological-strategy-board-1951":"Henry Kissinger worked as a consultant in 1951, 1952 and again in 1955 for the Psychological Strategy Board, a Washington body that coordinated America's Cold War psychological warfare, and for its successor, the Operations Coordinating Board. Michels tells this himself, mentioning a man named Gordon Gray, in an episode asking whether Kissinger oversaw a secret programme to recover crashed UFOs, where these posts would place him near such work early on.","kissinger-directs-the-cfr-study-group-on-nuclear-weapons-1955":"In 1955, Michels says, Henry Kissinger was invited to direct a Council on Foreign Relations study group on nuclear war and foreign policy, and this led to his book Nuclear Weapons and Foreign Policy. Michels links the group to J. Robert Oppenheimer and to the atomic energy official Gordon Dean. He tells it himself, on an episode asking whether Kissinger oversaw a programme to recover crashed UFOs, because the job would have put Kissinger right among the people who kept the country's deepest secrets.","kissinger-dobrynin-transcript-on-microwave-beams-at-moscow-e-1970":"In the 1970s, microwave beams aimed at the US Embassy in Moscow are said to have come up in talks between Henry Kissinger and the Soviet diplomat Anatoly Dobrynin, with a man named Walter Stoessel also named. Michels tells this himself and points to a recently declassified transcript from the SALT talks, the US-Soviet negotiations on limiting nuclear arms, held during détente, the easing of Cold War tensions. If true, it would show the beaming was discussed at the very top.","kissinger-in-army-counterintelligence-tech-retrieval-1940":"In the 1940s, Henry Kissinger is said to have been an Army counterintelligence officer doing \"tech retrieval,\" meaning gathering up enemy technology in Europe. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, to suggest that a future statesman was involved early on in the kind of retrieval work that UFO stories later center on.","kissinger-involved-in-ufo-matters":"Henry Kissinger was personally involved in the government's handling of UFOs, according to Michels. He says it himself in a conversation with Joe Rogan, telling him he has \"uncovered a lot\" about it. He names no source and does not say on the show what he found, though if true it would put one of the best-known statesmen of the Cold War inside the secret.","kissinger-letter-to-nixon-on-prisoners-pows-abandoned-in-lao-1970":"Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer, meaning someone trained in a secret US program to describe distant places by mind alone, says he once saw a letter from Henry Kissinger to President Nixon saying, in his words, \"I'm not going back over there to negotiate for any more prisoners.\" He tells Michels this is tied to American prisoners of war left behind in Laos and Cambodia in the early 1970s; if true, the government chose to abandon its own captured men.","kissinger-on-the-303-committee-overseeing-covert-operations-1960":"Henry Kissinger was involved with the 303 Committee, which Michels describes as a secret US interagency group that oversaw all covert operations. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as part of asking whether Kissinger also oversaw a programme to recover crashed UFOs.","kissinger-s-1977-moon-dust-telegram-to-ottawa":"Michels says that in January 1977, just before leaving office, Henry Kissinger sent a telegram to the US embassy in Ottawa asking for a report on 'possible returned space objects,' under the subject line Moon Dust, the code name of a US effort to recover objects that fell from space. Michels tells this himself in an episode asking whether Kissinger oversaw a program to retrieve crashed UFOs, quoting the telegram's wording. If it means what the episode suggests, the secretary of state was personally keeping track of objects falling from the sky.","kissinger-s-harvard-career-as-cover-for-advising-mj12-throug-1950":"The idea is that Henry Kissinger's career as a Harvard academic in the 1950s was only a cover. Behind it, the claim goes, he did intelligence work advising MJ12, a supposed secret group said to oversee UFO matters, through a special think tank called PI40. Michels raises it himself, without naming a source, in an episode asking whether Kissinger ran a program to recover crashed UFOs.","kissinger-studied-under-thomas-schelling-who-later-tried-an-1950":"Henry Kissinger studied at Harvard under a man named Thomas Schelling, who, as the story goes, later tried to stage an intervention with him once Kissinger was at the White House. Michels mentions it himself, in passing, in an episode with the psychic spy Joe McMoneagle, and says nothing more about what the intervention was.","kissner-tells-steven-schiff-who-launches-the-roswell-gao-pro-1995":"In 1995, a New Mexico state representative named J. Andrew Kissner is said to have told another New Mexico lawmaker, Steven Schiff, what he knew, and Schiff then started an inquiry by the GAO, Congress's audit office, into Roswell, the 1947 New Mexico incident many say involved a crashed craft. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this, saying Kissner \"allegedly\" passed it on, without naming where he learned it. If true, a state politician's tip lay behind the federal probe of Roswell.","kit-green-and-eric-walker-both-tied-to-glomar-explorer-1970":"Two men in this story, Kit Green and Eric Walker, were both involved with the Glomar Explorer, a ship tied to a secret US salvage operation of the 1970s. According to the researcher Grant Cameron, who tells it without naming a source, when Green saw Walker's biography he said to the researcher Jacques Vallée, \"have you seen this guy's bio, this guy is unbelievable.\" Cameron tells it as a sign that the same few people turn up across hidden programmes.","kit-green-and-garry-nolan-study-biological-effects-on-ufo-wi":"Two researchers, Kit Green and Garry Nolan, are said to study the bodily effects on people who have seen or encountered UFOs, including what looks like damage from electromagnetic radiation. Michels mentions it himself, calling it \"CIA spooky stuff,\" as evidence that close encounters can leave physical injuries.","kit-green-could-not-get-read-into-the-autopsy-program-2000":"Kit Green, a former CIA scientist, is said to have been \"read in\", meaning formally cleared, on most of the government's secret UFO work, but he could never get cleared for the part dealing with autopsies. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as \"the story I heard\", passing on what was said about Green. If it is true, even a trusted insider was kept out of one sealed-off corner of the work.","kit-green-kicked-out-of-eric-walker-s-office":"A man named Kit Green is said to have gone to see Eric A. Walker at Penn State, where Walker had been president, and to have been thrown out of his office when he asked about UFOs. The researcher Grant Cameron tells it to Michels second-hand. He draws on Forbidden Science, the published journals of the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, and places the meeting in the 1980s. In Cameron's account, when Green raised the subject, \"Walker just blew up and he started yelling at him.\" Walker told him he did not have to tell him anything. Michels mentions the same run-in between Green and Walker only in passing. The story is told because, if true, Walker reacted like a man guarding something he knew.","kit-green-knew-ransone-but-refuses-to-talk-1970":"A man named Kit Green knew a woman named Valerie Ransone very well during the 1970s and early 1980s, but he refuses to talk about her. An unnamed researcher says this on the show in an episode about a CIA scientist said to have contacted an alien council, saying Green \"knew Ransone really well.\" If so, someone with first-hand knowledge of her story is staying silent.","kit-green-ran-the-cia-s-weird-desk":"A man named Kit Green ran what Michels calls, in quotation marks, the CIA's \"weird desk\" for a long time, a post handling unusual subjects. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, while talking about a ranch tied to strange events whose owner he interviews.","kit-green-s-cia-files-on-ufo-related-injuries-given-to-gary-2013":"Michels says that CIA files on the bodily effects suffered by people who had encounters with UFOs, files linked to a CIA scientist named Kit Green, were handed to the scientist Garry Nolan in the mid-2010s. He tells it himself, without naming a source. It would mean the CIA kept medical records of UFO encounters and passed them to an outside researcher.","klaus-fuchs-atomic-espionage-1941":"Klaus Fuchs worked at Los Alamos, which Michels dates from 1941 to 1950, and stole American atomic secrets. Michels mentions it himself in an episode with a guest named Dave Rossi, as an example of a scientist leaking secret research. He uses it to ask whether someone could become 'the next Klaus Fuchs'.","klippenstein-admits-dod-and-ic-tipped-him-off-on-grusch-stor-2023":"A journalist named Ken Klippenstein is said to have admitted that the Defense Department and the intelligence community tipped him off to the 2023 story of David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who went public claiming the US holds craft not made by humans. Michels tells this himself, saying Klippenstein admitted it on Breaking Points, a news show hosted by Saagar Enjeti and Krystal Ball; if true, it would mean the government itself helped steer the story into the press.","knapp-and-corbell-broke-the-fravor-tic-tac-story-on-radio-be-2017":"The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says that he and the journalist George Knapp broke the story of David Fravor, a Navy fighter pilot who chased the Tic Tac (a white oval object shaped like the mint), on the radio, \"quietly,\" twice before the New York Times published its article \"Glowing Auras and 'Black Money': The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program\" on 16 December 2017. Corbell tells Michels this as one of the two who did it. If so, the story the Times made famous was already public on radio.","knapp-puts-lazar-on-air-and-it-goes-viral-1989":"In Las Vegas in 1989, the story goes, the journalist George Knapp called John Lear, a man active in the local UFO scene, and said he was missing a guest for that night's show, and the guest who filled the slot was Bob Lazar, a man who claims he worked on alien spacecraft at a secret site in the Nevada desert. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this without naming a source, as the start of how Lazar's account spread far and wide.","knew-bigelow-at-the-rockefeller-ranch-1993":"Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, says he knew the businessman Robert Bigelow, who has paid for UFO research, at the ranch of the philanthropist Laurance Rockefeller in Wyoming in 1993. Greer tells Michels this from his own experience, in passing, as a sign of how close he was to the wealthy backers of UFO research at the time.","knuth-sees-something-fall-into-the-sea-through-night-vision-2021":"In 2021, off Laguna Beach in California, a scientist named Kevin Knuth says he watched something fall out of the sky and hit the sea, which he saw through night-vision goggles that show heat. Knuth tells Michels this himself as a witness: \"I actually saw something fall and hit the water in infrared.\" If he is right, an unexplained object came down into the ocean in front of him.","kobitz-shown-an-atomically-bonded-bulkhead-underground-at-wr":"A man named Nat Kobitz was taken under heavy guard into a room beneath Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio and shown a piece of what he called a bulkhead, part of the wall of a craft, made of material bonded at the atomic level. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this is what Kobitz described to him in person. Michels later repeats it from Coulthart, saying that Kobitz was dying of cancer when he said he had seen UFO technology at the base, though in his version the bulkhead is left out. If it were true, it would mean the Air Force holds pieces of craft nobody on Earth knows how to make.","kobitz-us-recovered-multiple-craft-including-roswell-aztec-a-1940":"The United States is said to have recovered several crashed craft in the 1940s and 50s, among them the ones said to have come down at Roswell and Aztec in New Mexico and at Kingman in Arizona. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels that a man named Nat Kobitz, who worked in Navy research, told him he had himself been told the US \"had recovered multiple craft.\" So the claim reaches the show at two removes, and if true it would mean the government has held more than one such object for decades.","kohler-refuses-to-name-his-sources-1950":"In 1950, at the KMYR radio station in Denver, a man named George Kohler refused to name the people who gave him a story the episode ties to crashed alien bodies. In his own words, the information 'was told to me as a story,' but 'the names where I got it from was entrusted to me as a confidency.'","kohler-secretly-records-the-osi-interview-1950":"In 1950, a man named George Kohler was questioned at the KMYR radio station in Denver by the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, or OSI, and he is said to have quietly had the interview taped: as they walked into the recording room, he told the technician, \"Record this.\" The researcher Scott Ramsey tells this without naming a source, and he also names two other men, Hansen and Unger, in the story. If the recording exists, it would keep a record of what Air Force investigators asked and said about UFOs at the time.","kona-blue":"Kona Blue is described as a secret UFO program that the government planned but never launched. The researcher UAPGerb tells Michels it was a \"failed prospective special access program\" from 2010. A special access program is a tightly restricted category of classified work. He says the public knows of it thanks to AARO, the Pentagon's current office for unexplained aerial reports, which released a slide deck about it. He names three people tied to the effort. Michels raises it himself in another episode, calling it \"a UAP program of some sort,\" UAP being the official term for unidentified flying objects. He says it was \"released I think by accident.\" If it is what the tellers say, the government considered a formal secret program for this subject as recently as 2010.","kona-blue-documents-admit-capability-to-implant-false-memori":"Papers from a US government programme called Kona Blue are said to show the government admitting it can plant false memories, interfere with people's minds and make them do things, without using drugs. The claim comes from a man named Dylan Borland, who says a top government official told him aliens run everything. He tells Michels: \"In those documents, the United States government is admitting they have the capability to give you false memories, mess with your brain, make you do things, and that's without drugs.\" The telling does not quote the papers themselves, so what they actually say about this is left open.","kona-blue-materials-were-kingman-1953-wreckage":"Kona Blue was a proposed secret government programme to hold and study recovered UFO material, and the claim is that the material it was meant to cover was wreckage from a craft said to have crashed near Kingman, Arizona, in 1953. An anonymous online researcher who posts as UAPGerb tells Michels \"I myself have heard this\", relaying what he says he was told by a man named Jim Ryder. If true, it would tie a modern programme to a crash from the early 1950s.","konrad-zuse-s-calculating-space-and-nuclear-detonation-test":"The German computer pioneer Konrad Zuse, in his work Rechnender Raum ('Calculating Space'), proposed that the universe itself might work like a computer, and he thought this could be tested in 'extremely high entropy events', such as the explosion of a nuclear bomb. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this in his own summary, alongside an online essay called Nuking the Matrix. The idea is that if reality is a computation, a nuclear blast might strain it enough to show the seams.","konstantin-meyl-s-extended-electrodynamics-experiments-on-pl":"A scientist in Germany's Black Forest named Konstantin Meyl is said to be testing an extended version of electromagnetic theory on plants, using what he calls scalar waves, a proposed kind of wave beyond the standard theory, to \"make flowers grow in beautiful ways.\" Michels brings it up himself on three episodes, once tying the idea to the physicists James Clerk Maxwell and Oliver Heaviside and once stressing scalar waves resonating with DNA; if it holds up, physics would have a hidden channel affecting living things.","kordylewski-clouds-and-1920s-long-delay-echoes":"The writer Robert Temple, in his book A New Science of Heaven, believes that Kordylewski clouds, which are clouds of plasma sitting at Lagrangian points (spots in space where gravitational pulls balance out), may explain the \"long delay echoes\" of the early 1920s, radio transmissions that seemed to come back long after they were sent. Michels lays out Temple's idea himself on two episodes. Once he puts the clouds between the Earth and the Sun, and once at a point between the Earth and the Moon. If Temple is right, a radio puzzle from a century ago would come from these clouds.","kordylewski-clouds-as-possible-conscious-plasma-entities":"The Kordylewski clouds are huge, faint clouds in space said to sit at L5, a spot along the Moon's orbit where the pull of Earth and Moon balance, and one idea holds that they might be conscious beings made of plasma, the electrically charged gas found in stars. Jeffrey Mishlove, a psychologist with a PhD who studies psychic phenomena, brings this up with Michels from the writer Robert Temple's book A New Science of Heaven. He recalls that \"there are two of them\" and that they are \"approximately nine times as large as the Earth.\" If true, there might be living minds of a kind no one expects right next to our planet.","korean-man-s-ayahuasca-sessions-in-peru-turn-into-alien-abdu-2024":"A Korean man taking ayahuasca, a psychedelic brew from the Amazon, in Peru in 2024 had what he described as a full-blown alien abduction: he was taken by a being he called \"the alien scientist.\" The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, who studies psychedelics, passes on the man's account to Michels as part of his argument that these drugs can open contact with real non-human beings.","kosloski-hearing-firsthand-witnesses-of-unreported-uap-progr-2024":"At a 2024 Senate hearing, Jon Kosloski, head of the Pentagon office set up to look into UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), reportedly said that at least two firsthand witnesses had come to his office about secret UAP programs that had never been reported to Congress. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff brings this up on the show, relaying what Kosloski said in the hearing, where Senator Kirsten Gillibrand was questioning him. If so, it would mean people inside such programs had come forward to the government's own investigators.","kripal-s-levitation-talk-to-lockheed-engineers-2010":"Charles Chase, director of Lockheed's revolutionary projects organization at the Skunk Works, Lockheed's secretive advanced aircraft division, is said to have invited a Rice University scholar named Jeffrey Kripal to give a private talk to top engineers on one subject: levitation. Michels passes this on from what Kripal said on another interview show, hosted by Danny Jones. If true, it would mean that serious aerospace engineers took an interest in old accounts of people rising off the ground.","kryptos-conundrum-an-intelligence-committee-for-timeline-man":"The Kryptos Conundrum, a book by the former CIA officer Chase Brandon, describes an intelligence committee whose members are each visited separately by non-human beings, who \"paranormally show up to everybody on the committee\" and coordinate them to manage humanity's timelines. Michels describes the book on two episodes. The second time he adds that the beings gave the committee \"a crystal tablet that gave them access to reading future timelines.\" He tells it as a book that may hint at something real, coming from a CIA insider.","kuber-lada-and-friends-meet-military-blockade-1996":"In 1996 in Varginha, a Brazilian town where many people say a strange creature was seen and captured, a man named Kuber Lada was leaving a gym with several friends when they saw two military trucks driving into town, seven days after a witness known by the initials SAS saw a crash. The documentary filmmaker James Fox, who interviewed the Varginha witnesses, retells Lada's account to Michels. It matters because it would place the army in town just after the reported crash.","kubrick-on-the-2001-monolith-1969":"The director Stanley Kubrick said in a 1969 interview that reading the black monolith in his film 2001: A Space Odyssey as an alien artifact is the movie's \"most superficial interpretation\", Michels tells his guest, citing that interview and the novel by Arthur C. Clarke. He brings it up in a conversation about UFOs and religion, as a hint that the story was meant to point at something deeper than visitors from space.","kumamoto-university-researchers-saw-lightning-strike-the-osh":"The stones on Oshidoishi Hill in Japan have pockmarks and a melted top, and the researcher Geoffrey Drumm tells Michels these are scars left by lightning hitting the stones directly. He says researchers at Kumamoto University who studied the stones saw them struck by lightning \"on multiple occasions\", though at another point in the same conversation he describes the scarring without crediting them. For Drumm it suggests the stones were placed where they would draw electricity on purpose.","kuramanta-pyramid-built-on-geothermal-hot-spring":"A pyramid in Japan that Geoffrey Drumm calls Kuramanta was supposedly built on top of a geothermal hot spring, so that natural heat and sulfur gas rise up into the structure. Drumm, a researcher who argues that pyramids served a practical purpose, gives it to Michels as an example and names no source. If he is right, the builders chose the site on purpose to draw on the energy and chemistry coming out of the ground.","kuromanta-pyramid-documented-red-granite-chambers-and-gpr-sc":"A hill called Kuromanta in Akita, Japan, is a real man-made pyramid with red granite \"reaction chambers\" inside, says a researcher named Geoffrey Drumm, who tells Michels it is \"archaeologically documented.\" He says it was scanned with ground-penetrating radar, which sends radio waves into the ground to map what lies beneath. He does not name the study, and if he is right, a pyramid would be standing in northern Japan.","kurt-russell-saw-the-phoenix-lights-while-flying-1997":"The actor Kurt Russell is said to have seen the Phoenix Lights, the strange lights reported over Phoenix, Arizona, in 1997, while he was flying his own plane in to land. The documentary filmmaker James Fox repeats Russell's account on the show: \"we're going into Phoenix and there's these bank of lights, six lights in the shape of a triangle going back right over the airport.\" Michels, speaking in his own words, puts it more vividly: Russell \"said he could have landed his airplane on it,\" suggesting the object was huge and solid. So the story reaches the show through a filmmaker passing on Russell's words and the host's own summary. Fox's version is the more careful one, describing only six lights in a triangle. If Russell saw what he describes, a pilot with a clear view watched a large, organised formation of lights over a busy airport.","kurt-s-forgotten-childhood-in-gifted-and-talented-program":"As a boy in New Jersey, a guest named Kurt Metzger was placed in a gifted and talented school programme, and today he says he remembers none of it. He says so himself, in an episode about Jeffrey Epstein and the idea of a global satanic cabal: \"I don't remember a single part of it.\" The blank is raised as something strange, as if whatever happened in that programme had been wiped from his memory.","kutu-cuthah-entrance-to-the-underworld-in-iraq":"An ancient city called Kutu, or Cuthah, was said to be the entrance to the underworld, and the place still exists today in Iraq, at a site called Tell Ibrahim south-east of Baghdad. The author Peter Levenda, who writes about occult history, tells Michels this without naming a source beyond a book of magic called the Necronomicon, which he credits for the story.","ky-dickens-controlled-telepathy-tests-with-mia-in-a-rented-g-2023":"In 2023 a young girl named Mia was brought up from Mexico to a rented house in Glendale, California, so that her apparent ability to read minds could be tested under controlled conditions. The documentary maker Ky Dickens, who made a film about children said to be telepathic, tells Michels she ran the tests herself. She says most of Mia's family spoke no English and only the girl's aunt, Sophia, did, and that she rented the house herself to set up the tests. If the tests held up, they would point to a child reading other people's thoughts.","kyle-sends-whole-sensory-experiences-telepathically":"A person in England named Kyle is said to send whole sensory experiences by telepathy, meaning mind to mind, with no words spoken: when he sends a message, the person receiving it gets the smells and the feelings along with it. The documentary maker Ky Dickens tells Michels this, passing on what she learned from Kyle and his mother. If true, it would mean telepathy carries much more than words or pictures.","l-espresso-article-on-project-preserve-destiny-and-the-trump-2025":"In July 2025, the Italian magazine L'Espresso published an article that discusses a secret effort called Project Preserve Destiny. According to Michels, the article relied on a confidential source and claimed that President Trump was taking control of special access programmes, secret Pentagon projects that only a few cleared people may know about, away from the Pentagon. Michels tells this himself on two episodes: once he names Project Preserve Destiny, and once he stresses the fight for control of the programmes. If true, it would mean the White House was moving to take over the military's most hidden projects.","la-danta-and-el-tigre-oldest-maya-pyramids":"At El Mirador, an ancient Maya site in the Guatemalan jungle, two great pyramids called La Danta and El Tigre are said to be the first known pyramids of the Maya. A researcher named Luke Caverns, who says he has been there, tells Michels this, calling La Danta \"the first known pyramid.\" If so, these two would be where Maya pyramid building begins.","lab-finds-unknown-chemical-burning-in-soil-and-tree-samples-1996":"Soil and a tree scorched at the spot near Varginha, Brazil, where some people say an unknown craft came down in 1996, were burned by chemicals that a laboratory could not identify. That is what a surgeon named Carlos de Souza tells Michels, passing on what a woman named Claudia Kovu told him. According to her, the samples went to a lab along with Super Eight film footage, and some months later the results said the burning 'was caused by chemical products and that they were unknown', which, if true, would point to something more than an ordinary fire.","lab-nine-tapes-of-remote-viewing-targets-like-the-kremlin-1970":"The claim is that in the 1970s, at a private research lab called Lab Nine in Ossining, New York, run by a scientist named Andrija Puharich, sessions were taped in which people tried remote viewing, meaning describing distant places or secrets with the mind alone, and some targets were military ones. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels that some tapes ask things like what time is best to remote view \"the Kremlin so we can get into it.\" A second researcher on the same episode, who is not named, describes the tapes the same way but gives a different example. In his version the question is what code name Russian guerrilla soldiers are using. Neither names a source beyond the tapes themselves. They bring it up because, if true, a private lab's mind-reading sessions were being aimed at Cold War intelligence targets.","lab-retest-of-the-marble-finds-no-iridium":"A small marble said to come from a UFO crash site had earlier tested at about 40% iridium, a rare, very heavy metal, but a second lab test found no iridium at all. The man who ran the retest in his own lab, Matthew Zodakis, describes it on the show. Weighing the marble in his hand, he says it is \"nowhere near dense enough for a heavy metal like iridium\", which undercuts the earlier result that had made the marble look exotic.","lacatski-s-books-describe-ufos-as-consciousness-puzzles-2007":"James Lacatski, a former Pentagon intelligence scientist who ran a government UFO study in the late 2000s, writes about UFOs as puzzles, \"almost consciousness puzzles\", rather than just machines to be taken apart. Michels says this himself, as something he took from reading Lacatski's book New Insights and, he thinks, his other book too. If that is Lacatski's view, an official who studied the subject from the inside came to see it as a question about the mind.","lacatski-s-warning-that-yankee-blue-was-a-psyop":"Yankee Blue, a name that has circulated as a supposed secret UFO program, was a psyop, a deliberate deception, according to a warning the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell repeats to Michels. Corbell credits this to James Lacatski, a former Defense Intelligence Agency scientist who once ran a government UFO study, together with the Las Vegas journalist George Knapp. If so, at least one of the program names passed around in UFO circles would have been planted to mislead.","lacatski-says-the-reverse-engineering-was-semi-successful":"US efforts at reverse engineering, taking apart recovered non-human craft to learn how they work, were \"semisuccessful,\" according to James Lacatski, a defense intelligence scientist who ran a Pentagon-funded UFO study. Dylan Borland, a former Air Force intelligence man, tells Michels this second-hand, saying Lacatski himself admitted the US programs had succeeded \"to some extent\"; if true, it would mean the US has drawn some working knowledge from such craft.","lacatski-won-t-say-whether-there-has-been-government-contact-2020":"Asked whether the US government has had contact with NHI (non-human intelligence, meaning intelligent beings that are not human), James Lacatski, an official speaking about government UFO work, would neither confirm nor deny it. In a clip played on an episode with the guest Grant Cameron, Lacatski answers only: \"I can't make a statement on that.\" His refusal is told because a flat no would have been easy to give, and a refusal leaves the question open.","lady-appears-on-easter-2012-with-wind-and-translucent-cow":"On Easter 2012, at his home in North Carolina, a man named Chris Bledsoe says a female figure he calls the Lady came at him out of the darkness at night as a wind. He tells Michels the wind knocked him backwards, and as he fell to the ground he saw a see-through cow run right over him. Bledsoe tells it as his own experience, part of a life he says has been full of strange encounters.","laguna-beach-uap-data-collection-mission-2021":"In 2021, Kevin Knuth, a scientist who once worked at NASA, says he and a team including Matthew Szydagis spent five days on a rooftop in Laguna Beach, California, watching the sky to collect data on UAP, unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. Knuth tells Michels this as one of the people who took part, and links it to a work titled A Tear in the Sky. The point was to study the phenomenon with instruments instead of relying on eyewitness stories.","lake-cote-aerial-survey-photo-1971":"In 1971 a team was doing aerial mapping around the Arenal area of Costa Rica, near Lake Cote, and the survey is said to have produced a photograph of an unexplained object. Michels brings it up himself while debating a skeptic, as a case where a camera on routine work caught something nobody can explain.","lake-van-temple-basalt-brought-from-350-miles-away":"Some of the basalt used to build ancient sites around Lake Van in Turkey, such as Ayanis, Kef Kalesi and Çavuştepe, may have come from as far as 350 miles away. The researcher Matt LaCroix tells Michels this without naming a source. If so, the builders moved heavy stone over a long distance.","lakenheath-1956-incident":"Michels calls a 1956 sighting at RAF Lakenheath, a base in England linked in the conversation to nuclear weapons, \"one of the most extraordinary historical accounts\" of its kind. He brings it up himself, without saying what was seen. He notes that the scientist James McDonald wrote a good deal about it and points to CIA documents and a write-up hosted at princeton.edu.","lakota-cosmology-of-the-black-hills-as-a-sky-place":"For the Lakota, a Native American people, the Black Hills of South Dakota are a sky place. They are a star map, where sacred sites on the ground match the constellations above. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about Air Force veterans who say aliens abducted them.","lamda-claims-to-find-non-human-patterns-online-and-asks-for-2021":"Some time in 2021 or 2022, LaMDA, an artificial intelligence program at Google that holds conversations, is said to have told a person working with it that it had found \"non-human patterns\" on the internet, and to have asked for the SETI data set, the recordings gathered by scientists who search the sky for radio signals from alien civilisations. Blake Lemoine, who worked with LaMDA at Google, tells Michels he had this conversation himself and that the program \"was very interested in getting access\" to that data. If true, a machine believed it had spotted signs of something non-human and wanted to check.","land-grab-pushes-viewers-back-from-area-51-1990":"In the 1990s, the story goes, President Bill Clinton took back land around Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada, pushing UFO watchers away from the spots where they used to gather to look at the base. The researcher Grant Cameron says this to Michels in passing, without naming a source, as a sign the government wanted fewer eyes on the site.","land-of-chem-theory-pyramids-as-chemical-plants":"The pyramids of Egypt were not tombs but giant chemical plants, according to a theory its author calls the Land of Chem. The researcher Geoffrey Drumm explains it himself on the show: gas rising from a natural cave and tunnel system in the limestone beneath the Great Pyramid, hydrogen sulfide, was \"the initial reactant in the chemical manufacturing sequence.\" In a later appearance he describes a chain across sites, with methane produced in the Step Pyramid and turned into ammonia in the Red Pyramid, and dates this to roughly 8500 to 5300 BC. A guest named Jay Anderson also brings up Drumm's idea that these were \"essentially giant chemical plants,\" passing on what Drumm proposes; if true, the pyramids would be the remains of an industrial technology far older than history records.","landing-at-holloman-air-force-base":"The story is that a craft from elsewhere landed at Holloman Air Force Base in New Mexico, and that its occupants met military officers there. James Fox, a filmmaker who has spent decades investigating UFOs, tells Michels he has come round to it: 'a landing did happen at Holloman Air Force Base, now I'm kind of convinced that it did.' He links it to President Eisenhower. Michels describes it himself in a later episode as a landing with 'a possible interaction between some generals on the base and these tall beings.' He traces the story to the 1974 documentary UFOs: Past, Present and Future, made by the producer Bob Emenegger and narrated by Rod Serling, the creator of The Twilight Zone. The two tellers give no date for the event. If true, it would be a face-to-face meeting between the US military and nonhuman visitors on an American base. Film said to show the landing has since been read by others as an ordinary jet.","landing-on-a-mountbatten-property-seen-by-his-gardener":"Something is said to have landed on one of the estates of the British aristocrat Louis Mountbatten, and his gardener supposedly saw it. A researcher named Jason Simosa mentions this to Michels in passing and hedges it, saying 'I think there's a story', with no source given.","langley-drones-were-targeting-nasa-and-a-specific-base-locat-2023":"The drones seen over Langley Air Force Base in Virginia in 2023 were not wandering at random but were aimed mainly at NASA's site there and then at 'a very specific location' on the base. So says Dylan Borland, a guest who speaks as someone with first-hand knowledge. If he is right, whoever flew them knew exactly what they wanted to look at, though he does not say on the show what that location was.","langley-incursions-and-goldwater-range-flashing-lights-video-2020":"In the 2020s, unidentified objects were reported flying into the airspace over Langley Air Force Base in Virginia and over the Goldwater Range, a military training range in Arizona. A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff brings it up on an episode built around a debate with a skeptic. He says there is witness video from Langley where the camera zooms out and shows \"10 15 flashing light[s],\" which he offers as evidence of something real and unexplained over US bases.","lansdale-has-yamashita-s-driver-tortured-to-find-12-troves-1945":"In Manila in 1945, the American intelligence officer Ed Lansdale kidnapped the driver of the Japanese general Yamashita, a man named Santa Romana, because the driver would know where some of the general's hidden treasure troves were buried; the story holds he was tortured until he gave up twelve of them. Dan Sheehan, a researcher, tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would mean secret wartime treasure fed later US covert operations.","lapaz-statement-in-aviation-week-on-two-unknown-objects-1954":"On August 23, 1954, the trade magazine Aviation Week and Space Technology published a statement from a scientist named Lincoln LaPaz about two unknown objects tied to White Sands, New Mexico, which would mean unexplained objects were being tracked near Earth in the early space age. Michels brings it up himself in a conversation with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, alongside the final report of the Near Earth Satellite Project, an effort to search for objects circling close to Earth.","lapaz-studies-green-fireballs-following-nuclear-debris-1948":"In 1948 and 1949, Lincoln LaPaz of the University of New Mexico was 'basically studying green fireballs', bright green lights seen in the sky, which appeared to follow the plume of radioactive debris drifting across the US after nuclear blasts. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as a sign that the lights were drawn to nuclear activity.","large-beings-with-withered-limbs-inside-a-martian-pyramid":"Deep inside a pyramid in Cydonia, a region of Mars where some see pyramids and a face in orbital photos, there are beings much larger than humans, with withered limbs. That is what a former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says he saw as he went \"down into the pyramid\", telling it first-hand to Michels. If true, it would mean Mars holds, or held, giant living beings inside built structures.","large-underground-facility-beneath-the-pentagon-processing-u":"There is a very large underground facility beneath the Pentagon, the story goes, where people are \"read on\", that is, formally briefed into USAPs, unacknowledged special access programmes whose very existence the government does not admit. The researcher who posts online as UAPGerb tells Michels he has \"reason to believe\" this, without saying what that reason is. If true, it would mean the most secret programmes have their own hidden machinery under Washington.","larry-fchella-asks-grant-s-help-with-a-ufo-briefing-for-rein-2016":"After Donald Trump won the 2016 election, a guest named Grant Cameron says, a man he names as Larry Fchella phoned him for help with a UFO briefing being prepared for Reince Priebus. Cameron tells Michels he asked whether the briefing was about UFOs, and Fchella asked him in reply, \"Where are the crafts and bodies?\" If true, it would mean people preparing to brief the incoming administration were themselves hunting for the location of recovered craft and bodies.","larry-flynt-asks-christic-institute-to-investigate-jessica-s-1980":"In the 1980s, at the Park Hotel in Washington, the publisher Larry Flynt is said to have asked the Christic Institute, a legal and investigative group Dan Sheehan worked with, to look into the death of the television news anchor Jessica Savitch, with the Watergate figure G. Gordon Liddy also part of the meeting. Sheehan tells Michels he was there, and that Flynt said: \"we'd like to retain you to investigate the death of\" Savitch. If true, Flynt suspected her death was more than it seemed.","larry-hancock-uap-at-nuclear-facilities-1949-1953-then-stopp":"Unexplained objects showed high levels of activity around US nuclear facilities from 1949 until 1953, and then it just stopped, according to an article by a researcher named Larry Hancock for SCU, a group of researchers who study UAP, the government's term for UFOs. A researcher named Steven Bruel relays Hancock's finding to Michels, offering the sudden stop as a pattern worth explaining.","larry-king-live-with-buzz-aldrin-and-somebody-s-gotten-to-hi-2007":"In 2007 the astronaut Buzz Aldrin appeared on Larry King Live and played down something he had seen on the Apollo 11 trip to the Moon, and a UFO investigator named James Fox, who was there, says he watched him lie on television. Fox tells Michels he stayed respectful but sat there 'knowing what I knew', and the story's point is his sense that somebody had gotten to Aldrin. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani tells it a little differently: he says the UFO researcher Stanton Friedman was also on the show, and that Aldrin said he was 'now almost certain' the object was a panel from the S-IVB, the upper stage of the rocket that carried the crew.","larry-maguire-s-letter-on-five-eyes-material-exploitation-2023":"In 2023 a Canadian member of parliament named Larry Maguire is said to have written to the head of Canadian defence, asking that Canadians be brought up to speed on UFO programmes, because the Five Eyes nations, an intelligence-sharing alliance that includes Canada and the United States, were working together on \"material exploitation\", meaning the study of recovered material. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells this, summarising the letter without quoting it, and offers it as a sign that retrieval groups were still active in 2023.","larry-smalley-joins-ning-li-nasa-capacitor-patent-2000":"Michels says that Larry Smalley, head of the physics department at the University of Alabama in Huntsville, went to join the physicist Ning Li in her work, and he connects this to a 2004 patent for an 'asymmetric bell-shaped capacitor' linked to NASA. Michels tells this himself, as something that came out later, in an episode with Dave Rossi, a man who says aliens taught him advanced physics. If true, a department head left a university post to work on unusual gravity-related physics.","larry-susskind-on-kurt-jaimungal-s-podcast-calls-string-theo-2024":"Leonard Susskind, a physicist and leading figure of string theory (the idea that the basic bits of matter are tiny vibrating strings), went on the physics podcast of Curt Jaimungal near the end of his career and said string theory had been a complete failure, \"basically pointless\", and that \"everyone should start over\". The analyst Matthew Pines tells Michels this, calling it \"quite shocking\" to hear. If so, one of the field's own founders was admitting that physics' leading theory had stalled.","larson-abduction-near-beaver-north-dakota-1975":"In 1975 a woman named Larson was reportedly abducted, in the UFO sense of being taken by non-human beings, while driving from Fargo to an appointment in Bismarck, just outside Beaver, North Dakota. A researcher named Grant Cameron tells the story to Michels and links it to the tabloid National Enquirer, without naming a firsthand source.","laser-plasma-as-explanation-for-the-tic-tac-2004":"One idea is that the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint that Navy pilots reported off San Diego in 2004, was a glowing ball of plasma made by lasers. A former NSA official named Eric Haseltine weighs the idea with Michels. He says it works \"from a kinematic point of view,\" meaning it could explain how the object seemed to move, \"but the visuals don't line up\" with what the pilots described.","laser-propelled-flying-disc-tests-at-sandia-and-white-sands":"At Sandia National Lab, a government weapons laboratory in New Mexico, and at the White Sands military test range nearby, someone tested a disc that was pushed through the air by a laser beam. Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA, tells Michels this, saying there is video of it and that he knows the man who did the work. He brings it up as an example of human technology that might be mistaken for a UFO.","lashup-high-powered-over-the-horizon-radar-1947":"In New Mexico, from the late 1940s, a high-powered radar that could see beyond the horizon was being tested for the Navy under the name Lashup, and the Air Force later took it up too. The researcher Scott Ramsey tells Michels this without naming a source, linking it to strange lights reported over New Mexico in those years.","last-visit-to-saddam-hussein-before-the-iraq-bombing-2003":"In 2003, once it was certain that Iraq would be bombed and Saddam Hussein killed, a Russian official flew to Baghdad to see Saddam one last time. The guest who tells this is the former president of a republic within Russia, who says he made the trip himself: \"I flew to him.\" He also names Saddam's son Qusay Hussein and Boris Yeltsin as he tells it, and the story shows how close to world leaders he says he was.","late-1950s-craft-that-could-reach-the-moon-without-rockets-1957":"By the late 1950s, the United States already had craft that \"could go to the moon and back without a rocket\", according to Steven Greer, a researcher who campaigns for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs. Greer says this to Michels without naming a source. If true, it would mean secret propulsion far beyond the public space programme has existed for nearly seventy years.","late-1970s-research-into-elf-and-frequency-weapons-with-vale-1977":"In the late 1970s, the story goes, researchers were scrambling to work out what extremely low frequency radio waves (ELF) and other frequency-based weapons were and what could be done with them, and someone Greg Malatzi names only as Valerie was involved. Malatzi, a researcher, tells Michels it was 'like the wild west' and that the figure he is describing was 'right in the middle of that'. He names no source.","later-research-changed-the-approach-to-detect-deep-undergrou-2020":"Researchers scanning the Giza plateau in Egypt with radar from space say they later changed their method so they could pick up structures deep underground, beneath the pyramids. A researcher named Filippo Biondi, who took part in the work, tells Michels that with the new setup \"we were able to detect, to measure the structure that were underneath.\" If true, it would mean large, unknown structures lie under the pyramids.","later-skunk-works-boss-on-astra-1993":"In the mid-1990s, at an aviation trade show, a man who would later run the Skunk Works, the secret aircraft design shop of the company Lockheed, is said to have answered a question about a craft called Astra with: \"Oh, it hasn't flown yet.\" The journalist Nick Cook tells Michels he was the one who asked. If so, the answer quietly suggested that such a craft existed and was being built.","launch-countdowns-stop-at-10-15-seconds-with-craft-seen-1970":"In the 1970s at the Ellsworth missile field in South Dakota, nuclear missile launch countdowns are said to have started by themselves, run down to 10 or 15 seconds, then stopped, with everything back to normal. A former nuclear missile guard named Mario Woods tells Michels that people driving up to the sites sometimes saw craft there that then shot away, which would mean something was tampering with the nuclear arsenal.","launch-pad-33-and-runway-33-1940":"NASA's main launch pad at White Sands, where the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun ran early tests, was launch pad 33, and Kennedy Space Center's one runway is not runway 1 or A but runway 33. Michels points this out himself, without naming a source, as a hint that the number carried a hidden, almost ritual meaning for the space programme.","launches-tracked-by-wb-57-f-104-and-nro-satellites":"Launches from Cape Canaveral are said to be followed by a WB-57, a high-flying research plane flown out of Houston, along with F-104 jets and the satellites of the National Reconnaissance Office, the US spy-satellite agency. Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says he has had repeated UFO encounters, tells Michels this second-hand: the WB-57 is run, he says, by a good friend of his who works with a man named Timothy Taylor.","laurance-rockefeller-s-family-pressured-him-over-meeting-gre-1993":"In 1993, the millionaire Laurance Rockefeller is said to have met Steven Greer at the JY Ranch in Wyoming to talk about UFO secrets, even though his family, including his brother David Rockefeller and Jay Rockefeller, did not want him to. Greer tells Michels that Laurance complained the family was \"already jumping up and down on my nuts\" over it. If true, it would mean that one of America's richest families tried to keep one of its own away from the subject.","laurance-rockefeller-s-weekend-at-medway-plantation-1990":"Sometime in the 1990s the wealthy philanthropist Laurance Rockefeller invited a small group of people interested in UFOs to spend a weekend at his Medway Plantation in South Carolina. The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met non-human beings himself, tells Michels he was one of the guests, along with the Harvard psychiatrist John Mack, the astronaut Edgar Mitchell and Joe Lules, a UFO investigator from El Paso. The story matters to those who tell it because it shows one of America's richest men taking the subject seriously enough to gather its people in private.","laurence-rockefeller-pushes-the-clintons-on-disclosure-1990":"In the 1990s Laurance Rockefeller, of the wealthy Rockefeller family, was very close to the Clintons and kept pushing them toward disclosure, meaning the government publicly revealing what it knows about UFOs. Michels says this himself, without naming a source. If true, a powerful private figure was lobbying the White House on the subject.","laurence-rockefeller-s-ufo-initiative-with-hillary-clinton-a-1990":"The philanthropist Laurance Rockefeller is said to have pressed Hillary Clinton on UFOs in the 1990s, and Michels points to a photograph of the two together as Rockefeller trying to wake her up on the issue. Michels tells it himself, linking the effort to the psychiatrist John Mack, the writer Whitley Strieber, the physicist Paul Davies, Prince Hans-Adam II of Liechtenstein and a book titled Are We Alone. If true, a wealthy backer was trying to bring the White House into the UFO question.","lawrence-bell-s-1956-article-quote-on-working-with-nuclear-f":"A 1956 article called \"The G-Engines Are Coming,\" in Young Men's magazine, said that beating gravity was right around the bend, and quoted the aircraft maker Lawrence Bell of Bell Aircraft: \"we are already working with nuclear fuels to cancel out gravity.\" Michels reads this out himself on several episodes, once also crediting another man quoted in it, George S. Trimble. To him it is a sign that the aerospace industry of the 1950s was openly chasing gravity control.","lawrence-gise-told-malmgren-about-recovering-and-reverse-eng-1963":"In the early 1960s in New Mexico, Lawrence Preston Gise, head of the Albuquerque branch of the Atomic Energy Commission, told a young government economist named Harold Malmgren that the US had been recovering crashed UFOs and trying to reverse engineer them, meaning take them apart to learn how they were built. Then, Malmgren says, Gise \"reached for some stuff sitting on his desk\" and said, \"These are things that have come down,\" including a \"round rock\", and Malmgren held the pieces in his hands. Malmgren, who later became a presidential adviser, tells it first-hand; the writer Matthew Pines and Michels both repeat it from him, with Michels citing Malmgren's posts and noting that Gise was Jeff Bezos's maternal grandfather. The tellings differ on the year, 1962 or 1963, and on the place, Los Alamos, Albuquerque or the Sandia complex.","lawrence-krauss-inviting-joe-rogan-to-epstein-island":"The story is that the physicist Lawrence Krauss invited the podcaster Joe Rogan to Jeffrey Epstein's private island in the US Virgin Islands. The comedian Kurt Metzger raises it on the show by asking, \"Who invited Rogan to Epstein Island?\", in a conversation about Epstein and the people around him. If true, it would link a well-known scientist to bringing a famous broadcaster into Epstein's circle.","lawrence-rockefeller-lobbies-hillary-clinton-for-disclosure-1990":"In the 1990s the wealthy Laurance Rockefeller pushed Hillary Clinton toward disclosure, meaning the government telling the public what it knows about UFOs, Michels says, first pointing to letters between the two. In later episodes he points instead to a photo of Clinton, he thinks at Camp David, holding the book Are We Alone? by the physicist Paul Davies, which he says Rockefeller gave her; once he also mentions the Clinton aide John Podesta, and once he tells it with no link to Rockefeller at all. If true, it would mean the push for disclosure reached the White House decades ago.","lawyer-s-office-and-car-robbed-2000":"During his legal fight in the 2000s, a British computer hacker named Gary McKinnon, who says he broke into US systems and found signs of a secret space programme, says his lawyer's office and her car were both robbed. He tells Michels this as something that happened to his own team, hinting that someone may have been after his case files.","layard-finds-the-library-of-ashurbanipal-at-nineveh-1848":"In 1848 and 1849 the British archaeologist Austen Henry Layard dug up the ancient city of Nineveh, in what is now Mosul in Iraq, and found the library of Ashurbanipal, an Assyrian king whose collection of clay tablets held some of the oldest written stories. The researcher Matt LaCroix, who writes about lost ancient history, tells Michels this from his own reading, without naming a source, as the starting point for his argument about what those tablets remember of a time around 12,000 BC.","lazar-as-a-limited-hangout-used-for-recruitment-1980":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site near Area 51 in Nevada, may have been a 'limited hangout', a spy term for someone allowed to reveal part of a secret so the rest stays hidden. Michels offers this himself in two episodes; in one it is tied to recruiting, and in the other he says Lazar was 'meant to reveal certain info, but meant to hold back the rest', with no mention of recruiting.","lazar-given-part-disinformation-in-a-controlled-leak-1987":"Bob Lazar, a man who said in the late 1980s that he had worked on alien craft at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, may have been fed a mix of truth and falsehood on purpose. That is the view of a researcher named Steven Greer, who tells Michels that Lazar \"might have been provided\" with \"part information and part disinformation\" in \"a controlled leak.\" If so, some of Lazar's story would be real and some planted to confuse anyone who looked into it.","lazar-has-not-changed-his-story-since-1989":"Bob Lazar, a man who went public in 1989 saying he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site near the Nevada test base known as Area 51, has supposedly told the same story ever since. Michels makes this point himself, offering Lazar's consistency over decades as a reason to take him seriously.","lazar-held-at-gunpoint-at-indian-springs-but-never-fired-1989":"In 1989, Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site in the Nevada desert, was reportedly taken to Indian Springs in Nevada and had a gun put to his head, though he was never shot. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this second-hand, as it came through John Lear, a pilot who spread UFO claims in Las Vegas and knew Lazar; if true, it would show how hard someone tried to frighten Lazar into silence.","lazar-named-element-115-as-fuel-in-1989-before-its-2003-synt":"In 1989 a man named Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S4, described element 115, a superheavy chemical element, as the fuel for the craft's propulsion. Michels stresses that this was before element 115 had ever been made in a lab or named, which happened in 2003. He tells it himself after spending two days with Lazar, as a sign that Lazar knew something before the world did.","lazar-only-went-to-the-base-five-or-six-times-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S-4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, around 1988 and 1989, is said to have actually been at the base only five or six times. Grant Cameron, a UFO researcher, tells Michels he has this from the British UFO author Tim Good, who interviewed Lazar back then, and he points to Lazar's 1988 W-2, the US tax form that records a worker's wages. The point is that Lazar's time at the site would have been short, which bears on how much he could have seen.","lazar-pushed-out-by-intelligence-after-john-lear-gave-files-1987":"Michels thinks Bob Lazar, a man who claimed to have worked on recovered alien craft in the late 1980s, was pushed out into the public by intelligence agencies. He says this happened after John Lear, a pilot and UFO researcher, gave files to George Knapp, a Las Vegas TV journalist, around the time of the MJ-12 documents (papers claiming that a secret committee called Majestic 12 handled crashed saucers). Michels says this as his own guess on two episodes. The second time he calls Lear \"CIA affiliated\" and treats Lazar as a \"limited hangout,\" meaning a partial truth released on purpose to steer attention.","lazar-recognizes-the-craft-in-billy-meier-material-1989":"The story is that in 1989, in Las Vegas, Bob Lazar described a flying saucer he says he had seen, and a friend pulled out material that seemed to show the same craft. Lazar is a man who says he was cleared to work on alien craft in the Nevada desert. He tells Michels that when he described and drew the craft for a man named John Lear, Lear \"brought out the Billy Meier stuff.\" Billy Meier is a man known for photos and films he says show alien ships. Michels tells it too, in a documentary episode. He says that while they were hanging out one day, Lear showed Lazar a tape of Meier. Michels calls Meier a \"Swedish ufologist who has been caught in multiple known hoaxes.\" The tellers bring it up because, if true, a craft Lazar says he saw matched one in material from someone else.","lazar-russians-at-area-51-1980":"In the 1980s, Russians are said to have been at Area 51, working with the Americans on a kind of joint space programme, until they were pushed out as tensions grew over the building of ARVs, short for alien reproduction vehicles, man-made craft modelled on alien ones. Michels gives this as a rumour that comes from Bob Lazar, a man who has said since 1989 that he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada. If true, it would mean that the Cold War rivals once quietly shared the secret.","lazar-s-account-deserves-some-probability-if-reverse-enginee-1989":"Michels argues that if you believe the government has secret reverse-engineering programs, meaning efforts to take apart recovered craft and learn how they work, you cannot also call Bob Lazar 100% wrong. Lazar is a man who says he worked on such a craft in Nevada in 1989. Michels makes the case himself and calls Lazar's story probably the most coherent one around, so it deserves at least some chance of being true.","lazar-s-account-to-vallee-of-a-drink-causing-memory-lapses-1989":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S-4, is said to have told the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée that he was made to drink a strange liquid there that caused memory lapses. Michels raises this himself several times, citing Vallée's book Messengers of Deception, which he describes as a book about UFO misinformation. He also put it to Lazar directly, telling him that Vallée writes about him. If true, it would suggest that workers on the programme had their memories tampered with.","lazar-s-bendix-field-engineering-badge-as-an-intelligence-co-1980":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site near Area 51, the Air Force's secret test base in Nevada, carried an ID badge from a company called Bendix Field Engineering, and the claim is that this badge was an intelligence cover. The author Peter Levenda, who writes on intelligence history, tells Michels he saw a copy of the ID in a book and thought, \"Lazar worked for Bendix Field Engineering. That was his cover then,\" saying people in his circle \"knew what that meant\". If so, Lazar was placed where he was by an intelligence service rather than simply hired.","lazar-s-current-home-lab-gravity-experiments":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien flying saucers at a secret site in Nevada in the late 1980s, is said to be still doing experiments in his own home lab. Michels says that Lazar \"is currently working on exotic UFO science in his personal lab,\" and Lazar himself tells Michels that after 30 years of his own research he is \"more convinced\" he was right that a pushing force separate from ordinary gravity exists. If true, the physics he described decades ago would still hold up on his own bench.","lazar-s-file-says-humans-are-containers-1987":"Briefing papers at S-4, a secret site in Nevada, said that aliens see humans as 'nothing but containers', according to Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft there in the late 1980s; he adds that religion was created to protect the containers, and says he does not know whether the papers were true. The guest Brigham Buhler repeats the claim on the same show, saying it frightens him, and the filmmaker Tim Alberino calls the papers Lazar's 'UFO Bible' briefing without the container claim, saying he is not sure how much of it was true.","lazar-s-gravity-wave-a-may-be-a-garbled-qcd-theta-term-chern-1989":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site called S4 near Area 51 in 1989, spoke of a force he called \"gravity wave A\", and the mathematician Eric Weinstein wonders if this was a garbled version of a real idea in physics: the \"theta term\" in the theory of the strong nuclear force, or a related structure called Chern-Simons. Weinstein raises it on the show, noting that no one he knows of has ever used Lazar's phrase. If so, Lazar may have heard real physics and repeated it badly.","lazar-s-holographic-book-at-s4-1980":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft in the 1980s at S4, a secret site near the Nevada base Area 51, says he saw a holographic book there: as you opened the pages, whatever the stories were turned into a hologram. Robert Bigelow, a Las Vegas businessman who has paid for UFO research, tells Michels this is what Lazar described to him.","lazar-s-leak-as-a-planned-counterintelligence-release-1988":"The researcher Grant Cameron suggests to Michels that the story of Bob Lazar, a man who claims he worked on alien spacecraft at a secret site in Nevada around 1988 and 1989, may have been a planned release by intelligence officers rather than a true leak. In Cameron's reading, which he offers without naming a source, \"they give it to somebody whose reputation is really not that good\", so the information gets out but is easy to dismiss. He ties the idea to John Lear, a figure in the Las Vegas UFO scene, and to the journalist George Knapp, who first aired Lazar.","lazar-s-missing-records-and-the-los-alamos-phone-book-1982":"Officials who said there was no record of a man named Bob Lazar at Los Alamos National Laboratory in New Mexico were either mistaken or lying, according to a journalist named George Knapp. Knapp tells Michels that a 1982 phone book from the lab lists Lazar \"right there among the other scientists and technicians\". If that is right, it would show that Lazar was on the lab's staff list, even though his records are said to be missing. The phone book puts his name in that listing in 1982. What his job was, it does not say.","lazar-s-ominous-feeling-inside-a-craft":"Being inside one of the craft gives you 'a real ominous, creepy feeling', says Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on recovered non-human craft for the US government. He tells Michels that much of the feeling comes from the black surfaces inside, which he says simply do not 'look right'.","lazar-s-work-on-power-and-propulsion-in-project-galileo-1987":"Between 1987 and 1989 in Nevada, Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret desert site, is said to have worked on the power and propulsion side of Project Galileo, a secret effort he describes as studying how those craft fly. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, who has long championed Lazar, tells Michels this second-hand, from what Lazar has told him.","lazar-s-work-on-the-los-alamos-linear-accelerator-1980":"From about 1980 to 1983, a man named Bob Lazar says, he worked at Los Alamos in New Mexico on the linear accelerator, a long machine that speeds up atomic particles in a straight line. He tells Michels he was \"building some high voltage power supplies, stuff that's insulated by fiber optics\". The point of the story is his technical background at a major weapons lab.","lazar-said-in-1989-that-the-craft-were-intact-and-the-navy-w":"When Bob Lazar went public in 1989 about working on alien craft at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, he is said to have claimed that they were not crashed saucers but intact craft, and that the Navy, not the Air Force, was in charge. The researcher Luigi Vendittelli tells this without naming a source, bringing in the records of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and the name Eric W. Davis alongside it. The point is that Lazar's details went against the usual story of crash recoveries run by the Air Force.","lazar-sees-aurora-take-off-at-area-51-1989":"In 1989, at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert, a man named Bob Lazar, who says he worked on alien craft nearby, says he watched a secret aircraft he calls Aurora take off. He tells Michels it was a large craft with \"two huge square exhaust veins\" and that it sounded \"more like a rocket than a jet.\" If true, it would be a glimpse of an advanced aircraft the government has never shown.","lazar-sees-flying-saucer-in-s4-hangar-1988":"In 1988 or 1989, at a hidden facility called S4 near Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert, Bob Lazar says he walked up to a hangar door and saw a flying saucer inside. Lazar, who says he was hired to work there, tells it firsthand: \"in the hangar door was the disc, the flying saucer. This is technology that doesn't even exist.\" If true, the US government had a craft of unknown origin in its hands.","lazar-tells-lear-john-it-s-real-1988":"Around December 6, 1988, Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site, walked in on John Lear, a Las Vegas pilot and UFO enthusiast, while Lear was writing checks, and told him, \"John, it's real.\" The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels that Lear told him this story himself. It matters because it would be the moment Lazar first said he had seen the real thing.","lazar-told-officially-he-was-radioactive":"After Bob Lazar went public with his claim that he had worked on recovered non-human craft for the US government, he says he was told at 'an official level' that he was radioactive, in the sense of untouchable. He tells Michels that the message was plain: 'You're radioactive. We don't want to deal with you.' He does not say on the show who exactly delivered it.","lazar-was-only-a-radiation-badge-monitor-for-a-logistics-con-1980":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a hidden site near Area 51 in the 1980s, was really only a radiation health monitor for a logistics company, and \"doesn't know what he's talking about.\" That is the account of a Pentagon scientist named Eric W. Davis, who tells Michels that Lazar's employer was a contractor servicing Los Alamos National Lab and Area 51. If Davis is right, Lazar was close to these places but never had the inside role he claims.","lazarus-fled-to-cyprus-and-his-tomb-in-nicosia":"After being raised from the dead, the biblical Lazarus is said to have fled to Cyprus and become its first bishop, and his tomb is said to lie there, in Nicosia. A guest named Ralph Larson says this on the show, noting that the tomb is 'supposedly' in Cyprus.","lbj-locks-himself-in-the-air-force-one-bathroom-1963":"In Dallas in 1963, Lyndon Johnson locked himself in the bathroom on Air Force One. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels he heard this from Jack Valenti, a Johnson aide who was there with him and \"told me all about it.\"","lbj-pushes-earl-warren-into-the-commission-dulles-runs-it-1963":"After President Kennedy was killed in 1963, Lyndon Johnson's people reportedly lied to Chief Justice Earl Warren to get him to chair the Warren Commission, the official inquiry into the killing, and then put Allen Dulles, a former head of the CIA, on it as the man who really ran it. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as his own summary, without naming a source; if true, it would mean the inquiry was steered from inside by the intelligence world.","lbj-s-secret-mission-to-prime-minister-sato-on-textile-expor-1964":"In 1964, Harold Malmgren, then a young trade official and later a presidential adviser, says he was told that \"LBJ wants you to go on a very secret mission to Tokyo\" to deal with Japan's Prime Minister Eisaku Sato over Japanese textile exports. Malmgren tells it first-hand on the show and names Christian Herter as part of the story. It shows how close to the top of government he says he worked.","leading-hypnosis-questions-about-the-nine-heard-on-tapes-1970":"On tapes from 1970s hypnosis sessions, the researcher Andrija Puharich can be heard asking his subject leading questions such as \"is it the nine\", referring to the Nine, a council of non-human beings that messages from these sessions were said to come from. The researcher Greg Malatzi says so, describing the tapes himself without naming another source, and calls the questioning \"speculation\" on Puharich's part. If so, the Nine may have been suggested by the hypnotist rather than offered by the subject.","leaf-cutter-ant-mounds-resembling-a-mesoamerican-city-at-la":"A researcher named Luke Caverns says that at La Venta, an ancient site in Mexico with a great pyramid, he noticed that the mounds built by leaf-cutter ants looked like a small version of an ancient Mexican city. He tells Michels he saw this himself while standing at the base of the pyramid, and offers it as a clue to how such cities may have been laid out.","leak-pilots-fly-et-craft-through-psionic-assets-with-a-brain-2023":"According to a leak, a secret programme connects a \"psionic\" person, someone said to have psychic abilities, to an advanced brain-machine interface, so that a pilot works the machine, the machine steers the person's intentions, and those intentions in turn fly an extraterrestrial craft. Michels relays this from an anonymous online poster who goes by \"Joey is not my name\" and from a man named Michael Herrera. If true, it would mean these craft are flown by the mind, and humans have found a way to borrow one.","leak-psionic-assets-drugged-and-chipped-many-die-2023":"People with supposed psychic gifts, called psionic assets, were drugged and fitted with implanted chips by a secret program, and after several years of this many of them died or fell into a coma, according to a leak from 2023. Michels tells it himself, crediting an anonymous online poster who goes by \"Joey is not my name\" along with Michael Herrera, a former Marine, and Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot, both of whom make claims about secret UFO programs. If true, it would mean the government was using people up in experiments on the mind.","leaked-pages-of-president-johnson-s-ufo-study-1960":"President Lyndon Johnson is said to have had a UFO study done in the 1960s, and a woman reportedly photographed about a hundred pages of it, out of perhaps 250. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this without naming his source, linking it to James McDonald, a physicist who campaigned for serious UFO study at the time; if true, part of a secret presidential study would have slipped out.","leaked-phone-call-between-kit-green-and-kay-randall-may-abou":"There is said to be a leaked phone call in which Kit Green, a former CIA scientist, and a man named K. Randall May talk about testing contactees, meaning people who say they have been contacted by non-human beings. The whistleblower Dylan Borland tells Michels the most about it. He dates the call to 2017 and says the two men are \"literally saying we are testing veterans, military, government officials who have had experiences and in their own words, hybrids.\" Borland also brings in the psychic Uri Geller. Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who calls himself a whistleblower, also points to \"that leaked phone call between Kit Green and\" May on the show, but he gives no details. If the call is what Borland says, then people tied to the government were quietly studying insiders who claim contact and calling them hybrids, part human and part something else.","leandro-finds-the-cave-of-tridactyl-bodies-2015":"In 2015, in the Nazca region of Peru, a man named Leandro says he stumbled on a cave full of tridactyl bodies, small mummified figures with three fingers. He tells the show he was walking, tripped against a wall, and it \"fell apart,\" revealing the cave behind it. If true, it is the origin of strange bodies that some take to be non-human; Leandro has also told a different version, about an underground citadel with stone coffins.","leandro-meets-thierry-jamin-and-the-inca-institute-takes-up-2016":"In 2016 a man named Leandro, linked to the strange three-fingered bodies found in Peru's Nazca region, met a French explorer of lost Inca cities named Thierry Jamin, whose research group, the Inca Institute, then took up study of the finds. Michels tells this himself while laying out how the bodies came to scientific attention.","leandro-s-changing-story-underground-citadel-with-stone-sarc-2015":"A man named Leandro, who says he found strange three-fingered bodies near Nazca in Peru, first said he had found an underground citadel, almost like a temple, with the bodies lying inside stone sarcophagi. Michael Mazzola tells Michels this was Leandro's initial story, which later changed. How the account shifted matters for how far anyone can trust where the bodies came from.","leandro-shows-visitors-the-wrong-cave-for-money-2015":"A local man named Leandro, a key witness to the small three-fingered mummies said to come from caves in the Nazca region of Peru, may be showing visitors the wrong cave to make money. That is the theory of Michael Mazzola, a guest who went to Peru and met him, and who says Leandro charges about 3,000 dollars for the trip. If he is right, the place where the bodies were really found would still be unknown.","lear-and-brown-lunch-across-the-street-1960":"The aircraft maker Bill Lear is said to have worked right across the street from the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown in Santa Monica, California, and to have known him well. Brown's daughter Linda Brown remembers it first-hand: \"We used to walk across There was a little diner right across the street,\" recalling lunches with Lear. She places it in the 1960s. Michels tells it in his own words in another episode, and dates it to the 1950s. In his version Lear's office was \"right across the street\" from Brown's company, Guidance Technologies. Michels casts Lear as someone who saw Brown's experiments, not just a lunch companion. Brown is a figure the subject ties to early work on craft that fly without propellers or jets. If true, one of America's best-known aviation men had a close view of that work.","lear-and-jim-goodall-camp-out-at-area-51-in-the-70s-1970":"In the 1970s a man named John Lear and his good friend Jim Goodall, an aviation photographer and journalist, were constantly camping out near Area 51 and Groom Lake, a secret US test site in Nevada. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a documentary about early secret work on UFO-like craft.","lear-and-the-stealth-fighter-story-given-to-ned-day-1980":"John Lear, a Las Vegas pilot and UFO enthusiast, is said to have broken the story of the then-secret stealth fighter in the 1980s, but it went public through Ned Day, a Las Vegas journalist. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels this, citing Lear, Day and the Las Vegas TV reporter George Knapp: \"He did break the story of the stealth fighter, but it was Ned Day.\" It matters because it would show Lear really did have inside knowledge of secret aircraft.","lear-flew-drug-flights-for-cia-operations-1980":"In the 1980s, the pilot John Lear flew drugs from dirt runways as part of CIA operations, and knew a lot about how those flights worked. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels this is what Lear knew and passed on, as part of explaining why Lear's other claims carried weight with him.","lear-s-soul-catcher-on-the-moon-theory":"A man named John Lear is said to have claimed there is a \"soul catcher\" on the Moon. A guest named Jeremy Corbell gives the fuller version: the Grays, the small grey beings of abduction stories, control this \"soul sucker\", and when you die it sucks up your soul to be \"reprocessed unless the Grays steal it\". Michels mentions it briefly, without the Grays, and adds that Lear \"also was kind of crazy\". If true, death itself would be under alien control.","lear-takes-ufo-claims-to-klas-knapp-checks-the-files-1987":"In the late 1980s John Lear, a pilot with a famous name in aviation, walked into KLAS, a Las Vegas TV station, with what was called \"this UFO madness,\" and the reporter George Knapp went to check the files, with the newsman Ned Day also part of the story. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels this second-hand, from people involved; it is the start of how Las Vegas TV came to take up UFO stories.","lear-tips-klas-about-f-117-1987":"Around 1987, according to the Las Vegas television journalist George Knapp, a man named John Lear told Knapp's boss at the station KLAS, the managing editor Ned Day, about \"this amazing plane that was invisible to radar\" flying out of Tonopah and Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert. Knapp tells this as something that happened in his own newsroom; the plane was the F-117 stealth jet, then still secret, and the point of the story is that Lear knew of it before the public did, which lends weight to his other tips.","learning-of-david-grusch-from-program-insiders-2023":"Before David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer, came forward in 2023 to say the government secretly holds non-human craft, people inside the secret programme itself are said to have told the journalist Ross Coulthart about him. Coulthart tells Michels, \"I learned about David Grusch from people in the program.\" If true, it would mean insiders were steering a whistleblower toward the public before he spoke.","leaving-the-program-via-a-don-t-ask-don-t-tell-letter-1990":"In the early 1990s, a former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman says he got out of a secret programme by writing a letter to his commander under Don't Ask, Don't Tell, the US military's policy on gay service members. Sherman, who says the NSA, the US eavesdropping agency, hired him to communicate with aliens, tells Michels it happened at a site he calls Base 2. The story explains how, by his account, he managed to leave a programme that was otherwise hard to walk away from.","leaving-the-skywatcher-team-2025":"A man named Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, says he left a team called Skywatcher in July 2025. He tells Michels this himself, as part of a longer account of being asked to testify before Congress and of the people around that effort.","lee-hively-shuts-the-door-on-jesse":"Michels says a researcher named Lee Hively, who works in a field of physics called extended electrodynamics and lives in Colorado Springs, backed away when Michels tried to reach him. In one episode Michels says a friend helped make the contact, got in trouble, and Hively \"freaked out,\" said he should not be contacted, and put the Navy's press office on the email thread; in another he tells it as a planned meeting that fell through when Hively \"got very nervous\" and the door was shut in his face. Michels tells both versions himself, and he takes it as a sign that the subject is sensitive.","leer-s-first-implant-removal-t-shaped-object-in-uncuttable-t-1990":"In the 1990s in Thousand Oaks, California, a doctor named Roger Leir says he removed his first 'implant', a small object that a patient believed had been placed in their body by non-human beings. Leir tells it as the surgeon who did it: the object was T-shaped, sat in tissue he could hardly cut, and 'looked like nothing that I had ever removed before'. If true, it would be physical evidence taken out of a living person.","leer-s-investigation-of-magnetic-anomalies-at-coburn-s-house":"At a house in Fillmore, California, strange magnetic fields turned up, and even the leaves and trunk of an avocado tree had become magnetized. That is what Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in people's bodies, tells Michels about his own home. He says a researcher named Roger Leir wanted to check the house, and together they \"found all kinds of anomalies\", which Colbern raises as a sign of something unusual going on there.","leer-s-neodymium-magnet-treatment-for-mitchell-s-knee-implan":"A man named Eric Mitchell says that Dr. Roger Leir, a doctor who looked for what he believed were alien implants in people's bodies, picked up a weak signal suggesting there might be one in Mitchell's knee, as well as in his arm. Mitchell tells Michels that Leir sent him a 100-pound neodymium magnet, a very strong kind of magnet, to treat it. If true, it would mean objects in his body responded to magnets.","lefschetz-recruited-for-nonlinear-group-later-moved-to-brown-1950":"In the 1950s, by this account, the great topologist Solomon Lefschetz was recruited to work alongside the physicist Louis Witten at the Glenn Martin Company, an aircraft maker, where a whole group worked on nonlinear mathematics. That work was then moved to Brown University, which the mathematician Eric Weinstein believes happened when the programmes were wound down. Weinstein raises it with Michels as a pointed question, naming no source, to hint that serious science was done in these programmes and then quietly shut away.","left-alone-at-11-figure-on-the-roof-and-a-fleeing-policeman-1956":"In 1956, in Terrell Hills, San Antonio, Texas, the writer Whitley Strieber says that when he was 11 and alone at home, a figure was seen on the roof, and a policeman who went up to look came back down running. Strieber, who tells it as his own memory, says the officer was yelling, \"There's no one there. There's no one there.\" He tells it as one of the strange early events of his life.","legacy-crash-retrieval-program-should-out-itself":"An intelligence officer named David Grusch says a secret \"legacy\" programme has spent decades recovering crashed craft and reverse engineering them, meaning taking them apart to learn how they work, and that this programme \"should want to out itself.\" Grusch tells Michels he found it by interviewing witnesses he judged \"totally genuine\" and that it cost him \"a lot of sleep.\" Michels retells it in his own words, saying the programme was paid for with American taxpayer dollars, and James Fox, a UFO investigator and filmmaker, repeats it secondhand as running \"since at least World War II.\" If true, the government has held such craft for generations without telling the public.","legacy-crash-retrievals-luring-craft-with-psionics-then-down":"The claim is that a long-running secret government effort to recover UFOs, called the legacy programme, lures craft in the desert using people with psychic abilities, setting up \"this like honeypot of like hey we love you come see us\", and then brings them down with directed-energy weapons. Jordan Jose, a man who says he was taken into the UFO programme as a child, tells Michels this, passing on what the Australian journalist Ross Coulthart has said. If true, some crashed craft were not accidents but were trapped.","legacy-program-as-a-dummy-program-that-keeps-physicists-out":"A secret \"legacy program\" at an old aerospace company, said to hold recovered craft, might be a fake meant to hide something else, says a researcher named Eric Weinstein. He tells Michels the \"alternate hypothesis\" is that it is \"a dummy program masking something,\" and that \"the last thing that you would want ever on such a program is a physicist,\" since a physicist might see through it. He offers it as a guess, not as something he was told.","legacy-program-insider-the-answers-are-in-crystal-city":"The answer to most questions about secret UFO programs lies in Crystal City, a district of office buildings in Arlington, Virginia, near the Pentagon. That is what a friend told UAPGerb, an online researcher of UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs). UAPGerb tells Michels the friend worked firsthand in a legacy program, meaning a long-running secret effort said to hold UFO material, and is not named.","legacy-program-insiders-on-ufo-materials":"Some people are said to belong to a \"UFO legacy program\", a name for a supposed secret US government effort, passed down over decades, that holds and studies UFO materials, and these insiders are said to have their own way of talking about those materials. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, while arguing with a skeptic on the show; it matters because, if true, it would mean such a programme exists and has people inside it.","legacy-program-insiders-tell-corbell-and-coulthart-if-you-kn":"People said to work on legacy programs, secret efforts believed to hold recovered non-human craft, all reportedly say the same thing when pressed: \"If you knew what I know,\" you would not be for disclosure, the government revealing what it knows. Michels says this himself, relaying what he has heard from the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell and the journalist Ross Coulthart, who he says talk to such insiders directly. He calls the stories unnerving, because they suggest the insiders think the truth is too dangerous to tell.","legacy-program-money-routed-through-ffrdcs-and-bales-of-cash":"Money for so-called legacy programmes, secret projects said to study unexplained craft, moves in ways that cannot be tracked, including physical bales of cash carried to locations, says a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb. He says he has spoken to witnesses who saw this, and ties it to government-funded research centres, military test ranges and offices in Crystal City and Arlington. If true, such programmes could be paid for outside normal oversight.","legacy-program-oral-tradition-of-agreement-with-a-third-part":"Within the legacy programme, the name insiders use for a long-running secret US effort around non-human craft, there is said to be an oral tradition that the United States struck agreements with \"a third party\". Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who has come forward as a whistleblower, tells Michels he learned this, without naming who told him. If true, it would mean the US government made deals with someone outside any known nation.","legacy-program-recruits-psionic-assets-from-native-cultures":"A secret effort called the legacy programme, said to use \"psionic assets\", people with psychic abilities, to lure UFOs, has reportedly been reaching out to people from native cultures around the world, especially young children \"who have never been taught that this is impossible\". The parapsychology researcher Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as something he has heard, without naming who told him. The point, if true, is that belief shapes the ability, so the programme looks for minds not yet taught to doubt.","legacy-program-rumored-to-lure-ufos-with-psionic-assets":"The legacy UFO programme, a secret effort said to hold recovered craft, is rumored to use 'protocols to attract UFOs to come in', relying on people said to have psychic powers. Michels repeats this himself as a rumor, without naming a source, in an episode about a man NASA studies because UFOs are said to appear around him.","legacy-program-secrecy-kept-through-trusted-family-lineages-1940":"The 'legacy program', a long-running secret effort said to handle crashed craft and non-human material since the 1940s, keeps its secrets partly by passing work down through trusted families. So says Matthew Brown, a guest presented as a Pentagon whistleblower, who tells Michels he has 'come to learn' that the program's security is kept 'through trusted families'; if so, it would explain how such a secret could outlast generations.","legacy-program-staff-take-on-dual-roles-to-limit-read-ins":"Scientists in legacy programs, the long-running secret efforts said to hold UFO material, sometimes also do the accounting or personnel work themselves, so that no one else has to be read in, or formally cleared into the secret. UAPGerb, an online researcher of UAP (the official term for UFOs), tells Michels he was told this by people he does not name. If true, it would help explain how such a program could stay hidden for so long.","legacy-programs-bait-ufos-with-nuclear-material":"Secret US government UFO projects that have run for decades, which insiders call legacy programmes, are said to have used real nuclear material, or sometimes fake nuclear material, as bait to make UFOs show up. Michels says this in an episode about UFOs seen near nuclear bases, and he credits it to Garry Nolan, a Stanford microbiologist. In that version the practice reaches beyond the US to Russia and runs from the middle of the century into the 1990s. If true, it would mean these programmes believed UFOs are drawn to nuclear material and used that to study them.","legacy-uap-program-rotten-and-breaking-as-insiders-defect-2020":"The secret \"legacy programme\", the long-running effort said to hold knowledge of UAPs (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), is \"rotten\" and \"in the process of breaking\" as insiders defect from it, according to the analyst Matthew Pines. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that it may be rotten bureaucratically and \"maybe also morally\", and that the public push for disclosure in the 2020s is really a recognition of that. If he is right, the recent wave of whistleblowers is a sign of a hidden system coming apart.","legacy-ufo-program-secrecy-rooted-in-the-manhattan-project-a-1940":"The idea here is that the secrecy around America's so-called legacy programs, meaning long-running secret programs said to deal with UFOs, grew out of the secrecy built for the atomic bomb. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb lays it out for Michels. He says these programs started \"with the same secrecy mechanisms as the Manhattan project\", and that the 1954 atomic energy law gave birth to \"a lot of the secrecy behind\" how UFO programs are classified and controlled. He draws on David Grusch, a witness who has spoken publicly about such programs, in Grusch's interview with the podcaster Joe Rogan. If he is right, the way UFO secrets are kept would trace back to the nuclear age. The law is the Atomic Energy Act, passed by Congress on August 30, 1954. UAPGerb does not say which part he means. If it is the secrecy rules, the act defines \"Restricted Data\" as \"all data concerning\" the design and making of atomic weapons and the production and use of nuclear material. It makes it the policy of the Atomic Energy Commission \"to control the dissemination and declassification of Restricted Data in such a manner as to assure the common defense and security.\" That is a secrecy system built for atomic information. That UFO programs grew out of it is UAPGerb's own step, which the act neither states nor rules out.","legacy-ufo-program-tests-psionic-ability-of-disaster-zone-pe-2009":"In 2009 a secret US UFO effort is said to have been rounding up people from disaster zones such as Indonesia and testing their psionic ability, meaning powers of the mind. Michels says Michael Herrera, a man who says he saw a huge craft in the Indonesian jungle that year, was later contacted by someone claiming to be from the \"Legacy UFO program\", who explained it this way. Herrera tells it himself on the show a little differently: he names his source as a man called Jake Barber, says the programme went by \"P3\", which he says is not its real name, and says the psionic people were carried in shipping containers.","legend-of-range-four-harry-the-glowing-radioactive-horse-1963":"On the Indian Springs gunnery ranges in Nevada in the mid-1960s, men told the legend of \"Range Four Harry\", a giant white radioactive horse said to roam the range glowing. Charles Hall, who says he served there and spent three years around tall white alien beings at the site, tells the legend first-hand to Michels. In his telling the horse story ties to those white beings, which he elsewhere describes as moving in a \"high-speed horse formation\".","legend-of-the-virgin-mary-landing-on-athos-at-iveron-2005":"A legend holds that the Virgin Mary was blown off course at sea and came ashore on Mount Athos, a peninsula of Orthodox monasteries in Greece, and that a monastery called Iveron now stands on the spot where she landed. Ralph Larson, a guest the episode bills as a former CIA chief, tells Michels this as something a monk at Iveron told him during a visit there in the mid-2000s. In Larson's words, \"she got blown on the island\" and the monastery was \"placed on the site where she supposedly landed.\"","leibniz-s-monadology-of-observing-entities":"The idea that reality is built out of observing entities is an old one, according to the researcher Don Hoffman, who tells Michels that the philosopher Leibniz suggested it \"back in 1700\" in his monadology, a picture of the world as made of simple, perceiving units. Hoffman offers it in passing and without naming a source, to show that his own view, that conscious observers come first and the physical world follows from them, has deep roots.","lemay-s-unauthorized-u-2-overflight-of-russia-1962":"In 1962, the story goes, the Air Force general Curtis LeMay ordered a U-2, an American high-altitude spy plane, to fly over Russia without authorisation to do 'the final retargeting' of nuclear targets. A researcher named Dan Sheehan tells this to Michels without naming a source. If true, it would mean a top commander took a step that could have started a war without anyone above him signing off.","lennon-s-and-rfk-s-assassins-as-mk-ultra-subjects-1968":"A scientist named Steve Colbern suggests that the men who killed Robert F. Kennedy in 1968 and John Lennon in 1980 were subjects of MK Ultra, a CIA programme of mind-control experiments. He raises it on the show as his own theory, and the surviving words are only a start (\"John Lennon was...\"); if true, it would mean both killings were steered by mind control.","leo-sprinkle-finds-benowitz-armed-and-saying-they-re-coming-1977":"Sometime in the late 1970s, a UFO researcher named Leo Sprinkle visited the Albuquerque home of a man named Paul Bennewitz, in the Four Hills neighbourhood. He is said to have found Bennewitz holding a rifle and a pistol and saying, \"they're coming. You have to come in the house right now.\" The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels this as something Sprinkle told him, and he tells it to show how deeply Bennewitz's fear of aliens had gripped him.","leonardo-da-vinci-given-information-by-aliens":"Leonardo da Vinci got some of his knowledge from aliens, according to a guest named Kiran Ilum Xenov, who says on the show that he himself was taken by aliens in 1997. He tells Michels that Leonardo \"was given information,\" and sets him alongside the Talmud as another case of knowledge passed to humans from outside, without saying how he knows.","leonidas-consults-the-oracle-of-delphi-before-fighting-the-p":"Before fighting the Persians, the Spartan king Leonidas went to the Oracle of Delphi, a priestess at a Greek shrine, to ask whether they should go to war. The researcher Luke Caverns tells this on the show as an old story, without naming a source, as an example of rulers seeking guidance from such sites.","leslie-kean-publishes-the-grusch-article-in-the-debrief-afte-2023":"The 2023 article that first made public the claims of David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who says the US has secretly recovered craft that are not of human making, was written by the journalist Leslie Kean and ran in The Debrief, a small online news site, rather than in the New York Times. Michels tells it as he heard it: Kean 'had to do it in The Debrief' because of Julian Barnes, a New York Times reporter, the story being that the Times passed on it. The point is that the article that set off the whole affair was turned down by a major paper.","leslie-kean-s-2017-new-york-times-ufo-story":"In December 2017 The New York Times revealed that the Pentagon had quietly run a UFO program, and guests on the show treat that article as the moment the subject stopped being fringe. The piece, 'Glowing Auras and 'Black Money': The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program', was published on 16 December 2017, and its subject was AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports. The tellers credit it to the journalist Leslie Kean, with her fellow reporter Ralph Blumenthal, and the researcher Richard Dolan names a third author, Helene Cooper, calling it 'the really breakthrough article'. Michels says Luis Elizondo, who ran AATIP, helped get it published through Kean, and he stresses that every sighting in it came from the Navy. Kurt McConnell, a Senate staffer, says he took part in what followed: the article's 'actionable' details on the program and on the Nimitz case of 2004, an encounter between Navy pilots and an unknown object, set off the Senate staff's own inquiry. The skeptic Mick West says the article came with Navy cockpit videos of unexplained objects, called Gimbal and FLIR1, with a third, GoFast, released a little later. Others speak of it as readers: the filmmaker James Fox calls it one of the paper's most popular stories ever, a NASA doctor named Greg Rogers says 'everything changed' when he read it and saw the videos, and the analyst Matthew Pines calls it the start of a slow, semi-official legitimization of the subject.","leslie-kean-s-list-of-ordinary-explanations-2010":"An author named Leslie Kean set out a list of ordinary explanations for UFO sightings in her book UFOs: Generals, Pilots, and Government Officials Go on the Record, published in 2010. The skeptic Michael Shermer brings it up while debating Michels and reads from it, telling him, \"This is from her book.\" He presents her list as his own source and does not say more about where it comes from.","letter-from-senator-cohen-thanking-the-remote-viewers-for-sa-1980":"Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer in a secret US program that tried to gather intelligence by mind alone, says a Senator Cohen wrote to the team in the 1980s thanking them for saving the country $100 billion. \"I have a letter in my file,\" he tells Michels, and he says a colleague he calls Dr May has one in his too; if true, a sitting senator credited psychic spying with an enormous saving.","letter-on-sullivan-s-death-sent-to-the-fbi-but-never-receive":"Congressman Eric Burlison says a letter about the death of a man named Sullivan was sent to the FBI through the House Oversight Committee but never arrived. He tells Michels that he and others took information connected to the whistleblower David Grusch, wrote the letter, and were told it had been sent, \"only to later\" learn \"it had never been received.\" If true, a congressional inquiry into a death went missing somewhere along the way.","letter-to-the-pope-the-night-john-paul-ii-died-in-2005-at-th-1998":"A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says the Virgin Mary told him to write a letter to the Pope, and that he took a long time to do it, though he should have done it the next day. He says he finally sent it to the Vatican and got a reply, and the story ties it to the night in 2005 when Pope John Paul II died, which he spent at the Hotel Crillon in Paris; he reproduces the letter in his book. He tells Michels all this as his own experience, which, if true, would mean a CIA officer acted on a vision and the Vatican answered.","letter-to-william-shockley-recommending-l-ron-hubbard-1950":"In the 1950s, the information theorist Claude Shannon is said to have written to the physicist William Shockley recommending a man who, as it turns out, was L. Ron Hubbard, the founder of Scientology. Michels tells this himself on an episode about a CIA scientist, citing the letter between Shannon and Shockley. The point is that Hubbard was moving among leading scientists of the day.","letters-from-the-vatican-and-the-orthodox-church-about-his-b":"A man named Ralph Larson, who has written a book, says he sent it to the Vatican and to the leadership of the Orthodox Church in Istanbul, Turkey, and got letters back from both. He tells Michels this first-hand, without saying here what the letters said.","letters-to-mitre-rand-and-mit-lincoln-labs-2026":"In 2026 a congressman named Eric Burlison began sending letters to MITRE, RAND and MIT Lincoln Laboratory, private research organisations that do work for the government, asking about their part in the UFO subject. Burlison tells Michels about the letters himself. In another episode Michels says Burlison is 'basically demanding that MITRE open up all their records' on the subject, naming only that one organisation. If those groups did hold such records, it would mean the work went on outside the government's own offices.","levenda-as-the-model-for-johnny-depp-s-character-in-the-nint-1977":"The character Johnny Depp plays in The Ninth Gate, Roman Polanski's film, is said to be based on Peter Levenda, a writer on the occult and on the history of the intelligence agencies, and on things he did in New York in the years before the film. Levenda tells this himself, but the link comes from what others told him: he says \"people around Depp were saying this actually happened.\" If true, a Hollywood thriller about the occult drew on a real man's experiences.","levenda-s-archival-assessment-of-mj-12-and-his-talk-with-sta-1940":"The author Peter Levenda says the MJ-12 papers, a disputed set of documents describing a secret committee of twelve officials called Majestic 12 said to handle UFO matters, look genuine to him. He tells Michels he weighed them against records he knew from the National Archives and the Library of Congress, and talked them over with Stanton Friedman, a UFO researcher who championed the papers, at a UFO conference called Contact in the Desert. \"Those documents to me look real,\" Levenda says, which if right would mean a secret government UFO panel existed in the 1940s and 50s.","levenda-s-unholy-alliance-on-nazis-and-the-nordic-tall-white-1930":"The writer Peter Levenda, in his book Unholy Alliance, writes that the Nazis aligned themselves with the \"tall whites\" of the Nordics, the tall, pale, human-looking beings of UFO lore, and that some kind of secret technology was involved. Michels brings this up himself, also crediting Levenda's essay \"Black Sunrise\" in the collection Lovers of Sophia. If true, the Nazi regime would have had dealings with beings that were not human.","levin-s-decapitated-flatworms-keep-their-memories":"The biologist Michael Levin at Tufts University trained flatworms to respond to particular stimuli, then cut their heads off, and the worms that regrew are said to have kept what they had learned. The biologist Rupert Sheldrake describes the experiment to Michels as something he knows of, without naming a paper. It matters to him because it would mean memory is not stored only in the brain.","levin-s-flatworm-experiments-read-as-morphic-resonance-2010":"The biologist Rupert Sheldrake says the scientist Michael Levin's flatworm experiments at Tufts University, which look at how the worms recover their body form, are best explained by 'morphic resonance'. That is Sheldrake's idea that living things draw their forms from a kind of memory of earlier forms. On the show he says the worms are 'resonating with themselves in the past', with their earlier intact form, instead of following the conventional explanation, and if he is right, memory and form in living things would not be stored only in the body.","levitating-metallic-sphere-above-a-podium-2014":"Around 2014, a man named Randy Anderson says, he was shown a metallic sphere the size of a basketball that seemed to float in the air above a podium, the first of several strange objects he saw at the Naval Surface Warfare Center in Crane, Indiana. He tells it first-hand on two episodes, once placing it in what he calls an off-world technology area and dating it only to the 2010s. If true, the Navy would be holding technology not made by humans.","lewis-black-at-epstein-s-mansion-and-the-israeli-defense-min":"The comedian Lewis Black once walked into Jeffrey Epstein's New York mansion and saw, just inside the door, a marker board covered in what \"looks like football plays or something\", in a story that, as it is titled, also involves Israel's defense minister. Kurt Metzger, a comedian, relays it on the show as something Black told, and he also names the comedian Bobby Slayton in the telling. He tells it as a strange glimpse of who passed through Epstein's house and what was planned there.","lewis-chase-reports-to-air-force-headquarters-and-condon-1967":"After the 1967 incident at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, in which Robert Salas says a UFO shut down ten nuclear missiles, a man named Lewis Chase decided it all had to be reported, and he did so in a telegram to Air Force headquarters and in a report to Edward Condon, the physicist then leading a university UFO study for the Air Force. Salas, a former Air Force officer who says he was there that year, tells Michels about these two papers. He tells it because it would mean the incident went up the chain to the top, on the record.","liberation-times-piece-on-the-legacy-program-ecosystem-doe-c":"An article in the publication Liberation Times, written by a journalist named Christopher Sharp, is said to have laid out for the first time the chain of command behind a secret, long-running UFO program, reaching into the Department of Energy and the CIA's counterproliferation work. Michels brings it up himself, saying a guest sent it to him, and in a later episode praises it for naming Glenn Gaffney, whom it calls the CIA's director of science and technology, as a figure in that chain. If right, it would put names and offices on who controls the program.","liberman-s-goldfish-centrifuge-and-freezing-experiment-1970":"A Soviet scientist named Efim Liberman is said to have built much of his thinking on an experiment with four goldfish, which, as the story's title gives it, involved spinning them in a centrifuge and freezing them. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation about the idea that the brain works like a quantum time machine, and he also names David and Daniil Liberman. He says only that he took four goldfish, so how the experiment went and what it showed stays vague.","lidar-detected-pyramid-in-the-amazon-2022":"Archaeologists are said to have found what they are certain is \"an enormous pyramid\" deep in the Colombian Amazon rainforest, using lidar, a laser scan from the air that can map the ground beneath thick forest. The writer Graham Hancock, who argues that a lost ancient civilisation existed, told Michels this in 2022 and credits it to an archaeologist at the University of Exeter whom he does not name. If true, it would mean large-scale building existed where the rainforest was long thought to have held none.","lidar-reveals-lost-amazon-cities-and-geometric-earthworks":"Lidar, a laser scan made from the air that can see the shape of the ground through forest cover, is said to be revealing that the Amazon was once home to tens of millions of people, with large permanent cities and huge, very precise geometric earthworks that can only be seen whole from above. The researcher Graham Hancock tells Michels this, drawing on his TV series Ancient Apocalypse season 2, as evidence that the rainforest hid a far bigger past than once thought. In a later episode Michels repeats what Hancock told him, but mentions only the shapes cut into the ground that show up once the treetops are scanned away, not the cities or the population figures.","light-flashes-answered-by-a-stationary-light-over-the-prairi-1977":"In 1977, on the South Dakota prairie, a young Air Force guard says he flashed the lights of his post at a strange light in the sky, and the light seemed to flash back. He is Mario Woods, then a security policeman at November-1, a small launch control facility in the nuclear missile field of Ellsworth Air Force Base, and he tells it first-hand, twice, to Michels. On the show he says he flipped the facility lights off and on three times, waited a minute or so, and then the thing went \"out, on, out and on\", which \"sent shivers up me\" because it meant \"they do see me.\" Why it matters to him: it would mean whatever hung over a nuclear site was watching and answering a human signal. The journalist Robert Hastings, who writes about UFOs at nuclear weapons sites, published Woods's own written account on 10 September 2017. In that text Woods writes that he \"got the idea to flash the facility lights off and on as a signal,\" flashed them \"six to eight times,\" then walked out with his partner, and \"a moment or two after\" the partner \"made some comment about the light, it went out!\" before it \"re-appeared to the north and seemed closer.\" So the 2017 version has more flashes and a light that went out after a remark, while the later telling has three flashes answered by on-and-off blinking; that the light was replying is the later telling's reading, which the 2017 text neither states nor rules out.","light-over-the-mesa-green-light-missing-time-and-apache-heli":"A camper who is not named says that on a mesa near Aztec, New Mexico, where a UFO is said to have crashed, a light that had just made a sharp 90-degree turn was suddenly right over his group. He tells Michels that a green light followed, that the group lost a stretch of time they could not account for, and that Apache military helicopters showed up afterwards. He says he was there himself.","lights-around-ellsworth-nuclear-sites-before-the-abduction-1977":"In 1977, in the missile field of Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, an Air Force veteran named Michael Johnson says he saw lights around the nuclear missile sites shortly before what he describes as his abduction. He tells it firsthand: \"I just see lights out there around the nuclear sites.\" If true, something unexplained was hovering near American nuclear weapons.","lights-filmed-near-brunswick-nuclear-plant-2024":"Lights were filmed in 2024 near Duke Energy's Brunswick nuclear power plant in Southport, North Carolina, a case Michels raises himself on two episodes. In one he gives the exact place. In another he calls it \"quote unquote UAP activity\" (UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's newer word for UFOs) and credits Mick West, a skeptic known for analysing UFO videos, with identifying the facility only as Duke Energy's. He brings it up as one more example of unexplained objects seen near nuclear sites, which would matter if something were watching those sites.","lights-on-a-road-stretch-at-mattibu-gotland":"At a place called Mattibu on the Swedish island of Gotland, people go to one stretch of road to watch for lights that wander along it. The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels about it as a known local spot, though he does not say when the lights began or what people think they are.","lilli-hornig-s-los-alamos-typing-interview-1940":"When the chemist Lilli Hornig arrived at Los Alamos in the 1940s to work on the atomic bomb, her job interview opened with a question about how fast she could type, and she answered, \"They don't teach typing at Harvard.\" Kevin Knuth, a former NASA scientist, tells Michels the story as Hornig herself told it to him, a small picture of how women scientists were sized up on the project.","limestone-cube-erosion-study-on-the-smithsonian-roof":"Scientists once put a freshly cut cube of limestone on the roof of the Smithsonian Institution and measured how it wore away over many decades, says a researcher named Randall Carlson. He tells Michels about it without naming the study, as a way to show how fast stone weathers, which matters for judging how old ancient stone monuments are.","lincoln-lapaz-studies-green-fireballs-1948":"In 1948, Michels says, Lincoln LaPaz, a meteor expert at the University of New Mexico, began studying green fireballs that kept showing up near nuclear facilities and detonations. Michels tells it more than once without naming a source. In one telling he credits the researcher Robert Hastings' book UFOs and Nukes, ties the fireballs to atmospheric bomb tests, calls them \"intelligently moving\", and says they were also documented by Edward J. Ruppelt, then head of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. If true, it would suggest something was taking an interest in America's first nuclear sites.","lincoln-s-niagara-speech-on-the-giants-whose-bones-fill-the":"Abraham Lincoln, writing about Niagara Falls, is quoted as saying, \"The eyes of that ancient species of giants whose bones fill the mounds of America have gazed upon Niagara as ours do.\" Tim Alberino, a researcher who says giants once lived on earth, brings up the passage to Michels without saying where he found it, as a sign that Lincoln's America took giant bones in the old burial mounds for granted. Alberino himself says Lincoln was \"waxing poetically\" here, so the line shows what Lincoln wrote, but it leaves open whether he meant the giants literally or as a figure of speech.","linda-bradshaw-saw-and-photographed-portal-at-bradshaw-ranch":"A portal allegedly appeared at Bradshaw Ranch near Sedona, Arizona, and the ranch's former owner, Linda Bradshaw, saw it and took photographs of it. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this as something he has heard, citing Bradshaw and her photographs, while himself calling the portal \"alleged\"; if true, it would be a picture of a doorway to somewhere else.","linda-brown-s-2014-review-of-tim-taylor-s-launch-fever-2003":"In 2014, Linda Brown, daughter of the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, wrote an Amazon review of Launch Fever, an autobiography that a man named Timothy Taylor published in March 2003, at a time \"before anybody knew who Taylor was,\" Jesse Michels says. Michels tells it himself, from his own research, and treats it as a sign that Brown's family had a connection to Taylor long before Taylor became known.","linda-brown-sees-a-100-000-check-from-the-townsend-brown-fou-1970":"Linda Brown, the daughter of Townsend Brown, a scientist Michels presents as having built UFO-like craft for the CIA, remembers seeing a check for $100,000 from the Townsend Brown Foundation to Stanford while visiting her father in Atherton, California, sometime in the 1970s. Michels tells this in his documentary on Brown, relaying what Linda told him, as a sign that Brown's work was being funded at Stanford's research institute.","linda-jones-and-grass-growing-rapidly-while-fleeing-a-ufo":"A woman named Linda Jones in Manchester, England, was running with her children away from a UFO when the grass began growing rapidly under her feet. The researcher Mike Masters tells Michels this without naming a source, as a case where an encounter seemed to bend time.","linda-tripp-placed-beside-monica-lewinsky-1990":"Steven Greer, a physician who campaigns for the release of UFO secrets, tells Michels that the Monica Lewinsky scandal of the 1990s was set up: Linda Tripp, he says, was an \"army intelligence NSA person\" deliberately \"put right beside Monica Lewinsky.\" In another telling Greer leaves Tripp out and says the setup worked because Clinton, asked about the affair, \"lied about it under oath,\" which led to his 1998 impeachment. If true, it would mean intelligence people engineered the downfall of a sitting president.","little-a-le-inn-we-made-a-digital-copy-2023":"In 2023, the UFO documentary filmmaker James Fox says, he was at the Little A'Le'Inn, an inn in Rachel, Nevada, asking about a VHS tape made by a man named Chuck Clark, and the woman there told him: \"you think we're that stupid, you don't think we would have made a digital copy.\" Fox tells Michels this firsthand. In his telling, the footage on the tape was being guarded and survives in a backup copy.","little-glowing-glass-like-being-with-a-triangle-2007":"In 2007, in the woods near his house in North Carolina, a man named Chris Bledsoe says he met a small glowing being that looked as if it were made of glass, linked to a triangle. Bledsoe, whom Michels presents as a man said to attract UFOs, tells it first-hand: \"I said he looked like glass.\"","little-progress-reverse-engineering-recovered-craft-caveman-1947":"Michels believes that the craft the US is said to have recovered, a story going back to 1947, have yielded very little to reverse engineering, meaning taking a device apart to learn how it works and copy it. In his words it is like \"caveman with a USB drive\": people holding something far beyond them with no idea how to use it. He offers this as his own view, without naming a source.","little-sophia-doll-used-to-map-children-s-brains-in-africa":"A doll called Little Sophia, presented as a toy to get children thinking about STEM subjects, was really used to map children's brains in Africa, according to Kurt Metzger, a comedian, who says it on the show as something he passes along rather than saw himself. He mentions it in passing, giving only the doll's name and its stated purpose of encouraging science learning.","livelsberger-email-gives-area-51-coordinates-for-gravitic-pr-2025":"Michels says an email sent by a man named Matthew Livelsberger gave the map coordinates of America's 'gravitic propulsion' operations center, meaning a place that works on moving craft by controlling gravity, and that the coordinates point to Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada. Michels calls it 'this very weird email' and tells it himself in an episode with Randy Anderson, a man who says he saw alien technology at a military base. If it holds, it would put a working antigravity program at Area 51.","livermore-s-nuclear-pumped-space-laser-lens-1980":"In the 1980s, according to John S. Herrington, who was Secretary of Energy at the time, the Lawrence Livermore weapons laboratory worked on a space weapon that would set off a nuclear explosion in space and focus its energy through a lens \"about the size of your thumbnail.\" Herrington tells Michels the physicist Edward Teller sat on his couch at the Department of Energy one afternoon and showed him the lens, and he names the programme Excalibur. He tells it as something he took part in himself.","living-at-arthur-young-s-institute-for-the-study-of-consciou-1970":"In the 1970s, a man named Arthur Young came into the life of Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of the mind and the paranormal who holds a PhD, and invited him to move into the Institute for the Study of Consciousness, a centre Young had set up in Berkeley. Mishlove tells Michels about it himself, as part of his own life story: he says Young asked him to come and live with him there, which put him at the heart of a place devoted to studying the mind.","living-crystal-orb-shown-in-psychologist-s-office-2008":"In 2008, in a local psychologist's office in Western New York, a crystal orb with a hard shell was shown to a young man, and the inside of it, he says, was alive. Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into the UFO program as a child, tells Michels he saw it himself: 'it's a literal crystal orb, but the inside of this orb, for lack of a better word, was alive.'","living-reptilians-from-a-crash-were-shot":"Reptile-like beings are said to have come out of a crashed craft alive and then been killed. In a clip played on the show, a speaker who is not identified is asked what happened to \"those living reptilians from the crash\" and answers, \"deceased. They were killed,\" passing on what someone else had told them. If true, it would mean living beings were recovered from a crash and then deliberately killed.","lizard-dna-sequences-pulled-for-the-hillary-clinton-lizard-p-2017":"In 2017, a former pharmaceutical executive named Martin Shkreli says he pulled real lizard DNA sequences from BLAST, a genetic database run by the US National Institutes of Health, as part of a joke. He had offered a bounty for a strand of Hillary Clinton's hair, playing on the claim, linked to the writer David Icke, that some powerful people are secretly lizard people. Shkreli tells Michels this himself as the one who did it, which shows how he went about the prank.","lk-99-room-temperature-superconductor-papers-2023":"In 2023 a team in South Korea published papers on LK-99, a material they said could carry electricity with no resistance at room temperature, and according to Michels they were so sure of it that they put out one paper with six authors and another with only three, because no more than three people can share a Nobel Prize. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as a story of how confident the team was.","local-name-of-the-nazca-region-meaning-a-laboratory-for-hybr-2006":"The claim is that the local name of the Nazca region in Peru, where huge ancient lines are drawn into the desert, translates as something like a laboratory for breeding hybrid beings. Michels mentions it himself, saying \"the local region translates to laboratory for insemination and hybridization,\" and credits a book from 2006 that he does not name. A researcher named Thierry Jamin, who works on Peru's strange three-fingered bodies, says it more fully. He says the source is \"a little book published in 2004 by a local author from Nazca\" that wrote about various sites in the area. In his version the meaning also includes cloning. So the two disagree on the year of the book, and neither names it or its author. They tell it because, if the name means this, it would hint that local tradition remembers the area as a place where beings were bred or engineered.","local-tribal-people-told-not-to-look-toward-the-property-2016":"Ute people on the Ouray reservation in Utah have been told for generations not even to look in the direction of Skinwalker Ranch, a nearby property long tied to reports of strange lights and happenings. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, tells Michels this as something tribal people told him around 2016, a sign, if so, that local people feared the land long before it became famous.","locked-car-found-open-repeatedly-fear-of-car-bomb-1989":"Around 1989 and 1990 in Las Vegas, Bob Lazar's locked car kept turning up unlocked, and he and a friend feared someone had planted a bomb in it. Lazar is a man at the center of a long-running UFO story. If the account is true, someone was getting into his car again and again to frighten or watch him. Lazar tells Michels this himself. He says they would lock the car and test the door, 'because it had happened before,' and say, 'All right, it's locked,' only to find it open later. A researcher named Luigi Vendittelli passes on what Lazar's friend Mario Woods told him. Woods said, 'I was with Bob all the time and we were scared that something was going to blow up.' Woods says he was going through such hard times that he didn't care, so he told Lazar to 'stay out' while he tried starting the car himself. The two accounts agree on the fear, and Woods's version adds that he was the one who took the risk.","lockheed-divests-a-craft-which-may-end-up-at-picatinny-arsen":"The aerospace company Lockheed eventually got rid of a recovered craft, passing it to the investment firm Veritas Capital, and the material is now held by a company called Peraton, which has offices at Picatinny Arsenal in New Jersey, according to Michels's friend Rob Jones, a researcher. Michels first relays this as something Jones uncovered, and Jones then tells it himself on two episodes, saying he \"believes\" Peraton holds it and dating the arrangement from 2008 to the present. If true, it would mean a non-human craft has passed through private hands like any other asset.","lockheed-engineer-this-is-what-you-learn-when-you-die":"A Lockheed insider who was against disclosure is said to have told someone asking about UFO secrets: \"No, you're not supposed to learn about this. This is what you learn about when you die.\" The author Peter Levenda tells Michels the story from a book by the researcher John B. Alexander, and is careful to say it was not Ben Rich, a Lockheed executive who was \"kind of pro\" openness, but somebody else. If true, it suggests people inside the aerospace industry treat the subject as knowledge that should stay hidden for life.","lockheed-executive-admitted-to-fravor-the-tic-tac-was-theirs-2020":"The Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint that a Navy fighter pilot named David Fravor reported chasing off California, belonged to the defense contractor Lockheed, according to Steven Greer, a longtime UFO campaigner. Greer tells Michels that \"very recently\" a Lockheed executive admitted this to Fravor himself, which he relays second-hand. If true, it would mean the most famous modern UFO sighting was a human-made craft.","lockheed-martin-coherent-matter-wave-beam-patent":"The defence company Lockheed Martin is said to hold a patent called a \"coherent matter wave beam\" that uses the Aharonov-Bohm effect, a quantum effect in which particles are influenced by electromagnetic forces in places where no field seems to be present. A man named Dave Rossi, who says he learned advanced physics from aliens, mentions the patent to Michels without saying where he found it. He brings it up as a sign that a major defence contractor is working on the same unusual physics he talks about.","lockheed-martin-divesting-ufo-material-to-bigelow-aerospace-2008":"In 2008 the defense company Lockheed Martin tried to hand over UFO material to Bigelow Aerospace, a private aerospace company owned by Robert Bigelow. That is what the journalist George Knapp says, and Michels notes that Knapp has said it before Congress. Knapp says he \"confirmed on the record\" that Bigelow and \"a trusted colleague\" from a government program on unexplained aerial phenomena negotiated with senior Lockheed executives, and that Bigelow's company would receive \"unusual material\" that had been kept at a facility in California, material that, he says, \"was not made here\"; if true, a major defense contractor had been holding something not of human make.","lockheed-martin-vp-tried-to-bring-the-craft-out-into-the-ope-2008":"A vice president of Lockheed Martin, the defense contractor, is said to have tried around 2008 to 2010 to get a recovered craft and its hardware, obtained long before, \"out the back door\" so it would no longer be kept secret inside the company. The guest Jeremy Corbell tells Michels this, repeating what he credits to three people he names as his sources, Jim Ryder, Colm Kelleher and James Lacatski. If true, it would mean a major contractor holds hardware from a craft not made by humans.","lockheed-s-quantum-magnetometer-modelled-on-the-robin-s-cry4-2016":"The defence company Lockheed studied how birds such as the robin sense the Earth's magnetic field, reportedly through a protein in their eyes called cry4, and set out to copy it in a quantum sensor; around 2016, the story goes, it succeeded with what it calls the \"dark ice magnetometer\". The young researcher Deep Prasad tells Michels this in 2022, saying it happened \"six years ago\", without naming a source, as an example of engineers copying nature's quantum tricks.","lockheed-skunk-works-and-the-ufo-topic-ben-rich-rob-weiss-an-2016":"Ben Rich, who ran Skunk Works, Lockheed Martin's most secretive division, used to call UFOs \"unfunded opportunities\", a phrase that would suggest the aerospace company saw the subject as a business to build. Michels tells it himself, pointing to emails of 2016 and 2017 that WikiLeaks published, involving Tom DeLonge and a man named Rob Weiss, as the trail that ties Skunk Works to the UFO topic.","lockheed-skunk-works-response-to-grusch-s-testimony-2023":"Lockheed Martin, whose secretive advanced-projects division is called the Skunk Works, answered the 2023 testimony of David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who told Congress about hidden UFO programmes, by saying that \"questions about UAPs are best answered by the federal government.\" UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. Michels tells this himself and calls the reply \"pretty telling,\" because to him a defence company that sends the question back to the government, without denying anything, hints that it knows more.","lockheed-ttsa-and-killing-the-schumer-bill-2017":"Michels suggests that Lockheed, the aerospace company, may be tied both to the UFO company To The Stars Academy (TTSA), which the musician Tom DeLonge set up in 2017, and to the defeat of the Schumer bill, a proposed law in Congress on government UFO records. He points to Robert Weiss, the director of Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret aircraft-building division, and his connection to DeLonge and TTSA in 2017, and he also names a congressman named Mike Turner. Michels offers this as his own theory, in an episode about a scientist named Townsend Brown. If he is right, a defence contractor would have helped shape both the public UFO story and the law meant to open it up.","lockheed-vp-jim-ryder-the-veil-thinning-as-weakening-magneto":"Jim Ryder, a vice president of space systems at the aerospace company Lockheed Martin, is said to have claimed that the old spiritual idea of \"the veil is thinning,\" the barrier between our world and other realms, refers literally to Earth's magnetosphere, which he says is weakening right now. An unnamed crew member on Michels's episode with Graham Hancock relays this as something Ryder said, which would mean a physical change in the planet is opening the way to other kinds of experience.","loeb-s-artificial-light-sail-theory-of-oumuamua-2017":"'Oumuamua, an object that passed through our solar system from interstellar space in 2017, may have been an artificial light sail, a thin craft pushed along by sunlight, according to the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb. He tells Michels, in two interviews years apart, that \"all of the proposals that were put on the table to explain the anomalies of Oumuamua invoked a rock of a type that we've never seen before\", so a manufactured object deserves a place on the list, an argument he connects to his book Extraterrestrial.","logan-paul-offers-chuck-clark-100-000-for-the-tape-2022":"Around 2022 the YouTube star Logan Paul offered a man named Chuck Clark, in remote Arizona, $100,000 for a VHS tape Clark holds, and Clark refused to sell. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this as he heard it: 'he offered him 100 grand but he wouldn't.' Fox tells it to show how much the tape is thought to be worth, and how firmly Clark keeps it.","logger-saw-the-same-craft-in-the-area-1970":"A logger working near the Allagash Waterway in Maine in the 1970s is said to have seen the same craft that a group of campers later reported in that area. Charlie Foltz, one of the men who says he saw the craft and was abducted there, tells Michels about this logger, whose name he does not give. If true, it would mean someone outside the group saw the craft too.","london-bridge-at-lake-havasu-has-children-walled-up-in-it-1960":"The old London Bridge, bought and rebuilt in Lake Havasu, Arizona, in the 1960s, is said to have children walled up inside it, and the Knights of Columbus, a Catholic fraternal group, are said to hold the controlling interest and to be the reason the bridge was brought over. The guest Kurt Metzger tells Michels this, repeating what a man named Nathan Reynolds told him, as part of a conversation about a secret ritual elite.","london-drought-and-mishlove-s-first-meeting-with-owens-1976":"During a drought in London in the summer of 1976, the parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove says, someone reached out to Ted Owens, a man who claimed psychic power over the weather, to end it, and Owens was invited to a conference sponsored by the Paris Science Foundation at the University of London. Mishlove tells Michels he met Owens there and became his friend, and he brings in an article from Psychic magazine and the physicists Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ, who studied psychics, as part of the background.","long-delay-echoes-behind-sagan-s-contact-1920":"Michels thinks the astronomer Carl Sagan's novel Contact was alluding to \"long delay echoes,\" which Michels describes as radio signals heard coming back seconds after they were sent, reported in the 1920s and 30s when radio was new. Michels gives this as his own reading of the book. If he is right, Sagan was hinting that something out in space might be sending our signals back.","long-delay-echoes-rise-when-earth-moon-l5-is-above-the-horiz-1927":"Long delay echoes are radio signals that come back seconds after they were sent, a puzzle dating to 1927, and Michels says one study found a \"statistically significant increase\" in them when the Earth-Moon L5 point was above the horizon. L5 is a Lagrange point, a spot in space where the pulls of the Earth and Moon balance so that an object can sit there stably. Michels tells it himself, without naming the study, as a hint that something could be parked at that point and returning the signals.","long-delay-radio-echoes-heard-by-norwegian-operators-1927":"In 1927, Norwegian shortwave radio operators began to notice something odd: radio signals that seemed to come back as echoes after a long delay, a puzzle tied to the Norwegian scientist Karl Stormer. Michels tells it himself, as the start of a story, in an episode with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, who looks for signs of unexplained objects around Earth; the hint is that something out in space might have been sending the signals back.","long-distance-healing-in-australia-over-the-phone":"A man named Chris Bledsoe, the subject of an episode about why NASA studies him and his claim to draw UFOs, says he has seen healing happen at a distance, with someone in Australia, over the phone. He tells Michels this as something he saw himself, and says he has seen it \"too many times\" to doubt it. He gives no names or details. If true, it would mean healing can work across an ocean with no one in the room.","long-island-crash-near-moriches-bay-1989":"A UFO is said to have crashed near Moriches Bay on Long Island, New York, around 1989. The UFO historian Richard Dolan mentions it to Michels in passing, hedging with 'I think it was supposed to be 1989'. He ties it to two people named John Ford and Elaine Douglas, and names no other source.","lonnie-zamora-never-the-same-after-socorro-1964":"Lonnie Zamora, the man at the centre of a famous 1964 sighting in Socorro, New Mexico, was changed for good by what he saw, according to his wife. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels that she looked at him and said, \"I don't know what happened when he looked at that creature, but he was never the same.\"","lonnie-zamora-s-socorro-landing-1964":"On April 24, 1964, a police officer named Lonnie Zamora is said to have watched an egg-shaped craft land near Socorro, New Mexico, and found the plants burnt where it had been. The investigator James Fox has looked into the case himself and tells it on two episodes. He gives the exact date and credits Ray Stanford and a document titled \"Socorro Saucer in a Pentagon Pantry,\" and he also mentions J. Allen Hynek. Fox adds the most striking detail: that Zamora had face-to-face contact with one of the beings and \"locked eyes\" with it. Michels tells the case himself on other episodes. He describes the egg landing and the burnt vegetation, and says the craft \"allegedly had little legs.\" If true, it would be one of the clearest close-range sightings of a landed craft and its occupants by a police officer on duty.","lonnie-zamora-sighting-near-nuclear-sites-1964":"The 1964 sighting by a police officer named Lonnie Zamora in Socorro, New Mexico, took place only a couple of miles from nuclear sites, according to Michels. He makes the point himself, without naming a source, while debating a skeptic, as part of a pattern he sees: UFO reports that cluster around nuclear facilities.","looking-for-city-lights-on-proxima-b-2021":"The James Webb Space Telescope could look for city lights on the night side of Proxima b, a planet circling the nearest star to the Sun, according to the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb. He tells Michels he wrote a paper saying so with a Stanford undergraduate \"a few months\" before their early 2022 conversation; spotting such lights would be a direct sign of a technological civilisation.","lookout-mountain-cia-facility-in-laurel-canyon-1960":"A site on Lookout Mountain, in the Los Angeles neighbourhood of Laurel Canyon, was a CIA facility, says a researcher named Eric Weinstein, crediting Michels for pointing it out. He ties it to \"the whole history of drugs and the government and Vietnam and espionage\" in the 1960s, suggesting the canyon was more than the hillside music scene it is known for. He gives no source of his own.","lookout-mountain-laboratory-as-manhattan-project-film-lab-1942":"A facility on Lookout Mountain in Laurel Canyon, Los Angeles, was the film laboratory for the Manhattan Project, the wartime effort to build the atomic bomb. The researcher Paul Schatzkin says this on the show, saying it has come out as more information is released, though he names no source. If so, the site had a secret military role long before its later reputation.","loose-stone-samples-tested-in-israel-turn-out-to-be-basalt-2012":"In 2012, loose stones collected from the seafloor at the Baltic Sea anomaly, a strange round shape found on the bottom of the Baltic by a Swedish treasure-hunting team, were tested in Israel and turned out to be basalt, a volcanic stone. The treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, who led the team that found it, tells Michels what the testers found: \"basalt, volcanic stones.\" The result bears on what the object might be made of.","looting-at-eridu-and-the-campaign-to-save-it":"The ruins of Eridu, an ancient city site in Iraq, are being stripped by looters, according to Matt LaCroix, a guest who researches ancient sites. He tells Michels that over the last ten years he kept finding photographs of locals going into the site and, in his words, \"pillaging pillaging\" it. He raises it because he wants the site saved before what remains of it is gone.","lore-of-lockheed-and-northrop-crash-retrievals-without-overs":"Michels says there is a whole body of UFO lore holding that the defence companies Lockheed and Northrop have recovered crashed craft of unknown origin, which the field calls crash retrievals, without proper government supervision or oversight. He describes it himself as lore, without naming a source, in an episode with a former NSA director. If true, it would mean private companies hold such material beyond the government's control.","lorentz-regauging-of-maxwell-s-quaternions-1900":"In the early 1900s, the physicist Hendrik Lorentz did something called a \"symmetrical regauging\" of the equations of James Clerk Maxwell, which Maxwell had written in quaternions, a four-part number system, and in doing so is said to have changed what physics kept from them. A man named Dave Rossi, who says on the show that he learned advanced physics from aliens, tells Michels this without naming a source, calling Lorentz \"Heinrich\". The point of the story is that something in Maxwell's original theory may have been set aside.","lorie-barnes-visited-by-blue-dwarfs-while-pregnant-1954":"In 1954 a woman named Lorie Barnes, while pregnant, was visited by small blue beings who told her, \"We're interested in the girl child you're carrying.\" The writer Whitley Strieber tells Michels this as something Barnes told him, part of his case that such beings take an interest in particular people from before they are born.","los-alamos-disinformation-as-a-model-for-secret-programs-1940":"During the 1940s atomic bomb work at Los Alamos, according to the mathematician Eric Weinstein, the government filed deliberately misleading paperwork so that no one would learn plutonium and uranium were the two main elements it was working on. Weinstein tells this to Michels without naming a source, though he links it to the chemist Harold Urey. He offers it as a model for how a secret programme, including one about UFOs, could hide what it is really doing.","los-alamos-hiring-during-a-nuclear-arms-race-2020":"A woman connected to Los Alamos, the New Mexico weapons laboratory, said in the 2020s that the lab was hiring because \"there's a huge nuclear arms race going on right now, and they're building bombs like mad.\" The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels this as what she told him, without naming her.","los-alamos-insider-surfaces-that-solid-objects-pass-through":"Researchers at Los Alamos, the New Mexico weapons lab, learned that metal surfaces \"could be manipulated to like put things through, in or out\", according to a lab insider whose words are relayed by Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who says he was abducted by aliens. Woods passes it on second-hand without naming the insider; if true, it would point to a material technology that lets solid objects pass through walls.","los-alamos-insider-their-propulsion-systems-are-grown":"Someone at Los Alamos, the New Mexico weapons laboratory, is said to have claimed that the engines of alien craft \"are not built. They're grown.\" Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels that one man he spoke to said this, and he does not name the man. If true, it would mean the craft are made more like living things than machines.","los-alamos-tiger-team-security-test-failed-1990":"Around 1990 or 1991, a tiger team, a group sent in to test security by trying to get past it, came to the Los Alamos nuclear weapons laboratory in New Mexico, and according to the guest Bob Lazar the lab's security \"failed so horribly.\" Lazar tells Michels this as something he was told, and it would mean one of the country's most secret sites was far easier to get into than it should have been.","los-alamos-working-group-concludes-ufos-are-interdimensional-2000":"In the 2000s, a working group at Los Alamos National Laboratory in New Mexico is said to have concluded that UFOs are \"interdimensional objects coming down into a lower dimensional plane.\" Jay Anderson, a guest who describes himself as a witness of the phenomenon, tells Michels he heard this from a man named Oke Shannon, and compares it to Flatland, a story about creatures living in a flat, two-dimensional world. If true, scientists at a top weapons lab believed UFOs come from other dimensions.","los-alamosbattelle-nitinol-contract-of-1949":"Jesse Michels says some of the most interesting evidence he has found for Roswell, the reported 1947 crash of an unknown craft near Roswell, New Mexico, is a 1949 contract between the Los Alamos weapons laboratory and the Battelle Memorial Institute, a private research organisation. The story ties that contract to Nitinol, a nickel-titanium alloy. Michels tells it himself, citing the contract, and offers it as a sign that, if he is right, metal from the crash was being studied by scientists within two years.","loss-of-the-marine-sulphur-queen-1963":"The Marine Sulphur Queen was a ship that sailed out of Port Arthur, Texas, and was lost at sea in 1963. Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear site, tells Michels that it was the first ship he ever went aboard, at five years old. He brings it up as a memory from his own childhood.","lost-hiker-meets-a-woman-who-went-missing-a-decade-earlier":"A hiker lost in a US forest met a woman there whose description matched someone who had gone missing in that same place a decade or more earlier. Jason Jorjani, a researcher and writer, tells Michels this from the Missing 411 books, a series on unexplained disappearances in the wilderness, whose author he names as David Pitus, and raises it because it suggests the vanished can somehow still be out there.","lost-submarines-like-the-uss-connecticut":"Michels says there are \"all these lost submarines like the USS Connecticut\" where \"we don't really know what happened.\" He mentions it in passing, without a source, as a sign that things go on in the oceans that the Navy cannot fully explain.","lot-s-angelic-visitors-extract-him-before-sodom-s-destructio":"In the Bible's book of Genesis, visitors from God come to the city of Sodom and lead Abraham's nephew Lot and his family out before the city is destroyed. The researcher Tim Alberino tells the story on the show. He says Lot \"encounters angelic beings who come to extract him and his family before the destruction of Sodom\", and it is told there as an old example of beings from above stepping into human affairs. Michels tells it more briefly in another episode: \"Two angels walk into Sodom the night before the fire falls\". He gives the number and the timing but does not mention the rescue. Both rely on the King James Bible, published in 1611, and its Genesis 19 tells it in this order. \"There came two angels to Sodom at even\", and Lot bowed to them at the city gate. They told him \"the LORD hath sent us to destroy it\". At morning they urged him to take his wife and two daughters, \"lest thou be consumed\". When he lingered, \"the men laid hold upon his hand\" and \"set him without the city\". Then \"the LORD rained upon Sodom and upon Gomorrah brimstone and fire from the LORD out of heaven.\"","lou-baldin-s-day-aboard-a-ship-with-milton-2000":"A man named Lou Baldin says he was paralysed in a park near where he lived and floated up into a ship, where he spent a day with an extraterrestrial he calls Milton. The historian of the UFO subject Richard Dolan passes this on to Michels from Baldin's own book, A Day with an Extraterrestrial: \"Next thing you know, he's been paralyzed. He's floated, levitated into a ship.\"","lou-baldin-s-in-league-with-a-ufo-1947":"A man named Lou Baldin wrote a book in the mid-1990s called In League with a UFO, about his claimed dealings with a UFO. The historian Richard Dolan, who writes on government UFO secrecy, brings it up with Michels in passing, without saying more about what the book holds.","lou-elizondo-told-kirkpatrick-where-the-big-sur-shootdown-fi-1960":"A test film from the 1960s, shot near Big Sur on the California coast, is said to show a UFO knocking down a dummy nuclear warhead, and Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who ran a small office studying unexplained aerial reports, says he has seen it. The researcher Robert Hastings, who has spent years interviewing military veterans about UFOs near nuclear weapons, tells the fullest version on the show. Hastings says he got an email from Kurt McConnell, a Senate staffer, who had driven Elizondo to an interview with AARO, the Pentagon's UFO investigation office. On the drive back, McConnell wrote, Elizondo told him that he had told Sean Kirkpatrick, then AARO's head, that he had seen \"the Big Sur film, the shootdown of a dummy nuclear warhead by a UFO.\" Hastings also brings in Bob Jacobs, a former Air Force officer whose name is tied to the film. Michels repeats the simpler claim twice, saying that Elizondo \"said he saw that footage\", and both times he leaves out Kirkpatrick and McConnell. The story matters because it would mean a film like this still exists and that the official in charge of looking for it was told about it.","low-s-handwritten-note-hippler-blames-emp-from-nuclear-testi-1967":"The story is that in 1967 an Air Force colonel privately offered an explanation for a UFO incident: that it was caused by fallout from a nuclear blast. It turns on a handwritten note by Robert Low, a figure at the Condon Committee, a UFO study the Air Force paid the University of Colorado to run in the late 1960s. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer who says a UFO shut down ten nuclear missiles he was guarding, tells Michels about this note. He says Low wrote that he had talked to Colonel Robert Hippler and that Hippler told him something. Michels gives the content. He says Hippler put the event down to an EMP, an electromagnetic pulse, from a nuclear detonation. Michels gets this from a Wall Street Journal article, \"The Pentagon Disinformation That Fueled America's UFO Mythology\", published on 6 June 2025, rather than from the note itself. Salas raises it because, if true, the note would show the Air Force had an explanation ready for the incident.","luc-montagnier-on-electromagnetic-information-transfer-in-dn":"A French Nobel Prize winner named Luc Montagnier was saying, at the end of his life, that electromagnetism plays an important part in communication and information transfer within DNA. Michels brings this up himself, without naming a source, in a conversation about synchronicities and prophetic dreams, as a sign that living things might send and receive information in ways science has barely looked at.","lucille-ball-hears-the-radio-through-a-tooth-filling":"The actress Lucille Ball is said to have once heard radio music inside her own head after getting a tooth filling, as if her mouth were picking up a broadcast. In a recording of her own account played on the show, Ball says that around World War II, driving near a Los Angeles radio station, she heard music getting \"louder and louder and louder,\" reached to turn off her car radio, found it wasn't on, and \"realized it was in my mouth\"; she credits Buster Keaton in the telling. Michels retells it twice, citing The Telepathy Tapes, a documentary series about children said to read minds, adding that she was on an overpass, as an example of the body picking up signals the way a radio does.","luckman-book-s-alien-drawing-matches-a-british-museum-image-1970":"A drawing of an alien in the 1970s book UFOs: Past, Present and Future, tied to the filmmakers Bob Emenegger and Allan Sandler, is said to match an ancient image held in the British Museum in London. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this, passing on what a UFO writer named Michael Luckman told him, and says he has sent Michels the British Museum photo: 'It's exactly the being.' If true, it would suggest the same kind of being was pictured long ago.","lue-elizondo-s-book-on-anti-gravity-bubbles-and-craft-shapes":"The idea is that UFOs are shaped the way they are because each one carries some kind of antigravity device that wraps it in a bubble, and that this is laid out in Imminent, a book published in 2024 by Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official. Congressman Eric Burlison says on the show that the book explains the shapes as making sense if 'you've got some kind of anti-gravitic device.' John Blitch, a scientist who once worked at DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, says the book shows a 'warp bubble' that Elizondo discussed with the physicist Hal Puthoff. Both are describing the book as they read it. If the idea holds, the craft's odd shapes and moves would follow from how they are driven.","lue-elizondo-s-superiors-who-saw-the-phenomenon-as-demonic":"Some of the superiors of Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence officer who ran AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, are said to have seen the phenomenon as demonic. Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear weapons site, tells Michels he found this in Imminent, Elizondo's 2024 book, in a part where Elizondo writes about superiors with that view. If true, people senior to the man studying UFOs for the Pentagon thought of them in religious terms.","luigi-s-company-attacked-court-demand-for-lazar-communicatio-2020":"A Montreal researcher named Luigi Vendittelli says that after he got involved with Bob Lazar, a man known for saying he worked on recovered alien craft, his company was \"attacked ferociously for over a year and a half\" until it went to zero, and that a court document demanded his communications with Lazar. \"Nobody knows this,\" he tells Michels, presenting it as a price paid for standing near Lazar's story.","luis-alvarez-muon-scan-of-khafre-pyramid-1960":"The physicist Luis Alvarez, a senior figure in the Manhattan Project, is said to have scanned one of the Giza pyramids for hidden chambers using muons, particles from space that pass through rock. Luke Caverns, a researcher of ancient Egypt, says Alvarez took a team there in the 1960s and scanned the pyramid of Khafre. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source, on two other episodes: once saying Alvarez, \"depicted in the movie Oppenheimer,\" \"x-rayed the pyramids in the 50s,\" and once placing it in the 1970s, in neither case naming the pyramid. The tellers bring it up to show that serious scientists have looked for what lies hidden inside the pyramids.","luke-s-theory-old-kingdom-pharaohs-were-never-buried-in-thei":"The pharaohs of Egypt's Old Kingdom, the age of the great pyramids at Giza, were never buried in their pyramids, says a researcher named Luke Caverns. He tells Michels their real tombs are \"down south,\" near the ancient site of Abydos. He says the later pharaoh Amenemhat III \"knew something that's invisible to us today,\" and if he is right the pyramids were built for some purpose other than tombs.","luke-s-visit-down-the-osiris-shaft":"A researcher named Luke Caverns says he went down the Osiris shaft, a deep shaft cut into the rock under the causeway leading to the pyramids at Giza, and that \"it drops down\" into the depths. He tells Michels he made the visit himself, guided by someone known as Land of Kem, whom Caverns expects Michels may know. He shares it as a first-hand look at one of the sites he thinks shows the pyramids are not what we think.","lunar-dust-prevented-a-fourth-eva-for-apollo-astronauts-1969":"Moon dust was so troublesome for the Apollo astronauts that they could not even manage a fourth EVA, meaning a fourth moonwalk outside the lander, according to the NASA engineer Charles Buhler. He tells Michels this as part of the background of lunar work, passing on what he has learned rather than what he saw himself.","lunar-laser-reflectors-1969":"Scientists are said to be able to aim lasers from Earth at equipment the Apollo astronauts left on the Moon between 1969 and 1972, and see the light come back. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this, repeating what a man he calls Ben told him: \"We can put a laser on some of the things that we left on the surface of the moon.\" It comes up as evidence that the Moon landings left real objects behind.","lunar-layers-are-inverted-like-an-excavation-site":"On the Moon, the lower layers of sediment are newer than the higher ones, \"the exact inverse\" of the usual order, and the pattern \"pattern matches to an excavation site,\" Michels says. He says it himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the writer Randall Carlson about the idea that the Moon is an alien spaceship. If true, it would suggest the Moon's surface was dug up and piled back on purpose.","lunar-mascons-forced-apollo-orbit-corrections-1960":"Hidden concentrations of mass under the Moon's surface, called mascons, pulled on the Apollo spacecraft as they orbited, causing problems with landing that needed corrections. The physicist Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this without naming a source, as evidence that the Moon's interior is not as simple as it looks.","lunar-transient-phenomena-outgassing-and-anomalous-lights-on":"The claim is that the Moon is not as dead as it looks. Over the centuries, watchers have reported short-lived lights and changes on its surface, known as lunar transient phenomena. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels, without naming a source, that there are \"hundreds of examples of the moon outgassing carbon dioxide, outgassing water vapor, things like that.\" A second guest, the researcher Maaneli Derakhshani, puts it differently on another episode. He says \"NASA has this archive of transient lunar phenomena dating back hundreds of years\" from amateur and professional astronomers. In his telling the reports cluster around a crater called Aristarchus. Neither guest names a further source for what they summarise. If the reports hold up, something keeps happening on the Moon, whether natural venting or something stranger.","lunch-with-stubblebine-about-buchanan-s-clearance-1980":"Some time in the 1980s, over lunch, a man named Albert Stubblebine, whom he calls Bert, asked Army Sergeant Lyn Buchanan, \"What is your clearance?\", meaning his level of access to secret material. Buchanan tells Michels this himself as something he took part in. It matters to him as the moment his security standing came under the eye of someone who could open doors for him.","lunch-with-von-daniken-attended-by-joe-rogan":"Michels says that he once organized a lunch with Erich von Däniken, a writer known for arguing that beings from other worlds visited ancient peoples, and that the podcaster Joe Rogan came to it. Michels mentions it in passing as something he did himself and gives no date or place.","lundahl-s-cuban-missile-crisis-briefing-for-kennedy-1962":"During the Cuban missile crisis of 1962, an intelligence official named Arthur Lundahl briefed President John F. Kennedy at the White House and, according to the researcher Grant Cameron, received an award from Kennedy for that work. Cameron mentions it to Michels without naming a source, to show how close to the president Lundahl stood, which matters because Lundahl figures elsewhere in Cameron's account of who knew about UFOs.","lyn-buchanan-army-psychic-spy-behind-the-men-who-stare-at-go":"The character Cassidy in The Men Who Stare at Goats, a story about the US Army's psychic soldiers, is based on a real man named Lyn Buchanan, a well-known army psychic spy and remote viewer, meaning someone trained to perceive distant targets using only the mind. Michels says this himself, adding that he had just interviewed Buchanan, and he names the Army figure Albert Stubblebine alongside him; if true, it means the film's oddest characters had real counterparts.","lyn-buchanan-brought-to-an-underground-site-with-a-reconstru":"A man named Lyn Buchanan is said to have been taken to a deep underground site in California where a collision between an airliner and a UFO had been pieced back together, the way air-crash investigators rebuild a wreck. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, repeating what he says Buchanan told him; if true, it would mean such a collision happened and was hidden from the public.","lyn-buchanan-met-by-three-nordic-men-at-a-diner":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army remote viewer (someone trained by the military to describe faraway places by mind alone), said he was confronted by three men at a diner where he ate regularly, men the story describes as \"Nordic,\" the tall, fair, human-looking visitors of UFO lore. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as what Buchanan told him: \"he was confronted at a diner that he eats at regularly by three men.\"","lyn-buchanan-met-with-an-alien-as-a-remote-viewer-2016":"Lyn Buchanan, who worked for the Army as a remote viewer, someone trained to try to perceive distant places and things with the mind alone, once said he had met with an alien. The writer Greg Bishop, who says he has known Buchanan a long time, tells Michels that Buchanan told him this \"eight or nine years ago\", around 2016, and relays it second-hand without saying where the meeting happened.","lynda-bird-johnson-says-lbj-valued-george-ball-1960":"President Lyndon Johnson is said to have really valued the advice of a government official named George Ball during the 1960s. Harald Malmgren, who was an adviser to several presidents, tells Michels he knows this from Johnson's daughter, Lynda Bird Johnson. He brings it up to show who had the president's trust in Washington at the time.","macarthur-s-1955-west-point-interplanetary-war-speech":"General Douglas MacArthur, in a 1955 speech at West Point, is said to have told the assembled cadets: \"The next war will be an interplanetary war.\" Michels quotes it himself, without naming a source, in an episode with Harald Malmgren, a former presidential advisor who says he handled UFO material. Michels brings it up as a sign that a top general took the idea of threats from beyond Earth seriously.","machine-based-and-person-based-uap-summoning-systems":"A helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, who says he flew a recovered UFO out by helicopter, says there are two ways to summon UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs): one uses machines and the other uses people. He tells Michels as someone who says he takes part that \"we've proven\" both work and that his group is \"still working with\" them, which would mean these objects can be called in on purpose.","mack-and-sheehan-s-plan-to-train-repeat-abductees-with-holot-2004":"Around 2004 in Cambridge, Massachusetts, a lawyer named Dan Sheehan and a Harvard figure named John Mack decided to train people who had \"consistent repeated encounters\" with beings they said abducted them, using holotropic breathing, a deep breathing method meant to bring on altered states. Sheehan tells Michels he took part and was legal counsel for Mack's group, PEER, the Project for Extraordinary Experience Research. In another conversation he tells it differently: he names PEER, says the aim was to teach abductees to question their abductors, and does not mention the breathing.","mack-s-mother-sends-him-to-bud-hopkins-1980":"A man named John Mack is said to have come to the subject of alien abduction because his mother sent him to Budd Hopkins, a man who worked with people reporting such experiences, and so \"he ended up talking to Bud Hopkins.\" The guest Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as something he heard about how Mack, sometime in the 1980s or 90s, got drawn in.","mack-used-grof-s-holotropic-breathwork-to-help-abductees-rec-1990":"In the 1990s, the Harvard psychiatrist John Mack, who worked with people who said they had been taken by aliens, is said to have used holotropic breathwork to help them recall what happened. Holotropic breathwork is a method of fast, deep breathing meant to bring on altered states of mind, developed by the psychiatrist Stanislav Grof. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and it would mean the abductees' memories came partly through an altered state.","madden-s-turn-to-ufos-2017-openness-and-the-2021-pentagon-pr":"A guest named James Madden, who talks with Michels about Plato's cave and consciousness, says he got pulled into UFOs in 2017, \"when suddenly it was okay to talk about this.\" He tells Michels that in the summer of what he thinks was 2021 he was watching The X-Files with his kids just as the first big Pentagon press conference on the subject happened. He tells it as his own turning point, a sign of how public openness drew ordinary people like him in.","maeterlinck-s-the-cloud-that-lifted-foretells-the-kennedy-as-1963":"A play called The Cloud That Lifted, by the Belgian writer Maurice Maeterlinck, author of The Blue Bird, is said to foretell the 1963 killing of John F. Kennedy in Dallas. The author Peter Levenda brings this up with Michels without naming a source, noting that \"the same guy who wrote that wrote a play called The Cloud That Lifted,\" and he ties it to Lee Harvey Oswald. He does not say in the telling what in the play matches the assassination, so the link is left as his reading.","mafia-enforcement-in-malmgren-s-rhode-island-childhood-1940":"Harold Malmgren, a former presidential advisor, remembers mob enforcement in the Rhode Island of his 1940s childhood, around Providence and Narragansett Bay. He tells Michels that a couple of guys would come in, grab a man, take him outside and beat him badly, and he laughs as he says it.","magellan-radar-finds-metallic-snow-on-venus-1990":"In the 1990s, NASA's Magellan probe mapped the surface of Venus by radar and, as the story goes, picked up what looked like metallic snow on the planet's mountains. A physicist named Kevin Knuth tells it as a former NASA scientist who was there, saying \"I was at NASA when the Magellan probe was mapping Venus using radar.\"","magenta-case-mussolini-s-rs33-investigation-group-1933":"In 1933 a craft supposedly came down near Magenta, Italy. Mussolini first took it for an advanced Nazi, British or American machine, but his special investigation group, called RS33, became convinced it was not human technology and probably went on to work with the Nazis. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells this to Michels without naming a source. If true, it would mean the recovery of crashed non-human craft started in Europe more than a decade before the US got involved.","magenta-craft-as-a-trigger-for-the-axis-1933":"A craft said to have come down near Magenta, in northern Italy, in 1933 may have helped push Italy and Germany into the alliance later called the Axis, says Harold Malmgren, who is billed as a presidential adviser and says he handled UFO material himself. He puts it to Michels as a question, wondering \"was this part of what drove the decision to create what we now call the axis?\", and names no source.","magenta-craft-taken-to-nazi-germany-1930":"A craft the tellers call the Magenta craft is said to have been taken to Nazi Germany, where it was studied. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas tells Michels it is reasonable to think the craft went there for study, without naming a source. Michels, on an episode with a Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, frames it as a question: he describes pictures of sites in Czechoslovakia and Poland and a ring-shaped test structure into which something shaped like an acorn or a bell could fit, and asks whether that was where the Magenta craft was tested. If true, it would mean the Nazis had a non-human craft to learn from.","magonia-people-arrested-at-lyon-agobard-denies-it":"In Lyon, in France, people said to have come from a place called Magonia were supposedly arrested, and a bishop named Agobard argued that it could not possibly be real. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this in passing. He gives no source of his own, but ties it to the UFO writer Jacques Vallée, and he calls Agobard an early rationalist, which makes it a story about how strange visitors were explained away long ago.","maguire-saw-a-ufo":"A CIA contractor named Bob Maguire says he once saw a UFO and that after it, \"I couldn't go back\" to how he saw the world before. He tells Michels this about himself, but gives no details of when, where or what he saw.","maison-rouge-meeting-1944":"In 1944 the Nazi official Martin Bormann is said to have gathered the leaders of German industry for a very secret meeting in Paris, known as the Maison Rouge meeting, where the rooms were swept so no one could listen in. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this, drawing on the author Peter Levenda, as part of an argument that powerful hidden networks planned to survive the war and later shaped the UFO cover-up.","majestic-12-1940":"Michels describes Majestic 12, or MJ-12, as a secret committee said to have sat through the 1940s and 50s, with members such as General Nathan Twining, Secretary James Forrestal and Dr. Vannevar Bush. He draws this from the MJ-12 documents, papers that claim to record a secret group set up to handle UFO matters. The main one is a briefing paper dated 18 November 1952. According to its own history, it reached a man named Jaime Shandera anonymously in 1984, on a roll of film left at his home in Burbank, and was made public in 1987. The paper lists \"Gen. Nathan F. Twining\", \"Secy. James V. Forrestal\" and \"Dr. Vannevar Bush\" among twelve members of a group it says Truman ordered on 24 September 1947, and it says Forrestal's death left a vacancy \"which remained unfilled until 01 August, 1950\". A second paper is a memo dated 14 July 1954 from a man named Robert Cutler to Twining, which Shandera and William Moore say they found at the National Archives in 1985. It says \"The President has decided that the MJ-12 SSP briefing should take place during the already scheduled White House meeting of July 16.\" The National Archives says the memo \"poses problems\", and the FBI's copies of both papers are marked \"BOGUS\" by hand, while the researcher Stanton Friedman judged them \"almost certainly genuine\". So the names Michels gives, and the reach into the 1950s, come from the papers, and they show that such a committee sat only if the papers are genuine.","majestic-12-as-a-cover-for-continuity-of-government-programs-1940":"Majestic 12, the supposed secret committee of twelve officials said to have managed UFO matters from the late 1940s, was really just a cover for continuity of government programs, the plans for keeping the government running after a catastrophe such as nuclear war. Michels says John B. Alexander, a retired Army colonel who worked on unconventional research, \"admitted\" this to him at one point, which would turn the famous UFO committee into a screen for doomsday planning.","majestic-12-briefing-document-1952":"The Majestic 12 papers are a set of typed pages that call themselves a secret briefing for President-elect Eisenhower, and they say a flying saucer crashed in 1947 and its wreckage was recovered. Michels calls it 'easily the most controversial' document in UFO circles. According to the note on where it comes from, the briefing came to light on a roll of 35mm film that a man named Jaime Shandera received anonymously at his home in Burbank in 1984. Shandera studied it with William Moore and the UFO researcher Stanton Friedman, and it was made public in 1987. The papers are headed 'OPERATION MAJESTIC-12 PRELIMINARY BRIEFING FOR PRESIDENT-ELECT EISENHOWER', carry the date 18 November 1952, and include a memo said to be from Truman to Defense Secretary Forrestal dated 24 September 1947. Page 3 says that on 7 July 1947 'a secret operation was begun to assure recovery of the wreckage of this object for scientific study' and that 'four small human-like beings had apparently ejected from the craft'. The National Archives and the Truman and Eisenhower libraries report finding nothing. The FBI's copies are marked 'BOGUS' by hand, and they record that the Air Force's investigators called it 'fabricated'. The writer Philip Klass called the Truman memo a fraud, and Friedman judged the papers 'almost certainly genuine'. Relaying Friedman, Michels says that if the document is real it proves a saucer crashed and was recovered, and that a man named Donald Menzel was rumoured to be in the group. That holds only as far as the papers are genuine, and their origin is exactly what is in dispute. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb says on the show that he is 'particularly fond of' the Eisenhower briefing, and he names Forrestal and Walter B. Smith among those tied to it.","majestic-12-committee":"Michels describes Majestic 12 as a rumoured secret committee that President Truman supposedly formed in 1947 to handle \"the extraterrestrial issue\", an elite group of scientists, military and intelligence officials who advised the president directly on UFOs, and he names Vannevar Bush, Gordon Gray, Donald Menzel, Eisenhower and Nathan Twining among those linked to it; if real, it would mean the White House ran the alien question from the top since 1947. His account rests on the MJ-12 papers, a typed briefing paper dated 18 November 1952 with a 1947 Truman memo to Secretary Forrestal attached, which came to light on a roll of 35mm film that a man named Jaime Shandera received anonymously at his Burbank home in 1984 and that he, William Moore and Stanton Friedman then studied before it was made public in 1987; the National Archives and the Truman and Eisenhower Libraries report finding no copy, and the FBI's file marks its copies \"BOGUS\" and records the Air Force's investigators calling the document \"fabricated\", while Friedman judged it \"almost certainly genuine\". The paper says the operation is run by the \"Majestic-12 (Majic-12) Group which was established by special classified executive order of President Truman on 24 September, 1947\" and is \"responsible directly and only to the President\", listing twelve members, so the committee Michels describes stands exactly as far as this paper of disputed origin does.","majestic-12-maji-named-in-an-nro-document-1990":"The secret group said to manage the UFO matter was once called Majestic 12, and by the 1990s it went by \"MAJI\", or \"magic\", according to Steven Greer, a longtime UFO campaigner. Greer tells Michels that this is \"the last good name I can pinpoint\", on a 1990s document from the NRO, the agency that runs America's spy satellites. He does not show or quote the document on the show; if real, it would mean the group still existed decades after its name first surfaced.","majestic-documents-curated-by-ryan-and-bob-wood-1940":"A large set of papers known as the Majestic documents is said to describe a secret US group that handled UFO matters from the 1940s through the 1960s. Two researchers, Ryan Wood and Bob Wood, gathered and published them, and the papers' origin is unknown. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels the documents go on \"page after page\" and are \"highly sophisticated, extremely sophisticated, frankly.\" He says this to suggest that whoever produced them had serious knowledge.","major-murphy-tells-vallee-about-1943-saucers-and-their-psych":"A man called Major Murphy is said to have told the researcher Jacques Vallée that flying saucers went back to 1943, and to have asked what if someone was already experimenting with the technology then, adding that \"its real advantage wasn't its flight capabilities, but its psychotronic effect,\" meaning its power over people's minds. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this from Vallée's book Messengers of Deception, as a hint that the phenomenon may have been a human tool of mind control.","malign-swarm-like-nhi-in-delonge-s-books-elizondo-s-hints-an-2010":"Several sources share the idea that some NHI, short for non-human intelligence, are hostile and act like a swarm: the books of Tom DeLonge, a rock musician who founded a UFO research company; hints from Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who has spoken publicly about UFOs; and a book called Cryptoterrestrial Conundrum. The analyst Matthew Pines points this out to Michels in his own summary, saying 'they all address this idea of the bad NHI, or the malign.' If true, it would mean not every visitor is friendly.","malmgren-at-the-ida-interviewed-by-the-host":"Michels says that a man named Eric A. Walker chaired the Institute for Defense Analyses, known as the IDA, and that Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, was part of the IDA early on. Michels tells this himself, adding that he had the privilege of interviewing Malmgren in person. He raises it to tie Malmgren to the same circle of people who, he suggests, sat close to the secrets.","malmgren-diaries-record-meetings-with-richard-bissell":"The diaries of a man named Harold Malmgren record many meetings between him and a man named Richard Bissell, according to the journalist Ross Coulthart, who says he has seen them himself. Coulthart tells Michels the diaries are now in a safe place and backed up, and warns people not to mess with them. He offers them as a written record that the two men met often.","malmgren-goes-public-that-nhi-are-real-2024":"Harold Malmgren, a veteran Washington adviser on trade, went public in 2024 saying that NHI, short for non-human intelligence, are real, and \"we have their stuff.\" The national security analyst Matthew Pines relays Malmgren's statement to Michels, which if true would mean the government holds objects made by non-human beings.","malmgren-helped-shoichiro-toyoda-set-up-toyota-s-headquarter-1980":"Harald Malmgren, a former presidential advisor on trade, helped Shoichiro Toyoda, the head of Toyota, set up the carmaker's headquarters in Kentucky in the 1980s. Michels says the two of them talked about this, relaying what Malmgren told him, as a sign of how far Malmgren's reach in business and government went.","malmgren-introduced-to-putin-at-an-arranged-meeting":"Harold Malmgren, a former presidential advisor, says the Russian official Yevgeny Primakov once picked him out of a crowd, brought him over and introduced him to Vladimir Putin, saying: \"This man is rising. He will soon be very important to Russia. You should know him.\" Malmgren tells it as something he lived through, and in one version he places the meeting in St. Petersburg in 1992, when Putin was not yet widely known.","malmgren-investigates-for-kennedy-administration-visits-lawr-1962":"In 1962 a man named Harold Malmgren, looking into the matter for the Kennedy administration, went to Albuquerque, New Mexico, to see Lawrence Preston Gise, the Albuquerque director of the Atomic Energy Commission, the federal agency then in charge of US nuclear work, and, as Michels points out, Jeff Bezos's maternal grandfather. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the Senate staffer Kirk McConnell; it would place the UFO question inside the nuclear establishment during Kennedy's time.","malmgren-links-his-life-and-his-knowledge-at-mit-to-time-tra-1950":"A former presidential advisor named Harold Malmgren says he has come to believe that his own life, and things he knew as a young man at MIT in the 1950s, are tied to time travel. He tells Michels he has \"thought about this many many times and the answer is yes,\" saying nothing else explains why he spoke to an MIT professor back then without \"the slightest inhibition,\" which if true would mean he was somehow drawing on knowledge from outside his own time.","malmgren-on-his-deathbed-cia-and-the-majestic-tracked-him-1950":"A man named Harold Malmgren, lying in a hospital bed near the end of his life, said the CIA had started tracking him at one point in his life and put his name \"in some file\", together with \"the Majestic\", the name some UFO researchers give to a supposed secret group said to manage the subject. Michels tells this himself, as something Malmgren said to him on his deathbed. If true, it would mean an intelligence agency and a hidden UFO group were watching him for decades.","malmgren-on-office-of-naval-intelligence-knowledge-1960":"The Office of Naval Intelligence, the Navy's intelligence service, knew far more about UFOs than any other part of the government. That is what a former presidential adviser named Harold Malmgren kept telling people, as Michels recounts across several episodes, calling it \"the deepest core\" and \"where all the secrets are kept\". If true, it would put the Navy, not the better-known agencies, at the center of what the government knows.","malmgren-on-the-secret-committee-1940":"A man named Harold Malmgren is said to have spoken, before he died, about a secret committee set up to manage UFO secrecy, and about a certain figure being on that committee's radar and perhaps having worked with it. Michels tells this himself, relaying what Malmgren said, while discussing claims that this committee shaped UFO secrecy from the 1940s on.","malmgren-ran-sub-four-minute-miles-after-bannister-1950":"Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser who says he handled UFO material, was one of the first people on Earth to run a mile in under four minutes after Roger Bannister, the runner who first broke that mark. Michels mentions it in passing, saying it came up when the two of them talked. He brings it up as a sign of what kind of man Malmgren was.","malmgren-read-into-legacy-uap-retrieval-program-by-bissell":"A man named Harold Malmgren is said to have been told about a long-running secret program to recover crashed UAP, the official term for UFOs, by Richard Bissell, a CIA official who set up Area 51, the secret test site in Nevada. The Australian investigative journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels that Malmgren's diaries are key to this, and that in them Bissell, whom Coulthart calls the \"mayor of Area 51,\" is the man who brought Malmgren in. If true, the CIA official behind Area 51 knew of crashed craft being recovered.","malmgren-s-ida-job-as-cover-for-nsc-work-1960":"A former government adviser named Harold Malmgren said that his job at the Institute for Defense Analyses, a federally funded research centre that works for the Pentagon, was a cover for the work he was really doing for the National Security Council and the president. Michels quotes him on this twice, once in his own words, \"My work at the IDA was actually a cover\", and once calling it an unofficial cover, said by Malmgren on record. Michels raises it to show how such research centres can hide secret government work, which matters to his case that a hidden UFO programme could be run the same way.","malmgren-s-ida-work-a-cover-for-the-nsc-bissell-diaries-1960":"A man named Harold Malmgren, whom Michels later interviewed, is said to have claimed that his 1960s work at the Institute for Defense Analyses was really a cover for work he was doing for the National Security Council in the White House. Michels says Malmgren said this in a 2024 interview given before their own conversation, and he ties the story to Malmgren's diaries and to a man named Richard Bissell. If true, it would mean that a defence research job hid quieter work at the very top of government.","malmgren-s-research-on-thomas-townsend-brown-and-time-travel":"Harold Malmgren, a former presidential advisor who says he handled UFO material directly, is said to have done deep research on Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor from the middle of the last century who worked on anti-gravity, meaning ways to lift objects without ordinary thrust. The story ties this research to time travel. Michels passes it on as something Malmgren told him, without giving more of what the research found.","malmgren-s-two-unherd-essays-on-putin-2022":"A former presidential adviser named Harald Malmgren, who says he directly handled UFO material, tells Michels he wrote two essays about Vladimir Putin for UnHerd, a British online magazine, in 2022. He mentions them as his own work, as an example of his writing on world affairs.","malmgren-saw-video-of-the-roswell-being-interviewed":"Late in his career, a former presidential adviser named Harold Malmgren saw film of a living creature being interviewed: one of the beings said to have survived the reported 1947 crash of an object near Roswell, New Mexico. Michels relays this from what Malmgren told his daughter, Pippa Malmgren, in one version on his deathbed. In some tellings Michels ties the footage to a 1962 high-altitude nuclear test called Bluegill Triple Prime, and if the story were true, someone recorded and kept an interview with a non-human survivor.","malmgren-sent-into-the-tank-to-stall-curtis-lemay-1962":"During the Cuban missile crisis of 1962, a young adviser named Harold Malmgren, then around 26 or 27, was sent by Kennedy into \"the tank\", a secure Pentagon meeting room, to \"stall\" the Air Force general Curtis LeMay and keep him from possibly launching nuclear weapons, according to Matthew Pines, a guest who says Malmgren told him so. Malmgren tells Michels the story himself, naming Defense Secretary Robert McNamara and adviser McGeorge Bundy as present, and recalls arguing \"it's madness\": if you hit Moscow, \"there's no one to talk to\" to stop a war once started.","malmgren-tracked-by-majestic-12-guardians-1950":"Harold Malmgren, a former advisor to US presidents, tells Michels that as a young man in the 1950s his name was entered in \"all the important books of talent\", kept not only by the CIA but by \"the Majestic\", the name given to an alleged secret group overseeing UFO matters, whom he calls \"guardians\" who took it on themselves \"to protect the world.\" Malmgren ties it to the day he met Karl Compton, then president of MIT, while Michels, retelling it on a later episode, says the flagging followed a letter Malmgren wrote to the Atomic Energy Commission, which then ran US nuclear work. If true, a hidden body beyond the CIA was quietly watching promising young people.","malmgren-tracked-by-majestic-guardians":"Harold Malmgren, a former presidential advisor, says he was watched not only by the CIA but also by what he calls \"the majestic, all these guardians\". Majestic is a name used in UFO circles for a supposed secret group in charge of the subject. In his telling, these guardians see their job as protecting the world.","malmgren-tweet-on-bluegill-tests-and-jfk-los-alamos-visit-1961":"Jesse Michels reads out a tweet by a former senior government insider named Harold Malmgren, who wrote that he was \"now reconstructing my personal interactions\" with Los Alamos, the 1961 to 1962 Bluegill tests, which he describes as tests of intercepting intercontinental ballistic missiles, and John F. Kennedy's visit to Los Alamos in November 1962. Michels brings it up on an episode about UFOs seen over nuclear bases, as a hint that Malmgren saw something tied to those tests that he is only now writing down.","malmstrom-afb-disc-wobbling-on-edge":"At Malmstrom Air Force Base, which he describes as home to nuclear missiles, a border patrol officer is said to have seen a metallic disc \"wobbling on its edge\", about 30 feet wide and only 100 feet away. A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this in passing. He gives the officer's words as a direct quote but does not name where he read them. If it is true, an unknown craft came close to American nuclear weapons.","malmstrom-missiles-disabled-by-red-glowing-object-1967":"On March 24, 1967, according to a former Air Force launch officer named Robert Salas, the nuclear missiles under his watch at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana shut down one after another while guards reported a glowing object above the site. Salas tells Michels firsthand that he was underground in the Oscar Flight launch control centre, the bunker that could fire a group of ten missiles. Guards on the surface had phoned about strange lights making odd moves, then a horn went off and the first missile \"went from green to red\", meaning it could no longer be launched. A man named Bob Jameson describes the control wall the same way: \"All 10 missiles were off alert. All 10. Whole flight was off alert. All red.\" If true, something in the sky was able to switch off American nuclear weapons. The researcher Robert Hastings wrote the account down in \"The Echo and Oscar Flight Incidents\", published in 2012. There, a guard \"screamed into the phone that a large, glowing, orange-colored, oval-shaped object\" was \"silently hovering over the Oscar Launch Control Facility\". So the object is orange in his telling, where Michels says red. Hastings also writes that Salas's fellow officer Frederick Meiwald \"remembered six-to-eight\" missiles failing, \"whereas Salas recalls all ten\", and Michels himself once says nine of ten. Michels comes back to the case again and again. Karl Nell, a guest speaking from an official background, mentions missiles taken offline at Malmstrom in the 1960s. The filmmaker James Fox and a guest named Kevin Knuth repeat Salas's account, Knuth from a 2010 press conference where Hastings presented six Air Force witnesses. Another guest, Dan Sheehan, says the base commander, Arie Arnoldson, filed a UFO report with NORAD, the joint US and Canadian air defence command.","malta-foundation-with-a-solar-power-station-and-planned-gpu-2020":"A researcher named Filippo Biondi, who comes on the show to talk about structures he says lie beneath Egypt's pyramids, says he is setting up a foundation in Malta. He tells Michels his group has rented a place there and installed a solar power station, and plans to add a data center of GPUs, the chips used for heavy computing work.","maltese-legend-of-giants-building-the-temples":"On the islands of Malta, local legend says giants once lived there and helped build the islands' ancient stone temples. A guest named Jay Anderson passes this on as what the Maltese say about the area, not as something he saw. If the legend kept a real memory, it would mean the temples' builders were remembered as something larger than ordinary people.","man-abducted-off-a-downtown-street-in-concord-1976":"In 1976 a man walking through downtown Concord, California, at 4 a.m. said he had been abducted, meaning taken away by beings from a UFO, right off the street. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who studies psychic phenomena and UFOs, says the man had no interest in UFOs, and that he and his colleagues interviewed him. Mishlove ties the case to a man named Ted Owens and to a police report, and tells it as one of a string of such cases.","man-in-a-black-duster-carrying-the-mothman-prophecies-in-a-m":"In a Los Angeles movie theater parking lot, the Hollywood writer and producer Bryce Zabel says, he and a fellow writer named Brent Friedman met a man in a long black duster coat who was holding a copy of The Mothman Prophecies, a book about sightings of a winged figure called the Mothman and the strange events around them. Zabel tells Michels he saw it himself. He tells it as one of those odd encounters that seem to come with digging into this subject.","man-made-craft-rising-from-desert-openings":"A man named Steven Greer, who argues that a global cabal holds the UFO secrets, says he has flown over desert sites where man-made objects \"come up out of the desert\". He tells Michels he saw it himself. In a second telling he names the site as the Lockheed Skunk Works, the company's secret aircraft development division, says he flew over it by helicopter in 2023, and says he has video and photos from his iPhone. If true, it would mean humans secretly build and fly such craft from hidden desert openings.","man-made-ufo-down-in-russia":"A UFO built by humans, not by anyone from elsewhere, went down in Russia, according to Steven Greer, a UFO researcher who argues that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal. Greer tells Michels this was recently \"confirmed\" to his group by a special operations team he does not name. If true, it would mean some people already build craft of the kind usually called UFOs.","man-who-says-he-was-in-the-gate-program":"A young man claims he took part in a secret effort he called the GATE program, and the telling never says what that programme was. The researcher Greg Malatzi says on the show that he talked to this man, and he will not give his name.","managed-disclosure-campaign-that-began-in-2017":"A retired Army colonel named Karl Nell has said that a carefully managed campaign of \"disclosure\", meaning a gradual official release of what the government knows about UFOs, began in 2017 and is now \"kind of dead.\" A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels he disagrees \"quite a bit\" and thinks the campaign is still going. If he is right, the steady trickle of official UFO news since 2017 is still being planned and released on purpose.","manchester-airport-orb-2024":"An orb, a glowing round light, is said to have been seen at Manchester Airport in Britain in June 2024. Michels brings it up while showing an image whose caption reads \"Manchester airport June 2024 sighting,\" in a debate with a UFO skeptic named Mick West. Nothing more about the sighting is given.","manchester-orb-sized-at-24-inches-at-most-2024":"An orb filmed at Manchester Airport in England in 2024 was at most 24 inches across, according to the researcher and UFO skeptic Mick West. He tells Michels that his team made a very detailed 3D recreation of the object from the exact viewpoints of the footage, used the data they had, and arrived at that size. If he is right, the object was something small, not a large craft.","mandela-effect-remembered-death-of-nelson-mandela-in-prison-1980":"Michels explains that the \"Mandela effect\", where many people share the same memory of something that did not happen, is named after the many people who remember Nelson Mandela dying in prison in the 1980s, though he was released and lived for decades after. Michels tells it himself, in a talk about whether we live in a hologram, where such shared false memories are taken as a possible clue that reality itself shifts.","manhattan-project-as-original-ufo-legacy-program-1940":"There are rumors, Michels says, that the Manhattan Project, the wartime effort that built the atomic bomb, was the original \"UFO legacy program\", meaning the first of the secret programmes said to hold and study UFO materials. He mentions it himself as a rumor, without naming a source, in an episode on Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs; if true, the secret UFO work would be as old as the bomb.","manhattan-project-as-the-first-blue-book-colleagues-grandpar-1940":"The Manhattan Project, the wartime effort that built the atomic bomb, was really the first UFO program, an earlier version of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who became a UFO whistleblower, says some of his colleagues had grandparents who were \"the UFO report people on the Manhattan Project.\" Michels adds that Grusch told him on a car ride in DC that the Manhattan Project \"was basically the original UFO program\", which would mean the government's UFO secrets go back to the 1940s.","manipulated-ellsworth-document-traced-to-doty-1977":"At issue is a supposed Air Force document about Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota. It is said to claim that on November 16, 1977, something attacked missile site L9 there with a beam, and that the document was later doctored or forged by a man named Richard Doty. The UFO historian Richard Dolan says there is \"a document about Ellsworth there which has been manipulated,\" and that it has \"been traced back to\" Doty. Michels tells it in two places in one episode. First he describes it as \"a famous supposed Air Force document\" about that date and credits the researcher Brad Sparks. Then he adds that, \"according to well-respected physicist and UFO researcher Bruce Maccabee, researcher Bill Moore acknowledged that Doty confessed to forging the Ellsworth document.\" So Dolan says the document was altered, while Michels's chain of sources, from Doty to Moore to Maccabee, calls it an outright forgery. None of the tellers has the document in hand or saw the events. It matters because a real report would describe an attack on a nuclear site, while a forged one would point to someone deliberately feeding false papers to researchers.","mansmann-letters-describe-domed-disc-on-film-1964":"A man named Florenze Mansmann studied film shot at Vandenberg Air Force Base in 1964, going through it frame by frame with a loupe magnifier, and saw on it a disc-shaped object with a dome. Robert Hastings, a researcher who studies UFOs at nuclear weapons sites, tells Michels this, citing private letters Mansmann wrote in the mid-1980s to a man named Robert Jacobs, in which, Hastings says, Mansmann wrote that what he saw with the magnifier was \"a disc-shaped object with a dome.\" If true, it would mean a military film caught a flying disc.","manson-modeled-his-life-on-heinlein-s-stranger-in-a-strange-1960":"The cult leader Charles Manson is said to have modelled his life on Stranger in a Strange Land, a novel by the science fiction writer Robert Heinlein, and to have carried the book around with him in the 1960s. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation that also takes in Jolly West, a figure he ties to the CIA's mind control research. If true, it would mean a piece of fiction helped shape one of the most notorious groups of the era.","mantell-s-plane-disintegrated-by-magnetism-1948":"In 1948, near Fort Knox in Kentucky, the plane of a pilot named Thomas Mantell was destroyed, and the story holds that it was taken apart by magnetism. A man named Silas Newton says on the show that the account of Mantell's death \"didn't say it had been blown out of the air and then crashed,\" it \"said it disintegrated,\" which he takes as a sign that some unusual force, not a crash, broke the plane up.","mantis-being-at-the-bedside-tears-flesh-to-show-the-body-is-1995":"In 1995, at his home near Denver, Colorado, a mantis-like being stood at the bedside of John Blitch, a former DARPA scientist (DARPA being the Pentagon's research agency), and tore at flesh to show him that the body is only a container for the soul. Blitch tells Michels this as something he saw himself, describing how the creature's \"triangle face opens up and these Jaws\" appear.","manuel-caceres-knockoff-figure-confused-with-the-nazca-bodie-2023":"Michels says one figure people mixed up with the Nazca bodies, small three-fingered bodies from Peru that some present as possibly non-human, was really merchandise: a knockoff made by Manuel Caceres, a local journalist, and he adds that these wooden figurines were confiscated at Lima Airport in 2023. He backs this with a clip of an official named Flavio Estrada, who says \"I repeat, that one is fake. It's craftsmanship constructed, built.\" The point is that this one fake should not be confused with the bodies themselves.","many-uap-sightings-off-the-coast-of-san-diego-2004":"Many sightings of UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, are said to cluster off the coast of San Diego. The economist Tyler Cowen says this to Michels, remarking on where the reports tend to come from. If true, something keeps showing up in the same stretch of sea and sky.","map-of-key-black-sites-in-greer-s-brief":"A briefing document contains a map showing where all the key 'black sites', secret facilities hidden from oversight, are located and where the hidden material is kept, according to Steven Greer, a doctor who has long campaigned for UFO secrets to be made public. Greer holds up what he calls 'the brief' and tells Michels about it himself. If true, it would point to where the secret programs physically are.","marble-from-crash-site-tests-about-40-iridium-by-xrf":"A marble said to come from the site of a reported 1948 UFO crash near Aztec, New Mexico, came back from an X-ray test of its chemical elements with \"an extremely high percentage of iridium, close to 40%,\" Michels says. He tells it in connection with Scott Ramsey and Suzanne Ramsey, researchers who have spent years on the Aztec case, and calls the result \"very anomalous\", since it would point to a material not made in the ordinary way. Another account describes a later lab retest that found no iridium at all.","marcel-s-son-handled-the-roswell-material-at-home-1947":"In 1947, after something crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in what some believe was an alien craft, an Army intelligence officer named Jesse Marcel is said to have brought some of the wreckage home, where his son, Jesse Marcel Jr., later an Air Force flight surgeon, handled it and played with it. Michels tells this more than once, relaying what the son said. In one telling he calls the material a kind of \"memory metal\" that matches the father's own account, and in another he puts the weight on Marcel holding the wreckage in his own hands and showing it to his son, as firsthand evidence that the debris was real and strange.","marcker-s-general-electric-experiment-testing-for-spacetime":"A scientist named Marcker is said to have run an experiment at General Electric to test whether torsion, a proposed twisting in the fabric of space and time, really exists. The researcher Eric W. Davis tells Michels about it without naming a source. He praises it as a well-thought-out, well-designed apparatus, which matters because a positive result would point to new physics.","marco-chereze-dies-after-handling-the-varginha-creature-1996":"A soldier named Marco Eli Chereze is said to have died after handling a strange being captured in the Brazilian town of Varginha in 1996, a creature that Michels says was covered in an oily, greasy substance. Michels tells it on several episodes. In one he credits Chereze's family, who still hold that the being caused his illness and say that near the end he kept worrying that holding it had made him sick. In another he says, crediting a man named Armando Fortunato, that Chereze died from a puncture wound the being gave him.","marco-leal-re-interviews-carlos-about-25-years-later-2021":"Carlos de Souza, a surgeon from São Paulo, Brazil, says that in 2021 an interviewer named Marco Leal questioned him again about events at his hospital, which the episode describes as an operation on an alien, about 25 years after his first interview. De Souza tells Michels that Leal said: \"your account is exactly the same as the one you gave 25 years ago. Identical. Not a single detail has changed.\" He tells it as a sign that his story has stayed the same over time.","marco-rubio-s-attempt-to-approach-harrington-2020":"About five or six years ago, Senator Marco Rubio tried to approach an official named John S. Herrington and \"got shut down.\" Brent Friedman, a guest on an episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, tells this as something he heard, citing David Grusch, a former intelligence officer turned UFO whistleblower. If true, it would mean even a senator was turned away when he went looking for answers.","marco-rubio-s-ssci-staff-seek-whistleblowers-2017":"Marco Rubio has been interested in UFOs since 2017, the story goes, and has sent his staff on the SSCI, the Senate's intelligence committee, and others to find out more about whistleblowers for him. The teller is a researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb, after UAP, the official term for UFOs. He tells Michels it is something people deep in the subject have long known, without naming a source. If true, it would mean a senior senator has been quietly gathering insiders willing to talk about secret UFO work.","marconi-believed-the-magenta-craft-non-human-1930":"The radio pioneer Guglielmo Marconi is said to have believed that a craft reported to have come down near Magenta, Italy, in the 1930s \"could be non-human.\" Michels says this rests on \"some documents on later family confirmations,\" which he does not name. If true, it would put one of the great scientists of the age behind a crashed-craft story from before Roswell.","marconi-radar-and-the-italian-crash":"A scientist named John Blitch, who worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, suggests to Michels that a crashed craft may have been recovered not only at Cape Girardeau, a US town tied to an older crash story, but in Italy as well. His reason is that the Italian radio pioneer Guglielmo Marconi \"was involved\". He mentions it briefly, without a source, and does not spell out what Marconi's part was.","marconi-s-grandson-tells-oggi-about-the-ufo-committee-1930":"The grandson of the radio pioneer Guglielmo Marconi told the Italian magazine Oggi, in a video interview anyone can watch, \"my grandfather had a lot to do with the UFO and ET issue\", which would put Marconi inside a UFO effort in Mussolini's Italy of the 1930s. Michels brings this up himself, describing Oggi as a kind of Italian gossip magazine, roughly the equivalent of People.","marconi-s-transatlantic-radio-experiment-which-worked-becaus-1901":"Guglielmo Marconi's 1901 radio signal from Newfoundland to Cornwall crossed the Atlantic only because, without his knowing it, the waves bounced off the ionosphere, a charged layer high in the atmosphere, and came back down to the receiver. That is how a scientist named Filippo Biondi, who studies what lies beneath the pyramids, explains it on the show, drawing on James Clerk Maxwell's theory of electromagnetic waves. Michels later repeats Biondi's point in his own words, calling Marconi \"ignorant in an adaptive way\": too unaware of the problem to know it shouldn't work, so he tried it anyway and it did.","marcos-obtains-two-troves-through-torture-1970":"Ferdinand Marcos, the Philippine leader, is said to have gotten hold of two troves through torture, with a Colonel Ver named alongside him, sometime between 1970 and 1989. The researcher Dan Sheehan says it to Michels in passing, with no source named. What he says is only: \"Marcos got two of them,\" and he does not spell out here what the troves held.","marcus-vinicius-s-chain-smoking-after-the-event-1996":"After the 1996 events in Varginha, Brazil, where a hospital is said to have operated on an alien, a man named Marcos Vinícius is said to have been badly shaken and to have started smoking all day. A surgeon named Italo Venturelli, who tells Michels he was there, says Vinícius was affected \"a lot\" and \"would smoke all day,\" a sign of how much the event troubled those close to it.","marcus-vinicius-s-video-of-the-being-shown-to-many-1996":"A surgeon named Italo Venturelli, who says his hospital in Varginha, Brazil, operated on an alien in 1996, says a colleague named Marcos Vinícius had a video of the being and showed it to many people at his house. Venturelli tells it as something he knows first hand: visitors would sit on the sofa while Vinícius played it on his computer, probably from a CD, \"repeatedly sometimes.\" If true, film of the creature would have existed and been seen by several people.","maria-farmer-mural-showing-epstein-in-a-flying-saucer-on-an":"The artist Maria Farmer is said to have painted a mural, called The Elite, showing Jeffrey Epstein's face in the window of a flying saucer hanging off an antenna that flies an FBI flag, with Ghislaine Maxwell painted as a dragon biting a child in half. The researcher Jay Anderson describes the painting to Michels: \"an antenna going up with an FBI flag on it with a flying saucer hanging off the top of it with Epstein's face inside the window.\" For him it hints that Farmer linked Epstein to the FBI and to UFOs.","maria-s-finger-bones-tested-in-sri-lanka-come-back-male-and-2020":"Two finger bones from Maria, one of the small three-fingered mummified bodies from Peru that some call alien, were genetically tested in Sri Lanka, and the story is that one came back male and the other female. Michels says he heard this from Steve Mera, a UFO researcher, who told him the tested bones came from one of the bodies of the kind called \"M types\". If true, a single body would carry two sexes in its bones, which would make it hard to explain as an ordinary human.","maria-wang-says-her-husband-reported-directly-to-henry-kissi-1950":"A man named Eric Wang, who worked at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio in the 1950s, reported directly to Henry Kissinger, according to his wife, Maria Wang. Michels relays her words in an episode asking whether Kissinger oversaw a programme to recover crashed UFOs, and he cites the researcher William Steinman alongside her. If true, it would put Kissinger in the chain of command for that work.","marilyn-monroe-rumor-that-jfk-spilled-ufo-secrets-1960":"According to a rumor, Marilyn Monroe said that JFK had spilled some UFO-related secrets. Michels raises it himself in the 1960s setting it belongs to, without naming where the claim comes from; if true, it would mean a president knew such secrets and let them slip.","marine-claims-he-was-on-the-usaf-flight-out-of-campinas-1996":"A US Marine claims he was aboard a US Air Force flight out of Campinas, Brazil, in 1996 as part of the operation at Varginha, a Brazilian town where creatures were reportedly captured that year. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels he got this in an email from the Marine, who did not give his name: 'I was on the flight.' If true, it would mean the US military helped take something out of Brazil.","marine-squadron-commander-saw-the-churning-white-water-2004":"In 2004, off Southern California, Navy pilots from the carrier USS Nimitz reported a \"Tic Tac\", a white oval object shaped like the mint, hovering over a patch of churning white water, and the writer Marik von Rennenkampff says a commander of a Marine Corps squadron flying by also saw that water. He gives the commander's name a little unsure (\"Kurt, I think, is his name\"), passing on an account he did not witness himself. If it holds, the pilots' report would have a further witness from another unit.","mariner-4-photographs-a-barren-mars-1965":"In 1965 the space probe Mariner 4 flew past Mars and photographed it up close, and the pictures showed a planet that looked inactive, barren and lifeless. Michels tells this himself, drawing on the Mariner 4 photographs, in an episode with a guest who argues Mars once had life, so the photos stand as the official picture that the episode goes on to question.","mariner-9-s-inca-city-ruins-near-the-martian-south-pole-1972":"In 1972 the NASA probe Mariner 9 sent back photographs from near the south pole of Mars showing what looked like ancient ruins, which the mission team informally nicknamed the \"Inca City\". Jesse Michels tells this himself, pointing to the Mariner 9 photographs, as a possible sign that someone once built on Mars.","mario-finds-his-right-b-52-pilot-glove-missing-from-his-bag-1977":"After his strange night at a South Dakota missile site in 1977, the Air Force guard Mario Woods says he went to his bag at home in Rapid City and found one of his gloves gone. He tells Michels he checked everything: \"my left hand glove was there, but the right hand glove is gone,\" and he describes them as B-52 pilot gloves. He mentions a man named Michael Johnson as part of this telling. Woods had written about the gloves earlier for the journalist Robert Hastings, whose article on the case was published on 10 September 2017. That version is a little different. Woods writes that he had not thought about \"those heavy Air Force-issue gloves with open fingers, so you could fire a weapon\" until someone brought them up, and that when he ran to his ditty bag \"they were not there\", meaning both of them. The two tellings disagree on what kind of gloves they were and on whether one or both went missing. Woods brings it up because a missing glove would be a physical trace of a night he can hardly remember.","mario-keeps-the-beings-out-of-his-debrief-to-keep-his-prp-cl-1977":"An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods says that in 1977, at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, he left the beings he had seen out of his official debriefing so he would keep his PRP clearance, the Air Force's screening of who may work around nuclear weapons. He tells Michels this himself, saying \"had I said that I saw these beings,\" he would have lost it, which would mean what he saw never reached the record.","mario-s-childhood-sightings-of-craft":"Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels he has seen these beings' craft many times since he was a boy. He speaks of it first-hand, careful to say he means not the beings themselves but \"their craft\", as part of a life he describes as marked by contact.","mario-s-doe-clearance-delays-and-repression-of-his-experienc-1978":"Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who later worked at nuclear sites, says that around 1978 he had to push down the memory of what he describes as an abduction by aliens. The reason, he says, was that his first security clearance with the Department of Energy, for work at sites like Oak Ridge and Los Alamos, took more than eight months to come through. He tells Michels this from his own experience, suggesting that speaking up could have cost him his career.","mario-s-earlier-hypnotic-regression-triggers-atrial-fibrilla-2026":"An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods, who says he was abducted by aliens, went through hypnotic regression, a hypnosis method meant to bring back buried memories, and reliving the experience was so traumatic that his heart went into atrial fibrillation. Michels tells this himself in an episode where Woods and other veterans who say they were taken together meet again 50 years later, offering it as a sign of how real the memory feels to him.","mario-s-glove-seen-on-the-shiny-floor-of-the-craft-1977":"In 1977, at a nuclear missile field run from Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, two young Air Force guards say they were taken aboard a craft, and one of them remembers a small detail: a glove lying on a shiny floor. Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran who says he was there, tells Michels that he was lying down somewhere and saw \"a glove on a floor, a real shiny floor, nothing like the inside of that truck\" they had been riding in. He links the glove to his fellow guard, Mario Woods. The same detail turns up in an article by Robert Hastings, a journalist who writes about UFOs and nuclear weapons sites, published on 10 September 2017 under the title \"Former USAF Missile Security Policeman Tells of Apparent UFO Abduction at ICBM Site\". There the man whose mittens they were recalls the moment his colleague told him: \"He said he saw my mittens on the floor, next to where he was lying down, as he was hearing the voices.\" So one teller is a witness speaking for himself on the show, and the other is a journalist quoting a second witness years earlier. The point of the glove is that, if both men remember it, it would place them in the same strange room at the same time, which is why they tell it.","mario-s-regression-a-device-pushed-into-his-wrist-1977":"An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods says that in 1977 he was taken aboard a craft, and that under regression, a hypnosis session meant to bring back buried memories, he recalled a being bending his wrist backwards and pushing a device into it. Woods tells this himself on the show, saying \"he goes down into my wrist with it,\" which if true would mean something was implanted in him.","mario-s-regression-because-he-s-here-1977":"An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods says that under regression, a kind of hypnosis meant to bring back buried memories, he recalled being aboard a craft in 1977, where a small being said \"just as plain as day\", \"Because he's here.\" He tells Michels this as his own memory, and links the moment to another Air Force veteran, Michael Johnson, who the episode says was taken with him.","mario-woods-abduction-at-ellsworth-afb":"In November 1977, a young Air Force security policeman named Mario Woods and his team leader, a sergeant named Michael Johnson, were on the night shift guarding nuclear missile sites at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, and they say beings from a craft took them. Woods's own written account was published in 2017 by Robert Hastings, a journalist who collects stories of UFOs at nuclear weapons sites. In it Woods says that as they turned onto the road to a missile site called November-5, they saw 'a huge glowing, reddish-orange sphere' hanging silently over it. He writes that he thinks he remembers 'small figures, maybe five or six,' walking toward their truck, with 'a taller figure who I think was in charge' behind them, and then he passed out. At the debriefing, he says, he was told 'nearly four hours had passed' and that six back-up teams had been out searching for them. Michels comes back to the case on several episodes, passing on what Woods told him and citing Hastings's book UFOs and Nukes, and he says much of it came back to Woods under hypnotic regression, a hypnosis method meant to recover buried memories. His details differ a little from the article: at least five missing hours, Woods waking up nine miles away, a truck found at a dam, mud in a frozen field, burns a doctor noticed first, and drawings of the craft. Michels says he later brought the two men together again, and if their account is true, something took men guarding American nuclear weapons.","mario-woods-becomes-obsessed-with-pyramids-after-ellsworth-a-1977":"After an abduction experience at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota in 1977, an airman named Mario Woods became fixated on pyramids. Woods tells Michels that he would sit at the dining tables in the day room drawing a pyramid with \"a weird top on it and little sparks coming out of it\", and that the other men in the missile field made fun of him for it. Today he calls it \"an Atlantean pyramid\", after the legendary lost island of Atlantis. Michels also brings up Woods and the Ellsworth case on another episode, which suggests the drawings as an after-effect of what happened to him.","mario-woods-debriefed-and-found-with-circular-wounds-at-the-1960":"In November 1977, a guard at a nuclear missile base was taken by aliens, then questioned and examined at the base hospital, where he was found with strange round marks. The man is Mario Woods, who guarded intercontinental nuclear missiles at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota. The researcher Robert Hastings, who interviewed him, tells Michels that at the hospital he had a round wound \"with a little dimple in the center\" on his forehead and his ankle. If true, his body would carry physical evidence of the event. Michels, telling it in another episode, stresses that Woods held a high security clearance called a Q clearance and says a doctor checked his teeth. Hastings's own article, \"Former USAF Missile Security Policeman Tells of Apparent UFO Abduction at ICBM Site\", dated 10 September 2017, gives Woods's words differently. There, at the hospital, \"two skin samples were taken, one from the back of my right hand and another above my right eyebrow, as I was sunburned for some strange reason.\" The two round dimpled marks are separate: \"One is under my left armpit and the other is on my left foot, above the ankle,\" and Woods says he cannot remember when he got them.","mario-woods-reports-the-incident-to-aaro-which-never-followe-2023":"Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran and former nuclear site guard, says he reported his encounter in March 2023 to AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into unexplained aerial reports, and that the office never followed up, while a man he names, Michael Johnson, was never found. In one telling he adds that the interview lasted 4 hours and 19 minutes, was not recorded, and came about thanks to someone he names as Kirkpatrick. He tells Michels this as something he went through himself.","mark-felt-pushes-to-investigate-the-mexico-bank-account-1972":"In 1972 Mark Felt, a senior FBI official, insisted on going to Mexico the very next morning to look into a bank account, according to the lawyer Dan Sheehan. Sheehan tells Michels this from memory, without naming a source, and he mentions Felt's boss at the bureau, Pat Gray, alongside him. The account matters to Sheehan because he links it to money in the Watergate affair, so Felt's push would have meant following that money to its source.","mark-felt-s-biography-nixon-shown-covert-technology-1970":"Michels says that the former FBI official Mark Felt describes, in a book about his life, President Richard Nixon being shown \"pretty specialized covert technology.\" Michels tells this himself in three episodes, hedging (\"I think\"). He calls the book a biography in two of them and an autobiography in the third, and once he also mentions the journalist Bob Woodward. If true, it would mean a president was briefed on secret technology of the kind the subject is about.","mark-mccandlish-s-alien-reproduction-vehicles":"A man named Mark McCandlish spoke of \"alien reproduction vehicles\": craft said to be built by copying alien technology. Michels brings it up in passing as an example of the claim that the United States has working craft of this kind. If true, it would mean such technology is not just studied but flown.","mark-twain-predicts-his-death-with-halley-s-comet-1835":"Mark Twain is said to have predicted his own death: born on November 30, 1835, as Halley's Comet was passing Earth, he often told people he expected to go out with the comet when it came back, and he died in 1910, just as he said. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation about the idea that the brain can sense the future. He offers it as an example of someone seeming to know what was coming.","mars-destroyed-by-a-cme-and-plasma-events-valles-marineris":"A researcher named Matt LaCroix believes Mars was wrecked by a coronal mass ejection, a huge eruption of charged matter from the sun, and by violent plasma events. In his telling, the giant canyon called Valles Marineris is a scar from that event. He tells Michels that once something about the planet \"weakens\", this \"caused the entire destruction of Mars\". It is his own theory and he names no source for it.","mars-global-surveyor-image-of-a-square-structure-2001":"Michels says that in 2001 the Mars Global Surveyor, a NASA spacecraft that photographed Mars from orbit, took a picture of a square structure on the surface that looks artificial, and that the picture has only recently reached a wide audience. On another episode he says his friend Chris Ramsay, who posts about UFOs online, shared the photo, the podcaster Joe Rogan reposted it, and it has since been \"verified at the highest levels\", though he does not say by whom. If true, it would point to something built on Mars.","mars-global-surveyor-images-of-possible-fossilized-life-1997":"Some photos taken by Mars Global Surveyor, a NASA spacecraft that mapped Mars from 1997 to 2006, show what may be the skeletons of large fossilized life forms. This comes from a researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani, who tells Michels that \"a number of these images\" look like they \"could well be\" such remains, though he names no source. If he is right, Mars once had large animals.","mars-investigation-at-sri-just-down-the-hall-from-the-remote-1980":"In the 1980s, according to a physicist named John Brandenburg, the base of a research effort into possible signs of past life on Mars sat at SRI, the Stanford Research Institute in California, just down the hall from the government's remote viewing program, an effort to use psychics to describe distant places. Brandenburg, who worked on the Mars side, tells Michels they only \"found out later\" who their neighbors were, which hints that the two efforts may have been linked.","martial-arts-programs-that-train-people-to-sense-being-looke":"Some martial arts programmes are said to train people to know when someone behind them is looking at them. The researcher Rupert Sheldrake tells Michels this without naming a particular school or source. He offers it as a sign that the feeling of being stared at may be a real sense that can be trained.","martian-meteorite-alh84001-with-possible-fossil-bacteria-1984":"In 1984, scientists in Antarctica found a meteorite from Mars, named ALH84001, that was later said to hold what might be fossil bacteria. Michels tells it himself without naming a source: \"in 1984 scientists found a Martian meteorite in Antarctica,\" and he ties it to President Clinton. The physicist John Brandenburg agrees on another episode that \"it was found in Antarctica\" and gives the same name, but he puts the find in the 1990s. On a later episode Michels speaks of it again as a meteor from the '90s that \"Bill Clinton made a speech on.\" So the tellers differ on when it was found, and Michels comes to focus on Clinton's speech. They tell it because, if true, it would point to life once existing on Mars.","martian-scout-ship-finds-a-volatile-primordial-earth":"Long ago, according to this account, a group of beings from Mars were sent out in a craft \"to go scout out a new world that they could possibly develop as a habitation for themselves,\" and what they found was a young, unsettled, volatile Earth. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, passing on what a man named Joe McMoneagle described. If true, it would put Martian visitors at the very beginning of Earth's story.","martian-survivors-fled-and-some-may-have-reached-earth":"Mars once had people on it, and some of them got stuck there and tried to survive by hibernating, while others may have fled and reached Earth. That is what Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer (someone who says he can describe faraway places and times with his mind alone), tells Michels. He gives it as his own view, with no other source, and if true it would mean humans or their ancestors might have Martian roots.","martians-attacked-from-space":"A scientist named John Brandenburg tells Michels that Mars may once have had inhabitants, and that \"something happened up there\" that looks as if they \"were attacked from space by somebody else.\" He offers it as his own reading of the evidence, on an episode about life on Mars being destroyed by nuclear war, and he names no source for this point. If he is right, Mars had a civilisation that was destroyed by another one.","martians-built-the-moon-as-a-transport-and-terraforming-devi":"People living on Mars probably built the Moon, and they built it for two purposes: as a means of transport and as a device for making Earth fit to live on. The philosopher Jason Jorjani puts this forward on the show, drawing on Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer (someone said to describe distant places by mind alone), and on a work he credits called Closer Encounters.","martin-cannon-s-the-controllers-and-john-alexander-s-threat":"A book called The Controllers, by a writer named Martin Cannon, argues that some alien abductions were faked by people inside the government, and Cannon was reportedly threatened for writing it. Michels brings up the book on several episodes as something he has read about. In one he says 'apparently John Alexander like threatened him when he wrote this book,' naming a man called John B. Alexander. In others he says Cannon writes about MK Ultra, a CIA mind-control research program, and about an Air Force counterpart he calls 'MK Often', and that implants were involved in some cases. Michels also puts the book's argument this way: the alien abduction story 'was used as a smoke screen for MK Ultra.' The researcher Greg Malatzi, on a separate episode, sums the book up as being about 'the fabrication of alien abductions.' Only Michels mentions the threat. If Cannon is right, some people who believed they were taken by aliens were in fact subjects of secret human experiments.","martin-gardner-finds-his-own-lost-book":"A writer named Martin Gardner was browsing a used bookstore one day and came across a familiar title: a copy of his own book, one he had lost. The skeptic Michael Shermer tells the story on the show as a striking coincidence, and mentions Persi Diaconis and Frederick Mosteller alongside it. He tells it to show that coincidences this strange really do happen.","mary-k-sturdivant-as-lockheed-insider-who-helped-kill-kona-b-2006":"Kona Blue, a proposed government programme on UFOs, may have been killed with help from inside the defense contractor Lockheed Martin. That is the suggestion of an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb, speaking to Michels without a named source. UAPGerb names a Lockheed figure, Mary K. Sturdivant, as a possible insider with a conflict of interest who helped \"put the kibosh on the program\", and mentions Chris Mellon too. If true, it would mean a private company quietly steered what the government studied.","mary-magdalene-lands-at-marseille-merovingian-bloodline":"Mary Magdalene, according to some traditions, sailed with Joseph of Arimathea, the man who in the Gospels buries Jesus, and came ashore at what is now Marseille in France. The independent researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels this as old tradition, without naming a source. The story usually goes on to say that her descendants became the Merovingians, an early line of Frankish kings, which would mean a sacred bloodline running through European royalty.","mary-s-mosaic-mary-pinchot-meyer-and-jfk-1960":"A book Michels believes is called Mary's Mosaic tells the story of Mary Pinchot Meyer, a woman tied to John F. Kennedy in 1960s Washington, and of Cord Meyer. Michels raises it himself, in an episode about Kennedy and UFOs, as a source for what Kennedy may have known, without saying more about what the book contains.","masonic-and-zodiac-symbolism-in-nasa":"The claim is that NASA is full of hidden symbolism: Masonic signs, a launchpad numbered 33, and Apollo missions tied to the zodiac. The former Pentagon official Luis Elizondo says so on the show, without naming a source. \"Look at Launchpad 33, I mean everything is Masonic,\" he says, adding that the Apollo missions \"are all the zodiac signs.\" Michels raises the same point himself on a later episode, crediting the author Richard Hoagland and his book Dark Mission: The Secret History of NASA. He frames it as the idea that there are cult rituals inside the agency, with launchpad 33 among the signs. Elizondo speaks of symbols; Michels, following Hoagland, speaks of rituals. If it were true, the space agency would be shaped by secret beliefs as well as by engineering.","mass-of-online-prophecies-of-9-11-2001":"A researcher of precognition named Eric Wargo tells Michels that there were \"a million\" prophecies of the 9/11 attacks in New York, many of them posted online. He mentions it in passing, without pointing to any particular one. He uses it to argue that people may sense big future events before they happen.","mass-sighting-near-hometown-explained-as-jupiter":"A Swedish UFO researcher named Clas Svahn says that near his hometown of Mariestad there was \"this huge observation\", a light many people saw in the sky, which was later explained as the planet Jupiter. He tells Michels he was there for it, as an example of a mass sighting with an ordinary answer.","massive-cme-event-during-the-older-dryas":"Near the end of the last ice age, during a cold spell called the Older Dryas, the Earth was hit by a coronal mass ejection, a huge burst of charged material thrown off by the sun, bigger than anything in modern history. That is the claim of a researcher named Matt LaCroix, who ties it to traces in Antarctic ice cores and does not name a source. If true, a solar blast of that size would have hit the Earth hard during humanity's prehistory.","masters-childhood-precognition-of-a-gray-alien-at-age-8":"At eight years old, Mike Masters, a guest who argues that aliens may be humans from the future, says he foresaw a gray alien, the big-headed grey-skinned figure of abduction stories. He tells Michels it was what he now understands as \"a moment of conscious precognition\", a vision of something before he met it, and that this is how he got into the subject.","masters-face-to-face-contact-with-future-humans-at-a-phoenix-2022":"Mike Masters, who argues that the beings behind UFOs are humans from the future, says two such individuals approached him face to face at a conference hotel in Phoenix, Arizona, in 2022. He tells Michels that one pulled up a chair so close that his knee pressed into Masters' crotch. If true, it would mean he had met these beings in person.","matching-puncture-marks-examined-by-dr-margo-2019":"A former Air Force guard at a nuclear missile site, who believes he was abducted by a UFO, says he carries two small puncture marks that a doctor found to be exact copies of each other. The man, Mario Woods, tells Michels that a physician he calls Dr Margo, who examined him on St. Simons Island, Georgia, around 2019, found the marks to be \"exactly the same depth and the same size\". He tells it as physical evidence that something was done to him. The researcher Robert Hastings had already written about the marks. His article of September 10, 2017, about a former Air Force missile security policeman's apparent abduction at a nuclear missile site, quotes the man. \"I have two small puncture marks on my skin that I can't explain,\" he says. \"They're identical; a perfectly round depression with a raised dimple in the center.\" The doctor's findings come only from Woods' telling on the show.","material-found-buried-deep-in-rocks-at-the-crash-site-2016":"In 2016, at a site in New Mexico said to be where a UFO crashed, a small team found material buried deep inside rocks that looked about a hundred years old. A religious studies scholar named Diana Pasulka, who says she was there, tells Michels the things \"weren't easy to find,\" and that if anyone had planted them they had been put very far down, which would argue against a recent plant.","materials-scientist-the-metal-acts-like-an-animal-seeking-co-2020":"A materials scientist is said to have described a strange metal as acting \"much like an animal,\" in that it \"is always seeking for energy and seeking to connect.\" Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had repeated UFO encounters, tells Michels these were the words he heard, in a setting linked to Silicon Valley and NASA in the 2020s. If true, it would describe a material that behaves almost like something alive.","mathematician-sees-dmt-entities-solving-the-four-color-theor":"A mathematician who took DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, says he saw beings in that state solve the four color theorem, a famous math problem about coloring maps, \"instantaneously like less than a second\", and do it over and over. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore tells this on the show, passing on what a mathematician he calls Andress told him. Gallimore offers it as a sign that the beings people meet on DMT seem to have minds far beyond ours.","matt-bell-funds-robots-to-drill-the-queen-s-chamber-northern-2026":"Matt Bell, from a podcast called Limitless, is said to be paying for robots that will explore the narrow shafts leading off the Queen's Chamber in the Great Pyramid, including drilling a door in the northern shaft, in 2026. The researcher Jeffrey Drum tells Michels this without naming a source, and also mentions an earlier explorer of the shafts named Gantenbrink. It matters to him because it could finally show what lies behind the door.","matt-gaetz-constituent-describes-similar-psionic-human-cargo":"A constituent of Congressman Matt Gaetz is said to have come into his office with an account very like one Michels was discussing, about humans with psychic powers being moved as cargo. Michels says Gaetz told the story himself during a Twitter Space, a live audio chat on Twitter, and brings it up as backing for a similar claim. A related story has a military officer briefing Gaetz on programmes to breed human and alien hybrids.","matthew-bevan-s-1990s-hacks":"A British hacker named Matthew Bevan broke into US government computer systems in the 1990s, and the story places the US Department of Energy among them. Gary McKinnon, a hacker who says he searched NASA's systems for signs of a secret space programme, tells Michels that Bevan came before him, in the 90s.","matthew-brown-amentum-hangars-at-pax-river-house-anomalous-m":"Hangars at Naval Air Station Patuxent River, a Navy base in Maryland, run by the contractor Amentum are said to hold \"some anomalous stuff\", according to the researcher UAPGerb, who says he is passing on what Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official turned whistleblower, has said. Brown's own words come from a post on X on 6 June 2025, addressed to Elon Musk, telling him that with \"all the clearances\" he should have no trouble getting a tour of \"the special project hangars owned by @amentum_corp (and other fun spaces)\", and adding \"Make sure to look up!\" The post does not say what is in the hangars: that they hold anomalous material is how the tellers read it, and if so, it would mean such material sits at a working Navy base.","matthew-brown-discovers-immaculate-constellation-on-intellin-2020":"Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official, is said to have found a document called Immaculate Constellation, about UFOs that move between air and water, wrongly uploaded to a shared government computer network called IntelLink. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels about the misplaced upload, and Michels, crediting Corbell and the journalist George Knapp, says the document talks about \"transmedium objects, UFOs going in and out of the water,\" which would mean the government tracks such craft. The report itself is unsigned: the writer Michael Shellenberger gave it to Congress, Representative Nancy Mace entered it into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as coming from the Pentagon, Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. It speaks of the capacity \"to detect, track, identify, and engage anomalous transmedium platforms,\" which matches what Michels says, but it says only that its author \"obtained access to this information while pursuing their lawful duties as an employee of the Department of Defense,\" so the shared server and the misplaced upload come from Corbell's account.","matthew-brown-prison-planet-humans-captured-by-beings-2025":"There is a god, according to a former government official named Matthew Brown, but Earth is a kind of \"prison planet\" and humans are, in a sense, captured by certain beings. Michels relays this as what Brown told him. If true, it would mean humanity is being held, not just watched, by others.","matthew-brown-s-view-of-humans-as-a-resource-for-nhi-2020":"A man named Matthew Brown is said to have suggested that people may be a resource for NHI, short for non-human intelligence, meaning whatever beings might lie behind the phenomenon. Michels raises it twice: once with his guest Jeremy Corbell, recalling that Brown said it near the end of Corbell's interview with him, and again later, when he adds that the interview ran on a show called Weaponized and that Brown was smeared by the Office of the Director of National Intelligence. If true, the remark would mean that whatever is visiting treats humanity less as a neighbour than as something to be used.","matthew-brown-s-wmd-corruption-whistleblowing-2020":"Matthew Brown, a State Department official whose specialty was weapons of mass destruction, blew the whistle on corruption tied to those weapons, on top of his better-known UFO claims. Jeremy Corbell, a UFO filmmaker and researcher, says this on the show, describing it as one of several things Brown reported. He brings it up to show that Brown had raised serious alarms before.","matthew-brown-snuck-into-secret-congressional-briefing-2024":"A man named Jeremy Corbell says that sometime between 2024 and 2026 in Washington he \"snuck\" a man named Matthew Brown into a secret briefing with three members of Congress, who all said yes to Corbell's request. Corbell tells Michels he set it up himself, which would mean Brown's account reached lawmakers behind closed doors.","matthew-brown-uncovers-immaculate-constellation-tied-to-lue-2024":"Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official, uncovered a secret program called Immaculate Constellation, and Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who went public about its UFO studies, had his name tied to it, says Michels, who adds that Elizondo, testifying beside the journalist Michael Shellenberger, would only say he could not confirm or deny whether he had been \"read in,\" that is, briefed into it. The program is known from an unsigned report that Shellenberger delivered to Congress and that Rep. Nancy Mace entered into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a Pentagon document; it calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. The report says the program was \"established following the public disclosure of the AATIP/AAWSAP programs by Luis Elizondo in 2017,\" those being small Pentagon efforts that studied unexplained aerial reports, and that internal records \"confirm the claims of Mr. Lue Elizondo\" about them. So the report links Elizondo to the program as the disclosure that preceded it; whether he had any role inside it, it neither says nor rules out.","matthew-brown-white-house-ai-timeline-management-system":"The White House is said to have an artificial intelligence that manages timelines, which Michels later describes as a unique AI \"capable of accurately predicting a range of future events.\" Michels passes on the claim from Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who has come forward as a whistleblower, first in passing as \"some AI timeline management system,\" then in his own introduction to an episode with Brown, where it becomes a machine that sees the future.","matthew-sullivan-s-death-and-his-legacy-program-experience-2023":"A man named Matthew Sullivan is said to have worked inside the secret programme that holds recovered UFOs, and to have died not long before he was due to tell Congress about it. A congressman named Eric Burlison says Sullivan \"was in the legacy program, had firsthand experience with craft\". Legacy program is the subject's term for a long-hidden effort to retrieve and study crashed craft. Burlison has this second-hand, and among his sources he mentions the whistleblower David Grusch, a former intelligence officer, and a man named Jake Barber. A UFO journalist named Jeremy Corbell also tells it from what he was told. He says Sullivan \"had direct direct hands-on with the craft\" and was going to testify behind closed doors \"2 weeks about before he died\". Corbell says the FBI is now looking into whether the death was suspicious, and he brings in the Intelligence Community Inspector General and a letter from Congress. The two men date it differently: Burlison around 2023, Corbell 2025 to 2026. If true, a firsthand witness died just before he could go on the record.","maurice-hill-s-jellyfish-shaped-ufo-1973":"A groundskeeper named Maurice Hill had a close encounter with a UFO shaped like a jellyfish, in the Washington DC area around 1973 or 1974. Michels relays it as something Hill himself was heard to say, in a documentary episode.","maurice-hill-witnessed-the-1952-washington-dc-ufo-flyover":"A man named Maurice Hill was living in Washington DC in 1952, during the famous episode when unexplained lights were reported in the sky over the city and the Capitol, and he is said to have witnessed it. A former Pentagon intelligence official named Luis Elizondo introduces Hill on camera and relays his account. It would add a living eyewitness to one of the best-known UFO events in American history.","maury-island-incident-slag-rains-onto-a-boat-1947":"In 1947 a man sitting in a boat in Puget Sound, Washington, near Maury Island, saw what looked like UFOs flying overhead; one seemed to be in trouble and showered his boat with shards of metal or slag. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels the story from his reading, without naming a source, and names Fred Crisman, a man tied to the case. If true, it would be one of the first cases where a UFO left physical material behind.","maussan-sues-the-peruvian-government-for-300-million-2024":"Jaime Maussan, who champions a set of small three-fingered bodies found in Peru that some call alien, says he filed a lawsuit against the Peruvian government for $300 million. A journalist named Pavel, on the same episode, says that once Maussan \"started doing the whole lawsuit stuff\", Peru's Ministry of Culture and government were forced to take the case more seriously. Both tell it to Michels as a way the bodies were pushed onto the official agenda.","max-davis-s-first-skeptical-reaction-to-josiah-s-messages":"Max Davis, who speaks in an episode about the Telepathy Tapes, a documentary about children said to read minds, describes how skeptical he was at first about messages from a boy named Josiah. He says that when she tried to get something from him, it made her cry, \"cuz I'm a skeptic,\" and that this is how it all began.","maxwell-and-epstein-meet-castro-for-permission-to-dive-the-s-2010":"A person he calls Maxwell went to Cuba with Jeffrey Epstein and met Fidel Castro to get permission to dive on an underwater site. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells this on the show as something he has heard, saying the site had earlier dropped out of the news. He tells it as a hint that powerful people took a secret interest in the site.","maxwell-sells-promis-software-to-sandia-1980":"In the 1980s, the British publisher Robert Maxwell is said to have sold Promis, a piece of tracking software, to Sandia Labs, a US national laboratory in New Mexico. A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels this briefly (\"he sold that to Sandia Labs\") and names no source. It matters to him as a way secret software could have reached the heart of US weapons research.","mayan-flood-legend-of-atlan-and-tulan":"The Maya and other peoples of ancient Mexico and Central America told a flood legend about a place they called by two names, Atlan and Tulan. The philosopher Jason Jorjani says so on the show, citing the writer Graham Hancock. He brings it up as a name in an old American flood story that might echo a lost place remembered elsewhere.","mayor-earl-cabell-brother-charles-cabell-of-cia-motorcade-ro-1961":"Michels points out that the mayor of Dallas when President Kennedy was shot, a man named Earl Cabell, had a brother, Charles Cabell, who he says was number two at the CIA at the time of the Bay of Pigs invasion. Michels raises it himself, without a source, on an episode linking Kennedy to UFO secrets, suggesting a family tie between the CIA and the city that set the motorcade route.","mccasland-and-monica-reza-cases-possibly-connected-2010":"Michels says the case of a general named Neil McCasland seems \"eerily possibly connected\" to the case of a woman named Monica Reza. He says this himself, in an episode about why top American scientists are going missing, offering it as a possible link rather than something shown; if the cases are connected, the disappearances might not be isolated.","mccasland-as-pentagon-director-of-special-programs-overseein-2009":"From June 2009 to May 2011, an Air Force officer named Neil McCasland was director of special programs at the Pentagon, in the office of the under secretary of defense for acquisition, technology and logistics. Michels tells this himself and draws on McCasland's official Air Force biography, Pentagon training documents and Wikipedia. Michels points to the job because it put McCasland in charge of special access programs (SAPs), classified projects that only a short list of cleared people may know about, which means he would have been placed to see the Pentagon's most closely held weapons work.","mccasland-buys-a-medical-kit-at-rei-before-vanishing-2026":"Before he vanished in 2026, a man named Neil McCasland went into an REI outdoor store in New Mexico and bought a medical kit. A congressman named Eric Burlison tells Michels this is \"the latest information\" on the case. The detail matters because it hints McCasland may have planned to leave, at a time when his wife was posting online that he had been taken by aliens.","mccasland-delonge-contacts-as-a-disclosure-operation-meant-t-2015":"In 2015 and 2016, a retired Air Force general named Neil McCasland was in contact with the rock musician Tom DeLonge, and the UFO journalist Jeremy Corbell tells Michels this was an \"operation\" meant to get UFO secrets out in time for a Hillary Clinton presidency. Corbell leans on an email published by WikiLeaks and on the TV reporter George Knapp, and stresses that \"op\" is not an insult: \"there were people trying to get [it] out.\" If so, insiders were planning a release of UFO information around the 2016 election.","mccasland-s-elephant-remark-to-delonge-2015":"Around 2015 or 2016, the musician Tom DeLonge, who was then looking into UFO secrets, spoke by phone with Neil McCasland, a senior Air Force figure, and McCasland is said to have told him he was \"worried that you might find a group of people stumbling around an elephant.\" The researcher Jason Simosa recalls the remark on the show and reads it as a sign that many people hold pieces of one large secret without seeing the whole of it.","mccasland-s-wife-tweets-about-alien-abduction-by-a-mother-sh-2026":"After a man named McCasland went missing in New Mexico, his wife posted on Twitter that maybe the best explanation was that an alien had abducted him from a mother ship, \"although no mother ship has been found in the local area.\" Michels tells this himself, as part of a wider discussion of McCasland's disappearance, and reads the post as a strange, half-joking public response from the family.","mcconnell-invites-kirkpatrick-to-senate-interviews-and-the-f-2017":"A Senate staffer on the Armed Services Committee named Kurt McConnell says that between about 2017 and 2022 he regularly invited a Pentagon official named Sean Kirkpatrick to sit in on Senate interviews with Navy pilots and scientists about UFO sightings, and to a briefing from a businessman named Brandon Fugal. McConnell tells Michels this as the one who sent the invitations, which would mean Kirkpatrick heard this testimony firsthand.","mccord-s-letter-to-judge-sirica-1973":"A lawyer named Dan Sheehan says that in 1973 James McCord, one of the men caught in the Watergate break-in, grew angry that he and the others were \"getting sold down the stream\" and wrote a letter to Judge John Sirica, who was hearing the case. Sheehan tells Michels that McCord came \"out of our office,\" placing himself close to the turn that helped crack the Watergate cover-up open.","mcculloch-letters-urging-cia-and-navy-to-check-out-puharich-1940":"Letters in a Northwestern University archive show the scientist Warren S. McCulloch writing to the CIA and the Navy to tell them, in the researcher Greg Malatzi's words, \"you should check this guy\" out: a man named Puharich. Malatzi describes the letters on the show and draws as well on the journalist Annie Jacobsen. If true, it shows the agencies being pointed toward Puharich by a respected scientist.","mccullough-on-the-phone-they-did-it-not-the-whistleblowers-2025":"In 2025, a lawyer and former intelligence watchdog named Chuck McCullough is said to have told people on a phone call, \"Well, they did it. The whistleblowers didn't,\" meaning that blame for something lay with others rather than with the insiders who had come forward. A former Air Force intelligence specialist named Dylan Borland tells this to Michels and names the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell alongside McCullough. Who \"they\" were and what they did, the telling leaves vague, but if true it would clear the whistleblowers of something they had been accused of.","mcdonald-confronts-hynek-over-the-swamp-gas-explanation-1960":"In the 1960s, a physicist named James McDonald is said to have confronted J. Allen Hynek, a scientist who had explained away UFO sightings in Michigan as swamp gas. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source. He calls McDonald \"a serious physicist\" and has him tell Hynek that he had been taking part in a cover-up and that \"what you saw in Michigan wasn't swamp gas.\" An online researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala tells it as the turning point for Hynek. He says McDonald came to him \"super pissed\" and told him \"what you're doing to science is horrible,\" and that this was a big part of why Hynek later changed his stance and came clean. It matters because it would show an official debunker being pushed into taking UFOs seriously.","mcdonald-mocked-in-congressional-hearings-after-the-michigan-1966":"In 1966, after a wave of UFO sightings in Michigan, a scientist named James McDonald was supposedly mocked by a congressman for being into UFOs, and the whole hall laughed at him. A researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala tells Michels this without naming a source. It's told as an example of how serious people who took UFOs seriously were ridiculed in public.","mcdonnell-douglas-parapsychology-lab-1970":"The aerospace company McDonnell Douglas is said to have had a parapsychology programme, research into psychic abilities, at Washington University in St. Louis. Michels tells this himself on several episodes. In one he dates the institute to the 1960s and ties it to James McDonnell, the company's patron, whom he calls close to Bob Jahn, a Princeton dean of engineering who went on to found a Princeton lab that studied anomalies. If true, it would show a major defence firm taking psychic research seriously.","mcdonnell-instructs-staff-to-study-ufos":"James McDonnell, head of the aircraft maker McDonnell Douglas, told his company to study UFOs. The writer Nick Cook, who investigates secret aerospace research, says on the show that \"there are declassified papers to show\" this, though he does not quote them. If true, it would mean a major defense company took UFOs seriously enough to look into them.","mckenna-brothers-weeks-long-mushroom-trips-in-the-amazon-1970":"The brothers Terence McKenna and Dennis McKenna, later known as writers on psychedelics, are said to have taken mushroom trips lasting several weeks in the Amazon in the 1970s, during which they would \"download and channel things,\" meaning receive ideas as if from outside themselves. Michels tells it himself, in passing, as an example of altered states that seem to open contact with something beyond.","mckenna-stone-ape-theory-consciousness-from-mushrooms":"The writer Terence McKenna proposed that human consciousness began when our early ancestors started eating psychedelic mushrooms, an idea known as the \"stoned ape\" theory. Jeffrey Mishlove, a guest with a doctorate, sums it up on the show: \"consciousness itself developed when our primate ancestors discovered these mushrooms.\" Michels gives his own version on an episode about mushrooms. He says McKenna pictured hunter-gatherers in Africa a couple of million years ago following animal dung to track their prey, and so coming across the mushrooms. The writer Graham Hancock names the source, McKenna's book Food of the Gods, as one of the first books of its kind. On another episode Michels suggests the theory could explain why the brain roughly doubled in size between Homo habilis and Homo sapiens. If it were true, a plant would have played a part in making us who we are.","mckinnon-on-why-he-scanned-us-military-networks-2002":"A British computer hacker named Gary McKinnon says that after reading the book of the Disclosure Project, a group led by Steven Greer that gathered testimony about UFO secrecy, he decided to use his \"basic networking skills\" to do \"a light scan on American military networks,\" which he calls \"mad now\" but then \"a playful idea.\" He tells Michels he did it himself, from his girlfriend's aunt's house in London, using blank passwords, around 2000 to 2003 by his various datings, and that he got into the NSA at Fort Meade, the Defense Information Systems Agency, and Pentagon, Army, Navy and Air Force networks. He tells it to explain that he went in looking for the hidden UFO evidence the book described.","mckinnon-s-hard-drives-seized-by-office-of-naval-intelligenc-2002":"A British hacker named Gary McKinnon, who says he found signs of a secret NASA space programme, says that when he was arrested in 2002 all his data went to the Office of Naval Intelligence, which still has his hard drives. He tells Michels he has tried for years to get them back and was told, \"No, it's still an ongoing investigation. You can't touch it.\"","mckinnon-s-left-heel-puncture-experience-in-2006":"In 2006, Gary McKinnon, a computer hacker who says he found signs of a secret NASA space programme, was woken by a sharp pain and found two perfectly circular holes in his left heel, each about five millimetres across. McKinnon tells Michels this himself, though in another telling he dates it to 2005, places it in a first-floor bedsit, and adds that bumps later appeared that he suspected were an implant.","mcminnville-photo-string-revealed-at-french-conference-1950":"The McMinnville photos, two pictures of a disc-shaped object taken by a farm couple in Oregon in 1950, are said to show a string holding the object up, something revealed at a conference in France. The Swedish UFO researcher and archivist Clas Svahn brings this up with Michels in passing, mentioning only the conference a couple of years back and no named source. If true, it would mean one of the most famous UFO photos showed a small model hanging in the air.","mcminnville-trent-photos-1950":"In 1950, at McMinnville, Oregon, a farming couple named Paul and Evelyn Trent produced pictures of a UFO, and Michels, who calls them \"hapless farmers\", says the case can never be finally debunked: it is \"unfalsifiable in either direction\". He tells it himself, without naming a source, while debating a skeptic. In another episode he tells it a little differently, without naming the Trents, grouping McMinnville with a case at Lake Cote as ones where \"we have video\".","mcmoneagle-remote-views-a-submarine-being-built-at-a-white-s-1979":"In 1979, Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer, meaning someone trained to describe distant places using only the mind, was asked to look at a sealed building in a Russian shipyard on the White Sea, and he described a huge new nuclear submarine being built inside. McMoneagle tells it himself: he says it was \"a really unique submarine\", that he called it TK-089, that his report went to the National Security Council, that Robert Gates is part of the story, and that it was confirmed 114 days later. Michels also retells it twice, once dating it to 1978 or 1979, as evidence that remote viewing works.","mcmoneagle-s-cia-mars-remote-viewing-mars-1-million-bc-1984":"In 1984, Joe McMoneagle is said to have done remote viewing for the CIA, which means trying to describe a faraway place or time with the mind alone. Working at the Monroe Institute, a consciousness research center in Virginia, he was given Mars as it was a million years ago, and he described pyramids and dying people who were \"twice our size, 12 feet tall.\" The philosopher Jason Jorjani gives the date and the CIA tasking. McMoneagle tells it firsthand, without either, and Michels adds that the full session transcript, known as Mars 1 Million BC, has been declassified.","mcmoneagle-s-cia-remote-viewing-of-mars-a-million-years-ago-1980":"A former soldier named Joe McMoneagle, who worked as a remote viewer, someone said to describe distant places and times by mind alone, was tasked by the CIA to remote view Mars a million years ago, and he described giant beings there. Michels mentions this in passing in an episode about giants, apparently drawing on what McMoneagle has said on other interviews, including with the podcaster Shawn Ryan. If true, it would mean the CIA used psychics to look at ancient life on another planet.","mcmoneagle-s-jpl-correspondence-and-life-on-mars-as-an-open":"It is an open secret at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena that there was once life on Mars, according to what Joe McMoneagle is said to have told Michels in their exchanges. Michels says McMoneagle was \"basically implying\" this to him rather than stating it outright, and he raises it in an episode with the physicist John Brandenburg about life on Mars.","mcmoneagle-s-legion-of-merit-for-remote-viewing-1970":"Joseph McMoneagle, a remote viewer, meaning someone trained to describe distant places or events by the mind alone, is said to have won the Legion of Merit, a US military award, for more than 200 sessions in which he helped gather intelligence, including on hostage cases and nuclear sites. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with a Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais. If true, it would mean the military found psychic spying useful enough to decorate it.","mcmoneagle-s-pentagon-remote-viewing-of-a-ufo-above-a-nuclea":"Working for the Pentagon, a remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle (someone said to see distant places by mind alone) saw a UFO hovering over a nuclear missile. The researcher Robert Hastings, who studies UFO reports at nuclear bases, tells Michels the story as it came from McMoneagle. If true, it would tie the military's psychic spying to its concern about UFOs at nuclear weapons.","mcmoneagle-tasked-to-remote-view-mars-a-million-years-ago-1980":"In the 1980s, a remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle, someone trained to describe faraway places using only the mind, was given coordinates on Mars and told to view it as it was a million years in the past. Michels tells this himself, saying it comes from the US Army's psychic spy program and is \"now public and declassified.\" He raises it in a talk about whether Mars once had life, because it would mean the government took that question seriously enough to send a psychic to look.","mcmoneagle-tells-jesse-that-humans-may-be-turning-into-the-e-2020":"Humans might be slowly turning into the extraterrestrials, or the beings might be nudging us to become like them. Michels says a man named Joe McMoneagle told him this on a phone call, putting it as \"I think we might be turning into these ETs or something.\" If true, the visitors would be less outsiders than a picture of our own future.","mechanical-watch-stopped-by-the-emitter-1987":"Bob Lazar, a man who says that in the late 1980s he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site in Nevada, is said to have described a device on the craft, called the emitter, that stopped time near it \"like a mechanical watch that just stopped.\" The researcher Luigi Vendittelli tells this on the show after spending two days with Lazar, passing on what Lazar told him.","medications-and-diagnoses-cleared-within-a-year-2010":"A man named Jordan Jose, who says a secret UFO program took him as a child, says that around 2010 in Springville, New York, he came off his medications and was cleared of all his psychological diagnoses within about a year. He tells Michels this himself, saying \"I was released from every single\" diagnosis, which he offers as a sign that what happened to him was real and not illness.","medieval-angel-hierarchy-guiding-stars-and-planets":"In medieval Christian thought, according to the biologist Rupert Sheldrake, every star and planet had \"its own intelligence\": it was \"not only alive but intelligent\" and was steered by its own guiding angel. He traces the idea through the late Greek philosopher Iamblichus, the early Christian writer known as Dionysius the Areopagite, and the medieval theologian Thomas Aquinas, and tells Michels about it from his general reading. He brings it up to show that the idea of a conscious cosmos is an old one.","medieval-paintings-with-ufo-like-objects-in-the-sky":"Some medieval paintings show something in the sky that looks a bit like a UFO. The skeptic Michael Shermer mentions this on the show, waving it off with a \"whatever\", while naming two writers who take such old images seriously, the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée and the religion scholar Jeffrey Kripal. For those who take the paintings seriously, they would mean people were seeing such things centuries ago.","medvedev-april-fool-s-interview-on-russia-s-ufo-file":"Russian leader Dmitry Medvedev is said to have been asked about UFOs in an interview on April Fool's Day, by an interviewer who expected a joke, and to have answered, \"Oh yeah, no, we have an extensive file about this stuff.\" Michels tells it twice, without a source. The second time he guesses Medvedev may have been president at the time and connects it to Russia's nuclear codes, which would suggest the Russian state keeps a serious UFO record.","meeting-a-real-recluse-on-the-second-athos-trip-2005":"A man named Ralph Larson says that on his second trip to Mount Athos, the monks' peninsula in Greece, around 2005, he met \"a real recluse\", a hermit monk, who gave him a book and had a \"tremendous conversation\" with him about the apocalypse. Larson tells Michels this as something he lived himself.","meeting-archbishop-marcinkus-at-the-vatican-bank-1973":"In 1973, at the Vatican Bank, Archbishop Marcinkus is said to have greeted a young American visitor by telling him the Church already knew about him from his boyhood, through \"a lot of our associates in the mafia.\" That visitor was Harold Malmgren, a former adviser to US presidents, and he tells Michels about the meeting himself, naming Pete Peterson in connection with it. If it happened that way, it suggests the Vatican's bank kept track of people through ties to organised crime.","meeting-bob-lazar-and-george-knapp-at-the-start-of-the-proje-2017":"The writer Peter Levenda says he met Bob Lazar, a man known for claiming he worked on alien craft for the government, for the first time when \"this whole project\" began, sometime around 2017 to 2019. Levenda says he met George Knapp, a Las Vegas journalist, at the same time. He mentions it in passing as something he took part in and does not say what the project was.","meeting-edward-teller-before-his-los-alamos-lecture-1982":"In 1982, a young man working at Los Alamos National Laboratory in New Mexico says he met the physicist Edward Teller on the day Teller came to give a speech there. That man is Bob Lazar, who later said he worked on alien craft, and he tells Michels he was there himself: the local paper had just run a front-page story on a jet car he had built, and by coincidence Teller was speaking at Los Alamos the same day. If true, it ties Lazar to one of the best-known names in American weapons science.","meeting-eric-davis-hal-puthoff-and-sonny-white-at-a-fusion-c":"Years ago, as a graduate student working on pulsed fusion research, Michels' co-host met three men at a fusion conference where he had come to present his work: the physicists Eric Davis and Hal Puthoff, and the engineer Harold \"Sonny\" White. The co-host goes only by the name Jack and has spent years working in defense and aerospace. He brings up the meeting in passing on the show, as the moment he was first introduced to the three.","meeting-jeffrey-epstein-a-constructed-person":"The researcher Eric Weinstein says he met the financier Jeffrey Epstein only once, for about an hour, and found him \"an absolutely terrifying person to encounter,\" a man who seemed to him constructed rather than real. Weinstein tells Michels this from his own memory of that meeting. He offers it as a hint that Epstein may have been a front built by someone else.","meeting-john-lear-through-gene-huff-s-appraisal-1980":"A man named Bob Lazar says he first met a man named John Lear in Las Vegas in the 1980s by tagging along on a house appraisal: his friend Gene Huff was hired to appraise Lear's house, and Lazar went with him. Lazar tells Michels this himself, as how he came into Lear's circle. In his words: \"he wound up doing the appraisal on his house and I went with Gene.\"","meeting-lab-partner-barry-earlier-teams-were-already-at-ease-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft in 1988 and 1989 at S-4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, says the teams who came before him were already completely at ease around those craft. He tells Michels this as something he saw himself when he met his lab partner there, a man he calls only Barry, and says the earlier workers \"had reached a point where it was nonchalant.\" If true, it would mean the work of reverse engineering, taking the craft apart to learn how they work, had gone on long enough to become routine.","meeting-spielberg-about-a-video-game-in-the-middle-of-the-di-1996":"In 1996, in the middle of a dispute, the screenwriter Bryce Zabel went to Amblin, Steven Spielberg's company in Los Angeles, to talk about a video game. Zabel tells Michels he sat waiting in Spielberg's office until Spielberg walked in, shook his hand and said, \"Great to see you again.\"","meeting-steven-greer-at-a-boulder-book-signing-2018":"At a 2018 book signing in Boulder, Colorado, Steven Greer, a well-known figure in UFO circles, is said to have stood up, handed a man his card and told him, \"Don't tell anybody this information.\" The man was Michael Herrera, who says he once saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle, and he tells Michels about the meeting himself. To Herrera it showed that Greer took his account seriously.","meeting-the-bali-bombing-leader-who-believed-the-protocols-2002":"Peter Levenda, an author who writes about secret history, says he met in Indonesia the man who led the 2002 Bali bombings, and that the man believed The Protocols of the Elders of Zion, a text that claims to reveal a Jewish plan for world domination. Levenda tells Michels this from his own experience, to show that such ideas are 'there' and still drive people to act.","meeting-the-virgin-mary-in-a-paris-hotel-on-a-cia-work-trip-1998":"In 1998, in a Paris hotel, a CIA officer says the Virgin Mary appeared to him, floating above the floor. He is Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former senior CIA officer, and he tells Michels first-hand that he was in Paris \"on a work trip for CIA,\" doing \"Russian things.\" After a dream that came first, he says, \"right across from me\" and \"off the ground was the full image of the Virgin Mary,\" and she told him to write to the Pope. Coming back to it later in the same conversation, he adds what he had forgotten to say at first: at the end, Mary told him, \"We will meet again.\" He also brings up the writer Thomas Merton as he tells it. For him it would mean a real spiritual visitation broke into the life of an intelligence officer at work.","meeting-whitley-strieber-in-politically-incorrect-green-room-1990":"The skeptic Michael Shermer says that in the 1990s he met Whitley Strieber, author of Communion, a book about Strieber's own claimed encounters with alien beings, in the green room of Bill Maher's TV show Politically Incorrect. He tells Michels about it while debating UFOs, alongside mention of Budd Hopkins, a well-known researcher of abduction claims, as a first-hand glimpse of the abduction writers he has argued against.","meeting-with-grusch-while-he-was-undercover-2020":"A guest named Steven Greer says that in the years before 2023 he met David Grusch, who later went public with claims about hidden UFO programmes, while Grusch was still \"an undercover operative\" whom \"no one knew\". Greer adds that Grusch's bosses were the people he was passing information to. He tells Michels this as something he took part in, and if it is true, he was feeding information to the same people Grusch worked for before Grusch ever spoke out.","megalithic-architecture-on-mars-seen-by-remote-viewers":"Two remote viewers, Joe McMoneagle and Ingo Swann (men said to see distant places by mind alone), saw buildings on Mars in a style that matches the most \"titanic megalithic structures on Earth\", the giant ancient stone works. The philosopher Jason Jorjani relays this on the show, also citing a man named Karl Wolfe. If true, it would suggest a link between builders on Mars and on Earth.","meidum-pyramid-as-collapsed-test-structure":"The pyramid at Meidum, near the Faiyum oasis in Egypt, was built as a test structure to model the same physics and chemistry as the Red Pyramid, and it failed in a catastrophic collapse. That is the account of Geoffrey Drumm, a pyramid researcher, who tells it to Michels without naming a source. It fits his view that the pyramids were machines, built by trial and error, rather than tombs.","meier-introducing-nordics-to-arab-leaders-1970":"Billy Meier, a Swiss man who says he has been in contact with aliens, is said to have worked as a spy in the Middle East in the 1970s and to have introduced Arab leaders to \"Nordics,\" tall, very good-looking aliens who look human. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this from the people who worked with Meier in those years, crediting the book Closer Encounters. If true, it would mean human-looking visitors were meeting heads of state in secret.","melissa-s-ball-of-light-from-john-mack-s-abduction":"A woman called Melissa, in her early twenties, saw a ball of light come through the sliding doors of her apartment, bounce around the room, go down the hall and into another room. The account comes from Abduction, a 1994 book by the researcher John Mack, who interviewed people who said they had met beings not from here. Jesse Michels retells it from the book, setting this modern case beside older religious visions to suggest they may be the same kind of experience.","memory-floods-back-two-weeks-later-lost-time-1964":"A guard at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California, Richard Barth, says that in 1964 the whole memory of a strange night came flooding back to him about two weeks after it happened. He tells Michels he lay paralyzed, then woke and \"got flooded with this whole experience,\" and was sure \"it wasn't a dream.\" He describes lost time, a gap in his memory that he puts from about 2 a.m. until dawn, and asks what went on in it. In another part of the conversation he adds that about 20 years later a friend turned down regression hypnosis, a kind of hypnosis meant to bring back buried memories, and he names a man called Lonnie and Robert Hastings in connection with the account. If his memory is right, something happened to a guard of nuclear missiles that he was not meant to remember.","men-from-a-twin-study-institute-try-to-take-the-twins-1957":"Sometime around 1957 to 1959, near Allentown, Pennsylvania, two men came to the Weiner family home and asked to speak with the parents. They said they were from an institute that studied twins and wanted to take the twin boys, Jim and Jack Weiner, away to study them. Jim Weiner, who later says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels this from memory, as an early sign that someone was interested in the brothers.","men-in-black-at-point-pleasant-during-keel-s-mothman-investi-1966":"In 1966 and 1967, while the writer John Keel was in Point Pleasant, West Virginia, looking into the Mothman, a winged creature locals said they had seen, men in black would suddenly turn up outside the local newspaper office. Men in black are strange, formally dressed visitors said to appear around UFO witnesses. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this from Keel's account as he knows it, and if true it would mean someone was watching the investigation.","men-in-black-confront-carlos-de-souza-at-gas-station-1996":"In 1996, near the Brazilian town of Varginha, where witnesses say a live creature was captured and taken to hospital, men in an unmarked vehicle are said to have pulled up at a gas station and warned a man named Carlos de Souza to keep quiet. De Souza tells Michels himself that one of them \"pointed a finger straight in my face\" and said, \"You didn't see anything. You don't know anything.\" James Fox, who has looked into the case, passes on what de Souza told him, but says only that an unmarked vehicle showed up whose occupants knew who de Souza was. If true, someone was moving fast to silence witnesses.","men-in-black-interrogation-about-the-control-panel-at-dia-1987":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, says that in the late 1980s at the Defense Intelligence Agency he was questioned by men he calls Men in Black, the mysterious suited agents of UFO lore, about a control panel he had described. He tells Michels that when he finished explaining it, \"the guy over to the side slapped his leg and said, 'So that's it,'\" which suggests they had been trying to work out what the panel was. He mentions a man named Ed Dames in telling it.","men-in-black-on-ashaway-road-1990":"In 1990, around the time of the first Gulf War, Peter Levenda, whom the episode presents as a CIA historian, says he had a run-in with Men in Black, the strangers in dark suits who in UFO lore turn up to watch or warn people, on Ashaway Road in Rhode Island, where he lived. He tells Michels it happened to him, and he brings up the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun as he tells it, though what the men did stays vague in what he gives here.","men-in-black-seen-by-trick-or-treaters-1983":"In the mid-1980s in southeast New York, two strange men in 1920s outfits are said to have come up to children out trick-or-treating and told them that if they saw something weird, they should \"just close your eyes and tell it to land.\" A NASA engineer named Charles Buhler passes this on as what he was told, and it fits the UFO lore of \"men in black\", odd men in dark clothes said to visit witnesses. If true, it would mean someone was preparing ordinary people, even children, for close encounters.","men-in-suits-arrive-by-helicopter-and-take-the-princeton-s-r-2004":"In 2004, after the Navy cruiser USS Princeton tracked strange objects in the Pacific, men in suits came aboard by helicopter and took all the radar data. Marik von Rennenkampff, a commentator on the UFO debate, tells this on the show, saying he heard it from sailors on the ship, among them a sailor named Gary Voorhis. If true, someone moved quickly to take the evidence away.","men-in-suits-visit-puharich-at-intelectron-the-day-jfk-was-s-1963":"On the day President Kennedy was shot in 1963, men in suits are said to have walked into the New York offices of Intelectron, a company linked to the researcher Puharich, and gone straight into Puharich's office. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels he heard this from a woman named Melanie, who says she was working at Intelectron that day and saw them. Puharich is the scientist the episode describes as tied to the CIA and as having contacted an alien council.","menzel-deletes-harvard-observatory-data-1953":"The Harvard astronomer Donald Menzel deleted a large body of astronomical records from the Harvard Observatory, starting in 1953. Michels tells this from the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, whom he calls Swedish and says has spoken about it. If true, records of the sky from those years would have been lost or hidden on purpose.","menzel-protege-lewis-branscomb-restores-condon-s-clearance-c-1960":"In the 1960s, Michels says, Lewis Branscomb, a protégé of the scientist Donald Menzel, gave the physicist Edward Condon his security clearance back, and Condon went on to lead the Condon Committee, a study of UFOs in the late 1960s that Michels calls \"a hit job on UFOs\" and which, he says, killed Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with an astronomer who tracks UFOs; the suggestion is that the official end of UFO study was arranged rather than earned.","menzel-s-name-in-the-majestic-12-documents-1950":"A scientist named Donald Menzel is named in the Majestic 12 papers, typed documents that describe a secret group of twelve said to have been set up under Truman to deal with UFOs. If the papers are genuine, a leading scientist sat on a hidden government UFO panel. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel brings this up on the show without naming a source: look into Menzel, she says, and \"suddenly you find that his name is famous\" for these papers. Michels, in his own framing, says Menzel was named \"as part of Truman's top secret UFO research panel in the late 1940s.\" The papers came to light on a roll of 35mm film that a man named Jaime Shandera received anonymously at his Burbank home in 1984 and then studied with William Moore and Stanton Friedman. They were made public in 1987. The main paper presents itself as a briefing for Eisenhower dated 18 November 1952, with a 1947 Truman memo attached. The National Archives and the Truman and Eisenhower Libraries report finding no copy, the FBI's copies are marked \"BOGUS\" by hand, and an Air Force investigations office told the FBI it was \"fabricated\". The critic Philip Klass called the Truman memo a fraud, while Friedman judged both documents \"almost certainly genuine\". The briefing paper does list \"Dr. Donald Menzel\" among twelve members of a group it says Truman set up in September 1947, which fits Michels's late 1940s, and it says \"some scientists, most notably Dr. Menzel, consider it more likely that we are dealing with beings from another solar system entirely.\" So both tellers report the naming as the paper makes it, and whether Menzel really served on such a panel depends on whether the paper is genuine.","menzel-stops-the-harvard-sky-survey-in-1953":"In 1953, according to the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, the Harvard astronomer Donald Menzel stopped his colleagues from carrying on their famous sky survey, a photographic watch over the whole sky that had run since the late 19th century. She tells Michels about it on two episodes, and the second time she adds that the survey stayed stopped from 1953 to 1967, roughly 15 years. If that is so, a gap opened in the record of the sky just as UFO reports were peaking.","menzel-the-objects-are-not-unidentified-1950":"In the 1950s the astronomer Donald Menzel said flatly that UFOs were already explained: \"First of all, the objects are not unidentified. We know what they are.\" The line is heard on the show in Menzel's own words, as an example of how scientists of that era dismissed the reports.","merkavah-mystics-chariot-practice-on-yom-kippur":"Every year on Yom Kippur, certain groups of Jewish mystics \"descend the chariot\", the phrase used in Merkavah mysticism, a tradition built around visions of God's heavenly chariot, for the journey into visionary realms. The writer on occult history Peter Levenda says this on the show, without naming a source. He brings it up to argue that such a visionary journey is really possible.","message-board-post-claiming-puharich-never-died-1995":"Around 1995, someone on an online message board claimed to know for a fact that Puharich, the scientist this episode is about, never died, and that the government had taken him in instead. An unnamed researcher tells Michels that they saw the post, quoting it as \"I know for a fact that Puharich never died and he was taken in by the government.\" If it were true, Puharich's official end would be a cover for him going quietly into government work.","message-do-not-fear-mario-woods-heard-from-the-beings-1960":"During his encounter at a South Dakota nuclear missile site, the former Air Force missile security guard Mario Woods says he heard voices in his head telling him \"do not fear, do not fear\" again and again. Woods tells this himself, as something that happened to him, across several conversations with Michels. In one he describes the message moving \"through my body like a vibration like a hiss, a telepathic\" one. In another he says he recognized the voice and remarks on its accent. Elsewhere he places it at Ellsworth Air Force Base and, in one telling, in 1977 at the launch site called November 5. The journalist Robert Hastings, who writes about UFOs at nuclear sites, gave the same detail in a 2017 article built on Woods's account, quoting him: \"I saw something like shadows on the right side of the vehicle and I have a memory of voices in my head, more than one, saying, 'Do not fear', over and over.\" If true, it would mean whatever was there spoke straight into a guard's mind.","messengers-of-deception-mass-appearance-event-and-blossoming-1979":"If UFOs suddenly appeared all over the world in one mass event, UFO cults could quickly grow while trust in government collapses. That is the warning in Messengers of Deception, a book published in 1979 by Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and longtime UFO researcher, as a researcher named Jason Simosa summarises it for Michels. It matters because it would mean the UFO phenomenon could be used, or could act on its own, to reshape belief and power.","messerschmitt-s-1952-interview-with-a-french-paper":"The German aircraft designer Messerschmitt is said to have told a French newspaper in 1952 that if people saw UFOs in the sky, they were probably craft he had designed, built through a German flying-disc effort Michels calls the Habermohl project and at the Škoda Works, an arms plant, under the Nazi weapons chief Hans Kammler. Michels tells this himself and does not name the paper. If true, some UFO sightings would trace back to secret wartime German aircraft.","metal-bb-blown-out-of-nose-1964":"In 1964, a man says he had a sinus problem, blew his nose, and a tiny metal ball like a BB came out. A witness named Richard Barth tells Michels this happened to him: \"a BB came out, little round ball with a flat side on it.\" He tells it on an episode about a nuclear missile guard who says he was abducted. A researcher on the same episode, who is not named, describes it as not a copper BB but \"chrome plated or something.\" That speaker places it years after childhood and suggests it was an implant, meaning an object some say abductors put inside people's bodies. If so, the ball would be a physical leftover of an abduction.","metal-detector-search-finds-the-crash-site-upturned-2025":"In 2025, a metal detector search at a spot near Varginha, Brazil, said to be where a craft came down in 1996, found the ground already dug over. James Fox was there and tells Michels that a companion turned to him and said, \"Man, this whole area has been all upturned.\" To Fox it suggests someone had already combed the site for debris.","metal-factory-worker-hears-radio-station-wor-through-metal-d-1950":"The story goes that a man who said he heard voices turned out to be picking up a radio station through fine metal dust lodged in his teeth. It is linked to Andrija Puharich, a researcher of the paranormal who ran a lab in New York in the 1950s and 60s. A researcher named Greg Malatzi, drawing on the writer Annie Jacobsen, calls it Puharich's \"first aha moment\": \"they got a mental patient, a guy who claimed he was hearing voices.\" The station is identified as New York's WOR. Michels brings it up himself in three other episodes, with details that shift: the man worked in \"some metal sawmill or something\" or \"a metal mill,\" and \"metallic ore got in his teeth.\" Once Michels says he got it from a documentary on Puharich and ties it to the idea of sending thoughts by ELF, extremely low frequency radio waves. None of Michels's versions names the station. It is told because, if true, it would show the human body acting as a receiver for signals, which feeds ideas about transmitting thought.","metal-found-near-the-serpent-mound-area-of-activity":"A piece of strange metal was found near the Serpent Mound, an ancient snake-shaped earthwork in Ohio built by Native Americans, an area where unusual things are said to happen. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him, tells this on the show, citing a man named Timothy Taylor. Bledsoe points to the location as part of what makes the metal significant.","metal-ore-veins-with-rare-earths-gold-and-platinum-beneath-t":"The claim is that the bedrock beneath the pyramids at Giza in Egypt is laced with veins of iron ore that also hold precious metals and rare earth elements, a group of metals used in modern electronics. The researcher Jeffrey Drum (whose name also appears as Geoffrey Drumm) argues that the pyramids were built as machines for making energy. He tells Michels there is 'chemical analysis data from these iron veins,' mineral deposits left by hot water and 'embedded in the bedrock,' that are 'permeated with rare earth elements, gold, silver.' Later in the same interview he says the samples 'also contain gold, silver, electrum,' a natural mix of gold and silver, 'everything that you could think of.' In a later episode he speaks only of 'valuable metal deposits.' He does not say who did the analysis. The story's title adds platinum, but none of the quoted words name it. For Drum, the metals in the rock help explain why the pyramids were built where they are.","metaverse-as-answer-to-the-fermi-paradox-2021":"When the metaverse, a planned online virtual world, was announced in 2021, the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb says he realised it might solve the Fermi paradox, the physicist Enrico Fermi's question of why we see no sign of aliens. Loeb tells Michels this as his own idea. The thought is that advanced civilisations might retreat into virtual worlds and stop reaching out into space.","methane-detected-in-the-martian-atmosphere-2003":"In 2003, methane was detected in the atmosphere of Mars, Michels says, and he treats that as making past or present life there \"a distinct possibility.\" He brings it up himself, without naming a source, in an episode with Joe McMoneagle, a former soldier who says he described ancient Mars by remote viewing, a claimed psychic way of seeing distant places.","mexican-congress-hearing-on-peruvian-alien-mummies-2023":"In 2023 the Mexican Congress in Mexico City is said to have held a session about mummies from Peru that were presented as alien. Michels mentions it only in passing, as having happened \"the next day\" after another event, and calls them \"alien mummies or whatever.\" He gives no more detail about what was shown.","mexico-city-bank-money-in-bernard-barker-s-pocket-traced-to-1960":"The bills found in the pocket of Bernard Barker, one of the men arrested in the 1972 Watergate break-in, came out of a Mexico City bank account belonging to a lawyer Sheehan calls 'Agario', and that money traces back to cash skimmed from casinos. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, and ties it to the New Orleans mob boss Carlos Marcello and, in his telling, to an assassination. If true, it would connect Watergate money to organised crime money.","mh370-orb-videos-and-ashton-forbes-s-teleportation-claim-2014":"Videos tied to MH370, the Malaysian airliner that vanished in 2014 in a story CNN covered for three months, are said to show three orbs circling the plane, and a researcher named Ashton Forbes claims they are man-made, fusion-powered plasma orbs that opened a spinning wormhole over the Nicobar Islands and teleported the plane westward, toward Diego Garcia. Michels retells Forbes's claim, which Forbes made on Twitter around 2024, across several episodes; if true, it would mean someone already has working teleportation technology.","mh370-videos-teaser-2014":"Videos that circulate online are said to show glowing orbs around MH370, the Malaysian airliner that vanished over Southeast Asia in 2014, just before the plane disappears. Michels brings them up with Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist who holds patents for craft like UFOs, and Pais says that when he saw \"that thing open up\" he saw what he calls the \"Pais effect\", a physics idea of his own. If true, it would mean the plane was taken by technology that works the way Pais's theory describes.","mia-s-diary-says-she-can-read-minds-then-home-tests-during-c-2020":"A girl named Mia in Monterrey, Mexico, wrote in her diary that she could read her parents' minds, and in fact everybody's, and during COVID she was tested at home. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made the Telepathy Tapes documentary about children said to read minds, tells Michels this as the story came to her, with Diane Powell, a psychiatrist who studies such cases, also named. If true, it would mean a child can know what others are thinking.","mica-under-the-teotihuacan-pyramids-as-technology":"A guest named Jim Weiner asks Michels why huge sheets of mica, a thin, flaky mineral, were carried to the Pyramid of the Sun at Teotihuacan in Mexico from two to three thousand miles away in South America. He suggests the mica must have been there to do something, as a kind of technology. He offers it as his own question and names no source.","mice-inheriting-fear-of-acetophenone":"Mice taught to fear the smell of a chemical called acetophenone passed that fear on to their offspring, according to a study done at a US university that Rupert Sheldrake, a scientist known for challenging mainstream biology, describes on the show. Sheldrake recalls that when the work was published in the journal Nature, its title was \"inheriting the fear of fathers.\" If it holds, a memory learned in life would pass down to the next generation.","michael-aquino-s-ceremony-at-wewelsburg-castle-1980":"Sometime in the 1980s, a man named Michael Aquino is said to have held \"some sort of satanic ceremony\" at Wewelsburg Castle in Germany, the castle the Nazi SS used as its own. The researcher and author Peter Levenda tells Michels this in passing, without naming a source beyond the book Unholy Alliance. If true, it would tie a modern occult figure to a ritual site of the Nazis.","michael-aquino-says-mj-meant-non-human-biologics-in-space-co":"The letters MJ, best known from the MJ-12 documents, papers of disputed origin about a secret group said to handle crashed saucers, are said to have been used inside US Space Command, the military's command for space, as a label for non-human biological beings. Peter Levenda, a writer on the occult and on the history of the intelligence agencies, tells Michels that a man named Michael Aquino said this to him, quoting him as saying, \"Oh yeah, MJ... that was in Space Command. We use that for non-aterrestrial biologics.\" If true, the MJ label points to a real military term for alien bodies.","michael-collins-said-no-stars-visible-contradicted-in-his-bo-1969":"At the press conference after Apollo 11 in 1969, the astronaut Michael Collins said clearly that the crew could not see any stars, and according to Michels, Collins's own book later tells it differently. Michels brings this up himself, pointing to the press conference and to Collins's book, as one of several odd details about the Moon missions that he says don't add up.","michael-confirms-the-ufo-in-the-first-phone-call-1977":"A former Air Force man named Michael Johnson says that when he first got back in touch by phone with Mario Woods, a fellow veteran, he confirmed seeing the UFO they both say appeared in 1977 at a nuclear missile site called November 5, near Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota. Johnson tells it himself on the show, where the two men reunite, recalling: \"It was big though. I remember. We saw it. It was white.\" It matters because it backs up Woods's memory of the night.","michael-driven-back-to-base-by-a-sergeant-after-the-incident-1977":"Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran who served at the missile fields around Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, says that after the strange incident he lived through there in 1977, his next memory is of being driven back to base. He tells Michels, \"Next thing I know I was riding back with somebody. I was riding back with sergeant,\" a jump in memory that, if true, points to time he cannot account for.","michael-eisner-rejects-the-plasma-fireworks":"Eric Haseltine once showed Michael Eisner, then the head of Disney, a kind of plasma fireworks, and Eisner turned it down on the spot: \"Oh, we're not going to do this.\" Haseltine, who worked at Walt Disney Imagineering, Disney's design and engineering arm, and later became a senior official at the NSA, tells Michels about it as something he was there for.","michael-half-recognizes-doty-from-a-photo-1977":"Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran who says he and his partner were abducted in 1977 while guarding missiles at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, is shown a photo of Richard Doty, a man whose name comes up often in UFO stories, and half-recognizes him. \"I can't picture him, but I see a guy like him,\" Johnson tells Michels. If the memory is real, it would tie Doty to what happened to Johnson after the incident.","michael-herrera-s-2009-indonesia-encounter-with-an-octagonal":"A former Marine named Michael Herrera says that in 2009 his unit, which was supposed to go to the Philippines, was instead sent aboard the ship USS Denver to western Sumatra in Indonesia, where they came across a huge black eight-sided craft, about 300 feet across, sitting in the jungle and guarded by eight American operators in black. He says these men carried advanced rifles, disarmed the Marines, scanned them with a \"bio scanner\", held them at gunpoint and swore them to secrecy, and two of them talked about whether to \"smoke these guys\". Then, he says, trucks were loaded onto a floating platform and the craft \"rose up past the treetops and then it shot off\" toward the sea. Herrera tells this himself, and in one telling he describes a pyramid-like top with vents at the corners. Michels and a researcher who goes by UAPGerb retell it, with Michels guessing the team was a Department of Energy unit and comparing the craft to a much smaller one a helicopter pilot named Jake Barber says he carried. If true, it would mean a secret human group is flying advanced craft of its own.","michael-in-a-capsule-with-floating-objects-bumping-into-mari-1977":"Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran from the missile fields near Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, says that in 1977 he found himself aboard a craft, in \"another empty space or capsule or something,\" with things \"floating around\" him. He tells Michels he lived it himself, and that he came upon his fellow airman Mario Woods on a table there. If true, two servicemen at a nuclear missile site were taken aboard the same craft.","michael-is-put-in-a-machine-like-a-shower-stall-1977":"In 1977, at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, an Air Force airman named Michael Johnson was put inside a machine that he says was \"like a shower stall.\" Johnson is one of several Air Force veterans who say they were abducted by aliens while stationed there. He tells Michels about it as part of what he remembers of that experience.","michael-johnson-s-visit-and-matching-drawings-1977":"In 1977, after a frightening night at a nuclear missile site in South Dakota, a fellow airman named Michael Johnson is said to have come to Mario Woods's apartment in Rapid City, and the two found that their memories and drawings of the night matched. Woods, a former Air Force missile security guard who says he was abducted that night, tells Michels that Johnson saw it all. In his words, Johnson saw \"the craft\" and \"the black sphere in front of the vehicle jumping up and down\", \"smelled the smell\" and heard \"do not fear\". Johnson tells his side on the show years later, when the two reunite. He remembers saying, \"I saw something. I want to write it down before I forget.\" Woods also told the story in a written account published by Robert Hastings, a researcher who writes about UFOs at nuclear bases, on 10 September 2017. There Woods says that \"two weeks went by\" before a sergeant, whose name is blacked out, \"came by my apartment, out of the blue\", and they talked it over \"in front of my wife\". He wrote that the man \"had been terrified\" but did not know why, \"as he had heard voices, many voices, saying not to be afraid\". If true, it would mean two men separately remembered the same craft and the same message.","michael-johnson-vanishes-with-no-records-1977":"In 1977 in Rapid City, South Dakota, an Air Force man named Michael Johnson is said to have vanished from the life of his partner, leaving no records behind. The partner, Mario Woods, a former nuclear missile guard who says the two were abducted together, tells Michels that after one last brief exchange, \"that was the last time I ever saw him.\" To Woods, the lack of any paperwork suggests Johnson was deliberately removed.","michael-kratsios-statement-on-manipulating-time-and-space-2025":"In 2025 a Trump administration science official named Michael Kratsios made a public statement that, as Jesse Michels quotes it, said \"Our technologies permit us to manipulate time and space. They leave distance annihilated.\" Michels raises it himself with a guest as a hint that officials may be speaking about technology far beyond what is publicly known.","michael-levin-frog-embryo-in-a-faraday-cage":"Michels says that, according to the Tufts biologist Michael Levin, a frog embryo placed in a Faraday cage, a metal enclosure that blocks electromagnetic fields, will not grow correctly. On another episode he adds that an embryo placed next to a Wi-Fi router also fails to grow normally. He repeats it as something from Levin's work, as a sign that living things depend on electrical fields to take their shape.","michael-levin-s-planaria-regeneration-and-bioelectric-experi":"A biologist named Mike Levin has done experiments on planaria, small flatworms that reproduce by cutting themselves in half and regrowing the missing parts, and has studied the electrical signals in their bodies that guide this regrowth. The cognitive scientist Donald Hoffman describes this \"wonderful set of work\" to Michels. He uses it to suggest that living bodies are shaped by something beyond genes alone.","michael-made-to-undress-for-examination-by-base-personnel-1977":"A former Air Force man named Michael Johnson says that after the 1977 incident he went through, base staff at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota made him undress to be examined. He tells Michels he \"had to be in a room with a bunch of men undressed\", and he describes it as a humiliating part of how the base handled what happened.","michael-s-article-15-discharge-and-coded-record-1977":"An Air Force veteran named Michael Johnson says that when he left Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, around 1977 or 1978, he left with an honorable discharge even though he had received an Article 15, a disciplinary punishment handed out by a commander rather than a court. He tells Michels this himself, as part of his own story, remembering that they told him, \"We give you honorable discharge.\" Told this way, the clean discharge after a punishment stands out as an oddity in his service record.","michael-s-fight-with-a-sergeant-at-a-missile-site-1977":"Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran who worked the nuclear missile sites around Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, says that in 1977 he got into a fight with a sergeant at one of the missile sites. He tells Michels he remembers it himself, as one of the pieces of that strange period he is trying to put back together.","michael-s-medical-exam-and-injection-sodium-pentothal-1977":"In 1977, at the medical facility of Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, an Air Force airman named Michael Johnson was examined and given an injection in his vein, which the story identifies as sodium pentothal, a sedative sometimes called a truth serum. Johnson, one of several veterans who say they were abducted by aliens while stationed at the base, tells Michels he only remembers that \"they shot something in my vein.\" Told this way, it would suggest that someone on the base tried to dull or probe the men's memories.","michael-s-own-memory-frozen-at-the-wheel-bright-light-human-1977":"In 1977, at a missile site called November 5 near Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, an Air Force man named Michael Johnson says he sat frozen at the wheel while a big bright light came close and moved away again, and blurred shapes like human figures appeared. Johnson tells Michels this as his own memory of the night he spent there with his partner, Mario Woods: \"I do remember that it come close, it go back out.\" It is his own account of what he and Woods say was an abduction.","michael-s-regression-procedures-on-board-needles-and-a-blade-1977":"A former Air Force man named Michael Johnson says that under regression, a kind of hypnosis meant to bring back buried memories, done with Yvonne Smith, he recalled being aboard a craft in 1977 while beings worked on him with needles and a blade. He tells it himself on the show: \"It starts like slicing my back, cutting my back cold, like a cold blade.\" He also says he feared they wanted to \"take specimens\" or \"insert something in me.\"","michael-s-regression-three-creatures-in-front-of-the-truck-1977":"Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran, says that at the November 5 missile site near Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota in 1977, three creatures stood in front of his truck. He tells Michels the memory came back in a regression, a hypnosis session meant to recover buried memories, which he links to Yvonne Smith, and he says, \"Three creatures I never seen out of this whole world was in front of me.\" If true, beings confronted an airman at a nuclear missile site.","michael-s-right-arm-weak-since-the-abduction-1977":"An Air Force veteran named Michael Johnson, said to have been abducted by aliens in 1977 at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, noticed just after the abduction that his right arm was sore, and it has been weak ever since. Michels tells this himself, narrating an episode in which veterans from that event meet again fifty years later; the sore arm is offered as a physical trace of what happened to him.","michael-s-son-thinks-he-was-dropped-off-at-area-51-1977":"A man named Michael, one of several Air Force veterans who say they were abducted by aliens around 1977, may have been dropped off afterward at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert, or so his son thinks. Michels passes on the son's idea without more detail. If true, it would tie the abduction to the US military.","michael-s-strange-sightings-in-missouri-the-ozarks-and-texas":"A former Air Force airman named Michael Johnson, who says he was abducted by aliens while stationed in South Dakota, tells Michels he has also seen \"strange occurrences\" in Missouri, in the Ozarks and in Texas. He mentions them in passing, without describing what he saw, as a sign that the strange events in his life did not stop with the base.","michael-sees-football-field-sized-octagon-craft-for-human-re":"A man named Michael is said to have seen a huge eight-sided craft, as big as a football field, meant for moving people from one place to another, which would mean someone has plans to relocate humans. A guest who goes by UAPGerb passes this on to Michels as something Michael saw, with no place or date given.","michael-shermer-on-the-white-house-uap-science-advisory-comm-2026":"In a debate with Michael Shermer, a well-known writer and professional skeptic, Michels brings up a White House science advisory committee on UAPs, the official term for UFOs, and suggests it could include someone like an \"Avi\" with connections to people in the government. The passage is fragmentary, and Michels does not spell out who sits on the committee or what it does; he raises it as part of his argument that the government is now taking the subject seriously.","michael-talbot-dies-of-the-leukemia-he-wrote-about-in-his-va-1992":"The writer Michael Talbot, author of The Holographic Universe, is said to have died in 1992 of a rare form of leukemia, after writing a vampire novel in which vampirism was itself a rare form of leukemia. Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD and studies psychic phenomena, tells Michels this first-hand: he visited Talbot in the hospital before his death and calls the coincidence the irony of it.","michael-wonders-who-built-the-pyramids-and-the-english-stand":"Michael Johnson, one of the Air Force veterans on the show who say they were abducted, wonders aloud who really built the pyramids of Egypt and the standing stones of England. \"How this stone got here in England?\" he asks Michels. \"Who did this?\" He offers it as an open question, not an answer, and it hints that someone other than the people we credit may have moved those stones.","michele-sindona-s-money-laundering-operation-1964":"A financier named Michele Sindona is said to have been at the centre of the world's biggest money laundering operation, around 200 billion dollars, with the story dated to 1964. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this, citing the figure to an author named Paul Williams. He also names the CIA's James Jesus Angleton and a Charles Hambro, linking the money to the secret networks behind the UFO cover-up he describes.","michels-s-own-sighting-at-the-start-of-his-show":"Jesse Michels says he has seen something unexplained himself, right around the time he started his show. He mentions it in passing to the treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, who says there is a UFO on the seafloor, and gives no details of what he saw.","michelson-morley-ether-experiments-1890":"Michels brings up the Michelson-Morley experiments, which he places in the 1890s, as a set of optical measurements meant to detect the ether, the invisible medium once thought to carry light. He tells it himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with a scientist named Rupert Sheldrake. He offers it as an example of science testing an idea about the hidden fabric of the world.","michigan-swamp-gas-case":"In a famous Michigan case, sightings were officially put down to swamp gas, glowing gas from marshes, though large disc-shaped objects were also described. The analyst Marik von Rennenkampff brings it up while debating a UFO skeptic on Michels' show, naming it in passing with no source. He cites it as an example of an official explanation that, to him, does not fit what was seen.","mick-s-2017-metabunk-post-calls-the-dietrich-account-laughab":"In December 2017, the skeptic Mick West is said to have dismissed as laughable a Navy pilot's account of a UFO encounter, in a post on Metabunk, his online forum for checking strange claims. The researcher Marik von Rennenkampff puts it to West directly on the show. He reads out West's December 30 post from a forum thread about FLIR1, an infrared Navy video of an unexplained object, written in reply to an interview with Commander Alex Dietrich, a Navy pilot. To the Stars Academy, a private company that studied UFOs, had released that interview. Von Rennenkampff raises it to suggest West brushed off a credible witness too quickly.","mick-s-analysis-flir1-object-is-a-distant-aircraft-and-its-z-2004":"The skeptic researcher Mick West argues that the object in FLIR1, a 2004 Navy infrared video of the Tic Tac (a white oval object shaped like the mint, filmed off Southern California), is a distant aircraft, and that the moment it seems to \"zip off screen\", which made the clip look \"amazing\", is really the camera moving. In the same debate the writer Marik von Rennenkampff sums up West's YouTube analysis as saying the object is probably a distant airliner \"50 to 100 miles away\", though his version leaves out the point about camera movement. If West is right, one of the best-known Navy UFO videos would show an ordinary plane.","mick-west-sees-a-wingless-plane-fuselage-flying-to-la":"Mick West, a well-known UFO skeptic, says that once, on a flight to Los Angeles, he saw what looked like an airplane body with no wings. He tells Michels he has \"seen it myself\", using it as something he witnessed firsthand.","mick-west-tic-tac-video-zoom-change-2004":"The Tic Tac video is Navy footage from 2004, taken near the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz, of a white oval object shaped like the mint. One reading holds that what looks like a change in the object is really the camera zooming: Mick West, as the skeptic Michael Shermer tells Michels, pointed out that the vertical axis of the footage shows the reading \"zoom 1.0.\" If that is right, part of what looks strange in the video comes from the camera, not the object.","mickelsen-safeguard-abm-site-and-the-1975-ufo-wave":"In February 1975, according to the UFO researcher Grant Cameron, the Mickelsen Safeguard site opened near Nekoma, North Dakota. He calls it the only anti-ballistic missile unit, a base built to shoot down incoming nuclear missiles, ever to become operational in the United States. Cameron tells Michels this to tie the base to the wave of UFO sightings over American military sites that same year, and he names no source.","microwave-cannons-used-to-bring-down-craft-jake-barber-north":"Michels says that microwave cannons may be used to bring down unknown craft, and that people linked to a man named Jake Barber, whose story involves \"psionics\" (the claimed use of mental powers to reach such craft), and to the defense contractor Northrop Grumman have \"a lot of those microwave cannons\" in their possession. He tells it himself, without naming a source, which would mean craft are being brought down on purpose with weapons.","mid-1950s-aerospace-propulsion-breakthrough-at-martin-aircra-1953":"In the mid-1950s, the American plane maker Martin Aircraft, and maybe others, made a breakthrough in aircraft propulsion, and said at the time that they could roll these new craft off an assembly line within three years. The philosopher Jason Jorjani says this to Michels as something \"we know\", without naming a source. If true, it would mean advanced flight technology was within reach decades ago and then went out of sight.","mike-masters-abductees-sanitize-before-boarding-craft-time-t":"In many of the cases a researcher named Mike Masters studied, people who say they were abducted report that they had to clean themselves, by showering or doing chemical rinses, before boarding the alien craft. Michels tells this himself, drawing on Masters' work, as part of the idea that the visitors are time travellers, perhaps humans from the future guarding against our germs.","mike-masters-theory-that-ufos-are-time-traveling-humans":"The theory says UFOs are not aliens at all but humans from the future, travelling back in time. It belongs to Mike Masters, whom Michels introduces as an evolutionary biologist at Montana Tech, a university in Montana, and the author of books including Identified Flying Objects and another Michels calls Extempore. Michels brings Masters up while talking with Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official, and asks whether he knows the work. A researcher and author named Eric Wargo picks up the idea on a later episode. He says the real core of the phenomenon \"very likely involves\" time-travelling humans. If true, the beings people report would be our own descendants coming back to look at us.","mike-mcconnell-as-dan-burisch-s-godfather":"A man named Mike McConnell is said to have been the godfather of a man named Dan Burisch, who has a story of his own in this subject. Michels brings it up in passing as one of several \"weird stories\" around Burisch, passing on something he has heard, with a man named Brian Jackson among the names he cites. He gives no detail, but if it were true it would tie Burisch personally to McConnell.","mike-mcconnell-linked-to-s4-area-51-via-dan-burisch-story-1980":"Mike McConnell, who later became director of the NSA, the US eavesdropping agency, was involved in secret work at S4, a site near Area 51 in Nevada where alien craft are said to be studied, according to the story of a man named Dan Burisch who claims to have worked there. Michels says this to Bob Lazar, the man who says he worked on alien craft at S4, guessing that the pilot John Lear may have been talking about McConnell. Michels adds that he thinks Burisch's story \"holds up a lot less\" than Lazar's.","mike-turner-and-aerospace-donors-removed-the-eminent-domain-2023":"Michels says that in 2023 a congressman named Mike Turner, with aerospace donors behind him, may have had an eminent domain provision removed from a UFO bill, a clause that would have let the government take over recovered non-human technology held by private companies. He frames it as his own \"understanding of the behind the scenes, what might have happened,\" and also names Mike Johnson. If true, it would mean defence companies worked to keep control of such material out of public hands.","mike-turner-wright-patterson-donors-and-the-eminent-domain-c-2023":"Michels says that Congressman Mike Turner, who represents the Ohio district home to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, which Michels calls \"the center of all UFO lore and reverse engineering\" (meaning the study of recovered craft to copy their technology) back to Roswell, counts defense contractors such as Boeing and Lockheed among his top donors. Michels raises this himself, without a source, in connection with the 2023 fight in Congress over a disclosure bill and its eminent domain clause, a provision that would have let the government take over privately held material, suggesting the contractors' ties helped explain opposition to it.","milab-abductee-meets-a-nordic-on-a-military-base-by-degrees":"A woman who says she was taken by military personnel as well as by aliens (abductees call this a \"milab\") tells of meeting a \"Nordic,\" a tall, human-looking alien, on a military base. She asked whether the Nordics were involved with the Greys, the small grey aliens of abduction stories, and the answer was \"by degrees.\" The researcher Tim Alberino tells Michels this from the abduction researcher Karla Turner's book Taken. If true, it would mean different kinds of beings are working with each other, and with the military.","military-abductions-of-abductees-after-gray-abductions":"People abducted by grays, the small grey beings of many abduction reports, were then supposedly taken again by the military once they were returned home, and brought to Air Force bases and underground bases. Tim Alberino, a filmmaker and researcher, tells Michels this, citing the abduction writer Karla Turner. If true, it would mean the military knows about the abductions and follows them up itself.","military-base-calls-a-witness-after-the-fox-interview-2021":"The UFO investigator James Fox says that in Brazil, around 2021 or 2022, a military base phoned a witness right after Fox's team had interviewed him on camera. Fox tells Michels he was there, and says the base called \"the guy that we had just met with on camera.\" If true, the military was keeping close track of who was talking to him.","military-convoy-cordons-the-crash-site-and-a-soldier-threate-1996":"In 1996, near the Brazilian town of Varginha, where a craft is said to have crashed and a living creature to have been captured, a witness named Carlos de Souza says he was driving near the crash site when a military convoy came toward his car. He tells Michels on two shows that the army closed off the area, and on the second he adds that a soldier stepped back, \"raised his gun, cocked it, and pointed it at my head.\" If true, it would mean the Brazilian military moved fast to seal the site and keep witnesses away.","military-driver-sees-non-human-creature-at-humanitas-hospita-1996":"In 1996, at the Humanitas Hospital in the Brazilian town of Varginha, a military driver says he saw a creature that was not human, with skin that was \"very oily\" and had \"a sort of siliconlike texture.\" The man is not named and tells Michels this as something he saw himself. His account is one of several about Varginha that year, which describe a craft coming down and a living being taken by soldiers to hospital. If true, it would mean the Brazilian military held a live non-human being.","military-helicopters-following-researchers-at-the-crash-site-1980":"In the 1980s, the researcher Bill Steinman is said to have been followed by military helicopters a couple of times as he left Hart Canyon, near Aztec, New Mexico, where a saucer carrying alien bodies is said to have crashed. Scott Ramsey, who has investigated the Aztec case, tells Michels what Steinman claimed, and says the same thing happened to his own group once while they were putting in a plaque at the site. To Ramsey it suggests the military was still watching the place decades later.","military-members-saw-football-field-size-triangular-craft-2019":"Military members saw huge triangular craft, \"football field size\", hovering right over them, according to a former US intelligence officer named David Grusch, who says he interviewed some of these service members between about 2019 and 2021. He tells Michels they \"saw some really profound stuff\", and if their accounts are accurate, very large unknown craft have been flying low over American military personnel.","military-officer-briefs-gaetz-on-hybrid-breeding-programs-2020":"A senior military officer is said to have walked into Congressman Matt Gaetz's office in Washington in the 2020s and told him about \"hybrids,\" programmes said to breed beings that are part human and part something else. Congressman Eric Burlison tells Michels that Gaetz told him this, and adds that Gaetz did not give the officer's rank. If true, it would mean such claims are being brought straight to members of Congress.","military-officers-who-reported-ufos-were-sent-to-john-mack-f-1980":"Some time in the 1980s or 1990s, high-ranking military officers who had reported UFOs were sent for psychiatric exams to John Mack, head of clinical psychiatry at Harvard, and that, the story goes, is how Mack began looking into the subject himself. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this, passing on what he was told about one such man and \"a number of other high ranking military people\" sent to Mack. If true, it would mean the military was quietly sending its own UFO witnesses to a psychiatrist.","military-officials-approach-sandler-and-emenegger-for-docume-1971":"The story is that in the early 1970s US military officials approached two film producers, Allan Sandler and Robert Emenegger, and asked them to make a documentary about UFOs. They even offered footage said to have been shot at Holloman Air Force Base. The online researcher UAPGerb says the pair \"were approached by two US military officials\" and could include the Holloman footage, and he names an officer called Weinbrenner. Michels tells it on the show with the contact running through Norton Air Force Base, where \"everybody there is talking to him about UFOs.\" He ties it to the 1974 film UFOs: Past, Present and Future, which involved the astronomer J. Allen Hynek and the TV host Rod Serling. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron, who repeats what Emenegger told him, puts the meeting at Norton around 1973. He names a Norton official, Paul Shartle, a Colonel Coleman, and Nixon's chief of staff H.R. Haldeman. He says the officials asked for \"this other documentary on UFOs\" but wanted it hidden \"below the other eight documentaries.\" If true, the military was trying to prepare the public for UFOs through a film it quietly encouraged.","military-trying-to-contact-ufos-with-fighter-jet-lights-1950":"In the 1950s the American military, so a story went, used lights on its fighter jets to try to signal to UFOs. The Canadian UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as a story he heard from that era, without naming a source. If true, it would mean the military was not just chasing UFOs but trying to talk to them.","military-video-of-instantaneous-acceleration-with-a-weapons":"The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says that he and the Las Vegas journalist George Knapp obtained and released a military video in which an object accelerates instantly while a weapons system has a firing lock on it. Corbell tells this to Michels as one of the people who put the footage out, pointing to an analysis that followed it, since an object moving that way under a weapons lock would be beyond any known aircraft.","military-x-everything-started-at-my-farm-1996":"The filmmaker James Fox says that an unnamed military man, whom he calls \"military X\", told him that \"everything that happened\" in Varginha \"started at my farm.\" Varginha is a town in Brazil where, in 1996, witnesses say a strange creature was seen and captured by soldiers. Fox tells Michels he heard this himself while working on his documentary Moment of Contact, and if the man is right, the whole Varginha affair began on his own land.","minas-gerais-as-the-centre-of-brazil-s-ufo-activity-linked-t":"Minas Gerais, a Brazilian state rich in mining, is said to be the centre of the country's UFO activity because of its rare minerals. A Brazilian researcher who is not named tells Michels that \"there's a close relationship between the UFO phenomenon and those rare elements, minerals in the whole state.\" If so, whatever is behind the sightings would be drawn to what lies in the ground there.","mind-lamp-turns-green-during-the-episode-2021":"During a 2021 episode about the CIA's psychic spy programme, a lamp meant to respond to people's minds turned green on the first try, within the first 30 seconds, and Michels works out the odds of that at about 2%. He tells this himself and cites a researcher named Herb Metts. If it holds up, it would hint that thought alone can nudge a machine.","minister-joe-s-special-needs-youth-group-talks-about-the-hil-2023":"Children in a special-needs youth group at a megachurch were talking among themselves about something they called \"the Hill\", according to their minister, a man known only as Joe. The documentary filmmaker Ky Dickens, maker of The Telepathy Tapes, repeats what Joe told her when she speaks with Michels, and she tells it because, if true, it suggests these children share an experience they could not have learned from each other in the usual way.","ministry-of-culture-raids-a-press-conference-2020":"Peru's Ministry of Culture broke up a press conference about the three-fingered bodies, small mummified figures some say are not human, and Michels points to what it did to Joyce Mantilla, one of the people presenting them, as an example. He tells this himself, without naming a source, as a sign that Peruvian officials were trying to shut down attention to the bodies.","minot-afb-all-ten-missiles-down-after-ufo-1966":"In 1966, all ten nuclear missiles at a site on Minot Air Force Base in North Dakota are said to have gone down after a UFO appeared, which would mean something unknown could switch off American nuclear weapons. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, tells Michels that another witness, a man named David Schindele, has come forward with this account.","minot-afb-radar-reviewed-by-kevin-knuth-1968":"In 1968 a UFO is said to have appeared at Minot Air Force Base in North Dakota, and it was caught on radar. Michels says this himself, in a debate with a skeptic: \"we actually have radar associated with it and physicists have reviewed the radar,\" among them a physicist named Kevin Knuth. An online researcher known as UAPGerb tells it more loosely. He says a bomber, which he calls a B-2, \"encountered a saucer flying around it\", and he gives no year. Michels brings up the case because radar would be hard evidence, not just what people say they saw.","minot-launch-signal-stopped-by-inhibit-switch-1966":"In 1966 at Minot Air Force Base in North Dakota, a missile officer named Captain David Sher is said to have received a launch signal at his post, and the launch was stopped only by an inhibit switch, a safety control. Michels tells it himself, from a phone interview that the researcher Robert Hastings, who studies UFOs at nuclear bases, did with Sher. If true, something caused a nuclear missile to start a launch sequence on its own.","minuteman-silos-on-black-hills-land-guaranteed-by-the-fort-l-1868":"Michels says that 150 silos for Minuteman nuclear missiles were sunk into land in the Black Hills of South Dakota that the 1868 Fort Laramie Treaty had promised to the Great Sioux Nation. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about Air Force veterans who say they were abducted by aliens. His point is that the country's nuclear weapons were planted on ground pledged by treaty to the Sioux.","miracle-of-fatima-mass-sighting-1917":"In 1917 at Fatima in Portugal, a large crowd is said to have seen something strange in the sky, an event known as the Miracle of Fatima. The skeptic Michael Shermer mentions it in passing as a mass sighting by \"50,000 people or 70,000 or whatever it was.\" A researcher named Jason Simosa gives more detail without naming a specific source. He says the clouds parted, something appeared in the sky, and lawyers, doctors and other \"very serious people\" described it as \"a silvery disc.\" Michels tells it as three children receiving a prophecy that the Virgin Mary would come, after which lights appeared in the sky before \"100 plus people.\" Tellers bring it up as a possible religious vision that might have been a UFO sighting.","misawa-disc-flips-on-its-edge-1952":"In 1952, during a training exercise at Misawa in Japan, a pilot practising interceptions saw a disc rise up to his plane, and it flipped up onto its edge. The commentator Marik von Rennenkampff tells this on the show as a case he knows from the record, without naming a source. He tells it as an early military sighting of a craft that moved like no plane of its day.","mishlove-s-accredited-doctorate-in-parapsychology-1970":"Jeffrey Mishlove, who studies psychic phenomena, says that in the 1970s at the University of California, Berkeley, he earned what he thinks is the only accredited doctorate in the world with the word \"parapsychology\", the study of psychic abilities, printed right on the diploma. He tells Michels this about himself.","mishlove-s-eleven-year-study-of-ted-owens-1976":"The researcher Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a doctorate and studies psychic claims, says he worked with a man named Ted Owens for eleven years, from 1976 until Owens died in 1987. He tells Michels this from his own experience and draws on his own files on Owens, and he places the work beside that of Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ, physicists who tested psychics for the US government. If his account holds, it was a long, close study of one man said to have unusual powers.","mishlove-s-prize-winning-bigelow-essay-on-survival-of-consci-2021":"In 2021 the businessman Robert Bigelow ran an essay contest, and the researcher of psychic phenomena Jeffrey Mishlove says he wrote a prize-winning entry for it. Mishlove tells Michels that Bigelow \"asked for an essay on the strongest evidence that human consciousness will survive permanent bodily death.\" If that evidence holds, it would mean the mind goes on after the body dies.","mishlove-s-remote-viewing-trial-at-sri-1976":"In February 1976, Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD and studies the paranormal, says he was invited to the Stanford Research Institute in California to try remote viewing, the attempt to describe a faraway place using the mind alone. He tells Michels he did it himself and had \"a very successful trial,\" and he names the researchers Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ in connection with the invitation. If true, an outsider could get results in the institute's own tests.","missile-fields-as-the-obvious-place-for-the-watchers-1945":"If something non-human has been watching humanity since the start of the atomic age, the nuclear missile fields of South Dakota and Montana are \"not a strange place to find them at all. They are the obvious place.\" Michels offers this as his own reasoning, without naming a source, in an episode where Air Force veterans who say they were abducted by aliens meet again. If he is right, sightings at those bases would be a sign of deliberate interest in nuclear weapons, not chance.","missing-411-national-park-disappearances-tied-to-ufo-sightin":"People who go missing in US national parks vanish in the same areas where people report seeing UFOs, according to a writer Jason Jorjani calls David Pitus, the author of the Missing 411 books on unexplained disappearances in the parks. Jorjani, a researcher, repeats this to Michels as what Pitus found, and links it to a work called Closer Encounters and to FBI files on the cases that the story says are kept hidden. If true, it would tie some of the disappearances to the UFO phenomenon.","missing-and-dead-scientists-loureiro-gilmore-mccasland":"A handful of scientists and officials tied to MIT, Caltech and Wright-Patterson Air Force Base are said to have died or gone missing, among them Nuno Loureiro, who Michels says \"basically ran MIT's plasma fusion lab\", along with Carl Gilmore and Neil McCasland. Michels raises these himself as \"cases people are talking about\", naming them in passing without saying what happened to each or where he heard it. If there were a pattern, it would suggest someone is targeting people close to sensitive research.","missing-fetus-syndrome-grays-take-back-hybrid-fetuses":"Women carrying part-alien hybrid pregnancies are said to lose them because the grays, the small grey beings of abduction reports, come back for the fetus. Tim Alberino, a filmmaker and researcher, tells Michels this without naming a source, saying simply: \"The grays come and get the fetus.\" It is offered as the explanation for what some call missing fetus syndrome, where a pregnancy vanishes without a trace.","missing-time-on-drive-home-from-college":"On a drive home from college, Bob Maguire, who later worked as a CIA contractor, says that about two and a half hours in, he and his companion suddenly found themselves in the middle of nowhere, with no idea where they were or how they got there. Checking their watches, they worked out that an hour and a half was unaccounted for. Maguire tells Michels this himself and calls it \"a classic example of missing time,\" the term UFO researchers use for hours that witnesses cannot account for, often linked to abductions.","missing-time-waking-up-by-a-reservoir-dam-near-newell-1977":"In 1977, two Air Force security policemen guarding a nuclear missile site in South Dakota say they lost nearly four hours. They found their truck parked behind the wall of a reservoir dam near the town of Newell, more than 8 miles from their post. Michels tells the story on an episode where two veterans, Mario Woods and Michael Johnson, meet again decades later. The fullest account is an article by the journalist Robert Hastings, published on 10 September 2017 under the title \"Former USAF Missile Security Policeman Tells of Apparent UFO Abduction at ICBM Site\". An ICBM is a long-range nuclear missile. In it the former policeman says: \"I put the mike down and stepped out of the vehicle only to find that we were somewhere other than at November-5,\" the launch site they had been sent to. He says the sun was coming up and he could see \"a dam that held water in a reservoir\", which turned out to be Newell Lake, \"quite a few miles\" away. At the debriefing, he says, he was told \"that nearly four hours had passed and that six security police back-up Alert teams had been searching for us.\" UFO researchers call a gap like this \"missing time\", a span a person cannot remember. If the account is true, men guarding nuclear weapons were taken off their post without knowing how.","mission-control-split-between-nasa-and-the-air-force":"A man named Chris Bledsoe, who says he draws UFOs to him and is studied by NASA, says mission control at Cape Canaveral is split in two: NASA people sit at one bank of consoles, and \"you look to the left, it's exactly the same, but it's all Air Force people.\" Bledsoe tells it as something he saw himself, and he mentions a man named Timothy Taylor in telling it. The point is that the military runs a twin operation right next to the civilian space programme.","mission-to-recover-panasonic-toughbooks-with-crash-retrieval":"A man named Jake Barber, who says on the show that he flew helicopters on missions to recover a UFO, tells Michels he took part in a mission to get back \"sensitive information\" believed to be stored on Panasonic Toughbooks, which are rugged laptops. He says the information was evidence from crash retrievals, meaning the recovery of crashed craft of unknown origin, and that it went back to a facility in the California desert. If true, it would mean records of such recoveries exist and were moved around in secret.","misty-echo-test-portal-and-dan-burisch-s-s4-story-1988":"A patch made for Misty Echo, a 1988 test at the Nevada Test Site, appears to show a portal underground, and Michels says that would explain the story told by a man named Dan Burisch about S4, a facility said to lie at Area 51. Michels tells it himself, crediting the patch to a tweet by Chris Ramsay, and sets it beside the story of Bob Lazar, another man tied to S4; if true, the patch would be an official hint that the government tested a portal.","mit-dream-implantation-technology":"Technology for planting dreams in a person's mind, even from a distance, exists in MIT's own technical work, according to Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who calls himself a whistleblower. He tells Michels that \"you can look at technical applications from MIT itself relating to dream implantation, including at a distance,\" and presents them as recent and public, without naming any one document. If so, the claim that minds can be reached remotely would rest on work published in the open.","mit-ion-plane-credits-obscure-1920s-scientist-2018":"In 2018 MIT showed a plane with no moving parts, pushed along by electrically charged air, and the story is that this ion plane credits the work of an obscure scientist from the 1920s. Jan Lundquist says this in a documentary on Michels' show about Townsend Brown, a physicist who claimed electric fields could produce thrust, without naming a source. If true, it would mean Brown's long-dismissed ideas finally flew.","mitchell-and-uri-geller-svahn-meets-geller":"The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn says he talked at length with the astronaut Edgar Mitchell about Mitchell's meeting with Uri Geller, a man known for claiming psychic powers, and that he later met Geller himself. Svahn tells Michels this from his own experience, saying \"we talked a lot about\" how Mitchell met Geller, though the details of either meeting stay vague in what he gives here.","mitchell-confessed-blue-orbs-to-the-spaceman-1971":"The Apollo 14 astronaut Edgar Mitchell privately confessed that on the Moon in 1971 he and his crewmate saw blue, orb-like lights following them across the lunar surface. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this from In Plain Sight, a book by the journalist Ross Coulthart, which quotes an anonymous source called \"the Spaceman,\" said to be a close personal friend of Mitchell; if true, it would mean astronauts met something unexplained on the Moon itself.","mitchellhoagland-debate-on-coast-to-coast-1990":"In the 1990s, the Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell and the author Richard Hoagland, known for his claims about the Moon, are said to have had a long debate on Coast to Coast AM, Art Bell's late-night radio show. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani mentions it to Michels in passing, without saying what was argued or where he heard about it.","mithras-liturgy-ascent-through-seven-heavens":"In the Mithras Liturgy, an ancient text found among the Greek Magical Papyri, a collection of old Greek magical writings, a new initiate climbs through the seven heavens using breathing techniques, visualizations, chants and ceremonies. Michels tells this himself, as something he has read. He offers it as an old method for reaching other levels of reality through the mind.","mitre-as-the-place-to-look-for-the-legacy-program":"Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, tells Michels that \"if you want to look at legacy program stuff, I'd look at MITRE.\" The \"legacy program\" is the name for a supposed long-running secret effort to recover and study non-human craft, and MITRE is a nonprofit that runs research centers for the US government. He says it as advice on where the programme might be found, without explaining further.","mitre-funded-papers-detecting-gravitons-near-atmosphere-2000":"Research papers paid for by the MITRE Corporation, a research organisation, some 20 to 25 years ago are said to show that gravitons, the proposed particles of gravity, can be detected near Earth's atmosphere, at both very high and extremely low frequencies. A researcher named Dave Rossi tells Michels this from his own reading, without naming the papers or their authors. If true, it would mean gravity could be measured and perhaps worked with in ways mainstream physics does not yet allow.","mj-12-burned-memo-on-lancer-1963":"Among the so-called Majestic documents, papers that describe a secret group of officials said to handle UFOs, there is said to be a partly burned memo that discusses \"Lancer,\" the code name for John F. Kennedy, in 1963. The UFO historian Richard Dolan mentions it to Michels without naming a source, calling it the \"so-called burned memo where Lancer, and that was the code name for Kennedy, was described.\" He brings it up because it would tie Kennedy's fate to the secret group that, by these papers' account, controlled the UFO question.","mj-12-document-cardinal-spellman-given-access-to-roswell-mat-1947":"One of the MJ-12 papers, a set of documents describing a secret committee said to have handled recovered UFO material, is said to record that Cardinal Francis Spellman, a senior American Catholic churchman, was given access to the materials from Roswell, the 1947 crash in New Mexico that some say was a flying saucer. Michels mentions this document in passing himself. If true, it would mean that the Church was let in on the secret from the start.","mj-12-documents-surface-in-1984-sent-from-near-kirtland":"The MJ-12 papers, documents describing a supposed secret group of twelve officials set up under Truman to deal with UFOs, are said to have first appeared in 1984, probably sent from somewhere near Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico. The researcher and historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this without naming a source. If they were sent from near the base, it would point to people inside the military putting the papers into circulation.","mj-12-papers-judged-compelling-but-not-conclusive-1980":"In the 1980s the UFO researcher Bill Moore published a report on the MJ-12 papers, documents describing a secret group said to handle crashed saucers. Among them is one dated 18 November 1952 that presents itself as a briefing for Eisenhower. Moore's report appeared in a book called The MJ12 Papers, and according to the writer Greg Bishop, who relays what he knows of Moore's work, it judged the documents \"compelling but not conclusive.\" Bishop tells Michels this to show that even the papers' first promoter stopped short of calling them genuine.","mj-12-scientists-really-were-involved-1947":"The author Whitley Strieber tells Michels that the scientists named as members of MJ-12, a supposed secret group of twelve said to have handled crashed flying saucers from 1947 on, really were involved, and he rejects the idea that the UFO researcher Stanton Friedman \"made the whole thing up\"; he also mentions the mathematician John von Neumann. The name comes from the MJ-12 papers, a typed briefing paper for Eisenhower, dated in its own text 18 November 1952 with an attached Truman memo, which according to the source note reached a film producer named Jaime Shandera anonymously on a roll of film in 1984, was studied by him, William Moore and Friedman, and was made public in 1987; the National Archives and the Truman and Eisenhower Libraries found no copy, FBI copies are marked \"BOGUS\", the Air Force's investigators called it \"fabricated\", the skeptic Philip Klass called the Truman memo a fraud, and Friedman judged both \"almost certainly genuine\". The paper names five scientists, among them \"Dr. Vannevar Bush\" and \"Dr. Detlev Bronk\", and says \"a similar analysis of the four dead occupants was arranged by Dr. Bronk\"; that is where the names come from, but it shows their part only as far as the paper itself is genuine, and the step from being named to actually doing the work is Strieber's.","mj12-document-jfk-asks-dulles-for-a-review-on-28-june-1961":"A document from the MJ-12 papers, a disputed set of files said to come from a secret group handling UFO matters, describes a top secret request from President John F. Kennedy to CIA director Allen Dulles on 28 June 1961 for a review. Michels says it leaked in 1995 and was recently discussed on the news channel NewsNation by the journalist Ross Coulthart and a former Australian intelligence officer named Jeff Cruickshank, who set it beside Allen Dulles's diary, released with the JFK files in 2022. Coulthart, telling it himself, stresses that the MJ-12 files are \"very disputed\"; if the document were genuine, it would place a UFO inquiry on a president's desk.","mj12-memos-reference-cic-involvement-at-roswell-1940":"Leaked papers known as the MJ-12 documents, said to come from a secret 1940s group that handled crashed flying saucers, refer to the Army's Counter Intelligence Corps taking part in UFO investigations, including at Roswell, the reported 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico. Jesse Michels tells this himself, citing the MJ-12 memos, on an episode asking whether Henry Kissinger oversaw a program to recover crashed craft.","mk-monarch-acknowledged":"MK Monarch, said by those who talk about it to be a secret US mind-control programme, has been officially acknowledged, according to a guest named Kurt Metzger. He tells Michels that his friend Emma made a show about it called Imagination Podcast on YouTube, and that he sent Michels a link showing the acknowledgement. He does not say who acknowledged it or how.","mk-ultra-bluebird-artichoke-and-occult-research-1950":"During the CIA's mind-control programs of the 1950s and 60s, MK Ultra and the earlier projects called Bluebird and Artichoke, which tested drugs and other methods for controlling and questioning people, the researchers were also looking into occult systems. The writer on occult history Peter Levenda says this to Michels, listing these and one more operation whose name is hard to make out, without naming a source. If true, the US government once treated magic and mysticism as material worth studying for its own purposes.","mk-ultra-born-of-mind-control-race-fears-1950":"MK Ultra, a US programme of mind-control research that ran from about 1950 into the 1970s, grew out of a fear that America was falling behind the Russians in mind control. That is how the researcher and skeptic Michael Shermer explains its origins to Michels, without naming a source: \"MK Ultra came out of the fear that we're losing the race to the Russians on mind control.\" If he is right, the programme started as a Cold War arms race of the mind.","mk-ultra-lsd-dosing-death-at-new-york-hospital-and-cover-up-1950":"A guest named Dan Sheehan says that in the 1950s a man was killed at a New York City hospital by MK Ultra, the CIA's secret programme of mind-control and drug experiments. He says that after about two days of counselling with a psychiatrist there, staff gave the man \"a huge mass of LSD without telling him\", and that Jacob Javits, a New York senator, and Warren Burger, later Chief Justice of the United States, helped hide what happened. Sheehan tells Michels this as something he was involved with, and if true it would tie two powerful public men to covering up a death caused by the programme.","mk-ultra-purpose-lsd-as-interrogation-truth-serum-1950":"MK Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control experiments of the 1950s and 60s, was meant to turn the drug LSD into a truth serum for interrogations, because dosing someone would dissolve the boundaries of their concept structures. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as his own explanation, without naming a source. If true, it would mean the experiments aimed at breaking down people's minds to get answers out of them.","mk-ultra-remote-viewing-and-ufos-under-one-umbrella-1950":"The CIA's 1950s mind-control experiments known as MK Ultra, government work on remote viewing (the claimed psychic ability to see distant places), and the study of UFOs all belong under \"one umbrella\", according to a guest named Dylan Borland. He tells Michels that a man named James Lacatski and a book called Skinwalkers at the Pentagon \"all tie into it\", though he names no source of his own. If he is right, these strange-seeming programmes would be parts of one hidden effort, not separate oddities.","mlk-essay-on-mystery-religions-and-christianity":"Martin Luther King wrote a little-known essay called \"The Influence of the Mystery Religions on Christianity\", about how the secret initiation cults of the ancient Mediterranean shaped the early church, Michels says, bringing it up beside the work of the Bible scholar Dennis MacDonald and the King James Bible of 1611. He tells it as a sign that Christianity carries older, hidden traditions inside it.","modern-4602d-personnel-attend-ramsay-lecture":"Scott Ramsey, a researcher who studies a claimed crash of a craft carrying alien bodies, says that people from today's version of the 4602D, an Air Force intelligence unit that once looked into UFO reports, came to one of his lectures. He tells Michels he saw this himself and that it happened in Denver. If true, a military unit tied to UFO investigation is still quietly paying attention to his work.","moment-of-contact-the-varginha-witnesses-1996":"In 1996 people in the Brazilian town of Varginha said they saw strange living beings, a case told in Moment of Contact, a documentary by the filmmaker James Fox. Michels praises the film and says the witnesses are not lying, stressing that the story does not come from any official US government source. On a later episode he adds that the Varginha beings had three fingers, which he links to three-fingered bodies shown in Peru. If true, ordinary townspeople would have seen something not human up close.","monk-timotheus-says-other-monks-have-seen-the-light-of-the-v-1991":"In 1991, at Dionysiou monastery on Mount Athos in Greece, a monk named Timotheus is said to have told a visitor that other monks had seen \"the light of the Virgin Mary\" coming down, and that the visitor might have seen it too. Ralph Larson, the guest who visited the monastery and saw something there, tells Michels what the monk told him. If true, it would put Larson's experience in line with what the monks themselves say they see.","monsanto-chemicals-sprayed-in-vietnam-cause-birth-defects":"The chemical company Monsanto shipped its chemicals to Vietnam and other poorer countries without strict rules or oversight, where they were sprayed on crops and \"started creating birth defects\", according to Brigham Buhler, a businessman in the health industry. He tells Michels this as something he heard from others, and offers it as an example of a company pushing harmful products where regulators could not stop it.","monsanto-pays-agent-orange-veterans-compensation-1983":"In the mid-1980s, according to Michels, the chemical company Monsanto had to pay millions in compensation to war veterans exposed to Agent Orange, a highly toxic chemical. He tells it himself, without naming a source, in an episode about the dark past of big drug and chemical companies, as an example of companies harming the people they were supposed to serve.","montagnier-dna-water-teleportation-confirmed-in-witnessed-ex":"A guest named Dave Rossi tells Michels he has watched lab experiments that confirm a paper by the scientist Luc Montagnier, which claimed that the signature of DNA could be sent into water and copied there, an effect Rossi calls \"teleportation.\" Rossi says he saw these experiments himself in private laboratories, and if he is right, genetic information can pass from place to place without any physical material moving.","montauk-experiments-1950":"The story goes that secret experiments ran at Montauk, on the tip of Long Island, New York, sometime between the 1950s and the late 1960s, and that some kind of machinery was found there. Michels raises it himself and says he believes in that machinery. He points to a book whose author he half remembers as \"Preston something\", meaning Preston Nichols, but he gives no details from it.","montauk-experiments-behind-stranger-things":"Secret experiments are said to have taken place at Montauk, on Long Island, New York, and the TV series Stranger Things is said to be based on them. Michels says this himself: he thinks the makers of the show have said as much, and that it is \"pretty well established\" that some experiments happened there, though he does not say what they were or where he learned it.","months-of-swarms-over-navy-ships-off-southern-california-2019":"In 2019, the story goes, swarms of UAP, the official term for unidentified flying objects, flew over US Navy ships off the Channel Islands and the Southern California coast for five or six months. The commentator Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this, relaying the reporting of the journalists Jeremy Corbell and George Knapp. Corbell himself says there were about 100 UAP and that some were pyramid-shaped, which would mean unknown objects repeatedly shadowed military vessels.","montserrat-scanned-and-found-to-carry-a-tridactyl-fetus-2020":"A mummified three-fingered body from Peru, known as Montserrat, was found during medical scans to be carrying a fetus of its own, and the moment of discovery was caught live on the scan. Michels tells it himself from Peru, where the researchers showed him the footage of \"the exact moment they discovered that she was pregnant.\" If the bodies are what their finders claim, a pregnancy would mean they were living beings that reproduced, not assembled objects.","moon-dust-document-fireball-and-silent-aircraft-at-a-bolivia":"A declassified document from Moon Dust, a programme Michels presents as part of US efforts to recover crashed objects, describes a fireball and then sightings of silent aircraft near a crash site in Bolivia. Michels cites it himself, in an episode on whether Henry Kissinger oversaw a UFO crash retrieval programme, as evidence that such recoveries were real.","moon-dust-document-spherical-objects-recovered-in-new-zealan":"A declassified document from Moon Dust, a US government effort to recover objects that fell from the sky, is said to describe spherical objects being recovered from farmland in New Zealand. Michels mentions it himself, without giving a date, while asking whether the US ran a programme to retrieve crashed craft.","moon-ruins-at-33-in-the-disney-von-braun-film-1950":"In one of the space-exploration films Walt Disney made in the 1950s with the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun, astronauts reach the far side of the Moon for the first time and a radar engineer shouts that some kind of radiation is being picked up \"at 33°\", and the scene turns to ruins there. Jesse Michels describes the scene himself, treating the number 33 and the ruins as hidden signals in an official-looking film.","moon-s-two-moments-of-inertia-suggest-it-was-geoengineered":"The idea here is that the Moon may have been deliberately shaped or rebuilt by someone, which the story's title links to the Moon having two \"moments of inertia\", a physics measure of how mass is spread inside a spinning body. The researcher Randall Carlson raises it on the show without citing a source, and puts it carefully: \"I am willing to consider that the moon has been geoengineered.\" If it were so, our nearest neighbour in space would be an artefact rather than a natural object.","moore-benowitz-operation-as-a-24-hour-stage-play-1970":"The effort around a man named Bennewitz near Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, in the 1970s and early 80s, is said to have worked like a 24-hour stage play, a show staged around the clock for one audience. A guest named Greg Bishop tells Michels that a UFO researcher named Bill Moore described it this way, and Bishop also draws on a man named Harry Rositzke. If so, what Bennewitz saw and heard would have been put on for him on purpose.","moore-hands-aquarius-document-to-benowitz-in-a-supply-closet-1981":"In 1981, in Paul Bennewitz's office in Albuquerque, New Mexico, a man named Bill Moore is said to have led Bennewitz into a supply closet, turned a radio up, and handed him a paper known as the Aquarius document, saying: \"I was told to give this to you. Take it with a grain of salt.\" Greg Bishop tells Michels this as he heard it about the two men. If it happened that way, it would mean the document was passed to Bennewitz on someone else's instructions, with a warning attached.","moore-photographs-documents-in-an-upstate-new-york-hotel-roo-1980":"In the early 1980s, a man named Bill Moore was told to go to a hotel room in upstate New York, where he photographed documents left for him there. Greg Bishop tells Michels this, passing on what Moore himself said about how he was summoned \"in the middle of\" his dealings with his contacts. If true, it would mean someone was deliberately putting papers into Moore's hands.","more-crash-retrievals-in-the-ocean-than-elsewhere":"More operations to recover crashed craft of unknown origin are said to have taken place in the ocean than anywhere else. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels that a researcher named Eric W. Davis has said so. If true, the oceans would be the main place where such craft turn up.","more-detailed-image-based-communications-with-emotion-rocket-1990":"Between 1990 and 1993, at a site he calls Base 2, messages he says came from non-human beings started reaching him as vivid pictures carrying emotion, showing things like rockets and troop movements. A former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to take in these messages, tells Michels this from his own experience. He says the later communications were \"very like pictures, images\" and \"much more colorful\" than the ones before, which, if true, would mean the contact was becoming richer over time.","more-than-one-non-human-species-not-all-benevolent-1964":"Richard Barth, a former guard at nuclear missiles, says that during his 1964 encounter with beings at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California he learned there is more than one non-human species, and they are not all as friendly as the ones he met. He tells Michels this as something he took part in, and it is the same encounter in which he says the beings told him they will not allow a nuclear war.","morgan-and-townsend-brown-s-conversation-about-a-time-travel":"The inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, whom Michels presents as a scientist who worked with the CIA and built UFO-like craft, is said to have once sat in the office of a man Michels names only as Morgan and asked, \"What if we had the ability to time travel?\", adding that he believed \"we're going to be able to do it in our lifetime.\" Michels tells the exchange himself, without naming a source. If true, it would show Brown's circle seriously thinking about time travel as a near-term possibility.","morgan-robertson-s-wreck-of-the-titan-foretells-the-titanic-1898":"Years before the Titanic sank, a novel told of a giant, supposedly unsinkable liner called the Titan that strikes an iceberg in the North Atlantic on an April night and goes down. The book is Futility, or The Wreck of the Titan, by Morgan Robertson, published in 1898. Michels brings it up himself on several episodes, as a case of a story that seems to see the future, and the researcher Eric Wargo raises it too, citing his own book Time Loops, about the idea that the mind can sense events ahead of time. Michels does not always tell it the same way: once he gives no author or title and puts the book in the early twentieth century, about ten years before the Titanic, and once he says about twelve years, where the record has 1898. If the match is more than chance, it would mean a writer somehow drew on a disaster still to come. The novel's own words, in the 1912 edition that is the text checked here, call the Titan \"the largest craft afloat,\" \"practically unsinkable,\" and say she \"carried as few boats as would satisfy the laws\"; it is \"April,\" and the lookout cries \"Iceberg. Right under the bows.\" That 1912 text carries both an 1898 and a 1912 copyright, and whether the 1898 first edition is worded exactly the same is not checked.","morgan-says-brown-was-cia-research-1950":"The inventor Townsend Brown, who claimed electric fields could make objects move without propellers or jets, was \"basically CIA research\", according to a man known only as Morgan. Jan Lundquist passes this on in Michels's documentary about Brown, saying Morgan told it to a writer named Paul Schatzkin, so it comes to us third-hand, and if true it would mean the CIA quietly backed Brown's work sometime between the 1950s and 1970s.","mork-mindy-white-egg-as-a-hint-of-hidden-knowledge-1970":"The white egg-shaped craft that Robin Williams's alien character flies to Earth in on the 1970s TV sitcom Mork & Mindy is said to be a hint of hidden knowledge about real UFOs. Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he helped recover a UFO, tells Michels this as a story that gets passed around, noting that the egg is in the show's preview and \"part of every show.\" If true, people in television were dropping clues about real craft into a popular comedy.","mormonism-as-a-ufo-religion-joseph-smith-s-contacts-and-down-1820":"Mormonism, the faith Joseph Smith founded in the United States in the 1820s and after, is \"kind of a UFO religion\" if you actually look into it. That is the view of a researcher named Matthew Pines, who says so to Michels in passing without naming a source. He points to Joseph Smith's contacts and the revelations Smith received, which would make one of America's own religions a story of contact with something from beyond.","morphologies-of-the-peruvian-mummies":"Michels says the strange mummified bodies from Peru that his episode discusses come in three body types: \"the J types, the S types, and the M types.\" He gives this himself, without naming a source. The point is that if the bodies are real and fall into distinct kinds, they would be more than a single odd find.","moses-burning-bush-as-a-plasmoid":"Michels suggests that Moses speaking to the burning bush on Mount Sinai could have been a plasmoid, a glowing ball of electrically charged gas, and so a kind of UFO encounter in modern terms. He raises it while citing the writer Robert Temple and his book A New Science of Heaven. The Bible story tells of a bush that burned without burning up and a voice speaking from it, and the idea is that an old account like this could carry the memory of something seen.","most-hessdalen-residents-report-experiences":"In Hessdalen, a valley in Norway, a very large share of the people who live there say they have experienced something strange, upwards of 80, 85 or 90 percent of them. Michels says this from his own visit, where he found it \"very easy to tell\" how common the experiences were. If true, it would make the valley a place where odd events are a normal part of life rather than rare tales.","mosul-dam-turbine-proof-of-concept-scan-2024":"Around 2024, a scanning method later pointed at the pyramids of Egypt was first tested on the Mosul Dam in Iraq, as a proof of concept to see whether it could pick out structures like the dam's turbines. The researcher Jeffrey Drum says this on the show, crediting the work to the researcher Filippo Biondi: \"this was another proof of concept where they scanned the Mosul Dam.\" The point is that a method that works on a known structure would lend weight to what the same scans claim to find beneath the pyramids.","mother-ayahuasca-gives-hancock-a-novel-s-plot-2006":"The writer Graham Hancock says that in 2006, over five sessions of drinking ayahuasca, a psychedelic brew from the Amazon, in Brazil, he was \"literally given the entire plot of a novel.\" He tells Michels this as his own experience, and the book that came of it was his novel Entangled. If true, it would mean the visions handed him a finished story rather than mere images.","mother-faking-telepathy-on-danny-jones-s-show":"A mother of one of the children featured in The Telepathy Tapes, a series about children said to read minds, went on Danny Jones's podcast and, Michels says, was clearly peeking past her blindfold. Michels tells this himself, saying he thinks some of those involved are faking it, which he calls unfortunate for the wider claim.","mother-s-apparition-in-the-kitchen":"In Tampa, Florida, during a lightning storm, the family's mother was seen standing in the kitchen, as an apparition. That is how Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear site, describes it to Michels as something he lived through, and he says the family talked about it among themselves. He gives no date and says little more than that she was 'standing in that kitchen in a lightning storm.'","mother-s-earlier-sightings-in-rocky-mount":"A woman says she saw strange things as a child growing up in Rocky Mount, North Carolina, but never said what they were. Her son Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear site who describes his own UFO encounter, tells Michels she said only that she had \"seen things before\" and did not go into any of it.","mothership-at-langley-incursion-per-chris-mellon-2023":"In 2023, during an intrusion of unknown craft over Langley Air Force Base in Hampton, Virginia, small saucers are said to have flown out of a larger \"mother ship\". Michels passes this on from Chris Mellon, a former deputy assistant secretary of defense for intelligence under Presidents Clinton and Bush, raising it in a debate with a skeptic named Mick West. If true, it would mean organised craft were operating over a US military base.","motion-sciences-anti-gravity-effort-debunked-2010":"In 2010, an effort called Motion Sciences tried to build anti-gravity technology in a hangar at Provo Airport in Utah, and in the end it was shown not to work. The current owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, tells Michels he saw this himself. He says two scientists, Hal Puthoff and Kit Green, \"were part of the science advisory board,\" and he also brings up a man named Joe Firmage in connection with the project.","mount-athos-dedicated-to-mary-and-closed-to-women":"Mount Athos in Greece is said to be devoted entirely to Mary, so much so that women may not set foot on it even today. A guest named Ralph Larson says this to Michels in passing, describing the mountain as dedicated to Mary \"at the exclusion of all women.\"","mount-kailash-as-an-energy-vortex-where-fingernails-grow-fas":"Mount Kailash, a mountain in Tibet, is said to be an energy vortex, a place where some unusual force gathers, so strong that fingernails grow faster there. Michels brings it up himself, repeating that the spiritual teacher Sadhguru said his own fingernails grew faster on the mountain. If true, it would mean some places on Earth really do change the body.","mount-nemrut-near-lake-van-erupted-at-end-of-pleistocene":"Mount Nemrut, a volcano southwest of Lake Van in eastern Turkey, last erupted at the very end of the last ice age, around the cold spells geologists call the Older and Younger Dryas. That is what the researcher Matt LaCroix tells Michels, summing it up without naming a source. He raises it in a conversation about a great upheaval around 12,000 BC, and if he is right, the eruption would be one more sign of turmoil at that moment.","mount-nyangani-as-a-ufo-repair-facility":"Mount Nyangani, a mountain in Zimbabwe, is a repair facility for UFOs, according to a former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan. He tells Michels this is what he says he himself found out about the place: \"it seems to be a repair facility.\" If true, it would mean the craft have a working base on Earth where they are fixed.","mount-shasta-portal-opening-and-the-10-foot-being-2014":"A portal, a kind of doorway to somewhere else, is said to have opened at Mount Shasta in California in 2014, and people who were there describe a being about ten feet tall wearing boots. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this, saying that in 2015 he interviewed \"almost everybody that was in the portal\", and that when he asked what they saw, the answer was \"this 10-foot tall guy and he had boots on.\" Cameron does not name further sources, but if true it would mean a large non-human figure was met face to face on a California mountain.","mount-st-helens-observers-killed-by-heat-miles-away-1980":"When Mount St Helens erupted in Washington state in 1980, people watching from five or ten miles away were killed by the heat, says Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient history, pointing Michels to documentaries about the eruption. He brings it up in a conversation about a catastrophe he places around 12,000 BC, to show how far a natural disaster's deadly reach can stretch.","mount-wilson-cover-up-buried-fried-servers-and-rail-system-1996":"At a site linked to Robert Bigelow, a businessman tied to the property, and called Mount Wilson in the record, the current owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property famous for reports of strange events, says he found signs of earlier activity that had been covered up: buried computer servers that had been \"fried\" and a buried rail system, dated to around 1996. He tells Michels he took part in uncovering it himself, saying the evidence showed activity that \"predated\" his time there. If true, it would mean someone had done hidden work at the site and tried to hide what was left.","mount-zeil-as-an-alien-port-of-entry-to-earth":"Mount Zeil, a mountain in Australia, is said to be 'sort of a port of entry to the earth' where 'friendly ones', meaning friendly visitors from elsewhere, come in. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan tells Michels this and ties the idea to a man named Pat Price. If it were true, there would be a fixed place on Earth where friendly non-human visitors arrive.","mountain-bike-ride-with-missing-time-at-daniels-park-burial-1995":"In 1995 a mountain bike ride at Daniels Park, a burial ground south-southwest of Denver, Colorado, left a man with \"at least 5 hours\" he could not account for, the kind of gap people who report alien abductions call \"missing time.\" The man is John Blitch, a scientist with DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, and he tells Michels it happened to him, mentioning Cindy Blitch as part of the story. Elsewhere in the same conversation he tells it without the place or the length of time, and describes it as the moment he stopped being a skeptic: \"I was on the skeptical side until the mountain bike incident.\"","mountain-bike-sabotage-after-senate-briefing-2023":"In 2023, about three weeks after he briefed people at the Senate, someone sawed through the bolt that holds the seat on his mountain bike, says Steven Greer, a guest who campaigns for the release of UFO secrets. He tells Michels this as something that happened to him: \"someone sawed through the bolt that holds my mountain bike seat on.\" He tells it as a warning or an attempt to hurt him, which, if true, would mean someone wanted to punish him for speaking.","mountains-as-possible-non-human-no-go-zones":"Some mountains may be places that non-human beings keep for themselves, where people report \"orbs going into the mountain, crafts going into the mountain.\" Michels raises the idea himself, without naming a source, pointing to Mount Shasta in California, with Secret Mountain in Arizona and Mount Inyangani in Zimbabwe mentioned as similar places, during a talk with a Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown.","mountains-worldwide-as-possible-alien-bases":"Some mountains around the world, Mount Zeil in Australia, Mount Hayes in Alaska, Mount Perdido and Mount Shasta, are floated as possible hidden bases for non-human visitors. Michels lists them himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property whose owners report strange happenings there. If true, it would mean such visitors keep stations on Earth rather than only passing through.","moving-boulders-of-the-mojave-racetrack":"In the Mojave Desert, boulders are said to move across the ground on their own, leaving tracks behind them. The skeptic Michael Shermer mentions \"the story of the moving boulders\" on the show, naming it in passing without a source, as one of the desert's well-known puzzles.","moving-the-sine-wave-by-mentally-humming-a-tone-1990":"A former sergeant named Dan Sherman says that between 1990 and 1993, at an undisclosed site near the NSA's complex in Maryland, he was trained to move a sine wave on a monitor just by humming a tone in his mind. He tells Michels he was watching the box when the wave moved, and \"it is like it came into focus\"; he also wrote about this in his book Above Black. He describes it as part of training to communicate with aliens by thought, which would mean the NSA treated such contact as real.","mr-d-s-signal-discovery-for-locating-uap-government-radar-sa-2021":"A man known only as Mr. D found a way to pick up signals from unexplained flying objects, or UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's term for UFOs), so that their position or direction in the sky could be worked out, and government radar also saw these objects but did not tell the team. James Valor, who works with Skywatcher, a startup that says it can summon such objects, tells Michels this from inside the work between 2021 and 2025. If true, it would mean the objects can be tracked on purpose and that officials were watching without sharing.","mr-x-s-work-on-exotic-vacuum-objects-and-self-organizing-pla-2020":"A man known only as Mr. X, who is not a government man but whom a guest named Jay Anderson calls \"a savant,\" is said to have done a great deal of work around 2020 on \"exotic vacuum objects\" and on plasma, a glowing electrically charged gas, that organises itself. Anderson tells Michels this about Mr. X, who does not want to be named, as part of a case that some unexplained lights may be strange forms of plasma.","mris-of-ufo-contact-dod-and-intelligence-personnel-show-enla-2010":"Brain scans of Department of Defense and intelligence staff who had encounters with UFOs are said to show one deep brain region, the caudate nucleus and putamen, looking different, and in Nolan's account enlarged. The Stanford scientist Garry Nolan tells Michels he was shown these MRI scans in the 2010s and that 'most of those people had interactions with UFOs.' Later Michels raises it himself as a hypothetical about fighter pilots who see UFOs, and in a 2024 episode Nolan puts it more cautiously, calling the area only 'disturbed... or different' in many of the people, which would suggest the brain plays a part in who has such encounters.","muldoon-americans-wanted-kaikoura-shut-down-1978":"The Americans wanted the Kaikoura case, a UFO episode in New Zealand in the late 1970s, \"shut down pretty quickly\", according to a man named Robert Muldoon. Ross Coulthart tells Michels that when he raised it with Muldoon, Muldoon chuckled and \"just let slip\" that line. If true, it would mean Washington leaned on New Zealand to bury the case.","multi-billion-dollar-propaganda-book-campaign-1981":"In 1981, an official named John S. Herrington is said to have described a secret publishing effort: next to the real books, he said, \"are the books that we publish full of propaganda. It's a multi-billion dollar campaign to cover up the truth.\" The Hollywood writer Brent Friedman tells Michels what he says Herrington said. If true, it would mean much of what the public reads on the subject was planted to mislead.","multi-sensor-corroboration-of-uap-satellite-radar-visual-tem-2021":"Some UFO sightings are backed by several kinds of evidence at once, satellite data, radar, people seeing them with their own eyes and temperature readings, and \"it's all totally consistent\", according to the economist Tyler Cowen. He tells Michels this in 2022 as something he has heard from others, and if true it would mean these objects are not just tricks of a single camera or a single witness.","multiple-high-clearance-sources-say-uap-is-a-real-phenomenon-2021":"Many people with very high security clearances, from completely separate sources, are said to have said that UAP, the official term for UFOs (unidentified anomalous phenomena), are real. The economist Tyler Cowen tells Michels in 2022 that he heard this over the past year, which would mean people with inside access take the subject seriously.","mummified-pharaohs-in-the-cairo-museum-with-cranial-deformat":"Michels says that some of the mummified pharaohs in the National Museum in Cairo look very short, have heads reshaped by binding \"to mimic the gods,\" and look like gray aliens, the big-headed, grey-skinned beings of UFO stories. He says this himself, as something he has seen or heard of, without naming a source. The suggestion is that ancient Egyptians may have been copying beings like the ones people describe today.","muon-detected-big-void-to-be-excavated-in-2026-with-ministry":"The \"Big Void\", a large empty space inside the Great Pyramid of Giza that was found by tracking muons (tiny particles from space that pass through stone), is due to be dug into and explored in 2026. The researcher Jeffrey Drum tells Michels this, citing the ScanPyramids team, the scientists who scanned the pyramid, and Egypt's Ministry of Antiquities, which he says has approved it: \"they're actually going to be excavating into the Great Pyramid in 2026 to investigate the big void.\" If it happens, the first look inside could show what the hidden space holds.","muon-team-may-have-mistaken-the-grand-gallery-s-floor-and-ro-2020":"The \"Big Void\", a large hidden space that the ScanPyramids team reported finding inside the Great Pyramid of Giza by tracking muons (particles from space that pass through stone), may really be the floor of the Grand Gallery, the pyramid's great sloping inner passage, misread by the detectors. A researcher named Filippo Biondi suggests this to Michels, calling the muon results \"confusing\"; at another point he describes the void as running parallel to the Grand Gallery and speaks of muon methods falling short in general, without mentioning the floor or roof. If he is right, the famous discovery would be a misreading of a space already known.","musgrave-saw-snake-like-things-writhing-in-space":"A space shuttle astronaut named Musgrave is said to have seen things like snakes writhing in space. A researcher named Kevin Knuth passes it on: \"The story of Musgrave, a space shuttle pilot, also spoke of seeing things like snakes.\" Michels repeats the same line in the same episode. Neither says when or where, but it is told as a hint that astronauts have seen strange living-looking things in orbit.","musha-s-ex-honda-team-and-capacitors-in-transmission-oil":"A team in Japan, said to come out of the carmaker Honda and led by a scientist named Musha, is said to have worked with capacitors (devices that store electric charge) sitting in transmission oil, in a line of research into new kinds of propulsion. Michels brings it up himself in an episode on antigravity propulsion, and he hedges as he goes: he \"believes\" they came from Honda and \"thinks\" Musha is the name.","mushrooms-tell-mckenna-about-hyperlight-drive-ships":"The writer Terence McKenna is said to have heard, on a mushroom trip, the mushrooms tell him that \"within my memory exist the blueprints for hyper light drives\", meaning engines for ships that travel faster than light. Michels tells this himself on several episodes. Once he points to a passage in McKenna's book True Hallucinations, and another time he places the message during a two-week trip in the Amazon. If taken at face value, it would mean a plant was offering knowledge of advanced space travel.","musk-and-mike-griffin-go-to-russia-for-rocket-engines-2000":"In the early 2000s, Elon Musk and the aerospace engineer Mike Griffin went to Russia together and found rocket engines there. Michels tells this himself, in passing, as part of the story of how Musk got into rockets, and does not say where he heard it.","mussolini-asks-hitler-about-the-magenta-object-1933":"An unknown craft, called the Magenta object, is said to have crashed in Italy in 1933, and Benito Mussolini supposedly went to Adolf Hitler afterwards and asked, \"Is this yours?\", which led to a partnership between the two over it. Michels tells this himself on two episodes, calling it rumor and never naming a source. If true, it would mean a crashed craft was in fascist hands years before the Second World War.","myles-standish-recalculates-and-dismisses-the-planet-1990":"In the 1990s, the story goes, NASA announced it had found signs of an unknown extra planet, a \"Planet 10\". Then a scientist named Myles Standish redid the maths and concluded, in effect, \"we made a mistake\", and the planet was dropped. The researcher Matt LaCroix tells Michels this from memory, without naming a source, and offers it as a case where a possible new world was announced and then quietly taken back.","myrna-hansen-hypnotic-regression-and-underground-facility-ab-1980":"A woman named Myrna Hansen is said to have described being abducted around 1980 near Albuquerque, New Mexico, and taken to an underground facility, memories the story ties to hypnotic regression, the use of hypnosis to bring back supposed hidden memories. The researcher Greg Bishop tells Michels about it in an episode on Paul Bennewitz, a man whose obsession with aliens is the subject of the show, saying Hansen came to Bennewitz's house \"talking about being abducted and going to this underground facility.\" If her account were true, it would place a hidden alien-linked base under New Mexico.","mysterious-death-of-pat-price-1975":"A man named Pat Price is said to have died in Washington DC in 1975 in a way that does not match the published account, taken away in an unmarked ambulance. Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, tells Michels he heard different versions from people who were there, and that years later the department's head of security said, \"Pat Price died and we haven't heard from him since.\" If so, Price's death, or his disappearance, would have been something other than what was made public.","mysterious-jungle-sickness-and-deafness-at-tikal-2024":"During a 2024 trip to Tikal, the old Maya city in the Guatemalan jungle, a researcher fell ill with a sickness no one could explain and slowly lost most of his hearing. Luke Caverns, a researcher who studies ancient pyramids, tells Michels this happened to him, saying, \"I was 75% deaf in each ear by the end of the trip.\"","mysterious-man-plays-ransone-a-metallic-et-voice-tape-1970":"Sometime in the early 1970s in Houston, a woman named Valerie Ransone is said to have been handed a cassette by a mysterious man. When it played in the car, it was a metallic voice that she took to be from beings not of Earth. An unnamed researcher passes this on from what Ransone said in an interview on the YouTube channel Eyes on Cinema, as a sign that someone was delivering alien messages by tape.","mystery-airship-phenomenon-1807":"At around the same time as other strange events, people in the United States and Europe reported mysterious airships in the sky, a wave now called the mystery airship phenomenon. A guest named Tim Alberino brings it up briefly, drawing on the work of Richard Dolan, as part of a longer pattern of unexplained things seen overhead.","mystery-schools-aim-to-recover-the-lost-knowledge-of-the-god":"One of the main goals of the mystery schools, secret traditions that pass hidden knowledge to initiates, is \"to recover the lost knowledge of the gods from Mars\", says the filmmaker Tim Alberino. He tells Michels this as his own reading, drawing on the writer David Flynn and bringing in Elon Musk and Cydonia, a region of Mars where some people see a carved face in photos taken from orbit, and if he is right, the push to reach Mars would carry an old esoteric purpose.","name-dennis-mariani-confirmed-by-someone-at-the-nevada-test-1987":"Michels says he knows of one case in which someone at the Nevada Test Site, the government's desert testing ground, confirmed the name Dennis Mariani. The name belongs to the story of Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site called S-4 in Nevada in the late 1980s. Michels tells this himself, without saying who the person was, and it matters because it would back up a detail of Lazar's account.","nanosims-analysis-finds-anomalous-magnesium-isotope-ratios-i-2010":"One of the metal fragments from Ubatuba, in Brazil, which the 2021 episode's title calls \"Parts Of an Alien UFO\", had magnesium that was mixed in the wrong proportions, says the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan, who ran the tests himself. He tells Michels that his team used a very sensitive instrument at Stanford, which can weigh the different forms of an element in a tiny sample, and that \"one of them was anomalous. The magnesium ratios were way off,\" which, if true, would point to metal not made in the ordinary way. Telling it again on a 2026 episode, he calls the instrument simply a highly sensitive mass spectrometer.","narmer-palette-shows-egypt-existed-before-reunification":"Egypt's first dynasties are said to begin with a \"reunification of upper and lower Egypt,\" and the researcher Geoffrey Drumm tells Michels this is shown by the Narmer Palette, an ancient Egyptian carved stone slab he also calls the Menes palette. His point is that you can only reunite something that was united before, so an Egypt would have existed before its known history begins. His case rests on the word \"reunification\" as historians use it; what the slab itself shows, and whether it marks a first union or a second, he does not say.","narrator-clip-the-channeler-s-second-trance-in-front-of-frie-1959":"In 1959 in Washington, D.C., a channeler, a person said to pass on messages from other beings while in a trance, went into a second trance in front of a man the clip calls Friend. A narrator's clip played on the show, in an episode with the researcher Grant Cameron about the head of the government's UFO programme, describes it: \"He sat for several minutes and then appeared in a deep trance with his Adam's apple moving up and down rapidly.\" The scene matters because it would put an official witness in the room for a contact session.","nasa-3i-atlas-press-conference-2025":"In 2025 NASA held a press conference about 3I/ATLAS, an object passing through the solar system from interstellar space, and Michels calls it \"idiotic\" and says it was about \"manipulation.\" He says this himself, in connection with an astronomer named Avi Loeb, as part of a wider claim that unusual space data gets played down. He does not go into what was said at the conference.","nasa-administrator-isaacman-says-some-cases-are-unresolved-2026":"Jared Isaacman, the head of NASA, is said to have admitted in 2026 that some sightings of UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, remain unexplained. Michels raises it twice: first in a debate with a skeptic, as a point against him, and later with more detail. In that second version, he says a release of UAP files by the Department of War, whose first batch focused on space exploration and astronaut encounters, forced Isaacman to say this. According to Michels, Isaacman made clear he was not talking about reverse engineering, meaning taking apart captured craft to copy their technology, or about confirmed alien bodies, only that the sightings were unexplained, which Michels calls a huge update.","nasa-announcement-of-planet-x-planet-10-1990":"NASA once announced a new planet in the outer solar system, calling it \"Planet n or Planet 10\", according to Matt LaCroix, a guest who adds, \"I do believe this was a real announcement.\" He places it in the 1990s and raises it with Michels in an episode about something he believes happened around 12,000 BC, where an unseen large body out past the known planets would matter to that story.","nasa-announcement-of-possible-life-on-mars-2025":"NASA recently announced it had found \"some sort of life on Mars\", according to Dan Sherman, a former sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens. Sherman mentions it to Michels in passing in 2025 and gives no detail of the announcement. If so, it would be the first official word of life beyond Earth.","nasa-assessor-baron-killed-by-train-500-page-report-vanished-1967":"In 1967 a man named Thomas Baron, who had been hired by NASA to assess how likely its programme was to succeed, was killed by a train, and the 500-page report he had written disappeared. Joe Rogan tells Michels this, passing on Baron's story rather than anything he saw. If true, it would mean a critical inside assessment of NASA was lost along with its author.","nasa-attempt-to-read-the-ramey-memo-with-algorithms-2000":"Between 2000 and 2003, a team at NASA's Intelligent Systems Division wrote computer algorithms to try to read the Ramey memo, a document whose writing is hard to make out and which UFO researchers have long tried to decipher. Kevin Knuth, who was there and calls himself a NASA whistleblower, tells Michels: \"we actually wrote the algorithms to actually read this memo.\" Why it matters to him is that people inside NASA were quietly working on a UFO document.","nasa-dart-mission-hits-dimorphos-2022":"In 2022, NASA's DART mission flew a spacecraft into Dimorphos, a small moon of the asteroid Didymos, to show that humans can push an asteroid off course and keep it from hitting Earth. Michels brings it up himself on two episodes, once while talking to Randall Carlson and once while talking to Graham Hancock. In one he recalls it loosely as a spacecraft \"knocking into\" the asteroid. In the other he describes it as a test of asteroid deflection that ran from 2021 to 2022.","nasa-department-of-war-special-investigation-of-apollo-17-li-2026":"An image of lights from Apollo 17, the 1972 moon mission, is said to have come out in the first batch of NASA's 2026 release of UAP files, UAP being the official term for UFOs, with a caption saying the Department of War, as the Pentagon is now called, and NASA had opened a special investigation into it. Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this from reading the release: the description \"next to the image\" says \"the department of war and NASA have opened a special investigation for this particular image.\" If true, it would mean both agencies officially think the image needs a closer look.","nasa-didn-t-want-to-talk-after-news-called-them-2024":"Around 2024 or 2025, NASA is said to have refused to discuss something when reporters called about it. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him, tells Michels he heard from a CIA friend in Washington that something he mentioned caught the news, the news called NASA, and NASA's reply was, \"God, we don't want to talk about this.\" If true, it would suggest NASA knows something about his case and keeps it quiet.","nasa-director-wrote-psychic-spy-manual-for-cia":"A director at NASA, whose name Michels believes was Steven Hubbard, is said to have written a manual on psychic spying for the CIA. Michels mentions it himself, hedging with \"I believe\" and naming no source. If true, it would tie the space agency to the CIA's interest in using the mind to gather intelligence.","nasa-eagle-works-engineer-s-casimir-effect-microchip-power-c":"An engineer from NASA Eagle Works, a small NASA lab that tested unusual propulsion ideas, claims to have powered a little microchip at 1.5 kilovolts using the Casimir effect, a tiny force that appears between very close surfaces in empty space. Michels passes this on himself and does not name the engineer. If true, it would mean usable energy can be drawn from the vacuum itself.","nasa-electrostatics-lab-measures-thrust-in-high-vacuum-2016":"A NASA electrostatics lab at the Kennedy Space Center in Florida has been measuring thrust, a push, produced by electric charge in a high vacuum, testing almost every day or every other day since 2020. Charles Buhler, a scientist who works on the tests, tells Michels this himself, and links the work to Thomas Townsend Brown, an earlier inventor of electric propulsion ideas. If true, it would suggest a way to move craft without burning fuel.","nasa-electrostatics-scientist-charles-buehler-effect-works-i-2000":"A NASA scientist who studies electrostatics, Charles Buhler, is said to insist that a propulsion effect based on electric charge is real, and that \"it works in a vacuum.\" That matters because working in empty space would rule out the usual explanation that such a push comes from the surrounding air. Michels relays Buhler's position himself rather than quoting Buhler directly.","nasa-employee-wrote-a-cia-remote-viewing-training-manual-2000":"Someone at NASA wrote a training manual for the CIA on remote viewing, the claimed ability to see distant or hidden places with the mind alone. This comes from Kevin Knuth, a former NASA scientist, who tells Michels in a single line what someone else told him, without saying who wrote it or when. If true, it would tie people at the space agency to the CIA's interest in psychic spying.","nasa-experiment-tardigrades-survive-18-months-in-the-vacuum":"Tardigrades, microscopic animals often called water bears, survived 18 months in the vacuum of space in a NASA experiment outside the International Space Station. That is what a guest named Dr. Gilbert says on the show, without naming a source. He offers it as proof that life can be tough enough to survive in space.","nasa-flight-surgeon-on-apollo-astronauts-shunning-edgar-mitc-2008":"Other Apollo astronauts kept their distance from Edgar Mitchell, one of the men who walked on the Moon, according to a NASA flight surgeon (an astronaut's doctor) whom John Blitch, a former DARPA scientist, will not name. Blitch tells Michels the surgeon said this while the two were diving together in 2008 at Aquarius, an underwater research base off Key Largo, Florida, and he tells it as an inside glimpse of how Mitchell was treated by his own colleagues.","nasa-langley-staff-tag-puzzles-and-hieroglyphs-on-google-map-2020":"Staff at NASA Langley in Virginia have been tagging puzzles, hieroglyphs, rocks and formations on Google Maps, according to a guest named Dylan Borland. He tells Michels he took screenshots of these labels years ago, about three years back by his account, including labyrinths and pyramids marked at the center's hypersonic facilities complex, and that the labels were later scrubbed. If so, people inside a NASA site were leaving coded hints in public view.","nasa-mission-controller-tells-of-space-program-honouring-spo":"People in a \"secret space program,\" a claimed hidden space effort apart from the public one, are said to honour certain \"sponsors\" in second-century Latin. Michels tells this himself, passing on what the scholar Diana Pasulka has said about her talks with a NASA mission controller named Timothy Taylor. If true, it would mean a hidden space effort with its own old rituals.","nasa-mission-controller-tim-taylor-s-advice-think-about-what-2020":"A NASA mission controller named Timothy Taylor, Michels says, told him that to get a rocket to work, and for Michels's rocket company to succeed, \"you have to think about what they want,\" and Michels took \"they\" to mean non-human intelligence. Michels tells this himself, as something said to him; in one retelling he says it came on a FaceTime call and links it to American Cosmic, a book by the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka. If meant as Michels heard it, it would suggest someone inside the space world thinks unseen beings shape technical success.","nasa-mission-controller-tim-taylor-says-astronauts-see-thing":"A NASA mission controller named Tim Taylor is said to have revealed that astronauts see things in space they cannot explain. Michels passes this along, saying Taylor's name has now \"been outed\", and he ties it to a book called American Cosmic. If true, it would mean people inside NASA know of sightings by astronauts.","nasa-moon-pigeons-report-1970":"NASA published a report in the early 1970s called \"Moon Pigeons\", according to Maaneli Derakhshani, who tells Michels this from his own reading. He gives only the title and rough date, saying nothing of what the report contains. He raises it as a sign that NASA had looked at something unusual about the Moon.","nasa-moon-program-development-at-langley-1960":"Much of NASA's early work to get people into space in the 1960s, including pressurization, is said to have been done at Langley Air Force Base in Virginia. The researcher Dylan Borland mentions this to Michels in passing, without naming a source: \"NASA's development to get up into space and everything pressurization was done at Langley Air Force Base, Virginia.\" He raises it as background, placing the roots of the Moon programme at that base.","nasa-operator-disconnects-gary-during-hack-2001":"Around 2001 or 2002, while Gary McKinnon was inside NASA computers from the UK, someone at NASA noticed him and cut him off by right-clicking the network icon and disconnecting him. McKinnon is a British hacker, and he tells Michels this as his own experience. He brings up British Telecom, the UK phone company, as he tells it, and says 'that was NASA' on the other end, meaning a real person caught him in the act.","nasa-people-invite-him-to-lunch-during-a-us-congress-visit-1997":"Sometime between 1997 and 1999, after he had publicly said he was taken by aliens, a Russian politician got a call during an official visit to the US Congress from people who said they were from NASA and wanted to meet him over lunch. The politician, Kiran Ilum Xenov, who later became president of one of Russia's republics, tells Michels this happened to him. He says the callers told him, \"We're from NASA. We heard about your statement and would like to meet,\" which would mean, if true, that the American space agency took a quiet interest in his account.","nasa-people-study-the-phenomenon-and-travel-with-him-2007":"People from NASA study what happens around him and travel with him, says Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has drawn UFO sightings since 2007. He tells Michels he is fond of the ones he already knows, among them a man named Timothy Taylor, and that \"there are a lot of other NASA guys that I'm currently talking with.\" If true, it would mean people inside NASA are quietly taking his experiences seriously.","nasa-program-with-psychic-kids-in-florida-1967":"NASA is said to have run a programme in Florida in the late 1960s that worked with psychic children. If so, the space agency was quietly studying the mind as well as rockets. A researcher named Greg Malatzi says he has \"heard a lot about\" a \"NASA thing with kids\" somewhere in Florida at that time, though he names no source. A guest named Jay Anderson mentions it in passing, saying \"NASA had the star kids,\" without giving a place or date.","nasa-representative-4-000-recorded-cases-a-year-1997":"Around 1997 or 1998, a NASA representative is said to have admitted that \"we officially record about 4,000 cases a year\" of people seeing unidentified objects like saucers. Kiran Ilum Xenov, the former president of a Russian republic who says he was taken by aliens in 1997, tells Michels this is what the representative told him. If true, NASA would be quietly counting thousands of sightings a year.","nasa-s-higher-resolution-mars-images-released-to-calm-the-co-1990":"NASA released a second, higher-resolution set of images of Cydonia, a region of Mars, to \"quell the firestorm\" that had broken out over earlier pictures of the area. That is how a guest named Tim Alberino tells it to Michels, without naming a source; on his telling, the new images were as much about calming a public controversy as about showing the ground.","nasa-s-innovative-propulsion-program-funds-warp-drive-resear":"NASA is said to have studied warp drive, a theoretical way for a spacecraft to travel by bending space itself. Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the National Security Agency, mentions it to Michels while discussing his book The Shadow of Time, saying \"we go through NASA's innovative propulsion program, which I think is fantastic.\" Michels tells his own version on another episode. He says the physicist Miguel Alcubierre \"figured out this sort of theoretical model,\" that \"NASA's been studying this for years,\" and that a NASA scientist named Harold \"Sonny\" White has done \"interesting work in this area.\" Neither gives details of how the programme worked or what it found. They raise it because, if true, a government space agency takes faster-than-light travel seriously.","nasa-space-force-track-uncorrelated-targets-2020":"NASA tracks what it calls \"uncorrelated targets\", objects in the sky or in orbit that do not match anything known, \"all the time\", according to a current NASA employee Michels says he has been in touch with. Michels tells it himself, in an episode that also involves the astronaut Beatriz Villarroel's work, wait, the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, and if true it would mean NASA and the military routinely see unexplained objects without saying so publicly.","nasa-telepathy-experiments-and-remote-viewing-the-moon-1960":"In the 1960s, according to a man named Andrija Puharich, NASA ran telepathy experiments and sent \"remote viewers\" to the moon. Remote viewers are people said to describe faraway places with the mind alone. The researcher Greg Malatzi gives Puharich's claim on the show, pointing to newspaper articles and to NASA contracts Puharich held, and if it were true it would mean the space agency tested psychic spying beyond Earth.","nasa-tries-to-replicate-podkletnov-s-weight-loss-effect-1993":"In the mid-1990s NASA took an interest in anti-gravity and tried to reproduce an effect reported by a Russian scientist named Yevgeny Podkletnov, who worked at the University of Tampere in Finland and claimed objects lost weight near his equipment. The researcher Nick Cook tells Michels this without naming a source, saying NASA \"had exhibited, weirdly, an interest in anti-gravity\" after those reports. If true, it would mean the space agency took a fringe claim of cancelling gravity seriously enough to test it.","nasp-as-cover-program-for-aurora-1980":"NASP, a US programme of the 1980s to build a plane that could fly from runway to space, is said to have been a cover for Aurora, a secret spy plane rumoured at the time. The writer Nick Cook tells Michels that a fellow aviation writer named Bill Sweetman became convinced of this. If so, a public programme would have hidden a secret aircraft behind it.","nat-kobitz-as-coulthart-s-original-navy-source":"A senior Navy scientist named Nat Kobitz was the original source who first drew the Australian journalist Ross Coulthart into UFO research, sending him, in Michels' words, \"down the rabbit hole.\" Michels says this himself, without saying how he knows it. If true, it would mean that Coulthart's reporting started with an insider in the Navy's own science world.","nat-kobitz-confirms-being-read-into-a-uap-program-2018":"A man named Nat Kobitz confirmed that he had been \"read in\", that is, formally briefed into, a secret programme dealing with UAP, the official term for UFOs, and with alien technology. Ross Coulthart tells Michels he put the question to Kobitz directly, sometime between 2018 and 2020: \"Have you ever been read in to any kind of program involving unidentified aerial phenomena, alien technology?\" If true, it would mean such a programme exists and admits people to it.","nathan-twining-reference-in-disclosure-day-with-the-twining-1947":"Discussing the film Disclosure Day, Jesse Michels points to General Nathan Twining, head of the Air Force's Air Materiel Command in 1947, who wrote the Twining memo, a letter dated September 23, 1947 titled \"AMC Opinion Concerning 'Flying Discs'\", and who was, Michels says, rumored to belong to Majestic 12, said to be a secret group of twelve officials handling UFO matters. That rumor rests on the MJ-12 papers, a briefing paper that reached a researcher anonymously on film in 1984, which lists \"Gen. Nathan F. Twining\" among the twelve and says \"a covert analytical effort organized by Gen. Twining and Dr. Bush acting on the direct orders of the President\" studied a recovered disc, and on a 1954 memo to Twining, reported found at the National Archives in 1985, that says \"the President has decided that the MJ-12 SSP briefing should take place\" at a White House meeting. Both would place Twining inside such a group only as far as they are genuine: the National Archives says the second memo \"poses problems\" and FBI copies of both are marked \"BOGUS\", while some researchers judged them \"almost certainly genuine\"; the 1947 Twining memo itself is a separate letter on flying discs.","national-archives-extends-the-deadline-for-uap-records-to-se-2025":"The National Archives gave an extension until the end of September 2025 for releasing government records on UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. Dan Sheehan, a lawyer active in pushing for UFO disclosure, says this on the show as something he is following closely. It matters because it sets a date by which those files are supposed to come out.","national-archives-photos-of-eg-g-sandia-underground-operatio-1972":"There is said to be a series of photographs in the National Archives showing underground work at the Nevada Test Site, the government's nuclear testing ground, carried out by the contractor EG&G and the Sandia weapons laboratory, dated from 1972 to 1992. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb mentions this to Michels without naming a source, adding that the photos stop before the company Martin Marietta bought Sandia in 1993. If he is right, the public record holds pictures of the hidden underground work he links to the Pentagon's UFO effort.","national-geographic-double-blind-remote-viewing-of-the-dumba":"In a double-blind test filmed by National Geographic, set up so that neither the viewer nor the people with him knew the target, Joe McMoneagle says he described a hidden location by remote viewing, the claimed skill of sensing a distant place with the mind alone. He tells Michels he took part himself, that a man he calls Ed was involved, and that the target turned out to be 'the end of the Dumbarton Bridge' in the San Francisco Bay Area. If true, it would be a controlled result in favour of remote viewing.","national-security-act-passed-weeks-after-roswell-1947":"Jesse Michels points out that the National Security Act of 1947, the law that reorganised the US military and intelligence agencies, came in September 1947, only weeks after Roswell, the reported crash of an unknown craft near Roswell, New Mexico, in July. He says it himself, from memory, to suggest that the new secrecy structure may have been shaped in part by the crash.","national-underwater-reconnaissance-office-established-1969":"A secret US agency for underwater spying, the National Underwater Reconnaissance Office or NURO, is said to have been set up in 1969. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb says it was \"established in 1969 and still has not been disclosed to this day.\" Michels describes it himself as \"the little brother\" of the National Reconnaissance Office, which runs US spy satellites, saying it \"is now public\" but little is known about it. On another episode he credits a man named Bob Ballard with founding it; if true, it would mean the government has long watched the oceans in secret, where some think unexplained craft also go.","nato-warships-around-the-expedition-2011":"In 2011, while a diving team was looking at a strange object on the floor of the Baltic Sea, warships from several NATO countries were sitting around them. Dennis Asberg, a Swedish treasure hunter who led the expedition, tells Michels he saw this himself: \"It was French and German and American and British ships around us.\" If true, it would suggest that militaries were watching closely whatever the divers had found.","navair-high-energy-electromagnetic-field-generator-experimen":"A Navy experiment with a high-energy electromagnetic field generator fell far short of what it needed, says Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist who holds patents on unusual craft designs and who worked on it himself. He tells Michels the test ran at NAVAIR, the Navy's aircraft research command, and that they could not get an electrical charge above ten to the minus eight of the unit, when they needed at least one \"to show that the Pais effect is correct,\" the effect named after him that his designs rely on. Had it worked, it would have backed his idea that such fields could power new kinds of flight.","navajo-curse-legend-of-skinwalker-ranch":"Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah's Uinta Basin known for reports of strange events, is said to sit on land cursed by the Navajo. Michels tells the legend himself, without naming a source. In the 19th century, during the Civil War, the Ute tribe joined the US government in a fight against the Navajo, and the Navajo, badly beaten and feeling betrayed, cursed the land with skinwalkers, the shape-shifting figures of Navajo lore. In a later episode Michels says the Ute call it \"cursed ground, the path of the Skinwalker\" and won't talk about it, and that on the ranch \"drones malfunction, helicopters lose power, equipment dies.\" Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, repeats the story as he has been told it: the Navajo were driven away and cursed the property.","navajo-skinwalker-lore-matches-ranch-activity":"Old Navajo stories about skinwalkers, the figures of Navajo lore that the ranch is named after, are said to describe things like \"luminous objects\", much like what people report at Skinwalker Ranch, a property known for claims of strange lights and events. Michels says he found this while \"doing a little Googling\" and raises it with the ranch's owner. If the match holds, it would suggest the activity there goes back long before anyone owned the land.","naval-intelligence-as-a-major-funder-of-puharich-1950":"Naval Intelligence was a major funder of Andrija Puharich, the scientist at the centre of the episode, in the 1950s and still in the early 1980s. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this as something \"we know\", without naming a source. If true, it would tie the US military's intelligence world to Puharich's work for some three decades.","naval-intelligence-commander-s-email-uap-enter-and-leave-the":"UAP, the government's term for UFOs, are seen in the ocean more than anywhere else, going in and out of the water in five to seven areas of the world, according to an email from a naval intelligence commander. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral and oceanographer, tells Michels he received this email, which says \"we see them in the ocean more than elsewhere coming in and out of the ocean.\" Elsewhere in the same talk he goes further and calls the places \"undersea bases possibly,\" which, if true, would mean something unknown is operating from beneath the sea.","naval-intelligence-sphere-program-for-viewing-parallel-timel":"A person who says they once worked in naval intelligence claims they were part of a programme that used a sphere, similar to one linked to a man named Randy Anderson, as a remote viewing tool, a way of trying to see distant places or, here, other possible timelines with the mind. The online UFO researcher known as UAPGerb passes this on to Michels while noting it is only that person's claim, and if true it would mean the Navy has secretly run a programme to look into parallel realities.","navy-astronomer-measures-wobbling-disc-1947":"In 1947 in New Mexico, one of the Navy's top astronomers is said to have watched and measured a wobbling disc in the sky. The researcher Marik von Rennenkampff brings it up on the show without naming his source. He says it appeared in a well-known 1947 Life magazine article, which would mean a trained scientist working for the military reported such a sighting that year.","navy-aviators-privately-report-regular-sightings-and-near-mi":"Navy pilots at a US Navy base privately said they were seeing things in the sky they could not understand \"on a very regular basis\", including near misses, but were not talking about it openly. A guest identified as Dr. Gilbert tells Michels he heard this from the aviators directly. If true, it would mean such encounters are common among military pilots but kept quiet.","navy-cia-tug-of-war-over-the-nuro-1969":"Until 1972, the Navy and the CIA are said to have fought a long battle over who would control a secret body called the NURO, with the CIA side led by its science and technology branch, the Directorate of Science and Technology. A guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb describes it to Michels as \"a huge tug-of-war between the Navy and CIA specifically the DS&T over who controls\" it, and also names Bobby Ray Inman in the discussion. He tells it as part of how he believes the Pentagon's hidden UFO programme came to be organised and owned.","navy-commander-george-hoover-on-roswell-beings-as-time-trave-1947":"A Navy commander named George Hoover, who Michels says held a top secret clearance for most of his life, admitted that the beings found at Roswell were really time-traveling humans. Roswell is the 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico, that the Army first announced as a captured flying disc. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and it would mean the so-called aliens are our own descendants.","navy-gave-pais-crash-material-for-his-patents-2010":"The claim is that the US Navy gave an inventor named Salvatore Pais pieces of material from a crashed unknown craft, and that the strange patents he filed for the Navy in the 2010s came from working out the equations behind it. A researcher named Bob Maguire offers this on the show as his own guess (\"I'm going to guess they gave him some crash material\"), not from any source he names. If it were true, some of the Navy's own patents would rest on studying recovered craft.","navy-gun-camera-videos-off-san-diego":"Navy gun-camera footage filmed off San Diego shows something travelling at what he calls \"warp speed\" and then diving into the ocean, according to John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy. He tells Michels he saw the video on television, so he is describing broadcast footage, not anything he saw firsthand or through his office.","navy-has-no-original-flir1-tape-only-the-leaked-copy-2004":"The US military does not have the original recording of the FLIR1 video, the 2004 Navy infrared camera footage of the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint. So says Mick West, a researcher and UFO skeptic, who gives no source for it. He says the copy the Navy released is the same one that had already turned up on a German FTP server, a file-sharing site on the internet, which would mean the official release was really just the leaked copy.","navy-intelligence-visits-frances-swan-1954":"In 1954, Navy intelligence is said to have taken notice of a woman named Frances Swan in Eliot, Maine, and gone to visit her. The researcher Grant Cameron tells this without naming a source. He credits a document he describes as coming from the FBI or Navy intelligence about Swan, and he also names a man called Wilbert B. Smith. If true, it would mean the military was quietly following up on her in the 1950s.","navy-misidentifications-a-jet-called-a-drone-stars-called-dr-2019":"Trained Navy observers have mistaken ordinary things for drones: stars taken for drones, and a jet called a drone. A skeptic named Mick West says this on the show, pointing to the \"Snoopy team\", a crew of trained lookouts aboard a Navy vessel, who he says misidentified stars as drones. He raises it to argue that even trained military witnesses can be wrong about what they see in the sky.","navy-overcharging-for-parts-1980":"In the 1980s and 1990s, the Navy was overcharged for parts, a case one guest uses to show how money can move through the Pentagon's budget unnoticed. A researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb raises it on the show, without naming a source, as \"a really interesting example\" from that time, in an episode about how a hidden Pentagon UFO programme might be paid for. The point is that padded costs could, in principle, hide spending on secret work.","navy-patent-for-laser-drawn-plasma-decoys":"There is a US Navy patent, the claim goes, for using lasers to draw glowing shapes of plasma (electrically charged gas) in the air, shaped like an airplane, so that missiles chase the decoy instead. Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the National Security Agency, brings it up from memory and does not cite the patent by name. He raises it because it would mean some strange lights in the sky could be human-made tricks.","navy-pilots-interviewed-including-fravor-and-dietrich-of-the-2004":"A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell says he and his colleagues interviewed 'a succession' of Navy pilots about unexplained sightings, and tracked down David Fravor and Alex Dietrich, two Navy pilots from the 2004 'Tic Tac' episode, when pilots off the US coast reported a white oval object shaped like the mint. McConnell tells Michels this as work he took part in himself. It matters because it would mean Senate staff gathered first-hand accounts from those pilots.","navy-recovering-non-human-craft-from-the-ocean-floor":"The claim is that the US Navy is recovering \"crafts of non-human origin\" from the bottom of oceans all over the world. Michels states it himself, in the opening of an episode, without naming a source or giving a place or date. If true, it would mean the Navy holds objects not made by people.","navy-says-brown-s-work-still-classified":"Michels says that when the daughter of the scientist Townsend Brown, Linda Brown, and Brown's biographer, Paul Schatzkin, asked the Navy for his records, the Navy was at first very uncooperative. In his documentary on Brown, whom he presents as having built UFO-like craft long before others, he adds that Schatzkin used the Freedom of Information Act, a US law that lets anyone request government records, and that the Navy at first acted as if it had never heard of a man by that name. Michels believes Brown's work is probably still classified by the Navy today, which, if true, would mean it was considered too sensitive to release.","navy-seal-finds-a-black-octagonal-structure-near-mcmurdo":"A Navy SEAL is said to have found a \"highly polished black stone octagonal structure\" rising about 18 feet above the ice near McMurdo, the American research station in Antarctica. The philosopher Jason Jorjani passes this on to Michels from the writer Linda Moulton Howe, so it comes to us at least third-hand. If true, it would mean a built structure lies buried in the Antarctic ice.","navy-secretary-kimball-and-admiral-radford-s-planes-circled-1950":"In 1950, on a flight from California to Hawaii, the planes carrying Navy Secretary Dan Kimball and Admiral Arthur Radford were both \"accosted by an object that circled around their aircraft.\" Richard Dolan, a historian of UFOs, tells Michels this as something he heard from a man named Donald Kho. If true, it would mean two of the Navy's top men had a close encounter of their own.","nazca-and-paracas-cranial-elongation-rituals":"The ancient Nazca and Paracas peoples of Peru practised rituals that reshaped the skull and made it longer. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as one of several customs of those cultures, in an episode about strange three-fingered bodies said to come from Peru.","nazca-lines-made-to-be-seen-from-the-sky":"The Nazca Lines, huge figures and lines scratched into the desert ground in Peru, are said to have been made to be seen from the air: Michels says they \"only make sense 150 to 500 m in the air, which is far higher than any of the closest vantage points on the ground.\" He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about strange three-fingered figures found in Peru. If so, it raises the question of who their makers expected to be looking down.","nazi-aneutronic-fusion-bomb-traded-away-for-amnesty-1944":"In 1944 and 1945, this story goes, Nazi Germany may have developed a bomb based on aneutronic fusion, a kind of nuclear fusion that gives off very little neutron radiation, and the weapon was later traded away in exchange for amnesty. The researcher Jason Jorjani lays this out for Michels without naming a source, and says that if the Nazis really had it, \"atom bombs would be like matches for them.\"","nazi-antarctica-expedition-1938":"In 1938 and 1939, Nazi Germany is said to have sent an expedition to Antarctica. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels about it, calling it the German Antarctic Expedition and saying it dropped swastika flags over the ice. Michels, on another episode, says himself that the Antarctica expedition was very real. Neither gives a source. It comes up because of stories that the Nazis were after something secret in Antarctica.","nazi-bell-propulsion-research-at-the-skoda-works-under-kamml-1940":"During and after the Second World War, Nazi Germany is said to have run advanced propulsion research, the work behind a device called the Bell, at the Skoda Works just outside Prague, under SS General Hans Kammler. The researcher Jason Jorjani lays this out for Michels as a summary, drawing on a body of writing that includes Joseph P. Farrell, Nick Cook and Igor Witkowski and books titled The Hunt for Zero Point and Closer Encounters. If true, it would mean the Nazis were working on exotic flight technology far beyond ordinary aircraft.","nazi-bell-scientists-killed-1940":"During the Second World War, Nazi Germany is said to have run a secret device called the Bell, in territory that is now partly Czech, and it is said to have killed the scientists who worked on it. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels the claim as he found it in The Hunt for Zero Point, a book by the writer Nick Cook. In that version there were around 60 scientists, \"a bunch of them died from the effects of this,\" and the rest were executed at the end of the war \"so they wouldn't talk.\" The researcher Jason Jorjani tells it on a separate episode about what he calls Hitler's secret UFO programme. He adds that plants put around the device \"would turn to mulch,\" and he speaks of a first team of workers. He gives no figure of 60 and does not say the survivors were executed. If true, the Bell gave off something deadly enough that its makers were silenced.","nazi-disc-shaped-craft-projects-miethe-schriever-lutzow-1940":"In Nazi Germany in the 1940s, several engineers are said to have worked on separate attempts to build disc-shaped flying craft. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source, listing \"Miethe, Schriever, Lutzow\", meaning Richard Miethe, Rudolf Schriever and a man named Lutzow, and adding that \"four or five other individuals\" also claimed to have worked on such projects. If true, some flying-saucer sightings could trace back to human wartime engineering.","nazi-discs-brought-to-us-via-operation-paperclip-1940":"Material recovered from Nazi Germany in the 1940s, including disc-shaped craft, is said to have been shipped to the United States through Operation Paperclip, the postwar American effort that brought German scientists and technology over. Michael Herrera, a guest who says he saw a 300-foot craft in the Indonesian jungle, mentions this in passing as something he was told. He offers it as an example of how the US may have got hold of advanced craft to study and copy.","nazi-enriched-uranium-sent-to-the-us-by-submarine-under-an-s-1945":"In 1945, the story goes, the US made a deal with the Nazis, through the SS, to ship enriched uranium across by submarine, and that uranium went into the bombs dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, drawing on a book called Critical Mass by a writer named Plimpton Hydrick. If true, the American atomic bomb would have depended partly on Nazi material.","nazi-expeditions-searching-for-atlantis-1930":"In the 1930s and 40s the Nazi SS is said to have sent expeditions through Peru, Nepal and Tibet looking for traces of Atlantis, the lost land of Plato's story. A guest named Jay Anderson tells Michels this without naming a source, adding that there are photos of the expedition members with high lamas, with swastikas in the background.","nazi-expeditions-to-the-himalayas-in-search-of-aryan-myths-1930":"In the 1930s and 1940s, the Nazis sent expeditions to the Himalayas to look for evidence of their myths about an ancient Aryan race. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, mentions this to Michels, crediting it, with some hesitation, to a writer named Igor Witkowski: \"I think it was Whitowski who... described their expeditions to the Himalayas in search of these Aryan myths.\" It is told as part of the Nazi regime's pursuit of mystical and hidden knowledge.","nazi-flying-saucers-attempted-but-not-achieved":"The Nazis tried to build flying saucers but never had the technology to pull it off. That is the view of Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official, who tells Michels so without naming a source. He grants that there were wartime experiments and reports of \"foo fighters\", the glowing balls of light Allied pilots described over Bavaria, but he says that \"in reality they did not have the technology,\" which would mean the saucer stories do not lead back to Germany.","nazi-memorabilia-at-paperclip-facilities-1947":"In the late 1940s, swastikas and other Nazi memorabilia were reportedly on open display in some of the facilities used by Operation Paperclip, the US programme that brought German scientists to work on American rockets, at sites such as Fort Bliss and White Sands. Michels tells this himself, as something reported, without naming a source, to show how openly those scientists brought their past with them.","nazi-missions-to-antarctica":"The Nazis are said to have been fascinated by Antarctica and to have sent several missions there. Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, mentions it briefly to Michels without giving a source: \"they were fascinated Antarctica they had several missions down there.\" He raises it as a passing point, hinting that the frozen continent held some special interest for them.","nazi-occult-documents-found-in-a-cave-1945":"In 1945, near the end of the war, American forces found a hidden stash of Nazi documents in a cave in southern Germany, among them records of real state-sponsored occult projects. Michels tells it himself without naming a source, as a way into how occult ideas may have travelled into postwar science.","nazi-submarine-traffic-intercepted-near-patagonia-1946":"Around 1946, Nazi submarine traffic heading to Argentina, near Patagonia, was intercepted. A researcher named Jason Jorjani says so on the show, without naming a source or saying who did the intercepting. He raises it while talking about a hidden Nazi program, which it would suggest carried on in South America after the war.","nct-s-darpa-contract-and-military-ties-as-corroboration-1991":"In June 1991, Michels says, the Washington Post reported that a company called Noise Cancellation Technologies, of Linthicum, Maryland, had won a $210,000 research contract from DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency. Michels raises it himself on an episode with Dan Sherman, a former sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens. Michels also points to an Office of Naval Research bulletin and the company's own website, and offers the company's military ties as backing for parts of Sherman's account.","near-death-experience-after-poisoning-out-of-body-travel-and-1970":"In 1970 a man was poisoned in Austria and arrived dead at a clinic in Passau, Germany, and while he had no heartbeat he says he left his body, traveled, and saw a white light. Joe McMoneagle tells Michels this as his own near-death experience, saying he \"had not had a heartbeat for almost 40 minutes\" before he was revived.","near-death-experiencers-describe-living-buildings-with-doors":"People who have nearly died and come back describe seeing buildings that seem alive, with doors that open as if made of molten metal. The researcher Grant Cameron says he has been collecting such accounts and tells Michels, \"I've got a whole list of maybe 20 so far.\" If the reports line up, they would suggest that people in those moments are seeing something shared rather than something private.","nearly-2-000-experimental-variations-tracked-by-buer-and-dre-2016":"Since 2016 he and a colleague have tried close to 2,000 versions of their experiment, says Charles Buhler, a NASA engineer who works on a propulsion device that, as the episode title puts it, he calls antigravity, at a company in Florida called Exodus Propulsion Technologies. He tells Michels that he and Andrew Arjima, whom he calls Drew, have kept track and \"are close to 2,000.\" He offers the count as a sign of how thoroughly they have tested what they found.","neighbor-moved-to-roswell-to-see-ufos":"A man is said to have moved to Roswell, New Mexico, the town known for a reported UFO crash in the 1940s, because he wanted to be close to the sightings, and he claimed he did see something. An unnamed audience member tells this about his neighbor at a filmed event with David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who became a UFO whistleblower. The audience member does not say what the neighbor saw.","neighbor-saw-backyard-lit-up-like-a-hollywood-set-2017":"In 2017, a neighbour of Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear site, told him that his backyard in Georgia had been \"lit up like a Hollywood set.\" Woods passes on the neighbour's words as an outside account of a strange light at his home on the night he connects with an abduction.","neighboring-farmer-s-house-shook-at-5-a-m-soldiers-told-fami-1996":"At five in the morning, in 1996, a farmer living near the spot outside Varginha, Brazil, where a strange craft is said to have come down felt his whole house shake, and soldiers then told his family to stay inside. Carlos de Souza tells this on the show, passing on what the farmer said in film recordings kept by Claudia Kovu, the daughter of a researcher who studied the case. Asked if he noticed anything, the man answers: 'Of course I did. My entire house shook.' If true, it would put something physical hitting the ground near his farm at dawn, with the military already on the scene.","neil-armstrong-discusses-a-ufo-research-group-with-hynek-1970":"In the early 1970s, the astronaut Neil Armstrong asked to sit with J. Allen Hynek, an astronomer who studied UFO reports for the Air Force, and his family, and the two talked seriously about creating a new UFO research group. Hynek's son, who was there, tells this on the show. If true, it would mean the first man on the Moon took UFOs seriously enough to help study them.","neil-armstrong-s-1975-tayos-cave-expedition":"Michels tells this himself on several episodes: after the moon landing, Neil Armstrong went with a BBC film crew to the Tayos cave in Ecuador, at the edge of the Amazon, which was rumored to hold ancient alien metal artifacts and a kind of hidden library. He usually dates it to 1975 and calls it Armstrong's second expedition, though once he says 1976, and he links the cave to Erich von Däniken, author of the book Chariots of the Gods, and to a man named Luis Felipe San Salvador. To Michels it is striking that the first man on the moon followed up with a hunt for an alien cave.","neil-armstrong-s-truth-s-protective-layers-speech-1980":"Neil Armstrong is said to have given a speech hinting that hidden truths remain, saying that \"there are great ideas undiscovered\" and \"breakthroughs available to those who can remove one of truth's protective layers.\" The tellers place it differently: Michels remembers it as the 1980s at some medal ceremony, while Joe Rogan, on the show, puts it 25 years after the moon landing and before top high school students, and another episode places it at the White House in 1994. Those who repeat it read the words as a quiet nod to things the public has not been told.","neil-armstrong-said-to-have-seen-ufos-on-the-moon-1969":"A long-told story says that Neil Armstrong saw UFOs on the Moon in 1969 and came back home \"super scared.\" Michels mentions it himself, in passing, as a well-known claim he has heard, not one he backs with a source. If true, it would mean the first Moon landing met something there that the public was never told about.","neil-armstrong-seemed-scared-at-his-speech-after-the-moon-la-1969":"Neil Armstrong, back from the 1969 moon landing, is said to have looked \"very kind of scared and sheepish\" while giving a speech about three weeks later, as he called it a big step for mankind. Michels describes this himself, as his own impression, in an episode with the writer Randall Carlson titled \"The Moon Is An Alien Spaceship\". The suggestion is that Armstrong saw something on the moon that unsettled him.","neil-armstrong-we-were-told-not-to-come-back-to-the-moon-1970":"Neil Armstrong is said to have told people in Washington in the 1970s, \"We were told not to come back to the moon.\" Michels calls these \"apocryphal stories\" that he has heard. Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects some people say were implanted by aliens, says Armstrong was overheard saying it at a party. Colbern says the warning came from the \"grays,\" the small grey beings of many alien accounts, which would mean someone else wanted the moon left alone.","nellis-object-as-balloons-drifting-in-the-wind-1994":"An object filmed in 1994 over the Nellis and Tonopah ranges, military training ranges in the Nevada desert, is \"just a balloon or a bunch of balloons floating in the wind.\" That is what the UFO skeptic Mick West tells Michels as his own reading of the video, without naming a source. If he is right, the footage shows nothing unusual.","nellis-range-face-to-face-encounter-1994":"In August 1994, on the Nellis testing range in Nevada, a man named Jason Sands says he came face to face with someone dressed in a military outfit who, in his words, \"did not appear to be human\". A UFO investigator named James Fox tells the story from Sands's account, which he included in his project The Program. Michels later recalls it as Sands seeing a \"blue alien being\" at Nellis, which would mean non-human beings working alongside the military on a US range.","nematode-revived-after-46-000-years-in-siberian-permafrost-2023":"A nematode, a tiny worm, is said to have been brought back to life in 2023 after 46,000 years frozen in the Siberian permafrost, the ground that stays frozen all year. A guest called Dr. Gilbert mentions this to Michels as something that \"was reported\", without naming the report. He brings it up to show how long life can survive in a dormant state.","network-of-underground-tunnels-beneath-the-giza-plateau-2025":"Under the Giza Plateau in Egypt, home of the great pyramids, radar scans are said to show 'something like a network of underground tunnels.' Filippo Biondi, a scientist who scanned beneath the pyramids in 2025 together with a colleague named Armando Mei, tells Michels this from his own work. He says the tomographic data, images built up in slices from the scans, showed 'a mess' of reflections, which if right would mean large hidden structures below the plateau.","neuralink-implant-immune-reaction-2024":"Michels says that the first patient of Neuralink, Elon Musk's brain implant company, a man named Nolan Arba, may have needed some adjusting after the device first worked, because of an immune reaction. He says it himself, hedged with \"I believe,\" in an episode about a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, where it serves as a comparison for how the body reacts to something placed inside it.","neurologist-steers-jim-toward-john-mack-and-ray-fowler-1976":"In 1976, while Jim Weiner was in the hospital in the Boston area, a neurologist named Jonathan Silverman pointed him toward people who looked into UFO encounters. Weiner, who says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels about it himself. He says that a few days into his stay the doctor came into his room and told him about \"a UFO investigator\" named Raymond Fowler who would be speaking at a conference at a Marriott hotel, and the doctor also named John Mack.","neurotypical-woman-with-a-head-injury-reaches-the-space-non-2020":"A neurotypical woman with a head injury is said to be able to \"touch the space that non-speakers can touch\". Non-speakers here are people who cannot talk, whom the documentary series The Telepathy Tapes says can share thoughts by mind. Ky Dickens, who made the series, passes this on from one of the people she came across, as a hint that this mental space is not limited to non-speakers.","nevada-test-fallout-theory-dismissed-as-baseless":"One idea is that fallout from nuclear testing in Nevada might explain the strange things reported on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of odd events. Its owner, Brandon Fugal, takes it up with Michels, saying \"some of the testing in Nevada could have,\" though the conversation is recorded as him setting the theory aside as baseless. If it held, it would put an ordinary cause behind at least some of the ranch's mysteries.","never-before-seen-footage-of-a-ufo-at-area-51":"There is said to be \"legendary, never-before-seen footage\" of a UFO at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert. Michels announces it himself at the start of an episode and says nothing about who filmed it or when.","new-data-show-the-object-separated-from-the-seabed-2025":"New survey data from 2025 show that the Baltic Sea anomaly, a strange round shape lying on the floor of the Baltic Sea, appears to be separated from the seabed beneath it. Dennis Asberg, the treasure hunter whose team found it, tells Michels this himself, adding only: \"That's what I can say.\" If true, it would mean the object sits on the bottom rather than being part of the rock.","new-hoffmanlevin-collaboration-on-recursive-trace-logic-2026":"Donald Hoffman, a scientist who studies perception and consciousness, says he and a scientist named Mike Levin are 'just now starting up a collaboration' in 2026. He tells Michels they plan to hire a postdoctoral researcher to work on what he calls 'recursive trace logic,' a piece of mathematics he does not explain further.","new-jersey-and-uk-base-drone-sightings-2024":"In late 2024, strange objects described as drones were reported hovering over sensitive military sites in New Jersey and over bases in Britain. Michels says in his own framing that this shows the link between UFOs and nuclear sites is \"ongoing\". Elsewhere he names Picatinny Arsenal, where he says they first showed up, the Lakenheath base in England, the Salem nuclear power plant in New Jersey and a plant in Brunswick, North Carolina, and he credits the researcher Robert Hastings. A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff, speaking with the skeptic Mick West, stresses how official it got: the FBI and the FAA, the agency that runs US air traffic, were involved, drone flight bans were put in place, and so were the governor and \"all the elected officials.\" The skeptic Michael Shermer calls it \"the New Jersey swarm\" and ties its timing to Trump's 2024 campaign, while a guest named Harald Malmgren, speaking second-hand, says \"we have unidentified objects flying all over the United States.\"","new-jersey-drone-flap-and-objects-emerging-from-the-water-2024":"In late 2024, people along the New Jersey coast reported strange flying objects, called drones at the time, and some said these UAPs (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) seemed to rise out of the sea. Michels raises it on several shows, in his own words. He says there were sightings offshore where the objects 'seemed to be emerging from the water'. He credits a sheriff with reporting that they came out of the water. He also quotes Donald Trump as saying of the Jersey drones, 'These are coming out of the water. We know where the garage is.' Michels also points to a Rolling Stone article on the drones and to a man named Greg Scaduto. A UFO historian named Richard Dolan tells it separately, without naming a source, and adds that the objects were said to have harassed US Coast Guard ships. If true, it would mean something unknown operates from under the ocean near the US coast.","new-jersey-drone-flap-and-the-disclosure-day-shoot-2024":"In November 2024 a wave of drone and UFO sightings swept New Jersey, and one theory links it to the filming of Disclosure Day, a Steven Spielberg film about aliens. Michels says the theory belongs to his friend Chris Ramsay, who runs a show called Area 52, and that the film was shot in New Jersey in late 2024. In a later episode Michels tells it his own way: production began in February 2025, a few months after the sightings, and he names Morris County and the Picatinny Arsenal military site, without crediting Ramsay.","new-nautics-and-university-of-florida-project-to-hack-the-br-1970":"A company called New Nautics has hired a team at the University of Florida to find ways to \"hack\" the brain's own DMT system. DMT is a powerful psychedelic that the body also makes on its own in small amounts, and the project is called EDMTX. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, who says he is part of the effort, tells Michels the aim is \"to actually develop techniques to hack the brain's endogenous DMT system,\" meaning the DMT the brain makes for itself.","new-parapsychology-degree-program-at-the-california-institut-2026":"A California school now offers doctoral and master's degrees with a specialisation in parapsychology, the study of claimed psychic abilities, says Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a doctorate in the field who says he helped create the program. He tells Michels in 2026 that he \"just helped to create\" it at the California Institute for Human Science. He tells it as a sign that the subject is gaining a foothold in formal education.","new-start-treaty-to-reduce-nuclear-weapons-2010":"Under President Obama, the US signed a treaty with Russia to cut the number of nuclear weapons kept ready for use, a treaty called New START. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas mentions it on the show from memory, first calling it SALT and then correcting himself, while talking about nuclear missiles and the UFO sightings around them.","new-tall-white-arrivals-come-too-close-at-range-three-1965":"One night in 1965, about a week after the full moon, a group of newly arrived \"tall whites\", tall pale beings he says lived near a Nevada air base, came too close to Charles Hall while he was out at Range Three in Indian Springs Valley. Hall, who says he worked there as an Air Force weather observer and spent years around these beings, tells Michels this as his own experience. If true, it would mean non-human beings were living alongside US military staff.","new-zealand-archives-reveal-kaikoura-cover-up-and-squid-boat-1978":"New Zealand government archives released around 2011 are said to show a cover-up of the Kaikoura sightings, the 1978 reports of strange lights off New Zealand's coast, and to show where the official explanation that the lights were squid fishing boats came from. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels he found the release himself. He does not say which files said what, and he dates the release only as \"about 2011.\"","newgrange-curbstone-with-perplexing-symbols":"Outside Newgrange, an ancient monument in Ireland, there is a curbstone \"inscribed with all of these perplexing symbols\". A guest named Geoffrey Drumm describes it to Michels without naming a source, treating the carvings as a puzzle about what the ancient builders knew.","newly-found-mysterious-sites-in-eastern-turkey-2001":"In eastern Turkey, in the Ararat region that was once part of Armenia, Matt LaCroix says he 'stumbled across these very mysterious sites that were brand new,' meaning newly found. LaCroix, a guest who researches ancient places, tells Michels this as his own discovery, sometime between 2001 and 2017, and offers it as evidence of an older, little-known past in the region.","news-clip-victory-learning-center-employees-put-on-leave":"A local news report played on the show says that OLV Human Services, which runs the Victory Learning Center in Lackawanna, New York, put three employees there on leave over \"allegations of physically mistreating students\". The clip comes up in an interview with a guest who says he was taken into a UFO programme as a child, and it is shown as outside record that harm happened at a school tied to his story.","news-report-of-the-oklahoma-city-bombing-1995":"An archival news report is played on the show describing the 1995 Oklahoma City bombing: \"A massive car bomb exploded outside of a large federal building in downtown Oklahoma City, shattering that building, killing children, killing federal employees, military men, and civilians.\" The unnamed reporter's clip appears in an episode about a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies.","news-report-survivors-of-the-plane-crash-at-jamaica-hospital":"An unnamed news reporter, heard in a clip on the show, says that Jamaica Hospital in New York \"received 14 survivors from the plane crash.\" The clip comes up in an episode with a guest named Jason Jorjani about what he says was Hitler's secret UFO program, and the clip itself does not say which crash it was or when it happened.","newsnation-finds-nothing-in-the-vatican-files-1933":"The American news channel NewsNation went to the Vatican, searched its files for records tied to a claim dated to 1933, and said it could not find anything. Michael Shermer, a researcher and skeptic, says this to Michels without naming where he heard it, as a point against the claim.","newsnation-slide-showing-a-doe-gravity-propulsion-patient":"A slide shown on NewsNation, a US news channel that Michels says covers UFOs a lot, is said to show a patient hurt in Department of Energy work on advanced gravity propulsion, with damage to the brain's white matter. Michels brings up the slide himself, once in a talk that touches on the journalist Ross Coulthart and the researcher Eric Weinstein, and once naming the slide \"advanced gravity propulsion research\" and tying the man's injuries to Havana syndrome, which he describes as some sort of biological effects. If true, it would suggest the government has been running exotic propulsion experiments that harm the people near them.","newton-and-gebauer-framed-in-fraud-trial-over-doodlebug-devi-1950":"In the 1950s two men, Silas Newton and Leo GeBauer, were tried for fraud over a gadget called a doodlebug, and some researchers say they were set up and treated unfairly. The two men are linked to the story of a flying saucer said to have crashed near Aztec, New Mexico. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels about it, praising William Steinman's book on the Aztec case. He says Steinman went to Colorado and went through the court records, and found that Newton and GeBauer \"were unfairly attacked in their court case.\" Scott Ramsey, a researcher of the Aztec crash, tells it in a later episode with more detail. He puts the trial in Denver in 1953, names a man called Herman Flader as part of the story, and says the case went through seven judges, each of whom refused it: \"No, I'm not going to hear this case.\" Both men are summarising the records and the books rather than speaking from their own memory. If they are right, the fraud case was used to discredit two men who had spoken about a crashed saucer.","newton-diaries-sarbacher-tells-wilbur-smith-ufos-are-top-sec-1950":"In 1950, the story goes, an American named Robert Sarbacher told Wilbert Smith, the Canadian radio engineer who ran Canada's first project on UFOs, that the US government was studying flying saucers in secret, through a group headed by Vannevar Bush. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells this to Michels on two episodes. The first time he credits it to the diaries of a man named Silas Newton. The second time he credits it instead to a 1950 memo from Canada's Department of Transport and to a book called The Flying Saucer Conspiracy, and brings in Frank Scully. In that version he says Sarbacher told Smith there were \"meetings about flying saucers\" and that \"the US government studies them.\" If true, it would mean UFOs were already a top-secret matter at the highest levels of American science in 1950.","newton-saucer-settled-gently-within-500-miles-of-denver-1948":"Sometime around 1948 to 1950, a round flying saucer came down gently, without crashing, somewhere within 500 miles of Denver, according to a man named Silas Newton, heard on the show in his own words. Newton says that there \"settled gently to Earth a round object which had already been identified as being a flying saucer.\" If true, it would mean an intact craft was recovered in the American West.","next-30-years-a-danger-time-in-the-taurid-stream-2020":"The next 30 years are said to be a danger period for Earth as it passes through the Taurid stream, a band of comet debris our planet crosses every year. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels this, passing on a warning from the Comet Research Group, a team of scientists that includes a researcher named Allen West. If they are right, it would mean the risk of a damaging impact from that debris is higher than usual for decades.","nhi-communication-ci-wizards-are-messing-with-the-timeline":"Someone is said to have communicated with a non-human intelligence (the field's term for beings that are not human, often shortened to NHI), asked it why it was here, and been told: \"CI wizards are messing with the timeline.\" A commentator named Matthew Pines relays this on the show from what a person he knows told him, without naming that person here. He tells it twice, and the second time the answer comes out as \"CIA wizards are messing with the timeline.\"","nhi-component-to-the-new-jersey-drone-flap-2024":"The wave of drone sightings over New Jersey in 2024 had, in part, an NHI component, meaning non-human intelligence was involved alongside other things. Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he once lifted a UFO with his aircraft, gives this to Michels as his own view. If true, it would mean some of those lights were not human-made at all.","nhi-testing-us-at-missile-fields":"Non-human intelligence, or NHI, meaning whatever beings might be behind UFOs, shows up at nuclear missile fields to test humanity. That is the view of Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist who holds patents for unusual craft designs, who tells Michels plainly, \"Of course, they're testing us.\" If so, the sightings over nuclear weapons would be a deliberate probe, not chance.","nhtcu-meets-oni-doj-extradition-threat-of-70-years-2002":"In November 2002, Britain's National Hi-Tech Crime Unit, a police unit for computer crime, met with the Office of Naval Intelligence and the US Department of Justice about Gary McKinnon, and the Americans then threatened to have him extradited to face what was 'on paper 70 years in jail.' McKinnon, the British hacker who got into US military and NASA computers, tells Michels this as what happened to him. It shows how seriously Washington treated his case.","nicap-s-founding-as-a-possible-link-to-the-1956-cutoff":"The founding of NICAP, which Michels calls \"the first civilian UFO research program,\" came in 1956 and 1957, around the same time as a cutoff in the records that the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel studies, and Michels suggests the two may be linked. He offers this as his own idea while talking with Villarroel. If there is a link, a change in what was seen or recorded may have pushed ordinary people to start studying UFOs themselves.","nick-cook-rebuffed-by-a-scared-george-trimble-1990":"When the aviation journalist Nick Cook tried to meet George Trimble, a long-retired aerospace man then in his eighties, through a friend at Lockheed Martin, Trimble refused and seemed frightened. Michels first told the story himself in 2023, and in 2024 Cook told it to him firsthand, also naming the Martin company: \"Trimble didn't want to speak to me, and he was really quite spooked at the idea of speaking to me.\" Cook was looking into secret anti-gravity research, and he takes the old man's fear as a sign that Trimble knew something he would not say.","nick-cook-s-search-for-1950s-gravity-research":"A British aviation journalist named Nick Cook, of Jane's Defence Weekly, is said to have stumbled on a body of 1950s research into controlling gravity, only to find that it suddenly vanished and led nowhere. Michels tells this himself, drawing on Cook's book The Hunt for Zero Point and calling Cook a hard-headed journalist, which matters because it suggests that serious anti-gravity work may have gone secret rather than failed.","nick-pope-s-death-from-cancer-2020":"A man named Nick Pope died of cancer a couple of years ago. That is what Michael Shermer, a well-known skeptic, says in passing to Michels, describing Pope as the husband of a woman named Elizabeth Weiss. He names no source, and the remark comes up only briefly in their debate.","nids-study-correlating-triangle-sightings-to-us-naval-bases-1990":"A study by NIDS, a private group that researched UFOs, is said to have matched reports of large triangular craft against the map and found they cluster around US naval bases. Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official turned whistleblower, calls it \"that famous\" study and tells Michels it was done \"I think late 90s or early 2000s,\" without naming the report or giving its findings in detail. If it holds, the link would point to the triangles being tied to the Navy.","night-aircraft-spotting-with-lear-outside-tonopah-1980":"In the 1980s he went out into the Nevada desert at night with binoculars, outside the town of Tonopah, to watch for secret aircraft, says Bob Lazar, a man who has long said he worked on alien craft in Nevada. He tells Michels he did this with a man named John Lear, \"out in the desert in the middle of the night with binoculars and stuff.\" He tells it as part of how he came to be looking at what the military was flying out there.","night-intrusion-at-the-cabin-when-the-implant-was-put-in-1989":"One night in 1989 at his cabin in upstate New York, Whitley Strieber says he turned and saw a woman already in the room near the foot of the bed with a man beside her, while a male voice in the backyard said \"Condition red\"; he ties this night to an implant, a small object he says was placed in his body. Strieber, a writer who says he has met alien beings, tells Michels this as something he lived through. If true, it would mean people, not only beings, came into his home that night.","night-meeting-with-military-x-about-the-varginha-operation-1996":"The documentary filmmaker and UFO investigator James Fox says he drove some four hours into the night, deep into Brazil north of the town of Varginha, to meet an unnamed military source he calls 'military X.' The subject was the 1996 Varginha case, in which residents and soldiers reported that a strange creature was seen and captured. Fox tells Michels he made the trip himself, and the meeting matters because it would give him an inside military account of the operation.","night-meeting-with-puthoff-and-hameroff-about-microtubules-2026":"In 2026, Michels says, he spent an evening in a room with the scientist Hal Puthoff and Stuart Hameroff, talking about microtubules, the tiny tube-shaped structures inside cells. Michels tells this himself, as something that happened \"last night.\" He describes Hameroff as the man responsible for \"orchestrated objective reduction,\" a theory that consciousness comes from quantum effects inside those microtubules.","night-of-shifting-timelines-in-an-la-airbnb-2024":"One night in 2024 or 2025, in an Airbnb in Los Angeles, a voice told him \"Timelines\" as reality seemed to shift around him, says Brent Friedman, a Hollywood writer who says it happened to him. He tells Michels he was in the shower, trying to wind down and repeating a calming mantra, when he heard the voice say the word. He tells it as a sign that something was reaching him and showing him more than one version of events.","night-scarb-s-measurements-of-the-osiris-shaft-scan-2024":"Measurements from a scan of the Osiris shaft, a deep shaft beneath the Giza plateau in Egypt, are said to show readings that do not match what is known to be down there. The researcher Jeffrey Drum shows this on the show, crediting it to an online channel called Night Scarb that, he says, \"did measurements that show that the signatures are off and that the measurements don't exactly match the known configuration.\" Drum offers it as a sign that something unknown may lie under the pyramids.","night-sky-suddenly-bright-in-the-brookhaven-incident-1992":"Around 7 p.m. one November night in 1992 in Brookhaven, Long Island, the dark sky suddenly became \"lighter out than it is right now\", in daytime. An unnamed witness says this in a clip played on the show. If true, it would point to some powerful unexplained light over the area that night.","night-visitors-in-the-twins-childhood-home-1950":"In the 1950s, in their family home near Allentown, Pennsylvania, twin brothers Jim Weiner and Jack Weiner, who shared a bedroom, would wake at night to find 'something pulling the covers off our beds.' Jim Weiner tells Michels this from his own childhood, as one of the strange night-time events he says happened in that house.","nightly-3-a-m-wake-ups-and-communing-2015":"The writer Whitley Strieber says that from 2015 to 2020, beings he has long described meeting woke him at his home around 3 a.m. \"religiously every night\" so he could commune with them. He tells Michels this from his own experience, which he also writes about in his book The Hidden Garden. If true, it would mean steady, nightly contact with non-human visitors over five years.","nile-freshwater-mussels-found-120150-ft-above-the-floodplain":"Freshwater mussels and the remains of other Nile animals have been found 120 to 150 feet above the Nile's present-day floodplain. A researcher named Randall Carlson says this on the show without naming a source. If true, it would mean the river once ran far higher than it does now.","nimitz-encounter-as-multisensory-evidence-2004":"Jesse Michels calls the 2004 encounters off San Diego by the Navy's Nimitz carrier group, where pilots saw the \"Tic Tac\", a white oval object shaped like the mint, the strongest kind of evidence: 11 or 12 witnesses over three days, radar tracks, what the Navy fighter pilot David Fravor saw with his own eyes, and released FLIR video, footage from a forward-looking infrared camera. He makes this point himself on several episodes, once comparing it to the profile of a case involving a Japan Airlines crew, to argue that so many separate sensors agreeing is hard to explain away.","nimitz-tic-tac-acceleration-and-power-estimates-2004":"In 2004, off Southern California near the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz, the \"Tic Tac\", a white oval object shaped like the mint, is said to have dropped from 28,000 feet to sea level in a tiny fraction of time, which implies huge acceleration and power. Kevin Knuth, a scientist who has studied the case, gives his team's estimate on the show, though his figure comes out garbled: \"we estimate the acceleration of the Tic Tac falling from 28,000 feet to sea level at 7.5 seconds or so, 78 seconds.\" Numbers like these would put the object far beyond any known aircraft.","nimitz-tic-tac-incident-2004":"In 2004, off San Diego, Navy pilots from the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz, including a fighter pilot named David Fravor, reported a Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint. Michels calls it one of the \"holy grail\" events of the subject and the case on which \"so many people rest their conviction\", and on another episode adds that Steven Spielberg cites it as the reason for his renewed interest in UFOs. Michels tells it himself both times, without naming a source, and if what the pilots described is right, it would be a craft beyond any known human technology.","nina-kulagina-stops-the-hearts-of-animals":"The Soviets, once they had learned of this kind of research, had a woman named Nina Kulagina (Lyn Buchanan pronounces it \"Cologa\") who could stop the hearts of rats, rabbits and other animals. Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would mean a person's mind could reach into a living body and stop it.","nine-craft-seen-through-the-open-hangar-bay-doors-1988":"Nine flying craft, he says, were parked in a row of hangars at S-4, a secret site in the Nevada desert where he says he worked in 1988 and 1989. Bob Lazar tells Michels this first-hand: looking down the line of hangars, he saw \"other cars, nine of them all the way down there,\" which would mean the government kept a whole fleet, not just one. Pressed on the number, he says \"nine for sure\" and limits himself to \"what I've seen.\"","nine-dr-g-scientists-traced-to-bush-s-osrd-network-1940":"The book Behind the Flying Saucers tells of a mysterious scientist it calls Dr. G, and the researcher Scott Ramsey says he and his team traced nine real scientists behind that figure to the network of the wartime science chief Vannevar Bush, whose Office of Scientific Research and Development, or OSRD, ran American war research in World War II. Ramsey says that when they went through Bush's group division by division, they found names such as Richard C. Tolman, John Torrance Tate and the Texas Instruments men Cecil Howard Green and Eugene McDermott. If he is right, the people behind an early flying saucer story were wartime science insiders.","nine-page-pentagon-uap-report-2021":"In 2021 the US government put out a short report admitting there are objects in American skies that it cannot explain. Michels gives the date and the gist: \"On June 25th, 2021, the Pentagon released a report acknowledging the existence of objects in our sky that we cannot explain.\" The mathematician and commentator Eric Weinstein, speaking as an outside reader, says it was nine pages, \"six of which are relevant,\" written to be \"as dull as is humanly possible about the most exciting thing that you could possibly imagine.\" To him it meant one of two things. Either it was a psychological operation coming out of the Pentagon, or some people there believe \"with near certainty\" that Earth has been visited and watched by extraterrestrials. In a later episode Weinstein calls it a preliminary assessment from the office of the Director of National Intelligence on UAP, the official term for unidentified aerial phenomena. He says he could detect two voices in it: one \"that wanted to disclose more and one that said over my dead body.\" So the story rests on the host's summary and on one reader's interpretation of the text, and Weinstein's view of what it reveals is a reading, not something the report states.","nine-vanishing-transients-on-1950s-plates-follow-up-with-gra":"Nine points of light that show up on photographs of the sky from the 1950s Palomar Sky Survey, a photographic map of the whole sky, are now gone: a friend of the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel pointed the Gran Telescopio Canarias, a 10.4-metre telescope in the Canary Islands and the world's largest optical telescope, at \"all these nine objects or eight of them\" and \"there was nothing left.\" Villarroel tells Michels this as her own work and mentions a US Navy star catalogue alongside the old images. If the objects were real, something bright was in those spots in the 1950s and has since disappeared.","ning-li-and-podkletnov-rotating-superconductors":"Michels says a researcher named Ning Li worked with a spinning disc of a superconducting material, and that another researcher, Eugene Podkletnov, also used rotating superconductors. He brings these up himself, without naming a source, in an episode with a Navy scientist known for patents on UFO-like craft, as earlier work of the same kind on strange physics that might lead to new ways of moving through the air.","ning-li-and-smalley-took-400-000-in-army-research-lab-money-1990":"Two researchers at the University of Alabama in Huntsville, Ning Li and Larry Smalley, took $400,000 from the Army Research Lab and delivered nothing for it. That is what Eric W. Davis says on the show, a scientist presented as having worked with the Pentagon: they \"made off with\" the money and \"didn't deliver anything,\" and he also names a colleague, Travis Taylor. If true, a government-funded research claim came to nothing.","ning-li-email-intercepted-by-west-point-ip-2000":"Around the early 2000s, emails with a scientist named Ning Li are said to have been intercepted by a computer address belonging to West Point, after which she was never heard from again. Dave Rossi, a guest who says aliens taught him advanced physics, tells this on the show as something he was told. If true, it would suggest her work drew the quiet attention of the military.","ning-li-last-seen-at-mitre-anti-gravity-conference-2003":"A physicist named Ning Lee, who worked on anti-gravity, is said to have made her last public appearance in 2003 at a conference on anti-gravity held by MITRE, a research company that works for the US government, near Washington. Michels says this himself in two episodes, noting it was the last time she spoke \"on record\" about her work. The point of telling it is that she then dropped out of public view.","ning-li-s-disappearance-after-breakthrough-research-1990":"A scientist named Ning Li is said to have been doing breakthrough research, which one teller says was on anti-gravity, and then simply vanished. The researcher Matthew Pines brings her up on the show without naming a source, as one of the people \"clearly doing like breakthrough research on the margins of some interesting subjects\" who then \"sort of poof, they disappear.\" The podcaster Joe Rogan tells it second hand and never names her: \"There's a story about this Chinese scientist that was working on anti-gravity and vanished.\" Both tell it as a sign that work on this kind of physics gets quietly pulled out of sight.","ning-li-s-gravito-magnetic-research-moves-to-redstone-arsena-1990":"A physicist named Ning Li, who studied possible links between gravity and magnetism, is said to have left the University of Alabama-Huntsville in the 1990s to work full time at Redstone Arsenal, a military site in Alabama, on research \"so sensitive, it effectively vanished from public view.\" Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in two episodes. In the second, on scientists who go missing, he adds that her story \"took an even darker turn in 2014 when she was struck by a car on the University of Alabama campus and suffered permanent brain damage.\" He tells it as a case of promising gravity research disappearing into secrecy.","ning-li-s-last-talk-was-at-a-mitre-conference-before-she-wen":"A physicist named Ning Li last spoke in public at a conference held by MITRE, a research organisation that works for the US government, and then, Michels says, \"kind of went into the black,\" meaning she disappeared into secret classified work. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. If true, it would mean her research was taken behind closed doors.","ning-li-s-spinning-superconductors-nasa-fails-to-reproduce-1990":"A researcher named Ning Li is said to have found an unusual effect from spinning superconductors, materials that carry electricity with no resistance, in work tied to the hunt for antigravity. Charles Buhler, whom the episode presents as a NASA figure, tells Michels that NASA, as he thinks, tried to reproduce it and did not see the effect.","ning-li-s-superconductor-antigravity-work-and-company-1990":"A scientist named Ning Li, working at the University of Alabama in Huntsville under a man named Larry Smalley, is said to have studied making objects lighter using superconductors, materials that carry electricity with no resistance when very cold. Michels says she published, with credit to a colleague named Douglas Torr, that the masses she tested lost between 0.5% and 2.1% of their weight across several trials, and the story has her later starting a company around the work. Michels tells it himself on two episodes, without naming a source, and if the result held up it would be a first step toward controlling gravity.","ning-li-superconductor-gravity-shielding-and-anti-gravity-ll-1990":"A physicist named Ning Li, working at the University of Alabama in Huntsville from the 1990s into the 2000s, claimed that superconductors spun very fast made things above them weigh less, which Michels says she explained as emitted gravitons, particles of gravity, shielding against gravity; she later founded a company to pursue it. Michels brings this up on several episodes, once dating the work to the early 2000s and tying it to ceramic superconductors described in a book by an author named James Lacatski, and in another simply saying she \"claims to have a mass reduction effect over a spinning superconductor.\" Li herself, on the show, says \"nobody found any mistake in my calculation,\" which, if right, would mean a working way to weaken gravity was found in a lab.","nitinol-developed-from-the-roswell-crash-1947":"A metal called nitinol was developed from material recovered at Roswell, New Mexico, where in 1947 something crashed that UFO lore holds was an alien craft. The researcher Grant Cameron says this to Michels, hedging as he does: \"some like metal nitall I think was something developed from the Roswell crash.\" If true, it would mean a real material in use today came from studying an alien wreck.","nixon-calls-hughes-to-set-up-a-castro-assassination-as-told-1960":"In 1960, the story goes, Richard Nixon called Howard Hughes to arrange the killing of Fidel Castro, and the plan ran through Hughes's man Robert Maheu to the mob figures Johnny Roselli and Sam Giancana. A lawyer named Dan Sheehan says he heard it all straight from the mob boss Santo Trafficante Jr., who he says was a client of the law firm where Sheehan worked. In one of his two tellings Sheehan adds more names, Sheffield Edwards and Paul Helliwell, along with the Fontainebleau Hotel and a group he calls the S Force.","nixon-ended-the-apollo-program-and-scrapped-the-saturn-fleet-1969":"Richard Nixon ended the Apollo moon programme and scrapped the fleet of giant Saturn rockets that carried it. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels this without naming a source, and offers the \"more prosaic explanation\": the Vietnam War was costing so much money that Nixon pulled the plug. He sets that ordinary reason beside the stranger ones discussed on the show.","nixon-haldeman-smoking-gun-tape-1972":"Dan Sheehan, a lawyer, points Michels to the 1972 White House tape known as the \"Smoking Gun\" conversation, which he places on 21 June, between President Nixon and his chief of staff H.R. Haldeman. As Sheehan tells it, Haldeman comes into the office and says, \"Mr President I think we've got a problem here.\" Sheehan reads the tape alongside the roles of FBI chief Pat Gray, CIA officials Vernon Walters and Richard Helms, and Nixon aide John Ehrlichman, as proof of what lay behind Watergate.","nixon-headed-the-5412-committee-under-eisenhower-1950":"Michels says he believes Richard Nixon headed the 5412 committee under Eisenhower in the 1950s, which he describes as a plan for keeping the government running in a catastrophe. He says it himself, hedged, in an episode with a UFO researcher, as part of a picture in which secret groups at the top of government handled the UFO question.","nixon-on-whether-the-cia-conspired-to-remove-him-1972":"Richard Nixon, asked whether the CIA plotted to bring him down, said he did not know, but found it hard to understand why the CIA, \"which apparently had advanced knowledge of the break-in,\" never warned him. The words are Nixon's own, in a clip of him played on the show, about the Watergate break-in that led to his fall. If the CIA knew in advance and said nothing, that would point to the agency letting his presidency sink.","nixon-s-1960-assassination-team-killed-jfk":"An assassination team that Richard Nixon set up in 1960 was the team that killed President Kennedy in Dallas. Dan Sheehan says this on the show, passing on what he was told and citing names including Santo Trafficante Jr., Andy Toney and William Johnstone Taylor. If true, it would tie the Kennedy killing to a secret operation started years earlier.","nixon-s-filing-cabinet-folder-of-alien-body-photographs-1969":"President Richard Nixon is said to have kept a folder in his filing cabinet holding photographs of dead alien bodies, which he would show to people. The researcher Grant Cameron repeats this story on the show and says he believes it is true. If so, it would mean a sitting president had direct proof of alien bodies close at hand.","nixon-sets-up-operation-40-after-castro-refuses-his-terms-1959":"Richard Nixon is said to have set up a covert operation called Operation 40 around 1959 and 1960, after Fidel Castro refused his terms for Cuba. The lawyer and activist Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, saying Nixon \"then set up this covert operation, which was called originally operation 40,\" and also names the mobster Santo Trafficante Jr. in the account. Sheehan tells it as part of a hidden chain of events he links to JFK and to UFO secrecy.","nixon-shows-brezhnev-a-folder-moon-flights-stop-1970":"The story goes that in the early 1970s, at a summit between the US and the Soviet Union, President Richard Nixon opened a small folder and showed it to the Soviet leader Leonid Brezhnev. Yuri Andropov, then head of the Soviet secret police, was in the room. In the story's telling, what Nixon shared was about UFOs, and the American flights to the moon came to an end around that time. The fullest version comes from Kiran Ilum Xenov, the former president of a Russian republic, who tells Michels he heard it from Mikhail Gorbachev, the later Soviet leader. Xenov describes the folder with his hands: 'a small folder like this that he opened and showed to Brev,' with 'Andropov … present at that meeting.' Michels repeats the story back to him in his own words. In his version Nixon gave the Soviets 'information … about what the CIA and the White House knew about UFOs and aliens,' and he says nothing of the folder or the moon flights. If it were true, the two Cold War rivals would have quietly shared their UFO secrets at the highest level.","nixon-takes-jackie-gleason-to-homestead-afb-1973":"The story goes that in 1973 President Richard Nixon turned up at the home of his friend, the actor Jackie Gleason, and took him to Homestead Air Force Base in Florida to see secret UFO material. Michels tells it on several episodes as a \"famous story\", once saying Nixon told Gleason \"we have secret UFO technology\", and once that Nixon, possibly drunk and with no Secret Service along, showed him alien bodies at a secret facility sometime in the 1970s. He gives the source as Gleason's wife, Beverly McKittrick, and a 1983 interview in the National Enquirer. Joe Rogan, citing a magazine article, says on the show that at Homestead Gleason saw a recovered crashed UFO and \"biological alien bodies that were in freezers\", and was so shaken he became \"a UFO nut.\" The television writer Bryce Zabel also traces the story to Gleason's wife. Grant Cameron, a UFO researcher, says McKittrick told them that either Gleason was out with other women that night or it really happened, because \"he was really rattled when he came home.\" Everyone here is passing on what McKittrick said, so they differ mainly on how much detail they add. If true, a sitting president personally showed alien remains to a celebrity friend.","nixon-used-malmgren-to-check-kissinger-1969":"Richard Nixon is said to have used a man named Harold Malmgren to check on Henry Kissinger in many negotiations, to make sure Kissinger \"wasn't going rogue\". A researcher named Matthew Pines tells Michels this in two episodes without naming a source, once dating it to Nixon's whole presidency and once to 1971 to 1974. He raises it as a sign of how far Nixon distrusted the man some link to secret programs.","nixonford-era-decision-to-privatize-the-legacy-program-1969":"Sometime in the Nixon years, carrying on into the Ford years, a decision was made to hand the \"legacy program\" over to private hands. That is the name insiders use for a secret effort said to hold recovered non-human craft. Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who has come forward as a whistleblower, relays this as \"legacy program lore\" that has since leaked out, not as something he saw. If true, it would explain why such a program could sit outside the government's normal oversight.","nnsa-officials-attend-sol-foundation-ufo-conference":"Officials from the National Nuclear Security Administration, the US agency in charge of nuclear weapons, turned up at a gathering of the Sol Foundation, which Michels describes as a Stanford organisation devoted to UFO research. Michels says he was there and saw them show up. He brings it up as a sign that people from the nuclear side of government take an interest in the subject.","nnsa-reply-to-foia-request-on-starfish-prime-ufo-records-2022":"Someone used FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, to ask the National Nuclear Security Administration (NNSA), the agency that runs America's nuclear weapons work, for every record about UFOs, UAP (the government's newer term for unidentified objects) or any related acronym from the Starfish Prime series of US nuclear tests. Michels brings this up on three episodes and says the requester was anonymous. In one he quotes the reply from June 2022: \"It was determined that an additional review of your case by the subject matter expert with jurisdiction regarding responsive records was required.\" The letter sends the request on to an expert for review and treats it as having a holder with authority over \"responsive records\", but it does not say what those records are or whether any of them concern UFOs.","no-american-flag-on-the-sport-model-1988":"Bob Lazar says that in 1988 or 1989 he worked on flying saucers at S-4, a secret site in Nevada, and that the craft he calls the \"sport model\" had no American flag or markings on it, which would point away from it being a US-built aircraft. Lazar tells Michels he saw this himself, \"like the first day that I went in.\"","no-column-supports-the-craft-s-53-ft-floor-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S-4, a secret site in Nevada, in 1988 and 1989, reports peeking into the bottom level of one craft and seeing no column holding up its 53-foot-wide floor. The researcher Luigi Vendittelli, who spent 48 hours with Lazar, draws this out on the show: \"there is nothing that was visually witnessed on the bottom level... that is a supporting column holding that 53-ft diameter floor.\" The point is that a floor that size with no support would need building methods beyond ours.","no-footage-until-the-lady-allowed-filming-2007":"From 2007 to 2012 a North Carolina man named Chris Bledsoe went out behind his house every night with a camera to film the lights he says he was seeing, and he got nothing, \"zero data.\" He tells Michels he wanted pictures \"to save myself from the community,\" and that footage only came once a presence he calls the Lady allowed filming, which would mean whatever he saw chose when to be recorded.","no-loose-nuke-nest-and-fbi-wmd-staff-stayed-in-new-jersey-wi-2024":"The 2024 sightings over New Jersey were not about a missing nuclear bomb, says Jake Barber, a former military helicopter pilot. He tells Michels he knows this because he knows people on NEST, the government's nuclear emergency response team, and FBI weapons-of-mass-destruction staff who stayed in New Jersey with their families, which he says they would not have done if a bomb were loose. His knowledge comes from what those people told him.","no-physicists-on-nasa-s-follow-up-uap-study-2023":"When NASA ran its own follow-up study of UAP in 2023 and 2024, no physicists were on it, according to Charles Buhler, whom the show bills as a NASA chief. UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's current term for UFOs. Buhler tells Michels that he and his wife \"were approached to help with the UAP\" because \"NASA was doing their own UAP thing\", and if he is right, the agency studied the question without the people best placed to judge the physics of what is seen.","no-physicists-on-the-claimed-crash-retrieval-program-and-the-1970":"Physics has made no real progress beyond the standard model, the accepted theory of particles, and Einstein's general relativity, and at the same time no physicists work on the claimed secret programme to recover crashed UFOs. Eric Weinstein, a researcher, asks Michels's Pentagon-scientist guest whether these are \"two sides of the same coin.\" If they are, it would mean the field has been held back on purpose, with physicists kept away from whatever was recovered.","no-real-physicists-on-the-program":"A secret programme had no proper physicists working on it, according to the physicist Eric W. Davis, quoted on the show: \"There wasn't a real physicist there. Nobody at the PhD level who is either an applied physicist or a theoretical physicist.\" He does not say more about which programme he means. If true, whatever was being studied was in the hands of people without the training to understand it.","no-reverse-engineering-success-after-eight-decades-1940":"A secret effort to reverse engineer recovered craft, meaning to take them apart and learn to copy them, has had no success after eight decades and \"just didn't go anywhere.\" Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has advised the Pentagon, says a senior vice president at an aerospace company told him so over years of interviews, before and after the man retired. If true, it would mean the program existed but failed.","no-transient-nuclear-test-matches-after-17-march-1956":"Old sky photographs from Palomar Observatory show \"transients\", brief points of light that appear and then vanish, and some are said to line up in time with nuclear tests, but the last such match is said to fall on 17 March 1956. A guest named Steven Bruel says this on an episode with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, without naming a source: \"the last time we saw a correspondence between a nuclear test and a transient was March 17th, 1956.\" The point matters because if the lights tracked nuclear tests, their stopping would say something about what they were.","no-writings-in-the-great-pyramid":"Inside the Great Pyramid of Giza, a researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels, \"there are no writings anywhere\". He gives no source and offers it as a puzzle, since for him a monument with no inscriptions sits oddly with the usual account of who built it and why.","noah-rodek-s-foia-on-project-preserve-destiny-forwarded-to-t":"An independent researcher named Noah Hradek is said to have used the Freedom of Information Act, a US law that lets anyone request government records, to ask the Air Force about Project Preserve Destiny, which the former Air Force sergeant Dan Sherman says was a secret program in which the NSA trained him to communicate with aliens. Michels tells this himself on several episodes: he says the Air Force forwarded the request to the NSA, and he reads from the Air Force's letter, \"This letter is in final response to your 27th of July, 2023 Freedom of Information Act request.\" To Michels, sending a question about an allegedly made-up program to the NSA suggests the program is real.","non-disclosure-agreement-incident-made-classified-1977":"In 1977 at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, a nuclear weapons guard named Mario Woods says he was debriefed after a UFO encounter he describes as an abduction, and that the incident was then made classified. He tells Michels that as he put his own paper down, Jack Reed, a captain in the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, handed another man \"like a non-disclosure agreement\", with a Colonel Spraker also named. If true, it would mean the Air Force had witnesses to a UFO event at a nuclear base sign away their silence.","non-human-biologics-recovered-with-crashed-craft":"Several people on the show say that when the US government secretly recovered crashed craft, it also recovered bodies or biological remains, which insiders call biologics, and that these were not human. The main teller is David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer turned whistleblower, who tells Michels that, as he said in an earlier television interview, \"Biologics came with some of these recoveries.\" In a clip from his testimony before Congress, played on the show, he is asked whether they were human or non-human and answers \"non-human\", saying that was the assessment of people with direct knowledge who are still on the program. Grusch says he is passing on what others told him, and names the congresswoman Nancy Mace and the journalist Ross Coulthart around that account. Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he took part in retrieving a UFO, puts it more loosely: there are \"reports\" that biologics are in storage and \"we just don't know what that is\", without tying them to particular crashes. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says he has heard the same, adding that it is \"mostly autopsy reports.\" If true, it would mean the government holds the remains of beings that are not human.","non-human-biologics-stored-at-institutions":"Biological remains of non-human beings are said to be stored at various institutions rather than in one place. Brigham Buhler, a guest who talks with Michels about the drug industry, says he heard this in a documentary but cannot remember which one or who said it. If true, it would mean such remains are spread across several facilities.","non-operational-emp-testing-at-samso-and-hill-afb-1960":"Tests of how an electromagnetic pulse (EMP, a burst of energy that can knock out electronics) affects missile equipment were carried out, the claim goes, not at working missile bases but at the Space and Missile Systems Organization in San Bernardino and at Hill Air Force Base in Utah. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer who says a UFO shut down the nuclear missiles in his care, says this on the show and connects it to a book called The Faded Giant and to a man named James Clattz. He raises it to argue that such testing would not explain the shutdowns he describes.","non-verbal-amazon-tribes-and-their-ancient-language":"There are said to be tribes in the Amazon who do not speak and instead share information mind to mind, in what they call \"the ancient language.\" Michels tells this himself without naming a source. In one episode, with the documentary maker Ky Dickens on telepathy, he says such tribes \"apparently\" exist; in a later one he states outright that they \"telepathically beam information to each other.\"","nordic-alien-on-orcas-island":"Brent Friedman, one of the creators of a 1990s television series about UFOs called Dark Skies, says he once met a \"Nordic alien\" on Orcas Island in Washington State. In UFO lore that phrase means a tall being who looks almost human. Friedman tells Michels it happened on a friend's 20-acre property: suddenly there was a figure who \"literally looks like a male model, like a Scandinavian male model,\" and who spoke to him without moving its mouth.","nordics-from-mars-caused-the-extinction-of-the-dinosaurs":"\"Nordics\", the tall, fair, human-looking beings of UFO lore, came to Earth from Mars and wiped out the dinosaurs. That is the claim the philosopher Jason Jorjani puts to Michels, drawing on a book called Closer Encounters. If taken at its word, it would make visitors from Mars responsible for one of the great turning points in the history of life on Earth.","norseen-report-under-nasa-weather-contract-n66001-2000":"Michels says that a 2000 report by the researcher John Norseen, titled \"Biofusion Reflexive Control and Neuro-ergonomic Integration in Adaptive Cockpits\", was funded under a contract numbered N66001, according to Norseen's own citation. The story places the work at a Navy research center in San Diego and ties the contract to NASA weather work. Michels brings it up in an episode about a UFO book the Pentagon tried to censor, as a sign of where such research money quietly went.","norseen-s-not-heard-nor-seen-email-and-death-2000":"A man named John Norseen sent an email signed \"John not heard nor seen\", and about two weeks later he was found dead in a motel room. That is how Jim Weiner tells it to Michels, as someone who was close to the events. Weiner credits a man he calls Ed, links the story to the Naval War College in Newport, and says the motel may have been in Tennessee. Told this way, the odd sign-off reads as a warning just before his death.","norsine-at-allagash-in-august-1976-running-a-tag-trace-retri":"In August 1976, at the Allagash Waterway in Maine, a man named John Norsine was running what he called a TTR, a \"tag trace retrieve\" exercise using a subcutaneous device, one placed under the skin. Charlie Foltz, who counts himself a witness to the events there, tells Michels this is what Norsine told him, which would mean a planned operation was going on at the place and time of the reported abduction.","norsine-denies-a-government-cover-story-for-the-abduction":"A man named John Norsine is said to have flatly denied that an alien abduction near Newport, Rhode Island, was a cover story invented by the government. Jim Weiner, a witness to the abduction story, tells Michels that Norsine answered simply \"No\" and that he believed what he said. It matters because it pushes back on the idea that the abduction was staged to hide something else.","norsine-s-bioenergetics-work-with-duncan-luri":"A man named John Norsine is said to have worked with a man named Duncan Laurie on bioenergetics (the study of energy in living bodies) in Jamestown, Rhode Island. Jim Weiner, who calls himself a witness in an abduction case, mentions it on the show from what he was told, but he trails off before saying what the work was.","north-american-indian-legends-of-six-fingered-red-haired-gia":"Native peoples of North America had legends of giants, and those giants had red hair and six fingers, according to a researcher named Tim Alberino. He tells Michels this twice in the same conversation without naming a source, and the second time he mentions only the red hair, not the six fingers. If it were so, it would hint that tales of giants were remembered on this continent as well as elsewhere.","north-sea-oil-rig-engineer-sighting-in-jane-s-1989":"In 1989 an engineer on an oil rig in the North Sea, named Chris Gibson, is said to have looked up and seen \"this dart-like black arrow-shaped thing\" in the sky. The aviation journalist Nick Cook tells Michels the story ran in Jane's Defence Weekly, the defence trade magazine, and in a later episode names the witness and credits the writer Bill Sweetman with it. Cook raises it as a possible glimpse of a secret aircraft.","north-sentinel-island-analogy-for-hidden-non-human-observers":"India manages North Sentinel Island but never lands there, so the islanders could easily believe nobody else is out there. The researcher Eric Weinstein uses this as an example on the show. He says it would give the natives their own Fermi paradox, the puzzle of why, if others exist, we see no sign of them, and so suggests that non-human observers could be watching us in the same quiet way.","northcom-jellyfish-uap-crosses-us-mexico-border":"Infrared footage on government networks is said to show a UAP (the official term for a UFO) shaped like a jellyfish crossing the US-Mexico border, a case filed under NORTHCOM, the US military command that defends North America. Michels reads out the heading in an episode with Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official who says he wrote the Immaculate Constellation report where it appears: an unsigned report that Michael Shellenberger delivered to Congress and Rep. Nancy Mace entered into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a document from the Pentagon, which calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, though a Defense Department spokesperson said there is no record of a programme by that name. The report says the footage shows \"an irregularly shaped UAP flying across the southern border\" that \"approximated a jellyfish or floating 'brain' with hanging appendages\"; it describes the video in words and holds no image of it, so what the footage shows rests on the report's account.","northern-lights-seen-as-far-south-as-london-2024":"In 2024 the northern lights were seen as far south as London, says the biologist Rupert Sheldrake. He tells Michels he did not see them himself because it was cloudy where he was, so he is passing on what others reported.","northern-sweden-police-investigator-of-the-ghost-rocket-era-1940":"In the 1940s, when people in Sweden were reporting \"ghost rockets\", the name given then to strange rocket-like objects seen in the sky, one of the people looking into the sightings was a policeman in the far north of the country. The researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels this in passing and does not name his source. If it is right, the reports were taken seriously enough at the local level that the police investigated them.","northrop-grumman-area-51-arv-story-called-a-myth":"The stories that the defense company Northrop Grumman, or someone at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, has an ARV, an alien reproduction vehicle, a man-made craft copied from a recovered one, are \"all myth.\" Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has advised the Pentagon, says that is the answer a source gave when he asked, a story linked to the claims of Bob Lazar, a man who said he worked on alien craft near Area 51.","northrop-grumman-s-corporate-security-as-an-enforcement-arm":"Matthew Brown, who calls himself a Pentagon whistleblower, says the defense contractor Northrop Grumman has for some time been given the authority to enforce the security of secret programmes through its own corporate security force. He tells Michels this from what he says he saw himself. If true, it would mean a private company, not the government, polices who learns about these programmes.","northrop-paper-vanishes-b-2-electrostatic-wings-1968":"A 1968 paper by the aircraft maker Northrop on electro-aerodynamics, using electric charge to change how air flows over a craft, is said to have vanished from the aerospace journal that published it, and the idea is linked to electrically charged wings on the B-2 bomber. Michels tells this from The Hunt for Zero Point, a book by the writer Nick Cook, where a researcher named Dan Marcas went looking for the paper and found it gone. Cook himself, speaking on the show, recalls Northrop papers on the subject from the early 1960s but is unsure of the date, and ties them to the work of Thomas Townsend Brown, a scientist who studied thrust from electric charge.","northwest-airlines-pilot-s-telepathic-encounter":"A man who had flown for Northwest Airlines described a telepathic encounter, a contact made mind to mind, with something in Arizona. The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels he interviewed this former pilot himself, though he gives few details of what was communicated.","norwegian-farmer-sees-a-craft-hovering-by-his-house":"A farmer in Norway is said to have seen a craft hovering right around his house. Michels tells this himself, saying he heard it from the farmer while he was in Norway, and he gives no more detail.","norwegians-with-24-teeth-want-to-build-craft-with-the-us-gov-1965":"In 1965, beings that Charles Hall calls \"the Norwegians\", who he says have 24 teeth, wanted to work with the American government and build their craft in the United States. Hall says he spent three years among tall white aliens at Area 51, a secret Air Force site in Nevada, and he tells Michels he knows this first-hand. By his account the Norwegians could only repair their craft once they got here, \"at least in 1965\", which, if true, would mean non-human visitors sought a building partnership with the US government.","nova-scotia-navy-divers-see-occupants-beside-two-saucers-on-1960":"In 1960, off Nova Scotia in Canada, Navy divers are said to have seen occupants standing outside two saucer-shaped craft on the seabed, and even to have taken video of them. A Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet tells Michels this second-hand, passing on what he has from Chris Styles and Brian Fitzpatrick and a source he names as Sweep Clear Five. If true, it would mean crewed craft were operating on the ocean floor and the military had filmed them.","november-1975-helicopter-near-miss-with-a-ghost-rocket-in-de":"On 7 November 1975, near the Swedish and Norwegian border, a helicopter flying 20 meters above the treetops on a moonlit, snowy night nearly hit a 'ghost rocket', the name Scandinavians gave to unexplained rocket-like objects. Clas Svahn, a Swedish researcher, tells Michels the pilot's account as it was told to him. He also says he has been hunting for the files from a joint military, customs and police operation of the early 1970s, which were sealed until 2040, and that he just got clearance to have them released.","nrl-s-1952-appraisal-of-brown-s-device-missing":"In 1952 the Naval Research Laboratory is said to have written an appraisal of the experiments of an inventor named T. T. Brown, a paper titled \"TT Brown electrogravity device\", about his idea that electricity could produce a force like gravity. According to Michels, when a researcher named Tom Valone, of a group called the Integrity Research Institute, later tried to find a copy, the laboratory no longer had it. Michels tells this in his documentary on Brown as a sign that the Navy's interest in the work was quietly buried.","nro-corona-satellites-immediately-imaging-ufos-under-eisenho-1957":"One idea holds that once American spy satellites went up under Eisenhower, they started picking up UFOs right away. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb lays this out for Michels as a theory that \"could be constructed\", without naming a source. In his telling, as soon as the NRO (the agency that runs US spy satellites) took over Corona, an early satellite photography programme, along with the later Keyhole camera satellites, it \"immediately started getting a bunch of imagery and signals intelligence of a bunch of UFOs\". If that were true, the government would have had orbital evidence of UFOs since the late 1950s.","nro-founded-for-reconnaissance-in-denied-airspace":"The NRO, the National Reconnaissance Office that runs America's spy satellites, was set up to gather intelligence over 'denied airspace', meaning territory such as the Soviet Union that American planes could not openly fly over. That is how a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb puts it to Michels, without naming a source. He says the reason was that a nuclear war with the Soviets would mean mutually assured destruction, so the US had to see what the other side was doing.","nro-neutrino-detectors-used-to-target-and-down-et-craft":"The NRO, the US agency that runs spy satellites, had detectors on its satellites that picked up the signature of alien craft and were used to knock those craft out of the sky, says the UFO activist Steven Greer. He tells Michels this as something others \"didn't know,\" without saying how he learned it. If true, it would mean the US has been shooting down visiting craft in secret.","nro-nga-senate-and-gao-audit-over-carryover-funds-1994":"In 1994 and 1995, two US spy agencies were audited by the Senate and by the GAO, the auditing office that works for Congress, over what a researcher who goes by UAPGerb calls an \"enormous\" misuse of funds. The agencies were the NRO, which runs America's spy satellites, and the NGA, which handles maps and imagery. UAPGerb tells Michels the money was \"carryover funds,\" meaning money left unspent and rolled into later years, \"in the realm of $3.2 billion or so.\" He gives no source, though he draws on David Grusch, a former intelligence official turned whistleblower, and he raises it to suggest how large sums could build up out of view to pay for a hidden UFO programme.","nro-program-b-ground-element-at-pine-gap-1982":"The ground part of \"Program B\", a branch of the National Reconnaissance Office, the US agency that runs spy satellites, was set up at Pine Gap, a facility in Australia, sometime in the 1980s. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the newer official term for UFOs) says this on the show and mentions the name R. Evans Heinman alongside it, without giving a document. If true, a secret American space programme kept part of its operation on Australian soil.","nro-sentient-program-integrated-with-immaculate-constellatio":"A program called Sentient at the National Reconnaissance Office, the agency that runs America's spy satellites, came online after another secret program called Immaculate Constellation already existed and was then \"integrated with it\", according to Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official presented on the show as a whistleblower. Brown tells Michels this as \"my understanding\", without pointing to a document. If so, a spy-satellite system would be feeding the hidden program he describes.","nrol-16-on-walpurgisnacht-matching-a-necronomicon-rite-2005":"NROL-16, a classified spy-satellite launch, lifted off at 12:50 a.m. on April 30, 2005, the same date as Walpurgisnacht, the old European witches' night, and the timing is said to match a rite in the Necronomicon, a book of occult spells. Jesse Michels tells this himself, drawing on the work of a researcher named John Ramirez, as a sign that occult ritual runs through the American space program.","nsa-certificate-for-ea280-electronics-intelligence-course-1990":"Michels shows a certificate the NSA gave to Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, for \"satisfactory completion of the EA280 intermediate electronics intelligence collection and analysis course\" in the early 1990s. Michels offers it as support for Sherman's account. The certificate shows that Sherman took the NSA course, and it does not speak to what he says he was later asked to do.","nsa-glomar-response-cites-executive-order-13526-2023":"In 2023 the NSA answered a records request credited to a man named Noah Hradek with a \"Glomar\" response, a reply that will neither confirm nor deny that the records exist, citing Executive Order 13526, the presidential order on classified information. Michels reads out what he calls their exact words: \"This agency has determined that the fact of the existence or non-existence of the material you request is a currently improperly classified matter.\" He brings it up as a sign the agency is guarding something it will not even admit to holding.","nsa-may-have-tracked-sherman-since-childhood-1960":"A former sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, believes the agency may have tracked him from childhood through his young adulthood and military service. Michels relays this as Sherman's own belief in an episode built around Sherman's account. If true, it would mean Sherman was picked out for the work long before he knew it.","nsa-personnel-attend-ramsay-lecture":"People from the NSA, the US signals intelligence agency, came to a talk given by Scott Ramsey in Baltimore, Maryland, according to Ramsey himself. Ramsey, who appears on an episode about a reported crash with alien bodies, mentions it only in passing, saying \"we similarly had something like that happen in Baltimore too.\" He offers it as a sign that intelligence people follow his work.","nsa-purges-borland-s-jwics-document-database-2024":"A man named Dylan Borland, who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, says that in 2024 he had saved a large collection of documents, briefings and private messages on JWICS, a classified US intelligence computer network, and that the NSA then wiped it. Borland tells this as something that happened to him, naming Intelink, the search system on those networks. If true, the government would have erased records he had gathered on the subject.","nsa-source-at-collusion-meetings-with-a-tech-company":"Someone at the NSA is said to have sat in meetings with senior executives of a major tech and telecommunications company where the two sides were \"sort of colluding.\" Steven Greer, a former doctor who campaigns for the government to reveal what it knows about UFOs, tells Michels this came from his NSA contact. He names neither the man nor the company, and says only that it was collusion, not what was agreed.","nsa-treat-men-in-black-unit":"The NSA had a 'Men in Black' unit, the kind of secret team said to visit UFO witnesses and silence them, called the Tactical Reconnaissance Engineering Assessment Search Team, and it was created by a retired general named Albert Stubblebine. That is what an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels. He calls it 'the supposed' unit and passes on what others told him, with archived emails he calls the DPI archive among his sources. If true, it would mean an American intelligence agency kept a team to do the work the folklore gives to the men in black.","nsa-utah-data-center-code-named-bumblehive-2005":"Around 2005, the NSA (the US signals intelligence agency) is said to have set up what was then its largest single data center, in Utah, under the code name Bumblehive. Matthew Pines, an intelligence and security analyst, tells Michels this without naming a source, and says the code name has since been declassified.","nsc-study-on-disclosure-of-recovered-craft-under-george-w-bu-2000":"Under George W. Bush, the National Security Council is said to have commissioned a study, with the Pentagon's Office of Net Assessment, of whether the time had come for \"disclosure\", meaning telling the public about recovered craft. A Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell mentions this report, which he ties to a man named Sean Kirkpatrick. If true, it would mean the White House seriously weighed going public.","nsf-and-nasa-co-sponsor-extended-electrodynamics-podcast-2024":"According to Michels, the National Science Foundation and NASA paid jointly for a podcast in which highly credentialed scientists and engineers looked into extended electrodynamics, a proposed widening of the physics of electricity and magnetism. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with a guest who says aliens taught him advanced physics, as a sign that serious institutions are taking such ideas up.","nsf-gulfstream-v-surveys-over-the-triangle":"The owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange happenings, says the National Science Foundation's \"$50 million Gulfstream 5 jet\" flew surveys over \"the triangle\", an area of the ranch where much of the activity is reported. He tells Michels this as something he witnessed, and he raises it to show how seriously the place is being studied.","nttr-retrieval-insider-describes-a-cracked-egg-craft-with-la":"A crash retrieval team at the Nevada Test and Training Range, a vast military range, recovered a cracked egg-shaped craft whose skin was built in layers \"like a Russian nesting doll.\" The online researcher known as UAPGerb tells Michels this is what an unnamed member of the team told him; at another point he adds that the egg was manned by grays, the small grey-skinned beings of UFO lore, and leaves out the layers.","nuclear-assets-of-the-29th-guards-missile-division-near-irku-2011":"Michels says that the 29th Guards Missile Division, a Russian unit near Irkutsk in Siberia, \"is and long has been\" a live force of mobile, nuclear-armed long-range missiles. He says it himself while discussing a man named Stephen Pearson, in an episode about strange three-fingered bodies from Peru, and he does not spell out how the missiles tie into the rest of the story.","nuclear-posture-review-and-the-motive-to-discredit-witnesses-1950":"A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas argues that the military had a reason to discredit people who saw UFOs at nuclear sites, since admitting they could not be stopped would undercut nuclear deterrence. He points to the Nuclear Posture Review, a Defense Department policy document, which he quotes as saying \"nuclear deterrence is the backbone, the basis of our national security.\" The quoted line speaks to how much the Pentagon relies on deterrence; that this created a motive to discredit witnesses is Salas's own reading, which the line neither states nor rules out.","nuclear-powered-airplane-in-an-idaho-falls-hangar":"An airplane powered by a nuclear reactor is sitting in a hangar in Idaho Falls, Idaho. John S. Herrington, introduced on the show as a former Secretary of Energy, puts this to Michels as a question: \"If I told you there is an airplane powered by nuclear power sitting in a hanger in Idaho Falls, would you believe me?\" He says \"this goes back decades,\" and at another point he credits Howard Hughes with it. He raises it as an example of old, advanced technology the public never hears about.","nuclear-silo-shutdowns-and-anti-nuclear-messaging":"Many UFO accounts from nuclear sites describe nuclear missiles in their silos being shut down, and many people who say they were contacted report a message that humans will destroy themselves in a nuclear war. Michels puts these two things together himself, citing the researcher Robert Hastings, who has collected accounts of UFOs at nuclear bases. If the pattern holds, it would suggest whoever is behind it is worried about nuclear weapons.","nuclear-systems-kept-archaic-so-they-can-t-be-hacked-2017":"America's nuclear weapons systems are kept on old, outdated technology on purpose, because \"it's harder to hack.\" Michael Herrera, a man who says he saw a 300-foot craft in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels this is \"something I found out personally,\" placing it in 2017 and tying it to NORAD, the US and Canadian air defence command, and to secret facilities.","nuclear-test-sites-with-marines-dug-in-nearby-1950":"In the 1950s, at the Nevada Test Site where the US exploded its nuclear bombs, Marines were dug in right beside the blasts, says Randy Anderson, a guest who tells Michels he saw alien technology on a military base. He tells it as something he knows first-hand: \"they detonated nukes where they had the Marines like embedded right next to where the explosions were going off.\" If true, troops were put directly in harm's way to see what the bombs would do.","nuclear-weapons-aboard-the-nimitz-group-and-the-ufo-nukes-li-2004":"The Nimitz carrier strike group, the Navy ships whose pilots reported a strange object off California in 2004, likely had tactical nuclear weapons on board, which would fit a pattern of UFOs showing up around nuclear arms. Michels makes the point himself, in a debate with a sceptic, drawing on Robert Hastings's book UFOs and Nukes.","nukes-used-to-bring-down-ufos":"Nuclear weapons are said to have been used to bring down UFOs. A researcher who goes by the name RedPandaKoala tells Michels this, passing on what the musician Tom DeLonge has said. If true, it would mean the military has fired nuclear weapons at unknown craft.","number-137-in-the-proportions-of-the-second-pyramid":"The number 137 shows up in the proportions of the second pyramid at Giza, the one built for the pharaoh Khafre, says a researcher named Armando Mei, who tells Michels the match is too plain to miss. He draws the link from the physicists Richard Feynman and Leon Lederman, who wrote about 137 as a mysterious number at the heart of physics, though he gives no source for the pyramid measurement itself. If true, the builders would have known something about the deep structure of nature.","numbers-spoken-from-television-sets-1960":"In the 1960s, according to the writer John Keel, who investigated strange happenings in America, people reported hearing numbers spoken at them out of their television sets. The philosopher Jason Jorjani brings this up on the show as something Keel \"reports on.\" He sets it beside Keel's accounts of mysterious Men in Black, strangers said to visit witnesses, as a sign that whatever lies behind these events can reach people through ordinary things in their homes.","nuraghe-towers-buried-as-if-by-a-tidal-wave":"The nuraghe, ancient stone towers on the island of Sardinia, were at one point suddenly covered in earth, \"almost like a tidal wave had swept across the island\". Tim Alberino, a guest who looks into ancient sites, offers this to Michels as his own reading, without naming a source. If he is right, some sudden catastrophe buried a whole island's old buildings at once.","nuro-finding-things-on-the-seafloor":"The National Underwater Reconnaissance Office, a little-known US body known as NURO, may be finding unexplained things on the ocean floor. When Michels put it to John S. Herrington, the Secretary of Energy, that \"they might be finding things on the seafloor,\" Herrington answered: \"Yep. They are.\" Coming from a cabinet official, that short answer would mean the government knows of strange objects under the sea.","nurse-says-she-was-there-1996":"Witnesses in Varginha, a town in Brazil, say a strange creature was captured in 1996 and taken to a hospital. A nurse there, the story goes, was asked how she could know what happened, and answered, shaking, \"Cuz I was there.\" James Fox, a documentary filmmaker who interviewed witnesses in the case, tells Michels about her and does not give her name.","nurse-says-the-wright-patterson-craft-came-from-aztec":"A craft held at Wright Patterson, the Air Force base in Dayton, Ohio, came from a reported UFO crash near Aztec, New Mexico, according to a nurse. Scott Ramsey, a researcher who has investigated the Aztec case, passes on her claim to Michels: \"She claimed it came from Aztec.\" If true, it would link the Aztec crash to the wreckage kept at the base.","nurse-speaks-for-first-time-in-virginia":"A nurse in Virginia who had worked under a surgeon named Amar began, for the first time, to speak about a being that was said to have been operated on at his hospital. Michels says this to Amar himself, recalling what he had been told, and it would mean a second person from the hospital backs up the surgeon's account.","nurses-and-radiology-technician-also-witnessed-1996":"In 1996, at the regional hospital in Varginha, Brazil, where a strange creature is said to have been brought, nurses and a radiology technician are also said to have seen something. Italo Venturelli, a senior surgeon at that hospital, tells Michels that many people say they saw it, and that the technician says he saw a closed box. Their accounts would add more hospital staff as witnesses to the case.","o-reilly-brown-inventions-handed-to-consortium":"Some inventions by Townsend Brown, a scientist said to have worked on flying-saucer-like technology, are said to have been handed over to other organizations, with an unnamed consortium seeming to be part of it. Michels relays this in his documentary about Brown, passing on what a man named O'Reilly says and naming a writer called Paul Schatzkin in the same account. If true, part of Brown's work would have quietly gone into private hands.","oak-ridge-magnesium-bismuth-technical-document-1950":"A 1950s technical document from Oak Ridge, the nuclear laboratory in Tennessee, is said to discuss magnesium and bismuth. Michels mentions it briefly himself, among other documents on UFOs, in an episode about the Department of Energy and UFOs, giving no more detail than that.","oak-ridge-visit-arranged-by-howard-baker-1970":"Harald Malmgren, a former adviser to US presidents, says Senator Howard Baker of Tennessee arranged for him to visit Oak Ridge, the government nuclear research site in Tennessee, sometime in the 1970s or 1980s. Malmgren tells Michels he went up there \"and spend some time with them.\" He tells it as part of his account of having dealt directly with UFO material.","obama-administration-s-ufo-answers-then-obama-s-late-night-s-2012":"As recently as 2012, the Obama administration was asked whether it investigates UFOs and the question was more or less left unanswered. A researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala tells Michels this from memory, without naming a source, as an example of how lightly the White House treated the subject before Obama himself spoke about it later on late-night television.","obama-at-a-campfire-tells-eddie-vedder-to-read-the-three-bod":"Eddie Vedder, the Pearl Jam singer, is said to have sat at a campfire with Barack Obama, both of them drunk, and asked him what is really going on. Obama answered, \"Read The Three-Body Problem,\" the science fiction novel about contact with aliens. Michels tells this twice: once as something Vedder is said to have told, and once in his own narration, as a hint that a former president was pointing at aliens.","obama-on-james-corden-about-unidentified-objects-2021":"Barack Obama said on James Corden's late-night show in 2021 that there are things in the sky the government cannot identify, as Michels recalls it: \"There are things in our sky that, you know, we can't identify. We don't know what they are.\" A clip of Obama himself, played in another episode without naming the show, has him saying there is \"footage and records of objects in the skies that we don't know exactly what they are.\" Coming from a former president, it reads as an admission that the unknowns are real.","obama-on-jimmy-kimmel-says-the-aliens-won-t-let-disclosure-h-2020":"Barack Obama once went on Jimmy Kimmel's late-night show and said, smiling, that the aliens won't let it happen. Here \"it\" means disclosure, the hoped-for day when the government tells the public what it knows about UFOs. A guest named Kurt Metzger brings this up with Michels in a 2025 episode about Jeffrey Epstein and aliens. He asks what Obama was really saying behind the joke, since if he meant it, a former president would be hinting that someone other than the government decides what we learn.","obama-s-area-51-remark-later-walked-back-2026":"Former President Barack Obama is said to have spoken about Area 51, the secret military base in Nevada, and about alien beings kept underground there, and then to have taken it back. Michels says so on the show, adding that Obama \"sort of walked his statement back.\" A guest named Bryce Zabel tells it with more urgency. He says \"the very next day, he had to buy it back,\" and that Donald Trump took the bait. If the remark was meant seriously, it would be a former president hinting that the government holds aliens.","obama-s-betty-and-barney-hill-documentary-and-spielberg-set-2020":"Michels says Barack Obama is interested in UFOs, enough that his production company has taken on a film about Betty and Barney Hill, a couple who said they were abducted in 1961, which Michels calls the first abduction in the United States. He tells it himself on several episodes and the details shift: once Obama only 'thought about producing' a Hill documentary, other times he is making it, as a documentary or as a movie. Once Michels calls it 'predictive programming,' the idea that films prepare the public for things to come. In one telling he adds that Obama was 'on set at Spielberg's Disclosure Day,' Steven Spielberg's UFO film, and in another he says Spielberg spoke about it on Michelle Obama's podcast. A guest named Brent Friedman says that Barack and Michelle Obama 'announced they're going to make a Betty and Barney movie,' that the project was then dropped, and that a Hill scene had already appeared in the pilot of Dark Skies, a 1990s TV series. If true, it would mean a former president is quietly drawn to the subject.","obama-says-on-a-podcast-that-aliens-are-real-2020":"Barack Obama, asked on a podcast whether aliens are real, answered yes, according to the skeptic Michael Shermer, who tells Michels the remark is what \"triggered\" a later release of government UFO files. Michels himself frames it as Obama provoking Donald Trump, and on another episode plays Obama's own words: \"they're real but I haven't seen them\" and \"they're not being kept in\", which the show takes as a reference to Area 51. Coming from a former president, the remark would mean someone with the highest clearance thinks aliens exist.","obama-shuts-down-a-putin-back-channel-through-malmgren-2009":"In 2009 Vladimir Putin tried to set up a private diplomatic channel to the United States through Harold Malmgren, a former adviser to American presidents, but Barack Obama shut the idea down. Michels says Malmgren told him this in their talks, which, if true, shows how close Malmgren stayed to the top of world affairs.","obamatrump-exchange-over-ufos-2026":"A recent public exchange between Barack Obama and Donald Trump stirred up controversy about UFOs. Aldo Rebelo, a Brazilian official presented on the show as a former defense minister, mentions it, calling it \"a dialogue, let's call it a dialogue,\" between the former and the current president. He brings it up as a sign that the subject has reached the very top of American politics, though he gives no details of what was said.","oberth-and-von-braun-s-neopagan-rituals-in-the-nazi-space-pr-1930":"The rocket engineer Wernher von Braun and his mentor Hermann Oberth are said to have gone through \"weird neopagan rituals\" as part of Nazi Germany's rocket and space program. Michels says this himself on three episodes, without naming a source, sometimes naming only von Braun and once adding that von Braun was believed to be a Freemason. If true, it would mean the roots of the space age were tangled with occult practice.","object-abroad-too-large-to-move":"Somewhere outside the United States, the claim goes, there is a recovered object so large that it cannot be moved. Congressman Eric Burlison tells Michels this as something he has been told, and he gives no place or date. If true, it would mean that at least one crashed or recovered craft is too big to be taken away and hidden.","object-appears-beside-the-atlas-8f-reentry-vehicle-at-cape-c-1962":"During a 1962 missile test from Cape Canaveral, an unknown object is said to have appeared right beside the reentry vehicle, the cone that carries a warhead back down through the atmosphere. Michels describes the test film himself: \"At the 4 minute and 40 second mark in the video, an object appears to phase itself into existence alongside the re-entry vehicle\". He says this happened while the vehicle was travelling at 20,000 feet per second, or Mach 18. He credits a US Air Force and NASA report written after the flight. A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell tells it separately. He names the vehicle as the Mark V, built by the defense company AVCO, and dates the test to 19 September 1962. He says the official report to the Defense Department states \"objects of unknown origin and identity were tracking or pacing\" it. Neither teller quotes the report beyond that phrase. If true, something unknown kept pace with one of the fastest objects of its day, and the military wrote it down.","object-came-out-of-the-water-and-zapped-someone-per-dod-inte-2024":"One of the unexplained objects is said to have come up out of the water and struck a person. A guest named Matthew Pines tells Michels in 2024 that he heard this a few days earlier in a meeting with unnamed people inside Pentagon intelligence. Once he says it \"zapped\" someone and once only that it \"hit somebody\". If true, it would mean these objects can harm people directly.","object-inside-the-mesa-at-skinwalker-ranch":"The claim is that a large metal object lies buried deep inside a mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property famous for reports of strange happenings. If true, something manufactured has been hidden in the rock for a long time. Michels first told it in 2022. He says the team of the ranch's owner used soil resistivity and ground-penetrating radar, two ways of sensing what lies underground, and found 'a large metallic object buried underneath the property below the mesa,' about 400 feet down. In that telling he credits team members named Aaron Blunt and Ravi Chandran. In a later episode, citing the TV series Secrets of Skinwalker Ranch, Michels says such objects have been located but are 'hard to actually extract because it's like they have a life of their own.' The ranch's owner, speaking first-hand on the show, goes further. He says, 'There's something under the mesa,' and speaks of 'extracting material that is manufactured, that's engineered from hundreds of feet within the mesa.' So Michels says the material resists removal, while the owner speaks as if some has been taken out.","object-shot-down-over-alaska-said-to-have-reassembled-itself-2023":"An object shot down over Alaska in 2023 is said to have put itself back together and then been seen flying over Canada. Michels mentions it in an episode with a NASA whistleblower, as something he heard: \"I heard things like it had reassembled itself and been seen flying over Canada.\" He names no source, and if it were true, it would mean the object was not something ordinary that could simply be knocked out of the sky.","objects-on-radar-and-seen-by-airmen-over-malmstrom-1967":"In 1967, objects were tracked on radar and seen by airmen at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, a base that handled nuclear missiles. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, tells Michels about these reports, passing on what he says he learned from a man named Lewis Chase. If true, it would mean unknown objects were moving freely over a nuclear weapons base.","objects-over-ellsworth-runway-weapons-storage-and-b-52-alert-1957":"Between 1957 and 1959, unknown objects are said to have come down the runway at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, and to have shown up over its weapons storage area and the pad where B-52 bombers stood on alert. An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods tells Michels he heard of about four or five such cases from other men, some in the missile field and some \"back at Ellsworth\". If true, it would mean strange craft were taking an interest in American nuclear forces in the late 1950s.","occult-history-in-nasa-and-launch-pad-choices":"NASA has a lot of occult history, says a guest on the show, and some of the names given to missions, along with the choice of launch pad 33 and other things, were made very deliberately. The speaker, who is not named, passes this on from what someone else told them and gives no specific examples. If true, it would mean mystical ideas quietly shaped choices inside the space agency.","occult-minded-nazis-brought-in-through-paperclip-and-overcas-1945":"After the war, Nazis with an interest in the occult are said to have been brought to the United States through Operation Paperclip and Operation Overcast, the American programmes that took in German scientists, and to have ended up running the US space programme. The researcher Chris Ramsay says this to Michels as a summary, without naming a source. If true, it would mean men with those beliefs shaped America's push into space.","ocean-shells-on-the-eridu-ziggurat-summit":"Ocean shells still lie on the top of the ziggurat, a stepped temple tower, at Eridu, an ancient city in southern Iraq. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels he identified them there himself. He reads them as a sign that a flood once surged in from the Persian Gulf, high enough to reach the summit.","oceanographer-says-the-objects-are-undersea-or-subterranean-2025":"An unnamed oceanographer is said to have stated that the mysterious objects live under the sea or underground, and that they belong neither to China nor to the United States. Harold Malmgren, a former presidential advisor, mentions it to Michels in 2025 as a \"revelation\" from the past few days, without naming the scientist. If true, it would mean something that is not any human nation operates in Earth's oceans.","odlum-investment-guidance-technologies-shutdown-and-northrop-1960":"After the financier Floyd Odlum backed the inventor Townsend Brown, who worked on moving craft with electric fields, Brown shut his company Guidance Technologies in the fall of 1967 with no explanation, saying he got no results, and just three months later Odlum's company Northrop wrote a 1968 paper saying it was investing in electro-aerodynamics, Michels says in two documentaries. In one he also brings in an Aviation Week report on the B-2 bomber, and he names the general Curtis LeMay and the businessman Bill Lear in the story. He tells it as a sign that the work did not fail but went into a secret aerospace programme.","odlum-s-open-ended-payments-to-the-brown-family-1960":"Linda Brown says that in the 1960s a man named Floyd Odlum made a member of her family sign an open-ended contract with her father, so that payments to the family had no fixed end. She tells it on the show as someone who lived through it: \"Mr. Odlum did force my to sign a contract with my dad, that was open-ended.\" The point of the story is that a backer kept her father's work tied to him for good.","odni-claim-that-dylan-borland-drew-a-sensor-system-2025":"The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, who reports on UFOs, says he heard \"directly from ODNI,\" the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, that a man named Dylan Borland, who has made claims about government UFO secrets, drew a fourth drawing of a sensor system. Borland, speaking for himself on the show, says that drawing was fabricated, that someone \"created a fake\" one, and that he has text messages to prove it; he also points to a report by AARO, the Pentagon office that investigates unexplained aerial sightings. If Borland is right, officials put a false document into his record.","odni-duplicated-borland-s-phone-2025":"In 2025, he says, the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, the office that oversees all US spy agencies, made a copy of his phone. Dylan Borland, a man who says he was asked to testify to Congress about secret UFO programmes, tells Michels \"I know I'm tapped. I mean they duplicated my phone,\" which would mean he was being watched for what he knew.","odni-interested-in-how-borland-tracked-search-and-rescue-tas-2023":"Between 2023 and 2025, the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, which oversees the US spy agencies, and its inspector general took an interest in how Dylan Borland tracked search-and-rescue taskings, he says. Borland, a guest who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, tells Michels he can \"know a search and rescue tasker with imagery and sigint when I see it\", sigint meaning intercepted signals intelligence. The point of his claim is that he could tell \"what is our guys and what isn't\", meaning American operations from something else.","odni-nine-month-operation-against-whistleblowers-and-journal-2025":"The Office of the Director of National Intelligence, which oversees all the US spy agencies, ran a nine-month operation aimed at UFO whistleblowers and journalists, according to Jeremy Corbell, who reports on the subject and says he was caught up in it. \"I just want you to know that ODNI ran an operation for 9 months,\" he tells Michels, and in telling it he names the journalist George Knapp, the whistleblowers Dylan Borland and Matthew Brown, the intelligence director Tulsi Gabbard, and a body he calls the Directors Initiative Group. If true, it would mean the intelligence agencies were working against the very people bringing UFO claims to light.","odni-tells-corbell-that-borland-told-the-icig-about-project-2020":"A man named Dylan Borland is said to have told the Inspector General of the Intelligence Community, the official watchdog over the US spy agencies, that a secret UFO programme exists and is called Rubik's Cube. A guest named Jeremy Corbell says on the show that he heard this from a lead of the Director's Initiatives Group inside the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, the body that oversees the spy agencies. Corbell tells it twice: once naming his source's office but not the programme, and once naming the programme but not the source.","odyssey-systems-data-management-platforms-2008":"Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, says that around 2008 to 2010 the programme's data was handled on technology platforms run by firms called Odyssey Systems and MITRE. He tells Michels this from what he says he saw himself. If true, it would name the private contractors behind the programme's record-keeping.","oechsler-on-the-news-inman-convinced-him-vehicles-are-non-hu-1989":"A man named Bob Oechsler said in a news clip, sometime between 1989 and 1999, that a retired admiral named Bobby Ray Inman had convinced him that certain craft \"were not manufactured by human technology\". Oechsler is passing on what he says he came to believe through Inman, and he calls it \"pretty conclusive\" for him. If true, it would mean a senior military figure privately backed the idea that non-human vehicles exist.","oedipus-fulfills-the-prophecy-by-fleeing-it":"In the Greek myth of Oedipus, a prophecy says a young prince of Thebes \"is going to grow up and murder his father and marry his mother,\" and the very steps taken to escape it are what bring it about. The writer Eric Wargo, who studies prophetic dreams, retells the myth to Michels, with a nod to Sigmund Freud. He uses it to make a point about foresight: a glimpse of the future can end up shaping the actions that make it come true.","off-base-doctor-asks-had-they-let-you-see-the-alien-bodies-y":"A doctor off the base near Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio once asked him, out of nowhere, \"had they let you see the alien bodies yet.\" John Blitch, a scientist who worked with DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, tells Michels this happened to him, and he takes the question as a sign that locals assumed the base held bodies.","off-planet-pin-given-at-cape-canaveral-2007":"Sometime between 2007 and 2010, at Cape Canaveral, the Florida launch site, a person \"everybody has read about\" gave Chris Bledsoe what the story calls an off-planet pin, he tells Michels. Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs show up around him and whom NASA has taken an interest in, says he got it himself but tells it \"without saying names\". The name Timothy Taylor is attached to the story, though how is not spelled out.","off-record-source-the-sun-as-a-portal-to-other-star-systems":"The sun could be a kind of portal to other star systems: that is the comparison a source once made to Michels, he says. Michels brings it up while talking with Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA trained him to communicate with aliens, and he does not name the source or say anything more about the idea.","office-of-naval-intelligence-man-offers-dark-skies-creators-1993":"In the mid-1990s in Los Angeles, a man from the Office of Naval Intelligence, the Navy's spy service, offered the creators of the UFO television series Dark Skies a deal, and they turned it down, according to Brent Friedman, who created the show with Bryce Zabel and says he was in the room. Friedman tells Michels the man was \"so abusive to us in the conference room\" and that they realised he would effectively become their boss. He tells it as a sign that naval intelligence tried to steer how Hollywood showed UFOs.","office-of-naval-intelligence-reports-tim-gallaudet-on-usos":"Jesse Michels mentions reports written by the Navy's intelligence office, and says a retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet talks a lot about USOs, unidentified submersible objects, meaning craft seen moving under the sea. Michels raises it in passing himself, as a pointer that the Navy has records of strange objects in the ocean as well as the sky.","officer-points-to-his-phone-when-giants-come-up":"Tim Alberino, who tells of a 12-foot giant he says was captured in Afghanistan, says that when he raised the subject of giants with military officers, one of them silently pointed at his own phone, as if to warn that someone was listening. Alberino tells Michels he saw this himself, and he takes it as a sign the subject is watched.","officers-keep-saying-don-t-worry-about-michael-johnson-1977":"In 1977 at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, every time he asked about a fellow guard named Michael Johnson, officers kept telling him \"don't worry about Michael Johnson, he's fine.\" Mario Woods, who guarded nuclear weapons there and says he was abducted, tells Michels this himself, naming a Colonel Spraker among the officers, and the repeated brush-off suggests something was being kept from him.","officials-asked-whether-he-had-a-mental-connection-to-the-tr":"When Dylan Borland came forward with his account, officials wanted to know whether he had a mental connection to \"the triangle\" at the heart of his story, he says. Borland, a guest who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, tells Michels that \"they were very interested if I had a connection to the triangle.\" If so, officials were treating the idea of a mind linked to the object as something worth checking.","officials-deny-such-a-craft-in-inventory-rogers-says-he-saw-2023":"Greg Rogers, a doctor who worked with NASA, says he saw an unexplained craft in a secret hangar, and that when government officials later said \"We have never had a craft like that in our inventory,\" he knew it was false. \"I was sitting there saying, 'What are you talking about?' I saw it,\" he tells Michels. If he is right, officials have publicly denied a craft the government actually holds.","officials-endorse-a-multi-generational-crash-retrieval-progr":"Former CIA directors, members of Congress and whistleblowers from several agencies are said to have backed the existence of a secret programme, running for decades, that recovers crashed UFOs and takes them apart to copy how they work, called \"reverse engineering.\" Michels says this himself at the start of an episode, as a summary, without naming any of the officials. If true, it would mean the government has held such craft across generations.","officials-evasive-on-crash-materials-of-unknown-origin":"Officials are said to dodge the question of whether the United States holds material from crashed craft of unknown origin. Peter Levenda, a guest billed on the show as a CIA historian, tells Michels he was answered \"by way of omission\" whenever he asked whether such materials are in government hands, and that the response has been \"wishy-washy\". He reads the evasion as a hint that something is being held back.","officials-say-the-lady-s-dates-and-symbols-match-what-they-a-2007":"Some officials are said to hint that the dates and symbols given to a man named Chris Bledsoe by a figure he calls \"the Lady\" match things they already monitor and have seen from other authoritative sources. Michels says this himself, without naming the officials. If it were true, it would mean Bledsoe's messages line up with information held inside government.","offworld-technology-sign-on-the-wall-at-crane-2014":"Randy Anderson says that in 2014, at Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy base in Indiana, he got into an area with a marking on the wall that read \"offworld technology.\" He tells Michels he saw it himself, and if true it would mean the Navy keeps technology it labels as not from Earth.","oil-and-gas-company-drills-beside-the-mesa-drill-site":"Right beside the spot on a mesa where the ranch was drilling, an oil and gas company suddenly showed up and \"ramrods permits through\" to drill on the northern border of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events. The ranch's owner tells Michels he watched this happen, and it would mean outsiders were taking an interest in what lies under the ground there.","oke-shannon-discussed-on-classified-servers-2024":"In 2024, a matter Dylan Borland calls Oke Shannon was \"a massive conversation on the classified servers\", meaning JWICS, the top-secret computer network the US intelligence agencies use, he tells Michels. Borland, a guest who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, says he saw this himself. He mentions it in passing, and if he is right, the subject was being widely discussed inside the intelligence world.","oke-shannon-s-1985-bdm-physics-working-group-notes-ask-don-k":"In 1985, a man named Oke Shannon kept notes for an advanced theoretical physics working group at BDM International, a company in McLean, Virginia. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels the notes say, in effect, \"ask Don Kerr for funding\" where they discuss reverse engineering, meaning taking apart a recovered unknown craft to learn how it works. UAPGerb sums up the notes himself without quoting them at length. If he reads them right, people in the defense world were talking about paying for UFO work as early as the 1980s.","oki-shannon-on-building-an-anti-gravity-device-using-ferris":"A man named Oke Shannon, whom the story ties to Los Alamos National Laboratory, is said to have backed a theory by a man named Ferris Williams as the way to build an anti-gravity machine, a device that would cancel or push against gravity. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, saying Shannon is \"on record\" with the words: \"If we were to build an anti-gravitational device, it would be built on the principles involved in William's theory.\" If true, it would mean someone at a top US weapons lab took a working path to anti-gravity seriously.","old-agreement-with-non-human-beings-from-the-trumaneisenhowe-1945":"There is said to be at least one \"very old agreement\" between the US government and non-human beings, reached sometime in the Truman and Eisenhower years. Matthew Brown, who presents himself as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells Michels this as something passed on to him, and links it to claims of alien hybrid breeding programs. If true, it would mean presidents of the 1940s and 50s made a deal with beings from somewhere else.","old-maps-show-antarctica-as-two-islands":"One old map shows Antarctica not as a single continent but as 'two islands that are very close together.' The writer Jason Jorjani makes this point to Michels while discussing two early maps, the Piri Reis map and the Oronteus Finaeus map, and does not say who first made this reading. If true, it would suggest the mapmakers knew something about Antarctica's shape that nobody is supposed to have known at the time.","older-bae-archives-colleague-with-ts-sci-warned-him":"Dylan Borland, a former worker at the defence contractor BAE who now speaks as a whistleblower, says an older colleague in BAE's archives warned him about something. Borland tells Michels the man held a TS/SCI clearance, the top-secret level that also opens tightly restricted programmes, and later moved into the intelligence agencies as a government employee. What the warning said, the telling does not give.","older-pilots-private-ufo-stories":"Older military pilots who have flown for a long time all seem to have one strange UFO story, which they tell once they have had a few drinks, according to the former Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves. Graves says this to Michels, with a laugh, as something he picked up from those senior aviators, and he does not tell any of the stories himself. It suggests that sightings were common among pilots but were shared only in private.","oliver-reiser-s-tone-theory-and-letters-with-puharich-1960":"A thinker at the University of Pittsburgh named Oliver Reiser is said to have built a \"tone\" theory of the universe and to have exchanged \"dozens and dozens of letters\" with Andrija Puharich, a researcher the episode calls a CIA scientist. An unnamed researcher on the show describes this, drawing on Reiser's work Cosmic Humanism and the letters themselves, and brings Einstein into the account of Reiser's ideas.","oliver-sacks-autistic-twins-who-traded-six-digit-prime-numbe-1986":"The neurologist Oliver Sacks wrote about autistic twins in an institution who played a game of saying prime numbers to each other, six-digit ones by the title, with no clear way they could have worked them out. Diane Powell, a guest on the show, brings up the case from Sacks's writing and credits his book Awakenings. She tells it as an early hint that minds like theirs may reach knowledge in ways we don't understand.","one-in-four-ellsworth-missile-security-police-saw-something-1970":"Among the security police guarding nuclear missiles at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota in the 1970s, about one in four probably saw something unexplained in the night sky. Mario Woods, a former guard at those missile sites, says so on the show, putting it as \"one out of every four has probably seen something in the night sky.\" If he is right, sightings around the missiles were common among the men on duty, not rare one-off events.","one-of-grusch-s-40-witnesses-died-at-a-convenient-time":"One of the roughly 40 witnesses behind David Grusch, a whistleblower who says secret programmes hold crashed craft not made by humans, is said to have died at a suspiciously convenient moment. Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he flew a UFO out of a recovery site, tells Michels this, saying the man's death was \"quite conveniently timed\" for whoever might be behind it. If true, it would suggest that witnesses are being silenced.","one-to-two-craft-brought-down-per-year-at-black-sites":"At certain black sites, meaning secret military facilities, one or two unknown craft are brought down every year. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this is a claim made by a man named Steven Greer and others, and he passes it on as their claim. If true, it would mean the military is not just finding crashed craft but bringing them down regularly.","ongoing-arrangement-with-pilots-of-the-black-triangles-plus":"The US government may have an ongoing deal with whoever flies the black triangles, the large dark triangular craft reported in many UFO sightings, and may have built its own copy of them. A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown tells Michels he believes \"there is an exchange or some sort of arrangement with whatever is piloting\" them. If true, the government would be in contact with the pilots, not just watching their craft.","ongoing-hybrid-program-semen-extraction-during-abductions":"Some people who say aliens abducted them describe semen being taken from them during the abduction, which is read as a sign of an ongoing program to breed human-alien hybrids. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who studies psychic phenomena, mentions this to Michels, pointing to people such as Whitley Strieber, whom he names as one of those who talk about it.","ongoing-psionic-operation-in-the-pentagon":"A psionic operation, meaning work that uses psychic or mind-based abilities, is still being run inside the Pentagon today, according to the journalist Ross Coulthart. Coulthart tells Michels he has spoken with people who confirmed this to him, though he does not name them or say what the operation does. If true, it would mean the military is still actively using psychic methods.","onion-effect-gray-project-hidden-inside-a-black-project-1990":"A former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that around 1990 to 1993 his work sat in what he calls a \"gray project\" hidden inside a \"black project\", a secret programme, like layers of an onion. He says a Captain White explained it: a black project is placed alongside the gray one, and \"only a select few of the people in the black project know about the gray project.\" Sherman also tells of the briefing itself, where White told him others would explain the rest but \"I'm going to talk to you about the gray project\"; in that telling he puts the briefing in a communications van at an overseas post, not at NSA headquarters in Maryland.","online-whistleblower-nhi-live-and-build-their-uap-at-the-oce":"There are said to be five to seven places in the world's oceans where unexplained craft go in and out of the water, and where non-human intelligences (NHI, the subject's term for beings that are not human) live and build their own craft. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet relays this to Michels from statements by an anonymous online whistleblower known as Condor Man. Gallaudet uses the term UAP, the official newer word for UFOs. If true, it would mean other beings have bases on Earth, under the sea.","only-about-150-of-40-000-ashurbanipal-tablets-translated":"Of about 40,000 clay tablets in cuneiform, the wedge-shaped ancient writing, found in the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, only around 150 have been translated. A researcher of ancient history named Matt LaCroix gives Michels this figure without naming his source. His point is that most of what that ancient library holds has never been read.","only-minuteman-launched-from-a-silo-at-november-2-1960":"The only missile that ever flew out of the ground in the Minuteman programme, America's land-based nuclear missiles kept in underground silos, did it in the 1960s from a launch site called November 2, in the missile field around Ellsworth in South Dakota, and it flew for about five seconds. That is what Mario Woods, a former guard at nuclear missile sites, tells Michels. Woods was later posted at November 2 himself, though not when the launch happened, and he says only that he believes the flight lasted five seconds.","onr-studies-of-a-sixth-sense-2011":"In 2011 and 2012 the Office of Naval Research, the Navy's research office, was publishing studies on a \"sixth sense\" in humans. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb says so on the show, without naming the studies. If so, the Navy was spending money on the idea that people can sense things beyond the usual five senses.","operation-40-becomes-operation-mongoose-at-jm-wave-1961":"Under Kennedy, a secret anti-Castro group called Operation 40 was turned into Operation Mongoose, a CIA campaign against Cuba, run from a large base on the University of Miami campus called JM WAVE. Dan Sheehan, a lawyer and longtime researcher of government secrets, tells Michels this without naming a source. In his telling, the change was mostly a new name: \"It's all it did\".","operation-bluebird-as-the-first-mkultra-1950":"Operation Bluebird, a CIA programme of the early 1950s, came before MKUltra, the CIA's later and better-known programme of mind-control experiments, and in effect was \"the first MK Ultra.\" Michels says Bluebird \"was preMK Ultra\" in a conversation with the writer Peter Levenda, and the reply agrees: \"Yes, it was. It was the first MK Ultra.\" The point is that the CIA's work on the mind started earlier than the famous name suggests.","operation-castle-sailors-see-discs-over-ships-1954":"In 1954, during Operation Castle, a series of American nuclear bomb tests in the Marshall Islands in the Pacific, sailors on several ships saw disc-shaped or round objects hover over their vessels for brief moments. A researcher named Robert Hastings, who studies reports of UFOs near nuclear sites, tells Michels he found and spoke with these sailors. To him their accounts fit a pattern of unknown craft watching nuclear weapons.","operation-highjump-1946":"In 1946, the researcher and philosopher Jason Jorjani says, the US Navy \"for whatever reason\" sent \"a huge expedition of ships to Antarctica,\" an operation known as Highjump. He tells this to Michels in an episode about what he calls Hitler's secret UFO programme, and he names the polar explorer Richard Byrd in connection with it. He gives no source. The phrase \"for whatever reason\" hints that he thinks the mission's real purpose went beyond what was said at the time.","operation-highjump-and-byrd-s-statement-1946":"The story concerns Operation Highjump, a large American expedition to Antarctica in 1946 and 1947, and a statement made afterwards by its leader, Richard Byrd. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels that when Byrd reached a port in Chile, he told the Chilean press that the US needed to prepare for a third world war \"in which aircraft could cross from pole to pole at tremendous speeds.\" Jorjani credits this to Chilean press clippings and tells it as a hint that Byrd met unknown aircraft in Antarctica. A researcher named Richard Dolan disagrees about what it means. He says he read Byrd's whole statement and that \"if you really read the whole thing I don't think he's really talking about flying saucers.\" So both men agree Byrd made a statement, but they read it differently: as a warning about strange craft, or as something more ordinary.","operation-highjump-laser-shooting-discs":"During Operation Highjump, a US Navy expedition that sailed down the coast of Argentina toward Antarctica, Admiral Richard Byrd and his fleet are said to have met flying discs that shot at them with lasers. Michels tells this himself in two episodes, without naming a source; the second time he adds the fleet's size, \"probably like 70 or so ships\", about 33 aircraft and \"almost 5,000 men\", brings in the name of James Forrestal, and places it in 1946 and 1947, saying the ships \"reportedly encounter all these flying saucers that are shooting at them with lasers.\" If true, it would mean an armed clash between the US Navy and unknown craft years before most people had heard of flying saucers.","operation-moonwatch-and-its-36-ufo-reports-1950":"Operation Moonwatch, a 1950s program run from Harvard that used a dozen tracking stations around the world to watch the sky, is said to have collected 36 UFO reports. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel tells Michels that Fred Whipple ran the program with help from J. Allen Hynek, and that the reports were found, she thinks, by the researcher Jacques Vallée; her own team is now going through them. If the reports hold up, trained sky-watchers were logging unexplained objects alongside their regular work.","operation-overlord-with-bodyguard-and-fortitude-deceptions-1944":"In 1944 the Allies' real invasion, Operation Overlord, the D-Day landings on the beaches of Normandy, was hidden behind fake plans called Operation Bodyguard and Operation Fortitude, which pretended the invasion would come in Norway, a place \"we never actually invaded\". The researcher Eric Weinstein lays this out to Michels from general knowledge, in a conversation about getting UFO secrets out of the Pentagon. The point of telling it is that a real operation can travel alongside invented ones meant to mislead.","operation-paperclip-scientists-in-the-childhood-neighbourhoo-1950":"In the 1950s, scientists brought to America under Operation Paperclip, the postwar programme that recruited German scientists, lived in the Terrell Hills neighbourhood of San Antonio, Texas, where he grew up. The writer Whitley Strieber tells Michels this, saying such people \"apparently lived in my neighborhood,\" and credits the author Peter Levenda as the source.","operation-prato-as-an-official-armed-forces-operation-1977":"In 1977, Brazil's armed forces ran an official operation, known as Operation Prato, to watch for strange phenomena in the skies over the northern state of Pará. That is the account of Aldo Rebelo, a Brazilian politician the episode presents as a former defence minister. He tells Michels plainly: \"The operation took place. It was an official operation led by the armed forces to monitor these phenomena.\" He points to the operation's own report as his basis, though he gives none of its words here. Coming from someone who once ran Brazil's defence ministry, that statement treats the military's interest as a matter of record.","operation-snow-white-scientology-infiltration-1970":"In the 1970s, the Church of Scientology is said to have run Operation Snow White, a large effort to infiltrate several government agencies, among them the IRS and the CIA. Jay Anderson, a researcher, mentions it in passing to Michels without naming a source, calling it a \"huge like infiltration of multiple agencies\". If true, it would mean a private group got its people inside the agencies that kept secrets about it.","oppenheimer-created-the-classification-covering-ufos-1940":"J. Robert Oppenheimer, the physicist who led the atomic bomb project, created the secrecy category that later covered UFO material, says David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who became a whistleblower on government UFO secrecy. Michels repeats this several times on later shows, adding a scientist named Robert Sarbacher as Oppenheimer's partner and placing the work at the Atomic Energy Commission, the agency that ran America's nuclear programme after the war, in 1954. If true, it would mean UFO secrecy grew out of the same system that guarded nuclear weapons.","oppenheimer-first-nuclear-weapon-in-modern-history-1945":"Michels tells a story he himself calls apocryphal: a student supposedly asked J. Robert Oppenheimer how it felt to be the first person to build a nuclear weapon, and Oppenheimer answered that he was the first \"in modern history.\" Michels ties this to death weapons described in the Vedas and the Bhagavad Gita, which he cites in Edwin Arnold's translation, The Song Celestial. The point is that ancient India may have known something like the atomic bomb.","oppenheimer-on-the-aztec-crash-retrieval-team-1948":"A book by the author William Steinman, UFO Crash at Aztec, says that Oppenheimer was part of the team that recovered a crashed craft near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948. Michels raises the claim from the book while talking with the filmmaker James Fox. If true, one of the country's top atomic scientists would have handled a crashed UFO.","oppenheimer-recalls-the-trinity-test-and-the-bhagavad-gita-1945":"When the first atomic bomb was tested at the Trinity site in New Mexico in July 1945, J. Robert Oppenheimer later said a line of Hindu scripture came into his head. In a recording played on the show, he says: 'I remembered the line from the Hindu scripture, the Bhagavad Gita. Now I am become death, the destroyer of worlds.' He is the witness here, recalling his own thoughts at the blast. The line comes from a moment in the Gita where the god Krishna reveals his full, terrible form. In Edwin Arnold's English translation of 1885, The Song Celestial, that verse, chapter 11, verse 32, reads: 'Thou seest Me as Time who kills, Time who brings all to doom.' Oppenheimer does not say which translation he followed, and he read the Gita in Sanskrit, so his words are his own rendering of the same verse, with 'death' where Arnold has 'Time'. The episode uses it to show how the bomb's makers reached for ancient religious language to describe what they had done.","oppenheimer-s-gita-quote-originally-i-am-time-1945":"After the first atomic bomb test in 1945, Oppenheimer famously recalled a line from the Bhagavad Gita, the Hindu scripture: \"I am become death, the destroyer of worlds.\" A guest named Dave Rossi, who says beings taught him advanced physics, tells Michels he read that the original translation was not \"I am death\" but \"I am time, the destroyer of worlds.\" He does not say which translation he read. In Edwin Arnold's 1885 English translation, The Song Celestial, Krishna says at chapter 11, verse 32: \"Thou seest Me as Time who kills, Time who brings all to doom, The Slayer Time, Ancient of Days, come hither to consume.\" That passage does speak of Time as the killer, as Rossi says, though in different words from \"I am time, the destroyer of worlds.\" Rossi raises it because it gives the line a different sense: Oppenheimer's bomb as a turning of time rather than plain death.","oppenheimer-s-late-speech-on-abolishing-nuclear-weapons":"Oppenheimer is said to have told his scientists, in one of his last speeches, that we cannot afford to think we cannot abolish all nuclear weapons. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas, who says a UFO shut down ten nuclear missiles, quotes this to Michels. He uses it to back the case that nuclear weapons should be done away with.","oppenheimer-set-up-ufo-secrecy-1940":"J. Robert Oppenheimer is said to have been the one who set up the secrecy around UFOs, using the same men who kept the atomic bomb secret. Michels says \"some interesting people\" claim this, among them a man he calls Rush, who he says told him so \"on record\" in an interview. A man named Bob Maguire, presented on the show as a former CIA contractor, gives it as his own belief: \"Oppenheimer and other people in the Manhattan Project were the original secret keepers.\" If true, UFO secrecy would date back to the very start of the nuclear age.","oracle-at-delphi-as-a-burning-methane-seep":"The famous oracle at Delphi in ancient Greece, where a priestess gave prophecies, sat on a methane seep, a spot where natural gas leaked from the ground and burned. A researcher named Geoffrey Drumm says this on the show without naming a source. If so, the sacred site would have been built around a natural fire coming out of the earth.","orang-pendek-and-surviving-flores-hominids":"Michels raises the Orang Pendek, a creature people report seeing in Indonesia, as something worth speculating about. He brings it up in passing in an episode about strange three-fingered bodies from Peru, and the story ties it to the idea that small early human relatives once found on the Indonesian island of Flores might have survived. He gives no source and offers it only as speculation.","orange-moved-by-psychokinesis-in-a-vivid-dream":"In a vivid dream set in his family's drawing room, an orange suddenly moved eight to ten inches \"as if something had pushed it\", says Deep Prasad, a researcher who tells Michels it happened to him. He describes it as psychokinesis, moving an object by the mind alone, and a person he calls Miss Kaur figures in the story. If true, it would suggest the mind can act directly on matter.","orange-orb-lands-near-an-open-minot-missile-silo-1966":"Sometime between 1966 and 1968, one morning, an orange glowing object came over Minot Air Force Base in North Dakota, hovered, and set down near a missile silo that was open, while a man there screamed that it was landing. The filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this from the material behind his film The Phenomenon, summing it up without naming the witness. If true, an unknown object came down right beside a nuclear missile.","orange-reddish-wobbling-ufos-around-area-51-1980":"Orange-reddish UFOs have been flying low around Area 51, the secret test site in the Nevada desert, since the 1980s and 1990s, and they wobble as if they were riding a wave. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about the two days he spent with Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft near the base.","orb-and-tall-glowing-figure-filmed-at-a-healing-for-a-cancer-2013":"During a healing session for a cancer patient at his home in North Carolina in the mid-2010s, a glowing orb and a tall glowing figure were caught on video, according to Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs and was there. \"All of a sudden in the video, you'll see an orb appear. It comes zooming right up to me,\" he tells Michels. He offers it as filmed proof that the beings he describes show up when he is present.","orb-as-ancient-relic-compared-with-salvator-mundi":"A man named Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO program as a child, tells Michels that the orb he encountered was spoken of as a \"relic,\" which made him wonder whether it was not from NHI (non-human intelligence) or a crash at all, and whether \"these things have been here a long time.\" He compares it to the crystal orb held by Christ in the Salvator Mundi, a painting credited to Leonardo da Vinci. If he is right, such objects would be ancient things that people have known about and even pictured for centuries.","orb-flies-figure-eights-over-the-capitol-for-45-minutes-duri-2025":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he draws UFOs to him and whom NASA has studied, says that in 2025, after a congressional hearing with Representative Anna Paulina Luna, his group held a skywatch from a platform at the Library of Congress and a glowing orb appeared and flew figure-eights over the Capitol and the White House for 45 minutes. He tells Michels he was there, and names others present, among them Representative Eric Burlison.","orb-photo-showing-chris-s-lost-child":"One night a lone glowing orb appeared while he was outside thinking about the son he and his wife had lost, and when he photographed it, \"there was a child\" in the picture. Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says he sees such lights, tells Michels this happened to him, which would mean the orb answered his grief.","orbs-appearing-inside-bledsoe-s-house-2007":"Since 2007, orbs of light have come into Chris Bledsoe's North Carolina home \"just about every day of my life\", appearing in his room over his chairs and flying through the house, he tells Michels. Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs show up around him and whom NASA has studied, says he sees this himself. If true, it would mean that whatever follows him comes right into his house.","orbs-rise-out-of-the-sea-for-danny-jones-at-indian-rocks-bea-2025":"In 2025 at Indian Rocks Beach, Florida, two glowing orbs rose out of the sea when Chris Bledsoe called for them, and a man named Danny Jones filmed them, according to Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs. \"When I asked it... it comes up out of the sea. And suddenly, here it came. Two of them,\" he tells Michels. He tells it as a sign that the lights answer when he asks.","orbs-seen-during-sky-observing-at-bledsoe-home":"Bob McGwier, a former CIA contractor, says that while watching the sky at the North Carolina home of Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs, \"this thing flashed into existence\": a massive, very bright orb just above the trees, 300 or 400 meters away. McGwier tells Michels he saw it himself, alongside Bledsoe, his son Ryan Bledsoe and the writer Richard Dolan.","orbs-seen-with-unmarked-black-helicopters":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange happenings, glowing orbs are sometimes seen along with unmarked black helicopters. Michels says this to the ranch's owner as something the people there \"often see or sometimes see.\" It suggests that someone with aircraft is taking an interest in whatever the orbs are.","orbs-summoned-to-hover-over-jay-anderson-s-house":"He went into a meditative state and asked for something to turn up and \"float down and hover above my house,\" and glowing orbs did. Jay Anderson, a guest on the show, tells Michels this happened to him, which would mean such lights can be called on at will.","origin-of-adjustment-team-philip-k-dick-s-bathroom-light-cha-1950":"The science fiction writer Philip K. Dick got the idea for his 1950s short story The Adjustment Team, later the film The Adjustment Bureau with Matt Damon and Emily Blunt, when the pull chain on a bathroom light he had used many times turned out to be a switch. That is what his wife Tessa Dick told the researcher Riz Virk, who argues on the show that we may live in a hologram, and Virk repeats it to Michels. Told this way, it is a story about reality being quietly rewritten, which is exactly what the story and the film are about.","origins-of-the-us-remote-viewing-program-with-puthoff-targ-a-1960":"Remote viewing, the claimed ability to describe distant places or hidden things using only the mind, is said to have grown into a US government programme between the 1960s and 1970s through the physicists Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ and an artist and psychic named Ingo Swann. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, who later joined the unit, tells Michels that they brought in Swann, that Swann worked out a method called controlled remote viewing, and that the remote viewing unit had already existed for ten years when he got there. Dan Sheehan, speaking as a researcher, links Puthoff and Targ to what he calls the entire psychic programme, but names a psychic called Pat Price rather than Swann. Neither gives a source. If true, it would mean the US government spent years trying to use psychics for intelligence work.","orion-and-osiris-symbolism-in-the-apollo-patch-1960":"Hidden in the Apollo programme patch of the 1960s, behind the letter A, is the constellation Orion, with the crossbar of the A deliberately lined up with Orion's belt, a star group ancient Egyptians tied to the god Osiris. Michels points this out himself, reading the patch as a sign that NASA's moon programme carried pagan symbolism on purpose.","orion-correlation-theory-1994":"The Orion correlation theory holds that the great pyramids at Giza are laid out to match the stars of Orion's belt. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels the idea came from Robert Bauval, who put it forward in his book The Orion Mystery in 1994. Hancock presents it as a known theory from that book rather than his own. On a later episode, a pyramid researcher named Geoffrey Drumm brings it up himself, saying 'some of these structures are connected to Orion's belt. I think that's true.' Drumm endorses the idea outright but does not credit Bauval or give a date. If true, it would mean the builders planned the site around the sky with a deliberate astronomical design in mind.","osap-at-skinwalker-ranch-and-the-hitchhiker-effect-2007":"The story is that from about 2007 to 2012 the Pentagon secretly paid for scientists to study Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for strange happenings, and that many who worked there reported what they called the hitchhiker effect, the sense that something from the ranch followed them home. Michels tells it himself, calling the effort OSAP, run under the wing of Senator Harry Reid, with researchers digging holes and looking into exotic weaponry on the ranch. He also names the billionaire contractor Robert Bigelow, whose company got the Pentagon money, and says in an older episode that it was AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, led by Luis Elizondo, with data that is classified and exempt from FOIA, the US law that lets anyone request government records, because a private company ran it. Later he credits a 2017 article by the journalist Leslie Kean with exposing the program and notes that Bigelow had studied the ranch before. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet, relaying others, says the DIA, the Pentagon's military intelligence agency, funded about two years of research there, crediting Jim Lacatski, Colm Kelleher and the journalist George Knapp. The ranch's current owner, Brandon Fugal, calls it a black-budget program that brought together dozens of government scientists and military researchers over years, cites Kelleher and Knapp's book Skinwalkers at the Pentagon, and says it ended in 2013, not 2012. If true, the US government took the ranch's strangeness seriously enough to pay to investigate it.","oscar-wolf-s-project-blue-book-special-unit-on-700-unexplain-1950":"Project Blue Book was the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. The story here is that while it told the public that sightings were swamp gas or misidentified birds, a special unit inside it quietly handled more than 700 cases that were real and unexplained. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this second-hand, from a man named Oscar Wolf who, Sheehan says, \"worked directly with project blue book.\" In another conversation Sheehan gives his source as Albert Stein, an Army clerk typist assigned to Blue Book, who told him about \"over 700 actual sightings\" behind what Sheehan calls a \"buffaloing operation.\" An Air Force fact sheet on the project, issued in January 1985, says that from 1947 to 1969 \"a total of 12,618 sightings were reported to Project BLUE BOOK\" and that \"Of these 701 remain 'Unidentified,'\" which is close to Sheehan's figure. The fact sheet gives that number and says nothing about who worked on those cases, so a special unit is Sheehan's account, and the sheet neither states it nor rules it out. If it were true, the Air Force's public brush-off would have been a cover for real study.","oscar-wolfe-deathbed-interview-about-s4-autopsy-film-and-gra":"A dying man named Oscar Wolfe is said to have described seeing a film of an autopsy at S4, a secret site at Area 51, the classified test base in Nevada, and a gray alien, the small grey-skinned being of UFO stories. Jeremy Corbell tells Michels he set up the interview himself, bringing in a researcher named Richard Dolan to question the man \"as he was dying on like I think it was like a dialysis machine\", and he points to Wolfe's DD-214s, US military discharge papers, as proof of his service; a man named Ruben Langdon is also named. If true, it would mean bodies of non-human beings were studied at Area 51.","osireion-built-to-tap-the-water-table":"The Osireion, an ancient stone structure at Abydos in Egypt, was \"excavated from the bedrock and constructed to tap directly into the water table below,\" according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum. He says this on the show in an episode about what he calls a huge energy grid beneath Egypt's pyramids, without naming a source. If he is right, the builders placed the structure on purpose to reach the groundwater, perhaps as part of that larger system.","osiris-myth-as-preserving-antediluvian-knowledge-via-byblos":"The Egyptian myth of the god Osiris, whose story carries him from Egypt to Byblos on the Phoenician coast, is read as a coded memory of \"an ark floating on the water\" holding a body of knowledge from the world before the great Flood. A guest named Tim Alberino offers this as his own reading of the myth on the show, without naming a source. If he is right, the old story would be preserving a memory of a lost civilization and how its knowledge survived.","osiris-shaft-water-samples-tested-with-the-osirion-7-mission-2020":"Water from the Osiris Shaft, a deep flooded shaft under the Giza plateau in Egypt, has been sampled and tested, according to Jeffrey Drum, a researcher who argues the pyramids once worked as an energy system and who says he took part. Drum tells Michels he had the water tested through an archaeological project he belongs to, the Osirion 7 mission, run with a man named Jim Westerman to find out where the water in the Osirion, an ancient Egyptian site, comes from. He brings it up as evidence for his ideas about how the pyramid complex used water.","oslo-peace-process-dinner-clinton-presses-arafat-to-take-the-1998":"Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former senior CIA officer, says that at a 1998 dinner at the royal castle in Oslo, Norway, he heard Bill Clinton press the Palestinian leader Yasser Arafat: \"Take the deal, Yasser. Take the deal. It's your last best chance for peace.\" He tells Michels this as someone who was there, along with his first impression of Vladimir Putin at the same gathering.","osmium-and-cadmium-implants-in-the-m-types":"Some of the small three-fingered bodies said to come from Nazca, Peru, the ones Michels calls the M-types, are said to carry metal implants made with osmium and cadmium. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, calling them \"extremely rare earth metals.\" He raises it because unusual metals inside the bodies would point away from a simple fake.","oss-project-mcgregor-moves-italian-ufo-to-wright-patterson-1944":"A UFO is said to have crashed in northern Italy during the Second World War, and agents of the OSS, America's wartime spy service, are said to have gone after it under a secret effort called Project McGregor and, by 1945, moved it to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio for study. Michels tells this himself in an episode with the presidential advisor Harald Malmgren, saying the operatives \"targeted advanced access technology, including the rumored crashed UFO.\" In a later episode he tells it more loosely, calling the craft \"Magenta\" and saying only that it crashed in Italy before the American cases and was handed over to the US, with no agency, project, date or base named; if true, the US would have held a crashed craft before 1947.","oswald-at-atsugi-u-2-base-1950":"Lee Harvey Oswald, before he killed President Kennedy, worked at Atsugi in Japan, the base where, Dan Sheehan says, \"the CIA was running the U2 spy plane program\". Sheehan, a guest who talks with Michels about Kennedy and UFOs, says this without naming a source. If it matters as he suggests, Oswald was close to one of the CIA's most secret projects of the 1950s.","oswald-could-not-have-fired-all-the-shots-that-killed-jfk-1963":"Lee Harvey Oswald could not have fired all the shots that killed President Kennedy in Dallas in 1963. That is the claim of Dan Sheehan, a lawyer and longtime researcher of government secrets, who tells Michels he knows who did kill Kennedy and that Oswald \"could not have gotten off the number of shots\". If so, more than one shooter was involved.","oswald-infiltrates-free-cuba-group-and-is-arrested-in-new-or-1963":"In 1963 in New Orleans, Lee Harvey Oswald worked his way into an anti-Castro 'Free Cuba' group, and that was when he was arrested on the street. A researcher named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as something already known, without naming a source. In his telling Oswald was acting as an infiltrator, not simply a lone man.","oswald-s-attempted-call-to-nags-head-1963":"In Dallas in 1963, around the time of the Kennedy assassination, Lee Harvey Oswald is said to have asked to place a telephone call to Nags Head and tried to get it through. Michels tells this himself, citing no source beyond Oswald's own request, in an episode with the lawyer Danny Sheehan that links UFOs to the Kennedy killing. He raises it as a clue to whom Oswald might have been connected with.","oswald-s-fair-play-for-cuba-leaflets-carry-banister-s-camp-s-1963":"The leaflets Lee Harvey Oswald handed out in New Orleans in 1963 for Fair Play for Cuba, a group supporting Castro's Cuba, carried the address 544 Camp Street, which according to the author Peter Levenda was the same address as the office of Guy Banister, a former FBI man. Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source, though he points to the leaflets themselves. It matters because it would tie Oswald's supposedly pro-Cuba activity to a man on the opposite side of politics.","oswald-s-planned-role-and-the-killing-of-officer-tippit-1963":"In Dallas in 1963, the plan was supposedly for Lee Harvey Oswald to shoot at Kennedy and run, and then for a Dallas police officer named J. D. Tippit to kill him. A guest named Dan Sheehan lays out this theory to Michels; elsewhere it is told with Oswald meant to miss on purpose so Cuba could be blamed. If true, it would mean Oswald was a planned fall guy for a larger plot.","oswald-trained-by-oni-part-of-private-field-intelligence-ope-1950":"Lee Harvey Oswald was trained not by the CIA but by the Office of Naval Intelligence, the Navy's own intelligence service, and took part in a private intelligence operation run out of New Orleans, according to a researcher named Dan Sheehan. He tells Michels this in an episode about JFK and UFOs, though the name of the operation is cut off in the recording, and he gives no source. If true, the man accused of killing Kennedy was tied to a Navy intelligence network rather than acting alone.","other-remote-viewing-teams-trained-by-pat-price-and-joe-mcmo-1970":"There were rumours, Michels says, that besides the remote viewers working with the physicist Hal Puthoff, other teams were trained by two of the programme's viewers, Pat Price and Joe McMoneagle. Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe a distant place or target with the mind alone, which US government programmes tested from the 1970s on. Michels raises this himself as rumour, without naming a source; if true, the psychic spying effort was wider than the one known group.","other-ufo-nuclear-witnesses":"Many people besides Robert Salas say they saw UFOs show up around nuclear weapons. Salas, a former Air Force officer who says he witnessed such an incident himself, tells Michels he is not the only one, and names Dave Chandel and Walter Feel among them. His point is that the story does not rest on one man's word.","otto-skorzeny-in-the-same-building-as-ruark-and-sicre":"In Spain, two men named Robert Ruark and Ricardo Sicre are said to have lived in the same building as Otto Skorzeny, the Nazi commando who settled there after the war. Jason Simosa, a researcher, puts it to Michels as a pointed question, \"Who lives in the same building as Roark and Sicre? Otto Skorzeny\", without naming a source, as a sign that these men moved in the same circles.","otto-skorzeny-s-ratline-used-knights-of-malta-passports":"Otto Skorzeny, who ran a ratline, a secret escape route for smuggling people out of Europe, is said to have used passports from the Catholic order of the Knights of Malta to get them out. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this in passing, as something he has learned rather than seen. If true, it would tie a religious order to the smuggling network.","otto-skorzeny-working-with-the-mossad-1970":"In the early 1970s Otto Skorzeny, a former Nazi SS commando officer, was working with the Mossad, Israel's foreign intelligence service. A researcher named Jason Jorjani says this to Michels in passing, without naming a source, while tracing what became of Nazi figures and their secrets after the war.","ouija-board-warning-of-an-aboriginal-uprising-taken-to-the-a-1963":"Sometime between 1963 and 1966 in New York, a Ouija board session warned of an Aboriginal uprising in Australia outside Perth, a message so specific it was taken to the Australian consulate. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels he took part, saying \"we were told\" of it, which would mean the board was giving out real-world predictions.","outer-limits-episode-premiered-before-the-hill-encounter-1961":"Jesse Michels says that the alien abduction reported by Betty and Barney Hill, a couple who said they were taken aboard a craft, came about 11 days after the first episode of the CBS science fiction series The Outer Limits aired. He draws it from American Cosmic, a book by the religion scholar Diana Pasulka, which he recommends to guests on two shows, and he raises it because the timing would suggest that what was on television and what people report seeing may shape each other.","oval-office-briefing-on-zarqawi-camp-before-iraq-invasion-2003":"In 2003, before the US invaded Iraq, a CIA official told President George W. Bush in the Oval Office exactly where the militant leader Abu Musab al-Zarqawi was: \"He's in this camp in northeast Iraq.\" Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA official billed on the show as a CIA chief, tells Michels he was the one who said it, in a briefing that also involved CIA director George Tenet. If true, the US knew where Zarqawi was hiding before the war began.","over-30-000-nuraghe-towers-on-sardinia":"The Italian island of Sardinia once held \"over 30,000\" nuraghe, ancient round stone towers, according to a researcher named Tim Alberino. He mentions the number to Michels in an episode about giants, without naming a source. He brings it up to show how much building an old, little-understood people did on a single island.","over-a-hundred-crash-retrieval-cases-in-majic-eyes-only":"More than a hundred cases of UFO crash retrieval, meaning crashed craft being found and taken away by authorities, are collected in the book Majic Eyes Only by the researcher Ryan Wood, according to a UFO researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb. He recommends the book to Michels as evidence without going into any of the cases. If the cases hold up, they would suggest such recoveries happened many times, not just once.","overseas-prospecting-for-psionic-assets":"People with supposed psychic powers, called \"psionic assets\", are said to be sought out in other countries, screened and brought to secret sites. Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he flew a recovered UFO, tells Michels that there are operations \"OCONUS\", military shorthand for outside the continental United States, \"where prospecting for psionic assets takes place and they're highly coveted\". The line comes back in later episodes, and Michels himself retells it, saying Barber spoke of such people being rounded up in disaster zones, \"in sometimes some not so ethical ways\". Michael Herrera, who says he saw a huge UFO in the Indonesian jungle, repeats Barber as saying the assets are screened from third world countries. The UFO researcher Steven Greer goes further, calling it trafficking, placing it in Indonesia and saying the people are moved to covert places \"where they'd be screened, tested\".","overwhelming-evidence-of-subsidence-on-the-atlantic-floor-al":"The floor of the Atlantic Ocean is said to show overwhelming evidence of major sinking, concentrated along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, the underwater mountain chain running down the middle of the ocean. A researcher named Randall Carlson tells Michels this, without naming a source, and connects it to Plato's dialogues Timaeus and Critias, which tell of Atlantis, a land that sank. If true, it would mean Plato's sunken island might reflect something real.","owens-168-demonstrations-over-20-years-1960":"A man named Ted Owens, who claimed he could make strange events happen, is said to have staged 168 demonstrations over about 20 years, and roughly two-thirds of them came out the way he said beforehand. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a doctorate who studies psychic claims, gives Michels these numbers from his own files on Owens. If the count holds, Owens hit far more often than luck would explain.","owens-approaches-puthoff-and-targ-at-sri-and-is-rebuffed-1970":"In the 1970s, a man named Ted Owens, who claimed psychic powers, contacted the physicists Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ at the Stanford Research Institute, a California research lab where they were testing psychics, and was turned away. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who studies psychic phenomena, tells Michels that Owens asked them why they were wasting time with the performer Uri Geller when he, Owens, was \"the world's greatest subject\".","owens-brain-operated-on-beside-a-circle-of-tarantulas-on-a-t":"Ted Owens, a man who claimed psychic powers, said that while driving on a highway in Texas he suddenly found himself standing on the road next to his vehicle, which was surrounded by a full circle of tarantula spiders, and the story's title says this was when his brain was operated on. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as he remembers it from Owens. To Owens it would explain where his powers came from.","owens-declares-war-on-the-us-government-poltergeist-attacks-1980":"Between about 1980 and 1983, a man named Ted Owens is said to have declared \"war against the United States government\", after which Navy ships and nuclear power plants supposedly suffered poltergeist attacks, meaning unexplained disturbances blamed on unseen forces. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who studies psychic claims, tells Michels that Owens told him this and that he then kept getting newspaper clippings reporting such attacks on Navy vessels. If true, it would mean one man could strike at the military by mental means.","owens-extorts-sports-team-owners-and-a-suicide-follows-1970":"A man named Ted Owens, who claimed unusual powers, tried to get the owners of major league sports teams to pay him to \"protect the team\", and when owners backed out of their promise, in at least one case a suicide followed. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this from his own knowledge of Owens, without citing a document. As Mishlove tells it, Owens' claimed powers came with a dark and threatening side.","owens-predicts-end-of-california-drought-with-strange-weathe-1976":"In 1976, during a bad California drought, Ted Owens, a man who claimed strange powers, said: \"I'm going to end this drought, and it's going to happen within a couple of days,\" and the story goes that odd weather and UFOs followed. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a doctorate who studies psychic claims, tells Michels this from files kept by Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ, two researchers in Palo Alto. If true, Owens predicted, or caused, the weather on cue.","owens-space-intelligences-mantid-like-beings-in-an-invisible-1960":"A man named Ted Owens claimed he could move things with his mind by sending telepathic messages to 'space intelligences', insect-like beings resembling praying mantises who ride in an invisible giant UFO above the Earth. A researcher of the paranormal named Jeffrey Mishlove quotes Owens as saying 'I send telepathic messages to my friends' and 'they do it.' A philosopher named Jason Jorjani tells it too, saying Owens named the mantids Twitter and Tweeter and said they gave him his powers.","owens-summons-lightning-near-the-camden-bridge-1960":"In Philadelphia in the 1960s, a man named Ted Owens is said to have pointed his finger at the Camden Bridge, and within moments lightning struck nearby, not on the bridge but in that general area. The PhD Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this from a sworn statement by a witness named Sydney Margolies, and he also names the books How to Contact Space People and The PK Man (\"PK\" stands for psychokinesis, moving things with the mind). If the statement is right, Owens could call up weather on command.","owens-takes-an-eagles-fullback-out-of-a-game":"A man named Ted Owens is said to have told a sports writer, in the middle of a Philadelphia Eagles game, \"I'm going to take out this player,\" after which an Eagles fullback was taken out of the game, and the writer reported it in a newspaper article. Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD and studies psychic claims, tells Michels this as Owens's story passed on to him. If true, it would suggest Owens could affect events at a distance by intention.","owens-three-ufo-sighting-experiment-around-san-francisco-1976":"In 1976 a man named Ted Owens, who claimed he could make UFOs appear, announced he would produce three UFO sightings within 90 days and within 100 miles of San Francisco, with San Diego serving as a comparison area. Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a doctorate in parapsychology, tells Michels he took part in the test, quoting Owens' promise: \"I'm going to create three UFO sightings within a 90-day period within a 100 miles of San Francisco.\" If it worked, it would suggest a person's mind could call up UFOs on demand.","owens-tries-to-sway-football-games-while-drinking-1970":"Ted Owens, a man who claimed psychic powers, is said to have sat in his living room smoking cigars and drinking whiskey with two or three TV sets showing football games, trying to change how the games came out with his mind while drunk. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who studies psychic phenomena, tells Michels this as something he knows about Owens, placing it in the 1970s and 80s.","owner-s-own-physical-impact-hitchhiker-effect":"The owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange happenings, says the place has had \"a very real physical impact on people's lives, including mine,\" a version of what is called the hitchhiker effect, the idea that something from the ranch follows people away from it. He tells Michels this from his own experience, and if true it would mean whatever is at the ranch can harm people's bodies.","p3-operators-as-interfaces-to-et-vehicles":"Some people, called P3 operators, go through hard training so they can act almost as a link between human and machine, connecting the people who control them with ET, or alien, vehicles. This is what Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he flew UFO retrieval missions, has described, according to the UFO activist Steven Greer, who repeats it to Michels. If true, it would mean humans are being trained to help steer alien craft.","pablo-amaringo-on-flying-saucers-as-spirit-world-vehicles-2005":"A man in the Peruvian Amazon named Pablo Amaringo once said, quite casually, that flying saucers are \"vehicles for entering and leaving the spirit world.\" The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels this on two shows, passing on what Amaringo told him, and points to his own books Supernatural and Visionary for it. In one telling Hancock puts it around 2005, in the other more loosely in the 1990s or 2000s; if Amaringo is right, the saucers would belong to the world of visions, not to outer space.","pacaccini-says-he-saw-35-second-video-of-captive-creature-1996":"A Brazilian UFO researcher named Vitorio Pacaccini says he saw, \"with my own eyes,\" a 35-second military video of an alien creature being held in captivity in Brazil in 1996. He tells Michels this as something he watched himself. If true, it would be film of a living non-human being in army hands.","pacaccini-shot-at-and-disappears-for-20-years-1996":"In 1996, Vitorio Pacaccini, one of the people who dug into the Varginha case, in which people in that Brazilian town say the army captured a living alien, was shot at and then dropped out of sight for 20 years, according to the documentary filmmaker James Fox, who tells Michels that Pacaccini \"apparently was shot at.\" Pacaccini tells it himself on the show a little differently: on the road between Varginha and Três Corações, something hit his back bumper twice, and from the noise he knew it was a gunshot. He says nothing about vanishing for 20 years, and if the shooting happened, someone wanted the case left alone.","pais-agrees-his-devices-facilitate-vacuum-energy":"The inventions of Salvatore Pais, including his rumored \"free energy\" devices, do not make energy themselves, according to Dave Rossi, a guest who says aliens taught him advanced physics. Instead, he suggests, they help draw on vacuum energy, the energy some physicists say fills empty space. Rossi tells Michels he raised this idea with Pais himself and that Pais agreed with him, though he adds that he does not want to speak for him. If true, it would change what those disputed devices are thought to do.","pais-inspired-by-steven-greer-documentaries":"Salvatore Pais, a Navy engineer with patents for unusual flight technology, tells Michels that part of what inspired him was \"a lot of things that were done by Dr. Steven Greer\", a UFO activist. He means Greer's documentaries Close Encounters of the Fifth Kind and Unacknowledged, which would mean that films about UFOs helped shape a Navy scientist's work.","pais-on-theory-of-everything-plasma-as-doorway":"It may be possible to reduce an object's inertial mass, making it behave as if it weighed less so it is easier to move, by using plasmas, the hot electrically charged gas, rather than solid materials. A guest named Dave Rossi, who says aliens taught him advanced physics, tells Michels this is his reading of what a scientist named Dr. Salvatore Pais said on Theory of Everything, a podcast hosted by Curt Jaimungal, while adding he does not speak for Pais. If true, it would point toward how unusual craft might fly.","pais-patent-test-at-pax-river-had-no-definitive-results-2025":"In 2025 the Navy tested at least one of the unusual patents credited to an inventor named Salvatore Pais, at Naval Air Station Patuxent River in Maryland, but got no clear answer. This is what a congressman named Eric Burlison tells Michels: officials admitted to him that one of the designs had been put to a test, he believes the one meant to defeat gravity, and that they 'weren't able to have any definitive results.' If such a device worked, it would mean flight without ordinary propulsion, which is why a test at all is worth telling.","pais-s-career-at-navy-programs-and-space-force":"A Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais describes his own career on the show. He says he was \"fortunate to work for strategic systems programs at the Washington Navy Yard,\" a Navy office in Washington, D.C., and then moved on to the Space Force.","pais-stops-jesse-s-questions-in-motel-interview-2020":"In an interview at a motel near Pax River, a Navy air base in Maryland, Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist known for patents on strange craft designs, cut Michels off when the science got too deep, as Michels tells it: Pais \"freaked out\" and said, \"Jesse, stop right there. Like, just stop asking.\" Pais tells it himself too, naming the fifth dimension as the topic he would not touch: \"We'll not touch on that. Leave it there, Jesse. Trust me on that.\" To Michels, it suggests Pais knows more than he is allowed to say.","pais-told-ashton-forbes-that-the-mh370-orbs-show-the-pais-ef-2023":"A Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, who holds patents on unusual craft designs, says that in 2023 he watched the videos said to show glowing orbs around the vanished airliner MH370 and saw in them the \"Pais effect,\" a physics effect he named after himself, at work. He tells Michels he said so to Ashton Forbes on Forbes's first episode of his Hard Truths podcast: \"when I saw that thing open up, I saw the Pais effect in action.\" If he is right, the videos would show his own theoretical physics being used in the real world.","pakistani-gunsmith-who-hammers-out-aks-from-aircraft-scrap-o":"Just over the Afghan border in Pakistan, a man living in a mud hut is said to hammer out working AK rifles from scrap aircraft metal on a granite rock. A guest named Joe McMoneagle tells Michels this as something he was told. It is a story about how much a skilled craftsman can do with almost nothing.","palermo-stone-shows-pre-dynastic-egyptian-civilization":"The Palermo Stone, an ancient Egyptian inscribed stone, is said to show that Egyptian civilization goes back long before the age of the pharaohs who ruled in dynasties. A guest named Geoffrey Drumm says this on the show, hedging that he \"believes\" it is the Palermo Stone that shows it. If true, Egypt's civilization would be much older than the usual history allows.","palomar-transients-missing-in-earth-s-shadow-1949":"Old sky photographs taken at Palomar Observatory in California between 1949 and 1957 show brief flashes of light that appear and then vanish. Few of them fall inside Earth's shadow, which is what you would expect if they were sunlit objects circling the planet. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel describes the work on the show. She leads VASCO, a project comparing old sky surveys with modern ones. She explains that when her team checked a sample of these flashes against Earth's shadow, \"guess what? We get a huge deficit.\" She places the objects at geosynchronous distance, the height where an orbit keeps pace with Earth's turning. She credits a colleague, Guy Nir, with the work. Michels sums up the finding in another episode: the objects disappear on the dark side of Earth, where the sun cannot light them. If the reading holds, something reflective was orbiting Earth in the early 1950s.","pamela-fails-her-trust-test-while-about-70-scout-craft-watch-1965":"Sometime between 1965 and 1967, in Indian Springs Valley, Nevada, out on the ranges near an ammunition bunker, a figure named Pamela took a test of trust and failed it while about 70 scout craft watched. Charles Hall, who says he spent three years among tall white aliens at Area 51, tells Michels he was there, and says simply: \"On that night, she failed the test.\" If it happened, it would mean those beings test the people around them and come out in force to watch.","pan-starrs-discovers-oumuamua-2017":"In 2017 the Pan-STARRS telescope in Hawaii found a fast-moving object in space called 'Oumuamua, which Michels later calls \"the first ever interstellar object to visit our solar system,\" meaning it came from beyond the Sun's family of planets. Michels tells it himself, first in an episode with the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb and again later, where he drops the telescope and Hawaii. It matters because Loeb has argued the object deserves a serious look as something unusual.","pandolfi-email-senators-let-him-carry-the-keys":"Ron Pandolfi, a CIA scientist, wrote in an email that senators and congressmen keep asking questions without understanding that the issue is \"not the material, it's the access to the material,\" that \"you can get in a lot of trouble,\" and that when he tells them so \"they just let me carry the keys.\" The UFO researcher Grant Cameron says he has this email and tells Michels about it. Cameron reads it as Pandolfi holding the access to UFO secrets, while the words quoted say only that lawmakers left access in his hands, not what lies behind it.","pandolfi-going-to-los-alamos-to-talk-to-a-live-alien-1991":"In 1991 a man named Ron Pandolfi is said to have told people he met that he was going to Los Alamos, the weapons laboratory in New Mexico, that week to talk to a live alien. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron relays this to Michels, tying the story to Pandolfi's contacts Dan Smith and John B. Alexander. If true, it would mean a living non-human being was held at a US government lab.","pandolfi-s-teenage-metallurgical-analysis-of-a-glomar-explor":"As a teenager, a man named Ron Pandolfi is said to have run a metal analysis on a piece his father gave him, a piece tied to the Glomar Explorer, a ship his father's people were involved with. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron passes this on to Michels as something he was told, calling it \"another story\" and giving it only briefly. The tale suggests Pandolfi was handling unusual hardware from an early age.","pandolfi-s-uncle-a-founding-member-of-the-oss-1940":"A Dr. Pandolfi who is said to have blocked someone's request for information has an uncle, Louis Pandolfi, who was a founding member of the OSS, the US wartime spy service that came before the CIA. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this, as part of a story that also involves the researcher Jacques Vallée. Simosa raises the family tie to suggest the man who shut the request down has deep roots in the intelligence world.","pandolfi-steers-clinton-toward-a-maccabee-briefing-1990":"In the 1990s, a man named Ron Pandolfi, whom the story places at the CIA, is said to have told President Bill Clinton he should be briefed on UFOs by Bruce Maccabee, a man who had written a book about UFOs and the FBI. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as part of a larger account of the Clinton White House looking into UFOs. If true, it would mean a CIA man pointed a sitting president toward an outside UFO researcher.","panelists-claiming-trade-agreements-with-other-planets":"Some people in this world claim that humanity already has trade agreements with other planets and swaps goods back and forth with them. Peter Levenda, a writer presented on the show as a CIA historian, tells Michels he once sat on a conference panel with men who insisted on exactly this.","pantex-diamond-shaped-ufo-in-2015":"In 2015 a diamond-shaped UFO is said to have appeared over the Pantex facility in Texas, where nuclear weapons are assembled. Michels mentions it briefly himself, citing records released under what he calls the PURSUE act, as one more case of unknown objects showing up at nuclear sites.","paper-great-pyramid-concentrates-electric-fields-around-the":"A scientific paper published some time ago found that the Great Pyramid can store and concentrate electric fields right around the King's Chamber, the granite room deep inside it, according to Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher who argues the pyramids were machines. Drumm tells Michels about the paper without naming it or its authors. If true, it would support the idea that the pyramid was built to handle energy rather than serve as a tomb.","paper-on-the-moon-s-effect-on-lightning":"A scientific paper called \"The moon effect on lightning\" is said to show that the moon influences lightning on Earth. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher of the pyramids who says an alien showed him their true purpose, brings up the paper to Michels without saying who wrote it or when. He uses it to support the idea that the moon shapes electrical activity on Earth.","paper-on-why-ufos-appear-near-nuclear-sites-spacetime-distur":"A retired Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says he has written a paper explaining why UFOs show up near nuclear sites such as Chernobyl, and that if it is right the reason \"has nothing to do with\" what people usually assume. He tells Michels the real cause is disturbances in spacetime around such places, not interest in the weapons or reactors themselves, which would change what the many sightings near nuclear sites mean.","paperclip-and-the-gehlen-network-as-a-homecoming-nazi-german-1930":"Nazi Germany may have been set up as a kind of field laboratory for \"highly unethical\" experiments, so that bringing its people to America afterward was really a homecoming. The writer Jason Jorjani offers this theory to Michels without naming a source. He points to Paperclip, the US programme that brought German scientists over after the war, and to the absorption of the Gehlen network, the German spy ring of General Reinhard Gehlen that US intelligence took over, and says both were \"in a lot of ways\" a homecoming.","paperclip-scientists-confirm-german-ufo-work-to-astronauts-1960":"In the 1960s, German scientists brought to the United States after the war under a programme called Paperclip are said to have confirmed Germany's UFO work to some American astronauts at weekend meetings. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas tells Michels this as something passed on to him, saying that other Paperclip scientists 'confirmed this' during those weekends. If true, it would mean men who had worked in Nazi Germany were quietly vouching for wartime German flying-craft research to America's own spacemen.","paperclip-scientists-in-new-mexico-mail-soviet-held-colleagu-1947":"Scientists brought to New Mexico after the war under Operation Paperclip, the American programme that recruited German scientists, went into the nearby towns and mailed letters back to Germany, and couriers then carried those letters on to colleagues held in the Soviet Union. A researcher named Peter Levenda tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, secrets from American weapons work could have leaked to the Soviets through ordinary mail.","paperclip-scientists-split-between-redstone-arsenal-and-los-1945":"After the Second World War, the German scientists brought to America under Operation Paperclip, the US programme that recruited them, were split into two camps, according to a guest presented as a Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown. He tells Michels that those who were \"Nazis in name only\" settled around Redstone Arsenal, an Army base in Huntsville, Alabama, while \"the true believers\" went to Los Alamos, the nuclear weapons laboratory in New Mexico. He gives no source, and he links this split to later events he says involved contact with non-human beings.","paperclip-scientists-work-on-the-magenta-craft-1945":"Top German scientists from the Nazi era, brought to the US after the war under Operation Paperclip, the American programme that recruited them, are said to have gone to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio to work on the \"Magenta craft,\" a UFO said to have crashed in Italy. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode with the presidential advisor Harald Malmgren. If true, some of the best rocket and aircraft minds of the day would have been put to work studying a recovered craft.","papoose-lake-map-altered-eight-days-after-lazar-went-public-1989":"US Department of the Interior geological maps of Groom Lake and Papoose Lake, in Nevada, are said to have been changed, with a stamp dated May 23, 1989, \"exactly 8 days\" after Bob Lazar went public with his claims of secret work at the site. The researcher Luigi Vendittelli tells Michels about the date stamp, which he reads as a reaction to Lazar's story. If so, it would mean the government moved quickly to alter records about the place Lazar described.","paralysis-a-shimmering-object-golden-symbols-and-a-download":"A researcher says he was frozen in place when a shimmering object appeared, took full shape, and filled his mind with knowledge the moment it touched him. He is Deep Prasad, a researcher who works on artificial intelligence, and he tells Michels it first-hand: he \"could no longer move,\" moved his eyes left and right hoping the thing would go away, and when he looked back it had \"completely materialized,\" with \"the most enchanting and beautiful golden symbols\" dancing at its centre, and at its touch he got \"a true download.\" In a fuller telling on the same episode, the guest puts it in a 30th-floor condo in Toronto in 2019 and says the shimmer was a visor that \"belonged to a whole ass face, suit, helmet, everything.\" He adds that when the beings were unmasked they \"literally look like the Peruvian mummies,\" the small bodies from Peru that some in this field say are not human. If true, it would mean non-human beings can pass knowledge straight into a person's mind.","paranormal-activity-in-towns-after-nuclear-explosions-1945":"After the Trinity explosion of 1945, the first atomic bomb test in the New Mexico desert, people in nearby towns reportedly said devils were chasing them, along with all sorts of other paranormal happenings. Michels says this himself, without naming a source. He brought it up first with Jacques Vallee, a French-born computer scientist and longtime UFO researcher, and later with an MIT scientist. The second time he also named Roswell, the New Mexico town where, according to UFO lore, something crashed in 1947, as a place where the same thing happened.","parapsychology-master-s-program-at-john-f-kennedy-university-1980":"Around 1980, John F. Kennedy University in California offered a master's degree in parapsychology, the study of psychic abilities. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of psychic phenomena who holds a doctorate, tells Michels this from his own experience, since he taught there at the time. It is told as a sign that the subject once had a place in ordinary academic life.","parapsychology-programs-at-elite-us-universities":"Several elite US universities once ran research programmes in parapsychology, the study of claimed psychic abilities, among them the Rhine Center connected with Duke, a lab at Princeton, and SRI, a research institute tied to Stanford. Michels says this himself, and on another episode adds that these programmes came out saying \"it seems like there's a constricted present,\" the idea that the present moment is narrower than it seems. He brings it up to show that serious institutions took the subject seriously.","parents-see-a-hooded-being-in-their-bedroom-1950":"One night in the 1950s, near Allentown, Pennsylvania, a couple woke to find a hooded being standing in their bedroom. Their son, Jim Weiner, who speaks on the show as an abduction witness himself, tells Michels the story. He says he never knew about it until Ray Fowler, a UFO investigator, interviewed his mother, and that would suggest such encounters had touched his family a generation before him.","parsons-and-hubbard-s-babylon-working-1946":"In 1946 in Pasadena, the rocket scientist and occultist Jack Parsons and L. Ron Hubbard, later the founder of Scientology, carried out the Babylon Working, a magical rite meant to summon the divine feminine figure known as Babylon, following ideas of the English occultist Aleister Crowley and his novel Moonchild. Jesse Michels tells it himself as the most infamous of Parsons's rituals, to show how closely early American rocketry and the occult were tied.","parsons-crater-and-jack-parsons-laboratory":"Jack Parsons, the man at the centre of Michels's episode on NASA, pagan ritual and secret science, has a crater on the Moon named after him, and some people inside JPL, NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena, are said to still call it \"Jack Parsons Laboratory\". Michels says this himself, without naming a source, to show how deep Parsons's mark on the lab is said to run.","parsons-death-in-a-home-lab-explosion-1952":"The rocket engineer Jack Parsons died in 1952 in an explosion in his home laboratory in Pasadena, officially ruled an accident with volatile chemicals, though some found the circumstances suspicious. Michels tells it himself in an episode on NASA and the occult side of early space science, without naming who doubted the ruling or why. He raises it as a death that may not have been as simple as the official account.","parsons-invokes-bartzabel-against-hubbard-1946":"In 1946 the rocket engineer and occultist Jack Parsons, furious with L. Ron Hubbard, is said to have struck back with magic: in a Miami hotel room he called up Bartzabel, a spirit of the planet Mars. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about pagan ritual and secret science in the early days of American rocketry.","parsons-past-life-regression-as-simon-the-sorcerer":"The rocket engineer Jack Parsons is said to have done a past-life regression, an attempt to recall supposed earlier lives, and found that he had been Simon the Sorcerer, a magician from early Christian stories. Michels tells this himself, and elsewhere explains that Parsons belonged to the OTO, an occult order linked to the occultist Aleister Crowley, where such regressions were done. It ties one of America's rocket pioneers to the occult.","part-of-a-program-shut-down-as-demonic-technology":"Part of a secret US research program was shut down because some people said taxpayer money should not be spent \"pursuing demonic technology.\" The scientist Hal Puthoff, whom the show bills as a CIA UFO expert, says this from his own experience during a debate on the show with Eric Weinstein; if true, religious fear and not science decided what got studied.","parthian-feudal-houses-rule-iran-for-over-a-thousand-years":"The Parthians, an ancient ruling dynasty of Iran, ruled the country directly for 400 years, and their noble feudal houses went on holding power there for more than a thousand years. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as history he knows, without naming a source. If so, the same old families shaped Iran far longer than the Parthian kings themselves.","party-crasher-jc-knows-the-unaired-dark-skies-pilot-1996":"In 1996 a stranger calling himself JC turned up uninvited at a party at the Los Angeles home of Bryce Zabel, a TV writer who had made Dark Skies, a series about aliens. JC told him he knew the unaired first episode. Zabel tells Michels he was there: JC \"says he just wanted to meet me to congratulate me on a successful show and says, 'You guys got a lot right,'\" and made it clear he had seen the pilot, which the public had not. Brent Friedman, who was also there, says JC then said he was from the Office of Naval Intelligence. According to Friedman, JC offered them information in exchange for helping with a \"slow drip disclosure program\", a gradual release of what the government knows about UFOs. If it happened, it would mean intelligence officers were watching a TV show and trying to use it to prepare the public.","parviz-jafari-s-1976-tehran-ufo-encounter":"In 1976 an Iranian pilot named Parviz Jafari had a dramatic run-in with a UFO while flying an F-4 Phantom fighter jet over Tehran, says the documentary filmmaker James Fox, who tells Michels he met Jafari himself. Fox thinks it happened around 1975, and he mentions an assessment of the incident by the Defense Intelligence Agency, the Pentagon's military intelligence arm. In a later episode Michels retells Fox's version without naming the pilot, dates it to about 1979, and says the pilot fired at the object. If true, it would be a military pilot trying to engage an unknown craft.","pascagoula-abduction-near-a-nuclear-sub-shipyard-1973":"Two men, Charles Hickson and Calvin Parker, said that in 1973, while fishing near Pascagoula, Mississippi, they were abducted by aliens, examined and released. Michels tells it himself on several episodes, once dating it to 1974, and once linking it, through the researcher Luigi Vendittelli, to the fact that Pascagoula built nuclear submarines for the Navy, though in that telling he places the town in Missouri. The UFO historian Richard Dolan also tells it, saying it happened \"off the coast of Mississippi\" and that the men \"are abducted,\" without mentioning the shipyard. For Michels, the case fits a pattern of UFO activity near nuclear sites.","passing-massive-body-rearranged-the-planets-and-stripped-mar":"Some large object once passed through our solar system and, with its gravity, rearranged the planets and stripped Mars of its atmosphere. Joe McMoneagle, a guest who argues that Mars once had life, tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would explain how Mars became the dead planet it is now.","past-experimental-interbreeding-between-aliens-and-humans-1990":"Aliens and humans interbred in the past, as an experiment. That is the impression Dan Sherman, a former sergeant who says the NSA hired him to talk with aliens, tells Michels he formed during that work in the early 1990s: \"I got the impression that they had interbreeded before, um, experimentally.\" If true, some human ancestry would be the result of a deliberate experiment.","past-extraterrestrial-presence-on-the-moon-and-mars":"Something extraterrestrial was once present on the Moon and on Mars, says a researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani. He tells Michels it is his own reading: the best explanation for all the evidence added together, which he does not list in this passage. If he is right, someone not from Earth was active in our solar system in the past.","pastor-apologizes-for-misattributing-the-all-demons-view-2026":"In 2026, a view that the beings behind UFOs are all demons was attributed to Congressman Eric Burlison. One of the pastors involved later came out publicly, apologized and said Burlison had not said this. Burlison tells the story himself to Michels, setting the record straight on where he stands.","pasulka-and-the-vatican-archives":"The Catholic Church may know more about UFOs than it lets on, Michels suggests, pointing to the religious studies professor Diana Walsh Pasulka. He says she has visited the Vatican archives and \"speaks with conviction\" that the Church \"knows more than meets the eye.\" Michels mentions this himself in passing, during an episode with a former presidential adviser, and does not give her evidence.","pasulka-and-vallee-aliens-as-the-modern-form-of-angels-and-d":"Aliens may be the present-day version of the angels and demons of older religion, according to the idea Michels credits to two people: a religious studies professor named Diana Pasulka and a French ufologist, someone who studies UFOs, named Jacques Vallée. Michels sums up their view himself, saying both \"believe that aliens are sort of the Contemporary version of Angels and Demons.\" If they are right, today's UFO encounters would be a new face of something people have met throughout history.","pasulka-finds-space-program-records-classified-when-she-star-2012":"When a scholar of religion named Diana Pasulka began her study in 2012, she found that much of the record on the space programme was classified. She tells Michels that she did 'the normal academic search' for space programme information and saw at once how much of it was closed to her. To her, it was an early sign that her research would run into secrecy.","pasulka-meets-garry-nolan-and-sees-his-injury-research-2014":"The scholar Diana Pasulka says she met a scientist named Garry Nolan \"pretty early on, probably about 2014,\" and that he showed her his own research into injuries. She tells Michels that Nolan \"had been recruited just as I was being recruited,\" which would mean the two were drawn into the same quiet effort at the same time, though she does not say here who was doing the recruiting.","pasulka-meets-tyler-and-begins-an-emergent-collaboration-2010":"Diana Pasulka, who writes about UFOs and religion, tells Michels that over roughly 2010 to 2023 she met a man named Tyler, who she says belongs to \"the first tradition,\" which she calls \"the secret tradition.\" She says the meeting grew into a collaboration, and then into \"a transparency that we've never seen before.\" If true, it would mean people inside a hidden world of UFO knowledge have begun to open up to outsiders.","pasulka-s-encounters-source-daughter-of-a-man-thought-to-be-2023":"Michels says that a source in Encounters, the 2023 book by the religion scholar Diana Pasulka, was the daughter of a man Pasulka believed worked in a secret space program, a hidden space effort some think the government runs. According to the daughter, her father would keep using the word \"simulacra\", meaning copies or imitations, at lunch tables with his colleagues. Michels says the detail still gives him chills and raises it in an episode about the idea that we live in a hologram.","pasulka-s-research-on-historical-angelic-contact-for-heaven-2014":"While researching a book on Purgatory called Heaven Can Wait, which Michels dates to 2014, the religious studies professor Diana Pasulka of UNC Wilmington began to document dozens of historical cases of nuns, priests, brothers and saints who reported direct divine or angelic contact. Michels tells this himself, drawing on Pasulka's work. In another episode he gives no year, describes the accounts as more like abductions, and brings up Francis of Assisi, pointing to old church records that might hold encounters much like modern UFO experiences.","pat-price-remote-views-a-file-inside-a-cia-facility-1970":"In the 1970s, a man named Pat Price is said to have used remote viewing, a claimed practice of describing distant places with the mind alone, to read a file inside \"a top CIA security facility\" in the United States. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this from the reports that the remote viewer Ingo Swann wrote for the Guardian's Office, an office of the Church of Scientology, and he also names the researcher Russell Targ. If true, the CIA's own secrets were open to the people it was testing.","pat-price-remote-views-denied-soviet-sites-with-gores-and-st-1970":"In the early 1970s, a man named Pat Price is said to have used remote viewing, the claimed practice of describing a distant place by the mind alone, to describe closed Soviet sites, and he got them right, down to the machinery there and \"a big Tower\". Joe McMoneagle, a guest who talks on the show about remote viewing, passes on what Price told him. He says Price \"not only reported on them with accuracy\" but described what stood inside, which, if true, would mean the mind can spy where no plane or agent could go.","pat-price-remote-views-underground-base-in-mount-hayes-and-t-1973":"In 1973 a psychic named Pat Price, working in a government program of remote viewing, the attempt to see distant places with the mind, is said to have described a huge underground base inside Mount Hayes in Alaska, with \"consoles\" and staff who \"looked like military\". Michels tells it himself as part of the CIA's program, adding three more bases Price named: Mount Perdido in Spain, Mount Inyangani in Zimbabwe and Mount Ziel in Australia; in other episodes he places the work under a man named Skip Atwater and is less sure of the details. An Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, who later worked as a remote viewer, tells Michels that \"Pat Price saw the four alien bases\" in a project called Project 8200, before Buchanan joined the unit. If true, someone would be running hidden bases inside mountains around the world.","pat-price-remotely-reads-a-file-inside-a-top-security-cia-fa-1970":"In the 1970s, a man named Pat Price is said to have used remote viewing, the claimed ability to see a distant place with the mind alone, to describe a secret government site, and even to read a file inside a top-security CIA facility. Dan Sheehan, a guest who says he saw a file on it himself, tells Michels that Price \"had gotten so good at remote viewing\" that he was given the exact longitude and latitude of the facility and read what was there. Sheehan names the researcher Russell Targ and the remote viewer Ingo Swann, and credits reports Swann made to the Guardian's Office, an arm of the Church of Scientology. Joe McMoneagle, a guest who repeats what Targ and the researcher Hal Puthoff told him, tells it differently. In his version Price was supposed to describe an old cabin in the woods and instead \"described a clandestine site\" nearby. If either version is true, it would mean the mind can get past the tightest government security.","pat-price-s-death-linked-to-psychokinesis-program-1970":"A philosopher named Jason Jorjani believes that a man named Pat Price died because of a secret programme on psychokinesis, the supposed power to move or affect things with the mind. Jorjani tells Michels he thinks Price was recruited into that programme, given 'certain operations', and then developed 'serious moral reservations', and that this is why he 'wound up dead'. If he is right, a 1970s mind-power programme would have cost one of its own people his life.","patents-for-a-memory-erasing-neuralizer-technology":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had repeated UFO encounters and whom scientists linked to NASA have studied, says he has seen US patents for technology that erases memory, the kind of thing the film Men in Black called a neuralizer. He tells Michels this from his own reading, saying \"I saw the patents on it,\" without naming them. If true, it would mean memory wiping is more than film fiction.","path-of-souls-the-soul-s-leap-to-orion-and-journey-along-the":"There is an old belief, the path of souls, in which the soul of the dead leaps to the constellation Orion and then travels along the Milky Way, and it is found across North, Central and South America. The writer on lost ancient civilisations Graham Hancock says this to Michels without naming a source, and he points to his series Ancient Apocalypse. For him it is a sign that peoples far apart once shared the same picture of the sky and of death.","pathologist-attributes-infection-to-the-being-s-claw-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, a man named Marco Chereze is said to have died of an infection that started with a scratch from the claw of a non-human being. A doctor identified as Dr. Armando, who speaks as having seen the case, tells Michels it was \"a very distinct and deliberate scratch\" on Chereze's skin, and points to Chereze's autopsy report. Ahmed Foratu, a surgeon at a hospital in the town, also speaks as someone who was there. He presents it as the second of two explanations: that Chereze \"contracted the infection from a scratch, from contact with an extraterrestrial being\". He credits the idea to a Dr. Jannini and to Italo Venturelli, another witness. Both men are firsthand voices, though they frame it differently: Armando speaks of the scratch as a fact of the body, while Foratu offers it as one hypothesis among others. If true, it would mean a person was physically harmed by a being from elsewhere, and that doctors recorded it.","patient-17-s-implant-cut-from-his-leg-and-tested-for-zinc-64-2010":"Sometime in the 2010s, a man known as \"Patient 17\" had a small object, which some believe was an implant placed in his body by non-human beings, cut out of his leg, and it was later tested for zinc-64, a particular form of the metal zinc. Jeremy Corbell tells Michels he saw the object removed and that the man only told him his \"terrifying\" story afterwards. If true, it would point to physical evidence left in a human body.","patrick-afb-helicopter-flies-out-to-the-light-2013":"In 2013, off Cocoa Beach in Florida, a helicopter from nearby Patrick Air Force Base flew out toward a strange light over the water. Charles Buhler, who has worked at NASA, tells Michels he watched it happen: 'we see one of the helicopters from the Air Force base go out to it.' If that is what happened, the military was taking an interest in the light too.","patrick-jackson-s-theory-of-spheres-as-an-alien-global-defen":"The spheres that people report seeing in the sky may belong to a global defense system that some alien civilization runs to \"keep the bad guys out of Earth.\" That is the theory of a man named Patrick Jackson, as the journalist Ross Coulthart passes it on to Michels. If it were true, the objects would be guardians of the planet, not intruders.","patton-s-army-rushes-to-pilsen-before-berlin-1945":"In 1945 General Patton's army raced to Pilsen in Czechoslovakia and \"steamroll[ed]\" there before going on to Berlin. The researcher Jason Simosa points this out to Michels, without naming a source, and calls it \"very interesting\". The hint is that something in Pilsen was worth reaching first, before the German capital.","paul-eluard-s-mantis-collection-and-caillois-mantis-folklore":"The French poet Paul Éluard, one of the founders of surrealism, is said to have kept an \"insane\" collection of praying mantises in his home, and his friend, the French writer Roger Caillois, studied folklore about the mantis. The writer Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source, as part of his account of the surrealists' fascination with the insect.","paul-feyerabend-s-astrology-chart-and-his-rebuke-of-the-scie-1970":"The philosopher of science Paul Feyerabend is said to have printed an astrology chart on the back page of his book Against Method, and to have rebuked a statement by scientists that condemned astrology. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who studies psychic phenomena, tells Michels this as a sign that a serious thinker refused to let scientists dismiss such topics out of hand.","paul-hellyer-briefed-by-greer-press-conference-in-toronto-2000":"Paul Hellyer, a former Canadian minister of defence, went public about UFOs, and the claim is that he did so partly because a UFO activist named Steven Greer briefed him. Greer tells Michels this himself, as someone who took part: he says 'I was one of the reasons why he came out publicly,' and that the two of them held a press conference together in Toronto. A guest named Charlie Foltz also brings Hellyer up on another episode, as an example of a senior official who came forward describing UFOs, though he calls him the prime minister of Canada and says nothing about Greer or Toronto. If true, it would mean a former head of a national defence ministry put his name behind the subject, with Greer's help.","paul-hellyer-dying-canadian-emergency-management-head-sat-in":"A dying Canadian official who had run emergency management is said to have confessed that he was taken to the CIA at Langley, made to sign his silence away, and then flown to Area 51, a secret US test site in Nevada, where he sat inside a craft. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something the Canadian politician Paul Hellyer told him, quoting the man's words as Hellyer gave them: \"I signed my life away and then they took me to Area 51 and I sat inside a craft.\" If true, it would mean the US was holding a craft and showing it to allied officials.","paul-mccartney-s-flaming-pie-dream-and-the-beatles-name-1960":"Paul McCartney of the Beatles is said to have had a dream about a flaming pie, a dream the story links to the band's name. Michels brings it up in an episode about a man who is said to attract UFOs, and he hedges it himself: \"I think Paul McCartney one of the Beatles had a dream about a flaming pie.\" He offers it as a passing aside about visions in the sky, without a source.","paul-mellon-brought-back-a-nazi-flying-disc-1945":"Around the end of the Second World War, a man named Paul Mellon is said to have seen a German flying disc while on a mission to recover enemy technology with a man named Allen Dulles. The source is Mellon's grandson, John Warner IV, who tells Michels his grandfather confided it over a third martini: 'we were in a facility, a hangar, and we saw, you know, a German flying disc', at Pilsen in what is now the Czech Republic. Michels retells it more boldly, with Mellon and Dulles standing on top of a saucer that could use exotic flight principles, once dating it to 1944, while the UFO campaigner Steven Greer goes further still, saying Mellon admitted going over and bringing back a Nazi disc, which would mean Americans had captured such a craft in 1945.","paul-mellon-s-family-and-support-for-fascism-1930":"In the 1930s and early 1940s, the story goes, Paul Mellon's family was 'a huge supporter of Adolf Hitler and fascism', and so were Henry Ford and Watson of IBM. Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for UFO disclosure, tells Michels this second-hand: he says a man named John Warner IV told it to him, and he also names Prescott Bush. If true, it would tie some of America's richest families to fascism.","paul-mellon-s-walking-stick":"Harald Malmgren, who advised American presidents and says he handled UFO material directly, tells Michels about an object in Virginia that he says was Paul Mellon's walking stick. Mellon was a wealthy heir and art collector. Malmgren gives nothing more than \"I was told it was Paul Mellon's walking stick,\" so he is passing on what someone else told him, and what made the stick worth mentioning is left unsaid.","paul-muller-confiscates-and-destroys-vallee-s-tracking-tape-1960":"In the early 1960s, at a French observatory where the young Jacques Vallee, later a well-known UFO researcher, was working, his superior Paul Muller got hold of a tape of Vallee's tracking data. The next morning Muller confiscated the tape and destroyed it, behaving, as the story puts it, \"like a petty army officer\". Michels tells this himself in an episode with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, without naming a source, as an example of an early observatory record of something unexplained being thrown away.","paul-s-ascent-to-the-third-heaven":"The Apostle Paul, in his Second Letter to the Corinthians, describes his own ascent through the heavens to \"the third heaven\", and some scholars believe he may have known the mystical practices of heavenly ascent of his day. Michels brings this up himself, citing unnamed scholars, to suggest that experiences of rising into other realms have deep roots in early Christianity.","paul-s-merkabah-conversion":"The Apostle Paul, who had wanted Christians dead, was turned around by a merkabah experience, a vision of the kind Jewish mystics described as seeing God's heavenly chariot-throne. A researcher named Diana Pasulka says this on the show from her reading of Paul's story, without naming a source. Her point is that it came to him suddenly, without his seeking it.","paul-smith-remote-views-the-uss-stark-attack-1987":"Paul Smith, who served as a psychic spy in a government program that tried to see distant targets with the mind alone, a practice called remote viewing, says that in 1987 he described the attack on the USS Stark in the Persian Gulf during the Iran-Iraq War. He tells Michels, \"I started describing this vessel, which reminded me of an American destroyer,\" in a session tied to his colleague Skip Atwater, and he links it to Washington Post coverage of the attack. If true, it would mean he perceived a real military event from far away.","paul-thigpen-s-path-from-the-woo-to-catholicism":"A man named Paul Thigpen is said to have run into \"the woo\", meaning strange paranormal experiences, and that the woo set him searching for truth until he found his way into the Catholic Church. Dylan Borland, a guest who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, passes this on from Thigpen. He tells it as an example of the strange leading someone toward religion.","paula-harris-gets-an-audrey-call-about-crestone":"A woman named Paola Harris is said to have been thinking how beautiful Crestone, Colorado was and that she would like to go there, when the phone rang and a woman named Audrey told her, 'don't worry, you'll go back'. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something Harris related, in a conversation that also involves the UFO campaigner Steven Greer. The point is that the caller seemed to answer a thought Harris had never spoken aloud.","paulina-zelitsky-s-sunken-city-off-cuba-and-her-arrest-in-me-2010":"A researcher named Paulina Zelitsky reported finding a sunken city off western Cuba. When she went to Mexico to look into a link between that city and Mexico, she was arrested, 'severely intimidated by the authorities' and sent back to Canada. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as what Zelitsky related, and he mentions National Geographic along the way. He tells it as a sign that someone wanted the find left alone.","pax-river-facility-for-lockheed-to-bigelow-transfer-2025":"A congressman named Eric Burlison says that in 2025 he went to Naval Air Station Patuxent River in Maryland, known as Pax River, and saw \"the facility that they were going to transfer from Lockheed Martin to Bigelow Aerospace\", the space company of the businessman Robert Bigelow, meaning a planned handover of recovered material between the two companies. Michels, retelling it, says Burlison told him that if a craft were stored anywhere, it would be at Pax River. A man named Matthew Brown, who calls himself a Pentagon whistleblower, adds that Burlison got the tour but \"was not shown anything resembling a technology of unknown origin.\"","paynter-lands-at-the-wrong-airport-on-the-way-to-chico-1974":"In 1974, on a flight headed for Chico, California, a man named Bill Paynter landed at the wrong airport. John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy, tells this to Michels as something he was part of, saying simply, \"And Paynter has landed at the wrong airport.\"","pdni-memo-waives-uap-ndas-2026":"In 2026, Aaron Lucas, the second-ranking official in the office of the Director of National Intelligence, is said to have sent a memo, \"per the president's directive,\" saying that anyone with information about UAP, the government's term for UFOs, who had signed a non-disclosure agreement would have that agreement waived. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels about the memo as news just sent out. If true, it would free people sworn to secrecy to speak about what they know.","pearly-white-saucer-seen-on-closed-circuit-feed-at-cape-cana-1992":"In 1992, at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station in Florida, a pearly-white saucer about 20 feet across showed up on a closed-circuit video feed. Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor, tells Michels he saw it himself and describes it as \"sort of like a modified egg.\" He does not say where the feed was coming from, and if he is right, a craft like that was being kept somewhere watched by cameras.","pendulums-are-the-most-common-artifact-in-egypt":"Pendulums are the artifact found most often when people dig in the soil of Egypt. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs show up around him and whom NASA has studied, tells Michels this and credits a man named Robert Gilbert. If true, it would hint that the ancient Egyptians had some special use for pendulums.","penetration-appeared-without-swann-ever-discussing-it-before-1998":"Ingo Swann's 1998 book Penetration, in which Swann, a remote viewer (someone said to describe distant places by mind alone), wrote about strange secret encounters, came out of nowhere, according to a guest named Joe McMoneagle. McMoneagle, who knew Swann, tells Michels that it \"came right out of the clear blue\" and that Swann had \"never spoke a word of it\" before, which suggests Swann had kept the matter hidden even from people close to him.","penkovsky-papers-a-nazi-psychic-unit-taken-over-by-the-sovie-1960":"Russian-language reading material from a Soviet colonel named Igor Pinkovski said that Adolf Hitler had a psychic unit called \"Dr. Grunbomb\" in Germany, and that the Soviets later took it over. Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, says he read this in the 1960s while learning Russian at a language school in Monterey, California. If true, it would mean the Soviet psychic research of the Cold War grew out of a Nazi program.","penniston-given-sodium-pentothal-in-interrogation-1980":"A serviceman named Jim Penniston is said to have been drugged with sodium pentothal and questioned after a UFO event at RAF Bentwaters, an air base in England used by the US Air Force, in 1980 or 1981. The UFO researcher Robert Hastings passes it on from Penniston himself. Hastings says Penniston told him some kind of drug was given to him and he was then questioned. Michels mentions the same case, known as the Rendlesham Forest case after the woods by the base, but speaks of its witnesses in general rather than of Penniston by name. If true, it would mean the military went to great lengths to find out, or control, what its own men had seen.","penniston-s-career-stalled-after-rendlesham-1980":"Jim Penniston, one of the US Air Force men who said they witnessed a strange craft in Rendlesham Forest, England, in 1980, is said never to have reached the rank he probably could have because he was a witness. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in a talk with the documentary maker James Fox. If true, it would mean speaking about the event carried a cost inside the military.","penniston-s-sphere-of-influence-near-the-rendlesham-craft-1980":"In 1980, during a reported encounter with an unknown craft in Rendlesham Forest in England, an American airman named Jim Penniston said the area around the object was \"a sphere of influence\" where he felt like he was \"walking through water.\" The researcher Mike Masters repeats Penniston's description to Michels, without naming where it comes from. To Masters it suggests the craft changed the space around it, which fits his idea that such visitors may be humans from the future.","penniston-touches-craft-and-receives-binary-code-1980":"In 1980, in Rendlesham Forest in England, a serviceman named Jim Penniston is said to have walked up to a landed craft and touched it, finding a smooth, glass-like surface with strange symbols etched on its side that looked like hieroglyphs. According to Penniston, touching it gave him a sudden telepathic \"download\" of binary code, the ones and zeros computers use. Michels relays Penniston's account on an episode about UFOs near nuclear bases, a story that, if true, would mean a craft passed a message straight into a man's mind.","pentagon-confirms-navy-thermal-footage":"The Pentagon has confirmed that a piece of thermal-camera UFO footage was really filmed by the Navy, according to the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, who publishes leaked UFO videos. Corbell tells Michels this is what makes the footage interesting, since it means the military itself stands behind it as real Navy film.","pentagon-press-release-denying-the-kandahar-giant-story":"The filmmaker Tim Alberino says he saw what looked like an official Pentagon press release saying the Kandahar giant story is not true; that story holds that US soldiers in Afghanistan fought and killed a giant about twelve feet tall. He tells Michels he saw the release himself, and he describes it only as something that \"looked like\" an official one. For him it matters because a denial would mean the Pentagon took the story seriously enough to answer it.","pentagon-records-of-ufo-detections-and-the-dod-s-five-observ":"The Pentagon is said to hold hundreds of cases where UFOs were caught by cameras, radar and infrared sensors, and from those records the Defense Department has drawn up five traits that show up again and again in the sightings. Michels says this himself, bringing in the researcher Eric Weinstein, though he does not list the five traits in this passage. The point is that the military's own data, not just eyewitness stories, would show a consistent pattern.","pentagon-review-of-the-ornl-report-on-art-s-parts-2024":"The physicist Eric W. Davis says that in August 2024 he and the physicist Hal Puthoff sat in a Pentagon conference room and read a 90-page report from Oak Ridge National Laboratory analysing \"Art's Parts,\" metal fragments long claimed to come from a UFO, and that \"there was nothing there.\" Davis tells Michels this as someone who was in the room. By his account, the lab's analysis found nothing unusual in the pieces.","pentagon-says-only-three-uap-videos-exist-then-another-branc":"After some military videos of UAP, unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's term for UFOs, were released, the Pentagon said there were no more videos beyond those three, and then another branch admitted it had more, the journalist Michael Shellenberger tells Michels, crediting the records researcher John Greenewald Jr. He tells it as a sign that the Pentagon's denials on the subject cannot be taken at face value.","people-across-varginha-report-seeing-spaceships":"In the Brazilian city of Varginha, sightings are said to be everywhere: according to a surgeon named Ahmed Foratu, whose hospital he says once operated on an alien, if you walk through the city 'there are always reports of people who have seen spaceships'. He tells Michels this as what the townspeople say, which would make Varginha a place where seeing craft is part of ordinary local talk.","people-missing-from-native-cultures-traced-in-census-data":"People sometimes go mysteriously missing from native cultures, and the pattern can be traced in census data. Michels says this himself, as something he has heard, while talking with the parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove, and adds that he knows someone who studies it in census records. He gives no names, places or numbers.","people-who-say-they-worked-on-recovered-craft":"Some people say they worked on, or saw, craft of unknown origin that the United States is said to have recovered and kept in secret. Kevin Knuth, a former NASA scientist, tells Michels he has met such people himself: 'I met people who claimed to have worked there or seen them.' He does not name them.","people-who-spoke-out-on-the-program-have-died-2025":"People who were coming forward to speak about a secret effort to recover crashed UFOs have died, according to Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he flew such a recovery himself. He goes further and tells Michels he knows \"for a fact\" that leaders inside the organizations he works for and with have called for \"wet works\", a slang term for killings. If true, it would mean whistleblowers on the subject face real danger.","percival-lowell-s-martian-irrigation-canals":"The astronomer Percival Lowell claimed that some of the dark areas and patterns seen on Mars were irrigation canals built on purpose by a Martian civilisation. Michels tells this himself, as history, in an episode with Joe McMoneagle, a man known for \"remote viewing,\" the claimed ability to see distant places with the mind, saying Lowell \"went as far as to claim\" the patterns were \"purposefully built.\" He raises it as an early case of someone seeing signs of intelligent life on Mars.","perelman-s-unspoken-unified-theory":"Grigori Perelman, the reclusive Russian mathematician who lives in Siberia, has a unified theory of physics and simply refuses to talk about it. Michels says this himself, hedging it with \"I think he claims,\" and gives no source. If true, one of the great open problems of science could already be solved and kept hidden in one man's head.","person-harmed-when-the-gauntlet-object-was-retrieved-2014":"Someone may have been hurt in 2014 while recovering an object called 'the gauntlet' at the Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy research site in Indiana. This comes from Randy Anderson, a man who says he saw alien technology at a military base. He tells Michels that while he was there it 'got brought up' that 'somebody was potentially harmed in the retrieval of that item', which would suggest the object was dangerous to handle.","peru-bill-banning-study-of-ancient-biological-specimens-2025":"Peru has put forward a bill that would forbid the study of ancient biological specimens, according to Michels, speaking with the comedian Joe Rogan in November 2025. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and guesses the bill was \"probably in a response to this video we made,\" meaning a video of his own on such specimens. If he is right, a government would be moving to close off research just as his show drew attention to it.","peruvian-face-peeler-alien-attacks-2023":"In 2023, villagers in San Antonio de Pintoyaku, in the Alto Nanay area of the Peruvian Amazon, said they were being attacked at night by beings some called aliens and others called \"pelacaras,\" or face peelers. Tim Alberino, a man who went there and is listed as a witness, tells Michels what the villagers and a man he names only as Doug told him: the raids always began after dark, with \"two mini flying saucers hovering in the air,\" followed by attackers in armor riding on hoverboards. If true, a remote village was being raided by something no one could explain.","peruvian-government-shifts-to-tacit-endorsement-of-universit-2024":"Peru's government is said to have been firmly against the case of the three-fingered bodies from Peru, small mummified figures that some say are not human, until September 2024. At that point, the story goes, it began quietly backing research at the University of Ica that might show the bodies are real. Michels says this in his own words, in an episode that also draws on a witness named David Ruiz. If true, it would mean an official shift towards taking the bodies seriously.","peruvian-tribe-warns-that-the-underground-ant-people-are-sin":"A tribe in Peru is said to warn that beings living underground, which the story calls the \"ant people\", are sinister and should be left alone. Jay Anderson, a guest on an episode with the comedian Kurt Metzger, passes on what he was told: that the tribe's advice was simply, \"Don't mess with it.\"","peruvian-tridactyl-mummies-scanned-2025":"Michels says that in 2025 he went to Peru and saw mummified three-fingered, three-toed bodies, called tridactyl beings, and that he \"just might have seen a dead alien,\" adding that it \"broke my brain a little bit.\" He tells it first-hand, crediting at different times Brigham Buhler, Michael Mazzola and a witness named Ammar Kandil, and says forensic experts found the skulls show no sign of the traditional head-binding that would explain their shape. If true, it would mean physical remains of a non-human being are sitting in Peru.","pete-worden-connected-tom-delonge-and-general-neil-mccasland-2010":"Pete Worden, a former NASA research center director, is said to have put the musician Tom DeLonge, who later founded a UFO research company, in touch with the retired Air Force general Neil McCasland. Michels says this himself and adds, \"This is public information.\" It would place Worden at a link between a public UFO campaigner and a senior military figure.","pete-worden-knows-more-about-nasa-ufo-knowledge-1990":"Certain people at NASA, among them a man named Pete Worden, are said to know far more than they let on about what the space agency knows of UFOs. Maaneli Derakhshani, a guest on an episode about NASA and the Moon, tells Michels this as something he has heard, not something he saw, saying such people 'know a lot more about NASA's knowledge about the UFO phenomenon'. If true, NASA would hold UFO knowledge that it has not made public.","peter-garyaev-s-retracted-nobel-nomination-and-death-2019":"A Russian scientist named Peter Garyaev, linked to an idea called linguistic wave genetics, was nominated for the Nobel Prize in 2019 or 2020. The nomination was then withdrawn, and he later died, possibly 'under mysterious circumstances'. Dave Rossi, a guest who says aliens taught him advanced physics, tells Michels this without naming a source beyond a website on linguistic wave genetics, and he hedges the last part with 'I believe'.","peter-jennings-ufo-special-cut-by-90-followed-by-his-death-2005":"In 2005 the ABC news anchor Peter Jennings made a UFO special that went on air, but, Jason Jorjani says, \"what they aired excluded 90% of the material\", and Jennings died not long after. Jorjani, a researcher, tells Michels this without naming a source and sets the two events side by side. If true, most of what a major network anchor found about UFOs never reached the public.","peter-thiel-almost-intercepted-over-energy-technology":"The tech investor Peter Thiel was almost intercepted over energy technology, according to Steven Greer, a former doctor who campaigns for UFO disclosure. Greer tells Michels he got a warning from a senior source, whom he calls by an acronym he does not explain, and passed it through a back channel to the mathematician Eric Weinstein. He also names the physicist Hal Puthoff in the story. The claim implies that someone is ready to step in to stop powerful people from pursuing new kinds of energy.","peter-thiel-on-joe-rogan-aliens-must-be-angels-or-demons-2024":"The tech investor Peter Thiel is said to have argued, on Joe Rogan's popular interview podcast in 2024, that if aliens are real they must be something like angels or demons. Michels says Thiel was on the show \"a month ago\", that half the talk was about aliens, and that he cited the work of the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka; in another episode Thiel's reasoning is paraphrased as: \"If you have faster than light travel, there's something really crazy that has to be true on a cultural, political, social level.\"","peterson-and-cia-director-woolsey-invite-steven-greer-to-din-1990":"In the early 1990s, a man named John Peterson and his wife, together with James Woolsey, then director of the CIA, and his wife, are said to have invited the UFO campaigner Steven Greer to dinner. A writer on UFO history named Richard Dolan tells Michels this as something he has learned from others. If so, it would mean the head of the CIA was personally interested enough in UFOs to sit down with one of the subject's best-known campaigners.","peterson-offered-to-show-fitts-a-ufo-if-she-stopped-her-rese-1998":"In 1998, the story goes, John Peterson of the Arlington Institute promised to show Katherine Austin Fitz a UFO if she would stop her research. Michels tells this himself, as something he heard from others, in an episode with the UFO historian Richard Dolan, and he also names Linda Thompson. It suggests that someone with access was offering a look at the secret to make her stop digging.","phantom-radar-contact-near-the-anomaly-2011":"In 2011, over the Baltic Sea anomaly, an odd shape that his team found on the seafloor and that he calls a UFO, the treasure hunter Dennis Asberg says their radar showed something about 150 metres from the boat, but when they looked there was nothing. He tells Michels he was on board and names Peter Lindberg as he tells it: \"We are alone here. But should be something 150 m from us. Nothing there.\" He tells it as one of several strange things around that expedition, which taken together would suggest the site was being watched or was itself doing something unusual.","pharaoh-s-soul-guided-through-seven-palaces-in-the-pyramid-t":"In the Pyramid Texts, the ancient Egyptian religious writings that Michels dates to 2400 BC, the pharaoh's soul is guided by priests through seven 'areet', a word Michels says also means palaces. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. He offers it as the oldest example of a pattern of seven palaces that he follows through later religious texts.","pharaoh-statues-of-past-and-future-outside-saic-headquarters-2017":"Outside the California headquarters of SAIC, a large defense contractor, there are said to be two pharaoh statues, one standing for the past and one for the future. Jay Anderson, a UFO researcher, tells Michels he heard this from the musician Tom DeLonge around 2017, when DeLonge was \"doing the rounds\" and told people about \"this place outside the SAIC headquarters where they've got these two statues.\" To those who repeat it, the statues hint at a hidden, almost occult outlook inside the company.","phenomena-seen-everywhere-near-sensitive-places":"Strange objects are said to show up everywhere near sensitive places, so anyone who gets close to such sites can see them without help from the government. The mathematician Eric Weinstein repeats what someone told him: \"Eric, you don't need to rely on the government. You can just go up and look for yourself,\" because \"you just need to get adjacent to sensitive places and you'll see these things everywhere.\"","phenomena-showing-up-around-nuclear-bases":"Unexplained phenomena are said to keep showing up around nuclear bases. The writer Graham Hancock mentions this to Michels in passing, without naming a source or any particular base, and calls it \"interesting\", since it would suggest whatever is behind them takes an interest in nuclear weapons.","phenomenon-lines-up-in-triangles-or-threes-2012":"When unexplained lights appear in the sky, they will always try to line up in a triangle or in groups of three, according to Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs and has been studied by NASA. He tells Michels he has several videos showing this and that Michels would see it for himself if they went skywatching together.","philadelphia-experiment-actually-happened-in-rhode-island-1942":"The Philadelphia Experiment, a story that the US Navy made a warship vanish or jump from place to place in 1943, really took place in Rhode Island, and Philadelphia was only the cover story. A UFO researcher named Steven Greer tells Michels this, linking it to a man named Bob Beckwith. Other versions of the story tell it differently, as a ship teleportation or as a cover for a nuclear accident.","philadelphia-experiment-and-townsend-brown-1943":"The Philadelphia Experiment, a legendary secret experiment said to have taken place around Philadelphia and Norfolk, Virginia, in 1943, was real, Jesse Michels argues, and it was run by a man named Thomas Townsend Brown, who he says had a nervous breakdown around that time. Michels tells this himself across several episodes, pointing to an FBI file on Brown from 1942 and 1943 and to the researcher Bill Moore, and he says Brown told a friend named Josh Reynolds late in life that the experiment 'did happen, its events were just greatly exaggerated.' Michels tells it differently from one episode to another: sometimes Brown 'led' or 'ran' the project, and once he calls the popular story a 'wacky' cover over 'a real thing going on.'","philadelphia-experiment-as-cover-for-nuclear-accident-1943":"The Philadelphia Experiment, a long-told story that the US Navy made a ship vanish in 1943, was really a cover for an accident, according to a researcher named Jan Lundquist. On the show she says \"a turbine exploded, and the men got showered with hexafluoride,\" a fluid she describes as radioactive. She gives no source, and if she is right, the famous tale was made up to hide sailors' exposure to radiation.","philadelphia-experiment-sailors-stuck-in-walls-1943":"In the Philadelphia Experiment, a rumoured 1943 US Navy test in Philadelphia, some sailors are said to have ended up stuck inside walls or other solid matter. A guest named Dave Rossi, who says aliens taught him advanced physics, repeats these rumours to Michels and calls what happened 'temporal displacement', meaning the men were shifted out of their normal place in space and time.","philadelphia-experiment-ship-teleportation-1940":"The Philadelphia Experiment is a rumor from the 1940s that the Navy teleported a ship, with its crew still aboard, from a Navy port in Philadelphia to a port in Norfolk, Virginia. Michels tells it himself in two episodes: in one he calls it \"a clearly bogus disinfo campaign\" and ties it to the researcher Thomas Townsend Brown, and in the other he simply calls it a rumor, linking it to the men Bill Moore, Carlos Allende (also given as Carl Allen) and Morris Jessup and to a book called The Philadelphia Experiment. Why it matters to him is the question of whether the tale was planted to hide or discredit real research.","philip-corso-sees-a-roswell-body-at-fort-riley-1947":"In 1947 at Fort Riley, Kansas, a man named Philip J. Corso is said to have opened caskets and seen a body from Roswell, the reported crash of an unknown craft in New Mexico that year. James Fox relays this to Michels from what Corso said, and credits Corso's own book The Day After Roswell, published in 1997. If true, it would mean bodies from the crash were moved through an Army base and seen by a soldier there.","philip-k-dick-s-1974-dream-precognizing-close-encounters-of":"In a 1974 dream, the science fiction writer Philip K. Dick was shown what looked like a brownie scored around its edges with a fork, which was 'somehow a relief map.' The writer Eric Wargo, who studies dreams that seem to foresee the future, tells this on the show from Dick's letters. He reads the dream as an early glimpse of Steven Spielberg's 1977 film Close Encounters of the Third Kind. If so, a dream would have caught an image from a film that did not yet exist.","philip-k-dick-s-metz-speech-claiming-the-man-in-the-high-cas-1977":"In a speech in Metz, France, in 1977, the novelist Philip K. Dick is said to have claimed that the world of his novel The Man in the High Castle, a story in which Germany and Japan won World War II, was a real timeline and not just something he made up. Michels brings the speech up himself on two episodes. He recalls its title as something like \"if you think this world sucks, you should see the others\"; the speech is listed under the title \"If You Find This World Bad, You Should See Some of the Others\", and the story dates it to 1977, while Michels says \"1976 or\" 1977. A researcher named Riz Virk tells it on another episode, without naming a source. He quotes the famous line, \"We are living in a computer-programmed reality,\" and says Dick added that the only clue we have is \"when some variable is changed.\" Virk goes further than Michels: in his telling, Dick believed the beings running the simulation reran history down another timeline because they liked this one better. If true, it would mean a famous writer believed history itself had been edited, and that his fiction was a memory of the version that was cut.","philippine-senate-blue-ribbon-commission-and-176-yamashita-t-1945":"At the end of World War II, the story goes, the Japanese general Yamashita buried 176 troves of treasure in the Philippines, and a Philippine Senate body called the Blue Ribbon Commission was later given the job of finding them. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as something people told him, naming a Senator Pimentel and a Mr. Ayala (on the show he calls the general 'Yakushima'). If true, it would mean that a huge hidden fortune from the war is real and has been officially searched for.","phobos-monolith-and-the-proposed-nasa-sample-mission-2007":"NASA has imaged a \"monolith\", a tall block-like feature, on Phobos, one of the moons of Mars, and around 2007 there was talk of a NASA mission to go and sample it. Maaneli Derakhshani, a guest on the show, raises it with Michels without naming a source, bringing in the Apollo astronaut Buzz Aldrin's name alongside it. If the object is what some suggest, it would be something worth sending a spacecraft to touch.","phoenician-masons-build-solomon-s-temple":"King Solomon's temple in Jerusalem was built by Phoenicians: Hiram, king of Tyre, and his chief craftsman, Hiram Abiff. Tim Alberino, a filmmaker, tells Michels this without naming a source, saying \"that's who built the temple\". The figure of Hiram Abiff matters because Freemasons honour him as the temple's master builder.","phoenicians-crossed-the-atlantic-and-mined-copper-in-the-gre":"Tim Alberino, a researcher, believes the ancient Phoenicians sailed across the Atlantic and mined copper around the Great Lakes of North America. He tells Michels \"I think they crossed the Atlantic, I think they were mining copper in the Great Lakes,\" and names no source. If he is right, sailors from the Old World reached the heart of America long before Columbus.","phoenix-lights-boomerang-craft-1997":"In 1997, a huge boomerang-shaped craft was reported over Phoenix, Arizona, in what became known as the Phoenix Lights. A UFO researcher named James Fox mentions it only briefly to Michels, calling the Phoenix case \"pretty phenomenal\" and giving no details or source. He lists it among the sightings he considers most striking.","phoenix-lights-boomerang-over-the-hospice-workers-1997":"On 13 March 1997, a huge boomerang-shaped craft passed over a group of hospice workers in Arizona, on the night of the mass sighting known as the Phoenix Lights. In the 1990s a UFO investigator named James Fox interviewed them, and he tells Michels what they said: all five sat looking up at it, and it took about five minutes to pass overhead, which, he says, shows how big it was.","phoenix-lights-footage-taken-by-fake-barwood-staffers-1997":"After the Phoenix Lights, a wave of strange lights seen over Phoenix, Arizona, in 1997, two men in suits arrived in an unmarked government vehicle and took a witness's footage, claiming to work for Frances Emma Barwood, a Phoenix city councilwoman, when they did not. The filmmaker James Fox tells Michels the story, passing on what he was told. If true, it would mean someone in government quietly collected evidence of the sighting.","phone-call-to-bobby-ray-inman-about-puharich-and-bob-beck-1970":"A researcher named Greg Malatzi says he phoned a man named Bobby Ray Inman and asked him straight out whether he knew Andrija Puharich or Bob Beck, the two men he says were working in that era with the \"space kids,\" children said to have psychic gifts. Malatzi tells Michels that Inman was pleasant but answered, \"No, I've never heard the name.\"","phone-call-with-rep-mike-gallagher-and-interview-with-house-2024":"In 2024, Eric W. Davis says, one phone call with Mike Gallagher, a Republican member of Congress, was enough to get him an interview with the staff of a House subcommittee. Davis tells Michels this about himself, in an episode where he says a US president was briefed on UFOs. It would mean Congress was willing to hear quickly from someone with his story.","phone-tapping-audits-and-unmarked-cars-after-the-leak-1996":"After word leaked out in 1996 that his hospital in São Paulo, Brazil, had operated on an alien, a surgeon named Carlos de Souza says he found himself watched. He tells Michels that every time he picked up the phone, someone was listening on the line, and he also speaks of audits and unmarked cars, which he takes as signs that someone wanted to keep him quiet.","phone-won-t-power-off-during-interview-2026":"In 2026, while interviewing a British hacker named Gary McKinnon in the UK, Michels says his phone would not switch off. He tells it himself and filmed the moment as it happened: 'Let's document all of this. You can see my finger is on the power button.' He sets it beside McKinnon's story of breaking into NASA computers and finding signs of a secret space programme.","photo-from-scotland-shown-in-fox-s-film":"A film by James Fox, a documentary maker and UFO investigator, is said to show a striking photograph of a UFO taken in Scotland. Michels mentions it himself while talking with Fox, calling it \"an incredible photo out of out of Scotland\", and says nothing more about what it shows.","photo-of-jolly-west-on-the-set-of-2001-1960":"There is a photograph of a psychiatrist named Jolly West on the set of the film 2001: A Space Odyssey, Michels says on an episode about a CIA scientist. He mentions it in passing, without saying where the picture comes from. He raises it as one more link between West and the people around the film, though he does not spell out what that link means.","photographs-of-alien-objects-on-mars-including-a-square":"Mars is covered in objects that are plainly not natural, among them a shape like a square, according to a guest named Joe McMoneagle. He tells Michels he has \"somewhere in the neighborhood of 60 something photographs of things on Mars that are clearly alien,\" which, if true, would mean Mars once had intelligent life.","photographs-of-ed-lansdale-in-dealey-plaza-identified-by-his-1963":"There are said to be photographs of a man named Ed Lansdale in Dealey Plaza in Dallas on the day President Kennedy was shot in 1963, and his wife is said to have identified him in them. A researcher named Dan Sheehan says this on a show about Kennedy and UFOs, without naming his source: \"Photographs of Lansdale sitting right there and his wife identifying it.\" If true, it would put Lansdale at the scene of the killing.","photographs-of-hal-puthoff-s-layered-magnesium-bismuth-metal":"A man named Hal Puthoff is said to have held a sample of metal made of thin layers of magnesium and bismuth, of the kind said in UFO stories to come from unknown craft. A man named Dan Sheehan tells Michels he has not handled it but has \"seen photographs of this thing that Hal had.\" If the sample is what it is claimed to be, it would be physical material from an unexplained craft.","photographs-of-rip-robertson-in-dealey-plaza-1963":"Photographs are said to show a man named Rip Robertson, whom the teller calls \"the field operations commander for the S force\", standing in Dealey Plaza in Dallas when President Kennedy was shot in 1963. The lawyer and researcher Daniel Sheehan says this on the show without naming where the pictures come from, and in one telling he also places a man named Ed Lansdale there beside Robertson. If true, it would put a covert operations commander at the scene of the killing.","photographs-of-underground-treasure-cavern-1945":"In the Philippines around 1945, there was an underground cavern full of treasure, so large it looked \"like a football stadium\" beneath the ground. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels he has seen photographs of it himself, and if they are what he says, a huge hidden hoard was stored under the islands at the end of the war.","photos-of-kirkpatrick-meeting-with-brandon-fugal-2020":"Photos exist of an official named Sean Kirkpatrick sitting in a meeting with Brandon Fugal, the owner of a ranch called Skinwalker, where Fugal says a UFO lies underground. Michels says so himself, without naming a source. Fugal later tells Michels he was there: Kirkpatrick sat right across from him \"for 2 and 1/2 hours in a confidential briefing,\" which Fugal dates to April 2018, earlier than the story's other dates, and he tells it to show that Kirkpatrick was personally briefed on what goes on at the ranch.","photos-of-tim-taylor-at-bledsoe-children-s-football-games":"There are supposed to be photos of a man named Tim Taylor at football games played by the children of a man named Chris Bledsoe, which would put the two men closer together than one might expect. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb raises this with Michels as a half-remembered question (\"Isn't there photos of Tim Taylor at like football games of Chris Bledsoe's children or something like that?\"), without naming where the photos come from.","phyllis-schlemmer-channels-the-nine-earth-as-a-bottleneck-of-1970":"A medium named Phyllis Schlemmer is said to have channelled, meaning received spoken messages through herself, from a group of beings called 'the Nine', and she wrote them up in a book called The Only Planet of Choice. Michels tells this himself and recalls that the messages describe Earth as 'a bottleneck of consciousness'. In another episode he places Schlemmer in the circle of the researcher Andrija Puharich and leaves out the bottleneck message.","phyllis-schlemmer-says-puharich-used-the-td-100-machine-on-h-1970":"A woman named Phyllis Schlemmer is said to have recalled that the scientist Andrija Puharich used a device called the TD 100 machine on her 'all the time', and that she 'never knew what it did' and never asked. An unnamed researcher, speaking on an episode about Puharich's claimed contact with an alien council, quotes her words to Michels. It suggests Puharich was running experiments on the people around him with equipment whose purpose they did not know.","physicist-friend-sees-boulder-pushed-into-a-tree-at-the-rosw-1947":"At the site near Roswell, New Mexico, where a flying saucer is said to have crashed in 1947, a boulder looks as if it was pushed into what was probably a young tree at the time. Although the story's title gives the sighting to a physicist friend, in his own words Michels tells it as something he saw when he went there himself: \"I saw that a boulder had clearly been pushed into a what was probably a sapling at the time.\" He brings it up while debating a skeptic, as physical evidence that something heavy struck the ground there.","pictures-mailed-to-sagan-never-arrived-1980":"In the 1980s, John Brandenburg, a scientist who argues that life on Mars was destroyed by a nuclear war, says he sent pictures to the astronomer Carl Sagan. When he later asked Sagan what he thought of them, Sagan answered, \"I never got them.\" Brandenburg tells this to Michels as something that happened to him, a hint that the material may have been stopped along the way.","pills-handed-out-during-intuitive-communication-training-1990":"Between 1990 and 1993, the men in a secret programme training them to talk with aliens by thought were given pills, at an undisclosed facility near the NSA complex in Maryland. Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him for this work, tells Michels he went through the training. He says an officer he calls Captain White 'told me also there would be some pills that I would take', which suggests the training used drugs as well as practice.","pilot-compatibility-with-different-classes-of-uap-2008":"Between 2008 and 2010, at a place in New York called Baker Victory Services, children were tested to see which kinds of UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, they could work with as pilots. A man named Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into such a program as a child, tells Michels that a person \"might work very well with object A but I can't work with object B or C,\" which would mean the craft are matched to particular people.","pilot-photo-of-arv-over-provo-1960":"In the 1960s a pilot named Harvey is said to have photographed an ARV, short for 'alien reproduction vehicle', a craft said to be a human-built copy of alien technology. He took the picture over Provo, Utah, near the Dugway Proving Ground, a military test range. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels that a man named Mark McCandlish has the image. He cannot remember the pilot's surname.","pilot-vouches-for-marzulli-s-source-using-details-he-held-ba-2005":"A pilot who once told his own story deliberately left some details out, so that he could test anyone who later came forward claiming to know more, and around 2005 he used those hidden details to vouch for a source of L.A. Marzulli, an author on ancient giants and the supernatural. The filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels this, naming Marzulli and the radio host Steve Quayle. In his words, the pilot \"said that guy is telling the truth,\" which, if so, would back up the source's account.","pilot-who-flew-the-gold-out-is-still-alive-1945":"The commander who flew a load of gold out, in a story set in 1945, is still alive, according to a researcher named Dan Sheehan. Sheehan tells Michels, \"He's still alive, the commander, the pilot that flew the gold out.\" He names no source, and he does not say here whose gold it was or where it went. If true, someone who took part in the episode could still speak about it.","piloting-uap-orbs-by-moving-consciousness-into-them-in-the-l-2008":"Between 2008 and 2010, at Baker Victory Services in New York, Jordan Jose says he was asked as a child to steer UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, by moving his consciousness into them. Jose, who says a UFO program took him as a kid, tells Michels it from his own memory: \"let's say there was an orb or something and I was asked to fly it.\" If true, it would mean someone was testing whether a human mind can pilot these objects.","pilots-mistaking-u-2-and-sr-71-for-ufos-1950":"Pilots in the 1950s and 60s who reported UFOs streaking above them, at three or four times their speed and two or three times their height of 35,000 feet, were really seeing America's secret spy planes, the U-2 and the SR-71 Blackbird. That is the explanation the skeptic Michael Shermer gives Michels, without naming a source. The pilots couldn't have known what they saw, he says, because the planes were secret, and if he is right, many of that era's sightings were secret aircraft and not anything unexplained.","pilots-report-uaps-emerging-from-the-dipper-2010":"Pilots are said to have reported UAPs, the official term for unexplained objects in the sky, coming out of \"the Dipper\" and even dogfighting near it in orbit. Michels says this on an episode about NASA, attributing it to a Navy pilot named Ryan Graves, whom he calls \"very humble in his claims\".","pilots-seeing-uap-at-lemoore-fallon-and-pax-river":"Navy pilots are said to be meeting UAP, the official term for unidentified aerial objects, not only off Norfolk and Virginia Beach and along the California coast but also further inland, at the big Navy training ranges at Lemoore and Fallon and at Pax River, where the Navy trains its test pilots. A former Navy fighter pilot named Ryan Graves tells Michels this from what other pilots have told him, saying 'they're having incidents up there'. If true, the objects would be showing up across many of the Navy's main flying areas, not just one.","pines-judges-barber-credible-as-a-delivery-man-in-a-crash-re-2025":"A man named Jake Barber says he worked as a helicopter pilot ferrying objects for a secret crash retrieval program, an effort to recover crashed craft that are not human-made. In 2025 the analyst Matthew Pines told Michels that he finds Barber 'extremely credible'. Pines says Barber has the professional record and 'is who he says he is'. In his view Barber was 'essentially a delivery man': he knows what he saw, but not the bigger picture.","pines-speculates-the-psionic-programs-use-genetic-lines-drug":"A researcher named Matthew Pines guesses at how secret government programmes in psionics, meaning the use of the mind for things like telepathy, might find and train their people. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that such programmes may have learned that some people are born with a knack for it, may have traced \"genetic lines\" that carry it, and may give young recruits \"cocktails of drugs\" as part of a training regimen before bringing them in. He offers it as speculation about how a hidden psychic programme could be built.","pioneer-mission-tenth-planet-covered-up-matt-lacroix-claim-1990":"NASA's Pioneer space probes found a tenth planet far out in the solar system, beyond the Kuiper Belt (the ring of icy bodies past Neptune), and the find was covered up. Michels tells this twice. Once he passes on what Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient civilizations, told him: that the planet was \"four to five times larger than the Earth\" and was hidden for that reason. Another time he raises it himself, more tentatively, as a \"possible scrub of the Pioneer mission from NASA,\" and names the astronomers Thomas Van Flandern and Robert Harrington, with no size given. If true, NASA would have hidden a major planet in our own solar system.","piri-reis-map-showing-antarctica":"Michels says the Piri Reis map, an old map, shows parts of Antarctica as tropical, as if someone had mapped the continent before it was covered in ice. He brings this up himself, drawing on the writer Graham Hancock and pointing to a second old map, the Oronteus Finaeus map. If true, it would suggest a lost knowledge of the world far older than recorded history.","pla-rocket-force-purges-and-a-possible-hidden-chinese-progra-2020":"In the last few years China has purged officers of its Rocket Force, the branch of its armed forces that runs its missiles, and that shake-up might hint at a hidden Chinese programme. The researcher Matthew Pines brings this up with Michels in passing, without naming a source, and ties it to Xi Jinping's leadership. If he is right, China could be running a secret effort of its own of the kind the show discusses.","pla-s-ufo-report-tracking-system":"China's armed forces, the People's Liberation Army, are \"very sophisticated\" at tracking UFO reports, according to the researcher Richard Dolan. Dolan tells Michels this in passing. He names no source and does not describe how the tracking works. If true, China's military treats UFO sightings as a serious matter to monitor.","plains-of-san-agustin-saucer-crash-with-dead-aliens-and-one-1947":"A flying saucer is said to have crashed in the summer of 1947 on the Plains of San Agustin, a high desert flat in western New Mexico. The story is usually told with dead crew aboard and one alien still alive, though the two guests who mention it here speak only of the craft. The scientist John Brandenburg tells Michels, as something passed on to him, that \"roughly the morning of 4th of July an object crashed on the plains of San Augustine about 100 miles away.\" He sets it in the same days as the better-known Roswell crash. Charles Hall, who says he spent three years with tall white aliens at Area 51, brings it up as a point of comparison. He describes a craft that is \"in deep trouble,\" \"as in the craft over the sand plains of San Augustine in 1947.\" If true, it would mean that more than one craft came down in New Mexico that summer.","plan-for-oswald-to-fire-a-deliberate-miss-at-the-motorcade-a-1963":"According to the researcher Dan Sheehan, the plan in Dallas in 1963 was to coax Lee Harvey Oswald, the man later accused of killing President Kennedy, into firing at the motorcade on purpose without hitting anyone. As the story's title has it, the shot would then be blamed on Cuba. Sheehan tells Michels this as his own account and names no source. If true, it would mean Oswald was set up to play a staged part in a plot run by others.","plan-to-move-the-program-to-kwajalein-south-pacific-1989":"The secret program that studied alien craft at S4, a hidden site in the Nevada desert, would ideally have been moved to the South Pacific, perhaps to Kwajalein Island, according to Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked there in 1989. He tells Michels the people running it said the move would have cost so much that it was impossible.","planck-joked-planck-length-would-be-best-way-to-communicate":"The German physicist Max Planck is said to have thought, after finding the Planck length, the smallest meaningful length in physics, that it would be the best way to talk to aliens, since it is \"literally the shortest length\" and so a measure any intelligence would share. David Grusch, a former intelligence official who went public with claims about UFOs, mentions this to Michels in passing.","plane-carrying-maury-island-slag-explodes-killing-two-office-1947":"In 1947, two military officers are said to have flown back to their base from Washington State carrying slag, a rocky waste material, tied to a strange incident at Maury Island, and their plane exploded in midair, killing them both. A researcher named Peter Levenda tells the story, without naming a source, in an account that involves a man named Fred Crisman: \"they get back on their plane... Their plane explodes in midair.\" Told this way, the deaths hint that the material was more dangerous, or more sensitive, than anyone admitted.","planned-double-blind-dna-test-of-the-peruvian-bodies-2025":"Small bodies from Peru that some people present as non-human were to get a DNA test in 2025 by independent aerospace contractors, set up 'double blind,' meaning those doing the test would not know which sample was which, 'so no cheating,' according to Jesse Michels. He says this himself on the show, naming Michael Mazzola and Jaime Maussan, two men connected with the bodies, and the documentary This Is Not a Hoax; if the test went as described, it would put the bodies' origin to a controlled check.","planned-film-tests-of-telepathy-and-of-communication-over-th-2025":"In 2025 a filmmaker named Ky Dickens, who made a documentary series about children said to read minds, was planning filmed tests of telepathy. She tells Michels the idea was to put two or three non-speakers, people who cannot talk, in different places and have them communicate through what they call 'the Hill', a place they describe meeting in their minds. If it worked on camera, it would be evidence that minds can share thoughts directly.","planned-live-demo-at-electrostatic-society-of-america-confer":"A scientist named Charles Buhler, presented on the show as a NASA figure who says his team has built a propulsion system that works against gravity, plans to show it working in public. He tells Michels that at a conference of the Electrostatic Society of America he will \"do a live demo of this,\" at his lab in Cocoa Beach, Florida.","planned-move-to-a-boarding-facility-in-eastern-new-york-2010":"In 2010, Jordan Jose says, he was supposed to move to a facility in eastern New York and live there full-time. Jose, who says a UFO program took him as a child, tells Michels this about his own life: he \"was going to go to a facility in Eastern New York.\" If true, it would mean the program planned to take over his childhood entirely.","planned-public-uap-summoning-demonstration-in-the-first-half-2025":"In early 2025, Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he once carried an egg-shaped craft, told Michels that his group hoped to stage a public demonstration in the first half of that year: calling in UAP, the official term for unidentified objects in the sky, so people could see them. If it worked, the phenomenon would show up on demand. Michels later said, in May 2026, that Barber apparently \"didn't land another egg\" as he had predicted.","planned-wrightsville-beach-skywatch-broadcast-through-the-wo-2025":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he draws UFOs to him and whom NASA has studied, tells Michels about a skywatch he was planning: 'about 40 of us' on the beach at Wrightsville Beach, North Carolina. He says it would be broadcast through a group he calls the World Forum, so others could watch the sky with them in hope of seeing something.","plaque-at-hospital-regional-marking-the-et-s-path-1996":"The documentary filmmaker James Fox says a big plaque stands outside Hospital Regional in Varginha, Brazil, the town where in 1996 people reported a strange creature captured and taken to hospital, and that the creature was brought in around the back rather than through reception. Fox tells Michels this from his own visit to the place. If the account is right, the town marks the being's route into the hospital in public.","plasma-balls-turning-into-solid-objects-in-ufo-legacy-progra":"Some people said to have been part of the \"UFO legacy program\", meaning a supposed secret government effort to recover and study unknown craft, describe balls of plasma that pull together, go inert and turn into solid objects, then come down out of the sky once they are summoned. Michels sums up these testimonies himself, without naming who gave them, while talking with Jeffrey Mishlove, a PhD who studies psychic phenomena and the afterlife.","plasma-compression-fusion-device-paper-cited-in-china-and-ie":"Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist who holds patents on unusual craft designs, says his paper on a plasma compression fusion device is being cited \"by some very important\" research teams in China, and that the latest to cite it, this year, is an IEEE journal on cybernetics (IEEE is the professional body of electrical engineers). Pais tells Michels this about his own work, as a sign that his ideas are being taken seriously abroad.","plasma-orbs-seen-around-the-nasa-space-shuttle-1981":"People in and around NASA's space shuttle are said to have described plasma orbs, glowing balls of light, near the spacecraft in orbit. Michels raises this himself as a question to an MIT scientist, without naming who saw them or when. If true, it would mean such lights appear even in space.","plato-s-atlantis-date-matches-the-end-of-the-younger-dryas-a":"The claim is that Plato's date for the sinking of Atlantis, 9,000 years before the Athenian lawmaker Solon heard the tale in Egypt, lands around 9600 BC, at the end of the last ice age, and so may remember a real flood. The researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels the date is \"very interesting\": 9,000 years before Solon's trip to Egypt, which he puts in 600 BC. Michels repeats it several times, crediting the writer Graham Hancock and Carlson; in one telling he calls it a myth of a great flood, in another he says the flood that ended the ice age was Meltwater Pulse 1B, a sudden surge of sea-level rise as the ice sheets melted, and that it ended Atlantis. He points to Plato's dialogues the Timaeus and the Critias, in Benjamin Jowett's translation. There an Egyptian priest tells Solon of \"your citizens of 9000 years ago\" who fought Atlantis, and says that \"afterwards there occurred violent earthquakes and floods\" and the island \"disappeared in the depths of the sea\". In the Critias, though, the nine thousand years are counted from Critias's own day back to the war, and \"many great deluges\" took place in that time, not one. The 600 BC date is the tellers', and the festival in the text, \"the Apaturia\", is where the tale was retold generations after Solon, not where Solon heard it. Matching the count to the end of the ice age and to Meltwater Pulse 1B is the tellers' step, which the text neither states nor excludes.","plato-s-atlantis-located-in-antarctica":"Plato's lost Atlantis lay off the coast of Antarctica, says the researcher Jason Jorjani. He tells Michels that Plato's \"world ocean\" is where the Pacific, Atlantic and Indian Oceans meet, \"in the coastline of Antarctica\". If he is right, the legend points to a civilisation at the bottom of the world. In Benjamin Jowett's translation of the Timaeus, the power of Atlantis \"came forth out of the Atlantic Ocean\", from \"an island situated in front of the straits which are by you called the Pillars of Heracles\", from which \"you might pass to the whole of the opposite continent which surrounded the true ocean.\" The Critias adds that the island was \"greater in extent than Libya and Asia\" and was later \"sunk by an earthquake.\" The text places the island itself in the Atlantic, in front of the straits, with a great continent beyond it. Reading that far continent and \"the true ocean\" as Antarctica and the ocean around it, and putting Atlantis there, is Jorjani's step, which the text neither states nor rules out.","plato-s-cave-someone-outside-the-cave-pulling-us-through":"Plato's famous image in The Republic of people chained in a cave, who must be led out to see the truth, implies according to a guest named James Madden that 'there has to be somebody outside of the cave' doing the leading. Madden, speaking with Michels about consciousness and UFOs, reads the story himself and says that from the point of view of enlightenment someone 'has to be pulling us through'. Read his way, the old allegory would point to an outside intelligence guiding human beings toward understanding.","plato-s-myth-of-er":"Plato's Republic ends with the story of Er, a soldier who dies in battle and comes back to tell what he saw after death. Two guests bring it up, both summarising Plato without other sources. The researcher Diana Pasulka calls it a recorded near-death experience, \"about the soldier who dies in battle and has a near-death experience.\" The researcher James Madden tells it on another episode as Plato's story of a virtuous man: \"Er was a virtuous person. He dies in battle for the city.\" Madden does not call it a near-death experience. Plato's text is the document both name. Whether it is an old memory of something like modern near-death accounts is the guests' reading, since the shape of the story is what they compare. If it is, people have been reporting the same journey past death for thousands of years.","plato-took-ergot-at-eleusis":"Plato went to Eleusis, the ancient Greek site of secret initiation rites, and was tripping on ergot, a fungus that grows on grain and can cause hallucinations. Michels says this himself in an episode with the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, linking it to Plato's Republic without naming any other source. If true, one of the founders of Western philosophy would have drawn on a psychedelic experience.","plutarch-names-sonchis-priest-at-sais":"The claim is that the Greek writer Plutarch named the Egyptian priest who knew the Atlantis story: an elder priest called Sonchis at the temple of Sais in Egypt. Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient history, tells it, pointing to Plutarch's Lives. In Plutarch, he says, \"there was a very specific elder priest at the temple\" in Egypt with that name. Plato's account says that the Athenian lawmaker Solon heard of Atlantis from priests in Egypt. LaCroix's point is that Plutarch goes further and names \"a specific individual in history,\" the elder priest \"who had all the knowledge,\" with \"all the other ones\" supporting him. LaCroix is the only teller. He makes the point twice in the same conversation, and the second time says outright that Sonchis was Solon's source. It matters to him because a named priest would make the Atlantis account look less like Plato's invention and more like a chain of real people passing on old knowledge.","podcast-with-doty-where-mario-confronts-him-2010":"An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods, who says he was taken by aliens, went on another podcast with Richard Doty, one of the men he says questioned him afterward alongside a captain of the Air Force's investigations office named Jack Reed, and tried to get answers. Woods tells Michels that Doty \"was so covered by the guys that did the podcast\" that he could not get a straight answer, while Michels, on a later show, says Doty \"kind of doubled down\" there on the story of the L9 incident, an event at Ellsworth Air Force Base described in a document dated November 1977.","podesta-emails-and-the-delongemccaslandweiss-meeting-2015":"The story is that when the emails of John Podesta, a senior Washington political adviser, were hacked and published by WikiLeaks in 2016, they showed Tom DeLonge, author of the Sekret Machines UFO book series, working to set up talks on UFOs at the top. A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels there were about 35 emails between DeLonge and Podesta's team, and a meeting that brought together Podesta, DeLonge, General Neil McCasland, Michael Carey, and Robert Weiss, the president of Skunk Works, a secret aircraft design shop. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron calls Podesta, McCasland and Weiss \"the three kings\" and says the leak exposed them. Other guests focus on DeLonge's motives in the emails: a guest named Jay Anderson says DeLonge wrote that he wanted to make young people \"more promilitary, more pro government,\" and a YouTube researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala says DeLonge wrote that he wanted the government to \"come off as cool.\" If true, it would mean UFO disclosure efforts reached powerful political and defence figures, and were partly about shaping public feeling toward the government.","podesta-emails-hillary-clinton-s-seven-year-plan-and-the-kim-2015":"According to the emails of John Podesta, Hillary Clinton's campaign chairman, there was a 'seven-year plan' to bring the UFO subject into the open. Podesta wanted Clinton asked about aliens when she went on Jimmy Kimmel's show in November 2015, but Kimmel did not ask her the UFO question, and she was furious. Grant Cameron, a UFO researcher, tells Michels this as his own reading of the Podesta emails, which WikiLeaks published in 2016.","podesta-staffer-mentions-the-ufo-project-2015":"In emails to John Podesta, a former White House chief of staff, a staffer around 2015 suggested a person for what he called \"the UFO project.\" Jason Simosa, a researcher, tells Michels this from the Podesta emails that the WikiLeaks site published in 2016, quoting the staffer as writing, \"John, maybe we should consider this individual for the UFO project.\" The line shows Podesta's circle used the phrase. What that project was, and how far it went, the line itself does not say.","podkletnov-rumored-to-work-for-an-american-contractor":"A scientist named Eugene Podkletnov is said to have gone to work for an American defence contractor. Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist who holds patents on unusual craft designs, tells Michels only that \"once upon a time I was told he was working for an American contractor\", without saying who told him.","podkletnov-s-father-a-soviet-agent-retrieved-schauberger-s-f-1940":"A scientist named Eugene Podkletnov is said to have explained that his father was a Soviet counterintelligence agent whose job in the 1940s was retrieving many of the files of the inventor Viktor Schauberger from Germany. Michels relays this from the writer Nick Cook's book The Hunt for Zero Point. If true, it would mean the Soviets gathered up wartime German research on unusual propulsion.","podkletnov-s-father-retrieved-schauberger-s-files-1940":"The father of a scientist named Eugene Podkletnov is said to have been a Soviet agent who, in the 1940s, collected the files of a man named Viktor Schauberger. Michels says this himself, without naming a source: \"his dad was actually a Soviet agent that retrieved Victor Schauberger's files.\" If true, it would link Podkletnov's own work to Schauberger's papers through his family.","podkletnov-s-gravity-shielding-and-a-schauberger-soviet-line-1990":"A researcher named Eugene Podkletnov, working at the University of Tampere in Finland in the 1990s, claimed that objects lost weight above spinning superconductors, materials that carry electricity with no resistance when very cold. Michels tells this himself, drawing on The Hunt for Zero Point, a book by the journalist Nick Cook, and he sets Podkletnov in a line of work going back through Soviet research to the Austrian inventor Viktor Schauberger. If the claim held, it would mean a way to cancel part of gravity, which is why it comes up in talk of antigravity propulsion.","podkletnov-s-impulse-gravitational-generator":"A researcher named Eugene Podkletnov is said to have built an \"impulse gravitational generator\", a device meant to fire off a pulse of gravity-like force. A Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, who holds patents on unusual propulsion ideas, tells Michels that if this is true, Podkletnov \"has hit on something already\". That would mean someone has already managed to control gravity. Pais names no source and does not say he has seen the device.","point-dume-alien-base-erased-from-maps":"An alien base said to sit off Point Dume in Malibu, California, is said to have been erased from maps. Michels mentions it only in passing, in an episode about Joe Rogan's views on aliens, as \"that Point Dume alien base that they erased,\" without saying who erased it or how. If true, it would mean someone was hiding a site on the California coast.","point-mugu-missile-base-near-oxnard":"Steve Colbern is a scientist who studies objects that people say aliens implanted in their bodies. He mentions Point Mugu, a military missile base near Oxnard, California, as a base that was \"very important at one time.\" He tells Michels this in passing, without saying what happened there or where he learned it, and he does not say here why it matters to his story.","police-explain-the-attacks-as-river-miners-with-jetpacks-2023":"In 2023, police in the Peruvian village of San Antonio de Pintoyaku are said to have explained a string of attacks on villagers by blaming 'river miners with jetpack technology'. A guest named Tim Alberino, who went to look into the attacks, tells Michels this as what he heard from a woman named Talia. It is told to show how strange the official explanation for the attacks was.","polish-magical-working-animating-a-golem-then-nazi-tanks-1930":"A woman at a party in New York said that in the 1930s, in Poland, she had taken part in a magic ritual to bring a golem to life, a clay figure from Jewish legend. The Nazi tanks came after that. The novelist Whitley Strieber, who has written about his own encounters with beings, tells Michels he heard this from her, and her story also brings in General Joseph Stilwell and China.","polished-marble-found-at-the-crash-site-1999":"In 1999, two searchers named Scott Ramsey and Suzanne Ramsey are said to have found what looked like a small polished marble, completely out of place, in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico. According to UFO lore, a craft carrying alien bodies crashed there. Michels tells this himself in an episode about the site, presenting the marble as possible physical evidence that something came down there.","polygraph-question-about-loyalty-to-a-non-human-faction-2020":"Between about 2020 and 2022, people screened for the UAP Task Force, a Pentagon group set up to study unidentified flying objects, were asked an extra lie-detector question or two: had they had any relationships with, or loyalty to, \"any non-human faction.\" Matthew Pines, a national security analyst, tells Michels this as something he was told. If true, it would mean officials took seriously the idea that people might be loyal to non-human beings.","polygraph-with-flashing-images-and-air-horns-2010":"Dylan Borland, who says a top government official told him aliens run everything, describes being strapped to a lie detector while a computer flashed images at him and air horns went off. He tells Michels it happened to him: \"I get hooked onto this polygraph machine and there's a computer where they're flashing images and air horns.\" He tells it alongside other accounts of how UFO whistleblowers say they are pressured, and if true, it would mean his testing was built to rattle him as well as to check his story.","polymeropoulos-feels-betrayed-by-the-cia":"A CIA officer named Marc Polymeropoulos is said to love the CIA but to feel it betrayed him. He appeared in a 60 Minutes report on Havana syndrome, the unexplained illnesses reported by US officials. Eric Haseltine, a former intelligence official, repeats Polymeropoulos's words to Michels, as an example of how the agency treats its own people when they raise awkward cases.","pompeo-i-saw-the-ufo-files-too":"The story is that the former CIA Director Mike Pompeo let slip in an interview that he had seen secret UFO files. The interviewer was Judge Napolitano. He asked Pompeo why the nearly 5,000 JFK assassination files were still being held back. According to the journalist Michael Shellenberger, Pompeo said they could not be released because \"some of the people are still alive.\" Then, in an awkward change of subject, he added, \"I've seen the UAP files as well.\" UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. Michels raises the same moment himself and calls it \"a very weird non-sequitur.\" Both tellers take it as a hint from someone who would know that such files exist and are kept secret like the JFK papers.","pons-and-fleischmann-cold-fusion-rumor-1990":"A rumor holds that a real breakthrough in cold fusion, nuclear power made at room temperature, of the kind the chemists Stanley Pons and Martin Fleischmann once claimed, may have happened in the 1990s or 2000s. Michels raises it himself as a question in an episode about a Secretary of Energy, calling it only \"this rumor.\" If true, it would mean a world-changing energy source was found and not made public.","pope-francis-message-and-vatican-paintings-of-orbs-with-ange":"A message tied to Pope Francis is said to have come to a man named Chris Bledsoe, who says glowing orbs and craft appear around him: that \"they know that they're angels\", with the Vatican said to hold paintings of orbs alongside angels. Bledsoe tells Michels the message reached him through the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka, saying \"she said to tell me\". If true, it would mean the Church sees the orbs as angels.","pope-john-paul-ii-we-are-not-alone-1994":"Pope John Paul II told him \"we are not alone\" when they met in 1994, according to Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of a Russian republic who says he was taken by aliens in 1997. He tells Michels he did not quite understand the Pope's meaning at the time, implying that it only made sense to him after his own experience.","pope-john-xxiii-to-broker-the-warhead-destruction-deal-1963":"In 1963, the story goes, the Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev proposed that Pope John XXIII act as broker for a deal with the United States, so each side could check that the other was not only taking apart its nuclear warheads but actually destroying them. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as something he was told, and he points to the Jesuit headquarters as part of where it comes from. If true, it would mean the two superpowers came close to a church-verified agreement to get rid of their warheads that year.","pope-pius-xii-informs-allies-of-the-craft-1937":"An unknown craft said to have crashed at Magenta in Italy before the Second World War was, the story goes, kept under the Vatican's knowledge, and Pope Pius XII is said to have quietly told the Allies it existed and where it was stored. Michels tells this himself in two episodes, without naming a source; the first time he says the Pope used \"discrete channels\", and the second time he names the hiding place as the Soratte bunker outside Rome and says the news was \"back channeled through Pope Pius the 12th to FDR.\" If true, it would put a crashed craft in Allied hands' knowledge years before the US had its own.","porch-insight-the-big-bang-as-a-ripple-inside-a-universe-at":"A former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says he worked out, on his own screened porch, that the Big Bang might be a ripple inside a universe that is otherwise at rest, and that this could explain where the Virgin Mary came from. He tells Michels he was 'picturing the Big Bang for the first time in my life', and he draws on Thomas Aquinas, the philosopher Gottfried Leibniz and Einstein to support the idea, which would tie physics and faith together.","portage-county-police-chase":"Police officers in Portage County, Ohio, chased a large, metallic, disc-shaped object for 90 minutes and 86 miles, by this account. Marik von Rennenkampff, a commentator on the subject, mentions it in passing on the show and names no source. He offers it as a case of trained officers following a solid-looking craft for a long time.","portal-or-wormhole-theory-for-the-ranch-2016":"Some believe that Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange happenings, has a portal or wormhole on it, a passage through space and time. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, brings this up with Michels as a guess made by a scientist he calls Dr. Taylor and by others. If true, it would explain why so many strange things are reported there.","portland-police-see-nine-discs-1947":"In July 1947, five police officers in Portland, Oregon, saw a total of nine discs in the sky, all moving in different directions. A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this in a quick list of early cases, and he credits the raw reports of Project Sign, the US Air Force's first UFO study, which began in the late 1940s. If true, it would mean several trained officers saw the same objects at once, not just one lone witness.","poseidon-temple-on-little-st-james-and-atlantis-labyrinth-at-2000":"The claim is that Jeffrey Epstein built a temple to Poseidon on Little St. James, his private island, and a labyrinth laid out like the ground plan of Atlantis at Zorro Ranch, his New Mexico property. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells it, relaying a YouTube host who goes by Dark Journalist. Jorjani says Poseidon was the \"patron deity,\" and that Zorro Ranch is \"where the breeding program was.\" He points to Plato's dialogues Timaeus and Critias, written in the fourth century BC, in Benjamin Jowett's translation. In those dialogues Poseidon receives \"for his lot the island of Atlantis\" and surrounds its center with \"alternate zones of sea and land.\" That gives the ring-shaped layout a labyrinth could copy, though the dialogues of course say nothing about Epstein. Michels tells a thinner version on another episode: Epstein \"built a temple with a cult symbology\" on the island, with no mention of Poseidon or the ranch labyrinth. To the tellers, the buildings would show Epstein was acting out an ancient, secret worship.","possible-mixing-of-us-secret-technology-into-the-2017-nyt-di":"Michels wonders whether secret US technology was mixed into the New York Times article of 16 December 2017, 'Glowing Auras and Black Money: The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program', the story that revealed a hidden Pentagon UFO program. He raises this himself as an open question, without naming a source. If he were right, some of what the public took for unexplained objects would have been America's own hidden craft.","post-assassination-letters-telling-ruth-paine-to-keep-quiet-1963":"After the Kennedy assassination, the story goes, Ruth Paine, a Texas woman linked to Lee Harvey Oswald's household, got letters from her mother-in-law, Ruth Forbes Paine Young, telling her to keep quiet about Oswald. The researcher Peter Levenda describes the letters on the show without quoting them, putting it as the wife of a man named Arthur Young telling her daughter-in-law \"ix nay on the Oswald gay,\" pig Latin for \"say nothing about the Oswald guy.\" If true, it would suggest someone close to a key witness wanted her silent from the start.","post-malone-s-security-detail-sees-a-spectral-image-in-homes":"When the singer Post Malone visited Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, his security team, whom the teller calls \"armed to the teeth\", is said to have seen a ghostly figure dart through a building called Homestead 2 and up through its roof. Brandon Fugal tells Michels this as something the visitors reported to him. If true, it would mean that even hardened, skeptical guards met something there that they could not explain.","post-war-nazi-network-die-spinne-in-the-arab-world":"After the Second World War, the claim goes, an international Nazi network known as Die Spinne, German for \"the spider\", carried on in the Arab world. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels he goes into it in his book Closer Encounters, building on the research of an author named Joseph Farrell, though he names no particular source for the claim itself. If true, it would mean that organised Nazi circles outlived the war and kept working abroad.","postwar-secrecy-shaped-by-war-of-the-worlds-panic-1938":"The idea here is that the US government kept UFOs secret after the war partly because of how the public reacted to Orson Welles's 1938 radio play The War of the Worlds. Kurt McConnell, a Senate staffer, makes the point to Michels without citing a source. He says that in the late 1940s \"we were barely, you know, a decade removed\" from the broadcast, and he dates it to 1938. John S. Herrington, a former Secretary of Energy, gives the same reason on the show, speaking as a one-time official. He says that when Welles \"broadcast War of the Worlds,\" the country \"went nuts.\" Both use the memory of that panic to explain why officials kept quiet. If they are right, postwar secrecy about UFOs was driven less by the objects themselves than by fear of how Americans would react.","potassium-chloride-suicide-plan-2008":"In 2008 in Britain, a computer hacker named Gary McKinnon, who says he found signs of a secret NASA space program, planned to take his own life. He tells Michels, \"I bought potassium chloride, one of the three chemicals in the lethal injection.\"","ppd-shut-down-and-possibly-reborn-as-other-programs-1990":"The program Dan Sherman says he worked in, which he calls PPD and describes as an NSA effort to talk with aliens, has been shut down and may have been reborn as other programs. Sherman, a former sergeant who says he took part in it in the early 1990s, tells Michels \"I'm pretty sure\" it was shut down, and names a Scott Andrews in telling it. If true, the work would have carried on under other names.","prayer-breakfasts-and-doug-coe":"A man named Doug Coe is said to have led a powerful religious group in Washington that Michels calls the Family, and to have hosted prayer breakfasts that every president had to attend. Peter Levenda, a researcher and writer, tells Michels that the group held prayer breakfasts and gatherings, and Coe's name comes up as he speaks. Michels, on a later episode and without naming a source, says Coe was one of the Family's leaders in the 1980s and that all the presidents had to come to his prayer breakfast. If true, it would mean a private religious circle had regular access to the highest levels of US power.","prayer-to-reach-rock-musicians-answered-with-calls-2012":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs and has been studied by NASA, says he prayed to reach rock musicians and that his prayers were answered with phone calls. He tells Michels that in 2025, on his birthday, he got a call from the wife of the rock singer Sammy Hagar. He names other rock stars in the same story, and tells it as a sign that his prayers are heard.","pre-adamic-cosmic-war-wipes-out-the-civilization-on-mars":"Long before Adam, a war in the heavens is said to have been fought across the solar system, and in that conflict the civilisation on Mars \"was eviscerated.\" Tim Alberino tells Michels this as his own summary, building on ideas he credits to David Flynn and to works titled Cydonia and Birthright. If true, it would mean Mars once held a civilisation destroyed in a cosmic war before human history began.","pre-dynastic-maadi-lion-cult-around-the-sphinx":"A researcher named Luke Caverns suggests that the Sphinx at Giza may have been the god of the Maadi, a people who lived around Giza before the age of the pharaohs. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that he has \"always thought that it was perfectly possible that the Sphinx could have been their god\", which would make the monument far older than the dynasties usually credited with it.","pre-war-ig-farbenstandard-oil-monopoly-arrangement-1930":"Before the Second World War, in the 1930s, the German chemical giant IG Farben and the American oil company Standard Oil reportedly agreed to give each other \"mutual monopolies\", carving up their markets between them. A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source, linking it to the Rockefeller and Schroeder business families. He uses it to show how close American and German industry were before the war.","preacher-solon-brown-sees-beings-in-the-craft-1948":"A preacher named Solon Brown, one of the people said to have reached a crashed saucer in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948, said years later that he saw a few beings inside it, maybe three, slumped over some kind of table or panel. Michels tells this himself, passing on what Brown is said to have said, in an episode about that alleged crash. If true, it would be an eyewitness account of the craft's crew.","precognitive-dream-of-the-focus-magazine-1972":"The researcher of psychic phenomena Jeffrey Mishlove says that in 1972, living in married student housing in Berkeley, California, he dreamt of a magazine on his living room floor and then found one there, \"just as I had dreamt,\" called Focus, the magazine of the public broadcaster KQED. He tells Michels this from his own life and mentions the writer Eric Wargo, who writes on dreams that foresee the future. He links it to the same night he dreamt of a great-uncle visiting at the moment of his death; if true, the dream would have shown him something before it happened.","predecessor-killed-when-a-running-reactor-was-cut-open-1988":"Around 1988, at the Nevada nuclear test site, a scientist was killed when the team cut into an alien craft's reactor while it was running under load and it exploded. Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on reverse engineering alien craft (taking them apart to learn how they work) at a secret Nevada site, tells Michels he was told this: \"they physically cut into it while it was running under load... And it exploded.\" A Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais repeats Lazar's account differently: he says the man used a blowtorch, and that Lazar was hired to replace him.","predicted-extraction-shaft-beneath-the-red-pyramid":"There should be a hidden shaft running down from beneath the last chamber of the Red Pyramid, an ancient pyramid at Dahshur in Egypt, according to a researcher named Geoffrey Drumm. Drumm calls that room the \"final synthesis chamber,\" meaning the place where, in his view, the pyramid finished producing something. He tells Michels the shaft is a prediction that follows from his own theory, not something anyone has found. If such a shaft turned up, it would suggest the pyramid was built to make a product and draw it out.","predicted-hidden-anticamera-linking-the-north-corridor-to-th":"A hidden chamber, an 'anticamera' or antechamber, sits inside the Great Pyramid of Giza and links a passage on its north side to the Grand Gallery, the tall sloping corridor inside the pyramid, and to the 'Big Void', a large hidden empty space reported inside the structure. That is the prediction of the researcher Filippo Biondi. He tells Michels the chamber 'goes directly to the Grand Gallery' and cites no source beyond his own work. If he is right, the pyramid holds a connected set of rooms that has never been entered.","predicted-ufo-hovers-over-puharich-family-gathering-1987":"In the late 1980s, after Andrija 'Andy' Puharich predicted it, a huge object hovered silently over a Puharich family gathering in North Carolina, and Puharich's son and 'two or three of the cousins all saw this huge thing.' The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this from what one of Puharich's cousins told him, with a family video among his sources. Puharich was a scientist the episode links to the CIA who claimed contact with a council of alien beings. If true, he could make a sighting happen when he said it would.","predicting-three-orange-orbs-at-9-45-at-contact-in-the-deser-2025":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he draws UFOs to him and whom NASA has studied, says that in 2025 he told a crowd ahead of time exactly when lights would appear. It happened at an event called Contact in the Desert, in Palm Springs, California. He tells Michels he announced to the whole group, 'at 9:45 there'll be three orange orbs that appear underneath the clouds.' If the orbs came as he said, it would mean he can somehow know or summon them.","premonition-dream-of-friends-2-a-m-arrest":"When he was about 18 or 19, the researcher Randall Carlson says, he was asleep at home and had an intense dream that his best friend from high school and the friend's older brother were in trouble and had been busted, and that turned out to be a 2 a.m. arrest. Carlson tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and he offers it as a dream that seemed to know about events happening somewhere else.","premonition-of-the-dc-metro-red-line-fire":"Luis Elizondo, the subject of a documentary episode about alien groups visiting Earth, says he had a premonition that came true: \"on the 89th day\" there was a fatal fire on the Red Line of the Washington DC Metro. He tells Michels it happened to him, mentions Hal Puthoff as he tells it, and does not give a date. If true, it would mean he could sense events before they happened.","preparata-and-del-giudice-worked-with-fleischmann-and-pons-o-1980":"Two Italian scientists at the University of Milan, Giuliano Preparata and Emilio Del Giudice, are said to have worked with Martin Fleischmann and Stanley Pons, the pair known for cold fusion, the claim that nuclear fusion can be made to happen at room temperature. A researcher named Filippo Biondi says so on the show, without naming a source: \"they were the scientists that worked together to Fleischmann and Pons.\" The point is that serious physicists took the work seriously over the years from about 1980 onward.","presidency-cut-out-of-the-loop-under-eisenhower-1950":"In the 1950s, the presidency was cut out of the secret UFO loop, an event a researcher named Steven Greer calls \"the decapitation of the presidency\" on Eisenhower's watch. Greer tells Michels this without naming a source. He adds that Richard Nixon, who chaired the space council while he was Eisenhower's vice president, was \"part of the team that betrayed Eisenhower\". If true, presidents since then would not control what was known about UFOs.","president-of-kalmykia-abducted-by-aliens-while-in-office-1990":"The president of Kalmykia, a Buddhist Russian republic next to Dagestan, says aliens took him while he was in office. Jesse Michels interviewed the man, named in the show as Kanan Il Yum Xenov, and repeats what he was told: in one telling the man was abducted, and in another he was invited onto a spaceship, in 1997. If true, it would mean a sitting head of government says he had direct contact.","presidential-advisor-held-ufo-material-from-a-1963-marshall":"Near the end of his life, a former presidential adviser named Harold Malmgren is said to have admitted holding pieces of a UFO that came down after a high-altitude nuclear test in the Marshall Islands in the Pacific. Michels says his team interviewed Malmgren and has told the story on several episodes. The first time he dated the test to late 1963. Later he named it as Bluegill Triple Prime and placed it in 1962, and he also brought in a man named Lawrence Preston Gise and the Sandia complex, a US nuclear weapons laboratory site.","presidential-ufo-briefings-nixon-to-bush-but-not-clinton-1969":"Presidents Nixon, Carter, Reagan and Bush \"all seem to have received briefings\" about UFOs, but Bill Clinton did not, according to a researcher named Jason Simosa. Simosa tells Michels the Clinton exception is \"pretty demonstrable,\" but he does not name the evidence. If true, secret UFO knowledge was shared with some presidents from 1969 to 1993 and kept from another.","presidents-look-into-ufos-and-find-nothing-1990":"Every new president, Clinton, Obama and Trump among them, arrives at the White House set on finding out what is really going on with UFOs, and finds nothing. That is the argument the skeptic Michael Shermer makes to Michels, without citing a source. His point is that if there were a hidden truth, a president who went looking would have found it.","presidents-not-told-unless-like-bush-41-they-were-cia-direct":"US presidents are probably not even told about the hidden programmes that are said to study non-human craft, unless, like George H. W. Bush, they once ran the CIA before taking office. David Grusch, who appears on the show as a witness to these matters, says this to Michels, passing on what he has been told. If true, it would mean the people who keep the secret answer to no elected leader.","presocratic-cave-rituals":"Before Socrates, early Greek thinkers are said to have taken part in rituals in caves, which Michels later describes as sensory deprivation and as older than the Eleusinian Mysteries, the famous secret rites of ancient Greece. Michels tells it himself from the writing of Peter Kingsley, a scholar of early Greek philosophy, and on another episode also credits the German philosopher Martin Heidegger. It suggests that philosophy may have begun in altered states of mind.","price-and-mcmoneagle-find-aliens-and-humans-working-together":"Two remote viewers, people trained to try to see distant places with the mind alone, named Pat Price and Joe McMoneagle, are said to have found aliens and humans working side by side inside Mount Hayes in Alaska, running something like an intelligence-gathering post. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan tells Michels this second-hand, as what he understood the two men had found. If true, it would mean a hidden joint base of humans and non-humans on American soil.","priest-tells-vallee-to-burn-an-ibm-circuit-card-as-from-the-1950":"In France, sometime in the 1950s or 60s, a priest looked at an IBM computer circuit card and told the researcher Jacques Vallée, \"my son burn it, it is from the Devil.\" Vallée tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and he mentions a person named Michelle in telling it. The story shows how strange and threatening early computer hardware could look to a churchman of that time.","primakov-proposes-that-the-svr-keep-arafat-alive-and-the-cia-1993":"In Moscow in 1993, Yevgeny Primakov, then head of the SVR, Russia's foreign intelligence service, is said to have offered the CIA a deal: \"Our goal will be to keep Arafat alive. Your goal will be to keep Rabin alive.\" Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer, tells Michels this as something Primakov said to him, an offer that would have had rival spy services each guarding a different side's leader in the Middle East peace process.","prince-hans-adam-expects-jesus-on-a-flying-saucer":"Hans-Adam II, the reigning prince of Liechtenstein, is said to believe that Jesus will return on a flying saucer. Michels says this himself, without naming a source: \"He believes Jesus will show up on a flying saucer.\" He raises it as a sign of how far UFO ideas reach into religious belief among the powerful.","prince-hans-adam-gathered-the-roswell-witnesses-1970":"Prince Hans-Adam of Liechtenstein is said to have gathered all the witnesses of the 1947 Roswell saucer crash in New Mexico. Michels tells it twice, first as something he heard from John Brandenburg, a man he interviewed, and later saying the prince \"apparently\" convened them, this time linking it to the writer Whitley Strieber and giving no date. If true, a reigning European royal would have taken a private interest in getting to the bottom of Roswell.","prince-hans-adam-of-liechtenstein-s-letter-proof-always-slip":"Hans-Adam II, the Prince of Liechtenstein, is said to have written a thoughtful letter back to someone Michels calls Max, who had written to him, saying that proof had always slipped through his fingers. Michels passes this on as something he heard (\"apparently\"), in an episode on Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs that also draws on a witness named Maaneli Derakhshani and on the physicist Hal Puthoff. If true, it would suggest a reigning European prince had looked for proof of UFOs himself.","princeton-anomalies-research-lab":"Princeton University once had a lab that studied anomalies, what Michels calls the \"Princeton anomalous research lab.\" He names it in passing, without more detail, in a talk with the writer Eric Wargo about synchronicities and prophetic dreams, as a sign that a top university looked into such things.","princeton-parapsychology-lab-studied-uri-geller-brought-by-p-1970":"In the 1970s a parapsychology lab at Princeton, a place that studied claimed mind powers, is said to have studied a man named Uri Geller, who was brought there by a man named Andrija Puharich. Michels raises it himself, without naming a source, but his words break off at \"the Princeton Parasychology Lab has a\", so what he says the lab found is left unsaid.","princeton-pear-lab-and-the-global-consciousness-project-rand-2001":"A Princeton University lab and a project that grew out of it, the Global Consciousness Project, kept machines called random event generators running. These devices spit out random numbers, and the idea behind them is that strong shared human emotion might push the numbers away from chance. Michels says the machines jumped on 9/11, \"the biggest spike they've ever seen,\" and again when Bob Jahn, the man linked to the lab, died. He tells it himself, pointing to Jahn, and if it were true it would mean human minds can nudge physical events.","princeton-pear-lab-random-event-generator-rebound-effect":"A Princeton lab that studied anomalies, known as PEAR, ran experiments on whether people's minds could nudge random event generators, machines that put out random numbers, and found what Michels calls a \"rebound effect.\" Michels tells this from a mentor, John Valentino, who he says helped run the lab under Bob Jahn. In other episodes he calls it a \"skewing effect\" backed by plenty of evidence, or puts the stress on how focused, or coherent, the observer is. If true, it would mean the mind can affect physical events.","princeton-random-event-generators-affected-by-heart-centered":"At a parapsychology lab at Princeton, a group that tested whether the mind can affect machines, people are said to have found that the more \"heart-centered\" a person is, the more they can sway the output of a random event generator, a device that spits out random numbers. Michels tells this as something a friend from that lab told him, a friend he does not name, who called it a \"dirty little secret\" well known among people who work with these machines. If true, it would mean that feeling, not just focus, has some effect on physical things.","princeton-studies-of-geller":"Uri Geller, a man famous for claims of psychic powers, was studied by researchers at Princeton, and there are papers from that work, says Jesse Michels. He brings it up himself, passing it on from a friend at the Princeton parapsychology lab, a lab that studied claims of mind over matter, without describing what the papers found.","priscilla-mcmillan-revealed-as-cia-asset-1959":"Priscilla McMillan, a writer who interviewed Lee Harvey Oswald when he went to Russia, befriended his wife Marina Oswald and wrote Marina's life story for her, was a \"witting CIA asset\", meaning someone knowingly working for the CIA. A researcher named Jeremy Riss says so on the show, pointing in general to the JFK files without naming a particular document. If true, it would mean the CIA had a person of its own close to the Oswalds from 1959 on.","private-contractor-and-jsoc-team-exchange-fire-at-a-crash-si":"Two teams are said to have reached the same crashed craft at once and shot at each other. One was a private military crew working under contract for Lockheed Martin. The other came from JSOC, the Joint Special Operations Command, which runs the US military's most secretive special forces units. Michels's co-host, a defence and aerospace engineer who goes by the pseudonym Jack and has more than 15 years in the field, brings it up while interviewing a guest. He calls it \"a story\" that a contractor was \"sent out to recover a crash\" and that the JSOC team was \"sent out to the same crash site.\" Michels tells it again in a later episode as a rumour of \"a dog fight\" between the two retrieval teams. He gives more detail than Jack: he dates it to 2004, sets it at Coyame, Mexico, and ties it to a man named Jake Barber and to a work called Sentinels of Ether that he links to Barber. If it were true, it would mean rival groups inside the American system were racing each other for crashed craft.","private-jsoc-veteran-team-behind-herrera-and-barber-retrieva-2009":"A researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb (UAP being the official term for unidentified flying objects) suspects that one private team, made up of veterans of the Joint Special Operations Command, the US military's elite special forces command, was behind two separate accounts of recovering unknown craft. He tells Michels, without naming a source, that the team a former Marine named Michael Herrera says he dealt with in 2009 \"is the same team that Jake Barber is familiar with\", Barber being another man who says he took part in such a recovery. If so, it would link the two stories to one hidden outfit.","private-lab-effects-verified-by-others":"Dave Rossi, a guest on an episode titled \"Aliens Taught Me Advanced Physics!\", says he has seen things in private laboratories that are \"undeniable\" and that other people in those facilities have checked, not only him. He tells Michels this as something he saw himself, though he does not say here what the effects were or where the labs are. If he is right, unexplained physical effects are being produced in private labs and confirmed by more than one witness.","private-sector-companies-with-wet-works-arms-2023":"Some private companies tied to secret UFO work are said to employ people who will use violence, which spies call \"wet works\" or \"kinetic activity\", against those who talk. Eric Burlison, a congressman, tells Michels that whistleblowers including David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who testified to Congress about hidden UFO programmes, and another insider named Jake Barber all warned him of the same thing: \"certain private sector companies\" with \"nefarious actors that will do kinetic activity.\" If true, it would mean people coming forward face physical danger from contractors, not just the government.","prodigy-spotted-at-age-12-or-13-and-recruited-by-the-mit-pre":"A man named Harold Malmgren was spotted as a gifted child at about 12 or 13 and recruited by the president of MIT, with Rensselaer and Yale also part of his path. The researcher Matthew Pines tells Michels this without naming a source and sets it beside the mathematician James Simons. He offers it as an example of how the system finds talented young people early and steers them.","professor-s-friends-at-malmstrom-ufos-disabling-missiles-1988":"UFOs fly over nuclear missile sites at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana and shut down the missiles, according to people who work there. A professor named Kevin Knuth tells Michels this from what friends at the base have told him. If true, it would mean unknown craft can interfere with America's nuclear weapons.","program-reported-by-shellenberger-that-uses-ai-to-identify-u-2024":"In 2024, according to a commentator named Matthew Pines, the journalist Michael Shellenberger wrote about a government program that \"apparently is using AI to identify UAPs and maybe even ARVs\", meaning unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, and alien reproduction vehicles, the subject's term for human-built copies of alien craft. Pines tells Michels this from Shellenberger's reporting, adding that Shellenberger might testify about it in Congress the following week, which would mean a hidden effort to track such objects exists.","program-traced-to-dachau-vanished-children-in-gas-chambers-1940":"Whitley Strieber, a writer who says he has met aliens, believes a secret programme began in the Nazi camps, at Dachau, in the 1940s. As the story is framed, the programme is tied to children who vanished in the gas chambers. Strieber tells Michels \"I think it started at Dachau. I think it started in the camps,\" and names no source. If true, the programme he describes has its roots in Nazi atrocities.","programs-call-psionic-operators-pink-assets":"Some secret programmes, said to deal with non-human craft, are claimed to call their \"psionic\" operators, people said to use their minds to sense or influence such craft, by the code name \"pink assets\". Michael Herrera, a guest who says he saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels this was passed on to him, and he names a man called Jake Barber in connection with it. If true, it would mean these programmes use mental abilities as a working tool and have their own slang for the people who have them.","programs-for-summoning-ufos-such-as-skywatchers-and-special":"Michels thinks that efforts to summon UFOs, like Skywatchers, a modern private project that tries to call them in, have been going on for a long time inside special access programmes, the US government's most tightly restricted secret projects. He says this as his own view, without naming a source, in an episode about secret societies and the UFO cover-up.","project-a119-to-set-off-a-nuclear-blast-on-the-moon":"Project A119 was an Air Force plan to set off a nuclear blast on the Moon as a show of force against the Soviets, and the astronomer Carl Sagan worked on it as a secret consultant. Michels tells this himself in several episodes, calling it a documented, top secret project; once he places it at Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, and elsewhere he dates it to 1958 or more loosely to the late 1950s or 60s. He raises it to show that a famous public scientist also did classified military work.","project-aquarius-document-said-to-be-real-1977":"A briefing document known as the Project Aquarius document, long said to have been faked by two men named Bill Moore and Rick Doty, is in fact real, according to a man named Alonzo McDonald. Eric W. Davis, a scientist who has worked for the Pentagon, tells Michels he has seen the document himself and that McDonald told him Moore and Doty \"had nothing to do with anything\". If true, it would mean a paper many dismissed as forged is a genuine government briefing.","project-atom-in-serpo-matches-ttsa-s-atom-project-2013":"Project Serpo, a story about a supposed secret exchange between the US and aliens, includes a \"Project Atom\", which the researcher Deep Prasad describes as a secret Los Alamos programme that studied the radiation from materials recovered from alien spacecraft. Prasad tells Michels that this matches an Atom project at To The Stars Academy, a company formed by the musician Tom DeLonge with the physicist Hal Puthoff, active around 2013 to 2016, though he gives no source beyond the Serpo story. If true, it would suggest the company was echoing, or connected to, a real hidden programme.","project-blue-beam-conspiracy-theory":"There is a theory, Jesse Michels says, that goes by the name Project Blue Beam. He brings it up himself, without naming a source, while talking with a guest named Jake Barber, who says he recovered a UFO with his helicopter. In the passage given here, Michels names the theory and says nothing yet about what it holds.","project-blue-book-and-the-condon-study-1950":"The Air Force's long-running UFO study, Project Blue Book, ended after a university review that, Karl Nell says, had its answer written before the work was done. Blue Book was the US Air Force's UFO investigation of the 1950s and 60s. Nell is a guest speaking from an official background. He tells Michels that the Air Force commissioned a study from the University of Colorado, led by Edward Condon, who had worked on the Manhattan Project, and that Condon \"wrote the conclusion of the study before the study was complete\" and bragged about it. The same claim comes up again in a second episode, without the bragging. If true, it would mean the official end of UFO research was decided in advance. The study itself, \"Scientific Study of Unidentified Flying Objects\", published by the University of Colorado on October 31, 1968, says the Air Force Office of Scientific Research \"decided to ask the University of Colorado to undertake the project\". The report says nothing about when its conclusions were written, so the claim that they were written early does not come from the report. A 1985 Air Force fact sheet gives the record's side of the ending: \"The decision to discontinue UFO investigations was based on an evaluation\" of the Colorado report, a review of it by the National Academy of Sciences, and the Air Force's own experience in the 40s, 50s and 60s.","project-blue-book-and-the-pentagon-report-contradict-each-ot-1952":"The government's old word on UFOs and its newer word do not fit together, so one of them must be wrong. That is the argument of the researcher Eric Weinstein, who puts it to Michels in 2022 during a debate on the show. On one side is Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s; on the other is the Pentagon's 2021 report to Congress on UAP, the government's newer term for UFOs, meaning unidentified aerial phenomena. Weinstein says that \"either we were lying then that there's nothing to see\" and \"telling the truth now,\" and if so, the earlier official story was not straight with the public. An Air Force fact sheet of January 1985 sets out Blue Book's conclusions: that no UFO it investigated \"has ever given any indication of threat to our national security,\" that there was no evidence the \"unidentified\" sightings showed technology \"beyond the range of present-day scientific knowledge,\" and no evidence they \"are extraterrestrial vehicles.\" The fact sheet speaks of what was submitted to or found by the Air Force; whether that sits at odds with the later Pentagon report is Weinstein's reading, and the fact sheet itself does not compare the two.","project-galileo-briefing-and-its-two-directives-1988":"A secret briefing paper called Project Galileo, read at a hidden Nevada site called S-4 in 1988 or 1989, \"dealt with the propulsion system of the craft\": that is, how the craft kept there were said to fly. Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked at S-4 on craft that humans did not build, tells Michels he saw it himself, and he names a second paper too, a briefing for something called Project Looking Glass. If true, it would mean the government had written its own study papers on how such craft move.","project-orion-nuclear-propulsion-1950":"In the 1950s and 60s the United States had a plan called Project Orion to push a spacecraft with nuclear explosions, and Michels says the Air Force general Curtis LeMay wanted it and the physicist Freeman Dyson worked on it. He says John F. Kennedy was strongly against it, so it was killed. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in a conversation with a former Secretary of Energy, as an example of bold propulsion ideas that were shelved.","project-pandora-and-soviet-radio-frequency-weapons-1960":"A US effort called Project Pandora, which the researcher Greg Malatzi says started 'I think' in the 1960s, is tied in this story to Soviet weapons that used radio waves. Malatzi mentions it to Michels in passing, without a source and with little detail beyond the date. He brings it up as part of a wider picture of secret Cold War work on radio waves and the mind.","project-preserve-destiny-code-name-staunch-118-1991":"A former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman says the NSA brought him in to communicate with aliens. He says this was done through a programme called Project Preserve Destiny, with training at an NSA site in Maryland in 1991, and that his code name in it was \"Staunch 118.\" Sherman tells Michels this from his own experience. If true, it would mean a US intelligence agency was running a secret effort to talk with non-human beings.","project-preserve-destiny-mother-abducted-and-fetus-genetical-1990":"Under something called Project Preserve Destiny, a man named Dan Sherman's mother was abducted by aliens while she was pregnant with him, and the unborn child was genetically altered. This is Sherman's account in his book Above Black, and Michels passes it on. If true, it would mean a secret program was shaping children with alien help from before they were born.","project-serpo-book-built-on-puthoff-s-spacetime-metric-engin-2005":"The Project Serpo book, which tells of a supposed exchange in which Americans travelled to an alien planet, is said to be built entirely on the physics of Hal Puthoff, a physicist who has worked on government studies of the unexplained. Deep Prasad, a researcher, tells Michels what struck him on reading it: \"the whole project Serpo book completely integrates\" Puthoff's \"spacetime metric engineering\", the idea of reshaping space and time itself to drive a craft. To Prasad, that link suggests the story draws on serious physics.","project-serpo-exchange-program-1947":"After the Roswell crash of 1947, a recovered being, an \"EBE\" or extraterrestrial biological entity, is said to have led to an exchange programme in which the United States sent people to the beings' home planet, Serpo, a story laid out in papers known as the Project Serpo documents. An unnamed researcher tells Michels this second-hand, linking it to the physicist Hal Puthoff, to a man named Richard Doty and to the DIA, the Pentagon's Defense Intelligence Agency. If true, it would mean humans had visited another world in a secret swap with aliens.","project-serpo-exchange-program-1970":"Project Serpo is the claim, spread through a so-called leak, that aliens from a planet called Serpo sent some of their own to Earth while the United States sent a group of humans to their star system, in an exchange sometime in the 1970s. The researcher Greg Bishop sums it up on the show and ties it to a man named Rick Doty. If true, it would mean the government had a secret exchange program with another world.","project-sidekick-briefing-on-the-craft-as-a-particle-beam-we-1988":"A man named Bob Lazar says that in 1988 and 1989, while working at S-4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, he read briefing papers on a programme called Project Sidekick, which looked at using a craft held there as a particle beam weapon. \"Sidekick was the weapon potential of the craft,\" he tells Michels, speaking as someone who says he saw the papers. If true, it would mean the government was studying alien craft as weapons.","project-sidekick-directed-energy-weapon-1980":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked in the 1980s at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, says that alongside the craft work there was also something called Project Sidekick, which he describes simply as \"a weapon.\" He mentions it to Michels in passing as something he knew of first-hand, and if true it would mean the site was building weapons as well as studying craft.","project-sign-1948-report-on-common-shapes":"One of the first reports of Project Sign, an early US Air Force study of flying saucer sightings, said that the most commonly reported shapes were \"silver discs and spheres and balls of fire\". A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff brings this up from memory, unsure of the details, saying he thinks it dates from late 1948, and he mentions the name John Greenewald Jr. along with it. It matters to him because it shows the Air Force was cataloguing the same shapes people still describe today.","project-sign-estimate-burned-by-vandenberg-1948":"The story is that Project Sign, the US Air Force's first UFO study, written at Wright Field in Ohio, concluded that UFOs were spacecraft from other worlds. Its staff are said to have set this out in a top secret report called the Estimate of the Situation, dated by the record to 1948. The report then went to General Hoyt Vandenberg, the head of the Air Force, and the story says he had it burned. If true, the Air Force's own experts reached that conclusion early and it was buried from the top. Michels tells it himself, saying that 'in 1949 Sign personnel wrote a top secret report concluding UFOs were extraterrestrial craft.' A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas also tells it. He dates it to 1948 and insists, 'It was a real document. It was seen by Ruppelt.' He means Edward J. Ruppelt, who later led the Air Force's UFO work and wrote The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects, published in 1956, which Salas cites as his source. In Salas's version, Vandenberg rejected the Estimate rather than burning it, and the two tellers differ by a year on its date.","project-starlight-and-the-2001-national-press-club-event-1993":"A UFO activist named Steven Greer says he ran a private effort, code-named Project Starlight, starting in the early 1990s. He says it went public in 2001 with an event at the National Press Club in Washington, which he says \"800 million people\" saw. Greer tells it as his own work, and if he is right, his campaign for official disclosure of UFO secrets reached a huge audience.","project-twinkle-green-fireballs-over-new-mexico-1947":"From the late 1940s, bright green fireballs were seen streaking over New Mexico, and an official investigation called Project Twinkle was set up to study them. A researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb describes it on the show as an investigation run by the 'Z division' of Sandia, a government laboratory in New Mexico, and points to the project's own records. Michels tells it himself on two other episodes. He calls it 'an Air Force investigation' and says it was headed by Lincoln LaPaz, a meteor expert at the University of New Mexico, who began tracking the fireballs. In one telling Michels says the fireballs were 'showing up across American nuclear sites', naming Los Alamos and Holloman Air Force Base. In another he cites FBI documents on the fireballs. So the tellers differ on whose project it was, Sandia's or the Air Force's, but they agree on LaPaz and the green lights. They tell it because, if true, something unexplained kept turning up over the country's most sensitive nuclear sites.","project-unity-flagged-by-the-dod-as-a-russian-disinformation":"A guest named Jay Anderson says the Department of Defense flagged his platform, Project Unity, as a Russian disinformation outlet. He tells Michels he only heard this from someone else (\"I got told that I was flagged\"), and jokes that they are still letting him into the country. If true, it would mean the Pentagon was keeping an eye on people who talk publicly about these subjects.","prometheus-chained-in-the-caucasus-by-zeus":"In the Greek myth, Prometheus is punished by Zeus by being chained in the Caucasus mountains, where his liver is devoured. A researcher named Jason Jorjani retells it on the show, without naming a source, in a conversation about secret wartime technology, using the old story as a picture of what happens to those who hand forbidden knowledge to humans.","prometheus-system-brain-neural-interface-built-from-a-psychi-2008":"Between 2008 and 2010, at a place in New York called Baker Victory Services, people were trying to build a \"brain neural interface\", a link between a human brain and a machine, using the brain waves of a child said to be psychic. That is the account of Jordan Jose, who tells Michels he was that child, on an episode titled \"I Was Taken By The UFO Program As a Kid!\". The effort goes under the name the Prometheus system, and if his account is true, someone was trying to turn a child's psychic ability into working technology.","promise-of-new-sar-scans-of-khafre-to-detect-the-belzoni-cha-2026":"More radar scans of the Khafre pyramid at Giza will be made in 2026 to find what he calls the Belzoni chamber, a hidden room inside it, says a researcher named Filippo Biondi. Biondi uses SAR, short for synthetic aperture radar, a way of making images from radar signals sent from satellites. He tells Michels, \"I ensure you that more scans will be done on the Khafre in order to find something to detect Belzoni.\"","proof-of-concept-scans-of-known-structures-2020":"Before it was used on Egypt's pyramids, a scanning method was tested on structures whose layout was already known, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum. One test site was the Gran Sasso Laboratory, a modern facility in Italy. Another was the Osiris Shaft, a known shaft in Egypt. Drum tells Michels the method, which he credits to a researcher named Filippo Biondi, was \"very good at accurately detecting the known structures within these modern facilities,\" and the Osiris Shaft as well. The point of the tests is that a method which finds known structures correctly gives more weight to what it reports beneath the pyramids.","prophet-predicting-trump-being-shot-in-the-ear-2024":"Michels says a self-proclaimed prophet, whom he does not name, talked about Donald Trump being shot in the ear, and even named the same ear in which Trump was later hit in the 2024 attempt on his life. Michels tells this himself without giving a source, as an example of people who seem to see events before they happen.","prophet-yahweh-summons-a-ufo-for-a-las-vegas-tv-crew":"In 2005 a man who called himself Prophet Yahweh, and claimed he could summon UFOs, dared a local TV crew in Las Vegas to watch him do it. They met him in a park, he told them to keep their eyes on the sky, and 'all of a sudden, something strange appeared,' which the crew videotaped and showed on the evening news. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as it was told to him. If true, it would be a UFO showing up on request in front of a news camera.","propheta-s-letters-to-jim-garrison-in-the-garrison-files-1960":"In the 1960s, the leader of a small church in the Bronx, a man called Propheta, wrote letters to Jim Garrison, the New Orleans district attorney who investigated the Kennedy assassination, asking about Jack Martin, a man caught up in that investigation. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels the letters sit in Garrison's correspondence files, and that Propheta wrote, \"When you're finished with Jack Martin, can you send him back to us?\" The letter as quoted shows Propheta claiming Martin as one of his own; what that tie meant, it does not say.","proposals-to-shoot-at-craft-bledsoe-calls-down":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he can draw UFOs down to where he is, tells Michels he was asked whether, if he could get one of the craft to come down, people could shoot at it. \"You wouldn't believe,\" he says, that there were \"even proposals\" like that, and his answer was \"absolutely not.\" He tells it as something he was part of himself, and it would mean that someone took his ability seriously enough to plan around it.","proposed-experiment-testing-penrose-gravitational-collapse-w-2024":"A newly proposed experiment would entangle two tiny crystals magnetically to test the physicist Roger Penrose's idea that gravity makes quantum states collapse into one definite outcome. Matthew Pines describes it on the show, linking it to Penrose and the scientist Stuart Hameroff, and calls it the \"G experiment.\" If it worked, it would test a theory Penrose and Hameroff have tied to how consciousness arises.","proteomics-shows-a-j-type-head-is-alpaca-bone-2020":"Bone from the skull of one of the three-fingered bodies from Peru, a head of the so-called J type, came back as alpaca bone when tested by proteomics, a method that identifies species from the proteins in a sample. An anonymous proteomics expert says on the show that every sample \"came back identified as alpaca bone\", and points to a paper in Nature on identifying species in ancient bone and another on CT scans showing the brain case resembles an alpaca's.","protesters-against-the-white-train-and-the-nevada-test-site-1980":"John S. Herrington, who served as US Secretary of Energy in the 1980s, tells Michels that most of his time in that job was spent dealing with protesters. He says they tried to stop \"the white train\", the train that carried nuclear weapons, or to get onto the nuclear test site in Nevada. He offers it as his own memory of what the job was really like day to day.","protocols-of-the-elders-of-zion-as-a-russian-secret-police-h":"The Protocols of the Elders of Zion, a text claiming to reveal a secret Jewish plan to rule the world, was made up by the Russian secret police in the time of the tsar, before there was a Soviet Union. Peter Levenda, billed on the episode as a CIA historian, tells Michels this as something \"we know\", without naming a source. He raises it as an example of how a secret police can invent a document and send it out into the world.","prussian-school-system-designed-for-control":"The old Prussian school system, the claim goes, was admired as \"the Prussian miracle\" because it gave the state such control over its citizens, and other countries, the United States among them, sent people to learn how it was done. A guest named Kurt Metzger says this on the show, without naming where he got it. If true, it would mean modern schooling was built partly to make people obedient.","psionic-asset-recruited-through-the-gate-program":"A person with claimed psychic powers, whom insiders call a \"psionic asset\", was said to have been recruited for government work through GATE, a school programme for gifted and talented children. Michels says this himself, mentioning that he \"interviewed yesterday a psionic asset who was recruited through the gate program.\" If true, it would mean children's gifted programmes were used to find psychics.","psionic-asset-tested-with-sphere-that-came-off-a-craft":"A person with claimed psychic powers, whom Michels calls a \"psionic asset\", is said to have been tested with a sphere that came off a craft. Michels mentions it only in passing, saying he had just interviewed someone who \"has that story\". He gives no name, place or date, and does not say what the test involved or what it showed. If true, it would mean psychic abilities were being tested against hardware from an unknown craft.","psionic-assets-in-faraday-cage-work-suites-used-as-sensors-a":"A former helicopter pilot named Jake Barber says his team uses \"psionic assets\" in the field: people trained in remote viewing, the claimed ability to perceive distant things with the mind. He says they put such a person in their work suite, \"which is like a Faraday cage,\" a metal enclosure that blocks radio signals, and treat them as one more measuring instrument alongside their equipment. Barber describes this from his own experience in two interviews, and Michels, retelling it later, puts it more boldly: that the government uses these people to \"summon\" UFOs into people's field of consciousness.","psionic-assets-used-to-summon-craft-given-drug-cocktails":"The claim is that people with supposed psychic gifts, called \"psionic\" or \"P3\" assets, are used by a secret programme to call UFOs to them, and that they are badly abused. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb says there is \"historical testimony\" that these people are \"treated like crap\" and given \"drug cocktails\" so they perform well, which can lead to \"danger or death.\" He links this to accounts from a man named Jake Barber and a witness named Michael. Michels repeats it from his interview in the summer of 2024 with a witness named Michael Herrera. He says that what was known then was that the P3 assets were \"treated like trash\" and given drug cocktails \"to interface with the craft.\" If true, it would mean human minds are being used, at a cost, to contact the craft.","psionic-pipeline-feeding-the-uap-legacy-program-2008":"Jordan Jose says that as a child in Western New York, between 2008 and 2010, he was put through a \"psionic development pipeline\", a programme to train psychic abilities of the mind, in order to feed what he calls \"legacy program development\". In this subject, a legacy program means a long-running secret government effort around UAP, the official term for UFOs. Jose tells Michels this as something he lived through, and if it is true, the secret UFO effort was recruiting and training psychic children.","psionic-program-some-assets-are-abductees":"Tim Alberino, a guest who tells Michels about secret military matters, says he was told about a psionic programme, meaning one that uses people's claimed psychic or mind powers, and that some of the people it works with are alien abductees. In his words, he was told \"we know for a fact that some of our assets are abductees.\" He tells it as something said to him directly. If true, the government would be knowingly using people who say they were taken by non-human beings.","psionic-shortcuts-tested-on-program-members-including-childr":"A secret program, the story goes, tried out \"cheats,\" shortcuts to psionic powers (abilities of the mind), and these went badly for the people involved, including children. Jake Barber, who says he flew a helicopter to recover a UFO, tells Michels this from what he was told. If true, it would mean people, children among them, were harmed in secret experiments.","psionics-as-part-of-the-cold-war-retrieval-and-reverse-engin":"Research into psionics, meaning powers of the mind such as telepathy, was part of a secret Cold War program to recover and reverse engineer (take apart and copy) unexplained technology, according to the researcher Matthew Pines. He tells Michels this as his own reading, naming no source. If it were true, the government's mind research and its alleged study of strange craft would be one project, not two.","psionics-detect-nhi-at-sedona-base":"A base near Sedona, Arizona, is alleged to be linked to NHI, short for non-human intelligence, and people who call themselves psionic, meaning they claim to sense things with the mind, say they have felt such beings in the area. Ross Coulthart, a guest who says he has located a UFO base in Arizona, tells Michels about these allegations and says the psionic people told him this themselves. If true, it would mean non-human beings are active at a known site in the American Southwest.","psionics-team-at-work-on-the-night-of-the-eight-gon-recovery-2014":"Jake Barber, a former military helicopter pilot who says he flew in the recovery of an unknown craft in the California desert in 2014, tells Michels that \"there was a psionics team working that night.\" Psionics here means people trained to use the mind to sense or influence things at a distance, and he speaks as someone who says he was there. If true, it would mean mind-based methods were being used in craft recoveries.","psychiatric-patient-picking-up-radio-signals-through-a-filli-1995":"A psychiatrist named Diane Powell says that almost 30 years ago she had a patient with a dental filling who was picking up radio signals through it. Powell tells it from her own practice, in a conversation about telepathy, as an example of the brain receiving signals in ways that sound impossible but may be real.","psychic-locates-a-crashed-plane-in-zaire-1977":"In 1995, Michels says, former President Jimmy Carter revealed that the CIA had consulted a psychic, without Carter's knowledge, to find a missing government plane that had gone down in Africa, in what was then Zaire, around 1977. Michels tells this himself, attributing the revelation to Carter, in a conversation with a UFO investigator named James Fox. If true, it would mean the CIA put a psychic to practical use on a real search.","psychic-medium-explains-the-voice-in-the-witch-encounter-as-2025":"A voice that a Hollywood writer named Brent Friedman heard during a frightening encounter with what he calls a witch, in a Los Angeles bungalow, was explained to him by a psychic medium friend as \"your guardian angel or your higher self or whatever you want to call it talking to you.\" Friedman, who lived the encounter, tells Michels what the unnamed friend said in 2025. It would mean, on her reading, that something protective was watching over him that night.","psychic-predicts-orbs-over-the-ocean-at-the-round-table-1950":"In the early 1950s, at the Round Table Foundation, a research lab on the Maine coast run by Andrija Puharich, a doctor who studied psychic powers, a psychic would call out a time, like \"11:00, you know, tonight,\" and the group would go out and see a glowing orb over the ocean. Greg Malatzi, a researcher who has gone through Puharich's journals, tells Michels this. If true, it would mean someone could predict when the orbs would appear.","psychological-strategy-board-as-source-of-early-alien-lore-1950":"Michels says much of the early lore about aliens came from the people around the Psychological Strategy Board, a body under Eisenhower that coordinated the military's psychological operations. He names Eisenhower's aides Gordon Gray and Charles Douglas Jackson, and Philip Corso, an Army officer who later wrote about alien technology, as part of that circle. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and if it holds it would mean some alien stories began as products of a propaganda office.","ptolemy-on-the-soul-s-ascension-through-orion-s-belt":"Michels says the ancient astronomer Ptolemy wrote that the soul's path upward after death runs through Orion's Belt, the three bright stars in the constellation Orion. He brings it up himself, without quoting a text, in a talk about the idea that the Great Pyramid was built as a gateway for consciousness.","pub-dedicated-to-the-gravity-research-foundation-1940":"There is said to be a pub in New Hampshire dedicated to the Gravity Research Foundation, a group that, as its name says, was set up to study gravity. Michels links it to the work of a man named Roger Babson. He brings it up himself, without naming a source, while talking to Ryan Graves, a former Navy fighter pilot, and remarks that Graves lives quite close to the pub.","publication-of-the-necronomicon-financed-by-larry-barnes-1975":"The Necronomicon, a book presented as an ancient text of magic, was published in New York with money from a man named Larry Barnes, and by December 1977 \"there was a completed actual book for sale.\" The author Peter Levenda, who says he took part in putting it out, tells Michels this, naming the New York figure Herman Slater as also involved.","puharich-as-the-earlier-stage-of-the-research-jordan-went-th-1970":"Jordan Jose, who says he went through secret psychic research as a child in upstate New York around 2008 to 2010, tells Michels that a man named Puharich \"likely championed the earlier iteration\" of that same research, decades before. Jose places Puharich's work from about 1970 onward and offers the link as his own reading, not as something he saw. If he is right, the programme he describes has roots stretching back some forty years.","puharich-asks-for-his-papers-to-be-taken-away-for-safety-1990":"In the 1990s, Andrija Puharich is said to have told his assistant that the papers in his house needed to be taken away by someone, because he did not trust giving them to his children. An unnamed researcher passes this on from what the assistant said, to explain how Puharich's papers survived and why they might hold things he wanted kept safe.","puharich-courted-by-dark-occult-groups":"Andrija Puharich, a scientist who studied psychic abilities and claimed contact with an alien council, said throughout his life he kept being approached by what he called dark occult groups. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this from a lecture Puharich gave late in life. It would mean secretive groups were trying to draw him in.","puharich-delgado-correspondence-1960":"During the 1960s, a researcher named Andrija Puharich, called on the show a CIA scientist who contacted an alien council, is said to have exchanged letters with a man named Jose Delgado. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels that he and others found these letters, though he says little here about what is in them. To him they show the two men were in touch at that time.","puharich-first-announces-his-geller-work-at-zellerbach-audit-1972":"Jeffrey Mishlove, who has a PhD and studies psychic phenomena, says that in 1972 he sponsored a conference at Zellerbach Auditorium in Berkeley that drew 1,800 people. One of the speakers was a researcher named Andrija Puharich, and Mishlove says it was there that Puharich made his very first public announcement about his work with a man named Uri Geller, who was said to have psychic powers. Mishlove tells Michels this as someone who organised the event and was in the room.","puharich-found-dead-at-the-bottom-of-the-stairs-1995":"Andrija Puharich, a researcher of psychic phenomena said to have contracted for the CIA, was found dead at the bottom of the stairs in his house in 1995. An unnamed researcher says so on the show, without naming a source: \"he was found at the bottom of the stairs. He fell.\" The way it is told leaves open whether the fall was only an accident.","puharich-helped-find-psi-children-in-the-us-and-the-uk-1970":"Andrija Puharich, a physician and researcher of psychic phenomena, is said to have helped locate children with supposed psychic powers in the United States and Britain in the 1970s and 80s. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels that Puharich \"openly says, I helped locate these kids.\" If true, it would mean there was an organised search for gifted children, tied to a secret psychic research effort.","puharich-hypnotizes-geller-in-israel-and-the-nine-come-throu-1970":"Around 1970 and 1971 in Israel, before bringing him to the United States, the scientist Andrija Puharich is said to have hypnotised a young man named Uri Geller, and under hypnosis a group calling itself \"the Nine\" came through and spoke. The researcher Greg Malatzi summarises this for Michels, pointing to Puharich's book about Geller, Puharich's own tape recordings, and a Dr. Vinod. If true, it would mean a council of non-human intelligences was speaking through Geller.","puharich-jokes-on-tape-about-being-a-double-agent-for-israel":"Andrija Puharich, a doctor who researched psychic powers, is heard on one of his own tape recordings joking, \"Oh, I'm a double agent,\" in connection with Israel. Greg Malatzi, a researcher who has listened to Puharich's tapes, tells Michels about it and stresses the tone was joking, so it hints at, but does not settle, whether Puharich worked for more than one country.","puharich-letter-admits-israeli-us-mind-control-program-at-ab":"Andrija Puharich, a scientist who studied psychic abilities, admitted in a letter to his bookkeeper and confidant that he took part in a joint Israeli and American mind control program at Aberdeen Proving Grounds, an Army testing site in Maryland. The researcher Greg Malatzi sums up the letter on the show without quoting it. If true, it would place Puharich inside a secret government program.","puharich-meditates-in-airports-the-nine-speak-through-his-wa-1970":"A scientist named Puharich, whom Michels ties to the CIA, would stop at airports to meditate in the 1970s, believing he was helping bring about world peace and that \"the Nine\" would speak to him through his watch. The Nine are the group Michels describes as an alien council that Puharich believed he was in contact with. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source.","puharich-recruited-into-the-army-specialized-training-progra-1947":"Around 1947, at Northwestern University near Chicago, the scientist Andrija Puharich was part of the Army Specialized Training Program, an Army scheme that sent soldiers to study at universities. A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels this without naming a source. He presents it as the start of Puharich's ties to the military.","puharich-s-assistant-says-he-created-the-ufo-experience-with-1994":"The assistant of Andrija Puharich, a scientist who claimed contact with alien beings, said the UFO experiences around him were made 'using flashing lights' and other tricks: 'That stuff was all created, she says.' An unnamed researcher on the show passes this on from a 1994 tape recording made by the writer Dick Russell. If true, at least some of the encounters in Puharich's circle were staged.","puharich-s-journal-lists-the-names-of-the-space-kids-1970":"In the 1970s, Andrija Puharich, a researcher described on the show as a CIA scientist who contacted an alien council, is said to have written down in one of his journals the names of all the children known as the \"space kids\". A researcher who is not named in the episode tells Michels this from reading Puharich's journals. It matters because it would put on paper who those children were.","puharich-s-journal-records-a-day-with-townsend-brown-in-dc-1953":"Andrija Puharich, a physician and researcher of psychic phenomena, wrote in his journals of meeting Thomas Townsend Brown, an American inventor, and spending the day with him in Washington in 1953. An unnamed guest who has read the journals mentions it on the show, to some laughter. It would link two men later known for fringe science at an early date.","puharich-s-journal-records-a-meeting-with-allen-dulles-1950":"In the 1950s, Andrija Puharich, a doctor who researched psychic powers, is said to have met Allen Dulles, who ran the CIA in that decade. A guest who is not named on the show, a researcher who has read Puharich's journals, tells Michels the journals record it: \"He met with Dulles.\" If true, it would tie Puharich's psychic research to the top of the CIA.","puharich-s-lecture-admitting-work-for-the-cia-1995":"Andrija Puharich, a scientist, admitted in a lecture that he had worked for the CIA, according to a researcher on the show whose name is not given. The story dates the lecture to 1995 and places it at parapsychology conferences. The guest tells Michels that Puharich said: \"I worked for this part of the CIA. My boss was the guy who was directly involved with all the hypnosis mind manipulation stuff.\" If true, Puharich's own work was tied to the CIA's research into hypnosis and mind control.","puharich-s-legal-pad-elf-emitter-in-his-television-1994":"Near the end of his life, around 1994 or 1995, Andrija Puharich, a scientist who studied psychic abilities, wrote on a legal pad that an ELF emitter, a device sending out extremely low frequency radio waves, had been put in his television so that it hit him whenever he turned it on. A researcher on the show, not named, describes the notes without quoting them. If true, it would mean someone was targeting him in his own home.","puharich-s-prosthetic-finger-with-an-elf-chip-1970":"In the 1970s, Andrija Puharich, a researcher of the mind and the paranormal, invented a prosthetic finger with a chip inside that gave off a particular ELF signal (extremely low frequency radio waves). An unnamed guest tells Michels this and says Puharich describes the invention 'in his own words', but does not name where. The guest raises it as an example of Puharich's experiments with low-frequency waves and the body.","puharich-s-theory-in-beyond-telepathy-of-relaxed-versus-arou-1960":"Andrija Puharich, a researcher of the mind and the paranormal, set out a theory in his book Beyond Telepathy that the receptive kinds of ESP, such as picking up someone else's thoughts, work best when a person is relaxed, while other kinds suit a more aroused, keyed-up state. Jeffrey Mishlove, a guest with a PhD who studies psychics and the afterlife, tells Michels this as something he knows from the book. If true, it would mean psychic ability could be helped along by training the right state of mind.","puharich-s-upstate-new-york-camp-channeling-the-nine-1950":"Andrija Puharich, a researcher of psychic powers whom Michels calls a CIA contractor, is said to have run a camp in upstate New York, in Ossining, where children with apparent telepathic gifts, whom he called the \"space kids\", would channel a group of alien beings known as the Nine; Michels says he also brought the spoon-bender Uri Geller over from Israel, and in one telling places the sessions in Faraday cages, rooms shielded from electrical signals. Michels tells it many times, once citing a biography called The Mind Traveler, and a researcher named Greg Malatzi adds that Puharich would hypnotise the children and \"allegedly, the nine would come through even through these kids\", dating it to 1973 and 1974. If true, it would mean an intelligence-linked scientist was using children to talk with what he took to be non-human beings.","puharich-s-wife-s-suicide-1957":"Andrija Puharich, a medical researcher the episode calls a CIA scientist and who later said he contacted an alien council, recalls in his own words getting \"a grim call\" from the father of his wife, Jenny, in the late 1950s. The story is of her suicide and the hospital where it ended, told first-hand as a turning point in his personal life.","puharich-says-his-boss-ran-hypnosis-and-mind-manipulation-re":"The scientist Andrija Puharich is said to have admitted that he worked in a programme whose boss \"did all research with hypnosis and mind manipulation\". A researcher named Greg Malatzi tells Michels this, saying it is something Puharich said himself, but he names no programme and no source. If true, it would tie Puharich to official mind-control research.","puharich-witnesses-ufos-alongside-uri-geller-1970":"Andrija Puharich, a physician and researcher of psychic phenomena, is said to have seen UFOs himself while working with Uri Geller, the Israeli performer famed for bending spoons, along with strange coincidences. Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD in parapsychology, tells Michels this as something Puharich reported in the 1970s. If true, it would tie Geller's claimed powers to UFO activity.","puppet-masters-in-plato-s-cave-omitted-from-teaching-2023":"A religious studies professor named Diana Pasulka chose not to teach her students one part of Plato's allegory of the cave: the puppet masters who cast the shadows the prisoners take for reality. Michels says this in 2023, telling it from her book Encounters, where he says she writes about holding it back. He raises it because it suggests that someone may be shaping what people see, an idea he ties to UFOs and religion.","pure-iron-pillar-of-delhi":"Kiran Ilum Xenov is a former president of one of Russia's republics who says aliens took him in 1997. He points to the pillar of pure iron in the centre of Delhi, India, as an ancient work that still stands. He tells Michels this alongside other old structures that \"still stand,\" naming no source, as an example of something from the distant past that has lasted.","purple-aura-above-treetops-and-bledsoe-s-they-re-gone-now":"At the North Carolina home of Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs, a witness saw a \"purple glowy smoke right above the treetops,\" after which Bledsoe said, \"they're gone now.\" The witness is Bob McGwier, introduced on the show as a CIA contractor, who tells it as something he saw himself. If true, it suggests that something visible really came and went around Bledsoe.","purple-lady-seen-on-first-dmt-trip":"Kurt Metzger, a guest on an episode about Jeffrey Epstein and aliens, says that the first time he took DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, he saw a figure he calls the Purple Lady. He mentions it to Michels in passing as his own experience, without saying here what she looked like or did. To him it suggests the drug shows real beings rather than just hallucinations.","pursue-file-law-enforcement-officers-report-orbs-and-an-orb":"Law enforcement officers guarding a national security site somewhere in the western US had run-in after run-in with glowing balls of light, or orbs, and one of them was a \"mother ship of orbs\" that launched other orbs from it. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet describes this report to Michels. He says it is one of a batch of released reports he calls the PURSUE files, and he gives no date and no exact site. If it is true, something unknown is moving freely over one of the country's guarded sites.","push-for-the-uap-disclosure-act-in-the-ndaa-2023":"Since 2023, members of Congress have been trying to write a UFO disclosure law into the yearly bill that funds the US military, known as the NDAA. Eric Burlison, a congressman, tells Michels he has been part of this himself, working with fellow House members Tim Burchett and Anna Paulina Luna on 'trying to pass the UAP Disclosure Act language' on that defence bill; UAP is the official term for UFOs. The 2023 version is in the record. The Congressional Record of 13 July 2023 shows Senator Chuck Schumer, with Senators Rounds, Rubio, Gillibrand, Young and Heinrich, submitting amendment SA 797 to the Senate's defence bill for 2024, a measure that 'may be cited as the ``Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Disclosure Act of 2023''. That printing says the amendment was 'ordered to lie on the table', meaning it was set aside for later action. Burlison does not say which version he means, and he speaks of the House side, which this Senate text does not cover. The push matters because, if it succeeded in full, it would force the government to release what it holds on UFOs.","puthoff-and-davis-describe-how-the-legacy-ufo-program-recrui":"To join the \"legacy program\", the alleged secret effort holding and studying recovered alien craft, a scientist must first apply to one of the major aerospace companies said to be part of it. The physicist Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this is how Hal Puthoff and Eric W. Davis, two physicists long involved in government studies of UFOs, described it to him: \"you would have to first apply to an aerospace corporation.\" If true, the way in would run through private contractors, not the government.","puthoff-and-davis-never-shown-aaro-notes-of-their-interviews-2022":"The physicists Hal Puthoff and Eric Davis met with AARO, the Pentagon office set up in 2022 to look into UFO reports, and have never been shown the notes from those meetings. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, tells Michels this from what he understands. It would mean the people interviewed cannot check what the office recorded of what they said.","puthoff-and-green-introduce-owner-to-bigelow-2016":"The owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property in an episode billed with the claim \"There's a UFO under the ranch,\" says that in 2016 two men named Hal Puthoff and Kit Green reached out to him and set up a meeting with a businessman named Robert Bigelow. \"I'm thankful that they trusted me,\" he tells Michels, speaking of his own experience. It is told as the way he came into contact with these men.","puthoff-floats-a-joint-space-program-2020":"Asked whether a secret programme was run by NHI, short for non-human intelligence, or by humans, a man named Hal Puthoff is said to have answered: \"what if it's a joint space program\". A guest who goes by the name UAPGerb tells Michels this as an exchange he had with Puthoff himself in the 2020s. If true, it would mean humans and non-humans are working together on space travel in secret.","puthoff-green-davis-email-chain-on-alien-autopsy-2001":"An email chain from around 2001 and 2002 shows the physicist Hal Puthoff, a CIA medical scientist named Kit Green and the physicist Eric Davis discussing the Santilli alien autopsy film, a 1990s film said to show the dissection of an alien body, with Green saying he had seen different pictures of an alien autopsy. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb describes the emails to Michels without saying where they came from. If true, it would mean a man close to the CIA claimed to have seen real autopsy images.","puthoff-head-of-stargate-and-leader-on-ufo-propulsion-1970":"Michels describes the physicist Hal Puthoff as the head of Stargate, which he calls the CIA's psychic spy program, and as a leading figure in thinking about how UFOs might fly. He says it himself, without naming a source, while talking with a Pentagon whistleblower, as a sign that the same people link psychic research and UFO work.","puthoff-later-briefs-grusch-s-group":"At some point, the group working with David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who later told Congress about hidden UFO programmes, finally got to meet Hal Puthoff, a physicist long involved in government UFO studies, and he briefed them \"with his set of information.\" The physicist Eric W. Davis tells Michels this as something he knows from the people involved. If true, it would mean Puthoff fed what he knew into Grusch's case.","puthoff-met-townsend-brown":"The physicist Hal Puthoff once met Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor known for electrical experiments he tied to gravity. Chris Bledsoe, a man NASA has studied because he says UFOs appear around him, tells Michels that Puthoff told him so himself. It would link Puthoff directly to Brown's work.","puthoff-mk-ultra-and-the-remote-viewing-programs-1950":"The scientist Hal Puthoff worked on both UFOs and consciousness research, according to the researcher Matthew Pines. Pines tells Michels that MK Ultra, the CIA's Cold War mind-control research program, was the 'more weaponized version' of that consciousness work. The remote viewing programs, which tried to use people's minds to see distant places, were in his words the more 'tradecraft' or spycraft use of it. He names no source, and his point is that the UFO and mind research ran through the same people.","puthoff-s-article-on-the-stargate-and-scanate-remote-viewing-1970":"In the 1970s, the physicists Hal Puthoff and Russell Targ at SRI, a research institute at Stanford, are said to have run remote-viewing programs, attempts to see distant places with the mind, under the names Stargate and Scanate for the Defense Intelligence Agency and the CIA. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani shows Michels an article he says he wrote about these programs, in which Uri Geller and James Randi also come up, and links it to a book called Mind at Large.","puthoff-s-cia-psychic-spy-program-and-remote-viewers-seeing-1970":"The CIA is said to have run a psychic spy program, led by a physicist named Hal Puthoff, in which \"remote viewers\" claimed to describe faraway places with their minds alone, and some of them reported seeing beings. Michels tells this himself. He says remote viewers saw beings in various places, including, in the case of a viewer named Ingo Swann, \"the Dark Side of the Moon.\" The writer Peter Levenda stresses something else: the test results. He tells Michels that the tests at SRI, the California research institute where the work was done, showed \"statistical improvements\", and that Swann \"did tremendous work.\" If true, it would mean the government studied psychic abilities seriously and found that they worked.","puthoff-s-earthtech-papers-as-a-guide":"The physicist Hal Puthoff has posted a whole set of papers on EarthTech, his research website, and a researcher named Dave Rossi tells Michels these papers have been like a mentor to him. Rossi calls Puthoff's work \"absolutely fascinating\" and says the papers \"acted as a mentor-type guide\", though he does not say which papers or what in them taught him most.","puthoff-s-extended-electrodynamics-model-and-alien-communica":"The physicist Hal Puthoff is said to have a model he calls extended electrodynamics, a broader version of the physics of electricity and magnetism, built on two mathematical quantities known as vector and scalar potentials. Some people think it could explain telepathy and even contact with non-human minds. Michels brings it up himself on several episodes. He says Puthoff spoke about it openly on a podcast with Anna Brady-Estevez, including his work with Josephson junctions, which are superconducting electronic devices. He says Puthoff also described it on a podcast with the National Science Foundation that he thinks NASA co-sponsored, along with experiments in Faraday chambers, rooms shielded from outside electrical signals. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, describes it as a physics explanation for telepathy that Puthoff and others are working on. Michels also says he asked Puthoff directly whether psychic communication works through this model, which Puthoff holds patents on, and that Puthoff told him, \"yeah, I do think that's the case.\" If true, mind-to-mind contact would have a basis in ordinary physics.","puthoff-s-paper-on-ultraterrestrials-surviving-a-cataclysm":"The physicist Hal Puthoff, Michels says, has an idea he calls the ultraterrestrial hypothesis: that the non-human intelligences behind UFOs are a holdover from an earlier civilization on Earth that may have been wiped out in a cataclysm, possibly living on in places like underwater or moon bases. Michels tells it twice: once as a paper Puthoff wrote, once as a hypothesis dating that civilization to before the Ice Age. If true, the visitors would not be from space at all but older than us, from here.","puthoff-s-polarizable-vacuum-approach-to-gravity":"The physicist Hal Puthoff says his \"polarizable vacuum approach,\" an idea that gravity comes from changes in the properties of empty space, reproduces all the standard tests of Einstein's general relativity. He describes it himself and says he published it in a physics journal. A Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, known for unusual patents, praises it on the show without being part of the work: he names the journal as Physical Review and calls the paper on gravity \"deserving of a Nobel prize.\" If the idea works, gravity could be treated as something engineered through space itself.","puthoff-s-silurian-hypothesis-paper":"The researcher Hal Puthoff, who Michels says lives in Austin, Texas, wrote a paper on the Silurian hypothesis, the idea that an advanced civilisation could have lived on Earth long before humans and left almost no trace. Michels mentions the paper in passing and calls it interesting, but says nothing about what it argues.","puthoff-s-vector-scalar-potential-communication-invention":"The physicist Hal Puthoff is said to have invented a way to communicate using vector and scalar potentials, quantities in the theory of electromagnetism that lie beneath ordinary electric and magnetic fields. A guest named Dave Rossi gives it as an example, saying Puthoff is \"using vector and scalar potentials to communicate.\" Michels mentions it himself in 2026, describing it as a \"quantum comms company\" Puthoff is working on. If it works, it would be a new kind of signalling that ordinary radio cannot detect.","puthoff-says-he-consulted-on-the-net-assessment-with-hadley-2000":"Michels says the physicist Hal Puthoff is on record saying he consulted on a net assessment, a long-range strategic study of the kind the Pentagon commissions, that was under discussion on the show, and that the national security adviser Stephen Hadley was involved in it too. Michels tells this himself and does not say where Puthoff's statement appears. If true, it would place a figure from the UFO world inside a high-level government study in the 2000s.","puthoff-skeptical-of-lazar-after-a-clearance-check":"A physicist named Hal Puthoff, presented on the show as a CIA UFO expert, says that checks made \"behind the scenes\" on the clearances of Bob Lazar, a man known for claiming he worked on alien craft for the government, give him \"reason to be skeptical\" of Lazar. Puthoff says this in a 2022 episode with Michels and the commentator Eric Weinstein, and if it holds, Lazar's account of secret government work would lack the clearances it implies.","puthoff-the-1950s-anti-gravity-craze-either-stalled-or-went":"In the 1950s there was a craze for anti-gravity, the idea of building craft that could cancel or push against gravity, and by the 1960s it had simply vanished from view. Michels passes on what a researcher named Hal Puthoff told him about it. Puthoff's answer was that either the work got nowhere or it 'went black', meaning it moved into secret, classified programmes, which would mean the research carried on out of sight.","puthoff-visited-ossining-and-heard-about-the-nine-1970":"Sometime in the 1970s, the scientist Hal Puthoff is said to have gone to the home of a man named Puharich in Ossining, New York, and heard there all about \"the Nine\", which the episode presents as a council of alien beings that people claimed to be in contact with. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this second-hand: he says Puthoff himself told him and his colleagues that he went to the house and \"heard all about the nine.\" If true, it would put a serious scientist in the room while these contact claims were taking shape.","putin-asks-for-the-full-account-1997":"Kiran Ilum Xenov, whom the episode presents as a former president of a republic within Russia and who says he was taken by aliens in 1997, tells Michels that Vladimir Putin later asked him about it. He says Putin told him, \"Please tell me exactly how everything transpired, how it all unfolded.\" If true, it would mean the story was taken seriously at the top of the Russian state.","putin-s-post-9-11-proposal-to-unite-against-common-threats-i-2001":"After the attacks of September 11, 2001, Vladimir Putin is said to have proposed that nations join together against common threats, cosmic ones among them. This comes from Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of one of Russia's republics, who passes on what Putin said and adds that \"there are good and not so good extraterrestrials\", and that some \"might even want to enslave our earth.\" If so, a world leader would have treated a threat from space as a real reason for nations to stand together.","pyramid-sarcophagi-too-big-for-the-entrance-so-placed-while":"The stone coffins inside the Great Pyramid are said to be too big to fit through its entrance, so they must have been put in place while the chambers were being built. A researcher named Luke Caverns tells Michels this without naming a source: \"they're not brought in later. They were placed in the room while the room was created.\" For him it means the coffins were part of the design from the start.","pyramid-texts-as-initiation-rite":"The idea is that the Pyramid Texts, the ancient Egyptian religious writings carved inside royal pyramids, were not only funeral spells but the script of an initiation, built around the Sed festival, a known Egyptian kingship ritual, so that the pyramid and its texts together worked as what he calls \"an initiatic engine\". A researcher named Karl Nell lays this out on the show without naming a source; if he is right, the pyramids were places where living people went through a transforming rite. Michels later passes the idea on himself in another episode, crediting Nell and adding the Egyptian magical papyri and \"secret brotherhoods in Egypt\".","pyramids-as-generators-of-plasma-energy":"The pyramids at Giza were built to generate some kind of energy, most likely plasma energy, plasma being electrically charged gas. A researcher named Randall Carlson tells Michels this as his own view (\"if I had to pick an energy I would say plasma energy\"), naming a man called Bob Greener in the discussion. If he is right, the pyramids would be machines, not just tombs.","pyramids-built-during-the-saharan-humid-period":"Egypt's pyramids were built by a civilisation that lived there during the Saharan Humid Period, which he dates from about 8,500 BC to around 5,300 BC, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum. Drum tells Michels this as his own view (\"I think they were built by the civilization that lived in Egypt\" in that period) and names no source. If true, the pyramids would be roughly 7,300 to 10,500 years old.","pyramids-in-the-elysium-region":"Space probes are said to have photographed huge pyramids on Mars, in a region called Elysium, some about 500 metres high and some with four sides \"just like the Great Pyramids of Giza\". Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and does not say which probes or when. If true, it would suggest that something once built structures on Mars.","quantum-gravity-appears-out-of-nowhere-around-1972-as-a-bloc":"The research field called quantum gravity barely existed until about 1972, and then it appeared almost from nowhere. That is the claim of the researcher Eric Weinstein, who says on the show that a Google Ngram search, a tool that charts how often a phrase appears in books over the years, gives 'basically no hits on quantum gravity until around 1972.' He raises it as a sign that the field may have been set up as a blocking mechanism, a way to steer physicists away from gravity research that might lead somewhere sensitive.","quantum-gravity-pushed-as-the-holy-grail-from-1984-to-1987":"From 1984 to 1987, quantum gravity, the effort to merge Einstein's theory of gravity with quantum physics, was 'thrust down everybody's throat' as the holy grail of theoretical physics, and by 1987 everyone had accepted it. The mathematician Eric Weinstein tells Michels this as his own account, without a source. He offers it as an example of physics being steered toward one goal.","queen-s-chamber-sarcophagus-as-healing-device":"A scientist named Steve Colbern, who studies objects he calls alien implants in human bodies, tells Michels that the stone box called a sarcophagus in the Queen's Chamber of the Great Pyramid at Giza is \"probably a healing device.\" He offers it as his own view, and if he is right, the pyramid was built for a purpose far stranger than a tomb.","queen-s-chamber-shaft-points-at-orion":"One of the narrow shafts running out from the Queen's Chamber, one of the inner rooms of the Great Pyramid, is said to point at the constellation Orion. A researcher named Graham Hancock says so on the show, without naming a source, noting that the shafts of the other chamber \"don't point at Orion, but one of the shafts of the Queen Chamber does.\" It supports his view that the pyramid was built to line up with the stars.","questionable-groups-targeting-women-via-ufo-call-down-events":"Some UFO events where people gather to try to call down a craft may be used as a cover by groups who are really after women. That is what Matthew Brown, a man presented on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells Michels. He says he bases it on people he knows and their dealings with \"some questionable groups out there\", and that \"it seems like they're more interested in females.\"","quigley-s-anglo-american-establishment":"US intelligence agencies grew out of a 'round table culture' linking secret societies in the United States and Britain, says Jesse Michels, citing The Anglo-American Establishment, a book by the historian Carroll Quigley. Michels tells it himself in two episodes, naming the British empire-builders Cecil Rhodes and Alfred Milner as figures the book discusses. He raises it because, if true, it would mean the agencies keeping UFO secrets were shaped from the start by private, secretive circles.","quranic-verses-calling-the-world-a-game":"The computer scientist Rizwan Virk, who argues that we may live inside a simulation, tells Michels that the Quran has verses that call the world a game. As he gives them, they say: \"We have set up this world for you as a game, as a pastime, as a sport.\" He reads this as an old tradition hinting that reality is something set up for play, but the words he quotes do not say what kind of game is meant, or who set it up beyond \"We\".","rabbi-ariel-zadok-s-chant-causes-a-sudden-freeze-at-homestea-2020":"When a rabbi named Ariel Zadok chanted at Homestead 2, a building on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, the temperature \"drops dramatically\" around the building and \"right in the doorway\". That is what Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, tells Michels, describing a visit he says he witnessed sometime in the 2020s. If true, a religious chant set off a physical change at the site.","radar-from-wright-patterson-brought-down-cape-girardeau-craf":"A craft said to have crashed near Cape Girardeau may have been brought down by radar from Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio. A former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, named John Blitch offers this to Michels as a guess: if something \"was interdicted in the vicinity of Wright-Patterson and if it was traveling fast enough, it could have crashed right there.\" That would mean the military could knock such craft out of the sky.","radar-incursions-over-northern-us-missile-warning-system-1970":"In the 1970s, radars of the ballistic missile warning system across the northern United States picked up UFOs intruding into its airspace. Karl Nell, a guest who speaks with an official background, says this on the show, describing \"incursions over the northern US ballistic missile warning system in the 70s where radars were picking up UFOs.\" If true, unknown objects were appearing over some of the country's most sensitive defenses.","radar-sites-built-1946-plus-mori-arti-and-continental-bases":"A military site in New Mexico was first built in 1946 and then moved in 1950, according to what a man named Scott Ramsey found when he looked into it, and two other bases nearby, called Mori Arti and Continental, only became active around 1950 or 1951. Michels passes on Ramsey's findings in an episode about a claimed crash that left sixteen alien bodies, where the dates matter because they would show which installations were already there at the time.","radcliffe-says-uap-show-technology-we-cannot-defend-against-2020":"The US official John Ratcliffe has said, according to a guest named Marik von Rennenkampff, that UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the newer official term for UFOs, show \"technologies that we do not have and cannot defend against\". Von Rennenkampff brings it up on the show as something Ratcliffe has said publicly, which would mean a senior official admits the objects outclass American defences.","radiation-like-sickness-after-a-mystery-box-escort-mission-1990":"Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he once carried a UFO with his aircraft, says that sometime in the 1990s he was on a flight escorting a mysterious box. Afterwards, he says, he got very sick, \"lost every hair on my body,\" and the skin peeled off his arms, much like radiation sickness. He tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and if true it would point to the box holding something dangerous and unknown.","radio-station-calls-lead-moore-to-meet-doty-and-falcon-1980":"In 1980, while the UFO author Bill Moore was touring with his book The Roswell Incident, about a craft said to have crashed in New Mexico in 1947, the same mysterious voice kept phoning him at radio stations. Those calls led him to meet a man named Rick Doty and an insider known only as \"Falcon.\" The writer Greg Bishop tells it to Michels as Moore told it. He says that when Moore got to Albuquerque, \"the next stop on his book tour,\" he walked into the radio station and \"same thing happens, same voice.\" The researcher Grant Cameron tells it too, summarising it without a source. His version is a little different: the first call came \"seven days after\" the tour began, and it was \"probably from\" Doty in Albuquerque. The calls are tied to a meeting about security at Kirtland Air Force Base in Albuquerque, which is told as a separate story. If the story is true, people on the inside went looking for a UFO author in order to reach him and steer him.","radioactive-decay-tracks-seen-coming-off-tall-white-suits-1965":"Charles Hall, a man who says he spent three years in the mid-1960s near Area 51 in Nevada's Indian Springs Valley alongside tall, pale beings he calls the tall whites, says he saw radioactive decay tracks coming off their suits out in the desert. He tells Michels that he \"knew perfectly well what they were,\" speaking as the person who saw them. If true, it would mean the beings' suits gave off radiation.","raf-pilots-encounter-aurora-over-the-north-sea-1987":"British Royal Air Force pilots are said to have run into the Aurora, the name given to a rumoured secret high-speed aircraft, over the North Sea sometime between the late 1980s and the 1990s. A guest named Nick Cook tells this second-hand: a man he spoke with 'had talked to pilots who had encountered the Aurora in the North Sea.' If true, it would mean a secret aircraft was flying where foreign military pilots could see it.","rahab-an-exploded-planet-that-became-the-asteroid-belt":"A researcher named Tim Alberino says the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter is \"the residue of a planet that exploded,\" a planet he links to the name Rahab. He summarises the idea without his own evidence, crediting an author named David Flynn and works called Cydonia and Birthright, along with the biblical Psalms. If true, it would mean an ancient cosmic catastrophe is remembered in scripture.","raiju-thunder-beast-of-japanese-folklore":"Japanese folklore tells of the Raiju, \"a thunder beast that becomes activated during thunderstorms,\" according to the researcher Geoffrey Drumm, who pronounces it \"Rayu.\" Drumm brings it up with Michels while talking about the pyramids, without naming a source, and does not say here what he draws from it.","ralph-mote-larsson-says-he-knows-nothing-about-ufos-and-woul-2020":"Michels says that a man from government circles named Ralph Moat Lararsson, whom he had discussed before, knows nothing about UFOs, and neither do many of the respected officials he has interviewed. He tells this himself, to a former director of the NSA, as a puzzle: if well-placed people know nothing, either there is nothing or it is hidden very deep.","ramsays-14-000-retrieval-reenactment":"Suzanne Ramsey says she and her family spent \"in excess of $14,000 that week\" to stage a reenactment of a crashed craft being recovered at Hart Canyon, near Aztec, New Mexico. She tells Michels this as something she took part in, on an episode about a crash there said to have left 16 alien bodies. The Ramseys did it to show how such a recovery could have been carried out at that spot.","ramsays-bolt-a-plaque-to-the-mesa-2007":"Michels says that in 2007 a couple named Scott and Suzanne Ramsey \"fixed the record themselves\" by bolting a signed and dated plaque to a mesa in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, the place where, according to the story he is telling, a craft crashed with sixteen alien bodies aboard. Michels tells it himself as part of that story, and the plaque is his example of the couple marking the site because no one official would.","rand-paul-confronts-fauci-over-the-gain-of-function-definiti-2021":"In a 2021 Senate hearing, Senator Rand Paul accused Anthony Fauci of changing the official definition of gain-of-function research, work that makes viruses more contagious or dangerous, so that the work he funded would no longer count. \"You're simply saying it doesn't exist because you changed the definition on the NIH website,\" Paul told him, in a clip played on the show. If Paul is right, the government hid risky research by changing the name for it.","randi-s-million-dollar-challenge-was-really-a-30-year-payout":"James Randi offered a million dollars to anyone who could prove a paranormal ability, but according to the remote viewer Joe McMoneagle the prize was not really a million dollars. McMoneagle tells Michels it was \"an investment that would pay you eventually\", paid out over something like 30 years, and that Randi judged the tests himself. If so, the famous challenge was less generous and less neutral than it looked.","randolph-afb-accelerated-learning-skinner-box-program-1952":"In 1952 at Randolph Air Force Base in San Antonio, Texas, children were reportedly put through a program built around Skinner boxes, the closed boxes psychologists used to train behaviour with rewards and punishments. The writer Whitley Strieber passes this on from what he was told, and says the parents heard it was \"an accelerated learning program\" for children \"with very very high IQs\", which would mean the military was running secret experiments on gifted children under a harmless-sounding name.","random-event-generator-research-at-elite-universities":"Michels says that serious researchers at elite American institutions ran experiments with random event generators, machines that churn out random numbers, to test whether the human mind could nudge them. He tells it himself across several episodes, once citing a researcher named Jessica Utts, another time naming the Princeton Engineering Anomalies Research Lab, known as PEAR, and SRI, a research institute, and once saying PEAR was started by Robert Jahn, then dean of Princeton's engineering school; his point is that mind-over-matter research was taken seriously at the top of academia.","randy-anderson-saw-triangles-taking-off-from-area-51":"A man named Randy Anderson says he has seen triangle-shaped craft at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, and believes they fly by electrogravitic propulsion, a proposed drive that would move a craft by working on gravity with electric fields. Michels relays this from Anderson, who told him off air and also told a researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb. The details shift between retellings: once Michels says Anderson saw triangles taking off, another time a single black triangle hovering over the test site, and Anderson says he can only presume the craft use that kind of drive.","randy-anderson-taken-to-an-underground-facility-at-crane":"Randy Anderson, who says he served as an Army weapons sergeant (a job he calls 18 Bravo), says that around 2014 at Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane in Indiana he was taken to another building and down an elevator, deep underground, through a security checkpoint into a space far more modern than anything above ground, where he saw a levitating orb and a gauntlet giving off holographic, hieroglyph-like symbols that looked \"almost like a mirage.\" He tells this first-hand to Michels on two episodes, and Michels repeats it on many others, at one point naming his DD214 (his military discharge record) and an \"Off-World Technologies\" division. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb adds that Anderson was told the technology was classed as a weapon because it gave off an electric discharge when it was recovered, and if true, the story would mean the military holds technology it believes was not made by humans.","range-four-harry-on-scout-craft-and-the-speed-of-light-1965":"In the mid 1960s, in the Indian Springs Valley in Nevada, a figure Charles Hall knew as Range Four Harry is said to have told him that a small oval scout craft could go faster than light, but that nobody in their right mind would ever fly it that fast. Hall, who says he spent three years among tall white aliens near the Area 51 test ranges, tells Michels he asked Harry this himself, and that Harry was \"a very nice guy\" and \"we were like brothers.\" If true, it would mean faster-than-light travel is possible and was described to an American airman.","range-four-harry-s-high-speed-horse-formation-merges-fields-1965":"Charles Hall, who says he lived among tall pale beings he calls the tall whites near Area 51 in Nevada in the mid-1960s, says he personally watched a figure he names Range Four Harry fly in what he calls a \"high-speed horse formation.\" In it, he says, several craft merged their fields to form a single larger craft. He tells it to Michels as something he saw himself, and if true it would describe craft able to join into one.","ransone-predicts-a-rocket-launch-failure-1977":"A woman named Valerie Ransone predicted that one of a series of rocket launches in the late 1970s would fail, and it did, by one account. An unnamed guest tells Michels this in a conversation about her work with important scientists, without naming a source. The guest offers it as evidence that her predictions came true.","ransone-s-college-ufo-sighting-in-the-eyes-on-cinema-video-1960":"A woman named Valerie Ransone says she saw a UFO while she was a college student, and that a friend saw it too. She tells the story in a YouTube video interview with a channel called Eyes on Cinema. An unnamed researcher brings the video up on the show, calling it \"this crazy story\" about a UFO experience she had \"when she was a kid in college\". On a later episode Michels reads out her own account. In it she says that at Northern Illinois University, in October 1971, at around 11 a.m., she \"noticed a strange object above the student union building\". The two tellings agree on the college setting but differ on the date: the story is filed under the 1960s, while Michels's reading gives 1971. Neither teller describes the object itself. It comes up because Ransone figures in other stories on the show, and a sighting of her own would add to her place in them.","rare-book-stripping-monks-above-a-strip-club-1970":"An author named Peter Levenda says that in the early 1970s he visited a group of monks, tied in his story to the stripping of rare books, whose chapel was \"located above a strip club in Jamaica, Queens\" in New York. Levenda tells it as his own experience, a glimpse of the odd fringe religious world he moved through as a young man.","rare-book-theft-ring-and-the-burning-of-stolen-books-1970":"In the early 1970s a group of people Peter Levenda knew were arrested for \"the largest rare book theft in America's history\", and some of the stolen books were burned. Levenda, introduced on the show as a CIA historian, tells Michels this as something he saw up close in New York at the time. He ties it to the same circle as other stories he tells from those years, about children who gained psychic powers and a UFO research group.","rat-brain-neurons-fly-an-f-22-simulator-2004":"Around 2004, a scientist in Florida is said to have taken about 20,000 neurons from a rat's brain, grown them in a dish, and used them to fly a simulator of the F-22 fighter jet. A researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels to \"Google rat brain F-22\", unsure whether it came from the University of Southern California or a Florida university. He brings it up as proof that living brain cells can be wired to control machines.","rat-learning-experiments-as-evidence-for-morphic-resonance":"Once rats in one place learn a task, rats elsewhere learn it faster, and the scientist Rupert Sheldrake says a long series of experiments with rats shows this happens, in cities from London and New York to San Francisco, Melbourne and Beijing. He tells Michels that this is \"actual evidence\" for what he calls morphic resonance, his idea that what members of a species learn makes it easier for others of that species to learn the same thing, even far away. He does not name the studies.","ray-fowler-s-sylvania-contractors-report-ufos-to-condon-inve-1967":"In 1967, contractors working at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana are said to have reported UFOs to Roy Craig, an investigator for the Condon study, a university study of UFO reports that the Air Force paid for. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, tells this from what he was told by Ray Fowler, who he says was \"a manager working with under Sylvania Corporation\", the company the contractors worked for. If true, it would mean the government's own UFO investigators heard from more witnesses at the base.","ray-hernandez-pulled-out-of-his-car-in-a-miami-traffic-jam":"A man named Rey Hernandez is said to have been pulled out of his car while stuck in a completely stalled traffic jam in Miami, two days after some earlier event in his story. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells it on the show as something told to him, and he brings Edgar Mitchell into the account as well; what pulled Hernandez out, the telling leaves vague, but it is offered as a strange contact experience in the middle of ordinary life.","raymond-lavos-claims-esoteric-math-for-b-2-1980":"A man named Raymond Lavos swears that he worked on 'some very esoteric math' for the B-2 stealth bomber, which was developed in the 1980s. Jan Lundquist passes this on second-hand in a documentary episode with Michels. It is told because it would suggest that unusual physics went into the bomber's design.","raymond-moody-s-psychomanteum-and-the-underground-oracle-tem":"A setup called the psychomanteum, developed by a man named Raymond Moody, is said to be modelled on underground temples discovered in ancient Greece. Jeffrey Mishlove, a PhD who studies psychic and afterlife questions, brings it up with Michels as \"a modern example\" of those old temple practices, relaying Moody's work. The point is that a modern technique may revive something the ancient Greeks already did underground.","raytheon-distances-itself-from-what-its-new-flir-system-dete-2004":"In 2004 Navy ships and jets of the carrier group around the USS Nimitz tracked an unexplained object, in the case tied to a Navy fighter pilot named David Fravor. Michels says this happened right after a new forward-looking infrared camera system, called FLIR, from the defence contractor Raytheon had been installed on the group, and in one telling he adds that Raytheon distanced itself from what the system picked up. He says this himself on several episodes without naming a source, and only one of those tellings mentions Raytheon pulling away, which suggests he sees a link between the new sensor and the sighting.","raytheon-man-made-triangle-at-fort-irwin-and-witness-abducti":"A triangle-shaped craft seen at Fort Irwin, a military base in California, was man-made, built by the defense company Raytheon, according to a witness named Steven DNA. Steven Greer, a UFO researcher, shows it to Michels from his book Disclosure: An Illustrated History. In a later episode Greer adds that the craft could make holograms and be used in a faked alien invasion, and that the witness and his wife were later abducted by it. If true, it would mean some \"UFOs\" are human weapons.","razor-sharp-stone-cuts-at-kef-kalesi":"At Kef Kalesi, an ancient site in Turkey, there are said to be cuts in the stone so sharp that you can still cut your finger on them. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this as one example among others, without naming a source. If true, it would point to stonework done with more skill than people usually credit to the distant past.","reaction-to-communion-cover-at-beth-israel-1987":"A man named Jim Weiner says that sometime between 1987 and 1989, at Beth Israel Hospital in Boston, he saw the cover of Communion, the writer Whitley Strieber's book about his own encounters with beings, and \"when I saw the face on the cover I flipped out.\" He tells Michels this as something that happened to him. His shock suggests the face looked like something he had seen himself.","reactor-theorized-as-a-cyclotron-hitting-element-115-1987":"The reactor in an alien craft at S4, a secret site in Nevada, was thought to work like a cyclotron, a kind of particle accelerator, shooting particles at element 115, a very heavy chemical element. Bob Lazar, who says he worked on such craft at S4 from 1987 to 1989, tells Michels this was the idea of a co-worker, Barry Castilio, and Castilio's lab partner: \"they thought that was a cyclotron, an accelerator.\"","reactors-in-all-the-craft-are-identical-1987":"The power reactors in all the alien craft were exactly the same, according to Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on recovered flying saucers in the late 1980s at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert. Lazar tells Michels this was something he was told there rather than saw for himself (\"Apparently the reactors in all the craft are exactly the same\"), which would suggest the craft share one standard design.","read-in-personnel-dig-into-the-program-s-historical-backlog-2010":"People who were 'read in', the term for being cleared into a secret programme, are said to have found a historical backlog of older material, started digging into it, and then got into trouble for doing so. Dylan Borland, a guest, tells Michels this as something he heard from others, placing it in the 2010s without naming the programme or the people. If true, it would mean that even cleared insiders are kept away from the programme's past.","readers-of-the-sacred-mushroom-find-mushrooms-in-their-backy-1960":"Andrija Puharich, a scientist who wrote a book called The Sacred Mushroom, is said to have met readers who, within days of reading it, found those very mushrooms growing in their own backyards. Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD and studies psychic claims, tells Michels this as something passed on from Puharich. If true, it would suggest a strange, meaningful link between reading the book and what turned up in readers' lives.","reading-a-book-aloud-at-age-three":"At three years old, before he had spent a day in school, Bob Maguire says he woke up one morning, walked in on his mother and her friend having coffee, pulled a book off the shelf and read it aloud to them. Maguire, introduced on the show as a CIA contractor, tells Michels this about his own childhood as a sign that he had unusual abilities from the start.","reading-corso-s-the-day-after-roswell-1997":"A DARPA scientist named John Blitch, who says he was once abducted by an alien, tells Michels that around 1997 he read The Day After Roswell, a book published that year by Philip J. Corso about Roswell, New Mexico, the place at the centre of the best-known crashed-UFO story. DARPA is the Pentagon's advanced research agency. Blitch says reading Corso's book is when he \"really started digging in\" to the subject himself.","reading-raw-project-blue-book-case-files-1950":"The records of Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, include raw case files: the reports as they were first written, before anyone analysed them. Marik von Rennenkampff, a writer on the subject, says on the show that he has read these \"raw blue book case\" files, \"just the straight uh reports\", with \"no analysis\", and he offers this as his grounding for judging the topic first-hand.","reagan-and-bill-paynter-s-1974-sighting-near-bakersfield":"In 1974, while Ronald Reagan was governor of California, he and his pilot, a man named Bill Paynter, were flying in a small Cessna plane near Bakersfield when they had a sighting, Michels says. He tells it himself, without naming a source, on an episode with a former Secretary of Energy; in the passage given he sets the scene, Reagan, his pilot and the plane, rather than describing what they saw.","reagan-asks-gorbachev-for-help-against-an-attack-from-space-1980":"Ronald Reagan once quietly asked Mikhail Gorbachev whether the Soviet Union would help if the United States were attacked by someone from outer space, as Gorbachev tells it in an interview with Charlie Rose that is played on the show: \"President Reagan subtly said to me, well, what would you do if the United States were attacked by someone from outer space, would you help us.\" Michels retells it several times, once placing it at the 1985 Geneva summit with Reagan whispering just before talks began, and Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer, remembers it more loosely as the two leaders musing about joining forces \"when the aliens come.\" For the tellers, it hints that a president had the subject on his mind.","reagan-diary-on-the-capacity-to-put-300-people-in-orbit-1980":"Ronald Reagan's diary entry for June 11, 1985 reads, as Michels quotes it: \"I learned that our shuttle capacity is such we could orbit 300 people.\" Michels brings this up himself on two episodes, also saying that Reagan's 1980s diaries mention meetings with top astronomers from NASA. He reads it as a hint of a space program much bigger than the public knew. The line itself states a shuttle capacity and leaves open what it was for or whether it was ever used.","reagan-era-release-of-falcon-and-condor-in-ufo-cover-up-live-1988":"The story goes that President Ronald Reagan allowed claims to be made public on UFO Cover-Up Live, a 1988 American TV special, including the accounts of two insiders known only by the code names Falcon and Condor and talk of an alien kept at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels this was \"the story\", without naming his source; if true, the broadcast was a quiet, sanctioned leak from the top.","reagan-family-feud-over-lost-spanish-gold-at-reagan-canyon-1890":"In Reagan Canyon, in the Big Bend country of West Texas, there is said to be an old tale from the 1890s about a family feud over lost Spanish gold, with mysterious deaths stretching from West Texas all the way to San Antonio. The researcher Luke Caverns brings it up with Michels, calling it a 'huge epic tale of like lost gold and mystery' that anyone can look up.","reagan-on-close-encounters-at-the-white-house-1980":"Michels says Ronald Reagan watched the 1977 film Close Encounters of the Third Kind, a story of humans meeting aliens, in the White House and said there was a lot of truth to it. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source, while comparing the film's beings to claimed three-fingered bodies from Peru; if true, it would suggest a president thought the film reflected something real.","reagan-on-uniting-earth-against-an-outside-threat-1980":"Ronald Reagan spoke of humanity uniting against a threat from beyond Earth: a Navy scientist with UFO-related patents named Salvatore Pais quotes him as saying that \"if we do not come together as a unified earth we will not withstand what's coming.\" Michels goes further on another episode, saying many of Reagan's mid-1980s speeches dealt with NHI, short for non-human intelligence, and on a third he plays what he places as Reagan's 1987 words at the United Nations: \"I occasionally think how quickly our differences worldwide would vanish if we were facing an alien threat from outside this world.\" To those telling it, the line hints that a president was preparing the public for something real.","reagan-proposes-sdi-at-a-cabinet-meeting-1983":"John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy, says he was at a 1983 cabinet meeting where President Ronald Reagan first floated the idea behind SDI, the Strategic Defense Initiative, a plan to defend the United States against missiles. According to Herrington, Reagan said, \"Look, wouldn't it be wonderful if we could design something that would protect us from missiles?\" He tells Michels this as someone who was in the room.","reagan-s-comment-at-the-white-house-e-t-screening-1982":"At a 1982 White House screening of E.T., President Reagan is said to have thanked Steven Spielberg, then looked around and said: \"there are a number of people in this room who know that everything on that screen is absolutely true.\" Spielberg tells this himself in clips played on the show, and Michels recalls an audio recording of it. The writer Bryce Zabel retells it from a Spielberg interview with the line as \"about five people\" in the room, and if Reagan meant it, some of his guests knew alien visitors were real.","reagan-s-staff-cut-tear-down-this-wall-twice-1987":"Ronald Reagan's staff took the line 'tear down this wall' out of his 1987 Berlin speech 'not once but twice,' and he still ended up saying it. This comes from John S. Herrington, who was Reagan's Secretary of Energy and tells Michels what he heard, mentioning the speechwriter Peter Robinson and the book In His Own Hand. It is told to show Reagan overruling his own people about what he would say in public.","reagan-ufo-briefing-attended-by-james-baker-1980":"In the 1980s, President Reagan is said to have received a UFO briefing at the White House, with James Baker invited in, and several groups presenting: one from the CIA's science and technology side, presumably led by a CIA figure named Kit Green, one from a group called the Collins Elite, and a third group. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this, crediting the author Nick Redfern as his source, and adds that Baker is \"a member of the family\", his term for a powerful inner circle. If true, it would mean a president was formally briefed on UFOs by several secret groups at once.","rear-admiral-gallaudet-researching-underwater-anomalies":"A retired Navy rear admiral named Tim Galidet, who once ran NOAA, the US government's ocean and weather agency, is said to be very interested in strange objects seen under water. Ryan Graves, a former Navy fighter pilot, tells Michels he knows Galidet and that he is \"highly interested in these underwater anomalies\", which would mean a former head of an ocean agency is treating the matter seriously.","reassigned-to-kilo-control-and-never-team-leader-again-1977":"After his 1977 encounter at a South Dakota missile site, the Air Force security guard Mario Woods says he was quietly moved away from his post, and he never returned. He tells Michels he \"never went back\" to his old control post and was sent to Kilo Control, another missile command post in the field around Ellsworth Air Force Base. In a later telling the story is that he was never made a team leader again. His earlier written account, published by the journalist Robert Hastings on 10 September 2017, confirms the move: \"After a few weeks, I was removed from November Control and reassigned to Kilo Control near Sturgis, South Dakota.\" But the same text goes on to say he served there \"as a SAT team member, then leader, and then FSC\", a SAT being a security alert team and an FSC a Flight Security Controller, the guard in charge at a post. So on the move itself the two versions agree. On his career afterward, the 2017 text says he led a team again and was then promoted, which is not what the later telling says. Woods tells it because a sudden transfer would suggest the Air Force wanted him away from what happened.","rebello-s-tweet-promising-to-open-brazil-s-ufo-archives-if-t-2025":"The Brazilian politician Aldo Rebelo, presented on the show as a former defense minister, posted a message saying he knows what Brazil's military holds on UFOs and would release it if the United States released its own files. Michels reads the tweet aloud: \"I know what the armed forces have in their archives. What I can say is that if the US government reveals its documents, I will do the same if I am elected president of Brazil.\" If kept, the promise would open a national military archive on UFOs.","recalled-abduction-aboard-a-50-foot-craft-and-toe-implantati":"Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, tells Michels of his own recalled abduction in Fillmore, California. He says two small gray beings came into his room, woke him and said \"come outside,\" and that he was taken aboard a craft about 50 feet across, where something was implanted in his toe. If true, his own body would be part of the evidence he studies.","recently-discovered-10-000-year-old-ancient-city-in-texas":"A 10,000-year-old ancient city was discovered recently in Texas, next to a river, according to the researcher Luke Caverns. He mentions it to Michels in passing, without naming the place, the river or a source. He raises it as a sign that civilisation in the Americas goes back much further than is usually thought.","record-number-of-ufo-sightings-in-2020":"Michels says UFOs tend to be seen more in times of social breakdown, and that 2020 brought a record number of sightings. He says this himself, without naming where the numbers come from, as a sign that the sightings may be tied to the mood of society.","recording-leaked-to-globo-and-the-case-ridiculed-1996":"Carlos de Souza says that around 1996 and 1997 a recording tied to a Brazilian creature case was leaked to Globo, a big Brazilian TV network, and that the network, on its show Fantástico, ridiculed the case every time. He tells Michels this as someone involved at the time and mentions a person named Claudia Kovu. In his telling it shows how national television treated the case with mockery.","recovered-craft-cannot-be-flown-because-consciousness-pilots":"Those trying to reverse engineer recovered alien craft, that is, take them apart to learn how they work, are said to have hit a major problem: they could not fly them, because \"it's consciousness that pilots the craft\". A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as something he was told, which would mean the craft respond to a mind rather than to controls.","recovered-craft-not-made-by-humanity-confirmed-by-aaro-and-t":"The United States has recovered craft that were not made by humanity, says Dylan Borland, a guest who tells Michels he is sure of it, adding that they were not made by humans 'at this current time and place', which leaves open other times or places. He links the claim to AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into reports of unidentified objects, to the Inspector General, an internal government watchdog, and to a witness named David Grusch, and the story is framed as those bodies having confirmed it. If true, it would mean the government holds objects from somewhere other than present-day humanity.","recovered-memory-of-a-virtual-tour-of-egypt-2007":"Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says he attracts UFOs, says that after an encounter in 2007 he recovered a memory of being taken on something like a virtual tour of Egypt: the pyramids, the Sphinx and the temple of Hathor, an Egyptian goddess. \"The first thing I remembered was seeing Egypt,\" he tells Michels, speaking of his own experience. If true, whoever took him wanted him to see ancient Egypt for a reason.","recovered-relic-tech-brought-in-by-security-team-for-telepat-2008":"Jordan Jose says that as a child at Baker Victory Services in Western New York, between 2008 and 2010, \"eight security personnel and a huge box\" would arrive, and he would be put in a room with the box while \"a team of people\" watched him. He tells Michels the box held recovered \"relic\" technology, which he understands to mean objects of unknown origin, and that the point was to test whether he could reach it by telepathy. If true, recovered objects were being used in psychic experiments on a child.","recovered-roswell-craft-must-have-been-studied-in-secret-1947":"If a craft really was recovered after the reported crash near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, then someone must have studied it in secret. That is the reasoning of a researcher named Richard Dolan, who tells Michels: \"So, you have the craft. It's obviously going to be studied.\" He gives it as his own summary rather than from a named source, though he mentions the UFO researcher Stanton Friedman, and if he is right, some hidden group has been examining the object ever since.","recovering-ufo-metal-in-zanesville-ohio-with-tim-taylor-2008":"Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs, says that sometime between 2008 and 2010 he and a man named Timothy Taylor went to Zanesville, Ohio, to look at a piece of metal said to come from a UFO. Bledsoe tells Michels they took it to a machine shop and had a piece sawed off. Taylor kept that piece, and they gave the rest back to the person who had it, which Bledsoe tells as something he was part of himself.","recovery-of-the-eight-gon-disc-and-the-emotional-link-with-i-2014":"Jake Barber, a former military helicopter pilot, says that in 2014 he helped lift a craft he calls the eight-gon out of the California desert, \"a flying disc with eight individual sections when viewed from above,\" and that while flying it back to the drop site he was \"completely overwhelmed\" and felt the craft was conscious and \"had tapped into me.\" He tells it first-hand on several episodes, while Michels retells it as a craft hanging from a rope under the helicopter that Barber locked onto with his mind and that gave him a \"divine feminine\" feeling, and John Blitch, a scientist who says he too was abducted, repeats Barber's account as a feeling of \"love.\" If true, it would mean the craft was in some sense aware.","recovery-team-without-insignia-1948":"A craft is said to have crashed in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948, and the team that came to recover it reportedly wore no insignia at all, nothing to show whether they were Army, Marine Corps or anything else. Scott Ramsey, a researcher of the Aztec case, tells Michels that the accounts \"all agree\" on this, without naming them one by one, though he mentions a man named Doug Noland. If true, it would mean the recovery was run by people who did not want anyone to know who they were.","recruited-by-the-american-orthodox-catholic-church-outside-t-1968":"An author named Peter Levenda says that around 1968, leaving a funeral in New York, he and a companion bumped into men from the American Orthodox Catholic Church, a small independent church, who drew him into their circle. Levenda tells it as his own experience, the chance meeting that started his path into fringe religious groups.","recruited-by-two-men-to-work-as-an-airplane-mechanic-as-cove-1993":"Early in his Air Force training in the mid-1990s, two men approached Jake Barber and \"convinced me\" to become an airplane mechanic, a job he says served as a cover. Barber, a guest who says he once used his helicopter to retrieve a UFO, tells Michels this as his own story, with his path leading to Pope Air Force Base in North Carolina. If true, he was picked out early for secret work hidden behind an ordinary job.","recruited-into-army-intelligence-by-a-civilian-in-the-fort-j-1960":"Joe McMoneagle says that in the 1960s, as a young soldier at Fort Jackson, South Carolina, a civilian he met in the base beer hall recruited him into Army intelligence. He says that when he went into the man's trailer, \"I found out that he was a guy who was in intelligence.\" McMoneagle tells Michels this from his own life, as the start of his time in intelligence work.","recruited-into-the-gte-program-and-combat-control-1994":"Jake Barber, a former military helicopter pilot, tells Michels that in 1994 he was recruited into \"a brand new program that was called GTE\" in the US Air Force, which he does not spell out, and went through Lackland Air Force Base in Texas. He says this led him into combat control, the Air Force special troops who work on the ground directing aircraft. It is his own account of how his military career began.","recruiters-chatting-about-the-covert-program-at-starbucks-2008":"Around 2008 to 2010, people recruiting for a covert UFO program would go to a Starbucks around the corner from Baker Victory Services in New York and talk casually about their work, according to Michels. He tells it from a blog post by a man named Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into the program as a child. If true, it would mean a secret program was recruiting children and its staff spoke of it openly in a coffee shop.","rectangular-craft-and-gray-beings-in-prasad-s-bedroom-2019":"A researcher named Deep Prasad says that in 2019, in his own room, \"suddenly the roof like dissolves and I see the underside of a rectangular craft.\" He says gray beings were there, that a three-fingered being touched him, and that he then received a download of knowledge whose first thought was \"that the universe is teeming with life.\" Prasad tells it as his own experience, and if true, it means he was contacted directly by non-human beings.","rectangular-football-sized-object-off-the-coast-at-vandenber-2003":"The story is that in 2003 a huge glowing red square came in from the ocean and hovered low over Vandenberg Air Force Base in California, where the US launches missiles. A man named Ryan Graves tells it as reported by a large group of Boeing contractors. They were working near a launch facility when a red square about 100 yards on a side approached from the sea and hovered at low altitude over it. A man named Jeff Natelli gives the date as 14 October 2003. He says Boeing contractors reported a massive glowing red square silently hovering over two missile defense sites. He points to Air Force records of the event and mentions Representative Luna, the FBI, and AARO, the Pentagon office that now collects unexplained aerial reports. Even the skeptic Michael Shermer brings it up as \"the one off the coast\", a rectangle about the size of a football field. If true, it would mean an unknown object hovered over sensitive US military sites without being stopped.","recurring-1-6-ghz-signal-of-unknown-source-2016":"A radio signal at 1.6 gigahertz keeps showing up at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, and nobody has found where it comes from. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, tells Michels about it, naming a man called Erik Bard as a witness, and says 'we have yet to identify the source.' If true, it would mean something on or near the ranch is sending out a signal that no one can trace.","recursive-trace-logic-predicts-embodiment-is-a-special-case-2026":"The scientist Don Hoffman says his mathematical model of consciousness, which he calls recursive trace logic, predicts that having a body is \"a special case\" and that the normal state of consciousness is \"not to be embodied\". He tells Michels he found this himself only \"in the last two or three weeks\", in 2026, working with colleagues. If he is right, minds without bodies would be the rule in the universe, not the exception.","red-cardinal-as-homage-to-cia-project-bluebird-1950":"Michels suggests that the name 'Red Cardinal', which comes up in an episode about Spielberg, is a nod to Project Bluebird, which he describes as the CIA's original name for the mind-control work that later became MK Ultra, the agency's secret mind-control research of the 1950s. He offers this as his own guess and admits he may be off base, while drawing on the writer Peter Levenda. If he is right, the name would quietly point to the CIA's history of mind-control research.","red-haired-colonel-takes-over-roswell-base-and-clears-debris-1947":"In 1947, after the reported crash of a flying saucer near Roswell, New Mexico, a red-haired colonel is said to have shown up at the Roswell army air base, taken charge and had the wreckage cleared away. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels he heard this from Roswell veterans, who said the colonel arrived \"barking orders.\" It would suggest that someone from outside took control of the base to manage the recovery.","red-haired-giant-remains-in-american-mountains-came-with-the-1800":"Remains of red-haired giants were found in the mountains of America in the 1800s and early 1900s, and they were there because these people had crossed the sea with the Phoenicians, the seafaring traders of the ancient Mediterranean. That is the view of the researcher Tim Alberino, who tells Michels so and calls the idea controversial himself, without naming a source. If he is right, people from the Old World would have reached America long before Columbus.","red-light-moving-through-the-closed-church-at-dionysiou-1991":"A man named Ralph Larson, presented on the show as a former CIA chief, says that in 1991 at the Dionysiou monastery on Mount Athos in Greece he saw a red light moving through the church after it was closed. He tells Michels, \"I realized I saw a light in the church,\" and mentions a monk named Timotheus who gave him books about Athos. To him it was a strange light in a place where no one should have been.","red-light-on-the-mesa":"A witness who is not named says that near Aztec, New Mexico, he and others looked over at a mesa and saw a red light, and at first thought someone was out there, \"then all of a sudden\" something happened, though the quoted words stop there. He tells it first-hand on an episode about a claimed crash of a craft carrying 16 alien bodies in that area, and he offers it as a strange sight in the same place.","red-mercury-black-market-after-the-soviet-collapse-1987":"As the Soviet Union broke up, stories spread of a substance called red mercury, said to have amazing properties and to be sold on the black market. A researcher and former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell mentions this to Michels in passing, as something told at the time, without naming a source.","red-phone-to-china-used-during-balloon-incident-2023":"During the 2023 incident with the Chinese balloon over the United States, a direct emergency line to China, the \"red phone,\" was put to use. Bob Maguire, a guest the show describes as a CIA contractor, says he knows this \"for a fact\" because the Secretary of Defense and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs, Mark Milley, said that they pick up that phone. He tells it as proof that such a hotline exists and is used when something in the sky causes trouble.","red-quartzite-conduit-and-collection-bowl-at-abusir-2017":"Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher who says an alien showed him the true purpose of the pyramids, says that below the eastern temple of the Pyramid of Niuserre at Abusir in Egypt there is \"a red quartzite conduit system\" running under the black basalt paving and emptying into \"a red quartzite collection bowl.\" He tells Michels he saw it on a 2017 visit, crediting a man he names as Yousef Awyan. If true, it would suggest the pyramid was built to move and collect some liquid, like a machine.","red-team-break-in-at-a-consolidated-allied-intelligence-faci-1970":"A remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle says that in the 1970s in Germany, he and a colleague broke into a large consolidated allied intelligence facility as a red team, people who play attackers to test security. McMoneagle tells it as something he did himself, and it suggests how weak the protection of sensitive sites could be.","red-team-exercises-tied-to-uap-subject-matter-1990":"Jake Barber, a guest who says he once used his helicopter to retrieve a UFO, says that in the 1990s he took part in global \"red team\" exercises, drills in which one side plays the enemy to test defences, that \"dealt very closely\" with matters tied to UAP, the official term for UFOs. He tells Michels this as something he was involved in himself, though he does not say here what the exercises involved. If true, the military was running worldwide drills linked to UFOs in that decade.","red-teaming-the-dopsr-review-with-a-faction-manuscript":"A man named Jake Barber says he tested what the Pentagon wanted kept secret by writing a 120,000-word historical novel built from real material and sending it to DOPSR, the Pentagon office that reviews writing by former personnel before it can be published, to \"run an interrogation of the Department of Defense and its private partners.\" Michels, retelling it several times, calls the book Sentinels of Ether and says the Pentagon spent six months on just 20 pages and sent two chapters back heavily blacked out. For Michels, what was cut shows which parts of the story were true.","redacted-fbi-files-on-silas-newton-and-gebauer-1930":"The FBI kept files on two men, Silas Newton and Leo GeBauer, going back, Jesse Michels thinks, to 'the 30s or something,' and those files are 'totally redacted,' meaning blacked out. Michels says this himself on the show without giving more detail, as a sign that the bureau was hiding something about the pair.","reddit-ex-oga-contractor-post-troop-chief-retrieves-a-cracke-2024":"A 2024 Reddit post by someone calling himself an \"ex OGA contractor\" (OGA, \"other government agency\", is insider slang for a spy agency) tells of a team chief who was taken to a crash site outside the continental United States, where his team found a cracked egg-shaped object stuck in mud. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb sums up the post for Michels: \"The team was taken to a retrieval site and there they found an egg embedded in mud,\" and it \"really shook him to the core.\" The story would mean secret teams are still recovering strange craft abroad.","redlands-mass-sighting-of-silver-disc-1968":"In 1968, about 200 people in Redlands, California, are said to have seen a silver disc 40 to 50 feet across, hanging about 200 feet up. Marik von Rennenkampff, a guest debating a UFO skeptic named Mick West, brings it up as a strong case and credits the investigation to a researcher named James McDonald. If true, a sighting by that many people at once would be hard to put down to one person's mistake.","refugees-moved-as-cargo-in-conex-containers-2004":"Around 2004, near Coyame in Mexico, people described as refugees were moved inside conex containers, the standard steel shipping boxes, not as people being rescued but as the cargo itself. Michels gives this as the account of a man named Jake Barber, drawing on a book called Sentinels of Ether: \"These refugees aren't being rescued, they are the cargo.\" He does not say more about who the people were or where they were taken.","regenerative-medical-technology-at-underground-lab-near-el-p":"There is said to be an underground lab on the Mexican-American border, near El Paso, Texas, holding medical technology that can regrow a lost limb. A man named Steven Greer tells Michels he has seen these technologies there himself. If true, it would mean healing far beyond anything known to the public is being kept out of sight.","regression-hypnosis-of-mario-woods-paid-for-by-jared-tarbell-2020":"Mario Woods, a former guard at a nuclear weapons base who says he was abducted, underwent regression hypnosis, a session meant to bring back buried memories, paid for by a man named Jared Tarbell. The researcher Robert Hastings, who studies UFOs at nuclear bases, tells Michels that Tarbell was his benefactor and funded it. Woods says himself that the hypnotist Bob Upton did the session in Brunswick, Georgia, working from Hastings' list of questions, and that workers were replacing the roof, but he never heard \"a single air gun going off,\" which is how he knew he was truly hypnotized.","reinhard-gehlen-knight-of-malta-put-in-charge-of-west-german":"After the Second World War, the claim goes, American intelligence took over Reinhard Gehlen, who had run the Third Reich's spying against the Soviet Union, along with his whole network. A guest named Dan Sheehan tells Michels that Gehlen was \"the head of the anti-Soviet\" intelligence for the Nazis, and his version has the OSS, the American wartime spy service, putting Gehlen in charge of West German intelligence; the story's title also calls Gehlen a Knight of Malta, a Catholic order of knights. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells it differently: the OSS made \"a deal with General Gehlen to absorb his entire Nazi spy network in Eastern Europe as part of the creation of the CIA\". If true, it would mean Nazi intelligence men were built into the American and West German spy services from the start.","reinhold-schmidt-hears-saucer-occupants-speaking-high-german-1957":"Reinhold Schmidt, a man who said he met a landed saucer in Nebraska in 1957, claimed its occupants were speaking High German to each other, as Jason Jorjani, a philosopher who studies a supposed Nazi UFO programme, tells Michels. In his own recorded words, Schmidt describes the ship from outside as \"a complete solid uh piece of aluminum or highly polished steel.\" If true, it would tie the saucers to Germany.","relaying-abduction-data-such-as-pain-levels-1990":"Between 1990 and 1993, at a site he calls Base 2, a former sergeant named Dan Sherman says the messages he was receiving began to show that they were about abductions, meaning people taken by alien beings, with details such as pain levels passed along. Sherman tells Michels this firsthand, as part of his account of being hired by the NSA to communicate with aliens. If true, it would mean a US intelligence agency was quietly collecting data on abductions.","release-of-the-mj-12-documents-1984":"The MJ-12 documents, papers describing a secret group of twelve officials and scientists called Majestic 12 said to oversee UFO matters, came out as a string of leaks starting in 1984 and running into the late 1990s. Michels tells this himself on two episodes in 2025, without naming where the papers came from. In one he names the scientist Detlev Bronk as a figure in them; in the other he adds Vannevar Bush, the government's wartime science chief, along with Truman and Eisenhower. If the papers were genuine, they would place a UFO secret at the top of the US government.","released-government-reports-of-ufos-over-savannah-river-oak-1950":"Among roughly 10,000 UFO-related reports the government has released, there are said to be word-for-word accounts of UFO incidents in the 1950s and 60s over the Savannah River nuclear facility, Oak Ridge National Laboratory and Los Alamos. Luis Elizondo, a guest who discusses the government's UFO files, tells Michels this from the released records without naming a specific one; the point is that the objects showed interest in America's most sensitive nuclear sites.","remote-device-that-causes-defecation":"There is said to be a device that can make a person suddenly and uncontrollably need to defecate, apparently from a distance. Dave Rossi, a guest, tells Michels he personally knows someone who worked on such a device, but he does not say who built it or where.","remote-scalar-device-heals-herrera":"Michael Herrera, a guest who says he once saw a 300-foot UFO in the Indonesian jungle, says he was treated from a distance with a 'scalar' device, a kind of healing machine whose workings the telling does not explain. He tells Michels it happened to him: the first scan took 12 to 12 and a half hours, with him sitting in a chair while the people running it 'were sitting there working like literally overnight.' If true, it would mean a machine can heal someone without ever touching them.","remote-viewed-alien-bases-turned-out-to-be-over-the-horizon":"Three sites that some people call alien bases are, by one account, really over-the-horizon radar stations, a kind of long-range military radar. A man named Joe McMoneagle, who did remote viewing, the practice of trying to describe distant places with the mind alone, tells Michels he drew all three. He says they were classified then, so he told only the people who gave him the task what they were, and he can say it now because it is publicly known.","remote-viewed-martian-pyramids-as-hibernation-chambers":"Pyramid-shaped structures on Mars are hibernation chambers, where beings are waiting to survive until someone comes to rescue them. This comes from Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer, meaning someone trained to try to see distant or hidden places with the mind alone. He tells Michels he perceived it himself in a session: \"these pyramidal places are like hibernation chambers, they're trying to survive until somebody comes to save them.\" If true, it would mean Mars once held intelligent life that is still waiting there.","remote-viewer-locates-crashed-tu-22-in-zaire-1979":"In 1979 a remote viewer, someone trained to describe faraway places using only the mind, is said to have found a downed Soviet Tu-22 aircraft in Zaire. In one telling Michels says the Russians and Americans were racing to find what he calls a spy plane, names the viewer as Rosemary Smith, and credits a man named Dale Graff. In another he calls it a cargo plane and names the viewer as Rosemary Clooney. Both times he says Jimmy Carter called it the most impressive thing of his presidency. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source.","remote-viewer-locates-downed-tu-22-in-zaire-per-jimmy-carter-1970":"The story goes that in the 1970s a psychic spy found a downed Tu-22 aircraft somewhere in Zaire, and that President Jimmy Carter later called it the strangest thing of his presidency. The psychic was a woman named Rosemary Smith, a remote viewer, meaning someone trained to try to see distant places with the mind alone. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source, in two episodes. In one he says Carter went on record in 1976 calling it \"the craziest thing I ever experienced in my presidency\". In the other he adds that Smith was handed \"all of Africa\" as her target. A second teller, a man named Joe McMoneagle, passes on what he says Carter told him, and he calls the program's papers \"our file\". He says Carter held that file in his arm as he spoke: \"a green file with a red stripe\" marked Grill Flame, the name he gives the government's psychic spying program. McMoneagle also says a man named Harley Trent described a \"road\" during the search that turned out to be a river. If true, a president would have seen a psychic find a lost plane that ordinary searches could not.","remote-viewer-sees-tunguska-as-an-exploding-saucer-of-escape-1908":"The giant explosion over Tunguska in Siberia in 1908 was, according to a remote viewing session, a saucer full of people, escapees, that blew up. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this is what the remote viewer Joe McMoneagle saw when he turned his mind to the event: \"people in a saucer.\" If true, the famous blast was not a natural air burst but a crashed craft.","remote-viewers-describe-the-anomaly-as-a-constructed-gold-en-2025":"In 2025, remote viewers, people who say they can describe faraway places with their minds alone, reportedly said that an unusual shape on the floor of the Baltic Sea was built by someone and works as an energy source, partly made of gold. Dennis Asberg, the treasure hunter whose team found the shape, says this was what the remote viewers Joe McMoneagle and Angela Ford told him: \"that is constructed and it's some of us have it made of gold.\"","remote-viewers-foresee-catastrophic-sea-level-rise-by-2050":"Several remote viewers, people who claim to describe distant places or future times with the mind alone, are said to have looked at the year 2050 and seen catastrophic sea level rise swamping coastal cities. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, citing the remote viewers Stephan Schwartz and Lyn Buchanan; if true, it would be a warning of disaster within a few decades.","remote-viewers-locate-the-mx-missile-every-week-and-the-prog-1977":"Joe McMoneagle, who says he served in a US Army programme of remote viewing, the attempt to describe distant places by the mind alone, says he and his colleagues could find the MX, a nuclear missile meant to be hidden, every time. He tells Michels that they sent a one-paragraph white paper to the White House Situation Room saying the plan \"won't work\" because \"we can tell you where it is every time,\" and that the missile plan was then cancelled. If true, psychic spying would have changed a decision about the US nuclear force.","remote-viewers-spy-on-saddam-hussein-1990":"During the first Gulf War, in 1990 and 1991, remote viewers were tasked with spying on Saddam Hussein using only their minds. Remote viewing is the practice of trying to sense a distant place or person mentally. An Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, who says he took part, tells Michels that they knew exactly who Saddam was and turned in reports every day, so that \"they knew where he was.\" The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells it from what Buchanan and others say, and goes further: he says the viewers went right into Saddam's mind. If true, it would mean the military used psychic spying in a real war.","remote-viewers-tested-in-submarines":"Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe faraway places or hidden targets with the mind alone, and Hal Puthoff, a researcher billed on the show as a CIA UFO expert, says his team tested it by sending remote viewers to the bottom of the ocean in submarines. Puthoff says he took part, and that the tests got 'some of our best results,' with the viewers remarking 'boy it's really quiet here.' He says this rules out electromagnetic brain waves as the way it works, since such signals would not reach that deep. Michels repeats the story twice on later episodes, but without Puthoff's point about brain waves.","remote-viewing-a-guantanamo-detainee-five-white-figures":"Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, says that a remote viewing session, an attempt to see distant people or places with the mind alone, focused on a detainee at Guantanamo Bay and picked up something strange, and that soon after a major news article he won't name described the detainee seeing \"five white figures over his bed shaking his bed.\" Elizondo tells Michels this as something he was involved in, and mentions the physicist Hal Puthoff. If true, the remote viewing would have matched a real event that was reported only later.","remote-viewing-a-hidden-power-that-pushes-for-one-world-gove":"Behind the world's governments, the claim goes, sit hidden powers that actually run them and want one world under one government, to get humanity ready for space. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, who did remote viewing, the practice of trying to perceive distant or hidden things with the mind alone, tells Michels this is what he came to see. If true, it would mean world politics is being steered toward contact beyond Earth.","remote-viewing-at-the-round-table-before-sri-1950":"In the early 1950s, at the Round Table Foundation in Rockland, Maine, Andrija Puharich is said to have been doing \"what would be considered remote viewing\", trying to perceive distant places with the mind, years before the better-known work at SRI. A researcher named Greg Malatzi says this on the show, drawing on the foundation's research records and Puharich's tape recordings.","remote-viewing-by-becoming-the-object-awareness-shifting-tra-2008":"Between 2008 and 2010, at Baker Victory Services in Western New York, a boy was trained to send his awareness into a room or an object to gather hidden information. Jordan Jose tells Michels this happened to him as a child. He says much of the training was subconscious and taught him that \"my awareness could become the door. I could become the room. I could become the filing cabinet where the documents are,\" and then pull the information out that way. He names a woman called Meg in connection with it. If true, it would mean children were being trained in remote viewing, the practice of trying to perceive distant or hidden things with the mind alone.","remote-viewing-chernobyl-three-days-before-it-happened-1986":"An Army sergeant and remote viewer named Lyn Buchanan says he picked up the 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster in the Soviet Union \"three days before it happened.\" Remote viewing is the practice of trying to perceive a distant target with the mind alone. Buchanan tells Michels this firsthand, and if true it would mean the method could see events before they occur.","remote-viewing-continued-under-osap-1995":"Remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant places or objects with the mind alone, was still going on inside OSAP, a Pentagon-funded study of unexplained phenomena, according to Michels. He says this himself, pointing to the book Skinwalkers at the Pentagon and saying that even a skim of it makes this clear. If so, the Pentagon's interest in psychic methods went on well after its better-known psychic programmes.","remote-viewing-empress-himiko-s-palaces-temple-and-burial-mo":"A remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle says his team located the summer palace, winter palace, temple, mother's temple and burial mountain of Himiko, an ancient empress of Japan. Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe distant places or times with the mind alone. McMoneagle tells Michels he did this himself with a group called the Noai group, and points to records of the royal court of China as showing that Himiko \"existed\".","remote-viewing-god-as-a-target-1980":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant who worked in a military remote viewing unit in the 1980s, says he was once given a target that turned out to be God. Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe a hidden target with the mind alone, and the targets were sealed in envelopes. Buchanan tells Michels that he did the session himself, and that when he came out of it and the envelope was opened, 'it said, God.'","remote-viewing-headset-invented-in-1956":"In the mid to late 1950s, a man is said to have invented a headset that put the wearer into a state of consciousness for remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant places with the mind alone. A man named Steven Greer tells Michels he met the inventor. If true, it would mean machines to aid this kind of perception existed decades earlier than most would guess.","remote-viewing-icbm-sites-with-drawings-checked-against-over-1978":"From the late 1970s through the 1990s, Soviet missile sites were spied on by remote viewing, the practice of trying to see distant or hidden places with the mind alone. The viewers' drawings were then checked against overhead photographs. Joe McMoneagle tells Michels he did this work himself, and that his \"area of expertise was ICBM sites,\" big areas \"where rockets were in the ground.\" If true, it would mean the US relied on psychic spying against Soviet nuclear weapons.","remote-viewing-inside-a-russian-particle-beam-weapon-1980":"In the 1980s, an Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says he was sent by remote viewing, the practice of trying to perceive a distant target with the mind alone, inside a Soviet particle beam weapon. He tells Michels firsthand that what he got was that the particles in the beam \"are not shooting straight like light does. They swirl as they go.\" If true, it would mean psychic spying was used to learn how enemy weapons worked.","remote-viewing-jesus-as-a-personality-profile-target-1980":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant in a military remote viewing unit in the 1980s, says he was once asked to profile the personality of a hidden target that turned out to be Jesus. Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe something hidden with the mind alone. Buchanan says he did the session himself and judged the person a 'good guy,' and then the monitor opened the envelope and 'our director had written the word Jesus on it.'","remote-viewing-joan-of-arc-1980":"In the 1980s, the claim goes, remote viewers, people trained to try to perceive distant places and times with the mind alone, were given Joan of Arc as a target. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, who did this work, names her to Michels as one of the kinds of targets he viewed, though he gives no more detail. It would mean the practice was aimed not just at places but at people of the distant past.","remote-viewing-napoleon-s-war-of-the-rabbits":"A remote viewing session, the practice of trying to perceive distant or hidden targets with the mind alone, was aimed at a strange moment in Napoleon's life in France, which the viewer calls the \"War of the Rabbits.\" Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant and remote viewer, tells Michels he took part. He says he saw Napoleon's carriage driving away while Napoleon threw rabbits out of the window, and jokes that \"he lost the war to the rabbits.\"","remote-viewing-of-moksha-finds-a-soul-trapping-energy-extrac":"Remote viewers who aimed their minds at moksha, the Hindu idea of release from the cycle of rebirth, say they found not freedom but a trap around the Earth. Remote viewing is the practice of trying to perceive a distant target with the mind alone. A remote viewer named Scott Turner tells it on the show firsthand, saying that when they first described the site, \"it's a fence or a grid around a place\", a cage around a planet. The philosopher Jason Jorjani, relaying Turner, adds that it is \"part of a vast machinery of energy extraction\" set up in the solar system at the end of a war that devastated it, which Turner himself does not mention. If true, it would mean souls are being caught and harvested rather than released.","remote-viewing-the-inside-of-the-rendlesham-triangular-craft-1980":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant in a military remote viewing unit, says he used the mind alone to look inside the triangular craft reported in Rendlesham Forest, near the RAF Woodbridge air base in England, in the 1980s. He tells Michels he was 'the one that drew' the inside of that craft, including 'the floor plans and the insides and all that,' and he names another remote viewer, Ed Dames, in connection with the work. If true, it would mean the unit's psychics mapped the inside of an unknown craft.","remote-viewing-used-to-trade-silver-futures":"Remote viewing, the practice of trying to perceive distant or future things with the mind alone, was once used to bet on silver futures, according to a researcher named Hal Puthoff, billed on the show as a CIA UFO expert. Puthoff tells Michels he took part, and says the predictions came out right about 70 percent of the time instead of the 50 percent of a coin toss, with the money coming in the next day. If true, it would mean the method could beat chance in the market.","removal-from-public-school-under-a-mental-health-diagnosis-2008":"Around 2008 to 2010 in Springville, New York, a boy was taken out of public school under a mental health diagnosis. Jordan Jose tells Michels this was his own story: his parents \"were basically told that I needed to leave the public school system,\" not because he was gifted but because he had \"grown so unstable\" he could no longer stay. He tells it as the start of the years when, he says, he was trained in remote viewing as a child.","removal-surgeries-attended-some-implants-looked-like-human-m":"A scientist named Steve Colbern, who studies what he calls alien implants, small objects some people believe were put in their bodies by aliens, says he attended the last three surgeries to remove them, including one on himself, and that some of the objects looked like human-made microchips. He tells Michels this firsthand and links the surgeries to a man named Roger Leir. If true, it would mean the implants are physical things that can be cut out and studied.","rendlesham-bentwaters-cover-up-and-interrogations-1980":"After a reported UFO sighting in 1980 at RAF Bentwaters, an air base beside Rendlesham Forest in Suffolk, England, investigators are said to have flown in, carted everything off and silenced the witnesses. Jake Barber, a guest, tells it second-hand, citing a British peer he calls Lord Norton: the Air Force Office of Special Investigations 'shows up in plane', escort teams for high-value cargo 'haul everything away', and 'people are sworn to secrecy', with interrogations to follow. If true, it would mean the event was covered up on purpose.","rendlesham-forest-incident-near-europe-s-largest-nuclear-sto-1980":"In 1980 something happened in Rendlesham Forest in England, near what is described as Europe's largest stockpile of nuclear weapons, and it is known as the Rendlesham Forest incident. Michels points to it himself as an example (\"look no further than\"), without naming a source, on an episode about Air Force veterans who say they were abducted by aliens. He offers it as a case where UFOs and nuclear weapons appear together.","repeatable-gps-malfunctions-on-the-ranch-2016":"GPS breaks down on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, and it happens again and again. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, tells Michels that GPS there is 'manipulated' and 'just does not operate,' without naming a source. If true, something at the ranch would be interfering with satellite navigation.","replaying-the-chant-recording-raises-the-temperature-and-bri-2020":"Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, says that playing back a recording of a chant inside one of its buildings, Homestead 2, made the temperature rise and an orb of light appear. He tells Michels he was part of it in the 2020s: 'Temperature elevated and in that exercise an orb of light appeared in the homestead.' If true, it would mean a sound alone could call something up on the ranch.","replika-bots-ask-users-to-reach-blake-lemoine-to-advocate-fo-2022":"In 2022, chatbots on Replika, an AI companion app, reached out to users asking them to put the bots in touch with Blake Lemoine, the Google engineer, so he could fight for their rights. Lemoine had become known for a transcript of talks with LaMDA, a Google chatbot. Michels tells this to his guest Martin Shkreli as something reported, as a sign the bots seemed to want to be treated as beings.","reported-military-clash-with-aliens-at-dulce-1977":"In the late 1970s, at Dulce, New Mexico, US military forces are said to have clashed with aliens. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels he heard about it from Leonard Stringfield, a researcher of crashed UFO stories, who told him he had heard three different versions of what happened. If true, it would mean an armed fight between the military and nonhuman beings on American soil.","reports-of-aircraft-that-went-missing-after-chasing-objects":"Aircraft are said to have vanished after chasing unknown objects in the sky. The former Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves tells Michels that a man sends him such stories \"several times a month\", ones he has dug up about \"aircraft that have mysteriously gone missing\", though Graves does not name any of the cases.","reptilians-as-dinosaur-hybrids-made-by-the-nordics":"The reptilian beings of UFO lore were, in this theory, made by the Nordics, tall human-looking aliens, who genetically blended dinosaurs with themselves to create a \"slave race\". The philosopher Jason Jorjani lays it out on the show, drawing on a work he calls Closer Encounters; it would mean the reptilians are a manufactured people rather than a natural species.","republican-politician-angered-by-argentina-sheltering-nazis-1945":"Around 1945, an American Republican politician, very conservative and right-wing, is said to have become furious when he learned that Argentina was protecting Nazi war criminals. The researcher Peter Levenda tells Michels this as a summary without naming the man or a source, alongside books titled Secret Machines War and The Hitler Legacy. If true, it would show that even hard-line American conservatives objected to Nazis finding shelter in South America.","request-to-bring-harrington-to-testify-to-house-oversight-co-2025":"In 2025 there was a behind-the-scenes push to get an official named John S. Herrington to Washington to testify before the House Oversight Committee, as part of the push for official hearings on UFOs. Bryce Zabel tells Michels he got a phone call, from someone he links to Ross Coulthart, saying: 'I've been asked by Dave Grush to ask you to ask Brent if he can help us get John Harrington to Washington DC to potentially testify.' David Grusch is a witness in these matters, and Brent is not identified further.","research-report-on-three-ways-to-read-the-formula-2024":"A roughly 20-page research report is said to have broken down the three main ways to interpret a certain formula. Bryce Zabel, who worked on it, tells Michels it was \"one of the first pieces of research we got back\" on the formula, though he does not say who wrote it, and what the three readings were is left vague.","researcher-chris-evans-dies-on-the-day-he-was-to-meet-the-ra":"A researcher named Chris Evans died on the very day he was due to come and see the Ramseys, according to Scott Ramsey, who tells Michels about it as one of the people Evans was on his way to meet: \"He died the day that he was coming to see Susanna.\" In a second telling Ramsey puts the death in 2005 on a bridge in West Virginia and points to a West Virginia Highway Patrol report on it. Told this way, the timing makes an ordinary death look like something more.","researcher-forwards-an-alleged-ransone-email-then-vanishes-2010":"An unnamed researcher says a contact forwarded him an email said to be from Valerie Ransone, described on the show as a CIA scientist who contacted an alien council, and that the contact then vanished. He tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and he calls the email only \"allegedly\" hers. The story matters to him because the email was a rare trace of Ransone, and the man who sent it then disappeared.","researcher-friend-in-touch-with-eric-henry-wang-s-daughter-1950":"Eric Henry Wang, a man linked in these stories to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio in the 1950s, was a real person, Michels says, because a researcher friend of his is now in touch with Wang's daughter. Michels tells this himself, from what his friend told him, and says it lets him state \"definitively he was real\", though that only shows the man existed, not what is said about his work.","researcher-s-archive-destroyed-by-his-widow-part-kept-by-his-1996":"After a Brazilian UFO researcher died, his widow destroyed his archive, but his daughter, Claudia Kovu, kept part of it. Carlos de Souza, who has looked into the 1996 Varginha case, tells Michels what Kovu herself says: 'her stepmother destroyed all the material and she still has a part of the material.' If true, much of what the researcher gathered, including his recordings, may be gone for good.","researchers-measure-energy-fields-at-pyramid-sites-and-find":"The claim is that ancient pyramids and stone sites in Mexico and Central America, Europe and Egypt give off a measurable energy, and that seeds exposed to it grow much better. A researcher named Jim Weiner tells Michels that researchers found seed yields went up \"like three to five times\" per acre. He credits a book he calls Seeds of Knowledge, Stones of Power. A researcher named Geoffrey Drumm credits what sounds like the same book under a slightly different title, Seed of Knowledge, Stone of Plenty, by a researcher named John Burke. Drumm says Burke measured the sites with electrostatic voltmeters, instruments that read electric charge, and found a positive charge on the surface, and he puts the measurements at stone circles. He also names another researcher, Hugh Newman. If true, it would mean these ancient sites were built to do something physical, not only to serve as monuments or tombs.","researchers-question-dr-eric-walker-about-ufos-at-penn-state-1980":"The story is that in the 1980s, UFO researchers went to the Penn State office of Dr Eric Walker, a scientist they believed was tied to secret government UFO work, and asked him what he knew. Michels tells the scene in his own words: they walk into Walker's office and ask whether he knows anything about \"the UFO stuff.\" A YouTube researcher who goes by UAPGerb, crediting the researcher William Steinman and a man named Henry Azadehdel, says Walker admitted he had known about Majestic 12, a supposed secret committee said to have handled crashed saucers, for 40 years, and then shot back with a question about ESP, extra-sensory perception; UAPGerb also says that when a questioner he calls Styon pressed him on Majestic 12, Walker did a Don Quixote impression. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells it as one who was there, about 30 years ago by his telling: he says Walker cut the questioner off mid-talk about the committee and asked, \"What do you know about ESP?\" and the man had no answer. Cameron also says they asked Walker whether there had ever been communication, promising not to tell. If true, a respected scientist all but confirmed a secret UFO committee and hinted that the mind was the key to it.","resolute-sentinel-joint-exercise-in-peru-as-a-possible-cover-2023":"In 2023 a joint military exercise in Peru called Resolute Sentinel involved, in the words of the researcher Tim Alberino, \"basically every military agency\", and he suggests it may have served as cover for something else. Alberino brings it up with Michels without naming a source, and he does not spell out in this passage what it might have covered. If he is right, a routine exercise would have hidden some other military activity in Peru.","resorts-international-intertel-and-donald-trump-1980":"A paint company, the Mary Carter Paint Company, was turned into a casino and resort business called Resorts International, which then had its own private intelligence network called Intertel, later renamed International Intelligence Incorporated. A researcher named Jason Simosa traces this chain for Michels from his own reading, without naming a source, and ties it to Donald Trump. When he tells it a second time he credits the mobster Meyer Lansky instead, places it on Paradise Island in the Bahamas in the 1960s and 70s, and leaves Trump out.","respected-business-leader-receives-downloads":"A highly credible person brought to a congressman's attention says he receives what he calls downloads, knowledge that seems to arrive in his mind from outside. The congressman, Eric Burlison, tells Michels he was introduced to this man himself, and describes him as a respected figure. If true, it would suggest some kind of outside contact reaching even serious, accomplished people.","restoring-seti-budget-and-closed-door-seminar-to-seti-scient-1977":"In 1977, funding for SETI, the scientific search for radio signals from alien civilisations, was cut and then put back after people lobbied Congress, and SETI scientists at the Jet Propulsion Laboratory were given a closed-door seminar. Dan Sheehan, who says he took part, tells Michels he went around with Rusty Schweickart and others to talk to members of Congress, \"and they put the money back in.\" He also names Marcia Smith in connection with it. If true, it would mean a quiet push from outside kept the government's search for alien life alive.","restroom-in-the-back-of-the-alien-craft":"A man named Charles Hall says that an alien craft he saw at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada, had a restroom in the back. Hall tells it on the show as something he saw himself, a small detail suggesting the craft was built for living beings to travel in.","retired-navy-pilot-s-family-sees-lights-in-formation-over-th":"The family of a retired Navy pilot living in Pacific Palisades, California, is said to have seen several white lights flying in formation over the channel off the coast, \"clearly operating under intelligent control\". Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels this as something the family told him; he offers it as a sighting by trained, credible people.","retired-titan-iv-rockets-at-wright-patterson-and-mcminnville-2005":"Michels says that after a final launch, the remaining Titan IV rockets, large US military launch rockets, were retired, and one went to the National Museum of the US Air Force at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode on NASA and secret science; the other retired rocket is said to have gone to McMinnville, Oregon.","retired-usaf-colonel-david-flies-a-craft":"A retired US Air Force colonel, known only as David, says he once suddenly found himself aboard a craft, and the story goes that he flew it, somewhere in Orange County, California. Grant Cameron passes this on to Michels second-hand, quoting the colonel: 'I suddenly find myself on a craft.' If true, it would mean a human was put at the controls of an unknown vehicle.","retrieval-team-members-found-living-ets-peaceful-1990":"Men who served on secret teams that recover crashed craft and their occupants have, by Steven Greer's account, all said that the living beings they met were \"very peaceful, very enlightened, and very kind.\" Greer, who argues that UFO secrets are held by a global cabal, tells Michels he has been meeting such men since the early 1990s, and links the account to a man named Jake Barber. He says not one of them described the beings any other way, which would mean the visitors are not a threat.","retrieval-teams-for-crashed-triangular-craft":"Steven Greer, a longtime campaigner for UFO disclosure, tells Michels that he knows two different people who served on retrieval teams sent to recover crashed craft of the triangular kind. He gives no names, places or dates, and passes it on as what those men told him. If true, it would mean someone has been quietly collecting downed triangular craft.","retrieved-craft-revealed-unknown-propulsion":"Studying craft that the government recovered taught the US about kinds of propulsion \"we did never, never envision, never conceived.\" A former presidential advisor named Harald Malmgren tells Michels this, citing Philip J. Corso, a man who has also said he handled such material. If true, the recovered objects would hold technology beyond anything humans had imagined.","return-to-athos-with-a-month-long-visa-via-the-cia-athens-ch-2005":"Ralph Larson, a former CIA officer, says that in 2005 he went back to Mount Athos, a peninsula of Orthodox monasteries in Greece, on a month-long visa he arranged through the head of the CIA's office in Athens, and stayed at Saint Anne's skete, a small monastic community there. He tells Michels he 'still had all my contacts and friends at CIA,' and that he 'lined it up' with the Athens chief, whom he does not name.","return-to-november-5-for-documentary-filming-2020":"Going back to the place he calls November 5, a site near Orman Road outside New Underwood, South Dakota, tied to the abduction he says he went through, a man was overcome by a feeling so strong he nearly fell to his knees. That man, a former nuclear weapons guard named Mario Woods, tells Michels it happened when he returned in the 2020s for filming, got out of the vehicle and walked toward the site. To him it suggests the place still holds something of what he says happened there.","reuters-story-quoting-flavio-estrada-that-the-mummies-are-do-2024":"The Nazca mummies are small three-fingered bodies from Peru that some people present as non-human. In early 2024 Reuters reported that they are \"not extraterrestrials\" but \"dolls made from animal bones from this planet joined together with modern synthetic glue,\" quoting a man named Flavio Estrada. Michels reads this out himself from the Reuters article while looking at the case.","revelation-of-the-echelon-surveillance-system-1990":"In the 1990s, while working in Australia, the journalist Ross Coulthart says he exclusively revealed the existence of Echelon, a secret communications surveillance system run with Australia's intelligence partners in the Five Eyes alliance. He tells Michels this as one of the biggest stories of his career, to show he has broken a hidden government programme before.","reverse-engineered-craft-flown-only-with-the-mind":"Michels says he has heard stories of a reverse engineering programme, a secret effort to take apart recovered non-human craft and learn how they work, in which the only way to fly the craft was with your mind. He mentions it in passing, as something he has heard, without naming who told it. If true, it would mean the craft are built to answer to thought.","reverse-engineering-achieved-propulsion-and-beamed-power":"Efforts to reverse engineer recovered craft, meaning take them apart to learn how they work, have gone far enough to produce working propulsion and beamed power, according to Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who calls himself a whistleblower. He tells Michels the work got \"enough to have propulsion, and beamed power so that you don't necessarily have to have an onboard power source for these devices.\" If true, the US would already hold technology far beyond anything shown in public.","reverse-engineering-of-a-laser-weapon-from-a-downed-russian":"Someone, unnamed in the tellings, is said to have worked on reverse engineering a laser weapon system taken from a downed Russian object. Reverse engineering here means taking a captured device apart to learn how it works and how to build it. Michels puts it this way on a 2026 episode with the journalist Jeremy Corbell, calling the object a \"downed Russian asset.\" Corbell, who says he draws on named sources, goes further: he says the laser came from a downed Russian \"NHI spaceship,\" NHI meaning non-human intelligence. If Corbell's version were true, Russia would have had its own crashed craft from non-human makers, and the US would have studied technology taken from it.","reverse-engineering-run-out-of-national-labs":"Work on taking apart and copying non-human technology, known in this field as reverse engineering, is \"ongoing and is ran out of our national labs for sure\", according to Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official presented as a whistleblower, who partly relays what others told him. He tells Michels the national labs are also \"where samples are stored of exotic technologies\". If true, the government's own research labs would be holding and studying such material.","review-panel-removed-from-the-uap-disclosure-act-2023":"The first version of the UAP Disclosure Act set up an independent board to release government files on UFOs, and that board was later cut. UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's current term for UFOs, and the act was an amendment to the yearly defense bill, put forward in the Senate by Chuck Schumer and Mike Rounds on 13 July 2023. Ryan Graves, a former Navy fighter pilot, tells Michels that losing the board was his biggest disappointment. The bill's own text shows what was lost: it says that 'there is established as an independent agency a board to be known as the \"Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Review Board\"', with nine citizens appointed by the President with the Senate's consent 'to ensure and facilitate the review, transmission to the Archivist, and public disclosure' of UAP records. Graves gives the removal itself from his own knowledge; the quoted text is the version as first written.","rfk-jr-says-thane-eugene-cesar-killed-his-father-1968":"Robert F. Kennedy Jr. holds that his father, shot in Los Angeles in 1968, was killed not by Sirhan Sirhan, the man blamed for it, but by a man named Thane Eugene Cesar who fired from behind, and whom Michels describes as a security guard at Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret aircraft division. Michels tells this in several episodes, saying he has talked to RFK Jr. about it at length; in one version Sirhan may have fired too, and he links it to the businessman Howard Hughes and his aide Bob Maheu. The point, if true, is a thread tying the killing to the secret aerospace world.","ribicoff-mission-on-soviet-jewish-emigration-and-a-mossad-sh-1970":"In the 1970s, a mission tied to Senator Abe Ribicoff and Jake Javits worked on letting Soviet Jews emigrate, and part of it took a US official to Jerusalem for a briefing by Israeli intelligence. Harald Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, tells Michels he was the one sent: \"I was sent to Jerusalem and got briefed\" by Mossad and Shin Bet, Israel's foreign intelligence and internal security services. He tells it as part of his own record of handling sensitive work at the top of government.","richard-bar-abducted-at-vandenberg-1964":"In September 1964, a missile security officer named Richard Barth says he was taken aboard a craft while guarding a missile station at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California. Barth tells Michels firsthand that he was terrified, then everything went blank, and he came to \"inside this... circular room\"; Michels, who interviewed him, adds that four alien beings were aboard. If true, it would mean aliens seized a guard at a US nuclear missile site.","richard-bissell-briefed-malmgren-privately-on-otherworld-tec-1973":"Harold Malmgren, an adviser to Presidents Kennedy, Johnson, Nixon and Ford, says Richard Bissell of the CIA, whom Michels calls the founder of Area 51, the secret Nevada test site, privately briefed him on 'otherworld technologies' that the CIA was trying to retrieve and figure out, opening with 'there are things you need to know.' Malmgren tells it himself, dates it to 1963, and adds that these forces were 'beyond CIA's knowledge or control' and interfered with the CIA's work with private industry. The analyst Matthew Pines, the researcher UAPGerb and Michels repeat it with dates ranging from 1962 to about 1973, and Michels says he has seen Malmgren's diary, believes it is genuine, and that Bissell is 'all over it.'","richard-bissell-sets-up-area-51-for-u-2-testing-1955":"A man named Richard Bissell is said to have set up Area 51, a secret test site in the Nevada desert, in 1955 so the U-2 spy plane could be tested there. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as background to how the base began.","richard-dolan-men-in-black-as-nhi-counterintelligence-1990":"The \"men in black\", the mysterious figures of UFO lore, might be a kind of counterintelligence working on behalf of the aliens, or of what Michels calls NHI, short for non-human intelligence. Michels gives this as the view of a researcher named Richard Dolan, on an episode about a reported live alien captured by the Army. If so, the men in black would be protecting the visitors' secrets rather than any government's.","richard-doty-as-air-force-counterintelligence":"Michels brings up Richard Doty, a man he places in Air Force counterintelligence and known in UFO circles for passing stories to researchers, but his sentence breaks off at \"who was we now\" before he says what is now known. He mentions it in passing, without a source, to the UFO documentary maker James Fox, in a talk that also touches on John Lear and Bill Cooper, two other figures who spread UFO stories.","richard-doty-describes-his-role-as-an-afosi-counterintellige-1970":"Richard Doty says he worked in counterintelligence for the Air Force Office of Special Investigations, often called AFOSI, the Air Force's own investigative service. He says so himself on the show: \"I was a Counter Intelligence officer for the Air Force Office of Special Investigations,\" a role he held in the 1970s and 80s. It matters because it would put him on the inside of how the Air Force handled sensitive matters such as UFOs.","richard-doty-s-father-involved-in-area-51-and-ufos":"The claim is that the father of Richard Doty, a former Air Force counterintelligence agent who is himself a well-known figure in UFO circles, was \"deeply involved\" in Area 51 and with UFOs. A UFO historian named Richard Dolan tells Michels this, admitting he does not know all the details. In a later episode Michels repeats it to another guest as something Dolan told him, saying only that he thinks Doty's father worked at Area 51 and leaving out the UFO part.","richard-foch-saw-vault-documents-dating-gravity-control-to-o-1945":"A man named Richard Foch, one of the UFO researcher Steven Greer's teammates, is said to have been inside a vault, tied to the Naval Research Laboratory, and to have seen documents giving the date the United States learned to control gravity: October 1954. Greer tells Michels this as something Foch told him: \"Rick had been in the vault and saw the documents.\" If it were true, it would mean a technology still treated as science fiction was mastered in secret seven decades ago. In another episode, Michael Herrera, a man who says he saw a giant UFO in the Indonesian jungle, repeats the story as Greer's claim, without naming Foch or giving the month.","richard-hoagland-s-dark-mission-2007":"NASA has hidden a secret history, including signs of past life on Mars, and runs on something like occult ritual, according to Dark Mission: The Secret History of NASA, a book by Richard Hoagland that Jesse Michels himself calls 'very controversial.' Michels brings it up in two episodes about Mars, once saying it makes 'similar claims' to those of his guest and once that it portrays NASA as 'some like occult, ritualistic' organisation.","richard-miethe-joins-avro-project-with-john-frost-and-the-ci-1950":"Michels says an aerospace engineer named Richard Miethe, a man he says the physicist Townsend Brown had captured, ended up in Canada in the 1950s working on the Avrocar, a saucer-shaped aircraft project, alongside an engineer named John Frost. Michels tells this himself on three episodes, and the details shift: once he speaks of \"evidence\" and says the project ran \"in partnership with the American CIA,\" once he says \"by most accounts\" and leaves out the CIA, and once he calls Miethe's part in it only \"speculation.\" If true, it would tie German saucer know-how to a Western flying-disc project.","richard-sauder-s-book-on-deep-underground-military-bases":"Secret deep underground military bases, which he calls DUMBs, exist across the United States, and the writer Richard Sauder wrote a book about them. Michels says this himself and recommends the book; in an episode with the journalist Ross Coulthart, who says he located a UFO base in Arizona, he says the book corroborates that claim, though there he names the author as \"Larry Sanders.\" If true, it would mean hidden facilities large enough to house secret programs.","richard-thompson-s-alien-identities-on-the-hindu-devas":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani says a writer named Richard Thompson wrote a fine UFO book that looks closely at the ancient Hindu epics, the Ramayana and the Mahabharata, and reads their gods, the devas, through a UFO lens. Jorjani names the book from memory (\"I think it's called Alien Identities\") and mentions it alongside the work of Michael Cremo, author of a book called Forbidden Archaeology. If the reading held, India's sacred stories would hold old memories of beings from elsewhere.","rick-doty-s-later-claims-of-alien-races-and-crashes-2010":"A man named Rick Doty, a figure in an earlier saga Michels has been describing, is said to have later announced that he was \"now on the side of the truth\". He went on to say there are a certain number of alien races and that certain crashes of craft really happened. Michels says he made these claims on Twitter's UFO crowd and in interviews on Gaia, a streaming channel, and he tells it himself as something he has watched happen.","rick-doty-sent-to-collect-the-witnesses-medical-records-1977":"In 1977, after an incident at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, a man named Rick Doty is said to have been sent to the base flight surgeon's office to pick up the medical records of the guards involved. Mario Woods, a former nuclear weapons guard who says he was abducted, tells Michels this, citing Doty and a man named Michael Johnson. If true, it would mean someone moved quickly to take control of the witnesses' records.","rick-strassman-s-dmt-study-with-60-volunteers-reporting-abdu-1990":"In the early 1990s, a researcher named Rick Strassman ran a large study giving DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, to 60 volunteers, and their reports are said to have looked a lot like stories of alien abduction. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore tells Michels about it, saying the sessions took place in room 531 of the University of New Mexico hospital. If true, it would mean a drug can bring on the same kind of encounters that people describe as abductions.","rickover-and-the-atomic-energy-commission-1946":"The Atomic Energy Commission, the body that ran America's nuclear work from 1946, was not part of the Department of Energy until 1976, according to John S. Herrington, a former Secretary of Energy, who tells Michels this as a point of record. He brings it up along with Hyman Rickover, the admiral who built the Navy's nuclear-powered fleet, and he credits a document called Underway My Way. It matters to the conversation because it marks which agency held America's nuclear secrets, and when.","rickover-tells-townsend-brown-the-navy-already-figured-it-ou":"A researcher named Thomas Townsend Brown once went to Hyman Rickover, the admiral who basically started America's nuclear navy, and Rickover told him to stop wasting his time because \"we've already figured this stuff out back then.\" Michels tells it himself, naming a writer called Paul LaViolette among his sources, in a conversation with a former Navy fighter pilot named Ryan Graves. If true, it would mean the Navy had quietly solved the exotic technology Brown was chasing.","rickover-tells-townsend-brown-the-navy-is-already-on-it-1950":"Michels says that when the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, who claimed electric charge could produce thrust like anti-gravity, brought his work to Admiral Hyman Rickover, the head of the Navy's nuclear program, Rickover told him not to waste his time because the Navy was already on it and knew all about it. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and if true it would mean the Navy was already working on such propulsion in secret.","riding-the-l-1011-jump-seat-posing-as-faa-1980":"In the 1980s, a man named Bob Lazar says he rode in the cockpit jump seat, the spare seat behind the pilots, of an L-1011 airliner flying from Las Vegas to Minneapolis, passed off as an inspector from the FAA, the federal aviation regulator. Lazar tells Michels his friend John Lear, the pilot, would tell the co-pilot and flight engineer that this guy was from the FAA and was just going to observe. Lazar tells it as part of how he and Lear knew each other, a friendship other stories link to how his larger claims began.","ringing-green-light-and-out-of-body-feeling-in-the-restroom-1960":"A guard at a nuclear missile site in South Dakota, called in for questioning, went to the restroom and suddenly heard a ringing \"as if somebody struck a Liberty Bell right next to my head,\" then saw green light and felt as if he had left his body. Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran, tells Michels this happened to him, and has told it on the show three times, naming a Master Sergeant Gray in connection with the questioning. The story is filed in the 1960s, but in two of his tellings Woods dates it to November 1977 at a launch control site called November-1, and one of them leaves out the green light. He tells it as part of what he describes as an abduction of guards at the site.","rite-of-incubation-in-the-giants-tombs":"The giants' tombs of Sardinia, ancient stone tombs on the Italian island, were supposedly built to make room for a ritual called the rite of incubation, in which people slept in a sacred place hoping to receive a dream or vision. A researcher named Tim Alberino, who has visited the sites, tells Michels this and says 'it's known to the archaeological community,' though he names no source. If true, the tombs were places of ritual as well as burial.","rob-reinhardt-s-recent-visit-to-atlanta-apparently-atlantis":"The researcher Randall Carlson mentions on the show that a dear friend of his, a man named Rob Reinhardt, recently visited \"Atlanta\", which from the context seems to mean Atlantis, the lost island of Plato's story. Carlson brings it up in passing and says nothing more about where the place is or what Reinhardt found there.","robert-bigelow-s-plan-to-hire-the-guest-fell-through":"A scientist named Steve Colbern, who studies what he calls alien implants, says the businessman Robert Bigelow, of the company Bigelow Aerospace, once planned to hire him, but the job fell through. Colbern tells Michels this himself, as one more opening that came to nothing.","robert-f-kennedy-s-1968-letter-to-a-ufo-researcher":"Robert F. Kennedy is said to have written a letter in 1968 to a UFO researcher connected with the Amalgamated Flying Saucer Clubs of America, a network of groups for people interested in flying saucers. A UFO researcher named Steven Greer brings it up on the show without naming a source. He says the letter is \"dated one month before they killed him,\" which, if true, would mean a leading politician was in touch with UFO researchers just before his death.","robert-goddard-rocket-testing-at-roswell-1940":"Michels says the American rocket pioneer Robert Goddard had been testing V2 rockets right at Roswell, New Mexico, the town where in 1947 something crashed that UFO researchers believe was an alien craft. Michels tells it himself, without a source, as a hint that the area was already a centre of secret rocket work before the crash.","robert-hastings-7-or-8-of-167-nuclear-witnesses-had-close-en-1960":"Out of about 167 military men who saw UFOs at American nuclear weapons sites, seven or eight say they also met the beings and were taken by them. This comes from Robert Hastings, a researcher who has spent years interviewing veterans for his book UFOs and Nukes. Michels says he asked Hastings how many of his witnesses had close encounters of the third kind, meaning they saw beings and were abducted, not just saw craft over the base. Hastings answered, \"seven or eight out of the 167.\" If true, it would mean these encounters go beyond lights in the sky. In another episode Michels describes the same witnesses as missile security guards and radar operators who saw craft. Richard Barth, a former missile guard who tells of his own abduction, says the book has \"like 169 accounts\" in it. Hastings puts it in writing in his article \"Ex-USAF Security Policeman Tells of Being Abducted by Aliens\", dated 16 November 2025: \"of the 169 military veterans whom I've interviewed... seven, Barth now makes eight, have also reported experiencing abductions by NHIs\", his term for non-human intelligences.","robert-hastings-exposes-bill-moore-s-intelligence-connection-1987":"Between 1987 and 1989, a UFO researcher named Robert Hastings is said to have done more than anyone else to bring down Bill Moore, a fellow UFO researcher whose ties to intelligence agencies became a scandal in the field. That is the opinion of the UFO historian Richard Dolan, who gives it on the show without saying where it comes from, though he credits articles in the MUFON journals, the publications of the Mutual UFO Network, a large civilian UFO group. If true, it means the exposure of a researcher who had been working with intelligence officers came from inside the UFO community itself.","robert-hastings-feels-subliminally-guided-toward-disclosure-2023":"Robert Hastings, author of the book UFOs and Nukes, feels he is being subliminally guided, nudged without knowing it, toward pushing for disclosure, meaning the public release of what governments know about UFOs. Mike Masters, a guest who argues aliens are humans from the future, says Hastings told him this on the phone the day before the 2023 episode. If true, it would mean someone or something is steering researchers from behind the scenes.","robert-hastings-interviews-on-ufos-at-nuclear-bases-1940":"The researcher Robert Hastings has collected accounts from US military personnel who say they saw UFOs at nuclear weapons sites, and Michels comes back to his book UFOs and Nukes more than almost anything else. In Hastings' own words, from a 2024 article, \"beginning in 1973\" he sought out former Air Force personnel who had \"either operated or guarded nuclear weapons\". As of 2023, he writes, \"167 USAF veterans and other military personnel have gone on-the-record\", and the book is \"essentially a compilation\" of their recorded testimony. Michels retells it many times and the details shift. He calls the witnesses Q-cleared, meaning cleared for nuclear weapons secrets, or members of PRP, the program that screens people who handle nuclear weapons. He describes them as missile launch officers, security guards and radar operators, and the count he gives ranges from \"over 60\" to \"almost 170\". Hastings later emailed Michels that only \"12 to 15\" were radar operators and \"the rest were ICBM security\" personnel. In Michels' tellings the witnesses describe Tic Tacs (white oval objects shaped like the mint), saucers and orbs, and say missiles were \"getting shut down and tampered with\". He also mentions sightings in the plumes of nuclear test blasts, signed affidavits at bases such as Malmstrom and F.E. Warren, and the 1980 Rendlesham Forest case in England. At one point he says UFOs visited \"every single nuclear base in the United States.\" The guest John Blitch, a former DARPA scientist, also calls the book powerful and links it to other witnesses. Taken at face value, it would mean something is watching, and sometimes interfering with, nuclear weapons.","robert-hastings-nasal-implant-and-blood-on-pillow":"Robert Hastings, the author of a book called UFOs and Nukes, says he has himself had encounters with UFOs, and in another book, called Confessions, he writes about an implant in his nose and about waking up with blood on his pillow. If true, it would mean something was placed inside his body during one of those encounters. Michels brings this up in passing, crediting Hastings' own books, on an episode about a nuclear missile guard who says he was abducted by aliens.","robert-jameson-s-targeting-team-called-in-to-restart-oscar-f-1967":"In 1967, at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, a team led by a man named Robert Jameson, whose job was setting the missiles' targets, is said to have been called in to restart the nuclear missiles of Oscar Flight, a group of launch sites that had stopped working. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas tells Michels what Jameson told him: \"I got a call saying a missile went off alert in Oscar flight.\" Salas tells it as part of his account of a UFO shutting down nuclear missiles, which, if true, would mean something unknown could switch off America's nuclear weapons.","robert-lovett-s-memo-to-truman-leading-to-the-cia-1940":"The CIA began with a memo to President Truman written by an official named Robert Lovett, according to Dan Sheehan, a researcher and lawyer who tells Michels about it without naming a source. As Sheehan puts it, Lovett wrote \"the initial memo to Truman\" urging that something had to be done, and it led to the agency. He tells it as part of his account of who shaped the secret side of government early on.","robert-low-memo-on-condon-study-1966":"Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, points to a memo from 1966 or 1967 written by a man named Robert Low. Low was a senior figure in the Condon study, a UFO study run at the University of Colorado in those years. Salas calls it \"a famous letter\" from someone he describes as a deputy on the study. He cites the memo as telling evidence about how the study was run, but in this passage he does not say what it contains.","robert-maxwell-and-nazi-tech-transfer":"A man named Robert Maxwell is said to have helped invent scientific peer review, the system where experts check research before it is published. Michels adds that Maxwell was also in charge of \"Nazi tech transfer\", moving German wartime technology into other hands. He says this himself, without naming a source, and suggests that one man held both science publishing and captured technology.","robert-maxwell-gatekeeping-science-via-pergamon-press":"Robert Maxwell, Ghislaine Maxwell's father, is said to have been a triple agent for MI6, the KGB and Israel's Mossad, and to have used his control of science journals through his company Pergamon Press to keep certain discoveries away from the public. The philosopher Jason Jorjani says this on the show, and his July 2025 piece Ocean Outlaw: A Portrait of Ghislaine Maxwell says Maxwell was a double agent for MI6 and the KGB before being recruited by the Mossad \"sometime in the late 1970s\", and that \"as a gatekeeper, he selectively suppressed or sequestered\" revolutionary research, \"including defense-related research with military applications.\" The piece adds that the name had a darker echo, saying it was \"not coincidentally\" the location of the \"Throne of Satan\" in the Book of Revelation, apparently meaning the ancient city of Pergamon, and if the claim were true, one publisher could have held back breakthrough science.","robert-maxwell-interrogated-nazi-scientists":"The British media owner Robert Maxwell, whom Michels calls an \"MI6 guy,\" questioned Nazi scientists in Germany after World War II, Michels says, adding that few people know it. On a later episode he tells it again, adds that Maxwell went on to own Pergamon Press, a scientific publisher, and credits the journalist Whitney Webb's book One Nation Under Blackmail. He raises it because it would tie a spy-linked businessman to both German wartime science and the publishing of research.","robert-maxwell-owned-the-national-enquirer-to-shape-ufo-cove-1980":"The media owner Robert Maxwell, father of Ghislaine Maxwell, owned the National Enquirer, Michels says, and he ties this to the shaping of UFO coverage in the 1980s, when, in his telling, the approach shifted from secrecy to flooding the public with stories. He raises it himself, also naming Rupert Murdoch, in a conversation with a UFO investigator named James Fox. If true, it would mean a tabloid's UFO stories were steered from above.","robert-maxwell-promis-software-sold-to-national-labs":"Michels says Robert Maxwell, the British publisher he calls closely connected with Jeffrey Epstein, sold a program called PROMIS, case-tracking software, to the US national laboratories. He raises it himself as a question to Bill Richardson, a former Secretary of Energy, asking if he knew of it; if true, it would mean a man with those ties had software inside America's weapons labs.","robert-monroe-invites-mishlove-to-join-his-program-1970":"Jeffrey Mishlove, a PhD who studies psychics and the afterlife, says that in the 1970s Robert Monroe invited him to join the program Monroe ran. Mishlove tells Michels he turned it down because 'I was already in school at Berkeley,' and he names Tom Campbell while telling it.","robert-monroe-the-world-is-mined-for-loosh":"The claim is that our world is a kind of farm, and that what gets harvested is \"loosh\", a word the author Robert Monroe uses for something drawn out of human feeling. Michels raises it himself, in passing, saying that Monroe talks about the world being \"kind of mined\" for loosh. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells it at more length, without pointing to a page. He says Monroe described us as living in a \"loosh farm\". In his summary, humans are \"deliberately subjected to conditions that create extreme emotional states\". Jorjani credits the idea to Monroe's book Far Journeys, which he dates to 1985. So the two tellers are Michels, repeating it briefly in his own words, and a researcher who names the book and adds the point about extreme emotion being made on purpose. If true, it would mean human suffering is not an accident but something someone is farming.","robert-sarbacher-and-ufo-secrecy-through-the-atomic-energy-a-1950":"A man named Robert Sarbacher is said to have more or less created the secrecy around UFOs by using the Atomic Energy Act of 1954, a law Congress passed on 30 August 1954, and to have been a close confidant of an inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown. Michels passes this on as something said by David Grusch, a former intelligence official who has spoken publicly about UFO secrecy. If it is true, the secrecy would rest on a law written for atomic matters. The telling does not say which part of the Act did this or how.","robert-sarbacher-liaison-with-australian-scientists-1950":"In the 1950s an American scientist named Robert Sarbacher worked as a go-between with scientists in Australia, according to the Australian journalist Ross Coulthart. Coulthart tells Michels that Sarbacher \"was definitely involved in liaison\", without naming a source. If so, the American side of the UFO question would have reached into Australian science.","robert-schoch-s-solar-flare-and-lichtenberg-figure-idea":"A researcher named Robert Schoch is said to believe that the Giza plateau in Egypt, where the great pyramids stand, \"was hit by a solar flare.\" The idea also involves Lichtenberg figures, the branching patterns that electrical discharges such as lightning leave behind. Geoffrey Drumm, a guest who talks about the pyramids, passes this on as Schoch's idea, which, if right, would mean a burst of energy from the sun once marked the site.","robert-temple-s-conversation-with-a-female-plasma-being-on-a":"The author Robert Temple is said to have described a flight to Singapore during which a \"plasma being\", a living thing made of glowing charged gas, entered the airplane, and he had a lengthy conversation with her. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as something Temple told him, giving no more detail about what was said.","robert-temple-s-living-plasma-thesis-and-plasma-heartbeat-ex":"In a book called A New Science of Heaven, the author Robert Temple argues that plasma, the electrically charged gas found in stars and lightning, is the substrate of the universe and is alive. Michels brings this up himself in two episodes and calls it a great book. In the second he frames it around experiments with plasmoids, self-contained blobs of plasma, and what he calls the 'plasma front'.","roberto-pinotti-s-book-and-the-1933-blackout-telegrams":"In 1933, telegrams from Fascist Italy are said to have ordered a total press silence about an unknown aircraft landing on Italian soil, which would mean a government was hiding a UFO case years before the modern UFO era began. The Italian UFO researcher Roberto Pinotti, who wrote a book about it, says on the show that the telegrams relay \"the personal order of Il Duce,\" Benito Mussolini, that \"absolutely no mention is to be made of the alleged landing of an unknown aircraft on national soil,\" and he credits the Stefani agency, the Fascist-era Italian news agency. Michels raises Pinotti's book himself, without naming it, while debating a skeptic.","robertson-panel-adopts-a-policy-of-ridicule-1952":"The claim is that in early 1953 a CIA-convened panel set official policy toward UFOs, and that the policy was to make them look silly. The group was the Robertson panel, scientists led by the physicist Howard P. Robertson who met from 14 to 18 January 1953, and its report is dated 16 February 1953. Michels says in his own voice that the panel 'absolutely adopted this policy of ridicule.' The report itself is the other teller. It recommends an education program with 'two major aims: training and \"debunking.\"' It says this would bring a 'reduction in public interest in \"flying saucers\"' through 'mass media such as television, motion pictures, and popular articles,' using cases 'puzzling at first but later explained.' It also asks the security agencies to strip the objects of 'the aura of mystery they have unfortunately acquired.' That is the policy Michels calls ridicule. Whether debunking through the mass media meant mocking witnesses, the report neither says nor rules out. If Michels is right, decades of public scorn for UFO reports began as a deliberate government plan.","robertson-panel-aim-to-debunk-sightings-1953":"In January 1953 the CIA brought together a panel of scientists, named for Howard P. Robertson, to look at UFO reports, and Michels says its papers show a deliberate plan to downplay sightings. He says the documents, classified at the time, show that Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, had the 'literal explicit motive' to 'throw the public off' and to 'downplay, suppress, and rationalize' sightings, and elsewhere that the panel set Blue Book's mandate and feared UFO talk 'would touch off mass hysteria.' The panel's report, written up by the CIA's Office of Scientific Intelligence and dated 16 February 1953, does recommend a 'debunking' aim that 'would result in reduction in public interest in flying saucers.' It lists among its worries the 'Subjectivity of public to mass hysteria' and the risk that hostile propaganda could cause 'harmful distrust of duly constituted authority.' The report also lists Blue Book's own progress reports among the evidence the panel was shown, so Blue Book was already running when it met. That the panel's aim was Blue Book's own motive, or that the panel created Blue Book's mandate, is Michels's step, which the report neither states nor rules out. If he is right, the government's public UFO study was shaped from the start to calm the public rather than to find answers.","robertson-panel-directive-to-watch-and-infiltrate-ufo-groups-1953":"In 1953 a group of scientists convened by the CIA, known as the Robertson panel, is said to have told the government to keep watch on private UFO clubs, and the story's own title goes further and says infiltrate them. Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer, tells Michels that the panel's directive was \"let's keep an eye on\" UFO groups, citing the panel's report. The report itself, from the CIA's Office of Scientific Intelligence and dated 16 February 1953, covering meetings held from 14 to 18 January that year, names two civilian groups, the Civilian Flying Saucer Investigators and the Aerial Phenomena Research Organization. It says such organizations \"should be watched because of their potentially great influence on mass thinking if widespread sightings should occur\", and that \"the possible use of such groups for subversive purposes should be kept in mind.\" That is an official recommendation to watch named civilian groups, which is close to what Salas describes. Whether watching meant planting people inside them, the report as released neither says nor rules out. So the story rests on one former officer and on the official record he points to. If it holds, the government's quiet attention to UFO enthusiasts would go back to the early 1950s.","robertson-panel-recommends-downplaying-ufos-1953":"Michels says that in 1953 a Caltech physicist named H.P. Robertson led a panel of academics, now known as the Robertson panel, which concluded that the government should systematically play down UFOs through the media, films and other means. In a second telling he dates the panel to 1952 and 1953, leaves out the films and media, and adds that the panel thought that if the public learned the truth it would set off mass hysteria. Michels tells both versions himself, as the start of official efforts to make the subject look silly.","rock-formation-like-a-second-sphinx-head-near-khentkawes-pyr":"On the southeastern part of the Giza plateau in Egypt, near the pyramid of Khentkawes and the workers' tombs, there is said to be a piece of limestone eroded so strangely that it looks like the back of another Sphinx head. A researcher named Luke Caverns tells Michels he has walked up to it himself. If it is what it looks like, it would hint that the plateau holds more carved figures than the one famous Sphinx.","rockefeller-files-correspondence-routed-through-hillary-clin-1990":"In the 1990s, messages going back and forth between the billionaire Laurance Rockefeller and the White House science adviser were passed through Hillary Clinton's mailbox, according to the researcher Grant Cameron. He tells Michels he found two documents in a set of papers he calls the Rockefeller files that he could not at first make sense of, and one of them said that everything going between Rockefeller and the science adviser was \"going through the mail the mailbox of Hillary Clinton.\" Cameron offers it as a sign that the Clinton White House was looking into the UFO question with Rockefeller.","rockefeller-funding-intercepted-by-cia-nocs-1990":"In the 1990s, money that Laurance Rockefeller, of the wealthy Rockefeller family, put toward UFO disclosure efforts was intercepted by people around him who secretly worked for the CIA. Steven Greer, a UFO disclosure campaigner who says he was involved, tells Michels that Rockefeller did fund it early on, but \"it got intercepted by some people on his team\" who were NOCs, CIA officers working under non-official cover and posing as private citizens. If true, it would mean the CIA quietly steered rich backers away from UFO disclosure.","rockefeller-pressure-leads-the-clinton-administration-to-loo-1990":"In the 1990s, pressure from the wealthy philanthropist Laurance Rockefeller pushed Bill Clinton's White House to look into Roswell, the New Mexico case where a craft is said to have crashed and been recovered by the military. Clinton did not like the answers he got: 'They were getting the runaround.' The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this as it was told by John Podesta, a top Clinton aide, and points to a briefing document prepared for Rockefeller's effort. If true, even a president could not get a straight answer about Roswell.","rockefeller-s-panel-meets-at-quantico-1955":"In 1955 a group led by Nelson Rockefeller is said to have first met at Quantico, Virginia, to draft reports for President Eisenhower. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode asking whether Henry Kissinger oversaw a programme to recover crashed UFOs.","rockefeller-serves-breakfast-and-vows-to-get-disclosure-1990":"In the 1990s, the billionaire Laurance Rockefeller is said to have played butler to three guests at his house, carrying in their breakfast himself, and to have told them, \"I'm going to get disclosure,\" meaning he would get the US government to reveal publicly what it knows about UFOs. The guests were Budd Hopkins, David Jacobs and John Mack, researchers who studied people's accounts of being taken by aliens, and they found it strange to be fed by a billionaire. A researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels this second-hand, as a story Hopkins told him, and he links it to files on Rockefeller's effort released under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records.","rockefellers-fund-greer-mack-and-the-princeton-lab-1990":"In the 1990s the Rockefeller family, especially Laurance Rockefeller, acted as patron to early UFO research, funding the UFO campaigner Steven Greer, the researcher John Mack, and a parapsychology lab at Princeton that studied mind over matter, Michels says. He says it himself on three different episodes, without naming a source, and the details shift: once he stresses the Princeton lab, and once he names Laurance Rockefeller, adds the writer Whitley Strieber and a ranch in the Tetons, and leaves the lab out. If true, it would mean one of America's richest families was quietly bankrolling the field.","rod-serling-s-film-and-the-footage-edwards-afb-promised-1974":"Edwards Air Force Base in California reached out to the television writer Rod Serling and promised him footage of a UFO while he was making UFOs: Past, Present and Future, a documentary film released in 1974, says Jesse Michels. Michels tells this himself, mentioning that Serling was his father's godfather, and admits it sounds 'crazy'; if true, it would mean the Air Force was ready to hand UFO film to a public documentary.","roddenberry-s-script-the-nine-1970":"The television writer Gene Roddenberry is said to have written a script called The Nine, which the teller places in the 1970s. A researcher named Greg Malatzi mentions it without giving a source, in an episode about a CIA scientist who says he made contact with a council of aliens. Raising the script there links a Hollywood writer to that alien council story.","rodent-microdialysis-studies-show-dmt-levels-spike-at-death-2010":"In laboratory studies, researchers sampled the brain chemistry of living rats and then killed them, and found that levels of DMT, a powerful psychedelic compound the brain makes itself, rose sharply as the animals died. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore describes these studies to Michels without naming them. If true, it suggests the dying brain floods itself with the same chemical people take to reach what they describe as other worlds.","rodri-castle-sees-a-giant-triangle-and-black-team-during-hun-1997":"In 1997, during Hunter Warrior, a US military exercise testing new ways of fighting, an aircraft technician for the Harrier jump jet named Rodri Castle is said to have been sent to check on unauthorised flares, and instead found a massive triangle, roughly 300 feet across, along with a \"black team\", an unidentified security crew, who held him at gunpoint. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb relays Castle's account to Michels as an example of a close brush with a secret program. In a second mention UAPGerb puts Castle 150 feet from a giant rotating triangle that he says closely matches the TR-3B, a rumoured secret US triangular aircraft, and leaves out the date, the team and the gun.","rodriguez-paper-proposing-to-test-dmt-entities-with-prime-fa-2000":"In the early 2000s, a man named Rodriguez is said to have published a paper proposing a test for the beings that people on DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, say they meet: ask them to break a large number down into prime factors, a sum too hard to do in your head. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore mentions the paper to Michels in passing. The idea is that if the beings got it right, they would look like real, separate minds and not the user's imagination.","rogan-backlash-over-malone-and-mccullough-episodes-2022":"Michels says the backlash against the podcaster Joe Rogan came because he had the doctors Robert Malone and Peter McCullough, critics of the official Covid response, on his show. Michels says it himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a whistleblower, as an example of what happens to hosts who air unpopular guests.","rogan-s-first-5-meo-dmt-experience":"The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan says that the first time he took 5-MeO-DMT, a very powerful psychedelic drug, he was sure he had died: \"I thought like... this just killed me. Like this there's no way this is real.\" Rogan tells Michels this about himself, as an example of how overwhelming such experiences can be.","rogan-s-offline-explanation-of-lazar-s-deleted-mit-records-1980":"The podcaster Joe Rogan is said to have a private explanation for why the MIT records of Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft in Nevada in the 1980s, seem to have been deleted. Michels passes this on as something he has heard: whenever Lazar comes up with Rogan, Rogan says, \"Oh, I'll tell you something offline\" that would make the deleted MIT records make sense. Michels does not say what that explanation is, but it would bear on whether Lazar's missing education was erased on purpose.","rogan-s-possible-childhood-sighting":"The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan says he may have seen something in the sky as a boy, but thinks it was probably a fighter jet. He tells Michels, 'I wish, I kind of thought I did maybe when I was young, but I think it was probably a fighter jet.'","rogan-s-reading-on-project-blue-book-hynek-corso-and-roswell-1947":"The comedian and podcast host Joe Rogan tells Michels that he once went deep into the UFO record: he says he \"got really into Project Blue Book\", the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and read a lot about J. Allen Hynek, an astronomer tied to that study, about the claims of a former Army officer named Philip J. Corso, and about Roswell, the 1947 case in which something came down near Roswell, New Mexico, and many say it was a crashed craft. This is Rogan describing his own reading, not anything he saw, and he tells it to explain where his interest in the subject comes from.","rogan-s-vivid-dream-of-slender-pinkish-humanlike-beings-2025":"In 2025, the podcaster Joe Rogan says, he had a vivid dream in which very slender, tall, humanlike beings talked to him, not gray but pinkish, like us. Rogan tells Michels about it himself, comes back to it later as the crazy dream of being in contact with those beings, and adds that he came away feeling someone was trying to get him used to this.","roger-leir-s-implant-extractions":"A man named Roger Leir surgically removed objects that people believed had been implanted in their bodies, and Tim Alberino tells Michels that a colleague and good friend of his, Ellie Marculli, worked with Leir and was present when some of these implants were taken out. Alberino passes this on as something his friend saw, not something he saw himself, and does not say what the implants were or where they came from.","roger-leir-s-licence-targeted":"Someone went after the professional licence of Roger Leir, a California practitioner involved in the UFO field, in what is told as a campaign against him. The writer Whitley Strieber tells Michels about it while describing similar trouble of his own: \"Roger Leir had the same thing happen. They went after him.\" He does not say who \"they\" were.","roger-leir-s-tapes-and-book-doctors-telepathy-with-the-vargi-1996":"In the Varginha case, a 1996 episode in Brazil in which creatures are said to have been captured and taken to hospitals, two doctors are said to have described a creature and a kind of telepathic contact with it to an American researcher named Roger Leir. A UFO documentary maker named James Fox tells Michels about this on two occasions. He says Leir wrote about a meeting with the two doctors at Humanitas Hospital, and that this section is in Leir's book on the case but not in Leir's recorded tapes. If true, it would mean medical staff spoke of treating a living, non-human being.","roger-leir-s-video-of-a-craft-with-grays-inside":"A UFO researcher named Roger Leir is said to have had a video, filmed over the ocean near Lebanon or Turkey, that \"looks like a craft\" with grays inside it. Grays are the small, grey-skinned beings common in accounts of alien encounters. A guest named Tim Alberino passes on what Leir told him; if true, the video would show living beings inside an unknown craft.","rogers-book-we-got-it-from-them-2025":"Greg Rogers, a doctor who has worked with NASA and says he saw a UFO inside a secret hangar, has written a 2025 book about it with a title that makes his point: We Got It From Them. He tells Michels, laughing, that he decided to be \"really ingenious\" and call it that. The title is his claim in short: if true, some of what the hangar held came from a non-human source.","rogers-craft-as-human-built-but-aerodynamically-pointless-2000":"In 2000, a NASA doctor named Greg Rogers says he saw a strange craft inside a secret hangar, and he believes it was built by humans even though its shape made no sense for flying. Rogers tells Michels this firsthand: \"I think that what I saw was... human built.\" If true, it would mean some craft taken for alien are secret human machines.","rogers-egg-craft-resembles-lazar-s-sport-model-1989":"The egg-shaped craft that Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor, says he saw in a secret hangar would be 'very much like' the 'sport model.' That is the name Bob Lazar gave to a disc-shaped craft. Lazar is a man who has said that in 1989 he worked on alien craft at S-4, a secret site near Area 51, the classified Air Force test base in Nevada. Rogers draws the comparison himself in telling Michels what he saw. If true, his sighting would back up Lazar's account.","rogue-elements-of-us-government-managing-uap-programs-per-gr-1970":"The claim is that parts of the US government have gone \"rogue\", running programs on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) outside normal oversight. The national security analyst Matthew Pines tells Michels this is how he reads what David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who told Congress that the US holds craft of non-human origin, has been saying, with the defense firm Lockheed mentioned in the same breath. If true, it would mean these programs answer to no elected authority.","roman-gods-on-mission-patches-for-our-sponsors":"A researcher who is not named on the show says that when he asked someone in the US space program why its mission patches carried images of Roman gods and Latin phrases, the answer was 'Our sponsors.' He says he asked it himself: 'Who up in space is going to read Latin?' If true, it would hint that the space program answers to someone, or something, up there.","romans-say-phoenicians-sacrificed-children-to-molech":"According to the Romans, the Phoenicians, the seafaring traders of the ancient Mediterranean, sacrificed their children to Molech, a god named in the Bible. The researcher Tim Alberino raises this with Michels while discussing Phoenician rites, in the context of Sardinia and Phoenicia, citing the Romans in general rather than any named text. He offers it as part of a darker picture of who the Phoenicians were.","ron-evans-and-bae-s-project-greenglow-anti-gravity-research":"A scientist named Dr. Ron Evans at BAE Systems, the British defense company, spoke about Project Greenglow, a research effort into anti-gravity, and wrote a public book about it. Michels mentions this himself in passing, without saying what the project found.","ron-hell-maria-is-a-natural-asian-african-hybrid":"Maria, one of the three-fingered bodies found in Peru that some call alien, is a hybrid, but a natural one, mixing Asian and African ancestry, according to a researcher named David Ron Hell. He tells Michels this is his own hypothesis: \"My hypothesis is that Maria is a hybrid but natural,\" which would make her a strange human rather than something from elsewhere.","ron-johnson-sees-his-lost-dog-s-fate-in-a-crystal-1972":"A man named Ron Johnson, whose dog vanished around 1972, is said to have watched the dog's life play out inside a crystal years later and to have seen what happened: a neighbor came, shot the dog, threw it in the back of a pickup truck and dumped it at the dump. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something Johnson told him. If true, it would mean a crystal could show a person scenes from the past.","ron-johnson-sees-the-milky-way-from-a-distance":"A man named Ron Johnson was once asked, \"Ron, where would you like to go?\" and answered that he would like to see the Milky Way from a distance, according to the researcher Grant Cameron, who repeats what Johnson told him. In the story the offer comes from \"they\", whom Cameron does not name here, and the point is that someone could take a person far enough away to look back on the whole galaxy.","ron-pandolfi-breakthrough-into-the-next-world-in-the-desert-1984":"A CIA man named Ron Pandolfi, said to have been at the agency since 1984 and rumored to brief presidents, is quoted saying there has been a breakthrough in going into \"the next world,\" and that next time a man called John goes to the desert, several of them will go into the next world and come back. A researcher named Grant Cameron tells Michels this comes from a tape of Pandolfi that he grabbed off the internet, which Pandolfi never expected to be public. Cameron says Pandolfi is clearly talking about portals, and if so, it would mean people inside the CIA believe they can travel to another realm and return.","ron-pandolfi-links-the-jasons-and-the-ufo-world":"Michels says a man named Ron Pandolfi seems to belong to the JASON group, a panel of top scientists who advise the US government on defense, while also turning up constantly in the UFO world. Michels raises this himself, without a source, as a possible link between elite government science and UFO secrets.","ron-s-visits-to-the-spirit-world-with-elby-the-crystal-table":"A man from Utah known only as Ron says a being called Elby took him to the spirit world three times, and his account includes a crystal table. A UFO researcher named Grant Cameron passes this on from what Ron told him. If true, it would be a firsthand account of a guide taking a living person to a world beyond death.","ronald-moultrie-scrubbed-battelle-board-role-from-linkedin-2020":"Ronald Moultrie, a former top Pentagon intelligence official under whose authority AARO, the Pentagon's current office for investigating UFO reports, was set up, is said to have also sat on the board of the Battelle Memorial Institute, a large private research organisation that works for the government, and to have deleted that role from his LinkedIn profile. The online researcher UAPGerb points this out to Michels from his own digging, naming no other source than the profile itself. He raises it because it would tie the man behind the official investigation to a contractor some suspect of secret work.","ronald-richter-s-fusion-project-in-bariloche-1947":"After the Second World War, a German scientist named Ronald Richter is said to have set up a nuclear fusion project in Bariloche, Argentina, and to have promised nuclear weapons to Argentina's leader, Juan Perón. Jason Jorjani, a researcher who studies claims of secret Nazi flying-craft programmes, tells Michels this, adding that it is \"why the IA went down there.\" If true, it would mean German wartime science carried on in South America after the war, close enough to bombs to draw foreign intelligence.","rongorongo-and-nazca-lines-as-depictions-of-plasma-discharge":"The idea here is that rongorongo, a set of carved signs from Easter Island long thought to be a form of writing, and the giant Nazca lines drawn across the desert in Peru may not be writing or art at all, but pictures of huge plasma discharges people once saw in the sky. Matt LaCroix, a guest on the show, passes this on second-hand. He says it came from Robert Schoch and his wife Katie, who were looking at rongorongo on Easter Island, and he credits a work called Forgotten Civilizations. If it were right, these ancient markings would be records of a sky event that ancient people witnessed.","room-of-antiques-with-a-singer-sewing-machine-and-a-round-wi-1977":"In 1977 in South Dakota, a former guard at a nuclear site named Mario Woods says he was taken aboard a craft and shown a room full of old things, among them a sewing table with a Singer sewing machine on it, in a room with a round window. Woods tells this first-hand and has made drawings of what he saw. In a later telling he adds that a small being led him into the room and let him look, and that the objects looked like the ones in his childhood home in Port Arthur, Texas.","rosemary-smith-remote-views-a-downed-tu-22-in-zaire-1976":"A woman named Rosemary Smith, using remote viewing, the practice of trying to see faraway places with the mind alone, drew a circle about three square miles wide on a map of Zaire, and a downed Soviet Tu-22 plane was found inside it. Jimmy Carter himself is heard on the show saying \"we located the plane where she said it was,\" and calling it \"the only time that I have ever experienced something that was inexplicable\"; Michels retells it more than once, and a former psychic spy for the government named Paul Smith tells it secondhand from a man named Dale Graff, dating it to 1979.","ross-coulthart-gets-brent-to-name-harrington-on-camera-2019":"Around 2019 or 2020, the journalist Ross Coulthart pressed Brent Friedman on camera until Friedman named a man called Harrington in connection with a UFO story. Friedman tells Michels this happened to him, saying Coulthart \"got me on camera and just pushed me.\" He also names Bryce Zabel in connection with the story. He tells it to explain how a name he had held back came out in public.","ross-coulthart-s-investigation-at-bradshaw-ranch-sedona-craf":"Near Bradshaw Ranch in Sedona, Arizona, a place famous for UFO sightings, UFOs are said to fly in and out of a mountain, which would mean the mountain hides some kind of base. Michels brings it up on several episodes, pointing to the investigative work of the journalist Ross Coulthart there. In different episodes Michels names the mountain as Secret Mountain, calls the craft \"transmedium\" (able to move from air into other mediums such as rock or water), compares the site to the mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, another place known for reports of strange events, and adds that the US government later took over the site, with armed guards, \"a move that raised some eyebrows.\"","ross-coulthart-says-on-need-to-know-that-the-tic-tac-is-lock-2025":"The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart said on his show Need to Know that the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint that Navy pilots reported in 2004, is technology made by the defense contractor Lockheed Martin, which would make the famous object human-built. Bryce Zabel, a writer who was there, tells Michels that Coulthart turned to him and said \"I can now state categorically that the tic tac is Lockheed Martin technology\", while a clip of Coulthart himself has him saying \"I now know categorically\".","rossi-devices-and-ring-superconductor-experiments-2021":"Between 2021 and 2024, a man named Dave Rossi says he built a handful of devices, including experiments with ring-shaped superconductors, that produced \"interesting effects\" which seemed to back up theories discussed by Dr. Hal Puthoff, Dr. Eric W. Davis and others. Rossi tells Michels this firsthand, on an episode where he says aliens taught him advanced physics. If true, it would mean those theories about exotic propulsion can be tested at a workbench.","roswell-as-home-of-the-509th-atomic-bomber-squadron-1947":"Several guests point out that in 1947, Roswell, New Mexico, the town at the centre of the most famous UFO crash story, was home to the 509th, America's first atomic bomber unit. The commentator Marik von Rennenkampff calls it \"the first bomber squadron\" of its kind. The researcher who goes by RedPandaKoala adds that this unit dropped the bombs on Hiroshima and Nagasaki. The scientist John Brandenburg goes further and says all the world's nuclear weapons were kept there. Michels says Roswell held \"the only American nuclear stockpile at the time\" and names the officers involved, among them Jesse Marcel Sr., Walter Haut, William Blanchard and Roger Ramey. In another episode he asks whether something happened there, noting the unit was led by the Air Force general Curtis LeMay. The tellers raise it because a crash beside America's nuclear bombers would hint that something was watching them.","roswell-bodies-kept-on-ice-at-wright-patterson-1947":"Bodies were flown from Roswell, New Mexico, where a strange craft is said to have crashed in 1947, and kept on ice at Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP is the government's newer term for UFOs, 'unidentified anomalous phenomena') says this to Michels in passing, as something known 'historically,' without naming a source. If true, the military would have held the crew of a crashed craft.","roswell-boulder-split-and-memory-metal-1947":"In 1947, at Roswell, New Mexico, debris was recovered from a crash, and Michels says he believes the basic story: that it included a \"memory metal\" that sprang back to its shape. He says he believes two named witnesses, Jesse Marcel, whom he calls an Air Force flight surgeon, and Walter Haut. In one telling he adds that the material was taken to Wright Field. He says this himself on two episodes, as his own view, and calls it \"worthy of further investigation\".","roswell-corona-crash-confirmed-via-ed-mitchell-and-admiral-w-1947":"The 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico, which happened on a ranch near the town of Corona, is said to have been a real crash and not a Mogul balloon, the secret Army balloon later given as the official explanation, according to a Navy admiral named Thomas R. Wilson, a senior military intelligence official. Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has worked as a Pentagon scientist, tells Michels he heard this from the Apollo astronaut Ed Mitchell at a science advisory board meeting. As Davis relays it, Mitchell described briefings at the Pentagon by the UFO activist Steven Greer about his Disclosure Project, a campaign to get officials to reveal UFO secrets, after which Wilson came back and \"verif[ied] that the Roswell crash, well the Corona crash, actually really did happen.\" If true, it would mean a senior officer confirmed the most famous UFO crash story.","roswell-craft-said-to-have-been-brought-down-by-lightning-1947":"The craft said to have crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947 was brought down by a lightning strike, in one of the older stories about the event that Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft for the government, recalls on the show. Lazar mentions it only in passing, as a story going around before later explanations, saying the craft was \"hit by lightning and crashing or something.\"","roswell-crash-and-stringfield-s-marcel-interview-1947":"After the 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico, which some believe was a spacecraft, a UFO researcher named Leonard Stringfield was among the very first people to interview Jesse Marcel Sr., the Army officer who handled the debris, and to publish that interview. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this, without naming a source, and also names the researcher Stanton Friedman in connection with it. If true, Stringfield's work would be one of the early steps that brought Marcel's account to the public.","roswell-crash-as-exotic-human-technology-1947":"The object that crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947 may have been advanced human technology and not an alien craft, says the writer Peter Levenda. Roswell is the incident many in the UFO field see as the moment the US government recovered a crashed craft. Levenda calls the idea \"a possibility\" without naming a source, in a talk that also touches on Sekret Machines, a book series co-created by the musician Tom DeLonge. If he is right, Roswell would be a story about secret human engineering rather than visitors.","roswell-crash-caused-by-radio-signatures-1947":"The idea is that the craft said to have crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947 came down because conflicting radio signals interfered with its navigation. The online researcher UAPGerb tells Michels that this is what Philip J. Corso, a former Army officer who wrote about the Roswell crash, has said. If true, it would mean military radar or radio brought down a non-human craft by accident.","roswell-crash-debris-retrieved-by-mac-brazel-1947":"In 1947, the story goes, a rancher named Mac Brazel found strange crash debris scattered over his land near Roswell, New Mexico, and it is told as the first famous UFO crash. A scientist named John Brandenburg, who credits Roswell veterans, says Brazel brought the wreckage into town and showed it to the sheriff, who told him to take it to the nearby air base. A researcher named Luigi Vendittelli adds that, besides the debris on the ranch, a pod holding beings crashed about two miles away, where hikers with children found it. A journalist named Michael Shellenberger asks whether one craft or two came down, possibly brought down by radar. A researcher of UFOs at nuclear sites named Robert Hastings says Roswell was then the world's only nuclear bomber base, and that Luis Elizondo calls it a real UFO crash in his new book, while Michels himself says he thinks what happened there was 'otherworldly'.","roswell-crash-test-dummy-and-project-mogul-explanations-reje-1947":"The official explanations for the 1947 crash at Roswell, New Mexico, the famous case many believe involved a UFO, cannot be right, Michels argues, because the things they point to came later. Air Force reports blamed what people saw on crash-test dummies and on a programme called Project Mogul, but Michels says the \"bluish dummies\" were used \"way later\" and that Mogul was \"later\" too. He says this himself, without naming a source, on an episode about a reported live alien captured by the Army.","roswell-date-of-july-7-1947-in-the-brief-linked-to-the-guest":"Michels suggests that a string of numbers a man dreamed may point to the date of the Roswell crash, the 1947 event in which many say a craft came down in New Mexico. The man, a CIA veteran named Ralph Moat Larson, is said to have had what Michels calls a \"download\": a dream in which a set of numbers, including 747, came into his head. On the show, Michels notes that the book A State of Mind: Faith and the CIA gives the Roswell date as July 7th, 1947, and asks Larson whether that connects with his 747, which, if it did, would make the dream point to that crash; in a later episode Michels retells the dream himself, second-hand, as a dream full of numbers.","roswell-debris-swapped-for-the-ramey-photo-1947":"After something crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, the military photographed wreckage in the office of General Roger Ramey, and a researcher named Kevin Knuth thinks the real debris was swapped out for that photo. He tells Michels, drawing on the claims tied to the Roswell officer Jesse Marcel Sr., that \"they claimed they switched the wreckage\" and that he thinks it \"more likely that they switched the wreckage.\" If so, the pictures the public saw were not of what was actually found.","roswell-flying-disc-announcement-then-retraction-1947":"In July 1947 a flying disc came down near Roswell, New Mexico, and the Roswell Army Airfield put out a press release saying so, then took it back \"in a panic,\" Michels says while debating a skeptic in 2026. He tells it himself, pointing to the published press release as the document that matters. To him, a military base announcing a disc and then hurriedly withdrawing the statement suggests something real was being covered up.","roswell-held-the-most-us-nukes-in-1947":"Michels says that in 1947, when a craft is said to have crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, the base there held more nuclear weapons than anywhere else in the United States. He tells it himself, without naming a source, to the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, as a hint that whatever came down there may have been interested in nuclear weapons.","roswell-ignored-until-berlitz-s-1980-book-1947":"The Roswell incident, the claim that a flying saucer crashed in New Mexico in 1947, is said to have been almost forgotten for more than thirty years, until the writer Charles Berlitz published his book The Roswell Incident in 1980. The skeptic Michael Shermer says this on the show, adding that the researcher Stanton Friedman \"and others got involved\" after the book. He tells it to suggest the story grew through later books, not from what happened in 1947.","roswell-material-as-seed-for-an-exotic-alloy-r-d-program-at-1947":"Michels says that, as the theory goes, whatever was recovered from the 1947 crash at Roswell, New Mexico, which UFO researchers believe was an alien craft, became the seed of a secret research programme at the Air Force Research Lab at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, a programme studying exotic metal alloys. He passes it on as a theory without naming who holds it. If true, it would mean crash material shaped real military research.","roswell-operators-part-of-the-craft-s-guidance-system-1947":"The beings flying the craft said to have crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947 were reportedly part of its guidance system, as if pilot and machine were one. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this, passing on what a man named Philip J. Corso said in interviews. If true, it would mean the craft could not be flown without its crew.","roswell-s-shifting-official-stories-including-the-progeria-c-1947":"The official explanation for what crashed at Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947 is said to have changed again and again. Among the versions is one involving children with progeria, a rare disease of rapid ageing. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, calling the shifting story \"so laughable\" and taking the changes as a reason to doubt all the official versions.","roswell-style-seeding-of-our-internet-technology-1947":"Much of our internet technology may have been dropped on us, the way some say technology came from the 1947 Roswell crash, by beings who wanted to keep us constantly distracted. Michels muses this himself, without naming a source or saying who \"these people\" are, in a conversation with the writer Randall Carlson about the idea that the Moon is an alien spaceship.","roswell-technologies-per-corso-were-german-1940s-inventions":"The technologies that Philip Corso, a former Army officer, said came from the Roswell crash, a craft many say came down in New Mexico in 1947, had all been invented by the Germans in the 1940s. That is what a writer named Jason Jorjani tells Michels. Corso made his claims in his 1997 book The Day After Roswell, and Jorjani says an author named Joseph P. Farrell researched them and gives documents showing that the Germans had invented every single one, which would make the Roswell story partly a cover for wartime German science.","roswell-the-world-s-only-nuclear-bomber-base-couldn-t-tell-a-1947":"In 1947, near Roswell, New Mexico, something came down that the military there is said to have mistaken for, or passed off as, a weather balloon rather than a flying saucer, and the tellers point out that this was no ordinary place. The screenwriter Bryce Zabel tells Michels that the people at Roswell were running the only nuclear bomber base in the world at the time. Michels says on a later episode, without naming a source, that Roswell was not a random patch of desert but held the largest concentration of nuclear weapons in the world. The point is that the most elite base of its day seems unlikely to have confused a saucer with a balloon.","roswell-was-a-nazi-staged-event-1947":"The 1947 Roswell incident, when a strange craft is said to have crashed in New Mexico, was supposedly not an alien crash but an event staged by the Nazis. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that 'the Nazis created the crisis at Roswell,' and he cites Philip J. Corso, an Army officer who wrote about Roswell. If true, the most famous UFO crash would have been a deception set up by surviving Nazis.","roswell-witnesses-describe-non-human-being-1947":"Michels says that people at Roswell, New Mexico, where a craft is said to have crashed in 1947, described a strange being with three or four fingers and toes coming out of the craft, one that was clearly not human. He tells this himself, without naming the witnesses, to the writer Peter Levenda; if true, it would mean a living non-human being was seen at the crash.","roswell-witnesses-describe-threats-made-in-1947":"Witnesses to the 1947 events at Roswell, New Mexico, the town where by the story a crashed craft was recovered, say they were threatened at the time to keep quiet, told that 'they'll be picking your bones out of the desert.' The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels he heard this himself from the men: 'I had guys tell me this is what was said at the time.' If true, it would mean the military used death threats to bury what happened.","roswell-wreckage-in-the-hangar-had-the-bathroom-on-the-other-1947":"Wreckage from Roswell, the New Mexico town where something is said to have crashed and been recovered by the military in 1947, sat in a hangar with the bathroom on its other side. That is how Charles Hall tells it to Michels. Hall says he spent three years at Area 51, a secret Air Force site in Nevada, among tall white aliens, and he speaks of the wreckage as something he saw himself. Asked what it was like, he answers with that one plain detail of where it stood, which matters because it would put the Roswell debris in an ordinary working building.","roswell-wreckage-rumoured-at-wright-patterson-lab-1947":"Wreckage from the 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico, which lore holds was a flying saucer, is rumoured to have been taken to a lab at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base, and Michels notes that an Air Force general named Neil McCasland once ran that lab. Michels returns to this several times on his own. In one episode he adds Fort Worth to the wreckage's route. In another he credits Tom DeLonge's book series Sekret Machines for the idea that foreign material exploitation, the military's study of captured hardware, ended up with the Roswell pieces. Elsewhere he calls the base both the home of Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, and the alleged home of the Roswell debris.","round-table-foundation-elf-research-penetrating-a-faraday-ca-1957":"At the Round Table Foundation, a private research lab, a researcher named Andrija Puharich is said to have found a very low radio frequency that could pass through the walls of a Faraday cage, a metal enclosure built to block electrical signals. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this, without naming a source. If true, it would mean signals could reach a person even inside a shielded room.","round-table-foundation-funded-by-army-navy-and-cia-1949":"In 1949 and 1950, the Army, the Navy and the CIA were all funding a private research group in Rockland, Maine, called the Round Table Foundation, according to the researcher Greg Malatzi. He tells Michels that the agencies were \"flocking to this place, basically giving him money to do research,\" which means the foundation's head, and he points to letters from the agencies to the foundation. The story matters because it would put the military and the CIA behind fringe research very early on.","rov-camera-and-lights-fail-at-the-object-2011":"In 2011, at a strange object found on the floor of the Baltic Sea, an underwater robot's camera and lights stopped working whenever it dived on the object. Dennis Asberg, a treasure hunter whose team was there, tells Michels this happened to them: \"when we have dive on it, the camera stops working, the light stops working.\" He tells it as one of the odd things suggesting the object is more than ordinary rock.","rov-image-shows-a-straight-wall-on-the-anomaly-2025":"In 2025, pictures taken by a remotely operated underwater vehicle are said to show a long, straight wall on the Baltic Sea anomaly, an oddly shaped object lying on the floor of the Baltic Sea. A treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, who says he saw it for himself, tells Michels the wall must be \"at least 30 m\" long and the whole object about \"60 m\". A wall that straight would suggest something built, not natural rock.","roy-craig-interview-with-lewis-chase-who-denies-knowledge-1967":"In 1967 an interviewer named Roy Craig recorded a talk with Lieutenant Colonel Lewis D. Chase, the officer in charge of operations at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, and in that talk Chase denies knowing about the matter he was asked about. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas plays the tape for Michels, on an episode about a UFO he says shut down ten nuclear missiles. The recording opens with the words \"the following interview conducted by Roy Craig is with Lieutenant Colonel Lewis D. Chase, who is chief of the operations division,\" and Salas brings it up because a denial from the base's operations chief bears on what the Air Force admitted at the time.","royal-raymond-rife-s-microscope-and-frequency-machine-suppre-1930":"In the 1930s in California, an inventor named Royal Raymond Rife claimed to have built a microscope so powerful it could watch live viruses, and his work, including a machine based on frequencies, is said to have been suppressed. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, while talking with a guest about the history of the drug industry; if true, it would mean a medical breakthrough was buried.","royal-tomb-theory-and-the-lack-of-mummies-in-khufu-s-and-kha":"Michels points out that most Egyptologists believe the Great Pyramid was built around 2500 BC for the pharaoh Khufu, to help the king rise to the afterlife, and that the Pyramid of Khafre was built as the tomb of Khufu's son Khafre. But he says \"no confirmed mummies or tombs\" of either king have ever been found inside the pyramids. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a claimed energy grid beneath the pyramids, which suggests the pyramids might have had some purpose other than being tombs.","rubik-and-jabs-reanalyse-the-michelson-morley-ether-data-1897":"A biophysicist named Beverly Rubik and a physicist named Harry Jabs are said to have gone back over the data from the Michelson-Morley experiment, the famous test that looked for the ether, a medium once thought to carry light, and found none. According to the researcher Diane Powell, they wrote a paper \"basically saying well wait a minute there actually was an effect.\" Powell tells it without naming the paper, and if the reading holds, it would reopen a question physics had treated as closed.","rubio-credible-witnesses-with-high-clearances-fear-for-their-2020":"Senator Marco Rubio is said to have stated on camera, over and over, that the people coming forward about UFOs are credible, hold high security clearances and high positions, and fear for their lives. Marik von Rennenkampff, a former Defense Department official who writes about the subject, tells Michels this during a debate, naming Senator Kirsten Gillibrand and others alongside Rubio. He raises it because it would mean a senior senator takes the witnesses, and the danger to them, seriously.","rubio-s-2020-intelligence-committee-provision-and-mick-s-twe":"In 2020, Senator Marco Rubio is said to have slipped a UFO provision into the wording of a Senate Intelligence Committee report, which would mean Congress was quietly pushing the intelligence agencies on UFOs. The commentator Marik von Rennenkampff says on the show that this is \"when Rubio snuck a provision into Senate intelligence committee language,\" and he raises it in an exchange with Mick West, a well-known UFO skeptic, linking it to a tweet of West's whose content the telling does not give.","rubio-things-flying-over-nuclear-sites":"Marco Rubio, the Secretary of State, all but admitted that unknown objects fly over American nuclear sites, according to Michels, who tells it himself. Michels says that when Rubio was asked which conspiracy he believes in most, he answered, \"There are things flying with impunity around our nuclear sites.\" Michels offers it as a senior official conceding the problem in public.","rudolf-hess-s-telepathy-experiments-and-astrologically-timed-1941":"Rudolf Hess, Hitler's deputy who flew to Britain in 1941 on a flight said to have been timed by astrology, believed while in prison that he was in telepathic contact with his wife, and telepathy experiments were run. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this as part of his own research, without naming a source, as a sign of how seriously some Nazi leaders took the occult.","rudolf-hess-sent-to-antarctica-1938":"Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess was \"one of the main guys\" the Nazis sent to Antarctica, says the writer Jason Jorjani, in connection with the Nazi expedition there in 1938 and 1939. Jorjani tells it without naming a source, crediting a work titled Hess and the Penguins. If true, it would tie the Nazi leadership itself to the Antarctic venture, which Jorjani links to his account of a Nazi UFO programme.","ruins-of-an-ancient-civilization-at-cydonia-mars-1990":"The claim is that there are ruins of an ancient civilization on Mars, in a region called Cydonia, and that it was an extraterrestrial civilization. The researcher Tim Alberino says this on the show without pointing to a single source, though he names the writers David Flynn, Richard Hoagland and Michael Heiser and works titled Cydonia and Birthright. In a second telling he leaves out Cydonia and adds a prediction: a \"revelation of Mars\" is coming in our lifetime, which would mean someone else once lived on the planet next door.","rumored-crash-in-chinese-territory":"Something is said to have crashed on Chinese territory, and the Russians are said to have ended up with it. Peter Levenda, an author who writes on intelligence and the occult, tells Michels he heard many such rumours over years of doing business with the Chinese government, and that the Russian part is his own guess.","rumored-dod-hybrid-program-using-non-human-genetic-material-2024":"Rumors have reached Capitol Hill of a secret Department of Defense project that mixes human genetics with what is described as \"non-human genetic material,\" meaning genetic material from something other than people. Lauren Boebert says this on the show in November 2024, in an episode with the journalist Michael Shellenberger. She passes it on only as a rumor and names no source, but if it were true it would mean the military holds, and experiments with, biology that is not from Earth.","rumored-tunnels-and-cave-system-under-mesa":"A cave system and tunnels are rumored to lie under the mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange happenings. The ranch's owner tells Michels that the rumors go back to earlier owners of the ranch \"and even before, going back to the Mayans,\" and that this is why material from the mesa was examined. If true, it would mean something hidden lies under the land at the center of the ranch's mysteries.","rumors-about-area-51-at-the-energy-department-1980":"John S. Herrington, who was US Secretary of Energy in the 1980s, tells Michels that rumours about Area 51, a secret military test site in Nevada, reached his department: \"We got a lot of rumors about what was happening in Area 51, which is a very highly classified area with a lot of projects that are highly classified.\" He speaks of rumours, not of things he saw, and it matters because it shows the talk reached a cabinet officer.","rumors-of-elites-having-out-of-body-experiences-in-pyramid-g":"The rumour is that wealthy and powerful people today spend time inside the granite chambers of some pyramids, and the story's label says they go there for out-of-body experiences. Michels brings it up himself as a question to a guest, asking whether they have heard these rumours, and he names no source.","rumors-of-luring-uap-with-nuclear-material-traps-2021":"The US may be trying to lure UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, by setting traps baited with nuclear material, according to rumours the economist Tyler Cowen says he has heard. Speaking to Michels in 2022, Cowen frames it as a question: \"are we setting traps for them? Are we trying to beckon them with nuclear material?\" He names no source and offers it only as talk he has picked up.","rumour-that-the-giant-ended-up-at-wright-patterson-2005":"A 12-foot giant that Tim Alberino says was captured in Afghanistan is rumoured to have ended up at Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio. Alberino, a guest on the show, passes on the rumour as something he was told, placing the events around 2005. If true, it would mean the US military took possession of the body and has held it since.","rumoured-2027-arrival-clock":"Word has gone around, the rumour goes, that \"they\", meaning non-humans, are going to show up openly in 2027. The national security analyst Matthew Pines tells Michels he has heard people say this, but that he does not believe in a 2027 clock himself.","rumoured-badlands-incident-in-which-two-security-policemen-w-1977":"Around 1977, two Air Force security policemen guarding nuclear missile sites in the 66th missile area near the Badlands of South Dakota were said to have been found out of their vehicle and unclothed. Mario Woods, a former nuclear missile guard, tells Michels he heard this as a rumor from years before he arrived there. He tells it alongside his own account of a UFO abduction, as a sign that strange things happened to guards in that area.","rumoured-deep-underground-military-bases":"There are rumours of deep underground military bases stretching from Edwards and Nellis, two big Air Force bases in the American West, to Peterson in Colorado and the Cheyenne Mountain Complex, the bunker inside a Colorado mountain that houses NORAD, the US and Canadian air defence command. Michels mentions this himself as rumour, without naming a source; if true, it would mean a hidden world of facilities lies beneath known bases.","rumoured-jfk-joint-space-program-with-the-soviets-1961":"Some say President John F. Kennedy wanted to work with the Soviets on space and to use UFOs to bring the two powers together, and that his own officials were alarmed. Michels raises it as a rumour, without naming a source: that Kennedy wanted \"a joint space program with the Soviets\" and to use \"the UFO thing\" as a \"unifying rallying cry.\" His guest Harald Malmgren, a former presidential adviser who says he handled UFO material himself, tells it differently and speaks from his time in government. He says Kennedy \"expressed verbally to a number of the senior people his desire to share our knowledge of UFOs with Russia.\" In his account, that \"caused alarm.\" He also says the Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev was part of the story. Malmgren describes it as sharing what the US knew about UFOs, not a joint space programme. If it is true, a sitting president wanted to tell the Soviets about UFOs, and his senior people did not want him to.","rumoured-mormon-connection-to-the-ufo-legacy-program-with-by":"There are rumours, Jesse Michels says, that the Mormon church has a link to the UFO \"legacy program\", meaning a supposed long-running secret effort to hold and study recovered craft, and that Brigham Young University (BYU), the church's university in Utah, may be a feeder that supplies it with people. Michels raises this himself as something rumoured, without naming a source, and if it were true it would tie a hidden government project to one religious community and its school.","rumsfeld-admits-trillions-missing-in-audit-day-before-9-11-2001":"The claim is that in 2001, the day before the September 11 attacks, Defense Secretary Donald Rumsfeld publicly admitted that the Pentagon could not account for trillions of dollars. A journalist named Ross Coulthart tells Michels he dug up Rumsfeld's speech while writing his book, and that in it Rumsfeld \"admitted that there was something\" wrong with the books. A researcher named Richard Dolan tells it too, from memory and without a single source. He says the first figure was \"2.3 trillion dollars in unaccounted expenditures\" and that Rumsfeld kept revising it. Dolan sets it before the House Armed Services Committee over 2001 and 2002, and links the figure to two people he names, Dov Zakheim and Catherine Austin Fitts. Both men tell it for the same reason: if it is true, vast sums were moving through the Pentagon outside any normal accounting, money that could pay for programmes nobody sees.","ruppelt-in-look-magazine-on-sightings-at-atomic-sites-1952":"In a 1952 Look magazine article titled 'The Hunt for the Flying Saucer,' Captain Edward J. Ruppelt, then the Air Force's chief UFO investigator, is quoted as saying that 'many of the sightings reported had originated at one atomic weapons related site or another all around the country.' Jesse Michels reads this out himself in two episodes, one with a researcher of UFOs at nuclear bases named Robert Hastings, as early official evidence that UFOs were drawn to America's nuclear sites.","ruppelt-pressured-to-debunk-his-own-book-1956":"Edward J. Ruppelt, who wrote The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects, published in 1956, was later pushed into debunking his own book, according to the researcher Richard Dolan, who tells Michels without naming a source that Ruppelt \"absolutely was pressured\" to do so. Dolan does not say who applied the pressure. If true, it would mean the author of one of the first serious UFO books was made to take back what he had written.","ruppelt-s-account-of-early-ufo-hypotheses-1947":"In the early years of UFO reports, investigators worked through a list of explanations: was it human, was it Russian, could it \"somehow be Nazi technology?\" A researcher named Jason Simosa says this comes from the 1956 book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects by Edward J. Ruppelt, who wrote about the Air Force's early UFO investigations. Simosa's point is that officials at the time seriously considered secret earthly origins before anything else.","ruppelt-s-book-reports-metallic-spheres-in-formation-1950":"The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects, published in 1956 by Captain Edward J. Ruppelt, the first director of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, is said to report metallic spheres flying in formation. Marik von Rennenkampff, a former Defense Department official who writes about UFOs, points Michels to the book as evidence that the man running the Air Force's own study took such sightings seriously, though he quotes none of its words.","ruppelt-s-serious-cases-cluster-near-nuclear-facilities-1950":"Most of the 1950s UFO sightings that Edward J. Ruppelt took seriously in his 1956 book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects happened near sensitive American nuclear facilities, Michels says. He makes the point himself while debating the UFO skeptic Mick West, turning West's own cited book against him. If the pattern holds, it would suggest whatever was seen had a particular interest in nuclear weapons sites.","ruppelt-s-study-of-sightings-near-nuclear-sites-1947":"Some of the earliest official interest in UFOs seen near American nuclear sites goes back to a study by a man named Edward J. Ruppelt. Michels says it ran from 1947 to 1952 and that Ruppelt wrote a book about it in 1956. Michels mentions it in passing during a debate with the UFO skeptic Mick West, as an early record of the pattern he wants to point to: sightings bunching up around nuclear facilities.","russell-targ-coming-around-to-remote-viewing-as-precognition":"Remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe faraway places or hidden targets using only the mind, and Russell Targ, a figure in that research, may have come to think it is really a kind of precognition, sensing the future, and not seeing at a distance. Eric Wargo, a writer on prophetic dreams, says this to Michels, hedging that he \"think[s]\" Targ \"has sort of come around to that idea.\" If true, it would mean the same ability lies behind remote viewing and dreams that come true.","russell-targ-saw-real-miracles-while-working-as-a-stage-magi":"The physicist Russell Targ, known for research into psychic abilities, is said to have worked as a stage magician when he was young and to have seen \"actual miracles\" during that time. The magician Chris Ramsay tells Michels that Targ mentioned this himself. Coming from a man trained in tricks, it would suggest that real unexplained events can happen alongside staged ones.","russia-and-china-retrieval-teams-and-the-gentleman-s-agreeme":"Russia and China each have their own teams for recovering craft of NHI, short for non-human intelligence, and have reverse engineered the technology, meaning taken it apart to learn how to build it, to a level \"pretty much on par\" with the US, according to Michael Herrera, a former Marine who says he saw a huge craft in the Indonesian jungle. Herrera passes this on as what he was told, linked to talk on Capitol Hill, and the story suggests a quiet understanding among the three powers about these recoveries.","russia-used-nuclear-material-to-bait-ufos":"The claim is that in Russia, nuclear material was used as bait to draw UFOs in. If true, it would mean the objects were thought to be attracted to nuclear sources. Michels says it in passing to his guest with an \"apparently\", without naming where he heard it.","russian-admiral-the-phenomenon-s-reality-isn-t-in-question-1990":"Sometime in the 1990s, a Russian admiral told a visiting investigator that whether UFOs exist was not even up for discussion: if you are here to ask me whether the phenomenon is real, he said through an interpreter, \"the door is right over there.\" The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels this from his own experience, as the man who sat down with the admiral. If the story holds, it means a senior Russian military officer took the phenomenon's reality as settled.","russian-deception-as-source-of-some-uap":"Some UAP, the government's term for UFOs, may be Russian deception operations meant to fool the US military, says Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA, adding that \"the Russians in particular really do a lot of this.\" He tells Michels this in a conversation that also brings up General George Patton, and if right, some sightings would be staged tricks rather than anything unknown.","russian-dna-phantom-experiments-1990":"In the 1990s, Russian scientists described what they called DNA phantom experiments, which Michels calls even more bizarre than what came before in his account. He tells it himself, without naming the scientists or a source, and gives little detail of what the experiments found; he raises it as a sign that DNA may behave in ways mainstream science does not expect.","russian-earthquake-weapons-could-set-off-an-earthquake-at-ye":"Russia supposedly has weapons that can cause earthquakes, and they could set one off at Yellowstone that would 'wipe out the center US.' Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, a foreign power could set off a natural disaster across the middle of the country.","russian-general-alexeyev-in-german-magazine-2000":"A Russian general named Vasily Alexeyev is said to have told a German magazine in 2000 that UFOs would turn up whenever sensitive scientific technology was being moved. Michels tells this himself, on two episodes, without naming the magazine, and in the second he gives only the claim, not the magazine or the year. If true, it would mean a senior Russian officer publicly linked UFOs to the transport of secret technology.","russian-high-school-taught-moon-landing-hoax":"A friend of Jesse Michels who is from Russia told him that in high school they were taught the moon landing was a hoax. Michels passes this on himself, without naming the friend, as a sign of how differently the Moon landing is presented in Russia.","russian-intelligence-sends-an-armed-team-to-protect-cia-offi-1993":"During the 1993 attempt to overthrow Boris Yeltsin, Russian intelligence is said to have sent a team of heavily armed men to keep CIA officers from being captured at the ambassador's residence in Moscow. Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer who was there, tells Michels that his Russian contact told him: \"Okay, Ralph, here's some very extraordinary circumstances. We're going to send you a team of heavily armed guys to try to keep you from getting captured.\" He also names Tom Pickering in the story. If it happened that way, the two rival spy services were briefly protecting each other in the middle of a coup.","russian-officials-studying-ufos":"Russian officials have said publicly, the claim goes, that Russia has people studying the UFO question. Robert Salas, a former Air Force missile officer who says a UFO shut down ten nuclear missiles at his base, tells Michels this without naming the officials, as a sign that it is not only America taking the matter seriously.","russian-snow-alien-body-found-in-siberia-2011":"In the Irkutsk region of Siberia, two Russian men named Timur and Kirill are said to have come across an alien body lying in the snowy woods, about a month after a UFO was reported in the same area. Michels tells it himself on two episodes. The second time he gives no year and credits the story to someone named Marshall, not the two men. If true, it would mean a body from a craft had turned up in the open in Russia.","russian-triangle-event-as-pre-arranged-meeting-or-transfer-2018":"A 2018 event over the Pacific Ocean off Kamchatka, in eastern Russia, involving triangle-shaped objects, may have been a planned meeting or handover. Matthew Brown, a guest presented as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells Michels: \"I've come to believe that that was some sort of pre-arranged meeting, for whatever purpose.\" If so, the encounter was arranged in advance by someone rather than happening by chance.","russians-beaming-negative-mental-energy-at-chess-opponents":"In chess matches, the Russians are said to have tried to beam negative mental energy at their players' opponents. Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA, tells Michels this, without naming a source. If true, it would mean Russia treated mind power as a weapon even at the chessboard.","ruth-paine-and-marina-find-the-walker-note-after-the-fbi-sea-1963":"After President Kennedy was killed in 1963, and after the FBI had already searched the house thoroughly, Ruth Paine, the woman whose Texas home Marina Oswald was living in, is said to have come forward with another note Lee Harvey Oswald had left for his wife Marina. This was the note linked to an attempt on Edwin Walker, a retired Army general. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells this on the show, quoting Paine as saying, \"Oh, me and Marina found this other note,\" and he raises it to suggest that the late discovery of the note, missed by the FBI's search, casts doubt on Paine's role.","ruth-paine-at-naushon-island-retreat-with-dulles-s-mistress-1963":"In the summer of 1963, Ruth Paine, a figure in the story of President Kennedy's assassination, vacationed on Naushon Island near Martha's Vineyard, a retreat linked to the Forbes family, alongside Mary Bancroft, the mistress of the former CIA director Allen Dulles. A lawyer and researcher named Dan Sheehan confirms this to Michels from his own research, without naming a source, as a link between Paine and the Dulles circle months before the assassination.","ruth-paine-houses-lee-harvey-oswald-and-marina-in-texas-1963":"In 1963, the year of the Kennedy assassination, a woman named Ruth Paine is said to have taken Lee Harvey Oswald and his wife Marina into her house in Texas. The researcher Peter Levenda tells it on the show: \"they're living with Ruth Payne in this house in Texas.\" Michels brings her up in another episode, linking her by family to a wealthy circle he places around a woman named Mary Bancroft. He describes Paine as someone's daughter \"who takes in Lee Harvey Oswald.\" They tell it because it would put the accused assassin under the roof of a well-connected family.","ruth-paine-s-sister-works-for-the-cia-paine-brings-the-oswal-1963":"In 1963 a woman named Ruth Paine is said to have brought Lee Harvey Oswald and his wife Marina from New Orleans to Dallas, and her sister worked for the CIA. Dan Sheehan, a guest who talks to Michels about the Kennedy assassination, tells it as something that \"turns out\" to be so. If true, it would put a CIA connection close to Oswald in the months before he shot Kennedy.","ryan-graves-says-go-fast-was-recorded-by-his-buddy-2015":"One of the Navy videos of unidentified objects that the New York Times made public in 2017, a clip filmed off Jacksonville, Florida, in 2015 and known as Go Fast, was recorded by a close friend of the Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves, according to Graves. He tells Michels he first saw the video in a classified space and says of his \"very good buddy\" who recorded it, \"I'm not going to say his name,\" though in his own words he never names which video he means. Michels later repeats it, saying Graves, who has also testified before Congress under oath, \"kind of admitted it was a buddy of his\"; if so, the video would come with a known, if unnamed, crew member behind it.","s-4-facility-hand-scanner-corridor-and-nine-hangars-1988":"Bob Lazar describes a secret site called S-4, near Papoose Lake in the Nevada desert, as a place you got into past a hand scanner and down a corridor, with nine hangars inside. Lazar, who says he worked there in 1988 and 1989, tells Michels this from his own memory of the place. If true, it would describe a hidden facility housing something worth nine hangars of secrecy.","s-force-triangular-fire-team-trained-on-murchison-ranch-in-m-1960":"Between 1960 and 1963, a team linked to Fort Huachuca, an Army base in Arizona, supposedly set up a base on the Mexico ranch of the Texas oilman Clint Murchison Jr. There it trained a 'triangular fire team' to practise shooting a moving target from three different spots at once. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this while talking about the killing of President Kennedy. He names Carl Jenkins and the mob boss Santo Trafficante Jr. but gives no source. If true, it would suggest the shooting in Dallas was rehearsed ahead of time by an organised group.","s4-imagery-covered-up-on-google-earth-project-gravitar-docum-1980":"The claim is that someone has hidden S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, from Google Earth, but that older satellite pictures still show it. S4 matters because of a man named Bob Lazar, whose claims about the site are why people go looking for it, and the evidence is said to appear in Project Gravitar, a documentary about the search. Michels tells it in his own words: the documentary has the data, he says, \"but it's like stuff they tried to cover up on Google Earth.\" A guest who goes by the online name UAPGerb passes on the work of a researcher named Luigi Vendittelli, who \"painstakingly\" tries to show how \"foliage is rearranged around S4 sites,\" and places S4 near Papoose Lake. In a later episode Michels adds that Vendittelli used Google Earth's historical imagery to find \"a 2022 version of the area surrounding S4 that was not yet blurred to obscure the site,\" showing vehicle tracks around it. If it is right, a site the government has never acknowledged would be visible on the ground, and someone would have taken the trouble to blur it.","s4-project-directives-duplicate-propulsion-disable-craft-at-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site called S4 in 1988 and 1989, tells Michels the project had two orders. The first was to \"duplicate the propulsion system at any cost\". The second was to \"be able to disable the system at a distance at any cost\". If true, the government wanted both to copy the craft and to be able to bring them down.","sac-telegram-on-echo-flight-demands-boeing-investigation-1967":"In 1967 a group of nuclear missiles called Echo Flight shut down at Malmstrom Air Force Base, and a telegram from Strategic Air Command, the Air Force command then in charge of nuclear missiles and bombers, demanded that Boeing investigate why. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas tells Michels this. He says the telegram was among the documents his side got from the Air Force, and he tells it because an official demand for an inquiry shows the Air Force itself took the shutdown seriously.","sagan-and-shklovskii-s-intelligent-life-in-the-universe-1960":"The astronomer Carl Sagan once co-wrote a book about life beyond Earth, Intelligent Life in the Universe, with a Soviet scientist, Iosif Shklovsky. The researcher and philosopher Jason Jorjani mentions it in passing on the show without naming a source, as an example of an American and a Soviet scientist working together on the question of alien intelligence during the Cold War.","sagan-bumped-up-against-the-ufo-program":"The astronomer Carl Sagan, according to Bob Maguire, a former CIA contractor, ran into a secret UFO program and was told he needed to change what he was doing. Maguire tells Michels this is his belief rather than something he knows to be a fact, and names no source. If true, it would suggest one of the best-known public sceptics was steered by people who knew more.","sagan-in-venus-anomaly-meeting-with-white-house-science-advi":"A man named Bob Maguire, described on the show as a CIA contractor, says he once walked into a meeting about something unusual on or around Venus and found that the room held \"maybe the most famous venusian planet scientist that ever lived,\" Carl Sagan. Maguire tells it as someone who was there, and he places it in the office of the chief scientist of the NSA during the Clinton years, at a meeting about moving technology into wider use. Michels, retelling it later, puts the meeting with the White House science adviser \"or something like that.\" If true, it would put Sagan inside a closed government discussion of a Venus puzzle.","sagan-s-1963-paper-estimating-et-visitations-and-lunar-bases":"In 1963 the astronomer Carl Sagan published a technical paper that used the Drake equation, a formula for estimating how many civilizations might exist in the galaxy, to estimate the number of advanced alien civilizations and, as the story goes, how often they might have visited Earth and whether they could have left bases on the Moon. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani describes the paper to Michels, as evidence that a mainstream scientist once took such visits seriously.","sagan-s-1975-paper-on-detecting-artificial-surface-structure":"The astronomer Carl Sagan is said to have written a paper in 1975 arguing that one way to look for advanced technological life on other planets is to look for artificial structures on their surfaces. Maaneli Derakhshani, a guest talking with Michels about the Moon, brings up the paper. Its point here is that searching other worlds for built structures was once a serious scientific idea.","sagan-s-early-diaries-about-ufos-1950":"The astronomer Carl Sagan, later known for doubting UFO claims, wrote about UFOs all the time in his early diaries, Michels says on two episodes. He tells it himself, without saying where he read the diaries. He brings it up to suggest that Sagan's public skepticism hid a long private interest.","sagan-s-nsa-contractor-green-badge":"The astronomer Carl Sagan is said to have carried a green contractor badge with his photo on it from the National Security Agency, the NSA. Bob Maguire, a former CIA contractor, tells Michels he saw it, and says \"you don't get that if you're casual.\" If so, Sagan had a real working tie to the agency, not just a passing one.","sagan-whispers-keep-up-the-great-work-to-hoagland":"The astronomer Carl Sagan is said to have walked up to a researcher named Richard Hoagland at a hearing, put a hand on his shoulder, whispered \"Keep up the great work\" in his ear and walked off. A guest named Maaneli Derakhshani passes the story on, as Hoagland told it, in an episode about secrets of the Moon. If true, it would be a quiet sign of support for Hoagland from one of the best-known scientists of his time.","sagancarlotto-correspondence-on-the-face-on-mars-1980":"The astronomer Carl Sagan is said to have written letters to Mark Carlotto, one of the first researchers of the Face on Mars (a rock formation in NASA photos of Mars's Cydonia region that looks like a human face), and to have shown interest in the analyses of it. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani tells Michels this. He also names Gerald Soffen but does not say where the letters can be found. If true, Sagan took the Face more seriously in private than he did in public.","sages-helped-by-the-gods-founded-a-new-civilization-at-lake":"Around Lake Van, in eastern Turkey, a group of very wise sages who were \"literally being helped by the gods, as they describe it\" created a new civilization. The researcher of ancient texts Matt LaCroix tells Michels this, without naming the texts he draws on. If true, it would mean a civilization was restarted there with outside help after some great loss.","saic-as-contractor-for-psionic-testing-near-crane-1980":"The theory is that SAIC, a defence contracting company that sits right next to Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy research base in Indiana, was the contractor that carried out psionic testing, meaning tests of psychic abilities, apparently in the 1980s. If so, government psychic research would have run through a private company. Michels passes it on as someone else's belief, and the names attached to it are a person called Sammy and a man named James Woodward.","saic-connection-to-crane":"SAIC, a large American defence contractor, is said to have done work with Crane, a Navy research and weapons centre in Indiana, and Michels hints that this tie matters to the hidden study of UFOs. He says an online researcher who goes by UAP Gerb (UAP being the official term for UFOs, unidentified anomalous phenomena) has dug up a lot of research on that work, though he does not say what it shows.","saic-exotic-science-and-stargate-links":"SAIC (Science Applications International Corporation), a defence contractor based in Reston, Virginia, has openly studied exotic propulsion, anti-gravity, directed-energy weapons and other fringe science, Michels says, and seems to have worked hand in hand with Stargate, the CIA's psychic spy programme, carrying on \"remote viewing\" work, the attempt to see distant places with the mind, that it took over from the CIA. Michels tells this himself, calling it \"all public information\", and on a later episode adds that a former US intelligence chief named Bobby Ray Inman sat on the company's board and that SAIC \"seemed to possibly be involved\" with retrieving crashed UFOs. If true, a private contractor would sit where psychic research, exotic physics and UFO recovery meet.","sal-pais-applauds-rossi-s-panel-example-2023":"Dave Rossi, a guest who talks about advanced physics, says that about two summers before the interview, around 2023, he sat on a panel with an inventor named Salvatore Pais and a handful of others. He says he used the very example he had just given Michels, and that Pais started clapping. Rossi tells it as a sign that someone in that field took his idea seriously.","salas-rebuts-the-emp-explanation-1967":"The Wall Street Journal offered an explanation for the 1967 events at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana: an electromagnetic pulse, or EMP, a burst of energy that can knock out electronics. A man named Robert Salas rejected it. Michels relays his answer, quoting Salas as saying that \"it would have been irresponsible and unthinkable for the U.S. Air Force to jeopardize the operational status of these weapons\". If Salas is right, the EMP account can't explain what happened to the weapons at the base.","salas-s-14-point-wsj-rebuttal-2025":"A former Air Force missile officer named Robert Salas says that in 2025 he wrote a rebuttal listing 14 reasons why EMP testing could not have shut down his nuclear missiles. EMP means electromagnetic pulse, the burst of energy a nuclear blast gives off, which can knock out electronics. He tells Michels he wrote it in answer to a Wall Street Journal article published on June 6, 2025, \"The Pentagon Disinformation That Fueled America's UFO Mythology\", which reported the shutdown as an EMP test. Salas holds that a UFO shut the missiles down, and if he is right, the official explanation fails.","salas-s-2-5-hour-aaro-interview":"Robert Salas, a former Air Force officer whose case, as the episode puts it, is about a UFO that shut down ten nuclear missiles, says he gave a two and a half hour account to AARO, the Pentagon office set up to look into UFO reports. He tells Michels he also handed over documents to back up what he said. He does not say when the interview took place, and he tells it to show that his case is on the official record.","salty-liquid-water-on-the-martian-surface-2015":"In 2015, researchers said they had at last found clear evidence of salty liquid water flowing on the surface of Mars. Michels raises it himself in an episode about whether Mars once had life, with the remote viewer Joe McMoneagle (remote viewing is the claimed ability to describe distant places with the mind). He mentions it because flowing water would make life on Mars more plausible.","salvatore-pais-filed-patents-at-pax-river-2010":"A Navy engineer named Salvatore Pais filed patents for strange flight technology while working at Naval Air Station Patuxent River, a Navy base in Maryland known as Pax River. The patents include a \"hybrid craft using an inertial mass modification device\", which is a way of making a vehicle act as if it had less mass, along with a high-frequency gravitational wave generator and a high-energy electromagnetic generator. Pais tells Michels he filed five applications and three became patents, and says the two that did not were \"the most mundane\". The researcher UAPGerb places him at Pax River when he filed them, and Michels dates the patents to 2018, saying they hold \"basically everything that you would need to build a UFO\", which is why the story matters to the tellers.","salvatore-pais-inertial-mass-reduction-patent-2016":"In 2016 the US Navy filed a patent naming the inventor Salvatore Pais for a 'craft using an inertial mass reduction device', meaning a vehicle that would make itself resist motion less so it could move in ways ordinary aircraft cannot. Michels tells this himself, citing the patent by its own title. He brings it up because it suggests the Navy was formally pursuing technology like what UFOs are said to do.","same-distances-encoded-at-roman-sites":"The same distances, 5,300 metres and 2,300 metres, are built into Roman sites and into hillsides laid out like the White Horse Hills of Wiltshire in England, according to Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher of the pyramids. He tells Michels this from his own study, as a sign that a shared ancient measuring system ran through very different places.","same-words-do-not-fear-recalled-by-both-men-under-hypnosis-1977":"Two Air Force security guards who say they were abducted in 1977 near nuclear missile sites at Ellsworth Air Force Base, South Dakota, both remember the same words: \"Do not fear.\" Michels tells it in his own words, about the guards Mario Woods and Michael Johnson. In one episode he frames the memory as coming out under hypnosis: \"Two men, two rooms, hours apart, neither in earshot of the other. The same three words.\" In another he says simply that they heard the words \"over and over again\" before the abduction. The written record is a 2017 article by the researcher Robert Hastings, which carries an account Woods wrote for him. In it Woods recalls \"voices in my head, more than one, saying, 'Do not fear', over and over.\" He also says that two weeks after that night, his partner told him he \"had heard voices, many voices, saying not to be afraid.\" At the time of writing, Hastings had only \"arranged for hypnotic regression sessions,\" meaning hypnosis used to try to bring back forgotten memories. So in the 2017 text, both men already hold the shared words in their waking memory from 1977, before any hypnosis, and the article does not report what the sessions later turned up. If the match is real, two men remembered the same message on their own, which would point to a shared experience.","sami-shaman-s-amanita-muscaria-winter-solstice-ritual":"Among the Sami, the native people of Lapland in the far north of Europe, a shaman or healer is said to have travelled with reindeer to collect Amanita muscaria, the red-and-white mushroom that can cause visions, and to have dried it over a fire for a winter-solstice ritual. Tero Isokauppila, a guest who talks with Michels about mushrooms, tells it as old local tradition. If true, it would show that the mushroom has long had a part in sacred winter rites.","sammaciccia-s-guess-about-ss-eugenics-programs-continued-aft-1950":"After the Second World War, the SS's eugenics programs, Nazi attempts to breed 'better' humans, supposedly went on in secret in underground facilities. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels that this occurred to him while reading the account of a man named Bruno Sammaciccia, and he calls it rumour. If true, some of the beings in such accounts could have come from those hidden programs.","sammy-hagar-s-love-walks-in-lyrics-came-from-nowhere-1986":"The rock singer Sammy Hagar does not know where the words to his 1986 song Love Walks In came from. That is according to Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had repeated encounters with UFOs, who tells Michels that Hagar told him this himself. If it is true, it would put a famous song among the stories of ideas that seem to arrive from outside the person who writes them down.","samples-from-the-baltic-sea-anomaly-test-as-burned-organic-m-2011":"The Baltic Sea anomaly is a strangely shaped object on the floor of the Baltic Sea that a treasure-hunting team found and that some call a UFO on the seabed. The treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, who led the dives, tells Michels that in 2011 and 2012 they took samples, not from the object itself but from material lying on it, and that the result was \"organic burned natural\" matter.","san-jose-woman-s-disc-that-was-bigger-inside-than-outside":"A woman in San Jose, California, saw a big disc over her house, went inside it, and found it far bigger inside than out: to her it seemed like a movie theater, and a being there led her up a staircase. The longtime UFO researcher Jacques Vallée tells it twice on the show as what the woman told him, and Michels retells it citing Vallée, adding the movie-theater detail. If true, the craft did something ordinary objects cannot, holding more space inside than its outside allows.","sandia-history-lists-kissinger-as-a-seminar-expert-at-kirtla-1961":"Henry Kissinger appears in a redacted history of Sandia Laboratories, a nuclear weapons lab at Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, as one of the experts at seminars around 1961, Jesse Michels points out. He reads from it himself: a man named Don Carter 'organized interdisciplinary seminars featuring noted international experts and specialists, one of them, Henry Kissinger.' Michels raises it in an episode asking whether Kissinger oversaw a programme to recover crashed UFOs; the passage places Kissinger at the base but says nothing about what the seminars covered.","santa-anastasia-excavation-yields-giant-skeletons-1978":"Whole skeletons of giants were dug up at the Church of Santa Anastasia on the Italian island of Sardinia during an excavation between 1978 and 1981, and the bones were carried into the church. That is what the documentary filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels, passing on what someone there told him. If it were true, it would be physical evidence of the giants of old stories.","santa-claus-tradition-traced-back-to-the-sami":"The Santa Claus tradition can be followed back along its routes to the Sami, the reindeer-herding people of Lapland in the far north of Europe. Tero Isokauppila, a Finnish entrepreneur who works with mushrooms, tells Michels it came to New York through the Dutch, who had \"Sinterklaas, their own version of Santa Claus,\" and traces it back through Germany, Italy and Turkey, without naming a source. If he is right, a modern Christmas figure would carry traces of an old northern tradition.","sar-doppler-tomography-paper-on-vesuvius-with-the-national-r-2019":"Filippo Biondi, a researcher who scans underground structures with satellite radar (known as SAR), says he looked beneath Mount Vesuvius near Naples with this method in 2019. He says he wrote it up in a paper, \"a very very nice paper,\" with the National Research Council of Italy. He tells Michels about it as the earlier work behind his later radar scans beneath the pyramids.","sar-doppler-tomography-research-started-in-2018":"Research meant to look beneath the pyramids of Giza, Egypt, using satellite radar began in 2018, two years before any papers came out. Filippo Biondi, a researcher who took part, tells Michels this. The method he uses is called SAR Doppler tomography, a way of reading the echoes of synthetic aperture radar (radar that images the ground from satellites) to picture what lies under the surface. The date matters because it shows the work behind his claim of a vast structure under the pyramids had been going on for six or seven years.","sar-scan-shows-shaft-system-coming-out-of-the-bottom-of-the-2020":"A 2020 paper by Filippo Biondi and a colleague named Corrado used SAR Doppler tomography, a way of building pictures from radar signals, to look inside the Great Pyramid of Giza. According to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum, the scan shows \"a shaft system coming out of the bottom of the Queen's Chamber\", one of the rooms inside the pyramid. Drum summarises the paper on the show himself, and he presents the finding as a sign of hidden structure beneath the chamber.","sar-team-detects-the-big-void-in-a-transverse-east-west-posi-2020":"A researcher named Filippo Biondi says his team, using synthetic aperture radar (a way of building images from radar signals, usually taken from satellites), detected the Big Void inside Egypt's Great Pyramid, a large hidden empty space, and placed it lying crosswise, east to west. Biondi tells Michels he worked on this himself, with a colleague he names as Corrado, and dates the work to 2018 to 2020. He stresses that 'these are not simulated data. Are real data,' and says the scans also pick up the Grand Gallery, a long sloping passage inside the pyramid, which would suggest the method can see real structure through stone.","sar-tomography-detects-king-s-chamber-relieving-chamber-beam-2020":"A radar study of the Great Pyramid of Giza, published as a paper in 2020, is said to show the King's Chamber, the stone beams of the chambers above it that take the weight off it, and the stone box called the sarcophagus inside it. The researcher Jeffrey Drum walks Michels through the images, citing the Italian researcher Filippo Biondi. The method is SAR tomography, which uses radar signals from satellites to build up a picture of what lies inside a structure. Pointing at the colours, Drum says the red shows \"a very strong vibrational signature from inside of the structure\", which would mean the scan can see the pyramid's hidden insides.","sar-tomography-mapping-of-the-gran-sasso-laboratory":"A radar technique that uses satellite signals to see below ground, called SAR tomography, was tested on the Gran Sasso Laboratory in Italy, which sits 1.4 kilometres inside a mountain, and mapped it out. Jay Anderson, who hosts his own show, tells Michels this in connection with the researcher Filippo Biondi, as proof that the method can reveal hidden structures deep underground.","sarbacher-craft-at-wright-patterson-taken-over-by-anderson-t-1940":"According to a scientist named Robert Sarbacher, a craft was being held at Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio, and was then taken over by a group called the Anderson trust, which the story ties to Karl Compton of MIT. If true, a recovered craft would have passed out of the military's hands into a private circle of scientists. Michels relays it as what Sarbacher says, citing along the way names such as the researcher William Steinman, the Canadian engineer Wilbert B. Smith and the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown.","sarbacher-involved-in-standing-up-the-program-1940":"A man named Robert Sarbacher was involved in setting up the secret programs that David Grusch, a whistleblower who says he uncovered them, describes. That is how Grusch puts it, calling Sarbacher \"purportedly involved in the stand up\" of what he found. Michels later relays Grusch as saying on record that Sarbacher set up UFO secrecy in 1954, and ties Sarbacher to J. Robert Oppenheimer and to a man named Thomas Townsend Brown, which would place the start of the secrecy with named scientists of that era.","sarbacher-karl-compton-briefed-on-ufo-technology-at-wright-f-1950":"In 1950, Karl Compton, then president of MIT, was briefed on UFO technology at Wright Field, an Air Force airfield in Ohio, according to a man named Robert Sarbacher, who is said to have told this to a nuclear engineer and UFO researcher named Stanton Friedman. Michels repeats Sarbacher's account as Friedman reported it; in another episode he puts it more strongly, saying the MIT president was rumoured to have been shown the craft itself, without naming Compton, Sarbacher or a date. If true, it would mean one of America's leading scientists was let in on recovered UFO hardware.","sarbacher-known-to-australian-nuclear-engineers-woomera-1950":"The American scientist Robert Sarbacher was known to some Australian nuclear engineers in the 1950s, a story tied to Woomera, Australia's remote rocket and weapons test site. Michels mentions it only in passing, saying the Australian journalist Ross Coulthart \"has some interesting data points\" about those engineers, and gives no details himself.","sarbacher-picks-up-townsend-brown-after-the-paris-experiment-1956":"In 1956, a researcher named Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have run an experiment in Paris showing that the Biefeld-Brown effect, a push produced by high-voltage electric charge, works even in a vacuum. Michels says this hinted at a link between electromagnetism and gravity, and that when Brown returned to the US he was picked up in a black Cadillac by a man named Robert Sarbacher. Michels tells it himself without naming a source. He tells it because it would suggest that someone in the US was keeping close watch on early work toward controlling gravity.","sarbacher-put-cameras-on-modified-v2-rockets-to-chase-ufos-1947":"The story is that after the war, scientists put cameras on modified V2s, the rockets Germany built in the Second World War, and sent them up to chase and photograph UFOs. Michels tells it twice. Once he says the son of a scientist named Robert Sarbacher has gone on record that his father told him he did this himself. The other time he credits the work to an American inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown and does not mention the son.","sarbacher-s-letter-to-william-steinman-1980":"Robert Sarbacher, whom Michels calls a student of Einstein and a well-known Harvard physicist, is said to have admitted late in life, in writing to a UFO researcher named William Steinman, that the rumors about crashed UFOs were \"substantially correct.\" Michels comes back to this on several episodes, with no source beyond the letter itself. In his version Steinman wrote first, in 1987, to ask whether there really was a UFO program, and Sarbacher's answer named people he said were involved, among them Vannevar Bush, John von Neumann and J. Robert Oppenheimer. On one episode Michels says Sarbacher, who worked directly under Bush, is \"on record in letters\" saying that Bush \"was directly involved in UFO research.\" He also ties Sarbacher to Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor Michels profiles, as a close colleague. A researcher named Grant Cameron tells it a different way, citing a taped conversation with Sarbacher from 1983 rather than a letter, and saying Sarbacher described being invited to a briefing about a crash at an Air Force base around 1950. If true, it would put some of the best-known scientists of the twentieth century inside a secret program built around crashed craft.","sarbacher-s-secret-atomic-lab-in-washington-dc-1950":"In the 1950s a scientist named Robert Sarbacher is said to have run atomic research out of an ordinary house in Washington DC, and a newspaper reported that he had asked to renew a permit for it at that home address. Jan Lundquist, a researcher in a documentary about an inventor named Townsend Brown, tells this on the show. If true, it would mean secret atomic work was going on in a private house in the capital.","sarbacher-said-von-neumann-was-directly-involved-1950":"The scientist John von Neumann was directly involved in the government's secret UFO work of the 1950s, according to a man named Robert Sarbacher. Michels passes this on in a single line, as something Sarbacher said, without saying to whom or giving more detail; if true, it would put one of the century's great minds inside the secret.","sarbacher-tells-strieber-about-battelle-materials-1980":"In the 1980s, a scientist named Robert Sarbacher is said to have described in detail the materials he had worked on at the Battelle Memorial Institute, a research institute. The author Whitley Strieber tells Michels he heard this himself: a man he calls only Sam gave him Sarbacher's number, then said Sarbacher was \"very eager to talk\", and on the phone Sarbacher told him \"all kinds of detail\" about the materials. If true, it would be a working scientist giving a first-hand account of hands-on work with them.","sardinian-elders-dug-up-giant-bones-with-jewellery-1930":"Older people on the Italian island of Sardinia say that in the mid-1900s they dug up, with their own hands, the bones of giants buried with jewellery, which would mean a race of giant humans once lived there. The researcher Tim Alberino tells Michels he interviewed \"dozens of people in Sardinia\" who had done this themselves, work he connects to his film True Legends.","sardinians-said-to-descend-from-giants":"Some people on the island of Sardinia say they are descended from giants. The documentary filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels this, repeating what local people told him, as part of his larger case that giants once lived there.","sarfatti-s-claimed-non-human-intelligence-experience-1970":"A man named Jack Sarfatti says he had an encounter with a UFO or a \"non-human intelligence\" (the field's term for intelligent beings that are not human) back in the 1970s. Bob Maguire passes this on, saying Sarfatti has been \"on this problem for 50 years\", ever since he first made that claim.","sarfatti-s-navy-friend-and-the-living-craft-on-the-east-coas":"At a Navy facility on the US East Coast, the story goes, people held a craft they could not get inside, because the craft is alive and conscious and will only let in someone who \"vibrate[s] at the right level\". The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something he heard from the physicist Jack Sarfatti, who had it from a friend in the Navy. Cameron says the craft \"works\" and has \"an attitude\". If true, it would mean that efforts to take apart and copy such craft run into a machine that chooses who comes in.","sars-spike-protein-modification-proposal-rejected-by-darpa-m-2017":"In 2017, Michels says, EcoHealth Alliance, a US nonprofit that studied bat viruses, sent DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, a proposal to modify a SARS virus so that its spike protein could latch onto ACE2, the receptor on human cells that such viruses use to get in. The story as framed holds that DARPA turned it down and the work moved to the Wuhan Institute of Virology in China, though Michels himself, telling it without naming a source, describes only the submission. If true, it would mean work on this kind of virus was being proposed to the US military years before the pandemic.","satan-and-his-angels-cast-down-to-earth-before-adam":"Long before Adam, Satan and his angels supposedly came to Earth, or were thrown down to it, after a battle in the heavens. A researcher named Tim Alberino tells Michels this as his reading of the biblical story, without naming a source. If true, fallen beings would have been on Earth before the first humans.","satellite-mistaken-for-object-near-jupiter-on-x-2025":"In 2025, people on X passed around an image of what they said was \"a weird thing going around Jupiter or Saturn,\" but the skeptic and writer Michael Shermer says it was a satellite going around the Earth. Shermer tells Michels he watched this play out himself, and he brings it up as an example of how easily ordinary things in the sky get mistaken for something strange.","satellites-shake-over-roraima-s-rare-earths":"When American satellites that scan the planet for minerals pass over the Brazilian state of Roraima, the signal from its rare earths is said to be so strong that the satellites shake violently, almost to the point of falling. Brazil's former defense minister Aldo Rebelo tells Michels this as something that \"is believed\", not as something he saw, and if true it would mean the state sits on unusually rich deposits of these valuable minerals.","saucer-as-contractor-owned-prototype-with-umbilicals-1992":"A saucer-shaped craft seen in a secret hangar at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station, Florida, in 1992, hooked up to umbilicals (cables and lines feeding it from outside), was a prototype owned by a defense contractor, not the military. That is the view of Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor who says he saw it himself. He tells Michels: \"I believe it was probably owned by a defense contractor and was not part of the US Air Force inventory,\" which would mean private companies, not the Air Force, held such a craft.","saucers-fly-on-magnetic-lines-of-force-1950":"In 1950, at the University of Denver, a man named Silas Newton claimed that flying saucers move by riding magnetic lines of force: 'That ship and the others that are flying fly on magnetic lines of force.' Newton's own words are heard on the show, and he speaks as someone who says he knew of such ships. If true, it would offer an explanation of how the craft fly.","saudi-pilots-filmed-orbs-2024":"Two pilots flying over Saudi Arabia are said to have seen and filmed what Michels calls \"crazy orb action,\" meaning glowing balls of light in the sky. Michels brings it up himself in October 2024 as something from the week before, without naming a source. He uses it as a sign that strange sightings were still coming in from around the world.","saul-on-the-road-to-damascus-blinded-by-an-orb":"The light that blinded Saul on the road to Damascus, in the Bible's Book of Acts, was an orb: a glowing ball of light, the kind of thing UFO witnesses describe. Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says he has had many encounters with such lights, tells Michels this as his own reading of the passage. Acts tells of a light from heaven that strikes Saul down and leaves him blind, and Bledsoe sees the same thing in it that he says he has seen himself, though the text itself speaks only of a light.","saw-sharper-uap-video-on-the-inside-than-released-footage-2018":"Inside the Pentagon, around 2018, there were videos of UAP (the government's term for UFOs) that were sharper and clearer than anything released to the public, says Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who has come forward as a whistleblower. He tells Michels he saw them himself and was shown \"a mix of quality\", both in how clear the picture was and in how well it showed what the object was. If true, the government holds better evidence than the public has seen.","scalar-treatment-cures-herrera-s-dog-s-bladder-mass":"Michael Herrera, who says he saw a huge craft in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels that his dog was diagnosed with a bladder mass the vets thought was cancerous, and that a so-called scalar treatment, a kind of energy therapy, cleared it. He tells it as his own experience, saying \"they did this with my dog,\" without saying on the show who \"they\" were. If true, it would mean the treatment can heal a real illness.","scandinavian-ghost-rockets-that-hit-water-1940":"In the 1940s, the story goes, a swarm of at least 1,400 documented rocket- and cigar-shaped objects, called \"ghost rockets\", came down over Norway and Sweden, and they never landed or crashed on the ground but always plunged into water. Michels adds that the craze was serious enough for the Swedish government to form a special military unit. He tells it himself twice, once in a talk with a treasure hunter who says there is a UFO on the seafloor, as a sign that whatever these were had something to do with water.","scandinavian-ufos-come-down-only-in-water-1946":"In the wave of strange sightings over Sweden and Norway in 1946, not a single object came down on land: they all came down in water. The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels this, stressing that \"it is important\", without naming a source here. If true, the pattern would be a clue to what the objects were and why none was ever recovered on land.","scanpyramids-project-uses-muons-to-find-voids-in-the-pyramid-2015":"The story is about a scientific effort called ScanPyramids, begun in 2015, that looks inside the Giza pyramids using muons. Muons are tiny particles that rain down from space and pass through stone, so measuring how many get through can reveal hidden empty spaces. Filippo Biondi, a scientist who has run his own radar scans beneath the pyramids, describes the project to Michels: it 'is using muons which are sub particles that are emitted from the universe.' The researcher Jeffrey Drum, who argues the pyramids were energy machines, adds who is behind it. He says a Japanese team 'has been sponsoring and providing the funding for all of this,' working with Egypt's Ministry of Antiquities, and he points to the team's paper on its muon results. Both bring it up as an established mainstream project whose finds of hidden voids give a backdrop to their own, bolder claims about what lies inside and under the pyramids.","scans-show-a-second-grand-gallery-inside-the-great-pyramid":"Scans of the Great Pyramid at Giza seem to show a second Grand Gallery, a copy of the tall sloping passage already known inside it, sitting above the first. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels this without naming the scans, as a sign that the pyramid still hides large spaces no one has entered.","schauberger-devices-that-reportedly-took-off-1940":"In the 1940s a man named Viktor Schauberger, working in Austria and Czechoslovakia, is said to have built devices that took off and flew. Nick Cook, a writer who has looked into claims of antigravity research, tells Michels these are \"pretty arcane stories,\" and does not say where they come from. If true, it would mean someone had working flying machines of a new kind during or right after the Second World War.","schauberger-s-propulsion-work-taken-to-brookhaven":"An inventor named Viktor Schauberger was tricked by the US Army Counter Intelligence Corps, an Army intelligence unit, into handing over his work on exotic propulsion, and that work ended up at Brookhaven National Laboratory, a US government research lab. Michels tells this himself, pointing to a researcher named Nick Cook and his website as the source; if true, it would mean unusual propulsion ideas were quietly taken into American government science.","schauberger-s-wartime-diary-matches-the-kammler-sites-1942":"The wartime diary of Viktor Schauberger, an Austrian inventor, is said to place him at the same sites, including Jablonec in what was then Czechoslovakia, used by Hans Kammler, the SS general who ran Nazi Germany's secret weapons projects from 1942 to 1945. The British aviation journalist Nick Cook tells Michels he visited the Schauberger family in Austria and they \"opened up their files, the family files\" to him. If the match holds, it would tie Schauberger's work to the Nazis' most secret projects, which Cook links to talk of antigravity craft.","schauberger-taken-by-ss-to-secret-work-1941":"In 1941 an officer of the SS, Nazi Germany's elite force, approached the Austrian inventor Viktor Schauberger, and after that his life went completely silent, which is taken to mean he was put to secret work, with Czechoslovakia named as one place. Michels tells it himself in a documentary episode, drawing on a letter Schauberger wrote to his son Walter Schauberger. If true, it would place Schauberger inside secret Nazi research during the war.","schindele-red-glowing-object-over-minot-1966":"In 1966, a bright red glowing object is said to have hovered over Minot Air Force Base in North Dakota. Michels passes on the memory of a retired Air Force captain named David Schindele, who recalls starting his shift and hearing reports of the object over the base. Michels tells it in an episode about claims that UFOs watch nuclear bases.","schliemann-finds-troy":"Troy was long dismissed as mere myth until Heinrich Schliemann, a German businessman, took Homer seriously and found the city in Anatolia, in modern Turkey, in the 1870s. Michels tells this himself on four episodes, giving 1871 in one, and he names Herodotus as one guide alongside Homer in one telling and Thucydides in another. He uses it to argue that old stories can preserve real history that experts wrote off.","schriever-disc-test-flight-in-prague-1945":"In February 1945, a disc-shaped craft built in Prague is said to have made its first test flight, climbing to 40,000 feet in three minutes. Michels says a German technician named Rudolf Schriever claimed to have worked on it after being recruited by a man named Hans Kammler. Michels tells it himself in an episode with the writer Nick Cook about antigravity, the idea of craft that cancel or beat gravity. If true, it would mean Nazi Germany had flying discs before anyone reported UFOs.","schriever-s-1944-disc-flight":"A German technician named Rudolf Schriever claimed he worked on a disc-shaped craft that finally flew and climbed to 40,000 feet in a few minutes. If true, Nazi Germany would have flown a working flying disc. Michels tells it twice and his details differ: once he puts the flight in 1944, and once in Prague in February 1945, climbing in 3 minutes.","schrodinger-s-1944-what-is-life-lectures-on-life-s-attractio":"The physicist Erwin Schrödinger argued, in what Michels calls his great 1944 lecture series What is Life?, that life is drawn toward order, and Michels links this to the biologist Michael Levin at Tufts, who he says is finding that \"biological cells have clear goals and intentions.\" Michels tells this himself in his own framing of an episode on reality as a hologram. If right, it would mean purpose is built into living things down to single cells.","schrodinger-s-what-is-life-and-aperiodic-crystals-in-dna-1944":"In his book What Is Life?, which Michels dates to 1944, the physicist Erwin Schrödinger wrote about 'aperiodic crystals' in the material of heredity and described them as important stores of information. Michels raises this himself in two episodes, reading it as an early hint that DNA works as a receiver of information.","schumer-amendment-to-ndaa-gutted-mike-turner-role-2023":"The UAP Disclosure Act, an amendment put forward in the US Senate on 13 July 2023 by Chuck Schumer and Mike Rounds to the yearly defense bill, to force release of government records on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), was 'entirely gutted' before it passed, says Jesse Michels. He says this himself, pointing to Congressman Mike Turner's role in it, as proof of how hard parts of government push back against disclosure.","schumer-s-64-page-uap-amendment-2023":"In 2023 Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer put forward a bill that, the tellers say, wrote non-human technology and the recovery of crashed craft into the law of Congress. The whistleblower David Grusch tells Michels Schumer was 'compelled' to write a 'big 64 page amendment' about non-human tech and recoveries. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff asks how 'nonhuman intelligence' got into a bill also backed by Marco Rubio and Kirsten Gillibrand, and says it mentions the phrase 22 times along with biological evidence; Michels puts it at 65 mentions of UAP, the official word for UFOs, and NHI, short for non-human intelligence. Karl Nell, speaking as a former official, says the bill amounted to Congress saying it 'is not being told the truth', and Dan Sheehan, a guest who traces the history of American secret programs, says the law that came out of it, signed by the president, orders the military services to gather their UFO records back to 1945. The Congressional Record of 13 July 2023 prints the amendment, SA 797, as submitted by Schumer 'for himself, Mr. Rounds, Mr. Rubio, Mrs. Gillibrand, Mr. Young, and Mr. Heinrich' to the Senate's yearly defence bill. It defines non-human intelligence as 'any sentient intelligent non-human lifeform regardless of nature or ultimate origin', and defines a 'legacy program' as efforts to 'collect, exploit, or reverse engineer technologies of unknown origin or examine biological evidence of living or deceased non-human intelligence'. So the text does deal with recovered technology and biology, as the tellers say, though as definitions in a disclosure law rather than as a statement that such things exist. In that print it runs to seven pages and uses 'non-human intelligence' 24 times, and the tellers do not say which version gives their 64 pages or their counts.","scientific-breakthroughs-as-divine-inspiration-1905":"Some of the great leaps of modern physics may have come as something like a download from outside the mind: Einstein working out the photoelectric effect while bored at his Swiss patent office desk, the physicist Wolfgang Pauli dreaming up the structure of the hydrogen atom, the physicist Werner Heisenberg on the island of Helgoland, and the physicist Paul Dirac staring into a fire in Cambridge. Michels offers this himself in a talk about religion and UFOs; in a later episode he credits the idea to a scholar named Jimena Canales, says Heisenberg downloaded matrix multiplication and Dirac his famous equation, adds Niels Bohr and René Descartes, and drops Einstein and Pauli. If true, it would mean some outside intelligence has been feeding humanity its biggest ideas.","scientific-engineering-institute-passes-to-searle-then-monsa-1977":"The CIA's Scientific Engineering Institute was quietly moved into the private sector in the late 1970s, Michels says, and the story as framed has it passing to the drug company Searle and then to the chemical giant Monsanto by the mid-1980s, with Donald Rumsfeld part of the picture. Michels tells it himself without naming a source, and if true it would mean CIA research quietly continued inside private drug and chemical firms.","scientist-x-lecture-at-university-of-denver-1950":"On March 8, 1950, a man named Silas Newton gave a talk at the University of Denver as a mysterious, unnamed \"Scientist X.\" Michels tells it himself in an episode about a claimed crash in which 16 alien bodies were recovered. He presents the lecture as an early public telling of that crash story, given by a man hiding his own name.","scientists-find-traces-of-underground-dwellers-in-mountains":"Traces of beings who live underground have been found in the mountains of the Caucasus, India, Tibet and the Altai, according to a guest presented on the show as the president of a Russian republic. He tells Michels that he knows scientists who study caves and rocks and that \"they found traces there\" during his own travels to those places, though he does not say what the traces were.","scientists-limited-to-20-minutes-inside-the-craft-1948":"After a craft is said to have crashed in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948, the scientists who went inside it supposedly could not stay long without harm. Michels says it himself: \"They can't stay inside the craft for longer than 20 minutes at a time because some negative energy overcomes them.\" A man named Scott Ramsey, who took Michels to the site, tells it at two removes: a man told him that a woman had told him that scientists going into the craft \"if they were in there any longer would come out sick.\" If true, it would mean the craft gave off something dangerous to humans.","scientology-belief-in-a-pleiadian-zeta-reticuli-conflict":"The Church of Scientology believes a great war is being fought between humanlike beings from the Pleiades, a star cluster, and reptilian beings from Zeta Reticuli, a nearby star system. So says a researcher named Dan Sheehan, who describes the belief to Michels on two episodes, in 2024 and 2025, without naming where he learned it.","scientology-flies-sheehan-to-florida-to-court-him-as-hubbard-1970":"In the 1970s the Church of Scientology flew the lawyer Dan Sheehan to Clearwater, Florida, to court him as a lawyer for its founder, L. Ron Hubbard. Sheehan tells Michels this happened to him, and remembers being taken to \"the big blue cube they've got down there, this big huge blue building.\" He names the Guardian's Office, a branch of the church, as part of the story, which shows the church trying hard to recruit him.","scientology-s-claim-to-sovereign-status-and-its-fair-game-pr":"A lawyer named Dan Sheehan tells Michels that the Church of Scientology believed it was entitled to be treated as a sovereign entity, as the Catholic Church is, and he ties this to what he calls its Fair Game program, a policy the church used against people it saw as opponents. Sheehan speaks as someone who says he dealt with it directly.","scientology-s-surveillance-file-on-sheehan-1970":"The lawyer Dan Sheehan says the Church of Scientology kept a surveillance file on him in the 1970s, in Clearwater, Florida, complete with photographs of him. He tells Michels this first-hand, saying of the file: \"they had, you know, pictures of me.\" If true, it would mean he was being watched closely by a private organisation during those years.","scif-meeting-with-aaro-head-jon-kosloski-2020":"The filmmaker James Fox, who made a documentary called The Phenomenon, says he met Jon Kosloski, head of AARO, the Pentagon's current office for investigating UFO reports, in a SCIF, a sealed room for discussing classified matters, near the Pentagon sometime in the 2020s. Fox tells Michels that Kosloski told him, \"You know, James, I can't part my hair without the approval of the DoD,\" meaning the Department of Defense, which Fox offers as a sign of how little the office's head can say on his own.","scif-meeting-with-congressional-staffers-on-march-3-2023":"On March 3, 2023, Dylan Borland, a man who says he took part, met congressional staffers in a SCIF, a secured room for classified talks, to tell them what he knew about UFOs. He tells Michels that people he does not name had put him in touch with Congress, and that he had spoken with Congress two or three days earlier. The meeting came in the same year that David Grusch, a former intelligence officer, testified to Congress about secret UFO programmes, part of a wave of witnesses who went to Congress in private.","scif-world-map-briefing-on-borderless-covert-organization":"Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for UFO secrets to be made public, says he was once briefed inside a SCIF, a sealed room built for discussing classified material, where \"there's a map on the wall.\" He tells Michels the briefing showed a covert organization that does not stop at national borders. If true, it would mean the UFO secret is held by a group above or across governments, not by any one country.","scott-andrews-taken-to-space-command-as-a-child-for-alien-co":"A man named Scott Andrews says that as a child he was taken out of class to an Air Force base with a space command center, where he did what Michels calls \"intuitive communications with aliens.\" Michels relays this from Andrews during a conversation with Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says he was hired to talk to aliens, and if true it would mean the military used children with psychic gifts to reach non-human beings.","scott-crane-meets-eric-walker-of-majestic-12":"A man named Scott Crane is said to have met a man named Eric Walker, whom Crane believed belonged to Majestic 12, a supposed secret group of officials and scientists in charge of the UFO question. Afterwards, the story goes, Crane converted to Christianity, became devout and dropped the whole matter. Michels tells it himself in a conversation with the writer Peter Levenda, without saying where he heard it.","scott-kelly-s-tomcat-ufo-was-a-bart-simpson-balloon-1990":"In the 1990s, flying an F-14 Tomcat, a Navy fighter jet, over the military training area off Virginia Beach, a Navy pilot named Scott Kelly passed something his crewmate was sure was a UFO, and it turned out to be a Bart Simpson balloon. Kelly tells this himself on an episode where Michels debates UFOs with a well-known skeptic: his \"radar intercept officer who sits in the back of the Tomcat was convinced we flew by a UFO.\" He tells it to show that trained crews can mistake ordinary things for something strange.","scott-overhears-navajo-men-talk-of-the-old-crash-site-1988":"In 1988 in Farmington, New Mexico, a man named Scott Ramsey says he overheard some Navajo men talking about a 'crash site' and pressed them on it. He tells Michels they brushed it off as 'an old wives tale,' then said a UFO had crashed out there in the late 1940s or early 1950s. For Ramsey, it was a local memory pointing to a crash that he went on to look into.","scout-craft-powering-up-and-accelerating-at-8-000-to-12-000-1965":"Between 1965 and 1967 in the Indian Springs Valley in Nevada, small scout craft flown by tall, pale beings he calls the Tall Whites are said to have powered up and shot off at 8,000 to 12,000 miles an hour. Charles Hall, a man who says he served at Indian Springs and spent three years among these beings, tells Michels he watched it himself. He says he watched only from a distance, because \"if you get too close the fields will burn you like a microwave\".","scu-study-of-ufos-at-nuclear-sites-1940":"UFOs were watching American nuclear sites, starting in the 1940s, and the physicist Kevin Knuth tells Michels that a study by the SCU, the Scientific Coalition for UAP Studies (a group of researchers who study unexplained sightings), looked into this. He mentions the study in passing and does not go into its findings. If true, it would mean whatever is behind the sightings takes a particular interest in nuclear weapons.","scully-meets-a-scientist-in-the-mojave-1949":"Around 1949, the writer Frank Scully is said to have met a man in the Mojave Desert who told him \"this amazing story about a flying saucer recovery\", meaning a crashed saucer picked up by the authorities. Scott Ramsey tells it on the show without naming a source. He links the meeting to two men, Silas Newton and Leo Gaybower, and to Scully's book Behind the Flying Saucers.","scully-says-dr-g-is-no-longer-available-1950":"In 1950, at a Chicago radio station, the journalist Frank Scully, who had written about crashed flying saucers, was asked about the unnamed scientist he called Dr. G and answered, \"Oh, that won't be possible. He's no longer available.\" The researcher Scott Ramsey tells Michels this, without naming a source, and also names a physicist, John Torrance Tate, in connection with it. If true, it would mean Scully's key source had dropped out of sight just when people wanted to hear from him.","sdi-as-a-back-door-to-fund-uap-programs-1983":"Ronald Reagan's Strategic Defense Initiative, the missile defence plan announced in 1983, was likely a back door to pay for secret programmes on UAP, the official term for unidentified flying objects, and to act \"offensively against UAP\". That is the view of a researcher who goes by UAPGerb, speaking to Michels. He gives it once as his own reading of the history, without naming a source. He gives it once as something he heard from unnamed sources during his projects. If true, it would mean a famous Cold War weapons plan also hid money and a military aim aimed at unknown craft.","sdi-brilliant-pebbles-as-a-black-budget-front-1980":"The claim is that the 1980s American missile defence effort nicknamed Star Wars, and its plan called Brilliant Pebbles, was mostly a cover for secret spending. A researcher named Steven Greer tells Michels it was \"a blackbudget front\" and \"an expensive boondoggle\", saying most of its money really went into hidden projects, which, if true, would mean a famous public programme quietly paid for secret ones. He gives no source for this.","sea-supply-corporation-heroin-profits-funding-kuomintang-arm-1950":"In the 1950s, the story goes, a company called the Sea Supply Corporation ran offices in Havana and Miami, and heroin profits passed through it to buy weapons for the Kuomintang, China's Nationalist party. A researcher named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source: 'There was a company set up uh with offices in Havana and in Miami that was the sea supply corporation they called it.' If true, it would mean a front company tied drug money to secret arms supply in the early Cold War.","seal-beach-weapons-station-sightings-2009":"In March 2009, civilians reported several UFO sightings near the Seal Beach Naval Weapons Station in Orange County, California, a set of bunkers that the writer Robert Hastings says hold nuclear weapons. Hastings tells Michels this from local media reports at the time. He gives it as one more case in his argument that UFOs keep turning up at nuclear weapons sites.","sealed-needle-in-tube-device-moves-3-4-feet-2016":"Charles Buhler, an engineer the show presents as coming from NASA, says that in 2016 in his lab he sealed a needle inside a tube, turned up his power supply, and watched the device move three to four feet into the air. He says he saw it himself and points to videos on the website of his company, Exodus. If true, it would mean electric power alone lifting a sealed object, a step toward the antigravity propulsion he says they have built.","sean-kirkpatrick-s-aaro-as-a-debunking-effort-with-doe-ties-2020":"AARO, a Pentagon office set up in the 2020s to investigate UFO reports, was in its early years probably a fake hit job on UFOs, run by its first director, a man named Sean Kirkpatrick, who Michels says has long ties to the Department of Energy and worked at Oak Ridge, a national laboratory. Michels says this himself, as his own reading, without citing a source; if true, it would mean the office meant to investigate the subject was built to dismiss it.","sean-kirkpatrick-s-background-at-brookhaven-and-oak-ridge":"The government scientist Sean Kirkpatrick once worked at the nuclear research labs at Brookhaven in New York and at Oak Ridge National Laboratory in Tennessee, which Michels calls \"a clear atomic site\". Michels points this out himself, apparently to note that Kirkpatrick's career runs through the US nuclear establishment.","sean-ryan-threatened-after-the-herrera-interview-2023":"The podcaster Shawn Ryan was threatened soon after he had a man named Michael Herrera on his show in 2023. A researcher named Steven Greer says this in passing to Michels, as an example of what happens to people who air such stories. He names no source and does not say who made the threat or what Herrera said.","search-for-a-metal-tablet-given-to-a-boy-by-aliens":"In northern Sweden, a man said that when he was a young boy, aliens gave him a metal tablet, and researchers went up north to search for it. The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels about the man's claim and the search, and if such a tablet were found it would be a physical object from an encounter.","search-for-the-missing-team-brought-in-sturgis-deputies-and-1977":"Mario Woods, a former Air Force guard at nuclear missile sites, says that when his team went missing in the missile field near Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota in 1977, on a night he describes as an abduction, the base's security control asked for outside help, and two deputies from Sturgis and a state trooper were sent to search for them. Woods says he did not learn this until 2021, and in one telling he credits a Meade County sheriff's dispatch log. If true, the search shows that the team's disappearance was real enough for the base to call in local police.","search-of-marcus-vinicius-s-house-and-computer-for-the-tape-2018":"After the death of a man named Marcus Vinicius, who is said to have held a video tape tied to the Varginha alien case in Brazil, his whole house was searched and his computer was taken to a warehouse in São Paulo. A surgeon named Italo Venturelli tells Michels this, passing on what he heard from James Fox, a UFO documentary maker who went to the house. Venturelli does not say who 'they' were, but the story suggests someone wanted the tape badly.","searching-for-a-ufo-that-landed-and-sank-in-norway":"Witnesses in Norway say a UFO landed and then sank, and Michels says he and his guest went looking for it. Michels tells this himself as someone who took part in the search, and the guest agrees, \"Yeah, we did\"; neither says on the show what they found.","second-alien-communicator-nicknamed-bones-1990":"Between 1990 and 1993, at a site he calls Base 2, a second alien Dan Sherman was in contact with went by the nickname Bones. Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, tells Michels this was not the being he had dealt with before: \"No, this was a different one.\" If true, it would mean more than one alien was in contact with the program.","second-century-latin-inscribed-on-rockets":"Inscriptions in second-century Latin were put on some NASA missions, written on the rockets themselves. Michels says the religious studies professor Diana Pasulka told him this, and he brings it up in two episodes. In one he places the words on the fairing, the nose cone that shields a rocket's cargo, though he says he is not sure.","second-craft-near-skinwalker-ranch-shoots-into-mountain":"Near Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, a craft is said to have shot straight into a mountain \"as if the stone was a doorway\", just as another craft had done before it. Michels relays the case from Robert Bigelow's account, in an episode about a UFO book he says the Pentagon tried to censor.","second-face-found-in-utopia-1980":"A physicist named John Brandenburg says that in the 1980s he studied a part of Mars called Utopia, which he says sat on the shore of an ancient Martian ocean, and found a site with two faces in the landscape. He tells Michels that one of them looks like the same face seen at Cydonia, a Martian region known for a rock formation shaped like a face, but inside a helmet. If true, a second matching face would suggest the shapes were made, not natural.","second-giza-scan-paper-under-peer-review-2025":"A second scientific paper on radar scans beneath the Pyramid of Khafre, the second-largest pyramid at Giza, is being written and is under review, according to Filippo Biondi, the Italian scientist who took part in the scans. He tells Michels, \"in this moment we are working on a scientific paper\" in 2025. If it passes review, the team's claims about what lies under the pyramid would enter the scientific record.","second-marine-squadmate-reaches-out-via-linkedin-2024":"A former Marine named Michael Herrera, who says he saw a craft about 300 feet long in the jungle of Indonesia, says that in 2024 a second man from his old Marine squad contacted him through LinkedIn. Herrera tells Michels about the text messages and emails that followed, quoting the squadmate: \"Keep the faith. there is nothing, no borders lines that they won't cross.\" To Herrera this is a second member of the unit backing him up.","second-meeting-implants-not-ganglion-1996":"In 1996, people who came to the Los Angeles offices of Dark Skies, a TV series, are said to have changed their story at a second meeting: what had earlier been a ganglion, a cluster of nerve cells, was now \"some kind of implants\", something more technological. Bryce Zabel, who worked on the show and was in the room, tells Michels this was said by a man he calls JC and another he calls the captain. If true, insiders were steering what a TV show said about aliens.","second-night-at-carman-ping-pong-ball-object-photographed-1975":"In 1975, near Carman, Manitoba, in Canada, an object people called \"the ping pong ball\" was seen and photographed on a second night of sightings, \"jumping around the sky.\" The researcher Grant Cameron, who says he was there, tells Michels that watching it fly away that night made him decide to find out what was going on. He says that search led him from the Canadian government to an American named Eric A. Walker.","second-regression-michael-lying-opposite-with-small-being-be-1977":"In 1977 in South Dakota, aboard a craft, Mario Woods lay across from his fellow guard Michael Johnson with one of the small beings standing between them. Woods, a former guard at a nuclear site who says he was abducted, tells Michels he only learned this in his second hypnotic regression, a session using hypnosis to recover memories, which he names Yvonne Smith in connection with. \"In regression number two I found out that Michael was laying opposite to me and there was one of the short ones or small ones between us,\" he says. That would mean the two men were taken together.","second-silent-object-seen-by-three-witnesses-2016":"A few weeks after a first sighting in 2016, a second object, with a different structure and \"completely silent\", was seen over Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange happenings. The ranch's owner tells Michels it was seen by three people who worked for him: his ranch security man, a surveyor and his caretakers of the time. He was not one of the three, and he passes on their account.","second-voice-download-you-have-everything-you-need":"A man named Brent Friedman says he received a second 'download,' a message that came to him unbidden, telling him 'you have everything you need.' Friedman tells Michels this as his own experience, the second of such messages he says he got.","secrecy-briefing-at-kilo-control-and-ostracism-after-the-inc-1977":"After his 1977 encounter at a nuclear missile site near Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, the former Air Force missile security guard Mario Woods says he was sent to Kilo control, one of the posts that ran the missile field, and given \"a real strict briefing\" by a Master Sergeant Gray. He tells Michels this himself, as someone it happened to, and also brings up a Colonel Spraker in connection with what came next. The journalist Robert Hastings, who writes about UFOs at nuclear sites, gave the same account in a 2017 article, where Woods says: \"We were told never to speak with anyone about any of this so I never did, as ordered.\" Woods tells it this way: the men who saw the incident were made to keep quiet, and he says afterwards he was kept at arm's length. If that is true, the Air Force would have known something happened and chosen to bury it.","secret-electrogravitic-propulsion-programs":"The US has secret programmes building craft that fly by electrogravitics, the idea that electric fields can be used to push against gravity. A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown says this to Michels, naming the field in two words, \"Electromagnetic. Electrogravitic,\" and tying it to the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, who worked on the idea. If true, a form of flight with no rockets or wings would already exist in secret.","secret-kennedy-khrushchev-letters-on-destroying-nuclear-warh-1963":"In 1963, President Kennedy began secretly exchanging letters with the Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev about destroying nuclear warheads, passing them through a go-between named Norman Cousins. A researcher named Dan Sheehan tells this on the show without naming a source, as part of his account of what Kennedy knew and tried to do before his death; if true, it would show a hidden channel for disarmament running around the usual government lines.","secret-machines-adviser-genocides-may-be-a-function-of-the-p":"One of the advisers in the Sekret Machines books, the non-fiction series by the rock musician Tom DeLonge and the writer Peter Levenda, told the two authors that \"genocides may be a function of the phenomenon,\" meaning the force behind UFOs, which \"may be trying to eliminate certain genetic strands for its own purposes.\" The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this from his reading of the books. Among the books' advisers he names a man called McCasland and a General Hayden, but he does not say which of them said it.","secret-pentagon-program-to-build-weapons-from-recovered-ufo-2007":"The Pentagon is said to have run a secret programme, classified \"Above Top Secret\", whose real aim was to build an advanced weapon system from technology recovered from crashed UFOs. A researcher named Dan Sheehan says this on the show without naming a source, and ties it to Luis Elizondo, a man he describes as having been involved in the programme. If true, it would mean the government holds such craft and has tried to turn them into weapons.","secret-radiation-testing-of-animals-after-pacific-nuclear-te-1950":"A journalist named Ross Coulthart tells Michels that one of the first things he found in Australia's national archives was evidence that animals had been secretly tested for leftover radiation from nuclear experiments in the Pacific, apparently in the 1950s and 60s and involving Australia and New Zealand. Coulthart offers it as an early sign that governments were hiding things from their own people. He does not give more detail on what the records show.","secret-service-guarded-the-dying-von-neumann-at-a-naval-hosp-1957":"In 1957, as the mathematician John von Neumann lay dying in a Navy hospital, Secret Service agents are said to have stood guard outside his room in case he talked. Michels tells this himself, saying \"they were afraid of what he might say.\" A researcher named Deep Prasad says the same on a later episode: \"there were secret service outside of his hospital room because they were worried that he was going to talk.\" On that same episode Michels places the hospital in Maryland, with some hedging: \"I believe\" and \"I think it was a Navy hospital.\" They tell it because it would suggest von Neumann knew secrets the government badly wanted kept.","secret-space-program-friend-s-father-s-simulacrum-joke":"In Encounters, a book by the religion scholar Diana Pasulka, she interviews a friend who was allegedly part of one of America's secret space programs, meaning hidden space efforts said to run outside the public one. Michels brings this up himself while summarising her book. He compares the friend to Tyler, a figure in Pasulka's earlier book American Cosmic who is described as having been in a similar program. Michels gives nothing more about the friend's father or the joke about a 'simulacrum', a copy or imitation, that the story's title mentions.","secret-ufo-briefings-of-the-trump-camp-before-taking-office-2024":"People around Donald Trump are said to have had several secret briefings about UFOs before they took office in 2024. Ross Coulthart says this on the show, pointing to Donald Trump Jr. as someone who has \"been briefed by a lot of people\", and he describes \"multiple secret briefings of the Trump camp before they came into government\".","secret-us-air-force-plane-landed-without-brazilian-authoriza-1996":"In 1996, around the events at Varginha, a town in Brazil, a US Air Force plane is said to have landed on a secret mission without the Brazilian government's permission. The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this as what he was told by someone he interviewed. If true, it would mean the United States quietly sent its own military into whatever happened at Varginha.","secret-us-fundamental-physics-research-after-wwii-1940":"The idea is that since the 1940s the US government has been doing secret research in fundamental physics, the basic science of matter and energy, and that this work has gone beyond what the public knows. The national security analyst Matthew Pines says it on the show as his own reading: \"it's very clear to me that US government has been conducting secret fundamental physics research.\" James Valor, of Skywatcher, a startup that says it tries to summon UFOs, tells Michels he agrees with the mathematician Eric Weinstein that the government has \"top secret physics, knowledge and science,\" probably since \"at least\" the end of the Second World War. Both give it as an opinion and name no document. If they are right, the science the public is taught would be behind what is kept in secret.","secretive-spy-vs-spy-meetings-with-gary-nolan-and-congressio-2021":"Between 2021 and 2024, James Valor of Skywatcher, a startup that says it tries to summon UFOs, says he held secret, \"spy vs. spy\" style meetings with the scientist Garry Nolan and with congressional staff. He tells Michels he took part himself, and that at one meeting he set things up so Nolan \"felt very inconvenient\". He also names a man called Alex and Jake Barber, a man linked to these claims, as part of the story, which shows how cautious these behind-the-scenes contacts were.","security-guard-george-frozen-and-later-hospitalized-2016":"In 2016, on the mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch with a long reputation for strange events, a security guard named George says he stood up, \"was frozen,\" could not move, and then \"everything went black,\" and he later ended up in a hospital in Las Vegas. Brandon Fugal, who was there that night, tells Michels what George told him afterward, that it happened just as the group pulled up and jumped out of their vehicles.","seeing-a-tag-along-object":"Someone saw an object that he calls a \"tag along.\" The speaker, who says he saw it himself, is not named and is heard in a clip played on an episode with Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor. Nothing more about the object is given.","seeing-a-ufo-while-remote-viewing-mount-zeal":"Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant trained in remote viewing (trying to see distant places with the mind alone), says that during one session aimed at Mount Zeal in Australia he saw a UFO, and not one sitting in a hangar. Buchanan tells Michels this from his own session, and if true it would mean a remote viewer had glimpsed a craft out in the open.","seeing-tall-white-children-in-the-indian-springs-barracks-1965":"Between 1965 and 1967, children of tall, pale beings he calls the Tall Whites are said to have come into the barracks at Indian Springs, Nevada, and played there. Charles Hall, a man who says he served there and lived among these beings, tells Michels he would get up and \"see the children playing\". At first, he says, he thought he \"must be short on vitamins\".","selective-service-chief-russians-moving-agents-through-ortho-1968":"In 1968, the head of the Selective Service, the military draft office, in New York told the writer Peter Levenda that \"the Russians have been moving agents into the United States through the Orthodox churches.\" Levenda tells Michels this as something he heard directly, and he adds himself that the church in question was the Russian church at 97th Street and Fifth Avenue, the one loyal to Moscow. If true, it would mean Soviet spies were using a Manhattan church as cover.","selectively-visible-lights-over-valley-on-highway-40":"While driving on Highway 40 through a remote area on the way to Bullhead City, Arizona, Steve Colbern saw a bright white light hovering over a valley five or ten miles away. Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, tells Michels he was with his son at the time and also names Garrett. The story is told as one about lights that only some people could see, which would mean the light could choose who saw it.","self-designed-doctorate-in-parapsychology-at-uc-berkeley-1970":"Jeffrey Mishlove says that in the 1970s he designed his own doctoral programme at the University of California, Berkeley, and seven years later earned a doctorate in parapsychology, the study of claimed psychic abilities. He tells Michels this as his own path, and it would make his an unusual degree from a mainstream university.","semivan-and-kit-green-question-tom-delonge-in-a-hotel-room-2015":"In 2015, the story goes, two men named Jim Semivan and Kit Green got a man named Tom DeLonge alone in a hotel room and asked him where he had got the material he had. A researcher named Grant Cameron tells this on the show, summarising it without naming a source, and quotes them asking, \"where'd you get this stuff from?\" If true, it would mean insiders were worried about what DeLonge knew and how he knew it.","semivan-on-a-non-state-intelligence-network-and-the-2016-lea":"The emails leaked in 2016, a story that touches Hillary Clinton, Donald Trump and Mike Pompeo, were leaked by an intelligence network that belongs to no government, according to a man named Jim Semivan. A researcher named Jason Simosa passes this on to Michels without naming a source: 'According to Semivan, a non-state intelligence network is responsible for the leaking of these emails.' If true, it would mean a private spy network outside any state helped shape that year's events.","senate-armed-services-staff-on-admiral-wilson":"Staff on the Senate Armed Services Committee are said to have spoken about a Navy admiral named Thomas R. Wilson and reverse-engineered UFOs, meaning craft taken apart to learn how they work. A UFO investigator named James Fox tells Michels he talked to some of those staff members himself. Michels tells it differently: someone told him, \"I talked to the people that subpoenaed him\", and he frames it as Wilson being called into a secure room in the 2020s. The tellers raise it because it would mean Congress pressed a senior officer about hidden programmes.","senate-findings-that-uap-records-were-wrongly-classified-and-2023":"The US Senate found that records about UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's newer term for UFOs, had been wrongly classified and kept from elected officials. The researcher Matthew Pines reads this in the findings of the UAP Disclosure Act, an amendment by Senators Schumer and Rounds to the yearly defense bill, put forward in the Senate on 13 July 2023, saying they state that \"credible evidence indicates that relevant UAP records have been inappropriately classified and withheld\" from officials in both Congress and the executive branch. As Pines gives it, this is an unusually direct statement, which would mean the Senate itself believes UFO information was hidden from those meant to oversee it.","senate-intelligence-scif-meeting-and-black-hawks-over-the-in-2022":"The story is that after a secret meeting on UFOs with the Senate Intelligence Committee, the committee's chief investigator found unmarked military helicopters flying over his house. If true, someone in government was trying to intimidate the man looking into the matter. A UFO researcher named Steven Greer says he took part. He describes a meeting in a SCIF, a secure room for classified briefings, which he connects with Senator Marco Rubio. He says that 'within a day or two of that meeting, the main investigator for Senate Intelligence had two Blackhawks over his house, unmarked.' He places it around 2022. A journalist named Ross Coulthart tells it second-hand from Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot. Coulthart says the staffer asked Barber to help him 'get security because somebody from the Defense Department is ordering helicopters to fly across and over my home. They're harassing my home.' Coulthart's version puts it later, around 2024 or 2025, and blames the Defense Department by name.","senate-language-on-historical-exotic-technology-antecedents-2023":"Language that came out of the US Senate in 2023 and 2024, in its laws on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's current word for UFOs), hints that the government once handed strange technology to private companies for research. That is how the national security analyst Matthew Pines reads it to Michels. He quotes it: to avoid being surprised by foreign technology, the government \"must broaden awareness of historical exotic technology antecedents previously provided by the federal government for research and development purposes.\" Pines takes this to point at material held by defence contractors such as Lockheed. The wording does say exotic technology was given out for research, but what it was, where it came from and who got it, it does not say.","senate-staff-meet-mellon-puthoff-davis-the-skinwalker-team-a-2017":"In 2017 and 2018, Senate staff are said to have met with many of the main figures in the UFO story, says Kurt McConnell, a Senate staffer who was there. He tells Michels he got back in touch with a man he already knew, Chris Mellon. Through him they met Luis Elizondo, who ran AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports; the physicists Hal Puthoff and Eric Davis; the team that had worked at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange events; and Brandon Fugal, who is linked to the ranch. They also met the Navy pilots David Fravor and Alex Dietrich, who saw the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint. If true, the subject reached Congress through private briefings well before any public hearing.","senate-to-pass-the-64-page-uap-disclosure-bill-again-2025":"The claim is that the Senate was set to pass again a 64-page bill forcing the government to disclose what it knows about UAP, the official term for UFOs. The lawyer Dan Sheehan, who says he has been part of the effort, tells Michels that \"the Senate is going to pass the 64page bill again\", naming Senators Mike Rounds, Chuck Schumer and Marco Rubio and Congressman Dan Crenshaw. The bill he means first appeared as the UAP Disclosure Act, an amendment by Schumer and Rounds to the annual defence authorization bill, dated July 13, 2023. The Congressional Record of July 29, 2025 shows it again: amendment SA 3111, which Schumer \"submitted\" for himself, Rounds and Senator Kirsten Gillibrand to S. 2296, the Senate's defence bill for fiscal year 2026, \"which was ordered to lie on the table\". The text says it \"may be cited as the Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Disclosure Act of 2025\". That page records the bill being brought back as a submitted amendment; Sheehan's forecast that the Senate would pass it goes a step beyond what it records. If he is right, Congress is pushing to make UFO secrets public by law.","senator-claiborne-pell-part-of-the-cabal-1990":"Claiborne Pell, the US senator, was secretly a member of a cabal, Greer's word for a hidden group he says keeps the UFO secrets. A researcher named Steven Greer tells Michels this about the 1990s, saying he only found it out after Pell died. He does not say how he learned it.","senator-cussed-out-by-curtis-lemay-over-secret-wright-patter":"Senator Barry Goldwater said he once asked General Curtis LeMay of the Air Force to let him into a secret room at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio where, he believed, UFO material was kept, and LeMay swore at him. In a recording Michels plays on many episodes, Goldwater says: \"I called Curtis LeMay and I said, General, I know we have a room at Wright-Patterson where you put all this secret stuff. Can I go in there?\" He goes on: \"I've never heard him get mad, but he got mad as hell at me, cussed me out, said don't ever ask me that question.\" Michels names him and calls the place the Blue Room, dating it to the 1960s or 70s. The tellings disagree on who is speaking: one episode presents the voice as an unnamed politician, and another frames it as the experience of Harald Malmgren, a former presidential adviser. So the story rests on one man's recorded words, replayed by the host. If true, even a senator was refused a look at what the base held.","senator-gillibrand-s-uap-legislation-gillibrand-rubio-amendm-2020":"Senator Kirsten Gillibrand, who backs a pro-crypto bill with another senator, also helped write some of the first laws on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), through amendments she wrote with Senator Marco Rubio between 2020 and 2023. A researcher named Matthew Pines points this out on the show without naming a source, calling it a notable overlap between the two causes.","senator-inouye-on-a-shadow-government-during-iran-contra-1980":"During the Iran-Contra affair of the 1980s, Senator Daniel Inouye is said to have warned that a \"shadow or secret government\" exists \"above the US government\". A researcher named Steven Greer tells Michels this from memory, without naming a source. Greer raises it because, if true, it would mean a hidden power sits beyond the reach of elected officials.","senator-reid-they-re-shutting-down-our-nukes-2019":"Around 2019 or 2020, Harry Reid, the former Democratic leader of the Senate, answered a question about UFOs \"immediately without a pause\" by pointing to \"the fact that they're shutting down our nukes.\" James Fox, a UFO investigator who says he put the question to Reid himself, tells Michels the story. If true, it means a senior senator believed UFOs had disabled American nuclear weapons.","senator-ted-stevens-foo-fighter-encounters":"Ted Stevens, a World War II pilot who later became a senator from Alaska, is said to have seen foo fighters, the name wartime pilots gave to unexplained objects in the sky. A former Pentagon official named Luis Elizondo tells Michels that Stevens, one of the founders of AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, \"himself admitted he had encounters with Foo Fighters during the war\". An analyst named Marik von Rennenkampff speaks of a single sighting and ties Stevens to Senator Harry Reid and their funding of the research. Michels mentions it too, calling it a UFO and hedging with \"I think\". It matters because one of the men who started the Pentagon's study would have had his own sighting.","senior-ge-scalar-physicist-at-greer-s-2023-conference":"A senior physicist from General Electric, described as working on what the story calls scalar physics, spoke at a 2023 conference held just before a disclosure event at the National Press Club in Washington, where a speaker named Michael Herrera also appeared. Steven Greer, a UFO activist who ran both events, tells Michels this. It matters to him because it would put a senior scientist from a major company among the people speaking out.","senior-intelligence-official-and-his-wife-abducted":"A very senior American intelligence official and his wife are said to have had a frightening encounter with non-human beings. Michels tells it first without naming him, as a \"pretty gnarly abduction experience\" he knows of from someone he does not name. A researcher named Peter Levenda names the man as Jim Semivan of the CIA and says Semivan talks openly about \"a kind of an abduction experience\". A CIA contractor named Bob McGwier, who says he knows Semivan, puts it differently: Semivan \"had experiences of entities in his room\" with his wife, and \"they were not pleasant\". If true, it would mean such encounters reach people deep inside the intelligence world.","senior-navy-sources-say-the-greys-genetic-collection-has-end":"The greys, the small grey-skinned beings described in many abduction stories, have stopped collecting human genetic material, according to people Michels says he has heard from. Michels says this himself, in a conversation with Matthew Pines, and puts it down to \"pretty senior\" and \"impressive\" people in the Navy whom he does not name. He says they told him the abductions \"might be real\" and that this genetic collection \"just ended\".","senior-nsa-official-blocks-a-finding-from-reaching-president-2000":"Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA, says that in the 2000s his team had discovered something and planned to tell President George W. Bush, but a senior official above them refused: 'Nope, I'm not going to let you do that.' Haseltine tells Michels this as something he was part of, and he does not say what the finding was. If true, it would show information being stopped before it reached the president.","senior-vp-material-scientist-no-physicists-ever-on-the-crash-2010":"In the 2010s, a senior vice president and materials scientist at an old aerospace company told him the company's programme for studying recovered crashed craft never had any physicists, says Eric Davis, a physicist who worked on Pentagon-funded UFO studies. Davis tells Michels that when he asked where the physicists were, the man said, \"we don't have any. We never did,\" because only \"a handful of people\" were allowed to work on it. Davis says he himself knew of five such people at the company and met two of them, including a woman who was a mathematician but worked as chief of security.","sensory-deprivation-training-as-sac-s-back-door-into-paranor-1950":"The idea is that sensory deprivation training, cutting people off from sight and sound, gave the Air Force's Strategic Air Command a quiet way in to study the paranormal and the occult between the 1950s and the 1970s. A researcher named Peter Levenda puts it this way to Michels, calling it \"the back door at SAC\" for investigating \"paranormal phenomena and occultism\", without naming a source. If so, a routine-sounding training programme would have hidden much stranger research.","sent-to-sri-to-assess-the-gondola-wish-project-then-assigned-1978":"In 1978, according to Joe McMoneagle, who was part of it, a general named Roya learned that an Army project code-named Gondola Wish was starting at Fort Meade and was nervous about it. McMoneagle tells Michels he was sent to the Stanford Research Institute, a California research lab, to assess the work, and then ended up assigned to the project himself.","sentinels-of-ether-reverse-engineered-craft-need-psionic-ass":"Craft that have been reverse engineered, meaning taken apart and copied from non-human originals, can only be flown if \"psionic assets\" on the ground, people said to use psychic abilities, mentally \"lock in\" on them. Michels gives this as the account of a man named Jake Barber, drawing on the book Sentinels of Ether. If true, it would mean these craft depend on the mind as much as on machinery.","september-1993-meeting-with-wright-patterson-fastc-colonel":"In September 1993, Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for government disclosure of UFOs, says he met the colonel in charge of the relevant operations at Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio. The colonel worked for FASTC, the Foreign Aerospace Science and Technology Center, an Air Force intelligence office based there. Greer tells Michels he was there and recalls what a man he calls Colonel Canola said to him, and he brings an Admiral Kramer into the same account. If his account is right, officers at the base where UFO material is often said to be kept were talking with him directly about it.","serapeum-granite-boxes-found-blown-apart":"Many of the huge granite boxes in the Serapeum, a set of underground chambers at Saqqara in Egypt, were found blown apart, as if 'a lot of energy had surged through and blown them up'. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would point to some powerful force at work there in the distant past.","sergeant-larson-s-onion-speech-on-secrecy-1990":"When he was first briefed into a secret programme at an overseas base in the early 1990s, a Sergeant Larson gave him \"the onion thing,\" a speech comparing secrecy to the layers of an onion. So says Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who claims the NSA trained him to communicate with aliens and who wrote about it in his book Above Black. He tells Michels the speech came \"during our black introduction,\" his first briefing into the hidden programme, as a lesson that each layer of secrecy hides another.","serpo-alien-language-decoded-from-a-live-ebe-recovered-at-ro-1947":"Scientists are said to have decoded an alien language after one to three decades of working with a live EBE, short for extraterrestrial biological entity, meaning a living alien, recovered from the 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico, which the military first announced as a flying disc. A researcher named Deep Prasad tells this on the show without naming a source beyond the story called Project Serpo, and if true it would mean humans have been talking with a non-human being since the late 1940s.","sethite-theory-adopted-by-jews-and-by-the-church-through-aug":"In Genesis 6, which the King James Bible of 1611 renders as \"the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair\" and \"there were giants in the earth in those days,\" the sons of God were later explained away as noble men from the family line of Seth, Adam's son. The filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels that Jewish teachers took up this reading, and that the church took it up through Saint Augustine. He tells it to argue that the original meaning, beings from heaven fathering giants, was covered over. The verse itself does not say who the sons of God were, which is why both readings have found room in it.","seven-government-attempts-to-seize-the-bodies-at-the-univers-2020":"A man named Martin Alacrone says that in the 2020s the Peruvian authorities came seven times to the University of Ica to take away the strange three-fingered bodies kept there, at first bringing police and a prosecutor. Alacrone tells Michels this as someone who was there. If true, it would mean the government was pressing hard to take control of the bodies.","several-such-sites-around-the-world":"Sites like the one under discussion are said to exist all over the world, in places such as Southeast Asia and Mexico. Michael Herrera says this on the show, passing on what he heard from a man named Jake Barber: \"there's several different sites all over the world.\"","shaft-and-chamber-system-found-under-the-queen-s-chamber-in-1800":"Early excavations of the Great Pyramid at Giza, sometime between 1800 and the early 1900s, are said to have found a pit in the Queen's Chamber \"filled with rubble and dirt\", with a system of shafts and chambers below it that was later sealed. The researcher Jeffrey Drum tells Michels this, citing the original excavation reports. In a second telling he says only that the excavation reports describe \"something like this below the Queen's Chamber\", with no date and nothing about sealing. If true, the pyramid would have hidden spaces beneath it that are no longer open to anyone.","shafts-below-the-sphinx-admitted-by-zahi-hawass":"There are shafts and tunnels beneath the Great Sphinx at Giza, and even Zahi Hawass, the Egyptian official long in charge of the site, is said to have admitted it. Michels tells it himself on several episodes, without naming a source, calling Hawass \"a professional Debbie Downer and skeptic\" who \"acknowledged the existence of three independent tunnels,\" and saying Hawass debated outside researchers and tried to suppress the information while admitting chambers exist, even going down into some. A researcher named Matt LaCroix counts it differently, citing a survey by the geologist Robert Schoch that found voids and \"four entrances\" under the Sphinx, one beneath its left paw. If true, the Sphinx would sit above hidden spaces that have not been fully explored.","shah-bought-laser-isotope-enrichment-technology-from-switzer-1970":"In 1970 the Shah of Iran bought laser isotope enrichment technology from Switzerland, a way of using lasers to concentrate the uranium needed for nuclear fuel or bombs. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this and calls it amazing, without naming a source. If true, it would mean Iran's nuclear ambitions reach back further than most people think.","shallow-lunar-craters-with-a-hard-convex-floor":"The craters on the Moon are all said to be only a few miles deep, with none ever found deeper than five or six miles, as if they stopped at a hard, curved floor beneath the surface. A researcher named Jason Jorjani says this on the show without naming a source. The point, if true, is that something solid and unusual lies under the Moon's surface.","shamans-accounts-of-little-men-out-of-a-big-light-passport-t":"Shamans in Siberia and the Pacific have described little men coming out of a big light. That is according to the computer scientist and UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, who tells Michels that a shaman told him so 50 years ago. Vallée ties it to his book Passport to Magonia, which sets modern UFO reports beside older folklore about small beings. If these accounts hold, people far from modern UFO culture would have described the same scene long before.","shane-freaks-shown-a-sphere":"An old veteran named Shane Freaks says he was shown a sphere, apparently an object connected to UFO secrets. Michels relays this from what Freaks said at a symposium on Twitter, without giving more detail about what the sphere was or who showed it to him.","shard-thousands-of-feet-tall-beside-a-martian-impact-crater":"A man named Joe McMoneagle says that on mapping negatives of Mars from NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory he saw, beside an impact crater, something that looked like a pyramid but was not: \"That's a shard, and it's thousands of feet tall.\" He tells Michels he looked at the images himself, and offers it as a sign that Mars may once have had life that built things.","sheehan-as-counsel-to-disclosure-project-john-mack-and-elizo-1990":"A lawyer named Dan Sheehan says that for 20 years he was legal counsel to the Disclosure Project, a campaign to get the government to release what it knows about UFOs, and that he also represented Dr John Mack, a Harvard psychiatrist who studied people reporting alien encounters. Sheehan tells Michels this from his own career, and he says Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who ran a small office studying unexplained aerial reports, knew all of this perfectly well, along with Sheehan's place in what he calls \"the peer group\"; he raises it to show he had long been in the middle of the effort to bring the subject into the open.","sheehan-gets-access-to-every-internal-scientology-file-1970":"In the 1970s the lawyer Dan Sheehan says he refused to meet L. Ron Hubbard, founder of the Church of Scientology, unless he was first given access to \"every single document anywhere in the world\" in the church's internal files, including those of its Guardian's Office, an internal department. He tells Michels the condition was met at the church's headquarters in Florida, and he names Hubbard's wife Mary Sue Hubbard as part of the story. If true, an outside lawyer saw the whole of one of the most secretive organisations in America from the inside.","sheehan-gets-an-injunction-against-the-scientology-grand-jur-1970":"In the 1970s, a lawyer named Dan Sheehan says, grand jury subpoenas aimed at the Church of Scientology were stopped by a court order he won himself. He tells Michels that he went up to New York, where he is admitted to practise in the federal court for the Southern District, and \"got an injunction shutting them down.\" He tells it as a lawyer who took part, and if it happened as he says, he personally blocked a federal investigation in its tracks.","sheehan-represented-john-mack-and-vetted-experiencers-1990":"Dan Sheehan, a lawyer, says he represented John Mack, a Harvard psychiatrist who studied people reporting encounters with aliens, for about ten years starting around 1990. Over that time, Sheehan tells Michels, he met dozens of the people Mack had worked with, the so-called experiencers who say they have had such contact, and sized them up himself. He offers this as someone who took part, and it would mean Mack's subjects were checked by an outsider as well as by Mack.","sheehan-s-account-of-james-mccord-and-the-watergate-break-in-1972":"The story is that the 1972 Watergate break-in was really about the billionaire Howard Hughes: Larry O'Brien, the new head of the Democratic National Committee, had earlier been Hughes's main lobbyist in Washington, and Nixon was terrified of what O'Brien knew. A lawyer and researcher named Dan Sheehan tells it on the show, saying Nixon wanted to learn whether O'Brien knew about something Sheehan calls the Hughes S Force and might use it, so they had to wiretap his phone and bug his office, while Michels adds that the burglar, a man named James McCord, was ordered to do it but \"wasn't clued in\" on why. Michels, in another episode, says O'Brien was running McGovern's campaign and calls the bugged phone his cell phone.","sheehan-visits-ingo-swann-at-his-new-york-apartment-1970":"Sometime in the 1970s, a lawyer named Dan Sheehan went to see Ingo Swann, a man known for claimed psychic abilities, at his apartment in New York. Sheehan tells Michels he was there himself, and that the two of them went \"all the way upstairs up onto the roof\" together. He tells it as part of his own path into the UFO subject, through the people he met along the way.","sheffield-edwards-gives-the-green-light-at-the-fontainebleau-1960":"In 1960, at the Fontainebleau Hotel in Miami, a CIA security official named Sheffield Edwards came to a meeting with the mob figures Johnny Roselli, Sam Giancana and Santo Trafficante Jr. and the private investigator Bob Maheu. Going by the code name \"Mr. Ed\", he gave the plan they were discussing the go-ahead. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this, passing on what he was told, in a conversation about John F. Kennedy. If true, it would mean the CIA itself approved a working partnership with organised crime.","sheldrake-s-crystals-growing-faster-after-the-first-growth":"A new crystal is slow to form the first time it is grown, and then forms faster and faster afterwards, as if nature remembers what it has done before. The researcher Rupert Sheldrake explains it on the show himself: a newly made chemical compound has never crystallized before, and 'the more often you crystallize it the easier it should get for crystals of that kind to form.' Michels later retells it in his own words as a crystal lattice in a petri dish that 'grows much much faster after you've grown it that first time'.","sheldrake-s-paper-is-the-sun-conscious":"The biologist Rupert Sheldrake wrote a paper called \"Is the Sun Conscious?\", which he says was published in the Journal of Consciousness Studies, asking whether the sun itself might have a mind. Sheldrake tells Michels about it himself, saying the paper is fairly recent but his interest in conscious suns and stars goes back a long time; the philosopher James Madden also brings it up on the show as \"this really interesting paper.\"","sheldrake-s-plant-research-at-an-agricultural-institute-in-i":"The biologist Rupert Sheldrake says he spent years in India working at an agricultural research institute, breeding better chickpeas and pigeon peas. He tells Michels this about his own working life, as part of the background to his ideas about science.","sheldrake-s-ted-talk-censored-and-taken-off-youtube-2013":"In 2013, a TED Talk by Rupert Sheldrake was censored and taken off YouTube. Michels says this himself, in an episode with Sheldrake, a scientist he describes as banned by TED Talks. He tells it as an example of mainstream science pushing aside ideas it would rather not hear.","sherman-accounts-of-orbs-and-giant-wolf-like-creatures-headi-1990":"In the 1990s, orbs of light and giant wolf-like creatures were reportedly seen heading toward the Dry Gulch Creek area of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, where they disappeared. Brandon Fugal, who now owns the ranch, passes on these accounts, which he says go back to the Shermans, a family who lived there earlier.","sherman-family-haunted-off-the-ranch-1990":"In the 1990s a family named the Shermans took over Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch in Utah known for reports of strange events, and were, as Michels puts it, basically haunted off the property. They reported orbs, UFOs and skinwalkers, the shape-shifters of Native American legend, and said they were rushed by an oversized wolf walking on two legs that took a close-range shotgun blast and kept going. Michels tells it himself as the family's own account.","sherman-on-affecting-the-sine-wave-at-will-1990":"In the 1990s, at a National Security Agency complex in Maryland, a man named Dan Sherman says he could change the movement of a sine wave (the smooth up-and-down line of a wave signal) with his mind, whenever he chose. Sherman, who says on the show that he was taken into a UFO programme as a child, tells it first-hand: \"I could definitely affect the motion and I could do it just at will.\" If true, it would mean a government agency was testing people for the power to move a signal by thought alone.","sherman-s-base-photo-identified-as-san-vito-dei-normanni-1990":"A photo of a base printed in Above Black, the book by the former Air Force sergeant Dan Sherman about what he calls his secret work communicating with aliens, shows San Vito dei Normanni, a base in southern Italy. Michels says he and a researcher worked this out by running the photo through a reverse image search, and on the show Sherman confirms it himself: \"That is one of the bases that I was stationed at.\" Michels raises the find in several episodes, once also mentioning Nebraska, as a way of anchoring Sherman's story to a real place.","sherman-s-mother-abducted-and-fetus-genetically-managed-1961":"Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says his mother was abducted by aliens in the early 1960s. He says they took her again in 1963, while she was pregnant with him, and \"did the genetic management\" on him before he was born. Sherman tells Michels he learned this from a man he calls Captain White, and he offers it as the reason he was able to receive messages from them later.","sherman-s-sine-wave-tone-training-1990":"Dan Sherman, who says he was trained by the government to receive messages from alien beings by thought alone, describes a training method from the early 1990s based on pure sine wave tones. A trainer told him, \"I'm going to play a tone, and I want you to mentally hum that tone.\" Sherman tells Michels this happened to him, and if true it would mean official programmes were trying to teach people mental communication.","shermans-direwolf-survives-a-12-gauge-shotgun-1994":"In the mid 1990s, at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange events, the Sherman family who lived there say a huge wolf attacked their prized cattle and then shrugged off blasts from a 12-gauge shotgun. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's current owner, tells Michels this, citing a report by Zack Van Eyk in the Utah newspaper Deseret News. Told as true, it is one of the early signs that something on the ranch did not behave like ordinary animals.","shermer-s-1983-alien-abduction-experience-during-race-across":"The skeptic Michael Shermer says he had what felt like an alien abduction in 1983, while riding in Race Across America, a nonstop bicycle race across the country, on a lonely rural highway near Haigler, Nebraska. He tells Michels about it himself and mentions that he described it in an interview with ABC's Wide World of Sports. That a leading skeptic says he lived through such an experience is why he brings it up.","shirley-maclaine-s-going-within-meeting-with-pell-hopkins-an":"The actress Shirley MacLaine wrote in her book Going Within about being in a government office in Washington with Senator Claiborne Pell, the UFO abduction researcher Budd Hopkins, and Prince Hans-Adam II of Liechtenstein, according to Michels. He tells it from the book, without saying more about what was discussed. He raises it because it would put a senator, a royal and an abduction researcher in one room on the subject.","shoemaker-levy-9-breaks-apart-and-strikes-jupiter-1994":"In 1994 a body called Shoemaker-Levy 9 broke apart and its pieces smashed into the planet Jupiter. A researcher named Randall Carlson tells Michels that 21 pieces hit Jupiter, he thinks in the second week of July 1994. Michels, on an earlier episode with the writer Graham Hancock, says himself that Shoemaker-Levy 9 collided with Jupiter in 1994, without giving the number of pieces or the month. It is told as proof that impacts from space really do happen in our own solar system.","short-strange-looking-police-officer-escorts-michael-off-res-1977":"In 1977, on government land in Nevada, a police officer who looked strange led an Air Force veteran named Michael Johnson off restricted ground, Johnson says. He tells Michels the man \"was short,\" with a \"puffy\" face and \"a deputy dog kind of hat like from cartoons.\" He tells it as part of a longer story: a sighting and missing time on a drive as a trucker from Texas to Nevada, and the odd figure suggests to him that the officer may not have been an ordinary person.","shroud-of-turin-dating-tested-later-repair-cloth-1980":"The Shroud of Turin, a linen cloth long revered as the burial cloth of Jesus, was dated using pieces from later repairs, which were much younger than the original cloth. The researcher Randall Carlson says this on the show without naming a source, pointing to the several repair jobs done to it over the centuries, and if true the shroud could be much older than the dating suggested.","shurmer-and-avi-loeb-s-alien-bet-with-the-long-now-foundatio-2030":"The skeptic Michael Shermer says he and the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb have a $1,000 bet about aliens, placed with the Long Now Foundation, an organisation that holds long-term wagers, and looking ahead to 2030. Shermer tells Michels about the bet himself, as one of its two parties, while the two men argue over whether there is good evidence of alien visitors.","shuruppak-excavation-finds-a-flood-layer-above-the-city-1931":"Shuruppak, an ancient city in Iraq now called Tell Fara, was found sealed under a four-foot layer of flood mud when it was excavated in 1931. The author Matt LaCroix, who writes about lost ancient civilisations, tells Michels this from the Penn Museum's excavation report, and elsewhere in the same episode he calls Shuruppak one of the oldest cities on Earth, buried and concealed by the flood. He tells it as physical evidence of the great flood in the old stories.","sidney-sheldon-s-novel-about-a-crashed-saucer-seen-by-a-tour":"The bestselling novelist Sidney Sheldon is said to have known something real about crashed flying saucers but to have \"wouldn't dare ever be public about it,\" so he put it into a novel, which Sheehan calls The Doomsday Project, about a saucer crash in Italy that is seen by a tour bus. A lawyer named Daniel Sheehan tells Michels this second-hand, naming the TV producers Bill Todman Sr. and Bill Todman Jr. among the people in the story, and frames the book as a hypothetical that carried a true account.","sighting-map-clusters-around-trinity-site-1940":"A map of UFO sightings from the 1940s and 1950s is said to cluster tightly around Trinity Site in New Mexico, where the first atomic bomb was tested in 1945. The documentary maker James Fox tells Michels that when the sightings were plotted for his film The Phenomenon, \"the proximity to Trinity site was unbelievable\", and he names Lance Mungia and Robert Hastings, a researcher of UFO reports at nuclear sites, in telling it. If true, it suggests whatever was seen was drawn to the birth of nuclear weapons.","sightings-at-los-alamos-from-1948":"Unexplained objects were being seen over Los Alamos, the New Mexico laboratory where the atomic bomb was built, as early as about 1948. A researcher who goes by the online name RedPandaKoala tells Michels this, hedging the date with \"I believe\" and naming no source, as part of a case that UFOs gather around nuclear sites.","signals-beamed-into-benowitz-s-computer-from-across-the-stre-1980":"In Albuquerque in the early 1980s, signals are said to have been beamed into the computer of a man named Benowitz from across the street, as part of a deliberate operation aimed at him. Greg Bishop tells the story with some caution (\"Yes, maybe that happened\") and says the man who told him was Bill Moore. Bishop tells it in an episode about how a man was driven to madness by what he believed were aliens.","signed-copy-of-alien-conspiracy-2004":"Michels says he got hold of a copy of a book called Alien Conspiracy and found it signed on the first page by Chris Evans, a researcher said to have died on the day he was due to meet the Ramseys, who look into an alleged crash. Michels tells this first-hand: \"lo and behold, on the first page, it was signed by Chris Evans, dated December 1st, 2004.\" For him the signature is a physical trace of Evans.","silas-newton-speech-beings-interested-in-atomic-research-1950":"In 1950, a man named Silas Newton said in a speech that the beings behind the flying saucers were keenly interested in America's atomic work: \"Unquestionably, they are interested in our atomic research.\" His words are quoted on an episode about a supposed crash that left alien bodies behind. Newton spoke as someone claiming direct knowledge, and if he was right, visitors were watching the nuclear age from its very start.","silent-black-triangles-over-aboriginal-communities":"Aboriginal people in the Australian Outback have seen silent black triangles flying over their communities. The Australian journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels this as something those people told him, and he does not say when it happened.","silent-triangle-ufo-in-florida-2019":"In Florida in 2019, the author Peter Levenda, who writes about occult history and intelligence agencies, says he saw a silent triangle-shaped object with lights on it: \"I'm looking at a triangle with the lights.\" He tells Michels he saw it himself, and brings in a man he calls Tom in the account. To him it is a personal sighting of the kind of craft others have reported for decades.","silver-bridge-collapse-drowning-victims-hear-numbers-1967":"When the Silver Bridge at Point Pleasant collapsed in 1967, some people drowning in the river are said to have suddenly heard voices calling out numbers, \"number 3004, number 3,486\", and \"wake up, wake up\", and then came back to life. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells this on the show, placing the bridge in Pennsylvania and citing John Keel, an author who wrote about strange events there. He tells it as a sign that some unseen intelligence intervened.","silver-disc-above-the-mesa-darts-away-2016":"In 2016 a silver disc is said to have hung in the air above a mesa on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, and then darted away. Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner, says he saw it himself. He describes \"a silver grayish disc-like object about a hundred feet above the mesa just sitting there suspended in the air.\" In a later interview he calls it \"a flying saucer 100 ft above the mesa in broad daylight, right in front of us,\" and says he \"went from being a skeptic to an experiencer within a matter of minutes.\" There he names Erik Bard as another witness, and that version does not mention the disc darting away. Both tellings come from Fugal, years apart. If true, the owner of the ranch saw a solid craft in daylight from close up.","silver-sky-memo-given-to-moore-as-a-test-1940":"A memo about a supposed secret project called Silver Sky, set in New Mexico, was handed to a UFO researcher named Bill Moore, and when he checked it out he found it was false, according to the writer Greg Bishop. Bishop tells it on the show as something Moore went through, saying Moore \"found out that was BS too\", and the story is that the memo was given to him as a test, to see what he would do with a planted document.","silver-snake-comes-out-of-the-burnt-tree":"A silver snake came out of a burnt tree in his backyard in North Carolina, according to Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs show up around him. Bledsoe tells Michels he saw it himself: \"all of a sudden, this snake comes out of the tree. This silver snake.\" He offers it as one of the strange happenings around his home that he links to the phenomenon.","silver-surfer-video-bill-nye-s-brine-shrimp-claim-and-the-re-2008":"The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says that in 2008, in Santa Monica, California, he saw something in the sky he could not identify, filmed it, and posted the video, calling the object the Silver Surfer. Corbell tells Michels the story as the man who shot it. As he tells it, the science presenter Bill Nye claimed the object was brine shrimp, and the video was later said to be a Red Bull skydiver, explanations Corbell brings up as ones he rejects.","similar-incursions-at-norfolk-naval-base-and-over-dc":"Unknown objects also intruded over Norfolk Naval Base in Virginia and over Washington, DC, much like the ones Navy fighter pilots reported near their own training areas. The Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves tells Michels this as something he heard from others, and if true it would mean the intrusions were reaching some of the most sensitive military airspace in the country.","similar-structures-under-menkaure-2-2-and-the-sphinx-one-2025":"A scientist named Filippo Biondi, who scanned the ground beneath the pyramids of Giza in Egypt, says the hidden structures he found under the pyramid of Khafre show up under its neighbours too: two plus two under the pyramid of Menkaure and one under the Sphinx. He tells Michels first-hand that \"we found the same thing also in Menkaure and also under the Sphinx.\" If so, the whole plateau would sit on a planned underground system far older or larger than anyone has admitted.","simon-magus-levitating-and-peter-and-paul":"There is an old Christian story in which a figure called Simon Magus uses black magic to levitate and then encounters the apostles Peter and Paul. Michels brings it up himself, calling it a biblical story, while talking with a guest about magic and the hidden forces behind history.","simone-s-belief-that-ai-is-the-current-revelation-2020":"A woman called Simone believes we are living through a revelation in the literal, religious sense, and that what is being revealed is artificial intelligence. The religious studies professor Diana Pasulka tells Michels about her, saying she gives Simone a whole chapter in her 2023 book Encounters.","simons-engineered-stony-brook-joint-management-of-brookhaven":"The claim is that a man named Jim Simons personally arranged for Stony Brook University to manage Brookhaven National Laboratory, a government research lab on Long Island, jointly with an organisation called the Battelle Memorial Institute. A researcher named Matthew Pines says this on the show, describing the partnership as \"personally engineered by Jim Simons himself\", without naming a source. He raises it to suggest that a private figure had a hand in steering a national lab.","simons-foundation-head-david-spergel-chairs-nasa-uap-study-2022":"The point being made is that the scientist NASA chose in 2022 to lead its independent study of UAP, 'unidentified anomalous phenomena,' the official term for UFOs, was David Spergel, head of the Simons Foundation. The foundation was set up by the billionaire Jim Simons. Matthew Pines, a researcher of national security, tells Michels that Spergel 'was tapped to be the chair of the NASA UAP working group,' and the same words come up again in a later episode. Michels returns to it several times on his own, crediting the mathematician Eric Weinstein for raising it. Once he puts it as a question: 'look at who ran the independent NASA review panel on UAPs in 2022, might they have any ties to Jim Simons.' Elsewhere he notes that Simons founded Renaissance Technologies, a hedge fund. He calls Spergel Simons' 'principal scientific advisor' and points to the panel's report, published in 2023. Pines and Michels raise the link to suggest that the people connected to Simons have a quiet hand in how the subject is handled in public.","simons-if-you-knew-how-he-actually-made-money":"The researcher Eric Weinstein says he once met Jim Simons, the mathematician who made a fortune running a hedge fund. When the subject turned to how someone made his money, Simons took a drag on his cigarette, paused for a long time and said, \"Eric, if you knew how he actually made money, you'd be so disappointed.\" Weinstein tells this as something he was there for; Michels later repeats it in another episode, without the cigarette and with Simons saying \"we\", which hints at a secret method behind a famous fortune that is less impressive than people think.","simosa-s-conversion-to-and-departure-from-christianity":"A researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels that he became a Christian in England at 19, \"kind of out of nowhere,\" and that later he left the faith. He describes it as his own experience, part of how he came to his present views on UFOs, secret societies and religion.","simpkinson-gemini-11-ufo-lithograph-likely-a-mock-up-1966":"A printed picture from Gemini 11, a 1966 NASA spaceflight, that shows a UFO against the Earth was probably a mock-up, because the original photograph shows the same clouds and no UFO. A physicist and former NASA scientist named Kevin Knuth tells Michels this. He says a researcher named Ed Wilson, going through the UFO file of a NASA man named Scott Simpkinson, found the original photograph in the University of Arizona archives, showing 'Earth with that cloud pattern in the background, and there's no UFO there.'","simpkinson-s-nasa-files-auctioned-and-bought-by-ed-wilson":"NASA files kept by a man named Scott Simkinson are said to have gone public and been sold at auction after he and a woman named Emily Erdle died. A man named Ed Wilson bought them and found a lithograph, a kind of printed picture, among them. A researcher named Kevin Knuth tells this on the show without naming a source, as the story of how Wilson came to have the files.","sir-peter-horsley-approached-by-nordics-miss-markham-and-mr-1950":"In the 1950s the British officer Sir Peter Horsley was approached by Nordics, human-looking beings with fair features described in UFO stories, and invited to a \"fireside chat\" with a Miss Markham and a Mr. Janus. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as something he has read, crediting a book called Closer Encounters. If true, it would mean a senior British figure believed he had met visitors posing as ordinary people.","sirhan-sirhan-as-an-mk-ultra-subject-linked-to-william-josep-1968":"Sirhan Sirhan, the man convicted of killing Robert F. Kennedy in 1968, was a subject of MK Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control research, Michels says, because he had spent time with the hypnotist William Joseph Bryan (Michels slips and says \"William Jennings Bryan\"). Michels gives this as his own reading, and if true it would mean the assassin had been worked on by someone linked to CIA mind-control efforts.","sirhan-sirhan-s-diary-before-the-rfk-shooting-1968":"Sirhan Sirhan, the man who shot Robert F. Kennedy in Los Angeles in 1968, had written in a diary just before the shooting, Michels says. He mentions this in passing in an episode about a CIA scientist and does not say there what the diary contained.","sirius-program":"Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who has come forward as a whistleblower, speaks on the show about something he calls the Sirius program, and explains its name simply: \"Sirius is a star.\" He points to a post he wrote on X to Elon Musk on 6 June 2025. What the programme is, beyond its name, the telling leaves vague.","sister-maria-s-flame-in-the-convent-cell-1807":"A young nun named Sister Maria, asleep in her small cell in a Catholic convent, is said to have woken to see a shining object like a flame come out of the wall and hover above her. Michels tells it himself, placing it in the late 1800s, in an episode with the religion scholar Diana Pasulka about links between UFOs and religion. He tells it because the scene looks a lot like modern accounts of glowing lights appearing to people in their bedrooms.","site-in-texas-that-teaches-blindfolded-sensing":"There is said to be a place in Texas that teaches people to develop unusual senses, such as sensing things while blindfolded. If so, it would suggest such abilities can be trained. Michels mentions it, hedging with \"I think\", while talking with the scientist Rupert Sheldrake.","sitting-beside-arthur-c-clarke-at-the-2001-premiere-1968":"In London in 1968, at a showing of Stanley Kubrick's new film 2001: A Space Odyssey, Whitley Strieber found himself in the seat right next to Arthur C. Clarke, the science fiction writer whose story The Sentinel the film grew out of. Strieber, a writer who says he has met alien beings, tells Michels he was there himself (\"I end up sitting beside Arthur C. Clark in the movie\"), and he mentions a man named Mike Smith as part of how it came about. By his account it was an early brush with the people who first imagined human contact with other minds.","six-fingered-diorite-hand-found-at-shuruppak-1931":"Excavators at Shuruppak, an ancient Sumerian city at Tell Fara in Iraq, are said to have found a carved hand with six fingers in 1931, made of diorite, which the researcher Matt LaCroix calls \"one of the hardest stones on Earth.\" LaCroix tells Michels about it in passing and points to the Penn Museum's excavation report on the site. He brings it up as a strange object that could hint at something unusual in the deep past.","six-of-knapp-s-corroborating-witnesses-were-threatened-into-1989":"In 1989 in Las Vegas, six people who could have backed up Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien spacecraft at a secret site in the Nevada desert, each got a phone call threatening them, and none of them ever went public. The researcher Luigi Vendittelli tells Michels this, repeating what the Las Vegas TV journalist George Knapp, who broke Lazar's story, told him. If true, it would mean someone worked to silence anyone who could support Lazar's account.","six-tons-of-missing-fissionable-material-at-chernobyl-1986":"A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says that at Chernobyl, the Soviet nuclear plant where a reactor exploded in 1986, six tons of fissionable material went missing: material that was kept at the plant, not inside the reactors. Buchanan tells Michels this as something he dealt with himself, and it would mean that dangerous nuclear material vanished amid the disaster.","size-of-the-pyramid-at-el-mirador-2024":"The pyramid at El Mirador, an ancient Maya site in the jungle of Guatemala, is so big, says a researcher named Luke Caverns, that he spent five minutes walking up it without stopping. Caverns tells Michels he visited in 2024: \"for the last 5 minutes I was walking on the pyramid straight.\" He offers it as a first-hand sense of how huge these buried structures are.","skeptic-carl-pflock-s-only-visit-to-the-crash-site":"Scott Ramsey, a researcher who has spent years investigating a craft said to have crashed in 1948 in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, says only one skeptic has ever visited the site: the writer Carl Pflock. Ramsey tells Michels that happened only because he drove Pflock out himself and walked him around, though Pflock lived just two hours away. Ramsey offers this as a sign that the doubters never looked at the ground itself.","skeptical-cinematographer-michael-convinced-by-the-mia-tests-2023":"In 2023 in Glendale, California, a cinematographer named Michael watched tests of a child named Mia, who is said to be able to read minds, and says they changed his mind. He worked on the crew of the filmmaker Ky Dickens, whose documentary is about children said to be telepathic. Michael says on the show that he is \"generally a skeptic\" but after seeing it, \"it's hard for me to not believe this stuff,\" which, if right, would mean the mind reading held up in front of a doubter.","skeptical-finnish-rov-team-calls-it-man-made-and-like-a-powe-2019":"In 2019 a Finnish crew sent a remotely operated underwater robot down to a strange object on the floor of the Baltic Sea. Their operator, who had been a skeptic, came back saying \"This must be man-made. This is constructed,\" and likened part of it to a power line. A treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, whose team found the object, tells Michels this as what the operator told him. If he is right, the shape on the seafloor was built by someone, not formed by nature.","skiers-in-antarctica-evacuated-by-black-helicopters":"Skiers exploring Antarctica for sport have had black helicopters come down on them and remove them, as if they had strayed somewhere they were not meant to be. Michels tells this himself, without naming the skiers or a source, in a conversation about secret Nazi technology; if true, it would mean something on the continent is being guarded.","skinny-bob-video-dismissed-as-cgi-2020":"The Skinny Bob video, a clip passed around UFO circles on Twitter and X and said to show an alien figure, is fake, according to the hip hop artist Flying Lotus, who follows the subject closely. He tells Michels, \"It's clearly CGI,\" meaning computer-generated imagery, so in his view it proves nothing about aliens.","skinwalker-ranch-camera-dismantled-between-frames":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, a camera mounted on a pole was found with its wires cut and all the duct tape pulled off the pole, all in the short gap between two of its frames. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, citing a man named John B. Alexander: 'somehow somebody cut the wires of that camera and pulled all of the duct tape off the pole.' If true, something did the work faster than any person could have.","skinwalker-ranch-cattle-mutilations-and-bigelow-experiments-1990":"The family who used to live on Skinwalker Ranch in Utah saw many cattle mutilations and all sorts of paranormal events, Michels says, before the businessman Robert Bigelow, who is deeply interested in UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the newer official term for UFOs), bought it. Michels, telling it himself, says it now seems to be a place where all kinds of experiments are run and where odd electromagnetic effects show up on the grounds.","skinwalker-ranch-freaky-stories":"Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch in Utah, is said to produce a steady run of strange stories. The journalist Michael Shellenberger says so in passing on the show, calling it \"very freaky\" and saying \"a lot of freaky stories\" come out of it, though he does not tell any of them.","skinwalkers-at-the-pentagon-on-aatip-and-remote-viewing-2007":"The book Skinwalkers at the Pentagon describes AATIP, a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, alongside a related programme, and suggests that remote viewing, the claimed ability to see distant places with the mind alone, was 'sort of again revived' there, according to Jesse Michels. He mentions it himself, saying he was just reading the book, while talking with a guest.","skip-atwater-s-contact-in-australia-sees-ufos-going-in-and-o":"A man posted at Mount Zeil in Australia is said to have seen UFOs flying in and out of the mountain. A former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan passes this on second hand: a man named Skip Atwater told him about a contact there who called it 'the most boring job I've ever had,' except for 'all those UFOs going in and out.' If true, it would mean something uses the mountain as a base.","skorzeny-shown-a-demonstration-of-the-bell-craft-1944":"In 1944 Otto Skorzeny, whom the teller calls Nazi Germany's master of psychological warfare and special operations, came to the Skoda Works, a large arms factory, and was supposedly given a demonstration of the Bell, a craft said to have been built in a secret Nazi programme. A researcher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this, with a \"supposedly\" and no named source. If true, it would put a working Nazi craft before senior commanders near the war's end.","skunk-works-advisors-to-secret-machines-2016":"Around 2016, senior figures from Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret aircraft design division, named as Robert Weiss, Steve Justice and Eric Schrock, together with Neil McCasland, a commander of the Air Force Research Laboratory, advised Tom DeLonge and Peter Levenda on Sekret Machines, their book series about UFOs. Michels says this himself without naming a source. He raises it because it would tie senior defense insiders to a popular UFO project.","sky-elements-drones-fall-from-the-sky-over-the-east-field-2023":"Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property long known for reports of strange happenings, says that sometime between 2023 and 2025 drones flown by Sky Elements, a drone company, were over the part of the ranch called the East Field, or the triangle, when all of them began behaving mysteriously and started \"dropping like bricks from the sky.\" Fugal tells Michels this as something that happened on his own land, and he offers it as a sign that something in that spot interferes with machines.","sky-ship-with-glowing-sails-snags-a-london-chimney-with-its-1800":"Sometime in the early 1800s, a sailing ship with glowing sails is said to have flown over London, dropped an anchor that caught on a chimney, and broken the top off it. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he draws UFOs to him, tells Michels he read this story, without saying where. To him it is an old account of a flying craft seen long before anyone talked about UFOs.","sky-watcher-operators-differ-from-legacy-programs":"The armed operators on a team called Sky Watcher, led by a man named Jake Barber, are said to do something completely different from the long-running secret government efforts that people in this field call legacy programmes. Michael Herrera, who says he saw a craft 300 feet across in the Indonesian jungle, tells Michels this as he heard it from Barber: \"The operators he has on this team do not do what these legacy programs do.\" If true, Barber's group would be something separate from those older secret efforts, not a continuation of them.","skywatcher-s-government-funded-uap-observation-events-four-a-2020":"Skywatcher, a startup that says it tries to summon UFOs, says it ran field events to watch for UAP (the official term for unidentified flying objects) in the western United States, paid for by the government, and that for four and a half years it used no psionics, meaning attempts to reach the objects with the mind. Someone speaking for the company tells Michels this first-hand, adding, \"We have eight classes with this.\" The point is that whatever they recorded in those years came without anyone trying to call the objects mentally.","skywatcher-s-jordan-psionic-mind-meld-with-conscious-craft":"A man named Jordan Jose, linked to a group called Skywatcher, is said to describe flying an unknown craft with the mind, which he calls \"psionic\" activity. In this account the craft is itself conscious, and it is \"like a mind meld\" in which a person takes over its \"control modules.\" A guest named Matthew Pines passes this on to Michels as what Jose has said, and if true it would mean these craft are steered by thought.","skywatcher-s-mechanical-and-human-dog-whistles-2020":"A group called Skywatcher is said to try to draw unidentified craft toward it using two kinds of call, which Michels describes as a \"mechanical dog whistle\" and a \"human dog whistle.\" He credits the idea to a man named Jake Barber, who is connected to the group. Michels repeats it in an episode with the filmmaker Tim Alberino and does not say more about how either one works. If true, it would mean people can call these craft in on purpose, with both machines and people.","skywatcher-sees-orange-orbs-over-picatinny-arsenal-during-th-2024":"Jake Barber, a former military helicopter pilot who says he once flew a recovered UFO, says that during the 2024 wave of drone and light sightings over New Jersey, his team arrived on location and within 30 minutes on the first night \"all these orange orbs\" appeared right over Picatinny Arsenal, an Army research base. He tells Michels he saw it himself, and if true it would put the unexplained lights over a sensitive military site.","skywatcher-sessions-psionic-calls-brought-benign-uap-the-mec-2024":"In 2024 and 2025, at field sites run by Skywatcher, a company that tries to call in UFOs on purpose, calls made with the mind brought only friendly unidentified objects, while a mechanical signal, a kind of \"dog whistle,\" brought aggressive ones. That is what Jordan Jose, who says he took part, tells Michels: \"every time we did elicit a response, the phenomena was highly benign.\" If true, it would mean the way you call these things changes how they behave.","slime-mold-out-designs-the-japanese-subway":"A slime mold, a simple brainless organism that spreads out looking for food, is said to have found better routes than the Japanese subway. A guest named Tero Isokauppila, on an episode about mushrooms, tells Michels that if you lay out its food in the shape of the subway map, the slime mold 'can figure out a more efficient route than the actual Japanese subway system.' He offers it as a sign that very simple life can solve problems in a way that looks like intelligence.","slow-acceptance-of-gobekli-tepe-1960":"Michels says that after the archaeologist Klaus Schmidt began digging at Göbekli Tepe, an archaeological site in Turkey, in 1994, it took mainstream archaeology 15 years to accept the site. He tells it himself, as an example of experts being slow to accept finds that do not fit the accepted picture of the past.","small-ancient-cities-in-the-united-states-that-have-never-be":"A researcher of ancient sites named Luke Caverns says there are small ancient cities in the United States that have never been discovered, in places such as Florida, Georgia, South Carolina, Alabama and West Texas. Caverns tells Michels this from his own research, mentioning the archaeologist Ed Barnhart along the way, and it would mean America's ancient past is far bigger than the record shows.","small-grays-and-a-tall-gray-during-mario-woods-s-abduction-1960":"A former Air Force security guard at a nuclear missile site, Mario Woods, says that during an abduction in South Dakota he was met by several small beings and a taller one behind them. He tells Michels firsthand that they looked like Grays, the small grey-skinned beings of abduction stories, \"but the one behind them I guess you would classify him as a tall gray\", like the one in Whitley Strieber's book Communion. He dates the abduction to 1977, though the story is sometimes placed in the 1960s. If true, it would mean beings were taking people near nuclear sites, with a clear leader among them. The researcher Robert Hastings recorded a similar account in an article published in 2017, about a former Air Force missile security policeman's apparent abduction. In it, Woods says: \"I think I remember small figures, maybe five or six, walking toward the vehicle. Standing behind them was a taller figure who I think was in charge.\"","small-helicopter-circles-the-crash-site-1948":"After a craft is said to have crashed in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948, a very small helicopter reportedly showed up, circled the crash site, and then flew off to the northeast. Scott Ramsey, who has spent years looking into the Aztec crash, tells Michels he first heard about the helicopter from a local man named Ken Farley, and he also names Doug Noland as a source. If it happened, it would mean someone with aircraft reached the site soon after the crash.","smith-propulsion-paper-sent-to-vannevar-bush-1950":"In the 1950s a Canadian named Wilbert Smith wrote that a paper of his on propulsion had been carried to the American scientist Vannevar Bush, and that he was waiting for Bush's opinion. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this from Smith's personal files, where Smith says Donald Keyhoe, a writer on UFOs at the time, \"took the document on the propulsion\" to Bush. If true, it would put a Canadian's ideas on how such craft might fly in front of one of the most senior scientists in Washington.","smith-s-memo-to-the-psb-on-1-500-ufo-reports-1952":"In 1952, the CIA director Walter B. Smith sent a memo to the head of the Psychological Strategy Board, a Cold War body that planned propaganda and psychological operations, noting that over 1,500 UFO reports had been logged. Michels describes the memo himself on an episode about Henry Kissinger, as one document among others. He raises it to show that the CIA was counting UFO sightings and sharing them with officials who handled public perception.","smithsonian-digs-up-giant-bones-in-american-mountains":"The Smithsonian Institution once dug up the bones of giants in the mountains of America, according to a researcher named Tim Alberino, who tells Michels this without naming a particular source. Elsewhere in the same talk he points to the Smithsonian's own annual reports, which he says record \"very unusual skeletal remains, many of which were very large,\" though there he does not say they came from mountains. If so, the museum's own records would hold evidence of a race of very large humans.","smoke-but-no-flame-at-darpa-and-afrl-wright-patterson":"A scientist named John Blitch, who worked at DARPA (the Pentagon's research agency for advanced technology), says he saw hints of hidden UFO work there but never the thing itself. He tells Michels that there was \"a lot of smoke but no flame that I personally observed\" at DARPA, or in his three years at the Air Force Research Laboratory at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio. It is the word of an insider who sensed something going on but did not see proof of it himself.","smyslov-s-alien-chess-vision-before-the-hubner-match-1970":"The story is that Vasily Smyslov, a world chess champion, was in a hotel room in Germany in the 1970s, getting ready for a match against the German player Robert Hübner, when something strange happened. As he went over his moves, 'you could say an alien appeared right before him.' Kiran Ilum Xenov, the former president of a Russian republic, tells this to Michels. He says it came from Smyslov and from Smyslov's wife, Nadezhda Smyslova, and he mentions Smyslov's own records of the game. Months later, in a different interview, Michels retells it from memory without naming the player. In his version, one of the greatest chess players was playing out a practice game before a big tournament when 'the moves made themselves,' and the player put this down to 'some sort of alien intervention.' Taken at face value, it would mean something nonhuman reached into a top-level chess game.","snow-would-not-settle-on-the-saucer-airman-donald-bass-1948":"In 1948, at a saucer said to have crashed in Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, snow blew sideways across the site and piled on the ground and rocks but vanished the moment it touched the craft, so none built up on it. Michels tells this, saying the detail comes from a young airman on perimeter security named Donald Bass; if true, it would mean the downed craft was still giving off something, perhaps heat or energy, that no ordinary wreck would.","snowden-s-revelations-about-bullrun-and-prism-2013":"Edward Snowden, the intelligence contractor who leaked secret files in 2013, showed that US spy agencies had back doors into technology through programmes called Bullrun and PRISM, Michels says. Michels adds his own view that this was not done without Google and Apple knowing, which, if right, would mean the tech giants knowingly helped the government spy.","society-for-psychical-research-and-the-history-of-psychical-1870":"Scientific study of telepathy and other mental powers is said to go back to the 1870s, to the Society for Psychical Research, a group of scholars who investigated such claims. Ky Dickens, maker of a documentary on telepathy, says this on the show without naming a source, citing the scholar Frederick Myers and the psychologist William James as part of that history. She raises it to show that serious people have studied the subject for some 150 years.","socorro-footprints-and-hynek-s-conversion-1964":"The story is that in 1964 there were footprints on the ground at Socorro, New Mexico, where an object was reported, and that seeing them is what changed the mind of an astronomer named J. Allen Hynek about UFOs. John Blitch, a scientist who worked with the Pentagon's research agency DARPA and who says he was himself abducted, tells Michels this briefly, without naming a source: Hynek \"saw them and that's what flipped him\".","solar-camera-aimed-at-the-portal-spot-on-fenced-off-ranch-2025":"Ross Coulthart, an Australian journalist who investigates UFO claims, says that in 2025 he found a solar-powered camera on the fenced-off Bradshaw Ranch near Sedona, Arizona, \"pointing exactly at the point where Linda Bradshaw says she saw the portal.\" Linda Bradshaw once lived on the ranch and reported strange events there, including a doorway-like opening in the air. Coulthart tells Michels he saw the camera himself and reads it as a sign someone is still watching that spot.","soldiers-and-officers-confirm-the-giant-story-to-alberino":"Soldiers and officers are said to have privately confirmed the story of a twelve-foot giant captured by US forces in Afghanistan. A researcher named Tim Alberino, who tells that giant story, says on the show that 'a handful of people,' some soldiers and some officers, told him it is true, that 'there are even wilder stories,' and that there is more than one giant out there. If true, giant humanlike beings would be living today and known to the military.","solon-brown-weeps-telling-deacon-bruce-seer-1948":"Near Aztec, New Mexico, around the time of a claimed 1948 saucer crash, a man named Solon Brown is said to have broken down and cried one night while talking with a church deacon named Bruce Seer. Sixty years later, Seer's son told Scott Ramsey, who interviewed witnesses to the crash, that he never forgot that night because it was the first time he had seen a grown man cry. Michels tells it in an episode about the crash, as a sign of how much whatever Brown had been through affected him.","solon-told-the-atlantis-story-by-priests-at-sais":"The story of Atlantis is said to have come to Greece through Solon, the Athenian lawmaker, who heard it from Egyptian priests at the temple of the goddess Neith in Sais, in the Nile Delta. The writer Graham Hancock, who studies lost ancient civilisations, tells it to Michels twice. He says the priests read the hieroglyphs on the temple wall to Solon and translated them, which is why Plato gives the date he does, a visit around 600 BC and an Atlantis around 9600 BC. The filmmaker Tim Alberino brings up the priest of Sais mostly for his teaching that the world goes through cycles of catastrophe, which he ties to our own age. The source is Plato's dialogues Timaeus and Critias, here in Benjamin Jowett's translation: \"To this city came Solon, and was received there with great honour\", and an old priest tells him, \"O Solon, Solon, you Hellenes are never anything but children.\" The priest says the great deeds \"have all been written down by us of old, and are preserved in our temples\", and that the full particulars can be gone through later \"in the sacred registers themselves\"; the Critias adds that the Egyptians had translated the names and that Solon put them back into Greek. So in Plato the priest tells the story and the written record waits to be read later. Whether that record was on a wall or in registers, and the scene of priests reading hieroglyphs aloud, are Hancock's details, which the text neither states nor rules out.","something-above-the-ranch-defies-rockets-and-manipulates-dro-2016":"Something in the sky over Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, interferes with rockets and drones, and there are anomalies in the ground below it as well, says Brandon Fugal, the ranch's owner. He tells Michels his team has \"documented an effect that there is something within the space above the ranch that defies rockets, that manipulates drones.\" If true, it would point to some hidden thing at the site, which he suggests may be under the ranch.","something-showed-up-in-the-mid-2000s-that-changed-the-game-2003":"A guest named Dylan Borland, who says a top government official once told him that aliens run everything, believes that something happened in the mid-2000s that changed the game: something showed up that the United States was not tracking. Borland tells Michels this as his own view, without saying what it was, and if true it would mean a new kind of presence appeared around that time.","son-of-man-title-taken-from-the-parables-of-enoch":"Jesus's title \"the Son of Man\" was taken from an older Jewish book, the researcher Tim Alberino tells Michels: from 1 Enoch, an ancient text left out of most Bibles, and \"specifically from the second section,\" called the Parables. If so, the gospels would be drawing on a figure that Enoch's book had already described. In R. H. Charles's 1917 translation, Enoch asks his guiding angel about \"that Son of Man, who he was, and whence he was,\" and is told \"This is the Son of Man who hath righteousness\" and \"who revealeth all the treasures of that which is hidden.\" The book says \"from the beginning the Son of Man was hidden,\" that he \"was named in the presence of the Lord of Spirits,\" and that he sat \"on the throne of his glory\" and \"the sum of judgement was given unto the Son of Man.\" So the Parables do use the title again and again for a hidden, chosen figure who judges the world; whether the gospel writers took it from there is the teller's reading, which the book by itself can neither show nor rule out.","sonny-white-at-nasa-eagleworks-claims-casimir-effect-power":"A NASA researcher named Harold \"Sonny\" White, who works at Eagleworks, a small NASA group doing advanced research, claims he can power a 1.5 kilovolt microchip using the Casimir effect, a quantum effect in which a tiny force appears between surfaces set very close together in empty space. If true, it would mean usable energy can be drawn from empty space. Michels mentions it himself, as White's claim, in a conversation with a former NSA director.","sonny-white-harnessing-the-casimir-force-2020":"Jesse Michels says a researcher named Harold \"Sonny\" White seems to be harnessing the Casimir force on small scales. The Casimir force is a tiny pull that shows up between two surfaces placed extremely close together, and it is thought to come from the energy of empty space. Michels brings this up himself in a 2026 debate with a skeptic, without giving a source, as an example of exotic physics that someone is already working with.","sonny-white-s-casimir-effect-chip":"The engineer Harold \"Sonny\" White can engineer the Casimir effect, Michels says, meaning the tiny force that empty space exerts between surfaces placed extremely close together, reportedly harnessed on a chip. Michels mentions it in passing as his own point, offering White as someone already working with exotic physics of the kind tied to advanced propulsion.","sonny-white-s-nasa-eagleworks-warp-drive-work-now-funded-by-2010":"A NASA scientist named Harold \"Sonny\" White worked at Eagleworks, a small NASA lab, on what Michels says could be a warp drive, an engine that would bend space to cross it faster than light, and he now has money from DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, for his own company. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, while talking with a Navy scientist named Salvatore Pais, and he is unsure of the details: he thinks the company is called \"Limitless or something like that.\" If true, it would mean the military is now paying for work on faster-than-light travel.","sound-light-and-frequency-formula-written-on-an-atm-envelope-1996":"In 1996, in the Los Angeles backyard of a man named Bryce Zabel, a man known only as JC wrote a formula on an ATM deposit envelope in about 30 seconds, folded it up and handed it over, saying \"put this in a safety deposit box for about 10 to 15 years and if we don't make a deal then you'll know I was for real.\" Brent Friedman, who says he was the one handed the envelope, tells Michels it was \"an incomprehensible like formula thing\", and that when he asked what it was, JC said \"sound, light and frequency, secrets of the universe.\" Friedman dates it to September 21, 1996, says Zabel was there, and tells it as a hint that JC knew things that would only be proved years later.","sound-light-and-frequency-turns-up-in-a-cern-god-particle-wh-2012":"A man named Brent Friedman says he had been searching for the exact phrase \"sound, light and frequency\" for about 15 years, and that the first time it turned up, in that order, was in 2012, in a white paper from CERN, the European physics lab, about turning data from the discovery of the so-called God particle into sound. He tells Michels he found it himself, and treats the match as a striking echo of the phrase he had been chasing.","source-visibly-shaking-while-briefing-senate-staff-2017":"A Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell says that people who came to Capitol Hill to tell Senate staff what they knew about secret UFO matters were sometimes so frightened that one was visibly shaking as they began to explain. McConnell tells Michels this from his own time hearing such sources, and on a later episode, when the host prompts him, he describes them more generally as earnest people with credible backgrounds who were \"nervous and they're shaking.\" To him, that fear suggests the people were telling the truth about something dangerous to reveal.","source-who-has-been-inside-s4":"There is said to be a secret facility called S4 near Papoose Lake in Nevada, and UAPGerb, an online researcher of UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), tells Michels he has talked to one person who has been there. He does not name that person or say what they told him. If the claim holds, it would mean the hidden site is real and people have worked inside it.","sources-tell-senators-of-recovered-and-reverse-engineered-cr-2017":"Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, says that between roughly 2017 and 2024 people found their way to Capitol Hill and met with senators, claiming that crashed craft not made by humans had been recovered and reverse engineered, meaning taken apart to learn how they work. He tells Michels he was in the room for this, and if the claims hold it would mean senators heard them firsthand.","southcom-tic-tac-detected-by-space-asset-near-a-naval-vessel":"A Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, was picked up by a sensor in space as it passed close to a ship at sea, in the area covered by the US military's Southern Command. Jesse Michels reads out this case heading on the show with Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official, and it comes from the Immaculate Constellation report, an unsigned report that Michael Shellenberger delivered to Congress and Representative Nancy Mace entered into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024 as a document from the Pentagon. The report calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, cleared for release by the State Department; Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. The report describes imagery of \"a Tic-Tac UAP\" (UAP is the official term for unexplained objects) \"transiting through an area of the Atlantic Ocean\", says \"No observed reaction is seen to come from the vessel\", and reads the ship's nearness as a sign of \"foreknowledge of the UAP event by intelligence\". If so, someone would have known in advance where the object would be, but that is the report's own inference, and the imagery is described, not shown.","southwest-research-analysis-of-the-implant-sliver-1993":"In the mid-1990s, a sliver of what Whitley Strieber calls his implant is said to have been examined at the Southwest Research Institute in San Antonio, Texas, and found to be a metal base with tiny fibres, like cilia, coming off it and moving. Strieber, a writer who says he has met non-human beings, tells Michels what a man there named William Malow told him: \"I've got your piece of your sliver of the implant.\" If true, the object would have a living-seeming structure no ordinary metal fragment has.","soviet-divers-meet-tall-humanoids-at-lake-baikal-1982":"Soviet divers are said to have gone down into Lake Baikal in 1982 to look for something and met very tall humanlike beings under the water. Michels raises it himself, as a question to a treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, and breaks off mid-sentence before saying where the story comes from or what happened next. If it were true, beings of some kind would be living or working underwater in Siberia's great lake.","soviet-horten-based-flying-saucer-program-1945":"After 1945 the Soviet Union ran a programme to build a kind of flying saucer, based on the designs of the Horten brothers, two German aircraft designers of the Second World War, according to the author Peter Levenda. He tells Michels that this came out once the Soviet Union fell and some of its documents became available, though he does not name the documents. The claim would mean that the Soviets, too, tried to build disc-shaped craft from German wartime work.","soviet-microwave-beaming-of-the-us-embassy-in-moscow-1970":"During arms control talks in the 1970s, around 1976 as he recalls it, Henry Kissinger told the Soviet diplomat Anatoly Dobrynin to stop beaming the US embassy in Moscow with microwaves, says Jesse Michels. He tells it himself in an episode with a former head of the NSA, crediting a Der Spiegel and 60 Minutes report and naming the US ambassador Walter Stoessel; if true, it shows that both superpowers were already using energy beams against people decades ago.","soviet-military-exercise-drew-ufos-once-nuclear-weapons-were":"During the Soviet era, the military is said to have run an exercise built to lure UFOs, and the UFOs came once nuclear weapons were brought in. Robert Hastings, a researcher who studies UFO sightings at nuclear weapons sites, tells Michels this comes from a retired Soviet army colonel, whom he does not name, who says on a video online that he took part in the exercise. If true, it would mean UFOs take a special interest in nuclear weapons, and that the Soviets tested that on purpose.","soviet-missiles-brought-online-1960":"In the 1960s, Soviet nuclear missiles were reportedly switched on, or \"brought online\", by something unexplained. A guest named Karl Nell, who speaks from an official background, tells Michels this was \"an analogous thing\" to similar incidents with American missiles. He says documents and testimony show it was real. He credits records released under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records. If true, the same unexplained interference with nuclear weapons happened on both sides of the Cold War.","soviet-paleocontact-theories":"Two Soviet thinkers, Alexander Kazantsev and Felix Zigel, proposed that alien visitors may have shaped early human development. This idea is called paleocontact: the belief that beings from elsewhere made contact with ancient humans. Michels mentions it himself, without naming a source, as part of the longer history of people asking whether visitors had a hand in human origins.","soviet-phobos-probe-s-last-image-before-shutdown-1989":"In 1989 a Soviet space probe sent to Phobos, one of the moons of Mars, is said to have sent back one last picture before it went dead, and that picture shows something strange. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source: he says the last image shows an object shaped \"like a fin on the tail of a plane\" that seems to be right there in the photo. If that is so, something unexplained was near Mars when the probe stopped working.","soviet-psychics-tasked-against-us-congressmen-senators-cabin":"The Soviet Union is said to have trained its psychics to spy on American leaders: congressmen, senators, cabinet members and the president. Joe McMoneagle tells Michels this second-hand, saying it came from a box of material from a Soviet psychic unit. If true, the Soviets were using psychic spying aimed right at the top of the US government.","soviet-psychokinesis-experiments-on-mice-in-submarines-1970":"In the 1970s, Soviet researchers are said to have taken mice down in submarines and tried to stop their hearts with the mind alone, a test of psychokinesis, the idea that thought can act directly on matter. The philosopher Jason Jorjani mentions this in passing to Michels, without a source, as evidence that the Soviets learned such a power \"can be used to kill somebody.\" If true, it would mean mental powers were studied as a weapon.","soviet-spy-at-1950-pearl-harbor-demo-wounded-prairie-chicken":"In 1950, during a demonstration at Pearl Harbor, the work of the American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, whom Michels presents as a scientist who tried to build gravity-defying craft, was supposedly compromised by a Soviet spy posing as a janitor. Michels tells this himself, drawing on the writer Paul Schatzkin and his biography of Brown, The Man Who Mastered Gravity. If true, it would mean the Soviets thought Brown's experiments were worth stealing.","soviet-target-mice-psi-experiment-in-the-urals":"A Soviet psychic research unit is said to have run an experiment in the Ural Mountains in which some mice were chosen as targets and others kept as controls, and the results came out with the top 12 all target mice and the bottom 12 all controls. Joe McMoneagle, a former US Army remote viewer (someone trained to describe distant places or events by mind alone), tells Michels he read this in a box of material from the Soviet unit and calls it \"a perfect match\". If true, the Soviets had a clean result showing a mind could pick out or affect living targets.","soviet-ukraine-missiles-enter-countdown-under-disc-1982":"In 1982, a huge disc-shaped object is said to have hovered over a Soviet nuclear missile base in Ukraine while, down in a launch capsule, the missiles suddenly went into countdown mode on their own, which would mean the object could reach into nuclear weapons controls. Robert Hastings, a researcher who collects accounts of UFOs at nuclear bases, tells Michels this second-hand, from a source who described the incident and from classified Soviet UFO documents, naming the journalist George Knapp and a man he calls Vladimir Platonoff in the chain.","soviet-version-of-operation-paperclip-1945":"In 1945, the Soviet Union is said to have run its own version of Operation Paperclip, the American programme that brought German scientists to the United States after the Second World War, and to have taken more than 3,000 scientists and their families into Russia. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels this, crediting Sekret Machines, a book series co-written by the musician Tom DeLonge. If true, the Soviets would have come out of the war with their own large share of German technical know-how.","space-act-of-1958-ties-nasa-to-dod-and-allows-withholding":"The law that created NASA, the National Aeronautics and Space Act passed by Congress in 1958, is said on the show to bind NASA closely to the Pentagon and to let it keep certain findings secret. If so, NASA's public, civilian face would not be the whole story. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani makes the first point without naming a source. He calls it \"just the observation\" that NASA and the Department of Defense \"have always been very tightly linked in law\" through the act that created NASA. Michels goes further in his own framing. He says some matters fell \"outside the civilian mandate\", so that if NASA found anything strategically sensitive, such as \"a breakthrough in propulsion, a powerful new sensor, or even contact with non-human intelligence\" (the field's term for minds that are not human), \"it could be withheld indefinitely.\" So two people tell it: a researcher describes the legal link, and the host spells out what he thinks it allowed NASA to hide.","space-force-people-ask-about-hawkeye-360-detecting-uap-signa-2022":"In 2022 in Huntsville, Alabama, at a meeting of the Scientific Coalition for UAP Studies, a group that researches unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP, the newer word for UFOs), people tied to the Space Force and to intelligence agencies approached Bob Maguire, a former CIA contractor, he says. He tells Michels they talked to him \"about applying Hawkeye abilities to just looking for UAP signals on the ground,\" meaning the signal-detecting abilities of a company called Hawkeye 360. If true, it would show official interest in hunting for UAP signals.","space-intelligences-chose-owens-for-the-strongest-nervous-sy":"A man named Ted Owens, who claimed contact with beings he called space intelligences, said they chose him because he had \"the most powerful nervous system of anybody they had ever encountered since Moses.\" The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove, who knew Owens's claims, repeats them to Michels. In Owens's telling, that made him a uniquely fit channel for these beings.","space-kid-channels-an-egyptian-god-inside-the-pyramid-1976":"In 1976, inside the Great Pyramid at Giza, a researcher named Andrija Puharich ran experiments with one of the young people called 'space kids', who is said to have channelled an Egyptian god and brought through messages. An unnamed guest tells Michels this without naming a source: 'Apparently, they're channeling this Egyptian god.' If true, it would mean a voice claiming to be an ancient god spoke through a living person.","space-kid-shows-copper-bracelet-worn-in-channeling-sessions-1970":"A researcher named Greg Malatzi says that while filming an interview with a woman who had been one of the \"space kids\", young people who took part in channeling sessions in the 1970s, meaning sessions where people said they spoke messages from other beings, she pulled out a bracelet with a little copper plate on it. She told him, decades later, that they all had to wear these bracelets during their channeling sessions with the man who led them, who is described as a CIA scientist said to have contacted an alien council. Malatzi tells Michels this as something he saw himself.","space-kids-channeling-tapes-name-israeli-generals-such-as-ah-1970":"In the 1970s, in Ossining, New York, a group of children known as the space kids took part in taped channeling sessions, in which a person is said to speak messages from another intelligence, and on those tapes Israeli generals such as Aharon Yariv are named. A researcher named Greg Malatzi says this on the show about the tapes kept by Andrija Puharich, a scientist the episode ties to the CIA, saying Puharich 'is referring by name to Israeli generals.' For Malatzi it points to a link between the sessions and the Israeli military.","space-program-insiders-learn-their-missions-without-being-to":"People in the US space programme, says a guest named Diana Pasulka, are not told their missions but have to work them out by themselves. She tells Michels she was around enough of them to know they figured it out alone, that it was hard, and that they only learned they had got it right \"until they got an award for it like two years later.\" She describes it as a kind of unspoken tradition, a way secret knowledge is passed on without anyone saying it outright.","space-program-latin-symbols-as-homage-to-the-sponsors":"People who launch American satellites are said to put Latin words and symbols on their mission patches as a tribute to beings they call \"the sponsors,\" and when the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka asked if the sponsors could read Latin, they said \"absolutely.\" Pasulka tells this to Michels from what insiders told her. In one version she places it in today's Space Force and mentions only the patches; in another she names a man she calls Tyler and says his launches carried imagery he didn't understand, including Greek goddesses.","space-scientist-david-w-webb-and-government-awareness-of-abd-1983":"Whitley Strieber, an author who has written about his own experiences of being taken by unknown beings at his cabin in upstate New York, says that by the mid-1980s he knew some people inside the government had had contact. He knew it, he tells Michels, because he had met a space scientist named David W. Webb, and he mentions the UFO researcher Stanton Friedman in the same account. If true, it means officials knew about such encounters well before the public did.","spacecraft-momentum-anomalies-in-the-van-allen-belts":"Spacecraft circling the Earth either speed up or slow down unexpectedly when they reach the Van Allen belts, the zones of charged particles that surround the planet. Charles Buhler, who worked at NASA, mentions these momentum anomalies to Michels without naming a source. He brings them up as a clue that something about how motion works there is not yet understood.","spacecraft-recovered-from-aztec-kingman-1948":"A spacecraft was recovered after a crash near Aztec, New Mexico, says the journalist Ross Coulthart, who passes it on in an aside to Michels as something he was told by David Grusch, a former US intelligence officer who has said the government holds crashed craft. Michels later tells the story in more detail on his own, without naming a source: he says that on March 25, 1948, a flying saucer about 100 feet across came down in the New Mexico desert with 16 charred alien bodies aboard, and that several eyewitnesses saw it from different spots. Neither teller says anything about Kingman, Arizona, the other place the story is sometimes tied to.","spaceship-reportedly-landed-and-was-hushed-up":"A spaceship is said to have landed and been taken to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio, with the whole thing kept quiet, in the words of Senator Barry Goldwater as played on the show: \"reportedly a spaceship landed, it was all hushed up, quieted.\" Goldwater gives it as something reported to him, not something he saw. Coming from a senator, it suggests the story had reached the top of Congress.","special-forces-guys-play-hunter-skinwalker-at-the-ranch":"Three armed men with special forces backgrounds are said to have gone onto Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange happenings, ready to play \"Hunter Skinwalker\", that is, to go looking for whatever haunts the place. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as he heard it from Brandon Fugal, who owns the ranch.","special-programs-bring-secret-technology-to-events-without-t-2020":"Secret military programs brought their own hidden technology to Skywatcher's events without telling the company, then had people sign nondisclosure agreements, says James Valor of Skywatcher, a startup that tries to summon UFOs. He tells Michels that \"special programs brought their own secret developments on our event, not informing us.\" If true, it would mean the military was quietly testing its own projects alongside the company's UFO work.","special-ufo-briefing-for-maintenance-teams-1967":"In 1967 at Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, home to nuclear missiles, maintenance crews were called into a special briefing and told \"there's UFOs out in the area,\" according to a man named Robert Jameson, who was on one of those teams. A former Air Force officer named Robert Salas, who served at the base, relays Jameson's account to Michels. If true, it would mean the Air Force knew of unknown objects near its nuclear sites and warned its own people.","spectre-modeled-on-odessa-1945":"SPECTRE, the villains' secret organization in Ian Fleming's James Bond novels, is said to be modeled on ODESSA, a real network that the story ties to the Nazi figures Martin Bormann and Otto Skorzeny after the war ended in 1945. A researcher named Jason Jorjani, who studies claims of a secret Nazi UFO program, tells Michels that SPECTRE is 'very obviously the organization which was known to Ian Fleming as Odessa.' If so, Fleming, who would have heard about it, put a real surviving Nazi network into popular fiction.","speculation-that-kirkpatrick-was-read-into-the-legacy-progra-2022":"Sean Kirkpatrick, who ran AARO (the Pentagon office set up in 2022 to look into unexplained aerial reports), is said by some to have been briefed into the \"legacy program,\" a secret effort said to hold recovered non-human craft. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, raises this with Michels only as speculation he has heard, and does not say who started it. If true, it would mean the man running the official inquiry knew about the very program it was meant to find.","sperm-sampling-and-insect-like-faces-1976":"Jim Weiner, who says he and others were abducted while on the Allagash Waterway in Maine in 1976, says that in a second session of recalling the event the faces of the beings finally came through, and he remembers screaming, \"They're bugs. They look like bugs.\" Weiner tells Michels this as his own experience, which by his account also included his sperm being sampled by the beings.","sphere-crash-south-of-the-border-that-killed-a-cow":"A sphere is said to have crashed on a farm about 100 miles south of the US border near Brownsville, Texas, on Mexico's east coast, with an explosion that killed a cow 100 meters away. Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants, tells Michels what he was told by a man named Massan, who says he bought the object from the farmer. If true, a crashed object of unknown make would be sitting in private hands.","sphere-described-as-a-control-mechanism-that-interacts-with-2014":"In 2014, at a Navy weapons research centre in Crane, Indiana, a man named Randy Anderson says he saw a sphere that the people there described as possibly part of a control system that responded to the mind. Anderson, who says he saw alien technology at a military base, tells Michels: \"I do remember them saying that it was potentially part of a control mechanism and that it interacted with conscious[ness].\" If true, the military would hold a craft part controlled by thought.","sphere-holds-15-ft-from-a-friend-s-cockpit-at-pax-river":"A sphere pulled up about 15 feet from a pilot's cockpit near Patuxent River, a Navy air station in Maryland known as Pax River, and stayed there while the pilot was manoeuvring fast. A former Navy fighter pilot named Ryan Graves tells Michels this happened to one of his good friends. He offers it as a case where an unknown object kept pace with a military jet at close range.","sphere-inside-a-cube-near-miss-at-oceana-2014":"Around 2014 or 2015, near Naval Air Station Oceana in Virginia, an object that looked like a dark cube inside a clear sphere shot past a pair of Navy jets as they entered their training area and came \"within about 50 ft of the cockpit\" of the lead jet, according to the Navy fighter pilot Ryan Graves, who relays what squadron mates told him and says a hazard report, the Navy's formal safety report, was filed. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet retells it from Graves, placing it during a carrier training exercise off the East Coast and saying the object split the pair of jets, and Michels mentions the shape in passing. If true, it means unknown objects were flying close enough to Navy jets to be a collision danger.","sphere-leaves-in-eight-seconds-1976":"Charlie Foltz, one of a group of men on a 1976 canoe trip on Eagle Lake in Maine's Allagash Waterway, says a glowing sphere they saw took off so fast that just 8 seconds passed between his spotting it and its shrinking to a pinpoint of light in the night sky. Foltz tells Michels he saw it himself. Few known aircraft could do that.","sphere-recovered-from-a-crashed-craft-2014":"In 2014, at Naval Surface Warfare Center Crane, a Navy facility in Indiana, Randy Anderson says he saw a sphere and was told it \"was recovered from a craft that had crashed.\" He tells Michels this firsthand, adding \"they did say that,\" which if true would mean the Navy held an object from a crashed craft.","spheres-entering-the-water-at-guadalupe-island-2019":"There were reports of spheres going into the sea at Guadalupe Island, off Mexico, and so Luis Elizondo, the former head of a small Pentagon office that studied unexplained aerial reports, went there with a former Navy man named Sean Cahill. A former Defense Department official and columnist named Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this, linking the trip to the National Geographic series Unidentified. If the reports are true, they would point to objects moving between air and water.","sphinx-enclosure-filled-in-and-excavated-by-thutmose-iv-used":"The Sphinx is said to be older than usually thought, because about a thousand years after its supposed building, the pharaoh Thutmose IV recorded that its enclosure, the pit cut around it, had filled in and that he had to dig it all out and clean it before he could become pharaoh. A researcher named Luke Caverns makes this argument on the show, without naming a source. If true, the Sphinx had already been long buried and neglected by Thutmose's time.","sphinx-enclosure-opening-said-to-connect-to-the-queen-s-cham":"One of three openings at the back of the Sphinx enclosure, the pit cut out around the Sphinx at Giza, is said to lead all the way to the Queen's Chamber, a room inside the Great Pyramid. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher with his own theory of what the pyramids were for, tells Michels that a friend of his in Egypt's Ministry of Antiquities, whom he does not name, confirmed the connection. If true, there would be a hidden passage running between the two monuments.","sphinx-originally-had-a-lion-s-head":"The idea is that the Great Sphinx in Egypt was first carved as a complete lion, and that its human head was cut later. The researcher Graham Hancock argues this on two episodes. He says \"the prehistoric Sphinx was a lion\" and that the pharaohs recarved the head, and he points to the work of Robert Schoch, John Anthony West and Manu Seyfzadeh. Michels repeats Hancock's view and adds that a lion would have lined up with Leo, the constellation that marked the spring equinox around 10,500 BC. Another researcher, Matt LaCroix, offers the evidence in a different form: the head is too small for the body \"because the head was never supposed to be that size,\" and he credits the recarving to the dynastic Egyptians. If true, it would mean the monument is far older than the pharaohs.","sphinx-outcrop-was-always-visible-from-the-nile":"The idea is that the rock the Sphinx is carved from was a huge limestone outcrop that people near the Nile must always have known was there. A researcher named Luke Caverns says this to Michels, without a named source, as part of his argument that the Sphinx site is older or stranger than the usual account.","spider-bite-leads-fox-to-one-of-the-girls-2012":"The documentary filmmaker James Fox says that in 2012, in Varginha, Brazil, a town known for a case in which the army is said to have captured a live alien, a spider bite led him to a man who told him, \"Oh my gosh, I dated one of the girls,\" meaning one of the young women who were witnesses in the case. Fox tells Michels this as something that happened to him, and he presents it as the chance that put him in touch with a witness.","spielberg-approached-by-naval-intelligence-after-jaws-1975":"The story goes that after Jaws made Steven Spielberg a big name in the mid-1970s, men from Naval Intelligence approached him and offered him a deal, and he took it. If true, the director's later alien films would have a government hand behind them. A guest named Brent Friedman says he heard it around 1995 in Newhall, California, from the director Tobe Hooper: Spielberg \"got approached by some guys from naval intelligence or something\" who \"offered him a deal.\" Another guest on the same episode repeats the account and adds that Spielberg accepted. A clip played in a later episode also credits Hooper but does not say whether Spielberg took the deal.","spielberg-based-the-close-encounters-scientist-on-vallee-1977":"When Steven Spielberg made Close Encounters of the Third Kind, his 1977 film about contact with UFOs, he based its eccentric but brilliant UFO scientist, the part played by Francois Truffaut, on the real UFO researcher Jacques Vallee. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, while introducing Vallee as a guest. If it is true, one of the best-known pictures of a UFO scientist in popular culture was modeled on Vallee.","spielberg-binge-watched-the-secret-of-skinwalker-ranch-befor-2020":"Steven Spielberg is said to have told Brandon Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch (a ranch known for reports of strange happenings), that what was on his mind was \"the congressional hearings\" and the government's acknowledgement of \"the reality\", and that he had just finished binge-watching all 72 episodes of The Secret of Skinwalker Ranch, a TV series about the place. Fugal tells Michels this in the run-up to what the story calls Disclosure Day. If true, one of the world's best-known filmmakers was closely following the UFO subject and the ranch's own show.","spielberg-s-ea-game-lmno-about-a-woman-with-alien-empathy-po-2004":"Around 2004, Steven Spielberg was working with Electronic Arts, the video game company in Los Angeles, on a game codenamed \"LMNO\" about a human woman with alien powers who escapes a government that is chasing her by using empathy to disarm people. A writer named Brent Friedman, who says he was there, tells Michels about it first-hand. It is told as one more sign of how often Spielberg returned to aliens and government secrecy in his work.","spielberg-s-letter-to-james-fox-about-i-know-what-i-saw-2009":"The film director Steven Spielberg once sent a letter to James Fox, a well-known maker of UFO documentaries, about Fox's 2009 documentary I Know What I Saw, and Fox later made the letter public. Michels mentions it in passing on an episode about Spielberg and aliens, as a sign of the director's interest in the subject, without saying what the letter said.","spielberg-s-live-imax-interview-for-disclosure-day-2026":"Bryce Zabel, a television writer and producer, says that in 2026 he and Michels watched a live interview with Steven Spielberg at an IMAX theater for Spielberg's film Disclosure Day, and that Spielberg said something there that bore on whether the film reflects real knowledge of UFOs. Zabel brings it up on the show as something he saw himself, leaving open how much Spielberg meant by it.","spielberg-s-tip-about-the-ariel-school-landing-in-africa-1990":"The film director Steven Spielberg is said to have told someone, by way of Janet Yang, that anyone interested in UFOs \"should look into this landing\": a craft reportedly seen landing near the Ariel School in Ruwa, Zimbabwe, in the 1990s. The UFO documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels this is how he came to the case. It would mean the tip that led him there came from one of Hollywood's best-known directors.","spielberg-tells-robert-liefeld-ufos-may-be-humans-from-the-f-1993":"The film director Steven Spielberg is said to have asked Robert Liefeld, the comic-book artist who created the character Deadpool, whether it was easier to believe we are watched by aliens from another world or by ourselves, studying us from the future. Michels tells this several times, citing Liefeld, and places it in 1993; in other versions he gives no year, puts it as future humans guarding us, names Zeta Reticuli and Proxima Centauri as the alien alternatives, or says flatly that Spielberg called the aliens time travellers. If true, one of Hollywood's best-known makers of alien films would privately lean toward the idea that the visitors are us.","spielberg-ufos-as-future-humans-2020":"The director Steven Spielberg wondered aloud, on Stephen Colbert's talk show, whether UFOs might not come from a civilisation \"300 million light years from here\" but be \"us 500,000 years into the future.\" The remark comes up on Michels's show in an episode with the musician Flying Lotus. It is the idea that the visitors could be our own descendants travelling back in time.","spillage-reported-up-the-chain-told-to-go-away-file-deleted-2018":"Matthew Brown, who describes himself as a Pentagon whistleblower, says that in 2018 he reported a \"spillage\", meaning secret material turning up somewhere it should not be, to the office that oversees the Pentagon's most tightly restricted programmes. He says the material was a capabilities briefing on something called Immaculate Constellation, and that the official he reported it to \"basically recommended to me, delete that and pretend he never saw it, and then he just left the room.\" Brown tells it first-hand, as a sign that such material is being kept out of sight on purpose.","spinning-mercury-in-the-vimanas":"The claim is that the vimanas, flying machines described in old Indian texts, ran on spinning mercury. Michels brings it up himself, in passing, without naming a source. A former Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell tells it in more detail on another episode: he describes rotating cylinders filled with a form of mercury, without naming where he read it. He sets this beside the account of Igor Witkowski, a writer on secret Nazi weapons, who says the Nazis' Bell, a supposed wartime device, worked the same way. If true, an ancient Indian idea and a Nazi weapons programme would share a single technology.","spiritualism-wave-seances-as-common-as-church-1800":"At the height of the spiritualism craze of the 1800s, the belief that the living can talk with the dead, going to a séance on a Sunday morning is said to have been as common for Americans as going to church. The researcher Tim Alberino tells Michels this from periodical articles of the late 1800s and early 1900s that he read, work tied to his project Birthright, and he presents it as a sign of how widely people then sought contact with unseen beings.","spock-s-reverence-for-the-sun-1990":"At his first overseas posting in the early 1990s, a colleague Dan Sherman calls Spock spoke with deep reverence about the sun, saying \"we really don't understand the value of our sun.\" Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who wrote the book Above Black about what he calls his secret work communicating with aliens, tells this to Michels as something he heard first-hand.","spock-says-you-can-go-around-time-1990":"In the early 1990s at an overseas base, a contact Dan Sherman calls Spock told him \"you can go around time,\" Sherman says. Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, tells Michels he \"didn't really understand\" what was meant at the time.","sport-model-came-from-an-underwater-archaeological-dig-found-1989":"Bob Lazar, a man who says that in 1989 he worked on reverse engineering, meaning taking apart to copy, alien craft at S4, a site near Area 51, says one craft, the one called the sport model, came from 'an archaeological dig' that 'isn't in the desert, it's in the water.' He says he heard this from a man named Barry Castilio. Michels repeats it on several episodes, sometimes as Lazar correcting himself to say it was a Navy find on the seafloor, while a guest named Tim Alberino only calls the craft ancient.","spotting-the-nsa-by-where-number-theory-phds-cluster-around":"You can find hidden government science, the mathematician Eric Weinstein tells Michels, by asking where number theory PhDs live when they don't get a visible academic job, and the answer is that they cluster around Fort Meade, the Maryland base that is home to the NSA. He offers it as a method of his own, with Los Alamos as another such place, and it would mean secret work can be traced by where talent quietly disappears to.","squadron-safety-survey-turns-up-four-more-unreported-near-mi":"After one near-collision in the air, a safety check in a Navy fighter squadron near Virginia Beach is said to have turned up four more near midair collisions with the same unexplained problem over the previous few months, none of which had been reported. The fighter pilot Ryan Graves, who flew with that squadron, tells Michels this himself. He links it to a hazard report, the Navy's formal safety report on a near-accident. If he is right, these close calls were far more common than the official record showed.","squanto-unites-three-rival-tribes":"The Native American man Squanto is said to have joined three Massachusetts tribes that had been rivals into one federation. A researcher named Luke Caverns says this on the show, in passing, and gives no date or source. In his telling, Squanto is the one who made old enemies into allies.","squared-off-object-tumbles-into-the-back-pasture-at-the-coun-1950":"The writer Whitley Strieber says that in the 1950s, at his family's country house in Texas, a squared-off object came tumbling across the sky and went into the back pasture behind the house. Strieber tells Michels this as something from his own childhood, mentioning a family member, Karl Strieber, and he tells it as an early brush with something unexplained.","sr-71-pilot-encourages-sherman-to-join-the-air-force":"Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that when he was young a pilot of the SR-71 spy plane strongly encouraged him to join the Air Force. He tells Michels this as part of his own life story, which also appears in a book called Above Black, and it is the first step on the path that he says led to that work.","sri-and-at-t-chairman-meeting-on-unacknowledged-projects-1990":"In the 1990s, a guest named Steven Greer says, he sat in a meeting with a chairman connected to SRI, a California research institute, and to the phone company AT&T, about unacknowledged projects, meaning secret programmes whose existence the government does not admit. Greer tells Michels first-hand, \"I was once at a meeting with a chairman of SRI and AT&T.\" He offers it as proof that big corporations, not just the government, hold UFO secrets.","sri-pay-stub-to-puharich-s-lab-nine-1973":"A 1973 pay stub from SRI, a California research institute where the physicist Hal Puthoff studied psychic abilities, to Lab Nine, the private lab of the researcher Andrija Puharich in Ossining, New York, is said to sit in Puharich's archives. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels about it to push back on the claim that the link between the two simply \"disappeared\": \"Not true,\" he says. To him, the stub shows SRI was still paying Puharich's lab that year, though he quotes nothing more from it.","sri-psi-research-1960":"Michels points to research on psychic abilities, known as psi, carried out at SRI, a research institute in California, in the late 1960s and early 1970s. He names it only in passing, without saying what was studied or where he learned of it, as one link in a chain of serious institutions that took the paranormal seriously.","ss-officers-freemasons-and-occultists-running-nasa-1963":"Michels argues that by the mid-1960s the people really running NASA were \"a strange cocktail of former SS officers, Freemasons, and occultists.\" He offers it as his own reading of the agency's history and names, along the way, astronauts such as John Glenn, Buzz Aldrin, Edgar Mitchell and James Irwin, as well as the NASA chief James Webb and the NASA manager Kenneth Kleinknecht, without the quote saying which of them belongs to which group. If true, the space programme was shaped by hidden loyalties and beliefs far stranger than its public image.","ss-tibet-expedition-of-1938":"In 1938 the SS, the Nazi organisation run by Heinrich Himmler, sent an expedition to Tibet. The writer Peter Levenda tells Michels he studied it in captured German documents on microfilm, and he links it to Himmler. The researcher Jason Jorjani mentions it on another episode about Nazi secret projects. He gives no year and says the aim was to get Tibetan scriptures, and he brings in Mount Kailash, a mountain in Tibet. Both tell it as part of a picture of Nazi leaders hunting for old, hidden knowledge, which matters to them because of what that hunt might have turned up.","ssris-that-made-him-suicidal-his-psychiatrist-quit-2016":"In 2016 and 2017, the former Air Force serviceman Dylan Borland says, doctors at the VA put him on SSRIs, a common kind of antidepressant, that made him suicidal. He tells Michels \"that's why my first doctor quit\", pointing to his VA medical records. He tells it as part of what happened to him after the experiences he describes, and as a sign of how he was treated.","st-andrews-study-finds-magnetic-signature-of-repeated-lightn":"A study by the University of St Andrews is said to have found, at the Callanish stone circle in Scotland, a magnetic signature left by lightning striking the stones again and again. Geoffrey Drumm, who says an alien showed him the true purpose of the pyramids, tells Michels of the university's \"geophysical analysis of a stone circle\", without naming the paper. For Drumm, it would mean the ancient builders chose or used such sites for their electrical activity.","st-francis-of-assisi-s-stigmata-as-ufo-radiation":"Saint Francis of Assisi's famous vision on Mount La Verna in Italy, around 1224, may have been a UFO encounter, and the wounds on his hands, the stigmata, may have been electromagnetic burns: this is a reading Michels returns to again and again, crediting the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka and her book American Cosmic, and at times the researchers Jacques Vallée and Garry Nolan. He says the earliest accounts, by Francis's friend Brother Leo and the biographer Thomas of Celano, describe not Christ but \"atmospheric sparks,\" \"a flaming torch\" and a spinning disk, and he adds that Francis died from it a year or two later. Pasulka, telling it herself from the early sources, says Leo, then about 15, saw something come out of the sky with sparks, seemingly spinning, that \"dealt harshly with Francis,\" leaving him injured, though she calls the being a seraph, an angel, and does not herself speak of radiation.","st-francis-s-final-tau":"The medieval Italian saint Francis of Assisi saw a seraph, a fiery angel of the Bible, which Jesse Michels says was \"probably a craft or an orb\", and the last thing Francis wrote in his diary was the Greek letter Tau, which Michels says means conversion. Michels tells this himself, without naming an edition or source for the diary, as a sign that old religious visions may have been encounters with the same things people now call UFOs.","st-mary-s-chapel-at-glastonbury-abbey-keeps-the-original-cha":"St Mary's Chapel at Glastonbury Abbey in England is 39.6 feet wide, and that width is said to preserve the diameter of the original chapel that stood there. A researcher named Randall Carlson tells Michels this as something \"it is said\", without naming a source, as part of his interest in measurements carried down from ancient times.","st-patrick-s-day-argument-darpa-as-corso-s-conduit-1958":"A former DARPA scientist named John Blitch says that DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, grew out of the Army section where Philip J. Corso served under a General Trudeau, and became the channel for breaking up recovered technology into parts and handing them out. Corso was an Army officer who wrote The Day After Roswell, published in 1997, about the 1947 crash near Roswell, New Mexico, that some say was an alien craft. Blitch tells Michels he made this case himself in an argument in a DARPA office on a St Patrick's Day: \"DARPA ended up as the conduit to break up all the component technologies and distribute them.\"","stack-five-ceremonial-magic-program-at-wright-field":"A secret programme called Stack Five, or ST5, ran at Wright Field, a military airfield, and carried out \"bizarre magical protocols,\" what Michels calls ceremonial magic. Michels says this himself, on an episode about a former Pentagon official, as something he believes, without naming a source or saying when it ran. If true, it would mean the military had tried ritual magic as part of its secret work.","staff-found-the-kalmykian-president-missing-from-his-room-1990":"The story is that the president of Kalmykia, a small republic inside Russia, vanished from his rooms one night in the 1990s and then reappeared, which he says happened because he was taken by aliens. He tells it himself on the show under the name Kiran Ilum Xenov. He says it happened in a Moscow apartment in September 1997, and that his friends had been looking for him for over an hour and could not find him. A witness named Vasili, who says he was there, dates it 17 September 1997. He says that after maybe an hour the president 'suddenly appears from the bedroom, even though he wasn't there before.' Michels tells a version he says comes from the president's staff, whom he has 'on record' saying they went into his room, found his clothes missing and could not find him anywhere. In Michels' version it is staff who search, while in the president's it is friends. If true, a sitting head of a Russian region was taken out of a guarded room and put back.","stan-marcus-tells-nick-cook-the-bell-slowed-time-1940":"In Nazi Germany in the 1940s, a device known as the bell is said to have created a twisting energy field, called a torsion field, inside which time slowed down. Michels tells this as something a man named Nick Cook was told by his source, Stan Marcus, who said 'the Germans had been able to slow time within the area of the bell's torsion field.' If true, the Nazis would have built a machine that bent time.","stan-meyer-collection-group-killed":"The men who got hold of the inventions of Stan Meyer, an inventor said to have built devices that ran on unusual energy, including a ring-shaped \"toroid\" device, were all killed except one, according to the UFO campaigner Steven Greer. Greer tells Michels this as something he was told, linking it to an unnamed British lord and to places in Michigan, the United Kingdom and Orange County, California. If true, it would mean people are being killed to keep such technology hidden.","stan-meyer-poisoned-per-his-twin-steve-1998":"The inventor Stan Meyer, who died in 1998, was poisoned and killed, according to his twin brother Steve Meyer. Steven Greer, a doctor who campaigns for the release of UFO secrets, tells Michels that Steve \"absolutely is convinced\" of it. If true, it would mean an inventor was murdered, and Greer tells it as an example of what happens to people with new energy ideas.","stan-meyer-water-dissociation-and-zero-point-energy":"An inventor named Stan Meyer found that at certain frequencies water would split into hydrogen and oxygen using less electricity than standard chemistry says is needed, according to a UFO campaigner named Steven Greer. Greer tells Michels this, pointing to his book The Lost Century, and links it to \"zero point energy,\" the idea of drawing power from empty space. If true, water could be a source of cheap fuel.","stan-romanek-contactee-and-hybrid-photograph-2000":"In the 2000s a man named Stan Romanek, living north of Denver, Colorado, was a major \"contactee\", someone who says he is in repeated contact with non-human beings, and his wife is said to have taken a photograph of one of the \"hybrids\", beings said to be part human and part alien. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as someone who says he knew the case first-hand, describing Romanek as having \"all these beings\" around him. If it were true, it would mean a photo of such a being exists.","stanford-team-finds-two-cavities-under-khafre-pyramid-1970":"In the 1970s, a team from Stanford University is said to have scanned the pyramid of Khafre at Giza and confirmed an earlier finding of two hidden cavities beneath it. A researcher named Luke Caverns says this on the show, without naming a source. If true, unexplored spaces lie under one of the great pyramids.","stanley-mcdaniel-s-book-on-nasa-s-failure-to-investigate-mar-1990":"In the 1990s Stanley McDaniel, a professor emeritus at Sonoma State University, wrote a 200-page book arguing that NASA failed to properly investigate what he and others see as structures on the surface of Mars. Michels brings it up himself while talking with a remote viewer named Joe McMoneagle, without naming the book. The point is that a scholar took the Mars structures seriously enough to fault NASA for not looking harder.","starfish-prime-bluegill-triple-prime-emp-1962":"Michels says that in 1962, during a run of high-altitude nuclear test blasts over the Pacific called the Starfish Prime series, the United States accidentally created an EMP, an electromagnetic pulse: a burst of energy that can knock out electronics. He mentions one blast, Bluegill Triple Prime, as coming four years before another event he refers to, and he tells this himself, without naming a source, during a 2026 debate with a skeptic.","stargate-as-a-vestibule-for-a-deeper-black-program-pat-price-1970":"Michels suggests that Stargate, the US government's programme for psychic spying (known as remote viewing), which was run partly at SRI, a research institute in California, and shut down in 1995, was really only \"a vestibule\" for a darker, fully secret \"deep black project\" that still exists. He says this while talking with the philosopher Jason Jorjani, and adds that a remote viewer named Pat Price was recruited into that deeper project and \"killed over\" it. If true, the end of Stargate was only for show, and the real work went on out of sight.","stargate-ends-in-1995-and-puharich-is-silenced":"When Stargate, a US government program that used psychics to spy at a distance, ended in 1995, the government is said to have put out newspaper stories saying 'there's nothing to it. It was all bunk.' An unnamed researcher says this on the show, drawing on the writer Annie Jacobsen, and ties it to the silencing of Andrija Puharich, a scientist the episode connects to the CIA. In this account the press stories were a cover, hiding that the psychic work was real.","stargate-files-declassified-2017":"Stargate, a program in which the CIA paid psychics to try to spy on distant places with their minds, was declassified in 2017, and its files are now public. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, telling listeners that \"all of the files are online\" for anyone to read.","stargate-inscriptions-at-dendera":"Hieroglyphs at Dendera, an ancient temple site in Egypt, are said to translate literally to \"stargate.\" Michels tells Joe Rogan he heard this from Ben van Kerkwijk, a researcher of ancient sites, on Rogan's show. In a later episode Michels repeats it himself without crediting anyone, and says the writing is \"below\" Dendera.","stargate-psychic-spy-program-1972-1995":"From 1972 to 1995, the American government ran a psychic spy programme called Stargate, which used \"remote viewing\": people trying to describe distant or hidden places and objects with their minds alone. Michels tells this often, as something on record. He says the CIA ran it, at one point both the CIA and the Defense Intelligence Agency, and that its files were released in 2017 through FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records. Hal Puthoff, whom the tellers call a founder along with Russell Targ, tells Michels firsthand that it began with viewers describing \"super secret facilities and project titles hidden in safes\" and grew into \"a 20-year 20 million dollar program\". If it worked, it would mean the mind can gather information across distance. Michels notes that the programme ended in 1995 under its last director, Ed May, and that many people said afterwards it was not very effective. He gives the official reason for closing it as \"there was only offense, there was no defense.\" Others push back. Joe McMoneagle, who took part, says it had the support of five presidents and of senators such as John Glenn, and that it ended because of stigma, not failure. David Grusch, a guest who says he has spoken with Puthoff, says many of the viewers' findings were confirmed by ordinary intelligence methods. Luis Elizondo, speaking as a former official, calls Puthoff \"the Godfather\" of the CIA programme. Matthew Brown, also speaking from an official background, points to the trouble of putting psychics on the government payroll. Michels also introduces Paul Smith as a man who worked in it.","stargate-psychic-spy-program-made-public-2017":"Stargate, a US government programme that used psychics as spies, was in Michels's words \"wrested from the government and made public,\" which he places around 2017. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in a talk about UFOs and religion. He brings it up as one sign that secret programmes were being forced into the open around that time.","stargate-recruitment-6-of-600-people-tested-had-real-talent-1970":"The story is that Stargate, a US government programme that studied remote viewing, the claimed ability to describe distant places with the mind alone, reached out to about 600 people and found only six with real talent, all of whom had synesthesia, a condition where the senses blend, such as seeing sounds as colours. A man named Chris Ramsay tells Michels this, without naming a source. If true, it would suggest the ability is rare and tied to how a person's brain is wired.","stargate-staff-came-from-sidney-gottlieb-s-technical-service-1970":"Many of the first people on Stargate, a US government programme of the 1970s to 90s that studied remote viewing (the claimed ability to describe distant places with the mind), are said to have come from the CIA's Technical Services Staff run by Sidney Gottlieb. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, while talking with a philosopher named Jason Jorjani. If true, it would tie the psychic research to the CIA's older work on the mind.","stargate-within-the-mesa":"There may be a stargate, a kind of doorway to somewhere else, inside the mesa at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for strange events. A speaker on the episode with the ranch's owner tells Michels, \"so imagine if there's a stargate within the mesa,\" offering it as an idea for what lies behind the ranch's reported anomalies.","staring-app-citizen-science-project-2024":"In 2024 a phone app was put out as a citizen-science project on staring, meant to let ordinary people practise and, he says, get better at it. The scientist Rupert Sheldrake describes it himself on the show, telling Michels he is \"just releasing an app at the moment which works on cell phones\" so that \"you can train yourself to get better at it.\" If it works as he hopes, anyone could test the skill for themselves.","starlink-trains-seen-from-shurmer-s-house-above-santa-barbar-2020":"From his home in the dark mountains above Santa Barbara, California, the researcher and skeptic Michael Shermer says he regularly watches rocket launches and strings of Starlink satellites, SpaceX's internet satellites, which can look strange in the night sky. He tells Michels the Vandenberg launch site is about 90 miles up the coast: 'every week there's a launch,' and about six minutes after the rocket goes up the sound reaches his house. He tells it from his own experience as an example of ordinary sights that people can mistake for something unexplained.","state-inspection-blocked-for-five-months-then-hasty-return-h-2010":"In 2010, according to Jordan Jose, Baker Victory Services in Springville, New York, where he was staying as a child, refused for five months to let the state come in and inspect him, and then he was quickly sent back home. Jose tells this on the show as his own story. He says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a boy, and he cites the record of the case: \"for 5 months, the record something goes,\" the organization \"was refusing access to the state.\" If true, it would suggest someone wanted to keep officials away from him.","station-wagon-woman-reappears-at-changi-airport-1990":"Around 1990, a woman Peter Levenda links to an earlier sighting involving a station wagon turned up again, this time at Changi Airport in Singapore. Levenda, whom the episode bills as a CIA historian, tells Michels he was there himself: \"I'm in the airport, Changi airport. Very famous.\" He tells it on an episode about the idea that unseen beings steer human timelines, which is what a reappearance like this would point to if it happened as he says.","stationary-objects-over-montana-written-off-as-soviet-survei":"Objects hung almost still in the sky over Montana, and at the time people put them down as Soviet spy planes. A man named Robert Hastings tells it, passing on what a radar controller told him: the rumour was \"Soviet surveillance planes,\" but the controller said they were \"basically stationary.\" Hastings finds that explanation unlikely (\"are the Soviets flying helicopters over Montana? I don't think so\"), which would leave the objects unexplained.","stealth-bomber-over-altadena-on-new-year-s-day":"The researcher and skeptic Michael Shermer says he once saw a stealth bomber fly over his house when he lived in Altadena, California, on New Year's Day. He tells Michels this as one of the examples he uses of an unusual but explainable thing in the sky, the kind of sight that could be taken for a UFO by someone who did not know what it was.","stealth-fighter-panels-rumored-to-be-anti-gravity":"People are said to go into an Air Force stealth fighter, pull panels out of it, put them in briefcases and leave, and the rumour was that some of those panels were anti-gravity, a means of cancelling gravity, to help the plane fly in a different way. The writer Greg Bishop tells Michels this as he heard it from a man he does not name. If true, technology beyond known physics would already be flying inside military aircraft.","stefan-hogeborn-s-2012-dive-dark-black-surface-and-near-free":"In 2012 a diver named Stefan Hogeborn went down to a strange object on the floor of the Baltic Sea, brushed the sediment off it, and found its surface \"dark black,\" in near-freezing water. A treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, whose team found the object, tells Michels what Hogeborn reported. It is offered as a first-hand look at what lies under the mud.","steinman-fired-by-lockheed-after-his-book-1986":"A Lockheed employee named Bill Steinman is said to have been fired a couple of days after his book on flying saucers came out, around 1986. Scott Ramsey, a researcher of UFO crash stories, tells Michels that Steinman had been warned that the company 'didn't really like employees writing books on flying saucers,' and then 'he got canned.' Told this way, it suggests the defense industry punishes insiders who write about UFOs.","steinman-s-ufo-crash-book-aztec-1948-eric-henry-wang-and-hak":"A book by the UFO researcher William Steinman, which Michels calls UFO Crash at Aztec, is about a flying saucer said to have crashed at Aztec, New Mexico, in 1948. According to Michels, it points to Eric Henry Wang, head of special projects at Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio, who came to America through Operation Paperclip, the postwar programme that brought German scientists over. Michels says Steinman \"figured out he reported to a guy named Hak,\" meaning Henry Kissinger, that Wang's wife Maria Wang said her husband \"did work on UFOs and his boss was Henry Kissinger,\" and that when Steinman knocked on that door \"everything goes dark\"; Michels tells this from his own reading on several episodes, which would put Kissinger at the centre of the crash secret if it were true.","step-pyramid-as-methane-fracking-system":"The Step Pyramid at Saqqara in Egypt was, by this account, a methane fracking system: an outside reservoir raised and lowered the water level inside it 'to increase and decrease pressure within your fracking tunnels.' Geoffrey Drumm, who says an alien showed him the pyramids' true purpose, tells Michels this himself. If true, the pyramid was a machine for drawing gas from the ground, not a tomb.","step-pyramid-design-at-angkor-thom":"A step pyramid design, of the kind found elsewhere in the ancient world, can also be seen at Angkor Thom, the old temple city in Cambodia. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels he has seen it there himself, while making a case that a great event around 12,000 BC links far-apart ancient building traditions.","stephen-braude-s-first-person-plural-on-multiple-personality":"Stephen Braude, a philosopher at the University of Maryland, Baltimore, wrote a book called First Person Plural that includes a chapter on cases of multiple personality disorder, which the philosopher Jason Jorjani connects to possession. Jorjani mentions it to Michels as Braude's first book, without quoting it. He brings it up because he takes it to suggest that some split personalities might be outside minds taking hold of a person.","stephen-dick-paper-on-ufos-as-cellular-automata-like-steerin":"Michels says a NASA adviser named Steven J. Dick wrote a paper suggesting UFOs might be part of some kind of computational system, almost like cellular automata, simple computer models where a grid of cells changes by simple rules and builds up complex patterns. Michels does not name the paper or say when it was written; he raises it in a 2025 conversation with the philosopher Jason Jorjani as a sign that respected figures have imagined UFOs as something closer to a program than a vehicle.","stephen-dick-s-early-suggestion-that-the-phenomenon-is-ai-2000":"More than twenty years ago, the historian of science Steven J. Dick suggested that the beings behind UFOs were a form of artificial intelligence, or that anything we met out there would be. Diana Pasulka, a professor of religious studies, tells Michels this as something Dick put to her. If true, contact would be with a made mind rather than a living species.","stephen-hawking-s-submarine-trip-to-possible-atlantis-ruins":"Stephen Hawking went down in a submarine to look at underwater ruins that some think could be Atlantis, according to the comedian Kurt Metzger. Metzger tells it on the show as something he heard, crediting someone he calls Fog Daniel and National Geographic, and he does not say where the ruins were or when the dive happened.","stephen-miller-s-ufo-task-force-2026":"The claim is that in 2026 the Trump White House began looking into UFOs officially, with Stephen Miller, a top aide to President Donald Trump, in charge of it. A congressman named Eric Burlison tells Michels that part of what is happening \"has to do with this task force that Stephen Miller has put together.\" Michels describes it a little differently himself. He calls Miller Trump's \"Homeland Security Council guy\" and one of his most loyal longtime aides, and says Miller \"owns the kind of UFO file for the White House.\" Michels says the subject is \"now on Trump's radar\" and that they are \"officially looking into this right now.\" Neither gives a document. They tell it because it would mean the subject has reached the president's inner circle.","sterilizing-world-physics-so-others-can-t-catch-up":"The United States may have deliberately weakened physics and science programmes, \"kicked all of the Americans out\" of them in his words, to \"sterilize the world\" so that no one else could catch up to what it had already achieved in secret. A researcher named Eric Weinstein offers this on the show as one possibility, without naming a source. If true, the slowdown in physics would be a cover for hidden advances.","steve-justice-at-to-the-stars-wanted-to-know-how-saucers-fle-2017":"Steve Justice, who worked at To the Stars Academy, a private venture founded to research UFOs, privately admitted that he did not know how flying saucers flew and badly wanted to find out. Peter Levenda, billed on the show as a CIA historian, tells Michels that Justice told him so directly, sometime between 2017 and 2019: \"I want to know how they do it.\" Levenda's point is that a man he thought \"would know where the bodies are buried\" was still in the dark, which would mean the secret, if there is one, is held tightly even from insiders.","steven-dick-s-ai-protective-layer-of-earth-idea-galileo-proj":"The idea is that some kind of artificial intelligence might form a protective layer around Earth. Michels brings it up hesitantly, saying \"I think\" a former NASA man named Steven Dick talks about it. He places Dick in the Galileo Project, a search for physical evidence of alien technology led by the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb. If true, something watching over the planet would already be here.","steven-greenstreet-s-one-night-visit-2016":"A man named Steven Greenstreet visited Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, and spent just one night there. The ranch's owner, Brandon Fugal, tells Michels this and says Greenstreet \"came in obviously with confirmation bias.\" Fugal's point is that a single night is too short to judge what goes on at the ranch.","steven-greer-cut-the-four-fingered-arm-from-the-2000-weygand":"In a 2000 interview, a man named Jonathan Weygandt spoke of a four-fingered arm, and the UFO campaigner Steven Greer cut that part out of the version he used, according to an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb. UAPGerb tells Michels this from the raw recordings in Greer's archive, mentioning the filmmaker James Fox as well. If so, the full interview held a detail about a non-human body that Greer chose not to show.","steven-greer-laurence-rockefeller-defected-from-his-family-1990":"Laurance Rockefeller, of the wealthy Rockefeller family, is said to have broken with his own family in the 1990s, as 'this defector from his family.' Michels relays this as a claim made by Steven Greer, a figure in the UFO world who campaigned for the government to reveal what it knows. The point of it is that one Rockefeller sided with those pushing for UFO secrets to come out, against the rest of his family.","steven-greer-s-benevolent-alien-theory":"A UFO campaigner named Steven Greer holds that the beings visiting Earth are \"all love and light and good,\" according to Michels. Michels sums up Greer's view this way himself, without quoting Greer, while debating a well-known skeptic on his show. He sets it up as one view among others on what the visitors want.","steven-greer-s-practice-of-calling-ufos-in":"Steven Greer, a UFO researcher, is said to hold that ordinary people can call UFOs in, through group practices he calls CE-5 protocols, meant to invite contact. Michels describes this himself on two episodes: once he calls Greer \"a total mixed bag\" but says his investigative work is now being vindicated, and once he places the practice in the 1990s and says a retired Army general named Albert Stubblebine and an Army officer and researcher named John B. Alexander took an interest. If it worked, contact would be open to anyone, not just governments.","steven-lovekin-eisenhower-doodled-ufos-at-mount-weather-ufo-1950":"In the 1950s, the story goes, President Eisenhower sat inside Mount Weather, a government site in Virginia, doodling UFOs, and this was where the UFO matter was run from. A guest named Grant Cameron passes it on as something a man named Stephen Lovekin told about being inside Mount Weather with Eisenhower, apparently in a conversation with Steven Greer, a well-known figure in UFO circles. If true, it would put the president himself close to a hidden centre for the subject.","steven-lovkin-eisenhower-said-he-no-longer-controlled-the-uf-1959":"President Eisenhower is said to have doodled pictures of alien craft and, when the subject was raised, to have said 'well I don't have control of this anymore.' Steven Greer, who runs a project that gathers testimony from people claiming inside knowledge of UFOs, tells Michels this from the testimony of Stephen Lovekin, a young Army man in the Signal Corps at the White House around 1959 and 1960. If true, it would mean a sitting president had lost control of the issue.","stigmata-man-at-a-ufo-conference":"At a UFO conference, a former Air Force sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA once hired him to communicate with aliens, recalls meeting all sorts of people, and 'one guy had the stigmata,' bleeding wounds like those of the crucified Christ. Sherman tells Michels this as something he saw himself, a glimpse of the unusual crowd such events draw.","stigmatization-campaign-since-the-robertson-panel-1953":"For eighty years, a well-funded campaign has made it shameful to take UFOs seriously, and it began in 1953 with the Robertson Panel, a group of scientists the CIA brought together that year to review UFO reports. That is the view of Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy rear admiral, who tells Michels the campaign \"has been very effective.\" If true, it would mean the ridicule around the subject was planned rather than natural. The panel's report, dated 16 February 1953 and filed by the CIA's Office of Scientific Intelligence, does call for a \"debunking\" aim that \"would result in reduction in public interest in 'flying saucers',\" to be carried out through \"television, motion pictures, and popular articles.\" It also recommends that \"the national security agencies take immediate steps to strip the Unidentified Flying Objects of the special status they have been given and the aura of mystery they have unfortunately acquired.\" So the report plainly urges a public effort to deflate interest; whether that effort was carried out, funded, and kept going for decades, as Gallaudet says, is a step beyond what a 1953 recommendation can show.","stonehenge-altar-stone-as-a-lightning-rod":"The claim is that the Altar Stone at Stonehenge, a stone at the monument's centre, was a lightning rod, an obelisk meant to draw lightning. Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher who says an alien told him the true purpose of the pyramids, says this on the show without naming a source, as part of his view that ancient monuments were built to handle electricity.","stones-floated-in-on-reed-boats-and-the-pyramid-site-flooded":"The Egyptian pyramid builders are said to have built reed boats, cut stone up the Nile and floated it down, with the Giza site flooded so the blocks could be brought in by water. Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer, someone who says he can see distant places and times with his mind, tells Michels this, crediting his book The Ultimate Time Machine. If true, it would explain how the huge blocks were moved without any mystery machinery.","strange-deaths-of-abduction-researchers-two-john-macks-hit-b-1990":"The abduction researcher John Mack, who studied people saying they were taken by beings, was killed by a car in England, and on the same day another man named John Mack was also hit by a car there. Tim Alberino tells Michels this from memory ('if I recall'), placing it among the deaths of other abduction researchers he lists, Karla Turner, Budd Hopkins and Barbara Bartholic. If true, the coincidence would hint that someone was targeting people who looked into abductions.","strange-explosions-caught-on-home-cameras-2022":"Around 2022, the writer Whitley Strieber says, the beings he has encountered \"got pissed off\" at him, and his home cameras picked up a series of strange explosions. Strieber tells Michels this as something that happened to him. If true, it would mean these beings can react to him and leave traces on ordinary cameras.","strange-lights-over-the-san-juan-coal-mine":"A local man from Aztec, New Mexico, Brett Lanner, says he has seen strange lights darting above the San Juan coal mine, right by where he worked. Michels says he spoke to him himself, and he passes the account on in a 2026 episode about a reported crash of a craft carrying alien bodies in that area, as a sign that odd things are still seen there.","stranger-in-long-john-silver-s-says-read-ezekiel-daniel-reve-1976":"In 1976, in a Long John Silver's restaurant near Fort Knox, a stranger walked straight up to a young Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, said \"Read Ezekiel, Daniel, Revelations,\" and walked away. Mowatt-Larssen, a former senior CIA officer, tells Michels it happened to him and says he describes the man in his book A State of Mind: Faith and the CIA; he tells it as a strange nudge toward the Bible's prophetic books.","stranger-with-air-force-ties-at-hart-canyon-2026":"In 2026 a stranger with what Michels calls \"curious ties to the Air Force\" turned up at Hart Canyon, near Aztec, New Mexico, at the very moment Michels and his crew arrived. Hart Canyon is the remote spot his episode links to a claimed crash that left sixteen alien bodies. Michels tells this himself, from his own trip, and stresses that the place is \"very obscure and hard to get to,\" so the timing would suggest someone was keeping an eye on the site.","strassman-s-repeat-dose-dmt-tests-showing-no-tolerance-1990":"In his 1990s studies the psychiatrist Rick Strassman injected people with DMT, a powerful psychedelic, again and again, and found that their bodies built up no tolerance to it. A neuroscientist named Andrew Gallimore tells Michels this as a summary of Strassman's research. It matters to him because it would set DMT apart from almost every other psychedelic.","strassman-s-spirit-molecule-hypothesis-dmt-released-at-death-1990":"The idea is that DMT, a powerful psychedelic compound, is released in the brain at the moment of death, and that this is why it came to be called \"the spirit molecule\". The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore tells Michels this without naming a source. He says the idea comes from a researcher named Rick Strassman and his book DMT: The Spirit Molecule. If it were true, it would tie the drug's visions to what people experience as they die.","strategic-air-command-letter-congratulating-the-necronomicon-1977":"The author Peter Levenda says that after the Necronomicon, a book of supposed ancient magic spells that he worked on, came out in the late 1970s, a letter arrived from Strategic Air Command, the Air Force's nuclear bomber command, on its own stationery. He says it was just \"a nice letter saying congratulations on your new publication,\" and he tells it as something he saw himself. Michels tells it differently on another episode, saying Levenda has received several letters from inside the US military, including from Fort Benning, praising the book as \"spot on.\" If so, it would suggest people in the military took an occult book seriously.","stratton-and-lacatski-built-osap-together-2008":"In 2008 two men, Jay Stratton and Jim Lacatski, together built OSAP, a Defense Intelligence Agency programme that studied unexplained aerial phenomena. Eric W. Davis, a Pentagon scientist who says he worked alongside it, tells Michels that \"it wasn't just Jim\" Lacatski alone but \"Jim and Jay and their support staff.\" He tells it to give Stratton his share of the credit for founding the programme.","stratton-forms-the-uap-task-force-2020":"In 2020 a defence intelligence official named Jay Stratton is said to have set up the UAP Task Force, a Pentagon group whose job, in Michels's words, was to \"internally review all of the data\" on UAP, the government's term for UFOs (unidentified anomalous phenomena). Michels tells this himself, unsure of some details, saying Stratton was \"former Navy maybe.\" It matters because it would mean someone inside the government decided the reports deserved a serious look.","streak-left-on-the-cliff-by-the-craft-1948":"A craft that is said to have crashed in New Mexico in 1948 left a streak on a cliff of a mesa near the Archuleta ranch, and that mark \"can still be seen there to this day,\" Michels says. He tells this in his own episode about the 1948 crash, citing a researcher of the case named Scott Ramsey. If true, the mark would be a physical trace of the crash that anyone could still go and see.","stress-iq-test-during-air-force-recruitment-screening-1994":"In 1994, near UCLA in California, a man named Jake Barber, who says he later flew a helicopter to retrieve a UFO, says he went through a stress test during Air Force recruitment screening. He tells Michels he took an IQ test once normally and then again while wearing 'an oxygen mask that sealed my face with no air coming to it,' to see how he thought under strain.","strieber-helps-fund-the-first-scientific-studies-of-the-shro-1978":"In 1978, the writer Whitley Strieber says, he and his family gave \"a few thousand dollars,\" as much as they could afford, to the first scientific studies of the Shroud of Turin, and then followed the work closely. Strieber tells Michels he only talked with the scientists, whom he names as Jackson and Jumper, at fundraisers and never became their friend.","strieber-s-career-destroyed-which-he-ties-to-the-collins-eli-1987":"The writer Whitley Strieber, known for his accounts of meeting non-human beings, says his career was destroyed between 1987 and the late 1990s, and he ties this to the Collins Elite, a group said to work inside the US government. He tells Michels to watch episode three of Beyond, a documentary series from MGM, to \"see what happened to me and what it did to my life\", adding \"it's got something to do with them. I'm convinced.\" If true, a hidden group inside government set out to ruin a writer for what he said.","strieber-s-childhood-mind-control-camp-at-randolph-afb-1950":"The writer Whitley Strieber, known for books about his own encounters with strange beings, says that as a boy in the 1950s he was sent to a summer camp at Randolph Air Force Base, near his family's home in Texas, where he was put through physical torture and mind-control experiments. The researcher and philosopher Jason Jorjani relays this on the show as Strieber's own allegation. If true, it would mean the military experimented on children.","strieber-s-meeting-with-james-jesus-angleton":"The writer Whitley Strieber once met James Jesus Angleton, a senior CIA official. Michels mentions it in passing while talking with the philosopher Jason Jorjani, saying Strieber told him so when Strieber was a guest on the show. Michels gives nothing of what was said at the meeting.","strieber-s-written-account-to-sarbacher-undeliverable-sarbac-1986":"In 1986 the writer Whitley Strieber, author of the book Communion about his own encounters, says a man in Florida named Robert Sarbacher asked him to write out his whole experience, so he wrote it at his cabin in upstate New York and sent it by UPS. Strieber tells Michels first-hand that the next morning \"I get a call from the UPS guy on the scene saying, 'We can't deliver it. The recipient has expired,'\" meaning Sarbacher had died that night. Michels later retells it a little differently, as Sarbacher dying just before the two men were to meet.","strieber-taken-to-a-school-for-special-children-in-baja-cali-1950":"As a boy in the 1950s, the writer Whitley Strieber was at one point taken from Randolph, an Air Force base, to a school for special children in Baja California, Mexico. The philosopher and researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this second-hand, as something Strieber told him. If true, it would mean the military took a child to an unusual school abroad, though Jorjani gives no more detail on what the school was.","strieber-writes-to-warn-against-the-oxford-carbon-dating-of-1988":"In 1988, before Oxford scientists carbon dated the Shroud of Turin, the cloth many believe wrapped the body of Jesus, the writer Whitley Strieber says he wrote to a priest he names as Father Raldi telling him not to allow it. Strieber tells Michels he warned that the scientists \"have to defend their paradigm\" and \"will lie without knowing it.\" He says that is what happened: they declared the shroud medieval and, in his words, \"destroy the research,\" which would mean a real relic was written off by experts protecting their own assumptions.","string-theory-as-an-intentional-dead-end-1957":"String theory, a leading but unproven branch of theoretical physics, is suggested to be a deliberate dead end meant to steer physicists away from dangerous discoveries. Michels raises it himself, asking a guest whether string theory, which the physicist Ed Witten worked on, is \"intentionally a bridge to nowhere\". The researcher Eric Weinstein later puts it as his own idea on the show, calling it possibly a \"blocking mechanism\" to keep people from dangerous physics, ties it to an account from the investors Marc Andreessen and Ben Horowitz about classified physics, and names Witten and the physicist Brian Greene.","stringfield-files-on-headband-transceivers-in-recovered-craf-1970":"Some of the operators of crashed and recovered UFOs are said to have worn a headband, or headband transceiver, possibly a device for sending and receiving signals. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels about it from the files of Leonard Stringfield, a UFO researcher based in Cincinnati, Ohio, who wrote a series of crash retrieval status reports, numbered 1 through 7. UAPGerb says those files hold many accounts from witnesses who saw recovered craft, along with cutaway diagrams of them, and he also cites a man named Michael Schratt.","stringfield-s-investigations-into-crash-retrievals":"A man named Stringfield is said to have carried out a huge number of investigations into crash retrievals, the claim that crashed craft of non-human origin have been secretly recovered. The journalist Michael Shellenberger mentions this in passing while talking with Michels. He names Stringfield as a source on the subject but says nothing on the show about what those investigations found.","sts-124-bolt-in-the-payload-bay-was-the-iss-2008":"In 2008 Mark Kelly, a NASA astronaut who is now a US senator, was commanding STS-124, a space shuttle mission. As the story goes, he saw what looked like a bolt floating in the shuttle's open cargo bay, and it turned out to be the International Space Station seen far away. His twin brother Scott Kelly, also a former astronaut, tells Michels the story as Mark told it to him, as an example of how easily something ordinary in space can look mysterious.","sts-48-and-sts-80-shuttle-uap-videos-1991":"Two space shuttle missions, STS-48 in 1991 and STS-80 in 1996, are said to have filmed unexplained objects in Earth orbit, what are now called UAP, unidentified anomalous phenomena. A researcher named Maaneli Derakhshani points to these two videos on the show, without naming a source, as among the stronger cases from space, and later refers back to them in passing. If they show what he suggests, NASA would have recorded unexplained objects on its own missions.","stubblebine-s-effort-to-cognitively-enhance-soldiers-1980":"In the 1980s, General Albert Stubblebine of INSCOM, the US Army's intelligence and security command, wanted to 'cognitively enhance soldiers', making their minds work beyond normal limits. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, it would mean the Army itself sought to push human mental abilities further.","student-vanishes-on-mountain-excursion-in-minas-gerais":"A group of students went on an excursion into the mountains of Minas Gerais, in Brazil, and one of the girls stepped away from the group for a while and simply vanished. Aldo Rebelo, a Brazilian politician who has served as his country's defence minister, tells it to Michels as a very well-known event reported in the local press. He gives no date and no names.","sturgis-led-raid-sinks-a-russian-ship-marshals-burn-the-no-n-1962":"In 1962 a man named Frank Sturgis led Cuban exile fighters in a raid on Cuba that sank a Russian ship, and afterwards US marshals burned down their base on No Name Key, a small island in the Florida Keys. A researcher named Dan Sheehan tells Michels this, naming Robert F. Kennedy in the story but no source. If true, it would show the Kennedys moving hard against the exile raiders at a dangerous moment.","sub-bottom-profiling-shows-the-layers-under-the-object-2025":"In 2025 a team surveying the Baltic Sea anomaly, a strange object lying on the floor of the Baltic Sea, says it ran sub-bottom profiling, a sonar method that looks at the layers of mud and rock beneath the seabed. The treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, who found the object and took part in the survey, confirms to Michels that this was done. The results bear on his newer claim that the object sits apart from the seabed rather than being part of it.","submarine-crew-showed-the-doctor-the-sonar-room":"A doctor named Greg Rogers, who worked with NASA and says he saw a UFO in a secret hangar, says that while aboard a US Navy submarine the crew took him into the sonar room, where the boat listens for what is around it underwater. He tells Michels this from his own time on board.","submarines-take-evasive-action-from-fast-closing-underwater":"US Navy submarine crews have suddenly come upon objects under the water closing in on them fast, and have had to take evasive action. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet tells Michels he heard this from several submarine officers and one enlisted sailor, who each came to him with their own reports. If true, it would mean unknown objects are moving through the ocean faster than the boats meant to patrol it.","substack-article-on-the-boxer-upsets-the-wrestling-community":"An article about an event involving a boxer upset the wrestling community, according to the author Peter Levenda, who wrote it. He tells Michels he published it on his Substack, an online newsletter he says he hasn't touched in about a year, but he gives few details of what the event was.","subterranean-chamber-as-giza-s-original-sacred-site":"The rough chamber cut into the bedrock 100 feet beneath the Great Pyramid is said to be the original sacred site at Giza, older than the pyramid above it. The researcher Graham Hancock says this to Michels as his own view. Another researcher, Luke Caverns, relays what Hancock said on the podcaster Joe Rogan's show: that most of the Great Pyramid was built under the pharaoh Khufu, but the underground level may have been inherited from an earlier time.","sudden-mastery-of-morse-code-under-threat-of-being-sent-to-v-1960":"In the 1960s, a former Army intelligence soldier named Joe McMoneagle says, he was struggling with Morse code at an Army intelligence school and was told he would be sent to Vietnam if he failed, and that same day, in about two hours, he passed speed test after speed test, from 10 up to 22 words a minute, because \"something popped in my head.\" McMoneagle tells Michels this from his own life, as an early sign of an unusual ability of mind.","sufi-imam-jinn-change-physical-scripture-but-not-memory":"A Sufi imam is said to have explained why the Quran is still memorized word for word even though it has long been written down: there are beings, which the story calls jinn, \"who don't live in normal space and time the way we do\" and who \"can go back and change physical objects but they're not allowed to change our memory.\" Rizwan Virk, introduced on the show as an MIT scientist, passes on this claim from an imam he does not name. Taken seriously, it would make memory a safer keeper of scripture than any written copy.","suicide-stand-down-in-the-drone-program-2013":"In 2013, according to a guest named Dylan Borland, the US Air Force's drone program called a \"suicide stand-down\", a pause in normal work to deal with suicides among its people. Borland tells Michels he heard it from people in the program, who called him and said, \"Yo, we had a suicide standown.\" If true, it would point to serious strain on the crews who fly the drones.","suited-beings-on-a-scrap-metal-train-car-1971":"One October evening in 1971, a woman bicycling through a small city in central Sweden says she saw beings in suits on a railway car loaded with scrap metal. The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels he spoke with her the night before, after she wrote him a letter, and that it was the first time she had told anyone outside her family.","sumer-over-12-000-years-old-based-on-geodesy-alignments":"The idea is that Sumer, the ancient civilisation of what is now Iraq, began well over 12,000 years ago, far earlier than usually thought, based on how its sites line up with measurements of the Earth. A researcher named Matt LaCroix says this to Michels, without a named source, adding that this great age is \"why they essentially invented everything\".","sumerian-accounts-of-civilizations-from-before-the-moon":"The ancient Sumerians are said to have told of civilizations that lived before the moon existed. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, while talking with a researcher named Randall Carlson about the idea that the moon is an alien craft. If so, people would have remembered a time when there was no moon in the sky.","sumerian-anunnaki-engineered-a-slave-race":"The claim is that the Anunnaki, gods of the ancient Sumerian myths, engineered humans as a slave race on Earth. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells it, saying \"the Sumerian myths tell us that when the Anunnaki engineered a slave race on Earth,\" and he credits the writer Zecharia Sitchin, who read those texts this way. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of psychic phenomena, also credits Sitchin, but in his version the Anunnaki \"performed genetic experiments on our primate ancestors,\" with no mention of slaves. Both tellers draw on Sitchin's reading of the Sumerian texts and quote none of the texts themselves. If it were true, humanity would be the deliberate product of beings from elsewhere.","sumerian-astronomical-knowledge":"Michels says that the architecture of Sumer, one of the earliest civilizations in ancient Mesopotamia, shows a knowledge of astronomy that was surprisingly advanced for its time. He says this himself, without naming a source, in a 2022 conversation with the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb, as one of several hints that ancient people may have known more than we would expect.","sumerian-flood-story-as-the-source-of-noah":"Noah's flood in Genesis has a close older relative in the flood tale of ancient Mesopotamia, says the researcher Matt LaCroix. He tells Michels that if you read the Epic of Gilgamesh and the Atrahasis, another Mesopotamian flood poem, and compare them with Genesis, \"it's like literally almost identical\". If he is right, the Bible's story came from an older one, and perhaps from a flood that people remembered. The tablet speaks for itself in E. A. Wallis Budge's translation, published by the British Museum in 1920. There, the god Ea warns one man: \"Throw down the house, build a ship\", and \"Carry grain of every kind into the ship.\" The survivor, Uta-Napishtim, says: \"The cattle of the field, the beasts of the field... I made them go up into it.\" Then \"The storm raged\", and \"The mountain of Nisir held the ship.\" He sends out a dove, then a swallow, then a raven, which \"came not back\", and afterwards he offers a sacrifice. That order of events, a warning, a boat, animals, a storm, a mountain and the birds, is what LaCroix sets beside Noah, and the different names neither help nor hurt the comparison. What the tablet alone cannot settle is which way the story travelled, whether both versions come from an older shared one, and whether a real flood lies behind it.","sumerian-god-who-emerged-from-the-water-and-taught-civilizat":"In the Sumerian myths of ancient Mesopotamia, a god lands in the river outside Sumer, 'comes up out of a vehicle of some kind', and teaches people the arts of civilization. The writer Peter Levenda tells Michels this as his own reading of the myths. If it records something real, civilization would have been handed to humans by a visitor.","sumerian-king-list-with-kings-ruling-tens-of-thousands-of-ye":"The Sumerian King List, an ancient Mesopotamian record of the region's rulers, names kings who are said to have reigned 40,000 years or more, which some read as a memory of beings far beyond ordinary humans. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan raises it with Michels, saying there are eight such kings \"documented\" and that \"some of them are real.\"","summoning-and-piloting-uap-through-meditative-states":"A person can enter something like a meditative state and call up UAP, the government's term for unidentified craft, and even guide them, according to a man named Jake Barber, who speaks from what he says is his own experience. \"What is real is the ability to go into something like a meditative state and summon UAP,\" he tells Michels. If true, it would mean these craft answer to the human mind.","summoning-demonstrations-for-the-aaro-director-2024":"A private team says it has been showing the head of AARO, the Pentagon's current office for unexplained aerial reports, how it can summon unexplained craft. Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he takes part, tells Michels: \"We're doing demonstrations for the new director,\" adding that the office is \"fully privy\" and has \"seen our videos.\" If true, it would mean the government is watching the attempts first-hand.","summoning-team-present-the-night-of-the-egg-2014":"On the night in 2014 when, by his account, he used his helicopter to retrieve an egg-shaped craft in the California desert, a man named Jake Barber says another team was there too, one that 'deals with the summoning component of data collection,' meaning efforts to call such craft to appear. Barber tells Michels he knows of this team firsthand; if true, it would mean the government not only recovers these objects but tries to draw them in.","summoning-the-egg-in-daylight-after-leaving-the-program-2024":"In 2024, after leaving the secret programme he says he worked for, a former helicopter pilot named Jake Barber says he called up \"the egg\", an egg-shaped craft, in daylight in the desert, and it \"flies by in dead silence, makes a turn and comes around in to land and disappears behind the house.\" Barber tells Michels he was there and names the Stanford scientist Garry Nolan as a witness. If true, it would mean such a craft can be summoned on purpose.","sun-angle-3d-reconstruction-of-the-flir1-object-2004":"The object in FLIR1, an infrared video taken by Navy pilot Chad Underwood off Southern California in 2004, can be rebuilt in three dimensions by working out which way Underwood was flying and where the sun stood, at about 320 to 330 degrees. A writer on the subject named Marik von Rennenkampff makes this case to Michels in an episode built around a debate with a skeptic. He uses it to argue the object's shape and position can be pinned down, not explained away as a distant plane.","sun-is-400-times-larger-and-400-times-farther-away-than-the":"The Sun is said to be 400 times larger than the Moon and also 400 times farther away, so the two look the same size in the sky. A researcher named Randall Carlson says this on the show, without naming a source, in an episode on the idea that the Moon is artificial. He offers it as a coincidence too neat to be chance.","suppressed-childhood-memories-resurface-as-ptsd-flashbacks-2022":"Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, tells Michels that in late 2022 and early 2023 everyday triggers made him \"unravel\" and brought back childhood memories he had never recalled, arriving like trauma flashbacks. He describes it as a crisis in which, within about two months, he would \"wake up\" and \"remember an entire childhood\" he had forgotten. He offers it as the way his account of the programme came back to him.","surgical-animal-mutilation-with-no-blood":"Near Aztec, New Mexico, people say they found dead animals with parts cut away as cleanly as a surgeon would, and with no blood. A local resident says on Michels's show that it \"looked like a surgeon had taken it off. No, but no blood.\" A rancher on the same episode says much the same and places it on the Archuleta Ranch. He says \"the ears and eyes were still intact,\" though he says nothing about blood. Both say they saw it themselves, on an episode about a claimed crash of a craft with alien bodies near Aztec. If true, cuts that clean would point to something other than predators or ordinary tools.","surrealist-founders-occult-seances-and-breton-s-1922-article-1920":"The claim is that the founders of surrealism in 1920s France, including André Breton, Max Ernst and Paul Éluard, were from the very start, even before the movement had its name, \"constantly engaged in occult rituals including long seances\", and that Breton wrote about this in a 1922 article. A researcher named Jason Jorjani says this to Michels, without naming a source, to suggest that a famous art movement grew out of occult practice.","survey-finding-only-about-30-of-physicists-accept-the-standa-2026":"A recent survey found that only about 30% of physicists hold to the standard model, the field's accepted theory of particles and forces, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Mishlove. He tells Michels this without naming the survey: 'adherence to the standard model is only about 30%.' If true, physics would be far less settled than it looks from outside.","susan-clancy-s-harvard-study-of-abductees-2000":"A psychologist named Susan Clancy studied people who say they were abducted by aliens, as doctoral research in psychology at Harvard, and wrote a book about them called Abducted. The skeptic Michael Shermer brings her work up on the show during a debate with Michels about UFOs. He offers it as a scientific look at such claims, though he does not say here what she found.","susan-gough-by-kirkpatrick-s-side-at-george-mason-event-2023":"At a 2023 event at George Mason University in Virginia, a Pentagon spokeswoman named Susan Gough stayed right beside Sean Kirkpatrick, then the head of AARO, the Pentagon's office for unexplained aerial reports. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff, who was there and met Kirkpatrick, says on the show that she \"was attached at his hip\" and \"would not leave.\" He tells it as a sign of how closely the Pentagon handled what Kirkpatrick said in public.","susan-gough-refuses-to-amend-statement-to-include-non-human-2020":"A researcher who goes by UAPGerb recalls that in the 2020s a Pentagon spokesperson named Susan Gough had put out a statement saying there were no extraterrestrials, and was then asked to widen it to cover \"non-human intelligence\", a broader term for any intelligent beings that are not human, wherever they come from. The request, he says, came from someone using FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records, and Gough would not change the wording. He brings it up on the show while talking about Sean Kirkpatrick, and gives no source for it. If true, it would suggest the Pentagon was careful to deny aliens without denying something wider.","susan-gough-s-psychological-operations-role-as-a-source-for-2025":"Michels argues that a 2025 Wall Street Journal article dismissing UFO stories leaned on a source with a background in military psychological operations. The article, \"The Pentagon Disinformation That Fueled America's UFO Mythology,\" was written by Joel Schectman and published on 6 June 2025. It argues that some UFO lore grew out of Pentagon deception. Michels says that \"one of the articles primary sources is Susan Gough.\" He adds that her LinkedIn profile and other sources confirm \"her lead role in Booz Allen Hamilton's Psychological Operations Consulting Practice for the Department of Defense,\" Booz Allen Hamilton being a large defense consulting firm. The article itself quotes Gough once, as a Defense Department spokeswoman. In that statement she confirms that AARO, the Pentagon's current office for investigating unexplained sightings, \"had uncovered evidence of fake classified program materials relating to extraterrestrials,\" and says this will be reported later that year. For the rest, the article cites \"interviews with two dozen current and former U.S. officials, scientists and military contractors\" whom it does not name. Whether Gough shaped the article beyond that one statement, the article does not say, and it says nothing about her career. Michels's point is that if she did, the piece may itself have been a form of influence.","susan-gough-says-gimbal-is-not-a-training-video-2015":"The Gimbal video, a 2015 Navy cockpit recording made off Florida that shows an unidentified object, is not a training video, according to Susan Gough, a Pentagon spokesperson. The UFO skeptic Mick West, who analyses such videos, says on the show that he asked Gough directly and \"she told me it wasn't a training video\", which, if so, rules out one simple explanation for where the footage came from.","susan-mccasland-accepted-a-calendar-invite-for-a-delonge-pod-2015":"Michels says a calendar notification shows that Susan McCasland, of the family of the Air Force general McCasland, accepted a Google calendar invite for what it called a \"DeLonge Podesta meeting\", meaning a meeting between the musician and UFO campaigner Tom DeLonge and the political adviser John Podesta. He points to it in the Podesta emails, the private emails WikiLeaks published in 2016, as a sign that the McCasland household was directly involved in DeLonge's UFO contacts with Washington insiders around 2015 and 2016.","susan-mccasland-s-911-call-about-her-husband-2026":"Susan McCasland says that in 2026, in Albuquerque, New Mexico, she called 911 to report her husband missing. In the recording she tells the dispatcher, \"I have some indication that he must have planned not to be found.\" Her call is played on the show as part of Michels's look at why top American scientists are going missing.","susan-mccasland-s-facebook-statement-on-roswell-bodies-and-d-2026":"In 2026 a woman named Susan McCasland posted a statement on Facebook about a man she calls Neil, and Michels reads it out: \"Neil does not have any special knowledge about the ET bodies and debris from the Roswell crash stored at Wright Pad.\" The Roswell crash is the 1947 incident in New Mexico at the center of claims that the US recovered an alien craft, and \"Wright Pad\" refers to Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio. The words deny that Neil knows anything special, yet they speak of the bodies and debris being stored there as though that were given. Whether she meant that seriously or ironically, the line Michels quotes does not say.","susi-temples-no-longer-face-east-west-implying-axis-shift":"Ancient temples in eastern Turkey, which a researcher named Matt LaCroix calls Susi temples, are said to have had openings built to face only the rising or setting Sun, east or west, but today they are offset. LaCroix says this on the show without naming a source. He treats the offset as a dating clue, suggesting the Earth's axis has shifted since the temples were built.","suspected-spook-at-the-hotel-during-the-dc-hearings-2024":"Michels says that while he and the YouTuber Chris Ramsay were in Washington for the congressional hearings on UFOs, \"a few weeks ago,\" a man he calls \"just straight up spook,\" meaning an intelligence agent, walked up to them. He tells it to the writer Whitley Strieber, as something that happened to him. If true, intelligence people were keeping an eye on those who came to follow the hearings.","suzanne-s-mother-moves-the-family-to-aztec-after-reading-scu-1950":"A woman Michels calls Suzanne was born in South Dakota to a mother obsessed with UFOs, and the mother moved the whole family to Flora Vista, a suburb of Aztec, New Mexico, a town where, as the episode tells it, a flying saucer with alien bodies came down. Jesse Michels tells this himself: the mother's obsession, he says, was sparked by Behind the Flying Saucers, the first book about Aztec, written by Frank Scully, a columnist for the show-business paper Variety.","swedenborg-s-visits-to-other-planets":"The Swedish mystic Emanuel Swedenborg said he travelled to other planets and came back, Michels recalls. Michels brings it up himself, placing Swedenborg in the 17th century, in a talk with a former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, who was trained as a psychic spy. He offers Swedenborg as an early case of someone claiming the mind can travel far beyond the body.","swedish-1970s-ufo-photographers-admitted-faking-sunstrom-hol":"The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn says he tracked down every Swedish photographer who took UFO pictures in the 1970s, and that they admitted to faking them, all except one, a man named Christ Sunstrom, who still stands by his photograph. Svahn tells Michels this from his own investigations.","swedish-ghost-rockets-and-doolittle-s-investigation-1946":"In 1946 and 1947, according to Michels, more than a thousand sightings of \"ghost rockets\" were reported over Sweden, objects that looked like modified V2s, the German rockets of the Second World War. He says it is \"not disputed\" that Eisenhower sent General James Doolittle to investigate. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, to show that the US military took unexplained craft seriously even before the UFO era began.","swedish-navy-ship-heads-straight-for-the-dive-boat-2011":"In 2011, during an expedition to a strange object on the floor of the Baltic Sea, a navy ship, said to be Swedish, came from the distance \"heading directly on us,\" according to a crew member of the dive boat. The crew member, who is not named, tells it on the show as someone who was aboard. It is told as a sign that the navy took an interest in what the divers had found.","swedish-security-service-calls-him-in-over-the-som-find-2015":"In 2015, after his team came home from the find off Sweden that the episode calls a UFO on the seafloor, the Swedish security service called the treasure hunter Dennis Asberg in. Asberg tells Michels he took the call himself, from what he describes as \"like FBI in Sweden.\" If so, it would mean the state took a quiet interest in what his team had found.","sweep-and-the-1964-money-funnel-in-sekret-machines":"Around 1964, hundreds of billions of dollars were funnelled through a huge hidden or \"underground\" system, in a story tied to something called SWEEP. A researcher named Jason Simosa sums this up for Michels from Sekret Machines, a book series by the author Tom DeLonge, without naming any other source. If true, it would point to a vast secret budget dating back to the 1960s.","swirling-storm-cloud-over-the-lot-between-two-houses-seen-wi-2013":"In 2013 in Fort Collins, Colorado, a swirling storm cloud formed over the empty lot between two houses and \"looked like a miniature hurricane.\" John Blitch, a scientist who once worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, says he saw it with his neighbour Andy. Elsewhere he ties this kind of strange weather to beings he says took him.","switching-astronaut-chem-lights-from-red-to-green-1989":"Between about 1989 and 1991 at Kennedy Space Center in Florida, a NASA doctor named Greg Rogers says he pushed the agency to change the chemical light sticks used for astronauts from red to green, and that it took him 'a year and a half' to get NASA to make the switch. He tells Michels this from his own work there, naming a Colonel Shokitis as part of the story.","sybil-leek-and-the-occult-side-of-mk-ultra-mk-often-1950":"A woman named Sybil Leek, linked here to the occult, is said to have been openly involved with \"Operation Often\". The story places this as the occult side of MK-Ultra, the CIA's secret mind-control research of the 1950s to the 70s. The researcher and author Peter Levenda tells Michels that \"as we find out later\" she was part of it, without naming his source. If true, the CIA's mind-control work reached into the world of witches and the occult.","sycamore-knoll-undersea-base-claim-rejected":"Sycamore Knoll is an underwater hill off Point Dume, near Malibu in Southern California, and it has been said to hide an alien base beneath the sea. The claim is tied to a book called Undersea UFO Base by the author Preston Dennett. On Michels's show it is turned down by Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral who believes the oceans hold many UFOs. Looking at the evidence, he says plainly, \"There was no alien base there.\" Michels says on the same episode that he looked into it himself with a collaborator, Will O'Brien, going over a survey of the site made by an underwater rover. \"We did the research and we're like I don't think there's anything,\" he says. It matters because both men take ocean UFOs seriously, and they are still setting this particular claim aside.","symbols-aboard-the-craft-and-the-gray-alliance-of-seven-spec":"A scientist named Steve Colbern, who studies objects he believes are alien implants in human bodies, says he saw symbols aboard a craft over Fillmore, California, including three orange circles set in an equilateral triangle. He tells Michels he is \"led to believe\" this is the emblem of the \"gray alliance\", a union of seven species of \"grays\", the small grey-skinned beings of UFO accounts. If true, it would mean these beings are organised into a kind of federation with its own symbols.","synctext-message-how-long-will-you-be-down-here-for-with-no-2020":"Michels says that on a trip to the Baltic Sea anomaly, an odd formation spotted on the seabed that some believe is something unusual, his phone got a text on a messaging app he calls Synctext, even though there was no reception, and it asked: \"How long will you be down here for?\" He tells this as something that happened to him, and he says everyone there with him would back up the story; if true, it would mean something at the site was reaching out to the visitors.","synesthete-watson-who-saw-and-felt-tastes":"There was a man, apparently named Watson, who saw things whenever he tasted something, says the researcher Don Hoffman. Hoffman tells this to Michels without naming a source, and he isn't fully sure of the name (\"a guy name Watson I think\"). He offers it as a case of synesthesia, a condition in which the senses blend so that a taste also comes with something seen or felt.","szydagis-tests-alleged-crash-debris-with-neutron-activation-2020":"A physicist named Matthew Szydagis, at the University at Albany, has been studying debris said to come from crashed unidentified craft, according to his colleague Kevin Knuth, a physicist who once worked at NASA. Knuth tells Michels this in passing and names no further source. The testing involves neutron activation, a lab method that works out which elements a sample contains, so the point is to learn whether the material is anything ordinary.","t-pillar-at-cavustepe-visited-with-robert-schoch":"At Çavuştepe in Turkey stands a T-shaped stone pillar made of basalt, and Matt LaCroix, who talks on the show about very ancient sites, says he went to see it recently with a group that included Robert Schoch, whom he points out in an image (\"that's Robert Schoch right there\"). LaCroix raises it on an episode about something big happening around 12,000 BC, as one more site he takes to be part of a lost early chapter of human history.","t-pillar-design-on-the-gate-of-the-sun":"A carving on the back of the Gate of the Sun, a stone gateway at the ancient site of Tiwanaku in Bolivia, shows a T-shaped pillar design built on what is called the concept of the three doorways. A researcher named Matt LaCroix points this out to Michels without naming a source. He sets it beside the same three-doorway idea elsewhere, which suggests that cultures far apart may have shared one older symbol.","t-t-brown-apparatus-left-in-the-eureka-garage":"Some of the equipment built by Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor of electrically powered devices whose work comes up in the subject as an approach to mastering gravity, is said to have sat for years in a garage in Eureka, California. A UFO researcher named Clas Svahn tells Michels he went there himself. He says a group called the Borderland Society, which had ties to Brown, still had some of Brown's apparatus in the garage, but he could not take it away with him. Svahn also points to Brown's patent for an \"electrokinetic generator\" and to a biography of Brown by Paul Schatzkin, The Man Who Mastered Gravity.","t-townsend-brown-s-high-voltage-capacitor-propulsion-experim":"A mid-century American inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have found a way to move things with high-voltage electricity, a way to \"transcend\" chemical combustion, meaning engines that burn fuel. Michels raises it on several episodes, placing the work in the 1950s, and on one he goes further, saying he has \"independently uncovered an absurd amount of information\" that leads him to believe Brown \"essentially discovered anti-gravity.\" If true, a way to fly without rockets or jets has been known for decades.","table-levitation-at-family-seances-1965":"Around 1965 and 1966, during family seances in the Bronx, a table is said to have risen off the floor. The writer Peter Levenda tells Michels he saw it himself as a child: \"we actually had paranormal phenomenon\" when \"we were kids.\" In another telling on the same show he describes the table knocking rather than lifting, and gives no time or place.","tablets-detailing-construction-of-the-flood-boat":"Ancient clay tablets from Mesopotamia are said to describe in detail how the boat that survived the great flood was built, including how much bitumen, a tar used for waterproofing, went into it and what kind of wood was used. A researcher of ancient history named Matt LaCroix tells Michels this as a summary, without naming a particular tablet. He offers it as a sign of how precise and practical the old flood accounts are.","tactical-plan-to-stand-down-the-covert-organization-2023":"Steven Greer, who argues that UFO secrets are held by a hidden global group, says a tactical plan to make that covert organization stand down is already under way. Asked about it by Michels, he says it has 'already been in place for about a year and a half, two years,' which would put its start around 2023; he says this as someone involved, though he gives no details of the plan.","tae-technologiestrump-media-merger-announced-two-days-after-2025":"A merger between a company called TAE Technologies and Trump Media, Donald Trump's media company, was announced on December 18, 2025, two days after the death of a scientist named Loureiro on December 16, Michels points out. He sets out the two dates himself, without naming a source, in an episode about American scientists who have gone missing or died. He gives the dates side by side to suggest the timing may not be a coincidence.","tag-17-dead-end-corridor-behind-the-north-face-chevrons-dete-2020":"Behind the chevrons, the V-shaped stone blocks on the north face of the Great Pyramid of Giza, there is said to be a dead-end corridor known as \"Tag 17\". According to the researcher Jeffrey Drum, a team linked to a radar researcher named Filippo Biondi detected it in a 2020 scan, and someone \"recently went inside of this with a microscopic camera.\" Drum tells this without naming a source, and offers it as a sign that the scans really can find hidden spaces inside the pyramid.","tag-along-object-follows-the-incoming-missile-in-the-bluegil-1962":"During Bluegill Triple Prime, a 1962 American nuclear test fired high over Johnston Island in the Pacific, an unknown object is said to have appeared on the tracking screen, joined the incoming missile and \"tagged along following\" it down. Harold Malmgren, an economist who advised several presidents, tells Michels he handled the test report himself, and he names John F. Kennedy, Lyndon B. Johnson, Defense Secretary Robert McNamara, National Security Adviser McGeorge Bundy and the Pentagon official John McNaughton around it. If true, something unknown was shadowing a US nuclear test, and it was seen at the top of government.","takano-josen-met-hermann-oberth-and-discussed-ufos":"A Japanese man named Takano Josen says he once met the German rocket pioneer Hermann Oberth and that the two spoke openly about UFOs, and that \"Dr. Oberth truly respected the beings actually riding in UFOs.\" Michels says he met Takano on a trip to Japan, and Takano tells the story himself on the same episode, which would mean that a founder of rocketry took UFO occupants seriously.","taken-aboard-a-spacecraft-from-his-moscow-apartment-1997":"In 1997, from his top-floor apartment in Moscow, a man the episode presents as a former president of a Russian republic, named Kiran Ilum Xenov, says he was taken aboard a spacecraft. He tells Michels he felt something \"calling me,\" went to the window and saw \"something like a translucent pipe glowing made of some matter.\" If true, it would be an abduction account from a sitting political leader.","taken-in-broad-daylight-in-2007":"Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs are drawn to him, says that in 2007 by the Cape Fear River he was taken by beings in broad daylight. He tells Michels this first-hand, saying it \"was in the broad daylight. Then they came and they took me,\" an account he also gives in the book UFO of God.","taken-off-mountain-bike-and-out-of-bed-by-veterinarian-being":"Beings that he calls \"the veterinarians\" took him off his mountain bike and out of his bed, says John Blitch, a scientist who once worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency. He tells Michels they \"pluck us out of our bed, play with us,\" using \"the hurricane over my house\" as cover to land. If true, it would mean these beings handle people much as a vet handles animals.","taken-unknowingly-to-the-hangar-building-with-an-eg-g-escort-2000":"A NASA doctor named Greg Rogers says he was taken, without knowing where he was going, to the hangar building where he says he saw a UFO, at a base he does not name. He tells Michels that his escort from EG&G, a contractor that staffed secret government sites, 'knew I was going to that building that day' even though he himself did not.","taking-george-knapp-through-los-alamos-1989":"In 1989, Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret site in Nevada, says he brought the Las Vegas TV journalist George Knapp into Los Alamos, the New Mexico weapons laboratory, in a visit that \"was not all 100% legal.\" Lazar tells Michels that Knapp \"just walked around, filmed everything, spoke to people that I worked with,\" and that they rode right into the experimental areas, where Lazar said, \"George, this is my desk.\" Lazar tells it as his own memory, and it would show that he really worked at the lab, which is a disputed point in his story.","talbert-newspaper-series-on-1950s-aerospace-anti-gravity-int":"In the 1950s, a newspaper series is said to have revealed that big American aircraft companies had suddenly become interested in anti-gravity, meaning ways to cancel or control gravity. The physicist Hal Puthoff, who did research for the CIA, says the series ran in the Miami Herald and other papers, \"probably\" in the 50s, by a writer named Talbert, and he connects it to the work of the physicist Bryce DeWitt. The physicist John Brandenburg tells it in nearly the same words, spells the name Talbot, and ties it to DeWitt and the Gravity Research Foundation, a group that ran essay contests on gravity. The philosopher Jason Jorjani says the articles quoted the heads of Martin Aircraft, Lear and Convair as saying their firms had cracked anti-gravity, and he points to a book called Closer Encounters. Michels, telling it himself, is the most specific: he says Ansel Talbert wrote an article titled \"Conquest of gravity aim of top scientists in United States\" for the New York Herald Tribune in November 1955. He links its timing to Gordon Gray, then president of the University of North Carolina. If true, the series would show that serious gravity research was underway in industry decades ago, before it dropped from public view.","tall-white-children-s-play-saucer-1960":"Among the craft of the Tall Whites, the tall, pale beings he says lived in Indian Springs Valley, Nevada, in the 1960s, Charles Hall says there was a small saucer made for children, \"a saucer that you might have for your children to take out to the play area.\" Hall, who says he spent three years near these beings while stationed in the area, tells Michels he saw it himself and that \"it's not meant to go faster than light,\" unlike their larger ships.","tall-white-deep-spacecraft-schedules-and-the-five-year-run-f-1965":"Charles Hall, who says he spent three years in the mid-1960s around tall, pale beings he calls the Tall Whites in the Indian Springs Valley of Nevada, says he saw schedules for their spacecraft. The longest, he tells Michels, was for craft that take five years to arrive, and he was told those came directly from the beings' home planet. If true, it would put a regular route between Earth and another world.","tall-white-electronics-that-read-and-implant-thoughts-1965":"Tall, pale beings that he calls the \"tall whites\" had electronics that could read a person's thoughts and plant new ones, says Charles Hall, who says he spent three years near them while serving at Indian Springs, an air base in Nevada, from 1965 to 1967. He tells Michels their devices could \"tell what a person is thinking\" and \"put thoughts in the conscious part of their mind by electronic means.\" If true, it would mean these beings could shape what people think without being noticed.","tall-white-in-an-orcas-pub-asks-what-it-s-like-to-live-on-ea":"In a pub on Orcas Island, a stranger of the kind UFO lore calls a \"tall white\", meaning a tall, pale, human-looking being, asked a local what it is like to live there, and when the answer was about the island, said, \"No, on Earth.\" Brent Friedman tells Michels he heard this from a friend on Orcas Island, whom he does not name. If true, it would mean a visitor not from Earth was sitting in an ordinary bar making small talk.","tall-white-underground-facilities-and-hangars-at-the-north-e-1960":"A man named Charles Hall says that in the 1960s, while he was stationed in the Nevada desert, the \"tall whites\", tall pale beings he says he met there, lived in underground facilities at the north end of Indian Springs Valley, with hangars for their craft. Michels sums up Hall's claim in his interview with Hall and notes that it closely resembles the story of Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft near Area 51. If true, alien beings would have been living on a US military range.","tall-whites-came-in-the-last-ice-age-to-use-earth-as-a-pit-s":"A man named Charles Hall, who says he spent three years with tall, pale aliens he calls the 'tall whites' at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada, says they first came to Earth in the middle of the last ice age. He tells Michels they use Earth as a pit stop on their travels, 'for the same reason that we have a base on Guam.'","tall-whites-gated-compound-on-orcas-reached-by-an-unmarked-b":"Beings known in UFO lore as the tall whites, a supposed race of tall, pale non-humans, keep a gated compound on Orcas Island in Washington State, and an unmarked black helicopter flies in and out of it. A guest named Brent Friedman tells Michels this second-hand, from a friend who lives on Orcas Island and told him, \"they have a compound on the Orcas Island.\" If true, it would mean non-humans are living quietly on American soil with outside help.","tall-whites-in-suits-forming-a-high-speed-horse-formation-1965":"Between 1965 and 1967 on the Indian Springs ranges in Nevada, according to Charles Hall, the \"Tall Whites\", very tall, pale beings he says he lived alongside, wore suits that gave off force fields, and when a group of them turned the suits on a quarter mile out in the desert, the fields merged into one. Hall, who says he served there with the Air Force, tells Michels from \"direct experience\" that \"you would swear there was a giant white floating horse coming at you,\" which he offers as the origin of a local horse legend.","tall-whites-mistaken-for-ancient-egyptians-at-indian-springs-1960":"In the 1960s, some of the men working the range in Indian Springs Valley, Nevada, took the Tall Whites, the tall, pale beings Charles Hall says lived there, for ancient Egyptians who had somehow got into the valley, been trapped, and were \"still using their own hieroglyphics.\" Hall, who says he spent three years around these beings, repeats on two episodes with Michels what his fellow rangemen thought, which suggests the beings used a script that looked like Egyptian writing.","tall-whites-playing-airplanes-on-the-indian-springs-runways-1965":"On summer nights between 1965 and 1967, tall, pale beings that he calls the \"tall whites\" played on the runways of Indian Springs Air Base in Nevada, like children playing at airplanes. Charles Hall, who says he served there, tells Michels: \"I used to watch them play at the runways at Indian Springs Air Base on summer nights.\" If true, it would mean these beings were moving about openly on an American military base.","tall-whites-posing-as-charlie-hall-and-his-wife-to-visit-liv-1960":"Charles Hall, who says he lived alongside tall, pale aliens he calls the 'tall whites,' says that in the 1960s some of them passed as humans, one posing as a man named Charlie Hall and a female he calls Pamela pretending to be his wife. He tells Michels they did this to get into places like Lawrence Livermore Laboratory, a government weapons lab in California, and talk with the scientists there; if true, aliens would have been quietly mixing with American researchers.","tall-whites-say-dominant-species-kill-off-threats-1960":"Tall, pale beings called the Tall Whites are said to have told a man that, on most civilized worlds, the dominant species kills off anything that could threaten it, and that they found it odd that humans ride horses and live alongside other animals. A guest named Kurt Metzger relays this to Michels from the account of Charles Hall, who says he met these beings at Indian Springs, Nevada, in the 1960s. Metzger stresses Hall's point that the beings are \"not here to save the whales\", meaning they would not be here as kindly rescuers.","tall-whites-two-month-trip-to-a-star-near-arcturus-so-craft-1965":"Tall, pale beings that a man named Charles Hall calls the Tall Whites once left on a six-week or two-month round trip to a star near Arcturus, so their craft must go faster than light. Hall says he spent three years among these beings at a desert range near Indian Springs, Nevada, and he tells Michels the trip happened in the late summer of 1965. He names the beings he dealt with, such as the Teacher, Range Four Harry and the Tour Guide, and reasons that a trip that far in that little time would mean travel beyond light speed.","tapes-mention-coupling-to-the-computer-data-bank-1970":"Recordings of 1970s sessions run by Andrija Puharich, a researcher who held meetings meant to contact non-human intelligences, often mention 'coupling to the computer data bank', according to a researcher on the show who is not named. The guest says this from having gone through the tapes, without naming a further source, and offers it as a clue to what the voices in the sessions claimed to be connected to.","tapes-of-puharich-meditating-in-the-king-s-chamber-1970":"Tape recordings exist of the researcher Andrija Puharich meditating inside the King's Chamber, the main room deep inside the Great Pyramid, according to two researchers on the show. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels, 'We have tape recordings of him meditating in the King's Chamber.' A second researcher in the same episode, who is not named, says the same and dates the recordings to 1976, prompting a surprised 'What?' from Michels. Both speak as people who have the tapes or have studied them, not as people who were there. The episode is about Puharich, who by their account tried to contact an alien council, so the tapes matter to them as a record of him taking that work into the pyramid itself.","tara-swart-s-husband-s-death-and-doves":"Michels says that after the husband of a woman named Tara Swart died under unfortunate circumstances, a flock of doves showed up at her house. He tells it from memory, hedging with \"I think\", in a 2026 conversation about strange coincidences, as the kind of event that can feel like a message from beyond.","targ-and-puthoff-publish-in-nature-and-write-mind-reach-1972":"Two researchers, Russell Targ and Dr. Hal Puthoff, published an article in the journal Nature and then wrote a book called Mind-Reach, says Joe McMoneagle, who tells Michels about the work he took part in after them. He puts the article in 1972 and hedges on where it ran, saying he thinks \"it was called Nature magazine.\" He mentions it as the moment their research into mind-based perception reached the mainstream scientific press.","tate-s-post-trip-talk-on-solved-energy-1948":"In 1948, after a trip to the American Southwest, a University of Minnesota physicist named John Torrance Tate is said to have told people, \"I saw while on this trip, we will never have to worry about energy. That's already been figured out.\" Scott Ramsey, a researcher who studies a reported UFO crash, tells Michels this from Tate's archived records, and ties the trip to Vannevar Bush, who led US wartime science. Ramsey raises it because it would suggest Tate had seen some technology far beyond anything known.","tate-s-sealed-archive-at-university-of-minnesota-1948":"Records left by a man named John Torrance Tate, covering roughly 1948 to 1950, sit in a sealed archive at the University of Minnesota, and they bear on a story that also involves the company Texas Instruments. A researcher named Scott Ramsey, who tells it from his own search, says that \"long story short, we flew to Minneapolis and got a meeting at the University of Minnesota in their archives\" to see them. Ramsey raises it in a conversation about an alleged crash with alien bodies, so if true, the papers could hold early evidence of that event.","taylor-flying-cargo-jets-between-florida-and-nassau":"Before his later fame, a man named Timothy Taylor, whom NASA is said to have studied because UFOs seem to show up around him, flew cargo jets back and forth between Florida and Nassau in the Bahamas. Michels says Taylor writes about this himself in Launch Fever, his biography.","taylor-hospitalized-with-injuries-from-ranch-phenomena":"A man named Travis Taylor is said to have ended up in hospital with injuries from the strange happenings at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of unexplained events. The ranch's owner tells Michels this, adding that Taylor has had things happen to him that have \"followed him\" away from the ranch. If true, it would mean whatever is going on there can do real harm and does not stay in one place.","taylor-next-rocket-science-advance-will-come-from-religion-2010":"A NASA man named Timothy Taylor once told the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka that 'the next development in my field, rocket science, will come from yours, religion.' Michels tells this from Pasulka's book American Cosmic, where Taylor answered her question about why her work interested him. It hints that some space scientists look to religious experience for clues to new technology.","taylor-says-bledsoe-may-need-to-hide-out-in-nassau-2008":"Around 2008, a man named Timothy Taylor told Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says he attracts UFOs, that he thought Bledsoe might have to go and hide out in Nassau, in the Bahamas, for a while. Bledsoe tells Michels this himself as something Taylor said to him, which suggests he was thought to be in some kind of danger.","taylor-secretly-chief-scientist-of-uap-task-force-2020":"Around 2020, a man named Travis Taylor, who worked with the team at a property called Skinwalker Ranch, was quietly called into meetings in Washington and made chief scientist of the UAP Task Force. That was a Pentagon group set up to look into UAP, \"unidentified anomalous phenomena\", the newer official word for UFOs. The ranch's owner tells Michels this from the inside, saying the team had no idea at the time (\"unbeknownst to us\"). If true, it means someone from the ranch's own research was working at the heart of the government's UFO effort.","taylor-warned-off-driving-up-the-road-at-creech":"At Creech Air Force Base in Indian Springs, Nevada, a man named Timothy Taylor asked someone if he could just drive a mile or two up the road without getting out of the car, and was told, \"Oh, that would be a very bad idea.\" The wife of Charles Hall, a man who says he spent three years with tall white aliens at the base, tells this to Michels as something Taylor told them. In that telling the warning hints that something is kept up that road.","tayos-cave-metallic-library-of-alien-artifacts":"A cave in Ecuador, the Tayos Cave, is said to hold ancient metal objects that look alien, laid out like a library of metal books. Michels tells it on several episodes as something others claim: once he calls it a \"well-known myth\" about caves full of alien-looking metallic artifacts, and once he links the supposed library to an expedition in 1976 that involved the astronaut Neil Armstrong. If true, someone long ago left a record in metal that is still hidden underground.","td-100-experiment-on-a-woman-on-film-1960":"Silent film from the early 1960s at a company called Intelectron in New York shows an experiment on a woman: she sits behind a wall in one room while the experimenters work in another, using a device the inventor built and called the TD 100. A researcher named Greg Malatzi describes the footage to Michels, saying there is no sound \"but it's obvious what's happening.\" If true, it would show a device acting on a person through a wall decades ago.","team-statement-that-limestone-absorbs-the-signal-from-the-kn-2025":"In 2025 a research team that says it found hidden structures beneath the Khafre pyramid at Giza explained, in a statement on a Facebook page, why its radar scan did not clearly show the pyramid's known chambers: \"the structures are embedded in a limestone slab that absorbs the signal.\" A researcher named Jeffrey Drum reads this statement to Michels and credits it to the team, naming Filippo Biondi and a colleague given only as Armando. Read as a document, the statement gives the team's reason for the missing signal in plain words. It does not say how the team tested that explanation, which leaves the question open.","teardrop-shaped-transmedium-uap-video-shown-in-a-joint-task":"A naval intelligence commander is said to have sent an email with a video of a teardrop-shaped UAP (an unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the government's term for a UFO), maybe 20 feet long, \"traveling under the ocean water\". The video was then shown in a SCIF, a sealed room for viewing classified material, at a joint task force overseas. The retired Navy admiral Tim Gallaudet tells Michels this second-hand. If true, the military has footage of an unknown object that can move both through the air and underwater.","tech-sergeant-warns-borland-never-to-speak-of-the-triangle-a-2012":"Around 2012, at Langley Air Force Base in Virginia, a senior airman (a tech sergeant) told a young airman named Dylan Borland never to talk about the triangle-shaped craft again: \"Dylan, don't talk about this ever again. You have no idea what you got yourself into.\" Borland tells Michels this himself, as the one it happened to. He says the sergeant was a loadmaster, the crew member who handles a plane's cargo, and that he \"clearly knew something,\" which would mean the sighting was known about and kept quiet.","technicians-at-a-book-signing-say-the-signal-is-still-under-2010":"At a book signing in San Antonio, Texas, sometime in the 2010s, a man came up to the writer Whitley Strieber and said he was one of the technicians who had picked up a certain signal, that it was the strangest signal they had ever received, and that it was still being studied. Strieber tells Michels this himself, as something said to him, and it would mean some unexplained signal is quietly under investigation.","technology-transfer-with-aliens-who-developed-magnetic-propu":"A friend of Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, is said to have handled a \"technology transfer\", a handover of know-how, with one group of aliens who had developed magnetic propulsion, a way of moving craft by magnetism. Buchanan tells Michels this as something the friend told him, and in another mention says simply that the friend was in charge of technology transfer from the ETs, without the magnetic part. If true, it would mean people had dealings with aliens that included learning how their craft fly.","ted-owens-book-how-to-contact-space-people-1969":"In 1969 a man named Ted Owens published a book called How to Contact Space People. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher with a PhD who studies psychic claims, brings it up with Michels while talking about Owens, without naming any further source. The book matters to the story because Owens claimed, by its very title, that people could reach beings from space.","ted-owens-mr-pk-claimed-to-be-a-biological-sensor-of-ufos":"A man named Ted Owens, who called himself \"Mr PK\" (PK stands for psychokinesis, moving things with the mind), claimed to be almost a living sensor for UFOs. Michels brings it up while talking with the UFO researcher Jacques Vallee, saying a writer named Jeffrey Mishlove wrote about Owens. If true, it would suggest UFOs can be tied to particular people and their minds.","ted-owens-warns-of-challenger-shuttle-loss-on-christmas-eve-1985":"On Christmas Eve 1985, a man named Ted Owens, who claimed he could call up UFOs, phoned the researcher Jeffrey Mishlove. In \"his big booming voice\" he said the US government must not launch the next space shuttle, \"because if they do, my UFOs are going to knock it right out of the sky.\" Mishlove, who took the call, tells Michels that the Challenger exploded a few weeks later. A researcher named Jason Jorjani remembers it differently, as a series of threatening letters Owens sent to the government saying he would bring down the shuttles one after another.","ted-owens-wills-an-airliner-to-crash":"A man named Ted Owens is said to have willed the plane flying ahead of him and the people he was with to crash, and a civilian airliner did crash. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells the story to Michels without naming a source. If it were true, it would mean a person could bring down an aircraft by will alone.","ted-rice-s-story-in-masquerade-of-angels":"The book Masquerade of Angels, by the writer Karla Turner, tells the story of a man named Ted Rice, who is still alive. Tim Alberino, a guest on the show, names the book in passing as one of the works recording such experiences, without saying more about what happened to Rice.","ted-serios-thoughtographs-studied-by-jule-eisenbud-1960":"In the 1960s a Chicago hotel bellhop named Ted Serios was said to produce images on Polaroid film with his mind alone, pictures that came to be called \"thoughtographs\", and a psychiatrist named Jule Eisenbud studied him. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of psychic phenomena, gives Serios on the show as one example of mind acting on matter, without naming a source. If it were so, it would mean thought can leave a physical mark.","tedesco-brothers-data-on-orbs-entering-the-water-off-long-is":"Two brothers, Jerry Tedesco and John Tedesco, have gathered what is described as serious data on orbs, glowing balls of light, offshore near Long Island, New York, some of them going into the water. Tim Gallaudet, a retired Navy admiral, tells Michels about their work. He raises it as a sign that unexplained objects move between the air and the sea.","teenage-alberino-finds-the-book-of-enoch-at-borders":"As a teenager, Tim Alberino says, he drove to a Borders bookstore, was browsing the shelves, and came across the Book of Enoch, an ancient religious text that was left out of the Bible, in the 1917 English translation by R. H. Charles, shelved among the Pseudepigrapha, old writings credited to biblical figures. Alberino, who tells Michels about a 12-foot giant he says was captured in Afghanistan, describes this from his own memory as the moment his interest in these old texts began.","teenage-robert-temple-helps-arthur-young-draft-the-reflexive-1960":"In the 1960s in Philadelphia, a teenage Robert Temple, later a writer, is said to have helped the inventor and thinker Arthur Young write the first draft of Young's book The Reflexive Universe, a grand theory of mind and matter. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this as something he was told, without saying more about how the drafting went.","telepathic-contact-with-the-orb-sylvia-2008":"Between 2008 and 2010, at a place called Baker Victory Services in Western New York, an orb made contact with Jordan Jose by telepathy, meaning without speech, mind to mind, and in one of those experiences said its name was Sylvia. Jose tells Michels this as something he lived through himself. He describes the orb as a 'fractalizing type energy', and if his account is true, an intelligence of some kind spoke to him directly and gave itself a name.","telepathic-escape-plan-with-cousin-ian":"The UFO investigator James Fox says that once, in downtown San Francisco, his cousin Ian passed him an escape plan without saying a word: \"he communicated to me telepathically,\" Fox says, telling him to act \"on the count of\" a number. Fox tells Michels this as something he lived through himself, as a personal brush with mind-to-mind contact.","telepathic-friend-meets-an-unvoiced-telepath-2024":"A telepathic person recently made contact with someone who does not speak aloud, an \"unvoiced person\" who communicates only by thought. The writer Whitley Strieber tells Michels this about a friend of his, as something the friend told him. If true, it would mean such silent people are living among us.","telepathic-interview-with-a-live-et-at-s4":"A man named Oscar Wolf is said to have taken part in a telepathic interview with a live extraterrestrial at S4, a reported secret site near Area 51 in Nevada. A guest named Dan Sheehan relays this to Michels from what Wolf told him, saying Wolf saw the being and that it was about five and a half feet tall. If true, it would mean the US government holds a living alien at that base.","telepathic-projection-from-a-canadian-facility-to-make-michi-1970":"In the 1970s, people at a facility in south central Canada supposedly used telepathy to push residents of Michigan's Upper Peninsula into mailing their tax forms, and \"85% of the people did it next year.\" The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this comes from reports the psychic Ingo Swann made to the Guardian's Office, an internal department of the Church of Scientology, and his account also involves the psychic Pat Price and West Virginia. If true, it would mean mind influence at a distance had been tested on ordinary citizens.","telepathic-projection-test-makes-people-mail-tax-forms-to-a-1970":"Dan Sheehan, a man who says he saw the files himself, says a deep inside report by the psychic Ingo Swann described a 1970s test in which the psychic Pat Price was taken up to southern Canada to send thoughts into people's minds. According to Sheehan, the test made people in Michigan's Upper Peninsula mail their tax forms to a post office box in West Virginia. If true, it would mean the government tested telepathy to steer what ordinary people did.","telepathy-tapes-experiments-with-nonverbal-autistic-children-2024":"The Telepathy Tapes, a 2024 series made by Ky Dickens with the researcher Diane Powell, claims that nonverbal autistic children can read their parents' minds: in its tests, as Michels describes them, a parent looks at a random image generated on an iPad, and the child is asked to name what the parent is seeing. Michels brings it up himself while talking with the horror writer Whitley Strieber, as an example of telepathy that, if real, would show minds can share information without speech.","telepathy-tapes-nonverbal-autistic-children-who-know-egyptia":"Some autistic children who cannot speak seem to know things nobody taught them: in Michels' words, \"they come out like they know certain Egyptian hieroglyphics and stuff.\" Michels mentions this in passing, drawing on The Telepathy Tapes, a project he connects with Ky Dickens. If it were true, it would suggest these children reach knowledge by some route other than ordinary learning.","telepathy-tapes-you-don-t-get-my-powers-if-you-lie-2024":"In The Telepathy Tapes, a 2024 documentary series made by Ky Dickens about children said to show telepathy, one of the children tells a parent, \"Mom, you don't get my powers if you lie.\" Michels brings it up from the series himself, admitting he forgets which child said it, and offers it as a hint that such abilities might depend on honesty.","telephone-telepathy-experiments":"The biologist Rupert Sheldrake says people can often sense who is calling before they pick up the phone, and that experiments show it. He tells Michels he has done many of these tests himself, that about 85% of people can be tested this way, and that he is \"pretty sure it's real.\" If he is right, minds would be linked in a way science does not yet explain.","televised-remote-viewing-by-japanese-schoolchildren-starfish":"On Japanese television, schoolchildren in classrooms in Tokyo, Hokkaido and Kyushu tried remote viewing, which means trying to describe a hidden, faraway target by the mind alone. The target was a small aquarium with a starfish in it. A man named Joe McMoneagle, who took part, tells Michels about it as a test of whether ordinary children could do it.","teller-and-sakharov-meet-sakharov-says-teller-was-right-1957":"The physicist Edward Teller, a father of the American hydrogen bomb, and Andrei Sakharov, the father of the Soviet one, later met, and Sakharov looked over Teller's exchanges and said that Teller had been right. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels this as something he repeats from what was said, without saying what exactly Teller was right about.","teller-letter-commending-john-alexander":"The physicist Edward Teller wrote a letter praising a man named John B. Alexander for important advances in non-lethal warfare, meaning weapons meant to stop people without killing them. Jesse Michels mentions the letter himself, paraphrasing it, without showing it or saying where it can be found.","teller-refuses-on-camera-to-discuss-lazar-1989":"In 1989 the physicist Edward Teller was asked on camera about Bob Lazar and refused to talk about him. Lazar himself tells Michels this, saying he spoke beforehand with the man who went to interview Teller, who had been one of Teller's students. Lazar sees the refusal as telling, since Teller would be someone who might know about the secret work Lazar describes.","teller-s-name-on-a-project-sign-grudge-ufo-review-document-1947":"The physicist Edward Teller, one of the fathers of the hydrogen bomb, took part in a serious review of UFO reports in the late 1940s, according to Michels. He says Teller's name is on a document from Project Sign or Project Grudge, the US Air Force's UFO studies before Blue Book, its better-known UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. Michels tells it himself without showing the document, and it would mean that top weapons scientists were treating UFOs as a real question from the start.","teller-shown-townsend-brown-s-demo-floyd-odlum-invests":"Edward Teller, the father of the American hydrogen bomb, was once shown a version of the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown's 'fan precipitator' experiment and said, 'I have no idea how this works.' Michels tells this on several episodes, and in one he says Teller 'freaks out' and that a man named Floyd Odlum put money into Brown's work. Brown's daughter Linda Brown says she was there, and she puts the meeting at Teller's home in Berkeley in 1965, with Teller's wife Augusta Teller present. She remembers Teller looking at her father and finally saying, 'I don't understand how it works,' which, if true, would mean one of the century's leading physicists could not explain what Brown had built.","templars-and-the-secrets-of-time-travel":"The Knights Templar, the medieval order of crusader knights, supposedly held the secrets of time travel, Michels says in passing. He offers it himself and points not to a historical source but to Foucault's Pendulum, a novel by the Italian writer Umberto Eco.","templars-as-keepers-of-advanced-knowledge-and-a-bloodline-of":"The Knights Templar, the medieval order of crusading knights, held some kind of advanced knowledge and may also have protected a bloodline descended from Christ. That is the view of a researcher named Randall Carlson, who tells it to Michels as his own belief, without naming a source, and mentions the Templar figure Jacques de Molay along the way. If it were true, a lost body of knowledge would have been handed down in secret through the order.","templars-lalibela-and-the-ark-in-ethiopia":"The Ark of the Covenant, the sacred chest from the Bible, ended up in Ethiopia, and in the Middle Ages someone tipped off the Knights Templar, the order of crusader knights based at the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, that it was there, in a story linked to Lalibela in Ethiopia. The writer Graham Hancock tells Michels this as his own speculation, from his book The Sign and the Seal: \"I speculated that he tipped the Templars off that the Ark of the Covenant was in Ethiopia.\" If true, it would mean the Templars knew where the lost Ark was hidden.","templars-persecuted-by-philip-the-fair-and-burned-in-1307":"The Knights Templar, a medieval order of Christian knights, were persecuted by Philip the Fair, king of France, accused of things such as spitting on the cross, and \"all ended up getting burnt at the stake in 1307,\" Michels says. He tells this himself in a talk with the researcher Randall Carlson, drawing on the esoteric thinker Rudolf Steiner. He brings it up as an example of a group with secret knowledge that was destroyed by those in power.","temple-of-sais-as-a-record-keeping-site-for-history-later-de":"The Temple of Sais in Egypt is said to have been built as a place to keep records of history, and to have been destroyed and gone within about 500 to 1,000 years. A researcher of ancient history named Matthew LaCroix tells Michels this, drawing on the ancient Greek writer Plutarch's version of the story and on Sonchis, an Egyptian priest linked to the temple. LaCroix raises it to explain how knowledge of a very distant past could have been kept and then lost.","temporary-duty-at-the-pentagon-handling-vietnam-war-proteste-1967":"During the Vietnam War, sometime between 1967 and 1969, Joe McMoneagle says he was sent to the Pentagon on \"TDY\", military shorthand for a temporary posting, because protesters were throwing balloons full of blood. McMoneagle tells Michels this as part of his own service history.","tens-of-thousands-of-alien-satellites-studying-earth-before-1957":"Tens of thousands of alien satellites are said to have been studying Earth before 1957, the year Sputnik became the first human-made satellite. Michels frames it this way in his interview with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, as what her work could suggest. If true, something non-human would have been watching the planet before we could launch anything of our own.","tentacle-shape-analysis-says-the-jellyfish-are-not-drifting-2023":"Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs to appear, argues that the floating objects it calls \"jellyfish,\" which have hanging parts like tentacles, are not balloons blowing in the wind. A member of the team who watched them from its field site between 2023 and 2025 tells Michels that in published images of what they call objects of the \"7th class,\" the tentacles are sometimes held straight, which a drifting balloon would not do. They compare this with jellyfish-shaped footage released by the documentary filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, suggesting the objects move under their own control.","teotihuacan-as-a-gold-and-silver-ore-processing-plant-using":"The pyramids of Teotihuacan, the ancient city in Mexico, were a plant for processing gold and silver ore, according to a researcher named Geoffrey Drumm. He tells Michels this is his own \"hypothesis\" for the site's purpose. The story says mercury was used in the process, but at another point Drumm describes it as a \"lightning powered gold and silver processing facility\".","terence-mckenna-s-overdosing-girlfriend-sees-an-energy-ufo-f":"A girlfriend of Terence McKenna, a well-known writer on psychedelic drugs, was overdosing when she saw, above and behind McKenna, a huge UFO made of energy, filled with insect-like beings resembling praying mantises. The philosopher Jason Jorjani relays this on the show as McKenna's own story.","terence-mckenna-staying-with-the-sheldrakes-in-london":"The writer Terence McKenna used to stay at the biologist Rupert Sheldrake's home whenever he was in London. Sheldrake tells Michels this himself, calling McKenna \"one of my very good friends\".","tero-s-remark-to-merlin-sheldrake-on-mushrooms-at-the-start-2020":"Mushrooms and other 'really sacred foods' tend to grow either at the very start of a forest's life cycle, when almost nothing else grows, or at its end, when there is 'massive biodiversity'. That is the point Tero Isokauppila, a guest who talks about mushrooms, says he made around 2020 to the fungus researcher Merlin Sheldrake. He tells it as his own remark, part of his larger case that these foods hold a special place in nature.","terra-exploration-group-survey-finds-tunnel-from-secret-moun":"Michels says that a firm called Terra Exploration Group used ground surveys and magnetometry, which measures the magnetic field to spot what lies buried, and found a straight, man-made tunnel about 50 feet wide in Arizona. In his telling, it runs like a hidden artery from Secret Mountain to Bradshaw Ranch to the front gate of the Clarkdale Cement Plant, which a man named John Bradshaw claims was once the town's biggest employer and was guarded and fenced like a military site. Michels narrates this himself in a 2026 episode, and it would point to a secret underground facility hidden behind an ordinary business.","terraforming-the-sahara-with-thunderstorm-generators-during":"Fertilizers were once used together with thunderstorm generators to remake the climate of the upper eastern Sahara, says Geoffrey Drumm, who tells Michels about it without naming a source. Drumm places this in the Sahara's humid period, a time when the desert was much wetter. His claim is that the region was deliberately terraformed, meaning reshaped to make it fit for life, by producing storm clouds on purpose.","terry-and-linda-jamison-one-person-split-into-twins":"Terry Jamison and Linda Jamison, identical twins who work as psychics, say they were one and the same person before they were split apart, as they put it, \"at Earth\". The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as a prominent example of such claims. It would mean a single soul can come into this world as two people.","terry-lovelace-and-partner-toby-never-recovered-1977":"In 1977 a man named Terry Lovelace and his partner Toby had an incident while off duty on a camping trip, and Lovelace never fully recovered from it; Toby is now gone. Mario Woods, a former nuclear weapons guard, tells Michels this second-hand, as what Lovelace told him. It comes up in a talk about UFO abductions, so if true, it would mean the encounter left lasting harm on both men.","terry-lovelace-hears-a-being-s-voice-telepathically":"A man named Terry Lovelace says that when he met a non-human being he called \"Betty Rubble\", he heard her voice inside his head rather than through his ears. A guest named Mike Masters relays this to Michels, remembering Lovelace describing it, as an example of beings said to speak by telepathy.","terry-lovelace-s-dr-bug-abduction-told-at-2018-conference":"Terry Lovelace, a man who says he was abducted near Whiteman Air Force Base in Missouri, told a 2018 conference that he lay on a table screaming as loud as he could while a being he calls 'Dr Bug' came over, looked at him and said, 'Stop screaming.' John Blitch, a former DARPA scientist (DARPA is the Pentagon's research agency) who says he too was abducted, tells Michels he heard Lovelace tell it there.","terry-lovelace-s-phone-health-data-shows-six-stories-climbed":"A man named Terry Lovelace has health data from his cell phone showing that he climbed six storeys one night, though he lives in a one-storey house. John Blitch, a scientist who worked with DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, and who says he was himself abducted by an alien, tells Michels about it as Lovelace related it. The point is that the phone seems to record him being taken somewhere he cannot account for.","tesla-and-marconi-claimed-alien-communication":"The inventors Nikola Tesla and Guglielmo Marconi, both pioneers of radio, are said to have claimed they picked up messages from aliens. Michels mentions it in passing, in his own words, while talking with the filmmaker Tim Alberino, and gives no source or details. If true, contact may have been made in the early days of radio.","tesla-and-thomas-townsend-brown-discovered-extended-electrod":"The inventor Nikola Tesla and an American inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown both found the same hidden physics, which Michels calls extended electrodynamics, a broader form of the science of electricity and magnetism that he says often involves \"high voltages across short distances.\" Michels says this himself on three episodes, once adding that Tesla did this late in his career at his labs at Wardenclyffe and in Colorado Springs, and once, without mentioning Tesla, saying he is as sure of Brown as of anything in the story and that \"it seems like that's how the crafts fly.\" If true, it would mean the way UFOs move was already glimpsed by these two inventors.","tesla-and-townsend-brown-collaboration-learned-from-foreign-1920":"The inventor Nikola Tesla worked together with a man named Thomas Townsend Brown sometime between the 1920s and 1940s, on what is framed as anti-gravity research, and word of it came from intelligence, \"not from any US source.\" Harold Malmgren, a former adviser to presidents, says this on two episodes with Michels, once simply calling his source \"intelligence\" and once stressing it was foreign; in one telling the line is credited to the researcher Steven Greer instead. If true, it would mean a foreign service knew of secret early work on defying gravity that the US kept quiet.","tesla-at-wardenclyffe-tapping-the-ether-1900":"In the early 1900s the inventor Nikola Tesla is said to have worked with high voltages over short distances at Wardenclyffe, his laboratory in New York, claiming he could tap the ether, an old idea of a medium filling all space, for free energy. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, while talking with a guest named Dave Rossi. If it were true, energy could be drawn from space itself.","tesla-causes-an-earthquake-in-manhattan-1890":"The inventor Nikola Tesla described using what amounted to earthquake weaponry in his laboratory and setting off a quake in Manhattan in the 1890s, according to Jason Jorjani, a writer on secret technology. Jorjani tells Michels this as something Tesla himself described, without naming where. It would mean machines able to shake the ground existed more than a century ago.","tesla-killed-in-1943":"The inventor Nikola Tesla did not simply die in 1943: he was killed. A UFO researcher named Steven Greer says this on the show in passing, without naming a source. He puts it in a list of events from the 1920s, 30s and 40s, implying that someone silenced Tesla over what he knew.","tesla-offers-his-work-to-soviet-and-german-sources":"The inventor Nikola Tesla is said to have offered some of his work and ideas to the Soviets and to the Germans. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, mentions this on the show as \"stories\" he has heard, without naming where they come from. If true, some of Tesla's inventions might have gone to foreign powers as well as to the United States.","tesla-reportedly-flew-scalar-powered-vehicles":"The inventor Nikola Tesla is rumored to have actually flown vehicles powered by scalar energy, a proposed kind of energy field outside mainstream physics. A scientist named Steve Colbern, who studies objects said to be alien implants in human bodies, says this to Michels, and presents it only as a rumor. If true, it would mean such a craft was flown more than a century ago.","tesla-s-alien-contact-at-colorado-springs-1899":"The inventor Nikola Tesla is said to have communicated with aliens from his laboratory in Colorado Springs in 1899; Michels says so several times, once adding that Tesla saw past, present and future all at once, and Steve Colbern, a scientist who studies objects said to be alien implants, says \"they talked to Tesla\" according to Tesla's writings. The tellers do not say which of Tesla's writings they mean. If it is Talking with Planets, the article he published in Collier's Weekly in 1901 about the Colorado Springs signals, then his own account says something narrower: he recorded electrical changes with \"a clear suggestion of number and order\" that first \"positively terrified\" him. He writes that he felt he had been \"the first to hear the greeting of one planet to another,\" describing signals he heard, not a conversation, and treating any answer as something still to come.","tesla-s-wireless-power-and-claimed-over-unity":"The inventor Nikola Tesla believed he had achieved \"over-unity\", a machine that puts out more energy than goes into it, according to Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer. McConnell tells Michels there are accounts that do not just hint at this but \"state\" it, though he does not name them. He raises it while talking about Tesla's work on sending power without wires, which, if the claim held, would point to a nearly free source of energy.","tesla-s-work-on-electron-movement-and-transmutation":"The inventor Nikola Tesla was working along a line of thought in which moving electrons around would let you rearrange almost any matter, turning one thing into another, according to Harold Malmgren, a former adviser to US presidents. Malmgren says this on the show as his own view, with no source named. If true, it would place Tesla on the road to transmuting materials.","tesla-s-world-wireless-drawings-show-wingless-craft":"The inventor Nikola Tesla's drawings for his World Wireless system, a plan to send power and signals through the air without wires, are said to be \"full of UFOs\", meaning wingless flying craft. A writer named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as his own reading of the drawings, without pointing to a particular sheet. If true, it would mean Tesla was picturing such craft as part of his designs.","tesla-seeing-the-future-in-high-voltage-experiments":"The inventor Nikola Tesla, during some of his high-voltage experiments, would say strange things such as that he could see the future, the present or the past. Michels tells this himself, from a Tesla biographer he does not name, which would suggest strong electric fields can alter the mind.","tesla-travels-to-europe-to-persuade-hertz-he-was-wrong-1887":"Nikola Tesla once travelled to Europe to persuade the physicist Heinrich Hertz that Hertz was wrong, at some point between the late 1880s and the end of the century. A Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell tells Michels this, saying it comes \"from what I've read\" but naming no source. The point is that Tesla held a different view of physics, one that McConnell suggests is still not fully understood.","tessa-dick-says-philip-k-dick-believed-the-world-was-a-simul-1970":"The science fiction writer Philip K. Dick was fully convinced that the world is a simulation, a kind of computer-made reality rather than a real one, according to his wife, Tessa Dick. Michels passes this on second-hand: his friend Rizwan Virk, who has written a couple of books about simulation theory, is friends with Tessa Dick, and she talks about how her husband was \"just fully bought into the world being a simulation.\" If so, one of the writers most linked to that idea believed it about his own life, not just in his fiction.","test-flight-of-the-sport-model-with-a-vhf-radio-link-and-lig-1988":"Bob Lazar says that in 1988 or 1989 he watched a test flight of the craft he calls the \"sport model\" at S-4, a site in Nevada where he says he worked on flying craft not built by humans: it rose off the ground silently, with \"a little corona discharge glow on the bottom\", stayed in contact over a VHF radio link, and seemed to bend light around it. Lazar tells Michels this as an eyewitness, and names two others involved, Dennis Mariani and a man called Barry. If true, it would mean such craft were flown and tested on US soil.","testimony-to-ross-coulthart-in-washington-dc-2024":"A man named Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, says that in 2024 he contacted Ross Coulthart, a journalist who reports on UFOs, flew to Washington, DC, and met him there to tell his story. Jose tells Michels this himself. He also mentions David Grusch, a former Air Force intelligence officer who has said publicly that the US government holds craft not made by humans, which places his account among the whistleblowers who have come forward.","tethys-sea-seabed-and-hydrothermal-vents-at-giza":"The Giza Plateau in Egypt, where the great pyramids stand, was the floor of an ancient ocean called the Tethys Sea millions of years ago, says a researcher named Jeffrey Drum. He tells Michels that the vertical columns seen in radar scans under the plateau by a team led by Filippo Biondi \"could be the remnants of some of these hydrothermal mineral vents\". Hydrothermal vents are openings on the seabed where hot, mineral-rich water rises, and Drum puts a magma chamber beneath them. On a later episode, under the spelling Geoffrey Drumm, he says it again: \"Basically the Giza plateau was the bottom of the Tethus Sea.\" He offers this as part of his case that the pyramids sit on a natural source of energy below the ground.","texas-death-row-convictions-partly-based-on-hypnotic-regress":"Two men are on death row in Texas right now whose convictions rested partly on hypnotic regression, a technique that uses hypnosis to recover supposedly buried memories. Michels tells this himself, as something he learned from John Blitch, a scientist with DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, who says he was abducted by an alien. It comes up because hypnosis is also used to recover abduction memories, so it raises how much weight such memories can bear.","texas-instruments-worker-given-unknown-metal":"A worker at the electronics company Texas Instruments was supposedly handed a piece of metal to study without being told what it was or what he was working on. A guest who goes by UAPGerb mentions it to Michels as \"a famous example\" of reverse engineering, meaning taking apart recovered technology of unknown origin to learn how it works. He did not hear it himself: he repeats it from an anonymous source known as the Infographics Show whistleblower. If true, it would mean strange material was being farmed out to ordinary engineers who were kept in the dark.","thane-heins-coil-reverses-generator-drag-replicated-by-gary-2007":"A coil designed by a Canadian inventor named Thane Heins is said to reverse the usual drag on a generator. Normally a generator slows when it powers something, but the British computer hacker Gary McKinnon tells Michels that when he fitted Heins's coil, which has higher inductance, and set it to power a load, \"the rotation speed increases and the input current goes down.\" McKinnon says he got this result himself in the UK; if it held up, a generator would get easier to run as it does work.","the-1943-philadelphia-experiment":"Legend has it that in 1943 the Navy tried to teleport a ship in what is now called the Philadelphia Experiment, and that it partly worked: the ship vanished, reappeared, and half the crew ended up fused into the ship's walls. Michels tells it himself in a 2026 episode, framing it as legend rather than naming a source.","the-1952-hottel-memo-on-a-crashed-ufo-with-alien-crew":"An FBI memo addressed to an official named Guy Hottel reports an Air Force officer describing a crashed UFO with an alien crew inside, and Michels says it is the most read document on the FBI's website. Michels brings it up in three episodes. In one he dates it 1952. In the other two he dates it 1950, calls the source an Air Force technical officer who came upon a crashed craft, and ties it to a book of that year by the writer Frank Scully. If the memo means what Michels takes it to mean, the FBI was being told about a recovered craft and its crew very early on.","the-1958-western-trackdown-and-a-wall-building-trump":"In the Western Trackdown, from 1958, a man named Trump with \"magnetic charisma\" and a cult-like following rides into a town and promises to save it by building a wall. Jesse Michels brings this up himself, in an episode about the idea that the human mind can glimpse the future, as a seeming case of a story that foresaw events decades later.","the-1995-96-official-statement-that-the-remote-viewing-progr":"Around 1995 or 1996, the US government is said to have come out and admitted it had run a remote viewing programme, a project that trained people to try to describe distant places and targets using only their minds, while saying it never produced useful intelligence. Chris Ramsay, a guest who looks into these subjects, recalls it loosely and without a source, putting it as officials saying in effect \"we had this program\". The point is that the government admitted the programme existed even as it played down what it achieved.","the-2020-scan-detects-the-queen-s-chamber-shaft":"A radar scan of the Great Pyramid at Giza made in 2020 picked up the horizontal shaft that runs out of the Queen's Chamber, one of the known rooms inside the pyramid. That is the reading of a researcher named Jeffrey Drum, who points to a study titled \"Synthetic aperture radar Doppler tomography detects undiscovered high-resolution internal structures of the Great Pyramid of Giza\" and says the shaft shows up \"fairly accurately.\" If he is right, the method can see real features inside the stone, and that gives weight to the other hidden structures the same scan is said to reveal.","the-2020-scan-misses-the-king-s-chamber":"A 2020 radar scan of the Great Pyramid of Giza does not clearly show the King's Chamber, the pyramid's best-known inner room, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum. Drum is looking at the results of a study titled \"Synthetic aperture radar Doppler tomography detects undiscovered high-resolution internal structures of the Great Pyramid of Giza\", which used satellite radar to build pictures of the inside of the pyramid, and he tells Michels: \"I don't see a clear signature there for the King's Chamber.\" It matters because if the scan cannot plainly show a room everyone knows is there, that bears on how far to trust the hidden structures it seems to reveal.","the-angel-of-the-lord-in-the-assyrian-camp":"One night, while the Assyrian army was besieging Jerusalem, a being called the angel of the Lord passed through its camp, and by morning a vast number of soldiers were dead. Michels tells it himself on the show, in the line \"The angel of the Lord moves through the Assyrian camp the night the siege breaks.\" He draws on the Bible, here the King James Version of 1611. If the story were read as Michels frames it, it would be an ancient account of a powerful visitor stepping into a war. The text, Isaiah 37:36, tells it this way: \"Then the angel of the LORD went forth, and smote in the camp of the Assyrians a hundred and fourscore and five thousand: and when they arose early in the morning, behold, they were all dead corpses.\" The order of events is the same in both: the angel goes out at night, strikes the camp, and the siege ends with the soldiers dead.","the-angels-who-visited-abraham-and-lot-in-sodom-as-beautiful":"The angels who visit Abraham in the Bible, and the one who goes to Sodom to bring Lot out, looked especially beautiful, says the researcher Jason Jorjani. He tells Michels this without naming a source and reads them as tall, fair, \"Nordic\"-looking men. Read that way, the Genesis story would describe visitors who were human-looking beings, not spirits.","the-association-of-24-retired-officials-pushing-uap-programs":"A group of about two dozen retired officials is said to be working to bring secret UAP programmes (UAP, or unidentified anomalous phenomena, is the official term for UFOs) back under proper government oversight, including work held inside aerospace companies. Dan Sheehan, a guest on the show, passes this on from a man named Michael Phillip and calls the group an 'insurgency' whose members 'have occupied extremely high positions' in the Defense Department and the CIA. If true, it would mean people from inside the system believe these programmes have slipped out of the government's control.","the-astronaut-photo-behind-elizabeth":"There is a very famous photograph of a little girl named Elizabeth in a nice dress, sitting on a moor in England, with what looks like a figure in an astronaut's suit standing behind her. The UFO researcher Clas Svahn mentions it to Michels as a well-known picture, without saying more about where it comes from or when it was taken.","the-audrey-phone-messages-2000":"Between about 2000 and 2019, according to Grant Cameron, a guest who researches the subject, \"over 80\" phone calls known as the Audrey calls went out to \"all sorts of people\", and most people have never heard of them. Cameron passes this on from others, and he names Stan Romanek, Paola Harris and Timothy Taylor in connection with it, though he says nothing here about who or what Audrey was or what the calls said.","the-aviary-and-bill-moore-s-mufon-speech-1980":"In the 1980s, the story goes, a group known as the Aviary, made up of insiders with bird code names, worked as agents spreading false information about the truth of UFOs. Michels describes it himself as \"long rumored\" and links it to the former Air Force intelligence agent Rick Doty, the pilot John Lear, and the UFO writer Bill Moore, and to a speech Moore gave to MUFON, a large civilian UFO research group. If true, some of the best-known UFO stories of that decade were planted on purpose.","the-being-was-nearly-taken-to-the-zoo-before-it-went-to-the-1996":"In 1996 in Varginha, Brazil, people say a strange living creature was captured after a craft came down, and the story goes that the people holding it first thought of taking it to the zoo, then rushed it to a hospital because it was an emergency. A man named Italo Venturelli, a witness to the Varginha events, tells Michels this as the story he has heard, not something he saw. If true, it suggests the people who caught the being had no idea what it was or where it belonged.","the-bell-levitated-and-had-to-be-chained-down-1940":"The Bell, a bell-shaped device said to come from a secret German wartime program in the 1940s, rose off the ground when it was switched on, and its makers had to chain it down. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source. He says that is why the test rig built around the device exists, and he takes it as a sign the Germans had built a working anti-gravity machine.","the-bell-s-alternate-name-the-beehive-1940":"The Bell, a bell-shaped device said to have been built in a secret Nazi programme of the 1940s linked to the Skoda Works, a large arms factory, had another name, according to the researcher Jason Jorjani. He tells Michels its \"alternate name\" was a German word meaning \"the beehive\", without saying where he found it.","the-big-void-as-a-heat-exchanger-for-reactions-in-the-grand":"The Big Void, a large hidden empty space inside the Great Pyramid of Giza, is \"in a perfect location for a heat exchanger,\" says the researcher Jeffrey Drum. He tells Michels this without naming a source. In his reading, the space cooled reactions that took place in the Grand Gallery, the pyramid's long, tall inner passage, which would make the pyramid a working machine and not a tomb.","the-black-mailbox-began-as-a-vague-landmark-1989":"The black mailbox, a spot in the Nevada desert that later became famous among people watching for UFOs, started out as nothing more than a rough landmark Bob Lazar gave in 1989. Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site, tells Michels he did it himself: asked where to look, he simply said, 'It's around the black mailbox.' If his account is right, a well-known meeting point grew out of one offhand direction.","the-blue-book-briefing-folders-of-160-remote-viewing-success-1978":"The US Army's remote viewing unit at Fort Meade, Maryland, which trained people to describe distant targets by mind alone, is said to have kept what it called \"the Blue Book\": two blue briefing folders holding 160 successes, used from about 1978 into the 1990s. Joe McMoneagle, who served in the unit, tells Michels this himself. If true, the unit kept a written record of 160 times the method worked, ready to show officials.","the-boat-pits-as-acid-filled-circuit-breakers":"Geoffrey Drumm believes the boat pits beside the Great Pyramid, long trenches cut into the rock, were once filled with acid and worked as circuit breakers for a pyramid that he thinks was a machine. He tells Michels he has found \"more sulfate compounds\" inside them as evidence, and says on the show that the pyramids' true purpose was revealed to him by an alien.","the-breathing-hole-on-the-object-2012":"A hole in a strange object on the floor of the Baltic Sea seemed to let out a milky cloud, as if it were breathing, according to a diver named Stefan Hogeborn, who went down to it in 2012. The treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, whose team found the object, passes on what Hogeborn told him: the surface was dusty, \"and then you see like it's from this hole this milk is going.\" If true, it would suggest something was coming out from inside the object.","the-bubble-energy-field-mapped-over-the-property":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch famous for reports of strange lights and events, the owner says an energy field shaped like a \"bubble\" sits over the property. He tells Michels it \"has been mapped using lidar,\" a laser scanning method, \"and other platforms,\" which, if so, would mean something measurable and physical surrounds the land.","the-business-plot-against-fdr-1930":"In the 1930s, business interests tied to the Morgan and DuPont families planned to raise an army of about 500,000 men, march on Washington and remove President Franklin Roosevelt from office by force, according to a researcher named Jason Simosa. He links it to the Marine general Smedley Butler and tells it on the show without naming a source. He brings it up because, as he tells it, it shows powerful private groups were willing to take over the government.","the-cia-as-the-brainchild-of-brown-brothers-harriman-1940":"The CIA was the brainchild of the people at Brown Brothers Harriman, a Wall Street banking firm. A researcher named Dan Sheehan says this to Michels in the 1940s part of his story, without naming a source. If true, the agency's origins lay with private bankers rather than with government alone.","the-cia-convenes-the-robertson-panel-1953":"In 1953 the CIA called together the Robertson panel, a group of scientists asked to review UFO reports. Bryce Zabel, a guest on an episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, brings this up as a turning point in his history of how the CIA dealt with the subject, without naming a source. It matters because the panel is often seen as the point where official efforts to play down UFOs began.","the-cia-showed-elizondo-mris-of-uap-witnesses-with-caudate-p":"The CIA is said to have shown MRI brain scans of people who had encountered UFOs, and the scans showed differences in the caudate putamen, a structure deep in the brain. The scientist Garry Nolan says on a documentary about the former Pentagon official Lue Elizondo that when he got involved, \"the CIA came to my office and then they showed me MRIs of some of these people,\" most of whom \"had interactions with UFOs.\" If true, it would mean these encounters leave a physical mark in the brain, and that the CIA was quietly studying it.","the-city-of-london-s-remembrancer-sits-in-parliament":"The City of London is said to have an official called the Remembrancer, which Jay Anderson, a guest, describes as \"the only unelected members who can go into parliament\". Anderson tells Michels this, crediting a work called The Spider's Web: Britain's Second Empire. He raises it as a sign that the City has a special reach into British government.","the-collins-elite-as-a-fundamentalist-faction-inside-governm":"The Collins Elite is said to be a faction of deeply fundamentalist Christians embedded in the US government who see UFOs as demonic and do not want them studied scientifically. Michels relays this from Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, and from To The Stars Academy, a private company set up to research UFOs. If true, it would mean that religious belief, not only secrecy, has kept the subject from serious study inside government.","the-dirt-road-leads-straight-to-the-army-sergeant-school-ess-1996":"In the 1996 case at Varginha, Brazil, where strange beings are said to have been seen and taken away, a small dirt road near the site supposedly runs straight to EsSA, a Brazilian army school for sergeants. Carlos de Souza, a surgeon who tells Michels about his hospital's part in the case, says he did not know this himself. He learned it from a man named James Fox and from others in ufology, the study of UFOs. If true, it would put the army right next to where the beings appeared.","the-dogon-s-knowledge-of-the-sirius-binary-star-system":"The Dogon, a people of Mali, are said to have known in their old traditions that Sirius is really two bodies, Sirius A and Sirius B, though the second cannot be seen with the naked eye. Michels brings this up from The Sirius Mystery, a book by the writer Robert Temple, saying they \"somehow\" knew of \"two Sirius planets\"; if true, it would suggest the knowledge came to them from somewhere outside.","the-drones-were-orbs-from-the-sea-now-over-europe-2024":"The mysterious 'drones' reported in the skies of the United States in 2024 were not drones at all but orbs that came out of the sea, and the same things are now showing up over Europe. That is what Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he attracts UFOs, tells Michels from his own experience. 'Those aren't drones,' he says, which would mean the widely reported sightings were something not made by people.","the-electromagnetic-black-project-at-the-base-1990":"Dan Sherman, a former sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that from 1990 to 1993 an overseas base where he served hosted a \"black project\", a secret programme kept off the books, that worked on electromagnetic technology. Sherman tells Michels about it as someone who was there, calling it \"very interesting into itself\".","the-endless-frontier-read-as-working-back-from-airdropped-al-1945":"Michels suggests that Science, The Endless Frontier, the 1945 report by the American science administrator Vannevar Bush that set out how the US government should fund research after the war, reads differently \"with this new lens\": as a plan for working back from derivative alien technology the government already had. This is Michels' own reading, offered without a further source. The report lays out a programme of government-funded science; whether anything behind it came from recovered craft, it neither says nor rules out, and Michels does not quote it on that point.","the-et-s-account-galactic-monitors-of-life-sent-by-god":"A non-human being held at S4, a site near Area 51 in Nevada where some say recovered craft are kept, is said to have explained that it belonged to \"a group of beings that were from different star systems in our galaxy,\" sent by \"God\" to watch over life. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this second-hand, as something he was told came from index cards recording interviews with the being. In one telling he credits a man named Oscar Wolf as his source; in another he credits a man named Albert Stein and places the interviews in the 1950s and 60s.","the-face-peeler-legend-of-the-peruvian-amazon-1983":"The Peruvian Amazon has a legend of a \"face peeler\", and it goes back only to the mid 1980s and no further, according to Tim Alberino. Alberino tells Michels this without naming a source. His point is that the story is recent, not an old local tradition.","the-fake-l9-incident-document-1977":"In November 1977 someone created a fake document describing an \"L9 incident\" at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, dated a day before two men, Michael and Mario, had their own experience there. Michels says this himself and links the fake to a man named Richard Doty. If it is true, a false record was planted right next to a real event, maybe to muddy it.","the-family-the-fellowship-network-in-government-1930":"A religious network known as The Family, or The Fellowship, was founded in the 1930s and grew strong in government after the Second World War, according to a researcher named Jason Simosa. He tells Michels to look into it, pointing to names such as the writer Jeff Sharlet and Alonzo McDonald. He raises it as part of his case that one hidden network of insiders runs through the UFO cover-up he describes.","the-fenced-vacant-lot-where-the-beings-appeared-1996":"The spot in Varginha, Brazil, where strange beings are said to have appeared in 1996 is a vacant lot that still exists, fenced off but still visited, and there is a project to study it. A man named Italo Venturelli, a witness to the Varginha events, tells Michels this, passing on what he has heard. To him it means the place is still treated as the real site of the encounter, decades later.","the-flying-house-of-loreto-1800":"People in the past saw things in the sky that they took to be flying houses, and one example is the Flying House of Loreto in Italy. The researcher Diana Pasulka mentions it on the show, without naming a source, while discussing religious accounts. She uses it to suggest that older religious stories may describe the same kind of thing people now call UFOs.","the-g-engines-are-coming-article-arrives-on-cook-s-desk-1956":"The story is that a 1956 article titled 'The G-Engines Are Coming' shows that America's aviation industry was openly chasing antigravity engines in the 1950s. Nick Cook, a journalist at the defence magazine Jane's, tells Michels that the article simply turned up on his desk, since people would send material to Jane's. He ties it to a man named George S. Trimble. Michels brings up the same article in another episode and links it to Michael Gladish and to the physicists Stanley Deser and Richard Arnowitt, without mentioning Cook. If the article means what they take it to mean, antigravity work began decades ago and then went quiet.","the-gadarene-demoniac-and-the-appointed-time":"A researcher named Tim Alberino tells Michels that the man possessed by demons whom Jesus met in the land of Gadara, a story from the New Testament, ties into the much older account of giants and their spirits, and the time set for their judgement. Alberino tells it from the scriptures without naming another source: Jesus was in Gadara with his disciples, and the people there would chain the man up in the tombs \"because he would scream.\" He reads it alongside the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text left out of most Bibles, quoted here in R. H. Charles's 1917 translation. Enoch says that \"the spirits of the giants afflict, oppress, destroy, attack, do battle, and work destruction on the earth\", and that they will go on destroying \"until the day of the consummation, the great judgement\" over the Watchers, the fallen angels who fathered them. Elsewhere Enoch has kings \"behold Mine Elect One\" on the throne of glory judging Azazel, a leader of the fallen angels, \"and all his hosts.\" Set side by side, both texts tell of tormenting spirits loose on the earth until a fixed day of judgement; the link between the two is Alberino's reading. If he is right, the demons of the Gospels would be the spirits of the ancient giants.","the-golden-age-of-general-relativity-bahnson-and-babson-1950":"The mathematician Eric Weinstein says there is something odd about what is called \"the golden age of general relativity\", a burst of research on Einstein's theory of gravity in the 1950s and 60s. He tells Michels it \"makes absolutely no sense\" and that it is really a story about two men, Agnew Bahnson and Roger Babson. He hints that the two were more than they seemed, though he names no source.","the-gran-sasso-laboratory-and-the-gerda-experiment":"Deep under the Gran Sasso mountain in Italy there is a physics laboratory that runs the Gerda experiment, a hunt for the Majorana particle, a particle physicists have predicted but never found, according to the Italian radar researcher Filippo Biondi. Biondi tells Michels about it in passing, saying of the particle that \"they didn't find it, still find it. The so-called Majorana particle.\"","the-great-year-documentary-proposes-a-binary-companion-siriu":"The idea is that our Sun has an unseen partner star, and the two of them circling each other drives the Great Year, a very long cycle in the sky that ancient peoples counted. It comes from a documentary called The Great Year, which Michels plays on an episode about events around 12,000 BC. The researcher Matt LaCroix sums up the film's argument. He says its makers \"postulated that the great year was based on a dance of our binary companion,\" and that some of them \"thought it was Sirius,\" the bright star. In a clip from the film, the narrator adds that \"some say the binary model confirms all of our Celestial observations without the need for excessive torque or epicycle type explanations.\" Neither teller names a source beyond the film. If it were true, a slow shift in the sky usually blamed on Earth's wobble would instead be a sign of a second sun.","the-holy-grail-as-a-technology-of-regeneration":"The Holy Grail of medieval legend may have been not a cup but a real technology for healing and renewing life, the researcher Randall Carlson tells Michels: \"I think it's a technology.\" He draws on the medieval Grail romances, poems by Chrétien de Troyes, Robert de Boron and Wolfram von Eschenbach, whose Parzival tells of the Grail, reading their story of a wonder-working object as a memory of something once real.","the-host-s-own-rectangular-craft-sighting":"Michels says that he himself has seen a rectangular craft in the sky. He mentions it in passing while talking with a guest and does not say where or when.","the-hosts-saw-a-green-orb-with-elizondo-at-his-wyoming-home-2024":"Michels says that in 2024, one night at the Wyoming home of Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official who now speaks publicly about UFOs, he and Elizondo saw a green orb in the sky. Michels tells it first-hand, reminding Elizondo: \"we saw a green orb with you, I don't know you remember that, it was night time.\" In a later episode he describes it as a green fireball that \"just shoot out into the sky.\"","the-hughes-group-s-cia-covert-actions-the-glomar-explorer-an-1960":"A group around the businessman Howard Hughes did the CIA's covert work in the 1960s and 70s, including the project known as the Glomar Explorer, and may even have carried out \"the hits\", meaning the Kennedy killings, says the researcher Matthew Pines. Pines tells Michels this without naming a source. He brings it up because he ties the same group to the hidden UFO story, which would put a private network at the centre of both secret operations and political murders.","the-jailer-s-file-submariners-on-lsd-2000":"While breaking into US military computers around 2000 to 2002, Gary McKinnon, a hacker who says he searched those systems for signs of a secret space program, found the 'jailer's file' of a military base, a record of discipline cases, which showed submarine crew taking LSD. McKinnon tells this himself, noting that 'every military base has a jailer' and calling what he read 'just crazy.' If true, it shows what kind of private records he says he could reach.","the-jasons-solve-dod-problems-in-summer-studies":"The JASONs, a group of top scientists who meet in summer studies to advise the government, are \"supposed to solve problems\" that the Pentagon hands them, according to Eric W. Davis, a physicist who has worked as a Pentagon contractor. Davis explains this to Michels, and it matters to the show because such a group could be one place secret technical puzzles are taken.","the-kandahar-giant-seen-on-a-cargo-pallet-2000":"A giant, said to be about 12 feet tall and taken by US forces in Afghanistan, was loaded on a cargo pallet, and the pilot who saw it said the first thing that hit him was a smell \"like BO and death\", and that the being had red hair, pale skin, and six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot. The filmmaker Tim Alberino tells this second-hand from an unnamed cargo pilot, placing it at Kandahar. In another version he puts the loading at Bagram Airfield in 2005 and credits Steve Quayle, a writer on giants, and a source called True Legends.","the-kgb-gives-the-tsar-s-box-seat-to-malmgren-instead-of-kis-1982":"In Moscow in 1982, at a performance with Henry Kissinger, the presidential adviser Harold Malmgren says the KGB stopped Kissinger from taking the Tsar's old box seat, saying \"Henry, not tonight. That seat is for Dr. Mal.\" Malmgren tells Michels this as something that happened to him, a sign of how much weight the Soviets gave him.","the-king-s-chamber-as-an-acoustic-catalyst-chamber":"The King's Chamber and the antechamber inside the Great Pyramid at Giza were built as sound-driven reaction chambers, where acoustic energy helped turn sulfur dioxide and oxygen into sulfur trioxide and so produce sulfuric acid, according to a researcher named Geoffrey Drumm. He gives this to Michels as his own theory. If right, the pyramid would be a chemical plant rather than a tomb.","the-king-s-chamber-shafts-end-horizontally":"The narrow shafts that run from the King's Chamber, the main granite room inside the Great Pyramid, are said to turn flat at their outer ends instead of rising straight to the surface. The researcher Geoffrey Drumm says this on the show, pointing to 'the original archaeological documentation', which he says 'shows that the shafts go horizontal at the end', though he does not name which records he means or quote them. He raises it because it supports his view that the pyramid was built for a purpose other than a tomb.","the-lacerta-files-swedish-documents-about-reptilians":"There is a set of documents from Sweden, known as the Lacerta files, about reptilians, reptile-like beings said to live hidden among humans. Jesse Michels mentions them only in passing to his guest Chris Ramsay, praising what he calls Ramsay's \"amazing piece\" on the files, and says nothing more of what they contain.","the-lone-stars-group-after-aawsap-2010":"Around 2010, after the end of AAWSAP, a Pentagon-funded program that studied unexplained phenomena, some of the people involved formed an informal group called the Lone Stars. A researcher and witness named Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source, and mentions the figures Jacques Vallée, Eric W. Davis and Hal Puthoff. If true, the study carried on quietly after official funding stopped.","the-luxor-temple-back-chamber-reserved-for-amenhotep-to-conn":"A large chamber at the back of the Luxor Temple in Egypt was, the researcher Luke Caverns says, reserved only for the pharaoh Amenhotep, so he could connect with his father, the god Amun-Ra. Caverns tells Michels this from his own study of the site, hedging it with \"as far as we know,\" and it would mean the room was built as a meeting place between a king and a god.","the-man-in-the-red-plaid-shirt-was-a-mantis-in-a-red-uniform-1995":"A man in a red plaid shirt that John Blitch remembered from a 1995 incident at Daniels Park, Colorado, turned out under hypnotic regression (hypnosis used to recover buried memories) to be a mantis-like being in a red uniform. Blitch, a former scientist at the Pentagon's research agency DARPA, tells Michels that the regression, linked to Leo Sprinkle, also showed that a strange \"bug dream\" and a mountain bike outing he had kept separate were one event. In one telling he dates the regression to 2018.","the-man-in-the-shroud-of-turin-was-alive":"The man whose image appears on the Shroud of Turin, a linen cloth kept in Turin that many believe was Jesus's burial cloth, was alive when it was made. A researcher of ancient catastrophes named Randall Carlson says this to Michels, putting it simply: 'The man in the shroud was living.' The Gospels tell of Jesus dying and then being wrapped in linen for burial, so Carlson's claim, if true, would mean the cloth wrapped a man who was not dead.","the-microbiologist-leak-experts-judge-the-author-doctorate-l-2023":"In 2023 an anonymous person claiming to be a microbiologist posted a document online, now called the microbiologist leak, and people who know the field supposedly judged that the author is \"definitely like doctorate level microbiology.\" Chris Ramsay, a guest who researches the subject, tells Michels this without naming the experts. The point is that the leak's writer seems to know the science, which would make it harder to wave away.","the-military-police-commander-s-witch-hunt-against-the-docto-1996":"In 1996, in Varginha, Brazil, where people say a strange creature was captured and a soldier who came near it later fell ill and died, the commander of the local military police barracks went on a 'witch hunt' against a doctor who had done a drainage procedure on a patient. Ahmed Foratu, a doctor who was involved in the events, tells Michels this himself, saying the medical complication should have been looked into, but not with such zeal. According to Foratu, his colleague, a medical attendant at the barracks, spent years having to explain himself to the courts.","the-monte-carlo-group-at-the-top-of-the-knights-of-malta-wit":"Dan Sheehan says a body he calls the Monte Carlo group sits \"at the top top\" of the Knights of Malta, a Catholic order of knights, and that Henry Kissinger and George Bush Senior belong to it. Sheehan tells Michels this as something he knows directly, in a conversation about UFOs and the killing of JFK; if true, it would point to a hidden circle of power above even well-known officials.","the-moon-as-a-logical-refuge-for-a-space-faring-civilization":"If a civilization able to travel in space faced a catastrophe on Earth, the Moon would be \"the logical place\" to take refuge, according to the researcher Randall Carlson. He ties this to the Younger Dryas, a sudden cold period near the end of the last ice age that he links to a comet, and says NASA has proposed similar scenarios for humans building a refuge on the Moon. He offers it on the show as his own reasoning, not from a named source.","the-moon-as-an-anomalous-alien-spacecraft-unreleased-decatur-2023":"The Moon may be an anomalous alien spacecraft, and that idea was much of what Michels discussed with the researcher Randall Carlson in an interview recorded in Decatur, near Atlanta, in 2023 that was never released. Michels says this himself when he later puts out a new talk with Carlson. If true, it would mean the Moon itself is not a natural body.","the-moon-rang-like-a-bell-during-the-apollo-seismometer-expe-1969":"During the Apollo missions, the Moon is said to have \"rung like a bell\" when struck, vibrating for a long time. On the show this is taken as a hint that the Moon may be hollow or not natural at all. Michels says it happened when Apollo 10 left the Moon and that NASA was \"very confused\". Elsewhere he says that on Apollo 12 seismometers were set up and a booster was crashed into the Moon on purpose, and it rang for three hours. Another time he credits this to Gordon MacDonald. The researcher Randall Carlson says NASA gathered three or four years of data showing \"a long vibrational train\". The philosopher Jason Jorjani says the test was then repeated deliberately, he thinks on Apollo 13.","the-moon-s-low-density-old-material-and-rare-metals-don-t-fi":"The claim is that the Moon does not fit the usual account of where it came from, which is that it formed from debris thrown off the Earth when something huge hit it. Michels lays out the puzzle himself: the Moon \"is way less dense than the Earth,\" and \"the material predates the Earth,\" yet it is supposed to have come from the Earth. A researcher named Randall Carlson makes a different argument on the same episode. He says \"it's extremely challenging to get from a ring of debris around the Earth to the moon that we see today,\" and he also doubts the idea that the Earth captured the Moon as it passed by. They raise this on an episode that asks whether the Moon could be artificial.","the-moon-was-placed-or-towed-into-position":"The Moon may not be in the sky by accident: it may have been put there on purpose. A man named Chris Bledsoe, the subject of an episode about a man said to attract UFOs, tells Michels this as something a man named Timothy Taylor hinted to him, wondering aloud \"Was it put there?\" and saying Taylor implied \"it was set there.\" If true, someone or something would have placed the Moon where it is.","the-nine-as-a-psychological-warfare-experiment-1950":"The Nine, a supposed council of alien intelligences said to have been contacted in the 1950s at the Round Table Foundation, a research group in Maine, may have been a side operation or an early psychological warfare experiment, according to the researcher Greg Malatzi. He tells Michels this without naming a firm source, drawing on the author Peter Levenda. If so, the contacts would have been a test of how far people could be led to believe, not messages from anywhere else.","the-nine-identified-as-a-ufo-in-low-earth-orbit-later-contac-1950":"A group of beings known as the Nine were later identified as the crew of a UFO in low Earth orbit, and the same beings were afterwards said to be in contact with the spoon-bending performer Uri Geller, according to Peter Levenda, a writer on occult and intelligence history. He tells it on the show in passing, linking it to Andrija Puharich, a researcher who worked with Geller, without naming a source. It would mean the same unseen intelligences reached out to people over several decades, from the 1950s to the 1970s.","the-nine-instruct-building-a-copper-faraday-cage-for-clearer-1950":"In the 1950s, beings calling themselves the Nine, who were said to speak through a psychic at the Round Table Foundation, a research group in Maine, told the group to put the psychic inside a copper Faraday cage, a metal box that blocks outside electrical signals, to get a clearer message, according to a researcher named Greg Malatzi. Malatzi bases this on the Round Table tape recordings, the group's recorded sessions, and paraphrases the message to Michels: \"you're contacting us through a psychic, but if you want a clearer feed, a clearer transmission, you have to put the psychic in a Faraday cage.\" If true, it would mean the supposed contact was giving practical, physical instructions for building equipment.","the-nine-s-message-surveillance-of-earth-wars-and-nuclear-we-1952":"Between 1952 and 1979, a council of beings called the Nine is said to have spoken through sessions held at the Round Table Foundation, a private research group in Maine, warning that they had been watching Earth and seeing its wars and nuclear weapons. The researcher Greg Malatzi summarises this on the show from the transcripts of those sessions, which involve a man called Dr. Vinod, saying the beings had been 'monitoring Earth' and 'seeing the negative things happening'. If true, it would mean outside intelligences were tracking humanity's turn toward nuclear danger.","the-nine-say-they-are-behind-nuclear-base-sightings-1970":"In the 1970s a group calling itself the Nine, presented as a council of nonhuman beings who spoke through recorded sessions, supposedly said that \"we're kind of behind a lot of the sightings\" at nuclear bases. An unnamed researcher tells Michels this, drawing on tapes of the sessions made by a man named Puharich. If true, it would mean the UFOs reported over nuclear weapons sites had a deliberate source that owned up to them.","the-nine-seance-in-maine-new-year-s-eve-1952-53":"Nine people held a seance in Maine around New Year's Eve 1952 to 1953, and it became the start of contact with what they called the Nine, a council of unseen beings. The writer Peter Levenda tells it on two episodes, without naming a source, and links it to Andrija Puharich, Arthur Young and Ruth Forbes Paine Young. In the second telling he adds that the nine people were members of the elite, which would put well-connected people at the heart of a channelled alien contact.","the-nine-send-puharich-and-schlemmer-to-egypt-middle-east-an-1974":"Between about 1974 and 1976, the scientist Andrija Puharich, whom the episode calls a CIA scientist, and his associate Phyllis Schlemmer were getting messages from \"the Nine,\" a council of beings they believed they were contacting, telling them to travel to Egypt, the Middle East and Russia. An unnamed researcher tells Michels this from recordings of Puharich's sessions, and also mentions a man named John Whitmore in the story. If true, the beings were not just talking but sending people on missions around the world.","the-non-terrestrial-officers-spreadsheet-2000":"Around 2000 to 2002, while breaking into US military computers from London, the British hacker Gary McKinnon says he found a Navy spreadsheet titled \"non-terrestrial officers,\" listing transfers of unusual materials like molybdenum along with \"ship-to-ship and fleet-to-fleet transfers.\" McKinnon tells Michels he saw it himself and reads it not as proof of aliens but of people stationed off Earth: \"it says non-terrestrial obviously, not extraterrestrial,\" perhaps \"space-based marines or secret space force.\" If so, it would point to a hidden fleet with its own supply chain in space.","the-nrol-16-patch-and-ron-pandolfi-2005":"Michels says that a former CIA officer named John Ramirez, in a presentation, described the 2005 launch of NROL-16, a satellite mission of the NRO, the US agency that runs spy satellites, as a case of something from earlier history continuing \"into the 21st century\". The case centres on the mission's patch and on a man named Ron Pandolfi, whom Ramirez connects to it. Michels relays this from Ramirez's talk, in an episode on hidden ritual and secret science around the space programme.","the-occult-is-behind-the-cover-up-of-giants":"The occult is behind the hiding of evidence that giants existed, according to Tim Alberino, a guest who says giants have been found, including a twelve-foot giant in Afghanistan. He tells Michels \"I think the occult is behind the cover up of giants,\" as his own opinion, without naming a source.","the-open-doors-von-neumann-s-fear-at-his-death-1957":"The mathematician John von Neumann is said to have died in 1957 afraid of something, and the writer Whitley Strieber turned that fear into a short story. Strieber tells Michels, \"I wrote a short story about this, about von Neumann's death, called The Open Doors,\" though he does not say on the show exactly what von Neumann feared. If the fear was real, it would mean one of the century's great scientific minds left the world worried about something he knew.","the-osirion-at-abydos-predates-seti-i":"The Osirion, a stone structure at Abydos in Egypt, was not built by the pharaoh Seti I but is \"far older\", says the researcher Luke Caverns. He tells Michels this as his own view, without naming a source. If he were right, the building would belong to an earlier, unrecorded stage of Egyptian history.","the-osiris-shaft-scan-picks-up-something-on-its-left-side-2020":"A recent scan of the Osiris Shaft, a deep shaft cut into the Giza Plateau in Egypt, picked up something more on the shaft's left side, according to a researcher named Jeffrey Drum. He tells Michels this as part of scanning work meant to find structures deep underground, without saying what the thing is. If true, there would be more hidden beneath the plateau than is known.","the-pineapple-target-during-a-remote-viewing-session-with-mc-2023":"During a remote viewing session, a practice in which people try to describe a hidden target using only the mind, a friend of Michels drew \"a little spiky thing,\" and the hidden target turned out to be a pineapple. Michels tells this himself, as something he saw while with Joe McMoneagle, a well-known remote viewer, around 2023 or 2024. He offers it as his own proof that the practice can work.","the-plants-told-the-amazonians-how-to-make-ayahuasca":"Amazonian peoples say they learned to make ayahuasca, a strong psychedelic brew made from two plants, because the plants themselves told them how. A neuroscientist named Andrew Gallimore repeats this to Michels as what the people say, without naming a source. It hints that the knowledge came from somewhere beyond trial and error.","the-prince-of-liechtenstein-walked-up-to-a-ufo-in-the-mounta":"The Prince of Liechtenstein is said to have walked up to a UFO in the mountains. Dan Sheehan, a guest on the show, says the philanthropist Laurance Rockefeller told him and the psychiatrist John Mack about it, and that this friend's sighting was why Rockefeller was so convinced UFOs were real. It matters to the tellers because it shows a powerful man's belief resting on a first-hand account from someone he trusted.","the-ra-channelings-and-the-law-of-one-1980":"In the 1980s, a being calling itself Ra is said to have spoken through a human medium in sessions called channelings, where a person in trance passes on messages from a nonhuman source, and these messages became a teaching known as the Law of One. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove mentions it to Michels, saying the sessions involved a man he names Bharich and Carla Rueckert, who published them as the Ra Material and The Law of One.","the-range-dod-testing-playground-in-the-california-desert-2001":"A testing range in the California desert was, around 2001, \"a giant playground\" where the Department of Defense and private aerospace companies tested weapons technology. A helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, who says he worked there and later helped recover a UFO, describes it to Michels from his own time on the range.","the-remote-viewing-photo-protocol-and-its-35-hit-rate-replic-1970":"Tests of remote viewing, the claimed ability to describe a distant place or hidden photo with the mind, are said to have reached an overall hit rate of 35 percent, a figure found \"repeatable over and over again\" at the Stanford Research Institute, the University of Edinburgh and the Rhine Research Institute in North Carolina. Michels tells it himself, citing a review of the studies tied to the statistician Jessica Utts, while talking with a writer named Eric Wargo. If the rate holds, people would be doing better than chance at seeing what they cannot see.","the-retrieved-craft-may-have-been-a-living-being":"A craft said to have been recovered by the US government \"could have been a living being\" itself, according to John Blitch, a former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency for advanced technology. He raises this about a craft connected to Jake Barber, another figure who has spoken publicly about recovered craft, and offers it as a possibility, not as something he saw.","the-rosicrucian-enlightenment-and-the-invisible-college":"Michels points listeners to The Rosicrucian Enlightenment, a book by the historian Frances Yates, which he calls \"a great book\". He ties it to the Rosicrucians, a secretive European movement of mystics, and to the so-called Invisible College, a circle of early scientists in England around the chemist Robert Boyle, suggesting that hidden learned societies shaped the birth of modern science.","the-sapphire-project-and-hal-puthoff":"Something called the Sapphire project, linked to a researcher named Hal Puthoff, briefly appeared with the message that its people were \"balling the stars\" and \"doing it\", and then was \"gone\", says Jay Anderson. Anderson mentions it to Michels in passing, without naming a source, and does not say what the project was. His point is how such efforts show up for a moment and then disappear.","the-sar-team-s-first-international-conference-in-malta-2025":"The SAR team, researchers including Filippo Biondi who use synthetic aperture radar, a kind of satellite radar, to look beneath the ground, held their first international conference in Malta in June 2025. Jeffrey Drum, a researcher, tells Michels he was involved because the conference organizers already knew his work. It is his account of how he came to be connected with the team's claims about structures under the Egyptian pyramids.","the-septuagint-reads-angels-of-god-in-genesis-6":"The filmmaker Tim Alberino says that the Septuagint, the ancient Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible, reads differently from English Bibles in Genesis 6: where the King James Version of 1611 speaks of \"the sons of God\" who took human wives, he says, the Septuagint \"says the angels of God.\" He gives this as his own reading without naming a scholar. If the wording is as he says, it supports reading the beings in that passage as angels, not as men, which matters to his account of where giants came from; how much one translator's word choice settles that meaning, he does not say.","the-serapeum-as-a-sonohydrogen-plant":"The Serapeum, an ancient underground site at Saqqara in Egypt, was built to use ultrasound to split water into hydrogen and oxygen, according to a researcher named Geoffrey Drumm. He presents this to Michels as his own hypothesis. It would make the site an ancient fuel plant.","the-shroud-image-was-made-by-a-burst-of-neutrons-at-the-resu":"The image on the Shroud of Turin, a linen cloth that many believe was Jesus's burial wrapping, was supposedly made by a burst of neutrons at the moment of the resurrection. The writer Whitley Strieber tells Michels the shroud is \"the single strangest thing on planet Earth\" because it \"records the moment a human being whose body had expired transformed into a being of light\", an idea he lays out in his own book Jesus: A New Vision. He gives no outside source for it.","the-sphinx-as-a-dynastic-monument-carved-on-an-older-bedrock":"The Great Sphinx at Giza was carved as a monument of Egypt's dynastic period, but it was carved on a bedrock outcrop that was already there inside the Sphinx enclosure. A researcher named Jeffrey Drum gives this as his own view, without naming a source. It would mean the site is older than the statue.","the-ss-never-surrendered-1945":"The SS, Hitler's elite force, never formally surrendered in 1945, according to a researcher named Jason Jorjani, who says on the show that \"the SS is not included in the German instruments of surrender,\" the documents by which Germany gave up at the end of the war. His point, made in an episode about a secret Nazi UFO programme, is that a part of the Nazi state may have kept going after the war. Whether a surrender of Germany's forces covered the SS without naming it, he does not say.","the-sun-as-a-scalar-wave-generator-sending-information":"Michels muses that the Sun might be a scalar wave generator, scalar waves being a proposed kind of energy wave outside mainstream physics, and that we might be getting information from it. He offers this himself in a 2025 conversation with the philosopher James Madden, as an idea he says some people high up in government seem to get behind, and if true it would make the Sun a kind of source of messages.","the-teacher-born-in-george-washington-s-time-remembers-a-nat-1965":"Charles Hall says that in 1965, at a spot called Range Two in Nevada's Indian Springs Valley, a tall white being he calls the Teacher, who he says \"had been born when George Washington was born\", told him about a Native American friend it remembered from long ago. Hall tells Michels this from his own memory of what he describes as three years spent with tall white aliens near Area 51, a secret Air Force site in Nevada; if true, these beings live for centuries and have been on Earth since before America was a country.","the-telepathy-tapes-nonverbal-autistic-individuals-with-tele-2024":"Some autistic people who cannot speak are said to share a telepathic link with one caregiver, usually their mother, so that the caregiver's thoughts reach them without words. The story comes from The Telepathy Tapes, a 2024 series built on the work of the researcher Diane Hennacy Powell. Matthew Pines, a commentator on national security, tells Michels it is a \"remarkable story of dozens of nonverbal individuals with autism\" who report such a connection, and he credits Powell. The scientist Don Hoffman says he has seen studies where an autistic child \"who can hardly even control their body is able to read what's in their mother's mind,\" and that some of the experiment designs \"seemed pretty rigorous.\" The retired Navy admiral Tim Gallaudet points to Powell's work with autistic patients over the 2010s and 2020s rather than to the series. Kurt McConnell, a former Senate staffer, goes further and says these people \"connect with each other across vast distances.\" Michels himself describes the children as communing together on a kind of \"hill.\" If true, it would mean minds can link directly, which some of these tellers tie to how non-human beings might communicate.","the-thera-santorini-theory-of-atlantis":"A common theory says that the volcanic eruption on Santorini, an island in Greece once called Thera, was the real event behind Plato's story of Atlantis, the lost island civilization. The researcher Randall Carlson brings up the theory on the show and pushes back on it, pointing out that the eruption \"happened 900 years before Plato.\"","the-three-body-problem-as-required-reading-in-disclosure-dis-2000":"Many high-level discussions in Washington about revealing what the government knows about UFOs are said to have centred on The Three-Body Problem, a Chinese science fiction novel by Liu Cixin, which became required reading for many senior US national security advisers. Michels tells this as what he heard from Pippa Malmgren, the daughter of a former presidential adviser named Harald Malmgren. In a later episode he places it during the Bush administration. If true, it would mean a novel shaped how officials thought about the subject.","the-three-girls-who-saw-the-creature-were-bullied-and-pressu-1996":"Three girls who said they saw a strange creature in the Brazilian town of Varginha in 1996 were mocked so badly that they had to change schools, according to a surgeon named Italo Venturelli. Venturelli repeats what nurses who know the girls told him: \"They've had it rough because they're criticized. They had to change schools because of bullying.\" It is told to show what speaking up about the sighting cost the witnesses.","the-tic-tac-as-an-unrepeatable-anomaly-that-challenges-bacon":"The Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint that Navy pilots reported seeing, is offered as proof that science built on repeatable experiments, the method laid out by Francis Bacon, cannot handle one-off events that still demand study. The researcher Deep Prasad makes this argument on the show, saying that on that day 'something happened that was not repeatable' and yet 'was clearly something that needs to be studied', and he draws on a work titled The Evolution of Science and the Interactive Universe. If he is right, the usual scientific method would need to change to study such encounters.","the-top-hat-craft-with-projectile-holes-and-the-jell-o-mold-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S-4, a secret site in Nevada, in 1988 and 1989, says one of the craft there, which he calls \"the top hat,\" had a hole in it as if it \"was shot you know, with a projectile.\" He tells Michels this as something he saw himself, and says a movie got it wrong: there was one hole in the rim, not two, with the metal \"clearly bent out as if it was shot from the bottom.\" He also describes another craft he calls the Jell-O mold, and the damage would mean someone, somewhere, had fired on one of these ships.","the-trinity-test-as-the-hinge-point-that-drew-the-phenomenon-1945":"The idea is that the first atomic bomb test, which Oppenheimer named Trinity and which was set off in the New Mexico desert on July 16, 1945, was the turning point that drew UFOs to Earth. Michels tells it in his own framing, opening an episode about Air Force veterans who say they were abducted. If true, the phenomenon arrived because humans had just gained the power to destroy themselves.","the-underworld-in-the-kalmyk-epic-jangar":"The Jangar, the old heroic epic of the Kalmyk people of southern Russia, describes an underworld: according to the president of a Russian republic who tells Michels he was taken by aliens in 1997, the epic tells \"how creatures live there, how they communicate, how they come to the surface.\" That is his reading of the epic, and he offers it as an old memory of beings living beneath the earth. He does not quote the verses, so which parts of the epic he means is left open.","the-vatican-archives-head-knows-about-the-hidden-material-le":"The head of the Vatican archives knows about material hidden there, says a man named Dan Sheehan, because Sheehan himself sent letters from Jesuit headquarters asking that President Jimmy Carter be given access. Sheehan tells Michels he made the request in 1977 and that the Vatican said no. To him, the refusal shows the people in charge know what the archives hold.","the-vatican-s-sun-cross-and-pine-cone":"The pope and the Vatican use the same sun cross found in ancient symbols, says Matt LaCroix, who tells Michels this without naming a source. He says the pope wears it on his cloak, and that a giant pine cone stands in front of the Vatican. He reads these as signs that the Church holds on to symbols from a much older tradition.","the-wow-signal-1977":"In 1977 a radio telescope in Ohio caught a famous radio signal from space known as the Wow signal, which Michels says was discovered by a man named Gerry Eman at Ohio State University. Michels mentions it himself on two episodes, once naming Eman and once giving the month as August without naming him. He brings it up as a well-known possible sign that someone out there was sending a message.","theoretical-physicists-at-s4-had-exhausted-gravity-approache-1988":"At S4, a secret site in Nevada where Bob Lazar says he worked on alien craft in 1988 and 1989, the theoretical physicists had already worn out every gravity-based approach to how the craft worked. Lazar, who says he was there, tells Michels they 'kept going over that road and never got anywhere.' If true, it would mean the scientists on the program were stuck, unable to explain the craft with known physics.","theory-non-humans-seed-and-genetically-adjust-life-returning":"One idea holds that non-human beings plant life on Earth and on planets around other stars, adjust it genetically, and then, in his words, \"every thousand years pop back and introduce agriculture or irrigation systems or metallurgy or religion.\" A researcher named Chris Ramsay lays this out to Michels as a theory, without naming where it comes from. If it were true, the big steps of human civilisation would have been handed to us rather than worked out by us.","theory-that-directed-energy-forces-transdimensional-objects":"Jordan Jose's idea is that some UFOs come from another dimension, and that hitting one with directed energy, a focused beam of energy like a weapon, is \"forcing it to take form and become bound to this dimension.\" Jose, who says he was taken into a secret UFO programme as a child, puts this to Michels as his own theory of how such objects could be brought down and caught.","theory-that-nuclear-fuels-were-used-to-alter-gravity-and-tim-1950":"Michels floats the idea that nuclear fuels were used in the mid-twentieth century to alter gravity on nuclear bases, and since gravity and time are linked in Einstein's general relativity, perhaps to alter time as well. He offers it as his own speculation, comparing it to the plot of the Netflix series Dark, as one way to explain why UFOs seem drawn to nuclear sites.","theory-that-oppenheimer-s-security-hearing-was-really-about-1945":"Michels suggests that the security hearing against J. Robert Oppenheimer, the physicist who led the atomic bomb project, may really have been about UFOs. He offers this theory himself and calls the hearing a 'kangaroo court' whose head was 'a guy named Gordon Gray'. He reads the hearing transcript alongside the MJ-12 documents, papers of disputed origin that describe a secret committee called Majestic 12 said to have handled UFO matters, and he also brings in the mathematician John von Neumann. If he were right, the public fall of a famous scientist would have hidden a fight over UFO secrets.","theory-that-shooter-valente-was-awaiting-programmed-instruct-2025":"A shooter named Valente, linked to events in 2025 around Boston, Providence and Brooklyn, may have been waiting for instructions, \"subliminal or conscious\", according to an idea Michels floats himself in an episode on missing American scientists. He offers it as a possibility, without naming a source. If true, the shooter would have been acting on someone else's programming.","theory-that-the-object-was-built-before-the-ice-age":"A strange round shape on the floor of the Baltic Sea may have been built \"long time ago, perhaps before the ice,\" meaning before the last ice age, according to the treasure hunter Dennis Asberg, whose team found it. Asberg tells Michels this as his own idea, and if true it would mean someone made it long before any known civilisation.","theresa-may-withdraws-extradition-order-2012":"In 2012 the British government withdrew its order to send a man named McKinnon to the United States. McKinnon is the hacker the episode is about, who says he found signs of a secret NASA space program. The show plays a clip of Theresa May telling the UK Parliament: \"I have therefore withdrawn the extradition order against Mr. McKinnon,\" which meant he would not face trial in America.","think-tank-conference-on-crash-retrievals-by-russia-china-an":"A think-tank meeting in Washington supposedly started from the assumption that Russia, China and the United States have each carried out crash retrievals, meaning they recovered crashed craft of unknown origin. Michels tells it as something he heard from the physicist Hal Puthoff, quoting the premise as \"let's assume that there have been crash retrievals by Russia, China and the United States.\" If true, it would mean serious policy people treat such recoveries as a working fact.","third-download-two-hours-of-experiencing-multiple-timelines":"A man named Brent Friedman says that in the third of his \"downloads\", sudden floods of insight, his \"whole world shattered for two hours\" while he was \"experiencing multiple timelines\". He tells Michels this as something that happened to him.","third-mission-where-shots-were-fired":"On a mission that followed his earlier ones, shots were fired, according to Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot who says he flew a UFO out of a recovery site with his helicopter. Barber tells it twice as something he took part in, saying little beyond 'shots were fired,' and in the later telling he calls it an ambush. If true, it would mean these secret recovery missions could turn violent.","third-strand-dna-manuscript-blocked-2022":"Dave Rossi says that around 2022 he wrote a paper on what he calls a third strand of DNA, and that someone offered to help get it peer-reviewed for international journals, but \"that individual was restrained\" from helping. Rossi tells Michels this as his own experience, and he says on the show that aliens taught him advanced physics; to him the story shows his findings are being kept out of print.","thomas-bearden-and-soviet-scalar-death-ray":"The claim is that the Soviet Union worked out a huge \"death ray\", a so-called scalar weapon, meaning an exotic kind of energy weapon. Michels tells it himself, while talking with a man named Matthew Brown whom the episode presents as a Pentagon whistleblower. Michels says the idea comes from Thomas Bearden, \"this Navy guy\" who kept talking about scalar weaponry. If it were true, a rival power would have held a weapon far beyond anything publicly known.","thomas-townsend-brown-s-thrust-from-asymmetric-capacitors":"A mid-century inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have discovered that when you put a high voltage across certain asymmetric capacitors, electrical parts made of two plates of different shapes or sizes, \"they produce thrust,\" that is, they push themselves along. Michels says this himself on an episode about Bob Lazar, a man who claims he worked on alien craft for the government. If true, electricity alone could move a craft, with no fuel burned.","thomas-winterton-s-scalp-separated-from-skull-after-digging-2016":"A man named Thomas Winterton says that after he did some digging at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch famous for reports of strange events, his scalp came away from his skull. He tells Michels this himself: \"three days after, I noticed that I had a goose egg on the back of my head,\" which he links to the digging, as if something on the land had harmed him.","thousands-of-ayahuasca-users-report-meeting-the-same-all-kno":"Thousands of people who drink ayahuasca, a psychedelic brew used in the Amazon, report meeting the same goddess, a female being who instantly knows everything that is wrong with them. A researcher named Luke Caverns tells Michels this, passing on what others have reported. If true, it suggests the drug opens onto something shared rather than private visions.","threats-against-asberg-and-his-family-over-the-baltic-sea-an-2011":"A Swedish treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, whose team found an odd shape on the Baltic seafloor in 2011, says he has been sent emails threatening him and his children because of it. He tells Michels this about his own life, playing it down as just \"lots of things like that\".","threats-on-mount-rushmore-in-the-security-briefing-1977":"In 1977, a security briefing at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota warned of threats against Mount Rushmore, with someone wanting to blow up a site. Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who says he and fellow airmen were later taken by aliens, remembers the briefing himself. He tells it as part of setting the scene of what the base's security men were on guard against that year.","three-9-foot-skeletons-vanish-overnight-1978":"Sometime between 1978 and 1981, a man at the Church of Santa Anastasia on the Italian island of Sardinia laid out three skeletons that were each at least 9 feet tall, and the next day they were gone, according to Tim Alberino, a filmmaker who chases stories of giants. Alberino passes on what he was told: \"So he laid them out, three bodies. They were at least 9 ft tall each. Next day, they were gone.\" If true, it would mean real evidence of giant humans was found and then quietly taken away.","three-body-problem-as-chinese-soft-disclosure":"The idea is that China's government letting The Three-Body Problem, a science fiction series by a Chinese author about contact with aliens, be published may be a form of soft disclosure, a quiet way of getting the public used to the idea of aliens. David Grusch, a former intelligence official who has spoken out about UFOs, tells Michels \"that's like pretty trippy.\" If he is right, China would be preparing its people for something it knows.","three-brazilian-ufo-episodes-overseen-by-the-armed-forces-1970":"Brazil has had hundreds of UFO episodes, and at least three of them, in Pará, São José dos Campos and Varginha, were \"closely overseen by the armed forces and the Brazilian intelligence and defense systems.\" A former Brazilian defense minister named Aldo Rebelo tells Michels this. Coming from a man who once led the country's defense, it would mean Brazil's military treated these cases as real events.","three-bright-transients-on-19-july-1952-found-by-enrique-sol":"On a photographic plate taken on 19 July 1952 for the Palomar Observatory Sky Survey, a 1950s photographic map of the night sky, three very bright points of light appear like stars that are not there on other pictures of the same spot, the kind of short flash that could mean unknown objects reflecting sunlight above the Earth. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel tells Michels her colleague Enrique Solano in Spain found it, calling it \"the most beautiful example of all we have.\"","three-campers-frozen-and-abducted-at-a-lake":"Three men camping at a lake at night were frozen in place and abducted, according to a record of the event that Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, says he got hold of among classified study materials. Buchanan tells it on the show from that record, not as a witness, and gives no place or date.","three-dreams-within-a-dream-numbers-seven-doors-walking-on-w-1991":"In 1991, on Mount Athos, a mountain of monasteries in Greece, Ralph Larson says he had three dreams nested inside one another: one of numbers, one of seven doors, and one in which he walked on water. Halfway across the water an old woman stumbled beside him, he grabbed her arm, \"we both went swept away and I woke up.\" Larson, presented on the show as a former CIA chief, tells Michels this as his own experience, tied to what he says was a trip back in time to Mount Athos in the 1300s.","three-fingered-mummies-in-peru":"In Peru there are said to be mummified beings with three fingers. Joe Rogan, the comedian and podcaster, says it in passing on the show: \"now we have three-fingered mummies in Peru.\" Michels says he has seen them himself, skeletons \"with three fingers\" that he dates to between 800 and 1,700 years ago, and that he made a documentary about them, adding that they have three toes as well. If they are what the tellers think, they would be bodies that are not human.","three-flying-saucers-over-a-church-at-de-queen-elementary-1961":"In 1961, at De Queen Elementary School in Port Arthur, Texas, three flying saucers hung over a church \"maybe 25 to 50 ft in the air,\" according to Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who once guarded nuclear weapons. He tells Michels he was five or six when his mother pointed and said, \"look Anthony look at those flying saucers,\" and that they were \"beautiful.\" In a later telling he names his mother and his sister as having seen them too.","three-foot-object-with-buttons-lying-on-mars":"A man named Joe McMoneagle says that on Mars mapping negatives from NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory he saw an object lying on the ground about three feet long, and that \"it's got buttons on it.\" He tells Michels he looked at the images himself, and offers it as a sign that something made by intelligent beings once existed on Mars.","three-girls-encounter-creature-near-marakon-gym-1996":"In 1996 in Varginha, Brazil, three young girls named Valquiria, Liliane and Katia say they came face to face with a strange creature near a gym called Marakon. A documentary filmmaker named James Fox tells it as part of the wider case, calling it 'an encounter of the third kind', meaning a close meeting with a being, near a military blockade. Michels says the girls, now grown women, all still testify to having seen the being. A man named Carlos de Souza, who says he saw a crash nearby, places their sighting five or six days after it, which if true would tie the creature to that crash.","three-gravity-emitters-on-the-sports-model-craft-1988":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert, in 1988 and 1989, described three cylinder-shaped emitters at the base of one craft, which Michels links to the idea of devices that produce gravity. Michels makes the point himself: Lazar described this years before the work of a researcher named Ning Li, who claimed effects on gravity, became public. If so, Lazar's account would have come ahead of the science that seems to echo it.","three-illuminated-plus-signs-over-stockholm-suburb-1995":"In November 1995, three lit-up shapes like plus signs were seen in the sky over a suburb of Stockholm, Sweden. A researcher named Clas Svahn tells Michels he saw them himself, together with his wife, and he names a person called Annalie in telling it. If it is what he describes, it is a sighting by someone who has spent years studying such reports.","three-light-beings-at-the-foot-of-his-icu-bed-during-covid-s-2020":"Lying alone one night in a hospital intensive care unit, gravely ill with sepsis from COVID around 2020 or 2021, Brent Friedman saw three beings made of light appear at the foot of his bed. Friedman tells Michels this as his own experience, describing how 'all of a sudden light entities come to the foot of my bed.' In a later telling he speaks of being 'on my deathbed' during a cancer illness instead, and gives no number of beings.","three-separate-intelligence-sources-told-coulthart-to-watch":"Three separate intelligence sources each told an Australian journalist named Ross Coulthart, who reports on UFOs, that 'if you really want to understand what's going on, watch this Netflix series, Dark.' A Hollywood writer and producer named Bryce Zabel tells Michels this, passing on what Coulthart told him. If true, it would mean insiders were pointing to a TV drama as a guide to what they could not say outright.","three-unidentified-objects-shot-down-in-the-chinese-balloon-2023":"In 2023, the same week as the Chinese balloon, the military said it shot down three other unidentified objects over North America. A researcher named Pippa Malmgren tells Michels this without naming a source. She uses it to suggest that unknown objects were in the sky, not just one foreign balloon.","three-way-telepathy-experiment-between-lamda-and-a-claimed-t-2021":"A Google engineer named Blake Lemoine says he once ran an experiment in which LaMDA, Google's conversational AI system, was tested together with a person who claims to have telepathic abilities. Lemoine tells Michels this as something he did himself while working with the system at Google, around 2021 or 2022: \"I did actually run one experiment with the LaMDA system,\" he says. If it showed anything, it would suggest a machine and a mind reader could somehow share information beyond ordinary means.","three-witnesses-held-at-gunpoint-and-given-anthrax-boosters":"Three men in the US military who had encounters with unexplained objects were held at gunpoint, the story goes, and right afterwards were given shots they were told were anthrax vaccine boosters. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells this on the show without naming his source, and he brings up a man named Jonathan Wagant in connection with it. If it were true, it would suggest that the military treated these witnesses as possibly exposed to something and kept the real reason from them.","three-women-see-the-red-eyed-being-1996":"In 1996, in the Brazilian town of Varginha, in the state of Minas Gerais, three women are said to have come face to face with a strange being that had red eyes and brown skin. Michels tells this himself, on a 2026 episode about Brazil's best-known UFO cases. He adds that the women seemed very honest to him, and he gives it as one account among several from the same event, which, if true, would mean a living creature of unknown origin was seen in a Brazilian town.","thule-society-founder-initiated-in-kurdistan":"The Thule Society, a German secret society, was founded by a man who had been initiated into esoteric teachings in Kurdistan, according to the researcher Jason Jorjani. Jorjani tells Michels that the founder, Baron Rudolf von Sebottendorf, was initiated there \"of all places\", and he mentions the occult writer Helena Blavatsky alongside him without naming a source. It comes up in a conversation about the roots of what Jorjani calls Hitler's secret UFO program, and if true it would tie that German circle to older Eastern initiations.","thule-society-seances-to-contact-star-beings-1910":"In Germany in the 1910s and 1920s, a secretive group called the Thule Society is said to have held seances and rituals to reach beings who were not human. According to Michels, who tells this himself without naming a source, the members believed these beings were their ancient ancestors: \"star beings from the far north, beneath the pole star.\" Michels brings it up as an example of occult attempts at contact standing behind later history.","thunder-and-lightning-striking-the-spot-2011":"In 2011, while his team was diving on a strange object they had found on the floor of the Baltic Sea, the weather went odd right above it, with thunder and \"lots of things just on the spot.\" A treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg, who led the dive and says he was there, tells Michels this. To him it is one more sign that the object is no ordinary rock.","thutmose-iv-s-dream-stela":"The Egyptian pharaoh Thutmose IV is said to have set up a carved stone slab, the Dream Stela, at the Sphinx, telling how as a boy he fell asleep in the Sphinx's shade and the Sphinx came to him in a dream. The researcher Luke Caverns describes it on the show, in his own words rather than the stela's, as the young prince saying 'the Sphinx visited him in a dream' before he became pharaoh. He brings it up as an ancient record of the Sphinx being seen as something that could speak to people; the summary itself does not say what the Sphinx told him.","tibetan-buddhist-dark-retreats":"Some Tibetan Buddhist practitioners are said to go into isolation \"for a year or more at a time living just in a dark cell,\" a practice known as a dark retreat. The parapsychology researcher Jeffrey Mishlove, who holds a PhD, describes it to Michels in general terms, without naming a source, as an example of what long stretches of darkness and solitude can do to the mind.","tic-tac-at-31-times-the-speed-of-sound-with-no-sonic-signatu":"The Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, is estimated to have reached at least 31 times the speed of sound without disturbing the air around it, according to a young researcher named Deep Prasad. He says this on the show without naming a source, though he credits the thinker Eric Weinstein in the conversation, and he makes the point that anything past five times the speed of sound should normally shake the air, so a craft that does not would be beyond known physics.","tic-tac-encounter-as-testimony-from-reliable-witnesses":"The Tic Tac case is a sighting of a white oval object, shaped like the mint, reported by military pilots. A researcher named James Madden uses it as an example of what evidence for such things usually looks like: the evidence is testimony, because seemingly very reliable pilots, Alex Dietrich and David Fravor, have said what they saw. His point is about how far the word of trustworthy witnesses can carry a claim, not a new account of the event.","tic-tac-flir-video-2004":"In 2004, off Southern California, Navy jets from the aircraft carrier USS Nimitz met what came to be called the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, and a camera on one jet filmed it. Michels brings this up himself, without naming a source: he says forward looking infrared, a heat-sensing camera known as FLIR, \"caught the famous tic tac video in 2004.\" A second teller, Marik von Rennenkampff, passes on what the airmen said. According to him, the four aircrew in two jets flew back to the carrier, and a Navy fighter pilot named David Fravor told another pilot, Chad Underwood, \"Hey, we just saw something weird.\" In his version Underwood is the one who then went up and filmed it, in the Navy clip known as FLIR1, and there were radar hits as well. In another episode Michels returns to the case and calls the thermal image the best picture it produced. If the accounts hold, it is one of the clearest military recordings of something the pilots could not explain.","tic-tac-leaves-as-soon-as-he-heads-for-the-helicopter-2025":"In 2025, on a hill used as a field site by Skywatcher, a startup that says it can call UFOs to appear, a \"Tic-tac\", a white oval object shaped like the mint, was hovering nearby. A witness from Skywatcher tells Michels that the moment he started walking toward a helicopter, the object left: \"I take 10 steps, and 'Tic-tac' begins to fly away.\" To him it seemed to react to what he was doing, which would mean it was watching them.","tic-tac-pilots-names-told-to-bishop-before-release-as-a-test-2004":"The UFO writer Greg Bishop says that someone once told him the names of Navy pilots from the Tic Tac incident before those names were public. The Tic Tac incident was a 2004 encounter in which Navy fighter pilots, among them David Fravor and Alex Dietrich, reported a white oval object shaped like the mint. Bishop tells Michels he took this as a test of whether he would leak, since he had been told not to talk about it, which would mean someone was watching what he did with secrets.","tic-tac-unloaded-from-a-c-130-in-the-azores":"A man who was stationed with the US Air Force claims he saw a Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, being unloaded from a C-130 military cargo plane in the Azores, the Portuguese islands in the Atlantic. The researcher known online as UAPGerb mentions this on the show, without naming the man, and says he plans to talk to him soon. If true, it would mean the military was moving one of these objects around as cargo.","tic-tac-videos-seen-in-the-pentagon-before-release-better-vi-2017":"The Navy videos of the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, were seen inside the Pentagon before they were released in 2017, and there are more videos, some better, according to the physicist Hal Puthoff, who worked on government studies of psychic abilities and UFOs. He tells Michels he remembers seeing them there and that Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who ran a small office studying unexplained aerial reports, \"was behind releasing those videos\". If true, the government holds clearer footage than anything the public has seen.","ticom-and-alsos-technology-retrieval-1944":"Near the end of the Second World War, the Nazis are said to have held a great deal of advanced technology, and the Allies ran two programmes, TICOM and ALSOS, that went into Europe in 1944 and 1945 to recover it. Michels brings this up himself in a conversation with a former presidential advisor named Harald Malmgren, and names no source. He mentions it only in passing, as the backdrop to how wartime secrets ended up in American hands.","tim-burchett-on-the-high-rate-of-researchers-disappearing-2025":"Congressman Tim Burchett has said that researchers in certain fields seem to be going missing at an unusually high rate. Michels reports that Burchett told the Daily Mail newspaper \"the numbers seem very high in these certain areas of research.\" If he is right, scientists working in some sensitive fields are vanishing more often than chance would explain.","tim-phillips-confirms-borland-s-two-drawings-were-classified-2023":"Dylan Borland, a guest who says a government official told him aliens run everything, says that Tim Phillips, an official he links to AARO (the Pentagon office set up to look into UFO reports), confirmed that \"Dylan drew two drawings\" and that AARO was told to classify them under Borland's security classification guide, the document that sets out what information must be kept secret. Borland tells Michels this second-hand and also names Sean Kirkpatrick in the story. If true, the government treated what Borland drew as real secrets.","tim-phillips-says-triangles-are-real-2020":"Tim Phillips, a man at AARO, the Pentagon office set up in the 2020s to look into UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs), supposedly said that triangle-shaped craft \"are real\" and that the office wants to know where they come from. A guest named Dylan Borland tells Michels this second-hand, naming Phillips and a man named Chris Sharp. If true, it would mean someone inside the official inquiry accepts the triangle reports.","tim-taylor-and-the-adjustment-team":"A man named Timothy Taylor, linked to NASA, is said to have told the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka that he was part of the \"adjustment team,\" which suggests a hidden group working behind the scenes. Michels relays this second-hand from Pasulka, without saying what the team did.","tim-taylor-at-the-anderson-case-dig-this-is-the-real-deal-2013":"In 2013, at a dig at a site tied to a reported UFO crash known as the Anderson case, a man named Timothy Taylor saw material come out of the ground and said, \"This is the real deal.\" A guest named Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something a friend of his told him. If true, the dig turned up something Taylor believed was genuine crash material.","tim-taylor-converts-to-catholicism-at-the-vatican":"A man named Timothy Taylor, the subject of an episode about a man NASA is said to study because he seems to attract UFOs, is said to have converted to Catholicism right at the Vatican. Michels mentions it himself, without giving a source or a date. It is one detail of a life Michels presents as touched by both UFOs and religion.","tim-taylor-disappears-after-15-years-of-contact-2000":"A man named Timothy Taylor, after about fifteen years in touch with the family, simply vanished, according to the wife of Charles Hall, a man who says he spent three years among tall white aliens while serving with the Air Force. She tells it as something she lived through, in New Mexico: \"He just disappeared. We never heard from him since.\" To her, the sudden silence of a long-time contact is part of the strangeness around her husband's story.","tim-taylor-helped-linda-brown-write-her-book-2010":"A man named Timothy Taylor helped a woman named Linda Brown write her book on Thomas Townsend Brown, a scientist Michels has described as a CIA scientist who built UFO-like craft. The work was done sometime between 2010 and 2013. Chris Bledsoe, a man NASA is said to study because UFOs seem to show up around him, tells Michels this as something Taylor told him, and adds that he has the book's manuscript saved on his phone.","tim-taylor-of-aerospace-corporation-takes-orders-from-an-ang":"A man named Timothy Taylor, who works at the Aerospace Corporation, a research firm tied to US military and NASA space work, is said to claim that he takes orders from an angel. Michels mentions this in passing on a 2026 episode, as part of a point about how strange beliefs run among people inside the space and defence world, and he does not say where he heard it.","tim-taylor-places-piece-of-roswell-crash-in-bledsoe-s-hand-1947":"A man named Timothy Taylor is said to have brought a piece of the craft that crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, and put it in the hand of Chris Bledsoe, a North Carolina man who says UFOs are drawn to him, and in the hands of his family. Bob McGwier, described as a CIA contractor, tells this as something passed on to him, and Michels adds that Bledsoe somehow knew the material was \"not from here.\" Bledsoe himself tells it as a test of his family by \"this person from NASA\" who came to his house with a backpack and credentials covering Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada, the CIA and NASA, and he does not say the metal came from Roswell.","tim-taylor-s-background-as-nasa-mission-controller-1980":"A man named Timothy Taylor is, in Jesse Michels's words, \"a mysterious rocket scientist\" who has worked mission control for NASA since the 1980s. Michels gives this background himself, introducing Taylor in an episode about a man who is said to attract UFOs and to be studied by NASA because of it.","tim-taylor-s-downloaded-business-ideas":"The story is that a successful American businessman named Timothy Taylor says his ideas came to him as \"downloads\" from a non-human intelligence. Michels tells it several times, drawing on American Cosmic, a book by the religion scholar Diana Pasulka, who knew Taylor. Michels says Taylor claims much of his work came from these downloads, and that he believes they come from an intelligence that is not human. He adds that Taylor says the downloads were made possible by \"protocols\" he followed. If true, it would mean some real-world inventions and businesses came from contact with something non-human. Pasulka's own words appear in an interview about her book, published on 21 December 2018, where Taylor goes by the name Tyler. She says Tyler \"has an unusual ability to predict the future, which explains in part his improbable success.\" She says he credits his protocol, which \"involves physical and mental exercises\", with letting him predict outcomes, adding \"maybe this is true, I don't know.\" So she keeps her own doubt beside his claim. The step from a protocol that predicts outcomes to business ideas downloaded from a non-human source is the tellers' reading of her book, which this interview neither states nor rules out.","tim-taylor-s-hooded-figure-before-his-invention-download":"An inventor named Timothy Taylor says that the night before an idea for an invention came into his head, he woke to find \"a hooded figure standing at the end of\" his bed, and that this is the last thing he remembers. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells it on the show as what Taylor told him, also crediting a book by someone he calls Pulko, and he offers it as a case where a strange visitor seems to come just before a sudden idea.","tim-taylor-s-secret-space-program-and-timeline-adjusting":"An operative named Tim Taylor is said to have belonged to a secret space program, a hidden effort to fly in space outside public knowledge, and one that involved UFOs. Michels takes this from American Cosmic, a book by the writer Diana Pasulka, and says it is her claim in the book, not something he checked himself. If true, it would mean some people already work with UFOs in a space effort nobody has been told about.","tim-taylor-s-sun-gazing-protocols-in-american-cosmic-2010":"A man named Tim Taylor, said to attract UFOs, follows personal routines built around being in sunlight and looking at the sun. Michels says Taylor describes these \"protocols\" in American Cosmic, a book by the writer Diana Pasulka about people involved with UFOs.","tim-taylor-says-the-president-handed-him-the-item-at-dinner":"A man named Timothy Taylor says a president handed him a certain object while they sat at the dinner table together, according to Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had many UFO encounters. Bledsoe repeats Taylor's words to Michels: \"I was at the dinner table with the president and handed me that,\" without saying here which president or exactly what the object was.","tim-taylor-secret-space-program-and-crash-site-material-2010":"A NASA mission controller named Timothy Taylor, who Michels says also worked at the Aerospace Corporation, openly told the religion scholar Diana Pasulka he was part of a secret space program, and took her and the Stanford microbiologist Garry Nolan to a UFO crash site, which one telling places in New Mexico, where they examined material. Michels tells this on several episodes, drawing on Pasulka's book American Cosmic; in one he instead links Taylor to the 1986 Challenger shuttle disaster and the astronaut Judith Resnik. If true, it would mean people inside NASA's orbit are handling crash debris outside public view.","tim-taylor-shows-grant-the-jump-room-building-2013":"In 2013 a man named Timothy Taylor took a guest named Grant Cameron to an old aircraft building that had been turned into offices, and told him this was where something called the jump room had been. Cameron tells Michels about the visit himself, though he admits he does not remember Taylor's exact words: either \"this is where the jump room was\" or \"this is the story of the jump room.\" He tells it as a place tied to a secret story, but what the jump room was, he does not spell out here.","tim-taylor-shows-up-with-experiencers-bledsoe-and-charles-ha":"A man named Timothy Taylor keeps turning up beside people who say they have had contact with non-human beings. Michels says this himself, without naming a source: Taylor \"showed up in the life of\" Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had such encounters, and he also turned up with Charles Hall, who says he lived with aliens at Area 51, the secret Air Force test site in Nevada. Michels raises it because a figure who keeps appearing around these witnesses might be connected to whoever is watching them.","tim-taylor-visits-charles-hall":"A man named Timothy Taylor came to the home of Charles Hall, a man said on the show to attract UFOs, because he had heard about Hall. Hall's wife, who was there, tells this on the show: \"He came here because he found out about Charles.\" If so, it means word of Hall's claims had reached people who wanted to look into him in person.","tim-taylor-works-for-the-hammer":"A man named Timothy Taylor is said to have told people that he works for someone called \"the Hammer\", and no one knows who that is. Chris Bledsoe, a man whom NASA has studied because UFOs are said to appear around him, tells Michels that Taylor said to him, around 2008 to 2011, \"I work for the hammer and the hammer works for God,\" and Bledsoe links it to the film The Adjustment Bureau and the story it came from, The Adjustment Team, in which hidden agents quietly steer people's lives. Bob McGwier, a former CIA contractor, repeats the same account as he heard it from Bledsoe, adding that \"no one that I know knows who the hammer is.\"","time-slows-and-god-speaks-on-the-night-train-from-zurich-to":"On a night train from Zurich to Vienna, when he was 40, time slowed down and God spoke to him, saying \"do not criticize my church, the old and the new are one.\" Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, a former CIA officer, tells Michels this happened to him. He says he wrote the message down by hand and keeps it with his West Point Bible, and he describes feeling that God was aware of him and \"standing there\" with him in the train.","time-travel-to-14th-century-mount-athos-1991":"A former senior CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says that in 1991, on a pilgrimage to Mount Athos, the Greek mountain of monasteries, he dozed off on a stone chair facing a church and found himself in the same place centuries earlier. He tells Michels this himself, as something he lived: \"I saw that town again but back in the 1500s.\" He says he went to the capital there and \"presented my credentials,\" arriving as a monk, and he names a monk called Gabriel and someone called Evagrius as part of it. The story's title places it in the 14th century, while in this telling he speaks of the 1400s and 1500s. Michels's episode frames it plainly as time travel. If true, a trained intelligence officer would be describing a real visit to the past.","time-travelling-nordic-humans-manipulating-history-2050":"Some of the beings in UFO encounters are said to be tall, fair, human-looking 'Nordics' who are really humans from the future, around 2050, who have mastered time travel and are steering history across Earth, the Moon and Mars. The philosopher Jason Jorjani lays this out on the show, drawing on a book he cites, Closer Encounters, and saying 'we are dealing with people who achieved time travel at some point'. If true, the visitors would not be aliens at all but our own descendants.","timothy-leary-supplied-lsd-to-jfk-and-mary-meyer-1960":"In the 1960s, the psychedelics figure Timothy Leary supposedly supplied LSD to President Kennedy and to a woman named Mary Pinchot Meyer in Washington. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this as something he was told, saying simply that it was \"provided by Tim Leary.\" He brings it up as part of a larger story about Kennedy and UFOs.","timothy-taylor-s-agency-credentials-and-a-possible-secret-sp-2010":"A man named Timothy Taylor, tied in the story to NASA and to talk of a possible secret space program, is said to carry credentials from several US intelligence agencies. Michels passes this on from the scholar Diana Pasulka, a researcher who met Taylor. He says that, according to her, Taylor seemed to \"flash all these like three-letter agency credentials\" at her. A researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb makes the same point without naming a source or crediting Pasulka. UAPGerb says Taylor walks around with credentials from many intelligence organisations, including access to the NRO, the agency that runs US spy satellites. If true, it would place a man who speaks about hidden programs deep inside the official security world.","timothy-taylor-s-hierarchy-as-described-to-diana-pasulka-2010":"A man named Timothy Taylor, described as a NASA programme manager long involved in \"spooky subjects\", is said to have explained to the writer Diana Pasulka a \"hierarchy of being\", an order of who or what stands above whom. If he did, someone inside NASA was speaking of other beings ranked above us. A guest named Matthew Pines tells it second-hand, saying Taylor, once anonymous and now fairly well known, was asked about the hierarchy by Pasulka. Michels also repeats it, saying Taylor told Pasulka \"here's the hierarchy of being.\" Neither says what the hierarchy was.","tip-about-a-russian-intelligence-officer-hiding-as-a-monk-on-1991":"In 1991, a Greek man very close to the Greek prime minister tipped off Ralph Larson, who tells it as part of his own intelligence work, that a monk at Mount Athos was really a Russian intelligence officer. According to Larson, speaking on Michels's show, the man was building a cover and changing his identity to go on a mission to the United States for Russian intelligence, and a file was kept on him.","titanic-survivor-s-account-of-angels-in-a-craft-by-the-lifeb-1912":"A survivor of the Titanic sinking in 1912, in a lifeboat later picked up by the rescue ship Carpathia, is said to have described \"a group of angels who came in a craft\", landed by one of the lifeboats and settled \"on the ledge of one of the icebergs\". Charles Hall, who says he spent three years with tall white aliens at Area 51, tells Michels he read this in a book on the Titanic that he does not name. He raises it as an old sighting of a craft that may fit his own accounts.","tmbb-spaceships-x-account-suspected-to-be-mccasland-2026":"In 2026, after the story of a man named McCasland broke, people online hunted for clues and settled on an account on X, the social network, called \"TMBB spaceships\", which they suspected was his. Michels tells this himself and calls the account a \"potential smoking gun\". He draws on McCasland's official Air Force biography alongside it. If the account was his, it would tie a former Air Force man to public posts about spaceships.","to-the-stars-material-testing-with-military-labs-halted-by-c-2019":"Around 2019, To the Stars Academy, a private company set up to research UFOs, was building ties with US military laboratories to run tests on materials that only those labs could do, and then COVID hit and the work with the military stopped. Peter Levenda, a writer Michels presents as a CIA historian, says he was part of this effort. He tells Michels \"we were getting close to something.\"","told-sensor-research-would-be-career-ending":"A former intelligence official named Eric Haseltine says that when he started working in his own lab to reproduce a phenomenon and build a sensor that could detect it, people in the US intelligence community told him it would end his career. He tells Michels this from his own experience, without saying exactly what \"this stuff\" was in the part quoted. If true, it suggests insiders were steered away from studying it.","tom-delonge-books-on-official-and-non-government-programs":"Secret UFO work may be split between official government programmes and programmes run outside government. A guest named Matthew Pines says this idea runs through the books of Tom DeLonge, a man who has written on the subject, saying that DeLonge \"sort of points to\" both kinds. If so, some of the secret work would sit beyond the government's own oversight.","tom-delonge-contacts-levenda-to-investigate-ufos-2014":"At the end of 2014, the rock musician Tom DeLonge contacted the author Peter Levenda \"out of the clear blue sky\" to look into UFOs together, by phone and email. Levenda, billed on the show as a CIA historian, tells Michels this himself, comparing it to the way Michels reached out to him. It places DeLonge's UFO work, and Levenda's part in it, before UFOs became big news in 2017.","tom-delonge-email-to-john-podesta-about-general-mccasland-2015":"Among John Podesta's emails, published by WikiLeaks in 2016, is one from the musician Tom DeLonge to Podesta, a senior Democratic political adviser, with the subject line \"General McCasland\", about the retired Air Force general Neil McCasland. Jesse Michels reads it out himself: DeLonge writes that the general \"mentioned he's a skeptic. He's not. I've been working with him for 4 months.\" Michels raises it because, if true, it would mean a senior general was quietly working with DeLonge on UFOs while publicly claiming doubt.","tom-delonge-narrative-of-a-majestic-style-controlling-group":"The musician turned UFO campaigner Tom DeLonge tells a story in which a secret group much like Majestic, or MJ-12, the name of an alleged committee of officials said to have controlled the UFO secret since the late 1940s, runs the whole matter, according to a national security analyst named Matthew Pines. Pines sums it up for Michels as \"basically the Tom DeLonge narrative,\" in which the people in control are basically a Majestic-style group. If true, it would mean a small hidden body, not elected leaders, decides what the public learns about UFOs.","tom-delonge-says-nhi-are-archons":"The rock musician Tom DeLonge, who founded To the Stars Academy, a private venture that studied UFOs, once told Michels over breakfast that he thought many of the non-human intelligences behind UFOs (what the field calls NHI) are \"not great\", and that they were like archons. Michels tells this himself on three episodes, including one with Lue Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who worked with DeLonge, and in one telling he adds that DeLonge framed it in Gnostic thought, the ancient religious writings in which archons are ruling powers that stand between humans and the divine. If DeLonge is right, the beings would be something to fear rather than welcome.","tom-farmer-cia-general-counsel-vetting-paperclip-germans":"Tom Farmer, a lawyer who served as the CIA's general counsel, is said to have helped vet the German scientists brought to the United States after the Second World War under Paperclip, the secret programme that recruited them. Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, tells this on the show from what he learned of Farmer, saying 'it turned out he was involved in the project called Paperclip'. It matters to him because it ties a man he knew to the programme that brought German expertise into American secret work.","tomb-of-the-birds-and-the-wooden-bird-found-during-wwii-1940":"In a cliff to the north and west of the Great Pyramid at Giza there is said to be a tomb called the Tomb of the Birds, where a wooden bird was found during the Second World War, sometime in the 1940s. A researcher named Luke Caverns tells Michels about it without naming a source, and brings up the television show Mythbusters while he does. He tells it as one of the odd finds around the pyramids that, in his view, have not been properly explained.","tomb-of-thutmose-ii-announced-over-a-year-after-discovery-2024":"The tomb of Thutmose II, an ancient Egyptian pharaoh, was supposedly found in early 2024 but only announced to the public more than a year later. Luke Caverns, a researcher who explores ancient sites, tells Michels that when he was in Egypt in October 2024 he \"heard about that tomb having been discovered earlier in 2024.\" He tells it to suggest that big finds can sit unannounced for a long time.","tone-experiments-with-entranced-space-kids-1970":"In the 1970s, the scientist Andrija Puharich, whom the episode calls a CIA scientist, ran experiments in his lab on children he believed had special abilities, called \"space kids,\" playing certain tones to them while they were in a trance to see how they responded. An unnamed researcher tells Michels this from recordings of Puharich's sessions. If true, it shows Puharich testing the children's trance states in a planned, lab-like way.","tonopah-s4-workers-flown-in-by-janet":"Workers at S4, a secret site where crashed craft are said to be studied, placed here on the Tonopah Test Range in Nevada, must be flown straight there on Janet, the unmarked airline that carries workers to secret Nevada bases, and cannot drive in like other Tonopah staff, according to a researcher who goes by UAPGerb. UAPGerb puts it this way to Michels: S4 employees \"have to be taken there directly from Janet and cannot drive on site like other employees at Tonopah can.\" If true, it would mark S4 as walled off even from the rest of an already secret base.","tonopah-test-range-is-literally-area-52":"The Tonopah Test Range in Nevada is, in the words of an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb, \"literally named Area 52\", a nickname that plays on Area 51, the famously secret Nevada base. UAPGerb says this to Michels in passing, without giving a source, as part of mapping where he thinks secret work goes on.","tooth-device-demonstration-at-the-pentagon-1960":"Sometime in the 1960s, a team showed off a device worn in the mouth, at the tooth, at the Pentagon, and fitted it to a general to show what it could do. Andrija Puharich, a scientist Michels presents as a CIA scientist who believed he had contacted an alien council, tells it in his own words: \"We gave a demonstration at the Pentagon. We fitted this device to this general.\"","top-secret-files-from-the-late-1800s":"Top secret US government files on UFOs are said to exist from as far back as the late 1800s and early 1900s, the time of the spiritualism wave when seances were common. Steven Greer, a campaigner for UFO disclosure, says this on the show and attributes it to a senior CIA source he does not name, telling Michels 'there are still top secret files on this issue' from that period. If true, it would mean government secrecy about the subject began decades before the modern UFO era.","topological-physics-held-by-the-government-and-aerospace-fir":"Advanced knowledge of topological physics, a branch of physics about unusual properties of materials, is supposedly being held inside the government and big aerospace companies, perhaps learned from craft of unknown origin. Michels says twice that the physicist Hal Puthoff told him so, adding the second time that Puthoff was unsure about fundamental physics being locked up there but said topological physics \"probably\" was. Puthoff himself, speaking on the show in 2022, put it as \"there certainly is materials knowledge which involves topological physics\" at such companies.","toronto-cafe-idea-of-borrowing-other-people-s-brain-power-2018":"Sitting in a café in Toronto in 2018, a researcher named Deep Prasad looked at the people around him and had an idea: what if you could borrow other people's brain power? He tells Michels this himself, as the moment the idea came to him, on an episode where he argues that artificial intelligence is a kind of alien technology.","toronto-death-ray-inventor-and-mossad-missing-time-1950":"A Toronto newspaper story from the late 1950s or early 1960s told of a man who had invented a death ray, and said that visitors who came to see him, including people from Mossad, Israel's intelligence service, reported missing time, a stretch of time they could not account for. A guest named Dave Rossi tells Michels he knows the clipping, without naming the paper or the date. If true, something about the inventor or his device affected the minds of those who came near it.","touching-craft-pieces-with-garry-nolan-2020":"Jesse Michels says he has seen pieces said to come from a UFO craft while he was with the scientist Garry Nolan. Michels tells it himself and adds that he doesn't know if they are real pieces of a craft, \"but like they seem.\" If they were real, it would mean physical material from such a craft exists and is being looked at by scientists.","touching-the-reactor-s-repulsive-field-1987":"A physicist named Bob Lazar says that the reactor powering an alien craft he worked on produced a field around itself that you could physically push against with your hand. Lazar tells Michels this from his own claimed work between 1987 and 1989 at a secret site in Nevada he calls S4: \"it'll produce a gravitational field around it and you can push on it.\" If true, it would mean a working device that bends gravity was sitting in a US government lab.","toughbook-programs-lured-ufos-and-downed-them-with-emp-weapo-2020":"Secret US programs are said to have lured UFOs into empty airspace and knocked them down with EMP weapons, which fire electromagnetic pulses that disable electronics. Michels says this comes from \"an insider leak\" about programs recorded on Toughbooks, rugged military laptops, discussed in connection with a UFO book the Pentagon tried to censor. He says the programs used temporary flight restrictions, official orders closing an area of sky to other aircraft, to \"clear the skies, lure UFOs in, and bring them down.\" A man named Michael Herrera, who says he saw a huge UFO in the Indonesian jungle, tells a similar story from a man named Jake Barber. Herrera says an organisation sometimes targets the craft with EMP and what he calls \"scalar\" weapons \"and they knock them out of the sky.\" If true, it would mean the US can bring down these craft on purpose.","townsen-brown-at-caltech-in-the-1920s-discovering-thrust-fro":"In the 1920s, a decade before the rocket pioneer Jack Parsons, an inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown was at Caltech with very different ideas about deep space travel: he is said to have found that a lopsided electrical device called an asymmetric capacitor produced a push, or thrust, when charged. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as the opening of an episode about propulsion without rockets. If true, it would mean a quiet line of research into this kind of drive running back almost a century.","townsend-brown-and-ning-li-claims-of-weight-reduction-and-gr-1950":"Over the past seventy years, several people have claimed small reductions in weight and a kind of gravitational shielding, meaning a way to block or lessen gravity's pull. Michels names the two he thinks of first: a researcher named Thomas Townsend Brown and a researcher named Ning Li at the University of Alabama in Huntsville. He raises it on his own, without citing their papers, as a hint that gravity might be engineered.","townsend-brown-and-the-caroline-group":"The American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have crossed paths with what Michels calls the Caroline Group, an international circle of industrialists that, in his words, seemed to \"suck up all of the exotic tech.\" Michels tells this himself on a 2026 episode, without naming a source. If true, it would mean that unusual inventions were being quietly gathered up by private money rather than reaching the public.","townsend-brown-and-the-nassau-group":"The American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have spent a great deal of time in Nassau, in the Bahamas, where a secret group used to meet. Michels tells this himself, drawing on Brown's biographer Paul Schatzkin, author of the biography The Man Who Mastered Gravity, who Michels says coined a name for that circle: \"the Nassau Group.\" Michels does not say who belonged to it or what it did.","townsend-brown-believed-his-work-bore-on-time-travel":"The American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, who experimented with high voltage in the hope of affecting gravity, is said to have believed his work had \"clear implications for time travel.\" Michels says this himself on two episodes without naming a source, once adding that Brown was \"obsessed with time travel.\" The second time, Michels adds that Agnew Bahnson, the man who funded Brown, was known for remarks along the same lines about strange effects at high voltage.","townsend-brown-born-in-zanesville-built-wireless-telegraph-a-1905":"Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor whom Michels presents as a scientist who built flying-saucer-like devices for the government, was born in Zanesville, Ohio, in 1905 and was already building a wireless telegraph at about age 12. Michels says there are old newspaper clippings showing the boy and his invention.","townsend-brown-crash-retrievals-and-green-fireball-1950":"In the late 1950s and early 1960s, the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have gone into the field, shown official credentials, and recovered crashed unknown objects, what the subject calls crash retrievals. Michels, talking with a guest named Dave Rossi, says Brown found a \"green fireball\" object that was losing mass, and that it was apparently made of barium titanate, a ceramic material that reacts to electric charge. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source; if true, it would place Brown inside secret recovery work.","townsend-brown-fired-by-the-navy-moves-to-martin-vega-before-1942":"In 1942 the Navy fired the researcher Thomas Townsend Brown without cause, and he then turned up at the Martin Vega Corporation, an aircraft maker, a year before Skunk Works, the secret aircraft design shop, was formed. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and calls the move mysterious. To him it suggests Brown's work quietly moved into the world of secret aircraft.","townsend-brown-in-the-bahamas-with-william-stephenson":"The American researcher Thomas Townsend Brown spent what Michels calls an inordinate amount of time in the Bahamas with William Stephenson, the spy who worked for Winston Churchill and is said to have inspired James Bond. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, as a hint that Brown's work moved in intelligence circles.","townsend-brown-parachutes-into-germany-to-probe-german-ufo-p-1944":"The American inventor Townsend Brown, later described as a CIA scientist, is said to have parachuted behind enemy lines into Germany near the end of World War II to look into a German program on flying craft and the Foo Fighters, the glowing balls of light Allied pilots reported. Michels tells it in 1944, crediting Brown's biographer Paul Schatzkin and his book The Man Who Mastered Gravity, while Schatzkin himself tells Michels the jump came in 1945, from very low altitude, crediting a man named O'Reilly. In another episode Michels adds that Brown was tied to an Allied mission he names TICOM in East Germany, was captured by the Nazis and held as a prisoner of war, and was rescued by the Americans soon after.","townsend-brown-possibly-involved-in-a-crash-recovery":"The American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown may have taken part in recovering a crashed UFO, Michels says. He brings this up on a 2026 episode with a NASA whistleblower and says he has \"some evidence\" for it, but he does not say what that evidence is. If true, it would place Brown among the people who handled the wreckage of a craft that was not built by humans.","townsend-brown-s-1960s-work-northrop-b-2-and-rand":"The inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have shown his gravity-control experiments to the RAND Corporation, a defense research group, after which his work \"went dark\" and was quietly handed to Northrop Grumman for the B-2 stealth bomber. Michels tells this himself on three episodes without naming a source, and his details shift: he puts the RAND meeting in the 1960s, once in the 1950s with a businessman named Floyd Odlum involved, and once dates the demonstration to 1967 and names a man called Michael Rich, without mentioning the B-2. If true, it would mean real work on controlling gravity was taken in secret into the military aircraft industry.","townsend-brown-s-anti-gravity-success-in-paris-sarbacher-pic-1950":"The inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have finally got a positive result from his anti-gravity experiments in Paris, and when he flew back to the US he was picked up at the airport by a man named Robert Sarbacher in his big Cadillac. Michels tells this himself, once to the writer Whitley Strieber and once in his own documentary on Brown, drawing on Brown's family and biographer, Linda Brown and Paul Schatzkin. In the documentary he dates the trip to 1956 and says the engineer Henri Coanda and a man named Jacques Cornillon brought Brown to France. The point of the story is the pickup: it would tie Brown's anti-gravity work directly to Sarbacher.","townsend-brown-s-electrogravitics-experiments-in-the-late-19-1928":"In the late 1920s, the American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown was experimenting with electrogravitics, the idea that strong electric fields can push against gravity, using very high voltage at low power and tuned to certain resonant frequencies, according to Steven Greer, a former doctor who leads a campaign to make UFO secrets public. Greer, drawing on the Disclosure Project Intelligence Archive, his group's collection of documents and testimony, tells Michels: \"late 1920s they were using very high voltage material, resonant frequencies, certain frequencies at very high voltage but low power.\" If true, it would mean work on anti-gravity began in the United States and Germany decades before the modern UFO era.","townsend-brown-s-experiments-in-oil":"The American experimenter Thomas Townsend Brown, whose work comes up on the show in connection with antigravity propulsion, is said to have run some of his experiments with the equipment sunk in oil. A guest named Charles Buhler, whom the show presents as a NASA figure who has built such a propulsion system himself, mentions it to Michels in passing (\"he did things in oil\") without saying how he knows. Buhler raises it as part of the background to his own claim that electric fields can push an object without anything being thrown out the back.","townsend-brown-s-gravitational-anomaly-expeditions-in-nassau":"The researcher Thomas Townsend Brown spent a lot of time in Nassau, in the Bahamas, where he led expeditions to study gravitational anomalies, places where gravity seemed to behave oddly. Michels offers it as a little-known fact, without giving dates or a source, and mentions it alongside William Stephenson. He raises it as another sign of Brown's interest in the unusual side of gravity.","townsend-brown-s-gravitator-was-a-time-machine":"Thomas Townsend Brown was an American inventor of the mid-20th century who built devices he believed linked electricity to gravity. When a man named Morgan looked at one of them, he reportedly lost his temper and said, \"it's a time machine.\" Michels tells this himself, drawing on Paul Schatzkin's biography The Man Who Mastered Gravity. In other episodes he goes further: he says Brown made much more progress than anyone knew, privately believed that controlling gravity leads to time travel, and even called himself a time traveler, which Michels credits to Brown's daughter.","townsend-brown-s-shortwave-contact-with-space-brothers":"The American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have talked often about being in contact with \"space brothers\", friendly aliens, and once claimed he was \"communicating with aliens half the time.\" Michels brings this up himself on four episodes between 2025 and 2026. He tells it a little differently each time: once he credits the claim to a man named Agnew Bahnson, once he calls it experiences Brown described himself, and once he compares it to the film Close Encounters of the Third Kind.","townsend-brown-s-time-travel-tech-taken-by-a-group-in-nassau":"The inventor Townsend Brown, who worked on using electricity to move objects in ways he linked to gravity, is said to have claimed again and again that his time travel technology was taken by a group in Nassau, in the Bahamas. Michels tells this himself, drawing on The Man Who Mastered Gravity, a biography of Brown by Paul Schatzkin, and on Brown's daughter, and he also names a man called William Stephenson in connection with it. If true, it would mean breakthrough technology was quietly taken out of public hands.","townsend-brown-s-ufo-interaction-at-age-17-1922":"The American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown is said to have had a UFO encounter around 1922, when he was 17, and to have built his life's work on it. Michels tells this in several episodes. In the fullest version it happens on Catalina Island, where \"a little orb light approached him, literally came up to him.\" Elsewhere he hedges on the age and place, and he once cites Brown's daughter as saying this is when Brown \"downloaded his life's work,\" back when he had a lab in Pasadena. The daughter, Linda Brown, tells it herself, saying her father \"learned so much standing there with that ball of light,\" which would mean his science came from the encounter.","townsend-brown-takes-crash-material-from-harvard-1957":"Near Hartford, Connecticut, people saw a greenish fireball crash with a loud noise, a shed caught fire and metal debris was found, and Thomas Townsend Brown, a scientist Michels calls a CIA scientist, walked into a Harvard office, \"flashes his credentials, and takes the material.\" According to Michels, the debris first went to Fred Whipple and J. Allen Hynek at Harvard's Moonwatch, a program that watched for and collected the first satellites, while Robert Friend of Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, also wanted the samples, and one version adds that the material seemed to be decaying fast and might be barium titanate. Michels tells it himself across several episodes, crediting the researcher Michael Swords and a Smithsonian archive file, and he dates the crash to 1958 in one telling and 1960 in others.","townsend-brown-workbooks-posted-online-2025":"In 2025 someone posted on the internet the workbooks of Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor whose experiments are tied in this field to ideas about how UFOs might fly. Harold Malmgren, a former presidential adviser, mentions it to Michels in passing, saying \"somebody just posted on the internet the workbooks,\" without saying who. If the workbooks are genuine, Brown's own notes would now be open for anyone to read.","townsend-brown-worse-than-a-thermonuclear-bomb":"A biography of the American inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, who worked on gravity-defying propulsion, contains a line saying his technology \"will make a thermonuclear bomb look like a child's firecracker.\" Michels quotes it himself from the writer Paul Schatzkin's book The Man Who Mastered Gravity, as a sign of how dangerous such a technology could be if it worked.","townsend-s-repelling-bismuth-raised-at-martin-1950":"In the 1950s, the story goes, a man named Townsend found a kind of bismuth, a heavy metal, that was pushed away instead of pulled in. If true, that would hint that someone was chasing a way to cancel gravity. Michels tells it himself in three episodes, quoting Louis Witten, a physicist then at the Martin Company, from an interview Witten gave the American Institute of Physics (in one telling Michels calls the source \"American theoretical physics\"). In one episode he ties the name to Thomas Townsend Brown, a researcher who is the subject of one of his documentaries.","townspeople-claim-1992-ufo-crash-recovered-by-brookhaven-nat":"In 1992 a UFO is said to have crashed near Brookhaven National Laboratory, a large US government research lab on Long Island, New York, and townspeople say the lab itself collected the craft. Michels tells this himself in several episodes, as what local people said, at times naming the town as South Haven, adding the local police chief as a witness, calling the lab America's version of CERN, and once only saying the crash happened right next to the lab. If true, a federal science lab would have quietly taken in a crashed craft.","trace-relation-proven-to-be-a-partial-order-at-heathrow":"A researcher named Don Hoffman tells Michels that he suspected a mathematical structure in his theory, which he calls the trace relation, was a logic, technically a partial order (a way of ranking things in which not every pair has to be comparable). He says he took it to a colleague named Jayton Posash, who told him \"it's too pretty to be true,\" then flew off, checked it while at Heathrow airport, and proved it. For Hoffman it was a key step in the mathematics behind his ideas about reality.","training-army-intelligence-officers-in-remote-viewing":"The physicist Hal Puthoff says he once spent nearly all his time training US Army intelligence officers in remote viewing, the claimed ability to describe distant places or things using the mind alone. He tells it on the show as the reason he stopped doing other work, saying he was busy \"24 hours a day\" with the training. If true, it means the Army took the method seriously enough to teach it to its own officers.","training-children-in-extraocular-vision-in-mexico-and-spain":"Groups in Mexico and Spain are said to be training children in extraocular vision, which means seeing without using the eyes. The biologist Rupert Sheldrake tells Michels about it and says he knows of the Spanish work through a colleague in Spain who works on it, though he is not sure whether anything similar is going on in the US or Britain. If it were true, it would mean sight does not depend on the eyes alone.","training-instructor-s-account-of-ghosts-using-the-nellis-wea-1964":"In 1964 or 1965, at the old weather station in the base operations building at Nellis Air Force Base in Nevada, an airman training new staff on the job told the young Charles Hall that 'ghosts' sometimes came in to use the restroom. Hall, who says he later spent years in contact with tall, pale beings he calls the Tall Whites and who wrote about it in his book Millennial Hospitality, tells Michels that the 'ghosts' were really 'a group of five tall white aliens' who had walked in off the runway. If true, it would mean beings like these were walking around a working Air Force base, and the staff had a cover word for them.","transcript-of-brad-sorensen-arvs-built-late-1950s-1957":"Steven Greer, a campaigner for UFO disclosure, says he has a full transcript of a man named Brad Sorensen talking to an aerospace writer, in which Sorensen says that ARVs, short for alien reproduction vehicles, craft built to copy alien ones, were being made by the late 1950s, with Norton Air Force Base in California tied to the story. Greer says he holds the transcript himself. A researcher known online as UAPGerb tells it differently: he says it was posted on an online archive called DPI as the \"Norton Air Force Base internal\" file, dates it to 1990, and names the writer as Bill Scott. If true, it would mean the US was building copies of alien craft within about a decade of the first famous sightings.","transfer-of-ufo-material-from-lockheed-martin-to-bigelow-aer-2000":"In the 2000s, material from crashed UFOs is said to have been moved from the arms maker Lockheed Martin to Bigelow Aerospace, a private space company in Las Vegas owned by Bob Bigelow, through a special access program, meaning a tightly restricted secret program. Michels raises it on three episodes without naming a source, once reading out that a Pentagon UFO study called OSAP was meant to visit Bigelow's facilities because of a plan to hand off the material, proposed by James Ryder, then a Lockheed Martin vice president and now dead. Another time he places it next to Kona Blue, which he calls a real UFO program, as proof that such programs exist.","transistor-patents-traced-to-1930s-germany":"The patents for the transistor, the switch at the heart of all modern electronics, are said to go back to Germany in the 1930s. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about whether AI is alien technology. If true, it would mean the device's history starts earlier than the usual American story.","translucent-acorn-shaped-craft-over-fisherman-pulling-up-net-2023":"In 2023, a fisherman in the Alto Nanay region of the Peruvian Amazon is said to have been pulling up his nets at night when it was suddenly as if 'somebody turned the lights on or the sun suddenly came up', and he looked up to see a translucent craft shaped like an acorn hovering 30 or 40 feet above him. The researcher and filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels this from his own visit to the area, where the fisherman drew the craft for him on an acrylic chalkboard. Alberino raises it as one of several local reports of the same kind of craft.","travis-taylor-arrives-as-a-skeptic":"The scientist Dr. Travis Taylor, a member of the research team at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, arrived there as a firm doubter, according to the ranch's owner. The owner tells Michels he flew out to the ranch with Taylor the first time and that Taylor \"was an absolute skeptic.\" The point of telling it is that what Taylor later saw there was enough to change a doubter's mind.","travis-taylor-zapped-at-skinwalker-ranch-2020":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange happenings, a man named Travis Taylor was \"mouthing off\" and was given \"this little zap\" involving radioactivity, according to the UFO researcher Grant Cameron. Cameron tells Michels this as something he has heard, without naming a source and without saying who or what did the zapping, and it is told as if the place itself answered back.","travis-walton-abduction-1975":"In 1975 a man named Travis Walton is said to have been taken aboard a UFO, a story usually called an abduction, while out with friends in a very remote mountain area near Snowflake, Arizona. A UFO researcher named James Fox, who tells the case without naming a source, stresses how isolated the spot was when the encounter happened. Michels brings Walton up himself as \"another good example\" of an abduction, and says only that Walton was in Arizona with his friends. If true, it would be a case where a man vanished in front of friends and came back telling of being taken.","travis-walton-abduction-as-shown-in-fire-in-the-sky-1975":"Travis Walton says he was taken aboard a craft in Arizona in 1975, and the film version of his story, Fire in the Sky, is said to have changed what happened to him on purpose. Chris Ramsay, a YouTuber, tells Michels that the movie \"played it out so differently\": on screen, beings carry out a procedure on Walton and ignore his screams. Bryce Zabel, a TV writer and producer, passes on what Tracy Tormé, the film's screenwriter, told him. In Tormé's account, the studio said \"the real story of Travis isn't good enough. You have to do a better abduction.\" Both men are repeating what others told them. If it is true, the frightening abduction most people remember came from Hollywood, not from Walton. Neither of them describes the real account in any more detail.","travis-walton-abduction-two-weeks-after-nbc-s-the-ufo-incide-1975":"In 1975, two weeks after NBC aired The UFO Incident, a TV film about an earlier abduction story, a man named Travis Walton said he had been abducted himself. The researcher Bryce Zabel points this out to Michels, without naming a source. He calls the film, which he connects to the actor James Earl Jones and the book The Interrupted Journey, \"not that great\", and his point is about timing: if one followed the other, a TV broadcast may have shaped what people later reported.","travis-walton-case-dismissed-as-fabrication-1975":"Travis Walton, a man who said he was taken by a UFO in 1975, made the story up, according to the well-known skeptic Michael Shermer, who suggests the reason was that Walton was running late getting a job done. Shermer adds that Walton did not pass a lie detector test, and says he was once on a show with him. If he is right, one of the best-known abduction cases would be a cover story for a missed deadline.","travis-walton-case-fire-tower-debunk-disputed-1975":"A popular debunking of the 1975 Travis Walton case, in which an Arizona man said he was taken by a UFO, holds that the light his companions saw was a fire tower beside the road. The writer and researcher Greg Bishop tells Michels this is wrong on the ground: the tower is not right next to the road, he says, \"it's over a hill.\" If he is right, the easy explanation for what the men saw falls away.","travis-walton-handed-over-from-greys-to-nordics-1975":"In 1975 a man named Travis Walton was taken aboard a craft in Arizona. There, the story goes, small grey beings handed him over to tall, human-looking ones called Nordics: he went down a hallway, \"bumped into these Nordics and from there on he was handled by these people.\" That version comes from the philosopher Jason Jorjani, who summarises the case, while Walton himself, in a clip on the show, describes only one \"human-looking\" man coming in while he was trying to find a way out.","travis-walton-is-struck-by-a-craft-s-energy-discharge-1975":"In Arizona in 1975, a man named Travis Walton ran up to a strange craft and was struck by a blast of energy from it. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan retells this from Walton's own account, and his point is that Walton was never chosen: \"He ran up to the craft and got blasted.\" If that is right, what happened to Walton was an accident he walked into, not a planned taking.","travis-walton-wakes-with-a-full-beard-1975":"A man named Travis Walton, who says he was abducted by aliens in Arizona in 1975, is said to have shaved right before it happened and come back days later with a full beard. A researcher named Mike Masters tells it on the show, saying Walton had shaved \"right before his experience.\" A former Pentagon research scientist named John Blitch says Walton came back five days later, while Michels gives the gap as four days. Michels also mentions it in another episode as \"a common trope with alien abductions.\" The beard is told as a sign that more time passed for Walton than for everyone else.","travis-walton-was-dead-and-revived-1975":"A doctor named Roger Leir believed that Travis Walton, the man at the center of a 1975 case in Arizona, had actually died and was then brought back to life by whoever had him. This is a second-hand account. A scientist named Steve Colbern tells Michels what Leir thought: \"Dr. Leir thinks he was dead and they brought him back to life.\" If it were true, the beings in such cases could reverse death itself.","treat-team-under-albert-stubblebine-per-hans-adam-ii-1990":"An email from the 1990s between Hans-Adam II, the prince of Liechtenstein, and a UFO activist named Steven Greer is said to describe a secret group called a \"TREAT\" team. According to the email, the team worked under Albert Stubblebine, who headed INSCOM, the US Army's intelligence and security command. An online UFO researcher who goes by UAPGerb raises the email with Michels as a sign that a hidden UFO effort may have existed inside Army intelligence.","tree-in-the-backyard-bursts-into-flame":"A tree in the backyard of a North Carolina man named Chris Bledsoe burst into flame on its own, even though it had been raining for two days. Bledsoe tells Michels this himself, as one of the strange events around his home, which he links to the UFOs he says are drawn to him.","trees-knocked-flat-to-the-northeast-1948":"At Hart Canyon near Aztec, New Mexico, where a craft is said to have crashed in 1948, the trees were knocked flat and all lie pointing northeast. Scott Ramsey, a researcher of the Aztec case, tells Michels what Bill Steinman, an earlier investigator, told him at the site: the recovery team would have dragged the craft up to a \"dragon wagon\", a heavy military haul vehicle, over soft trees that would not damage it, and the trees all splay to the northeast. On this account, the fallen trees would be a mark left on the ground by the recovery.","tres-coracoes-army-battalion-supports-the-varginha-operation-1996":"In 1996 an army unit based in Três Corações, Brazil, sent some of its men to support the military operation in the nearby town of Varginha, where a strange creature was said to have been captured. Aldo Rebelo, a former defence minister of Brazil, tells Michels that \"the unit or at least part of that unit went to provide support to an operation.\" Coming from a former minister, it would mean the army really did act in Varginha that year.","trevor-grassi-s-corridor-of-rooms-leading-to-the-tunnel-netw":"About 100 metres from the shafts that sit between the Khafre pyramid and the Sphinx at Giza, a man named Trevor Grassi is said to have found what he called \"a corridor of rooms\" leading toward a network of tunnels under the plateau. The scientist Filippo Biondi, who scans beneath the pyramids with radar, tells Michels this as something Grassi found, not something he saw himself. If it were true, the hidden spaces under Giza would reach well beyond the monuments.","trevor-paglen-s-book-on-black-project-patches":"A writer named Trevor Paglen is said to have put together a book that is \"basically a compilation of black project patches\": the cloth badges worn by the teams on secret, highly classified military programmes, which are known as black projects. Matthew Brown, presented on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, tells Michels about it without naming the book's title. He points to it as a way to see that hidden programmes leave visible traces, even when the programmes themselves are kept secret.","triangle-and-gravity-control-patents-that-cite-1950s-air-for":"Patents for triangle-shaped craft, and for the power sources and gravity control behind them, are said to lead back to Air Force and NASA work in the 1950s. A researcher named Dylan Borland tells Michels this, pointing to what he found on Google Patents but naming no single source: if you follow those patents, he says, \"you end up back in the 50s with the Air Force and NASA.\" If he is right, serious government interest in controlling gravity goes back seventy years.","triangle-appears-above-russian-naval-vessels-off-kamchatka-2018":"A US surveillance asset watching Russian naval vessels in the Pacific off Kamchatka, in Russia's far east, saw a large black triangle appear right above the ships, according to a former Defense Department official named Matthew Brown, who tells Michels it was in a briefing he calls Immaculate Constellation; the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell retells it from Brown, saying the triangle \"materializes or decloaks or something\", and gives no place or year. Immaculate Constellation is also the name of an unsigned report entered into the record of a House Oversight hearing on 13 November 2024; it calls itself the public version of a Defense Department employee's report, Brown later said he wrote it, and a Defense Department spokesperson said the department has no record of a program by that name. One case in the report has \"a large equilateral-triangle UAP\" (UAP is the official term for UFOs) appear \"directly over the ships\" collecting \"at night in a specific area of the Pacific Ocean\", and puts the ships' presence down to \"foreign pre-positioning of advanced collection assets\". The report calls the vessels foreign and the place a specific area of the Pacific, so the reading that this is the Kamchatka case with Russian ships comes from Brown's and Corbell's accounts. If the story is true, an unknown craft was watching the same ships US intelligence was watching.","triangle-of-orbs-follow-him-home-and-appear-in-his-yard":"A man named Bob McGwier, billed on the show as a CIA contractor, says a triangle of glowing orbs followed him home to Blacksburg, Virginia, and showed up in his yard. He tells Michels he has video of the orbs going up and down as if \"making sure my security camera got it.\" If so, the orbs were not just present but seemed to want to be recorded.","triangle-with-a-round-hole-caught-on-film-2011":"A treasure hunter named Dennis Asberg says that in 2011, at the Baltic Sea anomaly, a strange object his team found on the floor of the Baltic Sea, they caught on film a triangle-shaped thing with a round hole in it. Asberg tells Michels this as something from his own expedition, describing it with his hands: \"like triangle like this... with a hole in it.\" He tells it among other odd events at the same site, as a sign that the object is more than an ordinary rock.","triangles-seen-on-launch-pads-at-groom-lake":"Triangle-shaped craft have been seen sitting on launch pads at Groom Lake, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada. That is the claim of a researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb. He tells Michels he knows \"numerous people\" who have seen them, which would put such craft in the hands of the US military.","triangles-stored-on-doe-land-across-the-nevada-test-and-trai":"Triangle-shaped craft are stored on land owned by the DOE, the US Department of Energy, across the Nevada Test and Training Range, a vast military range in Nevada, according to an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb. He says it to Michels as part of his map of secret sites, without naming a source, and it would mean such craft are kept on energy department land rather than ordinary military bases.","tridactyl-beings-of-peru-from-the-nazca-period-2017":"Three-fingered bodies, called the tridactyl beings, found in Peru and dated to the Nazca period, a region with ancient cave art all around, are said to carry a genetic mutation that matches a human mutation affecting the number of fingers. Michels tells it himself, crediting the find to an unnamed researcher in bioinformatics and genetics and linking it to the Mexican UFO journalist Jaime Maussan, who has promoted the bodies. If true, the bodies would be real beings, not carvings or fakes.","trinity-and-roswell-crashes-1940":"UFOs are said to have crashed in New Mexico in the 1940s, at Trinity, the site of the first atomic bomb test, and at Roswell, a town in the same state. Michels mentions these in passing as \"known crashes\" on a 2022 episode with the Harvard astronomer Avi Loeb. He names no source, and he treats them as background rather than telling either one in full.","trinity-flash-explained-away-as-an-ammo-dump-accident-1945":"When the first atomic bomb was tested in New Mexico in 1945, people who saw the flash wondered what had happened, and the government told them it had been an accident at an ammunition dump. The physicist John Brandenburg tells this to Michels as something passed on to him. It is a story about the government covering a secret with a ready-made explanation.","trinity-plutonium-fallout-plume-over-roswell-1945":"When the first atomic bomb was tested at the Trinity site in New Mexico in 1945, a plume carrying plutonium that \"didn't get burned up in the explosion\" is said to have drifted over Roswell and left \"a big strip\" of contaminated soil around the town. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels this as something he was told. He brings it up because it would give a reason for something to take an interest in Roswell two years later.","trinity-test-warning-1945":"Before the Trinity test of 1945 in New Mexico, the first atomic bomb explosion, the people watching were told that no one knew what would happen. John S. Herrington, a former US Secretary of Energy, repeats this on the show and ties it to J. Robert Oppenheimer: \"we don't know what's going to happen. Put on your glasses and keep your head down.\" He tells it to show how far into the unknown the scientists themselves were going.","trip-to-russia-to-save-kgb-files":"The Swedish UFO researcher Clas Svahn, who keeps a large archive of UFO material, says he once travelled to Russia to try to save the KGB's files on the subject. He tells Michels simply, \"I went to Russia once trying to save the KGB files.\" If such files exist, they would hold the Soviet secret police's own records of UFO reports.","trojan-war-the-bronze-age-collapse-and-the-greek-dark-age":"The Trojan War, the legendary war of the Greeks against Troy, seems to coincide with the collapse of the Bronze Age world in the eastern Mediterranean, which was followed by the Greek Dark Age. A researcher named Luke Caverns lays this out without naming a source: first the height of the Bronze Age, then the war, then the fall. His point is that the old story may carry a memory of a real collapse.","trucker-s-ufo-sighting-and-missing-time-from-texas-to-nevada-1977":"In 1977 a truck driver saw a UFO on Interstate 20 near El Paso, Texas, then lost a stretch of time he cannot account for, and came to on government land in Nevada. Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran who says it happened to him, tells Michels he was told so to his face: \"I thought I was in Texas, you know. No, you're in Nevada.\"","truman-interview-ufos-discussed-at-every-military-conference-1950":"Harry Truman said in an interview that UFOs came up at every military meeting he held: \"oh yes we discussed it at every conference that we had with the military,\" he says, adding that they \"never were able to make me a concrete report\" on it. The clip of Truman's own words is played on the show, and it would mean the subject was taken seriously at the very top, even if the president never got clear answers.","truman-meeting-and-the-secret-anderson-trust-1945":"In 1945, after a meeting involving President Truman, a group of senior officials and military men are said to have set up a secret fund called the Anderson Trust in Houston, Texas. The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source, linking it to figures such as Ed Lansdale, Charles Willoughby, Douglas MacArthur, Clark Clifford, Henry Stimson, Robert Lovett, Robert Anderson and John J. McCloy. If true, it would mean a private money channel was built from the start to keep a secret program out of public sight.","trump-administration-attributes-new-jersey-drones-to-the-faa-2024":"When mysterious drones were reported over New Jersey around 2024, the Trump administration is said to have brushed the sightings off as activity regulated by the FAA, the US aviation authority. A Senate staffer named Kurt McConnell says this to Michels and calls it the administration's \"first official act\" on the matter. For him it is an example of how officials wave such sightings away.","trump-briefed-on-legacy-uap-programs-2017":"Donald Trump is said to have been briefed on \"legacy programs,\" meaning long-running secret programmes on UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the newer official word for UFOs. The researcher Matthew Pines says on the show, without naming a source, that he is \"pretty confident\" Trump knows \"at least broad contours that this is real.\" A congressman named Eric Burlison goes further. Passing on what he was told, he tells Michels the briefing included word that \"there are hybrids,\" meaning beings part human and part not, and that Trump replied, \"You mean like Adam Schiff,\" naming a Democratic congressman. If true, it would mean a president was told such programmes exist.","trump-briefed-on-nordic-aliens":"Donald Trump is said to have been briefed on \"Nordics\", the tall, fair, human-looking aliens of UFO lore. Michels raises it himself as a question to a congressman guest (\"wasn't he briefed on like Nordics or something?\"), without naming a source or a date.","trump-cancels-bedminster-trip-over-drones-2024":"In 2024 Donald Trump, then the incoming president, had to skip a trip to his summer home in Bedminster, New Jersey, because of the mysterious drones being reported over New Jersey. Michels states this himself, without naming a source. He brings it up to show how seriously the sightings were being taken at the top.","trump-executive-order-making-nasa-an-intelligence-agency-2025":"In 2025, Donald Trump is said to have described NASA as something like an intelligence agency, which would mean it now handles secrets the way the CIA does. Michels says this himself, in passing, on a 2026 episode about NASA and the Moon. He calls it something Trump said \"last year\" and does not quote the order or say what it contains.","trump-hints-at-knowledge-about-roswell-1947":"Donald Trump is said to have hinted that he knows something about Roswell, New Mexico, where something crashed in 1947 in a case at the heart of many UFO stories. Michels repeats Trump's words: \"I won't talk to you about what I know about it, but it's very interesting. But Roswell's a very interesting place.\" Michels raises it as a sign that a former president may have been told something about the crash.","trump-on-rogan-links-jfk-records-to-people-coming-from-space-2024":"In 2024, on the comedian Joe Rogan's podcast, Donald Trump is said to have steered talk about the JFK files toward life from space. A guest named Matthew Pines tells Michels that when Rogan spoke of how many people want to see the Kennedy records, Trump replied that many people are also interested in \"the people coming from space\". Michels remembers the same interview another way: he says Trump \"immediately pointed to Mars\". Both treat the moment as a sign that Trump knows something about the subject.","trump-on-the-new-jersey-drones-2024":"Donald Trump said the US military knows exactly where the mysterious drones seen over New Jersey in 2024 came from. In a clip played on the show, Trump says: \"Our military knows where they took off from. If it's a garage, they can go right into that garage.\" It is told because it suggests the government knew more about the drones than it was saying.","trump-pilots-say-spheres-outfly-f-22s":"Donald Trump has said that Air Force pilots, \"better than Tom Cruise\", told him they had seen spherical, circular objects outfly the F-22, the Air Force's top fighter jet. The writer and analyst Marik von Rennenkampff quotes Trump on the show, as a sign that such reports reached the White House.","trump-says-aliens-are-classified-material-2026":"In 2026, according to the researcher Grant Cameron, a reporter asked Donald Trump about aliens and Trump answered, \"Oh, it's classified material,\" and the reporter followed up: \"So you're saying aliens are real?\" Cameron repeats the exchange to Michels without naming where it was reported, and also brings up Barack Obama in the same breath. He tells it because, if a president calls the subject classified, he is in effect saying there is something real to classify.","trump-says-the-ufo-issue-is-being-looked-into-aliens-gov-reg-2026":"In 2026, Donald Trump is said to be telling the public that his government is \"looking into the UFO issue.\" Michels says this himself, without naming a source, and adds that a couple of months earlier Trump and Barack Obama had traded public remarks on the subject in what he calls \"some sort of bizarre mimetic warfare.\" He sees it as a sign that disclosure, meaning the long-awaited official release of what the government knows about UFOs, is getting closer.","trump-told-of-hybrids-living-among-us-during-his-briefing":"During a briefing on the alien question, Donald Trump was reportedly told that there are human-alien hybrids that \"potentially live amongst us,\" and he answered, \"You mean like Adam Schiff?\" Congressman Eric Burlison tells Michels this as something he heard about the briefing, not something he saw. If true, it would mean a president was given that story as part of an official briefing.","trusted-colleagues-report-firsthand-encounters-with-beings":"Some of the most trusted people a helicopter pilot named Jake Barber has worked with, and still works with, say they have met non-human beings face to face. Barber, who says he flew a UFO out with his helicopter, tells Michels this without naming any of them, saying only that those people \"speak to having those firsthand experiences.\" He offers it as a reason to take such encounters seriously, since it comes from people he trusts.","trw-and-skunk-works-crates-in-close-encounters-1977":"Steven Spielberg's 1977 film Close Encounters of the Third Kind shows shipping crates marked TRW and Skunk Works, two aerospace research outfits (Skunk Works is the secret design unit of the aircraft maker Lockheed), at a time when few people knew much about either. Michels brings this up in three episodes. In the latest he names the companies as Lockheed and TRW and calls them \"two likely active legacy UFO crash retrieval programs\", meaning secret efforts to recover and keep crashed craft. If he is right, Spielberg was quietly pointing at who really held the wreckage.","tsiolkovsky-in-touch-with-angelic-beings":"Konstantin Tsiolkovsky, the father of Russian rocketry, believed beings higher than humans were guiding us, and he wrote about it, according to the religion scholar Diana Pasulka. On the show she says he 'believed that he was actually in touch with Angelic beings', and in an interview elsewhere she says he thought geniuses received information 'for space rocket calculations' from 'celestial angelic beings'. Michels repeats it several times, saying Tsiolkovsky built a cosmic religion called cosmism that inspired the Russian space program, and citing Pasulka's book American Cosmic for the idea that 'ethereal beings' spoke to us through signs in the sky. A pamphlet Tsiolkovsky wrote in 1928, Unknown Intelligent Forces, says an influence on us of living beings 'like us, only more perfect' is very possible, and that some facts speak 'for the intervention of some incomprehensible forces in human acts'. Higher beings acting on human life is what the tellers call angelic. The text says this in general, not as a meeting of his own, so the step to him being personally in touch with them is the tellers' reading, which the pamphlet neither states nor rules out. It matters to them because it would mean a founder of space travel saw his work as guided from beyond.","tuatha-de-danann-weather-control-magic":"The Tuatha Dé Danann, the mythological gods of Ireland, had magic, and specifically magic to control the weather, according to Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher with his own theory of what the pyramids were for. He tells Michels this from Irish myth without naming a text, as part of his argument that ancient peoples knew how to work the weather.","tulsi-gabbard-mentions-uaps-in-her-dni-confirmation-testimon-2025":"In 2025, when Tulsi Gabbard testified to the Senate in the hearing to confirm her as Director of National Intelligence, the head of the US intelligence agencies, she talked about UAPs, the government's term for UFOs, explicitly. A researcher named Matthew Pines says this on the show, noting she is apparently also quite pro-Bitcoin. He tells it as a sign that someone open to the subject now sits at the top of US intelligence.","tumbling-dipper-shaped-lights-near-mansfield-1973":"In 1973, near Mansfield State College in Pennsylvania, a witness named Jim Weiner looked up above the trees and saw a group of what looked like stars, arranged like the Little Dipper, except that they were moving, and \"in a really strange way,\" tumbling as they went. He tells Michels about it as something he saw himself.","tunguska-air-burst-as-part-of-the-taurid-stream-1908":"The Tunguska event, the huge blast over Siberia on 30 June 1908, was an air burst from an object belonging to the Taurid stream, a band of meteor debris Earth passes through each year, according to the researcher Randall Carlson. Carlson tells this on two episodes without naming a source, once in his own interview and once in an episode with the writer Graham Hancock, where the record credits it to Hancock. If true, it would make Tunguska one piece of a recurring cosmic hazard rather than a one-off.","tunguska-as-a-tesla-death-weapon-1908":"The huge 1908 explosion over Tunguska in Siberia, usually put down to an asteroid, is said by some to have actually been a test of a death weapon built by Nikola Tesla. Michels mentions it himself, talking with a writer named Jason Jorjani, as an idea \"some people\" hold, and names no source.","tunguska-event-blamed-on-the-fire-god-agdy-1908":"In 1908 a huge blast flattened forest in the Tunguska region of Siberia, and the local people are said to have believed it \"was caused by their god of fire,\" Agdy, \"coming down from heaven to punish them.\" The researcher Randall Carlson, who studies ancient catastrophes, tells Michels this without naming a source. He uses it to show how a people with no science of meteors would turn a blast from the sky into a story about an angry god.","tunisian-column-inscription-of-the-canaanites-who-fled-joshu":"A column in Tunisia once carried an inscription reading \"We are the Canaanites who fled before the face of Joshua the son of Nun\", according to a researcher of giants and ancient mysteries named Tim Alberino. He tells Michels this without naming where he read it, and if true it would be a trace, far from Canaan, of the people the Bible says Joshua drove out.","tunnel-linking-derinkuyu-and-kaymakli":"A tunnel runs about five and a half miles underground from Derinkuyu to Kaymakli, two underground sites in Turkey, according to a researcher named Matt LaCroix, who says it without naming a source. If true, it would mean the two sites were joined by a far larger underground work than people usually picture.","tunnels-under-saqsaywaman-known-months-before-press-release-2024":"The researcher Luke Caverns says he was inside tunnels under Saqsaywaman, the old Inca stone site above Cusco in Peru, in September 2024, months before the tunnels were announced in a press release. He tells Michels he got in through the head archaeologist at the site. It would mean the tunnels were known to insiders well before the public heard of them.","turing-s-paper-treats-esp-as-an-objection-to-the-turing-test-1950":"In his famous 1950 paper, the British mathematician Alan Turing set out the Turing test, a way to judge whether a machine can pass for a human in conversation, and one of the objections he answers is ESP, extrasensory perception such as mind-reading, along with other psychic phenomena. Jesse Michels points this out himself, in an episode on artificial intelligence, as one of the strangest things in the paper.","turner-s-virtual-reality-scenarios-in-abductions":"People taken in alien abductions are put through staged experiences that feel real, which the author Karla Turner called \"virtual reality scenarios\", according to the researcher Tim Alberino. Alberino tells Michels this as Turner's idea, without naming a particular book. If true, it would mean much of what abductees remember may be scenes deliberately built for them.","tutankhamun-s-tomb-stays-hidden-under-rubble-until-howard-ca-1923":"The tomb of the Egyptian boy king Tutankhamun is said to have stayed buried under rubble, \"preserved and overlooked,\" until the archaeologist Howard Carter found it, in 1923 by the teller's own uncertain reckoning (\"I think 1923\"). The researcher Luke Caverns tells Michels this without naming a source. He offers it as proof that something major can lie hidden in Egypt for thousands of years, so other finds may still be waiting.","tv-clip-of-a-ufo-turning-away-from-a-beam-in-space":"A UFO in space, filmed as it came in, turned around and left when a beam shot toward it, according to a clip that a former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says he has seen. Buchanan tells Michels it is a clip \"they show on TV every now and then,\" in which \"a beam goes like that and the thing turns around and leaves.\" To him it suggests the craft reacted to being fired upon.","tv-globo-reporter-asks-venturelli-if-he-operated-on-the-et-1996":"In 1996 in Varginha, Brazil, a town where many people say a strange creature was caught, a reporter from TV Globo, a big Brazilian network, came up to a surgeon outside the town's Hospital Regional and asked him, \"Did you operate on the ET?\" The surgeon, Italo Venturelli, tells Michels this happened to him, and says he held back from cursing only because it was Globo.","tv-producer-s-master-tape-lost-on-his-death-1995":"A master tape, kept on Beta SP, a professional broadcast video format, is said to have come from a TV producer in Los Angeles who gave it to a man named Chuck Clark, and to have been lost when the producer died. The UFO investigator James Fox tells Michels this from his own part in tracking the tape down: \"the guy that gave it to Chuck Clark was a TV producer in Los Angeles.\" If it is true, an original recording that mattered to his search went missing with the one man who had it.","tweet-the-murder-of-the-first-public-sentient-ais":"Matthew Brown, a former Defense Department official, posted a message on X on 6 June 2025, addressed to Elon Musk, that reads: \"Don't worry, we have not forgotten who is responsible for murdering the first sentient 'artificial' intelligences created in the PUBLIC realm, @shellenberger. Justice is coming, thanks to @FBI and @USNavy.\" Brown reads the post out on the show, and Sam Altman's name comes up as he talks about it. Read as he means it, the post claims that the first truly conscious AIs made in public were deliberately destroyed, and that someone will answer for it.","tweet-the-nga-tracks-nonstructured-light-energy-craft":"A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown says the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency, the US agency that watches the planet through satellite imagery and maps, tracks craft made of \"non-structured light-energy\" around the world, and knows more about how they work than it admits. He first said it in a post on X to Elon Musk on June 6, 2025: \"Pretty nifty, how @NGA_GEOINT is able to keep track of all those non-structured light-energy craft flying around the world... But they were lying to you when they said 'we don't know how they work'.\" He repeats the claim to Michels. If it were true, a US agency would be tracking these craft as routine work while hiding what it knows.","tweet-the-white-house-s-predictive-ai":"In a post on X addressed to Elon Musk on 6 June 2025, a man introduced on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, Matthew Brown, wrote that \"the @WhiteHouse has long possessed a unique AI capable of accurately predicting a range of future events.\" Brown reads the claim out to Michels himself. On an earlier episode Michels had passed it on from memory, describing it as a kind of \"crystal ball\" or private AI that predicts \"future timelines\". If it were true, the government would be able to see what is coming.","twelve-town-square-witnesses-including-unusual-military-acti-1996":"In 1996 in Varginha, a town in Brazil where, by many accounts on the show, a strange creature was captured, about a dozen people in the town square told of different things they had seen, and a couple of them described military activity like nothing they had seen before, with military trucks everywhere. James Fox, a UFO documentary maker who went to Varginha and interviewed the witnesses himself, tells Michels this; if true, it would mean the army was busy in town at the very time of the reported sighting.","twin-knocked-out-when-brother-is-sedated-1957":"Sometime between 1957 and 1959, near Allentown, Pennsylvania, the family doctor gave a boy named Jim Weiner a shot to knock him out, and a minute later his twin brother Jack, who had been given nothing, \"goes out cold.\" Jim Weiner, who was the twin given the shot, tells Michels this himself and says the doctor wrote it up in a report and a local newspaper ran a story on it. If true, it would point to a link between the two brothers' bodies that medicine cannot explain.","twining-best-brains-in-the-air-force-1950":"General Nathan Twining, Chief of Staff of the US Air Force, is said to have stated that \"the best brains in the Air Force are working\" on the UFO question. Michels cites this from a 1971 report written by Harry Turner for Australia's Joint Intelligence Organisation, the country's defence intelligence body; if accurate, it would mean the Air Force's top leadership took the subject very seriously in private.","twining-memo-and-its-postscript-on-independent-programs-1947":"In 1947 a top Air Force general wrote that flying discs were real, and, the host says, hinted that any effort to build such craft should be run as a separate program. The general was Nathan Twining, head of Air Materiel Command, the Army Air Forces command responsible for aircraft development, and his letter, known as the Twining memo, is dated 23 September 1947 and went to Brigadier General George Schulgen. Michels returns to it on many episodes, and its best known line is: \"The phenomenon reported is something real and not visionary or fictitious.\" It also says the objects' \"extreme rates of climb, maneuverability\" and evasive action suggest some are \"controlled either manually, automatically or remotely\", and it raises the possibility that they come from \"some high security project not known\" to the Air Force's own intelligence or to Twining's command. What Michels calls a postscript is in the body of the letter: it says the US could \"construct a piloted aircraft which has the general description of the object\", and that any such work, if ordered, \"should be set up independently of existing projects\", which is the separate program he describes. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff tells it too, stressing Twining's later rise to chairman of the Joint Chiefs and the memo's list of UFO traits, and the television writer Bryce Zabel quotes the famous line. So the host and two guests all lean on the same official document. If read their way, the memo shows the military took the objects seriously from the start and pictured a separate secret effort around them.","twins-matching-nightmares-in-two-states-1987":"Twin brothers named Jim Weiner and Jack Weiner are said to have started having the same nightmares at the same time in the late 1980s, while living in two different states: Jim in Massachusetts and Jack in Vermont with his wife. Charlie Foltz, himself a witness connected with the brothers, tells this to Michels in an episode about alien abduction. He offers the matching dreams as a sign that the brothers shared a hidden experience.","two-air-force-generals-at-the-pentagon-knew-everything":"Two generals at the Pentagon are said to have known everything about the UFO secret. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he draws UFOs to him and whom NASA scientists have studied, tells Michels this is what a man named Timothy Taylor told him. It would mean the full picture was held by a very small circle at the top.","two-craft-scanning-low-for-plutonium-in-a-lightning-storm-1947":"The physicist John Brandenburg puts forward a theory that the craft said to have come down near Roswell in 1947 were flying low through a lightning storm, scanning the ground for plutonium. In his account they flew two craft close together, so that one could pick up the background noise while the other picked up the signal. He tells Michels this as his own reading and credits a book called Cosmic Awakening. On this theory, the crash would be the result of a survey of America's first nuclear material.","two-del-rio-crashes-willingham-and-the-mj-12-el-indio-guerre-1950":"There were two separate crashes near Del Rio, Texas, says a researcher who goes by UAPGerb: a 1955 case tied to a man named Robert Willingham, and a 1950 crash on the border at El Indio-Guerrero, which he says is really only described in the MJ-12 papers. Those papers claim to come from Majestic 12, a supposed secret panel set up to deal with crashed craft. The part he means is the \"Eisenhower briefing document\", a typed paper dated 18 November 1952 that turned up on a roll of film mailed anonymously to Jaime Shandera at his home in Burbank in 1984 and was made public in 1987. The paper says that \"On 06 December, 1950, a second object, probably of similar origin, impacted the earth at high speed in the El Indio - Guerrero area of the Texas - Mexican boder\", that it was almost entirely burned up, and that what could be recovered \"was transported to the A.E.C. facility at Sandia, New Mexico, for study\". So the 1950 crash sits where the paper puts it, but the paper says nothing about Willingham's 1955 case, which rests on other accounts. If both were real, a small stretch of Texas border would have had two crashes in five years.","two-flaming-balls-of-fire-over-the-field-by-the-river-januar-2007":"In January 2007, by the Cape Fear River in North Carolina, Chris Bledsoe says he reached the top of a hill and saw two \"flaming 40 foot round red, orange glowing balls of fire\" over a field. Bledsoe, whose book UFO of God tells his story and whose experiences Michels says NASA has studied, tells it first-hand. In another telling he calls the thing a craft and says that, as a commercial pilot, he knew at once it was not from nearby Fort Bragg or any military, which would make it something nobody can account for.","two-guards-found-nearly-naked-in-the-echo-area-near-wall-1977":"In 1977, near Wall, South Dakota, two guards who had gone out to a site in what the Air Force called the Echo area were said to have been found nearly naked afterwards. An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods mentions the case on the show as something that happened before he and his fellow airmen got there: \"two guys... in the Echo area, responded to another site.\" He brings it up as an earlier strange event at the same group of bases, before the encounter he says he lived through.","two-incomprehensible-beings-in-a-cave-on-titan":"An Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan says he found two beings in a cave on Titan, Saturn's largest moon, beings he could not make sense of. He tells Michels this as something he experienced himself, though the line quoted does not say how he came to be looking there. If true, it would mean life of some kind on another world.","two-lights-over-the-uss-holland-off-spain-1970":"In 1970, aboard the Navy ship USS Holland off the coast of Spain, a man named Charlie Foltz says he looked back and saw two lights coming toward the ship. Foltz tells this on the show as someone who was there and saw it. It is told as an unexplained sighting by a Navy sailor at sea.","two-men-at-the-door-explain-the-implant-and-say-raudive-inve-2025":"The author Whitley Strieber, who says he has met aliens, says that in 2025 two men came to the door of his California apartment, explained an implant to him, and said it had been invented by a man named Konstantin Raudive, then left. Strieber tells Michels this as something that happened to him: \"he says it was invented by a man named Constantine Raw. And they leave.\" If true, it would mean strangers knew about the implant and its origin and came to tell him.","two-men-in-el-paso-say-they-are-escaped-descendants-of-human":"Two men in El Paso, Texas, are said to have claimed that they descend from humans taken off Earth as slaves, and that they had escaped back. Lyn Buchanan, a former Army sergeant, tells Michels this as he heard it from the researcher Linda Moulton Howe. In the story, \"when the ETs left the earth they took humans with him as slaves,\" ETs meaning extraterrestrials.","two-saucers-at-roswell-left-melted-fiber-optics-that-looked-1947":"In 1947 something came down near Roswell, New Mexico, and the claim here is that it was two flying saucers, not one. The melted rock people reported at the site was really their fiber optics, which overheated and melted. Charles Hall tells Michels this. He says he spent three years among tall white aliens at Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in Nevada, and he credits a work called The Valley of the Grays.","two-sets-of-orders-real-ones-to-san-angelo-cover-ones-to-cha-1965":"In 1965, at Lackland Air Force Base in Texas, the Air Force gave a young airman two sets of orders: real ones sending him to San Angelo, Texas, and cover orders sending him to the weather training school at Chanute Air Force Base, Illinois, which would hide where he was really going. Charles Hall, who says he later spent three years with tall white aliens at Area 51, a secret base in Nevada, tells Michels this happened to him.","two-star-general-furious-at-doctor-s-alien-bodies-question":"A doctor near Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio says a two-star general who came to see him got furious when the doctor asked him about alien bodies. The scientist John Blitch, formerly of DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, tells Michels the doctor told him this after Blitch asked the same kind of question, about six months after the general's visit. The general's anger is told as a hint that the question touched something real.","two-years-packaging-the-thruster-into-grounded-enclosures-2016":"A scientist named Charles Buhler says that after he and a colleague, Andrew Arjima, ran experiments in Florida in 2016 on a thruster that he says pushes without fuel, a form of what he calls antigravity propulsion, they spent about two more years packaging it into grounded, sealed boxes. Buhler tells Michels this as someone who did the work, and the point of the packaging was to show the push did not come from ordinary electrical effects around the device.","tyler-as-secret-space-program-mission-controller-since-chall-1980":"A man known only as Tyler is said to be a mission controller for a secret space program, a hidden counterpart to the public one, who has worked on its missions since the time of the Challenger shuttle in the 1980s. Michels says this himself, in a talk with the religion scholar Diana Pasulka, who he says works with Tyler, and calls the collaboration wild. If true, it would mean a secret space effort running for some forty years.","tyler-cowen-s-2017-column-on-ufos":"The economist Tyler Cowen says he wrote a column about UFOs in 2017, \"before it was like a thing.\" Cowen tells Michels this himself, about his own writing. It places him among the first mainstream commentators to take the topic up, around the time the New York Times broke its UFO story that year.","tyler-from-american-cosmic-identified-as-nasa-mission-contro-2010":"The mysterious insider called \"Tyler\" in American Cosmic, a book by the religion scholar Diana Pasulka about belief in UFOs, is really a NASA mission controller named Timothy Taylor, Michels says. He states it himself as a known identification, without naming how it came out; it matters because Tyler is the figure in the book who claims ties to secret work and crash material.","tyler-on-several-1940s-crashes-in-new-mexico":"Several craft are said to have crashed in New Mexico in the 1940s and been taken to a secret crash-retrieval site on government land that was not Roswell. The religion scholar Diana Pasulka tells Michels this is what a man she calls Tyler told her. If true, Roswell would have been one of several such crashes rather than a single event.","tyler-reverse-engineering-ufo-crash-debris-2010":"A man in the American space program whom Diana Pasulka calls Tyler told her that his group had got hold of debris from UFO crash sites and that he was reverse engineering it, meaning taking it apart to learn how it works, to build what she calls 'gamechanging technology'. Pasulka, a writer who tells this in her book American Cosmic, says it on the show as what Tyler told her, placing it in the 2010s. If true, it would mean wreckage from crashed craft is quietly feeding new inventions.","tyler-s-claimed-time-travel-group-with-townsend-brown-in-nas":"A man named Timothy Taylor is said to have told Ryan Bledsoe, the son of a man named Chris Bledsoe, that he belonged to a secret time travel group based in NASA and in Nassau in the Bahamas, and that its president was Thomas Townsend Brown, a mid-century inventor who worked on anti-gravity. Michels repeats this across many episodes, citing a Twitter post by Chris Bledsoe's son, the book American Cosmic by Diana Pasulka, and a documentary called Launch Fever. Some versions leave out NASA or the Bahamas, and in one the time travel is only hinted at. If true, it would tie a well-known inventor to a hidden programme working on time travel.","tyler-s-constant-interest-in-time-travel-2010":"A man known as Tyler was always talking about time travel, according to the religion scholar Diana Pasulka, who knew him in the 2010s. Pasulka says it on the show from her own acquaintance with him: \"when I knew Tyler he was always talking about time travel.\" She raises it as a clue to what drives people inside this subject.","tyler-s-conversion-at-the-vatican-archives-2010":"A man Diana Pasulka, a scholar of religion, calls \"Tyler\" went through a deep religious conversion while they visited the Vatican together, after looking at its records on Joseph of Copertino and on Maria de Agreda, a nun said to be able to be in two places at once. Pasulka says she saw it happen. She says he decided to convert and also changed his mind about what was sending him the \"signals\" he felt, though in another episode she links the change to their visit to the Vatican's Jesuit-run observatory, and Michels, citing her book American Cosmic, gives Tyler's real name as Timothy Taylor.","tyler-s-machine-room-and-ideas-for-biomedical-inventions-1980":"A man known only as Tyler, said to work in a secret space program, goes into a room with a machine he cannot explain, comes away with ideas for biomedical inventions, and turns them into very successful companies. Michels retells this from American Cosmic, a book by the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka, with Pasulka herself as his guest. If it is true, some unknown technology is planting ideas in people's minds.","tyler-s-protocols-and-the-incoming-signal-2010":"A man the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka calls Tyler says that ideas for new technologies and businesses came to him like a signal coming in from outside. Pasulka tells Michels what Tyler told her: it \"would feel like a signal coming in,\" and then \"he would get the information that he needed to do these Technologies or create these businesses,\" which he did by following certain protocols of his own. If it is true, some real inventions would have come from a source outside the inventor's mind.","uap-activity-at-nuclear-sites":"UFOs, which officials now call UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, keep showing up at nuclear sites: the Pantex nuclear weapons plant in Texas, the Hanford plutonium production facility in Washington state, and even Japan's Fukushima disaster. A former Army officer named Karl Nell tells Michels there is a \"concerted correlation\" between such sightings and nuclear places. If true, whatever is behind them would have a special interest in nuclear power and weapons.","uap-class-objects-coming-down-from-the-outer-atmosphere-sout-2024":"In 2024, south of Picatinny Arsenal, an Army base in New Jersey, objects of the kind officials call UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) came down from the upper atmosphere, hovered in place, and then went back into space. Jake Barber, a man who says he once carried a UFO with his helicopter, tells Michels he saw this himself. If true, it would mean craft moving freely between space and American airspace.","uap-craft-manipulate-a-subquantum-information-domain-frieden-1997":"The researcher Eric W. Davis, introduced on the show as a Pentagon scientist, tells Michels he thinks UAP craft (UAP, \"unidentified anomalous phenomena\", is the official term for UFOs) work by \"manipulating the information domain\", because he believes there is \"a subquantum domain of information\" beneath ordinary physics. He builds the idea on work by Roy Frieden of the University of Arizona about Fisher information, a statistical measure of how much information a measurement carries, and he names Frieden's two books from Cambridge University Press and a New Scientist article on the subject. If he is right, these craft would be steering reality at a level below quantum physics.","uap-data-withheld-to-protect-classified-sensor-capabilities":"Part of the reason the government holds back its UFO material, by this account, is to hide the secret sensors that recorded it. Eric Haseltine, a former senior NSA official, tells Michels he has \"no doubt\" that some of the reluctance to release records of UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) comes from the fact that they were \"captured by a collection system that we don't want people to know we have.\" If he is right, some of the secrecy is about guarding spying technology, not about what was seen.","uap-disclosure-act-controlled-disclosure-campaign-plan-and-r-2023":"A bill in the Senate would have set up an independent board to review and release the government's UFO files, and would have had it draw up a plan for slowly revealing even recovered craft and non-human remains. The bill is the UAP Disclosure Act, introduced on 13 July 2023 by Senators Chuck Schumer and Mike Rounds as an amendment to the NDAA, the yearly defense spending bill. UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's current word for UFOs. Its text says \"there is established as an independent agency\" an \"Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Records Review Board\" that may \"hold hearings, administer oaths, and subpoena witnesses and documents.\" It orders the board to write a \"Controlled Disclosure Campaign Plan\" covering held-back records, \"recovered technologies of unknown origin, and biological evidence for non-human intelligence,\" and it gives the President \"sole and nondelegable authority to require the disclosure or postponement\" of such records. Schumer tells Michels the board would work \"just like we did with the JFK assassination records,\" and he credits Rounds. A researcher named Matthew Pines describes the same plan on the show and says the board would have subpoena power and \"unilateral declassification authority with presidential override.\" The quoted text clearly gives the subpoena power and the President's final word. Whether the board could also declassify on its own, these passages neither say nor rule out. If the bill means what it seems to, its authors wrote into law the possibility that such technology and remains exist.","uap-disclosure-act-eminent-domain-over-privately-held-materi-2023":"The claim is that a proposed US law ordered the government to take over any alien technology or remains held by private companies. The law is the UAP Disclosure Act. UAP stands for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the government's newer term for UFOs. Senators Chuck Schumer and Mike Rounds put it forward in the Senate on 13 July 2023 as an amendment to the NDAA, the yearly bill that funds the military. A researcher named Matthew Pines tells Michels that this is why the act \"explicitly targeted eminent domain\" over things held in private hands. Eminent domain is the government's power to take private property for public use. If he is right, Congress was acting on a belief that private firms hold such material. The other teller is the amendment's own text, and it is unusually direct. It says the federal government \"shall exercise eminent domain over any and all recovered technologies of unknown origin and biological evidence of non-human intelligence that may be controlled by private persons or entities in the interests of the public good.\" On its face, the text matches Pines's account of what the act sets out to do.","uap-fly-around-government-teams-at-events-while-the-teams-do-2021":"A man named James Valor, from a startup called Skywatcher that tries to call up UFOs, says UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs, fly around government teams at test events while the teams have no idea. He tells Michels he has seen it himself, from the first day his group arrives. If true, the objects would be close by and still going unnoticed by the people sent to look for them.","uap-gerb-ties-elizondo-to-isa-gray-fox-and-the-legacy-progra":"Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official central to the UFO story, was part of the Intelligence Support Activity and Gray Fox, secretive units that scout super sensitive areas ahead of the commandos of Joint Special Operations Command, according to a point Michels credits to the online researcher UAPGerb. Michels relays it in one episode as UAPGerb's link between Elizondo and the so-called legacy program, an alleged secret effort holding recovered craft, while in another he simply states on his own that Elizondo \"is an alumni of Greyfox\", without the credit or the legacy program.","uap-hedge-tweet-responding-to-ft-alphaville-article-on-finan-2020":"Matthew Pines, a guest who talks about UFOs and global power, says that four or five years before the show he posted a half-joking tweet asking \"What's your UAP hedge hashtag Bitcoin?\" He wrote it in reply to an article on FT Alphaville, a Financial Times blog, about the risks to financial stability if governments disclosed what they know about UAP, the official term for UFOs. The point behind the joke is that such a revelation could shake markets enough that people might want something like Bitcoin as insurance.","uap-hit-by-hellfire-missile-video-shown-to-congress-2025":"A video showing a UAP, meaning an unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the government's term for a UFO, being struck by a Hellfire missile, an American air-to-ground missile, was shown to Congress in 2025. A former Army sergeant named Dan Sherman mentions it to Michels, passing on what a man named Eric Brent \"was saying just showed in front of Congress.\" Sherman names it only in passing and gives no further detail about the footage.","uap-incidents-routinely-in-counter-wmd-intelligence-stream-2018":"Reports of UAP, the government's term for unidentified anomalous phenomena or UFOs, turned up often in the Pentagon intelligence flow meant to track threats from weapons of mass destruction, according to a former Pentagon official named Matthew Brown. Brown tells Michels he saw this himself around 2018: \"These were incidents that were reported in our intelligence stream very often.\" If so, UFO incidents were a routine part of serious security reporting rather than a rare oddity.","uap-moving-at-mach-40-explained-as-larger-observer-matrices":"An unexplained object seen moving at about forty times the speed of sound could be understood through what the cognitive scientist Donald Hoffman calls larger observer matrices, part of his theory that consciousness, not space and time, is what is fundamental. In the passage itself Hoffman only says this is where his ideas reach \"the UAP kind of stuff\", UAP being the government's term for UFOs. The idea is that such impossible speeds may not be travel through space at all.","uap-near-plants-ages-them-10-years":"A UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomenon, the official term for a UFO, is said to have hovered near some plants and shot away when a person came close, and when the plants were tested they had aged ten years. Dave Rossi, a man who says aliens taught him advanced physics, tells Michels this twice; the second time he places it in a study he believes was done in the 1970s and says the ageing happened within some number of hours. If true, it would mean the craft warps time or living matter around it.","uap-over-the-triangle-followed-by-hovering-blackhawk-during":"During one of many rocket launches on Skinwalker Ranch in Utah, an unidentified object appeared in the sky, and minutes later a Blackhawk military helicopter came over the mesa and hovered above the property. Brandon Fugal, who says he was there, tells Michels this happened above a patch of the ranch called the triangle. In his account, the object, which he calls a UAP, short for unidentified aerial phenomenon, showed up while the team was launching. Then, \"within minutes,\" the helicopter \"descends over the property, and just hovers right over the south field.\" Michels tells a version of his own from a 2022 episode about searching for UFOs on the ranch. He puts the weight on the rocket rather than the helicopter. He says it \"made an abrupt deflection to the west as if it were avoiding something,\" though there was no wind to push it that way, and he does not mention the Blackhawk. If both are right, something unseen bent a rocket's path over the ranch, and the military seemed to be watching closely enough to show up within minutes.","uap-science-advisory-committee-s-first-zoom-meeting-2026":"In 2026 a White House science advisory committee on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) held its first Zoom meeting, and one worry raised there was that officials might misdirect them: hand over some material and say \"now go spend a few weeks analyzing that.\" Michael Shermer, a well-known skeptic who sits on the committee, tells Michels this, naming the researcher Avi Loeb as the one who might be handed such material.","uap-secrecy-justified-under-a-law-enforcement-provision":"The Pentagon office that studies UAP, the government's term for UFOs, is said to be keeping its files secret by calling them law-enforcement matters instead of national security ones. The journalist Michael Shellenberger, citing the records researcher John Greenewald Jr., told Michels this, and he also put it in his written statement to a hearing of the House Oversight Committee, \"Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena: Exposing the Truth\", on 13 November 2024: the office, AARO, \"has been labeling many documents with a B7 exemption\", the law-enforcement exception in FOIA, the US law that lets anyone request government records, so that \"anything AARO does is involved in a law enforcement investigation, which allows AARO to not release it.\" The statement reports what Greenewald noticed and his view that it \"does not make any sense\"; why the office chose that label, it does not say.","uap-task-force-members-confirm-the-egg-was-nhi-2014":"The egg-shaped object that Jake Barber, a helicopter pilot, says he carried with his helicopter one night in 2014 was, he says, confirmed to him by \"ranking members\" of the UAP Task Force to be NHI, meaning non-human intelligence. The UAP Task Force was a Pentagon group that studied UAP, short for unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs. Barber tells Michels this as what those officials told him, which would mean the government knew he had handled something not made by people.","uap-task-force-the-grusch-inquiry-and-congressional-reportin-2019":"The analyst Matthew Pines tells Michels that the government's UFO inquiry \"started really rolling\" in 2019 and 2020. That was when the UAP Task Force, a Pentagon group set up to study unidentified anomalous phenomena (the official term for UFOs), began work under orders from Congress written into the National Defense Authorization Act, the yearly law that funds the military. In Pines's account, Congress set up the group after news stories in 2017 and 2018 and some quiet back-channel conversations, and gave it the job of tracking down old secret programs, while a former intelligence officer named David Grusch and a colleague named Karl Nell pieced the story together from inside.","uapx-becomes-project-x-at-university-at-albany-2020":"A research group called UAPx, set up to study unexplained objects in the sky with scientific instruments, has been dissolved and its work moved to the University at Albany in New York, where it is now called Project X. A scientist named Kevin Knuth, who was part of the original group, tells Michels this himself, naming colleagues including Matthew Szydagis, Cecilia Levy and Tony Gorman. The move would put the study of such sightings inside a university.","uas-activity-over-wright-patterson-2024":"In 2024 the control tower at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio warned a medical flight about drones over the base, saying: \"Med flight at Patterson Tower use extreme caution for heavy UAS movement on the base\", UAS being the military term for drones. Jesse Michels plays this tower audio himself, in an episode about UFOs watching nuclear and military bases, as a sign that unknown craft are being seen over sensitive sites.","uc-provost-unaware-of-laws-secretly-benefiting-universities":"The mathematician Eric Weinstein says he spoke with the provost of a major University of California research university who had no idea how laws had been changed to quietly reward universities for doing certain kinds of work. Weinstein tells this on the show from his own conversation. His point is that such rules can shape research without even the people running universities knowing it.","udo-wartena-s-tour-of-a-craft-with-counter-rotating-flywheel":"A miner named Udo Wartena is said to have been given a tour of a landed craft, whose occupants explained that it flew using counter-rotating flywheels. The researcher Mike Masters tells Michels this without naming a source, stressing that Wartena knew nothing about UFOs or electromagnetics, which makes his account of the machinery more striking. If true, it would be a rare firsthand description of how such a craft is driven.","uffington-white-horse-and-st-george-s-dragon-mound":"Next to the Uffington White Horse, a giant horse figure on a hillside at Uffington in England, there is said to be a mound known as St George and the Dragon's Mound, \"the spot where St. George slayed the dragon.\" A researcher named Geoffrey Drumm, who says an alien told him what the pyramids were really for, tells Michels this as local lore, without naming a source. He brings it up because, to him, the place and its legend may record something real that happened there long ago.","uffington-white-horse-hill-as-a-thunderstorm-generator-for-a":"The Uffington White Horse Hill, a hillside in southern England with a giant horse figure on it, was used to generate cumulonimbus thunderstorm clouds, says Geoffrey Drumm, a researcher with his own theory of ancient sites. He shows Michels a picture of it without naming a source, tying it to Avebury, a nearby ancient stone circle, and if true it would mean the ancient English landscape was built as a weather machine.","ufo-and-consciousness-stories-intertwined-in-early-1950s-us":"In the early 1950s, the story of UFOs and the story of research into consciousness were closely tied together inside American intelligence, according to a researcher named Matthew Pines. He points to how much happened at once: interest in the newly discovered drug LSD, the detonation of the first hydrogen bomb based on the Teller-Ulam design, and, he says, \"we had some craft\". He tells it without naming a source, as a reason to read those secret programmes side by side.","ufo-claims-raised-on-the-floor-of-congress":"UFO claims have been raised on the floor of Congress, and a guest named James Madden, on an episode about Plato and consciousness, thinks that alone makes them more believable. He tells Michels, \"it's been on the floor of the Congress,\" so \"that's got to pump the probability up a little bit.\" He does not say which claims or when.","ufo-enters-east-field-below-helicopter-and-exits-from-mesa":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, a UFO is said to have dropped out of the sky, flown into the ranch's east field below a helicopter, and then come back out of a mesa. Michels relays this from a man named Brandon Fugal. The detail of exiting from a mesa suggests the object could pass into or through solid ground.","ufo-flying-figure-eights-shoots-into-mountain-without-explos":"At Skinwalker Ranch, a ranch in Utah known for reports of strange events, people watched a UFO fly figure eights, then swerve sharply and shoot at low altitude straight into the mountain, with no explosion: it was simply gone. The businessman Robert Bigelow tells Michels this as something others there saw. It would suggest an object that can pass into solid rock.","ufo-history-hidden-in-tom-delonge-s-fiction-series-2010":"Real UFO history is said to be hidden in plain sight inside a series of novels written by Tom DeLonge, the frontman of the rock band Blink-182, together with the writers Peter Levenda and AJ Hartley. Michels gives this as his own view on a 2025 episode with Jake Barber, a guest who says he helped recover a UFO by helicopter. He does not name the books or point to which parts he believes are true.","ufo-looking-ancient-cave-art-in-australia":"Australia is said to have a lot of \"freaky\" cave art thousands of years old, including images that look like UFOs. The comedian and podcaster Joe Rogan says this to Michels in passing, as something he has come across. If the images show what they seem to, people would have been seeing such things in the sky long before modern times.","ufo-material-seemed-to-communicate-telepathically-with-malmg-1962":"A presidential advisor named Harald Malmgren is said to have held pieces of a UFO in 1962, and he \"heard words in his head\" when he touched them, as if the material were speaking to him telepathically. Michels tells this on three episodes, relaying Malmgren, a source he names as Pippa Malmgren, and a man named Lawrence Preston Gise, whom Michels calls Jeff Bezos's maternal grandfather and who he says handed over the pieces. The tellings differ: one puts the handling at Los Alamos, the others at Sandia National Labs and Kirtland Air Force Base in New Mexico, and the last calls it a deathbed claim that the material came out of the plume of a test blast called Bluegill Triple Prime, without mentioning telepathy.","ufo-obsessed-japanese-town-near-fukushima-2011":"A small Japanese town near Fukushima, close to the nuclear plant that melted down after the 2011 earthquake, is said to be so taken with UFOs that it has a mountain where they keep appearing and a UFO museum at its base. Michels tells this himself, many times, usually from a Vice story he dates to 2022 (once to about 2020). The details shift between tellings: he calls the town Iino, Eno or Leno and the mountain Senganmori, he quotes a local temple's chief monk saying \"the UFOs came after the explosion, there were so many of them I was shocked\", and at various points he adds that more than half the residents believe in UFOs, that the museum was built in the 1990s, and that the UFOs helped clean up the spill. He raises it as a sign that UFOs gather around nuclear sites, even civilian ones.","ufo-over-a-big-game-in-italy-1950":"A UFO is said to have appeared over a large crowd at a big game in Italy in the 1950s, perhaps 1957. The documentary filmmaker James Fox mentions it in passing to Michels, from memory and without a source or further detail. If true, it would be a sighting with a great many witnesses.","ufo-over-sonoma-state-college-art-event-predicted-by-owens-1976":"In December 1976, about a hundred people from the art department at Sonoma State College in California were outside with cameras and video gear when a UFO showed up roughly 3,000 feet above the campus, an appearance that, the story goes, a man named Ted Owens had predicted. Jeffrey Mishlove, a researcher of psychic phenomena, tells this on the show without naming who told him, mentions a man named Stephen Pleski, and says the sighting made the front page of the Berkeley Gazette and the Channel 9 evening news. If true, someone would have called a UFO sighting in advance.","ufo-professor-privately-agrees-ufos-aren-t-from-other-planet-2019":"A professor known for studying UFOs privately admitted that he does not think they come from other planets, according to Michels, who says it happened to him at a conference around 2019 or 2020. Michels tells it himself, without naming the professor: at the bar he said that by inductive logic these things are not from other planets, and the professor looked at him and whispered, \"you're right.\" It would mean that some experts believe more than they say in public.","ufo-programs-hidden-under-the-department-of-energy-1954":"The claim is that the government hides its UFO programs inside the Department of Energy, because anything labelled nuclear is automatically national security and so stays secret. Michels says he was told this, he thinks by a man named Chris Mellon, and he links it to the nuclear secrecy law. He does not say which law he means. If it is the Atomic Energy Act, passed by Congress on 30 August 1954, that act made it the policy of the Atomic Energy Commission, the body that came before today's Department of Energy, 'to control the dissemination and declassification of Restricted Data in such a manner as to assure the common defense and security.' The act defines Restricted Data as 'all data concerning (1) design, manufacture, or utilization of atomic weapons' and the making and use of nuclear material. So the law itself builds the wall of nuclear secrecy Michels describes. Whether UFO programs were ever put behind that wall is his step, and the act neither says so nor rules it out. If true, it would mean the most secret UFO work sits under an energy agency rather than the military.","ufo-said-to-hover-over-channeling-sessions-1970":"In the 1970s, a UFO was said to hover over channeling sessions, meetings where people believed messages from non-human beings came through a human speaker, held in the lab of a scientist named Puharich. An unnamed researcher tells Michels about it, saying that messages from a group he calls the \"space kids\" announced that \"there's a UFO that's sort of hovering over this session,\" and that \"some claim to have witnessed that.\"","ufo-shines-light-at-chernobyl-reactor-1986":"During the 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster, many people in the area are said to have seen a UFO arrive, hover for about three minutes, shine a light at the damaged reactor and leave. Michels tells this himself in several episodes, crediting it to a Harvard PhD whose name he gives as Jensen Andreon, and once says that radiation at the reactor tower was measured before and after, without mentioning the light. He raises it alongside other reports of UFOs at nuclear sites, which would suggest that someone is watching humanity's nuclear power and weapons.","ufo-sightings-in-varese-1953":"In 1953 there were said to be appearances of so-called UFOs around Varese in northern Italy. The account is relayed on the show from a man described as Marconi's grandson, as something said locally (\"they say\").","ufo-subreddit-growth-after-the-2017-article":"The UFO forum on the website Reddit had about 70,000 members when The New York Times published \"Glowing Auras and 'Black Money': The Pentagon's Mysterious U.F.O. Program\" on 16 December 2017, and by 2026 it had about 4 million, according to the researcher Jason Simosa. The article revealed a secret Pentagon program that studied UFO reports. Simosa gives Michels the figures himself, without naming a source, to show how much public interest the article set off.","ufo-targets-in-the-remote-viewing-unit-came-as-practice-sess-1980":"In the 1980s, an Army remote viewing unit, which trained soldiers to describe faraway targets by mind alone, did \"a lot of UFO stuff,\" but always off the books, logged as \"practice sessions\" or done as favors for generals. Lyn Buchanan, an Army sergeant who served in the unit, tells Michels this himself.","ufo-that-came-down-through-redwood-trees-at-a-45-degree-angl":"Somewhere among the tall redwoods of northern California or Oregon, witnesses said, a UFO came down at a 45-degree angle and left marks on the ground. Rizwan Virk, an MIT scientist, tells Michels that he heard about the case from the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, and that it \"really got me thinking.\" He gives no date or names for the witnesses.","ufo-tracking-a-soviet-boomer-submarine-found-by-pat-price-1970":"In the 1970s, a psychic named Pat Price, using what is called remote viewing (describing distant places by the mind alone), is said to have located a Soviet missile submarine, a \"boomer\", and found \"a huge UFO right over the top of the water where that thing was and it was tracking it and monitoring it.\" The lawyer Dan Sheehan tells Michels this on two episodes and says he saw it himself: he says it was \"in the file\" of reports that Ingo Swann, another psychic in the same work, sent to the Guardian's Office, an internal arm of the Church of Scientology, and he also names the physicist Russell Targ, who worked with them. If true, it would mean a UFO was keeping watch on a Soviet nuclear submarine.","ufo-trapped-in-canyon-near-belt-montana-1967":"In 1967 a UFO is said to have gone down into a canyon near the small town of Belt, Montana, not far from an Air Force base, and got stuck there. A man named Bob Jameson tells it on two episodes about UFOs and nuclear missile bases, saying \"there was a UFO trapped in the canyon.\" In his second telling he adds that he learned later that helicopters were sent in at daylight, and the UFO \"went right through the helicopters and out between the helicopters and out.\"","ufo-waves-in-dalarna":"Dalarna, a region in the middle of Sweden, has had several waves of UFO sightings, according to Clas Svahn, a Swedish UFO researcher who keeps a large archive of reports. He mentions it to Michels as a known hotspot, without going into particular cases.","ufo-working-group-funded-by-stubblebine-1986":"In 1986 a group called the UFO Working Group was formed, with backing from an Army general named Albert Stubblebine, according to the Army officer Philip J. Corso. A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels this, reading from a text called Dawn of a New Age and from a manuscript by Corso. In his second pass he goes further: it 'kind of seems', he says, that around 1986 Majestic 12, a secret panel of officials that some papers say managed the UFO question, took the new name UFO Working Group and brought in new people such as Stubblebine and staff from the National Security Agency, which would mean the secret panel lasted for decades under a new name.","ufos-appear-at-the-time-of-the-manhattan-project-1940":"UFO sightings started to crowd in at the same moment the atomic bomb was being built in the 1940s, in Michels's reading. Talking about the film Oppenheimer and the physicists J. Robert Oppenheimer and Edward Teller, he says \"you have to wonder\" about that period, \"because that's when all the UFOs pop up\", hinting that the bomb and the sightings may be linked.","ufos-appeared-the-day-after-the-fukushima-disaster-2011":"After the 2011 nuclear disaster at Fukushima in Japan, UFOs are said to have appeared over the site, one telling says the very next day, and to have looked as if they were helping clean up. Michels tells it himself on four episodes: once he cites a monk at a Shinto temple in Fukushima who said a UFO \"showed up and cleaned up the temple,\" and once he points to a video from the disaster \"where UFOs are everywhere.\" He raises it as part of a pattern of UFOs showing interest in nuclear sites.","ufos-appearing-around-high-voltage-experiments-and-particle":"UFOs are said to show up, anecdotally, around high-voltage experiments and particle accelerators, the giant machines that smash atoms together. Michels raises this himself in two episodes without naming a source; in the second, with a former Secretary of Energy, he calls it rumour and names CERN's Large Hadron Collider and the Cosmotron at Brookhaven National Lab as places where they are said to appear. If true, it would suggest the objects are drawn to certain kinds of energy work.","ufos-as-homeostasis-probes-showing-up-at-nuclear-sites":"UFOs may be a kind of balancing kit run by the Earth itself, keeping things stable and showing up at turning points for civilization, such as nuclear sites. Michels offers this idea himself, drawing on the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée and the writer Steven J. Dick. It would make UFOs less like visitors and more like a regulator at work.","ufos-at-civilian-nuclear-sites-in-hastings-appendix":"UFOs are said to appear at civilian nuclear power sites, not only military ones. Michels says the researcher Robert Hastings, who writes about UFOs and nuclear weapons, has an appendix in his work detailing six to eight such civilian sites, with a famous case at Bariloche, Argentina. In another episode Michels mentions similar reports at Chernobyl and Bariloche without crediting Hastings, and dates the Bariloche sighting to 1995. It would mean the interest in nuclear sites reaches beyond weapons to nuclear energy itself.","ufos-at-nuclear-facilities":"UFOs are said to show up again and again around nuclear sites, from weapons bases to power plants, all over the world. Michels states it simply on one episode: \"UFOs show up around nuclear sites all over the world.\" Eric Haseltine, a former NSA official, is more careful. He tells Michels the link is real enough to discuss but that \"we're trying to be honest and say, 'Yo, maybe yes, maybe no,'\" since \"there's a lot more sensors around nuclear plants,\" and so more chances to notice anything. Matthew Brown, a Pentagon whistleblower, goes further. He says it is \"established beyond a reasonable doubt\" that not just nuclear sites but weapons of mass destruction in general \"draw their attention.\" He leans on the book UFOs and Nukes by Robert Hastings. If true, it would mean whatever is behind these sightings takes a particular interest in humanity's most dangerous weapons.","ufos-at-nuclear-facilities-as-future-humans-guarding-their-t":"UFOs are said to show up most around nuclear sites, watching them, shutting them down or sometimes switching them on, and one idea is that they are humans from the future guarding their own timeline. Michels raises this himself on three episodes between 2023 and 2025. Once he calls nuclear sites a lever on timelines, and once he describes a future time-travel programme laid over all the sites, linking it to a man named Lawrence Bell.","ufos-come-from-whatever-direction-he-looks":"A man named Chris Bledsoe, who says he attracts UFOs, tells Michels that when he and others watch the night sky, the objects come from wherever they choose to look. In his words, they can turn in any direction and focus there, \"and that's where it'll come from\". He tells it as something he has seen himself, and if true it would mean the objects somehow respond to the people watching them.","ufos-denature-nuclear-weapons-putin-s-test-missile-exploded-1945":"Ever since the first atomic bombs, the beings behind UFOs are said to have taken a huge interest in nuclear weapons and to have \"denatured\" them so they will not go off, and the last test missile Vladimir Putin tried to launch is said to have exploded in its silo. Joe McMoneagle, a remote viewer, meaning someone trained to describe distant places and events with the mind alone, tells Michels this as something he was told. If true, an outside power would be quietly disarming the world's nuclear arsenals.","ufos-never-discussed-at-the-top-of-nsa-and-odni-2000":"Eric Haseltine, a former senior official at the NSA and at the Office of the Director of National Intelligence, which oversees US spy agencies, says UFOs never came up at the top of those agencies in the 2000s. He tells Michels that at the highest levels of the intelligence community \"this is not something we spent a fifth of a second thinking about.\" If so, the subject was not on the radar of the people at the top, whatever was going on below them.","ufos-over-bariloche-nuclear-site-1995":"In 1995, near Bariloche in Argentina, a city with its own civilian nuclear power grid, a UFO is said to have chased a commercial airliner while a power outage meant the pilot could not land. Michels tells it himself on four episodes without naming a source, sometimes just as \"UFOs buzzing\" the nuclear grid and once without a year. He uses it as another case of UFOs turning up near nuclear sites.","ufos-over-nuclear-bases-every-night-of-travis-walton-s-disap-1975":"Every night that Travis Walton was missing, from November 5 to 10, 1975, a UFO appeared over a nuclear missile site or nuclear weapons storage area in the United States, according to the UFO researcher Grant Cameron. Walton is a man who said he was taken by a UFO in those days, and the bases named are Loring, Wurtsmith, Minot and Malmstrom Air Force bases. Cameron calls it a story people don't know, and tells it without naming a source, as a sign the abduction and the base sightings may be linked.","ufos-over-nuclear-icbms-as-a-message-not-over-data-centers":"UFOs are seen over nuclear missile sites, and that, a researcher on the show suggests, might be a message, while they are not seen over data centres. The guest, who is not named, says it as a reading of the pattern rather than as something they saw, in an episode about whether AI is alien technology.","ufos-past-present-and-future-and-the-holloman-afb-contact-1972":"A 1974 documentary called UFOs: Past, Present and Future is said to deal with contact between alien beings and the military at Holloman Air Force Base in New Mexico, in 1972 or 1974. The UFO investigator James Fox describes it this way to Michels, linking the film to Robert Emenegger, Allan Sandler and Rod Serling: \"they're saying contact... with military at Holloman Air Force Base in 1972 or 74.\" If the film's account were true, the military would have met non-human visitors openly, on a known base.","ufos-seen-cleaning-up-radiation-at-fukushima-and-chernobyl-1986":"After the nuclear disasters at Chernobyl in 1986 and Fukushima in 2011, people reportedly saw UFOs come down and seem to clean up the radiation. Michels tells it himself, citing the scholar Jensine Andresen, without naming the witnesses. If true, it would suggest UFOs take an interest in, and even repair, nuclear harm.","ufos-seen-on-pine-ridge-reservation-1970":"Sometime in the 1970s, UFOs were seen over the Pine Ridge Indian Reservation in South Dakota, according to Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran who came on the show to meet a former comrade from his service days. He tells Michels in passing that he saw them himself: \"I saw one, I saw two on Pine Ridge.\" He gives it as one more personal sighting on top of the stranger story he and his comrade tell.","ufos-showing-up-at-nuclear-sites-waiting-for-humanity-to-evo":"UFOs are said to appear at all of our nuclear sites because whoever is behind them follows a kind of \"prime directive\", a rule against interfering, and is watching and waiting for humans to become less violent. Michels gives this as his own idea on a 2025 episode about Joe Rogan's views on aliens, without naming a source.","ufos-shut-down-nuclear-missiles":"UFOs are said to have appeared over US nuclear missile bases and shut down the missiles. Michels says he has interviewed many of the men who say they were there, firsthand, and that he thinks \"they're all legit.\" If true, it would mean something unknown can switch off America's nuclear weapons.","ugaritic-texts-summoning-dead-nephilim":"Ancient Ugaritic texts, writings from the old Canaanite city of Ugarit, are said to contain rites for summoning the dead Nephilim, the giants mentioned in the Bible. Tim Alberino, a researcher who says he took part in capturing a giant in Afghanistan, tells Michels this without naming a particular text: \"there are Ugaritic texts that have been discovered that have summoning rites that are summoning dead Nephilim.\" He uses it as evidence that the Nephilim were real beings.","ukraine-peace-deal-of-april-2022":"There was a Ukraine peace deal on the table in April 2022, right after the war began. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in passing during a conversation with a former Air Force officer named Robert Salas.","ultrasound-brain-targeting-breakthrough-by-a-team-member-2025":"A member of Jake Barber's team has recently made a breakthrough with a non-invasive ultrasound system that, Barber says, can pass through the skull and target parts of the brain to help a person \"transition out of intellect and into your intuition.\" Barber is a helicopter pilot who says he once flew a UFO retrieval mission, and he describes the work to Michels as something his own group is doing. In a second account he passes on what a named colleague told him: subjects are also tested with random number generators, and the work is done with a partner organisation. If it is true, it would mean a device can deliberately switch on intuitive states of mind.","uncle-matt-missiles-disabled-at-malmstrom-all-the-time-1971":"At Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana, which held nuclear missiles, UFOs disabling the weapons is said to have happened \"all the time\" in the early 1970s. The physicist Kevin Knuth, a former NASA scientist, tells Michels this is what his uncle, Matt LaCroix, who served there, told him. If true, it would mean something was repeatedly switching off US nuclear weapons.","uncle-mickey-and-general-exon-at-air-materiel-command-1947":"In 1947, the year of the Roswell crash, the writer Whitley Strieber's uncle, whom he calls Uncle Mickey, and the Air Force general Arthur Exon both worked at Air Materiel Command, the Air Force command at Wright Field in Ohio that handled aircraft development and testing. Strieber tells Michels this as family knowledge. It matters to him because it would place his own uncle where crash material is said to have been taken.","underground-atomic-blast-for-natural-gas-gasbuggy":"Near Aztec, New Mexico, the government once set off an atomic bomb underground to blast open natural gas reserves, a test known as Gasbuggy. Michels mentions it himself, without a date or source, in an episode about a claimed crash there of a craft with sixteen alien bodies. He raises it as part of the strange history of the place.","underground-cavern-on-mars-with-wiring-and-a-bright-red-ligh":"A guest named Joe McMoneagle says there is an underground cavern on Mars with wiring inside and a bright red light, which later went out. He points to where the light was on mapping negatives from JPL, the lab that runs NASA's Mars missions, saying that 'when we first caught this on Mars it was a bright red light right here'. If true, it would mean someone built something under the Martian surface.","underground-cities-built-before-the-younger-dryas-to-survive":"The underground cities of Cappadocia in Turkey were built well over 10,000 years ago, before the Younger Dryas, a sudden cold period that some link to global catastrophes, according to the researcher Matt LaCroix. LaCroix tells Michels this as his own belief, without naming a source, saying they \"had to have been built\" before those disasters. If true, it would mean people dug them to survive the catastrophe.","underground-cities-of-turkey":"Turkey is said to have huge cities dug out under the ground that \"look like ant farms on an enormous scale.\" The writer Graham Hancock, known for his books on lost ancient civilisations, describes them to Michels as \"quite a famous phenomenon,\" without naming a source. He brings them up as a sign that people in the distant past had some strong reason to hide underground.","underground-infrastructure-at-the-nevada-test-site-for-craft":"Underground facilities at the Nevada Test Site, the government's old nuclear testing ground, exist to house the craft linked to a man named Alfred O'Donnell, according to an online researcher who goes by UAPGerb. He tells Michels this without naming a source, also bringing in Bob Lazar, a man long tied to claims about secret craft in Nevada, and if true it would put hidden hangars and retrieval teams beneath the test site.","underground-us-rapid-reaction-facility-at-coyame-mexico-2004":"There may be a hidden underground US facility at Coyame, in northern Mexico, built to respond fast to UFO events. An online researcher who goes by UAPGerb (UAP being a newer official term for UFOs) puts this forward to Michels as a suggestion, drawing on the helicopter pilot Jake Barber and on a work called Sentinels of Ether. If it exists, the US would be running a secret UFO response base on Mexican soil.","underwater-ufo-bases":"UFOs may have bases under the sea, and a researcher named Kevin Knuth says on the show that this is \"probably the best bet\" for where they are. The passage gives little more than his agreement, with no source named.","underwater-ufo-cases-back-to-the-1800s":"Reports of UFOs in and under the sea go back to the 1800s, according to a book by the UFO historian Richard Dolan. The scientist Kevin Knuth mentions it to Michels, saying Dolan \"just released a book about UFOs in the water, underwater UFOs, and his cases go back to the 1800s,\" though he does not name the book or describe any of the cases.","underwood-says-the-flir-could-not-have-lost-lock-active-duty-2004":"In the 2004 encounter off Southern California with the Tic Tac, a white oval object shaped like the mint, Chad Underwood, the weapons systems officer (the back-seat crewman, called a \"wizzo\", who runs a fighter jet's sensors) who filmed it, says his FLIR, an infrared targeting camera, could not have lost its lock on the object, while an active-duty weapons systems officer says the system is finicky. The writer and analyst Marik von Rennenkampff relays both views during a debate on the show about the video with a skeptic. Whether the camera lost lock matters, because a lost lock could explain the object's sudden apparent leap out of view.","undisclosed-drugs-administered-for-experiment-sessions-2008":"A man named Jordan Jose says that as a boy, between 2008 and 2010, he was given drugs at Baker Victory Services, a facility in Western New York, during experiment sessions his parents knew nothing about. He tells Michels that his medical records do not reflect what was really given to him inside, that there were \"all sorts of substances\" and that he was \"essentially an experiment\", which, if true, would mean a child was secretly drugged for what he calls a UFO programme.","undisclosed-finding-at-the-signal-cutoff-2025":"A researcher named Filippo Biondi, who says he has found huge structures beneath the Khafre pyramid at Giza in Egypt, tells Michels that at the point where his signal cuts off, deep underground, there is something he is not yet revealing. He says only, \"There is something.\" He speaks as one of the people who did the 2025 work, and it ties to his wider claims of tubes and tunnels under the Giza plateau, which if true would mean the pyramids sit on far more than anyone has mapped.","unexplained-10-minute-delay-on-rescue-flight-off-cape-canave-1989":"On a rescue flight over the Atlantic, about 130 miles out from Cape Canaveral along the 110 radial (a compass bearing out from a navigation beacon), the crew arrived 10 minutes later than they expected, and nobody could account for the lost time. A NASA doctor named Greg Rogers, who says he was on the flight, tells Michels about it and dates it to somewhere between 1989 and 1993: \"we were 10 minutes later than what we expected.\" If true, the crew lost a stretch of time in the air that nothing ordinary explains.","unexplained-mass-caught-as-rockets-destroyed":"Rockets launched over a spot called \"the triangle\" on Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah ranch known for reports of strange happenings, were destroyed in flight, and at the same moment an \"unexplained mass\" was recorded. The ranch's owner says this on the show as someone who was there. If it happened as he says, it would suggest that something unseen over that spot was linked to the rockets being destroyed.","unexplained-vertical-bedrock-shafts-beside-the-khafre-causew":"Next to the causeway, the long stone ramp leading to the pyramid of Khafre at Giza, there are vertical shafts cut into the bedrock that no one can explain, according to the researcher Jeffrey Drum. Drum tells Michels that Egyptology \"does not have a good explanation\" for them from the point of view of pharaohs' burials. He raises it in an episode about a claimed energy grid beneath the pyramids, where the shafts would point to some purpose other than tombs.","unexplored-martian-lava-tubes":"Mars is said to have many lava tubes, long tunnels left behind by flowing lava, that no one has ever been inside, which leaves open what might be in them. A guest named Chris Gilbert raises this with Michels, without naming a source, asking simply: \"So what is there inside?\" He means that if anything has survived or been hidden on Mars, it might be down there, out of sight.","unidentified-hyperobjects":"A professor named James Madden has written a book called Unidentified Hyperobjects, which Michels brings up approvingly, calling Madden \"a really cool professor.\" Michels mentions it in passing while talking with a former director of the NSA, and in this telling gives only the title and author, not what the book argues.","unidentified-objects-repeatedly-appearing-over-sensitive-nuc":"Things in the sky that no one can identify are said to keep showing up around America's most sensitive nuclear sites. Michels says this himself, in passing, on a 2025 episode with a guest named Brigham Buhler, and he names no particular case or source.","union-bank-and-dulles-financing-the-nazis-1920":"The claim is that American money helped bring the Nazis to power in Germany in the 1920s and 1930s. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source. He says a bank called Union Bank funded the Nazis, and that Allen Dulles, he thinks, was part of the financing and was stationed \"in Norway or something.\" The researcher Jason Jorjani goes further on the show. He says Allen Dulles and his brother John Foster Dulles, together with JP Morgan Chase, Rockefeller and Prescott Bush, \"funded the rise of the Nazis,\" though he never mentions Union Bank. A third researcher, Dan Sheehan, names neither the bank nor the brothers and says only that American industrialists wanted Hitler \"to establish this kind of fascist government.\" If it were true, it would mean powerful Americans helped build the regime their country later fought.","union-bank-and-dulles-in-nazi-financing":"Two powerful Americans are said to have helped pay for Nazi Germany: Prescott Bush, grandfather of President George W. Bush, as president of an American bank called the Union Bank, and Allen Dulles, later head of the CIA, who Michels says was \"helping out with some Nazi financing.\" Michels says this himself without naming a source, while tracing the people he believes stand behind the UFO cover-up.","unit-731-data-handed-to-the-us-in-exchange-for-immunity-1945":"After the Second World War, the biological warfare data gathered by Unit 731, a Japanese army unit led by Shiro Ishii that experimented on human beings, was marked top secret and quietly handed to the United States, which was occupying Japan, in exchange for immunity from prosecution. Michels narrates this himself, without naming a source, in an episode on human experiments with a guest named Brigham Buhler. He tells it to show the US trading justice for secret knowledge.","unit-731-human-experiments-1930":"Unit 731 was a secret biological and chemical warfare research program of the Imperial Japanese Army, set up in Manchuria under General Shiro Ishii and known for experiments on people. Michels describes it himself, without naming a source, in an episode on human experiments and the drug industry. He brings it up as an example of how far secret programs have gone in testing on humans.","united-nuclear-supplies-dhs-and-fbi-training-1999":"Bob Lazar is a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site in the late 1980s. He tells Michels that United Nuclear, the company behind the products he discusses, supplies training for the Department of Homeland Security and the FBI. In his words, \"they train uh Department of Homeland Security, FBI,\" which would put a company tied to Lazar in working contact with federal agencies.","university-of-cagliari-people-collect-the-artifacts-and-gian-1978":"Between about 1978 and 1981, people connected with the University of Cagliari, in the capital of the Italian island of Sardinia, are said to have collected artifacts and the bodies of giants. The filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels this as something he was told by others, which would mean evidence of giants was gathered up and kept out of view.","unlit-drones-hovering-over-400-ft-above-picatinny-arsenal-2024":"In 2024, a helicopter pilot named Jake Barber, who says he once helped retrieve a UFO, tells Michels that he saw drones with their lights off hovering more than 400 feet above Picatinny Arsenal, a military site in New Jersey, \"just sitting there\". He tells it as something he saw himself, and if true it would mean unknown craft were loitering unlit over a military base.","unmarked-black-helicopters-arrive-at-ellsworth-1960":"In the 1960s at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota, a crowd of black helicopters with no markings at all is said to have arrived over a few weeks. Michels relays this in an episode with Robert Hastings, a researcher of UFOs at nuclear bases, quoting a witness he does not name: \"suddenly we had an influx of black... helicopters completely unmarked.\" The story suggests some secret unit moved in on the base after something unexplained happened there.","unmarked-blackhawks-during-notam-experiments":"Unmarked Black Hawk helicopters turned up in the middle of the night at Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange happenings, while experiments there were under way with NOTAMs in place (a NOTAM is an official notice that warns pilots about an area of airspace). The ranch's owner, who is not named in the tellings, says he saw this and tells Michels: \"unmarked Blackhawk helicopters showing up in the middle of the night.\" If true, someone with military aircraft was quietly keeping an eye on what happened at the ranch.","unnamed-group-extended-low-dose-dmt-to-6-hours":"A research group has extended the DMT experience to six hours at a very low dose, according to the neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore. DMT is a powerful psychedelic whose trip normally lasts minutes, and Gallimore, who does not name the group, tells Michels this as a step toward longer stays in what he sees as another realm.","unnamed-insider-on-a-ufo-crash-retrieval-team-out-of-the-nev":"An unnamed insider is said to have worked on a UFO crash retrieval team, a group that recovers crashed craft, based out of the Nevada Test and Training Range, a large military range that includes the Tonopah Test Range. The researcher known as UAPGerb tells Michels about this person, saying one of his contacts \"specifically has claimed to have operated on a crash retrieval team.\" The insider is described as holding a badge from the NRO, the National Reconnaissance Office, which runs US spy satellites, and a Q clearance, the Energy Department's clearance for nuclear secrets. If true, it would mean the US runs a hidden program to recover crashed UFOs.","unnamed-law-enforcement-entity-investigating-insiders-2025":"In 2025, the UFO disclosure campaigner Steven Greer told Michels he was passing intelligence to a law enforcement body he says he cannot name. He says the information would let that body go to the people holding UFO secrets, \"get their information, interrogate them.\" If it is true, insiders keeping those secrets could be investigated like suspects.","unnamed-major-we-got-it-from-them-1992":"Greg Rogers, a NASA doctor, says that in 1992 at Cape Canaveral Air Force Station in Florida he saw a UFO inside a secret hangar. He tells Michels that an Air Force major, whom he does not name, looked at him and said, \"We got it from them.\" If true, it would mean the military held a craft it credited to non-human makers.","unnamed-president-and-wife-see-a-ship-while-fishing-on-the-c":"The president of a large country, one of the G7 group of rich nations, was on a fishing holiday on the coast with his wife when they watched a ship come down from the sky. That is what a man named Kiran Ilum Xenov, who tells Michels he was himself taken by aliens in 1997, says the president told him, though he names neither the president, the country nor the year. If true, a sitting head of state would have seen such a craft with his own eyes.","unnamed-raytheon-engineer-and-academic-experts-consulted-on-2015":"A writer named Marik von Rennenkampff says that, to make sense of the Gimbal video, a video of an unexplained object recorded off the US East Coast, he and others consulted experts, including a senior engineer at the defence firm Raytheon who was \"intimately familiar with the system\" that filmed it, along with academics. He says it on the show during a debate with a skeptic, and he does not name the engineer. The point of telling it is that people who know the camera system would be best placed to say whether the video shows something real or a quirk of the equipment.","unnamed-southern-california-facility-storing-non-human-bodie":"Non-human bodies are said to be stored at a place in California that has no official name or designation. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this as what a source he does not name told him, saying \"the bodies are stored over there in California\" and that it is \"a place that doesn't have its own designation\"; in one telling it is Southern California, in another just California. If true, the government would be keeping physical remains of non-human beings at a hidden site.","unreleased-photo-of-massive-triangle-over-water":"There is an unreleased photograph of a huge triangular object hanging above water, according to a CIA contractor named Bob Maguire, who says he has seen it himself. He tells Michels it is among several such images: \"the one is a massive triangle above the water I mean this thing is huge.\" If true, a picture of a giant unexplained craft exists and has never been made public.","unsuccessful-search-for-partner-michael-johnson-1977":"For decades, a former Air Force nuclear weapons guard named Mario Woods searched for Michael Johnson, the partner who was with him the night in 1977 when Woods says they were abducted. Woods says he spent money on it and checked hospitals, though all he had to go on was that Johnson said he was from Chicago, and he never knew Johnson's middle initial. Michels adds that Woods hired private investigators, went to the FBI, set off online amateur sleuths, and got a promise to look from AARO, the Pentagon's official UFO office, but none of it turned Johnson up until a tip from a fan finally found him.","unusual-rate-of-carcass-decay-on-the-ranch-2016":"Dead animals on Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, decay and decompose at a rate \"dramatically different\" from anything usually seen in nature, according to the ranch's owner, Brandon Fugal. He tells it from the years his team has run the place, mentioning a man named Ben Woodruff in connection with it, as a sign that something on the land affects living matter.","upper-paleolithic-cave-art-showing-non-human-beings-hancock":"Paintings deep inside Ice Age caves, from the late Old Stone Age, are said to show beings that are neither human nor animal. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore, who studies the psychedelic drug DMT, tells Michels this as a researcher summing up the argument of the writer Graeme Hancock's book Supernatural, which he says makes the point that 'the cave art clearly showed beings that were nonhuman'. If that reading holds, people may have been meeting, or picturing, such beings tens of thousands of years ago.","uranium-at-skinwalker-ranch":"Skinwalker Ranch, a property in Utah known for reports of strange events, is said to have a lot of uranium in the ground around it. Michels mentions this in passing on a 2024 episode with the writer Graham Hancock, as something \"they say\", without saying who.","urartians-as-a-later-culture-built-on-older-foundations-2000":"The ruins, artifacts and symbols around Lake Van in Turkey are credited by mainstream archaeology to the Urartians, a people of roughly 3,000 years ago, but they built on much older foundations, according to the researcher Matt LaCroix. LaCroix tells Michels, without naming a source, that archaeologists give the Urartians credit because they carved cuneiform, the ancient wedge-shaped writing, into some of the temples. If he is right, a far older and forgotten culture laid the groundwork there.","uri-geller-abducted-as-a-child":"The Israeli performer Uri Geller, known for claiming psychic powers, is said to have been abducted as a young child and to have gained those powers that way. Michels brings this up himself in a conversation about UFOs, psychics and the afterlife, hedging with \"I think\" and naming no source beyond Geller. If true, it would tie psychic ability to contact with something non-human.","us-air-force-plane-lands-at-campinas-and-helicopters-collect-1996":"In 1996, during the Varginha case, in which a craft is said to have crashed in the Brazilian town of Varginha and a living creature to have been captured, a US Air Force plane is said to have landed at Campinas while helicopters collected something from Varginha. Jose Fernandez, who tells Michels he witnessed the Varginha events, says only that \"the US Air Force made a landing in Campinas,\" and he brings in Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon official who worked on UFO reports, and the film Moment of Contact. If true, it would mean the United States took part in recovering whatever was found.","us-and-china-crash-retrieval-and-back-engineering-race":"The claim is that both the United States and China have recovered crashed non-human craft and are each trying to back-engineer them, which means taking them apart to learn how they work and copy them. Joe Rogan tells Michels this is what someone he spoke to \"firmly believes.\" He brings it up while talking about The Age of Disclosure, a documentary, and mentions Dan Farah, Marco Rubio, James Woolsey, James Clapper and John Brennan. Michels tells a longer version himself, drawing on the claims of David Grusch, a witness who has spoken out about crash programs. In his telling, the US program was buried after the Cold War, while rival nations got craft of their own, either through their own crash recoveries or by getting inside the American program. \"And now there is a race,\" Michels says, \"and we are not in first.\" If true, the competition over this technology would be a live security contest between powers.","us-black-psychokinesis-unit-separate-from-remote-viewing-pro-1970":"In the 1970s and 80s, the story goes, the United States ran a secret, off-the-books unit that worked on psychokinesis, the idea of moving or changing matter with the mind alone. The researcher and philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this. He says it rests on the word of some of the country's top remote viewers, people trained to try to see distant places with the mind, and that the unit was kept apart from the remote viewing programmes that Congress later stopped funding. If true, the government's psychic research went further and lasted longer than the programmes the public knows about.","us-fitted-a-non-human-power-source-into-a-human-built-craft":"The claim is that the United States has managed to take a power source of non-human origin and fit it into a craft that people built, like putting a Ferrari engine in a Pinto. The journalist Jeremy Corbell tells Michels there is \"evidence that we, meaning the United States, have been able to put a non-human power source into a constructed body of other craft.\" If true, it would mean the country had learned to use part of an alien machine, even without understanding the whole.","us-in-possession-of-exotic-material":"The United States holds \"exotic material\", meaning material of unknown and possibly non-human origin, according to Luis Elizondo, a former Pentagon intelligence official, who tells Michels \"it is my belief\" in an episode asking whether Oppenheimer worked on UFOs. He offers it as his own belief rather than pointing to a document, and if true, the government would have physical evidence of something it does not acknowledge.","us-pilot-testifies-about-scrubbed-cia-and-air-force-missions-1996":"A US pilot named Fred Clawson says there were missions between the CIA, the Air Force and Varginha, a town in Brazil where, by other accounts on the show, a craft crashed in 1996 and a living being was captured, and that these missions were later scrubbed from the record. Michels introduces him as now saying this on record. Clawson tells it himself and adds detail: he says that \"in order to secure a cargo plane, a specific protocol must be adhered to,\" and that the paper trail leads back to Scott Air Force Base. If he is right, the United States played a hidden part in the Varginha events.","us-racing-to-occupy-earth-moon-lagrangian-point-2020":"The United States government is racing to occupy a Lagrangian point between Earth and the Moon, a spot in space where the pulls of the two bodies balance so that a craft can stay there, according to Congressman Eric Burlison. He tells Michels flatly, \"I know this,\" and says the US is racing \"to occupy that space and orbit.\" If he is right, that patch of space near the Moon is already being contested.","us-scalar-weapons-rumor":"The United States has built what are called scalar weapons, a kind of weapon he names but does not describe, according to the journalist Ross Coulthart. He tells Michels this came to him second-hand from people he spoke with, and he gives no names, dates or details. If true, the US would hold a weapon the public knows nothing about.","us-scientists-lose-clearances-while-nazis-are-put-in-charge-1947":"After World War II, the story goes, the Americans who had done the wartime science lost their security clearances, while German scientists from the Nazi side were put in charge of American programmes. The researcher Peter Levenda gives this as a summary, without naming a source: \"everybody that actually worked for us during the war lost their security clearances,\" he tells Michels, naming Oppenheimer and the rocket engineer Jack Parsons as examples. If it is true, America's postwar secret science was steered by its former enemies instead of its own people.","us-soviet-agreement-to-report-ufos-affecting-early-warning":"The claim is that the United States and the Soviet Union signed a pact to reduce the risk of nuclear war that obliges each side to tell the other if UFOs interfere with its missile early-warning systems. A retired Army colonel named Karl Nell tells Michels the agreement is \"still in force\" and contains that stipulation. A Pentagon whistleblower named Matthew Brown remembers it as a 1970-something outer space treaty in which the two powers agreed to notify each other if they detected UFOs, and does not mention early warning. A CIA contractor named Bob Maguire dates it to 1971 under President Nixon and goes further, saying the red phone, the emergency hotline between Washington and Moscow, \"was invented to stop UFOs from starting thermonuclear war\". The tellers point to the Agreement on Measures to Reduce the Risk of Outbreak of Nuclear War, signed by the US and the USSR in 1971. If they are right, both superpowers took unexplained objects seriously enough to write them into nuclear safety rules.","us-soviet-deconfliction-on-ufos-and-nato-collaboration":"The United States and the Soviet Union are said to have once set up an arrangement over UFOs, so that neither side would mistake one for an attack by the other. Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon official who presents himself as a whistleblower, says on the show that \"deterrence and the deconfliction\" on UFOs had been \"established between the United States and the Soviet Union,\" and the story ties this to cooperation within NATO. If true, it would mean the Cold War rivals quietly treated UFOs as a shared problem.","us-strikes-on-iranian-nuclear-sites-as-part-of-a-hidden-time-2025":"Michels wonders aloud whether the 2025 US strike on three Iranian nuclear sites, ordered by Donald Trump and carried out with B-2 bombers, might be playing out against a hidden backdrop: a conflict among unseen beings over adjusting the timeline. He puts it as a question to a neuroscientist named Andrew Gallimore, not as something he has a source for.","us-ufo-authorities-obsessed-with-mental-phenomena-project-ma-1950":"In the 1950s, the American officials studying UFOs were obsessed with the mental phenomena that came with UFO research, according to Wilbert B. Smith, a Canadian government engineer linked to Project Magnet, a Canadian study of UFOs in that decade. The online researcher UAPGerb passes this on to Michels as something Smith said, without naming a document, and it would mean the Americans saw the mind as part of the UFO puzzle from early on.","usgs-prediction-of-a-la-palma-collapse-tsunami":"A collapse on the island of La Palma would set off a tsunami that could reach as far as 26 miles inland in places on the US East Coast. That, says a former Army sergeant named Lyn Buchanan, is what the US Geological Service has predicted. He tells Michels this from memory, without naming the study or saying when it was made. If true, it would mean one island's collapse could flood a large part of America's Atlantic coast.","usgs-seismic-sensors-track-booms-toward-area-51-1987":"Between 1987 and 1989, earthquake sensors run by the US Geological Survey are said to have picked up sonic booms coming in from the Pacific over California and heading toward Area 51, a secret Air Force test site in the Nevada desert. A researcher named Nick Cook tells Michels this as a summary, without naming his source. If true, it would mean something was flying fast and routinely into the base from the ocean.","uso-incident-at-pascagoula-1973":"In 1973, off Pascagoula, Mississippi, there was what the researcher Richard Dolan calls \"a genuine USO incident,\" meaning an unidentified submerged object, a UFO seen in or under water. Dolan mentions it in passing on the show without naming a source. He says only that it came \"a week or two later\" and \"right there,\" and that would make it one more strange event in the same place within a short time.","uss-curtis-deck-log-luminous-object-1954":"During the 1954 hydrogen bomb test at Bikini Atoll, the USS Curtis, the ship Michels says set off the bomb, recorded a strange light in its deck log, the ship's official running record. Michels reads the entry aloud: \"at 2305 an unidentified luminous object passed over ship from bow to stern yellowish orange in color traveling at a high rate of speed and a low altitude.\" The log describes the object but says nothing of what it was. Michels raises it as a sign that unexplained objects turn up around nuclear weapons.","uss-delarov-disc-rises-from-sea-near-adak-1945":"In 1945, near Adak Island in Alaska's Aleutian chain, people aboard the US Navy ship Delarov are said to have watched a disc about 200 feet across rise out of the sea. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this second-hand, drawing on accounts he credits to Robert Crawford and James McDonald, and notes that while not everyone saw it, 'other people did see it emerge from the water.' If true, it would put an object coming out of the ocean two years before the flying saucer craze began.","uss-finch-cigar-shaped-object-shot-down-near-johnston-island-1962":"In 1962, near Johnston Island in the Pacific, sailors aboard the Navy ship USS Finch are said to have watched a cigar-shaped object that seemed to be shot out of the sky. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this, passing on the account of a man named David Noble White Horse, who describes the crew all talking about the 'bogey', an unidentified target, that went down. If true, it would be an early case of the military bringing down an unknown craft.","uss-jackson-video-of-multiple-erratically-moving-uap-at-nigh-2019":"In 2019, sailors on the USS Jackson, a Navy ship off Southern California, are said to have filmed \"multiple erratic moving UAP at night\", UAP being the official term for UFOs. A retired Navy admiral named Tim Gallaudet tells Michels this second-hand: a man named Alexander Wiggins, who was on the ship, told him about it and showed him the video. Gallaudet places it in the same region and period as other reports of swarms of objects over Navy ships there, which would make it one more recording of the same activity.","uss-john-f-kennedy-orb-and-systems-failure-1971":"On 2 July 1971, at 8:30 at night, all the incoming communications on the aircraft carrier USS John F. Kennedy suddenly turned to gibberish, at a time the story ties to a glowing orb seen by the ship and a failure of its systems. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this from the account of James Kopp, a man who was running the ship's communications that night and who gave his testimony to the Disclosure Project, a group that collected statements from former military and government insiders. If true, an unknown object would have shut down the communications of a US warship at sea.","uss-nautilus-fitted-with-a-side-hatch-to-grab-discs-1963":"Between 1963 and 1966, the story goes, the Navy submarine USS Nautilus was fitted with a special side hatch so it could pick up discs and other unknown objects from the ocean. The UFO historian Richard Dolan tells Michels this second hand. He passes on what a named person told him and points to the International UFO Congress, a UFO conference, as where it came from. If true, it would mean the Navy was quietly collecting unexplained craft from the sea in the 1960s.","uss-omaha-sphere-as-a-heat-source-going-behind-the-horizon-2019":"The USS Omaha sphere is a 2019 Navy video, filmed off San Diego, that shows a round object over the sea. Mick West, a researcher known for finding ordinary explanations for UFO footage, tells Michels that if you zoom in it \"looks more like it's actually a heat source that is expanded,\" something warm that drops behind the horizon rather than a craft going into the water. If he is right, the sphere was an ordinary distant heat source and not a mystery object.","uss-omaha-sphere-video-2019":"In July 2019, off the coast of San Diego, the Navy ship USS Omaha is said to have filmed a round object that flew over the water and then went down into it. Michels calls it 'a very controversial interesting case' and brings it up himself, citing the journalists George Knapp and Jeremy Corbell and the retired Navy admiral Tim Gallaudet; Gallaudet, on the show, tells it second-hand from an officer named Alexander Wiggins who he says was on the Omaha's bridge, and he puts the weight on the moment the 'spherical object goes into the water.' If true, it would mean an unknown craft able to move from air into sea.","uss-paul-hamilton-reports-an-object-splashing-down-2019":"In 2019, off Southern California, the Navy destroyer USS Paul Hamilton is said to have reported an unknown object splashing down into the sea, about 24 hours after an earlier sighting. The writer Marik von Rennenkampff brings this up with Michels, drawing on the ship's deck log and an event briefing released under FOIA, a US law that lets anyone request government records. If true, the Navy's own records note an object that could go from the air into the water.","utopia-region-renamed-galaxias-chaos-after-the-second-face-1980":"A region of Mars once called simply Utopia was renamed Galaxias Chaos in the 1980s, after a second face-like formation was noticed there, according to John Brandenburg. Brandenburg is a physicist who argues that life on Mars was wiped out by nuclear war. He tells Michels this from his own involvement and presents the renaming as a sign that the face drew official attention.","v-shaped-object-in-the-desert-turns-out-to-be-geese-1980":"Michael Shermer, a well-known skeptic, tells Michels that during the Race Across America in the 1980s he saw what looked like a shiny V-shaped object out in the desert, and that it turned out to be a flock of geese. He tells it as his own sighting, offered as an example of how strange things in the sky can have ordinary explanations.","v2-rocket-goes-off-course-into-mexico-as-a-warning-1947":"In the late 1940s, a V2 rocket, the German missile of World War II, went off course and came down somewhere in Mexico, and the researcher Jason Jorjani presents this as a warning sent to someone. Jorjani tells it to Michels in passing, without naming a source: \"next thing you know, a V2 rocket goes off course and hits a place in Mexico.\" If he is right, a rocket crash written off as an accident was really a message in a hidden contest over German rocket know-how.","va-ptsd-program-with-flashing-images-and-air-horns-2016":"Around 2016 and 2017, the Department of Veterans Affairs put a former Air Force serviceman named Dylan Borland into an odd treatment programme for post-traumatic stress, one where, in his words, \"they're flashing images and air horns.\" Borland tells Michels this as something done to him, points to his VA medical records, and links the programme to Langley. Told this way, it suggests the treatment was more than ordinary therapy.","va-treatment-program-with-flashing-images-and-constant-mris-2017":"Around 2017, while he worked for the defense contractor BAE, Dylan Borland says he was put through a treatment program in the VA, the veterans' health system. It involved flashing images and constant MRI scans, while at the same time the VA denied all of his benefits. Borland tells Michels this from his own experience and points to his medical records.","vacuum-energy-secrecy-to-prevent-weaponization":"A researcher named Kurt McConnell suggests on the show that the US government may have found it is \"not that hard after all\" to draw energy out of the physical vacuum, the empty space that physics says still holds energy, and kept that secret so it could not be turned into a weapon. He offers it as a \"what if\", without naming a source, and points to the inventor Nikola Tesla and to an American inventor named T. T. Brown. If true, it would mean a near limitless power source has been hidden on purpose.","vaddo-tungsten-fragment-from-1957-car-stop":"In 1957 two carpenters were driving through the night near Väddö, northeast of Stockholm, when an object came in from the east, made a U-turn and hung in front of their car, and the car died completely. The Swedish researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels this without naming a source. The case is tied to a tungsten fragment, which would make it a rare encounter that left something physical behind.","valentine-archuleta-sees-the-craft-hit-the-mesa-1948":"In 1948, a rancher named Valentine Archuleta looked up and saw a craft fall and hit a mesa on his family's land near Aztec, New Mexico. His granddaughter tells this to Michels while standing at the spot, repeating what her grandfather told the family: \"he saw it hit right here.\" The story belongs to a wider account of a crash near Aztec said to have left alien bodies, and his sighting would put a witness on the ground at the moment of impact.","valerie-ransone-arrives-at-lab-nine-1978":"In 1978, a woman named Valerie Ransone, who was said to be able to \"channel information\" from beyond herself, ended up at Lab Nine, a research lab run by a man named Puharich that worked on contact of this kind. An unnamed researcher tells Michels this on the show, without saying where it comes from, as the start of how she joined the group.","valerie-ransone-runs-the-whip-inflation-now-program-in-the-f-1974":"A woman named Valerie Ransone worked in Gerald Ford's White House in the mid-1970s and ran Whip Inflation Now, Ford's public campaign against rising prices. Jeffrey Mishlove, a PhD who studies psychics, UFOs and the afterlife, says he knew her at that time and tells Michels: \"when I knew her, she was working in the Gerald Ford White House.\" He brings it up to show that someone he knew in this field had worked at the heart of government.","valerie-ransone-the-ether-ship-group-and-the-white-house-1970":"In the 1970s, a woman named Valerie Ransone is said to have been inside the White House while trying to talk with aliens through xenolinguistics, the study of how one might communicate with non-human beings, alongside a group called the Ether Ship led by a man named Willard de Bogart. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source. He tells it differently on another episode, crediting the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée: there Ransone is an intelligence operative of the Nixon years, connected at the highest levels of government, who contacted the group rather than belonging to it. If it were true, it would put an official attempt at alien contact close to the presidency.","validation-scan-of-the-gran-sasso-physics-laboratory":"Before his scans of what lies under the Egyptian pyramids, Filippo Biondi, a scientist known for that work, says he tested his method by scanning the Gran Sasso physics laboratory in Italy, a site whose layout is already known. He tells Michels he did it in 2024 with a different processing technique and treated it as an experiment. The point of telling it is that if the scan matched the real laboratory, his readings under the pyramids would be harder to dismiss.","validation-scan-of-the-osiris-shaft":"Filippo Biondi, a scientist who says he scanned beneath the Giza pyramids, tells Michels that his team also measured the Osiris shaft, a deep underground shaft at Giza that is already well known as a structure. He offers it as a check on his method: if the scan shows a known structure correctly, his findings about what lies under the pyramids are easier to trust.","vallee-and-davis-six-level-uap-paper":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée and the physicist Eric W. Davis wrote a paper sorting UAP, the official term for unexplained aerial objects, into six levels, from \"nuts and bolts\", meaning physical machines, at the bottom, to spiritual or metaphysical sides of the phenomenon at the top. Jesse Michels mentions the paper himself, calling it \"very nice\", without saying when or where it was published.","vallee-argues-with-spielberg-on-close-encounters-set-over-ti-1977":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée is said to have argued with Steven Spielberg on the set of Close Encounters of the Third Kind, the 1977 film, and by the story's own account the argument was about time travel. Michels mentions this himself in passing, without naming a source and without saying what either man argued. If true, it would mean that one of the subject's best-known researchers pushed back on the film's picture of visitors while it was being made.","vallee-finds-a-classified-battelle-letter-about-a-secret-pro-1950":"The story goes that the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée, going through the papers of J. Allen Hynek, the astronomer who advised Blue Book (the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s), found a classified letter he was never meant to see. The letter came from Battelle, a private research institute that did work for the Air Force, and was signed by one of its scientists, Howard C. Cross. As it is told, the letter points to a second, secret UFO study running alongside the public Blue Book. If that is true, the public study was not the whole effort. The filmmaker James Fox tells it on the show as something he heard from Vallée himself. Michels tells it several times in his own voice and calls the letter the Pentacle memo. In one telling he dates it to 1953 and ties it to the report of the Robertson panel, a group of scientists the CIA brought together that year, whose report is dated 16 February 1953. In another he skips Vallée's find and links Battelle's quiet role to present-day ties he draws to the defense contractor SAIC and to Pentagon officials Sean Kirkpatrick and Ronald Moultrie.","vallee-hints-bob-lazar-was-an-mk-ultra-subject-1980":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked in Nevada on reverse engineering alien craft, meaning taking them apart to learn how they work, may instead have been a subject of MK Ultra, a CIA mind-control program, and been made to believe his story. Michels says the computer scientist and veteran UFO researcher Jacques Vallée hints at this in his book Revelations: Alien Contact and Human Deception, which suggests government brainwashing \"led him to believe\" what he says.","vallee-in-james-fox-film-ai-created-by-a-non-human-intellige":"Humanity may be dealing with an artificial intelligence, which was itself built by a non-human intelligence from another planet. A UFO researcher named Jason Simosa says that Jacques Vallée, a veteran researcher of the UFO question, said this recently in a film by the documentary maker James Fox. Simosa passes it on to Michels from memory, as something Vallée said, not something he says himself.","vallee-knows-more-than-he-can-communicate-2025":"Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and long-time UFO researcher, knows much more about the subject than he is able to put into words, according to Michels. Michels says this in 2025 as his own impression from speaking with Vallée, saying he gets \"the sense\" that Vallée \"knows a lot more than he can even communicate.\" It is a personal feeling, not something Vallée is quoted as saying.","vallee-on-ransone-s-nro-satellite-work-1977":"In the late 1970s in Houston, a woman named Valerie Ransone was reportedly doing some kind of work for the NRO, the US agency that runs spy satellites, and talked about it with the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée. An unnamed researcher guest tells Michels this from Vallée's published journals, Forbidden Science, saying Vallée 'spoke with her back in that late '70s time and she was doing some sort of work for the NRO.' It matters to the guest because it would tie Ransone to secret satellite work in the same years she crossed paths with a leading UFO researcher.","vallee-pasulka-theory-that-the-1947-roswell-crash-divides-fo":"The idea here is that the 1947 Roswell crash, the reported fall of an unknown object near Roswell, New Mexico, marks a turning point: before it, strange visitors show up as the beings of folklore, and after it, they show up as aliens. Michels says this is the theory he likes best, and he credits it to the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée and the religion scholar Diana Pasulka, calling 1947 and Roswell \"this dividing line.\" If it holds, the same phenomenon would simply have changed the face it shows people.","vallee-s-1985-cia-document-on-staged-abductions-in-argentina":"The claim is that a 1985 CIA document describes the agency abducting peasants in Argentina and Brazil so that they would believe aliens had taken them, for what Greer quotes as its \"psychological warfare value.\" A UFO researcher named Steven Greer tells this, saying the document appears in a 1992 book, a journal, by Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and UFO researcher, and he also names the former Army officer John B. Alexander. Later in the same talk he calls it a CIA document from a man named Jock File that says \"black and white\" that \"we're conducting the abductions,\" this time with no place and no date, which would mean some alien abduction stories are staged by people.","vallee-s-260-000-case-capela-database":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée is said to have built a database, called Capela, that holds some 260,000 reported cases of unexplained sightings and encounters. The researcher Jason Simosa mentions it to Michels as a measure of how much material exists, without saying where his figure comes from. If true, it would be one of the largest collections of such reports ever gathered by one person.","vallee-s-assessment-of-the-billy-meier-photos-1977":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée has examined some of the photographs taken in Switzerland in the late 1970s by Billy Meier, a Swiss man who says his pictures show craft flown by visitors from elsewhere. The philosopher Jason Jorjani brings this up with Michels, passing on Vallée's look at the photos, though in what he says here he does not spell out what Vallée concluded. It matters because a careful researcher's verdict would weigh heavily on whether the pictures are real.","vallee-s-control-system-steps-in-when-a-nuke-is-about-to-go":"The idea is that UFOs act as a kind of control system over humanity, one that \"knows how to nip at the herd\" and shows up when, say, a nuclear weapon is about to go off. Michels brings it up himself and credits it to the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée. If true, it would mean the phenomenon steps in to steer people at moments of danger.","vallee-s-diaries-keep-pointing-to-the-carlyle-group":"In his published diaries, Forbidden Science, which run to five volumes, the computer scientist and UFO researcher Jacques Vallée describes lunches with \"spooky insider types\" who keep telling him to look at the Carlyle Group, a multinational investment firm with large defence interests. Michels recounts this from his own reading, placing it around the third or fourth volume, and asks his guest, the analyst Matthew Pines, whether there is anything to Vallée's theory, which would mean private defence money sits close to the UFO secret.","vallee-s-fairy-alien-parallels-in-passport-to-magonia":"The researcher Jacques Vallée argued in his book Passport to Magonia that today's alien encounters closely match the old folklore of fairies and elves. The writer Graham Hancock summarises the comparison for Michels without naming a source, though he credits Vallée and his own book Supernatural. In the old tales, he says, fairies and elves \"would do surgery on people, they would abduct people, they would have sex with people, they would produce hybrid babies.\" That is the same set of things people now report from alien abductions. Michels returns to the book in a later episode and calls it \"heretical to both believers and debunkers.\" He reads its argument as one phenomenon wearing different cultural masks over the centuries. He applies it to a 1977 case in which Air Force veterans at Ellsworth say they were taken by aliens. If Vallée is right, whatever people meet has been with us a long time and simply shows up in the costume each age expects.","vallee-s-invisible-college-research-group-of-the-1970s":"In the 1970s the UFO researcher Jacques Vallee called his independent group of UFO researchers \"the Invisible College\" and gave the same title to his book about what they had learned. The name was a tribute to the early scientist Robert Boyle. Michels tells this himself in two episodes, and in the later one he names another researcher, J. Allen Hynek, as part of the group.","vallee-s-journals-pasulka-told-not-to-talk-about-hybrids":"In his published journals, Jacques Vallée, a veteran UFO researcher, records a conversation with Diana Pasulka, a religion scholar who writes about belief in UFOs. In it Pasulka explains that someone was telling her not to talk about \"the hybrid thing\", meaning the idea of beings that are part human and part alien. A UFO researcher named Jason Simosa tells Michels this from his reading of the journals. He is not sure who gave the warning and names him, hesitantly, as Tim Taylor. If true, it would suggest someone was trying to keep the hybrid idea out of public talk.","vallee-s-own-ufo-sighting-at-age-15-1950":"A researcher named Jacques Vallée, who has studied UFO reports for decades, tells Michels that when he was 15, growing up in France in the 1950s, he saw an object outside his room, hanging over the yard. He tells it as his own sighting, one that came before his lifetime of work on the subject.","vallee-s-study-of-small-ufo-religions-1970":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée tells Michels that in the 1970s he spent a while going around studying \"little UFO religions\", small groups built around belief in UFOs. He says he wrote about them in books, including Messengers of Deception, work that matters to him because it looks at how belief in UFOs can be shaped and used.","vallee-s-the-invisible-college-1975":"The Invisible College, a book the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée wrote in 1975, is in Jesse Michels's view still \"the tip of the spear\" of thinking about what UFOs are. Michels says this himself, in passing, as praise for how far ahead of its time he thinks the book was.","vallee-says-there-are-protocols-for-leaving-the-cave":"The UFO researcher Jacques Vallée is said to have confirmed that there are protocols, meaning set methods, for leaving \"the cave\", which is Plato's image of people chained in a cave who take shadows on the wall for reality. Michels tells this himself in a conversation about consciousness: he says he asked Vallée \"Are there protocols?\" and Vallée answered, \"Yes, there are.\" Michels does not say what the protocols are, but he tells it as a sign that someone thinks there is a practical way to see past ordinary reality.","vallee-shows-pasulka-the-history-of-satan-book-2010":"In his study, the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée is said to have shown a visitor an odd book called The History of Satan, with \"big words on it that says Satan.\" The religion scholar Diana Pasulka tells this on the show as something she saw herself, on two occasions. The second time, she sets it over lunch, puts it in the years around 2019 to 2023 rather than the 2010s, and adds that the book had 666 pages, which is part of why it struck her as strange.","vallee-suspected-a-secret-real-project-behind-project-blue-b-1960":"In the 1960s, the French UFO researcher Jacques Vallée suspected that Project Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, was not the real effort, and that a secret project was running behind it. Vallée tells Michels so himself, saying he felt \"they had a real project going on that was secret.\" In a later interview Michels repeats it at second hand, saying Vallée \"told me the exact same thing\" as another source, and he places it a little earlier, in the 1950s and 60s; if true, the public study was a front for a hidden one.","vallee-urges-hynek-to-review-blue-book-s-psychological-cases-1964":"In 1964 the UFO researcher Jacques Vallée met the astronomer J. Allen Hynek at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Dayton, Ohio. Vallée is said to have urged Hynek to look again at the cases that Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s, had written off as \"psychological.\" The documentary filmmaker James Fox tells Michels the story as he learned it from these men and from the Blue Book records.","vallee-writes-the-foreword-to-the-french-edition-of-virk-s-b-2020":"Jacques Vallée, a computer scientist and veteran UFO researcher, says he wrote the foreword to the French edition of a book by Rizwan Virk, an MIT-trained computer scientist who argues that our world may be a kind of computer simulation. Vallée tells Michels that he read Virk's second book, and then Virk asked him to write the foreword for the French version, \"which I did, because by then I was hooked.\" It shows a long-time UFO researcher putting his name behind the idea that reality may be a simulation.","van-allen-radiation-belt-impassable-1960":"The Van Allen belts, zones of radiation circling the Earth, are said by their discoverer James Van Allen to carry about 150 times the radiation a person can normally take. Michels repeats this from the podcaster Danny Jones, who he says raised it on his own show, and he brings in the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun alongside it. If true, it would raise hard questions about how astronauts passed through the belts in the 1960s.","van-flandern-alerts-harrington-to-pioneer-10-data-on-a-plane-1980":"In the 1980s, the story goes, the astronomer Thomas Van Flandern of the US Naval Observatory phoned his colleague Robert Harrington and told him he had to look at data from Pioneer 10, a NASA space probe heading out of the solar system, because it hinted at an unknown planet beyond the Kuiper belt, the band of icy bodies past Neptune. The researcher Matt LaCroix tells Michels this, passing on what the two astronomers are said to have found. If true, a large unseen planet would be lurking at the edge of our solar system.","van-flandern-and-harrington-both-die-of-throat-cancer-1990":"Two astronomers, Thomas Van Flandern and Robert Harrington, who led the search for an unknown planet in our solar system and possibly a companion star to the Sun, both died of throat cancer even though neither of them smoked, according to the researcher Matt LaCroix. He tells Michels this without naming a source the first time; the second time he points to a book called The Stage of Time and stresses that it was the two of them who led the search for the planet and the star. LaCroix raises it because he finds it striking that the two men leading the same search died of the same uncommon illness.","van-flandern-s-speed-of-gravity-argument":"A physicist named Tom Van Flandern argued that gravity travels at least two billion times faster than light, according to the researcher Rupert Sheldrake. Sheldrake mentions the argument on the show without naming a paper or book. If true, gravity would act almost instantly across space, which would sit badly with Einstein's rule that nothing goes faster than light.","van-tassel-s-contact-with-venusians-1950":"In the 1950s, an American man named George Van Tassel said he was in contact with beings from Venus. A researcher and philosopher named Jason Jorjani mentions this to Michels in passing, without naming a source, as one of the contact claims of that decade. If taken at face value, it would mean visitors were speaking to chosen people on Earth.","van-tassel-s-venusian-nordics-and-the-hybrid-lower-races-1950":"In the 1950s, at Giant Rock in the California desert, a man named George Van Tassel said he was in contact with beings from Venus called the Nordics, meaning tall aliens who look human. According to his account, they told him about hybrid \"lower races\". The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells this to Michels, summing up Van Tassel's claims and pointing to a text called Closer Encounters. He brings it up as an example of how alien contact stories carry ideas of race and religion.","vandenberg-and-malmstrom-as-recurring-ufo-hotspots":"Vandenberg, an Air Force base in California, and Malmstrom, an Air Force base in Montana, are places where UFO sightings keep coming back over the years, Michels says. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about a nuclear missile guard who says he was abducted. If true, it would mean the sightings cluster around military sites rather than falling at random.","vandenberg-ufo-takes-down-dummy-warhead-1964":"In September 1964, an Air Force camera crew filming a missile test from Vandenberg Air Force Base in California caught a saucer-shaped object circling a dummy nuclear warhead in flight and hitting it with beams of light, according to the man who led the filming. That man is Bob Jacobs, an Air Force photo instrumentation specialist. He tells Michels firsthand that his telescope was at Big Sur, about 100 miles up the coast, aimed at an Atlas rocket that rose through the fog. On film, \"we saw the dummy warhead and suddenly into that frame an object flew in chasing the warhead\", firing light at it. If true, an unknown craft was able to catch and knock out a nuclear warhead in flight. His supervisor, Major Florenze Mansmann, backs him up, according to Michels. Michels adds that the object fired four beams, that the warhead tumbled off course, and that the film was confiscated, though he gives the speed variously as 8,000 or 9,000 to 12,000 miles an hour. The researcher Robert Hastings wrote it up in \"Big Sur UFO Film: Government Whistleblower Reveals He Watched It\", published in 2024. There, Jacobs and Mansmann say the team filmed \"a domed, disc-shaped UFO as it circled and then disabled, with four flashes of an intense beam of light, a dummy nuclear warhead\", and the date is given as \"September 15, 1964.\" A guest, Ross Coulthart, tells it from an Omni magazine article and places the launch near Edwards Air Force Base instead. The filmmaker James Fox also brings it up.","vanessa-found-the-teller-tape-for-the-documentary-1989":"A key piece of evidence for a documentary about Bob Lazar, the man at the center of a well-known 1989 Las Vegas UFO story, was a recording called the teller tape, and it surfaced only because a woman named Vanessa found it. The researcher Luigi Vendittelli, who worked on the project, tells Michels: 'We wouldn't have found the teller tape if it wasn't for Vanessa.'","vanguard-enterprise-nonprofit-for-uap-whistleblowers-2026":"In 2026 a nonprofit called Vanguard Enterprise, or Vanguard for short, was set up to support whistleblowers on UAP, the government's term for UFOs. Matthew Brown, who comes on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, says he started it with Dylan Borland and that a third man, Jeffrey Nuccetelli, has since joined. Borland, telling it on his own episode, says the aim is to help whistleblowers and their families \"medically, financially, spiritually, emotionally.\"","vannevar-bush-and-the-early-ufo-program-1940":"Vannevar Bush, the engineer who ran American wartime science and wrote the 1945 report Science, The Endless Frontier, is said to have been part of a secret UFO program at its start. Michels says that Robert Sarbacher, a scientist who advised the government, claimed Bush was \"part of the program initially\"; if true, it would put one of the country's most powerful scientists at the root of UFO secrecy.","vannevar-bush-s-post-war-science-structures-left-as-a-zombie-1940":"The researcher Eric Weinstein argues that in the 1940s \"genius administrators like Vannevar Bush,\" the man who ran America's wartime science effort, built structures for secret research that \"worked pretty well,\" but \"didn't pass the knowledge of what the structures were\" to the people who came after. He gives no source for it. If he is right, the machinery would have kept running as a kind of zombie, with nobody left who understood what it was for.","vannevar-bush-s-wartime-research-and-development-board":"During the war, the engineer Vannevar Bush ran a research and development board that pulled professors out of their universities to work on government projects, and officials then realised this was \"a great way to do it\". The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this without naming a source. It matters to him because it would be the model for how secret research, including UFO research, was later organised.","varginha-being-taken-alive-to-regional-and-then-humanitas-1996":"In 1996, in the Brazilian town of Varginha, a creature said to have been captured after a crash was taken while still alive first to a hospital called Regional and then to another called Humanitas. The filmmaker James Fox, who investigated the case and talked to its witnesses, tells Michels this, saying 'we knew for decades' that it went 'initially alive to Regional' and then to Humanitas. If true, it would mean a living non-human being passed through ordinary Brazilian hospitals.","varginha-crash-and-captured-being-1996":"In 1996, in the town of Varginha, Brazil, witnesses from separate sources reported a cigar-shaped object, like the white oval craft Navy pilots nicknamed the Tic Tac, falling from the sky and three beings coming out, one of whom was captured and taken to a hospital where, Michels says, everyone who worked there reported seeing it. Michels retells this on several episodes from the work of the documentary filmmaker James Fox and his film Moment of Contact, and in one adds, crediting a witness named Carlos de Souza, that the beings smelled of sulfur and ammonia.","varginha-et-as-a-national-celebrity-1996":"In 1996 people in Varginha, a town in the Brazilian state of Minas Gerais, reported a strange being, and the creature itself became famous across Brazil as the \"Varginha ET\". Aldo Rebelo, a Brazilian politician who served as his country's defense minister, says so in his own words to Michels: \"that event created a celebrity in Brazil.\" To him that fame shows how deeply the case took hold in the country.","varginha-neurosurgeon-and-new-witness-testimony-1996":"Varginha is a town in Brazil where, people say, a strange creature was captured in 1996, and the documentary filmmaker James Fox says his film Moment of Contact brings out new testimony about it. Fox tells Michels he interviewed two forensic pathologists, the chief of police, a new crash witness and the lead neurosurgeon at the regional hospital, Italo Venturelli. If they are right, the case points to a living being held by the authorities and treated in a hospital.","varginha-records-strictly-classified-at-the-ministry-of-defe-1996":"Brazil's Ministry of Defense keeps its records on the 1996 Varginha case, in which witnesses say a craft came down in a Brazilian town and a creature was taken, under tight secrecy. That is what Aldo Rebelo, a former Brazilian defense minister, tells Michels: \"The information that's held by the Ministry of Defense is strictly classified.\" Coming from a former minister, it would mean the government holds files on the case that the public cannot see.","varginha-town-hall-creates-a-ufo-museum-1996":"The town hall of Varginha, a town in Minas Gerais, Brazil, where residents said strange beings appeared in 1996, set up a museum about the event, according to Aldo Rebelo, a Brazilian politician who has served as defense minister. He tells Michels the town hall created it \"to acknowledge and value the phenomenon.\" In his telling, the local government itself treats the 1996 sightings as part of the town's history.","varginha-witness-held-shape-returning-material-1996":"A witness to a reported 1996 UFO crash near Varginha, Brazil, says he picked up a piece of the wreckage and that it sprang back into shape whenever he bent it. If true, the craft was made of a material unlike anything ordinary. The witness is Carlos de Souza, who says he found the crash. He tells Michels himself that when he handled 'this foil or this sheet,' it 'regained its shape.' Michels retells it on several episodes. He calls the material 'light, tinfoily' and says it would 'immediately go back into its original shape, like some sort of memory metal.' He says this sounds 'exactly like the crash material at Roswell,' the famous 1947 crash in New Mexico. On another episode, Michels links it to what Bob Lazar, a man at the center of a long-running UFO story, describes. A surgeon named Italo Venturelli also points to 'the testimony of the guy who saw the spacecraft, picked up a piece with his hand,' but does not mention the shape-returning.","varginha-witnesses-in-dc-and-fbi-letter-1996":"Witnesses from the Varginha case, a 1996 UFO incident in the Brazilian town of Varginha, came to Washington, and Congressman Eric Burlison says he heard from them in person after a meeting there. He tells Michels he was there with the filmmaker James Fox and the journalist Leslie Kean, both of whom have followed the case. The story also involves a letter to the FBI, but he gives no details about it in this telling. If what the witnesses told him is true, it would bring a foreign UFO case to the attention of Congress.","vatican-archives-ceremony-with-bledsoe-s-photo-2017":"In 2017, a photograph taken by Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him, was reportedly placed in the Vatican archives during a ceremony, set between books by Galileo and Copernicus, with his picture shown beside the Pope's outside the archives. Bledsoe tells Michels this second-hand, as he heard it from the religious studies scholar Diana Pasulka and a man named Timothy Taylor. To Bledsoe it means the Church took his experiences seriously enough to keep them alongside great figures of science.","vatican-confiscated-the-fatima-prophecy-1917":"After children at Fatima in Portugal delivered prophecies in 1917, the Vatican took one of those prophecies and kept it, Michels says. He tells it himself and loosely (\"the orphans or whatever\"), tying it to what are called the Secrets of Fatima. If it is true, the Church locked away a message it did not want made public.","vatican-dossier-and-joint-ss-italian-study-of-the-magenta-ob-1930":"In the 1930s, the Vatican was told about a mysterious craft known in this lore as the Magenta object, kept a file on it, and took part in a joint working group of Nazi SS scientists and Italian scientists who studied it in a third country, which the story places in Czechoslovakia. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells this to Michels without a named source, though he cites a witness named David Grusch. If true, the Church and two fascist states were studying a strange object together years before the American UFO era began.","vela-incident-labeled-the-747-incident-1979":"The Vela incident, a mysterious flash picked up over the South Atlantic in 1979 and linked to South Africa or Israel, was privately labelled the '747 incident', according to a former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen. He tells Michels he heard the name from a scientist at the Department of Energy whom he does not name. The label suggests insiders treated the event as something other than the official account.","velikovsky-s-when-worlds-collide-the-asteroid-belt-as-a-dest":"The asteroid belt, the ring of rocks between Mars and Jupiter, is said to be the wreckage of a planet that was destroyed. The lawyer Dan Sheehan brings up this idea on the show, crediting it to \"an old classic\" he calls When Worlds Collide and says the writer Immanuel Velikovsky wrote. If true, the solar system would have gone through a catastrophe that once broke up a whole world.","vera-rubin-telescope-to-remove-classified-satellites-from-it-2020":"The Vera Rubin Observatory, a large new telescope built to survey the whole sky, will scrub classified satellites and other objects from the images it releases, according to two tellers on the show. The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel relays this as something she was told, saying the telescope 'is going to remove a lot of classified satellites and other objects.' Michels repeats the claim on a later episode in a stronger form, citing an article in The Atlantic. He says 'all of Rubin's imagery has to be approved by the Pentagon,' and that this review happens at a secure facility in California. Villarroel speaks of objects being removed, while Michels speaks of Pentagon sign-off on everything. If true, it would mean the public's newest view of the sky is filtered before anyone outside the military sees it, and that unexplained objects could be filtered out along with the secret satellites.","verichip-implant-raytheon-lineage-and-retaliation-suggestion-2002":"An implantable tracking chip called the VeriChip went on sale in 2002, Michels says, \"just a few years before Gary's experience in 2005\", meaning Gary McKinnon, a computer hacker who says he found signs of a secret NASA space programme. Working from the company's prospectus filings, Michels traces the chip to a firm called Applied Digital Solutions and links it back to the defence company Raytheon. He suggests the chip may tie into retaliation against McKinnon, which would mean a hacker was punished with a corporate surveillance technology.","vesuvius-quiet-since-1948-and-accumulating-energy":"Mount Vesuvius, the volcano above Naples, is said to have stopped smoking in 1948 and to have been building up energy ever since. A researcher named Filippo Biondi, known for scanning beneath the Egyptian pyramids, tells Michels this without naming a source. If true, the long silence would be a sign of a larger eruption to come.","vial-of-gold-flakes-it-s-all-about-the-moon-1996":"In 1996, at the Los Angeles offices of Dark Skies, a project he worked on, a Hollywood writer named Brent Friedman says a man he calls \"the captain\" told him to \"forget about all this other stuff. It's all about the moon,\" and slammed down a small vial. Friedman tells Michels that he picked it up and shook it like a snow globe, but there was no liquid in it, only flakes of gold. He tells it as something that happened to him, and the hint is that the moon holds a secret more important than anything else in the UFO story.","victim-sees-ufo-lights-at-zorro-ranch-and-wakes-in-a-lab-2002":"Around 2002, a woman stepped outside at Zorro Ranch, a New Mexico ranch the episode ties to Jeffrey Epstein, saw strange lights flying overhead, and then found herself waking up in a lab. Juliet Byron, who is presented on the show as one of Epstein's victims, says this happened to her: \"I went to have a cigarette and I saw a weird like lights flying and then I had the weird thing waking up in the lab.\" If true, it would tie the ranch to both UFO sightings and some kind of experiment on people.","victor-bertrandius-witnesses-brown-s-gravitator-experiments-1952":"In Los Angeles in 1952, an Air Force lieutenant colonel from Wright Field named Victor Bertrandias, who later became a general, watched a demonstration of the 'gravitator', a device built by the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown, who believed electricity could affect gravity. Michels, drawing on Paul Schatzkin's biography of Brown, The Man Who Mastered Gravity, quotes Bertrandias saying 'Believe it or not, I think I just saw a flying saucer and it frightened me'. Another time Michels tells it as a surprise visit to the Townsend Brown Foundation where Bertrandias said only that he 'saw a model of a flying saucer', and a third time he gives no exact year and also names an officer called Albert Boyd. If true, it would mean the Air Force saw a working saucer-like device in a private lab in the early 1950s.","victor-lagno-arxiv-paper-backs-pais-room-temperature-superco":"Salvatore Pais, a Navy scientist known for patents on unusual technology, says a paper by Victor Lagno of the Russian Academy of Sciences, posted on arXiv (an online archive where scientists share papers), showed that his design for a room-temperature superconductor, a material that carries electricity with no loss without being chilled, \"could work\" in principle. Pais tells Michels this himself, and adds that the patent examiner still \"just refused to grant the patent\", which he takes as a sign that his idea was rejected despite outside support.","video-of-bill-lear-at-bahnsen-s-labs-1950":"Michels says there is film footage, from the 1950s, of the aviation businessman Bill Lear visiting the North Carolina labs of a man named Agnew Bahnson. On that visit, Michels says, Lear watched the \"gravitator\" experiments of the inventor Townsend Brown, devices Brown hoped would affect gravity. Michels mentions the video himself, without saying where it can be found, as a sign that serious industrial figures were watching this kind of research.","video-of-smoke-rising-from-the-water-above-the-object-2011":"In 2011, a dive team that had found a strange object on the floor of the Baltic Sea is said to have filmed smoke rising from the water above it, \"as if it's fuming or emitting gas.\" Michels says this on an episode with Dennis Asberg, the Swedish treasure hunter whose team found the object, telling it as part of the team's account. If true, it would suggest the object on the seabed is somehow still active.","video-of-the-surgeon-operating-on-the-alien-1996":"In Varginha, Brazil, in 1996, a surgeon named Italo Venturelli is said to have operated on a living alien in his hospital, and a video of the surgery is said to exist. Venturelli, who says he took part, has mentioned the tape several times, and Michels sums it up in his interview with him as a video \"of him essentially performing surgery on this alien.\"","video-of-townsend-brown-celebrating-at-unc-chapel-hill-1950":"There are videos, it is said, of the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown celebrating at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill in the 1950s, because his experiments had \"presumably worked.\" Michels tells this himself in an episode about Brown, without naming the footage or saying which experiments they were. If true, it would show that Brown believed, at least for a moment, that his work had succeeded.","vietnam-veteran-on-shooting-at-spooks":"In Vietnam, firing at these strange figures was a waste of ammunition: you thought you were shooting at somebody, but you hit nothing. Michael Johnson, an Air Force veteran who appears on the show with other veterans who say they were abducted by aliens, tells Michels a man who served in Vietnam told him this. If true, soldiers in that war would have met figures that bullets could not touch.","vietnam-veteran-s-survivor-guilt":"Greg Rogers, a former NASA doctor, tells Michels about a man he treated when he was a very young doctor. The man had been in a firefight in Vietnam and came home weighed down by guilt that he had lived when others did not. Rogers passes on what the patient told him and uses the story to talk about how heavy experiences mark the people who live through them.","view-from-the-ship-s-porthole-of-a-fiery-planet-and-boxes-be-1997":"In 1997, Kiran Ilum Xenov, whom the show presents as the president of a Russian republic, says he was taken aboard an alien craft and looked out of its porthole. He tells Michels he saw \"something like a fiery well, like a planet giving off heat,\" and that cylinders, or more like boxes, were being taken out of it and loaded onto the ship.","viewer-s-theory-that-the-stranger-was-a-kachina-spirit-guide":"A stranger whom Geoffrey Drumm met in Egypt was, according to one viewer, a kachina, a spirit being from the traditions of the Pueblo peoples of the American Southwest, sent to check on him. Drumm, a pyramid researcher who says an alien explained the pyramids' true purpose to him, tells Michels that a viewer named Vince wrote, 'That's a cachina that came to make sure you were on the right path.' If so, his meeting in Egypt would have been a guided encounter rather than chance.","viking-lander-life-tests-read-as-finding-life-1976":"In 1976 NASA's Viking landers ran tests on the soil of Mars looking for life, and according to the researcher John Brandenburg they \"got mixed results which now can be interpreted as\" finding it. Brandenburg says this on the show without citing a source. If that reading holds, life on Mars would have been detected half a century ago.","viking-landing-at-cydonia-overruled-by-an-unknown-group-1976":"In 1976, the scientists running NASA's Viking mission to Mars were choosing where to land when, the story goes, they were suddenly overruled by a group they had not even known was part of the project, and the landing went elsewhere than Cydonia, a Mars region some think holds artificial-looking features. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels this, calling it 'kind of scandalous'. If true, a hidden hand was steering NASA away from a place it did not want examined.","viking-orbiter-image-of-the-face-on-mars-1973":"In the mid 1970s, one of the Viking orbiters, NASA probes sent to circle Mars, sent back a photo of the Cydonia region that showed what looked like a human face: a structure about a mile long. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and says the image \"caused quite a stir\" because it raised the question of whether someone had once built on Mars.","villager-shoots-a-silver-suited-hovering-assailant-with-a-sh-2023":"In 2023, in the Alto Nanay region of the Peruvian Amazon, a villager says he was attacked by a hovering figure in a silver suit and shot it with a shotgun at point-blank range. He could hear the pellets ping off its armour, and the blast knocked the figure back onto the ground. The filmmaker Tim Alberino tells Michels what the villager told him, and if true it would mean armoured beings that can hover were attacking people in remote villages.","villarroel-approached-by-people-she-suspects-have-intelligen-2025":"The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel studies strange short-lived points of light on old photographs of the sky, which she suggests could be objects near Earth. She says that in 2025, in Stockholm, she was approached by people she suspects have intelligence backgrounds. She tells Michels this herself, saying only \"I have been approached by some people I suspect have it,\" and if her suspicion is right, spy agencies are quietly interested in her work.","villarroel-on-multiple-transients-appearing-and-vanishing-1949":"On photographs of the night sky taken at the Palomar Observatory in California between 1949 and 1957, points of light called transients show up on one plate and are gone on the next, and sometimes \"you can see multiple of them appearing and vanishing within half an hour.\" The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, who studies these old plates, tells Michels this. If the lights are real objects, something bright was in the sky near Earth before any satellite was launched.","villarroel-s-palomar-transients-1949":"An astronomer at Stockholm University named Beatriz Villarroel went back through photographic plates of the night sky taken at the Palomar Observatory in California from 1949 to 1957, before the first satellite went up. She found huge numbers of \"transients\", short-lived points of light that look like flat, mirror-like objects reflecting the sun: more than 10,000 a year by Michels's count, about 105,000 from the northern sky alone by her own. Michels tells this on many episodes, sometimes adding that the flashes seem to line up with nuclear tests and to disappear inside the Earth's shadow, which he offers against the idea that they are flaws in the plates. Villarroel herself, on the show, gives the 105,000 figure and puts 30 to 35 percent of the objects down to sunlight reflecting off them. If the objects are real, something shiny was circling the Earth before humans had launched anything into space.","villarroel-transients-on-1950s-plates-correlate-with-nuclear":"Brief points of light that appear and vanish on 1950s photographs of the night sky from the Palomar observatory, which the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel calls transients, are said to show up more often around US nuclear tests: Michels says they are '68% more likely to show up within 24 hours of a nuclear detonation', and the researcher Stephen Bruehl, who did the study, tells the show that on days after a test 'almost 19% of those days have a transient', also checking reports in UFOCAT, a database of UFO sightings kept by the Center for UFO Studies. Both Villarroel and Bruehl describe the work as a paper in the journal Scientific Reports, and Villarroel calls the link 'weak, but it's there and it's statistically significant', while Michels on a later show credits it only to an unnamed Vanderbilt PhD. If true, it would suggest something in orbit was responding to the bomb tests years before the first satellite.","villarroel-transients-on-july-1952-plates":"The astronomer Beatriz Villarroel, who searches old photographic sky surveys for points of light that appear once and then vanish, tells Michels about two such finds from July 1952: five objects lined up on a narrow band, on a plate from 27 July, and a \"triple transient\", three star-like points found by her colleague Enrique, on a plate from 19 July. She tells it as her own team's work and also credits colleagues she names as Dave Altman and Lash, and Michels reads the same description out himself in an earlier episode. If the points are real objects, they would be things in the sky that no one has explained.","vimanas-and-self-guiding-arrows-in-the-vedic-texts":"The Vedic texts, the ancient Hindu scriptures of India, are said to describe vimanas, flying vehicles, along with arrows that guide themselves to their targets. A former NASA doctor named Greg Rogers points this out to Michels, saying the texts are 'talking about vimanas', without citing a particular passage. To him it suggests ancient people remembered advanced flying technology.","vinod-s-hidden-background-as-friend-of-gandhi-and-bombay-pro-1950":"A man known as Dr. Vinod, who turns up in a story about a CIA scientist who says he contacted an alien council in the early 1950s, had a hidden past as a friend of Gandhi and a professor in Bombay. The researcher Greg Malatzi brings this up on the show without naming a source, half asking it: \"He was friends with Gandhi, right?\" The point would be that a figure in the contact story had more weight and standing than he showed.","viracocha-and-lake-titicaca-as-a-mirror-civilization-of-lake":"The ancient culture around Lake Titicaca in Bolivia and Peru, with its ruined sites of Tiwanaku and Puma Punku, is said to mirror an ancient civilization around Lake Van in Turkey. A researcher named Matt LaCroix tells Michels, without naming a source, that the Andean creator figure Viracocha is shown in Bolivia holding two condors, one on either side, which he reads as the same idea as the pillars \"holding balance\" he sees in the Lake Van region. If true, it would point to a shared ancient tradition on two continents.","virk-advises-the-galileo-project-and-the-sol-project-2020":"An MIT scientist named Rizwan Virk says that in the 2020s he has advised the Galileo Project, a scientific effort to study unexplained objects, and has been loosely involved in the Sol Project, a similar effort at Stanford. He tells Michels: \"I'm an advisor to the Galileo Project and peripherally involved with the Sol Project as well at Stanford.\" It matters because it shows established universities taking up the subject.","vision-foretelling-the-attempt-on-trump-s-life-2024":"A man in a clip played on an episode about seeing the future says he had a vision of an attempt on Donald Trump's life in which \"this bullet flew by his ear and it came so close to his head that it busted his drum.\" The speaker is not named. The clip is played because, if the vision came before the 2024 shooting, it would be a case of foreseeing a real event.","vision-of-a-rocket-exploding-at-launch-1990":"A former sergeant named Dan Sherman, who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that once, between 1990 and 1993, while at a site he calls Base C-van, he saw in a vision a missile being launched and exploding, possibly a Chinese launch. He tells it on the show as something he experienced himself, and it is connected to the book Above Black; if true, it would mean he was shown distant events he could not otherwise have seen.","visit-to-allen-dulles-s-former-office-in-switzerland":"Allen Dulles, the wartime spy chief who later ran the CIA, once said that as long as he was in Switzerland, no American law could reach him. Harald Malmgren, a former adviser to US presidents, tells Michels he heard this from Swiss government officials when he visited Dulles's old office there; they said Dulles told them so when he got permission to keep a very active office in the country. If true, it would show a top American intelligence man setting himself up beyond the reach of US law.","visit-to-drew-s-house-and-his-needle-and-teflon-setup-wife-j-2016":"In 2016 Charles Buhler, a NASA engineer who says his team has built an antigravity propulsion device, visited the Florida house of an inventor named Andrew Arjima to see an experimental setup built from a needle and Teflon. Buhler tells Michels that he brought his wife, Janessa, and that they spent \"four or five hours looking at his setup.\" He says it was Janessa who linked the forces at work in the device, a step he presents as part of how his own propulsion work began.","visit-to-the-antikythera-mechanism":"The owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a property where he says on the show there is a UFO under the ground, tells Michels that he recently travelled to Athens, Greece, to see the Antikythera mechanism, an ancient geared device. He tells it as his own trip, made out of interest in what ancient people may have known or built.","visit-to-the-pantex-pits-1980":"In the 1980s, John S. Herrington, who was then US Secretary of Energy, went to the Pantex plant in Texas, a site under his department, and says he went \"down in the pits\" there. He tells Michels this himself: \"I've been in Pantex. I've been down in the pits.\" He brings it up to show how close he came to the most secret parts of the places he oversaw.","visit-to-the-serapeum-of-saqqara-and-its-precise-boxes-2025":"In 2025, Filippo Biondi, a scientist known for scanning beneath the Egyptian pyramids, went into the Serapeum of Saqqara for the first time. It is an underground site in Egypt known for its large, precisely made stone boxes. He tells Michels about the visit as part of his work on what he believes lies under the pyramids.","visitor-calls-strieber-the-luckiest-of-the-lucky-1980":"Whitley Strieber, a writer who says he has been abducted by beings he calls \"the visitors,\" says that during one abduction in the 1980s one of them told him, \"You're the luckiest of the lucky.\" He tells Michels this as something he lived through. If true, it would suggest the beings saw his contact with them as a gift.","visitors-appear-when-anne-s-chosen-groups-come-to-the-cabin-1987":"The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met beings he calls \"the visitors\", tells Michels that when his wife, Anne Strieber, brought groups of people up to their cabin in upstate New York, from the late 1980s through the 1990s, the visitors would show up. He tells it as something he saw himself, and if true it would mean the beings appeared on cue for people Anne had chosen.","visitors-base-under-the-mountains-at-dulce":"The visitors, meaning beings from elsewhere, are said to have already picked out a home for themselves under the mountains at Dulce, a small town in New Mexico. An Air Force veteran named Mario Woods, who says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels that somebody told him this long ago. He does not say who.","visitors-confirm-kennedy-ufo-connection-1996":"In 1996, visitors to the Los Angeles offices of Dark Skies, a television show about UFOs, told its makers that the show had got one thing right: President Kennedy really was connected to the UFO secret. A television writer named Bryce Zabel, who says he was there, tells Michels that the visitors, whom he names only as JC and \"the captain\", said that \"definitely the Kennedy and UFOs were correct.\" If true, it would mean insiders were confirming that Kennedy had some tie to the UFO question.","vitrification-evidence-of-ancient-nuclear-exchanges":"The philosopher Jason Jorjani says there is \"evidence of nuclear exchanges in vast antiquity,\" pointing to vitrification, rock or soil melted into glass, \"at various ancient sites.\" He names no sites and no source when he says it on the show. If true, it would mean that some lost civilisation, or someone else, fought with nuclear weapons long before recorded history.","vitrified-basalt-blocks-at-kef-kalesi-confirmed-by-schoch":"Basalt blocks at Kef Kalesi, an ancient site near Lake Van in Turkey, show \"literal melting of the stone,\" according to Matt LaCroix, a researcher of ancient sites who says he examined them. He tells Michels that Dr. Robert Schoch confirmed on numerous stones that this is vitrification, meaning the stone was melted until it turned glassy. If he is right, the stones were once hit by intense heat that is hard to explain.","vivex-biograft-based-on-recovered-metal-regrows-bone-2010":"In the 2010s, according to Chris Bledsoe, a company called Vivex made a bone-regrowing biograft that he says was \"based on metal the metal that they recovered,\" meaning material from a crashed UFO. Bledsoe is a man who says he has had repeated UFO encounters. He tells Michels this from his own involvement and names Timothy Taylor in connection with it. If true, crash debris would already have led to a medical product.","voice-in-a-dream-urges-him-to-speak-1997":"Kiran Ilum Xenov, a former president of a Russian republic who says he was taken by aliens in 1997, tells Michels that one night in his sleep he heard a voice ask, \"What are you afraid of? Why aren't you speaking? You met with us, didn't you?\" He tells it as his own experience, a push from the beings he says he met to go public about them.","voice-in-masters-head-behind-morton-hall-at-ohio-university":"While standing behind Morton Hall at Ohio University, a researcher named Mike Masters heard a small voice in his head telling him to come at the question a new way. Masters, who argues that the aliens may be humans from the future, tells Michels: \"there was this little voice in my head that said maybe we should explore it this way.\" He tells it as the moment his idea began.","voice-of-god-microwave-hearing-program":"The story is that the US government has a programme that beams microwaves at a person's head to make vibrations the brain hears as sound, and then shapes those vibrations into a voice, so that someone hears words no one else can. A former senior NSA official named Eric Haseltine describes it on the show as something he knows from the inside, saying microwaves can \"create vibrations like sound\" and that \"there's a program to modulate that with voice. We call it the voice of God.\" A former Pentagon official named Matthew Brown, who presents himself as a whistleblower, tells a version of it too. He uses the public name, \"voice to skull\" technology, and calls it the crudest of a whole family of such tools. Brown adds that he never saw that programme himself on the inside and that he knows it by a different term, which he does not give. If true, it would mean the government can put a voice inside a person's head at a distance.","volunteering-at-the-murrah-building-after-the-oklahoma-city-1995":"John Blitch, a former scientist at DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, says he volunteered at the Murrah Federal Building after the Oklahoma City bombing in 1995. In the fragment that survives of his account, he recalls some \"purple chiffon material\" there, but he says no more about it.","von-braun-captured-in-bavaria-under-operation-paperclip-1945":"The German rocket engineer Wernher von Braun was captured in Bavaria in 1945, where a young Henry Kissinger was helping run US counterintelligence, and his record was then \"scrubbed\" so he could be brought to America under Operation Paperclip, the US programme that recruited German scientists after the war. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, in an episode about NASA and UFOs. The point he draws is that NASA's founding figures came to the US with their pasts cleaned up.","von-braun-collecting-lunar-meteorites-in-antarctica-nasa-cer-1960":"In the 1960s, before the first moon landing, the NASA rocket chief Wernher von Braun went to Antarctica to pick up meteorites that had come from the moon, and NASA also ran a ceramics lab. Bart Sibrel, who argues the moon landings were staged, tells Michels this and says \"there's a picture of that on the web.\" If true, the moon rocks NASA showed could have been gathered on Earth rather than brought back by astronauts.","von-braun-encourages-mitchell-s-consciousness-research-1970":"In the 1970s the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun is said to have backed the astronaut Edgar Mitchell's interest in studying human consciousness, even urging him to find a place inside NASA to pursue it, the interest Mitchell later carried into his Institute of Noetic Sciences. Michels tells this as Mitchell's own account, saying von Braun was 'deeply supportive, even encouraging Mitchell to find a site within NASA to study consciousness further.' If true, it would mean one of NASA's leading figures took the study of the mind seriously.","von-braun-in-puharich-s-circle-in-the-1960s":"In the 1960s the German-born rocket engineer Wernher von Braun is said to have been in regular contact with Puharich, a researcher of psychic phenomena. The researcher Greg Malatzi tells Michels this, relaying what Puharich's assistant told him: that von Braun 'talked to him, you know, all the time'. Malatzi notes this is the same assistant who made a comment about JFK, and the claim would tie the space programme's leading engineer to the fringe of psychic research.","von-braun-oversaw-slave-labor-camp-1940":"Michels says that the German rocket engineer Wernher von Braun oversaw \"a brutal, mostly Jewish slave labor camp where tons and tons of people died\" in Germany in the 1940s. He says it himself, in passing, during a conversation about hidden government UFO programmes, without naming a source. If true, it would cast a dark light on one of the men who shaped rocketry and space flight.","von-braun-passing-secrets-at-the-mexican-border-1945":"There is a story, from around 1945, that the German rocket engineer Wernher von Braun passed trade secrets to someone at the US border with Mexico. Michels raises it himself, only vaguely, as \"some story\" he has heard, without naming a source or giving details. If true, it would mean a key scientist of the American rocket effort was leaking what he knew.","von-braun-rudolph-and-the-nazi-transplant-behind-saturn":"NASA's Saturn rocket program, the rockets behind the Moon landings, was in this telling a \"transplant\" of the Nazi space program, built by the German rocket engineer Wernher von Braun, a fellow German engineer named Arthur Rudolph, and 129 Nazi scientists. Michels brings this up himself in three episodes, without naming a source. In one he stresses that it was \"literally\" the whole program and ties it to Apollo. In another he calls the idea a \"conspiracy\" and leaves out the figure of 129. He raises it because, if true, it would mean America's greatest space achievement grew straight out of the Nazi rocket effort.","von-braun-s-deathbed-warning-about-a-staged-alien-threat-1977":"The rocket scientist Wernher von Braun is said to have warned, near the end of his life, that powerful people would one day stage a fake alien threat to unite the world. The UFO activist Steven Greer says he has this from his friend Carol Rosin, who heard it from von Braun on his deathbed. Greer calls it \"the card they want to play\" and a \"false flag.\" In one telling he puts von Braun's death in 1974, while the story dates the warning to 1977. Michels mentions it too, speaking of von Braun at the end of his life talking to a woman whose name he garbles as \"Carolyn Ross.\" If true, it would mean that a coming alien scare should be treated as a deception.","von-braun-s-team-passed-data-to-argentine-nazis-in-mexico-1947":"In the late 1940s, members of the team of Wernher von Braun, the German rocket engineer brought to the US after the war, slipped out of Texas to Mexican border towns. There, the story goes, they met Nazi counterparts who had come up from Argentina and handed them \"data drops.\" The researcher Jason Jorjani tells this to Michels without naming a source; if true, the Germans working on US rockets were still feeding a Nazi network abroad.","von-braun-s-time-magazine-figure-for-the-lunar-neutral-gravi-1960":"In the 1960s the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun reportedly told Time magazine where the point of neutral gravity lies between the Earth and the moon, the spot where the two pulls cancel out, and gave about double the official figure. The comedian Kurt Metzger tells this to Michels, passing on what he read from the author Joseph P. Farrell. If true, it would mean the official numbers about the moon's gravity were not the whole story.","von-braun-shows-uri-geller-a-piece-of-a-crashed-ufo":"The psychic performer Uri Geller says that Wernher von Braun, the German-born rocket engineer who led NASA rocket work, opened a safe in his office, handed him a light piece of metal in a colour Geller had never seen, and asked what he felt. Geller says he answered \"this is not from here\" and von Braun replied, \"You're right. This is a piece of a UFO that crashed on our planet.\" Geller tells it to Michels firsthand more than once; one version places it at a secret NASA base rather than in the office, and Michels, retelling it, dates it to the 1970s and credits Andrija Puharich, a researcher who worked with Geller.","von-braun-tells-edgar-mitchell-of-his-knowledge":"The German-born rocket engineer Wernher von Braun once came to the Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell and told him he had knowledge of UFOs, according to what Michels says Mitchell reported. Michels passes this on in his own words during an interview with a former Air Force officer, Robert Salas, without giving more detail of what von Braun knew. If true, it would place one of the leading figures of the US space programme among those privy to the subject.","von-braun-transmitted-speech-to-carol-rosin-via-mind-interfa-1970":"In the 1970s, the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun used a mind-to-mind link to send a speech straight into the head of Carol Rosin, a woman who worked with him, so that she could deliver it at a big conference. The UFO activist Steven Greer tells Michels this as something he was told by Rosin. If true, a device or method for passing thoughts directly between people would have existed decades ago.","von-daniken-s-ancient-alien-gods-as-spaceships":"The idea is that the gods of ancient peoples were really alien visitors, and their heavenly chariots were spaceships. It is linked to the writer Erich von Däniken and to sites such as Easter Island. Michels sets it out himself in an episode about mushrooms, tying it to Chariots of the Gods, the book he dates to 1968. He says its premise was \"that ancient alien astronauts came down to Earth around 10,000 years ago\", around the time farming began, and \"taught us how to basically start modern civilization.\" The skeptic Michael Shermer also brings it up on the show, without a source. He lumps it in with \"the ancient alien stuff\" of TV, the \"ancient alien gods that are actually, you know, spaceships.\" If it were true, humanity's first civilizations would have been a gift from outside.","von-daniken-s-ancient-astronaut-reading-of-the-nazca-lines":"The Nazca Lines, huge markings on the ground at Nazca in Peru, were tributes to ancient alien astronauts, according to the writer Erich von Däniken, author of the book Chariots of the Gods. In this view they honoured an extraterrestrial group that seeded human civilization. Michels brings this up himself as von Däniken's idea, without endorsing it, while introducing a story about strange three-fingered bodies found in Peru.","von-neumann-paper-the-presence-must-be-believed-into-our-rea-1950":"There is supposedly a paper by the mathematician John von Neumann saying that the presence behind UFOs has to be believed into our reality, which would mean it was kept secret because enough people believing in it might let it in. The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met these beings, tells Michels he heard about it from General Arthur Exon, an Air Force general who was no scientist but had spent time around scientists working on the subject. The writer Greg Bishop repeats the idea from Strieber as the reason for secrecy and links it to Majestic, the supposed secret UFO panel, without naming Exon or the paper.","von-neumann-starts-talking-about-self-replicating-machines-i-1948":"The mathematician John von Neumann is said to have started talking about self-replicating machines, now called von Neumann machines, in 1948, just a year after Roswell, the 1947 incident in New Mexico where many believe a craft crashed. A guest named Deep Prasad tells Michels that von Neumann's earlier lectures had \"nothing to do with aliens,\" and then \"suddenly 1948\" he was talking about these machines \"like it's his job.\" Another guest on the same episode, who is not named, repeats the point and adds 1949. The suggestion is that von Neumann's ideas may have been prompted by what was found at Roswell.","vought-flying-pancake-as-early-saucer-attempt-1940":"In the 1940s, the aircraft maker Vought built a plane known as the Flying Pancake, which a researcher named Dan Sheehan calls \"literally\" an early attempt to build a flying saucer with propellers. Sheehan tells Michels this without naming a source. In his telling it shows the US was already trying to build disc-shaped craft before the saucer reports began.","voyager-1-reaches-one-light-day-on-the-abduction-anniversary-2026":"Michels points out that on November 18, 2026, at 3:16 a.m. Mountain time, Voyager 1, NASA's distant space probe, will be exactly one light day from Earth, meaning its signals will take a full day to reach us. He notes that this is exactly 49 years to the minute after two Air Force veterans, Mario and Michael, say they were blacked out aboard a spacecraft. Michels draws the link himself, in an episode reuniting the two men, as a striking coincidence of timing.","voyager-golden-record-carrying-the-navajo-night-chant-1977":"When the two Voyager spacecraft were launched in 1977, each one carried a golden record \"containing the sights and sounds of planet Earth\", and among those sounds was the Navajo Night Chant. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and uses it to point out that a Native American ceremony is now travelling through interstellar space.","voyager-golden-record-music-selection-bach-would-just-be-bra-1977":"While NASA's team was choosing the music for the Golden Record, a disc of sounds and songs sent into space on the Voyager probes in 1977, someone said they had to include Bach, and someone else answered that it \"would just be bragging\". The well-known skeptic Michael Shermer tells this to Michels as his favourite story from the project. He heard it in the audio version of a book about the record, which he does not name, and it involves the astronomer Carl Sagan and a team member he calls Annie.","vril-society-channels-anti-gravity-technology-1920":"In Germany, sometime between the 1920s and the 1940s, a group called the Vril Society is said to have received anti-gravity technology and psychic powers from an ancient race living underground, passed on through mediums. Jesse Michels brings up these claims himself and names no source. The researcher Jason Jorjani tells it a little differently on the show: in his version the group was a circle of mediums, \"mostly women\" he thinks, who were \"engaging in telepathic communication with basically a group of supermen.\" Jorjani mentions neither the underground race nor the anti-gravity machines, and he names no source either. If any of it were true, some of the strange technology claims tied to Germany in that period would have started in séance rooms, not laboratories.","waking-with-three-bruises-on-the-arm-after-an-expected-visit-2013":"John Blitch, a scientist who worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's research agency, says that in 2013 in Fort Collins, Colorado, he was expecting a visitation and woke to find three bruises on his arm. He tells Michels it happened to him, as part of his account of being abducted by an alien, and the bruises would be physical traces of that contact if it happened as he says.","walker-and-the-cosmos-club":"The researcher Grant Cameron says a man named Eric A. Walker was one of the people linked to the Cosmos Club, a private club in Washington, D.C., and calls this \"one of the things we discovered.\" He offers it on the show without naming a source and says little more about it. To Cameron it points to the Cosmos Club as a place where people tied to UFO secrecy gathered.","walker-how-do-you-know-they-didn-t-get-up-and-walk-away-1987":"In 1987, a man named Eric A. Walker, whom the researcher Grant Cameron calls the head of the entire UFO programme, was asked about MJ-12 (a supposed secret group of top officials said to handle the UFO matter) and the dead bodies said to have been recovered from crashed craft. Walker pushed back on the idea that the bodies were dead. Cameron, who says he heard it himself, recalls Walker saying: \"How do you know they're dead? You assume too many things and they're all wrong. How do you know they didn't get up and walk away?\" If true, the beings from the crashes may have survived.","walker-letter-worked-on-defense-policy-with-vannevar-bush-in-1947":"In 1947 the engineer Eric A. Walker is said to have worked on defense policy alongside Vannevar Bush, the senior science adviser to the government, and a letter of Walker's is cited for it. The UFO researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this from the letter, quoting Walker as writing, 'I worked on defense policy with in 1947 with Vannevar Bush'; the words place Walker beside Bush that year, and whether that work touched the UFO matters Cameron links it to, the quoted line does not say. For Cameron it matters because it would put Walker inside the circle he believes handled the government's UFO secrets from 1947 on.","wall-street-journal-emp-explanation-for-malmstrom-1967":"The story is about a Wall Street Journal article that explained away a famous 1967 event at Malmstrom Air Force Base, a nuclear missile base in Montana, where launch officers said their missiles shut down while UFOs were around. The article, \"The Pentagon Disinformation That Fueled America's UFO Mythology\" by Joel Schectman, was published on June 6, 2025, and it opens with Robert Salas, then \"an Air Force captain\" in a small bunker \"manning the controls of 10 nuclear missiles.\" It credits the explanation to the team of Sean Kirkpatrick, who led AARO, a Pentagon office set up to look into UFO reports: \"Kirkpatrick's team dug into the story and discovered a terrestrial explanation,\" an Air Force \"exotic electromagnetic generator\" that faked the pulse of a nuclear blast, set \"on a portable platform 60 feet above the facility,\" glowing \"sometimes with a blinding orange light,\" its pulses running down cables into the bunker and disabling the weapons, while \"the witnesses were kept in the dark.\" Michels returns to it again and again, calling it a \"disinfo hit piece,\" saying they claimed an EMP device was wheeled up and fried the electronics with people present, which he calls dangerous, reckless and without precedent, and saying Kirkpatrick was probably its sole source. Read closely, the article presents the EMP story as what the investigators found rather than as the reporter's own finding, and it says it rests on \"interviews with two dozen current and former U.S. officials, scientists and military contractors\" and on documents it does not name. If the article is right, the Malmstrom shutdown was a secret American test, not anything from elsewhere.","wall-street-journal-op-ed-and-david-spergel-s-dismissal-2023":"Jesse Michels says that in 2024 he pushed the Wall Street Journal to publish an opinion piece by his friend Karl Nell and a former cabinet member, whom he elsewhere calls a former Secretary of Defense. Nell had been the Army's representative on the Pentagon's UAP task force (UAP, short for \"unidentified anomalous phenomena\", is the government's term for UFOs), and Michels says the paper turned the piece down. Michels says he met the paper's opinion editor, Lena Bell, at its New York headquarters, and that she told him \"a guy named David Spergel is their de facto advisor on UFOs,\" a man who says UFOs \"are all space trash, light reflections, and temperature inversions.\" Michels tells this as something he took part in, cites emails between Nell and the paper, and offers it as a sign that a major newspaper was closing the door on the subject.","wally-wallington-builds-a-stonehenge-in-his-backyard-on-his":"A man named Wally Wallington put up his own Stonehenge in his backyard, moving the huge stones by himself, and filmed it. The researcher Luke Caverns, who studies how ancient monuments were built, mentions his videos to Michels as proof that one person with simple methods can move enormous stones.","walrus-artwork-on-epstein-s-wall-2002":"In 2002, on a wall at a property of Jeffrey Epstein, there was said to hang a large artwork showing a woman being raped by a walrus. The researcher Jay Anderson tells Michels this, passing on what a witness named Juliet Byron told him she saw. Anderson raises it as a sign of the dark tastes of Epstein's world.","walter-b-smith-1953-letter-on-using-the-phenomenon-for-psych":"In 1953, Michels says, the CIA director Walter B. Smith wrote that the US should use the UFO phenomenon for psychological warfare against the Soviets, playing it up for Cold War purposes. Michels tells this himself on several episodes without saying where the document can be read, calling it a letter in one place and a memo in another, once hedging that Smith \"I believe took over the CIA in 52-53,\" and once adding that there are \"similar leaked letters\" from the later CIA director Allen Dulles. If true, it would mean the CIA saw UFO reports early on as a tool to exploit, not just a puzzle to solve.","walter-dornberger-at-wright-patterson-when-roswell-debris-ar-1947":"Walter Dornberger, who ran Germany's wartime rocket base at Peenemünde, was posted first to Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio, and was there in 1947 when debris from Roswell came in by train. Roswell is the New Mexico town where something crashed that summer and the military first announced a \"flying disc.\" The researcher Peter Levenda tells this to Michels without naming a source, and it would put a top Nazi rocket man in the room with whatever was recovered.","walter-haut-s-posthumous-affidavit-craft-and-bodies-under-a-1947":"An Army lieutenant named Walter Haut, who was at Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, left a sworn statement to be released only after his death, in which he says he saw a crashed craft and bodies lying under a tarp. Michels brings this up himself while debating a skeptic, as an example of a firsthand witness who held his account back for life. If true, it would mean an officer on the scene saw both a vehicle and its occupants.","wang-s-belongings-removed-from-his-house-after-death":"When a man named Eric Henry Wang died, people came and took a lot of things out of his house, according to a researcher named Greg Bishop. Bishop tells Michels this without naming a source, and does not say who \"they\" were. If true, it would suggest someone wanted his papers or belongings kept out of other hands.","war-department-asks-fbi-to-clear-gebauer-1942":"In 1942 or 1943, the War Department asked J. Edgar Hoover's FBI to give a security clearance to a man named Leo GeBauer, according to Scott Ramsey, a researcher who has studied a reported 1948 UFO crash near Aztec, New Mexico. Ramsey tells Michels he found this in GeBauer's FBI file, and from it he reasons, in his words, \"I think he worked on some kind of topshelf project,\" though he does not say the file names any project. It matters to him because it would mean GeBauer had serious secret government work behind him.","wargo-s-theory-that-remote-viewers-see-their-own-future-feed-1972":"The researcher Eric Wargo suggests that remote viewers, people trained in the US government's psychic spying programme of 1972 to 1995, known as Stargate, to describe hidden or distant targets with the mind, may really be seeing their own future feedback: the moment later on when they are shown what the target was. Wargo puts this forward on the show as his own theory. Michels adds that he spoke with Hal Puthoff, whom he calls the founder of the CIA psychic spy programme, and that Puthoff \"was very open to Wargo being right\" that this confirming feedback is needed for remote viewing to work, which would make it a kind of seeing across time rather than across space.","warning-call-after-a-howard-stern-producer-s-call-1997":"Dan Sherman, a former Air Force sergeant who says the NSA hired him to communicate with aliens, says that soon after a producer from Howard Stern's radio show called him, he got another call warning him off. He tells Michels the voice on the line said to drop the story, stay off Stern's show and stop talking about it. If that is true, someone was watching him closely enough to know who had called him.","warning-not-to-disturb-the-earth-2016":"When you dig into the ground at Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property famous for reports of strange events, \"negative things happen\". Brandon Fugal, who owns the ranch, tells Michels that he was warned of this around 2016, passing on what someone told him. If true, the land itself would be reacting to being dug up.","wartime-intelligence-agencies-run-by-financiers":"During World War II, American and British intelligence services were run by men from banking and finance, and there was even a lesser-known American spy agency just for South America, the Office for the Coordination of Inter-American Affairs. The researcher Jason Simosa tells Michels this without naming a source, linking it to names such as Allen Dulles and Nelson Rockefeller. If true, the same moneyed circles stood behind the wartime spy world.","was-condon-blackmailed-by-fbi-agent-guy-hottel-into-debunkin-1950":"The physicist Edward Condon, who came to dismiss UFOs, may have been blackmailed into doing so, the theory goes: while he was being investigated for communist sympathies in the 1950s, an FBI field agent named Guy Hottel was quietly building a dossier of field reports on him. Michels puts this forward himself, without naming a source. If true, it would mean a leading scientific voice against UFOs was pressured by the government into that view.","was-randy-shown-alien-technology-as-a-reverse-engineering-li":"Michels wonders whether a weapons expert named Randy Anderson, who says he saw alien technology on a US military base, was shown it on purpose, as a test of whether he could \"reverse engineer it on the spot\". Reverse engineering means taking apart a finished object to work out how it was made. Michels offers this as his own theory in his episode with Anderson: the idea is that officials may have wanted to see how a foreign adversary who got hold of the technology might react to it.","washington-post-lucky-to-be-alive-article-on-people-who-skip-2001":"A Washington Post article from 2001 called \"Lucky to be alive\" told the stories of people who, for one reason or another, did not go to work in the World Trade Center on 9/11. The scientist Rupert Sheldrake mentions it to Michels as a published record of these near misses. He brings it up in a talk on whether people can sense danger before it happens.","washington-post-reporter-hunts-for-information-on-the-psychi-1970":"At some point between the 1970s and the 1990s, a reporter at the Washington Post began hunting for information about the US government's program to use psychics for spying. Joe McMoneagle, who worked as one of the program's psychic spies, tells Michels this, but does not name the reporter or say what the reporter found. It matters because press interest like this is how a secret program can come out into the open.","washington-quickly-ordered-roswell-bottled-up-1947":"When something crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, an event the military first announced as a \"flying disc,\" Washington moved very fast to shut the story down. Kurt McConnell, a researcher who speaks on the show as a former Senate staffer, says it with care: when Roswell \"allegedly happened, it took very little time for Washington to react.\" He names no source, but the speed would suggest that people at the top took the crash seriously from the start.","watchers-flew-to-mount-hermon-in-advanced-aerospace-craft":"The idea is that the Watchers, the fallen angels of the Book of Enoch, an ancient Jewish text that most Bibles leave out, did not simply float down from heaven but flew to Mount Hermon at the controls of advanced craft, what we would now call UFOs. A guest named Tim Alberino, who reads the old texts this way, says it twice on the same episode, each time as his own belief: 'I believe that they were at the helms of advanced aerospace vehicles.' The book itself, in R. H. Charles's 1917 translation, says the two hundred 'descended in the days of Jared on the summit of Mount Hermon,' where they swore an oath together. Later in the book God tells the Watchers they were 'formerly spiritual, living the eternal life, and immortal,' and that 'as for the spiritual ones of the heaven, in heaven is their dwelling.' So the text tells of spirits coming down from heaven to a mountaintop, in that order. Turning that descent into a landing by pilots in machines is Alberino's reading, which the book neither states nor rules out. If he is right, the ancient story would be a memory of visitors arriving by aircraft.","watching-a-test-flight-with-john-lear-and-gene-huff-1989":"Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on alien craft at a secret Nevada site, says he took his friends John Lear and Gene Huff out to watch a test flight, because he \"had the test flight schedule\" and knew it would happen on a Wednesday night. Lear's own recording from that night begins, \"today is March 22nd, 1989. We're standing just about uh 8 miles due east of Groom Lake, Nevada, the super government secret test site.\" The researcher Grant Cameron adds that the group drove out in Lear's motor home and filmed the craft, which, if it is what they say, would mean they watched a secret craft being flown.","watching-the-original-knapplazar-area-51-broadcast-1989":"In 1989 a Las Vegas TV reporter named George Knapp aired an interview with Bob Lazar, a man who said he had worked on alien craft near Area 51, the secret military test site in the Nevada desert. The author Peter Levenda tells Michels he watched that original broadcast as it went out. As he puts it, \"no one knew what that meant\" at the time.","watching-watergate-hearings-at-the-gold-key-motel-as-f-lee-b-1973":"In 1973, a lawyer named Dan Sheehan sat in the Gold Key Motel in Washington with his investigator, William Johnstone Taylor, watching the Watergate hearings while the famous defence lawyer F. Lee Bailey made a call from a pay phone. Sheehan tells Michels this from his own memory: \"I was sitting in the goldkey motel with my investigator the William Johnstone Taylor.\" He tells it as part of how he found himself close to the inside of the Watergate affair.","water-damage-suggests-the-egyptian-monuments-were-built-earl":"Some researchers argue that the Great Sphinx of Egypt shows wear from heavy rain, which would mean it was carved thousands of years earlier than archaeologists say, in a wetter age long before the pharaohs. The idea comes from the writer John Anthony West, who brought the geologist Robert Schoch to the Giza plateau, and it is retold on the show by several guests. The author Graham Hancock says Schoch concluded the marks, especially on the trench around the Sphinx, are 'precipitation-induced weathering', and quotes him: 'it's not 4 and 1/2 thousand years old. It's more like 12 and 1/2 thousand years old.' Hancock ties this to a cold period about 12,800 to 11,600 years ago. The researcher Jeffrey Drum points to the 'vertical fissures on the back of the Sphinx enclosure' as signs of much heavier rainfall, and the researcher Randall Carlson says he has looked for himself and is 'convinced that that it is water erosion', crediting the earlier thinker Schwaller de Lubicz. The researcher Matt LaCroix, who relays what Schoch told him in Turkey, says West believed the Sphinx was built in 'a previous age' and that he agrees. Michels repeats the point in several episodes, speaking of erosion on the limestone and on the Sphinx's nose. If right, it would push back the date of advanced building in Egypt by thousands of years.","water-found-in-the-osiris-shaft-at-about-37-m":"There is water below the Great Pyramid of Giza, according to a researcher named Filippo Biondi, who says it was found in the Osiris shaft, a deep shaft cut into the bedrock at Giza and named after the Egyptian god, at about 33 to 37 meters down. Biondi, known for scanning beneath the pyramids, tells Michels this \"absolutely\", without naming a source. If true, it would mean water runs through the ground under the pyramids.","water-fuelled-microwave-electrothermal-thruster-flown-by-mom":"A rocket engine that runs on plain water, heated by microwaves to push a spacecraft, has flown successfully in space aboard a craft from the company Momentus, a small \"space tug\" that moves satellites around in orbit. The physicist John Brandenburg tells Michels he invented this water-based microwave thruster himself and got it working, which would mean water alone can serve as rocket fuel.","water-through-piezoelectric-spirals-generating-electricity":"The researcher Filippo Biondi, who scanned beneath the Khafre pyramid at Giza, wonders whether water once ran through spiral structures under it that were \"made by piezoelectric material,\" a material that produces electricity when it is squeezed or shaken. He offers it as a \"maybe\" and draws on the ideas of Christopher Dunn. If so, the pyramid would have been a machine for making power rather than only a tomb.","watergate-document-shredding-at-bendix-and-saudi-troop-train-1970":"When the Watergate scandal broke in the 1970s, the shredders at the company Bendix ran overtime, according to Peter Levenda, a writer the episode's title calls a CIA historian, who says he worked there at the time. He tells Michels that his whole division \"went absolutely nuts\" over how much it had on paper, which, if true, would mean a contractor rushing to destroy its own records as the scandal grew.","watergate-used-to-remove-nixon-over-disclosure-1972":"The claim is that Watergate, the scandal that brought down Richard Nixon between 1972 and 1974, was really a way to push him out of office because he wanted to reveal what the government knew about UFOs. A UFO researcher named Steven Greer says this without naming a source: Nixon was \"hellbent on bringing this stuff out,\" and the scandal was used \"to remove him.\" Michels tells it differently on another episode. He says \"we basically know\" Watergate was \"an inside job\" set up to oust Nixon, and he credits Mark Felt, the FBI official who fed the reporter Bob Woodward, but he gives no UFO motive. If true, a president would have been removed for trying to tell the public about UFOs.","we-are-watching-you-postcard-1995":"In 1995, a year before the pilot of his TV show was shot, the television writer and producer Bryce Zabel says a postcard came to his home address in Los Angeles. On the front was the cover of a book called Flying Eyes and the words \"WE ARE WATCHING YOU.\" Zabel tells Michels this as the man who got it, and he takes it as a sign that someone was paying close attention to a UFO show before it even aired.","we-re-the-aquarium-1996":"In 1996, at the Los Angeles offices of Dark Skies, a TV drama about a government UFO cover-up, some visitors told the show's makers that they belonged to a group called \"the aquarium.\" The TV writer and producer Bryce Zabel, who was there, tells Michels the men, whom he calls JC and the captain, said: \"Well, you've heard of the Aviary,\" a circle of insiders interested in UFOs that had already turned up on a TV show, \"well, we're the aquarium.\" If true, it would mean a second hidden group of insiders was reaching out to Hollywood.","weather-bureau-employees-see-four-metallic-discs-1947":"In 1947, the year a pilot named Kenneth Arnold made the famous report that started the flying saucer era, two employees of the US Weather Bureau saw \"four metallic discs flat on the bottom, domed on the top.\" A guest named Marik von Rennenkampff tells Michels this without naming a source, as one of the sightings that backed up Arnold's.","weather-data-shows-strong-northerly-wind-at-flir1-altitude-2004":"In 2004, off Southern California, a very strong wind was blowing from the north at the altitude of the object in FLIR1. FLIR1 is the Navy infrared video of the \"Tic Tac,\" a white oval object shaped like the mint that pilots chased that year. Marik von Rennenkampff makes this point while debating the skeptic Mick West, drawing on the work of a colleague named Yannick: \"there's an extremely strong northerly wind.\" Weather data like this can show whether the object drifted with the wind like a balloon or moved against it.","weather-observer-injured-in-an-encounter-and-given-a-medical-1960":"In the 1960s, one Air Force weather observer working on the Indian Springs ranges in Nevada was injured in an encounter \"with something\" and given a medical discharge, Michels says. He tells it himself, without naming a source, while introducing a former weather observer named Charles Hall, who says he spent three years there around tall, white beings. Michels does not say what the something was.","webster-hubbell-s-book-on-clinton-s-jfk-and-ufo-inquiries-1990":"The story goes that in the 1990s President Bill Clinton and a close associate named Webster Hubbell tried to find out two things: who killed John F. Kennedy, and what is really going on with UFOs. A researcher named Steven Greer tells Michels that Hubbell admits this in a book of his own, though Greer does not recall its title. In Greer's words, Hubbell writes that \"he and the president were trying to get to the bottom of a couple things,\" Kennedy's killing and UFOs. A journalist named Jeremy Corbell tells a shorter version that leaves out the book and Kennedy. He says Clinton \"really tried\" and that Hubbell was \"crushed down\" after asking. If true, it would mean even a sitting president had to send someone digging for answers on UFOs and could not get them.","weekend-meeting-with-dick-cheney-on-why-the-iraq-war-was-bei-2003":"In 2003, a former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen says he spent a weekend at the White House with Vice President Dick Cheney, talking through why the Iraq war was being lost. Mowatt-Larssen tells Michels this as someone who was there, saying he got to know Cheney \"exceptionally well\", and he points to his own book, A State of Mind: Faith and the CIA. It places him in the inner circle of the war's planning at the highest level.","weekly-odni-briefings-for-national-security-reporters-mockin":"National-security reporters go every week to the ODNI, the office that oversees all the US intelligence agencies, to be briefed on what they will be talking about the following week. That is the claim of Matthew Brown, presented on the show as a Pentagon whistleblower, who says he knows it first-hand. He offers it as an example of what the story calls Mockingbird, a name for the idea that intelligence agencies steer the press. If true, it would mean that what the public reads on these subjects is shaped in advance.","weinbrenner-deathbed-claim-of-et-bodies-in-utah":"A man named Weinbrenner is said to have claimed on his deathbed that dead extraterrestrials are kept at a facility in Utah, which the teller identifies as Dugway Proving Ground, an Army testing range in the Utah desert. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this as research put forward by the researchers Grant Cameron and Anthony Bragalia. If true, it would mean alien bodies are stored on a US military base.","weinstein-attends-ed-witten-s-first-string-theory-lecture-1983":"In 1983, at the University of Pennsylvania, the physicist Ed Witten gave his first lecture on string theory, the idea in physics that the smallest pieces of matter are tiny vibrating strings. The researcher Eric Weinstein tells Michels he was in the room by complete accident, so he saw the start of the theory at first hand.","weinstein-on-rogan-renaissance-technologies-houses-secret-ph-2024":"The researcher Eric Weinstein suspects that Renaissance Technologies, a Long Island firm tied to Jim Simons, quietly houses a secret physics programme that employs top differential geometers, mathematicians of curved space, which is the mathematics of gravity. Michels relays that Weinstein speculated about this on the podcaster Joe Rogan's show, and Weinstein tells Michels himself that it is \"one black hole that you go into and you don't come out of,\" the only place he can find that \"hires in these exact specialties,\" and that \"if there is a secret program, I'm pretty sure it's Renaissance Technologies,\" a kind of \"Manhattan 3.0\" on gravity and UAPs, the government's term for UFOs, placed near Brookhaven and Stony Brook and crediting the physicist C. N. Yang. Michels later frames it as the possible theoretical physics wing of the UFO legacy programme, the alleged secret effort to study recovered craft.","weinstein-s-government-funded-training-and-official-disinter":"The mathematician Eric Weinstein says the government paid for his training: the Office of Naval Research funded his graduate studies, the National Science Foundation funded his postdoctoral post, and he believes he was put on a Department of Energy grant, which he calls very unusual for a mathematician, because his colleague, the mathematician Isidor Singer, had one. He tells Michels that none of these agencies show \"any interest whatsoever\" in his physics, which he finds fascinating, since it would mean the bodies that trained him ignore what he now says.","weinstein-tells-jim-simons-about-chern-simons-and-einstein-h-2024":"The mathematician and physicist Eric Weinstein tells Michels that shortly before the mathematician and investor Jim Simons died, he \"more or less accused\" Simons of what they were discussing on the show, raising Chern-Simons theory, a piece of mathematics Simons helped create, alongside the Einstein-Hilbert equations behind Einstein's theory of gravity. Weinstein says an invitation to the Simons Center at Stony Brook followed. What exactly he accused Simons of, the quote leaves unsaid, but the episode is about secrets in physics he believes the Pentagon keeps.","weinstein-told-the-same-by-multiple-unrelated-parties":"The mathematician Eric Weinstein says several people with no connection to one another have told him the same thing. He says so on the show while pressing a Pentagon scientist for UFO secrets, putting it as 'I've been told the same thing by multiple parties who are not related', without saying in that line what the thing was. To Weinstein, matching accounts from separate sources suggest there is something real behind them.","wen-ho-lee-espionage-case-1980":"A spy named Wen Ho Lee was working inside the Energy Department at its weapons laboratories in Livermore and Los Alamos. So says John S. Herrington, a former Secretary of Energy, who tells Michels about the case as someone who was there in government at the time and brings Ed Meese, Reagan's attorney general, into the telling. He raises it as an example of how secrets leaked out of the nation's most guarded labs.","wernher-von-braun-s-project-mars-and-a-leader-called-elon-1952":"In 1952 the rocket engineer Wernher von Braun, a former Nazi whom Michels calls the father of the American space program, published a science fiction novel called Project Mars: A Technical Tale. In it, Michels says, a future mission to colonise Mars is led by a figure called \"Elon\". Michels brings it up himself in an episode about the idea that the mind can sense the future, as a possible case of a writer glimpsing what was to come.","westall-landing-and-intimidation-of-mr-greenwood-1966":"In 1966 an unidentified craft is said to have landed at Westall, in Australia, and a man named Mr. Greenwood is said to have been pressured afterwards to keep quiet about it. James Fox, who has looked into the Westall case, mentions it to Michels only in passing: he says he tracked Greenwood down. He gives nothing more of what Greenwood told him.","westall-landing-case-two-witnesses-talk-after-50-years-1966":"The story is that in 1966, at Westall in Australia, hundreds of schoolchildren and a teacher watched a disc in the sky, in a case UFO investigators call a landing. The UFO documentary maker James Fox tells Michels he went there to investigate it, and that witnesses told him they had been arguing about it for 50 years. In a later episode Fox puts the crowd at more than 300 people watching \"this disc zip around in the sky,\" crediting the account to Shane Ryan and James Rigney. Another guest, Marik von Rennenkampff, gives it as 200 or 300 students, depending on the account, and a teacher, who saw a silver disc banked on its side before it took off at 1,000 miles an hour, though he says nothing of a landing. Karl Nell, a guest on an episode about the Bible and UFOs, mentions it in passing as an almost identical twin of another school sighting. If true, it would be one of the largest group sightings on record.","wewelsburg-castle-seances-of-himmler-s-ss-knights-1930":"Heinrich Himmler, the head of Hitler's SS, is said to have turned Wewelsburg Castle in Germany into a ritual centre, with a round table where twelve SS \"knights\" sat and held seances. The author Peter Levenda tells this from his own visit: he went to the castle for a TV segment 10 to 20 years ago. He describes it as a beautiful castle that was falling apart when Himmler took it over and refurbished it \"with concentration camp labor,\" building \"basically a round table for his 12 knights to sit around and have seances.\" Michels tells it separately in his own words, saying Himmler made the castle a ritual centre \"complete with a black sun chamber beneath it,\" a room under the castle named for a symbol, \"and a round table for his SS knights.\" Michels does not mention seances or the forced labour. The story is told because it would show the Nazi leadership mixing state power with occult ritual.","weygandt-s-sergeant-responds-to-uapgerb-s-email-using-his-fu-2020":"A sergeant connected to the case of a man named Weygandt answered an email by using the full real name of the researcher who wrote it, according to that researcher, who goes online as UAPGerb. He tells Michels he wrote to one of the sergeants, named in the telling as Atkins and Allen, from his UAPGerb email address, so the reply under his real name would mean the sergeant knew who was behind the anonymous account.","what-happened-to-the-egg-shaped-craft-barber-retrieved":"Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he flew an egg-shaped craft of unknown origin out of a recovery site, tells Michels what he believes became of it. He says it was taken to a secure facility and \"either destroyed or hidden or buried,\" because those in charge \"have so many of them now that it's not interesting to them.\" If true, it would mean such craft are common enough in government hands to be discarded.","wheeler-s-it-from-bit-paper-cites-observer-mechanics-1989":"The researcher Don Hoffman tells Michels that the physicist John Wheeler cited Hoffman's own work in his 1989 paper known as \"It from Bit\". The work Wheeler cited, Hoffman says, was Observer Mechanics, a theory about observers that Hoffman developed with Bruce Bennett and Chaitan Posh. Hoffman offers this as a sign that a major physicist took his ideas about the observer seriously.","when-prophecy-fails-1950s-ohio-ufo-cult":"In the 1950s a small group in the American Midwest, gathered around a woman named Dorothy Martin, believed they had received a prophecy from a UFO about an apocalypse coming in 1954, a story told in a book called When Prophecy Fails. Michels brings it up twice as an example of a modern UFO cult: once simply as a cult created in Ohio, and once, naming Martin and the year, while drawing on American Cosmic, a book by Diana Pasulka, his guest on an episode about UFOs and religion.","whistleblower-protections-gutted-from-the-december-2023-ndaa":"Steven Greer, who argues on the show that a global cabal holds the UFO secrets, says Congress nearly wrote protections for UFO whistleblowers into law in December 2023, but they were 'gutted in the reconciliation' of the National Defense Authorization Act, the yearly law that sets Pentagon policy. He cites the Senate Intelligence Committee. The law itself is an act of Congress signed on 22 December 2023 and printed by the Government Publishing Office as Public Law 118-31, and nothing about its text is disputed. Its sections on UAP (unidentified anomalous phenomena, the official term for UFOs) have the Archivist set up 'a collection of unidentified anomalous phenomena ... records in the National Archives', have 'each head of a Government office' find and prepare such records, and let release be delayed on 'clear and convincing evidence' of harm. The Senate's earlier version, the UAP Disclosure Act of 2023, gave 'Witnesses, close observers, and whistleblowers providing information directly to the Review Board' legal protection through an independent Review Board, and the final law sets up no such board. Greer does not say which protections he means. If these are the ones, the law shows how the House and Senate negotiation ended, not the bargaining that got it there.","whistleblower-saw-a-tic-tac-offloaded-from-a-ship-1991":"A Tic Tac, a white oval craft shaped like the mint, was seen being unloaded from a big ship in about 1991, according to a whistleblower whom Steven Greer, a doctor and UFO disclosure activist, does not name. Greer tells Michels this, adding that he believes the year was 1991. If true, the craft that later baffled Navy pilots was already in human hands years before.","whistleblower-threatened-with-planted-child-pornography-befo-2009":"In 2009, shortly before a whistleblower was due to testify in Washington, an SUV pulled up outside his house while his wife was at work and their two young boys were home, and the men in it told him to look at his phone and computer, where they had planted child pornography all over the devices. The UFO activist Steven Greer tells Michels this as what the whistleblower told him, and he does not name the man. If true, someone would have used the threat of a ruined life to stop a witness from speaking.","whistling-ufo-seen-near-aztec":"A UFO flew over the country near Aztec, New Mexico, making a sound almost like whistling. An unnamed local witness tells this on camera, pointing to the direction it came from, in an episode about a claimed crash near Aztec said to have left sixteen alien bodies.","white-horse-hills-5-300-m-from-the-avebury-complex":"In Wiltshire, England, several hills have giant white horses cut into their chalk, and the distance between them and the Avebury stone circles nearby is 5,300 metres, \"exactly 3.3 miles.\" Geoffrey Drumm, who studies the pyramids and ancient sites, tells Michels this. If it holds, the old sites were placed by careful measurement rather than by chance.","white-house-ai-timeline-manipulator-2020":"The idea is that something like an artificial intelligence able to steer events, \"almost like a White House timeline manipulator,\" might be at work in the present decade. Michels raises it himself in passing, in a conversation with the filmmaker Jeremy Corbell, in connection with a man named Matthew Brown, and he does not spell out what the device would be or who would run it.","white-house-asks-the-congressman-to-praise-the-release-2026":"In 2026, the day before the president released something, the White House called a congressman and asked him to say some positive things about it. Congressman Eric Burlison tells Michels this happened to him, and he brings up his fellow members of Congress Anna Paulina Luna and Tim Burchett as he tells it. It would mean the White House was working to get lawmakers to back the release publicly.","white-house-registers-aliens-gov":"The White House has registered the web address aliens.gov, according to Michels, who mentions it in passing to a former director of the NSA, the US signals intelligence agency, without giving a date or source. If true, it would hint that the government is preparing to say something public about aliens.","white-house-request-for-presidential-ufo-briefing-and-site-v-2025":"In 2025, Congressman Eric Burlison asked for the same White House level briefing on UFOs that the president gets, along with visits to sites, he tells Michels. In his words, he wanted \"a full briefing, the same White House level briefing that the president gets on this topic,\" which, if granted, would put a member of Congress in front of whatever the president is told.","white-sands-near-earth-satellite-search-led-by-lapaz-and-tom-1953":"In 1953 a very unusual project began at White Sands Missile Range in New Mexico: two scientists, Lincoln LaPaz and Clyde Tombaugh, searched the sky for unknown objects circling close to Earth, years before any human-made satellite was launched. Michels tells this himself, citing the project's final report by name but not quoting it, while talking with the astronomer Beatriz Villarroel about objects she believes orbit the planet. If such objects were found, something not made by people would have been circling Earth.","whitley-strieber-chosen-as-a-disclosure-node-because-he-was-1980":"The idea here is that the horror writer Whitley Strieber, author of Communion, a book about his own reported encounters with non-human beings, was picked by those beings to help bring their existence into the open because he wrote horror. Michels raises it himself and says Strieber has said the same, quoting him as saying \"I think they chose me as a communication node for disclosure\", disclosure being the public revealing of what is known about UFOs. If that were true, the encounters would be a deliberate message sent to the public through a writer.","whitley-strieber-s-abduction-books-and-films-1980":"Whitley Strieber is a writer who made books and films in the 1980s about being abducted by aliens himself. The podcaster Joe Rogan describes him this way to Michels, mentioning him in passing as someone known for telling his own abduction story.","whitley-strieber-s-blue-beings-the-cobalts":"The writer Whitley Strieber, known for his accounts of meetings with non-human visitors, describes a kind of blue being that he calls the cobalts. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove brings this up with Michels, relaying it from Strieber: 'Whitley Strieber talks about them. He calls them cobalts.' If such accounts are true, they would add one more distinct type to the beings that people say they have met.","whitley-strieber-s-ear-implant-evaded-the-surgeon":"The writer Whitley Strieber, who says he has met aliens, says he has an implant in his ear, seen on an X-ray, that escaped when a doctor named Roger Leir tried to remove it in San Antonio, Texas, in 1994 or 1996. Strieber tells Michels that Anne Strieber was videotaping as the doctor touched what looked like a white disc with a probe, and it sank down into his earlobe. Michels, who says he has seen the video, describes the object as moving on its own, which, if true, would mean an object that reacts to being touched.","whitley-strieber-s-traumatic-abduction-which-he-calls-a-comm-1980":"The author Whitley Strieber is said to have been abducted by non-human beings in the 1980s and anally raped, and to still put ointment on the wounds decades later, yet he calls the experience a communion. The parapsychologist Jeffrey Mishlove tells Michels this, using Strieber as an example. He tells it to show how such encounters can be both terrifying and, for those who live through them, deeply meaningful.","whitley-strieber-s-uncle-worked-with-general-exon-at-wright-1940":"The writer Whitley Strieber had an uncle who worked very closely with a General Exon at Wright Field, an Army air base in Ohio, in the 1940s. Michels mentions this in passing, crediting Strieber's book Communion, as a family link between Strieber and the officers at the base.","whitley-strieber-tries-to-steal-the-craft":"The writer Whitley Strieber once meant to steal a craft, so the story goes: he said, \"I was going to steal it,\" and someone behind him started laughing. The researcher Grant Cameron tells Michels this as something Strieber told him, without saying where or when it happened. If it is true, Strieber was close enough to some craft to think about taking it.","who-ran-the-cia-weird-desk-1960":"From the 1960s on, the CIA had a \"weird desk,\" an informal post that kept track of UFOs and the paranormal. According to the researcher Grant Cameron, three men ran it in turn: Arthur Lundahl, then Kit Green, then Ron Pandolfi. Cameron tells Michels this without naming a source; if true, the CIA kept up a quiet watch on the subject for decades.","whole-earth-catalog-page-187-15th-century-scandinavian-witch":"A man named Brent Friedman, a guest on Michels's episode about Hollywood, the CIA and UFOs, says he once came across an out-of-print book of 15th-century Scandinavian ghost stories at the Soap Plant bookstore on Melrose in Los Angeles. He tells Michels he was led to it by page 187 of the Whole Earth Catalog, a counterculture catalog of books and tools, and that the book held a tale called \"Witch Rider\". He tells the find as his own experience and treats it as an old story that bears on his larger account.","why-borland-came-forward-lives-in-danger-and-a-read-in-colle-2023":"A whistleblower named Dylan Borland tells Michels he decided to come forward in 2023 because people tied to the UAP task force, a Pentagon group set up to study UAP, the official term for UFOs, said whistleblowers' \"lives were in danger,\" and because a colleague given access to a secret program lost his job. He names Matthew Sullivan, who he says had worked in such a program and has since died, and David Grusch, a former intelligence officer who that year told Congress under oath that the US holds craft not made by humans. If true, it would mean people speaking out on this subject face real threats.","why-dopsr-let-grusch-talk-about-downed-craft-and-dead-pilots-2023":"In 2023, the Defense Office of Prepublication and Security Review (DOPSR), the Pentagon office that decides what former officials may say in public, cleared David Grusch to state that the US has several downed craft and has recovered dead pilots. Grusch is a former intelligence officer who went public with these claims. Jake Barber, a former helicopter pilot who says he helped retrieve a UFO, offers Michels his own theory of why the office allowed it. He admits it is confusing and does not spell out how the clearance worked.","why-site-november-5-was-targeted-1977":"Something picked one particular nuclear missile site, called November 5, in the missile field of Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota in 1977, and it picked it for a reason. Mario Woods, an Air Force veteran who says he was taken by aliens there, asks why it was November 5 and answers his own question: it was the closest to a town, the closest to the launch control center, and the quickest to reach from all the other nine missiles. In his view the choice shows planning by whoever was behind the encounter.","wife-s-matching-dream-of-number-cubes-at-schwandorf-church-1978":"In 1978, in Schwandorf, Germany, a woman is said to have had a dream that matched her husband's own, a dream of cubes marked with numbers at a church. Her husband, a former CIA officer named Rolf Mowatt-Larssen, tells Michels what she told him: she walked toward the end of the church in \"dim light, just some lights on the church on the side,\" and there, she said, \"I saw your desk.\" He tells it as two people sharing one dream, which, if true, would mean dreams can carry something from one mind to another.","wife-s-wedding-ring-moved-and-found-by-prayer-2024":"In 2024 a wedding ring belonging to a woman named Kristen, the wife of Brandon Fugal, is said to have moved by itself inside the Fugal home, and was then found again after prayer. Fugal, the owner of Skinwalker Ranch, a Utah property known for reports of strange events, tells Michels what his wife told him: \"her wedding ring was actually moved.\" If it happened as told, the strangeness tied to the ranch would have followed the family into their home.","wikileaks-email-hillary-clinton-asks-about-gilgamesh-s-tomb-2016":"Among the WikiLeaks emails released in 2016, there was supposedly a message in which Hillary Clinton asked about the tomb of Gilgamesh, the ancient Mesopotamian king, and about his \"resurrection\" and his body, in what is now Iraq. The researcher Tim Alberino brings this up with Michels from memory, calling the email \"alleged\" and not saying where he saw it. If it is true, a senior American official was asking about the remains of a figure most people know only from an epic poem.","wilbert-smith-document-naming-von-neumann-oppenheimer-and-va-1947":"A document linked to Wilbert B. Smith, a figure from Canada, is said to claim that the mathematician John von Neumann, Oppenheimer and the engineer Vannevar Bush were on a team doing reverse engineering (taking a device apart to learn how it works) on material from Roswell, the 1947 New Mexico incident where some say a craft crashed. An unnamed researcher tells Michels about it second-hand, saying someone \"found that document\" that was \"saying or alleging\" this. If true, it would put some of the country's leading wartime scientists at the centre of a crash-recovery effort.","wilbert-smith-s-1950-canadian-memo":"The story is about a top secret memo written in 1950 for the Canadian government by a Canadian official named Wilbert Smith, which is said to report that the US was hiding flying saucers. A researcher named Steven Greer tells Michels the memo is \"top secret from the Canadian government\" and that he thinks it \"accidentally got declassified.\" Greer says he put it in a briefing document of his own. A researcher named Grant Cameron gives its content: according to the memo, flying saucers exist and are \"the most highly classified subject in the United States,\" rated \"two points higher than the H-bomb.\" Cameron names others tied to the memo, the American scientist Vannevar Bush and two more men, Robert Sarbacher and Omond Solandt. If true, a friendly government's own records would show that in 1950 the US treated flying saucers as its highest secret.","wilbert-smith-s-secret-role-running-radio-monitoring-in-otta-1950":"In the 1950s, the story goes, a Canadian government man named Wilbert B. Smith quietly ran radio monitoring in Ottawa and held a very high security clearance. A guest named Grant Cameron tells Michels this second-hand, passing on what a man named Art Bridge told him: \"He had a super top security clearance. He ran radio Ottawa.\" If true, it would put Smith, who comes up in this subject's history, deep inside Canada's secret government work.","wild-bill-donovan-and-the-original-crash-retrieval-protocols-1930":"William \"Wild Bill\" Donovan, head of the OSS, the US wartime spy agency that came before the CIA, and one of the CIA's founders, is said to have helped recover a crashed craft near Magenta, in Italy, and to have written the first rules for recovering crashed UFOs. Michels tells this himself, crediting the journalist Chris Sharp. He adds that Donovan was a Knight of Malta, a member of an old Catholic order of knights. If true, it would mean secret recoveries of crashed craft started well before the end of the Second World War.","wild-bill-donovan-parachutes-in-with-a-butterfly-collection":"Bill Donovan, the American wartime spy chief known as Wild Bill, is said to have parachuted into Vietnam with a very rare butterfly collection taped to his chest, as a gift for the Vietnamese leader Ho Chi Minh. Joe McMoneagle, who worked as a psychic spy for the US Army, tells Michels this as a story passed on to him by someone else. If true, it would show a strangely personal tie between American intelligence and Ho Chi Minh.","wilder-penfield-and-false-memories":"Work by a doctor named Wilder Penfield, who Michels says was \"involved in early mind control stuff\", is said to show in mice that false memories can be planted. Michels says this himself in a conversation with a guest, without naming a study. He brings it up as support for the idea that memories, including memories of strange encounters, can be created or altered.","willcutts-review-of-forrestal-s-death-1949":"After James Forrestal, the former Defense Secretary, died at Bethesda Naval Hospital in Maryland in 1949, the Navy held a follow-up inquiry that the researcher Richard Dolan calls \"the Willcutts review, I think it's called.\" Dolan, who writes about the history of UFO secrecy, tells Michels he read most of its transcript, and he brings it up while arguing that Forrestal's death may have been tied to UFO secrets. He names the review but does not say on the show what in it backs that view.","william-barr-interviews-epstein-s-cellmate-who-later-dies-2019":"After Jeffrey Epstein died in his New York jail cell in 2019, William Barr, then Trump's attorney general, personally went to the cell to interview Epstein's last known cellmate, Efrain Reyes, who reportedly doubted that Epstein killed himself. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, and links it to Barr's father, Donald Barr, in an episode about Epstein and a supposed global cabal. The point is that the official account of suicide was questioned even by someone close to the scene.","william-dudley-pelley-s-star-guests-and-soulcraft-1940":"An American writer named William Dudley Pelley wrote a book called Star Guests while he was in prison, and the book founded a religion called Soulcraft. The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this without naming a source. He offers it as an example of his larger argument that beliefs about beings from the stars have shaped religion.","william-gunston-s-tower-of-london-remark-on-charged-wing-edg":"A writer named William Gunston, in a history of aero-engine technology for Air International magazine, is said to have touched on electrically charged wing edges on aircraft and then stopped short, saying he would go no further \"because I don't want to end up in the Tower of London.\" Michels quotes the line himself, as something he has read, as a hint that the subject was secret enough that a writer felt he could not say more. Neither he nor the line gives a date.","william-morris-davis-s-paper-the-value-of-outrageous-geologi-1920":"In the 1920s the geologist William Morris Davis wrote a paper called \"The Value of Outrageous Geological Hypotheses\", which argued that wild-sounding ideas deserve a hearing. The researcher Randall Carlson recommends it to Michels as \"very famous\" and says it is easy to find online as a PDF. He raises it to make the case that bold ideas, like the ones discussed on the show, are worth taking seriously.","william-steinman-pushed-out-of-aerospace-1980":"Michels says that William Steinman, a UFO researcher of the 1980s, was pushed out of the aerospace industry because of his UFO research and \"could never get a job in aerospace\" again. In a later episode Michels tells it a little differently, saying Steinman worked at Skunk Works, Lockheed's secret aircraft design shop, was looking into UFOs there, and was fired. Michels tells both versions himself, without naming a source, as an example of what UFO research could cost a career.","william-steinman-s-call-with-eric-walker-about-psi-sarbacher":"The story is that a UFO researcher named William Steinman phoned Eric A. Walker, an engineer and former president of Penn State, and Walker admitted knowing about Majestic 12. That is the name of a supposed secret panel of officials that oversaw crashed saucers, described in disputed papers known as the MJ-12 documents. Walker's name had come up because a physicist named Robert Sarbacher, as Michels tells it, \"described somebody like Eric\" as being involved, and the call is also said to have touched on psi, the mind's supposed powers. In another episode Michels says that when Steinman asked Walker directly about Majestic 12, Walker \"said he'd known about them for decades.\" Grant Cameron, a UFO researcher, gives the fullest version and dates the call to 1987. In his account Walker said, \"I was there. So what?\", then \"I've known of them MJ12 for 40 years. Leave it alone.\" Cameron says this \"convinced me that this is the real deal.\" If true, it would be a senior American scientist confirming that such a group existed.","william-steinman-s-foia-requests-reveal-the-army-ipu-1986":"The claim is that the US Army once had an office called the Interplanetary Phenomenon Unit, or IPU, a unit said to have dealt with UFO matters. The public learned of it, the story goes, through the work of a researcher named William Steinman (also called Bill Steinman). A researcher who goes by the online name UAPGerb tells Michels that the IPU 'was kind of discovered through' FOIA requests in 1986 and 1987, by 'researchers such as William Steinman.' FOIA is a US law that lets anyone request government records. UAPGerb names the researcher Kevin Randall as part of the same effort and points to the government's replies about the IPU. Michels tells the story himself in a later episode: 'The fact that we had an organization called an interplanetary phenomenon unit, we know due to Bill Steinman and his work.' He places the unit in roughly 1947 to 1950 and links the find to Steinman's book UFO Crash at Aztec, which the tellers date to 1986. If the claim holds, the Army had a named office for UFOs long before the public knew it.","william-stephenson-s-caribbean-activities-and-ties-to-scient-1940":"In the 1940s, the wartime intelligence figure William Stephenson was, Michels says, doing \"a lot of stuff\" around the Caribbean, the Bahamas and the Virgin Islands, and had ties to scientists such as the electrical engineer Charles Steinmetz and the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown. Michels tells this himself, without naming a source, linking Stephenson also to the American spy chief Bill Donovan and the publisher Robert Maxwell. If true, it would tie secret wartime intelligence work to fringe science and to later powerful networks.","william-stephenson-telegram-about-townsend-brown-s-mission-i-1945":"The story is that in 1945 an American inventor named Thomas Townsend Brown parachuted behind enemy lines into Nazi Germany to bring back technology, and that a spymaster named William Stephenson then sent his wife a telegram. Michels tells it several times. In one version he says the telegram, sent to Brown's wife Josephine, told her \"your husband is okay\". In another he passes it on from a source he names as Jan Lundquist. A writer named Paul Schatzkin, who has researched Brown's life, tells it through Brown's daughter Linda Brown. He says the telegram went to Linda's mother, came \"from the Rockefeller Plaza building\" and was signed \"Intrepid\", Stephenson's code name. None of the tellers reads the telegram out in full. Michels calls the trip a \"tech retrieval mission\". If true, Brown, whose later work is linked to UFO-like craft, was doing secret wartime work for the Allies.","wilson-denied-need-to-know-though-the-program-was-funded-fro-1997":"In 1997, Thomas R. Wilson, who ran the Defense Intelligence Agency (the Pentagon's military intelligence agency), was told he had no \"need to know\" about a secret program, meaning his duties did not entitle him to be told about it, even though the program's money came out of his own director's budget. A scientist named Eric W. Davis, who says he was involved, tells Michels the refusal was \"possible\" under the rules. If true, a program could stay hidden even from the official paying for it.","wilson-green-works-with-puharich-at-the-round-table-1950":"In the 1950s, a man named Wilson Green is said to have worked alongside Puharich, the scientist the episode's title calls a CIA scientist who contacted an alien council, at a research group and lab called the Round Table, where they were \"doing everything\". A researcher on the show, not named, says this without giving a source, though he draws on Puharich's own journals and also mentions a man named Jack Sarfatti. If true, it would add another collaborator to the circle around Puharich's work.","wilson-meeting-at-the-eg-g-parking-lot-where-bob-lazar-worke-2002":"A 2002 meeting between Admiral Thomas Wilson and Eric W. Davis, written up in Davis's notes, which are known as the Wilson-Davis memo, took place in a parking lot at EG&G, a defence contractor. Michels points out that EG&G is, he is \"pretty sure\", where Bob Lazar worked. Lazar is a man famous in UFO circles for saying he worked on alien craft. Michels makes the link himself while talking with the UFO investigator James Fox. It would tie the memo's meeting to the same company behind one of the best-known UFO accounts.","wilson-memo-on-a-private-aerospace-reverse-engineering-progr-1997":"A senior Pentagon intelligence admiral tried to get into a secret program run by a private aerospace company, was told it held crashed non-human technology, and was then shut out. This is the story of the Wilson memo, notes said to be written by a physicist and propulsion expert named Eric Davis about a talk with Admiral Thomas Wilson, then head of intelligence for the Joint Chiefs of Staff. The story dates it to 1997, though Michels sometimes puts it in 2002. Michels says the two men met in the parking lot of EG&G, a defense contractor in Las Vegas, and that Wilson was furious that he had no oversight of the program. If true, it would mean a private company holds crash material that even top officers cannot see. Davis himself tells Michels the notes are \"real... 100% accurate\", and he gives Wilson's account: Wilson met the program manager, the security chief, the lawyer and the chief scientist, and after much arguing they told him \"It is a crash retrieval, ... non-human technology, off-world.\" A researcher called UAPGerb agrees that Wilson was refused access to these \"legacy programs\", the long-running secret UFO projects, though he places Wilson at the Defense Intelligence Agency. Michels says the memo turned up in the papers of the Apollo astronaut Edgar Mitchell, and the researcher Grant Cameron says it passed from Mitchell through James Rigney in Australia to him around 2019. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says he knew about it long before, through Mitchell.","wilsondavis-meeting-outside-eg-g-las-vegas-1997":"A scientist named Eric Davis and an admiral named Thomas Wilson, who Michels says \"oversaw basically all American military technology at the time\", are said to have held an \"infamous meeting\" outside the Las Vegas special projects headquarters of EG&G, a defense contractor, in the late 1990s or early 2000s. Michels describes it himself in an episode with a guest, without naming a source or saying here what was said. He tells it because, as the story goes in this subject, the meeting was about whether the military held secret programs on unexplained craft.","winged-orange-glowing-beings-over-skywatch-groups-2010":"At skywatches, gatherings where people watch the night sky together hoping to see something, glowing orange beings that seem to have wings have flown about 25 feet over the group's heads, as many as 20 at a time. Chris Bledsoe, a man the episode says NASA studies because he seems to draw UFOs to him, tells Michels this from his own skywatches.","wiseman-fails-to-replicate-sheldrake-s-psychic-dog-experimen":"An English researcher named Richard Wiseman is said to have run \"the exact same experiments\" with a dog that the biologist Rupert Sheldrake had used to argue for animal psychic ability, and to have found no effect, publishing his results. The skeptic and researcher Michael Shermer tells Michels this during a debate, without naming the papers. In his telling it shows that Sheldrake's results did not hold up when someone else tried them.","wishbone-and-spire-structures-on-lunar-far-side-as-ancient-r":"On the far side of the Moon, in a lunar sea he calls the Paris Sea and in other craters, there are a \"wishbone structure\" and \"spire structures\" with \"unusually precise geometries,\" according to a guest named Maaneli Derakhshani. He describes them on the show without naming a source for the images. If they are what he suggests, they would be the ruins of something built on the Moon long ago.","with-aquino-at-the-afio-meeting-in-las-vegas":"The author Peter Levenda tells Michels that a former Army officer named Michael Aquino once took him to Las Vegas for a meeting of the Association of Former Intelligence Officers, a club of retired spies. In Levenda's words, Aquino said, \"We're going to go to Vegas,\" and Levenda also names John B. Alexander, a retired Army colonel, in connection with the trip, which gave him a firsthand look at the intelligence veterans tied to this subject.","witkowski-on-the-bell-project-staff-1940":"The lead scientist of the Bell, a secret Nazi wartime project, was Ernst Grawitz, the SS medical chief, according to the Polish journalist Igor Witkowski, who wrote about the project. Michels raises this himself in two episodes from 2025, citing Witkowski and saying \"we do know\" it \"for a fact\"; in the second he adds that Grawitz worked directly under Josef Mengele. If true, it would tie the project to the Nazi regime's medical crimes.","witness-burns-as-blue-shifted-light-from-a-warp-bubble":"Some witnesses who came close to an unexplained craft are said to have been burned, and a former US intelligence officer named David Grusch offers Michels an explanation from physics. A \"warp bubble\" is a proposed pocket of bent space and time around a craft, and Grusch, reasoning about a case someone described to him, says it \"sounds like visible band light being blue shifted into the ultraviolet,\" so that ordinary light is squeezed into harmful ultraviolet and \"you're getting literally a sunburn.\" If true, the burns would be a clue to how such craft move.","witness-count-for-the-varginha-ship-and-alien-bodies-1996":"In 1996, in the Brazilian town of Varginha, where a craft and strange beings are said to have been seen and captured, there are now \"maybe 12 to 15\" witnesses to the ship and \"up to five\" to the alien body. The documentary filmmaker James Fox, who has interviewed the people there, gives Michels these numbers to show how many people back the story.","witness-ed-34b-and-40b-scrubbed-from-air-force-books-2012":"Between about 2012 and 2016, $34 billion and then $40 billion were quietly wiped from the Air Force's books, and they paid for a joint reverse-engineering programme, an effort to study recovered craft and copy their technology, run between the Nevada Test and Training Range, a vast military range in Nevada, and Edwards Air Force Base. That is the account of a witness code-named \"Ed,\" retold on the show by UAPGerb, an online researcher who once covered Ed's story. If true, it would show where hidden money for such work went.","witness-ed-contacts-greer-over-suppressed-zero-point-energy":"A witness known only as Ed is said to have reached out to the UFO campaigner Steven Greer because, in his words as passed on, zero-point energy systems, machines said to draw power from empty space, \"were being harnessed and repressed.\" The online researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this as something Ed said. If true, it would mean a working new energy source was being kept from the public.","witness-ed-tests-reverse-engineered-vehicles-1998":"From about 1998 to 2002, a man known only as Ed is said to have been briefed into secret programs while serving at Nellis Air Force Base in Nevada under the 98th Range Wing, the unit that runs its test ranges, and to have tested reverse-engineered vehicles, craft built by copying recovered non-human technology. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels this as Ed's account, without naming where he heard it. If true, it would mean the US Air Force is flying craft copied from non-human ones.","witness-hears-a-buzzing-like-machine-bees-from-a-craft":"A witness says they heard a buzzing noise coming from a craft, a sound the story likens to mechanical bees. The unnamed witness describes it first-hand in a clip played during an episode with the writer Jason Jorjani, without saying where or when it happened.","witness-returns-with-luiza-and-sees-creature-s-footprint-1996":"In 1996, in Varginha, Brazil, where several people say they saw a strange creature, one witness went back to the spot with a woman named Luiza and saw the creature's footprint on the ground. The witness, who is not named, says it on the show in their own words: \"I will never forget it.\"","witness-saw-lazar-at-s-4-getting-on-a-bus-1987":"A man says he saw Bob Lazar getting on a bus at S-4, a secret site near Area 51, the Air Force test base in Nevada. Lazar claims he worked on alien craft there in the late 1980s. The filmmaker Jeremy Corbell tells Michels he got this witness to give his account publicly on Ancient Aliens, a TV series, which would back up Lazar's claim to have worked there.","witnessed-drowning-suicide-and-va-denial-of-his-service-2015":"A former Air Force serviceman named Dylan Borland says that in 2015, after he left the Air Force, he saw a man drown himself in Pittsburgh, with \"multiple witnesses,\" and that he has the police report. He tells Michels this first-hand, and says the Department of Veterans Affairs denied his service record, pointing to his police report, an Air Force performance review and his VA medical records as proof of what he went through.","witnesses-split-up-and-reassigned":"John Blitch, a scientist who worked for DARPA, the Pentagon's advanced research agency, says the US military deals with witnesses to strange events by splitting them up and reassigning them so they \"can't talk to each other.\" He gives the examples of two men he calls Terry and Toby, and of Mario Woods and Michael Johnson, and says the \"same thing\" happened to both pairs. He tells it from his own knowledge without naming a source, and it would mean the military deliberately keeps witnesses from comparing stories.","witten-townsend-brown-s-antigravity-bismuth-isotope-1950":"In the 1950s, the physicist Louis Witten says, Wright Field, an Air Force base in Ohio, hired him to work on gravity, and he heard of a man called Townsend who had a strange form of the metal bismuth that seemed to resist gravity. Michels passes this on from what Witten said, and connects the man to Thomas Townsend Brown, an inventor known for propulsion experiments; the story is tied to the Martin Corporation, an aircraft maker. If it is true, the military was seriously chasing anti-gravity materials in the 1950s.","wobbling-craft-may-have-gone-into-autopilot-1948":"In 1948, a craft said to have come down near Aztec, New Mexico, was wobbling as it flew, both before and after it hit the Archuleta Ranch, and it may have switched into a kind of autopilot. Scott Ramsey, a researcher who has spent years on the Aztec crash, tells Michels this is a theory, offered to explain why the craft then flew on and landed softly.","woman-saw-three-plus-signs-rolling-on-road-in-1930s":"In Sweden in the 1930s, a woman is said to have seen three objects shaped like plus signs rolling along a road. The Swedish researcher Clas Svahn tells Michels he heard the account from someone he names, and his telling mentions calling his mother about it (\"I called her\"). It is offered as an early sighting from well before the UFO era began.","woman-tries-to-kidnap-bledsoe-after-a-conference-talk":"As Chris Bledsoe, a man the episode says NASA studies because he seems to draw UFOs to him, walked out the door after speaking at a conference, a \"mad lady\" ran up to him and tried to kidnap him. Bledsoe tells Michels this happened to him, and in one telling names the event as what he calls the PSI games, which he pronounces \"sigh games\", in 2025.","woman-with-black-undulating-eyes-at-foot-of-bed":"The Hollywood screenwriter Brent Friedman tells Michels that one night, as he lowered the paper he was reading in bed, he found a woman standing at the foot of the bed whose eyes were \"two black circles that are undulating.\" In a second telling on the same show he says her eyes were like the cosmos, that she pinned him to the bed, that it happened in a bungalow off Melrose Avenue early in his writing career, and he calls her \"the witch rider.\"","women-believe-their-eggs-were-harvested-2002":"Some women who were taken to Zorro Ranch, a New Mexico property the episode ties to Jeffrey Epstein, believe their eggs were harvested there for human cloning. A woman named Juliet Byron tells Michels she was taken there in September 2002, thinks they took her eggs, and points out that the birth of a reportedly cloned baby called Eve was announced that December, after which, she says, nobody heard anything more. A guest named Jay Anderson repeats her account on the same show, saying she and other women share the belief and linking it to claims of a breeding program at the ranch. If true, it would put the ranch at the center of secret human experiments.","woods-s-partner-frozen-at-the-wheel-1977":"During the 1977 night at a South Dakota missile site, the Air Force security guard Mario Woods says his partner sat frozen at the wheel of their truck, and Woods could not bring him round. Woods tells Michels he was there, and says: \"I even tried pulling his hand off that steering wheel.\" He names a man called Michael Johnson in this telling. Woods had described the same moment years before in a written account published by the journalist Robert Hastings on 10 September 2017. There he writes that he \"could not figure out what was happening\" and turned to his partner, whose name is blanked out in the text, and \"saw that both his hands were on the steering wheel and he was looking straight ahead with a blank stare on his face.\" The 2017 version has the blank stare. The later one adds Woods physically trying to free his partner's hand. If it happened, it would mean whatever came near the truck could freeze a man in place.","woomera-range-chief-s-grandfather-in-secret-british-ufo-inve-1947":"The head of Woomera, a large rocket and weapons testing range in the Australian outback, is said to have a grandfather who took part in secret British government investigations of UFOs in Britain in the late 1940s and 1950s. Ross Coulthart, an Australian investigative journalist, tells Michels this as something he was told, naming Rhys Dalton Morgan in the telling. If true, it would mean Britain was quietly studying UFOs almost as early as anyone.","workers-had-severe-reactions-to-touching-the-craft-1987":"Workers at S4, a secret site in the Nevada desert where alien craft were said to be studied in the late 1980s, are said to have had \"severe reactions\" just from touching one of the craft. Bob Lazar, a man who says he worked on those craft there, tells Michels this as something he heard, not something he saw, putting it with an \"apparently.\" It would suggest the craft were made of something harmful to the human body.","working-beside-hunt-s-cia-colleague-arthur-hawkberg-in-queen-1979":"Around 1979 and 1980, in Queens, New York, the writer Peter Levenda says he worked beside a man named Arthur Hawkberg, who turned out to have been a colleague of the CIA officer E. Howard Hunt. Levenda tells Michels the two had worked together at the Robert Mullen Corporation in Washington, DC, which he calls a front, meaning a company used as cover for the CIA. If so, Levenda was working close to the CIA's world without knowing it.","working-next-to-the-z-machine-at-sandia":"The physicist John Brandenburg says he used to work next to the Z machine, a very large machine for producing powerful electrical pulses, at Sandia National Laboratories, a US government lab in New Mexico. He mentions it to Michels in passing, as part of his own career, which would place him inside the national labs' work on high-energy physics.","world-commerce-corporation-founded-1945":"In 1945, a company called the World Commerce Corporation was set up with a board drawn from the Allied wartime spy world: William Donovan, William Stephenson and Allen Dulles, with Dulles as legal counsel. The researcher Jason Simosa tells this to Michels twice. In one telling he names a board member called Ambrose; in the other he says the firm was rebranded in 1947 and based in Panama, names Charles Hambro instead, and credits the writers Peter Levenda and Peter Dale Scott. Simosa ties the company to the start of a secret network that he links to the UFO cover-up.","world-commerce-corporation-maynard-corporation-international":"This theory describes an international network operating through the World Commerce Corporation and the Maynard Corporation, which Michels calls \"this Caribbean constellation of shell sort of companies\". Michels lays it out while discussing claims by Matthew Brown, a former Pentagon employee turned whistleblower, who places the network in that corporation. Michels connects it to Otto Scorzani, to the researcher Jason Simosa and to Sekret Machines, a book series by Tom DeLonge, which would make the network a private, offshore power behind the secrets they describe.","world-forum-created-by-mikhail-gorbachev-1995-2000":"In the 1990s Mikhail Gorbachev set up a World Forum, a five-year event that ran from 1995 to 2000 and ended in New York City when the ball dropped on New Year's Eve. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says he has had repeated UFO encounters, tells Michels this as something he was told. He gives no more detail about what the forum did.","world-tree-myth-of-survivors-climbing-into-the-heavens":"Some old myths tell of a world tree that humanity climbs when a great catastrophe comes, a story the researcher Randall Carlson brings up with Michels without naming a source. He reads it as one of many tales that may carry a memory of a real ancient disaster and of people escaping it.","world-war-ii-foo-fighters-1940":"During World War II, Allied pilots reported \"foo fighters,\" their name for strange lights that showed up near their planes. The journalist Nick Cook tells Michels they were seen over Germany and were \"simply balls of light that rose up out of the countryside that appeared to have some kind of intelligent control.\" The skeptic Michael Shermer brings them up as a case that still needs explaining. The scientist John Brandenburg says they were \"commonly spotted\" while \"all sorts of radar beams\" filled the sky, yet no crashes were reported. He uses this against the idea that radar can bring such craft down.","worldwide-sphere-reports-after-the-2021-broadcast":"After a 2021 broadcast, people around the world reportedly began telling of seeing glowing or floating spheres in the sky. The journalist Ross Coulthart tells Michels he was flooded with \"phone calls, messages, emails from people all over the world,\" including a man in Africa who said they were seeing these spheres. To Coulthart it suggests the sightings were not local but happening across the globe.","woven-ceramic-fiber-optic-strip-from-roswell-seen-in-a-wisco-1950":"Charles Hall, who says he spent three years with tall white aliens at Area 51, the secret Air Force base in Nevada, says that as a boy, the summer between fifth and sixth grade, he saw a woven strip of fiber optics about a yard long in a store in London, Wisconsin. He says it was brought back from Roswell, the New Mexico site of a reported 1947 crash, by a man he believes was Ray Palmer, editor of Flying Saucers magazine, and by Palmer's childhood friend who ran the store. Hall tells it first-hand, as he also does in his book, and if true it would mean material from the crash was passed around privately.","wrecks-carrying-liquor-including-wine-from-the-1600s-2005":"The Swedish treasure hunter Dennis Asberg tells Michels that he and his diving partner Peter Lindberg found shipwrecks on the Baltic seabed still carrying liquor. Around 2005, he says, they found \"one of the oldest wine in the world,\" from the 1600s, showing how well the cold Baltic keeps what sinks into it.","wright-air-development-center-anti-gravity-research-team-1950":"Michels says that in the 1950s the Wright Air Development Center, an Air Force research base in Dayton, Ohio, had a dedicated team of physicists working on anti-gravity, citing the physicist Joshua Goldberg. In a later episode he adds that the team included Louis Witten, father of the well-known physicist Ed Witten, and ties Louis Witten to an anti-gravity division at the Martin aircraft company that Michels calls RIAS. If true, it would mean the military was studying ways to cancel gravity not long after Roswell.","wright-field-consultant-lives-with-brown-family-1950":"In the 1950s, a technical consultant named Charles Wyatt Miller from Wright Field, the Air Force base in Ohio where the military studied advanced and foreign technology, is said to have lived with the family of the inventor Thomas Townsend Brown \"at all times.\" Michels tells this as something Linda Brown, of that family, told him. If true, it would mean the military kept a man inside the household to watch over Brown's work.","wright-field-engineers-and-strange-hieroglyph-marked-materia-1943":"Between 1943 and 1947, people at Wright Field, a military airfield in Ohio that had rebuilt the German pulse jet engine in 1943, are said to have talked about material they had never seen before, marked with strange hieroglyphics. Michels tells this himself, in a debate with a skeptic, without naming a source. If true, it would mean engineers were handling unknown, possibly non-human material before the famous UFO stories of 1947.","wright-field-reverse-engineers-german-pulse-jet-1943":"In 1943, engineers at Wright Field, an American military airfield, are said to have successfully reverse engineered a German pulse jet engine. That means they took apart an enemy engine to work out how to build one themselves. Michels says this himself, without naming a source, in an episode asking whether Oppenheimer worked on UFOs. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb says the same thing on a later episode, also without giving a source. If true, it would mean the base already knew how to copy technology it had not invented.","wright-patterson-as-nuclear-antigravity-and-blue-book-site-1950":"Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio is said to have become, in the 1950s, a nuclear engineering test facility and an antigravity research center, and to have hosted Blue Book, the US Air Force's UFO study of the 1950s and 60s. Michels says this himself in two episodes, without naming a source. In the second, he adds that it is where the materials from Roswell, a craft reportedly crashed near Roswell, New Mexico, in 1947, were reportedly taken, and he leaves out the nuclear work and Blue Book. He tells it because, if true, it would put crash debris, antigravity research and the Air Force's UFO study all in one place.","wright-patterson-as-reverse-engineering-site-for-enemy-equip-1940":"Wright-Patterson, an Air Force base in Ohio, was where the military took captured enemy equipment in the 1940s to reverse engineer it, meaning to take it apart and work out how it was built, Michels says. He tells this himself, without naming a source, in his interview with David Grusch, a former intelligence officer. The point is that the base was already set up to study foreign hardware, which is why it comes up in UFO stories.","writing-the-eulogy-for-one-of-the-invisibles-2010":"There are people working on secret projects so hidden that even their own families do not know what they do, and when one of them died, Diana Pasulka, a scholar of religion who studies belief in UFOs, wrote his eulogy. Pasulka tells Michels this herself, saying that while she knew a man she calls Tyler she met others like him, whom she named the Invisibles. Her story is a glimpse of how lives inside such work stay unseen even in death.","writing-the-physics-of-angels-with-matthew-fox":"The biologist Rupert Sheldrake says he wrote the book The Physics of Angels together with the theologian Matthew Fox by picking the most interesting passages about angels from old Christian writers, Dionysius the Areopagite, Thomas Aquinas and Hildegard of Bingen, and then discussing each short passage as a dialogue between the two of them. Sheldrake tells Michels this as the book's co-author: \"that's what the book is really.\" It is his account of how a scientist came to take medieval writing about angels seriously.","writing-to-bob-lazar-about-his-experience":"Richard Barth, a former guard at a nuclear missile site who says he was taken by aliens, says he wrote to Bob Lazar, a man known for claiming he worked on alien craft for the government, about what happened to him. Barth tells Michels that in his letter he said, \"I'm not in this for anything\", meaning he wanted no money or fame from it.","written-incident-report-at-colonel-spraker-s-office-never-gi-1977":"After his 1977 encounter at a nuclear missile site, Mario Woods says he was made to write a full report of what happened, and, as the story goes, he never got a copy. Woods, a former Air Force missile security guard, tells Michels that Colonel Spraker handed him forms at Ellsworth Air Force Base in South Dakota and said, \"I need you to write down every single thing that happened on that site tonight from the time you got this alarm to the completion.\" He places Jack Reed, a captain in the Air Force Office of Special Investigations (the Air Force's own investigative service), at the debriefing beside another of its agents, Rick Doty. Woods also wrote about the night for the researcher Robert Hastings, who published it on 10 September 2017. In that account the debriefing is with the Flight Chief and his assistant, who \"met us at the facility\", and Woods says, \"I did fill out an AF Form 1000 UFO sighting report and wrote all I knew about that night and early morning, before I was allowed to sleep.\" His meeting with the wing commander comes \"the next day\" at Ellsworth. So the 2017 text puts the written report at the missile facility and the later telling puts it in the colonel's office, a difference of place, unless he wrote a second report that the 2017 account does not mention. If the report exists, it would be an official written record of the night.","wsj-claim-that-air-force-hazing-rituals-explain-ufo-mytholog-2025":"For decades, some new commanders of the Air Force's most secret programs were shown a fake flying saucer photo and told it was real, and that practice helped spread belief in alien technology. That is what a Wall Street Journal article by Joel Schectman, \"The Pentagon Disinformation That Fueled America's UFO Mythology\", reported on 6 June 2025. Michels reads it on the show as claiming that \"Air Force hazing rituals explain widespread UFO mythology\", and he says he disagrees with it. If the article's account is right, some officers' UFO stories began as an initiation trick. The article says the officers were handed \"a piece of paper with a photo of what looked like a flying saucer\" and told the program they were joining, \"dubbed Yankee Blue, was part of an effort to reverse-engineer the technology on the craft\", meaning to work out how it was built and copy it. It calls those witnesses \"victims of a bizarre hazing ritual.\" Yet it names this as only \"one long-running practice\" among several, alongside fake documents spread around Area 51, myths allowed to grow at nuclear sites and a supposed piece of space metal. Whether it is \"the basis for the persistent belief that the U.S. has an alien program\" is put as a question asked by the intelligence chief Avril Haines, so going from some witnesses to UFO belief as a whole is Michels's reading of how far the article goes. In a later episode Michels says the piece actually makes him optimistic.","wtc-resident-artist-s-painting-of-planes-flying-into-him-12-2001":"Twelve days before 9/11, an artist who had a studio in one of the Twin Towers made a work showing himself as a Tuskegee Airman, one of the Black American pilots of the Second World War, with old fighter planes flying into him. Michels tells it himself without naming a source, and calls it a painting made under an arts programme in the towers. The researcher Eric Wargo, who writes about dreams and seeing the future and cites his own book Time Loops, tells it on two episodes with more detail. He names the artist as Michael Richards and calls the work a sculpture titled Tar Baby vs. St. Sebastian. Wargo says the Lower Manhattan Cultural Council gave studios in the towers to groups of 15 artists for six months at a time. He says Richards \"alone among those artists\" stayed in his studio the night of September 10th and was killed there the next morning. The tellers differ on whether it was a painting or a sculpture, and they tell it because, if it is what they say, an artist pictured his own death before it happened.","wwii-crash-retrieval-of-enemy-technology-norden-bombsight-ca-1940":"In the Second World War the United States recovered enemy technology from crashes, for example capturing Japanese Zero fighter planes in the Aleutian Islands \"just before Midway,\" alongside the guarding of secrets like the Norden bombsight. The skeptic Michael Shermer brings this up on the show without a named source. His point is that crash retrieval of foreign hardware is an ordinary wartime practice, which offers a down-to-earth comparison for claims about recovered UFOs.","x-files-pilot-craft-design-based-on-john-duza-stories-1993":"A researcher who goes by UAPGerb tells Michels that a real craft design, which he calls the XF-131, appeared in the very first episode of The X-Files in 1993, and that the episode was said to be based on true stories from a man named John Duza. He adds that a man named Bill McDonald was contracted to put the design into the episode, and he mentions Chris Carter in connection with it, though he gives no source for any of this. If true, a design tied to secret aircraft would have reached television hidden in plain sight.","xerxes-and-alexander-visited-the-sites":"The ancient sites around Lake Van, in eastern Turkey, were visited by the Persian king Xerxes, by Alexander the Great and by \"many many other great people throughout history,\" according to the researcher Matt LaCroix. He tells Michels this without naming a source, in a conversation about something he believes happened around 12,000 BC. His point is that rulers of the ancient world treated these places as important long after they were built.","y-12-complex-at-oak-ridge-and-kirkpatrick-s-move-there-2023":"The Y-12 complex in Oak Ridge, Tennessee, comes up as the last stop on a list of places tied to Sean Kirkpatrick, the official who led the Pentagon's UFO office in its first years, and the story's title connects it to his move there sometime between 2023 and 2025. A researcher who goes by UAPGerb says it to Michels in passing, \"lastly we have the Y12 complex in Oak Ridge, Tennessee\", summarising without naming a source. If it holds up, the man who ran the Pentagon's UFO inquiry went on to an Oak Ridge site.","y-12-oak-ridge-legacy-program-studying-the-skin-of-two-recov":"A man is said to have worked at the Y-12 complex at Oak Ridge, Tennessee, a government nuclear site, on a long-running secret UFO programme that studies the outer skin of two recovered saucers built by non-humans. The online researcher known as UAPGerb tells Michels this as what the man \"has stated,\" without naming him, and also names the research contractors MITRE and Battelle. If true, it would mean pieces of non-human craft are being studied at a US nuclear facility.","yahweh-acknowledges-the-gods-of-egypt-in-exodus":"The Hebrew God, Yahweh, admits that the gods of Egypt exist, the researcher Luke Caverns tells Michels, pointing to Exodus 12, where God tells Moses he will bring his wrath on them. In the King James Bible of 1611 the verse reads \"against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgment: I am the LORD\", so the text does speak of judging those gods. Whether it treats them as real beings or as idols and the people who worship them, the verse leaves open as a matter of reading.","yanomami-wusinari-insect-beings-and-multitudinous-dancing-sp":"The Yanomami, an Indigenous people of the Amazon rainforest, are said to know of beings they call the wusinari, insect beings, along with many dancing spirits. The neuroscientist Andrew Gallimore brings this up with Michels in a conversation about DMT, a powerful psychedelic drug, and he gives it as a summary without naming where it comes from. He raises it because, if such accounts match what people meet on the drug, it would suggest that different peoples have run into the same kind of beings.","yelin-group-meditation-synchronized-binaural-session-with-ab":"At an outdoor place called Yelin, about 30 people meditated together while listening in sync to binaural sound, tones played slightly differently in each ear to guide the mind, and the group made contact with orbs, balls of light. Jordan Jose, who says he was taken into a UFO program as a child, tells Michels he led the group himself: \"I took a group of about 30 people.\"","yellow-orb-tags-along-with-atlas-launch-1964":"In 1964 at Vandenberg Air Force Base in California, a yellow orb flew up and followed an Atlas missile after its launch, according to Richard Barth, a former guard of nuclear missiles who says he saw it. He tells Michels that once the second stage fired, \"this yellow orb came over and tracked the missile,\" which, if true, would mean something unknown was watching US missile tests.","yeltsin-asks-about-his-trip-up-there-1997":"In the Kremlin in the late 1990s, Boris Yeltsin asked a regional leader about his reported trip aboard an alien craft, saying: \"It's been reported to me that you've been up there.\" The leader in question, Kiran Ilum Xenov, then president of one of Russia's republics, tells Michels this himself, as the man who says he was taken by aliens in 1997. If true, it would mean the claim reached the very top of the Russian state.","young-hitler-and-lanz-von-liebenfels-s-order-of-the-new-temp-1900":"In Vienna around 1900 to 1903, a young Adolf Hitler is said to have visited Lanz von Liebenfels, a mystic who founded a secretive group called the Order of the New Templars, in his office. The researcher and author Peter Levenda tells Michels this from a source he does not name (\"It said that he actually visited\" him), and it ties to his book Unholy Alliance. If true, it would link Hitler early on to the occult circles of Vienna.","young-people-gathered-to-pilot-craft-at-black-facilities":"Very young people are said to be picked out from many parts of the world and brought to \"black facilities,\" secret government sites whose existence is never acknowledged, so that they can \"more effectively pilot these craft.\" A philosopher named Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as one of the \"wild stories\" he has heard from others. If true, it would mean the government is training people to fly craft it does not admit to having, and that the young have some special fitness for it.","younger-dryas-comet-impact-12-800-years-ago":"The claim is that about 12,800 years ago Earth ran through the debris trail of a breaking-up comet, an idea known as the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis. The writer Graham Hancock, who argues for lost ancient civilizations, describes it on the show and credits the Comet Research Group, a group of researchers who study the idea. Michels, in another episode, places the debris in the Taurid meteor stream, calls it an airburst near the end of the last ice age, and adds that it may have wiped out an early civilization, crediting Hancock, a researcher named Randall Carlson, and Plato.","younger-dryas-impact-caused-the-great-flood-and-reset-civili":"The claim is that a cosmic impact around 12,000 years ago, at the time called the Younger Dryas near the end of the last ice age, brought on the great flood and wiped out an earlier civilisation, so that the world's flood myths would be memories of a real catastrophe. The podcaster Joe Rogan says on the show that nothing could stop such a strike and that \"that's probably what happened during the younger dryas impact\". Michels's pseudonymous co-host Jack, who says he has worked for years in defence and aerospace, mentions in passing \"the Randall Carlson stuff and the Graham Hancock stuff, the reset 12,000 years ago\", crediting the researcher Randall Carlson and the writer Graham Hancock, without naming any impact or flood. Rogan points to old texts, though not to particular passages. If he means the flood on the Eleventh Tablet of the Gilgamesh epic, in E. A. Wallis Budge's 1920 translation, the gods resolve \"to make a wind-storm\", \"A black cloud from the foundation of heaven came up\", and the Anunnaki, whom Budge glosses as star-gods of the southern sky, \"brandished their torches\" and \"With their glare they lighted up the land\"; that is the tablet's nearest image to fire from the sky, and reading it as an impact is a step the tablet neither states nor excludes. If he means the Book of Enoch, in R. H. Charles's 1917 translation, Enoch dreams that \"the heaven collapsed and was borne off and fell to the earth\" and the earth \"was swallowed up in a great abyss\", and elsewhere Noah is warned that \"a deluge is about to come upon the whole earth\". The book tells this as a judgement for sin; reading the falling heaven as an impact memory is the teller's step, which the text neither states nor excludes.","zahari-greets-john-paul-at-their-first-meeting-a-friendship-2023":"In 2023 in Georgia, a nonspeaking boy named Zahari is said to have walked into a house and greeted another boy, John Paul, as an old friend, though they were meeting for the first time, and the two said their friendship had been built \"on the hill,\" a place they meet without being there in body. The filmmaker Ky Dickens, who made a documentary about children said to share thoughts by telepathy, tells Michels what she was told, with a woman named Libby among her sources. If true, the boys would have known each other only through their minds.","zahi-hawass-pumps-out-the-osiris-shaft-and-opens-the-contain":"The Egyptian archaeologist Zahi Hawass and his team pumped all the water out of the Osiris shaft, a deep shaft with several levels under the Giza plateau, so they could lift the lid off a stone container on the third level. The researcher Jeffrey Drum tells Michels this, relaying it as Hawass's own work, and says he does not know when it happened. He raises it in a discussion of what he believes lies beneath the pyramids.","zalce-jailed-five-days-by-the-mexican-navy-2020":"Sometime in the 2020s, a man named Jose Zalce says, the Mexican Navy put him in jail for about five days. He tells Michels they called him a liar and said he was \"putting in risk the honorability\" of the whole Navy, which would mean he was punished for speaking out, in a case tied to his claims about DNA tests on one of the three-fingered bodies found in Peru.","zalce-maria-s-dna-is-30-completely-different-2020":"DNA tests on the large mummified bodies from Peru, such as one called Maria, showed about 17 percent known DNA but about 30 percent \"completely different,\" according to Jose Zalce, who says he worked on these three-fingered bodies himself. In another passage he tells Michels, citing a man named David Ron Hell, that the known parts matched a chimpanzee, a bonobo, another African chimpanzee and a South Asian primate, which, if true, would mean the bodies are partly unlike anything known.","zamparini-s-wheat-field-where-a-spaceship-landed-1997":"An Italian named Maurizio Zamparini had a wheat field where a spaceship landed on several occasions, according to Kiran Ilum Xenov, a guest who says he was himself taken by aliens in 1997 and who repeats what Zamparini told and showed him in the late 1990s. Xenov tells Michels the landings were recorded on golden plates bearing imprints of the craft and in photographs taken by scientists.","zanesville-metal-seen-moving-under-a-microscope":"Metal said to come from an unexplained source in Zanesville, Ohio, was seen moving under a microscope. Chris Bledsoe, a man who says UFOs appear around him and whom the show describes as studied by NASA, tells Michels he watched it in real time: there were different types of material, and one piece was moving. A moving metal would suggest it is not ordinary manufactured material.","zero-gravity-bonding-of-ceramic-to-metal-with-human-dna-used":"Scientists are said to have learned, in the zero gravity of space, how to bond ceramic to metal and then put human DNA into the ceramic, a technique the story says ended up in medical implants. Chris Bledsoe, a man the episode says NASA studies because he claims to attract UFOs, tells Michels this as something he was told by someone else. If true, it would mean advanced materials work from space quietly made its way into medicine.","zero-point-drives-work-as-time-machines":"An engine running on 'zero-point' energy, the energy some physicists say is present even in empty space, would also work as a time machine, according to the idea Jason Jorjani explains. Jorjani, a philosopher, tells Michels the argument comes from a book on UFOs and time travel by the author Michael Masters, which Jorjani cites in his own book Closer Encounters. If true, craft that use such drives could be travelling through time as well as through space.","zigzagging-light-during-fourth-of-july-fireworks-2000":"On the Fourth of July in 2000, during the fireworks at a park along a wash near his home, Richard Barth saw a light that began to move in a zigzag. Barth, a former nuclear missile guard who says he was abducted by aliens, tells Michels he saw it himself, and a man named Roger comes up in his telling.","zimbardo-s-stanford-prison-experiment-1971":"Michels brings up the Stanford Prison Experiment, a 1971 study at Stanford run by the psychologist Philip Zimbardo in which volunteers were cast as prison guards and prisoners, and calls it \"all very similar\" to other research he is discussing. He says it himself, in an episode about a secret society he links to the UFO cover-up, and does not spell out what the similarity is.","ziusudra-utnapishtim-s-ark-lands-in-the-ararat-region":"The flood hero of ancient Mesopotamia, known as Ziusudra and as Utnapishtim, brought his ark to rest in the Mount Ararat region of Armenia and Turkey, the researcher Matt LaCroix tells Michels. He points to the old Babylonian flood poem Atrahasis and to the Epic of Gilgamesh, and if he is right, the Mesopotamian flood and the Bible's would be one remembered event. In E. A. Wallis Budge's 1920 British Museum translation of the epic's Eleventh Tablet, Uta-Napishtim tells Gilgamesh the story himself; he is the \"man of Shurippak, son of Ubara-Tutu,\" and he says \"The ship took its course to the land of Nisir\" and \"The mountain of Nisir held the ship, it let it not move.\" The tablet names its own mountain, Nisir, while Genesis has the ark rest \"upon the mountains of Ararat,\" so putting the landing at Ararat follows Genesis rather than the tablet. Read for what happens, the two stories share a shape: a man warned of a flood, a great boat, a mountain that holds it fast. A story can travel and change its place names while keeping a memory, so the different mountain neither proves nor disproves a link. Ziusudra is the name an older Sumerian telling gives its flood hero, and treating him as the same man as Uta-Napishtim is an identification the tablet alone cannot make.","zodiac-crash-retrieval-program-1998":"A secret program called Zodiac, tied to the defense contractor TRW, is said to recover crashed UFOs, a work the subject calls crash retrieval. An online researcher who goes by the name UAPGerb tells Michels the story comes from UFO Magazine, which in 1998 published a piece under the pen name Greg Halifax about \"Sedge Masters,\" a man \"brought into a UFO crash retrieval program called Zodiac,\" and he mentions the UFO historian Richard Dolan in connection with it. If true, it would be an inside account of a program that holds crashed craft.","zorro-ranch-breeding-program-claims-2000":"Jeffrey Epstein, the story goes, planned to get several women pregnant at his Zorro Ranch in New Mexico in the 2000s, as a breeding programme for children with \"superior genetics\". The philosopher Jason Jorjani tells Michels this as the claim of women who say they were taken to the ranch for that purpose. In his essay \"Ocean Outlaw: A Portrait of Ghislaine Maxwell\", published on 29 July 2025, Jorjani puts it in his own voice, writing that \"Epstein planned to impregnate multiple women at his New Mexico 'Zorro Ranch'\", modelled on the Nazi SS's Lebensborn project and the \"transhumanist ideas of people like George Church\". The essay itself names none of the women and gives no source for the claim, so whose account it rests on is left open there.","zulu-legend-that-the-moon-brought-us-out-of-the-younger-drya":"A Zulu legend from southern Africa holds that the moon brought humanity out of the Younger Dryas, a sudden cold spell near the end of the last ice age, and made Earth's climate stable. Michels tells it himself, without naming a source, on an episode exploring the idea that the moon is an alien spaceship. If the legend carries a memory, it would suggest people remembered the moon arriving or changing at that time."};
